const SURAHS = [
{
  num:69, name:"Al-Ḥāqqah", ar:"الحاقة", en:"The Inevitable Reality", verses:52, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"AL-ḤĀQQAH (v.1) → ḤAQQ AL-YAQĪN (v.51). Told (102:5) → Seen (102:7) → BEING IN IT (69:51).",
  special:["It opens with al-ḤĀQQAH and closes with ḤAQQ al-yaqīn — and that last phrase is the top rung of the ladder you climbed in Takāthur.","V.1: al-ḥāqqah — \"The Inevitable Reality.\" v.51: wa innahu la-ḥaqqu'l-yaqīn — \"And indeed, it is the TRUTH of certainty.\"","Recall Takāthur 102:5–7 — the three rungs of certainty: - ʿilm al-yaqīn — certainty by being TOLD (Takāthur 102:5) - ʿayn al-yaqīn — certainty by SEEING (Takāthur 102:7) - ḥaqq al-yaqīn — certainty by BEING INSIDE IT — HERE, Ḥāqqah 69:51.","When I wrote \"a third rung found elsewhere\" in Takāthur — this is where. The lab report, the scan, and being the patient: this surah is the third.","And the surah is full of a sound you met in Qāriʿah 101:10 (mā hiyah): the pause-hāʾ (hāʾ as-sakt) — kitābiyah, ḥisābiyah, māliyah, sulṭāniyah (v.19–29). Two men speak on Judgment Day, and every line ends on that held breath. One is ecstatic; one is ruined. Same sound, opposite feelings.","--"],
  big:["The name, three times (v.1–3) — the Inevitable; what is it?","The nations who denied it (v.4–10) — Thamūd, ʿĀd, Pharaoh, the overturned cities.","The flood and the ship (v.11–12) — when the water overflowed, We carried you.","The blast (v.13–18) — earth and mountains crushed; the sky torn; the Throne carried by eight; nothing hidden.","The right-hand man (v.19–24) — \"Here! Read my book!\"","The left-hand man (v.25–37) — \"I wish I'd never been given my book.\"","The oath (v.38–43) — by what you see and don't see: this is no poet, no soothsayer.","If he had fabricated (v.44–47) — We would have cut his aorta.","The close (v.48–52) — a reminder, a regret, the truth of certainty — so glorify."],
  traps:["صَلُّوهُ (v.31) — ROAST him (ṣaliya), NOT \"pray\" (ṣallā) — identical spelling in Arabic letters.","حَمِيمٌ (v.35) — a WARM FRIEND here — but SCALDING WATER in Nabaʾ 78:25. Same word, opposite senses.","ظَنَنتُ (v.20) — CERTAINTY here, not \"assumption\" (compare Jinn 72:5).","أَحَدٍ... حَاجِزِينَ (v.47) — singular aḥad with a PLURAL adjective — because aḥad in negation means \"anyone at all.\"","الْخَاطِئُونَ (v.37) — khāṭiʾ (Form I) = DELIBERATE sinner; mukhṭiʾ (Form IV) = honest mistake.","كِتَابِيَهْ etc. — the final hāʾ is a hāʾ as-sakt (pause), NOT \"her\" — kitābī = MY book. Tajwīd: māliyah halaka — pause or merge.","هَاؤُمُ (v.19) — an ism fiʿl (\"here, take!\") — not a pronoun.","ثَمُودُ / عَادٌ (v.4) — Thamūdu = DIPTOTE; ʿĀdun = fully declined.","ثَمَانِيَةَ أَيَّامٍ (v.7) — numbers 3–10 take the OPPOSITE gender.","أَرْجَائِهَا (v.17) — arjāʾ = EDGES, ≠ rajāʾ (HOPE).","الْمُؤْتَفِكَاتُ (v.9) — the OVERTURNED cities — same root as ifk (a lie).","لَمْ أُوتَ / لَمْ أَدْرِ (v.25–26) — jussives: final vowel letters DROP.","تَذَكَّرُونَ (v.42) — tatadhakkarūna with a tāʾ dropped.","وَمَا هُوَ بِقَوْلِ (v.41) — the extra bi- after negation = emphasis.","وَالْمَلَكُ (v.17) — singular for COLLECTIVE (\"the angels\").","لَقَوْلُ رَسُولٍ كَرِيمٍ (v.40) — identical to Takwīr 81:19 but usually a DIFFERENT messenger (Muhammad ﷺ here; Jibrīl there).","مُلَاقٍ (v.20) · لَيَالٍ (v.7) — MANQŪṢ (final yāʾ drops with tanwīn).","دُكَّتَا (v.14) — DUAL (earth + mountains)."],
  links:[{w:"الْحَاقَّةُ ×3 (v.1–3)",e:"الْقَارِعَةُ ×3 (Qāriʿah 101:1–3)",t:"Identical three-step opening"},{w:"الْحَاقَّةُ (v.1) · حَقُّ الْيَقِينِ (v.51)",e:"",t:"Bookends: the TRUE Day / the TRUE Book"},{w:"حَقُّ الْيَقِينِ (v.51)",e:"عِلْمَ / عَيْنَ الْيَقِينِ (Takāthur 102:5, 7)",t:"The TOP RUNG of certainty"},{w:"بِالْقَارِعَةِ (v.4)",e:"الْقَارِعَةُ (101:1)",t:"Same Day, two names"},{w:"بِالطَّاغِيَةِ (v.5)",e:"بِطَغْوَاهَا (Shams 91:11)",t:"Transgression → destroyed by excess"},{w:"طَغَى الْمَاءُ (v.11)",e:"78:22 · 79:17 · 89:11 · 96:6",t:"Ṭaghā in its LITERAL sense — water overflowing"},{w:"صَرْصَرٍ (v.6)",e:"عَسْعَسَ · دَمْدَمَ · زُلْزِلَتْ · الْوَسْوَاسِ",t:"Reduplicated roots — + silsilah (v.32)"},{w:"لَيَالٍ (v.7)",e:"وَلَيَالٍ عَشْرٍ (Fajr 89:2)",t:"Manqūṣ"},{w:"أَعْجَازُ (v.7)",e:"نُّعْجِزَ (Jinn 72:12)",t:"Stumps ← ʿajz (incapacity) ← muʿjizah"},{w:"بَاقِيَةٍ (v.8)",e:"أَبْقَىٰ (87:17) · لَا تُبْقِي (74:28)",t:"Nothing lasts but the Hereafter"},{w:"الْمُؤْتَفِكَاتُ (v.9)",e:"Ifk (a lie)",t:"Overturned cities / overturned truth"},{w:"بِالْخَاطِئَةِ (v.9) · الْخَاطِئُونَ (v.37)",e:"خَاطِئَةٍ (ʿAlaq 96:16)",t:"Deliberate sin"},{w:"أَخْذَةً رَّابِيَةً (v.10)",e:"أَخْذًا وَبِيلًا (Muzzammil 73:16)",t:"Swelling seizure / downpour seizure"},{w:"الْجَارِيَةِ (v.11)",e:"الْجَوَارِ (81:16) · عَيْنٌ جَارِيَةٌ (88:12)",t:"The ship = \"the runner\" — ṣadaqah jāriyah"},{w:"أُذُنٌ وَاعِيَةٌ (v.12)",e:"فَأَوْعَىٰ (Maʿārij 70:18) · أَصَابِعَهُمْ فِي آذَانِهِمْ (Nūḥ 71:7)",t:"Retaining ear / hoarding vessel / blocked ears"},{w:"نَفْخَةٌ وَاحِدَةٌ (v.13)",e:"زَجْرَةٌ وَاحِدَةٌ (79:13) · الصُّورِ (78:18)",t:"ONE blast"},{w:"دَكَّةً وَاحِدَةً (v.14)",e:"دَكًّا دَكًّا (Fajr 89:21)",t:"ONE blow / blow upon blow"},{w:"حَمَلْنَاكُمْ · حُمِلَتْ · يَحْمِلُ (v.11, 14, 17)",e:"",t:"Three carryings: rescue, ruin, majesty"},{w:"انشَقَّتِ (v.16)",e:"انشَقَّتْ (Inshiqāq 84:1)",t:"Identical verb"},{w:"وَاهِيَةٌ (v.16)",e:"سَبْعًا شِدَادًا (Nabaʾ 78:12)",t:"FIRM → FRAIL"},{w:"وَالْمَلَكُ (v.17)",e:"وَالْمَلَكُ صَفًّا صَفًّا (Fajr 89:22)",t:"Singular for collective"},{w:"عَرْشَ (v.17)",e:"ذِي الْعَرْشِ (81:20 · 85:15)",t:"The Throne"},{w:"تُعْرَضُونَ (v.18)",e:"يُعْرِضْ (72:17) · مُعْرِضِينَ (74:49)",t:"Turned their side → put on display"},{w:"V.19 (right hand)",e:"Inshiqāq 84:7",t:"Word-for-word"},{w:"ظَنَنتُ (v.20)",e:"ظَنَّ (Qiyāmah 75:28) · ظَنَنَّا (Jinn 72:5, 12)",t:"Ẓann = certainty vs assumption"},{w:"مُلَاقٍ (v.20)",e:"فَمُلَاقِيهِ (Inshiqāq 84:6)",t:"He KNEW he'd meet it"},{w:"كِتَابِيَهْ · حِسَابِيَهْ · مَالِيَهْ · سُلْطَانِيَهْ",e:"مَا هِيَهْ (Qāriʿah 101:10)",t:"Hāʾ as-sakt ×6"},{w:"عِيشَةٍ رَّاضِيَةٍ (v.21)",e:"Qāriʿah 101:7",t:"Word-for-word"},{w:"جَنَّةٍ عَالِيَةٍ (v.22)",e:"Ghāshiyah 88:10",t:"Word-for-word"},{w:"قُطُوفُهَا دَانِيَةٌ (v.23)",e:"دَانِيَةً... قُطُوفُهَا (Insān 76:14)",t:"Same two words"},{w:"أَسْلَفْتُمْ (v.24)",e:"تُقَدِّمُوا (73:20) · قَرْضًا (73:20) · رَهِينَةٌ (74:38)",t:"The banking set: advance payment"},{w:"بِشِمَالِهِ (v.25)",e:"وَرَاءَ ظَهْرِهِ (Inshiqāq 84:10) · الشَّام (Quraysh 106:2)",t:"Left = north"},{w:"يَا لَيْتَنِي (v.25, 27)",e:"78:40 · 89:24",t:"The four impossible wishes"},{w:"الْقَاضِيَةَ (v.27)",e:"لَمَّا يَقْضِ (ʿAbasa 80:23)",t:"Never fulfilled → wishes to be finished"},{w:"مَا أَغْنَىٰ عَنِّي مَالِيَهْ (v.28)",e:"92:11 · 111:2",t:"Principle → named man → his own mouth"},{w:"فَغُلُّوهُ · سِلْسِلَةٍ (v.30, 32)",e:"سَلَاسِلَ وَأَغْلَالًا (Insān 76:4)",t:"Insān listed; Ḥāqqah applied"},{w:"صَلُّوهُ (v.31)",e:"ṣallū (pray) · 74:43",t:"Wouldn't PRAY → is ROASTED — identical spelling"},{w:"فَاسْلُكُوهُ (v.32)",e:"72:17 · 72:27 · 74:42 · 71:20",t:"The salaka thread"},{w:"الْعَظِيمِ (v.33, 52)",e:"",t:"Rejected → glorified (rukūʿ)"},{w:"V.34 (whole verse)",e:"Māʿūn 107:3 · Fajr 89:18",t:"Word-for-word with Māʿūn"},{w:"حَمِيمٌ (v.35)",e:"حَمِيمًا (Nabaʾ 78:25) · حَمِيمٌ حَمِيمًا (Maʿārij 70:10)",t:"Warm friend / scalding water"},{w:"غِسْلِينٍ (v.36)",e:"ضَرِيعٍ (88:6) · غُصَّةٍ (73:13)",t:"Hell's foods"},{w:"لَا أُقْسِمُ (v.38)",e:"75:1 · 81:15 · 84:16 · 90:1",t:"The oath-opener"},{w:"لَقَوْلُ رَسُولٍ كَرِيمٍ (v.40)",e:"Takwīr 81:19",t:"Word-for-word — Jibrīl there, Muhammad ﷺ here"},{w:"كَاهِنٍ (v.42)",e:"Jinn 72:8–9, 26–27",t:"The soothsayers' supply cut off"},{w:"تَنزِيلٌ (v.43)",e:"تَنزِيلًا (Insān 76:23) · أَنزَلْنَاهُ (Qadr 97:1)",t:"In stages / all at once"},{w:"رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ (v.43)",e:"Fātiḥah 1:2 · Takwīr 81:29",t:""},{w:"الْوَتِينَ (v.46)",e:"حَبْلِ الْوَرِيدِ (Qāf 50:16)",t:"Aorta (His power) / jugular (His nearness)"},{w:"أَحَدٍ... حَاجِزِينَ (v.47)",e:"أَحَدٌ (Ikhlāṣ 112:4) · أَحَدٌ (Jinn 72:22)",t:"Aḥad in negation = plural meaning"},{w:"حَسْرَةٌ (v.50)",e:"يَا لَيْتَنِي (v.25, 27)",t:"Regret in action"},{w:"فَسَبِّحْ بِاسْمِ رَبِّكَ الْعَظِيمِ (v.52)",e:"سَبِّحِ اسْمَ رَبِّكَ الْأَعْلَى (Aʿlā 87:1)",t:"RUKŪʿ and SUJŪD"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"الْحَاقَّةُ", tr:"Al-ḥāqqah", v:"1, 2, 3", root:"The \"TRUTH / what is firmly established and DUE\" family — ḥaqqa = to be true, to be due, to be inevitable.", meaning:"\"The Inevitable Reality\" — a name for the Day.", magic:"Another NAME for the Day — and the one that sums up all the others.", grammar:"Active participle (fāʿilah) as a NAME, feminine (an implied as-sāʿah, the Hour).", why:"ḥāqqah (the due, the true) — the opposite of everything its deniers said: \"it's false, it won't come.\"", note:"Al-ḤĀQQAH = the Day that is TRUE and DUE — the bill that must be paid. Opening = Qāriʿah 101:1–3 exactly.", more:["ḥaqq = TRUTH and a RIGHT (ḥuqūq al-insān = human rights); al-Ḥaqq = a NAME OF ALLAH; taḥqīq = investigation; ḥaqīqah = reality.","You own: an-Nabaʾ (the News, 78:2), aṭ-Ṭāmmah (the Flood, 79:34), aṣ-Ṣākhkhah (the Deafener, 80:33), al-Ghāshiyah (the Enveloper, 88:1), al-Qāriʿah (the Striker, 101:1). Al-Ḥāqqah = the one that is TRUE and DUE — the bill that must be paid.","Classical: it realizes (taḥuqqu) everything — what people denied becomes real, and every person gets what is due (ḥaqq) to them.","And the three-step opening is IDENTICAL to Qāriʿah 101:1–3: al-qāriʿah · ma'l-qāriʿah · wa mā adrāka ma'l-qāriʿah al-ḥāqqah · ma'l-ḥāqqah · wa mā adrāka ma'l-ḥāqqah.","Name → question → \"what could tell you?\" And v.4 even uses al-qāriʿah as a synonym!","The root is everywhere in your guides: al-yawmu'l-ḥaqq (Nabaʾ 78:39), wa ḥuqqat (Inshiqāq 84:2, 5 — the sky \"rightly obeyed\"), tawāṣaw bi'l-ḥaqq (ʿAṣr 103:3) — and its peak, ḥaqq al-yaqīn, ends this surah.","v.1 is a one-word verse (like Qāriʿah 101:1). Ḥāqqah has a doubled qāf (from ḥ-q-q).","Opens al-ḥāqqah → closes ḥaqq al-yaqīn.","Ḥuqūq al-insān = HUMAN RIGHTS."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ", tr:"Wa mā adrāka", v:"3", root:"Adrā — to make someone know (the darā family).", meaning:"\"And what could make you know what the Inevitable is?\"", magic:"The formula you now own from ELEVEN places (82:17–18, 83:8, 83:19, 86:2, 90:12, 97:2, 101:3, 101:10, 104:5, 74:27).", grammar:"Two mā's — interrogative + subject clause.", why:"The standard Qurʾānic pause before something larger than description.", note:"Wa mā adrāka — TWELFTH appearance. Past = answer follows; present (80:3) = none.", more:["And here, 69:3. Adrāka (past) = an answer FOLLOWS (v.4–52 is the answer); yudrīka (present, ʿAbasa 80:3) = no answer."]},
  {ar:"كَذَّبَتْ ثَمُودُ وَعَادٌ بِالْقَارِعَةِ", tr:"Kadhdhabat thamūdu wa ʿādun bi'l-qāriʿah", v:"4", root:"Kadhdhaba — to DENY / call a lie (Form II).", meaning:"\"Thamūd and ʿĀd denied the Striking Calamity.\"", magic:"The surah immediately swaps names: v.1 al-Ḥāqqah, v.4 al-Qāriʿah — the same Day, two names — the TRUE one and the STRIKING one. They denied it was TRUE; it arrived STRIKING.", grammar:"Thamūdu = DIPTOTE (no tanwīn); ʿĀdun = fully declined (tanwīn).", why:"Al-qāriʿah — renaming the Day mid-breath, so its truth and its impact are fused.", note:"V.1 AL-ḤĀQQAH (TRUE) → v.4 AL-QĀRIʿAH (STRIKING) — same Day. Denied it was true; it arrived striking.", more:["Thamūd / ʿĀd — two ancient Arab nations (Fajr 89:6–9, Shams 91:11, Burūj 85:18). al-qāriʿah — the STRIKER (Qāriʿah 101:1 — qarʿ al-bāb = knocking on a door).","And the order is reversed: Thamūd before ʿĀd — even though ʿĀd came first historically — classical note: v.5 deals with Thamūd first because its punishment-word (ṭāghiyah) launches the rhyme.","Also: Thamūd is nearer to the Quraysh (their ruins at al-Ḥijr were on the trade road to Shām — Quraysh 106:2's summer caravan passed them!).","You own both nations: Fajr 89:6–9 — ʿĀd (Iram of the pillars) and Thamūd (who carved rocks in the valley).","Shams 91:11–14 — Thamūd and the she-camel.","A three-letter name with a quiet middle letter is allowed full declension.","kadhdhabat = feminine verb (nations treated as feminine).","Thamūdu = DIPTOTE; ʿĀdun = fully declined.","Quraysh's caravans passed Thamūd's ruins."]},
  {ar:"فَأُهْلِكُوا بِالطَّاغِيَةِ", tr:"Fa-uhlikū bi'ṭ-ṭāghiyah", v:"5", root:"Uhlikū — the \"DESTRUCTION / perishing\" family — halaka = to perish (Balad 90:6 ahlaktu mālan lubadā; istihlāk = CONSUMPTION — modern economics!).", meaning:"\"As for Thamūd, they were destroyed by the overwhelming blast.\"", magic:"The ṭaghā thread — and a perfect mirror.", grammar:"Uhlikū = Form IV passive (destroyed — doer unnamed). bi- = instrumental (\"by\").", why:"ṭāghiyah — makes the punishment the same SHAPE as the sin.", note:"Shams 91:11: Thamūd denied through ṬAGHWĀ (transgression). Ḥāqqah 69:5: destroyed by AṬ-ṬĀGHIYAH.", more:["ṭāghiyah — the \"OVERFLOWING its limit\" family — ṭaghā = water bursting its banks → transgression; ṭāghiyah = an overwhelming excess (also, in modern Arabic, ṭāghiyah = a TYRANT!).","Shams 91:11 — kadhdhabat thamūdu bi-ṭaghwāhā — \"Thamūd denied — through their TRANSGRESSION.\" Ḥāqqah 69:5 — fa-uhlikū bi'ṭ-ṭāghiyah — \"destroyed by the OVERWHELMING [blast].\" They overflowed their limits (ṭughyān) → they were destroyed by something that overflowed ALL limits (ṭāghiyah).","Same root: their sin and their punishment.","Classical: aṭ-ṭāghiyah = the shout (ṣayḥah) that exceeded every limit of loudness.","The ṭaghā thread — now EIGHT surahs: Nabaʾ 78:22, Nāziʿāt 79:17, 37, Fajr 89:11, Shams 91:11, ʿAlaq 96:6, Ḥāqqah 69:5, and v.11 (ṭaghā'l-māʾ — the LITERAL meaning!).","fa-ammā opens the two-panel structure (Thamūd / ʿĀd).","Overflowed limits → destroyed by what overflowed all limits.","Ṭāghiyah = a TYRANT (modern); istihlāk = CONSUMPTION."]},
  {ar:"بِرِيحٍ صَرْصَرٍ عَاتِيَةٍ", tr:"Bi-rīḥin ṣarṣarin ʿātiyah", v:"6", root:"Rīḥ — WIND (rūḥ and rāḥah share letters — Qadr 97:4). ṣarṣar — a REDUPLICATED root.", meaning:"\"And as for ʿĀd, they were destroyed by a roaring, freezing wind, violently defiant.\"", magic:"Say ṣar-ṣar — the word HOWLS.", grammar:"ṣarṣarin and ʿātiyatin = two adjectives of rīḥ. rīḥ is FEMININE (hence ʿātiyah).", why:"ṣarṣar — onomatopoeia: you hear the gale in the word.", note:"ṢAR-ṢAR — the word HOWLS. Ṣirr = bitter COLD. Reduplicated family: ʿasʿasa, damdama, zalzala, waswasa, ṣarṣar.", more:["A howling, roaring, bitterly COLD wind (ṣirr = intense cold; the sound itself is ṣar-ṣar — a hiss-roar).","ʿātiyah — the \"EXCEEDING all bounds / insolence\" family — ʿatā = to be insolent, to go beyond limits; ʿutuww = arrogant insolence; ʿātin = defiant.","The reduplicated-root family you own: ʿasʿasa (81:17), damdama (91:14), zalzala (99:1), waswasa (114:4), ṣarṣar (69:6). Doubled roots = repeated, relentless actions — a wind that roars again and again.","ʿĀtiyah = INSOLENT — the wind itself is described with a word for arrogance.","Classical: it \"defied\" its keepers — it came out beyond the measure normally released — and it matched ʿĀd's own arrogance (they boasted man ashaddu minnā quwwah — \"who is stronger than us?\" 41:15). Arrogant people → an \"arrogant\" wind.","The same pattern as v.5: the punishment mirrors the sin.","And rīḥ — the same letters as rūḥ (the Spirit) and rāḥah (rest) — air that gives life, rest — or destroys.","ʿĀdun — fully declined (contrast Thamūdu).","ʿĀtiyah = INSOLENT — an arrogant wind for an arrogant people."]},
  {ar:"سَخَّرَهَا عَلَيْهِمْ سَبْعَ لَيَالٍ وَثَمَانِيَةَ أَيَّامٍ حُسُومًا", tr:"Sakhkharahā ʿalayhim sabʿa layālin wa thamāniyata ayyāmin ḥusūmā", v:"7", root:"Sakhkhara — the \"SUBJECTING / putting into service\" family.", meaning:"\"He unleashed it upon them for seven nights and eight days in succession — cutting them off.\"", magic:"Three precise words. Sakhkharahā — He SUBJECTED it — the wind was a SERVANT carrying out orders.", grammar:"Layālin = MANQŪṢ (final yāʾ drops with tanwīn).", why:"ḥusūm — both the unbroken duration and the clean severing in one word.", note:"ḤUSĀM = a SHARP SWORD → ḥusūm = UNBROKEN and CUTTING — a week-long sword. Ḥāsim = DECISIVE.", more:["Sakhkhara = to make something serve a purpose, to subjugate (taskhīr = harnessing; sukhrah = forced labour; sukhriyah = MOCKERY — making someone your object of ridicule!).","layālin — NIGHTS (manqūṣ — the final yāʾ dropped: layālī → layālin — like Fajr 89:2 wa layālin ʿashr!).","ḥusūm — the \"CUTTING OFF / decisive severing\" family — ḥasama = to cut off decisively; ḥusām = a SHARP SWORD (one that cuts cleanly!); ḥāsim = DECISIVE; ḥusūm = consecutive, unbroken — and cutting them off.","The same verb is used for the sun, moon, rivers, ships \"subjected\" to serve humanity (14:32–33). The same wind that serves you was sent against them.","Seven nights and EIGHT days — classical: it began on a morning and ended on an evening, so the days outnumber the nights by one.","Precise, eyewitness-level detail.","Ḥusūmā — two meanings fused: (a) CONSECUTIVE — no break, no relief, no pause; (b) CUTTING — like a ḥusām (sword), severing them from existence.","And ḥāsim = DECISIVE — the end was decisive.","The layālin hook: Fajr 89:2 — wa layālin ʿashr — same manqūṣ form.","thamāniyata — numbers 3–10 take the OPPOSITE gender of the counted noun: ayyām (masc.","plural) → thamāniyah (fem.","ḥusūman = ḥāl or maf'ūl muṭlaq.","Sakhkhara = SUBJECTED — the wind as a servant.","Layālin = manqūṣ (Fajr 89:2); thamāniyah = reverse gender."]},
  {ar:"فَتَرَى الْقَوْمَ فِيهَا صَرْعَىٰ", tr:"Fa-tara'l-qawma fīhā ṣarʿā", v:"7", root:"The \"FELLING / throwing to the ground\" family — ṣaraʿa = to throw someone down, to fell (muṣāraʿah = WRESTLING!", meaning:"\"…so you would see the people lying in it, struck down…\"", magic:"Fa-tarā — \"you WOULD SEE\" — the Qurʾān puts YOU at the scene, as a witness. You stand among the bodies.", grammar:"Tarā — 2nd person, addressed to the listener (vivid).", why:"ṣarʿā — the defeat of the mighty, pictured as a wrestling throw.", note:"MUṢĀRAʿAH = WRESTLING — the mightiest people (89:8) PINNED BY WIND. Fa-tarā — YOU are placed at the scene.", more:["ṣirāʿ = a STRUGGLE / conflict; ṣarʿ = EPILEPSY — the \"falling\" sickness — a clinical word!); ṣarʿā = plural of ṣarīʿ — those FELLED, struck down.","Ṣarʿā = FELLED, thrown down — the giants of ʿĀd (famous for their size and strength — allatī lam yukhlaq mithluhā fi'l-bilād — Fajr 89:8) — wrestled to the ground by WIND.","Muṣāraʿah = WRESTLING — the strongest people on earth, pinned by air.","Clinical hook: ṣarʿ = EPILEPSY (the \"falling\" illness) — same root.","ṣarʿā = ḥāl, broken plural of ṣarīʿ (faʿlā pattern, weak-final).","Ṣarʿ = EPILEPSY (the falling sickness)."]},
  {ar:"كَأَنَّهُمْ أَعْجَازُ نَخْلٍ خَاوِيَةٍ", tr:"Ka-annahum aʿjāzu nakhlin khāwiyah", v:"7", root:"Aʿjāz — the \"REAR / the base / INCAPACITY\" family — ʿajuz = the REAR, the hindmost part (here the STUMPS / trunks of palms); ʿajz = INCAPACITY — the root of muʿjizah (MIRACLE) — Jinn 72:12!; ʿajūz = an old person.", meaning:"\"…as if they were the trunks of hollow, uprooted palms.\"", magic:"THE SIMILE — and three precise layers: (1) PALMS — tall, like the giants of ʿĀd.", grammar:"Ka-anna = \"as if.\" aʿjāzu + nakhlin (iḍāfah).", why:"Khāwiyah (hollow) — reveals that the \"mighty\" were empty inside all along.", note:"Headless PALM STUMPS, HOLLOW inside. The giants were empty all along.", more:["khāwiyah — the \"EMPTY / HOLLOW\" family — khawā = to be empty; khāwī = EMPTY (everyday: maʿidah khāwiyah = an empty stomach!).","(2) AʿJĀZ — the STUMPS / bases — the heads gone, only the trunks left on the ground (classical: the wind tore off their heads, leaving bodies like headless trunks).","(3) KHĀWIYAH — HOLLOW — empty inside, rotted out.","The giants who seemed so solid were HOLLOW all along.","And aʿjāz shares a root with ʿajz (INCAPACITY) — the root of muʿjizah!","Jinn 72:12 — lan nuʿjiza'llāha fi'l-arḍ — \"we can never render Allah INCAPABLE.\" ʿĀd thought themselves incapable of defeat; they became aʿjāz — stumps, the very shape of INCAPACITY.","Compare Qāriʿah 101:5's mountains like carded wool, Fīl 105:5's army like chewed straw — the Qurʾān's images of the mighty reduced: hollow palm-stumps.","And compare Qamar 54:20 — ka-annahum aʿjāzu nakhlin munqaʿir (palms uprooted) — same simile, different adjective.","khāwiyatin — feminine adjective (nakhl as a collective treated as feminine).","AʿJĀZ (stumps) ← ʿajz (INCAPACITY) — the root of MUʿJIZAH (Jinn 72:12). Maʿidah KHĀWIYAH = an EMPTY STOMACH.","Chewed straw (105:5), carded wool (101:5), hollow stumps (69:7)."]},
  {ar:"فَهَلْ تَرَىٰ لَهُم مِّن بَاقِيَةٍ", tr:"Fa-hal tarā lahum min bāqiyah", v:"8", root:"The \"REMAINING / lasting\" family — baqiya = to remain; bāqī = remaining (al-Bāqī = the Everlasting).", meaning:"\"So do you see any remnant of them?\"", magic:"A question that answers itself: NO.", grammar:"Hal = rhetorical (expecting \"no\"). min + indefinite in a negative/question = min az-zāʾidah lil-istighrāq.", why:"Bāqiyah — total erasure: not one survivor, not one trace.", note:"\"Do you see ANY remnant?\" — NO. Iram of the pillars (89:7) — no trace.", more:["Baqāʾ = permanence; Aʿlā 87:17 — khayrun wa abqā; Muddaththir 74:28 — lā tubqī wa lā tadhar.","Hal tarā — \"do you SEE\" — continuing v.7's \"you would see.\" Min bāqiyah — the min = \"even ONE\" — \"any remnant AT ALL?\" The nation that built Iram of the pillars (Fajr 89:7) — no trace.","And bāqiyah can mean a surviving person, a surviving soul, or a lasting legacy — none.","The baqiya thread: Aʿlā 87:17 — the Hereafter is abqā (more LASTING).","Muddaththir 74:28 — Saqar lā tubqī (leaves NOTHING remaining).","Ḥāqqah 69:8 — ʿĀd left no bāqiyah (NOTHING remaining).","Only the Hereafter is abqā; dunya's mightiest leave no bāqiyah.","\"ANY at all\" (total negation).","Baqiya: the Hereafter is ABQĀ (87:17); Saqar lā TUBQĪ (74:28); ʿĀd: no BĀQIYAH (69:8). Min + indefinite = \"ANY AT ALL.\""]},
  {ar:"وَجَاءَ فِرْعَوْنُ وَمَن قَبْلَهُ وَالْمُؤْتَفِكَاتُ بِالْخَاطِئَةِ", tr:"Wa jāʾa firʿawnu wa man qablahu wa'l-muʾtafikātu bi'l-khāṭiʾah", v:"9", root:"Firʿawn — PHARAOH (diptote).", meaning:"\"And Pharaoh came, and those before him, and the overturned cities — with sin.\"", magic:"THE BEST ROOT IN THIS SECTION — the overturned cities and the overturned truth.", grammar:"Al-muʾtafikāt = Form VIII participle, feminine plural (the cities).", why:"Muʾtafikāt — names the cities by the shape of their destruction — and the root links it to the lie.", note:"MUʾTAFIKĀT = the cities TURNED UPSIDE DOWN (Lūṭ's). IFK = a LIE — it turns TRUTH upside down (Ḥadīth al-Ifk).", more:["muʾtafikāt — the \"OVERTURNING / turning upside down\" family — afaka = to turn something over, to invert; muʾtafikāt = the OVERTURNED CITIES (the cities of Lūṭ — Sodom and its sisters — turned upside down, 11:82); ifk = a LIE / slander (a lie TURNS THE TRUTH UPSIDE DOWN — Ḥadīth al-Ifk = the slander against ʿĀʾishah!).","khāṭiʾah — the \"SIN / missing the mark\" family — khaṭiʾa = to sin deliberately; khaṭīʾah = a SIN; ʿAlaq 96:16 — nāṣiyatin kādhibatin khāṭiʾah!","Al-muʾtafikāt = the cities that were TURNED UPSIDE DOWN — literally inverted (jaʿalnā ʿāliyahā sāfilahā, \"We made their highest part their lowest\" — 11:82). And the same root gives IFK — a LIE — because a lie turns the truth on its head.","The people who turned NATURE upside down had their cities turned upside down.","The punishment, again, shaped like the sin.","Bi'l-khāṭiʾah — \"with SIN\" — khaṭiʾa (Form I) = to sin KNOWINGLY — different from akhṭaʾa (Form IV) = to make an honest MISTAKE.","ʿAlaq 96:16 — nāṣiyatin kādhibatin khāṭiʾah — the lying, SINFUL forelock of the man who blocked prayer.","And v.37 returns: al-khāṭiʾūn — the ones who eat ghislīn.","And PHARAOH again — Nāziʿāt 79:17–25, Fajr 89:10, Burūj 85:18, Muzzammil 73:15–16 — the most-mentioned tyrant in your study.","khaṭiʾa (Form I) = DELIBERATE sin; akhṭaʾa (Form IV) = honest MISTAKE.","They inverted nature; their cities were inverted.","Khaṭiʾa = SIN on purpose; akhṭaʾa = honest MISTAKE.","ʿAlaq 96:16: the khāṭiʾah forelock."]},
  {ar:"فَعَصَوْا رَسُولَ رَبِّهِمْ فَأَخَذَهُمْ أَخْذَةً رَّابِيَةً", tr:"Fa-ʿaṣaw rasūla rabbihim fa-akhadhahum akhdhatan rābiyah", v:"10", root:"ʿaṣaw — they DISOBEYED (Muzzammil 73:16 fa-ʿaṣā firʿawnu'r-rasūl; ʿaṣā = a staff).", meaning:"\"They disobeyed their Lord's messenger, so He seized them with a seizure ever-increasing [in severity].\"", magic:"Rābiyah = SWELLING — the root of RIBĀ. A seizure that GREW — exceeding every other punishment, mounting beyond what they'd seen.", grammar:"ʿaṣaw — weak-final plural (ʿaṣayū → ʿaṣaw).", why:"Rābiyah — a punishment that compounds, like interest.", note:"RIBĀ = USURY (money that SWELLS) → rābiyah = a seizure that SWELLED. Rabwah = a HILL.", more:["akhdhah — a SEIZURE (Muzzammil 73:16 akhdhan wabīlā).","rābiyah — the \"SWELLING / INCREASING / rising\" family — rabā = to grow, swell, rise; RIBĀ = USURY / INTEREST (money that swells!); rabwah = a HILL (ground that rises!); rābiyah = swelling, exceeding, ever-increasing.","Like ribā — debt that keeps swelling — their punishment kept growing.","Rabwah = a HILL — a punishment that rose up like a hill over them.","Compare Muzzammil 73:16 — fa-akhadhnāhu akhdhan wabīlā — Pharaoh seized with a seizure heavy as a DOWNPOUR.","Here: akhdhatan rābiyah — a seizure that SWELLED.","Heavy (Muzzammil), swelling (Ḥāqqah) — the same seizure, described twice.","Rasūla rabbihim — singular \"messenger\" for several nations — classical: each nation's own messenger (a generic singular) — or Mūsā specifically.","akhdhatan = maf'ūl muṭlaq (a single seizure — the -ah = \"one instance\") + adjective rābiyatan.","Muzzammil 73:16: akhdhan WABĪLĀ (heavy as a downpour).","Here: akhdhatan RĀBIYAH (swelling).","Akhdhah = ONE seizure (the -ah of one instance)."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّا لَمَّا طَغَى الْمَاءُ حَمَلْنَاكُمْ فِي الْجَارِيَةِ", tr:"Innā lammā ṭagha'l-māʾu ḥamalnākum fi'l-jāriyah", v:"11", root:"ṭaghā — WATER OVERFLOWING ITS LIMITS — the original, physical meaning.", meaning:"\"Indeed, when the water overflowed [its limits], We carried you in the ship.\"", magic:"Ever since Nabaʾ 78:22, I've told you: ṭaghā = water BURSTING ITS BANKS → transgression = a person overflowing his limits.", grammar:"Lammā + past = \"when.\" al-jāriyah = active participle as a noun (the noun safīnah, ship, is implied).", why:"ṭaghā — reveals the origin of the transgression-word you've tracked through eight surahs.", note:"ṬAGHA'L-MĀʾ — the WATER overflowed — THE LITERAL MEANING of ṭaghā, finally. They overflowed in sin → the water overflowed on them.", more:["ḥamalnā — We CARRIED (ḥaml = pregnancy; ḥammāl = a porter — Masad 111:4 ḥammālat al-ḥaṭab).","al-jāriyah — the \"FLOWING / running\" family — jarā = to flow, run; jāriyah = a SHIP (the one that runs on water); ʿayn jāriyah = a flowing spring (Ghāshiyah 88:12); al-jawārī = the running stars (Takwīr 81:16); ṣadaqah jāriyah = ONGOING charity (that keeps flowing!).","THE BIGGEST ROOT PAYOFF IN THE WHOLE SERIES — ṭaghā in its LITERAL sense.","HERE IS THE WATER ITSELF: ṭagha'l-māʾ — \"the WATER overflowed.\" Nūḥ's flood.","The root's physical picture, finally shown — and in the SAME surah as Thamūd destroyed bi'ṭ-ṭāghiyah (v.5).","And the full irony: the generation of Nūḥ OVERFLOWED (ṭaghaw) in sin → and the WATER overflowed (ṭaghā) on them.","The metaphor became literal.","Ḥamalnākum — \"We carried YOU\" — not \"your ancestors\" but \"YOU\" — because you were inside them; every human alive descends from those in the ark.","Al-jāriyah — the \"RUNNING one\" — a ship named for its flowing motion.","The same root: ʿayn jāriyah (Paradise's flowing spring, 88:12), al-jawārī (the running stars, 81:16), and — your best hook — ṣadaqah jāriyah, charity that keeps FLOWING after you die.","The ark: a jāriyah that carried humanity forward.","And this sets up Sūrat Nūḥ (71), two surahs later — the full story of the man who built the jāriyah.","\"We carried YOU\" — you were on that ship.","Al-JĀRIYAH = the SHIP (the \"runner\") — like ṢADAQAH JĀRIYAH (flowing charity), ʿayn jāriyah (88:12), al-jawārī (81:16). Sets up Sūrat Nūḥ (71)."]},
  {ar:"لِنَجْعَلَهَا لَكُمْ تَذْكِرَةً", tr:"Li-najʿalahā lakum tadhkirah", v:"12", root:"Tadhkirah — a REMINDER (the juzʾ's spine).", meaning:"\"…that We might make it a reminder for you…\"", magic:"The flood and the ark were made a TADHKIRAH.", grammar:"Li- = purpose + subjunctive. -hā = it (the event / the ship).", why:"History told not as information but as reminder.", note:"The ARK = a tadhkirah (v.12); the QURʾĀN = a tadhkirah (v.48). Both carry people to safety.", more:["The dhikr thread: Insān 76:29, ʿAbasa 80:11, Takwīr 81:27, Muzzammil 73:19, Muddaththir 74:49, 54 — and the surah's own end: v.48 wa innahu la-tadhkiratun li'l-muttaqīn.","The ark was a reminder (v.12); the Qurʾān is a reminder (v.48).","Both carry people to safety."]},
  {ar:"وَتَعِيَهَا أُذُنٌ وَاعِيَةٌ", tr:"Wa taʿiyahā udhunun wāʿiyah", v:"12", root:"Taʿiya / wāʿiyah — the \"CONTAINING / retaining / holding in a vessel\" family.", meaning:"\"…and that a retaining ear might retain it.\"", magic:"Udhunun wāʿiyah = \"an ear that RETAINS\" — an ear like a VESSEL (wiʿāʾ) — what goes in, STAYS in. Not an ear that hears and leaks — an ear that holds.", grammar:"Taʿiya = subjunctive of the doubly-weak verb waʿā (the initial wāw drops in the present: yaʿī).", why:"Waʿā (to hold in a vessel) — because remembering is retaining, not just hearing.", note:"Udhunun WĀʿIYAH = an ear like a VESSEL — what goes in STAYS in.", more:["Waʿā = to hold, to retain, to keep inside; wiʿāʾ = a CONTAINER, a vessel (awʿiyah dammawiyyah = BLOOD VESSELS!); waʿy = AWARENESS, CONSCIOUSNESS (modern: waʿy = consciousness; fāqid al-waʿy = unconscious!).","udhun — an EAR (adhān, the call to prayer; idhn, permission — Qadr 97:4).","THE MOST BEAUTIFUL PHRASE IN THE SURAH.","And waʿy = CONSCIOUSNESS — the retaining ear is the CONSCIOUS ear.","The waʿā root returns in the NEXT surah — Maʿārij 70:18 — wa jamaʿa fa-awʿā — \"he gathered [wealth] and put it in a VESSEL\" (hoarded it).","Same root: one man's VESSEL holds the reminder (69:12); another's holds money (70:18). What does your container hold?","And the EAR thread: Qadr 97:4 — bi-idhni rabbihim (permission — what you \"give ear\" to).","Nūḥ 71:7 — jaʿalū aṣābiʿahum fī ādhānihim — Nūḥ's people put their FINGERS in their EARS.","Ḥāqqah: the ear that RETAINS.","Nūḥ: ears BLOCKED with fingers.","Two surahs apart, the two kinds of ears.","Udhunun is singular and indefinite — \"even ONE ear\" — classical: the reminder is worth it even if only one listener truly retains it.","Clinical hook: awʿiyah dammawiyyah = BLOOD VESSELS; fāqid al-waʿy = UNCONSCIOUS.","wāʿiyatun = feminine active participle (udhun is feminine).","WAʿY = CONSCIOUSNESS; awʿiyah dammawiyyah = BLOOD VESSELS; fāqid al-waʿy = UNCONSCIOUS.","Maʿārij 70:18: awʿā — hoarding MONEY in a vessel.","Nūḥ 71:7: FINGERS IN THEIR EARS.","Even ONE retaining ear makes it worth it."]},
  {ar:"فَإِذَا نُفِخَ فِي الصُّورِ نَفْخَةٌ وَاحِدَةٌ", tr:"Fa-idhā nufikha fi'ṣ-ṣūri nafkhatun wāḥidah", v:"13", root:"Nufikha — the \"BLOWING\" family — nafakha = to blow (nafkh = blowing / INFLATION — munfakh = inflated; nafkhat ar-rūḥ = the breathing-in of the soul).", meaning:"\"Then when the Trumpet is blown with a single blast…\"", magic:"Not a long campaign — ONE breath, and the universe collapses. Nafkhah — the -ah of ONE INSTANCE (ism al-marrah) — and wāḥidah says it again: just ONE.", grammar:"Nafkhatun = ism al-marrah (faʿlah = ONE instance) — NOMINATIVE here as the nāʾib al-fāʿil (deputy subject) of the passive nufikha.", why:"Nafkhatun wāḥidah — the smallest possible act causing the largest possible event.", note:"NAFKHATUN WĀḤIDAH = ONE blast — the -ah of ONE instance + \"wāḥidah\" = double singleness. ONE blast (v.13) → ONE crush (v.14).", more:["NAFKHATUN WĀḤIDAH — ONE blast.","Double emphasis on its singleness = on Allah's effortlessness.","The \"one blast\" thread: Nāziʿāt 79:13 — fa-innamā hiya zajratun wāḥidah (ONE shout).","Nabaʾ 78:18 — yawma yunfakhu fi'ṣ-ṣūr (same verb, same horn).","Muddaththir 74:8 — nuqira fi'n-nāqūr (the horn PECKED).","And v.14 continues: dakkatan wāḥidah — ONE crushing.","One blast (v.13), one crush (v.14).","Nafakha is also the verb of life — nafakha fīhi min rūḥih (Allah breathed His spirit into Ādam, 32:9). The same \"blowing\" that gave life ends this world.","wāḥidatun = adjective (emphasis).","Nafkh = INFLATION; nafakha fīhi min rūḥih = the breath of LIFE (32:9)."]},
  {ar:"وَحُمِلَتِ الْأَرْضُ وَالْجِبَالُ فَدُكَّتَا دَكَّةً وَاحِدَةً", tr:"Wa ḥumilati'l-arḍu wa'l-jibālu fa-dukkatā dakkatan wāḥidah", v:"14", root:"ḥumilat — was LIFTED / carried (ḥamala; v.11 ḥamalnākum!).", meaning:"\"…and the earth and the mountains are lifted up and crushed with a single crushing.\"", magic:"THE dakk ROOT — and a beautiful DUAL. Fajr 89:21 — idhā dukkati'l-arḍu dakkan dakkā — \"pounded — pounding upon pounding\" (REPEATED blows).", grammar:"Dukkatā = DUAL passive past (the earth + the mountains).", why:"Dakkah wāḥidah — complements Fajr's \"pounding upon pounding\" with the divine view: one act.", note:"Fajr 89:21: dakkan DAKKĀ (blow after blow). Ḥāqqah 69:14: dakkatan WĀḤIDAH (ONE blow). Our view vs His.", more:["dukkatā — the \"POUNDING FLAT / levelling\" family — dakka = to pound, to crush flat (dakkah = a BENCH / a raised platform — everyday!; dukkān = a SHOP — originally a raised platform for selling!).","Ḥāqqah 69:14 — fa-dukkatā dakkatan wāḥidah — \"crushed — with ONE crushing.\" Fajr: blow after blow.","Both true — from our side it feels like endless pounding; from His side it's one act.","The DUAL — dukkatā (\"the TWO were crushed\") — the earth and the mountains, treated as TWO masses — lifted up together and slammed together into one.","Ḥumilat — LIFTED up — v.11: We CARRIED you (ḥamalnākum) in the ship to SAVE you.","v.14: the earth is CARRIED (ḥumilat) to be CRUSHED.","Same verb, rescue and ruin.","The mountain-fates thread — now SEVEN images: mirage (78:20), set moving (81:3), pounded (89:21), carded wool (101:5), sliding sand (73:14), lifted and crushed in one blow (69:14), and v.9 of Maʿārij (ka'l-ʿihn).","Everyday hook: dukkān = a SHOP (from a flat platform).","dakkatan = maf'ūl muṭlaq + ism al-marrah (ONE instance).","DUKKATĀ = DUAL — earth + mountains slammed together.","v.11 CARRIED to be saved; v.14 CARRIED to be crushed.","Dukkān = a SHOP; dakkah = a BENCH."]},
  {ar:"فَيَوْمَئِذٍ وَقَعَتِ الْوَاقِعَةُ", tr:"Fa-yawmaʾidhin waqaʿati'l-wāqiʿah", v:"15", root:"The \"FALLING / occurring / landing\" family — waqaʿa = to fall, to happen (wāqiʿ = REALITY.", meaning:"\"On that Day, the Event will have befallen.\"", magic:"Waqaʿati'l-wāqiʿah — \"the FALLING will have FALLEN\" — a cognate pair: the verb and its own participle.", grammar:"Yawmaʾidhin = \"on that day\" (idh + tanwīn replacing the deleted clause). waqaʿat = past for certain future.", why:"Al-wāqiʿah — another name for the Day that stresses its arrival as fact.", note:"Waqaʿati'l-WĀQIʿAH — \"the HAPPENING happens.\" Al-WĀQIʿ = REALITY. Al-Ḥāqqah (TRUTH) + al-Wāqiʿah (REALITY) = same Day.", more:["Modern: al-wāqiʿ = reality, wāqiʿī = realistic!; mawqiʿ = a LOCATION / a WEBSITE!; tawqīʿ = a SIGNATURE — what \"falls\" at the end of a document!); al-Wāqiʿah = the Event / the Inevitable — the name of Sūrah 56!","\"The happening happens.\" And wāqiʿ = REALITY in modern Arabic — the Day is REALITY itself arriving — matching al-Ḥāqqah (the TRUE one, v.1).","Ḥāqqah (truth) + Wāqiʿah (reality) — two names, same Day.","Sūrat al-Wāqiʿah (56) begins: idhā waqaʿati'l-wāqiʿah.","Hooks: mawqiʿ = a WEBSITE / location; tawqīʿ = a SIGNATURE.","Mawqiʿ = a WEBSITE; tawqīʿ = a SIGNATURE."]},
  {ar:"وَانشَقَّتِ السَّمَاءُ فَهِيَ يَوْمَئِذٍ وَاهِيَةٌ", tr:"Wa'nshaqqati's-samāʾu fa-hiya yawmaʾidhin wāhiyah", v:"16", root:"Inshaqqat — the \"SPLITTING / cracking\" family — shaqqa = to split; Inshiqāq 84:1 — idha's-samāʾu'nshaqqat!", meaning:"\"And the sky will split open — for that Day it is frail.\"", magic:"IDENTICAL opening verb to Sūrat al-Inshiqāq. Inshiqāq 84:1 — idha's-samāʾu'nshaqqat Ḥāqqah 69:16 — wa'nshaqqati's-samāʾ.", grammar:"Form VII (inshaqqa — \"split itself / became split\"). wāhiyatun = khabar of hiya.", why:"Wāhiyah — the most solid-seeming thing made flimsy.", note:"Inshaqqat — IDENTICAL to Inshiqāq 84:1. Sky-splitting verbs: infaṭarat (82:1), inshaqqat (84:1, 69:16), kushiṭat (81:11). Nabaʾ 78:12: SHIDĀD (FIRM) → Ḥāqqah 69:16: WĀHIYAH (FRAIL).", more:["Shiqqah = an APARTMENT (a \"section\" split off!); mashaqqah = HARDSHIP (what splits you!).","wāhiyah — the \"FRAILTY / weakness / sagging\" family — wahā = to become weak, flimsy, torn; wāhin = FLIMSY (ḥujjah wāhiyah = a flimsy argument!).","And the THREE \"sky-splitting\" verbs you now own: infaṭarat (Infiṭār 82:1, Muzzammil 73:18 munfaṭir) — split apart; inshaqqat (Inshiqāq 84:1, Ḥāqqah 69:16) — cracked open; kushiṭat (Takwīr 81:11) — stripped away.","Wāhiyah = FRAIL — the sky that was described as sabʿan SHIDĀDĀ (seven FIRM ones — Nabaʾ 78:12) becomes WĀHIYAH (FLIMSY).","Ḥujjah wāhiyah = a FLIMSY argument — the sky becomes as weak as a bad argument.","Hooks: shiqqah = an APARTMENT; mashaqqah = HARDSHIP.","Ḥujjah wāhiyah = a FLIMSY argument; shiqqah = an APARTMENT."]},
  {ar:"وَالْمَلَكُ عَلَىٰ أَرْجَائِهَا", tr:"Wa'l-malaku ʿalā arjāʾihā", v:"17", root:"Al-malak — the ANGELS (singular used collectively — \"angel-kind\").", meaning:"\"And the angels will be at its edges…\"", magic:"The sky splits — and the angels stand at its RIMS — at the torn edges, like people standing at the edge of a broken well. Al-malak — singular, but collective (\"the angel-host\").", grammar:"Al-malak = singular for collective (ism al-jins) — same as Fajr 89:22.", why:"Arjāʾ — pictures the torn sky as a ring with angels standing round it.", note:"The sky TORN — angels standing at its RIMS (arjāʾ).", more:["arjāʾ — plural of rajā = an EDGE, a side, a rim (the rim of a well; arjāʾ al-bilād = the far corners of the land); different from rajāʾ (HOPE)!","Arjāʾ (EDGES) vs rajāʾ (HOPE) — look alike; different words (Nūḥ 71:13 — lā tarjūna li'llāhi waqārā uses the HOPE root!).","Compare Nabaʾ 78:38 — yawma yaqūmu'r-rūḥu wa'l-malāʾikatu ṣaffā (angels in RANKS) — and Fajr 89:22 — wa'l-malaku ṣaffan ṣaffā (SAME collective singular al-malak!).","Al-MALAK = singular for COLLECTIVE — like Fajr 89:22 (wa'l-malaku ṣaffan ṣaffā).","ARJĀʾ (edges) ≠ RAJĀʾ (HOPE)."]},
  {ar:"وَيَحْمِلُ عَرْشَ رَبِّكَ فَوْقَهُمْ يَوْمَئِذٍ ثَمَانِيَةٌ", tr:"Wa yaḥmilu ʿarsha rabbika fawqahum yawmaʾidhin thamāniyah", v:"17", root:"Yaḥmilu — CARRIES (ḥamala — third use in this surah!).", meaning:"\"…and eight will bear the Throne of your Lord above them that Day.\"", magic:"The surah's most majestic image. ʿArsh rabbik — the Throne of your Lord — borne by EIGHT (classically: eight angels, or eight ranks/rows of angels — \"Allāhu aʿlam\" on the details).", grammar:"Thamāniyatun = SUBJECT, delayed to the very end (after ʿarsh, fawqahum, yawmaʾidhin).", why:"ʿarsh — unveils the Throne at the moment the sky is torn away.", note:"The THRONE borne by EIGHT.", more:["ʿarsh — the \"THRONE / canopy / raised structure\" family — ʿarsh = a THRONE (Burūj 85:15 — dhu'l-ʿarshi'l-majīd; Takwīr 81:20 — ʿinda dhi'l-ʿarshi makīn); ʿarīsh = an ARBOUR / trellis (a raised shade); ʿarasha = to build a trellis for vines.","The verb yaḥmilu (CARRIES) — its THIRD use in four verses: v.11 — We CARRIED you in the ark (rescue).","v.14 — the earth CARRIED to be crushed (ruin).","v.17 — the angels CARRY the Throne (majesty).","Three carryings: mercy, judgment, sovereignty.","The ʿarsh thread: Takwīr 81:20 — ʿinda dhi'l-ʿarshi makīn (Jibrīl, firmly placed near the Owner of the Throne).","Burūj 85:15 — dhu'l-ʿarshi'l-majīd (the Owner of the Glorious Throne).","Ḥāqqah 69:17 — the Throne borne by eight.","And Āyat al-Kursī — kursī — a related word, the footstool.","Note thamāniyah — v.7 used thamāniyata ayyām (eight DAYS) for ʿĀd's destruction; v.17 uses thamāniyah (EIGHT) bearing the Throne.","Eight days of ruin; eight bearers of majesty.","Suspense: \"and bearing the Throne… above them… that Day… are EIGHT.\"","Ḥamala ×3: CARRIED in the ark (v.11, rescue), earth CARRIED to be crushed (v.14, ruin), Throne CARRIED (v.17, majesty).","ʿArsh: Takwīr 81:20, Burūj 85:15, here.","ʿArīsh = an ARBOUR / trellis.","Eight DAYS of ruin (v.7) / EIGHT bearers (v.17)."]},
  {ar:"يَوْمَئِذٍ تُعْرَضُونَ لَا تَخْفَىٰ مِنكُمْ خَافِيَةٌ", tr:"Yawmaʾidhin tuʿraḍūna lā takhfā minkum khāfiyah", v:"18", root:"Tuʿraḍūn — the \"DISPLAYING / presenting / exhibiting\" family — ʿaraḍa = to display, to present for inspection.", meaning:"\"That Day you will be exhibited — not one hidden thing of yours will stay hidden.\"", magic:"TUʿRAḌŪN — \"you will be PRESENTED / put on DISPLAY\" — like goods at an EXHIBITION (maʿraḍ), turned over for inspection — classically: like soldiers presented before a king for review.", grammar:"Tuʿraḍūna = passive (displayed — by Allah). khāfiyatun = subject of takhfā — cognate (jinās).", why:"ʿarḍ (display) — makes judgment a public inspection, the opposite of the secrecy people relied on.", note:"TUʿRAḌŪN = put on DISPLAY — like an EXHIBITION (maʿraḍ).", more:["Maʿraḍ = an EXHIBITION; ʿarḍ = a DISPLAY / an OFFER (ʿarḍ taqdīmī = a PRESENTATION!); Jinn 72:17 yuʿriḍ / Muddaththir 74:49 muʿriḍīn (turning the side away).","khāfiyah — the \"HIDDEN\" family — khafiya = to be hidden; khafī = hidden (mukhābarāt?","no — ikhfāʾ = concealment, also a TAJWĪD rule — ikhfāʾ of the nūn!).","And the root's irony across your surahs: Jinn 72:17 & Muddaththir 74:49 — they AʿRAḌŪ (turned their SIDE away) from the reminder.","Ḥāqqah 69:18 — they will be TUʿRAḌŪN (DISPLAYED, fully faced).","Same root (ʿ-r-ḍ): those who turned sideways to the truth will be put on full display before it.","Lā takhfā minkum khāfiyah — \"no HIDDEN thing of yours will be HIDDEN\" — a cognate pair: the hidden will not hide.","Ṭāriq 86:9 — yawma tublā's-sarāʾir (the day SECRETS are tested).","ʿĀdiyāt 100:10 — wa ḥuṣṣila mā fi'ṣ-ṣudūr (the chests threshed).","Same truth, three phrasings.","Tajwīd hook: ikhfāʾ — the \"concealing\" rule for nūn sākinah — same root as khāfiyah.","They AʿRAḌŪ (turned their side) from the truth (72:17, 74:49) → they'll be TUʿRAḌŪN (fully displayed).","\"No HIDDEN thing will HIDE\" — Ṭāriq 86:9, ʿĀdiyāt 100:10. Ikhfāʾ = the tajwīd CONCEALMENT rule."]},
  {ar:"فَأَمَّا مَنْ أُوتِيَ كِتَابَهُ بِيَمِينِهِ", tr:"Fa-ammā man ūtiya kitābahu bi-yamīnih", v:"19", root:"ūtiya — was GIVEN (passive of ātā). kitāb — the BOOK / record (kataba = to write; Infiṭār 82:11 kirāman kātibīn).", meaning:"\"As for the one given his record in his right hand…\"", magic:"NEARLY IDENTICAL to Inshiqāq 84:7. Inshiqāq 84:7 — fa-ammā man ūtiya kitābahu bi-yamīnih Ḥāqqah 69:19 — fa-ammā man ūtiya kitābahu bi-yamīnih. WORD-FOR-WORD.", grammar:"Fa-ammā man… fa-… = the two-panel structure (v.19 / v.25). ūtiya = passive.", why:"Repeating Inshiqāq's exact words, then adding the man's own speech.", note:"WORD-FOR-WORD = Inshiqāq 84:7. Inshiqāq told his reckoning; Ḥāqqah gives him a VOICE.", more:["yamīn — the RIGHT HAND (al-Yaman = Yemen; yumn = blessing; Balad 90:18 maymanah).","Inshiqāq described his reckoning (ḥisāban yasīrā — easy) and his return (masrūrā — joyful).","And the \"right hand\" thread: Inshiqāq 84:7 (easy reckoning), Balad 90:18 (aṣḥāb al-maymanah), Muddaththir 74:39 (aṣḥāb al-yamīn — RELEASED from pledge).","Right-hand thread: easy reckoning (84:7), maymanah (90:18), released (74:39)."]},
  {ar:"فَيَقُولُ هَاؤُمُ اقْرَءُوا كِتَابِيَهْ", tr:"Fa-yaqūlu hāʾumu'qraʾū kitābiyah", v:"19", root:"Hāʾum — an INTERJECTION: \"HERE! TAKE!\" (ism fiʿl amr — a noun acting as a command; singular hāʾa, plural hāʾum).", meaning:"\"He will say: 'Here — everyone — read my book!'\"", magic:"Hāʾum! — \"HERE! LOOK!\" — he's waving his book at everyone, like a student who just got top marks. He WANTS people to read it. The opposite of hiding.", grammar:"Hāʾum = ism fiʿl amr (\"take!\") — a word that behaves like a command but is technically a noun.", why:"Hāʾum — pure exuberance; he can't wait to show it.", note:"HĀʾUM! = \"HERE! TAKE! READ!\" — waving his record like a top-mark exam.", more:["(iqraʾ — ʿAlaq 96:1, the first word revealed!).","kitābiyah — \"MY book\" + hāʾ as-sakt.","THE MOST JOYFUL SENTENCE IN THE JUZʾ.","Compare v.18 — \"no hidden thing hidden\" — for him, that's good news.","Iqraʾū — \"READ!\" — the first command of revelation (ʿAlaq 96:1, iqraʾ) — now said BY a believer, ABOUT his own record.","He was told \"read!\" in dunya — and read.","Now he says \"read!\" about his life.","Kitābiyah — the hāʾ as-sakt: \"kitābī\" (my book) + a pause-hāʾ — kitābiyah — you met this ONE time before: Qāriʿah 101:10, mā hiyah.","Here it comes SIX times in eleven verses (v.19, 20, 25, 26, 28, 29) — twice from the right-hand man, four times from the left-hand man.","The breath catches at the end of each line — for him: breathless JOY.","kitābiyah = kitābī + hāʾ as-sakt (pausal hāʾ — pronounced at the stop).","The verb iqraʾū governs kitābiyah.","IQRAʾŪ — ʿAlaq 96:1's first command, now said ABOUT his own record.","KITĀBIYAH — hāʾ as-sakt (Qāriʿah 101:10's mā hiyah) — the breathless pause."]},
  {ar:"إِنِّي ظَنَنتُ أَنِّي مُلَاقٍ حِسَابِيَهْ", tr:"Innī ẓanantu annī mulāqin ḥisābiyah", v:"20", root:"ẓanantu — I was CERTAIN (here ẓann = certainty, not assumption!).", meaning:"\"Indeed, I was certain that I would meet my reckoning.\"", magic:"WHY was he saved? This one sentence: \"I was CERTAIN I'd face my account.\" He lived as someone who KNEW the audit was coming.", grammar:"ẓanantu = certainty (verb of the heart).", why:"ẓanantu (I was sure) — identifies the root of his salvation: living with the reckoning in mind.", note:"WHY saved? \"I was CERTAIN I'd meet my account.\" RIGHT: \"I KNEW\" (v.20). LEFT: \"I never KNEW\" (v.26).", more:["mulāq — the \"MEETING\" family — lāqā = to meet; liqāʾ = a MEETING / an INTERVIEW; Inshiqāq 84:6 — fa-mulāqīh (you will MEET Him); manqūṣ (mulāqī → mulāqin).","ḥisāb — RECKONING / account (ḥisāb maṣrifī = a BANK ACCOUNT!).","Ẓanantu = CERTAINTY here — compare the ẓann trap you own: Qiyāmah 75:28 — wa ẓanna annahu'l-firāq (certainty — the dying man KNOWS).","Jinn 72:5 — wa annā ẓanannā (a wrong ASSUMPTION).","Ḥāqqah 69:20 — (certainty, and it saved him).","Mulāqin ḥisābiyah — Inshiqāq 84:6 — innaka kādiḥun ilā rabbika kadḥan fa-mulāqīh (you're toiling toward your Lord — and you WILL MEET Him).","Inshiqāq: you WILL meet Him.","Ḥāqqah: \"I always KNEW I would.\" And contrast the left-hand man (v.26): wa lam adri mā ḥisābiyah — \"I didn't KNOW what my reckoning was.\" Right-hand: \"I KNEW I'd meet my account.\" Left-hand: \"I never knew what my account was.\" The difference between them is AWARENESS — v.12's udhunun wāʿiyah (the retaining, conscious ear).","Hook: ḥisāb maṣrifī = a BANK ACCOUNT — he checked his balance.","mulāqin = manqūṣ active participle (yāʾ drops with tanwīn — like layālin, v.7).","The difference = AWARENESS (v.12's wāʿiyah).","Ẓann trap: CERTAINTY (75:28, 72:12, 69:20) vs ASSUMPTION (72:5). Inshiqāq 84:6 fa-mulāqīh.","Ḥisāb maṣrifī = a BANK ACCOUNT."]},
  {ar:"فَهُوَ فِي عِيشَةٍ رَّاضِيَةٍ", tr:"Fa-huwa fī ʿīshatin rāḍiyah", v:"21", root:"ʿīshah — a WAY OF LIFE (ʿāsha = to live; Nabaʾ 78:11 maʿāshā; ʿĀʾishah = \"one who lives\").", meaning:"\"So he will be in a pleasing life.\"", magic:"WORD-FOR-WORD IDENTICAL to Qāriʿah 101:7. Qāriʿah 101:7 — fa-huwa fī ʿīshatin rāḍiyah Ḥāqqah 69:21 — fa-huwa fī ʿīshatin rāḍiyah.", grammar:"Rāḍiyah = ACTIVE participle describing the life (majāz ʿaqlī).", why:"Repeating Qāriʿah's exact reward, so both descriptions of the saved converge.", note:"IDENTICAL to Qāriʿah 101:7. Rāḍiyah = ACTIVE — the LIFE itself is pleased (majāz ʿaqlī).", more:["rāḍiyah — PLEASED / content (the riḍā thread).","And the grammar trick I showed you there: rāḍiyah is ACTIVE — \"a life that IS PLEASED\" (not \"pleasing to him\") — majāz ʿaqlī: the contentment is so total even the LIFE is happy.","Qāriʿah: the man with HEAVY scales.","Ḥāqqah: the man with the book in his RIGHT hand.","Riḍā thread — now EIGHT surahs.","Heavy scales (101) = right-hand book (69)."]},
  {ar:"فِي جَنَّةٍ عَالِيَةٍ", tr:"Fī jannatin ʿāliyah", v:"22", root:"Jannah — a GARDEN (janna = to cover).", meaning:"\"In a lofty garden…\"", magic:"WORD-FOR-WORD IDENTICAL to Ghāshiyah 88:10. Ghāshiyah 88:10 — fī jannatin ʿāliyah Ḥāqqah 69:22 — fī jannatin ʿāliyah.", grammar:"ʿāliyatin = active participle adjective (feminine with jannah).", why:"Lifts the reward physically and in rank — and echoes Ghāshiyah exactly.", note:"IDENTICAL to Ghāshiyah 88:10. v.21 = Qāriʿah 101:7; v.22 = Ghāshiyah 88:10 — two verbatim rewards in a row.", more:["ʿāliyah — the \"HEIGHT / elevation\" family — ʿalā = to be high; al-Aʿlā (Aʿlā 87:1); ʿillīyyūn (Muṭaffifīn 83:18–19).","Two reward-phrases in a row, both verbatim from other surahs (v.21 = Qāriʿah, v.22 = Ghāshiyah).","ʿĀliyah = HIGH — classical: high in place AND high in rank — and the ʿ-l-w thread: al-Aʿlā (the Most High, 87:1), ʿillīyyūn (the high register, 83:18), jannah ʿāliyah (the high garden).","The Most High's garden is high.","ʿ-l-w: al-Aʿlā (87:1), ʿillīyyūn (83:18), ʿāliyah (69:22)."]},
  {ar:"قُطُوفُهَا دَانِيَةٌ", tr:"Quṭūfuhā dāniyah", v:"23", root:"Quṭūf — the \"PLUCKING / picking fruit\" family — qaṭafa = to pluck, to pick; qiṭāf = HARVEST time.", meaning:"\"Its clusters of fruit hanging low, within reach.\"", magic:"The fruit COMES DOWN TO YOU. Dāniyah = hanging LOW, NEAR — no climbing, no ladders, no reaching — classical: you can pick it standing, sitting, or lying down.", grammar:"Quṭūfuhā = subject (broken plural of qiṭf); dāniyatun = predicate (active participle).", why:"Dāniyah — effortless reward, for someone who made the effort in dunya.", note:"Fruit hanging LOW — picked standing, sitting, or lying down. Insān 76:14: dāniyatan… quṭūfuhā — SAME TWO WORDS.", more:["Quṭūf = clusters ready to be plucked; iqtiṭāf = quoting / excerpting (\"plucking\" a passage!).","dāniyah — the \"NEARNESS / lowness\" family — danā = to come near / hang low; dunyā = \"the nearer life\"; adnā (Muzzammil 73:20).","And you own this from Insān 76:14 — wa dāniyatan ʿalayhim ẓilāluhā wa dhullilat quṭūfuhā tadhlīlā — \"its shade hanging LOW over them, its CLUSTERS made easy to pick.\" SAME TWO WORDS — dāniyah and quṭūf — in Insān and Ḥāqqah.","Dāniyah shares a root with DUNYĀ — the \"NEAR\" life.","In dunya, the near things distracted people from the Hereafter.","In Paradise, the Hereafter's fruit is made NEAR.","The nearness you chased in dunya, given properly in the Garden.","Hook: iqtiṭāf = EXCERPTING / quoting — \"plucking\" a passage.","Dāniyah ← same root as DUNYĀ (\"the near\").","Qiṭāf = HARVEST; iqtiṭāf = EXCERPTING."]},
  {ar:"كُلُوا وَاشْرَبُوا هَنِيئًا بِمَا أَسْلَفْتُمْ فِي الْأَيَّامِ الْخَالِيَةِ", tr:"Kulū wa'shrabū hanīʾan bimā aslaftum fi'l-ayyāmi'l-khāliyah", v:"24", root:"Kulū / ishrabū — EAT / DRINK.", meaning:"\"[Told:] Eat and drink with enjoyment, for what you paid in advance in the days gone by.\"", magic:"THREE beautiful words in one welcome. HANĪʾAN — \"BON APPÉTIT!\" — the phrase hosts say to guests: hanīʾan marīʾan! Allah welcomes them to Paradise with the words of a generous host.", grammar:"Hanīʾan = ḥāl or maf'ūl muṭlaq (\"eat pleasantly\"). bimā = \"because of what.\" Form IV aslafa.", why:"Aslaftum (paid in advance) — frames worldly deeds as a deposit and Paradise as its withdrawal.", note:"HANĪʾAN MARĪʾAN! = \"BON APPÉTIT!\" — Allah welcomes them like a host. Tahniʾah = CONGRATULATIONS.", more:["hanīʾ — the \"WHOLESOME / easy to digest / pleasant\" family — hanīʾan = \"enjoy, with pleasure!\" — hanīʾan marīʾan!","= the everyday Arab blessing said to someone eating — \"BON APPÉTIT!","May it do you good!\"; tahniʾah = CONGRATULATIONS (hanniʾ!","aslaftum — the \"GOING BEFORE / paying in advance\" family — salafa = to precede; salaf = PREDECESSORS (as-salaf aṣ-ṣāliḥ — the righteous early generations!); sulfah = an ADVANCE PAYMENT / a LOAN (modern banking!); aslafa = to send/pay in advance.","khāliyah — the \"EMPTY / passed away\" family — khalā = to be empty, to pass; khāliyah = BYGONE, past and gone; khāl = empty (khalwah = seclusion!).","Tahniʾah = CONGRATULATIONS — same root.","ASLAFTUM — \"what you PAID IN ADVANCE\" — sulfah = an ADVANCE PAYMENT.","Their good deeds were a DEPOSIT made ahead of time — and now they collect.","Compare Muzzammil 73:20 — wa mā tuqaddimū li-anfusikum min khayrin tajidūhu ʿinda'llāh (whatever good you SEND AHEAD, you'll FIND it).","And Muzzammil's qarḍ (LOAN to Allah).","The banking set is complete: qarḍ (loan, 73:20), sulfah (advance payment, 69:24), rahn (collateral, 74:38), dayn (debt — yawm ad-dīn).","Al-ayyāmi'l-KHĀLIYAH — \"the days gone by\" — khāliyah = EMPTY / passed — dunya's days are now EMPTY, over — but what they paid in during those days remains.","The days emptied; the deposit stayed.","Compare Fajr 89:24 — yā laytanī qaddamtu li-ḥayātī (I WISH I'd sent ahead — the regretful man).","Here: they DID — and now they eat.","SULFAH = an ADVANCE PAYMENT → aslaftum = \"what you PAID IN ADVANCE.\" Banking set: qarḍ (73:20), sulfah (69:24), rahn (74:38), dayn (yawm ad-dīn).","Al-ayyām al-KHĀLIYAH = the days now EMPTY — but the deposit stayed.","As-SALAF aṣ-ṣāliḥ — same root."]},
  {ar:"وَأَمَّا مَنْ أُوتِيَ كِتَابَهُ بِشِمَالِهِ", tr:"Wa ammā man ūtiya kitābahu bi-shimālih", v:"25", root:"Shimāl — the \"LEFT / NORTH\" family — shimāl = the LEFT hand.", meaning:"\"And as for the one given his record in his left hand…\"", magic:"The mirror of v.19 — RIGHT becomes LEFT. v.19 — bi-yamīnih (right) v.25 — bi-shimālih (left).", grammar:"Wa ammā — the second panel. bi-shimālihi — bi- instrumental.", why:"Shimāl — the left hand, symbol of ill-omen throughout the juzʾ.", note:"SHIMĀL = LEFT = NORTH (ash-Shām, Quraysh 106:2). Inshiqāq 84:10: BEHIND his back. Ḥāqqah 69:25: LEFT hand. Twisted behind.", more:["Ash-shamāl = the NORTH (facing east, north is on your LEFT — just like ash-Shām (Syria) — Quraysh 106:2!); shamāl = the north wind; shimāl also = a character trait (one's \"nature,\" shamāʾil = noble traits — ash-Shamāʾil al-Muḥammadiyyah = the book of the Prophet's ﷺ characteristics!).","Compare Inshiqāq 84:10 — wa ammā man ūtiya kitābahu warāʾa ẓahrih — \"BEHIND his back.\" Inshiqāq: behind his back.","Classical reconciliation: his left hand is twisted behind his back — he receives it in the left, from behind, as if trying not to see it.","Shimāl = LEFT = NORTH — Quraysh 106:2: the summer caravan went NORTH to ash-Shām — named for the LEFT.","Balad 90:19: aṣḥāb al-mashʾamah.","The left and the north, one word.","Hook: ash-Shamāʾil al-Muḥammadiyyah — a famous book on the Prophet's ﷺ traits — same root!","Ash-Shamāʾil al-Muḥammadiyyah — same root."]},
  {ar:"يَا لَيْتَنِي لَمْ أُوتَ كِتَابِيَهْ", tr:"Yā laytanī lam ūta kitābiyah", v:"25", root:"Yā layta — \"OH, I WISH…!\" (an impossible wish).", meaning:"\"He will say: 'Oh, I wish I had never been given my book!'\"", magic:"THE MIRROR SENTENCE — and the yā laytanī thread.", grammar:"Yā layta + -nī = \"oh, would that I…\" (tamannī — an impossible wish).", why:"The exact inversion of v.19 — so you hear both men side by side.", note:"RIGHT: \"HERE — READ my book!\" LEFT: \"I WISH I'd never been GIVEN it!\" Same kitābiyah — joy vs horror.", more:["ūta — I was [not] given (jussive passive — the final ā drops: ūtā → ūta).","Right-hand man (v.19): \"HĀʾUM — iqraʾū kitābiyah!\" — \"HERE — READ my book!\" Left-hand man (v.25): \"YĀ LAYTANĪ lam ūta kitābiyah\" — \"I WISH I'd never been GIVEN my book!\" One waves it at everyone; the other wishes it didn't exist.","Same word kitābiyah, same pause-hāʾ — joy and horror.","The YĀ LAYTANĪ thread — the Qurʾān's regret formula: Nabaʾ 78:40 — yā laytanī kuntu turābā (I wish I were DUST).","Fajr 89:24 — yā laytanī qaddamtu li-ḥayātī (I wish I'd SENT AHEAD for my life).","Ḥāqqah 69:25 — yā laytanī lam ūta kitābiyah (I wish I'd never been given my book).","And v.27 again — yā laytahā kānati'l-qāḍiyah (I wish death had been the END).","FOUR wishes: to be dust, to have prepared, to have no record, to have been annihilated.","lam ūta = jussive passive — the final alif drops.","Yā laytanī: to be DUST (78:40), to have SENT AHEAD (89:24), to have NO record (69:25), to be ANNIHILATED (69:27). Ūta = jussive passive (alif dropped)."]},
  {ar:"وَلَمْ أَدْرِ مَا حِسَابِيَهْ", tr:"Wa lam adri mā ḥisābiyah", v:"26", root:"Adri — I KNEW (jussive — final ī drops: adrī → adri). ḥisāb — RECKONING.", meaning:"\"And I had never known what my reckoning was!\"", magic:"Right-hand man (v.20): innī ẒANANTU annī mulāqin ḥisābiyah — \"I was CERTAIN I'd meet my reckoning.\" Left-hand man (v.26): wa LAM ADRI mā ḥisābiyah — \"I never KNEW what my reckoning was.\" SAME WORD ḥisābiyah, same pause-hāʾ.", grammar:"Lam adri = jussive — the final yāʾ DROPS (adrī → adri). mā = interrogative (\"what my reckoning is\").", why:"The precise antonym of v.20 — ignorance where the other had certainty.", note:"V.20 RIGHT: \"I was CERTAIN I'd meet my ḥisābiyah.\" v.26 LEFT: \"I never KNEW my ḥisābiyah.\" The whole difference = AWARENESS.", more:["THE EXACT OPPOSITE of v.20.","One KNEW; one never thought about it.","That's the whole difference between them — the udhunun wāʿiyah of v.12 (the conscious ear) vs a life lived unaware.","Lam adri — the darā root — ʿAbasa 80:3 wa mā yudrīka (what makes you know?), wa mā adrāka formula (v.3 of this surah).","The Qurʾān kept asking \"what could make you know?\" — he never let it.","Lam adri = jussive, yāʾ dropped."]},
  {ar:"يَا لَيْتَهَا كَانَتِ الْقَاضِيَةَ", tr:"Yā laytahā kānati'l-qāḍiyah", v:"27", root:"Al-qāḍiyah — the \"FINISHING / DECIDING / settling\" family — qaḍā = to finish, complete, decide, settle.", meaning:"\"Oh, I wish it [death] had been the final end!\"", magic:"He wishes death had been TOTAL — no resurrection, no reckoning. Al-qāḍiyah = the \"finisher\" — the death that ENDS you, with nothing after.", grammar:"Yā laytahā — -hā = \"it\" (the death / the first death). al-qāḍiyata = khabar of kānat (accusative).", why:"Qāḍiyah — his denial in dunya returns as a desperate wish.", note:"He wishes death had been AL-QĀḌIYAH — the FINISHER, with nothing after. His false BELIEF became his only WISH.", more:["Qāḍī = a JUDGE (one who decides!); qaḍāʾ = DECREE / judgment / fulfilment; qaḍāʾ also = making up a missed prayer (qaḍāʾ aṣ-ṣalāh!); ʿAbasa 80:23 — kallā lammā yaqḍi mā amarah (he has NOT YET FULFILLED what He commanded!); al-qāḍiyah = the FINAL, decisive end — DEATH that finishes everything.","In dunya he hoped death was the end (he denied resurrection); now he WISHES it had been.","His false belief has become his only wish.","The qaḍā irony: ʿAbasa 80:23 — kallā lammā yaqḍi mā amarah — \"he has NOT YET FULFILLED (yaqḍi) what He commanded.\" Ḥāqqah 69:27 — he wishes death had FINISHED (qāḍiyah) him.","He never FULFILLED (qaḍā) his duties; now he wishes death had FINISHED (qaḍā) him.","Same root: the unfulfilled life wishes for annihilation.","Compare Nabaʾ 78:40 — yā laytanī kuntu turābā — \"I wish I were DUST\" — the same wish for non-existence.","And Aʿlā 87:13 — lā yamūtu fīhā wa lā yaḥyā — he won't get his wish: neither dies nor lives.","Hooks: qāḍī = a JUDGE; qaḍāʾ = making up a missed PRAYER.","ʿAbasa 80:23: he never FULFILLED (yaqḍi) his duty → now wishes death had FINISHED (qāḍiyah) him.","QĀḌĪ = a JUDGE; qaḍāʾ = a MAKE-UP prayer.","Nabaʾ 78:40: \"I wish I were DUST.\""]},
  {ar:"مَا أَغْنَىٰ عَنِّي مَالِيَهْ", tr:"Mā aghnā ʿannī māliyah", v:"28", root:"Aghnā ʿan — to AVAIL / suffice for (the ghinā root — your longest thread). māl — WEALTH + hāʾ as-sakt.", meaning:"\"My wealth has not availed me!\"", magic:"Layl 92:11 — wa mā yughnī ʿanhu māluhu (his wealth WILL NOT avail him — present, 3rd person, a principle).", grammar:"Mā = negation. ʿannī = ʿan + -nī. māliyah = mālī + hāʾ as-sakt.", why:"The thread's final form: the denier's own lips.", note:"Layl 92:11 (WILL NOT avail — principle) → Masad 111:2 (DID NOT avail — Abū Lahab) → Ḥāqqah 69:28 (MY wealth did not avail ME — his own mouth).", more:["THE GHINĀ THREAD — now in the FIRST PERSON.","Masad 111:2 — mā aghnā ʿanhu māluhu (his wealth DID NOT avail him — past, 3rd person, Abū Lahab).","Ḥāqqah 69:28 — mā aghnā ʿannī māliyah (MY wealth did not avail ME — past, 1st person, his own confession).","Principle → a named man → his own mouth.","The same sentence, three voices.","Māliyah — \"MY wealth\" with a gasp — the thing he loved most (Fajr 89:20 — ḥubban jammā; ʿĀdiyāt 100:8 — la-shadīd; Humazah 104:2–3 — counted it, thought it made him eternal).","The ghinā thread — the juzʾ's spine: istaghnā (80:5, 92:8, 96:7) — claiming to need nothing; aṣ-Ṣamad (112:2) — the only One who truly needs nothing; mā aghnā (92:11, 111:2, 69:28) — wealth never sufficed.","Tajwīd: māliyah · halaka — in Ḥafṣ, read either with a brief pause (sakt) on the hāʾ, or merging it into the next hāʾ (idghām) — a well-known recitation point.","Same sentence, three voices.","Tajwīd: māliyah halaka — pause or merge."]},
  {ar:"هَلَكَ عَنِّي سُلْطَانِيَهْ", tr:"Halaka ʿannī sulṭāniyah", v:"29", root:"Halaka — PERISHED (v.5 uhlikū).", meaning:"\"My authority has perished from me!\" (or: \"my argument / proof has vanished\")", magic:"WEALTH (v.28), then POWER (v.29) — the two things he trusted, both gone.", grammar:"Halaka ʿannī = \"perished away from me\" (the ʿan = separation).", why:"Sulṭān (power AND proof) — both the status and the arguments he leaned on, gone at once.", note:"V.28 WEALTH gone; v.29 POWER gone — the two idols. Sulṭān = POWER (a SULTAN) and PROOF (sulṭān mubīn). His rank AND his arguments.", more:["sulṭān — the \"POWER / authority / proof\" family — sulṭān = AUTHORITY, POWER (Sulṭān = a SULTAN!); sulṭān also = a PROOF / argument (the \"authority\" of evidence — sulṭān mubīn = a clear proof); salīṭ = sharp-tongued; sulṭah = AUTHORITY (as-sulṭah at-tashrīʿiyyah = the legislative authority!).","Sulṭāniyah — two classical readings, both devastating: (a) \"my POWER / rank / authority\" — the status he held in dunya; (b) \"my PROOF / argument\" — the excuses he used to dismiss the truth.","Both have \"perished from me\" (halaka ʿannī).","Wealth + power — the twin idols of the juzʾ's arrogant men: al-Walīd (Muddaththir 74:12–14 — wealth, sons, a smoothed path), Pharaoh (ana rabbukumu'l-aʿlā, Nāziʿāt 79:24), the humazah (Humazah 104:2). Here they confess: both gone.","Halaka — v.5 Thamūd were uhlikū (destroyed); now his power halaka (perished).","Hooks: Sulṭān = a SULTAN; sulṭah = AUTHORITY.","sulṭāniyah = hāʾ as-sakt — the fourth and last gasp.","FOUR gasps: kitābiyah, ḥisābiyah, māliyah, sulṭāniyah."]},
  {ar:"خُذُوهُ فَغُلُّوهُ", tr:"Khudhūhu fa-ghullūh", v:"30", root:"Khudhū — SEIZE! (akhadha).", meaning:"\"[It will be said:] 'Seize him and shackle him!'\"", magic:"His four gasps are answered by FOUR COMMANDS (v.30–32): khudhūhu (seize him) → ghullūhu (collar him) → ṣallūhu (roast him) → usllukūhu (thread him in).", grammar:"Two plural imperatives (to the angels) + -hu (him). fa = immediately. ghullū — doubled verb imperative.", why:"Short, rapid imperatives — the speed of execution after the slow gasps of regret.", note:"FOUR GASPS (v.25–29) → FOUR COMMANDS: seize → COLLAR → roast → THREAD (v.30–32).", more:["ghullū — the \"SHACKLING the neck / collar\" family — ghull = an iron COLLAR binding hands to neck; aghlāl = collars (Insān 76:4 — salāsila wa aghlālā!); ghill = RANCOUR / hidden hatred (a \"collar\" on the heart!","— wa nazaʿnā mā fī ṣudūrihim min ghill, 7:43); ghullah = a CROP / yield; ghalīl = intense THIRST (the burning of thirst).","Short, harsh, clipped — the angels' orders.","No hāʾ as-sakt, no breath — just commands.","The rhythm changes from his gasping to their command.","Ghull = an iron COLLAR — Insān 76:4 — innā aʿtadnā li'l-kāfirīna salāsila wa aghlālā wa saʿīrā — CHAINS, COLLARS, and a blaze — and here: ghullūhu (collar him), then silsilah (a chain, v.32), then jaḥīm (v.31).","Insān listed the three; Ḥāqqah applies them to one man.","And ghill = RANCOUR — the hatred he carried in his heart becomes a collar on his neck.","His neck: the man who wouldn't \"free a neck\" (Balad 90:13, fakku raqabah) — his own neck collared.","Insān 76:4 listed CHAINS, COLLARS, BLAZE → Ḥāqqah applies all three to one man.","Ghull = a COLLAR; ghill = RANCOUR — his heart's collar becomes his neck's.","Wouldn't free a neck (90:13) → his own neck collared."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ الْجَحِيمَ صَلُّوهُ", tr:"Thumma'l-jaḥīma ṣallūh", v:"31", root:"Jaḥīm — the BLAZE. ṣallū — Form II of ṣaliya = ROAST him, make him burn (NOT ṣalāh!).", meaning:"\"Then into the Blaze — roast him!\"", magic:"THE WORD ORDER: al-jaḥīma FIRST, then the verb. \"THE BLAZE — roast him [there, and nowhere else].\" Fronting = restriction: only the Blaze.", grammar:"Al-jaḥīma = object FRONTED before its verb (restriction).", why:"Fronting + the ṣaliya/ṣalāh look-alike — a destination named first, a cause hidden in the spelling.", note:"AL-JAḤĪMA ṣallūhu — Blaze FRONTED = there and NOWHERE else. ṢALLŪHU (ROAST him) looks identical to ṢALLŪ (PRAY)!", more:["Ṣallūhu (ROAST him) — the ṣaliya root — vs ṣallū (PRAY!) — the ṣalāh root — IDENTICAL SPELLING in Arabic letters!","And v.33–34 tell you why: he didn't believe, didn't urge feeding the poor — Muddaththir 74:43 adds: lam naku mina'l-MUṢALLĪN — \"we weren't among those who PRAYED.\" He wouldn't ṣallā (pray); so he is ṣallā (roasted).","The look-alike verbs as cause and effect.","The ṣaliya thread — now NINE surahs: 82:15, 83:16, 84:12, 87:12, 88:4, 92:15, 111:3, 74:26, 69:31. Thumma — \"THEN\" — a pause between each stage: seized, collared — THEN the Blaze — THEN the chain.","ṣallū = Form II imperative (intensive: roast thoroughly).","He wouldn't PRAY (74:43) → he is ROASTED."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ فِي سِلْسِلَةٍ ذَرْعُهَا سَبْعُونَ ذِرَاعًا فَاسْلُكُوهُ", tr:"Thumma fī silsilatin dharʿuhā sabʿūna dhirāʿan fa'slukūh", v:"32", root:"Silsilah — a REDUPLICATED root — silsilah = a CHAIN (modern: silsilah = a SERIES — a TV series, a chain of events!", meaning:"\"Then into a chain whose length is seventy cubits — thread him!\"", magic:"THREE vivid elements — and the salaka thread reaches its most physical form.", grammar:"Fī silsilatin FRONTED before the verb (fa'slukūhu) — restriction again (in THAT chain).", why:"Salaka (thread through) — the most confining, piercing image of restraint.", note:"SILSILAH = a CHAIN (and a TV SERIES) — reduplicated root: the word IS a chain. SEVENTY CUBITS (~35 m) — beyond human scale.", more:["Al-Silsilah aṣ-Ṣaḥīḥah = a famous ḥadīth collection!; salāsil — Insān 76:4). dharʿ / dhirāʿ — the \"FOREARM / cubit\" family — dhirāʿ = the FOREARM and a CUBIT (elbow to fingertip, ~half a metre); dharʿ = a measured length; dhirāʿ = (modern) an ARM / a branch (adh-dhirāʿ al-ʿaskarī = the military wing!).","Silsilah — a CHAIN — a reduplicated root (s-l-s-l): link after link, repeating — the word itself is a chain of sounds.","(Add it to your list: ʿasʿasa, damdama, zalzala, waswasa, ṣarṣar, silsila.) Silsilah = a SERIES in modern Arabic.","Sabʿūna dhirāʿan — SEVENTY CUBITS (~35 metres) — classical: \"seventy\" may be literal or signify vast length — either way: a chain beyond any human scale.","Fa'SLUKŪHU — \"THREAD him [through it]\" — like thread through a needle's eye — classical: the chain passes THROUGH him — the most physical use of salaka in your study.","The salaka thread: Jinn 72:17 — yaslukhu ʿadhāban ṣaʿadā (threaded into a climbing punishment).","Jinn 72:27 — yasluku… raṣadā (guards threaded around revelation).","Muddaththir 74:42 — mā salakakum fī saqar (what THREADED you into Saqar?).","Ḥāqqah 69:32 — fa'slukūhu (THREAD him through the chain).","Nūḥ 71:20 — li-taslukū minhā subulan fijājā (to TRAVEL its wide roads — the merciful use!).","And Muddaththir's question (\"what threaded you in?\") is answered right here: v.33–34.","Hooks: silsilah = a SERIES; dhirāʿ = an ARM / a CUBIT; lā-silkī = WIRELESS (silk = wire).","dhirāʿan = tamyīz after the number 70.","Fa'SLUKŪHU = THREAD him through it.","Salaka: 72:17, 72:27, 74:42 (\"what threaded you?\"), 69:32, 71:20 (travel — the merciful use)."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّهُ كَانَ لَا يُؤْمِنُ بِاللَّهِ الْعَظِيمِ", tr:"Innahu kāna lā yuʾminu bi'llāhi'l-ʿaẓīm", v:"33", root:"Yuʾminu — BELIEVE (īmān ← amn, security).", meaning:"\"Indeed, he did not believe in Allah, the Magnificent…\"", magic:"Two reasons (v.33–34) — and they're the two pillars, again. (1) He didn't BELIEVE in Allah the Magnificent (his duty to Allah).", grammar:"Kāna lā yuʾminu = habitual past negation (\"he used to not believe\"). al-ʿaẓīm = adjective of Allāh.", why:"Al-ʿAẓīm — sets up the surah's final command, where the same Name is glorified.", note:"WHY? (1) no BELIEF in Allah (2) no URGING to feed the poor — the two pillars broken.", more:["al-ʿaẓīm — the \"GREATNESS / magnitude\" family — ʿaẓīm = MIGHTY, tremendous; ʿaẓm = a BONE (the solid, great framework of the body!","— Qiyāmah 75:3 ʿiẓāmah; Nāziʿāt 79:11 ʿiẓāman nakhirah); ʿaẓamah = majesty; al-ʿAẓīm = a NAME OF ALLAH (subḥāna rabbiya'l-ʿaẓīm — in rukūʿ!).","(2) He didn't URGE feeding the poor (his duty to people).","Belief + compassion — Balad 90:17 — thumma kāna mina'lladhīna āmanū wa tawāṣaw bi'ṣ-ṣabri wa tawāṣaw bi'l-marḥamah (believed + urged MERCY).","Al-ʿAẒĪM — and the surah ENDS on the same Name (v.52): fa-sabbiḥ bi'smi rabbika'l-ʿAẒĪM — the Name he refused to believe in, the believer glorifies.","Subḥāna rabbiya'l-ʿaẓīm — what you say in every RUKŪʿ comes from v.52.","ʿAẓm = a BONE — the same root as \"magnificent\" — the bones he thought could never be rebuilt (Qiyāmah 75:3) were made by al-ʿAẓīm.","Al-ʿAẒĪM — refused here (v.33), GLORIFIED at the end (v.52) — subḥāna rabbiya'l-ʿaẓīm in RUKŪʿ.","ʿAẓm = a BONE (Qiyāmah 75:3). Balad 90:17: belief + mercy."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا يَحُضُّ عَلَىٰ طَعَامِ الْمِسْكِينِ", tr:"Wa lā yaḥuḍḍu ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn", v:"34", root:"Yaḥuḍḍu — to URGE (ḥaḍḍa). ṭaʿām — FOOD. miskīn — the POOR (one made still, sakana, by poverty).", meaning:"\"…and did not urge the feeding of the poor.\"", magic:"WORD-FOR-WORD IDENTICAL to Māʿūn 107:3. Māʿūn 107:3 — wa lā yaḥuḍḍu ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn Ḥāqqah 69:34 — wa lā yaḥuḍḍu ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn. EXACT.", grammar:"Lā yaḥuḍḍu = habitual negation (continuing kāna). ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn = \"the food OF the poor\" — their right.", why:"Repeating Māʿūn exactly — so the same sin is heard in both worlds.", note:"WORD-FOR-WORD = Māʿūn 107:3. Fajr 89:18 (society), Māʿūn 107:3 (dunya), Ḥāqqah 69:34 (Hell).", more:["And Fajr 89:18 — wa lā taḥāḍḍūna ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn (Form VI — \"you don't urge EACH OTHER\").","Three surahs, one sin: Fajr (the society), Māʿūn (the man in dunya), Ḥāqqah (the man in Hell).","And the bar is LOW — not \"he didn't FEED\" but \"he didn't even URGE.\" Urging costs NOTHING.","He failed at the free part.","Remarkable: this sin is placed RIGHT NEXT TO disbelief in Allah (v.33).","Not urging food for the poor sits beside denying God — showing how heavy this omission is.","The miskīn thread — now SIX surahs: fed (Insān 76:8), climbed for (Balad 90:16), not urged (Fajr 89:18, Māʿūn 107:3, Ḥāqqah 69:34), confessed (Muddaththir 74:44).","Placed RIGHT BESIDE disbelief in Allah (v.33) — that's how heavy it is.","Not even URGING — the free part."]},
  {ar:"فَلَيْسَ لَهُ الْيَوْمَ هَاهُنَا حَمِيمٌ", tr:"Fa-laysa lahu'l-yawma hāhunā ḥamīm", v:"35", root:"Hāhunā — \"RIGHT HERE\" (hunā = here + hā attention).", meaning:"\"So today he has no close friend here…\"", magic:"THE DOUBLE-MEANING WORD — and here it's the FRIEND sense. Nabaʾ 78:25 — illā ḥamīman wa ghassāqā — ḥamīm = SCALDING WATER (Hell's drink).", grammar:"Laysa + lahu (fronted predicate) + ḥamīmun (subject, delayed). al-yawma = adverb (\"today\"). hāhunā = \"here.\"", why:"ḥamīm — the word's double life lets one word hold both the absent friend and the present torment.", note:"ḤAMĪM = (a) a WARM FRIEND (b) SCALDING WATER — root ḥ-m-m = HEAT (ḥammām, ḥummā). Nabaʾ 78:25: SCALDING water.", more:["ḥamīm — a word with TWO opposite meanings: (a) a CLOSE, WARM FRIEND (one whose love is \"hot\"); (b) SCALDING WATER — the root ḥ-m-m = HEAT (ḥammām = a BATHHOUSE; ḥummā = a FEVER).","Ḥāqqah 69:35 — laysa lahu'l-yawma hāhunā ḥamīm — ḥamīm = a CLOSE FRIEND.","And Maʿārij 70:10 (next surah!) — wa lā yasʾalu ḥamīmun ḥamīmā — \"no FRIEND will ask about a FRIEND.\" Classical: \"ḥamīm\" for a friend because his love is WARM.","The man who didn't feed the poor (v.34) — has no warm friend (v.35); he'll get only the \"warm\" drink.","The friend he lacks and the drink he gets share one word.","Hāhunā — \"RIGHT HERE\" — pointing at the spot — maybe he had friends in dunya; here, none.","And the logic (fa): he didn't care for the poor → no one cares for him.","Maʿārij 70:10: \"no FRIEND asks of a FRIEND.\" Didn't care for the poor → no one cares for him."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا طَعَامٌ إِلَّا مِنْ غِسْلِينٍ", tr:"Wa lā ṭaʿāmun illā min ghislīn", v:"36", root:"ṭaʿām — FOOD.", meaning:"\"…nor any food except from the washing of wounds.\"", magic:"v.34: he didn't urge ṬAʿĀM al-miskīn (FOOD for the poor). v.36: he has no ṬAʿĀM (FOOD) except ghislīn.", grammar:"Lā ṭaʿāmun — negation; illā min = \"except from.\" ghislīn = fiʿlīn pattern.", why:"Ghislīn — closes the food-loop: withheld food → filthy food.", note:"V.34 he withheld ṬAʿĀM from the poor → v.36 his only ṬAʿĀM is GHISLĪN (wound-washings).", more:["ghislīn — the \"WASHING\" family — ghasala = to wash; ghusl = the ritual BATH; ghassālah = a WASHING MACHINE; ghislīn = what is WASHED OFF the wounds of the people of Hell — pus, blood, filth.","THE MEASURE-FOR-MEASURE IS COMPLETE.","Same word ṭaʿām: he withheld food from the hungry → his own food is the filth washed from wounds.","Ghislīn — from ghasala (to WASH) — the root of GHUSL (the purifying bath) — the most PURIFYING act in Islam shares a root with the most DEFILING food.","What's washed OFF in purity vs what's washed off wounds.","Hell's foods in your study: ḍarīʿ (Ghāshiyah 88:6 — a thorn that doesn't nourish), ṭaʿāman dhā ghuṣṣah (Muzzammil 73:13 — food that CHOKES), ghislīn (Ḥāqqah 69:36 — the washings of wounds).","Doesn't nourish, won't swallow, is filth.","And the contrast with v.24 (the right-hand man): kulū wa'shrabū HANĪʾAN — \"eat and drink with ENJOYMENT.\" Same surah: hanīʾ (bon appétit) vs ghislīn (wound-washings).","GHUSL (purifying bath) & GHISLĪN (wound-filth) — same root.","Ghassālah = a WASHING MACHINE.","Hell's foods: ḍarīʿ (88:6), ghuṣṣah (73:13), ghislīn (69:36). v.24 HANĪʾAN vs v.36 GHISLĪN."]},
  {ar:"لَّا يَأْكُلُهُ إِلَّا الْخَاطِئُونَ", tr:"Lā yaʾkuluhu illa'l-khāṭiʾūn", v:"37", root:"Khāṭiʾūn — those who SIN DELIBERATELY (khaṭiʾa, Form I — not mukhṭiʾ, one who errs innocently).", meaning:"\"None eat it except the sinners.\"", magic:"The root returns from v.9 — wa'l-muʾtafikātu bi'l-khāṭiʾah (the overturned cities came with SIN). And ʿAlaq 96:16 — nāṣiyatin kādhibatin khāṭiʾah (the lying, SINFUL forelock).", grammar:"Lā… illā = restriction (\"none but\"). al-khāṭiʾūna = Form I active participle, plural.", why:"Khāṭiʾūn (deliberate sinners) — carefully excludes those who merely erred.", note:"KHĀṬIʾ (Form I) = sins ON PURPOSE; MUKHṬIʾ (Form IV) = honest MISTAKE. The ghislīn is for the DELIBERATE.", more:["Khāṭiʾ (Form I) = one who sins ON PURPOSE; mukhṭiʾ (Form IV) = one who makes an honest MISTAKE — the Prophet ﷺ said the mujtahid who errs (akhṭaʾa) still gets ONE reward.","The ghislīn is for the deliberate, not the mistaken.","A merciful distinction hidden in the verb form.","v.9 al-khāṭiʾah; ʿAlaq 96:16 khāṭiʾah.","The mujtahid who errs still gets ONE reward."]},
  {ar:"فَلَا أُقْسِمُ بِمَا تُبْصِرُونَ · وَمَا لَا تُبْصِرُونَ", tr:"Fa-lā uqsimu bimā tubṣirūn · wa mā lā tubṣirūn", v:"38–39", root:"Lā uqsimu — \"NO! I swear…\" (the emphatic lā before an oath).", meaning:"\"So I swear by what you see, and by what you do not see.\"", magic:"Every other oath swears by ONE thing (the dawn, the fig, the moon, time).", grammar:"Lā uqsimu — the lā is emphatic/rejecting, not a real negation.", why:"Seen + unseen = the whole of existence, as witness.", note:"The WIDEST OATH: by ALL that is SEEN + ALL that is UNSEEN — the whole universe.", more:["tubṣirūn — the \"SEEING / insight\" family — baṣura = to see clearly; baṣar = SIGHT; baṣīrah = INSIGHT (Qiyāmah 75:14 — bali'l-insānu ʿalā nafsihi baṣīrah); al-Baṣīr = the All-Seeing (a Name of Allah); al-Baṣrah (the city in Iraq — named for its whitish stony ground!).","THE WIDEST OATH IN THE QURʾĀN — it covers EVERYTHING.","This one swears by ALL THAT IS SEEN + ALL THAT IS UNSEEN — the entire created universe, visible and invisible, in five words.","Classical: the visible = creation you witness; the invisible = angels, jinn, the soul, the Hereafter — Al-Jinn (72) was all about the unseen!","Fa-lā uqsimu — the oath-opener you own from Qiyāmah 75:1–2, Takwīr 81:15, Inshiqāq 84:16, Balad 90:1 — the lā is emphatic (\"No — I DO swear!\") or rejects their claim first (\"No!","[it's not as you say] — I swear…\").","And the unseen thread: Jinn 72:26 — ʿālimu'l-ghaybi fa-lā yuẓhiru ʿalā ghaybihi aḥadā (He alone knows the UNSEEN).","bimā — \"by what\" (mā = relative, all-inclusive).","Lā uqsimu — 75:1, 81:15, 84:16, 90:1. Jinn 72:26: He knows the unseen; here He SWEARS by it.","Baṣīrah = INSIGHT (Qiyāmah 75:14)."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّهُ لَقَوْلُ رَسُولٍ كَرِيمٍ", tr:"Innahu la-qawlu rasūlin karīm", v:"40", root:"Qawl — WORD / speech. rasūl — MESSENGER.", meaning:"\"Indeed, it is the word of a noble messenger.\"", magic:"WORD-FOR-WORD IDENTICAL to Takwīr 81:19. Takwīr 81:19 — innahu la-qawlu rasūlin karīm Ḥāqqah 69:40 — innahu la-qawlu rasūlin karīm.", grammar:"Inna + emphatic lām = double emphasis. rasūlin karīmin = indefinite (honorific vastness).", why:"Repeating Takwīr's exact words so the two messengers — angelic and human — are joined.", note:"WORD-FOR-WORD = TAKWĪR 81:19. Takwīr = JIBRĪL (near the Throne). Ḥāqqah = MUHAMMAD ﷺ (contrasted with poet/soothsayer).", more:["karīm — the \"NOBILITY / generosity\" family — karīm = NOBLE, generous, honoured (karam = GENEROSITY; al-Karīm = a Name of Allah; Infiṭār 82:6 — mā gharraka bi-rabbika'l-karīm).","EXACT — but most scholars say they refer to DIFFERENT messengers: Takwīr 81:19 = JIBRĪL (described next as dhī quwwah ʿinda dhi'l-ʿarshi makīn — powerful, near the Throne).","Ḥāqqah 69:40 = MUHAMMAD ﷺ (contrasted next with a poet and a soothsayer — accusations made against him).","The same five words for the angel who brought it and the Prophet who delivered it — both \"noble messengers\"; the word is Allah's, carried by both.","\"Word of a messenger\" — classical: his word in the sense of DELIVERING it — v.43 makes it explicit: tanzīlun min rabbi'l-ʿālamīn — sent down from the Lord.","Same five words, two noble messengers.","Karam = GENEROSITY; Infiṭār 82:6 al-Karīm."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا هُوَ بِقَوْلِ شَاعِرٍ ۚ قَلِيلًا مَّا تُؤْمِنُونَ", tr:"Wa mā huwa bi-qawli shāʿir · qalīlan mā tuʾminūn", v:"41", root:"Shāʿir — the \"PERCEIVING / FEELING\" family — shaʿara = to perceive, to sense, to feel.", meaning:"\"And it is not the word of a poet — little do you believe!\"", magic:"The first accusation rejected: POETRY. Quraysh called the Prophet ﷺ a shāʿir because the Qurʾān was rhythmic and moving.", grammar:"Mā huwa bi-qawli — the extra bi- after negation for emphasis (like Takwīr 81:22 bi-majnūn, Infiṭār 82:16 bi-ghāʾibīn, Tīn 95:8 bi-aḥkam).", why:"Shāʿir — the charge most plausible to Arab ears, refuted by the very people who knew poetry best.", note:"NOT a SHĀʿIR — the Arabs KNEW every poetic metre, and the Qurʾān fits none. Shāʿir ← shaʿara = to FEEL → SHUʿŪR = FEELING.", more:["Shuʿūr = FEELING, emotion, awareness (everyday!); shiʿr = POETRY (the art of feeling what others miss); shāʿir = a POET (\"one who perceives\"); shaʿr = HAIR (a fine thing you feel!); shiʿār = a SLOGAN / emblem — and the inner garment (Muddaththir's shiʿār/dithār!); shaʿāʾir = RITES (shaʿāʾir Allāh — symbols of Allah).","But the Arabs were masters of poetry — they KNEW its metres — and the Qurʾān fits none of them.","Al-Walīd himself (Muddaththir 74:18–24) rejected the \"poetry\" charge for that reason before settling on \"magic.\" Shāʿir from shaʿara = to FEEL/PERCEIVE — a poet speaks from his own feelings; the Qurʾān comes from outside, from the Lord.","Shuʿūr = FEELING — your free hook.","Qalīlan mā tuʾminūn — \"LITTLE do you believe!\" — classical: an understatement meaning \"you hardly believe at all\" (taqlīl for negation).","Mā here is extra (zāʾidah) for emphasis.","qalīlan mā = mā zāʾidah (emphatic).","A poet speaks from his feelings; the Qurʾān from his Lord.","Shaʿr = HAIR; shiʿār = a SLOGAN.","Extra bi- after negation (81:22, 82:16, 95:8). Qalīlan mā = you hardly believe AT ALL."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا بِقَوْلِ كَاهِنٍ ۚ قَلِيلًا مَّا تَذَكَّرُونَ", tr:"Wa lā bi-qawli kāhin · qalīlan mā tadhakkarūn", v:"42", root:"Kāhin — a SOOTHSAYER / diviner — one who claims knowledge of the unseen, usually through jinn informants (kahānah = soothsaying; in other languages kohen = a priest — the same Semitic root!).", meaning:"\"Nor the word of a soothsayer — little do you take heed!\"", magic:"The second accusation rejected: SOOTHSAYING — and ALL of Sūrat al-Jinn is the background. A kāhin claimed jinn told him secrets overheard from the heavens.", grammar:"Tadhakkarūna = tatadhakkarūna with one tāʾ DROPPED (Form V contraction — like talaẓẓā, Layl 92:14; tanazzalu, Qadr 97:4).", why:"Kāhin — ties directly to Al-Jinn's sealed sky, making the refutation factual.", note:"NOT a KĀHIN — Jinn 72:8–9: the sky was SEALED; the soothsayers' jinn-informants were CUT OFF.", more:["tadhakkarūn — you take heed / remember (tatadhakkarūn with a tāʾ dropped!).","Jinn 72:8–9 — the jinn THEMSELVES confessed: the sky is now FILLED with guards; whoever tries to listen meets a meteor.","Jinn 72:26 — Allah discloses His unseen to NO ONE except an approved messenger, guarded on all sides (72:27). So the soothsayers' supply line was CUT at the very moment the Qurʾān began.","The Qurʾān can't be soothsaying — the soothsayers' source was blocked.","And soothsayers spoke in short, rhymed riddles (sajʿ al-kuhhān) — vague, two-sided, meaningless phrases.","The Qurʾān is clear, rich, and makes testable claims (like Masad's prophecy about Abū Lahab).","And note the matching verbs: v.41 — POET → qalīlan mā TUʾMINŪN (little do you BELIEVE).","v.42 — SOOTHSAYER → qalīlan mā TADHAKKARŪN (little do you REFLECT).","Classical: to call it poetry, you must ignore its CONTENT (a failure of belief); to call it soothsaying, you must ignore its STYLE and SUBSTANCE (a failure of reflection).","Each accusation needed a different blindness.","POET → \"little do you BELIEVE.\" SOOTHSAYER → \"little do you REFLECT.\" Each charge needs a different blindness.","Tadhakkarūna = tatadhakkarūna, tāʾ dropped.","Kohen (priest) — same Semitic root."]},
  {ar:"تَنزِيلٌ مِّن رَّبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ", tr:"Tanzīlun min rabbi'l-ʿālamīn", v:"43", root:"Tanzīl — Form II verbal noun of nazala = SENDING DOWN gradually, in stages (Insān 76:23.", meaning:"\"[It is] a revelation sent down from the Lord of the worlds.\"", magic:"The positive answer after two denials: not a poet (v.41), not a soothsayer (v.42) — TANZĪL from the LORD of the worlds.", grammar:"Tanzīlun = predicate of an implied subject (\"[it is] a revelation…\"). Form II maṣdar (tafʿīl).", why:"Tanzīl — names the source and the gradual delivery in one word.", note:"Not POET, not SOOTHSAYER — TANZĪL from the LORD OF THE WORLDS.", more:["Nazzalnā ʿalayka'l-qurʾāna tanzīlā!; nuzūl = descent; manzil = a house).","ʿālamīn — the WORLDS (ʿālam = a world — \"that by which [the Creator] is known\" — from ʿ-l-m!).","Tanzīl (Form II) = sent down IN STAGES — Insān 76:23 (nazzalnā… tanzīlā) vs Qadr 97:1 (anzalnāhu — Form IV, ALL AT ONCE).","Two descents: whole to the lower heaven (Qadr), piece by piece to the Prophet ﷺ (Insān, Ḥāqqah).","Rabb al-ʿālamīn — the second verse of AL-FĀTIḤAH!","(al-ḥamdu li'llāhi rabbi'l-ʿālamīn).","And Takwīr 81:29 — illā an yashāʾa'llāhu rabbu'l-ʿālamīn (the last verse of Takwīr).","ʿĀlam (a world) from ʿilm (knowledge) — classical: every world is a SIGN by which its Creator is KNOWN.","Tanzīl (Form II, in STAGES — Insān 76:23) vs anzalnā (Form IV, ALL AT ONCE — Qadr 97:1). Rabb al-ʿālamīn — AL-FĀTIḤAH 1:2. ʿĀlam ← ʿilm: every world = a SIGN by which He is KNOWN."]},
  {ar:"وَلَوْ تَقَوَّلَ عَلَيْنَا بَعْضَ الْأَقَاوِيلِ", tr:"Wa law taqawwala ʿalaynā baʿḍa'l-aqāwīl", v:"44", root:"Taqawwala — Form V of qāla (to say) = to FABRICATE sayings, to put words in someone's mouth, to attribute falsely (the Form V sense of forcing/pretending: \"to make oneself say\").", meaning:"\"And if he had fabricated against Us some sayings…\"", magic:"A HYPOTHETICAL — and one of the strongest proofs of the Qurʾān's authenticity, from within.", grammar:"Law + past = hypothetical condition; its answer comes in v.45 (la-akhadhnā).", why:"Taqawwala (to fabricate attribution) — names precisely the crime the Prophet ﷺ is cleared of.", note:"TAQAWWALA (Form V) = to MAKE UP sayings and ATTRIBUTE them. Even baʿḍ (SOME) — not one word could be invented.", more:["aqāwīl — plural of plural of qawl (qawl → aqwāl → aqāwīl) = invented SAYINGS, fabrications — aqāwīl = (modern) RUMOURS / hearsay!","Law = \"IF\" (contrary to fact) — he DIDN'T — but if he had… Taqawwala (Form V) — not just \"said\" but \"MADE UP and attributed\" — to claim \"Allah said\" when He didn't.","Baʿḍa'l-aqāwīl — even SOME sayings, even a FEW — the standard is total: not one word could be invented.","Aqāwīl = a DOUBLE plural — the \"plural of a plural\" carries a dismissive tone — \"stories, made-up tales\" — and in modern Arabic, aqāwīl = RUMOURS.","Compare the claim of al-Walīd (Muddaththir 74:25) — in hādhā illā qawlu'l-bashar — \"it's just the WORD of a human.\" Here Allah answers: if it were his own invention, this is what would happen (v.45–46).","aqāwīl = jamʿ al-jamʿ (double plural), a diptote-shaped pattern.","AQĀWĪL = a DOUBLE PLURAL — modern: RUMOURS.","Answers Muddaththir 74:25 (\"just the word of a human\").","Law = contrary to fact: he DIDN'T."]},
  {ar:"لَأَخَذْنَا مِنْهُ بِالْيَمِينِ", tr:"La-akhadhnā minhu bi'l-yamīn", v:"45", root:"Akhadhnā — We would SEIZE.", meaning:"\"We would have seized him by the right hand [with power].\"", magic:"Two classical readings: (a) \"We would seize him BY HIS right hand\" — like an executioner grabbing a condemned man's right hand to hold him for the strike; (b) \"We would seize him WITH POWER (bi'l-quwwah)\" — yamīn = strength.", grammar:"La- = the lām in the answer of law. minhu = \"from him\" / \"of him.\"", why:"Yamīn — the strongest hand, and an executioner's grip.", note:"Seized BY THE RIGHT HAND — like an executioner holding the condemned. Or: seized with POWER.", more:["al-yamīn — the RIGHT HAND (and POWER / strength — the right hand is the strong hand; and an OATH).","Both: total, immediate, inescapable seizure.","Al-yamīn in THIS surah: v.19 — the righteous given his book bi-YAMĪNIH (in his right hand — HONOUR).","v.45 — the hypothetical fabricator seized bi'l-YAMĪN (by the right hand — EXECUTION).","Same word: the hand that receives honour or the hand that's grabbed for punishment.","v.19 book bi-YAMĪNIH (HONOUR) vs v.45 seized bi'l-YAMĪN (EXECUTION)."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ لَقَطَعْنَا مِنْهُ الْوَتِينَ", tr:"Thumma la-qaṭaʿnā minhu'l-watīn", v:"46", root:"Qaṭaʿnā — We would CUT (qaṭʿ; qiṭʿah = a piece).", meaning:"\"…then We would have cut his aorta.\"", magic:"Al-watīn — the great artery leaving the heart — cut it, and death is instant.", grammar:"Thumma = sequence (\"then\"). la- = still the answer of law. al-watīna = object.", why:"Watīn (the aorta) — the one cut that guarantees death — making the hypothetical absolute.", note:"AL-WATĪN = the great ARTERY from the heart — the AORTA. Cut it: instant death.", more:["al-watīn — the great ARTERY of the heart — classically: \"a vessel attached to the heart; when it is cut, its owner dies\" — commonly identified with the AORTA.","The root w-t-n = to be fixed, firm, continuous (wātin = constant) — the vessel that FIXES life to the heart.","THE MOST ANATOMICALLY PRECISE THREAT IN THE QURʾĀN.","The image: grab the right hand (v.45), THEN (thumma) sever the aorta (v.46) — an execution, described step by step.","And the logic is the PROOF: the Prophet ﷺ lived 23 years after revelation began, delivering verse after verse in Allah's name — and died naturally in his wife's lap.","If he had invented even ONE saying, his watīn would have been cut.","His natural life and death are the evidence.","And a reported detail, often cited: in his final illness the Prophet ﷺ said he felt \"the cutting of my abhar\" (a major vessel) from the poisoned meat at Khaybar — a different vessel-word, but the classical commentators connected the anatomy.","(Mention as reported.) And recall Qāf 50:16 — wa naḥnu aqrabu ilayhi min ḥabli'l-warīd — \"We are closer to him than his JUGULAR VEIN.\" Two vessels in the Qurʾān: the warīd (jugular — His NEARNESS) and the watīn (aorta — His POWER).","The PROOF: he lived 23 years delivering revelation and died naturally — no watīn was cut.","Qāf 50:16: closer than the WARĪD (JUGULAR) — His nearness.","Ḥāqqah 69:46: the WATĪN (AORTA) — His power.","Grab the right hand → THEN cut the aorta."]},
  {ar:"فَمَا مِنكُم مِّنْ أَحَدٍ عَنْهُ حَاجِزِينَ", tr:"Fa-mā minkum min aḥadin ʿanhu ḥājizīn", v:"47", root:"Aḥad — ANYONE (in negation).", meaning:"\"And not one of you could have shielded him from it.\"", magic:"A GRAMMAR GEM — and it closes the Ikhlāṣ lesson. Min aḥadin (SINGULAR \"anyone\") → ḥājizīn (PLURAL \"barriers / blockers\")! Why plural for a singular?", grammar:"Min aḥadin — the min is extra (zāʾidah) for total negation (\"not a single one\").", why:"The plural adjective makes \"no one\" mean \"none of you, all combined.\"", note:"Min AḤADIN (singular) → ḤĀJIZĪN (PLURAL)!", more:["ḥājizīn — the \"BARRIER / blocking / holding back\" family — ḥajaza = to block, to separate, to hold back; ḥājiz = a BARRIER (ḥājiz amnī = a security barrier; ḥājiz ṣawtī = the SOUND BARRIER!); ḥajz = a RESERVATION / BOOKING (you \"block off\" a seat or room!","— ḥajz fundaq = a hotel booking!); al-Ḥijāz = the region of Makkah and Madīnah (the \"barrier\" between Najd and Tihāmah!).","Because aḥad in a NEGATIVE sentence means \"ANYONE AT ALL\" — ALL of you — exactly what you learned in Ikhlāṣ 112:4 (wa lam yakun lahu kufuwan aḥad — \"not ANYONE\").","Its MEANING is plural, so its adjective is plural.","\"Not one of you — none of you, all together — could block it.\" Ḥājiz = a BARRIER — the companions and family who loved him couldn't form a barrier between him and Allah's punishment.","No one could stand in the way.","Hooks: ḥajz = a BOOKING / reservation; al-Ḥijāz — the land of the revelation itself.","Compare Jinn 72:22 — lan yujīranī mina'llāhi aḥad — \"NO ONE can protect me from Allah.\" The Prophet ﷺ said it himself; Ḥāqqah confirms it.","ḥājizīna = plural adjective / khabar for singular-form aḥad — because aḥad in negation has plural meaning.","Because aḥad in NEGATION = \"ANYONE AT ALL\" — plural meaning (Ikhlāṣ 112:4). ḤAJZ = a BOOKING; ḥājiz = a BARRIER; AL-ḤIJĀZ = the land of revelation.","Jinn 72:22: \"NO ONE can protect me from Allah.\""]},
  {ar:"وَإِنَّهُ لَتَذْكِرَةٌ لِّلْمُتَّقِينَ", tr:"Wa innahu la-tadhkiratun li'l-muttaqīn", v:"48", root:"Tadhkirah — a REMINDER. muttaqīn — those who SHIELD themselves (ittaqā ← wiqāyah, protection — NOT \"fear,\" but ARMOUR).", meaning:"\"And indeed, it is a reminder for those who guard themselves.\"", magic:"The surah's five-step close begins (v.48–52) — each with inna + lām: la-tadhkirah (a REMINDER, v.48) → mukadhdhibīn (deniers among you, v.49) → la-ḥasrah (a REGRET, v.50) → la-ḥaqqu'l-yaqīn (the TRUTH of certainty, v.51) → fa-sabbiḥ (so GLORIFY, v.52).", grammar:"Inna + lām = double emphasis (×3 in v.48–51!). li'l-muttaqīn = \"for\" (benefit).", why:"Frames the whole Qurʾān's purpose before the final verdicts.", note:"FIVE-STEP CLOSE: REMINDER (48) → DENIERS (49) → REGRET (50) → TRUTH OF CERTAINTY (51) → GLORIFY (52). One Qurʾān: reminder for the muttaqīn, regret for the deniers.", more:["The same Qurʾān is: a reminder (for the muttaqīn), a regret (for the kāfirīn), and the truth of certainty (for everyone).","Li'l-MUTTAQĪN — a reminder FOR those who already have taqwā — compare Baqarah 2:2 — hudan li'l-muttaqīn (guidance FOR the God-conscious).","The reminder lands on those whose shields are already raised (Nāziʿāt 79:45, mundhiru man yakhshāhā — you warn those who already have awe).","And v.12 called the ARK a tadhkirah; v.48 calls the QURʾĀN a tadhkirah.","Both carry people to safety.","v.12 the ARK = tadhkirah; v.48 the QURʾĀN = tadhkirah.","Baqarah 2:2: hudan li'l-muttaqīn."]},
  {ar:"وَإِنَّا لَنَعْلَمُ أَنَّ مِنكُم مُّكَذِّبِينَ", tr:"Wa innā la-naʿlamu anna minkum mukadhdhibīn", v:"49", root:"Naʿlamu — We KNOW. mukadhdhibīn — those who DENY / call it a lie (Form II participle).", meaning:"\"And indeed, We know that among you are deniers.\"", magic:"A quiet, chilling sentence: \"We KNOW.\" No threat stated — just knowledge. After v.18 (\"not one hidden thing will stay hidden\") — He already knows who denies.", grammar:"Inna + lām again. minkum fronted (\"among you [are] deniers\"). mukadhdhibīna = subject of anna (accusative).", why:"Knowledge as threat — nothing more needs saying.", note:"\"We KNOW there are deniers among you\" — knowledge as the threat. Minkum = SOME of you — the door is still open.", more:["And minkum — \"AMONG you\" — not \"all of you\" — some will deny; some will believe.","Compare Muzzammil 73:11 — wa dharnī wa'l-mukadhdhibīn — \"leave Me with the DENIERS.\" Same word.","Muzzammil 73:11: dharnī wa'l-mukadhdhibīn."]},
  {ar:"وَإِنَّهُ لَحَسْرَةٌ عَلَى الْكَافِرِينَ", tr:"Wa innahu la-ḥasratun ʿala'l-kāfirīn", v:"50", root:"ḥasrah — the \"UNCOVERING / exhaustion → bitter REGRET\" family — ḥasara = to uncover, to strip (ḥāsir = bare-headed).", meaning:"\"And indeed, it will be a source of anguish for the disbelievers.\"", magic:"The SAME Qurʾān — for the believer, a REMINDER (v.48); for the disbeliever, a REGRET (v.50). Why regret?", grammar:"Inna + lām (third time). ʿalā = \"upon / against\" (burden).", why:"ḥasrah — the exact emotion of the left-hand man, named as the Qurʾān's effect on deniers.", note:"SAME Qurʾān: REMINDER for believers (v.48), REGRET for deniers (v.50).", more:["ḥasra = intense, grieving REGRET — classically, regret so deep it leaves you exhausted, stripped bare; yawm al-ḥasrah = the Day of Regret (19:39); taḥassur = sighing with regret (yā ḥasratā!","= \"Oh, the regret!\"); inḥisār = receding / recession (water \"uncovering\" the ground!).","Because they'll see the believers' reward and realize: the cure was handed to them, and they refused it.","The book they mocked becomes the source of their deepest anguish.","Ḥasrah = regret that STRIPS you bare — from ḥasara, to uncover — the excuses fall away, and you stand exposed.","Compare v.18 — lā takhfā minkum khāfiyah — nothing hidden.","And the regret is the voice of v.25–29: yā laytanī… yā laytahā… — that IS ḥasrah.","The yā laytanī thread (78:40, 89:24, 69:25, 27) = ḥasrah in action.","Ḥasrah ← ḥasara = to STRIP BARE — regret that strips every excuse.","The left-hand man's yā laytanī = ḥasrah in action.","Yawm al-ḥasrah (19:39); yā ḥasratā!"]},
  {ar:"وَإِنَّهُ لَحَقُّ الْيَقِينِ", tr:"Wa innahu la-ḥaqqu'l-yaqīn", v:"51", root:"ḥaqq — TRUTH, what is established and due (v.1 al-Ḥāqqah!).", meaning:"\"And indeed, it is the truth of certainty.\"", magic:"Takāthur 102:5 — ʿilm al-yaqīn — certainty by being TOLD (the lab report). Takāthur 102:7 — ʿayn al-yaqīn — certainty by SEEING (the scan).", grammar:"ḥaqqu'l-yaqīni = iḍāfah — classical: an iḍāfah of a thing to its own description (\"the certain truth,\" \"truth that is certainty itself\") — maximum emphasis.", why:"ḥaqq al-yaqīn — the highest grade of certainty there is, claimed for the Qurʾān — and rhyming the surah's ending with its opening.", note:"THE TOP RUNG: ʿilm al-yaqīn (TOLD, 102:5) → ʿayn al-yaqīn (SEEN, 102:7) → ḤAQQ AL-YAQĪN (BEING IN IT, 69:51). Lab report → scan → being the patient.", more:["yaqīn — CERTAINTY (and in the Qurʾān, al-yaqīn = DEATH — 15:99, Muddaththir 74:47).","THE PAYOFF OF THE WHOLE SERIES — the TOP RUNG.","Ḥāqqah 69:51 — ḥaqq al-yaqīn — certainty that is REAL, experienced, the thing itself (being the patient).","Ḥaqq al-yaqīn = \"the TRUTH of certainty\" / \"certain truth itself\" — beyond hearing and beyond seeing: the reality you are INSIDE.","Classical illustration: knowing there's fire (ʿilm), seeing the fire (ʿayn), being burned by it (ḥaqq) — or: knowing there's honey, seeing honey, TASTING honey.","The Qurʾān, in this verse, claims to be the third: not a report about reality — REALITY ITSELF.","AND THE SURAH'S BOOKEND: v.1 — AL-ḤĀQQAH — the Inevitable TRUTH (the Day).","v.51 — ḤAQQU'L-YAQĪN — the TRUTH of certainty (the Qurʾān).","The surah opens with the TRUE Day and closes with the TRUE Book.","Both are ḥaqq; both are coming whether believed or not.","And Muddaththir 74:47 — ḥattā atāna'l-yaqīn (until DEATH came) — the deniers reached yaqīn only through dying.","The believer reaches it through the Qurʾān.","inna + lām (fourth double-emphasis in four verses).","Hear of honey → see honey → TASTE honey.","v.1 AL-ḤĀQQAH (the TRUE Day) → v.51 ḤAQQU'L-YAQĪN (the TRUE Book).","Muddaththir 74:47: deniers reached yaqīn only by DYING."]},
  {ar:"فَسَبِّحْ بِاسْمِ رَبِّكَ الْعَظِيمِ", tr:"Fa-sabbiḥ bi'smi rabbika'l-ʿaẓīm", v:"52", root:"Sabbiḥ — GLORIFY (tasbīḥ — from sabaḥa, to swim/glide away from every flaw). ism — NAME.", meaning:"\"So glorify the name of your Lord, the Magnificent.\"", magic:"A ḥadīth (ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir — Abū Dāwūd, Ibn Mājah): when fa-sabbiḥ bi'smi rabbika'l-ʿAẒĪM was revealed, the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Place it in your RUKŪʿ\" — → subḥāna rabbiya'l-ʿAẒĪM.", grammar:"Fa = consequence (\"so\"). Form II imperative (sabbiḥ).", why:"Ending the surah of certainty with the formula of the bowed position.", note:"SUBḤĀNA RABBIYA'L-ʿAẒĪM in RUKŪʿ comes FROM THIS VERSE (ḥadīth: \"place it in your rukūʿ\").", more:["al-ʿaẓīm — the MAGNIFICENT (ʿaẓm = bone).","THE ORIGIN OF WHAT YOU SAY IN EVERY RUKŪʿ.","And when sabbiḥi'sma rabbika'l-AʿLĀ (Aʿlā 87:1) was revealed, he said: \"Place it in your SUJŪD\" — → subḥāna rabbiya'l-AʿLĀ.","So the two phrases you say in EVERY rakʿah come from TWO SURAHS: BOWING → al-ʿAẒĪM (the Magnificent) — Ḥāqqah 69:52. PROSTRATING → al-AʿLĀ (the Most High) — Aʿlā 87:1. And the logic is perfect: when you BOW (lower yourself a little), you praise His MAGNIFICENCE; when you PROSTRATE (lowest point), you praise Him as the MOST HIGH — the lower you go, the higher you call Him.","And al-ʿAẒĪM closes the loop from v.33: the left-hand man lā yuʾminu bi'llāhi'l-ʿAẒĪM (didn't believe in Allah the Magnificent) → the surah ends: fa-sabbiḥ bi'smi rabbika'l-ʿAẒĪM.","The Name he rejected is the Name you glorify.","And the fa = \"SO\" — because it's ḥaqq al-yaqīn (v.51), SO glorify.","Certainty produces worship.","Compare Naṣr 110:3 — fa-sabbiḥ bi-ḥamdi rabbik — victory → glorify.","Here: certainty → glorify.","And it ends the surah on tasbīḥ — after Thamūd, ʿĀd, Pharaoh, the flood, the blast, the two books, the aorta — the last word is PRAISE.","bi'smi — the bi- here (vs Aʿlā's direct sabbiḥi'sma) — classically: glorify [Allah] BY MENTIONING His Name.","BOW → al-ʿAẒĪM (Ḥāqqah 69:52). PROSTRATE → al-AʿLĀ (Aʿlā 87:1). The lower you go, the HIGHER you call Him.","v.33: he didn't believe in al-ʿAẒĪM → v.52: GLORIFY al-ʿAẒĪM.","Certainty (v.51) → worship (v.52)."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:70, name:"Al-Maʿārij", ar:"المعارج", en:"The Ascending Stairways", verses:44, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"HALŪʿ = JAZŪʿ (panics in loss) + MANŪʿ (hoards in gain). The cure: dāʾimūn (constant) in prayer — the opposite of Māʿūn's sāhūn.",
  special:["It uses a rare word to diagnose human nature — and then DEFINES that word in the very next two verses.","V.19: inna'l-insāna khuliqa halūʿā — \"Man was created HALŪʿ.\"","What's halūʿ? Most readers wouldn't know. So the Qurʾān tells you: - v.20: idhā massahu'sh-sharru jazūʿā — \"when harm touches him — FRETFUL,\" - v.21: wa idhā massahu'l-khayru manūʿā — \"and when good touches him — WITHHOLDING.\"","Halūʿ = panicky when things go wrong, stingy when things go right. The Qurʾān is its own dictionary (tafsīr al-Qurʾān bi'l-Qurʾān). All three words are the same intensive shape — faʿūl — a disposition, not a mood.","Then v.22: illa'l-muṣallīn — \"EXCEPT those who PRAY\" — and eight traits follow, BRACKETED by prayer at both ends (v.23 and v.34). The cure for anxious, grasping human nature is a life built inside prayer.","And recall Māʿūn 107:4–5: fa-waylun li'l-muṣallīn · alladhīna hum ʿan ṣalātihim sāhūn — WOE to the praying who are heedless of their prayer. Maʿārij 70:22–23: illa'l-muṣallīn · alladhīna hum ʿalā ṣalātihim dāʾimūn — EXCEPT the praying who are CONSTANT in their prayer. Same word muṣallīn: one group cursed, one group exempted — and the difference is sāhūn (heedless) vs dāʾimūn (constant).","--"],
  big:["The mocking request (v.1–4) — someone asked for the punishment; it's coming from the Lord of the Ascents.","Beautiful patience (v.5–7) — they see it far; We see it near.","That Day (v.8–14) — sky like molten metal, mountains like wool; no friend asks of a friend; a man would ransom his whole family.","Laẓā (v.15–18) — the flame that strips the scalp and calls those who turned away and hoarded.","Human nature (v.19–21) — halūʿ: fretful and withholding.","The exceptions (v.22–35) — eight traits of those who pray.","The scattered deniers (v.36–41) — why do they rush around you in clusters? Each wants Paradise…","Leave them (v.42–44) — let them wade and play until their Day."],
  traps:["هَلُوعًا (v.19) — DEFINED by the next two verses (jazūʿ + manūʿ). Don't translate it independently; let v.20–21 define it.","عَلَىٰ صَلَاتِهِمْ (v.23) vs عَن صَلَاتِهِمْ (Māʿūn 107:5) — ʿalā (planted ON it) vs ʿan (drifting AWAY). One preposition, two destinies.","الْمَشَارِقِ وَالْمَغَارِبِ (v.40) — PLURAL — singular in 73:9, dual in 55:17. No contradiction: three zoom levels.","الْمَحْرُومِ (v.25) — the poor who does NOT ask, distinct from as-sāʾil (who asks).","مُّشْفِقُونَ (v.27) — TENDER, caring fear (from shafaq, twilight) — NOT panic (halaʿ).","يُوفِضُونَ (v.43) — from w-f-ḍ (to race), NOT afāḍa (f-y-ḍ — departing ʿArafāt).","حَمِيمٌ حَمِيمًا (v.10) — here = FRIENDS, not scalding water (Nabaʾ 78:25).","يَخُوضُوا وَيَلْعَبُوا (v.42) — JUSSIVE (nūn dropped) — answer to the command dharhum.","مَسْبُوقِينَ (v.41) — extra bi- after negation (bi-masbūqīn) for emphasis. And masbūq in fiqh = a latecomer to prayer.","نَزَّاعَةً (v.16) — two recitations: nazzāʿatan (accusative ḥāl) / nazzāʿatun (nominative).","يَوْمِئِذٍ (v.11) — recitation variant yawmaʾidhin.","خَمْسِينَ أَلْفَ سَنَةٍ (v.4) — alf + SINGULAR genitive (sanah, not sanawāt).","الْمَعَارِجِ · الْمَشَارِقِ · الْمَغَارِبِ — DIPTOTE patterns (mafāʿil) — kasrah only because of al-.","ذِي (v.3) — the five-noun dhū/dhā/dhī (genitive).","فَمَالِ (v.36) — the lām is written SEPARATELY in the muṣḥaf (fa-māli'lladhīna).","بِشَهَادَاتِهِمْ (v.33) — variant bi-shahādatihim (singular).","مَا مَلَكَتْ أَيْمَانُهُمْ (v.30) — a historical legal category, understood in its time and regulated toward reduction."],
  links:[{w:"سَائِلٌ (v.1) · لِّلسَّائِلِ (v.25)",e:"السَّائِلَ (Ḍuḥā 93:10)",t:"Asks for PUNISHMENT / asks for FOOD"},{w:"وَاقِعٍ (v.1)",e:"وَقَعَتِ الْوَاقِعَةُ (Ḥāqqah 69:15)",t:"Back-to-back surahs"},{w:"دَافِعٌ (v.2)",e:"نَاصِرٍ (86:10) · حَاجِزِينَ (69:47)",t:"No helper, no barrier, no repeller"},{w:"الْمَعَارِجِ (v.3)",e:"Al-Miʿrāj",t:"The Night Ascension"},{w:"تَعْرُجُ الْمَلَائِكَةُ وَالرُّوحُ (v.4)",e:"تَنَزَّلُ الْمَلَائِكَةُ وَالرُّوحُ (Qadr 97:4)",t:"DESCEND / ASCEND"},{w:"مِقْدَارُهُ... أَلْفَ (v.4)",e:"خَيْرٌ مِّنْ أَلْفِ شَهْرٍ (Qadr 97:3)",t:"Two alf-measurings"},{w:"صَبْرًا جَمِيلًا (v.5)",e:"هَجْرًا جَمِيلًا (Muzzammil 73:10)",t:"The jamīl triad"},{w:"بَعِيدًا / قَرِيبًا (v.6–7)",e:"أَقَرِيبٌ (Jinn 72:25)",t:"\"I don't know\" / \"it IS near\""},{w:"كَالْمُهْلِ (v.8)",e:"وَاهِيَةٌ (69:16) · مَهِّلْهُمْ (73:11)",t:"Sky-fates; respite ends"},{w:"كَالْعِهْنِ (v.9)",e:"كَالْعِهْنِ الْمَنفُوشِ (Qāriʿah 101:5)",t:"Nearly identical"},{w:"حَمِيمٌ حَمِيمًا (v.10)",e:"حَمِيمًا (78:25) · حَمِيمٌ (69:35)",t:"Scalding water → no friend → friend ignores friend"},{w:"بِبَنِيهِ... (v.11–14)",e:"يَفِرُّ... بَنِيهِ (ʿAbasa 80:34–36)",t:"FLEE dearest-LAST / RANSOM dearest-FIRST"},{w:"يَفْتَدِي (v.11)",e:"رَهِينَةٌ (Muddaththir 74:38)",t:"Collateral tries to pay a ransom"},{w:"تُؤْوِيهِ (v.13)",e:"الْمَأْوَىٰ (Nāziʿāt 79:39, 41)",t:"The clan was his refuge; now Hell is"},{w:"لَظَىٰ (v.15)",e:"تَلَظَّىٰ (Layl 92:14)",t:"The verb → the name"},{w:"نَزَّاعَةً (v.16)",e:"وَالنَّازِعَاتِ (Nāziʿāt 79:1)",t:"Souls pulled out → skin pulled off"},{w:"أَدْبَرَ وَتَوَلَّىٰ (v.17)",e:"كَذَّبَ وَتَوَلَّىٰ (92:16) · أَدْبَرَ (74:23 · 79:22)",t:"Same Fire, same turning"},{w:"فَأَوْعَىٰ (v.18)",e:"أُذُنٌ وَاعِيَةٌ (Ḥāqqah 69:12)",t:"Vessel of reminder / vessel of gold"},{w:"جَمَعَ (v.18)",e:"جَمَعَ مَالًا (Humazah 104:2)",t:"Gather, count, lock away"},{w:"هَلُوعًا (v.19)",e:"كَنُودٌ (100:6) · يَطْغَىٰ (96:6) · خُسْرٍ (103:2)",t:"Four inna'l-insān diagnoses"},{w:"جَزُوعًا (v.20)",e:"صَبْرًا جَمِيلًا (v.5)",t:"Ṣabr vs jazaʿ"},{w:"مَنُوعًا (v.21)",e:"يَمْنَعُونَ (Māʿūn 107:7)",t:"Withholding"},{w:"إِلَّا الْمُصَلِّينَ (v.22)",e:"فَوَيْلٌ لِّلْمُصَلِّينَ (Māʿūn 107:4)",t:"Woe to / except — same word"},{w:"عَلَىٰ صَلَاتِهِمْ دَائِمُونَ (v.23)",e:"عَن صَلَاتِهِمْ سَاهُونَ (Māʿūn 107:5)",t:"ʿalā / ʿan — constant / heedless"},{w:"حَقٌّ مَّعْلُومٌ (v.24)",e:"الْحَاقَّةُ (69:1) · طَعَامِ الْمِسْكِينِ",t:"The poor's share is a RIGHT"},{w:"يُصَدِّقُونَ بِيَوْمِ الدِّينِ (v.26)",e:"يُكَذِّبُ بِالدِّينِ (82:9 · 95:7 · 107:1 · 74:46)",t:"Affirm vs deny"},{w:"مُّشْفِقُونَ (v.27)",e:"بِالشَّفَقِ (Inshiqāq 84:16)",t:"Twilight → tender fear"},{w:"غَيْرُ مَأْمُونٍ (v.28)",e:"وَآمَنَهُم (Quraysh 106:4)",t:"Amn in dunya, none from His justice"},{w:"حَافِظُونَ · يُحَافِظُونَ (v.29, 34)",e:"حَافِظٌ (Ṭāriq 86:4) · مَّحْفُوظٍ (85:22)",t:"Guarding"},{w:"مَلُومِينَ (v.30)",e:"اللَّوَّامَةِ (Qiyāmah 75:2)",t:"Blame"},{w:"أَزْوَاجِهِمْ (v.30)",e:"أَزْوَاجًا (Nabaʾ 78:8)",t:"Pairs"},{w:"الْعَادُونَ (v.31)",e:"وَالْعَادِيَاتِ (ʿĀdiyāt 100:1)",t:"Charging horses / running past the line"},{w:"ابْتَغَىٰ (v.31)",e:"ابْتِغَاءَ (92:20) · يَبْتَغُونَ (73:20)",t:"Seek His face / bounty / beyond the limit"},{w:"رَاعُونَ (v.32)",e:"Kullukum rāʿin",t:"Every one a SHEPHERD"},{w:"قَائِمُونَ (v.33)",e:"73:2 · 74:2 · 72:16 · 95:4 · 98:5",t:"The q-w-m thread"},{w:"دَائِمُونَ / يُحَافِظُونَ (v.23, 34)",e:"Al-Muʾminūn 23:2, 9",t:"The prayer frame"},{w:"مُّكْرَمُونَ (v.35)",e:"أَكْرَمَهُ (89:15) · تُكْرِمُونَ (89:17)",t:"Honoured → didn't honour → honoured forever"},{w:"فَمَالِ (v.36)",e:"فَمَا لَهُمْ (Muddaththir 74:49)",t:"Flee / rush to mock"},{w:"الْيَمِينِ / الشِّمَالِ (v.37)",e:"Ḥāqqah 69:19, 25 · Balad 90:18–19",t:"Right and left"},{w:"عِزِينَ (v.37)",e:"قِدَدًا (72:11) · أَشْتَاتًا (99:6)",t:"Disbelief scatters"},{w:"أَيَطْمَعُ كُلُّ امْرِئٍ مِّنْهُمْ (v.38)",e:"يَطْمَعُ (74:15) · كُلُّ امْرِئٍ مِّنْهُمْ (74:52)",t:"Both Muddaththir phrases fused"},{w:"مِّمَّا يَعْلَمُونَ (v.39)",e:"مَاءٍ دَافِقٍ (86:6) · نُّطْفَةٍ (80:19)",t:"The euphemism IS the insult"},{w:"الْمَشَارِقِ وَالْمَغَارِبِ (v.40)",e:"الْمَشْرِقِ وَالْمَغْرِبِ (Muzzammil 73:9)",t:"Singular / dual / plural"},{w:"لَقَادِرُونَ عَلَىٰ أَن (v.40–41)",e:"قَادِرِينَ عَلَىٰ أَن (Qiyāmah 75:4)",t:"Fingertips / a whole people"},{w:"مَسْبُوقِينَ (v.41)",e:"فَالسَّابِقَاتِ (Nāziʿāt 79:4)",t:"Not outrun"},{w:"فَذَرْهُمْ (v.42)",e:"ذَرْنِي (73:11 · 74:11)",t:"Leave them to Me → leave them be"},{w:"يَخُوضُوا (v.42)",e:"نَخُوضُ (Muddaththir 74:45)",t:"Wading now / confessing later"},{w:"يُلَاقُوا (v.42)",e:"فَمُلَاقِيهِ (84:6) · مُلَاقٍ (69:20)",t:"The meeting"},{w:"الْأَجْدَاثِ (v.43)",e:"Yāsīn 36:51 · الْقُبُورِ (82:4, 100:9)",t:"Graves"},{w:"نُصُبٍ (v.43)",e:"نُصِبَتْ (88:19) · نَاصِبَةٌ (88:3) · فَانصَبْ (94:7)",t:"N-ṣ-b — four senses"},{w:"خَاشِعَةً أَبْصَارُهُمْ (v.44)",e:"أَبْصَارُهَا خَاشِعَةٌ (Nāziʿāt 79:9)",t:"Nearly identical — and khushūʿ in prayer"},{w:"تَرْهَقُهُمْ ذِلَّةٌ (v.44)",e:"تَرْهَقُهَا قَتَرَةٌ (ʿAbasa 80:41)",t:"Darkness / humiliation covers"},{w:"ذَٰلِكَ الْيَوْمُ (v.44)",e:"ذَٰلِكَ الْيَوْمُ الْحَقُّ (Nabaʾ 78:39)",t:"The TRUE Day / the PROMISED Day"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"سَأَلَ سَائِلٌ بِعَذَابٍ وَاقِعٍ", tr:"Saʾala sāʾilun bi-ʿadhābin wāqiʿ", v:"1", root:"Saʾala — the \"ASKING\" family — suʾāl = a question; sāʾil = one who asks / a BEGGAR (Ḍuḥā 93:10.", meaning:"\"A questioner asked for a punishment bound to fall…\"", magic:"A MOCKING request — answered with the very word for certainty.", grammar:"Saʾala bi- = \"asked FOR / called for\" (the bi- conveys \"calling for\").", why:"Wāqiʿ — answers mockery with certainty in one adjective.", note:"A MOCKER asked for punishment (\"rain stones on us!\" 8:32) → it is WĀQIʿ — certain to FALL.", more:["Wa ammā as-sāʾila fa-lā tanhar!); masʾūl = RESPONSIBLE (one who will be asked!).","wāqiʿ — the \"FALLING / happening\" family — al-Wāqiʿah (Ḥāqqah 69:15!); wāqiʿ = REALITY.","Classical: an-Naḍr ibn al-Ḥārith (among others) said: \"O Allah, if this is the truth from You, rain stones on us from the sky!\" (8:32) — demanding the punishment as a dare.","Bi-ʿadhābin WĀQIʿ — \"a punishment that WILL FALL\" — he asked mockingly; the Qurʾān answers: it is wāqiʿ — REAL, certain, already falling.","And the previous surah, Ḥāqqah 69:15 — fa-yawmaʾidhin waqaʿati'l-wāqiʿah — the same root in back-to-back surahs: the Event FELL (69:15); the punishment WILL FALL (70:1). Saʾala sāʾil — a cognate pair: \"an asker asked\" — unnamed, contemptuous.","And the sāʾil returns in v.25 — li's-sāʾili wa'l-maḥrūm — the BEGGAR who asks for charity.","Same word: v.1 a man ASKS for punishment; v.25 a poor man ASKS for food.","One request mocks, the other is a right.","sāʾilun = indefinite (unnamed, contempt).","Ḥāqqah 69:15 waqaʿati'l-wāqiʿah → Maʿārij 70:1 ʿadhābin wāqiʿ — back-to-back.","SĀʾIL: v.1 asks for PUNISHMENT; v.25 asks for FOOD.","Masʾūl = RESPONSIBLE; al-wāqiʿ = REALITY."]},
  {ar:"لِّلْكَافِرِينَ لَيْسَ لَهُ دَافِعٌ", tr:"Li'l-kāfirīna laysa lahu dāfiʿ", v:"2", root:"The \"PUSHING AWAY / repelling\" family — dafaʿa = to push, to repel, to PAY.", meaning:"\"…upon the disbelievers — none can repel it.\"", magic:"Laysa lahu DĀFIʿ — \"it has NO REPELLER\" — no defence, no army, no shield, no payment can push it back.", grammar:"Laysa lahu dāfiʿun — fronted lahu (\"for it — no repeller\"). li'l-kāfirīna = \"for / upon the disbelievers.\"", why:"Dāfiʿ — names the futility of every defence, army or payment.", note:"Laysa lahu DĀFIʿ = NO REPELLER — no DEFENCE (difāʿ), no PAYMENT (dafʿ).", more:["Difāʿ = DEFENCE (wizārat ad-difāʿ = the Ministry of Defence!); dafʿ = PAYMENT (dafʿ ilikitrūnī = electronic payment!); madfaʿ = a CANNON; dāfiʿ = a repeller / motive (dāfiʿ = a MOTIVE / incentive!).","Difāʿ = DEFENCE — every \"Ministry of Defence\" on earth combined couldn't repel it.","Compare Ṭāriq 86:10 — fa-mā lahu min quwwatin wa lā nāṣir (no strength, no helper) and Ḥāqqah 69:47 — fa-mā minkum min aḥadin ʿanhu ḥājizīn (no one can BLOCK it).","No nāṣir (helper), no ḥājiz (barrier), no dāfiʿ (repeller).","And dafʿ = PAYMENT — you can't PAY your way out of it (v.11–14: he'd ransom his whole family — rejected).","No nāṣir (86:10), no ḥājiz (69:47), no dāfiʿ (70:2). Madfaʿ = a CANNON; dāfiʿ = a MOTIVE."]},
  {ar:"مِّنَ اللَّهِ ذِي الْمَعَارِجِ", tr:"Mina'llāhi dhi'l-maʿārij", v:"3", root:"The \"ASCENDING / climbing / stairways\" family — ʿaraja = to ascend, to climb.", meaning:"\"…from Allah, Lord of the Ascending Stairways.\"", magic:"THE SURAH'S NAME — and the root of AL-MIʿRĀJ. Dhu'l-maʿārij = the Owner of the STAIRWAYS — classical: the heavens rising in levels, which the angels ascend (v.4).", grammar:"Dhī = genitive five-noun, describing Allāh.", why:"Maʿārij — lifts the reader's eyes upward, toward the source of the punishment and of the angels' ascent.", note:"MAʿĀRIJ = STAIRWAYS — the root of AL-MIʿRĀJ (the Night Ascension). Dhu'l-maʿārij = Lord of the Stairways.", more:["Maʿārij = ASCENTS, STAIRWAYS, ladders (plural of miʿraj); al-MIʿRĀJ = the Prophet's ﷺ ASCENSION through the heavens!; ʿurūj = ascent; and — surprisingly — aʿraj = LAME / limping (one who goes up and down in his step!).","Al-MIʿRĀJ — the Night of Ascension, when the Prophet ﷺ was taken up through the heavens — the same root: he ascended the maʿārij of the One who owns them.","The mocker in v.1 demands punishment from below; the punishment comes from the One whose stairways reach the highest heaven.","Dhu — one of the FIVE SPECIAL NOUNS (dhū / dhā / dhī) — dhi'l-maʿārij (genitive) — like dhi'l-ʿarsh (Takwīr 81:20), dhū'l-ʿarsh (Burūj 85:15). Odd hook: aʿraj = LAME — the limp goes \"up and down.\"","maʿārij = DIPTOTE (mafāʿil pattern) — here genitive with kasrah because it has al-.","Dhī = five-noun (81:20, 85:15). Aʿraj = LAME (up and down)."]},
  {ar:"تَعْرُجُ الْمَلَائِكَةُ وَالرُّوحُ إِلَيْهِ", tr:"Taʿruju'l-malāʾikatu wa'r-rūḥu ilayh", v:"4", root:"Taʿruju — ASCEND (ʿaraja). al-malāʾikah — the ANGELS. ar-Rūḥ — the SPIRIT (Jibrīl, per most) — Nabaʾ 78:38, Qadr 97:4.", meaning:"\"The angels and the Spirit ascend to Him…\"", magic:"THE MIRROR OF SŪRAT AL-QADR — descent vs ascent. Qadr 97:4 — tanazzalu'l-malāʾikatu wa'r-rūḥu fīhā — \"the angels and the Spirit DESCEND\" (on the Night of Decree).", grammar:"Taʿruju = feminine present (for the plural malāʾikah). ilayhi = \"to Him.\"", why:"ʿaraja — completes the picture of the stairways: traffic upward to the Throne.", note:"Qadr 97:4: TANAZZALU'l-malāʾikatu wa'r-rūḥ (DESCEND). Maʿārij 70:4: TAʿRUJU'l-malāʾikatu wa'r-rūḥ (ASCEND).", more:["Maʿārij 70:4 — taʿruju'l-malāʾikatu wa'r-rūḥu ilayh — \"the angels and the Spirit ASCEND\" (to Him).","SAME SUBJECTS, SAME ORDER, opposite directions: tanazzalu (DOWN) vs taʿruju (UP).","The stairways (maʿārij) go both ways.","And ar-Rūḥ named separately again (ʿaṭf al-khāṣṣ ʿala'l-ʿāmm — joining the special to the general for honour) — Nabaʾ 78:38 (standing silent in ranks), Qadr 97:4 (descending), Maʿārij 70:4 (ascending).","Three appearances of the Rūḥ: silent, descending, ascending.","Same subjects, opposite directions.","The Rūḥ: silent (78:38), descending (97:4), ascending (70:4)."]},
  {ar:"فِي يَوْمٍ كَانَ مِقْدَارُهُ خَمْسِينَ أَلْفَ سَنَةٍ", tr:"Fī yawmin kāna miqdāruhu khamsīna alfa sanah", v:"4", root:"Miqdār — the \"MEASURE / amount\" family — qadr (Qadr 97:1, Muzzammil 73:20 yuqaddiru); miqdār = an AMOUNT, a measure, a quantity (everyday!).", meaning:"\"…in a Day whose measure is fifty thousand years.\"", magic:"FIFTY THOUSAND YEARS — and the qadr root is the measuring word.", grammar:"Khamsīna = accusative (khabar of kāna); alfa = iḍāfah.", why:"Miqdār — ties the vastness of the Day to the root of divine measuring.", note:"A Day of FIFTY THOUSAND years — (ḥadīth: shorter than one prayer for the believer). Qadr 97:3: a NIGHT > a THOUSAND months.", more:["khamsīn — FIFTY (khamsah = five; al-khamāsīn = the hot desert wind of ~50 days!).","alf — THOUSAND (Qadr 97:3 alf shahr).","Classical readings: (a) the length of Judgment Day itself — long for the disbeliever — (a ḥadīth: for the believer it will feel shorter than the time of an obligatory prayer); (b) the distance of the angels' ascent — what would take humans 50,000 years, they cover in a day.","Miqdār (measure) — the qadr root: Qadr 97:3 — one night khayrun min ALFI shahr (better than a THOUSAND months).","Maʿārij 70:4 — one day = FIFTY THOUSAND years.","Two \"measurings\" with alf: a night worth a lifetime; a Day lasting fifty millennia.","Time is His to stretch and compress.","And Muzzammil 73:20 — wa'llāhu yuqaddiru'l-layla wa'n-nahār — He measures night and day.","Here He measures a Day of 50,000 years.","Hook: miqdār = an AMOUNT; al-khamāsīn = the 50-day desert wind.","Sanatin = singular genitive after alf (numbers 100/1000 take a singular counted noun!).","Maʿārij 70:4: a DAY = FIFTY THOUSAND years.","Time is His to compress and stretch.","Miqdār = an AMOUNT (qadr root)."]},
  {ar:"فَاصْبِرْ صَبْرًا جَمِيلًا", tr:"Fa'ṣbir ṣabran jamīlā", v:"5", root:"ṣabr — RESTRAINT / patience (ṣabir = ALOES — bitter, healing). jamīl — BEAUTIFUL (jamāl).", meaning:"\"So be patient with a beautiful patience.\"", magic:"THE JAMĪL TRIAD — and this is the second of the three. Muzzammil 73:10 — wa'hjurhum hajran jamīlā — BEAUTIFUL LEAVING. Maʿārij 70:5 — fa'ṣbir ṣabran jamīlā — BEAUTIFUL PATIENCE.", grammar:"Imperative + cognate accusative with adjective (ṣabran jamīlan) — the adjective specifies the MANNER.", why:"Jamīl — because the quality of patience matters, not just its duration.", note:"The JAMĪL TRIAD: hajr jamīl (beautiful LEAVING, 73:10), ṣabr jamīl (beautiful PATIENCE, 70:5), ṣafḥ jamīl (beautiful FORGIVENESS, 15:85). Ṣabr jamīl = NO COMPLAINT except to Allah — Yaʿqūb: \"I complain ONLY to Allah\" (12:86). SO be patient — justice is coming.", more:["(Ḥijr 15:85 — fa'ṣfaḥi'ṣ-ṣafḥa'l-jamīl — BEAUTIFUL FORGIVENESS.) Three beautiful responses to being wronged: leave, endure, forgive — all jamīl.","Ṣabr jamīl — the definition (classical): patience WITHOUT COMPLAINT to anyone but Allah.","Yaʿqūb (peace be upon him), losing Yūsuf: fa-ṣabrun jamīl (12:18, 12:83) — and he said innamā ashkū baththī wa ḥuznī ila'llāh — \"I complain of my grief ONLY to Allah\" (12:86). Crying to Allah is allowed; complaining about Him to people is not.","And the fa = \"SO\" — because the punishment is certain (v.1) and none can repel it (v.2) — SO be patient with the mockers.","Justice is coming; you don't need to force it."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّهُمْ يَرَوْنَهُ بَعِيدًا · وَنَرَاهُ قَرِيبًا", tr:"Innahum yarawnahu baʿīdā · wa narāhu qarībā", v:"6–7", root:"Yarawn / narā — SEE (raʾā). baʿīd — FAR (buʿd = distance; baʿīdan ʿan = far from).", meaning:"\"Indeed, they see it as far off, but We see it as near.\"", magic:"Two \"seeings,\" two distances — a perfect ṭibāq. THEY see it FAR; WE see it NEAR. Same verb (raʾā), opposite adjectives.", grammar:"Yarawnahu / narāhu — raʾā in the sense of judging / considering (a verb of the heart, taking two objects).", why:"The same verb with opposite adjectives — so the gap is in the viewer, not the event.", note:"THEY see it FAR; WE see it NEAR — same verb, opposite distances. Their \"far\" = their DOUBT, not the calendar.", more:["qarīb — NEAR (qurb; ʿAlaq 96:19 iqtarib).","Classical: \"far\" in their eyes = impossible, unlikely; \"near\" in His = certain, imminent.","Because what is certain IS near — everything coming is close (kullu ātin qarīb).","And the Fifty-Thousand-Year Day (v.4) — seems impossibly far — yet \"We see it near.\" Their \"far\" is a measure of their doubt, not of time.","Compare Jinn 72:25 — qul in adrī a-qarībun mā tūʿadūna am yajʿalu lahu rabbī amadā — the Prophet ﷺ: \"I don't know if it's NEAR or far.\" Maʿārij: Allah says it IS near.","The Prophet ﷺ doesn't know the date; Allah knows it's near.","The \"seeing\" thread across your surahs (yarā — Balad 90:7, ʿAlaq 96:14, Zalzalah 99:7, Takāthur 102:6) — here: two ways of seeing the SAME thing.","baʿīdan / qarīban = second objects.","Jinn 72:25: the Prophet ﷺ — \"I don't know if it's NEAR.\" Here: Allah — \"it IS near.\" Everything coming is close."]},
  {ar:"يَوْمَ تَكُونُ السَّمَاءُ كَالْمُهْلِ", tr:"Yawma takūnu's-samāʾu ka'l-muhl", v:"8", root:"Muhl — classically two meanings: (a) the DREGS of oil (the dark, turbid sludge at the bottom of an oil jar).", meaning:"\"The Day the sky will be like molten metal [or the dregs of oil].\"", magic:"The SKY — the bright, clear canopy — becomes MOLTEN BRASS, or the black sludge at the bottom of an oil jar. Both images: liquid, dark, burning, unstable.", grammar:"Yawma = adverb of time (linked to v.6–7 or v.1). ka- = simile.", why:"Muhl — liquefies the sky — the most stable-looking thing made molten.", note:"MUHL = MOLTEN METAL or OIL-DREGS — the SKY melts.", more:["(b) MOLTEN METAL (copper, brass, silver — melted and bubbling).","The letters m-h-l also give mahl = slowness (Muzzammil 73:11 mahhilhum — \"give them RESPITE\"; mahlan!","Compare Ḥāqqah 69:16 — fa-hiya yawmaʾidhin wāhiyah (the sky FRAIL) and Raḥmān 55:37 — fa-kānat wardatan ka'd-dihān (red like oil-dregs).","The sky-fates thread: split (infaṭarat, 82:1), cracked (inshaqqat, 84:1), stripped (kushiṭat, 81:11), frail (wāhiyah, 69:16), MOLTEN (ka'l-muhl, 70:8). And the letters m-h-l: Muzzammil 73:11 — wa MAHHILHUM qalīlā — \"give them RESPITE a little.\" The respite (mahl) ends; the sky becomes muhl.","(Same letters; classical lexicons list both under m-h-l.) And Kahf 18:29 — bi-māʾin ka'l-muhli yashwi'l-wujūh — Hell's water \"like muhl,\" that GRILLS faces — (and yashwī = grill → v.16's shawā!).","Sky-fates: split (82:1), cracked (84:1), stripped (81:11), frail (69:16), MOLTEN (70:8). Muzzammil 73:11: mahhilhum (RESPITE) — same letters: the respite ends, the sky melts.","Kahf 18:29: water like muhl that GRILLS faces."]},
  {ar:"وَتَكُونُ الْجِبَالُ كَالْعِهْنِ", tr:"Wa takūnu'l-jibālu ka'l-ʿihn", v:"9", root:"ʿihn — DYED, coloured WOOL.", meaning:"\"…and the mountains will be like [tufts of dyed] wool.\"", magic:"NEARLY IDENTICAL to Qāriʿah 101:5. Qāriʿah 101:5 — wa takūnu'l-jibālu ka'l-ʿihni'l-manfūsh (like CARDED wool). Maʿārij 70:9 — wa takūnu'l-jibālu ka'l-ʿihn (like wool).", grammar:"Parallel to v.8 (wa takūnu… ka-…).", why:"Echoes Qāriʿah so the reader hears the same Day in both surahs.", note:"= Qāriʿah 101:5 (minus manfūsh). ʿIhn = DYED wool — multicoloured strata blowing loose. v.8 SKY → molten; v.9 MOUNTAINS → wool.", more:["Same opening, same simile — Qāriʿah adds manfūsh (carded, fluffed).","And ʿihn = DYED wool — classical: mountains of different colours (white, red, black strata) → like tufts of multicoloured wool blowing loose.","And the PAIR in v.8–9: SKY like molten metal, MOUNTAINS like wool — the HEAVIEST things made liquid and light.","Mountain-fates — now EIGHT images: mirage (78:20), moving (81:3), pounded (89:21), carded wool (101:5), sliding sand (73:14), crushed once (69:14), dyed wool (70:9), vs.","pegs (78:7) and set up (88:19).","Mountain-fates: EIGHT images."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا يَسْأَلُ حَمِيمٌ حَمِيمًا", tr:"Wa lā yasʾalu ḥamīmun ḥamīmā", v:"10", root:"ḥamīm — a CLOSE, WARM FRIEND (one whose love is \"hot\") — and SCALDING WATER — root ḥ-m-m = HEAT (ḥammām = bathhouse; ḥummā = fever).", meaning:"\"And no close friend will ask about a close friend.\"", magic:"THE ḤAMĪM THREAD — now in THREE consecutive appearances. Nabaʾ 78:25 — illā ḥamīman wa ghassāqā — SCALDING WATER.", grammar:"ḥamīmun (subject) + ḥamīman (object) — the same word in two cases: one friend, then the other (jinās tāmm).", why:"ḥamīm — the warmest word for friendship, doubled, then frozen into silence.", note:"ḤAMĪM = WARM FRIEND / SCALDING WATER. 78:25 SCALDING water → 69:35 NO friend → 70:10 FRIEND doesn't ask FRIEND.", more:["Ḥāqqah 69:35 — fa-laysa lahu'l-yawma hāhunā ḥamīm — NO FRIEND (for the man who didn't feed the poor).","Maʿārij 70:10 — wa lā yasʾalu ḥamīmun ḥamīmā — no FRIEND asks about a FRIEND (for EVERYONE).","The word is DOUBLED here: ḥamīmun ḥamīmā — friend-friend: two best friends, side by side — and NEITHER asks \"how are you?\" Each is too consumed by his own fate.","Compare ʿAbasa 80:37 — li-kulli'mriʾin minhum yawmaʾidhin shaʾnun yughnīh — \"every person will have an affair occupying him.\" And the next verse (v.11): yubaṣṣarūnahum — they will SEE each other — they're not separated; they're right there, face to face — and still don't ask.","That's what makes it unbearable.","Ḥamīmun ḥamīmā — two best friends, side by side, SILENT.","ʿAbasa 80:37: each has his own concern.","v.11: they SEE each other — and still don't ask."]},
  {ar:"يُبَصَّرُونَهُمْ", tr:"Yubaṣṣarūnahum", v:"11", root:"The \"SEEING clearly / insight\" family — baṣura; Form II passive buṣṣira = to BE MADE TO SEE, to be shown (tabṣirah = enlightenment / a clear lesson; baṣīrah = insight).", meaning:"\"They will be shown them [made to see each other].\"", magic:"ONE WORD — and it makes v.10 devastating. Yubaṣṣarūnahum = \"they will be MADE TO SEE them\" — friends, family — right there, in plain view.", grammar:"Form II PASSIVE + -hum (them) — \"they are shown them.\" A rare, compact form — a whole clause in one word.", why:"Visibility makes the silence intentional — a choice forced by fear.", note:"YUBAṢṢARŪNAHUM = they are MADE TO SEE each other — face to face. The silence (v.10) isn't distance — it's TERROR.", more:["Not hidden, not lost in a crowd — VISIBLE.","So the silence of v.10 isn't because they can't find each other — it's because each is drowning in his own terror.","The baṣar root: Qiyāmah 75:14 (baṣīrah — witness against oneself), Ḥāqqah 69:38 (bimā tubṣirūn — by what you SEE).","Here: forced to see — and unable to help.","A whole clause in one word.","Tabṣirah = a clear LESSON."]},
  {ar:"يَوَدُّ الْمُجْرِمُ لَوْ يَفْتَدِي مِنْ عَذَابِ يَوْمِئِذٍ", tr:"Yawaddu'l-mujrimu law yaftadī min ʿadhābi yawmiʾidh", v:"11", root:"Yawaddu — the \"LOVE / longing / wishing\" family — wadda = to love, to wish dearly.", meaning:"\"The criminal will wish he could ransom himself from the punishment of that Day…\"", magic:"Yawaddu — he will LONG, dearly — the root of LOVE (mawaddah). The only thing he'll love that Day: escape.", grammar:"Yawaddu + law = \"wishes that / if only.\" yaftadī = Form VIII (ransom oneself).", why:"Fidāʾ — a transaction word: he wants to BUY his way out — with people.", note:"YAWADDU ← MAWADDAH (LOVE) — his only love that Day: escape. Al-Wadūd (85:14). FIDYAH = a RANSOM (and the fasting compensation!).", more:["Mawaddah = AFFECTION (mawaddah wa raḥmah — between spouses, 30:21!); al-Wadūd = the Loving (a Name of Allah — Burūj 85:14!); Nūḥ 71:23 — Wadd, an idol named \"Love\"!","mujrim — a CRIMINAL (jarīmah = a crime; Muddaththir 74:41). yaftadī — the \"RANSOM\" family — fidāʾ / fidyah = a RANSOM, compensation (fidyah for missed fasts!; fidāʾī = one who sacrifices himself; fadaytuka!","= \"may I be your ransom!\").","And al-Wadūd (the Loving — Burūj 85:14) — the One whose love he ignored; now his wadd is for a ransom.","Yaftadī — RANSOM — the Arab practice of paying to free a captive — and in fiqh, fidyah (feeding the poor for fasts you couldn't make up).","And recall Muddaththir 74:38 — kullu nafsin bimā kasabat rahīnah — every soul is HELD as COLLATERAL.","The collateral (74:38) tries to pay a RANSOM (70:11) — and the ransom he offers is his own FAMILY (v.11–14).","Law = \"IF ONLY\" (a wish, contrary to fact).","yawmiʾidhin — two recitations: yawmiʾidhin (genitive) and yawmaʾidhin (built on fatḥah) — both authentic.","= \"may I be your ransom!\" Muddaththir 74:38: HELD as COLLATERAL → tries to pay a RANSOM."]},
  {ar:"بِبَنِيهِ · وَصَاحِبَتِهِ وَأَخِيهِ · وَفَصِيلَتِهِ الَّتِي تُؤْوِيهِ · وَمَن فِي الْأَرْضِ جَمِيعًا ثُمَّ يُنجِيهِ", tr:"Bi-banīh · wa ṣāḥibatihi wa akhīh · wa faṣīlatihi'llatī tuʾwīh · wa man fi'l-arḍi jamīʿan thumma yunjīh", v:"11–14", root:"Banīn — SONS. ṣāḥibah — WIFE / companion. akh — BROTHER.", meaning:"\"…[ransom himself] with his sons, his wife, his brother, his clan that sheltered him, and everyone on earth — if only it would then save him.\"", magic:"THE RANSOM LIST — and it's the EXACT REVERSE of ʿAbasa.", grammar:"All joined to bi- (instrument of ransom) → genitive. jamīʿan = ḥāl (\"all together\").", why:"The list's order — dearest first — is the argument.", note:"ʿABASA 80:34–36: he FLEES — brother → mother → father → wife → SONS (dearest LAST).", more:["faṣīlah — the \"SEPARATING / dividing into sections\" family — faṣala = to separate; faṣīlah = the CLAN, the close kin-group (a \"section\" of the tribe); yawm al-faṣl = the Day of SORTING (Nabaʾ 78:17!); faṣl = a CHAPTER / a SEASON / a CLASSROOM!; faṣīlah dammawiyyah = a BLOOD GROUP / blood type (clinical!).","tuʾwīh — the \"SHELTERING / giving refuge\" family — awā = to take shelter; maʾwā = a REFUGE (Nāziʿāt 79:39, 41!); īwāʾ = SHELTER / housing (markaz īwāʾ = a shelter!).","yunjīh — the \"RESCUE\" family — najā = to be saved; najāt = SALVATION; munjī = a rescuer.","ʿAbasa 80:34–36 — yawma yafirru'l-marʾu min akhīh · wa ummihi wa abīh · wa ṣāḥibatihi wa banīh — he FLEES: brother → mother → father → wife → SONS (ending with the dearest).","Maʿārij 70:11–14 — yaftadī bi-banīh · wa ṣāḥibatihi wa akhīh · wa faṣīlatih… wa man fi'l-arḍi jamīʿā — he RANSOMS: SONS → wife → brother → clan → everyone (starting with the dearest).","ʿAbasa builds UP to the dearest he flees from; Maʿārij STARTS with the dearest he'd sacrifice.","In dunya, he'd have died for his sons; that Day, he'd hand them over FIRST.","The order reveals how total the terror is.","Faṣīlatihi'llatī TUʾWĪH — \"his clan that SHELTERED him\" — the people who protected him all his life — he'd throw them in too.","Tuʾwīh — the maʾwā root: Nāziʿāt 79:39 — fa-inna'l-jaḥīma hiya'l-maʾwā (Hell is the REFUGE).","The clan was his maʾwā in dunya; now Hell is.","Wa man fi'l-arḍi JAMĪʿĀ — and EVERYONE ON EARTH — the whole of humanity offered as ransom for one man.","Thumma yunjīh — \"THEN it would save him\" — the thumma = even after all that, if only… The answer (v.15): KALLĀ!","Hooks: faṣl = a CHAPTER / SEASON; faṣīlah dammawiyyah = BLOOD TYPE; īwāʾ = SHELTER.","yunjīhi — the verb's subject is \"the ransom\" (implied).","MAʿĀRIJ 70:11–14: he RANSOMS — SONS → wife → brother → clan → EVERYONE (dearest FIRST).","In dunya he'd die for his sons; that Day he'd hand them over FIRST.","Tuʾwīh ← MAʾWĀ (refuge — Nāziʿāt 79:39). Faṣīlah ← faṣl (CHAPTER/SEASON); faṣīlah dammawiyyah = BLOOD TYPE."]},
  {ar:"كَلَّا ۖ إِنَّهَا لَظَىٰ", tr:"Kallā innahā laẓā", v:"15", root:"The \"LEAPING FLAME / blazing\" family — laẓiya = for fire to blaze, to flare with leaping tongues.", meaning:"\"No! Indeed, it is Laẓā [the flaming Fire]…\"", magic:"— the answer to v.11–14's ransom fantasy: NO. No sons, no wife, no clan, no humanity — will be accepted.", grammar:"Kallā = stern rejection. laẓā = DIPTOTE (feminine proper name ending in alif maqṣūrah).", why:"Laẓā — the name of leaping flame, refusing the ransom with heat.", note:"KALLĀ! — NO to the ransom. Layl 92:14: nāran TALAẒẒĀ (the VERB). Maʿārij 70:15: LAẒĀ (the NAME).", more:["Laẓā = a NAME of Hell — \"the Flame\"; talaẓẓā = to flare up (Layl 92:14!); talaẓẓin = (modern) a flaring / burning thirst / ardent longing.","And the NAME: Laẓā — Layl 92:14 — fa-andhartukum nāran talaẓẓā — \"I warn you of a Fire that FLARES.\" Layl used the VERB; Maʿārij uses the NAME.","Hell-names you own: Jahannam, Jaḥīm (blaze), Saʿīr (flare), Hāwiyah (abyss), Ḥuṭamah (crusher), Saqar (scorcher), Laẓā (leaping flame).","Innahā — \"indeed IT\" — the feminine pronoun pointing at the Fire before it's named — suspense: \"it is… LAẒĀ.\"","Note: in Layl 92:14 talaẓẓā drops a tāʾ (tatalaẓẓā).","Hell-names: Jaḥīm, Saʿīr, Hāwiyah, Ḥuṭamah, Saqar, LAẒĀ."]},
  {ar:"نَزَّاعَةً لِّلشَّوَىٰ", tr:"Nazzāʿatan li'sh-shawā", v:"16", root:"Nazzāʿah — the \"PLUCKING OUT / tearing away\" family — nazaʿa = to pull out, to extract forcefully.", meaning:"\"…stripping away the scalp [and limbs].\"", magic:"TWO ROOTS — and one is the opening of Sūrat an-Nāziʿāt.", grammar:"Nazzāʿatan = accusative ḥāl (describing Laẓā) — there's also a recitation nazzāʿatun (nominative, predicate) — both authentic.", why:"Nazzāʿah + shawā — repeated stripping of the body's exposed surface, in the language of the butcher's grill.", note:"NAZZĀʿAH — PULLS OFF again and again (faʿʿālah).", more:["An-nāziʿāt = those who pull out (souls) — Nāziʿāt 79:1!; nizāʿ = a DISPUTE / conflict (pulling against each other!); naziʿa = to remove (nazaʿa thiyābahu = took off his clothes).","shawā — the \"GRILLING / roasting\" family — shawā = the SCALP / the extremities (hands, feet, head-skin — what's exposed to fire); shiwāʾ = GRILLING, BARBECUE (mashwī = GRILLED meat — everyday Arabic!; shawarma — from Turkish çevirme, not this root — but mashwī is!).","Nazzāʿah — the faʿʿālah INTENSIVE: \"one that PULLS OUT again and again\" — it strips the skin, then it grows back, then it strips again (compare Muddaththir 74:28 — lā tubqī wa lā tadhar; and Nisāʾ 4:56 — kullamā naḍijat julūduhum baddalnāhum julūdan ghayrahā — \"every time their skins are roasted through, We replace them\").","Nāziʿāt 79:1 — wa'n-nāziʿāti gharqā — the angels who PULL OUT the souls of the wicked \"drowningly\" (violently).","Maʿārij 70:16 — nazzāʿatan li'sh-shawā — the Fire that PULLS OFF the skin.","The pulling starts at death (Nāziʿāt) and continues in the Fire (Maʿārij).","Shawā = scalp / extremities — the root of SHIWĀʾ = BARBECUE / GRILLING — mashwī = grilled meat.","The word for the body part is the word for grilling.","Kahf 18:29 — bi-māʾin ka'l-muhli yashwi'l-wujūh (water like molten metal that GRILLS faces) — same root.","Faʿʿāl(ah) intensive thread: lawwāmah (75:2), lawwāḥah (74:29), tawwāb (110:3), ḥammālah (111:4), khannās (114:4), nazzāʿah (70:16).","Nāziʿāt 79:1: angels PULL OUT souls (nāziʿāt) → Maʿārij 70:16: the Fire PULLS OFF skin (nazzāʿah).","SHAWĀ (scalp) ← SHIWĀʾ = BARBECUE; MASHWĪ = GRILLED meat.","Nisāʾ 4:56: skins replaced."]},
  {ar:"تَدْعُو مَنْ أَدْبَرَ وَتَوَلَّىٰ", tr:"Tadʿū man adbara wa tawallā", v:"17", root:"Tadʿū — it CALLS / summons (daʿā; duʿāʾ; daʿwah = an invitation!). adbara — turned his BACK (dubur).", meaning:"\"It calls whoever turned his back and turned away…\"", magic:"Tadʿū — the Fire itself issues a daʿwah (an invitation / summons) — classical: literally calling them — \"come to me, O so-and-so!\" — or figuratively, drawing them like a magnet.", grammar:"Tadʿū = feminine present (subject: Laẓā).", why:"Tadʿū — the Fire's \"invitation\" answers their rejection of the Prophet's ﷺ invitation.", note:"The FIRE issues a DAʿWAH — calling them by name. They rejected the Prophet's ﷺ daʿwah (Jinn 72:19) → the Fire's daʿwah comes.", more:["tawallā — turned AWAY (walla; ʿAbasa 80:1 ʿabasa wa tawallā; Layl 92:16 kadhdhaba wa tawallā).","THE FIRE CALLS PEOPLE BY NAME.","The bitter irony: they REJECTED the daʿwah of the Messenger (yadʿū — Jinn 72:19 — qāma ʿabdu'llāhi yadʿūhu — the servant of Allah stood calling) — so the Fire issues its own daʿwah.","Refuse the call to Paradise, receive the call to Laẓā.","Adbara wa tawallā — the two \"turning\" verbs: adbara — Muddaththir 74:23 — thumma adbara wa'stakbar (al-Walīd turned his back); Nāziʿāt 79:22 — thumma adbara yasʿā (Pharaoh).","tawallā — Layl 92:16 — alladhī kadhdhaba wa tawallā (the one who denied and turned away — who burns in nāran talaẓẓā (92:14–15)!). Layl 92:14–16: nāran TALAẒẒĀ… alladhī kadhdhaba wa TAWALLĀ.","Maʿārij 70:15–17: LAẒĀ… tadʿū man adbara wa TAWALLĀ.","The SAME Fire, the SAME turning-away, in two surahs.","man = \"whoever.\" adbara / tawallā — two near-synonyms (back turned + face turned away).","Layl 92:14–16: talaẓẓā… kadhdhaba wa TAWALLĀ.","Maʿārij 70:15–17: Laẓā… adbara wa TAWALLĀ.","Adbara: Pharaoh (79:22), al-Walīd (74:23)."]},
  {ar:"وَجَمَعَ فَأَوْعَىٰ", tr:"Wa jamaʿa fa-awʿā", v:"18", root:"Jamaʿa — GATHERED (jamʿ; Humazah 104:2 jamaʿa mālan).", meaning:"\"…and gathered [wealth] and hoarded it [in containers].\"", magic:"THE BEST CROSS-SURAH ROOT IN THIS SURAH — back to back with Ḥāqqah.", grammar:"Fa = immediately (\"gathered, then stored away\"). awʿā = Form IV, weak-final (doubly weak root w-ʿ-y).", why:"Awʿā — the vessel-word, making him the dark mirror of Ḥāqqah's \"retaining ear.\"", note:"Ḥāqqah 69:12: udhunun WĀʿIYAH — an ear-VESSEL holding the REMINDER. Maʿārij 70:18: fa-AWʿĀ — a VESSEL holding MONEY.", more:["awʿā — the \"CONTAINING / retaining in a VESSEL\" family — waʿā = to hold, retain; wiʿāʾ = a CONTAINER, a VESSEL; awʿā (Form IV) = to put away in a container, to HOARD; waʿy = CONSCIOUSNESS (Ḥāqqah 69:12!).","Ḥāqqah 69:12 — wa taʿiyahā udhunun wāʿiyah — \"that a RETAINING ear might RETAIN it\" — an ear like a VESSEL, holding the REMINDER.","Maʿārij 70:18 — wa jamaʿa fa-awʿā — \"he gathered and put it in a VESSEL\" — a man hoarding MONEY.","SAME ROOT (w-ʿ-y), CONSECUTIVE SURAHS: one man's VESSEL holds the REMINDER; another's holds GOLD.","What does your wiʿāʾ hold?","And waʿy = CONSCIOUSNESS — the hoarder's \"consciousness\" is full of money.","Jamaʿa — Humazah 104:2 — alladhī jamaʿa mālan wa ʿaddadah — gathered and COUNTED.","Maʿārij: gathered and LOCKED AWAY.","Fajr 89:20 — tuḥibbūna'l-māla ḥubban jammā (loving it).","Gather, count, lock up, love — the hoarder's whole life.","And the two charges in v.17–18 are the TWO HALVES of every sinner in the juzʾ: v.17: turned away from ALLAH (adbara wa tawallā).","v.18: hoarded from PEOPLE (jamaʿa fa-awʿā).","Like Ḥāqqah 69:33–34 (didn't believe + didn't urge feeding).","Object deleted — gathered what?","Same root, consecutive surahs: what does YOUR container hold?","Humazah 104:2: gathered and COUNTED; here: gathered and LOCKED AWAY.","v.17 turned from ALLAH + v.18 hoarded from PEOPLE = the two halves.","Wiʿāʾ = a CONTAINER; waʿy = CONSCIOUSNESS."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّ الْإِنسَانَ خُلِقَ هَلُوعًا", tr:"Inna'l-insāna khuliqa halūʿā", v:"19", root:"The \"ANXIOUS PANIC / restless greed\" family — haliʿa = to be seized by anxious alarm, to be restlessly impatient and greedy; halaʿ = PANIC, terror, extreme anxiety (modern: halaʿ = PANIC — nawbat halaʿ = a PANIC ATTACK!); halūʿ = intensely anxious and greedy by nature (faʿūl intensive).", meaning:"\"Indeed, man was created anxious and impatient.\"", magic:"THE DIAGNOSIS — and the Qurʾān DEFINES its own word.", grammar:"Khuliqa = passive (created — by Allah). halūʿan = ḥāl (in a state of…).", why:"Halūʿ — a rare, compressed word, unpacked by the next two verses.", note:"HALŪʿ = DEFINED BY THE QURʾĀN ITSELF: jazūʿ (v.20) + manūʿ (v.21) — Ibn ʿAbbās. NAWBAT HALAʿ = a PANIC ATTACK (modern Arabic).", more:["Halūʿ was a rare word even for the Arabs — classical report: Ibn ʿAbbās was asked what halūʿ means, and he answered: \"Allah has explained it: idhā massahu'sh-sharru jazūʿā wa idhā massahu'l-khayru manūʿā.\" The Qurʾān is its own dictionary (tafsīr al-Qurʾān bi'l-Qurʾān): halūʿ = jazūʿ (v.20) + manūʿ (v.21).","Halaʿ = PANIC in modern Arabic — nawbat halaʿ = a PANIC ATTACK.","The Qurʾān names a human tendency that clinical language still uses.","And \"khuliqa\" (was CREATED so) — it's a starting condition, not a fixed fate — v.22 shows the way out.","The faʿūl intensive — halūʿ, jazūʿ, manūʿ — three on the same pattern = a disposition, a temperament — (like kafūr, Insān 76:3; kanūd, ʿĀdiyāt 100:6). And compare: ʿĀdiyāt 100:6 — inna'l-insāna li-rabbihi la-kanūd (man is ungrateful) ʿAlaq 96:6 — inna'l-insāna la-yaṭghā (man transgresses) ʿAṣr 103:2 — inna'l-insāna la-fī khusr (man is in loss) Maʿārij 70:19 — inna'l-insāna khuliqa halūʿā (man is anxious).","FOUR \"inna'l-insān\" diagnoses: ungrateful, transgressing, losing, anxious — and every one is followed by an EXCEPTION or a cure.","al-insān = the genus (all humans), hence the exception in v.22.","Inna'l-insāna… FOUR diagnoses: kanūd (100:6), yaṭghā (96:6), fī khusr (103:2), halūʿ (70:19) — each with a cure.","Khuliqa = a STARTING condition, not a fate."]},
  {ar:"إِذَا مَسَّهُ الشَّرُّ جَزُوعًا", tr:"Idhā massahu'sh-sharru jazūʿā", v:"20", root:"Massa — to TOUCH (mass = a touch; mass kahrabāʾī = an electric shock!; mamsūs = possessed / touched by jinn).", meaning:"\"When harm touches him — fretful [in despair].\"", magic:"Half of halūʿ: PANIC in hardship. Jazaʿ is the exact OPPOSITE of ṣabr — v.5 commanded ṣabran jamīlā (beautiful patience); v.20 describes jazaʿ (fretful despair).", grammar:"Idhā = \"whenever\" (habitual condition). jazūʿan = second ḥāl (continuing halūʿā). faʿūl intensive.", why:"Jazūʿ — names the exact failure of patience, set against v.5's command.", note:"JAZAʿ = the OPPOSITE of ṢABR. v.5 ṣabran jamīlā (beautiful PATIENCE) vs v.20 jazūʿā (fretful DESPAIR).", more:["jazūʿ — the \"IMPATIENT DISTRESS / fretting\" family — jaziʿa = to lose patience, to be overcome with distress, to wail; jazaʿ = the OPPOSITE of ṣabr — anxious, complaining distress.","The surah sets them face to face: ṢABR (v.5) vs JAZAʿ (v.20).","Same hardship — one meets it with beautiful patience, the other with wailing.","Massahu — \"TOUCHES him\" — just a TOUCH of harm — not a flood, a touch — and he collapses.","The smallness of the trigger shows the fragility.","Compare Fajr 89:16 — wa ammā idhā mā'btalāhu fa-qadara ʿalayhi rizqahu fa-yaqūlu rabbī ahānan — \"when He tests him by restricting his provision, he says: 'My Lord has HUMILIATED me!'\" That's jazaʿ in speech.","Just a TOUCH (massa) of harm — and he collapses.","Fajr 89:16: \"my Lord HUMILIATED me!\" = jazaʿ in words.","Mass kahrabāʾī = an ELECTRIC SHOCK."]},
  {ar:"وَإِذَا مَسَّهُ الْخَيْرُ مَنُوعًا", tr:"Wa idhā massahu'l-khayru manūʿā", v:"21", root:"Khayr — GOOD / wealth (as in ʿĀdiyāt 100:8, li-ḥubbi'l-khayr = love of WEALTH).", meaning:"\"…and when good touches him — withholding.\"", magic:"The other half of halūʿ: STINGINESS in ease. Hardship → he panics (v.20); prosperity → he grips it tight (v.21).", grammar:"Parallel to v.20 (idhā massahu… X). manūʿan = third ḥāl, faʿūl.", why:"Manūʿ — names hoarding as a temperament, setting up the surah's cure.", note:"HARDSHIP → JAZŪʿ (panic). EASE → MANŪʿ (grip). Both = FEAR = HALAʿ. Māʿūn 107:7 yamnaʿūna'l-māʿūn — withhold even a POT.", more:["manūʿ — the \"WITHHOLDING / preventing\" family — manaʿa = to withhold; mamnūʿ = FORBIDDEN; mamnūʿ min aṣ-ṣarf = a DIPTOTE (grammar!); manūʿ = habitually WITHHOLDING, stingy (faʿūl intensive).","Both from the same root: FEAR — fear of losing makes him wail when he loses, and clutch when he has.","That's halaʿ — PANIC — in both directions.","And the manaʿa root — MĀʿŪN 107:7 — wa yamnaʿūna'l-māʿūn — they WITHHOLD even the small things (a pot, a bucket).","Maʿārij 70:21 — manūʿā — WITHHOLDING by nature.","And the cure in v.22–25: illa'l-MUṢALLĪN … fī amwālihim ḥaqqun maʿlūm — those who PRAY and GIVE a known right.","Māʿūn's sinners: heedless in prayer + withhold.","Maʿārij's exception: constant in prayer + give.","The two surahs are mirror images.","Massahu'l-KHAYR — \"when GOOD touches him\" — again just a TOUCH — and he grips it.","Grammar hook: mamnūʿ min aṣ-ṣarf = DIPTOTE — \"WITHHELD from full declension\" — same root as manūʿ.","Māʿūn: heedless prayer + withholding.","Maʿārij: CONSTANT prayer + GIVING.","Mamnūʿ min aṣ-ṣarf = DIPTOTE — same root."]},
  {ar:"إِلَّا الْمُصَلِّينَ", tr:"Illa'l-muṣallīn", v:"22", root:"Muṣallīn — those who PRAY (Form II participle of ṣallā).", meaning:"\"Except those who pray…\"", magic:"THE EXCEPTION — and the mirror of Māʿūn.", grammar:"Illā = exception from al-insān (v.19). al-muṣallīna = accusative (excepted).", why:"Muṣallīn — names prayer as the antidote to the anxious nature just described.", note:"Māʿūn 107:4: fa-WAYLUN li'l-muṣallīn (WOE to those who pray). Maʿārij 70:22: ILLA'l-muṣallīn (EXCEPT those who pray).", more:["Māʿūn 107:4 — fa-waylun li'l-MUṢALLĪN — \"WOE to those who pray —\" Maʿārij 70:22 — illa'l-MUṢALLĪN — \"EXCEPT those who pray.\" SAME WORD — one cursed, one exempted.","The next verse explains the difference.","And why PRAYER is the cure for halaʿ (panic): five times a day, you stand before the One who controls harm and good — so harm can't crush you (He's in control) and good can't own you (it's His).","Prayer resets the anxious heart.","The illā exception structure: ʿAṣr 103:3 (illa'lladhīna āmanū — except believers, from loss), Tīn 95:6 (except believers, from the lowest), Muddaththir 74:39 (illā aṣḥāba'l-yamīn — except the right-handed, from pledge), Maʿārij 70:22 (illa'l-muṣallīn — except those who pray, from halaʿ).","Same word — cursed vs exempted.","Prayer = the antidote to halaʿ: harm can't crush you, good can't own you.","Illā exceptions: 103:3, 95:6, 74:39, 70:22."]},
  {ar:"الَّذِينَ هُمْ عَلَىٰ صَلَاتِهِمْ دَائِمُونَ", tr:"Alladhīna hum ʿalā ṣalātihim dāʾimūn", v:"23", root:"Dāʾimūn — the \"LASTING / continuing / constancy\" family — dāma = to last, to continue; dāʾiman = ALWAYS (everyday!).", meaning:"\"…those who are constant in their prayer.\"", magic:"Māʿūn 107:5 — alladhīna hum ʿan ṣalātihim sāhūn — HEEDLESS of their prayer. Maʿārij 70:23 — alladhīna hum ʿalā ṣalātihim dāʾimūn — CONSTANT in their prayer.", grammar:"Hum (fronted subject) + ʿalā ṣalātihim (fronted) + dāʾimūna — fronting = emphasis on the prayer.", why:"Dāʾimūn — constancy, the precise antidote to the drifting heedlessness of Māʿūn.", note:"Māʿūn 107:5: ʿAN ṣalātihim SĀHŪN (drifting AWAY, heedless). Maʿārij 70:23: ʿALĀ ṣalātihim DĀʾIMŪN (planted UPON it, constant).", more:["Dawām = WORKING HOURS / a shift (dawām kāmil = full-time!); mā dāma = as long as; ad-Dāʾim = the Everlasting.","THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THE CURSED AND THE SAVED — in one word.","Look at the prepositions: ʿAN (AWAY from) vs ʿALĀ (UPON, holding firm to).","One drifts away from prayer; one is planted on it.","And sāhūn (heedless) vs dāʾimūn (constant).","Dāʾimūn = CONSTANT, unbroken — the ḥadīth: \"The most beloved deeds to Allah are the most CONSTANT (adwamuhā), even if small.\" Same root.","Not dramatic bursts — steady presence.","Dawām = WORKING HOURS — prayer as a shift you never skip.","Classical readings: (a) never missing it; (b) remaining focused within it (not turning the face/heart away).","ʿalā = being firmly upon something.","Two prepositions, two destinies.","Ḥadīth: \"most beloved deeds = the most CONSTANT, even if small.\" Dāʾiman = ALWAYS; dawām = WORKING HOURS."]},
  {ar:"وَالَّذِينَ فِي أَمْوَالِهِمْ حَقٌّ مَّعْلُومٌ", tr:"Wa'lladhīna fī amwālihim ḥaqqun maʿlūm", v:"24", root:"Amwāl — WEALTH (māl).", meaning:"\"And those in whose wealth there is a known right…\"", magic:"NOT \"they give charity\" — \"in their wealth is a RIGHT.\" The poor person's share is ALREADY THERE, inside your money — it belongs to them before you hand it over.", grammar:"Fī amwālihim (fronted predicate) + ḥaqqun maʿlūmun (subject, indefinite).", why:"ḥaqq (a right owed) — reframes charity as obligation, not favour.", note:"NOT \"they give charity\" — \"in their wealth is a ḤAQQ (a RIGHT).\" The poor's share is ALREADY inside your money.", more:["ḥaqq — a RIGHT, what is DUE (ḥuqūq = rights; Ḥāqqah 69:1!). maʿlūm — KNOWN, specified (ʿilm; maʿlūmāt = INFORMATION / data!).","That's ḥaqq — what is OWED.","Giving isn't generosity; it's paying a debt.","And recall: ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn — \"the food OF the poor\" (Māʿūn 107:3, Ḥāqqah 69:34, Fajr 89:18) — the poor's food, held in your hands.","Maʿlūm — KNOWN, specified — classical: zakāh — a fixed, KNOWN percentage (2.5% etc.) — or a portion they've personally set and stick to.","Not random, not when the mood strikes — KNOWN.","The opposite of v.21's manūʿ: instead of gripping, a fixed portion already marked for others.","And ḥaqq — the root of AL-ḤĀQQAH (69:1). Ḥāqqah: the Day that is TRUE and DUE.","Maʿārij: the poor's share that is DUE.","Pay the ḥaqq now, or face al-Ḥāqqah later.","Fronting the wealth = \"IN THEIR WEALTH [specifically] is a right.\"","MAʿLŪM = KNOWN, fixed — zakāh's set portion; opposite of manūʿ.","Ḥaqq ← AL-ḤĀQQAH (69:1): pay the ḥaqq now or face al-Ḥāqqah."]},
  {ar:"لِّلسَّائِلِ وَالْمَحْرُومِ", tr:"Li's-sāʾili wa'l-maḥrūm", v:"25", root:"Sāʾil — one who ASKS / a beggar (Ḍuḥā 93:10 as-sāʾil; v.1 sāʾil!).", meaning:"\"…for the one who asks, and the one who is deprived [but doesn't ask].\"", magic:"TWO kinds of poor — and the second is the hidden one. As-SĀʾIL — the one who ASKS openly.", grammar:"Li- = \"for\" (the right is owed to them).", why:"Maḥrūm — directs attention to the poor who stay invisible.", note:"SĀʾIL = the poor who ASKS; MAḤRŪM = the poor who DOESN'T ask — you must SEEK HIM OUT.", more:["maḥrūm — the \"FORBIDDEN / SACRED / DEPRIVED\" family — ḥarama = to deprive, to forbid; ḥarām = FORBIDDEN; al-Ḥaram = the SANCTUARY (Makkah!); ḥirmān = DEPRIVATION; maḥrūm = the DEPRIVED one; muḥarram = the sacred month; ḥarīm = women's quarters (protected!).","Al-MAḤRŪM — classical: the poor person who does NOT ask — too dignified, too ashamed, or unknown — deprived, but silent.","You must SEEK OUT the maḥrūm; the sāʾil comes to you.","Compare Baqarah 2:273 — yaḥsabuhumu'l-jāhilu aghniyāʾa mina't-taʿaffuf — \"the ignorant think them rich because of their restraint.\" Sāʾil in THIS surah: v.1 — a man ASKS for PUNISHMENT (mockingly).","v.25 — a poor man ASKS for FOOD (rightfully).","Ḍuḥā 93:10 — wa ammā as-sāʾila fa-lā tanhar — \"as for the one who ASKS — do not repel him.\" Ḍuḥā: don't repel the asker.","Maʿārij: he has a RIGHT in your wealth.","Maḥrūm — the root of ḤARĀM and AL-ḤARAM — the same three letters for \"forbidden,\" \"sacred,\" and \"deprived.\" The core idea: something set APART, off-limits.","The maḥrūm is someone set apart from provision.","two participles: active (sāʾil) and passive (maḥrūm) — one acts (asks), one is acted upon (deprived).","Baqarah 2:273: \"the ignorant think them rich.\" Sāʾil v.1: asks for PUNISHMENT; v.25: asks for FOOD.","Ḍuḥā 93:10: don't repel the asker.","Maḥrūm ← ḤARĀM, AL-ḤARAM, ḥirmān (deprivation)."]},
  {ar:"وَالَّذِينَ يُصَدِّقُونَ بِيَوْمِ الدِّينِ", tr:"Wa'lladhīna yuṣaddiqūna bi-yawmi'd-dīn", v:"26", root:"Yuṣaddiqūn — the \"TRUTH / affirming as true\" family — ṣadaqa = to be truthful.", meaning:"\"And those who affirm the Day of Reckoning…\"", magic:"THE EXACT OPPOSITE of the root sin you've tracked through FOUR surahs. Infiṭār 82:9 — bal tukadhdhibūna bi'd-dīn. Tīn 95:7 — fa-mā yukadhdhibuka baʿdu bi'd-dīn.", grammar:"ṣaddaqa bi- (fixed preposition). Present (yuṣaddiqūna) = ongoing, habitual.", why:"Taṣdīq — the precise antonym of takdhīb, closing the denial thread with its cure.", note:"YUKADHDHIBU bi'd-dīn (DENY — 82:9, 95:7, 107:1, 74:46) vs YUṢADDIQŪNA bi-yawmi'd-dīn (AFFIRM — 70:26). Two Form II verbs = damned vs saved.", more:["ṣaddaqa (Form II) = to CONFIRM as true, to believe; ṣadaqah = CHARITY (proof of TRUE faith!); ṣadīq = a FRIEND (one TRUE to you!); aṣ-Ṣiddīq = Abū Bakr's title (the constant affirmer!); Layl 92:6 — wa ṣaddaqa bi'l-ḥusnā.","yawm ad-dīn — the Day of RECKONING (dayn = debt).","Māʿūn 107:1 — alladhī yukadhdhibu bi'd-dīn.","Muddaththir 74:46 — wa kunnā nukadhdhibu bi-yawmi'd-dīn.","And now: Maʿārij 70:26 — yuṢADDIQŪNA bi-yawmi'd-dīn.","Kadhdhaba (call it a LIE) vs ṣaddaqa (affirm it TRUE) — two Form II verbs, the whole difference between the damned and the saved.","And the ORDER makes sense: they give a ḥaqq maʿlūm (v.24) BECAUSE they affirm the Day of Reckoning (v.26).","Māʿūn reasoned the same way in reverse: denies the Day → shoves the orphan.","Belief in the Reckoning produces generosity.","Ṣaddaqa — Layl 92:6 — wa ṣaddaqa bi'l-ḥusnā (affirmed the best — and was \"eased toward ease\").","Ṣadaqah (charity) = proof of taṣdīq (affirmation) — the ḥadīth: \"aṣ-ṣadaqatu burhān\" — charity is a PROOF.","Your charity proves your belief.","Ṣadaqah = CHARITY — \"charity is a PROOF\" (of taṣdīq).","Aṣ-Ṣiddīq = Abū Bakr; ṣadīq = a FRIEND.","Layl 92:6 ṣaddaqa bi'l-ḥusnā."]},
  {ar:"وَالَّذِينَ هُم مِّنْ عَذَابِ رَبِّهِم مُّشْفِقُونَ", tr:"Wa'lladhīna hum min ʿadhābi rabbihim mushfiqūn", v:"27", root:"The \"AFTERGLOW / tender anxious care\" family — shafaq = the TWILIGHT AFTERGLOW (the red glow after sunset.", meaning:"\"And those who are fearful [with tender concern] of their Lord's punishment…\"", magic:"THE MOST BEAUTIFUL ROOT IN THE LIST — from the evening sky.", grammar:"Hum + min ʿadhābi rabbihim (fronted) + mushfiqūna — Form IV participle; ashfaqa min = \"fear of.\"", why:"Ishfāq — a fear that is gentle, loving, and alert — the opposite of panic.", note:"SHAFAQ = the TWILIGHT AFTERGLOW (Inshiqāq 84:16) → ISHFĀQ = soft, tender FEAR. A mother watching a sick child.", more:["Inshiqāq 84:16!); shafaqah = PITY, compassion; ashfaqa = to be anxiously concerned, to fear for someone/something WITH TENDERNESS; mushfiq = one who fears with care (mushfiq also = kind, compassionate).","Shafaq = the RED AFTERGLOW after sunset — Inshiqāq 84:16 — fa-lā uqsimu bi'sh-shafaq — \"I swear by the TWILIGHT GLOW.\" Why would \"fear\" share a root with \"twilight\"?","Classical: shafaq is a soft, thin, fading light — ishfāq is a soft, tender, careful FEAR — not panic (halaʿ), not terror — the anxious tenderness of a mother watching a sick child (shafaqah = compassion).","It's fear mixed with LOVE.","The mushfiq fears losing something precious — his Lord's pleasure.","And notice: the CURE for halaʿ (panic) includes a kind of FEAR!","v.19: halūʿ — panicked about the world (losses, gains).","v.27: mushfiq — tenderly anxious about his Lord.","The fear doesn't disappear — it's redirected: from the world to Allah, from panic to tender care.","The fear-words you own: khawf (plain fear — Muddaththir 74:53), khashyah (awe from knowing — Nāziʿāt 79:19, Aʿlā 87:10, Bayyinah 98:8), ishfāq (tender, anxious care — 70:27), and its opposite halaʿ (panic — 70:19).","v.19 HALAʿ (PANIC about the world) → v.27 ISHFĀQ (TENDER FEAR of his Lord).","Fear redirected, not removed.","Fear-words: khawf, khashyah, ishfāq."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّ عَذَابَ رَبِّهِمْ غَيْرُ مَأْمُونٍ", tr:"Inna ʿadhāba rabbihim ghayru maʾmūn", v:"28", root:"Maʾmūn — the \"SAFETY / trust\" family — amina = to be safe; maʾmūn = SAFE FROM / trusted (maʾmūn al-jānib = reliable!).", meaning:"\"Indeed, the punishment of their Lord is not to be felt safe from.\"", magic:"WHY they're mushfiqūn (v.27): no one is SAFE from it — not even the righteous. The pious don't assume they're immune — that's what keeps them pious.", grammar:"Inna + subject + ghayru maʾmūnin (predicate). ghayr + genitive = negating an adjective.", why:"Maʾmūn — explains why fear stays alive in the righteous.", note:"GHAYRU MAʾMŪN = NOT to be felt SAFE from — not even by the righteous. Fear + hope = two wings (khawf + rajāʾ).", more:["Amn = security (Quraysh 106:4 āmanahum); īmān; al-Maʾmūn — the Abbasid caliph's name (\"the trusted one\")!","Classical: the believer lives between khawf and rajāʾ — fear and hope — like a bird with two wings.","Feeling SAFE from Allah's plan is a sign of danger (a-fa-aminū makra'llāh, 7:99). Maʾmūn — the amn root: Quraysh 106:4 — wa āmanahum min khawf — He made them SAFE from fear (in dunya).","Tīn 95:3 — al-baladi'l-amīn (the SECURE city).","Maʿārij 70:28 — but His punishment is NOT something to feel safe from.","Allah gives amn in this world — but no one should feel amn from His justice.","Ghayru maʾmūn — the ghayr + adjective negation (Tīn 95:6 ghayru mamnūn; Muddaththir 74:10 ghayru yasīr).","And v.30 — ghayru malūmīn.","Three ghayrs in this surah's list.","Quraysh 106:4: He gives amn in dunya — but no amn from His justice.","Ghayr + adjective: 95:6, 74:10, 70:28, 70:30. Al-Maʾmūn = the caliph \"the trusted.\""]},
  {ar:"وَالَّذِينَ هُمْ لِفُرُوجِهِمْ حَافِظُونَ", tr:"Wa'lladhīna hum li-furūjihim ḥāfiẓūn", v:"29", root:"Furūj — the \"OPENING / gap / relief\" family — farj = an OPENING, a gap → the private parts (a modest Qurʾānic term).", meaning:"\"And those who guard their chastity…\"", magic:"The word for CHASTITY is the word for MEMORIZING the Qurʾān. Ḥāfiẓ = one who GUARDS — his private life (here) — or the Qurʾān in his heart (a ḥāfiẓ).", grammar:"Li-furūjihim — the li- strengthens the object of a participle (lām at-taqwiyah). ḥāfiẓūna = Form I participle.", why:"ḥifẓ — frames chastity as GUARDING a trust, the same act as preserving revelation.", note:"ḤĀFIẒ = one who GUARDS his chastity — and one who has MEMORIZED the Qurʾān. Both = PROTECTING something precious.", more:["Faraj = RELIEF, a way out of hardship (al-faraj baʿda'sh-shiddah = relief after hardship!); furjah = a SPECTACLE / sightseeing (tafarraja = to watch!); infirāj = easing (of a crisis).","ḥāfiẓūn — the \"GUARDING / preserving / memorizing\" family — ḥafiẓa = to guard, to keep safe, to MEMORIZE; ḥāfiẓ = a GUARDIAN / one who has MEMORIZED the Qurʾān; Ṭāriq 86:4 — lammā ʿalayhā ḥāfiẓ (over every soul is a GUARDIAN); Burūj 85:22 — fī lawḥin maḥfūẓ (the PRESERVED Tablet); ḥifẓ = memorization.","Both are acts of PROTECTING something precious from loss.","Ṭāriq 86:4 — in kullu nafsin lammā ʿalayhā ḥāfiẓ — every soul has a GUARDIAN over it (from Allah); here, the believer GUARDS himself.","Guarded by Him, guarding for Him.","Farj — \"an OPENING\" — the Qurʾān's modest, indirect term — and the same root gives FARAJ = RELIEF (a \"gap\" opening in hardship).","(Kept modest here.) And note the Form I ḥāfiẓūn (v.29) vs Form III yuḥāfiẓūn (v.34 — guarding their PRAYERS) — same root, guarding body and guarding prayer.","The list's guarding begins and ends with ḥ-f-ẓ.","Ṭāriq 86:4: a ḥāfiẓ over every soul; Burūj 85:22: lawḥ maḥfūẓ.","Ḥ-f-ẓ: v.29 guarding the BODY; v.34 guarding the PRAYER.","Faraj = RELIEF (same root as farj)."]},
  {ar:"إِلَّا عَلَىٰ أَزْوَاجِهِمْ أَوْ مَا مَلَكَتْ أَيْمَانُهُمْ فَإِنَّهُمْ غَيْرُ مَلُومِينَ", tr:"Illā ʿalā azwājihim aw mā malakat aymānuhum fa-innahum ghayru malūmīn", v:"30", root:"Azwāj — the \"PAIRING\" family — zawj = a SPOUSE / one of a pair (Nabaʾ 78:8 — wa khalaqnākum azwājā — created in PAIRS!", meaning:"\"…except from their spouses or those their right hands possess — for they are not to be blamed.\"", magic:"The exception sets lawful limits — and the key word is BLAME. Ghayru malūmīn — \"NOT blameworthy\" — lawful intimacy carries no blame.", grammar:"Illā ʿalā = exception. ghayru malūmīna — ghayr + participle (third ghayr in the list).", why:"Malūmīn — lifts blame from what is lawful, linking to the self-blaming soul.", note:"GHAYRU MALŪMĪN = NOT BLAMEWORTHY — lawful intimacy carries no blame.", more:["Takwīr 81:7 — wa idha'n-nufūsu zuwwijat!; zawāj = MARRIAGE).","mā malakat aymānuhum — \"what their right hands possess\" — a historical legal category in the Qurʾān (bondwomen), referring to the institution of that era.","malūmīn — the \"BLAME\" family — lāma = to blame; lawm = BLAME; an-nafs al-lawwāmah = the SELF-BLAMING soul (Qiyāmah 75:2!).","Islam doesn't treat lawful desire as shameful; it defines its proper place.","Malūm ← lawm — the root of AN-NAFS AL-LAWWĀMAH (Qiyāmah 75:2) — the soul that blames itself.","Here: no need for self-blame — within the lawful.","Azwāj — PAIRS — Nabaʾ 78:8 — wa khalaqnākum azwājā — \"We created you in PAIRS.\" The pairing is part of creation; marriage honours it.","Mā malakat aymānuhum — a historical category that Islamic law regulated heavily and steadily worked to reduce (through manumission as expiation, charity, mukātabah); understood within its time.","Malūm ← lawm — an-nafs al-LAWWĀMAH (Qiyāmah 75:2). Azwāj = PAIRS — Nabaʾ 78:8 \"created in pairs\"; zawāj = MARRIAGE.","Mā malakat aymānuhum = a historical category, regulated toward reduction."]},
  {ar:"فَمَنِ ابْتَغَىٰ وَرَاءَ ذَٰلِكَ فَأُولَٰئِكَ هُمُ الْعَادُونَ", tr:"Fa-mani'btaghā warāʾa dhālika fa-ulāʾika humu'l-ʿādūn", v:"31", root:"Ibtaghā — to SEEK (Form VIII of baghā; Layl 92:20 ibtighāʾa wajhi rabbih; Muzzammil 73:20 yabtaghūna min faḍli'llāh).", meaning:"\"But whoever seeks beyond that — they are the transgressors.\"", magic:"Al-ʿĀDŪN — those who RUN PAST the line.", grammar:"Man + past + fa = conditional. hum = ḍamīr al-faṣl (separating pronoun).", why:"ʿādūn (those who run past) — pictures transgression as overrunning a marked line.", note:"AL-ʿĀDŪN = those who RUN PAST the line — same root as AL-ʿĀDIYĀT (the CHARGING horses, 100:1). ʿUdwān = AGGRESSION; ʿaduww = an ENEMY.", more:["ʿādūn — the \"RUNNING / exceeding / aggression\" family — ʿadā = to run, to exceed; ʿudwān = AGGRESSION, transgression; ʿaduww = an ENEMY; al-ʿādiyāt = the CHARGERS (ʿĀdiyāt 100:1!); ʿādūn = those who RUN PAST the limit.","The root of al-ʿĀDIYĀT — the war horses CHARGING at full gallop (ʿĀdiyāt 100:1)! A charging horse runs; a transgressor runs too — past the boundary.","Same root: running, and running over the limit.","ʿUdwān = AGGRESSION (running AT someone); ʿaduww = an ENEMY (one who runs against you).","Ibtaghā — the SEEKING verb — Layl 92:20: seeking His FACE; Muzzammil 73:20: seeking His BOUNTY; Maʿārij 70:31: seeking BEYOND the limit.","Same verb — three objects: His face, His bounty, or what lies past His boundary.","Ulāʾika HUMU'l-ʿādūn — the separating pronoun hum = restriction: \"THEY — they specifically — are the transgressors.\" (Bayyinah 98:6–7, Kawthar 108:3.)","Ibtaghā: His FACE (92:20), His BOUNTY (73:20), BEYOND the limit (70:31). Hum = separating pronoun."]},
  {ar:"وَالَّذِينَ هُمْ لِأَمَانَاتِهِمْ وَعَهْدِهِمْ رَاعُونَ", tr:"Wa'lladhīna hum li-amānātihim wa ʿahdihim rāʿūn", v:"32", root:"Amānāt — TRUSTS — plural of amānah (amn root; al-Amīn — the Prophet's ﷺ title; Tīn 95:3 al-baladi'l-amīn.", meaning:"\"And those who are shepherds of their trusts and their covenants…\"", magic:"RĀʿŪN — SHEPHERDS of their trusts. Not just \"keepers\" — SHEPHERDS — who watch over their flock day and night, guard it from wolves, lead it to water.", grammar:"Li-amānātihim — lām at-taqwiyah (strengthening the participle's object).", why:"Rāʿī (shepherd) — makes trustworthiness an active, constant care, not a passive non-betrayal.", note:"RĀʿŪN = SHEPHERDS of their trusts — watchful, constant care.", more:["Amānah = (modern) a SECRETARIAT / trusteeship!).","ʿahd — a COVENANT / promise (muʿāhadah = a TREATY; maʿhad = an INSTITUTE!; wilāyat al-ʿahd = CROWN PRINCEHOOD).","rāʿūn — the \"SHEPHERDING / caring for\" family — raʿā = to shepherd, to tend, to look after; rāʿī = a SHEPHERD; riʿāyah = CARE (riʿāyah ṣiḥḥiyyah = HEALTHCARE!); raʿiyyah = the SUBJECTS / citizens (the \"flock\"!).","That's how the believer treats an amānah (trust) or an ʿahd (promise): with a shepherd's constant watchful care.","And the famous ḥadīth: \"Kullukum rāʿin wa kullukum masʾūlun ʿan raʿiyyatih\" — \"EACH of you is a SHEPHERD, and each of you is RESPONSIBLE for his flock.\" Same root — and masʾūl (responsible) is the saʾala root of v.1 and v.25!","Amānah — the Prophet ﷺ was al-AMĪN (the Trustworthy) before prophethood.","The ḥadīth: \"No faith for one who has no amānah, no religion for one who has no ʿahd.\" The two are paired here exactly.","Hooks: riʿāyah ṣiḥḥiyyah = HEALTHCARE (shepherding health!); maʿhad = an INSTITUTE; muʿāhadah = a TREATY.","rāʿūna = active participle, weak-final.","Ḥadīth: \"EACH of you is a SHEPHERD (rāʿin), each RESPONSIBLE (masʾūl) for his flock.\" RIʿĀYAH ṢIḤḤIYYAH = HEALTHCARE.","Al-AMĪN = the Prophet's ﷺ title; maʿhad = an INSTITUTE; muʿāhadah = a TREATY.","\"No faith without amānah, no religion without ʿahd.\""]},
  {ar:"وَالَّذِينَ هُم بِشَهَادَاتِهِمْ قَائِمُونَ", tr:"Wa'lladhīna hum bi-shahādātihim qāʾimūn", v:"33", root:"Shahādāt — TESTIMONIES (shahādah; ash-Shahādah — the declaration of faith!; shahādah = a CERTIFICATE / diploma!).", meaning:"\"And those who stand firm in their testimonies…\"", magic:"QĀʾIMŪN — STANDING by their testimony — the q-w-m root again (stand → upright). They don't sit on the truth, hide it, or bend it — they STAND it up.", grammar:"Bi-shahādātihim + qāʾimūna — \"upholding their testimonies.\" Recitation variant: bi-shahādatihim (singular).", why:"Qāʾim — truth-telling as an upright posture, maintained under pressure.", note:"QĀʾIMŪN = STANDING UP their testimony — not sitting on it, hiding it, bending it.", more:["qāʾimūn — STANDING / upholding (the q-w-m root — qiyām, mustaqīm, taqwīm, qayyimah).","Even against themselves or relatives (kūnū qawwāmīna bi'l-qisṭi shuhadāʾa li'llāhi wa law ʿalā anfusikum, 4:135 — \"stand firm for justice, witnesses for Allah, even against yourselves\").","The q-w-m thread: qum (Muzzammil 73:2, Muddaththir 74:2), aqwam (73:6), istaqāmū (Jinn 72:16), taqwīm (Tīn 95:4), qayyimah (Bayyinah 98:5), qāʾimūn (Maʿārij 70:33). Shahādah — the sh-h-d root — ʿĀdiyāt 100:7 — wa innahu ʿalā dhālika la-shahīd (man is a WITNESS against himself) Burūj 85:3 (shāhid wa mashhūd).","Shahādah = also a CERTIFICATE — and THE Shahādah — the first testimony you uphold.","4:135: stand firm for justice, even against yourselves.","Q-w-m thread: qum, aqwam, istaqāmū, taqwīm, qayyimah, qāʾimūn.","Shahādah = a CERTIFICATE — and THE Shahādah."]},
  {ar:"وَالَّذِينَ هُمْ عَلَىٰ صَلَاتِهِمْ يُحَافِظُونَ", tr:"Wa'lladhīna hum ʿalā ṣalātihim yuḥāfiẓūn", v:"34", root:"Yuḥāfiẓūn — Form III of ḥafiẓa = to GUARD diligently, to preserve carefully, to watch over persistently.", meaning:"\"And those who carefully guard their prayers.\"", magic:"THE CLOSING BRACKET — the list ends where it began: PRAYER. v.23 — alladhīna hum ʿalā ṣalātihim dāʾimūn — CONSTANT in prayer.", grammar:"Form III present (yuḥāfiẓūna) — ongoing, effortful. ʿalā = \"over / upon\" (watching over).", why:"Yuḥāfiẓūn — closes the list on the quality of prayer, as v.23 opened on its constancy.", note:"PRAYER BRACKETS THE LIST: v.23 DĀʾIMŪN (never MISS it) → v.34 YUḤĀFIẒŪN (do it RIGHT). Frequency + quality.", more:["Muḥāfaẓah = a GOVERNORATE / province (a region guarded!); muḥāfiẓ = a GOVERNOR (and muḥāfiẓ = CONSERVATIVE — one who preserves!); miḥfaẓah = a WALLET (where you keep things safe!).","v.34 — alladhīna hum ʿalā ṣalātihim yuḥāfiẓūn — GUARDING prayer.","Classical distinction: dāʾimūn = they never MISS it (constancy — frequency); yuḥāfiẓūn = they PROTECT its quality — its times, its conditions, its ablution, its focus (care).","One is about doing it always; the other is about doing it RIGHT.","And everything between v.23 and v.34 — charity, belief, fear, chastity, trusts, testimony — sits INSIDE the prayer-frame.","Form III (fāʿala) = sustained, effortful, back-and-forth engagement — guarding it like a governor guards a province: constantly, actively.","Muḥāfiẓ = GOVERNOR; miḥfaẓah = WALLET — guard your prayers like your wallet.","And the list mirrors al-Muʾminūn 23:1–9 — which also opens with prayer (khāshiʿūn — humble in it) and closes with prayer (yuḥāfiẓūn — guarding it).","Two surahs, the same frame.","And ḥ-f-ẓ: v.29 Form I (guarding chastity) → v.34 Form III (guarding prayer — more intensive).","Same frame as al-Muʾminūn 23:1–9. MUḤĀFIẒ = a GOVERNOR / CONSERVATIVE; MIḤFAẒAH = a WALLET — guard prayer like your wallet.","Ḥ-f-ẓ: chastity (v.29, Form I) → prayer (v.34, Form III)."]},
  {ar:"أُولَٰئِكَ فِي جَنَّاتٍ مُّكْرَمُونَ", tr:"Ulāʾika fī jannātin mukramūn", v:"35", root:"Mukramūn — the \"HONOUR / generosity\" family — karuma = to be noble; akrama (Form IV) = to HONOUR someone.", meaning:"\"Those will be in gardens, honoured.\"", magic:"MUKRAMŪN — HONOURED — the ikrām thread from Fajr completes: Fajr 89:15 — fa-akramahu — Allah HONOURED him (with wealth) — and he said \"my Lord honoured me!\" as if he deserved it.", grammar:"Ulāʾika = \"those\" (far demonstrative — elevated status). mukramūna = Form IV passive participle.", why:"Mukram — the reward is honour, matching a life of honouring others.", note:"Fajr 89:15: honoured with wealth (akramahu) → 89:17: didn't HONOUR the orphan (lā tukrimūn) → Maʿārij 70:35: those who honoured others are MUKRAMŪN forever.", more:["Ikrām = honouring / hospitality; karāmah = DIGNITY (al-karāmah al-insāniyyah = human dignity!); Fajr 89:15 — fa-akramahu wa naʿʿamah; Fajr 89:17 — kallā bal lā tukrimūna'l-yatīm!","Fajr 89:17 — kallā bal lā tukrimūna'l-yatīm — \"No!","You do not HONOUR the orphan!\" Maʿārij 70:35 — mukramūn — those who DID honour others (gave the ḥaqq, shepherded trusts) — are HONOURED forever.","Fajr: honoured with wealth, didn't honour the orphan.","Maʿārij: honoured others, honoured in Paradise.","And the PASSIVE: mukramūn — \"HONOURED (by Another)\" — they don't honour themselves; they're honoured BY Allah.","The honour of being a guest.","Ikrām aḍ-ḍayf = honouring a guest — they are His guests.","Karāmah = DIGNITY — your free hook.","PASSIVE = honoured BY Him — His GUESTS.","Karāmah = DIGNITY; ikrām aḍ-ḍayf = hospitality."]},
  {ar:"فَمَالِ الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا قِبَلَكَ مُهْطِعِينَ", tr:"Fa-māli'lladhīna kafarū qibalaka muhṭiʿīn", v:"36", root:"Fa-mā li- — \"So what is WRONG with…?\" (Muddaththir 74:49 — fa-mā lahum; Zalzalah 99:3 — mā lahā).", meaning:"\"So what is wrong with those who disbelieve, rushing toward you with necks outstretched…\"", magic:"A STRANGE SCENE — the deniers RUSH toward the Prophet ﷺ. Why?", grammar:"Fa-māli — the lām is written separately in the muṣḥaf (fa-māli'lladhīna).", why:"Ihṭāʿ — a vivid posture: craning, staring — close but not open.", note:"MUHṬIʿĪN = necks CRANED, eyes FIXED — rushing toward him to MOCK. Muddaththir 74:49–50: they FLEE like donkeys.", more:["qibal — the \"FACING / in front of\" family — qibal = toward, in the direction of (qiblah = the direction you FACE in prayer!; qabila = to ACCEPT; istiqbāl = RECEPTION).","muhṭiʿīn — rushing forward with necks STRETCHED OUT and eyes FIXED, staring — the posture of a frightened or eager crowd running toward something.","Classical: they crowded around him to MOCK, to stare, to hear (and ridicule) what he said — necks craning, eyes fixed (ihṭāʿ) — close to the truth physically, far from it spiritually.","Fa-mā li- — \"WHAT'S WRONG with them?\" — Muddaththir 74:49 asked the same about those fleeing like donkeys.","Muddaththir: they FLEE from the reminder.","Maʿārij: they RUSH toward the Prophet ﷺ — but only to mock.","Running away or running toward — both without faith.","Muhṭiʿīn — Qamar 54:8 — muhṭiʿīna ila'd-dāʿ — \"rushing, necks stretched, toward the CALLER\" — on Judgment Day.","They rush with craned necks toward the Prophet ﷺ to mock (70:36) — and they'll rush with craned necks toward the Caller on the Day (54:8). The same posture, voluntary now, forced then.","Qibalaka — \"toward YOU\" — the qiblah root — they faced him, but not in the way of a qiblah.","muhṭiʿīna = ḥāl (Form IV participle).","Maʿārij 70:36: they RUSH toward him — to mock.","Qamar 54:8: same posture toward the CALLER on the Day.","Qibal ← QIBLAH; istiqbāl = RECEPTION."]},
  {ar:"عَنِ الْيَمِينِ وَعَنِ الشِّمَالِ عِزِينَ", tr:"ʿani'l-yamīni wa ʿani'sh-shimāli ʿizīn", v:"37", root:"Yamīn / shimāl — RIGHT / LEFT (Ḥāqqah 69:19, 25!; al-Yaman / ash-Shām — Quraysh 106:2). ʿizīn.", meaning:"\"…from the right and from the left, in scattered groups?\"", magic:"RIGHT and LEFT — the two hands of the previous surah. Ḥāqqah 69:19 — book in the RIGHT (saved); 69:25 — book in the LEFT (damned).", grammar:"ʿizīna = second ḥāl, plural of ʿizah (a sound-masculine-style plural of a feminine noun — unusual).", why:"ʿizīn — pictures disbelief as fragmented clusters around a single centre.", note:"RIGHT and LEFT of the Prophet ﷺ — like Ḥāqqah 69:19/25's right-hand and left-hand books.", more:["Plural of ʿizah = a small GROUP, a cluster, a knot of people — scattered in separate groups.","Maʿārij 70:37 — they cluster ʿani'l-yamīni wa ʿani'sh-shimāl — on the right and the left of the Prophet ﷺ — standing physically on both sides, but their books will all go to one side.","And Balad 90:18–19 (maymanah / mashʾamah), Quraysh 106:2 (Yemen / Shām).","The right-left thread across five surahs.","ʿIzīn — scattered CLUSTERS — not one united crowd — little knots of people, each muttering, each mocking — divided even in their opposition.","Compare Jinn 72:11 — kunnā ṭarāʾiqa qidadā (we were split into factions) and Zalzalah 99:6 — yaṣduru'n-nāsu ashtātā (people in scattered groups).","Disbelief scatters; faith gathers.","And the contrast with v.22–35: the believers are described ONE BY ONE, trait by trait — a united character.","The deniers are ʿizīn — fragments.","ʿan = \"from / on\" (the side).","Standing on both sides; their books will all go one way.","ʿIZĪN = scattered CLUSTERS — divided even in opposition.","Jinn 72:11 qidad, Zalzalah 99:6 ashtāt.","Disbelief scatters; faith gathers."]},
  {ar:"أَيَطْمَعُ كُلُّ امْرِئٍ مِّنْهُمْ أَن يُدْخَلَ جَنَّةَ نَعِيمٍ", tr:"A-yaṭmaʿu kullu'mriʾin minhum an yudkhala jannata naʿīm", v:"38", root:"Yaṭmaʿu — to COVET, crave greedily (ṭammāʿ = greedy; Muddaththir 74:15 — thumma yaṭmaʿu an azīd!).", meaning:"\"Does every one of them covet being admitted into a garden of bliss?\"", magic:"NEARLY IDENTICAL to TWO verses in Muddaththir. Muddaththir 74:15 — thumma yaṭmaʿu an azīd — al-Walīd COVETED MORE (wealth).", grammar:"A- = rebuking question. yudkhala = passive subjunctive (Form IV). jannata naʿīmin — iḍāfah.", why:"ṭamaʿ — exposes the delusion: reward expected without the path.", note:"YAṬMAʿU (74:15) + KULLU'MRIʾIN MINHUM (74:52) — both Muddaththir phrases fused here.", more:["kullu'mriʾin — EVERY single man (Muddaththir 74:52 — bal yurīdu kullu'mriʾin minhum!).","yudkhala — to BE ADMITTED (passive).","naʿīm — BLISS (Insān 76:20, Infiṭār 82:13, Muṭaffifīn 83:22, Takāthur 102:8).","Muddaththir 74:52 — bal yurīdu kullu'mriʾin minhum an yuʾtā ṣuḥufan munashsharah — EVERY ONE of them wanted personal scrolls.","Maʿārij 70:38 — a-yaṭmaʿu kullu'mriʾin minhum an yudkhala jannata naʿīm — EVERY ONE of them COVETS Paradise.","Yaṭmaʿu + kullu'mriʾin minhum — BOTH phrases from Muddaththir, fused into one verse.","They covet MORE WEALTH (74:15), PERSONAL SCROLLS (74:52), and now PARADISE (70:38) — all without earning any of it.","Ṭamaʿ = GREED — not hope (rajāʾ), which is paired with effort; GREED — wanting the reward while mocking the path.","They think: we're rich and honoured here, so we'll be honoured there (Fajr 89:15 — rabbī akraman; Kahf 18:36 — wa la-in rudidtu ilā rabbī la-ajidanna khayran minhā).","Contrast v.35: the believers are mukramūn — honoured — after a life of prayer, giving, trust.","The deniers COVET what the believers EARNED.","Yudkhala — PASSIVE: \"to BE ADMITTED\" — entry is granted, not taken.","Fajr 89:29–30 — fa'dkhulī fī ʿibādī · wa'dkhulī jannatī — the invitation to the content soul — not to the greedy.","They COVET: more wealth (74:15), personal scrolls (74:52), PARADISE (70:38) — without earning any.","v.35 believers EARNED mukramūn; v.38 deniers COVET it.","Yudkhala = PASSIVE — entry is GRANTED.","Ṭamaʿ (greed) ≠ rajāʾ (hope with effort)."]},
  {ar:"كَلَّا ۖ إِنَّا خَلَقْنَاهُم مِّمَّا يَعْلَمُونَ", tr:"Kallā innā khalaqnāhum mimmā yaʿlamūn", v:"39", root:"Kallā — NO! khalaqnāhum — We CREATED them. mimmā yaʿlamūn — \"from what they KNOW\" (a deliberate euphemism).", meaning:"\"No! Indeed, We created them from what they know.\"", magic:"Mimmā yaʿlamūn — \"from what they KNOW\" — the Qurʾān won't even NAME it — the despised drop of fluid (Ṭāriq 86:6 — māʾin dāfiq (gushing fluid); ʿAbasa 80:19 — min nuṭfah.", grammar:"Mimmā = min + mā (relative — \"from what\"). yaʿlamūna — the object left unnamed = euphemism (kināyah).", why:"Kināyah (euphemism) as rebuke — unnamed, and therefore more humiliating.", note:"MIMMĀ YAʿLAMŪN = \"from what they KNOW\" — the Qurʾān won't even NAME it. The euphemism IS the insult.", more:["Mursalāt 77:20 — māʾin mahīn (a despised fluid); Insān 76:2 — nuṭfatin amshāj).","A CRUSHING ANSWER — through MODESTY.","The euphemism is itself the insult: \"you arrogant men who think you've EARNED Paradise by birth and rank — you came from something you'd be embarrassed to name.\" Your origin doesn't qualify you; only your deeds do.","And note: kallā answers v.38's GREED: \"Do they covet Paradise?","— NO!\" The basis of their claim (pedigree, wealth, status) is demolished by reminding them of their humble origin.","Classical: noble lineage means nothing — all came from the same fluid.","Compare Ṭāriq 86:5 — fal-yanẓuri'l-insānu mimma khuliq — \"let man LOOK at what he was created FROM.\" Maʿārij doesn't tell them to look — they already KNOW.","Ṭāriq 86:6 māʾ dāfiq, ʿAbasa 80:19 nuṭfah, Insān 76:2 nuṭfatin amshāj.","KALLĀ answers GREED: your ORIGIN doesn't qualify you.","Ṭāriq 86:5: \"LOOK at what you were created from.\""]},
  {ar:"فَلَا أُقْسِمُ بِرَبِّ الْمَشَارِقِ وَالْمَغَارِبِ", tr:"Fa-lā uqsimu bi-rabbi'l-mashāriqi wa'l-maghārib", v:"40", root:"Mashāriq — plural of mashriq = EASTS / places of sunrise.", meaning:"\"So I swear by the Lord of the Easts and the Wests…\"", magic:"THE SINGULAR-DUAL-PLURAL PUZZLE — solved by astronomy.", grammar:"Mashāriq / maghārib = DIPTOTES (mafāʿil pattern) — here genitive with kasrah because of al-.", why:"The plural — pictures daily, ceaseless change, which grounds v.41's claim of power to replace.", note:"SINGULAR (73:9) = the DIRECTIONS. DUAL (55:17) = the two SOLSTICE extremes.", more:["maghārib — plural of maghrib = WESTS / places of sunset (al-Maghrib = Morocco; Ṣalāt al-Maghrib).","The Qurʾān uses ALL THREE numbers for East and West: - SINGULAR — Muzzammil 73:9 — rabbu'l-mashriqi wa'l-maghrib — the EAST and the WEST (the two directions).","- DUAL — Raḥmān 55:17 — rabbu'l-mashriqayni wa rabbu'l-maghribayn — the TWO easts and TWO wests (the two extreme points of sunrise — at the summer and winter solstices).","- PLURAL — Maʿārij 70:40 — bi-rabbi'l-mashāriqi wa'l-maghārib — ALL the easts and wests (the sun rises at a slightly different point EVERY DAY — some 365 sunrise-points across the year!).","No contradiction — three zoom levels of the same sky: the general direction (1), the two solstice extremes (2), every single day's point (many).","The Arabs, watching the horizon in the desert, knew the sun \"walks\" along it through the year.","v.41: \"We are ABLE to REPLACE them with better.\" The One who moves the sunrise to a new point every single day — can easily replace a people.","Constant renewal in the sky = power to renew humanity.","Fa-lā uqsimu — the oath-opener (Ḥāqqah 69:38, Qiyāmah 75:1, Takwīr 81:15, Inshiqāq 84:16, Balad 90:1).","PLURAL (70:40) = EVERY DAY'S sunrise point (~365). Three zoom levels — no contradiction.","He moves the sunrise DAILY → He can replace a people (v.41)."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّا لَقَادِرُونَ · عَلَىٰ أَن نُّبَدِّلَ خَيْرًا مِّنْهُمْ وَمَا نَحْنُ بِمَسْبُوقِينَ", tr:"Innā la-qādirūn · ʿalā an nubaddila khayran minhum wa mā naḥnu bi-masbūqīn", v:"40–41", root:"Qādirūn — ABLE / powerful (the qadr root — POWER sense; Qiyāmah 75:4 — bālā qādirīna ʿalā an nusawwiya banānah!).", meaning:"\"Indeed, We are able to replace them with better than them, and We are not to be outrun.\"", magic:"The QĀDIRŪN echo — from Qiyāmah.", grammar:"Inna + lām (la-qādirūna) = emphasis. khayran = object (\"better ones\").", why:"Masbūq — a race image: no one gets ahead of Him.", note:"QĀDIRŪNA ʿALĀ AN… — Qiyāmah 75:4 (restore FINGERTIPS) & Maʿārij 70:40–41 (REPLACE a people).", more:["nubaddila — the \"REPLACING / exchanging\" family — badala = to replace; badal = a SUBSTITUTE (badal in GRAMMAR = APPOSITION!; badlah = a SUIT (a \"change\" of clothes!); tabdīl = changing).","masbūqīn — the \"OUTRUNNING / preceding\" family — sabaqa = to outrun, to go ahead; musābaqah = a RACE / COMPETITION; as-sābiqāt — Nāziʿāt 79:4 (those who race ahead); masbūq = OUTRUN / beaten (and in fiqh, masbūq = a LATECOMER to congregational prayer!).","Qiyāmah 75:4 — bālā qādirīna ʿalā an nusawwiya banānah — \"Yes, We are ABLE to restore his very FINGERTIPS.\" Maʿārij 70:40–41 — innā la-qādirūna ʿalā an nubaddila khayran minhum — \"We are ABLE to REPLACE them with better.\" Qādirūna ʿalā an… — identical construction.","Rebuild the fingertip; replace a whole people — both within His power.","Nubaddila khayran minhum — REPLACE them with BETTER — and history did exactly that: the mocking chiefs of Quraysh were replaced by the generation of the Companions — who carried the message to the ends of the earth.","Wa mā naḥnu bi-MASBŪQĪN — \"We are not OUTRUN\" — no one can race ahead of Him, escape Him, beat Him to the finish.","Compare Jinn 72:12 — lan nuʿjiza'llāha fi'l-arḍi wa lan nuʿjizahu harabā (can't escape Him by FLIGHT).","Masbūq in fiqh = a LATECOMER to the jamāʿah — the one who arrives after the imam started — Allah is never a masbūq.","Grammar hook: badal = APPOSITION — the grammar term you've used (e.g., Quraysh 106:2) — same root as \"replace.\"","bi-masbūqīna — extra bi- after negation (emphasis — like bi-majnūn, 81:22).","History: mocking chiefs REPLACED by the Companions.","Mā naḥnu bi-MASBŪQĪN = We are NOT OUTRUN.","Musābaqah = a RACE; masbūq = a LATECOMER to prayer.","BADAL = SUBSTITUTE / grammatical APPOSITION; badlah = a SUIT."]},
  {ar:"فَذَرْهُمْ يَخُوضُوا وَيَلْعَبُوا", tr:"Fa-dharhum yakhūḍū wa yalʿabū", v:"42", root:"Dhar — LEAVE (them) — the DEFECTIVE verb wadhara (no past tense in use; Muzzammil 73:11 dharnī; Muddaththir 74:11 dharnī!).", meaning:"\"So leave them to wade [in falsehood] and play…\"", magic:"ONE — DHAR (leave them): Muzzammil 73:11 — wa dharnī wa'l-mukadhdhibīn — \"leave Me with the deniers.\" Muddaththir 74:11 — dharnī wa man khalaqtu waḥīdā.", grammar:"Dhar = imperative of a defective verb.", why:"Khawḍ + laʿib — wading deeper into falsehood, treating it as a game — until v.42's end.", note:"DHAR: Muzzammil 73:11 & Muddaththir 74:11 \"leave them TO ME\" → Maʿārij 70:42 \"LEAVE THEM [be].\" KHAWḌ: Maʿārij 70:42.", more:["yakhūḍū — the \"WADING into water / plunging into talk\" family — khawḍ (Muddaththir 74:45 — wa kunnā nakhūḍu maʿa'l-khāʾiḍīn!).","yalʿabū — the \"PLAY\" family — laʿiba = to play; laʿib = PLAY, games; malʿab = a STADIUM / playground; luʿbah = a TOY / a game; laʿb (luʿāb) = SALIVA (drooling — a child's play!).","\"leave Me with the one I created alone.\" Maʿārij 70:42 — fa-dharhum — \"so LEAVE THEM.\" Muzzammil & Muddaththir: \"leave [them] to ME.\" Maʿārij: \"leave THEM [be].\" The same defective verb — from \"hand them over\" to \"let them go.\" TWO — KHAWḌ (wading): Muddaththir 74:45 — wa kunnā nakhūḍu maʿa'l-khāʾiḍīn — the CONFESSION from Saqar: \"we used to WADE with the waders.\" Maʿārij 70:42 — yakhūḍū — \"let them WADE\" — Maʿārij shows them wading NOW; Muddaththir hears them confess it LATER.","TWO CROSS-SURAH PAYOFFS IN THREE WORDS.","Same verb — present permission, future confession.","Yalʿabū — PLAY — like children — mocking the truth as a game.","Malʿab = a STADIUM — life treated as a playground (innamā'l-ḥayātu'd-dunyā laʿibun wa lahw, 47:36).","yakhūḍū / yalʿabū = JUSSIVE (the nūn dropped) — because they're the \"answer\" (jawāb) of the command dharhum (\"leave them [and they will] wade and play\").","WADING now → Muddaththir 74:45 — CONFESSING it from Saqar.","Laʿib = PLAY; malʿab = a STADIUM — life as a playground.","Jussive (answer of the command)."]},
  {ar:"حَتَّىٰ يُلَاقُوا يَوْمَهُمُ الَّذِي يُوعَدُونَ", tr:"ḥattā yulāqū yawmahumu'lladhī yūʿadūn", v:"42", root:"Yulāqū — the \"MEETING face to face\" family — lāqā (Form III) = to meet, encounter.", meaning:"\"…until they meet their Day which they are promised.\"", magic:"Ḥattā — \"UNTIL\" — the play has an END-POINT. The wading and playing continue — UNTIL the meeting. Yulāqū — Form III = a FACE-TO-FACE MEETING — like an appointment you can't skip.", grammar:"ḥattā + subjunctive (yulāqū, nūn dropped) = \"until.\" yūʿadūna = passive present.", why:"Liqāʾ — makes the Day an unavoidable personal appointment.", note:"ḤATTĀ = UNTIL — the play has an END-POINT.", more:["Liqāʾ = a MEETING / an INTERVIEW (liqāʾ ṣaḥafī = a press interview!); Inshiqāq 84:6 — fa-mulāqīh; Ḥāqqah 69:20 — annī mulāqin ḥisābiyah.","yūʿadūn — they are PROMISED / threatened (waʿd; mawʿid = an APPOINTMENT).","The liqāʾ thread: Inshiqāq 84:6 — fa-mulāqīh — you WILL meet Him.","Ḥāqqah 69:20 — innī ẓanantu annī mulāqin ḥisābiyah — the righteous man: \"I KNEW I'd meet my account.\" Maʿārij 70:42 — yulāqū yawmahum — the deniers will meet it — without having prepared.","One expected the meeting (69:20); they'll stumble into it (70:42). Yawmahum — \"THEIR Day\" — personal: the Day assigned to THEM.","Alladhī yūʿadūn — \"which they are PROMISED\" — repeated as the surah's last words (v.44) — a bookend.","Liqāʾ: Inshiqāq 84:6 (you WILL meet Him), Ḥāqqah 69:20 (he KNEW he'd meet it), Maʿārij 70:42 (they'll meet it UNPREPARED).","Liqāʾ ṣaḥafī = a PRESS INTERVIEW; mawʿid = an APPOINTMENT."]},
  {ar:"يَوْمَ يَخْرُجُونَ مِنَ الْأَجْدَاثِ سِرَاعًا", tr:"Yawma yakhrujūna mina'l-ajdāthi sirāʿā", v:"43", root:"Yakhrujūn — they COME OUT (kharaja; khurūj = exit; makhraj = an EXIT — and in tajwīd, makhraj = the POINT OF ARTICULATION of a letter!).", meaning:"\"The Day they will emerge from the graves, rushing…\"", magic:"They WADED and PLAYED slowly (v.42) — now they RUSH (v.43). Sirāʿan = at SPEED — no more leisure, no more games — racing out of the graves.", grammar:"Yawma = badal of yawmahum (v.42) — specifying the Day. sirāʿan = ḥāl (plural of sarīʿ).", why:"Sirāʿ — the leisurely players forced into a sprint.", note:"They WADED and PLAYED slowly (v.42) → they RUSH out of the graves (sirāʿā, v.43). AJDĀTH = GRAVES — Yāsīn 36:51 (same scene).", more:["ajdāth — GRAVES (plural of jadath; Yāsīn 36:51 — fa-idhā hum mina'l-ajdāthi ilā rabbihim yansilūn).","sirāʿ — the \"SPEED / haste\" family — sarīʿ = FAST; surʿah = SPEED (surʿat aḍ-ḍawʾ = the speed of light!); sirāʿan = hastening, racing.","Ajdāth — a word for GRAVES — the qabr thread you own (ʿAbasa 80:21, Infiṭār 82:4, ʿĀdiyāt 100:9, Takāthur 102:2) — here a different word, jadath.","Yāsīn 36:51 — same word, same scene: \"from the GRAVES to their Lord, they RACE.\" And Zalzalah 99:6 — yawmaʾidhin yaṣduru'n-nāsu ashtātā — people ISSUING FORTH in scattered groups.","Nabaʾ 78:18 — fa-taʾtūna afwājā (you come in CROWDS).","Maʿārij 70:43 — RUSHING out.","Three pictures of the emergence: scattered, in crowds, at speed.","Tajwīd hook: makhraj = the POINT OF ARTICULATION — same root as \"come out.\"","Emergence: SCATTERED (99:6), in CROWDS (78:18), at SPEED (70:43). Surʿat aḍ-ḍawʾ = SPEED OF LIGHT; makhraj = tajwīd ARTICULATION POINT."]},
  {ar:"كَأَنَّهُمْ إِلَىٰ نُصُبٍ يُوفِضُونَ", tr:"Ka-annahum ilā nuṣubin yūfiḍūn", v:"43", root:"Nuṣub — the \"SETTING UP / erecting\" family — naṣaba = to set up, to erect, to plant upright; nuṣub = an ERECTED STONE.", meaning:"\"…as if they were racing toward an erected idol / a target-post.\"", magic:"THE IRONY OF THE SIMILE — they rush like they used to rush to their IDOLS.", grammar:"Ka-annahum = \"as if they.\" yūfiḍūna = Form IV present. Recitation variant: naṣbin (singular-looking).", why:"Nuṣub — turns their old idolatrous eagerness into the image of their terrified rush.", note:"They RACE like they used to race to their IDOL-STONES (nuṣub) at dawn. Old eagerness for false gods → forced haste on the Day.", more:["Classically: (a) an IDOL-STONE / altar they rushed to in pre-Islamic rituals; (b) a TARGET-POST / a raised banner that racers dash toward; nuṣb tidhkārī = a MEMORIAL MONUMENT (modern!); manṣib = a POSITION / post (a job title!); Ghāshiyah 88:19 — wa ila'l-jibāli kayfa nuṣibat (mountains SET UP!); Ghāshiyah 88:3 — ʿāmilatun nāṣibah (toiling, WEARIED — standing exhausted!); Sharḥ 94:7 — fa-idhā faraghta fa'nṣab (stand up and STRIVE!).","yūfiḍūn — to RACE / hurry toward a goal (from w-f-ḍ) — NOT afāḍa (f-y-ḍ, \"pour forth / depart from ʿArafāt\").","Classical: in the pre-Islamic era, people RACED toward the nuṣub (idol-stones) at dawn, competing to be first to touch them — so the very eagerness they once showed toward false gods — they'll show on the Day, racing toward the Caller.","Or the image of a RACE: competitors sprinting toward the finishing POST (nuṣub) — but this race has no prize.","The n-ṣ-b root — FOUR places in your study: Ghāshiyah 88:19 — mountains nuṣibat (SET UP firm).","Ghāshiyah 88:3 — faces nāṣibah (WEARIED, toiling).","Sharḥ 94:7 — fa'nṣab (stand up and STRIVE).","Maʿārij 70:43 — racing to a nuṣub (an erected STONE).","Set up, wearied, striving, an idol-stone — all from \"standing something upright.\" Sharḥ: STRIVE (inṣab) toward your Lord in dunya — or race to a nuṣub on the Day.","Hooks: manṣib = a POSITION / job; nuṣb tidhkārī = a MONUMENT.","N-ṣ-b: mountains SET UP (88:19), faces WEARIED (88:3), STRIVE!","(94:7), an idol-STONE (70:43). Manṣib = a POSITION; nuṣb tidhkārī = a MONUMENT.","Yūfiḍūn (w-f-ḍ) ≠ afāḍa (f-y-ḍ, ʿArafāt)."]},
  {ar:"خَاشِعَةً أَبْصَارُهُمْ تَرْهَقُهُمْ ذِلَّةٌ", tr:"Khāshiʿatan abṣāruhum tarhaquhum dhillah", v:"44", root:"Khāshiʿah — the \"HUMBLED LOWERING\" family — khashaʿa = to be lowered, subdued, humbled (of the gaze, the voice, the heart); khushūʿ = HUMILITY / focus IN PRAYER (everyday Islamic vocabulary!).", meaning:"\"Their eyes humbled, humiliation covering them.\"", magic:"NEARLY IDENTICAL to Nāziʿāt 79:9. Nāziʿāt 79:9 — abṣāruhā khāshiʿah — \"its EYES HUMBLED\" (the hearts on that Day).", grammar:"Khāshiʿatan = ḥāl, feminine (agreeing with the broken plural abṣār), FRONTED before its subject (abṣāruhum).", why:"Khushūʿ — the prayer-word, forced on those who refused prayer.", note:"= Nāziʿāt 79:9 (abṣāruhā khāshiʿah), reversed.", more:["abṣār — EYES / gazes (baṣar).","tarhaquhum — OVERTAKES / covers them (the rahaq root — ʿAbasa 80:41, Jinn 72:6, 13, Muddaththir 74:17). dhillah — the \"HUMILIATION / lowliness\" family — dhalla = to be abased; dhull = HUMILIATION; dhalīl = humiliated; but also dhalūl = DOCILE, tame (of a riding animal!; Insān 76:14 — wa dhullilat quṭūfuhā — fruit made EASY / tame!).","Maʿārij 70:44 — khāshiʿatan abṣāruhum — \"their EYES HUMBLED.\" Same words, reversed order.","And KHUSHŪʿ — the word you use for FOCUS and humility IN PRAYER — al-Muʾminūn 23:2 — alladhīna hum fī ṣalātihim khāshiʿūn (the believers are HUMBLE in their prayer).","The believers chose khushūʿ in PRAYER; the deniers are FORCED into khushūʿ on the Day.","Lower your gaze before Him now — or have it lowered for you then.","The same humility — voluntary and rewarded, or forced and useless.","Tarhaquhum dhillah — \"HUMILIATION OVERTAKES them\" — ʿAbasa 80:41 — tarhaquhā qatarah — \"DARKNESS OVERTAKES them [their faces].\" Same verb (tarhaqu) — ʿAbasa: darkness covers the faces; Maʿārij: humiliation covers them.","The rahaq thread: burden added (Jinn 72:6), no burden for believers (72:13), a steep climb imposed (74:17), darkness (80:41), humiliation (70:44). And the arc of the surah: they ran AROUND the Prophet ﷺ with necks CRANED and eyes STARING (muhṭiʿīn, v.36) — now their eyes are LOWERED (khāshiʿah).","Proud staring → forced lowering.","tarhaquhum dhillatun = verbal clause, ḥāl.","KHUSHŪʿ = HUMILITY IN PRAYER (23:2) — the believers CHOSE it; the deniers are FORCED into it.","Lower your gaze now, or have it lowered then.","TARHAQUHUM: ʿAbasa 80:41 (DARKNESS covers) → Maʿārij 70:44 (HUMILIATION covers).","v.36 eyes STARING → v.44 eyes LOWERED.","Dhalūl = DOCILE; Insān 76:14 dhullilat = made easy."]},
  {ar:"ذَٰلِكَ الْيَوْمُ الَّذِي كَانُوا يُوعَدُونَ", tr:"Dhālika'l-yawmu'lladhī kānū yūʿadūn", v:"44", root:"Dhālika — THAT (far demonstrative). yūʿadūn — they were PROMISED (passive).", meaning:"\"That is the Day which they were being promised.\"", magic:"THE BOOKEND — the surah ends where it began. v.1 — saʾala sāʾilun bi-ʿadhābin wāqiʿ — a man MOCKINGLY demanded the punishment.", grammar:"Dhālika (subject) + al-yawmu (predicate / badal). kānū yūʿadūna = habitual past passive.", why:"Closing on the promise fulfilled — the answer to v.1's mocking request.", note:"BOOKEND: v.1 a mocker DEMANDS the punishment → v.44 \"THAT is the Day they were promised.\" v.42 yūʿadūn (ARE promised) → v.44 KĀNŪ yūʿadūn (WERE promised) — the tense shift IS the arrival.", more:["v.44 — dhālika'l-yawmu'lladhī kānū yūʿadūn — \"THAT is the Day they were promised\" — here it is.","And v.42 said: yawmahumu'lladhī yūʿadūn (their Day which they are promised) — now (v.44): kānū yūʿadūn — \"they WERE promised\" — the promise is past; the Day is present.","The tense shift is the arrival.","Dhālika — \"THAT\" — the far demonstrative — pointing at the Day as something massive, standing apart.","Compare Nabaʾ 78:39 — dhālika'l-yawmu'l-ḥaqq — \"THAT is the TRUE Day.\" Nabaʾ: \"that is the TRUE Day.\" Maʿārij: \"that is the PROMISED Day.\" And Ḥāqqah: al-Ḥāqqah — the TRUE one.","Kānū yūʿadūn — past continuous: \"they used to be promised — again and again\" — every warning they mocked.","Nabaʾ 78:39: \"that is the TRUE Day.\" Maʿārij 70:44: \"that is the PROMISED Day.\""]}
  ]
},
{
  num:71, name:"Nūḥ", ar:"نوح", en:"Noah", verses:28, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"950 years: NIGHT, DAY, PUBLIC, PRIVATE — and they FLED, BLOCKED their ears, HID in their clothes. Echo of -ārā. And the cure for drought, poverty, and childlessness: ISTIGHFĀR.",
  special:["It's a prophet's REPORT to his Lord after 950 years — every method he tried, and every way they refused.","The methods (v.5–9): - laylan wa nahārā — NIGHT and DAY (v.5) - jihārā — OPENLY, publicly (v.8) - aʿlantu… wa asrartu — in PUBLIC and in PRIVATE (v.9)","The responses (v.6–7): - firārā — they FLED (v.6) - aṣābiʿahum fī ādhānihim — FINGERS IN THEIR EARS (v.7) - istaghshaw thiyābahum — COVERED THEMSELVES WITH THEIR CLOTHES (v.7) - aṣarrū wa'stakbarū'stikbārā — PERSISTED and grew arrogant — utterly (v.7)","And look at the rhyme: almost every verse ends in -ārā — firārā, istikbārā, jihārā, isrārā, ghaffārā, midrārā, anhārā, waqārā, aṭwārā, khasārā, kubbārā, anṣārā, dayyārā, kaffārā, tabārā. A long, open, mournful \"-āā-rā\" — the sound of a call echoing for nine and a half centuries.","And the surah's most famous promise — v.10–12: istaghfirū rabbakum innahu kāna ghaffārā · yursili's-samāʾa ʿalaykum midrārā · wa yumdidkum bi-amwālin wa banīn… \"Seek your Lord's forgiveness — He is ever-Forgiving — He will send rain, wealth, children, gardens, rivers.\" Istighfār as the key to provision.","--"],
  big:["The mission (v.1–4) — warn your people; worship, fear Allah, obey me; He'll forgive and give you respite.","Nūḥ's report (v.5–9) — night and day, public and private — they fled, blocked their ears, covered themselves.","The promise of istighfār (v.10–12) — rain, wealth, sons, gardens, rivers.","The signs (v.13–20) — your stages of creation, the layered heavens, moon and sun, growth from earth, the earth as a carpet.","Their response (v.21–24) — they obeyed the rich, plotted hugely, clung to five idols.","The end (v.25) — drowned, then admitted into Fire.","Nūḥ's two prayers (v.26–28) — leave no dweller of the disbelievers; forgive me and my parents and the believers."],
  traps:["غَفَّارًا (v.10) vs كَفَّارًا (v.27) — ONE LETTER apart, same faʿʿāl pattern — Allah endlessly forgiving / them endlessly ungrateful.","اسْتَكْبَرُوا (v.7) vs اسْتَغْفِرُوا (v.10) — SAME Form X, opposite meanings — the disease and the cure, three verses apart.","أَنبَتَكُم... نَبَاتًا (v.17) — MIXED forms (Form IV verb + Form I noun) — deliberate, like Muzzammil 73:8. But v.18 yukhrijukum ikhrājā MATCHES.","نُورًا / سِرَاجًا (v.16) — moon = REFLECTED light; sun = a burning LAMP. Never swapped.","فَأُدْخِلُوا (v.25) — the fa = IMMEDIATELY — classical evidence for punishment in the BARZAKH.","يَغُوثَ / يَعُوقَ (v.23) — DIPTOTES (verb-shaped names) — no tanwīn, unlike Waddan, Suwāʿan, Nasran.","يَا قَوْمِ · أَطِيعُونِ · رَبِّ (v.2, 3, 5) — the \"my/me\" yāʾ is DROPPED (kasrah remains).","مِّمَّا (v.25) — the mā is EXTRA (emphasis) — \"because of their sins — their very sins.\" And it's FRONTED.","دَيَّارًا (v.26) — used ONLY in negation — \"not a single dweller.\"","يُمْدِدْكُم (v.12) — unmerged doubled jussive (like tamnun, 74:6).","يُرْسِلِ / يَغْفِرْ / يُمْدِدْ / يَجْعَلْ (v.4, 11–12) — all JUSSIVE — the answer to the commands (jawāb aṭ-ṭalab).","تَبَارًا (v.28) — t-b-r (ruin), NOT t-b-b (tabbat, Masad 111:1).","وَالِدَيَّ (v.28) — dual + \"my\" — the yāʾs merge with a shaddah. And baytiya — yāʾ with fatḥah.","وَقَارًا (v.13) — from waqr (HEAVINESS) — same root as waqr = DEAFNESS. And tarjūna in negation may mean FEAR.","كُبَّارًا (v.22) — fuʿʿāl — rarer and stronger than kubār and kabīr.","نُوحًا (v.1) — foreign but FULLY DECLINED (three letters, quiet middle — like ʿĀdun).","وَلَدُهُ (v.21) — recitation variant wulduhu.","أَجَلٍ مُّسَمًّى (v.4a) vs أَجَلَ اللَّهِ (v.4b) — two different \"terms\" — one belief can earn; one nothing delays."],
  links:[{w:"The whole surah",e:"طَغَى الْمَاءُ... الْجَارِيَةِ (Ḥāqqah 69:11)",t:"Ḥāqqah: the flood in one verse; Nūḥ: the 950 years before it"},{w:"أَنذِرْ (v.1)",e:"قُمْ فَأَنذِرْ (Muddaththir 74:2)",t:"First and last messengers — same first command"},{w:"يَا قَوْمِ (v.2) · أَطِيعُونِ (v.3)",e:"دِينِ (Kāfirūn 109:6) · يَسْرِ (Fajr 89:4)",t:"Dropped yāʾ"},{w:"مُّبِينٌ (v.2)",e:"الْبَيِّنَةُ (98:1) · الْمُبِينِ (81:23)",t:"Clarity = separating"},{w:"اتَّقُوهُ (v.3)",e:"تَتَّقُونَ (Muzzammil 73:17) · التَّقْوَىٰ (74:56)",t:"Taqwā = armour"},{w:"ذُنُوبِكُمْ (v.4)",e:"Dhanab (tail)",t:"Sin trails behind like a tail"},{w:"أَجَلٍ مُّسَمًّى / أَجَلَ اللَّهِ (v.4)",e:"الْعَاجِلَةَ (Qiyāmah 75:20)",t:"The named term / the final term"},{w:"لَوْ كُنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ (v.4)",e:"لَوْ تَعْلَمُونَ (Takāthur 102:5)",t:"Deleted answer"},{w:"لَيْلًا وَنَهَارًا (v.5)",e:"Muzzammil 73:2, 7",t:"Night and day"},{w:"دَعَوْتُ (v.5)",e:"تَدْعُو (Maʿārij 70:17)",t:"Nūḥ calls to Paradise; the Fire calls at the end"},{w:"فِرَارًا (v.6)",e:"فَرَّتْ (Muddaththir 74:51) · الْمَفَرّ (75:10)",t:"Fleeing the call — then and now"},{w:"زَادَ... إِلَّا ×4 (v.6, 21, 24, 28)",e:"فَزَادُوهُمْ (72:6) · نَزِيدَكُمْ (78:30)",t:"Fleeing → loss → straying → ruin"},{w:"أَصَابِعَهُمْ فِي آذَانِهِمْ (v.7)",e:"أُذُنٌ وَاعِيَةٌ (Ḥāqqah 69:12)",t:"Blocked ears / retaining ear"},{w:"اسْتَغْشَوْا ثِيَابَهُمْ (v.7)",e:"الْغَاشِيَةِ (88:1) · ثِيَابَكَ فَطَهِّرْ (74:4)",t:"Self-covering → enveloped; the Prophet ﷺ threw his off"},{w:"اسْتَكْبَرُوا (v.7) · اسْتَغْفِرُوا (v.10)",e:"اسْتَكْبَرَ (74:23) · اسْتَغْفِرْهُ (110:3)",t:"Form X: disease and cure, one surah"},{w:"أَصَرُّوا (v.7)",e:"Iṣrār vs istighfār",t:"Persistence vs forgiveness"},{w:"جِهَارًا (v.8)",e:"ṣalāh jahriyyah",t:"Audible prayer"},{w:"أَسْرَرْتُ (v.9)",e:"السَّرَائِرُ (Ṭāriq 86:9) · مَسْرُورًا (84:9)",t:"Secret / joy — one root"},{w:"غَفَّارًا (v.10) · كَفَّارًا (v.27)",e:"تَوَّابًا (Naṣr 110:3)",t:"GH vs K — one letter, the surah's two poles"},{w:"مِّدْرَارًا (v.11)",e:"غَدَقًا (Jinn 72:16) · ثَجَّاجًا (78:14)",t:"Rain for uprightness / istighfār"},{w:"يُمْدِدْكُم بِأَمْوَالٍ وَبَنِينَ (v.12)",e:"مَالًا مَّمْدُودًا... بَنِينَ (Muddaththir 74:12–13)",t:"Promised vs given — same m-d-d, same pair"},{w:"وَقَارًا (v.13)",e:"وَقْرٌ (41:5) · v.7",t:"Heavy ears, weightless God"},{w:"مَا لَكُمْ (v.13)",e:"مَا لَهَا (99:3) · فَمَا لَهُمْ (74:49) · فَمَالِ (70:36)",t:"\"What's wrong?\""},{w:"أَطْوَارًا (v.14)",e:"عَلَقٍ (96:2) · أَمْشَاجٍ (76:2) · فَقَدَّرَهُ (80:19)",t:"All the stages in one word"},{w:"طِبَاقًا (v.15)",e:"طَبَقًا عَن طَبَقٍ (Inshiqāq 84:19)",t:"Your stages / the sky's layers"},{w:"أَلَمْ تَرَوْا كَيْفَ (v.15)",e:"أَلَمْ تَرَ كَيْفَ (89:6 · 105:1)",t:"See how He destroyed / created"},{w:"نُورًا / سِرَاجًا (v.16)",e:"سِرَاجًا وَهَّاجًا (78:13) · تَلَاهَا (91:2)",t:"Reflected light / burning lamp"},{w:"أَنبَتَكُم... نَبَاتًا (v.17)",e:"تَبَتَّلْ... تَبْتِيلًا (Muzzammil 73:8) · فَأَنبَتْنَا (80:27)",t:"Mixed forms; He grows your food / grows YOU"},{w:"يُعِيدُكُمْ... يُخْرِجُكُمْ (v.18)",e:"يُبْدِئُ وَيُعِيدُ (Burūj 85:13) · يَخْرُجُونَ (70:43)",t:"Resurrection as second sprouting"},{w:"بِسَاطًا (v.19)",e:"مِهَادًا (78:6) · سُطِحَتْ (88:20) · دَحَاهَا (79:30)",t:"Cradle, surface, spread, carpet"},{w:"لِتَسْلُكُوا... فِجَاجًا (v.20)",e:"72:17 · 74:42 · 69:32 · Ḥajj 22:27",t:"Salaka: chains for deniers, wide roads for you"},{w:"عَصَوْنِي (v.21)",e:"أَطِيعُونِ (v.3) · 79:21 · 69:10",t:"Obey → disobeyed"},{w:"خَسَارًا (v.21)",e:"خُسْرٍ (ʿAṣr 103:2)",t:"Loss"},{w:"مَكْرًا كُبَّارًا (v.22)",e:"كَيْدًا (86:15) · كَيْدَهُمْ (105:2)",t:"The bigger the plot, the bigger the fall"},{w:"لَا تَذَرُنَّ... لَا تَذَرْ (v.23, 26–27)",e:"ذَرْنِي (73:11 · 74:11) · فَذَرْهُمْ (70:42) · لَا تَذَرُ (74:28)",t:"The dhar thread — thrown back"},{w:"آلِهَتَكُمْ (v.23)",e:"إِلَٰهِ النَّاسِ (114:3) · أَحَدٌ (112:1)",t:"Many gods / One"},{w:"وَدًّا (v.23)",e:"الْوَدُودُ (Burūj 85:14) · يَوَدُّ (70:11)",t:"An idol named LOVE"},{w:"الظَّالِمِينَ (v.24, 28)",e:"ẓulmah (darkness)",t:"Injustice = darkness"},{w:"ضَلَالًا (v.24)",e:"ضَالًّا (Ḍuḥā 93:7) · تَضْلِيلٍ (105:2)",t:"Ḍalla"},{w:"خَطِيئَاتِهِمْ (v.25)",e:"الْخَاطِئَةِ (69:9) · خَاطِئَةٍ (96:16)",t:"Deliberate sins"},{w:"أُغْرِقُوا (v.25)",e:"غَرْقًا (Nāziʿāt 79:1)",t:"Drowning"},{w:"فَأُدْخِلُوا نَارًا (v.25)",e:"فَادْخُلِي (89:29–30) · يُدْخَلَ (70:38)",t:"Forced vs invited — and the barzakh"},{w:"أَنصَارًا (v.25)",e:"نَاصِرٍ (86:10) · نَاصِرًا (72:24) · نَصْرُ (110:1)",t:"No helpers vs Allah's help"},{w:"دَيَّارًا (v.26)",e:"أَحَدٍ (Ikhlāṣ 112:4 · Ḥāqqah 69:47)",t:"Negation-only words"},{w:"فَاجِرًا (v.27)",e:"75:5 · 76:6 · 80:42 · 82:3, 14 · 89:1 · 91:8 · 97:5",t:"F-j-r — NINE appearances"},{w:"يَلِدُوا / وَالِدَيَّ (v.27–28)",e:"لَمْ يَلِدْ (112:3) · وَالِدٍ (90:3)",t:"Wicked offspring / blessed parents"},{w:"رَبِّ اغْفِرْ لِي وَلِوَالِدَيَّ (v.28)",e:"Ibrāhīm 14:41 · وَاسْتَغْفِرْهُ (Naṣr 110:3)",t:"Every great mission ends in istighfār"},{w:"بَيْتِيَ (v.28)",e:"هَٰذَا الْبَيْتِ (Quraysh 106:3)",t:"The house"},{w:"تَبَارًا (v.28)",e:"تَبَّتْ (Masad 111:1)",t:"Look-alike roots"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"إِنَّا أَرْسَلْنَا نُوحًا إِلَىٰ قَوْمِهِ", tr:"Innā arsalnā nūḥan ilā qawmih", v:"1", root:"Arsalnā — We SENT (rasūl = messenger; risālah = a message).", meaning:"\"Indeed, We sent Nūḥ to his people…\"", magic:"The surah named after a man who preached for 950 years (fa-labitha fīhim alfa sanatin illā khamsīna ʿāmā, 29:14). And you met him in the previous surahs' shadow: Ḥāqqah 69:11.", grammar:"Innā = \"indeed We\" (majestic plural + emphasis).", why:"Opening with Allah's own sending — establishing that the 950-year mission was His.", note:"NŪḤ — 950 YEARS of preaching (29:14). Ḥāqqah 69:11 gave you his FLOOD and SHIP in one verse; Sūrat Nūḥ gives the 950 years before it.", more:["Nūḥ — the prophet NŪḤ (peace be upon him) — a non-Arabic name, but fully declined (Nūḥan) because it has three letters with a quiet middle.","qawm — a PEOPLE / nation (from qāma — those who stand together; qawmī = NATIONAL; qawmiyyah = NATIONALISM).","Innā lammā ṭagha'l-māʾu ḥamalnākum fi'l-jāriyah — \"when the WATER overflowed, We carried you in the SHIP\" — that was HIS flood, HIS ark.","Ḥāqqah gave you the flood in one verse; Nūḥ gives you the 950 years that led to it.","Ilā qawmihi — \"to HIS people\" — his own people, his own family and tribe.","The hardest audience: those who knew him before he was a prophet — exactly the Prophet's ﷺ situation with Quraysh.","This surah is comfort: Nūḥ endured 950 years; your 13 in Makkah are not in vain.","Qawm from qāma (to STAND) — a people = those who STAND together.","Nūḥan — fully declined despite being foreign (three letters, quiet middle — like ʿĀdun, Ḥāqqah 69:4).","Ilā qawmihi = to HIS OWN people — like the Prophet ﷺ with Quraysh.","Qawm ← qāma (STAND together); qawmiyyah = NATIONALISM."]},
  {ar:"أَنْ أَنذِرْ قَوْمَكَ مِن قَبْلِ أَن يَأْتِيَهُمْ عَذَابٌ أَلِيمٌ", tr:"An andhir qawmaka min qabli an yaʾtiyahum ʿadhābun alīm", v:"1", root:"Andhir — WARN! (indhār = an ALARM; Muddaththir 74:2 — qum fa-andhir!).", meaning:"\"…[saying]: 'Warn your people before a painful punishment comes to them.'\"", magic:"Muddaththir 74:2 — qum fa-andhir — the Prophet ﷺ's first public command: \"rise and WARN.\" Nūḥ 71:1 — an andhir qawmak.", grammar:"An = an at-tafsīriyyah (the \"namely / saying\" an, explaining the content of the sending).", why:"Indhār — the alarm-call shared by the first and last messengers.", note:"Muddaththir 74:2 qum fa-ANDHIR (Prophet ﷺ) = Nūḥ 71:1 an ANDHIR qawmak (Nūḥ). First and last messengers — same first command.", more:["alīm — the \"PAIN\" family — alima = to feel pain; alam = PAIN (everyday!); alīm = PAINFUL (faʿīl — intense).","Nūḥ's first command: \"WARN your people.\" The first and the last (major) messengers — given the same first word.","THE SAME FIRST COMMAND AS MUDDATHTHIR.","Min qabli an yaʾtiyahum — \"BEFORE it comes\" — a warning is only useful BEFORE.","The urgency is in the timing.","ʿAdhābun alīm — a PAINFUL punishment — Muzzammil 73:13 — wa ʿadhāban alīmā.","Alam = PAIN — your free hook.","andhir = Form IV imperative.","Min qabli = BEFORE — a warning is only useful before."]},
  {ar:"قَالَ يَا قَوْمِ إِنِّي لَكُمْ نَذِيرٌ مُّبِينٌ", tr:"Qāla yā qawmi innī lakum nadhīrun mubīn", v:"2", root:"Yā qawmi — \"O my people!\" (the possessive yāʾ — qawmī — dropped: yā qawmi). nadhīr — a WARNER.", meaning:"\"He said: 'O my people, indeed I am a clear warner to you.'\"", magic:"YĀ QAWMI — \"O MY people\" — with the possessive \"my\" — tender, familial.", grammar:"Yā qawmi = vocative with dropped possessive yāʾ (kasrah remains as the trace).", why:"Yā qawmi — a warner who never stopped calling them his own.", note:"YĀ QAWMI = \"O MY people\" — 950 years, and he still called them HIS.", more:["mubīn — the \"CLARITY / making distinct\" family — bāna = to become clear; mubīn = CLEAR, making things plain (bayn = between; Takwīr 81:23 al-ufuq al-mubīn; Bayyinah 98:1 al-bayyinah!).","He doesn't say \"O disbelievers!\" (as in Kāfirūn 109:1) — he says \"O MY PEOPLE.\" For 950 years, he called them HIS.","And the dropped yāʾ (qawmi not qawmī) — a common feature in vocative address, softening the sound (like dīn[ī], Kāfirūn 109:6). Nadhīrun MUBĪN — a CLEAR warner — mubīn from bayn (BETWEEN) — clarity = separating truth from falsehood (Bayyinah 98:1). Nothing vague, nothing hidden: plain warning.","And compare Ḥāqqah 69:41–42 — not a poet, not a soothsayer (whose speech was VAGUE) — a prophet's warning is mubīn.","lakum fronted (\"to you [specifically]\").","Not \"O disbelievers\" (109:1). The possessive yāʾ is DROPPED (kasrah trace) — like dīn[ī] (109:6). MUBĪN ← bayn (between) — Bayyinah 98:1. Clear — unlike the soothsayers' riddles (69:42)."]},
  {ar:"أَنِ اعْبُدُوا اللَّهَ وَاتَّقُوهُ وَأَطِيعُونِ", tr:"Ani'ʿbudu'llāha wa'ttaqūhu wa aṭīʿūn", v:"3", root:"Uʿbudū — WORSHIP / serve (ʿabd). ittaqūhu — SHIELD yourselves [from His displeasure] (taqwā ← wiqāyah — ARMOUR).", meaning:"\"[Saying]: 'Worship Allah, fear Him, and obey me.'\"", magic:"THREE COMMANDS — the whole message in three words: (1) uʿbudū'llāh — WORSHIP Allah (the relationship); (2) ittaqūhu — SHIELD yourselves (the inner guard); (3) aṭīʿūn — OBEY ME (the practical following of the messenger).", grammar:"An = tafsīriyyah again. aṭīʿūni — possessive/object yāʾ dropped (aṭīʿūnī). Three imperatives joined by wa.", why:"The three-part creed: worship, guard, follow.", note:"THREE COMMANDS: WORSHIP Allah · SHIELD yourselves (taqwā) · OBEY me. Every prophet's message.", more:["aṭīʿūn — the \"OBEDIENCE / compliance\" family — ṭāʿa = to obey; ṭāʿah = OBEDIENCE; istiṭāʿah = ABILITY / capacity (mustaṭāʿ = possible!); taṭawwuʿ = VOLUNTEERING (doing more than obliged — ṣalāt at-taṭawwuʿ = voluntary prayer!).","Belief, God-consciousness, and following the messenger — the same three pillars every prophet brought.","Aṭīʿū[nī] — the hidden yāʾ!","The full form is aṭīʿūnī (\"obey ME\") — but the yāʾ is DROPPED in the muṣḥaf, leaving the kasrah — to match the rhyme / for brevity.","You own this feature: dīn[ī] (Kāfirūn 109:6), yasr[i] (Fajr 89:4), qawm[ī] (v.2 just now).","Two dropped yāʾs in two verses.","Ittaqūhu — the taqwā root (ARMOUR): Muzzammil 73:17 — fa-kayfa tattaqūna in kafartum yawmā (how will you SHIELD yourselves from a Day?).","Muddaththir 74:56 — huwa ahlu't-taqwā (He is worthy of being shielded from).","Istiṭāʿah = ABILITY — obedience (ṭāʿah) and ability share a root: you obey what you're ABLE to.","Aṭīʿū[nī] — the \"ME\" yāʾ is DROPPED (like qawm[ī], v.2).","Istiṭāʿah = ABILITY; taṭawwuʿ = VOLUNTEERING; ṣalāt at-taṭawwuʿ = VOLUNTARY prayer."]},
  {ar:"يَغْفِرْ لَكُم مِّن ذُنُوبِكُمْ", tr:"Yaghfir lakum min dhunūbikum", v:"4", root:"Yaghfir — He will FORGIVE / cover (mighfar = a HELMET).", meaning:"\"He will forgive you some of your sins…\"", magic:"DHANB (SIN) shares a root with DHANAB (TAIL). Why? Classical: a sin is like a TAIL — it trails BEHIND you, following you wherever you go — its consequences drag after the deed.", grammar:"Yaghfir = JUSSIVE (answer of a command — jawāb aṭ-ṭalab). min = partitive or emphatic (debated).", why:"Dhanb — sin pictured as a tail that follows; forgiveness as its removal.", note:"DHANB (SIN) ← DHANAB (TAIL) — a sin TRAILS BEHIND you like a tail. Forgiveness (mighfar = HELMET) covers it.", more:["dhunūb — the \"TAIL / what follows behind\" family — dhanab = a TAIL!; dhanb = a SIN (classically: a sin is something that FOLLOWS you like a tail — its consequences trail behind you!); mudhnib = a sinner; dhanab also = the end / last part.","Forgiveness (maghfirah — from mighfar, a HELMET) COVERS it — cuts off the tail.","Min dhunūbikum — \"SOME of your sins\" / \"FROM your sins\" — classical readings: (a) min = \"some\" — the sins of the past, before you believed (later sins need fresh repentance); (b) min = extra, emphatic — \"ALL your sins.\" Both classical.","Yaghfir — JUSSIVE (sukūn) — because it's the ANSWER to the commands of v.3 (\"worship, fear, obey [and then] He will forgive\").","The forgiveness is the CONSEQUENCE of obeying.","(Like Maʿārij 70:42 — dharhum yakhūḍū.)","Yaghfir = JUSSIVE — the answer to v.3's commands.","Min = \"some\" or emphatic — both classical."]},
  {ar:"وَيُؤَخِّرْكُمْ إِلَىٰ أَجَلٍ مُّسَمًّى", tr:"Wa yuʾakhkhirkum ilā ajalin musammā", v:"4", root:"Yuʾakhkhir — the \"DELAYING / putting back\" family — akhkhara = to postpone; taʾkhīr = DELAY.", meaning:"\"…and give you respite until a named term.\"", magic:"TWO TERMS — and the difference is the key.", grammar:"Yuʾakhkhirkum — jussive (joined to yaghfir). musamman — passive participle, weak-final (musammā).", why:"Ajal musammā — distinguishes the delay belief earns from the death no one escapes.", note:"TWO AJALs: v.4a a NAMED natural term (belief EARNS it — escape the flood) vs v.4b Allah's FINAL term (NOTHING delays it — death).", more:["Taʾakhkhur = LATENESS (Muddaththir 74:37 yataʾakhkhar!); ākhir = LAST; ākhirah = the Hereafter.","ajal — the \"TERM / deadline / appointed time\" family — ajal = an appointed TERM, a deadline — and the END of a lifespan (death); ājil = DEFERRED / later (ʿājil vs ājil = now vs later — ad-dunyā ʿājilah wa'l-ākhirah ājilah!); taʾjīl = POSTPONEMENT.","musammā — NAMED, specified (ism = a name; tasmiyah = naming / saying bismillāh!).","v.4a — wa yuʾakhkhirkum ilā ajalin musammā — \"He will DELAY you to a NAMED term\" — i.e., you'll live out your natural lifespan, not be destroyed early by punishment.","v.4b — inna ajala'llāhi idhā jāʾa lā yuʾakhkhar — \"Allah's term, when it comes, will NOT be delayed.\" Two ajals: (1) a NAMED, natural term — which belief can earn you (escaping the early, collective punishment); (2) Allah's FINAL term — death, which NOTHING delays.","Belief can save you from the flood; nothing saves you from death.","Yuʾakhkhir (delay) — the akhkhara root — Muddaththir 74:37 — an yataqaddama aw yataʾakhkhar (to step forward or fall behind).","Qiyāmah 75:13, Infiṭār 82:5 — qaddama wa akhkhara.","Here Allah does the delaying — giving TIME.","Ajal = a DEADLINE — ājil = DEFERRED, ʿājil = IMMEDIATE — the Qurʾān's contrast of this world (al-ʿājilah, 75:20 — bal tuḥibbūna'l-ʿājilah!) and the next.","Musammā — NAMED — the term already has a name; it's written (tasmiyah = saying bismillāh).","ʿĀJIL (now) vs ĀJIL (later) — Qiyāmah 75:20 tuḥibbūna'l-ʿājilah.","Ajal = a DEADLINE; taʾjīl = POSTPONEMENT.","Musammā = NAMED (tasmiyah)."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّ أَجَلَ اللَّهِ إِذَا جَاءَ لَا يُؤَخَّرُ ۖ لَوْ كُنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ", tr:"Inna ajala'llāhi idhā jāʾa lā yuʾakhkhar · law kuntum taʿlamūn", v:"4", root:"Ajal Allāh — Allah's TERM (death / the final appointed time). lā yuʾakhkhar — is NOT DELAYED (passive).", meaning:"\"Indeed, Allah's term, when it comes, is not delayed — if only you knew.\"", magic:"The second ajal — and a deleted answer you know.", grammar:"Inna + subject + idhā jāʾa (condition) + lā yuʾakhkharu (passive present).", why:"Law… taʿlamūn — the deleted answer forces the listener to supply the consequence.", note:"LĀ YUʾAKHKHAR — Allah's term is NOT DELAYED, not by a moment (7:34). LAW KUNTUM TAʿLAMŪN.", more:["law kuntum taʿlamūn — \"IF ONLY you knew\".","Lā yuʾakhkhar — NOT DELAYED — not by a moment (fa-idhā jāʾa ajaluhum lā yastaʾkhirūna sāʿatan wa lā yastaqdimūn, 7:34). Law kuntum taʿlamūn — \"IF ONLY you knew…\" — TAKĀTHUR 102:5 — kallā law taʿlamūna ʿilma'l-yaqīn — \"If only you knew with the knowledge of certainty —\" and the answer is DELETED — the sentence just stops.","Same device here: \"if only you knew [ — you would believe / you would hurry / you would weep].\" The silence is bigger than any filler.","Ajala'LLĀH — \"ALLAH'S term\" — attributed directly to Him — no one negotiates with it.","law — answer DELETED (ḥadhf jawāb ash-sharṭ).","\"IF ONLY you knew —\" ANSWER DELETED, like TAKĀTHUR 102:5. The silence is bigger than any filler."]},
  {ar:"قَالَ رَبِّ إِنِّي دَعَوْتُ قَوْمِي لَيْلًا وَنَهَارًا", tr:"Qāla rabbi innī daʿawtu qawmī laylan wa nahārā", v:"5", root:"Rabbi — \"My Lord!\" (rabbī with the yāʾ dropped — vocative).", meaning:"\"He said: 'My Lord, I have called my people night and day.'\"", magic:"THE REPORT BEGINS — a prophet accounting to his Lord. Rabbi — \"MY Lord\" — intimate, direct. Compare v.2: yā qawmi (O MY people) → v.5: rabbi (MY Lord).", grammar:"Rabbi = vocative with dropped yāʾ (rabbī). laylan wa nahāran = adverbs of time (accusative).", why:"A merism of time — nothing left out.", note:"LAYLAN WA NAHĀRĀ = NIGHT and DAY — a merism: ALL the time. Yā qawmi (v.2, MY people) → rabbi (v.5, MY Lord) — caught between both.", more:["daʿawtu — I CALLED / invited (daʿwah = a call, an invitation — the root of \"daʿwah\"!).","laylan wa nahārā — NIGHT and DAY (a merism = all the time).","He belongs to both — his people and his Lord — and he's caught between them.","Laylan wa nahārā — NIGHT and DAY — a merism (two opposites meaning ALL) — he never stopped.","Muzzammil 73:2, 7 — the night for PRAYER, the day for SWIMMING through tasks (sabḥan ṭawīlā).","Nūḥ used BOTH night and day for daʿwah.","Daʿawtu — the root of DAʿWAH — Maʿārij 70:17 — tadʿū man adbara wa tawallā — the FIRE calls those who turned away.","Nūḥ CALLED them to Paradise for 950 years; the Fire will call them in the end.","Jinn 72:19–20 — qāma ʿabdu'llāhi yadʿūhu… qul innamā adʿū rabbī — the Prophet ﷺ calling (on) his Lord.","Daʿawtu = the root of DAʿWAH — Nūḥ called them 950 years; the FIRE calls them at the end (70:17). Rabbi = rabbī, yāʾ dropped."]},
  {ar:"فَلَمْ يَزِدْهُمْ دُعَائِي إِلَّا فِرَارًا", tr:"Fa-lam yazidhum duʿāʾī illā firārā", v:"6", root:"Yazidhum — INCREASE them (zāda; jussive after lam — the long ī shortens: yazīd → yazid).", meaning:"\"But my calling only increased them in flight.\"", magic:"THE TRAGIC IRONY — the more he called, the faster they ran. Not \"my calling didn't work\" — \"it INCREASED their fleeing.\" The call itself drove them away.", grammar:"Lam yazid — jussive of a hollow verb: the long vowel shortens (yazīdu → yazid).", why:"Firār — makes their rejection active flight, not passive indifference.", note:"The more he CALLED, the more they FLED — the call itself drove them away.", more:["duʿāʾ — my CALLING (here = calling them, not supplication).","firār — the \"FLEEING in panic\" family — farra = to flee; mafarr = an escape (Qiyāmah 75:10!); ʿAbasa 80:34 — yawma yafirru'l-marʾ; Muddaththir 74:51 — farrat min qaswarah!; firār = flight / desertion (modern: firār = ESCAPE — from prison!).","And firār — FLEEING — you own it from Muddaththir 74:50–51: ka-annahum ḥumurun mustanfirah · farrat min qaswarah — \"like WILD DONKEYS bolting, FLEEING from a lion\" — the Quraysh fleeing the Prophet's ﷺ reminder.","Nūḥ's people FLED from his call (71:6); Quraysh FLED from the reminder (74:51). The same reaction, separated by thousands of years.","The comfort to the Prophet ﷺ: this has always happened.","THE \"ZĀDA… ILLĀ\" PATTERN — appearing FOUR times in this surah: v.6 — lam yazidhum duʿāʾī illā firārā (my call only increased them in FLEEING) v.21 — lam yazidhu māluhu wa waladuhu illā khasārā (his wealth only increased him in LOSS) v.24 — wa lā tazidi'ẓ-ẓālimīna illā ḍalālā (increase them only in STRAYING) v.28 — wa lā tazidi'ẓ-ẓālimīna illā tabārā (increase them only in RUIN).","Fleeing → loss → straying → ruin.","The surah's whole tragedy in one repeated structure.","And Jinn 72:6 — fa-zādūhum rahaqā (the false refuge only increased them in burden); Nabaʾ 78:30 — fa-lan nazīdakum illā ʿadhābā.","Hook: firār = an ESCAPE (from prison).","illā = restriction (\"nothing but\").","Nūḥ 71:6 FIRĀRĀ = Muddaththir 74:51 FARRAT (donkeys fleeing a lion).","Same reaction, thousands of years apart.","ZĀDA… ILLĀ ×4: FLEEING (v.6) → LOSS (v.21) → STRAYING (v.24) → RUIN (v.28).","Firār = an ESCAPE from prison."]},
  {ar:"وَإِنِّي كُلَّمَا دَعَوْتُهُمْ لِتَغْفِرَ لَهُمْ", tr:"Wa innī kullamā daʿawtuhum li-taghfira lahum", v:"7", root:"Kullamā — \"EVERY TIME / whenever\" (kull + mā). li-taghfira lahum — \"so that YOU might forgive them\".", meaning:"\"And indeed, every time I called them so that You might forgive them…\"", magic:"LOOK AT THE PURPOSE: li-taghfira lahum — \"so that YOU might FORGIVE them.\" He didn't call them to win an argument, or to gain followers, or for his own honour — he called them so ALLAH would FORGIVE them.", grammar:"Kullamā = \"whenever\" (repetition). li- + subjunctive (taghfira) = purpose.", why:"Li-taghfira — reveals the motive behind the warning: forgiveness, not condemnation.", note:"WHY did he call? LI-TAGHFIRA LAHUM — \"so that YOU might FORGIVE them.\" 950 years of MERCY. Kullamā = EVERY SINGLE TIME.", more:["The whole 950 years were an act of mercy toward them.","And they responded by blocking their ears (next).","Kullamā — \"EVERY SINGLE TIME\" — not once; every time, without exception, the same reaction.","And notice: he speaks TO ALLAH (taghfira — \"YOU forgive\") — the report is a conversation.","2nd person (taghfira) — addressing Allah."]},
  {ar:"جَعَلُوا أَصَابِعَهُمْ فِي آذَانِهِمْ", tr:"Jaʿalū aṣābiʿahum fī ādhānihim", v:"7", root:"Aṣābiʿ — FINGERS (plural of iṣbaʿ; buṣmat al-iṣbaʿ = a FINGERPRINT!).", meaning:"\"…they put their fingers in their ears…\"", magic:"THE EARS — and the contrast with Ḥāqqah is the best in this batch.", grammar:"Jaʿalū = \"they placed.\" aṣābiʿ — majāz mursal (the whole named for the part — fingers for fingertips).", why:"Aṣābiʿ — the hyperbole shows a frantic refusal to hear.", note:"Ḥāqqah 69:12: the ear that RETAINS (udhunun wāʿiyah). Nūḥ 71:7: FINGERS IN EARS — BLOCKED. Same organ, opposite choices.", more:["ādhān — EARS (plural of udhun) — the root of adhān (the call to prayer!), idhn (permission — Qadr 97:4), udhunun wāʿiyah (the retaining ear — Ḥāqqah 69:12!).","Ḥāqqah 69:12 — wa taʿiyahā udhunun wāʿiyah — \"so that a RETAINING EAR might hold it\" — an ear like a VESSEL, open and keeping.","Nūḥ 71:7 — jaʿalū aṣābiʿahum fī ādhānihim — \"they put their FINGERS IN THEIR EARS\" — ears PLUGGED.","Two surahs apart: the ear that RETAINS vs the ear that's BLOCKED.","Same organ, opposite choices.","Aṣābiʿ — FINGERS — but you can't fit a whole finger in your ear!","Classical: this is majāz — \"fingers\" for \"FINGERTIPS\" — the whole is named for the part, to show how FORCEFULLY they jammed them in, as if they'd push the entire finger in.","Exaggeration that reveals their desperation not to hear.","Udhun → ADHĀN — the call to prayer you hear five times a day.","They blocked their ears to the call; Muslims rise to the adhān.","Hook: buṣmat al-iṣbaʿ = a FINGERPRINT (and Qiyāmah 75:4 — ʿalā an nusawwiya banānah — restoring the FINGERTIPS!).","\"FINGERS\" for FINGERTIPS — majāz: they JAMMED them in.","Udhun → ADHĀN (call to prayer), idhn (permission).","Buṣmat al-iṣbaʿ = a FINGERPRINT (Qiyāmah 75:4 banān)."]},
  {ar:"وَاسْتَغْشَوْا ثِيَابَهُمْ", tr:"Wa'staghshaw thiyābahum", v:"7", root:"Istaghshaw — Form X of ghashiya = to COVER oneself completely, to wrap oneself up.", meaning:"\"…and wrapped themselves in their garments…\"", magic:"They didn't just block their EARS — they covered their EYES and FACES too.", grammar:"Form X (istaghshā) = seeking / doing to oneself — weak-final, plural (istaghshayū → istaghshaw).", why:"Istaghshā — the root of al-Ghāshiyah, so their self-covering foreshadows their enveloping.", note:"ISTAGHSHAW = they WRAPPED themselves — fingers in EARS + cloaks over EYES = total shutdown.", more:["The \"COVERING / enveloping\" family — al-Ghāshiyah = the ENVELOPER (Sūrah 88!); Layl 92:1 — wa'l-layli idhā yaghshā (the night when it COVERS); Shams 91:4 — yaghshāhā; ghishāwah = a COVERING over the eyes (2:7); ghashsh = CHEATING (covering the truth!).","thiyāb — GARMENTS (Muddaththir 74:4 — wa thiyābaka fa-ṭahhir!).","Istaghshaw — Form X = they SOUGHT to cover themselves — pulled their cloaks over their heads — classically: so they wouldn't SEE Nūḥ, wouldn't HEAR him, and so he wouldn't recognize them.","Total sensory shutdown: fingers in ears (hearing), garments over heads (sight).","And the gh-sh-y root — the root of AL-GHĀSHIYAH: Ghāshiyah 88:1 — hal atāka ḥadīthu'l-ghāshiyah — the ENVELOPING Day.","Layl 92:1 — wa'l-layli idhā yaghshā — the night that COVERS.","Nūḥ 71:7 — istaghshaw thiyābahum — they COVERED themselves.","They wrapped themselves to escape the warning (71:7); the Enveloping Day (al-Ghāshiyah) will wrap THEM (88:1). Self-covering now, being covered then.","AND THE GARMENT CONTRAST — Muddaththir!","Muddaththir 74:1, 4 — yā ayyuha'l-muddaththir… wa thiyābaka fa-ṭahhir — the Prophet ﷺ was COVERED in a cloak — and told to RISE and PURIFY his garments.","Nūḥ 71:7 — istaghshaw thiyābahum — Nūḥ's people COVERED themselves in their garments — to HIDE from the truth.","The Prophet ﷺ threw off his covering to WARN; they pulled on theirs to AVOID the warning.","Same garments, opposite purposes.","Hook: ghashsh = CHEATING (covering the truth); ghishāwah = a VEIL over the eyes.","Gh-sh-y = AL-GHĀSHIYAH (88:1): they covered themselves now → the Enveloping Day covers them then.","Muddaththir 74:1, 4: the Prophet ﷺ THREW OFF his cloak to WARN.","Nūḥ 71:7: they PULLED ON theirs to AVOID."]},
  {ar:"وَأَصَرُّوا وَاسْتَكْبَرُوا اسْتِكْبَارًا", tr:"Wa aṣarrū wa'stakbarū'stikbārā", v:"7", root:"Aṣarrū — the \"PERSISTING / insisting stubbornly\" family — aṣarra = to persist, to insist.", meaning:"\"…and persisted, and grew arrogant with [great] arrogance.\"", magic:"THE FOUR RESPONSES complete: (1) fingers in ears, (2) cloaks over heads, (3) PERSISTED, (4) ARROGANCE.", grammar:"Aṣarrū = Form IV, doubled root (ṣ-r-r).", why:"Iṣrār + istikbār — the twin roots of refusal: stubbornness and pride.", note:"FOUR RESPONSES: fingers in EARS, cloaks over HEADS, PERSISTED, ARROGANCE.", more:["Iṣrār = PERSISTENCE / INSISTENCE / DETERMINATION (modern: iṣrār = determination — positive or negative!); al-iṣrār ʿala'dh-dhanb = PERSISTING in sin (a fiqh/akhlāq term!).","istakbarū — Form X of kabura = to CONSIDER ONESELF GREAT (Muddaththir 74:23 — thumma adbara wa'stakbar!; Iblīs: abā wa'stakbara, 2:34).","Aṣarrū — PERSISTED — the ḥadīth/saying: \"No sin is minor if PERSISTED in (maʿa'l-iṣrār), and no sin is major with istighfār.\" And v.10 is ISTIGHFĀR!","Iṣrār (persistence, v.7) vs istighfār (seeking forgiveness, v.10) — the two roads, three verses apart.","Istakbarū'STIKBĀRĀ — the cognate accusative — \"grew arrogant with an ARROGANCE\" — Arabic's underline: TOTAL arrogance.","The Form X ego family: istaghnā (I need nothing — 80:5, 92:8, 96:7), istakthara (seek more — 74:6), istakbara (I'm great — 74:23, 71:7). vs istaghfara (I need forgiveness — 110:3, 73:20, 71:10). Nūḥ's people: istakbarū (v.7).","Nūḥ's cure: istaghfirū (v.10).","Same form, opposite souls — in one surah.","And Muddaththir 74:23 — thumma adbara wa'stakbar — al-Walīd — the same verb across the ages.","istakbarū'stikbāran = Form X + cognate accusative (maf'ūl muṭlaq) = maximum emphasis.","IṢRĀR (persistence, v.7) vs ISTIGHFĀR (forgiveness, v.10) — \"No sin is minor with iṣrār, none major with istighfār.\" ISTAKBARŪ (v.7) vs ISTAGHFIRŪ (v.10) — same Form X, opposite souls, one surah.","Muddaththir 74:23 wa'stakbar."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ إِنِّي دَعَوْتُهُمْ جِهَارًا", tr:"Thumma innī daʿawtuhum jihārā", v:"8", root:"The \"OPENNESS / loudness / publicity\" family — jahara = to make public, to speak aloud.", meaning:"\"Then I called them openly.\"", magic:"Private calling failed (v.7) → thumma → PUBLIC calling (v.8). Jihārā — openly, loudly, in the gathering places — no more one-on-one.", grammar:"Thumma = sequence + escalation (tarākhī). jihāran = maf'ūl muṭlaq or ḥāl (\"openly\").", why:"Jihār — the shift from private to public — a new tactic after the old one failed.", note:"THUMMA = a CHANGE OF STRATEGY: private failed → JIHĀRĀ (OPENLY). ṢALĀH JAHRIYYAH = AUDIBLE prayers (Fajr, Maghrib, ʿIshāʾ).", more:["Jahr = LOUDNESS (ṣalāh jahriyyah = an AUDIBLE prayer — Fajr, Maghrib, ʿIshāʾ, recited aloud!); jihāran = OPENLY, publicly; mijhar = a MICROSCOPE (makes things visible!); jahwarī = (a voice) LOUD / resonant.","THUMMA — \"THEN\" — a CHANGE OF STRATEGY.","Ṣalāh jahriyyah — the AUDIBLE prayers — Fajr, Maghrib, ʿIshāʾ, where the imam recites ALOUD — same root.","Mijhar = a MICROSCOPE — makes the hidden visible.","The thumma-escalation (v.8, v.9): (1) night and day (v.5) → (2) thumma — OPENLY (v.8) → (3) thumma — openly AND secretly (v.9).","Every method, in sequence.","Compare Muddaththir 74:21–23's thummas — the man producing a slander step by step.","Nūḥ's thummas: a prophet trying every door.","Nūḥ's thummas: a prophet trying every door.","Muddaththir 74:21–23's thummas: a man building a lie."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ إِنِّي أَعْلَنتُ لَهُمْ وَأَسْرَرْتُ لَهُمْ إِسْرَارًا", tr:"Thumma innī aʿlantu lahum wa asrartu lahum isrārā", v:"9", root:"Aʿlantu — the \"ANNOUNCING / making public\" family — ʿalana = to become public.", meaning:"\"Then I announced to them publicly, and I confided to them privately.\"", magic:"Aʿlantu — PUBLIC announcement (iʿlān = an ADVERTISEMENT!). Asrartu… isrārā — PRIVATE, secret, whispered — one-on-one counsel, in confidence.", grammar:"Two Form IV verbs (aʿlana, asarra) — ṭibāq. isrāran = cognate accusative.", why:"Iʿlān / isrār — every possible manner of address, exhausted.", note:"PUBLIC (aʿlantu) + PRIVATE (asrartu isrārā) — every channel tried. Time: night/day (v.5). Manner: open/secret (v.8–9).", more:["Iʿlān = an ANNOUNCEMENT / an ADVERTISEMENT (everyday!); ʿalanan = publicly; ʿalāniyah = openness.","asrartu — the \"SECRET\" family — sarra = to conceal; sirr = a SECRET; sarāʾir = SECRETS (Ṭāriq 86:9 — yawma tublā's-sarāʾir!); isrār = speaking secretly / confiding; sirrī = CONFIDENTIAL; surūr = JOY (same letters, s-r-r!","— Inshiqāq 84:9 masrūrā!).","THE THIRD STRATEGY — BOTH at once: PUBLIC and PRIVATE.","A ṭibāq (opposite pair) — public vs secret — he tried EVERY channel: day/night (time), open/hidden (manner).","A complete da'wah strategy, 950 years old.","Isrārā — the cognate accusative (asrartu… isrārā) — \"confided with real confidentiality\" — genuine, heartfelt private counsel.","And the s-r-r root's DOUBLE life: sirr = SECRET (Ṭāriq 86:9 — the Day SECRETS are tested) and surūr = JOY (Inshiqāq 84:9 — returning to his family masrūrā, JOYFUL!).","Classical link: joy is something felt in the SECRET depths of the heart.","And Ṭāriq 86:9 — yawma tublā's-sarāʾir — \"the Day SECRETS are tested.\" Nūḥ spoke to them in SECRET (isrār); their secrets will be exposed (sarāʾir).","IʿLĀN = an ADVERTISEMENT; SIRRĪ = CONFIDENTIAL.","S-r-r: SIRR (SECRET — Ṭāriq 86:9) & SURŪR (JOY — Inshiqāq 84:9) — joy lives in the heart's secret depths."]},
  {ar:"فَقُلْتُ اسْتَغْفِرُوا رَبَّكُمْ إِنَّهُ كَانَ غَفَّارًا", tr:"Fa-qultu'staghfirū rabbakum innahu kāna ghaffārā", v:"10", root:"Istaghfirū — Form X of ghafara = SEEK covering / forgiveness (mighfar = a HELMET).", meaning:"\"And I said: 'Seek your Lord's forgiveness — indeed, He is ever Forgiving.'\"", magic:"THE HEART OF THE SURAH — and the Form X turnaround. v.7: wa'STAKBARŪ'stikbārā — they grew ARROGANT (Form X — \"I'm great\").", grammar:"Form X imperative (istaghfirū). ghaffāran = faʿʿāl intensive, khabar of kāna.", why:"Ghaffār (endlessly forgiving) — because after 950 years of rejection, the door is STILL open.", note:"V.7 ISTAKBARŪ (arrogance — Form X) → v.10 ISTAGHFIRŪ (forgiveness — Form X). Disease and cure, same form.", more:["ghaffār — faʿʿāl INTENSIVE = ONE WHO FORGIVES AGAIN AND AGAIN, endlessly — the highest of three forgiveness-intensities: ghāfir (one who forgives) → ghafūr (abundantly forgiving) → ghaffār (forgiving over and over without limit); al-Ghaffār = a NAME OF ALLAH; ʿAbd al-Ghaffār (a name!).","v.10: ISTAGHFIRŪ rabbakum — SEEK FORGIVENESS (Form X — \"I need\").","Same form, three verses apart: the disease (istikbār) and the cure (istighfār).","Nūḥ's whole message in one Form X swap.","And the istighfār thread you've built: Naṣr 110:3 — wa'staghfirh innahu kāna tawwābā — VICTORY ends in istighfār.","Muzzammil 73:20 — wa'staghfiru'llāh inna'llāha ghafūrun raḥīm — WORSHIP ends in istighfār.","Nūḥ 71:10 — istaghfirū rabbakum innahu kāna ghaffārā — istighfār as the KEY to PROVISION (v.11–12).","Three surahs: istighfār after victory, after worship, and BEFORE provision.","GHAFFĀR — the faʿʿāl intensive: Naṣr 110:3 kāna TAWWĀBĀ (He turns back again and again) — Nūḥ 71:10 kāna GHAFFĀRĀ (He forgives again and again).","Same structure (innahu kāna + faʿʿāl).","AND THE ONE-LETTER CONTRAST WITHIN THIS SURAH: v.10 GHAFFĀRĀ (Allah — forgiving endlessly) vs v.27 KAFFĀRĀ (the disbelievers — ungrateful endlessly).","GH vs K — one letter, the two poles of the surah.","Kāna — \"He has ALWAYS been\" — permanence: forgiveness is His nature, not a mood.","GHĀFIR → GHAFŪR → GHAFFĀR — three intensities; Ghaffār = endlessly.","v.10 GHAFFĀRĀ (Allah) vs v.27 KAFFĀRĀ (them) — ONE LETTER.","Istighfār: after VICTORY (110:3), after WORSHIP (73:20), before PROVISION (71:10). Naṣr 110:3 kāna tawwābā = 71:10 kāna ghaffārā."]},
  {ar:"يُرْسِلِ السَّمَاءَ عَلَيْكُم مِّدْرَارًا", tr:"Yursili's-samāʾa ʿalaykum midrārā", v:"11", root:"Yursil — He will SEND (Form IV; jussive). as-samāʾ — the SKY — here = RAIN (the sky named for what comes from it).", meaning:"\"He will send the sky [rain] upon you in abundant downpours.\"", magic:"RAIN pictured as MILK flowing from an udder. Midrār — from dirr, flowing MILK — the sky becomes a nursing udder, pouring generously. For a desert people: rain = milk = life.", grammar:"Yursili = JUSSIVE (answer to istaghfirū) — the kasrah is added to avoid two sukūns before as-samāʾ.", why:"Midrār — rain as milk: nourishment, generosity, life.", note:"MIDRĀR ← DIRR = flowing MILK — the sky becomes a nursing UDDER.", more:["midrār — the \"ABUNDANT FLOWING (of milk / rain)\" family — darra = for milk to flow abundantly from the udder; dirr = flowing MILK; midrār = POURING ABUNDANTLY, copious (mifʿāl intensive — rain that keeps coming); lillāhi darruh!","= \"How excellent he is!\" (lit.","\"to Allah belongs his milk-flow!\" — an idiom of praise!); mudirr li'l-bawl = a DIURETIC (clinical — \"causing flow\"!).","And the cross-surah promise: Jinn 72:16 — wa an lawi'staqāmū ʿala'ṭ-ṭarīqati la-asqaynāhum māʾan ghadaqā — \"if they stood upright on the Way, We'd give them ABUNDANT water.\" Nūḥ 71:11 — istaghfirū… yursili's-samāʾa ʿalaykum midrārā — \"seek forgiveness… He'll send ABUNDANT rain.\" Two surahs, one law: uprightness and istighfār → RAIN.","Ghadaq (72:16) and midrār (71:11) — two words for abundant water.","And Nabaʾ 78:14 — wa anzalnā mina'l-muʿṣirāti māʾan thajjājā (rain pouring in torrents).","Rain words: thajjāj (torrents), ghadaq (copious), midrār (flowing like milk).","Clinical hook: mudirr li'l-bawl = a DIURETIC — \"causing flow.\"","midrāran = ḥāl (mifʿāl intensive — usable for masculine and feminine).","Jinn 72:16 ghadaqā (upright → ABUNDANT water) = Nūḥ 71:11 midrārā (istighfār → ABUNDANT rain).","Rain words: thajjāj (78:14), ghadaq (72:16), midrār (71:11). Lillāhi darruh!","= \"How excellent!\"; mudirr = a DIURETIC."]},
  {ar:"وَيُمْدِدْكُم بِأَمْوَالٍ وَبَنِينَ وَيَجْعَل لَّكُمْ جَنَّاتٍ وَيَجْعَل لَّكُمْ أَنْهَارًا", tr:"Wa yumdidkum bi-amwālin wa banīna wa yajʿal lakum jannātin wa yajʿal lakum anhārā", v:"12", root:"Yumdid — the \"EXTENDING / SUPPLYING\" family — madda = to extend; amadda (Form IV) = to SUPPLY, reinforce.", meaning:"\"…and supply you with wealth and sons, and make for you gardens, and make for you rivers.\"", magic:"A famous application — al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī: one man complained to him of DROUGHT, another of POVERTY, another of having NO CHILDREN, another that his GARDEN was dry.", grammar:"Yumdidkum — jussive of a DOUBLED verb, UNMERGED (yumdid, not yumidd) — like Muddaththir 74:6 tamnun.", why:"Imdād (supply / extension) — the same root used for al-Walīd's wealth, now offered as a gift for repentance.", note:"FIVE GIFTS for istighfār: RAIN, WEALTH, SONS, GARDENS, RIVERS.", more:["Imdād = SUPPLY (imdādāt = SUPPLIES — military/logistics!; imdād kahrabāʾī = power supply!); madd — tajwīd lengthening; Muddaththir 74:12 — mālan mamdūdā!; Humazah 104:9 — ʿamadin mumaddadah.","anhār — RIVERS (nahr; nahār = DAY — same root?","n-h-r — both = flowing broadness, the day \"flowing\" with light!).","He told every one of them: \"Seek Allah's forgiveness\" — and recited these verses.","FIVE GIFTS for istighfār: (1) RAIN (v.11), (2) WEALTH, (3) SONS, (4) GARDENS, (5) RIVERS.","When asked why he gave the same answer to all, he said: \"I said nothing from myself — Allah says istaghfirū rabbakum…\" (And ʿUmar (رضي الله عنه) is reported to have gone out for the rain-prayer and done nothing but ISTIGHFĀR — saying he sought rain through \"the keys of the sky.\") THE MUDDATHTHIR CONTRAST: Muddaththir 74:12–13 — wa jaʿaltu lahu mālan mamdūdā · wa banīna shuhūdā — al-Walīd was GIVEN extended WEALTH and SONS — and he was ungrateful.","Nūḥ 71:12 — wa yumdidkum bi-amwālin wa banīn — Nūḥ's people were PROMISED extended wealth and sons — for istighfār.","SAME m-d-d root, SAME pair (wealth + sons).","One received them and denied; the others were offered them and refused.","And v.21 completes the irony: wa'ttabaʿū man lam yazidhu māluhu wa waladuhu illā khasārā — they followed the man whose WEALTH and CHILDREN only increased him in LOSS.","Wealth and children from istighfār = blessing; wealth and children without it = loss.","Yajʿal lakum… wa yajʿal lakum — \"make FOR YOU… make FOR YOU\" — repeated lakum: personal, generous.","Hook: imdādāt = SUPPLIES; nahr = a river / nahār = day.","yajʿal ×2 — jussive (answer to istaghfirū).","Al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī: drought, poverty, childlessness, dry garden → \"ISTIGHFĀR\" for all four.","Muddaththir 74:12–13: al-Walīd GIVEN māl mamdūd + banīn (ungrateful).","Nūḥ 71:12: OFFERED yumdidkum bi-amwālin wa banīn (refused).","Yumdid = unmerged doubled jussive (like tamnun, 74:6)."]},
  {ar:"مَّا لَكُمْ لَا تَرْجُونَ لِلَّهِ وَقَارًا", tr:"Mā lakum lā tarjūna li'llāhi waqārā", v:"13", root:"Mā lakum — \"WHAT IS WRONG WITH YOU?\" (Muddaththir 74:49 — fa-mā lahum; Maʿārij 70:36 — fa-māli'lladhīna).", meaning:"\"What is wrong with you that you do not hold Allah in due reverence?\"", magic:"To give someone waqār = to give them WEIGHT in your heart — treat them as weighty, serious, to be reckoned with. They gave Allah NO WEIGHT.", grammar:"Mā lakum + lā tarjūna (ḥāl: \"what's with you, not hoping…\"). waqāran = object.", why:"Waqār (weight → reverence) — they gave Allah no weight, while making their own ears heavy.", note:"WAQĀR = DIGNITY ← waqr = HEAVINESS — giving someone WEIGHT in your heart.", more:["tarjūna — the \"HOPE / EXPECT (and sometimes FEAR)\" family — rajā = to hope, to expect; rajāʾ = HOPE (ar-rajāʾ!","= \"PLEASE!\" — on signs: ar-rajāʾ ʿadam at-tadkhīn = please don't smoke!); classically, rajā in a negative sentence can mean to FEAR / to reckon with.","waqār — the \"HEAVINESS / GRAVITY → DIGNITY\" family — waqura = to be heavy, grave, dignified; waqr = a heavy LOAD; waqr fi'l-udhun = DEAFNESS (heaviness in the ear!","— fī ādhāninā waqr, 41:5!); waqār = DIGNITY, gravitas, solemn respect; tawqīr = RESPECT, reverence (muwaqqar = respected!).","WAQĀR — DIGNITY / GRAVITY — from HEAVINESS.","Lā tarjūna li'llāhi waqārā — classical readings: (a) \"you don't HOPE for any dignity from Allah\" (no hope of honour from Him); (b) \"you don't FEAR Allah's majesty\" (rajā in negation = fear); (c) \"you don't attribute to Allah the greatness He deserves.\" All: they treated Him as WEIGHTLESS.","AND THE ROOT'S HIDDEN LINK TO v.7: WAQR fi'l-udhun = DEAFNESS — \"heaviness in the ear\" — Fuṣṣilat 41:5 — wa fī ādhāninā waqr — \"in our ears is DEAFNESS.\" v.7: they put FINGERS IN THEIR EARS.","They made their ears \"heavy\" (waqr — deaf) — and gave Allah no \"weight\" (waqār — reverence).","The same root: heavy ears, weightless God.","Deafness and disrespect, one root.","Mā lakum — \"WHAT'S WRONG with you?\" — astonished question — Zalzalah 99:3 (man asks about the earth), Muddaththir 74:49, Maʿārij 70:36 (Allah asks about them).","Hooks: ar-rajāʾ = \"PLEASE\" (on signs); tawqīr = RESPECT; muwaqqar = RESPECTED.","WAQR in the ear = DEAFNESS (41:5) — they plugged their ears (v.7) and gave Allah no waqār (v.13).","Heavy ears, weightless God — one root.","Rajā in negation can mean FEAR.","Ar-rajāʾ = \"PLEASE\"; tawqīr = RESPECT.","Mā lakum: 99:3, 74:49, 70:36."]},
  {ar:"وَقَدْ خَلَقَكُمْ أَطْوَارًا", tr:"Wa qad khalaqakum aṭwārā", v:"14", root:"Qad + past — \"He has INDEED…\" (certainty).", meaning:"\"…when He created you in stages?\"", magic:"Aṭwārā = in STAGES — classical: drop (nuṭfah) → clot (ʿalaqah) → lump (muḍghah) → bones → flesh → a new creation (al-Muʾminūn 23:12–14) — and then infancy → childhood → youth → maturity → old age.", grammar:"Wa qad = a circumstantial clause (ḥāl) — \"WHEN [in fact] He created you…\" aṭwāran = ḥāl (in stages).", why:"Aṭwār — compresses the entire embryology and life-cycle into a single argument.", note:"AṬWĀRĀ = in STAGES — the embryo (nuṭfah → ʿalaqah → muḍghah → bones → flesh) + the life-cycle, in ONE WORD.", more:["aṭwār — the \"STAGE / phase / boundary\" family — ṭawr = a STAGE, a phase, a state (also ṭawr = a time / an occasion); taṭawwur = DEVELOPMENT / EVOLUTION (modern!); taṭwīr = DEVELOPING / upgrading (taṭwīr al-barāmij = software development!); aṭwār = stages.","And the argument: \"He took you through STAGES you didn't choose — and you don't give Him waqār (v.13)?\" The proof of His power is written in your own body's history.","The CREATION-STAGES thread across your surahs: ʿAlaq 96:2 — khalaqa'l-insāna min ʿalaq (from a CLINGING clot).","Insān 76:2 — min nuṭfatin amshāj (from a MIXED drop).","ʿAbasa 80:19 — min nuṭfatin khalaqahu fa-qaddarah (from a drop — then PROPORTIONED).","Qiyāmah 75:37–39 — nuṭfah → ʿalaqah → fa-khalaqa fa-sawwā.","Nūḥ 71:14 — aṭwārā — all the stages in ONE WORD.","Maʿārij 70:39 — mimmā yaʿlamūn (from what they KNOW).","Modern hook: taṭawwur = DEVELOPMENT — taṭwīr al-barāmij = SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT — you are His \"development,\" stage by stage.","Creation thread: ʿalaq (96:2), amshāj (76:2), qaddarah (80:19), ʿalaqah (75:38), dāfiq (86:6), AṬWĀR (71:14). TAṬAWWUR = DEVELOPMENT; taṭwīr al-barāmij = SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT.","He developed you — give Him waqār."]},
  {ar:"أَلَمْ تَرَوْا كَيْفَ خَلَقَ اللَّهُ سَبْعَ سَمَاوَاتٍ طِبَاقًا", tr:"A-lam taraw kayfa khalaqa'llāhu sabʿa samāwātin ṭibāqā", v:"15", root:"A-lam taraw — \"Have you not SEEN…?\" (Fajr 89:6, Fīl 105:1 — a-lam tara kayfa…).", meaning:"\"Have you not seen how Allah created seven heavens in layers?\"", magic:"Ṭibāqā = in LAYERS, one above another — like the STOREYS (ṭawābiq) of a tower, or stacked PLATES.", grammar:"A-lam + jussive (taraw, nūn dropped).", why:"ṭibāq — layers that also MATCH — order and perfection in one word.", note:"ṬIBĀQ = LAYERS like the FLOORS (ṭawābiq) of a building — and muṭābiq = MATCHING (no flaw — Mulk 67:3). Inshiqāq 84:19: ṭabaqan ʿan ṭabaq (stage after stage).", more:["ṭibāq — the \"LAYERING / matching / covering\" family — ṭabaqa = to cover, to fit exactly over; ṭabaq = a LAYER, a STAGE — and a PLATE / dish (everyday: ṭabaq = a plate of food!; aṭbāq = dishes); ṭābiq = a FLOOR / STOREY of a building (aṭ-ṭābiq ath-thālith = the third floor!); ṭabaqah = a CLASS / social stratum (ṭabaqah wusṭā = middle class!); muṭābiq = MATCHING / identical; taṭbīq = an APPLICATION (an APP on your phone!).","THE HEAVENS AS FLOORS OF A BUILDING.","And muṭābiq = MATCHING — classical: layered AND fitting perfectly, each matching the next — no gaps, no flaws (Mulk 67:3 — alladhī khalaqa sabʿa samāwātin ṭibāqā mā tarā fī khalqi'r-raḥmāni min tafāwut — \"you see no FLAW\"!).","THE INSHIQĀQ CONNECTION: Inshiqāq 84:19 — la-tarkabunna ṭabaqan ʿan ṭabaq — \"you will surely ride STAGE after STAGE\" (layer upon layer of your life's journey).","Nūḥ 71:15 — sabʿa samāwātin ṭibāqā — seven heavens LAYER upon LAYER.","And v.14 — aṭwārā (STAGES of YOU) → v.15 — ṭibāqā (LAYERS of the HEAVENS).","Your stages (aṭwār) and the sky's layers (ṭibāq) — side by side.","The same Designer layered both.","A-lam taraw kayfa — \"have you not SEEN HOW\" — Fajr 89:6 — a-lam tara kayfa faʿala rabbuka bi-ʿĀd; Fīl 105:1 — a-lam tara kayfa faʿala rabbuka bi-aṣḥābi'l-fīl.","There: see how He DESTROYED; here: see how He CREATED.","The same formula for His power to make and to unmake.","Modern hooks: ṭābiq = a FLOOR; ṭabaqah wusṭā = MIDDLE CLASS; taṭbīq = an APP; ṭabaq = a PLATE.","ṭibāqan = ḥāl or adjective (verbal noun of ṭābaqa, Form III — \"matching layers\").","v.14 AṬWĀR (your STAGES) → v.15 ṬIBĀQ (the sky's LAYERS).","A-lam tara kayfa: see how He DESTROYED (89:6, 105:1) / how He CREATED (71:15). Ṭabaq = a PLATE; taṭbīq = an APP; ṭabaqah wusṭā = MIDDLE CLASS."]},
  {ar:"وَجَعَلَ الْقَمَرَ فِيهِنَّ نُورًا وَجَعَلَ الشَّمْسَ سِرَاجًا", tr:"Wa jaʿala'l-qamara fīhinna nūran wa jaʿala'sh-shamsa sirājā", v:"16", root:"Qamar — the MOON.", meaning:"\"And made the moon therein a light, and made the sun a lamp.\"", magic:"THE PRECISE DISTINCTION — and it's astronomically exact. MOON = NŪR (light) — a gentle, REFLECTED light (it has no fire of its own).", grammar:"Two parallel clauses (jaʿala X Y) — jaʿala takes two objects. fīhinna = feminine plural pronoun (the heavens).", why:"Nūr vs sirāj — a precise physical distinction between reflected and generated light.", note:"MOON = NŪR (REFLECTED light). SUN = SIRĀJ (a burning LAMP — its OWN light). Never swapped in the Qurʾān.", more:["nūr — the \"LIGHT (gentle, reflected)\" family — nūr = LIGHT (an-Nūr = a Name of Allah; munawwar = ILLUMINATED — al-Madīnah al-Munawwarah!).","sirāj — a LAMP — one that BURNS and produces its own light (Nabaʾ 78:13 — wa jaʿalnā sirājan wahhājā!; sirāj munīr — the Prophet ﷺ, 33:46!; sirāj = an oil LAMP that burns its own fuel).","SUN = SIRĀJ (a LAMP) — a BURNING source that PRODUCES its own light and heat.","The Qurʾān never calls the moon a sirāj or the sun merely nūr — one SOURCES light, the other RECEIVES and REFLECTS it.","Compare Yūnus 10:5 — jaʿala'sh-shamsa ḍiyāʾan wa'l-qamara nūrā — the sun = ḍiyāʾ (radiance, from its own source), the moon = nūr (reflected light).","Consistent across the Qurʾān.","And SHAMS 91:2 — wa'l-qamari idhā TALĀHĀ — \"the moon when it FOLLOWS it [the sun]\" — I noted there: the moon follows / recites the sun, transmitting light it didn't make.","Nūḥ 71:16 states it outright: moon = nūr, sun = sirāj.","And Nabaʾ 78:13 — wa jaʿalnā sirājan wahhājā — \"a BLAZING LAMP\" — the sun as sirāj in both surahs.","And the Prophet ﷺ himself is called sirājan munīrā (33:46) — \"a LIGHT-GIVING LAMP\" — a source of guidance, not a reflection.","Fīhinna — \"IN THEM\" (the heavens) — classical: the moon is in the nearest heaven, but \"in them\" = in their collective set (like saying \"in the house\" for one room).","Shams 91:2: the moon FOLLOWS (talāhā) the sun — transmitting borrowed light.","Nabaʾ 78:13: sirājan wahhājā.","Yūnus 10:5: sun = ḍiyāʾ, moon = nūr.","The Prophet ﷺ = sirājan munīrā (33:46). Al-Madīnah al-MUNAWWARAH."]},
  {ar:"وَاللَّهُ أَنبَتَكُم مِّنَ الْأَرْضِ نَبَاتًا", tr:"Wa'llāhu anbatakum mina'l-arḍi nabātā", v:"17", root:"Anbata — the \"SPROUTING / growing (plants)\" family — nabata = to sprout; anbata (Form IV) = to make GROW.", meaning:"\"And Allah caused you to grow from the earth like plants.\"", magic:"Anbatakum… nabātā — \"He made YOU sprout — a sprouting\" — as if you were a CROP.", grammar:"Anbatakum (Form IV) + nabātan (Form I maṣdar) = mixed-form cognate accusative (like 73:8). wa'llāhu fronted (emphasis: ALLAH did this).", why:"Nabāt — pictures human life as plant life — dependent, grown, and returning to the soil (v.18).", note:"HUMANS GROWN LIKE PLANTS — from soil, through food, to you.", more:["Nabāt = PLANTS, vegetation (nabātī = VEGETARIAN / VEGETABLE!","— zayt nabātī = vegetable oil!); manbat = a place of growth / origin; Nabaʾ 78:15 — li-nukhrija bihi ḥabban wa nabātā; ʿAbasa 80:27 — fa-anbatnā fīhā ḥabbā.","Classical: Ādam from earth; and every human's body built from the earth's produce (food → blood → the drop → you).","You are, literally, grown from soil.","A GRAMMAR GEM — MIXED FORMS: The verb is Form IV (anbata, \"caused to grow\") — but the cognate accusative is the Form I noun (nabāt, \"growing / plants\") — not the expected Form IV maṣdar inbāt.","Classical explanation: the mixture means BOTH — \"He caused you to grow, and you grew like plants grow\" — combining His action and your natural growth.","You've seen this EXACT device: Muzzammil 73:8 — wa TABATTAL ilayhi TABTĪLĀ — Form V verb + Form II maṣdar.","Two surahs, the same deliberate mixing of forms to double the meaning.","Nabāt — the plant thread: Nabaʾ 78:15 (ḥabban wa nabātā — grain and plants), ʿAbasa 80:27–31 (fa-anbatnā fīhā ḥabbā… — We made grow grain, grapes, olives…), Nūḥ 71:17 — anbatakum — \"We made YOU grow.\" ʿAbasa: He grows your FOOD.","Hook: nabātī = VEGETARIAN; zayt nabātī = VEGETABLE OIL.","ANBATA (Form IV) + NABĀTĀ (Form I noun) = MIXED FORMS — like Muzzammil 73:8 tabattal tabtīlā.","ʿAbasa 80:27: He grows your FOOD.","Nabātī = VEGETARIAN; zayt nabātī = VEGETABLE OIL."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ يُعِيدُكُمْ فِيهَا وَيُخْرِجُكُمْ إِخْرَاجًا", tr:"Thumma yuʿīdukum fīhā wa yukhrijukum ikhrājā", v:"18", root:"Yuʿīdu — the \"RETURNING / repeating\" family — ʿāda = to return.", meaning:"\"Then He will return you into it, and bring you out [again] — a bringing out.\"", magic:"THE COMPLETE CYCLE: grown FROM the earth (v.17) → returned INTO it (v.18a) → brought OUT of it again (v.18b).", grammar:"Thumma = sequence.", why:"Iʿādah + ikhrāj — the burial and the resurrection, completing the plant-cycle of v.17.", note:"THE CYCLE: GROWN from earth (v.17) → RETURNED into it (v.18a) → BROUGHT OUT again (v.18b) — resurrection as a second sprouting.", more:["Aʿāda = to RETURN something, to REPEAT (iʿādah = a REPLAY / repetition; ʿīd = a FESTIVAL — the day that RETURNS each year!; ʿādah = a HABIT — what you return to!; al-Muʿīd = the Restorer — a Name of Allah).","yukhriju — to BRING OUT (ikhrāj = EXTRACTION — and ikhrāj = DIRECTING (a film)!; mukhrij = a FILM DIRECTOR!).","Minhā khalaqnākum wa fīhā nuʿīdukum wa minhā nukhrijukum tāratan ukhrā (20:55) — \"from it We created you, into it We return you, from it We bring you out again.\" The plant image continues: a seed goes into the soil and comes out as new growth — resurrection pictured as a second sprouting.","Ikhrājā — the cognate accusative — \"bring you out with a BRINGING OUT\" — emphatic: a REAL, certain emergence.","And the kh-r-j thread: Maʿārij 70:43 — yawma yakhrujūna mina'l-ajdāthi sirāʿā (the Day they COME OUT of the graves rushing) — the previous surah describes the very event Nūḥ promises.","Nāziʿāt 79:31 — akhraja minhā māʾahā wa marʿāhā (brought out its water and pasture).","Zalzalah 99:2 — wa akhrajati'l-arḍu athqālahā (the earth brought out its burdens).","Yuʿīdukum — RETURN — ʿĪD = a FESTIVAL (the day that comes BACK each year) — and al-Muʿīd (the Restorer) — Burūj 85:13 — innahu huwa yubdiʾu wa yuʿīd — \"He originates AND RESTORES.\" Same verb.","Hooks: mukhrij = a FILM DIRECTOR (ikhrāj = directing — \"bringing out\" a film!); ʿādah = a HABIT; iʿādah = a REPLAY.","ikhrājan = cognate accusative (Form IV maṣdar — here the verb and maṣdar MATCH, unlike v.17).","Maʿārij 70:43: yakhrujūna mina'l-ajdāth — the very event.","Yuʿīd — Burūj 85:13 yubdiʾu wa yuʿīd; ʿĪD = the festival that RETURNS.","Mukhrij = a FILM DIRECTOR; ʿādah = a HABIT.","Here verb + maṣdar MATCH (unlike v.17)."]},
  {ar:"وَاللَّهُ جَعَلَ لَكُمُ الْأَرْضَ بِسَاطًا", tr:"Wa'llāhu jaʿala lakumu'l-arḍa bisāṭā", v:"19", root:"The \"SPREADING OUT / flattening / simplicity\" family — basaṭa = to spread out, to stretch flat.", meaning:"\"And Allah made the earth for you a spread-out carpet.\"", magic:"Bisāṭā — a CARPET rolled out for a guest — flat enough to walk, sit, build, farm. Hospitality: the host lays the carpet before the guest arrives.", grammar:"Wa'llāhu fronted (emphasis — the second time, v.17 and v.19). jaʿala + two objects (al-arḍa, bisāṭan).", why:"Bisāṭ — domestic, welcoming: the earth as a host's carpet, laid for you.", note:"BISĀṬ = a CARPET — the earth rolled out like a host's rug.", more:["Bisāṭ = a CARPET, a rug (bisāṭ aḥmar = the RED CARPET!); basīṭ = SIMPLE (everyday!); al-Bāsiṭ = the Expander (a Name of Allah); inbisāṭ = CHEERFULNESS / ease (feeling \"spread out\"!); basṭ = the numerator of a fraction (math!).","The \"earth spread out\" thread — now FOUR images: Nabaʾ 78:6 — a-lam najʿali'l-arḍa mihādā — a CRADLE (soft bed).","Ghāshiyah 88:20 — wa ila'l-arḍi kayfa suṭiḥat — SURFACED / flattened (saṭḥ = a roof!).","Nāziʿāt 79:30 — wa'l-arḍa baʿda dhālika daḥāhā — SPREAD out (like an egg / an ostrich nest).","Nūḥ 71:19 — bisāṭā — a CARPET.","Cradle, roof-surface, spread nest, carpet — four homely images for one planet.","Basīṭ = SIMPLE — the earth is laid out \"simply,\" flat to the walker — and al-Bāsiṭ = He who EXPANDS (provision, hearts — paired with al-Qābiḍ, who constricts).","Hook: bisāṭ aḥmar = RED CARPET; basīṭ = SIMPLE; inbisāṭ = CHEERFULNESS.","Earth images: CRADLE (mihād, 78:6), SURFACED (suṭiḥat, 88:20), SPREAD (daḥāhā, 79:30), CARPET (bisāṭ, 71:19). BASĪṬ = SIMPLE; bisāṭ aḥmar = RED CARPET; al-Bāsiṭ = the Expander."]},
  {ar:"لِّتَسْلُكُوا مِنْهَا سُبُلًا فِجَاجًا", tr:"Li-taslukū minhā subulan fijājā", v:"20", root:"Taslukū — the \"THREADING / travelling a path\" family — salaka = to thread, to travel along.", meaning:"\"…so that you may travel along it on wide roads.\"", magic:"THE SALAKA THREAD — and here, at last, its MERCIFUL use.", grammar:"Li- + subjunctive (taslukū, nūn dropped) = purpose. subulan fijājan — noun + adjective (both plural).", why:"Salaka + fijāj — the confinement-verb of the punishment surahs, turned into the freedom of wide roads.", note:"SALAKA — THREADED into punishment (72:17, 74:42, 69:32) → here: TRAVEL along WIDE roads (71:20). Narrow chains for deniers; WIDE passes for the grateful.", more:["Silk = a WIRE / thread (lā-silkī = WIRELESS!); maslak = a PATH / track; sulūk = BEHAVIOUR.","subul — plural of sabīl = ROADS, ways (ibn as-sabīl = a TRAVELLER; fī sabīli'llāh).","fijāj — plural of fajj = a WIDE MOUNTAIN PASS, a broad valley-road (Ḥajj 22:27 — min kulli fajjin ʿamīq — \"from every DEEP mountain pass\" — the pilgrims!).","You've seen salaka as CONFINEMENT and PUNISHMENT: Jinn 72:17 — yaslukhu ʿadhāban ṣaʿadā (THREADED into a climbing punishment); Muddaththir 74:42 — mā salakakum fī saqar (what THREADED you into Saqar?); Ḥāqqah 69:32 — fī silsilatin… fa'slukūhu (THREAD him through the chain).","And once as PROTECTION: Jinn 72:27 — yasluku… raṣadā (guards threaded around revelation).","NOW: Nūḥ 71:20 — li-TASLUKŪ minhā subulan fijājā — \"so you may TRAVEL along WIDE roads.\" The same verb: threaded through a chain (punishment) vs travelling broad roads (blessing).","Narrow passages for the deniers; WIDE ones for you — if you're grateful.","Fijāj — WIDE mountain passes — the opposite of a needle's eye!","Ḥajj 22:27 — min kulli fajjin ʿamīq — the pilgrims come \"from every DEEP mountain PASS\" — same word.","The roads Allah opened through the mountains are the roads pilgrims walk to His House.","Subul — ROADS — Muzzammil 73:19 — fa-man shāʾa'ttakhadha ilā rabbihi sabīlā — ONE road to your Lord; many roads (subul) across the earth.","The carpet (v.19) has ROADS on it (v.20) — a furnished home, with pathways.","FIJĀJ = WIDE mountain PASSES — Ḥajj 22:27 min kulli fajjin ʿamīq (the pilgrims' roads).","Ibn as-sabīl = a TRAVELLER; lā-silkī = WIRELESS.","Muzzammil 73:19: ONE sabīl to your Lord."]},
  {ar:"قَالَ نُوحٌ رَّبِّ إِنَّهُمْ عَصَوْنِي", tr:"Qāla nūḥun rabbi innahum ʿaṣawnī", v:"21", root:"ʿaṣaw — the \"DISOBEYING\" family — ʿaṣā = to disobey (Nāziʿāt 79:21, Muzzammil 73:16 — Pharaoh ʿaṣā; Ḥāqqah 69:10.", meaning:"\"Nūḥ said: 'My Lord, indeed they have disobeyed me…'\"", magic:"The report resumes — after the signs (v.13–20), back to the verdict. ʿAṣawnī — \"they DISOBEYED ME\" — answering v.3: wa aṭīʿūn (\"and OBEY me\").", grammar:"ʿaṣawnī = ʿaṣaw + -nī (me). rabbi = vocative, yāʾ dropped.", why:"ʿaṣā — answers v.3's aṭīʿūn directly.", note:"V.3 AṬĪʿŪN (OBEY me) → v.21 ʿAṢAWNĪ (they DISOBEYED me).", more:["Fa-ʿaṣaw rasūla rabbihim; Jinn 72:23 — wa man yaʿṣi'llāha); ʿaṣā = a STAFF (same letters!); maʿṣiyah = a SIN / act of disobedience; ʿiṣyān = REBELLION / mutiny (modern!).","v.3 AṬĪʿŪN (obey me) → v.21 ʿAṢAWNĪ (they disobeyed me).","The command and its refusal, bookending the argument.","The ʿaṣā thread: Pharaoh (79:21, 73:16), the nations (69:10), whoever (72:23), Nūḥ's people (71:21). ʿIṣyān = REBELLION / mutiny.","Qāla NŪḤUN — his NAME, in the third person — after speaking in the first person (v.5–20) — a narrative shift (iltifāt): the narrator re-introduces him for the final section.","ʿAṣā thread: Pharaoh (79:21, 73:16), nations (69:10), Jinn 72:23, Nūḥ's people (71:21). ʿIṣyān = MUTINY; ʿaṣā = a STAFF."]},
  {ar:"وَاتَّبَعُوا مَن لَّمْ يَزِدْهُ مَالُهُ وَوَلَدُهُ إِلَّا خَسَارًا", tr:"Wa'ttabaʿū man lam yazidhu māluhu wa waladuhu illā khasārā", v:"21", root:"Ittabaʿū — Form VIII of tabiʿa = to FOLLOW (tābiʿ = a FOLLOWER; at-tābiʿūn = the generation after the Companions!; mutābaʿah = FOLLOW-UP!).", meaning:"\"…and followed the one whose wealth and children increased him only in loss.\"", magic:"THEY FOLLOWED THE RICH — not the prophet.", grammar:"Man = \"the one who\" (relative). lam yazid — jussive, hollow (long ī shortened).", why:"Khasār — a trade word: wealth that should have profited, instead compounding the loss.", note:"They FOLLOWED THE RICH — whose wealth and children brought only LOSS. v.12 PROMISED: amwāl + banīn as BLESSING (with Allah).", more:["māl / walad — WEALTH / CHILDREN.","khasār — the \"LOSS — trade loss\" family — khasira; khusr (ʿAṣr 103:2!); khasārah = financial LOSS.","Man lam yazidhu māluhu wa waladuhu illā khasārā — the chiefs, the wealthy, the men with many sons — whose wealth and children brought them only LOSS.","The people chose the rich man's way over the messenger's way — because wealth LOOKS like success.","THE IRONY WITH v.12: v.12 — Nūḥ PROMISED: yumdidkum bi-AMWĀLIN wa BANĪN — istighfār would bring wealth and sons as BLESSING.","v.21 — they FOLLOWED a man whose MĀLUHU wa WALADUHU brought only LOSS.","Wealth and children WITH Allah = blessing; WITHOUT Him = loss.","Same goods, opposite outcomes.","And KHASĀR — ʿAṢR 103:2 — inna'l-insāna la-fī khusr — every human is in LOSS (by default, as time drains).","Here: their wealth and children only ADDED to the loss.","And the \"mā aghnā\" thread: Layl 92:11 (mā yughnī ʿanhu māluhu), Masad 111:2 (mā aghnā ʿanhu māluhu wa mā kasab — \"what he EARNED\" = his children!), Ḥāqqah 69:28 (mā aghnā ʿannī māliyah).","Nūḥ 71:21 goes further: wealth didn't just fail to help — it INCREASED the loss.","And the zāda… illā pattern — #2 of 4 (v.6 firārā, v.21 khasārā, v.24 ḍalālā, v.28 tabārā).","And Muddaththir 74:12–13 — al-Walīd's mālan mamdūdā wa banīna shuhūdā — exactly the type they followed.","waladuhu — recitation variant wulduhu (plural sense — \"his children\").","v.21: māl + walad = only LOSS (without Him).","KHASĀR = ʿAṢR 103:2 khusr.","Wealth thread: won't avail (92:11, 111:2, 69:28) → here it ADDS to loss.","Muddaththir 74:12–13: al-Walīd = the type they followed."]},
  {ar:"وَمَكَرُوا مَكْرًا كُبَّارًا", tr:"Wa makarū makran kubbārā", v:"22", root:"Makarū — the \"SCHEMING / cunning plotting\" family — makara = to plot craftily, to scheme.", meaning:"\"…and they plotted a tremendously great plot.\"", magic:"KUBBĀR — a RARE intensive: the \"BIGGEST big.\" Arabic grades bigness: kabīr = big kubār = very big (fuʿāl) kubbār = ENORMOUSLY, overwhelmingly big (fuʿʿāl).", grammar:"Kubbāran = the fuʿʿāl intensive (rarer than faʿʿāl) — maximum bigness. makran = cognate accusative.", why:"Kubbār — the rarest, biggest form of \"big,\" matching the scale of their opposition.", note:"KABĪR (big) → KUBĀR (very big) → KUBBĀR (ENORMOUS) — Arabic GRADES bigness.", more:["Makr = a SCHEME, a cunning plot; makkār = CRAFTY / sly; wa makarū wa makara'llāh (3:54) — they plotted, and Allah plotted.","kubbār — the faʿʿāl intensive of kabīr = ENORMOUSLY, HUGELY great — the GRADED ladder of \"big\": kabīr (big) → kubār (very big) → kubbār (ENORMOUS!).","The plot was the BIGGEST possible — classical: inciting the masses against Nūḥ, mocking him, threatening his followers, and organizing the whole society to cling to the idols (v.23 is the plot in words).","Makr / kayd — the plotting thread: Ṭāriq 86:15–16 — innahum yakīdūna kaydā · wa akīdu kaydā (they plot; I plot).","Fīl 105:2 — a-lam yajʿal kaydahum fī taḍlīl (their plot sent astray — Abrahah).","Nūḥ 71:22 — wa makarū makran kubbārā (an ENORMOUS plot).","Abrahah's plot became a lost camel; Nūḥ's people's ENORMOUS plot ended under the flood.","The bigger the plot, the bigger the fall.","And the k-b-r root — Muddaththir: v.3 fa-kabbir (MAGNIFY Allah), v.23 istakbara (made himself great), v.35 iḥda'l-kubar (one of the GREAT calamities).","And Nūḥ 71:7 — istakbarū'stikbārā (arrogance), 71:22 — makran kubbārā (an ENORMOUS plot).","Arrogance (istikbār) breeds enormous plots (makr kubbār).","Cognate accusative makarū makran — \"plotted a plot\" + the adjective kubbārā.","Plot thread: Ṭāriq 86:15 (kayd), Fīl 105:2 (Abrahah's plot → a LOST CAMEL), Nūḥ 71:22 (makr KUBBĀR → the FLOOD).","The bigger the plot, the bigger the fall.","K-b-r: istakbarū (v.7, arrogance) → kubbārā (v.22, an enormous plot)."]},
  {ar:"وَقَالُوا لَا تَذَرُنَّ آلِهَتَكُمْ", tr:"Wa qālū lā tadharunna ālihatakum", v:"23", root:"Tadharunna — the DEFECTIVE verb wadhara (to leave — no past tense in use!) + the HEAVY nūn of emphasis (Takāthur 102:6 la-tarawunna; Humazah 104:4 la-yunbadhanna).", meaning:"\"And they said: 'Never abandon your gods…'\"", magic:"THE PLOT IN WORDS — and the dhar verb completes its arc. Lā TADHARUNNA — \"Do NOT — EVER — LEAVE your gods!\" — the heavy nūn makes it a sworn, desperate command.", grammar:"Lā tadharunna = prohibition + DEFECTIVE verb + HEAVY nūn — the plural nūn drops before the emphatic nūn (tadharūna → tadharunna).", why:"Dhar + heavy nūn — a desperate oath to cling to idols, answered by Nūḥ's lā tadhar (v.26).", note:"LĀ TADHARUNNA = \"NEVER EVER LEAVE your gods!\" — defective verb + HEAVY nūn.", more:["ālihah — GODS (plural of ilāh — Nās 114:3 ilāhi'n-nās!).","THE DHAR THREAD — now FIVE uses: Muzzammil 73:11 — wa dharnī wa'l-mukadhdhibīn (Allah: \"LEAVE ME with the deniers\").","Muddaththir 74:11 — dharnī wa man khalaqtu waḥīdā (Allah: \"LEAVE ME with him\").","Muddaththir 74:28 — lā tubqī wa lā tadhar (Saqar LEAVES nothing).","Maʿārij 70:42 — fa-dharhum yakhūḍū (Allah: \"LEAVE THEM to wade\").","Nūḥ 71:23 — lā tadharunna ālihatakum (the chiefs: \"NEVER LEAVE your gods!\").","Nūḥ 71:26–27 — lā tadhar ʿala'l-arḍ… in tadharhum (Nūḥ: \"LEAVE no dweller… if You LEAVE them\").","The chiefs said \"never LEAVE your gods\" — and Nūḥ prayed \"LEAVE not one of them on earth.\" The same verb, thrown back.","Āliha — GODS (plural) — the opposite of NĀS 114:3's ILĀHI'n-nās (THE God of mankind — singular) and IKHLĀṢ 112:1 — Allāhu aḥad.","Ālihatakum — \"YOUR gods\" — possessive, tribal: \"OUR heritage, YOUR identity\" — the appeal to tradition (innā wajadnā ābāʾanā ʿalā ummah, 43:22 — \"we found our fathers on a way\").","ālihah = broken plural of ilāh.","DHAR thread: Allah \"leave Me\" (73:11, 74:11), Saqar \"leaves nothing\" (74:28), \"leave them\" (70:42), chiefs \"never LEAVE your gods\" (71:23) → Nūḥ \"LEAVE not one\" (71:26). ĀLIHAH (many gods) vs ILĀH (Nās 114:3) / AḤAD (Ikhlāṣ 112:1). The appeal to TRADITION (43:22)."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا تَذَرُنَّ وَدًّا وَلَا سُوَاعًا وَلَا يَغُوثَ وَيَعُوقَ وَنَسْرًا", tr:"Wa lā tadharunna waddan wa lā suwāʿan wa lā yaghūtha wa yaʿūqa wa nasrā", v:"23", root:"FIVE IDOLS — each name has a meaning: Wadd — \"LOVE\" (wadda = to love; al-Wadūd — Burūj 85:14; mawaddah; Maʿārij 70:11 yawaddu).", meaning:"\"…and never abandon Wadd, nor Suwāʿ, nor Yaghūth, Yaʿūq, and Nasr.\"", magic:"THE ORIGIN OF IDOLATRY — in five names. Bukhārī (from Ibn ʿAbbās): these were the names of RIGHTEOUS MEN among Nūḥ's people.", grammar:"Yaghūtha and Yaʿūqa = DIPTOTES (verb-shaped proper names — wazn al-fiʿl) — NO tanwīn — while Waddan, Suwāʿan, Nasran HAVE tanwīn.", why:"Naming the idols — preserves the history of how idolatry began, as a warning for all time.", note:"FIVE IDOLS = five RIGHTEOUS MEN → STATUES to remember them → WORSHIPPED by the next generation (Bukhārī, Ibn ʿAbbās).", more:["Suwāʿ — (name; meaning uncertain — classically linked to sāʿah, an hour / time).","Yaghūth — \"HE HELPS / rescues\" (ghāth = to rescue; ghawth = RESCUE; istighāthah = a CRY FOR HELP; ghayth = RAIN — the rescue from drought!).","Yaʿūq — \"HE HINDERS / prevents\" (ʿāqa = to hinder; ʿāʾiq = an OBSTACLE; muʿawwaq = DISABLED — modern!; iʿāqah = DISABILITY!).","Nasr — \"VULTURE / EAGLE\" (nasr = vulture / eagle; an-Nasr al-Wāqiʿ = the star Vega!).","When they died, Shayṭān whispered to the people: \"set up STATUES in their gathering-places and name them after them\" — to remember them.","They did — but didn't worship them.","When THAT generation died and knowledge faded, the next generation WORSHIPPED them.","Idolatry began as RESPECT for the righteous, turned into memorials, then into gods — in a few generations.","That's why Islam guards so carefully against exaggerating reverence for the pious and against images in worship.","And these idols survived to pre-Islamic Arabia — Ibn ʿAbbās reported that the Arab tribes later worshipped idols with these same names: Wadd (Kalb tribe at Dūmat al-Jandal), Suwāʿ (Hudhayl), Yaghūth (Murād), Yaʿūq (Hamdān), Nasr (Ḥimyar).","Nūḥ's idols, inherited by the Prophet's ﷺ world.","THE IRONY OF THE NAMES: WADD = \"LOVE\" — but al-WADŪD (the Loving) is ALLAH (Burūj 85:14). YAGHŪTH = \"HE RESCUES\" — but no idol rescued them from the flood (v.25 — falam yajidū lahum… anṣārā).","YAʿŪQ = \"HE HINDERS\" — and the idol truly HINDERED them — from the truth.","NASR = a VULTURE — the bird that feeds on the dead.","Names promising love, rescue, power — delivering none.","Yaghūth → ISTIGHĀTHAH (a cry for help) — in Islam, istighāthah belongs to Allah alone (idh tastaghīthūna rabbakum, 8:9). Ghayth = RAIN — v.11's midrār (rain) came from istighfār to Allah, not from Yaghūth.","Modern hooks: muʿawwaq = DISABLED / iʿāqah = DISABILITY (from ʿāqa, hinder); istighāthah = a CRY FOR HELP; nasr = an EAGLE (on several national emblems!).","The repeated wa lā breaks off after Yaghūth — wa Yaʿūqa wa Nasrā (no more lā) — classical: rhythm / they were grouped.","Idolatry began as RESPECT.","WADD = LOVE (but al-WADŪD is Allah, 85:14); YAGHŪTH = HE RESCUES (no rescue from the flood); YAʿŪQ = HE HINDERS (it did — from truth); NASR = a VULTURE.","Survived into pre-Islamic Arabia.","Muʿawwaq = DISABLED; istighāthah = CRY FOR HELP; ghayth = RAIN.","Yaghūtha, Yaʿūqa = DIPTOTES."]},
  {ar:"وَقَدْ أَضَلُّوا كَثِيرًا ۖ وَلَا تَزِدِ الظَّالِمِينَ إِلَّا ضَلَالًا", tr:"Wa qad aḍallū kathīrā · wa lā tazidi'ẓ-ẓālimīna illā ḍalālā", v:"24", root:"Aḍallū — Form IV of ḍalla = to LEAD ASTRAY (ḍāllah = a LOST CAMEL — Ḍuḥā 93:7; Fīl 105:2 taḍlīl; Muddaththir 74:31 yuḍillu).", meaning:"\"And they have led many astray. And [my Lord], increase the wrongdoers in nothing but straying.\"", magic:"TWO HALVES: (a) aḍallū kathīrā — \"they [the idols / the chiefs] have misled MANY\" — the five idols misled countless generations (all the way to pre-Islamic Arabia!).", grammar:"Wa qad + past = \"and indeed they have.\" lā tazidi = jussive (prohibition/supplication) of a hollow verb (tazīd → tazid), kasrah added before aẓ-ẓālimīn.", why:"ẓālimīn — names them by the darkness they chose, before praying they stay in it.", note:"They MISLED many — the five idols, all the way to pre-Islamic Arabia.", more:["kathīr — MANY (k-th-r — Takāthur, Kawthar!).","ẓālimīn — the \"DARKNESS / wrongdoing\" family — ẓalama = to wrong, to put something in the wrong place; ẓulm = INJUSTICE; ẓulmah = DARKNESS (same root!); ẓālim = a WRONGDOER / tyrant.","(b) wa lā tazidi'ẓ-ẓālimīna illā ḍalālā — Nūḥ's first prayer AGAINST them: \"increase the wrongdoers only in straying.\" After 950 years, the prophet of patience finally prays against them — because Allah told him: lan yuʾmina min qawmika illā man qad āman (11:36 — \"none of your people will believe except those who already have\").","He prayed against them only once the door was confirmed shut.","Ẓulm (INJUSTICE) and ẓulmah (DARKNESS) — ONE ROOT — the ḥadīth: \"Aẓ-ẓulmu ẓulumātun yawma'l-qiyāmah\" — \"INJUSTICE will be DARKNESSES on the Day of Resurrection.\" Wrongdoing is darkness; the wrongdoer walks in the dark.","Ẓalama = putting a thing in the WRONG PLACE — and the greatest ẓulm is shirk (31:13) — putting worship in the wrong place (in Wadd and Nasr).","Zāda… illā #3: ḌALĀLĀ — v.6 fleeing → v.21 loss → v.24 STRAYING → v.28 ruin.","Ḍalla thread: Ḍuḥā 93:7 — wa wajadaka ḍāllan fa-hadā (He found you SEARCHING → guided).","Fīl 105:2 — their plot sent astray.","Muddaththir 74:31 — yuḍillu man yashāʾ.","Nūḥ 71:24 — they misled, and stray further.","The shift from report (\"they misled\") to prayer (\"increase them…\") = iltifāt.","Nūḥ's FIRST prayer AGAINST them — only after Allah said \"none will believe except those who have\" (11:36). ẒULM (injustice) = ẒULMAH (DARKNESS) — \"injustice will be DARKNESSES on that Day.\" Greatest ẓulm = shirk (31:13). Zāda… illā #3: ḌALĀLĀ."]},
  {ar:"مِّمَّا خَطِيئَاتِهِمْ أُغْرِقُوا", tr:"Mimmā khaṭīʾātihim ughriqū", v:"25", root:"Mimmā — min + mā — here the mā is EXTRA (mā ṣilah / zāʾidah) for EMPHASIS — \"because of their sins.", meaning:"\"Because of their sins they were drowned…\"", magic:"THE FLOOD — in ONE WORD: ughriqū (they were DROWNED). 950 years of warning → one word of ending.", grammar:"Mimmā = min + extra mā (ṣilah — emphasis).", why:"Fronting the SINS before the DROWNING — cause before effect, unmistakably.", note:"UGHRIQŪ = they were DROWNED — 950 years of warning, one word of ending. Ḥāqqah 69:11: the ARK side (CARRIED).", more:["Their very sins.\" khaṭīʾāt — SINS (deliberate — khaṭiʾa; Ḥāqqah 69:9, 37 — al-khāṭiʾah, al-khāṭiʾūn; ʿAlaq 96:16 — khāṭiʾah).","ughriqū — the \"DROWNING / sinking\" family — ghariqa = to drown; gharaq = DROWNING; gharīq = DROWNED (mustaghriq = absorbed / engrossed — \"drowned\" in a task!; istighrāq = total absorption); Nāziʿāt 79:1 — wa'n-nāziʿāti gharqā (pulling out souls \"drowningly\" — violently!).","Compare Ḥāqqah 69:11 — innā lammā ṭagha'l-māʾu ḥamalnākum fi'l-jāriyah — the water OVERFLOWED (ṭaghā); the believers were CARRIED in the ship.","Ḥāqqah 69:11 shows the ARK side (saved); Nūḥ 71:25 shows the WATER side (drowned).","The same flood, from both sides, in surahs two apart.","MIMMĀ khaṭīʾātihim — \"because of their SINS\" — FRONTED — the cause comes FIRST, before the punishment.","Emphasis: \"it was their SINS — nothing else — that drowned them.\" And the extra mā doubles the emphasis.","Not bad luck, not nature — their deliberate sins (khaṭīʾāt, Form I).","Gharqā — Nāziʿāt 79:1 — the angels pulling out wicked souls \"DROWNINGLY\" (with violent force) — and here, literal drowning.","Mustaghriq = ENGROSSED — \"drowned\" in something.","And PHARAOH was drowned too (Muzzammil 73:16 akhdhan wabīlā — the \"downpour\" seizure).","Two great drownings: Nūḥ's people and Pharaoh.","mimmā khaṭīʾātihim FRONTED before the verb = restriction/emphasis on the cause.","ughriqū = Form IV passive.","Nūḥ 71:25: the WATER side (DROWNED).","MIMMĀ KHAṬĪʾĀTIHIM FRONTED + extra mā = \"because of their SINS — nothing else.\" Nāziʿāt 79:1 gharqā; mustaghriq = ENGROSSED."]},
  {ar:"فَأُدْخِلُوا نَارًا", tr:"Fa-udkhilū nārā", v:"25", root:"Udkhilū — they were MADE TO ENTER (Form IV passive of dakhala). nār — FIRE.", meaning:"\"…and then [immediately] made to enter a Fire.\"", magic:"WATER → then FIRE — and the fa is the evidence.", grammar:"Fa = immediate sequence (taʿqīb) — the key to the barzakh reading. udkhilū = Form IV passive.", why:"The fa compresses drowning and burning into one moment — and opens a window on the barzakh.", note:"DROWNED → FA (IMMEDIATELY) → FIRE. The fa = no gap → evidence for punishment in the BARZAKH (the grave).", more:["Ughriqū FA-udkhilū nārā — \"they were drowned, and THEN IMMEDIATELY (fa) made to enter a Fire.\" The fa means IMMEDIATE sequence — no gap — but the Day of Judgment is still to come!","So classical scholars cite this verse as evidence for punishment in the GRAVE / the BARZAKH — the interval between death and resurrection.","They drowned in WATER and immediately entered FIRE.","The paradox: drowning in water → burning in fire — opposites, back to back — classical: \"they drowned in water and burned in fire\" — a striking ṭibāq.","Nāran — INDEFINITE — \"A fire\" — unspecified, vast.","Udkhilū — MADE TO ENTER (passive) — contrast Fajr 89:29–30 — fa'dkhulī fī ʿibādī · wa'dkhulī jannatī — the content soul is INVITED to enter (active imperative); Nūḥ's people are FORCED to enter (passive).","And Maʿārij 70:38 — an yudkhala jannata naʿīm — the greedy COVETED being admitted to Paradise.","Drowned in WATER, burned in FIRE — opposites back to back.","Fajr 89:29–30: INVITED to enter (udkhulī).","Nūḥ 71:25: FORCED to enter (udkhilū).","Maʿārij 70:38: COVETED being admitted."]},
  {ar:"فَلَمْ يَجِدُوا لَهُم مِّن دُونِ اللَّهِ أَنصَارًا", tr:"Fa-lam yajidū lahum min dūni'llāhi anṣārā", v:"25", root:"Yajidū — they FOUND. min dūni'llāh — \"BESIDES Allah / apart from Allah\".", meaning:"\"…and they found for themselves no helpers besides Allah.\"", magic:"v.23: \"never abandon Wadd, Suwāʿ, Yaghūth, Yaʿūq, Nasr!\" v.25: \"they found NO HELPERS.\" YAGHŪTH — \"HE RESCUES\" — didn't rescue. Not one idol lifted a finger as the water rose.", grammar:"Lam + jussive (yajidū, nūn dropped). anṣāran = object (plural of nāṣir).", why:"Anṣār — the idols' entire purpose (help, rescue) shown to be empty.", note:"WHERE WERE THE IDOLS? YAGHŪTH (\"he RESCUES\") — didn't rescue. NO helpers.", more:["anṣār — HELPERS (al-Anṣār of Madīnah; Naṣr 110:1 naṣru'llāh; Ṭāriq 86:10 wa lā nāṣir; Jinn 72:24 aḍʿafu nāṣirā).","WHERE WERE THE FIVE IDOLS?","The names promised help; the reality was silence.","The NĀṢIR thread: Ṭāriq 86:10 — fa-mā lahu min quwwatin wa lā nāṣir (no strength, NO HELPER).","Jinn 72:24 — man aḍʿafu nāṣiran wa aqallu ʿadadā (who is WEAKER in helpers).","Nūḥ 71:25 — lam yajidū lahum… anṣārā (found NO helpers).","Naṣr 110:1 — idhā jāʾa naṣru'llāh (when ALLAH'S HELP comes).","The believers: naṣr Allāh.","Help is His alone to give.","Min dūni'llāh — \"besides Allah\" — every helper except Him failed — compare Jinn 72:22 — wa lan ajida min dūnihi multaḥadā (I'll find no REFUGE besides Him).","Nāṣir thread: no helper (86:10), weaker in helpers (72:24), NO HELPERS (71:25) vs NAṢR ALLĀH (110:1). Help is His alone.","Jinn 72:22: no refuge besides Him."]},
  {ar:"وَقَالَ نُوحٌ رَّبِّ لَا تَذَرْ عَلَى الْأَرْضِ مِنَ الْكَافِرِينَ دَيَّارًا", tr:"Wa qāla nūḥun rabbi lā tadhar ʿala'l-arḍi mina'l-kāfirīna dayyārā", v:"26", root:"Lā tadhar — \"do not LEAVE\" (the defective verb wadhara — again!).", meaning:"\"And Nūḥ said: 'My Lord, do not leave upon the earth any dweller from among the disbelievers.'\"", magic:"v.23 — the chiefs: lā TADHARUNNA ālihatakum — \"NEVER LEAVE your gods!\" v.26 — Nūḥ: lā TADHAR ʿala'l-arḍi… dayyārā — \"LEAVE not one of them on earth!\" They swore never to LEAVE their idols — Nūḥ prays that THEY not be LEFT.", grammar:"Lā tadhar = prohibition/supplication + jussive of the defective verb.", why:"Dayyār — a negation-only word for total absence, matching the totality of the flood.", note:"V.23 chiefs: \"NEVER LEAVE your gods\" (lā tadharunna) → v.26 Nūḥ: \"LEAVE not one of them\" (lā tadhar). The verb thrown back.", more:["dayyār — the \"DWELLING / house\" family — dār = a HOUSE, an abode (dār al-ākhirah = the Abode of the Hereafter; diyār = homes / lands; dāʾirah = a CIRCLE / a department — from dāra, to go around!); dayyār = a DWELLER, an inhabitant — \"anyone at all who lives in a house\" (used ONLY in negation: mā fi'd-dāri dayyār = \"there's not a soul in the house\").","THE DHAR VERB THROWN BACK.","The same defective verb, turned against them.","Dayyār — \"a single DWELLER\" — a word used only in NEGATION — mā fi'd-dāri dayyār = \"not a SOUL in the house\" — total emptiness.","Like aḥad in negation (Ikhlāṣ 112:4, Ḥāqqah 69:47). Not one house left inhabited by a denier.","And why such a severe prayer from the most patient prophet?","v.27 explains — and Allah had already told him (11:36) that no more would believe.","It's a prayer born of knowledge, not anger.","Dāʾirah = a CIRCLE / a government DEPARTMENT — same root as dār (what \"goes around\" a space).","dayyāran = object — only used in negative contexts.","DAYYĀR = a single DWELLER — NEGATION-ONLY: mā fi'd-dāri dayyār = \"not a SOUL in the house.\" Like aḥad in negation (112:4, 69:47). Severe — but born of KNOWLEDGE (11:36), not anger.","Dār = HOUSE; dāʾirah = CIRCLE / DEPARTMENT."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّكَ إِن تَذَرْهُمْ يُضِلُّوا عِبَادَكَ", tr:"Innaka in tadharhum yuḍillū ʿibādak", v:"27", root:"Tadharhum — [if] You LEAVE them (the defective verb, again). yuḍillū — they will LEAD ASTRAY (Form IV of ḍalla).", meaning:"\"Indeed, if You leave them, they will mislead Your servants…\"", magic:"THE REASON for the prayer — protection of the believers.", grammar:"In (condition) + jussive (tadhar) + jussive answer (yuḍillū, nūn dropped). ʿibādaka = object.", why:"ʿibādak — reveals the motive: protecting Allah's servants.", note:"WHY the prayer? \"If You leave them, they'll MISLEAD YOUR SERVANTS.\" Protection, not revenge.", more:["ʿibād — SERVANTS (ʿabd; Fajr 89:29 fa'dkhulī fī ʿibādī!).","In tadharhum yuḍillū ʿibādak — \"if You LEAVE them, they will mislead YOUR SERVANTS.\" Nūḥ isn't praying out of revenge — he's protecting the few believers and future generations.","He's seen 950 years of evidence: they mislead (v.24 — aḍallū kathīrā), they'll keep misleading.","Dhar — FOURTH use in this surah: v.23 lā tadharunna (×2 — \"never leave your gods\"), v.26 lā tadhar (leave not one), v.27 in tadharhum (if You leave them).","The whole ending pivots on who LEAVES whom.","ʿIbādak — \"YOUR servants\" — possessive, protective — \"Your own\" — Fajr 89:29 — fa'dkhulī fī ʿibādī — \"enter among MY servants\" — the same honoured group.","Dhar ×4 in this surah's end: v.23 (×2), v.26, v.27.","ʿIbādak — Fajr 89:29 fī ʿibādī."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا يَلِدُوا إِلَّا فَاجِرًا كَفَّارًا", tr:"Wa lā yalidū illā fājiran kaffārā", v:"27", root:"Yalidū — they BEGET (walada — Ikhlāṣ 112:3 lam yalid!; Balad 90:3 wālid wa mā walad).", meaning:"\"…and they will not beget except wicked, utterly ungrateful [children].\"", magic:"THE TWO ROOTS YOU'VE TRACKED LONGEST — together, at the end.", grammar:"Lā yalidū = jussive (joined to yuḍillū). fājiran kaffāran = two descriptions — faʿʿāl intensive for kaffār.", why:"Kaffār — the exact faʿʿāl mirror of ghaffār in v.10.", note:"V.10 GHAFFĀRĀ (Allah — forgiving ENDLESSLY) vs v.27 KAFFĀRĀ (them — ungrateful ENDLESSLY). ONE LETTER, same faʿʿāl.", more:["fājir — the \"BURSTING through limits\" family — fajara = to burst open; fujūr = wickedness (bursting moral limits); fajr = DAWN (light bursting through!); fujjār — Infiṭār 82:14; fujūrahā — Shams 91:8. kaffār — the faʿʿāl INTENSIVE of kāfir = one who DISBELIEVES / is UNGRATEFUL again and again, endlessly (kafara = to bury a seed / cover over; kaffārah = EXPIATION).","FĀJIR — the f-j-r root — your longest thread (EIGHT appearances): sin (Qiyāmah 75:5), a spring (Insān 76:6), the wicked (ʿAbasa 80:42, Infiṭār 82:14), seas bursting (82:3), dawn (Fajr 89:1, Qadr 97:5), the soul's capacity (Shams 91:8) — and now: fājiran (Nūḥ 71:27). KAFFĀR — the faʿʿāl intensive — and the ONE-LETTER CONTRAST: v.10 — innahu kāna GHAFFĀRĀ — ALLAH: forgiving AGAIN AND AGAIN.","v.27 — fājiran KAFFĀRĀ — THEM: ungrateful AGAIN AND AGAIN.","GHAFFĀR vs KAFFĀR — GH vs K — ONE LETTER, same faʿʿāl pattern.","Endless forgiveness offered; endless ingratitude returned.","The whole surah in two words.","Kaffār — the k-f-r intensive ladder: kāfir (one who disbelieves) → kafūr (Insān 76:3 — immā shākiran wa immā kafūrā — utterly ungrateful) → kaffār (Nūḥ 71:27 — endlessly so).","ʿĀdiyāt 100:6 — la-kanūd (ungrateful — barren ground).","Lā yalidū — \"they will not BEGET\" — the w-l-d root of Ikhlāṣ (lam yalid — Allah does NOT beget) and Balad 90:3 (wa wālidin wa mā walad).","Nūḥ knew from experience: 950 years, generation after generation, the children turned out like the parents — the corruption had become hereditary culture.","The whole surah in two words.","FĀJIR — the f-j-r thread: NINE appearances now.","K-f-r ladder: kāfir → kafūr (76:3) → kaffār (71:27). Lā yalidū — w-l-d of Ikhlāṣ 112:3. Corruption had become hereditary."]},
  {ar:"رَّبِّ اغْفِرْ لِي وَلِوَالِدَيَّ", tr:"Rabbi'ghfir lī wa li-wālidayya", v:"28", root:"Ighfir — FORGIVE (imperative — mighfar = helmet).", meaning:"\"My Lord, forgive me and my parents…\"", magic:"THE MOST BELOVED DUʿĀʾ IN THE SURAH — and the prophet begins with HIMSELF.", grammar:"Ighfir = imperative (supplication).", why:"Self first, parents second — the humble order of every prophetic duʿāʾ.", note:"RABBI'GHFIR LĪ — after 950 YEARS, he asks forgiveness for HIMSELF FIRST.", more:["wālidayya — MY TWO PARENTS (dual of wālid + -ya, my — wālidayn = parents; birr al-wālidayn = dutifulness to PARENTS!).","Rabbi'ghfir LĪ — \"forgive ME\" — after 950 years of tireless da'wah, the first person he asks forgiveness for is HIMSELF.","Exactly like NAṢR 110:3 — after the Prophet's ﷺ completed mission: wa'staghfirh — seek forgiveness.","Every great task ends in istighfār — Nūḥ's after 950 years, Muhammad's ﷺ after 23. And v.10 — he told his people istaghfirū (seek forgiveness); v.28 — he does it himself.","The preacher practises what he preached.","Wa li-WĀLIDAYYA — \"and my PARENTS\" — second in line — classical note: Nūḥ's parents were believers (unlike his son — 11:42–46). This duʿāʾ is used constantly by Muslims — compare Ibrāhīm's: rabbana'ghfir lī wa li-wālidayya wa li'l-muʾminīna yawma yaqūmu'l-ḥisāb (14:41) — nearly IDENTICAL — two great prophets, the same prayer for themselves, their parents, and all believers.","Wālidayya — the w-l-d root again — v.27: the disbelievers BEGET (yalidū) only wicked children → v.28: Nūḥ prays for those who BEGOT HIM (wālidayya).","The same root: bad offspring vs blessed parents.","Birr al-wālidayn = honouring PARENTS.","wālidayya = dual wālidayn + -ya — the nūn drops and the two yāʾs merge with a shaddah (wālidayni + ya → wālidayya).","= Naṣr 110:3 wa'staghfirh: every great mission ends in ISTIGHFĀR (Nūḥ after 950 years, Muhammad ﷺ after 23). v.10 he TAUGHT istighfirū; v.28 he PRACTISED it.","= Ibrāhīm 14:41: rabbana'ghfir lī wa li-wālidayya — NEARLY IDENTICAL.","v.27 they BEGET wicked → v.28 he prays for those who BEGOT him.","Wālidayya = dual + yāʾ, merged."]},
  {ar:"وَلِمَن دَخَلَ بَيْتِيَ مُؤْمِنًا وَلِلْمُؤْمِنِينَ وَالْمُؤْمِنَاتِ", tr:"Wa li-man dakhala baytiya muʾminan wa li'l-muʾminīna wa'l-muʾmināt", v:"28", root:"Dakhala — ENTERED. bayt — a HOUSE (al-Bayt — the Kaʿbah, Quraysh 106:3 rabba hādha'l-bayt!; bayt = a line of POETRY!).", meaning:"\"…and whoever enters my house as a believer, and the believing men and the believing women…\"", magic:"THE CIRCLES OF MERCY — widening outward: (1) ME (lī) → (2) my PARENTS (wālidayya) → (3) whoever ENTERS MY HOUSE as a believer → (4) ALL believing men and women — for all time.", grammar:"Muʾminan = ḥāl (\"as a believer\" — the condition). baytiya — yāʾ with fatḥah.", why:"The condition muʾminan — so proximity to a prophet isn't mistaken for faith.", note:"WIDENING CIRCLES: ME → my PARENTS → whoever enters my HOUSE as a believer → ALL believing men and women, for all time.", more:["muʾmin — a BELIEVER (īmān ← amn, security).","muʾmināt — believing WOMEN.","From the self to the whole ummah of believers across history — including YOU, thousands of years later.","Man dakhala BAYTIYA MUʾMINAN — \"whoever enters my HOUSE as a BELIEVER\" — classical readings: his house / his mosque / his ARK (the ship as his house!) — and the condition muʾminan (as a believer) excludes his disbelieving son and wife (66:10). Being in the prophet's house isn't enough — you must enter it AS A BELIEVER.","Al-muʾminīna wa'l-muʾmināt — believing men AND believing women, named separately — equally included in the prayer (compare 33:35 — the famous verse listing men and women side by side).","Bayt — the HOUSE thread: Quraysh 106:3 — fal-yaʿbudū rabba hādha'l-bayt (the Lord of THIS HOUSE — the Kaʿbah).","Nūḥ 71:28 — li-man dakhala baytiya muʾminā (whoever enters MY house as a believer).","And dayyār (v.26) — a DWELLER in a house — no disbelieving dweller left; every believing guest forgiven.","Baytiya — the possessive yāʾ carries a FATḤAH here (baytiya, not baytī) — to avoid two sukūns / for flow before muʾminan.","MUʾMINAN (AS a believer) = the CONDITION — excludes his son and wife (66:10). Proximity ≠ faith.","Bayt: Quraysh 106:3 (the Kaʿbah) / 71:28 (Nūḥ's house — or ark).","Men AND women named separately.","Baytiya — yāʾ with fatḥah."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا تَزِدِ الظَّالِمِينَ إِلَّا تَبَارًا", tr:"Wa lā tazidi'ẓ-ẓālimīna illā tabārā", v:"28", root:"Tazid — INCREASE (jussive — supplication). ẓālimīn — WRONGDOERS (ẓulm = injustice = ẓulmah, darkness).", meaning:"\"…and increase the wrongdoers in nothing but ruin.\"", magic:"THE SURAH'S LAST WORD — TABĀRĀ (RUIN) — and the zāda… illā pattern completes.", grammar:"Lā tazidi = jussive supplication (hollow verb shortened; kasrah for two sukūns). illā tabāran = restriction.", why:"Tabār — completes the four-step zāda… illā descent with its final stage.", note:"ZĀDA… ILLĀ ×4: FLEEING (v.6) → LOSS (v.21) → STRAYING (v.24) → RUIN (v.28). The four-step descent.", more:["tabār — the \"DESTRUCTION / ruin\" family — tabbara = to destroy, shatter; tabār = RUIN, destruction; mutabbar = destroyed; tibr = GOLD DUST / unminted gold (gold that's been \"broken\" into fragments!).","NOT the same root as tabbat (Masad 111:1 — t-b-b, to perish) — this is t-b-r.","Zāda… illā — FOUR times: v.6 — lam yazidhum duʿāʾī illā FIRĀRĀ (my call increased them only in FLEEING).","v.21 — lam yazidhu māluhu wa waladuhu illā KHASĀRĀ (wealth increased him only in LOSS).","v.24 — wa lā tazidi'ẓ-ẓālimīna illā ḌALĀLĀ (increase them only in STRAYING).","v.28 — wa lā tazidi'ẓ-ẓālimīna illā TABĀRĀ (increase them only in RUIN).","FLEEING → LOSS → STRAYING → RUIN.","The four-step descent of a people who refused 950 years of mercy.","And v.24 and v.28 are IDENTICAL except the last word: ḍalālā → tabārā.","The surah's shape: it opens with andhir (WARN, v.1) and ends with tabārā (RUIN, v.28) — the warning ignored, the ruin arrived.","But the verse just before (v.28a) is FORGIVENESS for all believers — so the surah ends on two destinies: forgiveness for those who entered \"the house\" believing, ruin for those who wronged.","Tabār (t-b-r, RUIN) ≠ tabbat (t-b-b, PERISH — Masad 111:1) — look-alike roots, similar meanings, different words.","Tibr = GOLD DUST — \"shattered\" gold.","And note the -ārā rhyme closes the surah: tabārā — the long, open echo ends on ruin.","v.24 and v.28 IDENTICAL except ḌALĀLĀ → TABĀRĀ: straying ends in ruin.","Opens ANDHIR (warn, v.1) → closes TABĀRĀ (ruin, v.28).","TABĀR (t-b-r) ≠ TABBAT (t-b-b, Masad 111:1). Tibr = GOLD DUST."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:72, name:"Al-Jinn", ar:"الجن", en:"The Jinn", verses:28, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Humans heard it for years and called it magic. Jinn heard it once and called it ʿajab. Aḥad ×6 — Ikhlāṣ, narrated.",
  special:["It's a review of the Qurʾān written by a non-human audience — and their creed is Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ, told as a story.","V.1: innā samiʿnā qurʾānan ʿajabā — \"We have heard a WONDROUS Qurʾān.\"","The jinn listened once, and immediately: believed (v.2), declared His majesty (v.3), denied He has a consort or child (v.3), admitted they had been fooled (v.4–5), and said \"we will never associate anyone with our Lord\" (v.2).","Now count the word aḥad in this surah — it appears SIX times (v.2, 7, 18, 20, 22, 26). And v.3 is Ikhlāṣ in other words: mā'ttakhadha ṣāḥibatan wa lā waladā — \"He has taken no consort and no child\" = lam yalid wa lam yūlad. You just finished Ikhlāṣ; Al-Jinn is the same creed, spoken by the first beings who heard it and believed on the spot.","And the shame in it: Quraysh heard the Qurʾān for years and called it magic (Muddaththir 74:24). A band of jinn heard it ONCE and called it a wonder.","The rhyme: almost every verse ends in -adā — ʿajabā, aḥadā, waladā, shaṭaṭā, rahaqā, raṣadā, rashadā, qidadā, ḥaṭabā, ghadaqā, ṣaʿadā, libadā, multaḥadā, amadā, ʿadadā — a surah full of verse-ending nouns, exactly the category you study.","--"],
  big:["The listening (v.1–2) — a wondrous Qurʾān that guides to maturity.","Their creed (v.3–7) — His majesty, no consort or child, the lies they once believed.","The sealed sky (v.8–10) — guards, meteors, and a humble \"we don't know.\"","Two kinds of jinn (v.11–15) — righteous and otherwise; submitters and deviators.","The promise of rain (v.16–17) — uprightness brings abundance — and a test.","The mosques and the crowd (v.18–19) — worship belongs to Allah; they crowded around His servant.","The Messenger's limits (v.20–23) — say: I own no harm or guidance for you; I only convey.","Who is weaker? (v.24–25) — they'll see; and I don't know when.","The guarded Unseen (v.26–28) — revelation escorted by guards; everything counted."],
  traps:["الْقَاسِطُونَ (v.14) — qāsiṭ (Form I) = UNJUST; muqsiṭ (Form IV) = JUST (al-Muqsiṭ is a Divine Name). Same root, opposite meanings.","ظَنَنَّا in v.5 vs v.12 — v.5 = a mistaken ASSUMPTION; v.12 = CERTAINTY. Same word, same surah.","رَصَدًا in v.9 vs v.27 — v.9 guards BLOCK thieves; v.27 guards ESCORT revelation. Read them together.","V.10 adab — evil is PASSIVE (urīda, no doer); good is ACTIVE with \"their Lord\" as subject. Don't miss the asymmetry.","يُجِيرَنِي (v.22) — from j-w-r (neighbour/protection), NOT ajr (wage) or ījār (rent).","إِنْ أَدْرِي (v.25) — in = NOT, not \"if.\" Same as Takwīr 81:27.","لَمَّا (v.13, 19) — + PAST = \"when / as soon as.\" Compare ʿAbasa 80:23: lammā + JUSSIVE = \"not yet.\"","عَجَبًا (v.1) — a NOUN used as an adjective (waṣf bi'l-maṣdar) = \"wonder itself,\" not merely \"wonderful.\" Same device in raṣadā (v.9) and ṣaʿadā (v.17).","اسْتَمَعَ (v.1, 9) — Form VIII = LISTEN attentively (≠ samiʿa, hear). Used for faithful listening (v.1) AND eavesdropping (v.9).","لَهُ... خَالِدِينَ (v.23) — man = singular FORM, plural MEANING — hence the switch from lahu to khālidīna.","وَأَنَّا / وَإِنَّا (v.1–14) — two authentic recitations (fatḥah / kasrah). Same meaning.","Diptotes — maqāʿida (v.9), ṭarāʾiqa (v.11), al-masājida (v.18), jahannama (v.15, 23) — no tanwīn, fatḥah in the genitive.","V.16 onward — generally read as Allah's words, no longer the jinn's quoted speech.","يَعْصِ (v.23) — jussive drops the final yāʾ."],
  links:[{w:"أَحَدًا ×6 (v.2, 7, 18, 20, 22, 26)",e:"أَحَدٌ (Ikhlāṣ 112:1, 4)",t:"Ikhlāṣ narrated — six aḥads"},{w:"مَا اتَّخَذَ صَاحِبَةً وَلَا وَلَدًا (v.3)",e:"لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ (Ikhlāṣ 112:3)",t:"Same denial, in the jinn's words"},{w:"أُوحِيَ (v.1)",e:"أَوْحَىٰ لَهَا (Zalzalah 99:5)",t:"Revelation to the earth / to the Prophet ﷺ"},{w:"نَفَرٌ (v.1)",e:"مُّسْتَنفِرَةٌ (Muddaththir 74:50)",t:"Rushing TOWARD vs. bolting AWAY"},{w:"يَعُوذُونَ (v.6)",e:"أَعُوذُ (Falaq 113:1 · Nās 114:1)",t:"False refuge vs. true refuge"},{w:"رَهَقًا (v.6, 13)",e:"تَرْهَقُهَا (ʿAbasa 80:41) · سَأُرْهِقُهُ (74:17)",t:"Burden added (v.6) / removed (v.13)"},{w:"جَدُّ (v.3)",e:"Wa taʿālā jadduk (opening duʿāʾ of ṣalāh)",t:"You say it every prayer"},{w:"شَدِيدًا (v.8)",e:"78:12 · 76:28 · 85:12 · 100:8",t:"The shadīd thread"},{w:"رَصَدًا (v.9, 27)",e:"مِرْصَادًا (78:21) · الْمِرْصَادِ (89:14)",t:"Block thieves / escort revelation"},{w:"مَقَاعِدَ (v.9)",e:"قُعُودٌ (Burūj 85:6)",t:"Two kinds of sitting"},{w:"أَشَرٌّ أُرِيدَ... أَمْ أَرَادَ... رَبُّهُمْ (v.10)",e:"مِن شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ (Falaq 113:2)",t:"Adab: evil passive, good attributed to Him"},{w:"قِدَدًا (v.11)",e:"شَتَّىٰ (92:4) · أَشْتَاتًا (99:6)",t:"Creatures diverge"},{w:"نُّعْجِزَ (v.12)",e:"Muʿjizah",t:"The root of \"miracle\""},{w:"بَخْسًا (v.13)",e:"الْمُطَفِّفِينَ (83:1) · مِثْقَالَ ذَرَّةٍ (99:7)",t:"Never short-changed"},{w:"الْقَاسِطُونَ (v.14–15)",e:"Al-Muqsiṭ",t:"Form I unjust / Form IV just"},{w:"حَطَبًا (v.15)",e:"حَمَّالَةَ الْحَطَبِ (Masad 111:4)",t:"She carries the firewood / they ARE the firewood"},{w:"اسْتَقَامُوا (v.16)",e:"81:28 · 95:4 · 98:3, 5",t:"The q-w-m thread"},{w:"لِّنَفْتِنَهُمْ (v.17)",e:"فَتَنُوا (Burūj 85:10) · ابْتَلَاهُ (Fajr 89:15)",t:"Even abundance is a test"},{w:"يُعْرِضْ (v.17)",e:"يَتَجَنَّبُهَا (87:11) · مُعْرِضِينَ (74:49)",t:"Turning your side"},{w:"صَعَدًا (v.17)",e:"صَعُودًا (Muddaththir 74:17) · الْعَقَبَةَ (90:11)",t:"BOTH VERSE 17 — the climbing punishment"},{w:"الْمَسَاجِدَ (v.18)",e:"وَاسْجُدْ (ʿAlaq 96:19)",t:"Prostration belongs to Him"},{w:"عَبْدُ اللَّهِ (v.19)",e:"عَبْدًا إِذَا صَلَّىٰ (ʿAlaq 96:10)",t:"The praying servant — blocked / crowded around"},{w:"لِبَدًا (v.19)",e:"لُّبَدًا (Balad 90:6)",t:"Wealth piled up / listeners piled up"},{w:"ضَرًّا وَلَا رَشَدًا (v.21)",e:"بِمُصَيْطِرٍ (Ghāshiyah 88:22)",t:"The Messenger's limits"},{w:"مُلْتَحَدًا (v.22)",e:"وَزَرَ (75:11) · الْمَفَرّ (75:10)",t:"Three refuges denied"},{w:"بَلَاغًا (v.23)",e:"مُذَكِّرٌ (88:21) · مُنذِرُ (79:45)",t:"The Prophet's ﷺ job titles — and balāghah"},{w:"خَالِدِينَ فِيهَا أَبَدًا (v.23)",e:"Bayyinah 98:8",t:"Same phrase, opposite destinations"},{w:"نَاصِرًا (v.24)",e:"نَاصِرٍ (86:10) · نَصْرُ (110:1)",t:"The \"few\" got help; the boasters had none"},{w:"إِنْ أَدْرِي (v.25)",e:"لَا نَدْرِي (v.10, same surah)",t:"Jinn and Prophet ﷺ: \"I don't know\""},{w:"أَمَدًا (v.25)",e:"مُرْسَاهَا (Nāziʿāt 79:42)",t:"The Hour has a harbour; the date is hidden"},{w:"الْغَيْبِ (v.26)",e:"بِغَائِبِينَ (Infiṭār 82:16)",t:"The unseen"},{w:"ارْتَضَىٰ (v.27)",e:"رَاضِيَةً مَّرْضِيَّةً (89:28)",t:"Allah approving a messenger"},{w:"أَحَاطَ (v.28)",e:"مُّحِيطٌ (Burūj 85:20)",t:"Encircling — trap for enemies, safeguard for messengers"},{w:"وَأَحْصَىٰ كُلَّ شَيْءٍ (v.28)",e:"وَكُلَّ شَيْءٍ أَحْصَيْنَاهُ (Nabaʾ 78:29)",t:"Counted into a book / counted by number"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"قُلْ أُوحِيَ إِلَيَّ", tr:"Qul ūḥiya ilayya", v:"1", root:"The \"swift, subtle communication\" family — waḥā = to signal quickly and secretly; waḥy = REVELATION.", meaning:"\"Say: It has been revealed to me…\"", magic:"The passive ūḥiya is the whole point of the opening. The Prophet ﷺ did not see the jinn listening — he was told.", grammar:"ūḥiya = Form IV passive, weak-final. ilayya = ilā + -ya (to me), the yāʾ doubled.", why:"Waḥy rather than \"I learned\" — it marks the jinn story as revealed knowledge, not observation.", note:"He didn't SEE them — it was REVEALED to him (ūḥiya). Waḥy to the earth (99:5) and to the Prophet ﷺ (72:1).", more:["The surah is itself a piece of news about the unseen, delivered through the unseen channel.","You own awḥā from Zalzalah 99:5 (bi-anna rabbaka awḥā lahā) — the revelation-word used for the earth.","The same word: revelation to the earth (so it testifies), and revelation to the Prophet ﷺ (so he knows who listened)."]},
  {ar:"اسْتَمَعَ", tr:"Istamaʿa", v:"1 · 9", root:"The \"hearing\" family — samiʿa = to hear; Form VIII istamaʿa = to LISTEN ATTENTIVELY, deliberately, with effort.", meaning:"\"…that a band of jinn listened [attentively].\"", magic:"Hearing vs. listening. Samiʿa (Form I) is sound reaching your ears; istamaʿa (Form VIII) is turning your whole attention toward it. The jinn didn't overhear — they listened.", grammar:"Form VIII = effort/for oneself (the infixed -t-).", why:"Istamaʿa rather than samiʿa — because their attentiveness is the contrast with Quraysh's turning away.", note:"Samiʿa = HEARD. Istamaʿa = LISTENED (on purpose). v.1: listening to the Qurʾān → guidance.", more:["Istimāʿ = listening; mustamiʿ = a listener.","And the same verb returns in v.9 — fa-man yastamiʿi'l-āna — for jinn trying to eavesdrop on the heavens.","One Form VIII verb, two kinds of listening: stealing secrets from the sky (v.9), and receiving the Qurʾān (v.1).","The first earned a meteor; the second earned guidance.","Anna-hu'stamaʿa — the -hu is ḍamīr ash-shaʾn (\"that [the matter is]…\").","v.9: listening to steal from the sky → a meteor."]},
  {ar:"نَفَرٌ", tr:"Nafar", v:"1", root:"The \"rushing out / setting off in a group\" family — nafara = to go out, to rush forth.", meaning:"\"a small band [of jinn]\"", magic:"The root holds both directions of rushing — toward (a nafar setting out, a nafīr mobilizing) and away (nufūr, aversion).", grammar:"Nafarun = indefinite (\"a band\"), subject of istamaʿa. A collective noun (no singular from its root).", why:"Nafar names a small group on the move, which is exactly what they were — travellers searching.", note:"Nafīr = a CALL TO ARMS; nufūr = AVERSION. Jinn 72:1: a nafar rushes TOWARD the Qurʾān.", more:["Nafar = a small band (classically 3 to 10); nafīr = a CALL TO ARMS / general mobilization; nufūr = aversion (rushing away from something).","And it returns in Muddaththir 74:50: ka-annahum ḥumurun mustanfirah — the deniers as wild donkeys bolting in panic.","Same root: a nafar of jinn rushing TOWARD the Qurʾān (72:1); humans mustanfir — bolting AWAY from it (74:50). And the number: just a small band — not a nation.","Small, sincere, and changed.","Muddaththir 74:50: humans mustanfirah — bolt AWAY."]},
  {ar:"الْجِنِّ", tr:"Al-jinn", v:"1", root:"The \"COVERING / concealing\" family — janna = to cover; jinn = the hidden beings.", meaning:"\"[a band] of the jinn\"", magic:"You own this root from Takwīr 81:22 (bi-majnūn), Fajr 89:30 (jannatī), and Nās 114:6 (mina'l-jinnah wa'n-nās).", grammar:"Al-jinn = collective; genitive after min. Compare al-jinnah (Nās 114:6) — same meaning, collective with tāʾ.", why:"The surah is named after them because the hidden world's testimony is the argument.", note:"Janna = TO COVER: jinn (hidden), jannah (shaded garden), janīn (fetus), majnūn (covered mind), junnah (shield).", more:["Jannah = a garden (covered by foliage); janīn = a fetus (covered in the womb); majnūn = one whose mind is covered; junnah = a shield.","Nās ended the Qurʾān with whisperers \"from the jinn and people\" — Al-Jinn shows you jinn who became believers.","The hidden beings aren't all enemies — some heard and submitted (v.14).","Nās 114:6 warned of whispering jinn; Al-Jinn shows believing ones."]},
  {ar:"قُرْآنًا عَجَبًا", tr:"Qurʾānan ʿajabā", v:"1", root:"The \"WONDER / astonishment\" family — ʿajiba = to marvel; ʿajab = wonder (the noun itself); ʿajīb = wonderful.", meaning:"\"We have heard a wondrous Qurʾān.\"", magic:"The grammar is the praise. They don't say qurʾānan ʿajīban (a wonderful Qurʾān, using an adjective).", grammar:"Waṣf bi'l-maṣdar — a maṣdar used as an adjective = maximum intensity. Both accusative (object of samiʿnā).", why:"ʿajab (the noun) instead of ʿajīb (the adjective) — because the adjective would allow degrees; the noun says it is wonder itself.", note:"Not \"a WONDERFUL Qurʾān\" — \"a Qurʾān that IS WONDER.\" A noun used as an adjective = intensity.", more:["Taʿajjub = astonishment; ʿajab adh-dhanab = the tailbone (the \"wonder\" from which man is rebuilt, per hadith!).","They say qurʾānan ʿajabā — using the verbal noun \"wonder\" itself as the description.","This is al-waṣf bi'l-maṣdar — describing something by a noun = \"a Qurʾān that IS wonder itself.\" Like saying \"he is justice\" instead of \"he is just.\" And note they call it by name — Qurʾān.","Their very first word about it is its title: the Recitation (ʿAlaq 96:1, iqraʾ).","ʿAjab adh-dhanab = the TAILBONE — the \"wonder\" bone from which man is rebuilt."]},
  {ar:"يَهْدِي إِلَى الرُّشْدِ", tr:"Yahdī ila'r-rushd", v:"2", root:"The \"RIGHT CONDUCT / maturity / sound judgment\" family — rashada = to be rightly guided, to reach sound judgment.", meaning:"\"It guides to right conduct.\"", magic:"Rushd isn't just \"guidance\" — it's MATURITY. The Qurʾān, in the jinn's words, grows you up: it brings you to the age of sound judgment.", grammar:"Yahdī (present = ongoing) + ilā.", why:"Rushd (maturity) rather than hudā — because the jinn experienced it as waking up, growing up.", note:"Sinn ar-RUSHD = the AGE OF MAJORITY; ar-RĀSHIDŪN = the Rightly-Guided Caliphs; irshādāt = INSTRUCTIONS. The Qurʾān GROWS YOU UP.", more:["Rushd = maturity of mind, right guidance; sinn ar-rushd = the age of legal majority; al-Khulafāʾ ar-Rāshidūn = the Rightly-Guided Caliphs; murshid = a guide/mentor; irshād = guidance (irshādāt = instructions, e.g.","Its opposite is ghayy — reckless error (qad tabayyana'r-rushdu mina'l-ghayy, 2:256). The r-sh-d root appears FOUR times in this surah — v.2 (rushd), v.10 (rashadā), v.14 (taḥarraw rashadā), v.21 (ḍarran wa lā rashadā).","It's the surah's thread: what is maturity, who gives it, who seeks it, and who can't give it.","Rushd (fuʿl) and rashad (faʿal) are two verbal-noun forms of one root, same meaning — the second carries the -adā rhyme.","R-sh-d ×4 in this surah (v.2, 10, 14, 21)."]},
  {ar:"فَآمَنَّا بِهِ ۖ وَلَن نُّشْرِكَ بِرَبِّنَا أَحَدًا", tr:"Fa-āmannā bihi wa lan nushrika bi-rabbinā aḥadā", v:"2", root:"āmannā — the \"safety / trust\" family — amn (security), īmān (faith) (you own it from Tīn 95:3, Quraysh 106:4). nushrik — the \"PARTNERSHIP / sharing\" family — sharika = to share; sharīk = a PARTNER; sharikah = a COMPANY (business!); shirk = associating partners with Allah.", meaning:"\"So we believed in it, and we will never associate anyone with our Lord.\"", magic:"Look at the speed. Fa-āmannā — the fa means \"and so, immediately.\" Heard → believed. No debate, no delay. And lan = \"will NEVER\" — the strongest future negation.", grammar:"Lan + subjunctive = emphatic future negation (\"never\"). nushrika takes bi- for the One associated with.", why:"Shirk — the exact sin the Qurʾān was sent to remove, named in their very first sentence.", note:"SHARIKAH = a COMPANY — shirk = treating Allah like a firm with shareholders. Fa-āmannā = heard → IMMEDIATELY believed.", more:["And aḥadā — the first of SIX in this surah.","You just learned from Ikhlāṣ 112:4 that aḥad in a negative sentence = \"anyone at all.\" Their first creed: \"we will never share our Lord with ANYONE.\" And sharikah = a COMPANY — shirk is treating Allah like a business with shareholders.","Aḥadā — first of SIX in this surah (Ikhlāṣ narrated)."]},
  {ar:"وَأَنَّهُ تَعَالَىٰ جَدُّ رَبِّنَا", tr:"Wa annahu taʿālā jaddu rabbinā", v:"3", root:"Jadd — a word with three famous meanings: (a) MAJESTY / grandeur; (b) a GRANDFATHER (jadd = grandfather in everyday Arabic!); (c) good FORTUNE / luck.", meaning:"\"And exalted is the majesty of our Lord.\"", magic:"You say this phrase in every prayer.", grammar:"Taʿālā = Form VI past, weak-final. jaddu = subject (nominative), in iḍāfah with rabbinā.", why:"Jadd (majesty) — a rare, weighty word, chosen by beings who had just been stunned into awe.", note:"\"Wa taʿālā JADDUK\" — the OPENING DUʿĀʾ of your prayer. Same phrase. Jadd = MAJESTY / GRANDFATHER / FORTUNE.", more:["Taʿālā — Form VI of ʿalā = to be exalted, high above (al-Aʿlā, Aʿlā 87:1).","The opening supplication of ṣalāh (duʿāʾ al-istiftāḥ / thanāʾ): subḥānaka'llāhumma wa bi-ḥamdik, wa tabāraka'smuk, wa taʿālā jadduk, wa lā ilāha ghayruk — \"and exalted is Your MAJESTY.\" Same two words the jinn used.","And the post-prayer dhikr: wa lā yanfaʿu dha'l-jaddi minka'l-jadd — \"the FORTUNE of the fortunate cannot avail him against You.\" There jadd = fortune/wealth; here jadd = majesty.","Same word, two senses, both in your daily prayers.","The idea: the jinn are saying His greatness is far too high to need a consort or child (next word).","Post-prayer: \"the jadd (fortune) of the fortunate won't help against You.\""]},
  {ar:"مَا اتَّخَذَ صَاحِبَةً وَلَا وَلَدًا", tr:"Ma'ttakhadha ṣāḥibatan wa lā waladā", v:"3", root:"Ittakhadha — Form VIII of akhadha (to take) = to TAKE FOR ONESELF, to adopt.", meaning:"\"He has not taken a wife or a child.\"", magic:"This is Ikhlāṣ, in the jinn's words. Ikhlāṣ 112:3 — lam yalid wa lam yūlad (He neither begets nor was begotten).", grammar:"Mā + past = negation. Ittakhadha = Form VIII, with the hamzah of akhadha assimilated into a doubled tāʾ.", why:"Ittakhadha (\"take for oneself\") — it denies even adoption, not only biological begetting.", note:"Ikhlāṣ 112:3: lam yalid. Jinn 72:3: no wife, no walad. The jinn's creed = Ikhlāṣ.", more:["ṣāḥibah — a female companion / wife (ṣāḥib = companion).","walad — a child (walada = to beget).","Jinn 72:3 — ma'ttakhadha ṣāḥibatan wa lā waladā (He has taken no wife and no child).","Same root (w-l-d), same denial — and the jinn add the logical step: no wife either, because a child needs a mother.","You own ṣāḥibah from ʿAbasa 80:36 (wa ṣāḥibatihi wa banīh — the wife you flee on that Day) and ittakhadha from Insān 76:29 (fa-man shāʾa'ttakhadha ilā rabbihi sabīlā).","Ittakhadha = take FOR ONESELF — denies even adoption."]},
  {ar:"سَفِيهُنَا", tr:"Safīhunā", v:"4", root:"The \"LIGHTNESS / flimsiness → foolishness\" family — safiha = to be light-headed, foolish.", meaning:"\"And that our foolish one used to say about Allah an outrageous falsehood.\"", magic:"The root pictures thin, flimsy cloth — a mind with no weight to it, that anything can blow through. Classical readings: the \"safīh\" is Iblīs, or the foolish leaders among the jinn.", grammar:"Safīh = faʿīl adjective used as a noun + -nā (our). Kāna + present = habitual past (\"used to say\").", why:"Safīh (flimsy-minded) rather than kādhib (liar) — it diagnoses lightness of mind, not only dishonesty.", note:"Thawb safīh = FLIMSY CLOTH. Safah = a mind with NO WEIGHT. They call their own leader \"OUR FOOL\" — instant disavowal.", more:["Originally thawb safīh = a FLIMSY, thinly-woven cloth; safah = folly; sufahāʾ = fools (2:13, 2:142).","Notice the jinn's honesty: they call their own former leader \"OUR fool.\" No defending their tribe — immediate disavowal.","Compare Kāfirūn 109 — disavowal of false worship.","And note kāna yaqūlu = used to say, habitually (past continuous)."]},
  {ar:"شَطَطًا", tr:"Shaṭaṭā", v:"4", root:"The \"going FAR beyond the limit\" family — shaṭṭa = to go far away, to exceed.", meaning:"\"…an outrageous / extreme falsehood [about Allah].\"", magic:"The image: a statement so far from the truth it has reached the far bank. Not a small error — a claim that has crossed all the way over.", grammar:"Shaṭaṭan = a verbal noun as cognate-type accusative (\"a saying of extreme excess\"), carrying the -adā-family rhyme.", why:"Shaṭaṭ (distance from truth) — the falsehood is measured by how FAR it went.", note:"SHAṬṬ al-ʿArab — a river's far BANK. Shaṭaṭ = a claim gone all the way to the far bank of falsehood.", more:["Shaṭṭ = a RIVERBANK / shore (the far edge — Shaṭṭ al-ʿArab, the river in Iraq!); shaṭaṭ = excess, going way beyond the truth.","Compare the ṭaghā thread (water bursting its banks: Nabaʾ 78:22, Fajr 89:11, ʿAlaq 96:6). Two water-and-bank images for excess: ṭaghā (overflowing the bank) and shaṭaṭ (going to the far bank).","Compare ṭaghā: water OVER the bank."]},
  {ar:"وَأَنَّا ظَنَنَّا... كَذِبًا", tr:"Wa annā ẓanannā… kadhibā", v:"5", root:"ẓanna — the \"supposing / thinking\" family — ranges from assumption to certainty depending on context. kadhib — a LIE.", meaning:"\"And we had assumed that humans and jinn would never speak a lie about Allah.\"", magic:"A confession of NAÏVETY. The jinn explain why they believed the safīh: \"we assumed no one would DARE lie about Allah.\" They trusted because the subject was too sacred to lie about.", grammar:"ẓanannā = past, 1st plural (doubled nūn). lan taqūla = lan + subjunctive (\"would never say\").", why:"ẓann — admitting their belief was a mere assumption is part of their repentance.", note:"\"We ASSUMED nobody would lie about Allah\" — the trust that misled them. Ẓann here = ASSUMPTION; in Qiyāmah 75:28 = CERTAINTY.", more:["It's a deeply human (and jinn) mistake: assuming that anything said in God's name must be true.","TRAP — ẓanna changes meaning: here it's assumption (and a wrong one); in Qiyāmah 75:28 — wa ẓanna annahu'l-firāq — it means certainty (the dying man knows it's the parting)."]},
  {ar:"الْإِنسُ", tr:"Al-ins", v:"5, 6", root:"The \"familiarity / intimacy / being seen\" family — anisa = to be familiar, at ease with; uns = intimacy, companionship.", meaning:"\"humans\" (paired with al-jinn)", magic:"Ins and jinn are named as OPPOSITES: ins = the SEEN, the familiar (from uns, being at ease with); jinn = the HIDDEN (from janna, to cover). Visible vs.", grammar:"Al-ins = collective noun (nominative, subject of taqūla).", why:"Ins (not nās) — because it pairs with jinn as seen vs. hidden.", note:"INS = the SEEN, the familiar (uns = intimacy). JINN = the HIDDEN (janna = to cover). Visible vs. invisible creation.", more:["Ins = humankind; insān = a human; istiʾnās = seeking familiarity / (modern) domestication of animals; and the classical play: insān ← nisyān (forgetting) — from Nās.","invisible creation, in two words.","And in v.6 the jinn reveal a human failing: humans sought refuge in them — the seen begging the unseen."]},
  {ar:"يَعُوذُونَ بِرِجَالٍ مِّنَ الْجِنِّ", tr:"Yaʿūdhūna bi-rijālin mina'l-jinn", v:"6", root:"The \"SEEKING REFUGE / clinging for safety\" family — ʿādha = to take refuge, to cling like a young animal to its mother; maʿādha'llāh!", meaning:"\"And there were men of humankind who used to seek refuge in men of the jinn…\"", magic:"THE PERFECT PAYOFF from the surah you just finished.", grammar:"Kāna… yaʿūdhūna = habitual past (\"used to seek refuge\"). Hollow verb (ʿ-w-dh). bi- = in (the refuge sought).", why:"ʿādha — so the reader instantly hears Falaq and Nās against it.", note:"Falaq/Nās: aʿūdhu bi-RABB. Jinn 72:6: yaʿūdhūna bi-JINN. True refuge vs. false refuge — same verb.", more:["= \"God forbid!\" (literally \"refuge in Allah!\").","Falaq 113:1 & Nās 114:1 — qul aʿūdhu bi-rabbi'l-falaq / bi-rabbi'n-nās — \"seek refuge in the LORD.\" Jinn 72:6 — yaʿūdhūna bi-rijālin mina'l-jinn — \"they sought refuge in JINN.\" Same verb — the true refuge vs.","The historical practice: a pre-Islamic traveller stopping in a valley at night would say, \"I seek refuge in the master of this valley from the foolish of his people\" — addressing the jinn believed to own the place.","And the jinn themselves testify: it didn't protect them (next word).","The Muʿawwidhatān are the correction: seek refuge in the Lord of the jinn, not in the jinn.","Pre-Islamic travellers: \"I seek refuge in the master of this valley.\""]},
  {ar:"فَزَادُوهُمْ رَهَقًا", tr:"Fa-zādūhum rahaqā", v:"6", root:"Zāda — to INCREASE (ziyādah).", meaning:"\"…so they only increased them in burden / fear / folly.\"", magic:"The refuge backfired. They sought safety in the jinn — and the jinn (or the practice) increased their fear and oppression.", grammar:"Zāda takes two objects (-hum and rahaqan). Hollow verb.", why:"Rahaq — a heavy, overtaking burden — the exact opposite of the relief they sought.", note:"Murhaq = EXHAUSTED; murāhiq = an ADOLESCENT. False refuge ADDED weight (v.6); true faith removes it (v.13: \"fears no rahaq\").", more:["rahaq — the \"overtaking / burdening / covering with distress\" family — rahiqa = to overtake, to come upon; rahaq = burden, fear, oppression, folly; murāhiq = an ADOLESCENT (one \"approaching/overtaking\" maturity!); murhaq = EXHAUSTED (modern: anā murhaq = \"I'm worn out\").","Classical readings: the jinn grew arrogant (\"even humans seek refuge in us!\") and humans grew more afraid.","False refuge doesn't just fail — it adds weight.","You own rahaq from ʿAbasa 80:41 — tarhaquhā qatarah (blackness overtakes their faces), and it returns in v.13 (fa-lā yakhāfu bakhsan wa lā rahaqā — the believer fears no burden) and Muddaththir 74:17 (sa-urhiquhu ṣaʿūdā).","v.6: the false refuge ADDED rahaq.","v.13: the believer has NO rahaq.","Same word, seven verses apart.","And zāda — you own it from Nabaʾ 78:30 (fa-lan nazīdakum illā ʿadhābā — \"We'll only INCREASE you in punishment\").","Also ʿAbasa 80:41, Muddaththir 74:17."]},
  {ar:"أَن لَّن يَبْعَثَ اللَّهُ أَحَدًا", tr:"An lan yabʿatha'llāhu aḥadā", v:"7", root:"Baʿatha — the \"raising up / sending forth\" family — baʿth = RESURRECTION; also to send a messenger (baʿatha rasūlan).", meaning:"\"And they [humans] assumed, as you [jinn] assumed, that Allah would never raise anyone.\"", magic:"Two things. (1) The jinn turn and ADDRESS their own people — and humans: kamā ẓanantum (\"as you assumed\"). They're now preaching.", grammar:"Lan + subjunctive = \"would never.\" kamā = \"just as\" (comparison).", why:"Baʿatha — one verb holding both denials they had held: no Messenger, no Resurrection.", note:"Baʿatha = RESURRECT and SEND a messenger. Both were denied; both just came true. Biʿthah = a SCHOLARSHIP abroad. Aḥadā #2.", more:["Biʿthah = a delegation / (modern) a scholarship abroad; inbaʿatha (Shams 91:12).","(2) yabʿatha has two classical readings — both true: \"would never resurrect anyone\" or \"would never send anyone (as a messenger).\" Both were denied by jinn and humans alike — and both were just disproven: a Messenger HAS been sent, and his message is resurrection.","And aḥadā — the second of six."]},
  {ar:"وَأَنَّا لَمَسْنَا السَّمَاءَ", tr:"Wa annā lamasna's-samāʾ", v:"8", root:"The \"TOUCHING / feeling for / seeking\" family — lamasa = to touch; lams = touch (shāshat lams = a touchscreen!).", meaning:"\"And we reached for / sought [to reach] the heaven…\"", magic:"Lamasa is \"touching to FEEL for something\" — like reaching into the dark. The jinn describe groping toward the sky — trying to get close enough to overhear.", grammar:"Past, 1st plural. as-samāʾa = direct object.", why:"Lamasa (feeling for) — captures their stealth: not marching up, but feeling their way.", note:"Shāshat LAMS = a TOUCHSCREEN. Iltimās = a PETITION. They \"felt for\" the sky — and found guards.", more:["Iltamasa = to SEEK / request (iltimās = a petition); malmūs = tangible.","The image is of reaching up and feeling — and finding it full of guards (next)."]},
  {ar:"فَوَجَدْنَاهَا مُلِئَتْ حَرَسًا شَدِيدًا", tr:"Fa-wajadnāhā muliʾat ḥarasan shadīdā", v:"8", root:"Muliʾat — the \"FILLING\" family — malaʾa = to fill; milʾ = fill/fullness; malīʾ = full; al-malaʾ = the chiefs/council (those who \"fill\" the eye).", meaning:"\"…and found it filled with strong guards and flaming meteors.\"", magic:"Muliʾat — FILLED. Not \"had some guards\" — packed. The sky became suddenly sealed at the moment of revelation, so nothing could interfere with it.", grammar:"Muliʾat = passive (filled — by Allah, unnamed). ḥarasan = tamyīz (specification: \"filled in terms of guards\").", why:"Malaʾa (filled) — total coverage: no gap to slip through.", note:"Ḥāris al-marmā = a GOALKEEPER; al-ḥaras al-waṭanī = the NATIONAL GUARD. The sky was FILLED (muliʾat) — no gaps.", more:["ḥaras — the \"GUARDING\" family — ḥaras = guards (collective); ḥāris = a GUARD (ḥāris al-marmā = a GOALKEEPER!); al-ḥaras al-waṭanī = the NATIONAL GUARD.","And shadīd — you own it in four places: the firm heavens (Nabaʾ 78:12), your firm frame (Insān 76:28), His severe grip (Burūj 85:12), and man's intense love of wealth (ʿĀdiyāt 100:8). Here: the heavens' guards are shadīd — and they guard revelation.","Shadīd thread: sky, bones, His grip, love of money — and now the guards."]},
  {ar:"وَشُهُبًا / شِهَابًا", tr:"Wa shuhubā / shihābā", v:"8, 9", root:"The \"FLAME / blazing brightness\" family — shihāb = a shooting star, a METEOR, a flaming brand; shuhub = its plural.", meaning:"\"…and flaming meteors\" (v.8); \"…will find a meteor lying in wait for him\" (v.9)", magic:"The shooting stars you see at night are, in this surah's picture, guards' weapons — fired at eavesdroppers.", grammar:"Shuhuban = broken plural, accusative (joined to ḥarasan). shihāban (v.9) = singular, object of yajid.", why:"Shihāb — the visible night-sky event the audience already knew, given its hidden meaning.", note:"SHIHĀB = a METEOR / shooting star. Shihāb ad-Dīn = \"Flame of the Religion.\" Shooting stars = guards' fire at eavesdroppers.", more:["Ashhab = grey-white (bright-coloured); the name Shihāb ad-Dīn = \"Flame of the Religion.\"","The jinn learned it the hard way: every attempt to listen now meets a flame.","And the change happened at the Prophet's ﷺ mission — that's what sent the jinn searching for the cause and led them to the Qurʾān."]},
  {ar:"نَقْعُدُ مِنْهَا مَقَاعِدَ لِلسَّمْعِ", tr:"Naqʿudu minhā maqāʿida li's-samʿ", v:"9", root:"The \"SITTING\" family — qaʿada = to sit; maqʿad = a SEAT (modern: a car seat, a plane seat, maqʿad in parliament!).", meaning:"\"And we used to sit there in positions for listening.\"", magic:"The image is almost comic: jinn with reserved SEATS in the sky, like an audience at a performance, listening in on the angels. Maqāʿid li's-samʿ = \"listening seats.\" That was then.", grammar:"Maqāʿida = diptote (mafāʿil pattern) — fatḥah, no tanwīn. kunnā naqʿudu = habitual past (\"used to sit\").", why:"Maqāʿid (seats) — pictures it as an established, habitual position — which made its sudden loss so striking.", note:"MAQʿAD = a SEAT (car, plane, parliament). Jinn had \"listening SEATS\" in the sky — then lost them. Maqāʿid = DIPTOTE.", more:["Qāʿidah = a base / a rule (what something \"sits\" on); quʿūd = sitting (Burūj 85:6!). samʿ — hearing.","Now (v.9): fa-man yastamiʿi'l-ĀNA — \"whoever listens NOW\" finds a meteor.","And compare Burūj 85:6 — idh hum ʿalayhā quʿūd — the persecutors SITTING by the fire-ditch watching.","Two kinds of sitting: spectators of cruelty (Burūj), and eavesdroppers on heaven (Jinn).","Burūj 85:6: persecutors SITTING (quʿūd) by the ditch."]},
  {ar:"فَمَن يَسْتَمِعِ الْآنَ يَجِدْ لَهُ شِهَابًا رَّصَدًا", tr:"Fa-man yastamiʿi'l-āna yajid lahu shihāban raṣadā", v:"9", root:"Al-ān — NOW (the root ʾ-n-y: reaching its moment — the same root as āniyah, boiling at its peak, Ghāshiyah 88:5). raṣad — the \"WATCHING / lying in wait\" family — raṣada = to observe, ambush; marṣad = an OBSERVATORY; raṣīd = a (bank) BALANCE — what's been watched/recorded!", meaning:"\"But whoever listens now will find a meteor lying in wait for him.\"", magic:"The raṣad thread is one of your best: - Nabaʾ 78:21 — inna jahannama kānat mirṣādā (Hell lies in wait) - Fajr 89:14.", grammar:"Man + jussive + jussive = conditional (\"whoever listens, finds\").", why:"Raṣad — the meteor isn't random; it is waiting, positioned, watching.", note:"MARṢAD = an OBSERVATORY.", more:["Inna rabbaka la-bi'l-mirṣād (your Lord is at the lookout) - Jinn 72:9 — shihāban raṣadā (a meteor lies in wait for eavesdroppers) - Jinn 72:27 — yasluku min bayni yadayhi wa min khalfihi raṣadā (guards escort revelation) In this one surah, raṣad appears TWICE with two jobs: v.9 — guards lying in wait AGAINST thieves of revelation; v.27 — guards escorting and PROTECTING revelation.","The same watching serves to block and to protect.","And note the Form VIII yastamiʿ again (from v.1): good listening vs.","yastamiʿi gets a kasrah to avoid two sukūns before al-ān.","raṣadan = verbal noun used as an adjective (\"an ambushing meteor\") — waṣf bi'l-maṣdar again (like ʿajabā, v.1).","Raṣad thread: Hell (78:21), your Lord (89:14), a meteor (72:9), revelation's escort (72:27). v.9 BLOCKS thieves; v.27 PROTECTS the message.","Al-ān = NOW — same root as āniyah (boiling, 88:5)."]},
  {ar:"لَا نَدْرِي أَشَرٌّ أُرِيدَ... أَمْ أَرَادَ بِهِمْ رَبُّهُمْ رَشَدًا", tr:"Lā nadrī a-sharrun urīda… am arāda bihim rabbuhum rashadā", v:"10", root:"Nadrī — the \"knowing by perception\" family (darā; you own yudrīka/adrāka). sharr — evil/harm.", meaning:"\"And we do not know whether harm is intended for those on earth, or whether their Lord intends for them right guidance.\"", magic:"THE MOST REFINED VERSE IN THE SURAH — a lesson in adab (courtesy toward Allah), from jinn. Look at the two halves: - a-sharrun URĪDA — \"is harm intended\" — PASSIVE, no doer named.", grammar:"Urīda = PASSIVE; arāda = ACTIVE with explicit subject. a-… am… = \"whether… or…\" (alternative question).", why:"The contrast of voices — passive for evil, active for good — is the entire point.", note:"EVIL: passive, no doer (urīda). GOOD: active, \"their LORD intends\" (arāda rabbuhum).", more:["urīda / arāda — the \"WILLING / intending\" family — irādah = will; murīd = a disciple (one who wills the path).","- am ARĀDA bihim RABBUHUM rashadā — \"or their Lord INTENDS guidance\" — ACTIVE, Allah named as doer.","The jinn will not attach evil to Allah's name — but gladly attach guidance to Him.","You saw the IDENTICAL courtesy in Falaq 113:2 — min sharri mā khalaq (\"the evil of what He created,\" not \"the evil He created\").","And in Ibrāhīm's words (26:80): wa idhā mariḍtu fa-huwa yashfīn — \"when I fall ill, He heals me.\" Three voices — the Qurʾān, Ibrāhīm, and the jinn — all keep evil off His name.","And the humility: lā nadrī — \"we don't know.\" After the sky was suddenly sealed, they didn't pretend to understand.","Admitting ignorance of the unseen — the theme of the surah's end (v.25–26).","Same adab as Falaq 113:2 (\"evil of what He CREATED\") and Ibrāhīm (26:80: \"when I fall ill, HE heals\").","Lā nadrī = \"we DON'T KNOW\" — humility before the unseen."]},
  {ar:"مِنَّا الصَّالِحُونَ وَمِنَّا دُونَ ذَٰلِكَ", tr:"Minna'ṣ-ṣāliḥūna wa minnā dūna dhālik", v:"11", root:"ṣāliḥūn — the \"sound / fit for purpose / repairing\" family — ṣalaḥa = to be sound; iṣlāḥ = repair/reform.", meaning:"\"And among us are the righteous, and among us are those below that.\"", magic:"Remarkable honesty about their own kind. The jinn don't say \"we're all good now\" — they say some of us are righteous, some are dūna dhālik (\"short of that\").", grammar:"Minnā (fronted) + subject = \"among us [are]…\" — a nominal sentence with fronted predicate.", why:"Dūna dhālik (below that) — refuses to flatten their community into one verdict.", note:"\"Some of us righteous, some DŪNA DHĀLIK (short of that).\" Jinn vary, like humans — a spectrum, not a single category.", more:["ṣulḥ = a peace treaty (you own it from ʿAṣr 103:3). dūna — below / short of / other than.","Dūna is a gentle word — not \"evil,\" but \"below the mark\" — a spectrum, not two boxes.","It answers a question humans often ask: are all jinn evil?","The jinn themselves say: no — like humans, they vary.","dūna = an adverb of place used for rank.","Iṣlāḥ = REPAIR (ʿAṣr 103:3)."]},
  {ar:"طَرَائِقَ قِدَدًا", tr:"ṭarāʾiqa qidadā", v:"11", root:"ṭarāʾiq — plural of ṭarīqah = a WAY, a path, a method (ṭarīq = a road; ṭarīqah = a Sufi ORDER; ṭarīqat al-istikhdām = instructions for use!).", meaning:"\"We were [on] divided paths / of different ways.\"", magic:"The image is a single hide CUT INTO STRIPS — one thing sliced into separate, diverging pieces.", grammar:"ṭarāʾiqa = DIPTOTE (faʿāʾil plural) — fatḥah, no tanwīn.", why:"Qidad (strips cut lengthwise) — shows the division as an act of tearing apart, not natural variety.", note:"QADD = STATURE (a lengthwise outline); qidd = a LEATHER STRIP. Qidad = one hide SLICED into separate strips.", more:["qidad — the \"CUTTING LENGTHWISE / slicing into strips\" family — qadda = to cut lengthwise, to slit; qidd = a strip of leather; qadd = STATURE / figure (a person's lengthwise outline!); qidad = pieces cut apart, separate factions.","The jinn community wasn't just \"diverse\" — it was cut apart into factions going different ways.","Compare Layl 92:4 — inna saʿyakum la-shattā — \"your strivings are SCATTERED.\" And Zalzalah 99:6 — yaṣduru'n-nāsu ashtātā (people come out in scattered groups).","Jinn 72:11: ṭarāʾiqa qidadā — paths sliced apart.","Three surahs, one truth: creatures diverge.","And ṭarīqah returns in v.16 — istaqāmū ʿala'ṭ-ṭarīqah — \"stand straight on THE path.\" v.11: many sliced paths.","v.16: THE one path — and standing straight on it.","It's the khabar of kunnā (with an implied \"of\": \"we were [people of] divided ways\").","v.11: many sliced paths (ṭarāʾiq).","v.16: THE path (aṭ-ṭarīqah).","Ṭarīqat al-istikhdām = INSTRUCTIONS for use."]},
  {ar:"لَّن نُّعْجِزَ اللَّهَ", tr:"Lan nuʿjiza'llāh", v:"12", root:"The \"INABILITY / incapacity\" family — ʿajaza = to be unable; ʿajz = incapacity, weakness.", meaning:"\"And we became certain that we could never escape Allah on earth, nor escape Him by flight.\"", magic:"The root of the word for MIRACLE. A muʿjizah is something that leaves you unable to reproduce it. The Qurʾān is the muʿjizah — it renders every challenger ʿājiz (incapable).", grammar:"Lan + subjunctive ×2 = \"never.\" Form IV aʿjaza = to put out of power / escape.", why:"Aʿjaza — shares a root with muʿjizah, quietly setting the miracle of the Qurʾān against the powerlessness of its hearers.", note:"MUʿJIZAH = A MIRACLE = \"that which makes others UNABLE.\" ʿAjūz = an OLD person (became unable).", more:["Muʿjizah = A MIRACLE (literally \"that which renders others incapable\" — of matching it!); ʿajūz = an old person (one who has become unable); Form IV aʿjaza = to make someone unable / to escape someone's power.","And here the jinn confess the reverse: WE can never render ALLAH incapable (lan nuʿjiza'llāh).","The Qurʾān makes creatures ʿājiz; no creature can make Allah ʿājiz.","One root, both directions.","Two scenarios covered: \"on earth\" (staying and resisting) and \"by flight\" (running).","And note ẓanannā here = certainty (the realization of believers), not the mistaken assumption of v.5!","Ẓann in v.12 = CERTAINTY; in v.5 = mistaken ASSUMPTION — same word, same surah.","The Qurʾān makes creatures ʿājiz; no creature can make Allah ʿājiz.","Ẓanannā v.5 = wrong ASSUMPTION; v.12 = CERTAINTY."]},
  {ar:"هَرَبًا", tr:"Harabā", v:"12", root:"The \"FLEEING / escaping\" family — haraba = to flee; harab = flight; hārib = a FUGITIVE.", meaning:"\"…nor escape Him by flight.\"", magic:"The jinn — beings who can move fast and hide — admit even THEY can't outrun Allah.", grammar:"Haraban = verbal noun as ḥāl or tamyīz (\"by way of flight\").", why:"Harab — the most natural plan for a hidden, fast being, explicitly ruled out.", note:"Hārib = a FUGITIVE; tahrīb = SMUGGLING. Even the fast, hidden jinn can't flee Him.", more:["Tahrīb = SMUGGLING (making things escape the law!); mahrab = an escape route.","Compare Qiyāmah 75:10 — ayna'l-mafarr (\"where is the escape?\") — and ʿAbasa 80:34 (yawma yafirru'l-marʾ).","Farra = to flee in panic; haraba = to flee / escape.","Humans will ask \"where's the escape?\" on that Day; jinn already concluded: there is none.","Compare Qiyāmah 75:10: \"where is the ESCAPE (mafarr)?\""]},
  {ar:"سَمِعْنَا الْهُدَىٰ آمَنَّا بِهِ", tr:"Samiʿna'l-hudā āmannā bih", v:"13", root:"Samiʿa — to hear. hudā — the \"guidance\" family (hadā = to guide; hadiyyah = a gift — something guided to you).", meaning:"\"And when we heard the guidance, we believed in it.\"", magic:"They call the Qurʾān simply \"AL-HUDĀ\" — \"THE GUIDANCE.\" Not \"a book,\" not \"a recitation\" this time — its function as its name.", grammar:"Lammā + past = \"as soon as\" (temporal).", why:"Al-hudā — naming the Qurʾān by what it does for you.", note:"They call the Qurʾān \"AL-HUDĀ\" — THE GUIDANCE. Wonder (v.1), maturity (v.2), guidance (v.13). Lammā + PAST = \"as soon as\" (here).", more:["Compare v.1 (qurʾānan ʿajabā — its wonder) and v.2 (yahdī ila'r-rushd — its effect).","Three descriptions in thirteen verses: wonder, maturity, guidance.","And lammā samiʿnā… āmannā — \"the moment we heard, we believed.\" Lammā + past = \"as soon as / when.\" No gap.","Compare ʿAbasa 80:23's lammā yaqḍi = \"has NOT YET\" — lammā + jussive = negation.","Same word, two functions: with PAST = \"when\"; with JUSSIVE = \"not yet.\"","Lammā + JUSSIVE = \"not yet\" (ʿAbasa 80:23)."]},
  {ar:"فَلَا يَخَافُ بَخْسًا وَلَا رَهَقًا", tr:"Fa-lā yakhāfu bakhsan wa lā rahaqā", v:"13", root:"Bakhs — the \"UNDERVALUING / short-changing / cheating on weight\" family.", meaning:"\"So whoever believes in his Lord fears neither being short-changed nor burdened.\"", magic:"Two fears removed — and both connect to earlier surahs. Bakhs = being SHORT-CHANGED. Muṭaffifīn 83:1–3 cursed those who short-measure others (al-muṭaffifīn).", grammar:"Fa- in the answer of a conditional with man.", why:"Bakhs (short-changing) — a market word, promising full, honest payment.", note:"Thaman BAKHS = a CHEAP PRICE — Yūsuf was sold for one (12:20). Allah never short-changes.", more:["Bakhasa = to reduce someone's due, to give less than deserved; thaman bakhs = a paltry PRICE (Yūsuf 12:20: his brothers sold him bi-thamanin bakhs — for a cheap price!); lā tabkhasū'n-nāsa ashyāʾahum (7:85) — \"don't short-change people.\" rahaq — burden / oppression (v.6).","Here: the believer will NEVER be short-measured by Allah — every good deed paid in full.","Compare Zalzalah 99:7 — an atom's weight of good will be seen.","Rahaq = burden — v.6: those who sought refuge in jinn got rahaq ADDED.","v.13: the believer fears NO rahaq.","Same word, the two refuges compared.","The memorable hook: Yūsuf was sold bi-thamanin bakhs — the most precious boy, at a bargain price.","lā yakhāfu — the present is raised (not jussive) because of the fa + implied subject (\"then he does not fear\").","Muṭaffifīn cursed the short-measurers; here the believer is never short-measured.","Rahaq: ADDED by false refuge (v.6), REMOVED by faith (v.13)."]},
  {ar:"الْمُسْلِمُونَ وَمِنَّا الْقَاسِطُونَ", tr:"Al-muslimūna wa minna'l-qāsiṭūn", v:"14 · 15", root:"Muslimūn — the \"wholeness / submission / safety\" family (salima; islām; salām — Qadr 97:5). qāsiṭūn.", meaning:"\"And among us are the Muslims, and among us are the deviators.\"", magic:"QĀSIṬ (Form I participle) = UNJUST, one who deviates from what's right. MUQSIṬ (Form IV participle) = JUST, one who acts with equity.", grammar:"Qāsiṭ (Form I) = unjust; muqsiṭ (Form IV) = just — the hamzat as-salb (privative hamzah) reverses the meaning.", why:"Qāsiṭ (swerving from justice) — names their failure as a deviation from the straight path of v.16.", note:"QĀSIṬ = UNJUST (Form I). MUQSIṬ = JUST (Form IV) — al-Muqsiṭ is a NAME OF ALLAH. Same root, OPPOSITE meanings.", more:["A root with OPPOSITE meanings depending on the form: Form I qasaṭa = to DEVIATE, to be UNJUST; Form IV aqsaṭa = to be JUST, equitable; qisṭ = JUSTICE, equity; al-Muqsiṭ = THE JUST — a Name of Allah; qisṭ also = an INSTALMENT (modern: paying in aqsāṭ — fair portions!).","THE BEST GRAMMAR TRAP IN THE SURAH.","(Allah loves al-muqsiṭīn — 5:42, 49:9, 60:8.) Same three letters (q-s-ṭ).","The Form IV prefix doesn't add — it reverses: aqsaṭa = \"removed the qasṭ (injustice)\" → became just.","(Arabic's hamzat as-salb — the \"removing\" hamzah.) Compare the Form X contrast you own: istaghnā (\"I need nothing\") vs.","istaghfir (\"I need forgiveness\") — same form, opposite souls.","Here: same ROOT, different form, opposite souls.","And note the pairing: muslimūn vs qāsiṭūn — not \"muslims vs.","disbelievers\" but \"those who submitted vs.","those who swerved.\" Submission is defined against deviation.","Qisṭ = JUSTICE — and an INSTALMENT (paying in fair portions).","The single best trap in Al-Jinn."]},
  {ar:"تَحَرَّوْا رَشَدًا", tr:"Taḥarraw rashadā", v:"14", root:"The \"SEEKING CAREFULLY / aiming deliberately for the most fitting\" family.", meaning:"\"So whoever has submitted — those have sought out right guidance [carefully].\"", magic:"Islam described as an INVESTIGATION.", grammar:"Form V past, weak-final — the plural taḥarrayū contracts to taḥarraw. Form V = effort applied to oneself.", why:"Taḥarrā — makes guidance the result of a deliberate, careful search.", note:"Taḥarrī khāṣṣ = a PRIVATE DETECTIVE. Taḥarrī al-qiblah = carefully working out the qiblah direction.", more:["Taḥarrā = to seek out with care, to aim for precisely; taḥarrī = INVESTIGATION (modern: taḥarrī khāṣṣ = a PRIVATE DETECTIVE!); ḥarī = fitting, worthy (\"ḥarīy bi- = worthy of\"); in fiqh, taḥarrī al-qiblah = carefully determining the qiblah direction when unsure!","The submitters didn't stumble into guidance — they taḥarraw it: searched carefully, aimed precisely, like someone working out the qiblah in an unfamiliar place.","And that's exactly what these jinn did — they noticed the sky change, went searching across the land (Bukhārī), found the Prophet ﷺ, listened (istamaʿū), and believed.","Their own story is taḥarrī.","And rashadā — the third r-sh-d in the surah.","Submission = a careful SEARCH for rushd — exactly what these jinn did."]},
  {ar:"فَكَانُوا لِجَهَنَّمَ حَطَبًا", tr:"Fa-kānū li-jahannama ḥaṭabā", v:"15", root:"ḥaṭab = FIREWOOD, kindling — ḥaṭṭāb = a woodcutter.", meaning:"\"And as for the deviators — they will be firewood for Hell.\"", magic:"Two stunning connections. ONE — Masad 111:4 — wa'mraʾatuhu ḥammālata'l-ḥaṭab — Abū Lahab's wife, \"the carrier of FIREWOOD.\" There: she CARRIES the firewood.", grammar:"Li-jahannama = \"for Hell\" (lām of purpose); jahannama = diptote (foreign/feminine proper noun) — fatḥah in the genitive.", why:"ḥaṭab (firewood) rather than \"people of the Fire\" — reduces them to fuel — a thing consumed, not a person present.", note:"Masad 111:4: she CARRIES the firewood (ḥammālat al-ḥaṭab). Jinn 72:15: they ARE the firewood (ḥaṭabā).", more:["Here: the deviators ARE the firewood.","The talebearer who fed fires between people, and the jinn who fuel the Fire itself.","TWO — the irony of FIRE fuelling FIRE.","The jinn are created from fire (mārij min nār, 55:15). And the unjust among them become FUEL for fire.","Fire burning fire — classical scholars noted: Allah can make even beings of fire suffer by fire.","Being made of a thing doesn't make you immune to it.","And kānū (\"they will be / have become\") — past tense for a certain future.","Beings made of FIRE become FUEL for fire."]},
  {ar:"اسْتَقَامُوا عَلَى الطَّرِيقَةِ", tr:"Istaqāmū ʿala'ṭ-ṭarīqah", v:"16", root:"Istaqāmū — Form X of qāma (to stand) = to stand UPRIGHT and STAY straight (istiqāmah = steadfastness).", meaning:"\"And [it was revealed] that if they had kept straight on the Way…\"", magic:"The q-w-m thread you've built across the juzʾ: - Takwīr 81:28 — li-man shāʾa minkum an yastaqīm (whoever wills to go straight) - Tīn 95:4.", grammar:"Law + past = hypothetical condition; its answer is la-asqaynāhum (with the lām of the answer).", why:"Istiqāmah — the condition for blessing is not perfection but STAYING upright.", note:"Aṣ-ṣirāṭ al-MUSTAQĪM — 17× a day.", more:["Aṣ-ṣirāṭ al-mustaqīm — the straight path you ask for 17× a day.","aṭ-ṭarīqah — THE way (v.11).","Fī aḥsani taqwīm (the best upright form) - Bayyinah 98:3, 5 — kutubun qayyimah… dīnu'l-qayyimah (upright writings/religion) - Jinn 72:16 — istaqāmū ʿala'ṭ-ṭarīqah And the internal echo: v.11 — kunnā ṭarāʾiqa qidadā (we were many sliced paths) → v.16 — ʿala'ṭ-ṭarīqah (on THE path).","Plural and scattered → singular and definite.","Form X = seeking/maintaining — not one upright moment, but staying upright.","Note: the voice shifts here — v.16 onward is generally read as Allah's words (continuing \"it was revealed to me that…\"), no longer the jinn's quoted speech.","Q-w-m thread: yastaqīm (81:28), taqwīm (95:4), qayyimah (98:3), istaqāmū (72:16). v.11: many SLICED paths."]},
  {ar:"لَأَسْقَيْنَاهُم مَّاءً غَدَقًا", tr:"La-asqaynāhum māʾan ghadaqā", v:"16", root:"Asqā — the \"giving to drink / irrigating\" family — saqā = to give drink; istisqāʾ = THE PRAYER FOR RAIN.", meaning:"\"…We would have given them abundant water to drink.\"", magic:"Uprightness → RAIN. To desert people, abundant water is the image of total prosperity — crops, herds, wealth, life.", grammar:"Form IV (asqā = to provide drink — often of irrigation, giving water for land/crops).", why:"Ghadaq — the most generous word for water, matching the generosity of the promise.", note:"ISTISQĀʾ = the PRAYER FOR RAIN. Upright on the Way → ABUNDANT water (ghadaq).", more:["Suqyā (Shams 91:13 — the she-camel's drinking-turn); saqqāʾ = a water-carrier.","ghadaq — ABUNDANT, copious (water) — rain pouring plentifully.","It echoes the promise of Nūḥ (71:10–12): istaghfirū rabbakum… yursili's-samāʾa ʿalaykum midrārā — \"seek forgiveness… He will send rain upon you in abundance.\" And it connects to Nabaʾ 78:14 — māʾan thajjājā (water pouring in torrents).","Spiritual straightness and physical rain are linked throughout the Qurʾān.","Istisqāʾ — the rain prayer — is your hook.","la- = the lām in the answer of law.","Compare Nūḥ 71:10–12: seek forgiveness → abundant rain.","Nabaʾ 78:14: thajjājā (torrents)."]},
  {ar:"لِّنَفْتِنَهُمْ فِيهِ", tr:"Li-naftinahum fīh", v:"17", root:"The \"TESTING BY FIRE\" family — fatana = originally to put GOLD INTO FIRE to test its purity.", meaning:"\"…so that We might test them by it.\"", magic:"The twist: even the BLESSING is a TEST. v.16 promised abundant rain; v.17 adds — so We can test them with it.", grammar:"Li- = lām of purpose + subjunctive (naftina). fīhi = \"by / in it\" (the water).", why:"Fitnah (gold in fire) — shows the purpose of blessing: to reveal what's genuine.", note:"Fatana = putting GOLD IN FIRE to test its purity. Even ABUNDANCE is a fitnah — like Fajr 89:15 (wealth = a test).", more:["Melting away what's false; fitnah = a TRIAL, a test (and by extension, temptation, discord); fattān = an enchanter, a charming temptress (fātinah = charming!); maftūn = infatuated.","Prosperity is gold in the fire: it shows whether the gratitude is real.","You saw this exact principle in Fajr 89:15–16 — idhā mā ibtalāhu rabbuhu fa-akramahu… — wealth is a TEST, not a verdict.","And the root image: gold is put into fire not to destroy it, but to show what's real.","The f-t-n root across your surahs: Burūj 85:10 — inna'lladhīna fatanu'l-muʾminīn (those who persecuted / tried the believers — with literal fire, the Ditch!) Burūj: humans put believers through FIRE.","Jinn: Allah puts people through ABUNDANCE.","And Muddaththir 74:31 — wa mā jaʿalnā ʿiddatahum illā fitnah (even a number is a test).","Burūj 85:10: humans tried believers with literal FIRE.","Fātinah = CHARMING (a temptress)."]},
  {ar:"وَمَن يُعْرِضْ عَن ذِكْرِ رَبِّهِ", tr:"Wa man yuʿriḍ ʿan dhikri rabbih", v:"17", root:"The \"WIDTH / showing the side\" family — ʿarḍ = width, breadth.", meaning:"\"And whoever turns away from the remembrance of his Lord…\"", magic:"The image is BODILY — like Aʿlā 87:11's yatajannabuhā (sidestepping, from janb, a side). Aʿraḍa = to turn your SIDE toward something instead of your face.", grammar:"Man + jussive (yuʿriḍ, sukūn) = conditional; answer yaslukhu (also jussive). Form IV + ʿan.", why:"Iʿrāḍ (turning your side) — a posture of refusal, not mere absence.", note:"MAʿRAḌ = an EXHIBITION; ʿarḍ = WIDTH. Aʿraḍa = turning your SIDE instead of your FACE.", more:["Aʿraḍa ʿan = to TURN AWAY from (literally, to show your side/breadth to something instead of facing it); muʿriḍ = one who turns away; ʿarḍ also = an exhibition / an offer (maʿraḍ = an EXHIBITION!); ʿarīḍ = wide.","You're not running away — you're standing sideways, refusing to face it.","Two \"side\" roots for rejecting the reminder: janb (Aʿlā 87:11) and ʿarḍ (Jinn 72:17). And dhikr rabbih — the juzʾ's master thread: the reminder.","It returns in Muddaththir 74:49 — fa-mā lahum ʿani't-tadhkirati muʿriḍīn — \"what's wrong with them, turning away from the reminder?\"","Two \"side\" roots for rejection: janb (87:11), ʿarḍ (72:17). Returns as muʿriḍīn in Muddaththir 74:49."]},
  {ar:"يَسْلُكْهُ عَذَابًا صَعَدًا", tr:"Yaslukhu ʿadhāban ṣaʿadā", v:"17", root:"Yasluk — the \"THREADING through / inserting / travelling a path\" family.", meaning:"\"…He will thread him into an ever-mounting, severe punishment.\"", magic:"TWO vivid images in three words. Yaslukhu = He THREADS him into it — like thread through a needle's eye: forced into a narrow, tight passage, with no room around it.", grammar:"Yaslukhu — jussive (answer of man), takes two objects (-hu and ʿadhāban).", why:"ṣaʿad (endless ascent) — makes punishment feel like exhausting effort that never reaches a top.", note:"LĀ-SILKĪ = WIRELESS; silk = a WIRE/THREAD; sulūk = BEHAVIOUR. Threaded into it like a needle's eye. MIṢʿAD = an ELEVATOR.", more:["Salaka = to make something go into, to thread (a needle!); silk = a THREAD, a WIRE (silk kahrabāʾī = an electric wire; lā-silkī = WIRELESS!); maslak = a path/track; sulūk = BEHAVIOUR, conduct (the \"path\" you walk!).","ṣaʿad — the \"ASCENDING / climbing\" family — ṣaʿida = to go up; miṣʿad = an ELEVATOR / LIFT; ṣuʿūd = ascent; ṣaʿīd = elevated ground, also surface earth (tayammum with ṣaʿīd!).","And it returns in v.27 (yasluku… raṣadā — guards threaded around revelation) and Muddaththir 74:42 — mā salakakum fī saqar (\"what threaded you into Saqar?\").","Ṣaʿad = a punishment that keeps CLIMBING — rising, steepening, harder every moment, like walking up an endless slope.","Compare Balad 90:11 — fa-la'qtaḥama'l-ʿaqabah — the steep climb of good deeds they refused.","They wouldn't climb the ʿaqabah of good (Balad); they're made to climb the ṣaʿad of punishment (Jinn).","AND THE CROSS-SURAH SYMMETRY: Muddaththir 74:17 — sa-urhiquhu ṣaʿūdā (\"I will burden him with a steep climb\").","Jinn 72:17 and Muddaththir 74:17 — BOTH VERSE 17, both the same root.","ṣaʿadan = verbal noun used as adjective (waṣf bi'l-maṣdar) — \"a climbing punishment.\"","Ṣaʿad = a punishment that keeps CLIMBING.","Jinn 72:17 ṣaʿadā = Muddaththir 74:17 ṣaʿūdā — BOTH VERSE 17. Refused the climb of good (Balad 90:11) → made to climb this."]},
  {ar:"وَأَنَّ الْمَسَاجِدَ لِلَّهِ", tr:"Wa anna'l-masājida lillāh", v:"18", root:"The \"PROSTRATION\" family — sajada = to prostrate; masjid = \"the place of prostration\" (a mosque); sajjādah = a PRAYER MAT; sujūd.", meaning:"\"And the places of prostration belong to Allah, so do not call upon anyone alongside Allah.\"", magic:"Classical readings of al-masājid — and they're all rich: (a) MOSQUES — buildings of worship belong to Allah alone.", grammar:"Al-masājida = diptote (mafāʿil). fa-lā tadʿū = prohibition + jussive (tadʿū drops the nūn).", why:"Masājid (places of prostration) — ties the oneness of worship to the most physical act of worship.", note:"SAJJĀDAH = a PRAYER MAT; masjid = place of prostration.", more:["(b) the SEVEN BODY PARTS of prostration (forehead, two hands, two knees, two feet) — your forehead on the ground belongs to Him; (c) all acts of sujūd — the worship itself.","Reading (b) is unforgettable: every limb that touches the floor in sajdah is His.","So don't put that forehead down for anyone else.","You own the root from ʿAlaq 96:19 — wa'sjud wa'qtarib (prostrate and draw near — a sajdah verse).","And fa-lā tadʿū maʿa'llāhi aḥadā — aḥadā #3. Note the fronting: lillāh — \"to ALLAH\" (only).","Al-masājid may mean the SEVEN LIMBS of sajdah — your forehead on the ground is HIS."]},
  {ar:"لَمَّا قَامَ عَبْدُ اللَّهِ يَدْعُوهُ", tr:"Lammā qāma ʿabdu'llāhi yadʿūh", v:"19", root:"Qāma — to stand (the q-w-m root — qiyām in prayer!). ʿabd — a servant.", meaning:"\"And when the servant of Allah stood up, calling upon Him…\"", magic:"The Prophet ﷺ is called \"ʿABD ALLĀH\" — \"the SERVANT of Allah\" — at the moment of his greatest honour. Not \"the Messenger,\" not by name.", grammar:"Lammā + past = \"when.\" yadʿūhu = ḥāl (\"while calling Him\").", why:"ʿabd Allāh — because standing in prayer is servanthood at its peak.", note:"He's called ʿABD ALLĀH (servant) at his HIGHEST moments — Isrāʾ (17:1), revelation (53:10), here. Servanthood IS the honour.", more:["yadʿū — the \"calling / supplicating\" family (duʿāʾ).","The Qurʾān does this at his highest moments: the Night Journey (subḥāna'lladhī asrā bi-ʿabdih, 17:1), receiving revelation (fa-awḥā ilā ʿabdih, 53:10), the Qurʾān's descent (nazzala'l-furqāna ʿalā ʿabdih, 25:1). Servanthood is the title of honour.","And the scene is the prayer the jinn saw: he stood (qāma) — the qiyām of ṣalāh — calling on Him.","Compare ʿAlaq 96:10 — ʿabdan idhā ṣallā — \"a servant when he prays\" — someone tried to stop him.","Here, crowds pressed in to hear him.","ʿAlaq 96:10: someone tried to STOP the praying servant; here crowds pressed in."]},
  {ar:"كَادُوا يَكُونُونَ عَلَيْهِ لِبَدًا", tr:"Kādū yakūnūna ʿalayhi libadā", v:"19", root:"Kādū — \"they ALMOST / nearly\" (kāda = to be on the verge).", meaning:"\"…they almost became layered upon him in crowds.\"", magic:"A ROOT YOU OWN — and the contrast is beautiful. Balad 90:6 — yaqūlu ahlaktu mālan lubadā — the arrogant man boasting of wealth \"piled in a solid mass\" that he destroyed.", grammar:"Kāda + present = \"almost\" did. libadan = khabar of yakūnūna (plural of libdah).", why:"Libad (felt-like layers) — pictures bodies pressed together until they seem one mass.", note:"LIBDAH = FELT (fibres pressed into one sheet). Balad 90:6: WEALTH piled lubadā (to squander).", more:["libad — the \"layering / sticking together in a dense mass\" family — labada = to cling together, to mat; libdah = FELT (wool pressed into dense layers!) and a felt cap; lubad = heaped up.","Jinn 72:19 — kādū yakūnūna ʿalayhi libadā — beings pressing around the Prophet ﷺ layered densely to hear him.","Same root (l-b-d): MONEY piled up to be squandered (Balad); LISTENERS piled up to hear the Qurʾān (Jinn).","What you heap around yourself reveals you.","Classical readings of who crowded: (a) the jinn, so eager to hear they almost climbed over each other; (b) the hostile Quraysh, crowding to block him.","Libdah = FELT — wool fibres pressed so tightly they become one sheet.","That's the density of the crowd.","Jinn 72:19: LISTENERS piled libadā (to hear).","Same root — what you heap around you reveals you."]},
  {ar:"قُلْ إِنَّمَا أَدْعُو رَبِّي وَلَا أُشْرِكُ بِهِ أَحَدًا", tr:"Qul innamā adʿū rabbī wa lā ushriku bihi aḥadā", v:"20", root:"Adʿū — to call upon / supplicate (duʿāʾ). ushrik — to associate partners (shirk, sharīk; v.2).", meaning:"\"Say: I only call upon my Lord, and I associate no one with Him.\"", magic:"The Messenger repeats the jinn's own creed. v.2 (the jinn): wa lan nushrika bi-rabbinā aḥadā v.20 (the Prophet ﷺ): wa lā ushriku bihi aḥadā Almost word for word.", grammar:"Innamā = restriction (\"nothing but\"). lā + present = negation of present/future.", why:"Echoing v.2 exactly — so the reader hears the jinn and the Prophet ﷺ in unison.", note:"V.2 (JINN): lan nushrika… aḥadā. v.20 (PROPHET ﷺ): lā ushriku… aḥadā. Same sentence — one creed for both. Aḥadā #4.", more:["The new believers and the Messenger say the same sentence — one creed, for jinn and humans alike.","And innamā = \"ONLY\" — restriction."]},
  {ar:"لَا أَمْلِكُ لَكُمْ ضَرًّا وَلَا رَشَدًا", tr:"Lā amliku lakum ḍarran wa lā rashadā", v:"21", root:"Amlik — the \"OWNING / having power over\" family (malik = king — Nās 114:2; lā tamliku — Infiṭār 82:19). ḍarr.", meaning:"\"Say: I possess no power to harm you or to guide you aright.\"", magic:"Look at the PAIR — it's deliberately uneven. You'd expect \"harm or BENEFIT\" (ḍarr wa nafʿ) — the usual pairing. Instead: \"harm or GUIDANCE\" (ḍarr wa rashad).", grammar:"Iḥtibāk — mentioning one half of two pairs and leaving the other halves implied. lā amliku + lām (for you).", why:"ḍarr / rashad — the most humble statement of prophetic limits possible.", note:"ḌARŪRAH = NECESSITY (a pressing harm). \"No HARM, no GUIDANCE\" — each implying its opposite (iḥtibāk).", more:["The \"HARM\" family — ḍarra = to harm; ḍarar = damage (ḍarūrah = NECESSITY — a pressing harm that forces you!); ḍarīr = blind.","Classical note: the opposite of each is implied — \"I can't harm you or benefit you; I can't misguide you or guide you.\" One word from each pair, the other implied (iḥtibāk — a recognized balāgha device).","The Prophet ﷺ — the most powerful guide in history — says he OWNS no guidance.","He conveys it (v.23); Allah gives it.","Compare Ghāshiyah 88:22 — lasta ʿalayhim bi-muṣayṭir (\"you are not a controller over them\") — same limit.","And rashadā = #4 of the r-sh-d thread.","The thread completes: the Qurʾān GUIDES to rushd (v.2); the jinn don't know if Allah intends rashad (v.10); the submitters SEARCH for rashad (v.14); the Prophet ﷺ cannot OWN rashad (v.21).","He CONVEYS guidance; Allah GIVES it.","R-sh-d thread complete: guides to it (v.2) → unknown (v.10) → sought (v.14) → not owned by the Prophet ﷺ (v.21).","Ghāshiyah 88:22: \"not a controller.\""]},
  {ar:"لَن يُجِيرَنِي مِنَ اللَّهِ أَحَدٌ", tr:"Lan yujīranī mina'llāhi aḥad", v:"22", root:"The \"NEIGHBOURHOOD / protection\" family — jāwara = to be next to; jār = a NEIGHBOUR.", meaning:"\"Say: None can protect me from Allah.\"", magic:"The root is the NEIGHBOUR — and Arab culture made neighbours protectors. In Makkah, a man could enter another's jiwār — his protection — and be safe from enemies.", grammar:"Lan + subjunctive (\"will never\"). -nī = me (with the nūn al-wiqāyah). Hollow verb, Form IV.", why:"Ajāra (to grant neighbourly protection) — evokes the strongest human safety-net of that society — and declares it useless against Allah.", note:"JĀR = a NEIGHBOUR; jiwār = PROTECTION/asylum. No neighbour can shelter you FROM Allah.", more:["Jiwār = protection, asylum (in Arab custom, a neighbour was under your protection); ajāra = to grant protection, to give asylum; istijārah = seeking protection; mujāwir = one who stays near the Ḥaram!","NOT the root of ajr (a wage) or ījār (rent).","(The Prophet ﷺ himself returned from Ṭāʾif under the jiwār of al-Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdī.) Here: no jiwār exists that could protect anyone FROM Allah.","Even the Messenger has no \"neighbour\" to hide behind.","And aḥad #5 — here nominative (aḥadun), as the subject: \"no one will protect me.\" TRAP: yujīr (j-w-r, protection) ≠ ajr (ʾ-j-r, wage) ≠ ījār (rent).","Look-alikes, different roots.","Yujīr (protection) ≠ ajr (wage) ≠ ījār (rent).","Aḥad #5 — here as SUBJECT (nominative)."]},
  {ar:"مُلْتَحَدًا", tr:"Multaḥadā", v:"22", root:"The \"TURNING ASIDE / inclining to one side\" family — laḥada = to incline, to turn aside.", meaning:"\"…and I will never find any refuge besides Him.\"", magic:"ONE ROOT: a grave, atheism, and refuge. Laḥd — the niche in the grave, cut into the side, where you'll be laid. Mulḥid — the atheist, who turns aside from the straight.", grammar:"Form VIII noun of place (multaḥad = place of iltiḥād). min dūnihi = \"besides Him.\"", why:"Multaḥad — the image of ducking aside into a niche — and the root's grave-and-atheism echoes make it haunting.", note:"LAḤD = the GRAVE'S SIDE-NICHE. MULḤID = an ATHEIST. Multaḥad = a HIDING-NICHE.", more:["Laḥd = the SIDE-NICHE dug into the wall of a GRAVE (where the body is laid — \"turned aside\" from the main shaft!); ilḥād = DEVIATION → ATHEISM; mulḥid = an ATHEIST (one who turns aside from the truth); multaḥad = a place to turn aside into, a refuge.","Multaḥad — a hiding place you turn aside into.","The Prophet ﷺ says: there is NO multaḥad — no side-pocket in all of existence to tuck into away from Allah.","The only \"side niche\" you're certain to enter is the laḥd — and there too, you're with Him.","Compare Qiyāmah 75:11 — kallā lā wazar (\"No!","There is no refuge\" — a mountain stronghold) and 75:10 (ayna'l-mafarr).","Three refuge-words denied: wazar (mountain fort), mafarr (escape route), multaḥad (side-niche).","All \"turning aside.\" No side-pocket in existence to hide from Him.","Refuges denied: wazar (75:11), mafarr (75:10), multaḥad (72:22)."]},
  {ar:"إِلَّا بَلَاغًا مِّنَ اللَّهِ وَرِسَالَاتِهِ", tr:"Illā balāghan mina'llāhi wa risālātih", v:"23", root:"Balāgh — the \"REACHING / arriving at the target\" family — balagha = to reach; balāgh = conveying, delivering a message.", meaning:"\"[My only power is] conveying from Allah, and His messages.\"", magic:"Every entry in these guides has a \"Word magic (balāgha)\" layer.", grammar:"Illā — exception from v.22 (lā amliku… illā balāghan: \"I own nothing except delivery\").", why:"Balāgh — defines prophethood as making the message ARRIVE, not forcing its acceptance.", note:"BALĀGHAH (rhetoric) = speech that REACHES its target — the \"Word magic\" layer of every guide you've read!", more:["Tablīgh = proclamation, da'wah; bāligh = an ADULT (one who has reached maturity — bulūgh = puberty!); BALĀGHAH = ELOQUENCE — the science of rhetoric (speech that reaches its target!); mablagh = a SUM of money (the amount \"reached\").","risālāt — messages (risālah = a letter, a message; rasūl = messenger).","THE WORD FOR WHAT YOU'VE BEEN STUDYING ALL ALONG.","Balāghah — Arabic rhetoric — comes from balagha, TO REACH: eloquent speech is speech that REACHES its target — the listener's mind and heart.","And here the Prophet's ﷺ whole job is balāgh — making the message reach.","And bāligh = an ADULT — one who has reached maturity.","Connect to rushd (v.2) — the Qurʾān brings you to maturity (rushd); the Messenger's job is balāgh (reaching).","Two \"maturity\" roots in one surah.","The restriction is total: \"I own no harm, no guidance (v.21), no refuge (v.22) — illā balāghan — EXCEPT delivery.\" His only power is to make it reach you.","Compare Ghāshiyah 88:21 — innamā anta mudhakkir (you are only a reminder) and Nāziʿāt 79:45 (innamā anta mundhir).","Reminder, warner, deliverer — the Prophet's ﷺ three job titles, all limited to transmission.","balāghan = accusative (exception).","Bāligh = an ADULT (reached maturity); mablagh = a SUM of money.","His only power: balāgh — making it REACH you.","Job titles: mudhakkir (88:21), mundhir (79:45), balāgh (72:23)."]},
  {ar:"وَمَن يَعْصِ اللَّهَ وَرَسُولَهُ... خَالِدِينَ فِيهَا أَبَدًا", tr:"Wa man yaʿṣi'llāha wa rasūlahu… khālidīna fīhā abadā", v:"23", root:"Yaʿṣi — the \"DISOBEYING\" family — ʿaṣā = to disobey (Nāziʿāt 79:21, Pharaoh kadhdhaba wa ʿaṣā; and ʿaṣā = a staff — same letters!).", meaning:"\"And whoever disobeys Allah and His Messenger — for him is the Fire of Hell, abiding therein forever.\"", magic:"Allah and His Messenger mentioned TOGETHER — disobeying the conveyed message is disobeying its Sender. That's the logic of balāgh (v.23): the courier carries the King's word.", grammar:"Yaʿṣi — jussive, final yāʾ dropped (from yaʿṣī).", why:"Pairing Allah and His Messenger — closes any loophole of \"I obey God but not the messenger.\"", note:"Disobeying the MESSENGER = disobeying the SENDER (the courier carries the King's word).", more:["khālidīn — the \"ETERNITY\" family (Khālid; Bayyinah 98:6, 8; Humazah 104:3 akhladah).","abad — FOREVER (abadī = eternal; ilā'l-abad = forever).","A grammar detail worth noticing: fa-inna lahu (singular — \"for him\") → khālidīna (plural — \"abiding\").","Man (\"whoever\") is singular in FORM but plural in MEANING — the Qurʾān honours both: singular pronoun first, then plural.","And Bayyinah 98:8 used the same pair — khālidīna fīhā abadā — for the believers in Paradise.","Identical phrase, opposite destinations.","Man: singular form, plural meaning — hence lahu (sg.) then khālidīna (pl.).","Man = singular FORM, plural MEANING → lahu then khālidīna.","Khālidīna fīhā abadā — the SAME PHRASE as Bayyinah 98:8 (Paradise)."]},
  {ar:"فَسَيَعْلَمُونَ مَنْ أَضْعَفُ نَاصِرًا وَأَقَلُّ عَدَدًا", tr:"Fa-sa-yaʿlamūna man aḍʿafu nāṣiran wa aqallu ʿadadā", v:"24", root:"Aḍʿaf — WEAKER (ḍaʿufa = to be weak; ḍuʿf = weakness; ḍiʿf = DOUBLE — a different sense of the same root!).", meaning:"\"…then they will know who is weaker in helpers and fewer in number.\"", magic:"The surah answers Quraysh's favourite boast. Quraysh mocked the early Muslims for being few and weak — a handful of slaves, the poor, the young.", grammar:"Afʿal comparatives ×2. nāṣiran, ʿadadan = tamyīz (specification: \"weaker in terms of helpers\").", why:"Two comparatives — because the boast was itself comparative: \"we have more.\"", note:"AQALLIYYAH = a MINORITY.", more:["nāṣir — a HELPER (naṣr; Ṭāriq 86:10, wa lā nāṣir; Naṣr 110:1; al-Anṣār).","aqall — FEWER (qalīl = few/little; aqalliyyah = a MINORITY!).","ʿadad — NUMBER (Humazah 104:2 ʿaddadah; ʿaddād = a meter).","The verse throws two comparatives back: aḍʿafu (weaker) and aqallu (fewer).","\"You'll find out who's actually weak and few — when you see what you were promised.\" And it came true historically: Naṣr 110:2 — people entering the religion afwājā (in crowds).","The \"few\" became crowds; the boasters had no nāṣir (Ṭāriq 86:10). And ʿadad — Humazah 104:2: the man who counted (ʿaddada) his wealth.","Counting money, counting followers — both will be recounted.","And note ʿadadā returns in v.28 — wa aḥṣā kulla shayʾin ʿadadā — Allah counts EVERYTHING.","v.24: they boast of their numbers.","v.28: He has numbered all things.","Quraysh mocked the \"FEW and WEAK\" — \"you'll see who's weaker and fewer.\" The few became CROWDS (Naṣr 110:2, afwājā).","ʿAdad: v.24 they boast numbers; v.28 He has numbered ALL things.","Ḍiʿf = DOUBLE — same root as weakness!"]},
  {ar:"قُلْ إِنْ أَدْرِي", tr:"Qul in adrī", v:"25", root:"In — here = NEGATION (\"not\"), not \"if.\" adrī — I know (darā).", meaning:"\"Say: I do not know…\"", magic:"The Prophet ﷺ says \"I DON'T KNOW.\" The most knowledgeable of creation, asked when the Hour comes, answers with plain honesty.", grammar:"In = NEGATION here (in an-nāfiyah), equal to mā.", why:"In adrī — a short, bare admission — with no softening.", note:"V.10 JINN: \"lā nadrī\" (we don't know). v.25 PROPHET ﷺ: \"in adrī\" (I don't know). Same humility.", more:["And it mirrors the jinn exactly: v.10 (jinn): wa annā lā nadrī — \"we don't know.\" v.25 (Prophet ﷺ): qul in adrī — \"I don't know.\" Same root, same humility, jinn and Messenger together.","Compare Nāziʿāt 79:42–44 — asked ayyāna mursāhā (\"when does it drop anchor?\"), the answer was fīma anta min dhikrāhā · ilā rabbika muntahāhā — \"that's not yours to know — its end is with your Lord.\" Same truth, two surahs.","You met this trap in Takwīr 81:27 (in huwa illā dhikr = \"it is nothing but a reminder\").","IN = NOT here (like Takwīr 81:27). Nāziʿāt 79:44: \"its end is with your Lord.\""]},
  {ar:"أَقَرِيبٌ مَّا تُوعَدُونَ أَمْ يَجْعَلُ لَهُ رَبِّي أَمَدًا", tr:"A-qarībun mā tūʿadūna am yajʿalu lahu rabbī amadā", v:"25", root:"Qarīb — NEAR (qurb; ʿAlaq 96:19 iqtarib).", meaning:"\"…whether what you are promised is near, or whether my Lord will set for it a distant term.\"", magic:"The two options are left OPEN — on purpose. Near, or far? The Prophet ﷺ doesn't know — and that uncertainty is itself the warning: it could be today.", grammar:"A-… am… = \"whether… or…\" (the same alternative-question as v.10!).", why:"Amad (a span to a limit) — affirms the Hour has a fixed end while hiding its distance.", note:"NEAR or FAR? Unknown — and that's the warning: it could be TODAY. Amad = the distance to a fixed LIMIT.", more:["tūʿadūn — you are PROMISED/threatened (waʿd = promise; waʿīd = threat; passive).","amad — a SPAN of time, a set term, a far limit — the distance to an end-point.","Amad = the distance to a limit — the word implies there is an end-point, just not a known one.","Like Nāziʿāt 79:42's mursāhā (its anchoring) — the Hour has a harbour; the date is hidden.","And note tūʿadūn is passive — \"you are promised\" — the Promiser unnamed, the promise certain.","tūʿadūna = passive present of waʿada (the initial wāw becomes the long ū).","Like Nāziʿāt 79:42's mursāhā — the Hour has a harbour; the date is hidden.","A-… am… — same structure as v.10."]},
  {ar:"عَالِمُ الْغَيْبِ فَلَا يُظْهِرُ عَلَىٰ غَيْبِهِ أَحَدًا", tr:"ʿālimu'l-ghaybi fa-lā yuẓhiru ʿalā ghaybihi aḥadā", v:"26", root:"Ghayb — the \"ABSENCE / the hidden\" family — ghayb = the UNSEEN; ghāʾib = absent (Infiṭār 82:16 bi-ghāʾibīn).", meaning:"\"[He is] the Knower of the Unseen, and He does not disclose His Unseen to anyone.\"", magic:"This verse answers the whole first half of the surah. The jinn used to sit in listening-seats (v.9) trying to steal glimpses of the unseen.", grammar:"ʿālimu = khabar of an implied \"He is.\" aẓhara ʿalā = \"to disclose to / let someone overpower\" — the preposition ʿalā is fixed.", why:"Ghayb (the absent/hidden) — the exact thing the jinn tried to steal and the soothsayers claimed to sell.", note:"GHAYBŪBAH = a COMA (being \"absent\"); ghībah = BACKBITING (about the absent). ẒUHR = NOON — aẓhara = make apparent.", more:["Ghībah = backbiting (talking about the absent!); ghaybūbah = a COMA / unconsciousness (being \"absent\"!).","yuẓhir — the \"APPEARING / the back / prominence\" family — ẓahara = to appear; aẓhara ʿalā = to DISCLOSE to, to give mastery over; ẓahr = the back (Sharḥ 94:3); ẓuhr = NOON (when the sun is most apparent — Ṣalāt aẓ-Ẓuhr!).","Soothsayers (kuhhān) claimed jinn told them secrets.","v.26 closes the door completely: He discloses His ghayb to NO ONE.","SIX aḥads — and the last one says: no one gets the unseen except by His choice.","Ẓuhr (noon) is your hook: aẓhara = to make apparent — like the noon sun showing everything.","He doesn't put His ghayb in the noon light for anyone.","The clinical hook: ghaybūbah = a COMA — the patient is ghāʾib, \"absent.\"","He puts His ghayb in nobody's noon light.","Aḥadā #6 — the last: NO ONE gets the unseen except by His choice."]},
  {ar:"إِلَّا مَنِ ارْتَضَىٰ مِن رَّسُولٍ", tr:"Illā mani'rtaḍā min rasūl", v:"27", root:"The \"CONTENTMENT / approval\" family — raḍiya = to be pleased; irtaḍā (Form VIII) = to APPROVE AND CHOOSE for oneself.", meaning:"\"…except a messenger whom He has approved [and chosen].\"", magic:"The one exception — and it's chosen by APPROVAL. Not by effort, not by sitting in \"listening seats\" (v.9), not by magic.", grammar:"Illā man = exception from v.26's aḥadā. Form VIII of raḍiya, weak-final.", why:"Irtaḍā — makes access to the unseen a matter of divine approval, not technique.", note:"MURTAḌĀ = \"the APPROVED one\" (ʿAlī al-Murtaḍā). The unseen isn't STOLEN (v.9) — it's ENTRUSTED to whom He approves (v.27).", more:["Murtaḍā = the approved/chosen one; you own riḍā across seven surahs (88:9, 89:28, 92:21, 93:5, 98:8, 101:7).","Irtaḍā — Form VIII = He selected with satisfaction, for Himself.","The unseen isn't stolen; it's entrusted — to whom He's pleased with.","The riḍā thread gets a new face: not the servant pleased with Allah (Fajr 89:28), but Allah pleased with a messenger — enough to trust him with the unseen.","Murtaḍā — you may know it as a name (ʿAlī al-Murtaḍā — \"the approved one\").","min rasūlin = min bayāniyyah (\"namely a messenger\").","Riḍā thread: now ALLAH approving a messenger."]},
  {ar:"فَإِنَّهُ يَسْلُكُ مِن بَيْنِ يَدَيْهِ وَمِنْ خَلْفِهِ رَصَدًا", tr:"Fa-innahu yasluku min bayni yadayhi wa min khalfihi raṣadā", v:"27", root:"Yasluk — to THREAD / insert / send along a path (silk = wire/thread — v.17).", meaning:"\"…then He sends, in front of him and behind him, guards [to watch over him].\"", magic:"v.9: yajid lahu shihāban raṣadā — a guard lying in wait to BLOCK the thief. v.27: yasluku… raṣadā — guards threaded around the messenger to PROTECT the message.", grammar:"Bayna yadayhi — dual (yadayn), genitive, nūn dropped in iḍāfah.", why:"Raṣad again — so the opening's \"guarded sky\" and the ending's \"guarded messenger\" are revealed as one system.", note:"RAṢAD v.9 = guards BLOCKING thieves of revelation. RAṢAD v.27 = guards ESCORTING revelation.", more:["bayna yadayhi — \"between his two hands\" = IN FRONT OF him (an idiom).","khalf — BEHIND (khalīfah = a successor, one who comes after).","raṣad — watchers / guards lying in wait (v.9).","THE SURAH'S TWO HALVES MEET IN ONE WORD.","Same word, same surah: raṣad that STOPS the stealing of revelation, and raṣad that ESCORTS the delivery of revelation.","The jinn learned (v.8–9) that the sky was guarded; now we learn why: the message is under armed escort from heaven to the messenger's lips, so that nothing is added or stolen.","And yasluku — the thread verb from v.17 (yaslukhu ʿadhāban ṣaʿadā): v.17: the denier THREADED into a climbing punishment.","v.27: guards THREADED around the messenger.","Same verb — one is confinement, the other is protection.","And bayna yadayhi — \"between his two hands\" = in front — a lovely idiom: what's in front of you is what's between your hands.","raṣadan = object of yasluku (a collective/verbal noun: \"a guard-band\").","One system: the message under armed escort.","Yasluk: v.17 threaded INTO punishment; v.27 threaded AROUND for protection.","Bayna yadayhi = \"between his hands\" = IN FRONT.","Khalīfah = one who comes AFTER (khalf)."]},
  {ar:"لِّيَعْلَمَ أَن قَدْ أَبْلَغُوا رِسَالَاتِ رَبِّهِمْ", tr:"Li-yaʿlama an qad ablaghū risālāti rabbihim", v:"28", root:"Ablaghū — Form IV of balagha = to make reach, to deliver (v.23 balāgh; balāghah, rhetoric!).", meaning:"\"…so that He may make evident that they have delivered the messages of their Lord.\"", magic:"The purpose of the guards (v.27): to ensure the messages are DELIVERED intact.", grammar:"Li- = purpose + subjunctive. qad + past = \"have indeed.\" Form IV (causative: made it reach).", why:"Ablagha — closes the balāgh thread: commanded (v.23), guarded (v.27), completed (v.28).", note:"Balāgh thread: the DUTY (v.23) → GUARDED (v.27) → COMPLETED (v.28: qad ablaghū). Qad + past = HAVE indeed.", more:["risālāt — messages (risālah = a letter).","Li-yaʿlama = \"so that He may know\" — classical note: not that Allah learns something new, but that it becomes manifest / established in reality (ʿilm ẓuhūr).","And qad ablaghū — \"they HAVE delivered\" — qad + past = certainty / completion.","The messengers' job (balāgh, v.23) is confirmed DONE.","Compare Naṣr 110 — the completion of the Prophet's ﷺ mission.","Balāgh in v.23 (the duty) → ablaghū in v.28 (the duty fulfilled).","Note the shift from singular (rasūl, v.27) to plural (ablaghū, risālāt) — the principle covers every messenger, not just one.","Singular rasūl → plural ablaghū: every messenger."]},
  {ar:"وَأَحَاطَ بِمَا لَدَيْهِمْ", tr:"Wa aḥāṭa bi-mā ladayhim", v:"28", root:"The \"SURROUNDING / encircling\" family — ḥāṭa = to surround; aḥāṭa bi- = to ENCOMPASS fully, to surround on all sides.", meaning:"\"And He has encompassed whatever is with them.\"", magic:"Aḥāṭa = to surround completely — like an OCEAN surrounds land. Nothing the messengers carry is outside His encirclement.", grammar:"Aḥāṭa bi- — the bi- is fixed. ladā = \"with / in the presence of\" (more immediate than ʿinda).", why:"Aḥāṭa — total encirclement, with no gap for loss or tampering.", note:"AL-MUḤĪṬ AL-HĀDĪ = the PACIFIC OCEAN. The ocean SURROUNDS land — He surrounds all they carry. Ḥāʾiṭ = a WALL.", more:["Muḥīṭ = an OCEAN (al-Muḥīṭ al-Hādī = the PACIFIC Ocean; al-Muḥīṭ al-Aṭlasī = the Atlantic!) — the water that surrounds the land; muḥīṭ also = a CIRCUMFERENCE / perimeter (math!) and an ENVIRONMENT (surroundings); ḥāʾiṭ = a WALL (that encloses!).","Ladayhim = \"with them / in their possession\" — every word, every detail.","You own muḥīṭ from Burūj 85:20 — wa'llāhu min warāʾihim muḥīṭ — \"Allah encompasses them from behind\" — said of the persecutors.","Burūj: He encompasses the ENEMIES (so they can't escape).","Jinn: He encompasses the MESSENGERS (so nothing is lost).","The same encircling — a trap for one, a safeguard for the other.","Hooks: al-muḥīṭ = the OCEAN; ḥāʾiṭ = a WALL; muḥīṭ ad-dāʾirah = a circle's circumference.","Burūj 85:20: He encompasses the PERSECUTORS (trap).","Jinn 72:28: He encompasses the MESSENGERS (safeguard)."]},
  {ar:"وَأَحْصَىٰ كُلَّ شَيْءٍ عَدَدًا", tr:"Wa aḥṣā kulla shayʾin ʿadadā", v:"28", root:"Aḥṣā — the \"COUNTING with PEBBLES\" family — ḥaṣā = PEBBLES.", meaning:"\"…and has counted every single thing in number.\"", magic:"THE SURAH'S LAST WORDS — nearly identical to a verse you own.", grammar:"Form IV (aḥṣā), weak-final. ʿadadan = tamyīz or maf'ūl muṭlaq-like (\"counted in terms of number\").", why:"Aḥṣā (pebble-counting) — total, item-by-item precision — the surah's final word on the unseen: nothing escapes the count.", note:"IḤṢĀʾ = STATISTICS; ḥaṣā = PEBBLES; ḥaṣāh = a KIDNEY STONE. A PEBBLE for every single thing. Nabaʾ 78:29: counted into a BOOK.", more:["Aḥṣā = to count precisely, item by item (the ancient way: a pebble for each thing); iḥṣāʾ = STATISTICS (modern: al-iḥṣāʾ = statistics; iḥṣāʾiyyāt = figures/statistics!); ḥaṣāh = a kidney/gall STONE (clinical!).","Nabaʾ 78:29 — wa kulla shayʾin aḥṣaynāhu kitābā — \"and every thing We have counted in a record.\" Jinn 72:28 — wa aḥṣā kulla shayʾin ʿadadā — \"and has counted every thing in number.\" Same verb, same phrase kulla shayʾ.","Nabaʾ: counted into a BOOK (kitābā).","Jinn: counted by NUMBER (ʿadadā).","Every thing written AND numbered.","And the pebbles: ḥaṣā = pebbles — the ancient tally: drop a pebble for each item.","And the ʿadad closes the surah's argument: v.24 — the boasters thought they had the bigger NUMBERS (aqallu ʿadadā).","v.28 — He has NUMBERED every single thing (ʿadadā).","They counted their followers; He counts everything — including them.","The clinical hook: ḥaṣāh = a kidney/gall STONE (ḥaṣā kulwiyyah).","And iḥṣāʾ = STATISTICS — the science of counting.","The final rhyme: ʿadadā — the last of 28 verses ending in -adā.","kulla shayʾin = object, fronted in Nabaʾ, here after the verb.","Jinn 72:28: counted by NUMBER.","v.24: they boasted NUMBERS.","v.28: He has numbered EVERYTHING."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:73, name:"Al-Muzzammil", ar:"المزمل", en:"The Enshrouded One", verses:20, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"19 verses of HARD night prayer → 1 verse of EASE. One hardship, then ease — the Sharḥ promise, lived out.",
  special:["Nineteen short verses give a heavy command — and then one enormous verse lifts it.","V.1–19: short, rhythmic verses, nearly all ending in -īlā (qalīlā, tartīlā, thaqīlā, qīlā, ṭawīlā, tabtīlā, wakīlā, jamīlā…). The command: stand most of the night in prayer, reciting the Qurʾān slowly.","V.20: one of the longest verses in this part of the Qurʾān — and it eases the command: \"He knows you cannot keep exact count of it, so He has turned to you in mercy — so recite what is easy of the Qurʾān.\"","It is reported (from ʿĀʾishah, in Muslim) that Allah held back the end of this surah for about a year, during which the Prophet ﷺ and companions prayed through the nights until their feet swelled — then v.20 came down with the relief. Many scholars consider v.20 Madinan, since it mentions fighting in Allah's way.","The surah teaches by STRUCTURE: first the training, then the mercy. And the key word of the relief is tayassara — \"what is EASY\" — the yusr root you traced through ʿAbasa, Aʿlā, Layl, and Sharḥ (inna maʿa'l-ʿusri yusrā). Hardship (v.1–19), then ease (v.20) — Sharḥ 94:5–6, enacted.","--"],
  big:["The address (v.1) — O you wrapped in garments!","The night prayer (v.2–4) — half the night, a little less or more; recite slowly.","Why the night (v.5–7) — a heavy Word is coming; night is firmer and more upright; the day is for swimming through tasks.","Devotion (v.8–9) — remember His name, devote yourself wholly; take Him as your Trustee.","Patience (v.10–11) — bear what they say; leave them beautifully; leave the deniers to Me.","What awaits (v.12–14) — shackles, blaze, choking food — the day the mountains become sliding sand.","The Pharaoh precedent (v.15–16) — a witness sent to you, as to Pharaoh.","The Day that greys children (v.17–19) — and this is a reminder.","The relief (v.20) — recite what is easy; pray, give, lend to Allah, seek forgiveness."],
  traps:["النَّعْمَةِ (v.11) — naʿmah (FATḤAH) = LUXURY; niʿmah (KASRAH) = a BLESSING. One vowel — gratitude vs. self-indulgence.","V.19 = Insān 76:29 word-for-word — don't learn them as two verses.","فَتَابَ عَلَيْكُمْ (v.20) — NOT about sin here — Allah turning to them with EASE. Tāba ʿalā (Allah relents) ≠ tāba ilā (servant repents).","الْمُزَّمِّلُ (v.1) — Form V with ASSIMILATION (mutazammil → muzzammil). Same rule as ʿAbasa's yazzakkā and Muddaththir's muddaththir.","مُنفَطِرٌ (v.18) — MASCULINE for a feminine samāʾ — \"a thing of splitting\" / sky as \"ceiling.\" Not an error.","تَبَتَّلْ... تَبْتِيلًا (v.8) — MIXED forms (Form V verb + Form II maṣdar) — deliberate, combining both meanings.","وَذَرْنِي (v.11) — dhar is from a DEFECTIVE verb (no past tense); wāw al-maʿiyyah (\"with\") makes al-mukadhdhibīn accusative.","يَضْرِبُونَ فِي الْأَرْضِ (v.20) — an IDIOM: to TRAVEL, not \"strike.\"","وَبِيلًا (v.16) — from w-b-l (heavy downpour), NOT wabāʾ (epidemic, w-b-ʾ).","آخَرُونَ (v.20) — ākharūn = OTHERS; ≠ ākhirah (the Hereafter/last).","هُوَ خَيْرًا (v.20) — khayran is ACCUSATIVE — the second object of tajidūhu; huwa is just emphasis.","رَسُولًا → الرَّسُولَ (v.15–16) — indefinite then definite = \"THE one just mentioned\" (al-ʿahd adh-dhikrī).","ثُلُثَيِ (v.20) — DUAL with nūn dropped; niṣfahu/thuluthahu has two authentic recitations (accusative / genitive).","نَاشِئَةَ (v.6) — NOT related to inshāʾallāh (that's shāʾa).","أُولِي (v.11) — the wāw is written but SILENT.","يَوْمًا (v.17) — OBJECT of tattaqūn — \"shield yourselves FROM a Day,\" not \"on a day.\""],
  links:[{w:"الْمُزَّمِّلُ (v.1)",e:"الْمُدَّثِّرُ (Muddaththir 74:1)",t:"Twin garment-addresses — Form V assimilation"},{w:"الْمُزَّمِّلُ (v.1)",e:"يَزَّكَّىٰ · يَذَّكَّرُ (ʿAbasa 80:3–4)",t:"Form V assimilation"},{w:"قُمِ (v.2)",e:"قُمْ فَأَنذِرْ (Muddaththir 74:2)",t:"Rise to PRAY / rise to WARN"},{w:"زِدْ (v.4)",e:"نَزِيدَكُمْ (78:30) · فَزَادُوهُمْ (72:6)",t:"Increase"},{w:"تَرْتِيلًا (v.4)",e:"لَا تُحَرِّكْ بِهِ لِسَانَكَ (Qiyāmah 75:16)",t:"Don't RUSH / recite with TARTĪL"},{w:"ثَقِيلًا (v.5)",e:"ثَقِيلًا (76:27) · ثَقُلَتْ (101:6)",t:"Heavy Day / heavy Word / heavy scales"},{w:"أَشَدُّ (v.6)",e:"78:12 · 76:28 · 85:12 · 100:8 · 72:8",t:"The shadīd thread"},{w:"أَقْوَمُ (v.6) · أَقِيمُوا (v.20)",e:"81:28 · 95:4 · 98:3, 5 · 72:16",t:"The q-w-m thread — stand → upright speech → establish prayer"},{w:"سَبْحًا (v.7)",e:"السَّابِحَاتِ (79:3) · سَبِّحِ (87:1) · فَسَبِّحْ (110:3)",t:"Swimming / gliding / glorifying"},{w:"وَاذْكُرِ اسْمَ رَبِّكَ (v.8)",e:"وَذَكَرَ اسْمَ رَبِّهِ (Aʿlā 87:15)",t:"Described → commanded"},{w:"وَكِيلًا (v.9)",e:"بِمُصَيْطِرٍ (88:22) · لَا أَمْلِكُ (72:21)",t:"Control nothing → entrust everything"},{w:"اتَّخِذْهُ (v.9)",e:"مَا اتَّخَذَ صَاحِبَةً (Jinn 72:3)",t:"He took no consort / take HIM as Trustee"},{w:"وَاصْبِرْ (v.10)",e:"فَاصْبِرْ (74:7) · بِالصَّبْرِ (103:3, 90:17)",t:"Twin commands of patience"},{w:"وَاهْجُرْهُمْ (v.10)",e:"وَالرُّجْزَ فَاهْجُرْ (Muddaththir 74:5)",t:"Leave PEOPLE beautifully / leave IDOLATRY absolutely"},{w:"وَذَرْنِي (v.11)",e:"ذَرْنِي (Muddaththir 74:11)",t:"BOTH VERSE 11"},{w:"ذَرْنِي (v.11)",e:"تَذَرُونَ (75:21) · يَذَرُونَ (76:27)",t:"The defective verb"},{w:"النَّعْمَةِ (v.11)",e:"نَعَّمَهُ (Fajr 89:15) · بِنِعْمَةِ (93:11)",t:"Naʿmah (luxury) vs niʿmah (blessing)"},{w:"وَمَهِّلْهُمْ (v.11)",e:"فَمَهِّلِ... أَمْهِلْهُمْ (Ṭāriq 86:17)",t:"Nearly identical"},{w:"أَنكَالًا (v.12)",e:"نَكَالَ (Nāziʿāt 79:25)",t:"The word → the shackle"},{w:"غُصَّةٍ (v.13)",e:"ضَرِيعٍ (Ghāshiyah 88:6)",t:"Food that won't nourish / won't go down"},{w:"تَرْجُفُ (v.14)",e:"تَرْجُفُ الرَّاجِفَةُ (Nāziʿāt 79:6)",t:"Same opening"},{w:"كَثِيبًا مَّهِيلًا (v.14)",e:"78:20 · 81:3 · 89:21 · 101:5",t:"Mountain-fates"},{w:"رَسُولًا... الرَّسُولَ (v.15–16)",e:"",t:"Indefinite → definite = referring back"},{w:"فَعَصَىٰ فِرْعَوْنُ (v.16)",e:"فَكَذَّبَ وَعَصَىٰ (Nāziʿāt 79:21)",t:"Pharaoh disobeyed"},{w:"فَأَخَذْنَاهُ (v.16)",e:"فَأَخَذَهُ اللَّهُ (Nāziʿāt 79:25)",t:"Pharaoh seized"},{w:"الْوِلْدَانَ (v.17)",e:"وِلْدَانٌ مُّخَلَّدُونَ (Insān 76:19)",t:"Children who never age / children who age instantly"},{w:"مُنفَطِرٌ (v.18)",e:"انفَطَرَتْ (Infiṭār 82:1)",t:"The sky splits"},{w:"V.19 (whole verse)",e:"Insān 76:29",t:"WORD-FOR-WORD IDENTICAL"},{w:"تُحْصُوهُ (v.20)",e:"أَحْصَيْنَاهُ (78:29) · أَحْصَىٰ (72:28)",t:"Allah counts all / you can't count one night"},{w:"فَتَابَ عَلَيْكُمْ (v.20)",e:"تَوَّابًا (Naṣr 110:3)",t:"He turns to forgive / to ease"},{w:"مَا تَيَسَّرَ ×2 (v.20)",e:"80:20 · 84:8 · 87:8 · 92:7 · 94:5–6",t:"The yusr thread — Sharḥ enacted"},{w:"فَاقْرَءُوا (v.20)",e:"اقْرَأْ (ʿAlaq 96:1)",t:"The first word revealed returns"},{w:"مَّرْضَىٰ (v.20)",e:"مَّرَضٌ (Muddaththir 74:31)",t:"Body-illness excused / heart-illness exposed"},{w:"يَبْتَغُونَ (v.20)",e:"ابْتِغَاءَ وَجْهِ رَبِّهِ (Layl 92:20)",t:"Seek His face / seek His bounty"},{w:"أَقِيمُوا... آتُوا (v.20)",e:"Bayyinah 98:5 · Kawthar 108:2",t:"The two pillars"},{w:"أَقْرِضُوا اللَّهَ (v.20)",e:"الصَّمَدُ (Ikhlāṣ 112:2) · الْمَاعُونَ (107:7)",t:"The One who needs nothing asks a LOAN"},{w:"تُقَدِّمُوا لِأَنفُسِكُم (v.20)",e:"قَدَّمْتُ لِحَيَاتِي (Fajr 89:24)",t:"The regret → the invitation"},{w:"وَاسْتَغْفِرُوا (v.20)",e:"وَاسْتَغْفِرْهُ (Naṣr 110:3)",t:"Victory and worship both end in istighfār"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"يَا أَيُّهَا الْمُزَّمِّلُ", tr:"Yā ayyuha'l-muzzammil", v:"1", root:"The \"WRAPPING oneself up in clothing\" family — zammala = to wrap; tazammala (Form V) = to wrap ONESELF in garments; zimmal = a bundle wrapped up.", meaning:"\"O you who wraps himself [in garments]!\"", magic:"He isn't called by NAME — he's called by his STATE.", grammar:"Form V with ASSIMILATION: mutazammil → muzzammil — the tāʾ of Form V melts into the zāy and doubles it.", why:"Muzzammil — meets him in the exact moment he was in, before lifting him out of it.", note:"\"ZAMMILŪNĪ!\" — \"wrap me up!\" after Ḥirāʾ → Allah calls him \"al-MUZZAMMIL\", the wrapped one. Called by his STATE, with tenderness.", more:["When the Prophet ﷺ came home from Ḥirāʾ shaking and said \"zammilūnī\" (\"wrap me up!\"), Allah addresses him with that exact word: al-muzzammil, \"the wrapped one.\" Arabs addressed someone by their current state as affection — like calling a sleeping child \"O little sleeper.\" It's tender, not a rebuke: I saw you wrapped up, frightened — now rise.","And its twin is the very next surah: Muddaththir 74:1 — yā ayyuha'l-muddaththir — \"O you covered in a cloak.\" Two surahs, two garment-words, two first commands: rise to PRAY (73:2), rise to WARN (74:2).","The SAME rule you learned in ʿAbasa 80:3–4 (yatazakkā → yazzakkā, yatadhakkaru → yadhdhakkaru).","And Muddaththir 74:1 (mutadaththir → muddaththir) does it again.","Mutazammil → muzzammil — Form V assimilation (like ʿAbasa's yazzakkā)."]},
  {ar:"قُمِ اللَّيْلَ إِلَّا قَلِيلًا", tr:"Qumi'l-layla illā qalīlā", v:"2", root:"Qum — the \"STANDING\" family — qāma = to stand; qiyām = standing (in prayer).", meaning:"\"Stand [in prayer through] the night — except a little.\"", magic:"The first command is to STAND. Qum — \"rise up.\" The one who was lying wrapped (v.1) is told to get UP.", grammar:"Qum = imperative of a hollow verb (the long ū drops).", why:"Qum (stand) rather than ṣalli (pray) — names the physical act of rising from sleep — the hardest part.", note:"QIYĀM AL-LAYL = \"STANDING the night\" — the term comes from THIS verse.", more:["Qiyām al-layl = the NIGHT PRAYER (literally \"standing the night\"!); istaqāma, mustaqīm (the straight path).","qalīl — little, few (aqalliyyah = a minority; Jinn 72:24 aqallu ʿadadā).","And qumi'l-layl — \"stand the night\" — is literally the origin of qiyām al-layl, the term you use for night prayer.","You stand the night, not just pray in it.","Muddaththir 74:2 begins the same way: qum fa-andhir — \"rise and WARN.\" Two qums: rise in private to worship (73:2), rise in public to warn (74:2). The private standing comes first in the Qurʾān's order — the night builds the man who warns by day.","And illā qalīlā — \"except a little\" — the flexibility is built in from verse 2.","qumi — a kasrah added to avoid two sukūns before al-layl.","al-layla = accusative of time (\"through the night\").","Two qums: 73:2 rise to PRAY (private); 74:2 rise to WARN (public).","The night builds the man who warns by day."]},
  {ar:"نِّصْفَهُ أَوِ انقُصْ مِنْهُ قَلِيلًا · أَوْ زِدْ عَلَيْهِ", tr:"Niṣfahu awi'nquṣ minhu qalīlā · aw zid ʿalayh", v:"3–4", root:"Niṣf — HALF (muntaṣaf = the middle; munṣif = fair — one who gives the half that's due!).", meaning:"\"Half of it — or reduce it a little — or add to it.\"", magic:"A command with THREE built-in options. Half the night — or a little less than half — or a little more than half. Allah gives a range, not a stopwatch.", grammar:"Niṣfahu — accusative, a badal (apposition) of al-layl or of qalīlā.", why:"Offering naqṣ and ziyādah — makes worship a range you choose within, not a rigid quota.", note:"HALF the night — bid DOWN (unquṣ) or bid UP (zid). Munāqaṣah = a TENDER (bidding down); mazād = an AUCTION (bidding up).", more:["unquṣ — the \"DECREASING\" family — naqaṣa = to decrease; naqṣ = a deficiency (naqīṣah = a DEFECT, a shortcoming; munāqaṣah = a TENDER / auction — bidding the price down!).","zid — the \"INCREASING\" family — zāda; ziyādah; mazād = an AUCTION (bidding UP!).","And the auction hook makes it unforgettable: munāqaṣah (bidding DOWN) and mazād (bidding UP) — the verse lets you bid down (unquṣ) or bid up (zid) from the half.","And this flexibility foreshadows v.20 — \"He knows you can't keep exact count\" — the room to adjust was there from verse 3. The zāda root you own from Nabaʾ 78:30 (fa-lan nazīdakum — \"We'll only INCREASE you in punishment\") and Jinn 72:6 (fa-zādūhum rahaqā).","Increasing punishment, increasing burden — and here, increasing worship.","unquṣ / zid — imperatives; zid is from a hollow verb (long ī drops).","Naqīṣah = a DEFECT; munṣif = FAIR.","The flexibility foreshadows v.20."]},
  {ar:"وَرَتِّلِ الْقُرْآنَ تَرْتِيلًا", tr:"Wa rattili'l-qurʾāna tartīlā", v:"4", root:"The \"EVENLY SPACED / well-ordered\" family — ratila = to be evenly arranged.", meaning:"\"…and recite the Qurʾān with measured, distinct recitation.\"", magic:"The root image is TEETH — evenly spaced, each one distinct.", grammar:"Form II imperative (rattil) + cognate accusative (tartīlā) = maximum emphasis.", why:"Tartīl (evenly spaced) — because the night is for UNDERSTANDING, and speed kills understanding.", note:"Thaghr rattal = EVENLY-SPACED TEETH — tartīl = every word distinct, evenly paced.", more:["Thaghr rattal = TEETH that are evenly spaced, well-set (the classical image!); rattala (Form II) = to recite in measured, distinct, well-ordered sequence; tartīl = slow, clear, measured recitation; tartīlah = a HYMN / a chant (modern).","Tartīl = recitation where every letter and word stands clearly SEPARATE, evenly paced — no rushing, no swallowing letters, no running words together.","ʿAlī (رضي الله عنه) is reported to have defined tartīl as: \"perfecting the letters (tajwīd al-ḥurūf) and knowing the stops (maʿrifat al-wuqūf).\" That's tajwīd and waqf — the two pillars you learn.","The cognate accusative tartīlā — \"recite it with a reciting\" — Arabic's underline: REALLY slowly, REALLY clearly.","And compare Qiyāmah 75:16 — lā tuḥarrik bihi lisānaka li-taʿjala bih — \"don't move your tongue to HURRY with it.\" Qiyāmah: don't RUSH.","Muzzammil: recite with TARTĪL.","Two surahs, one instruction for reciting.","And Aʿlā 87:6 — sa-nuqriʾuka fa-lā tansā (\"We'll make you recite; you won't forget\").","rattili — kasrah added before al-qurʾān to avoid two sukūns.","ʿAlī: \"perfecting the LETTERS and knowing the STOPS.\" Qiyāmah 75:16: don't RUSH.","Muzzammil 73:4: recite with TARTĪL."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّا سَنُلْقِي عَلَيْكَ قَوْلًا ثَقِيلًا", tr:"Innā sa-nulqī ʿalayka qawlan thaqīlā", v:"5", root:"Nulqī — the \"CASTING / throwing down\" family — alqā = to throw, to cast.", meaning:"\"Indeed, We will cast upon you a heavy Word.\"", magic:"WHY must he stand the night? Because the Qurʾān is HEAVY — and you train for heavy loads.", grammar:"Sa- + present = near future (\"We will cast\"). Form IV alqā, weak-final. qawlan thaqīlan = object + adjective.", why:"Thaqīl — explains the command to stand the night: you build strength for a weight.", note:"A HEAVY Word — he SWEATED on cold days; his CAMEL KNELT under it. The night prayer = the gym; the Qurʾān = the weight.", more:["Talaqqā = to RECEIVE (catching what's thrown!); ilqāʾ = a LECTURE / delivery (modern: ilqāʾ kalimah = giving a speech); Inshiqāq 84:4 wa alqat mā fīhā (the earth casts out its contents).","thaqīl — the \"HEAVINESS\" family — Insān 76:27 (yawman thaqīlā), Zalzalah 99:2 (athqālahā), 99:7 (mithqāl), Qāriʿah 101:6 (thaqulat).","Heavy in three ways (classical): (a) PHYSICALLY — it's reported that when revelation came, the Prophet's ﷺ forehead streamed with sweat even on a cold day, and if he was riding, his camel would kneel under the weight; (b) in RESPONSIBILITY — its commands are weighty to carry out; (c) in VALUE — heavy as gold is heavy, weighty with meaning.","Nulqī = We will CAST it upon you — like a load dropped onto your shoulders.","The night prayer (v.2) is the gym; the heavy Word (v.5) is the weight.","The thaqīl thread: Insān 76:27 — the HEAVY Day (they leave behind).","Muzzammil 73:5 — the HEAVY Word (you carry).","Carry the heavy Word now, or face the heavy Day later.","And Qāriʿah 101:6 — man thaqulat mawāzīnuh — \"whose scales are HEAVY.\" The heavy Word, carried well, makes the scales heavy.","Insān 76:27: the HEAVY Day.","Muzzammil 73:5: the HEAVY Word.","Qāriʿah 101:6: HEAVY scales.","Ilqāʾ = a LECTURE; talaqqā = to RECEIVE."]},
  {ar:"نَاشِئَةَ اللَّيْلِ", tr:"Nāshiʾata'l-layl", v:"6", root:"The \"ARISING / coming into being / growing up\" family — nashaʾa = to arise, to originate, to grow.", meaning:"\"Indeed, the rising by night [for prayer]…\"", magic:"Nāshiʾat al-layl = \"what ARISES in the night\" — classical readings: (a) the act of RISING from sleep to pray.", grammar:"Active participle, feminine (fāʿilah), used as a noun; iḍāfah with al-layl. Subject of inna.", why:"Nāshiʾah (arising/originating) — makes the night prayer the birthplace of something new in you.", note:"NASHʾAH = UPBRINGING / origin. Nāshiʾat al-layl = what ARISES at night — prayer after sleeping. Munshaʾah = a FACILITY.", more:["Nashʾah = ORIGIN, upbringing (an-nashʾah al-ūlā / al-ukhrā = the first / the second creation — dunya and resurrection!); munshaʾah = a FACILITY / installation (something \"brought into being\"); inshāʾ = construction / composition; inshāʾallāh is a different root (shāʾa)!","(b) the hours of the night themselves, as they come into being one after another; (c) worship that is \"born\" in the night.","The root is origin and growth — nashʾah = upbringing — the night prayer is where a person is RAISED UP, spiritually.","And note: rising after sleep — Ibn ʿAbbās and others specified that nāshiʾah is prayer after having slept — the harder, and therefore the more powerful.","Don't confuse with inshāʾallāh — that's from shāʾa (to will), a different root.","NOT the root of inshāʾallāh (shāʾa)."]},
  {ar:"هِيَ أَشَدُّ وَطْئًا", tr:"Hiya ashaddu waṭʾā", v:"6", root:"Ashadd — STRONGER / firmer / more intense (the shadīd thread).", meaning:"\"…is firmer in impact [heavier on the body, deeper in effect].\"", magic:"Waṭʾ = a FIRM FOOTSTEP pressing down.", grammar:"Ashaddu = comparative (afʿal). waṭʾan = tamyīz (\"firmer in terms of impact\").", why:"Waṭʾ (a firm tread) — captures both the weight on the body and the mark it leaves.", note:"Taḥta WAṬʾAT = \"under the PRESSURE of.\" Waṭʾ = a FIRM FOOTSTEP.", more:["waṭʾ — the \"TREADING / pressing down underfoot\" family — waṭiʾa = to tread on, to step on firmly; mawṭiʾ = a FOOTHOLD / a footstep; waṭʾah = PRESSURE, impact (modern: taḥta waṭʾat = \"under the pressure of\" — e.g.","under the pressure of debt!).","Night prayer is ashaddu waṭʾā — two classical meanings, both true: (a) HARDER on the body — it presses down on you (waking, standing tired); (b) leaves a DEEPER IMPRINT — a firm footstep leaves a clear mark; the night's worship presses deeper into the heart.","The difficulty and the depth are the same thing — what costs more, marks more.","The shadīd thread again: firm sky (78:12), firm frame (76:28), His severe grip (85:12), man's intense love of wealth (100:8), the strong guards (72:8) — and now: the night prayer's FIRM tread.","hiya = separating/emphatic pronoun.","Night prayer: HARDER on the body AND a DEEPER imprint — what costs more, marks more."]},
  {ar:"وَأَقْوَمُ قِيلًا", tr:"Wa aqwamu qīlā", v:"6", root:"Aqwam — comparative of qawīm = MORE UPRIGHT / more straight / more correct — the q-w-m root (qāma, qiyām, mustaqīm, taqwīm, qayyimah).", meaning:"\"…and more upright in speech [more fitting for recitation].\"", magic:"Qum (v.2) = stand. Aqwam (v.6) = more upright — SAME ROOT (q-w-m). The physical standing produces upright words.", grammar:"Aqwamu = comparative; qīlan = tamyīz.", why:"Aqwam — ties upright speech to upright standing through one root.", note:"QUM (stand, v.2) → AQWAM (more upright speech, v.6) — SAME ROOT. Standing straight at night makes your words stand straight.", more:["qīl — a noun from qāla (to say) = SPEECH, utterance (qīl wa qāl = gossip, \"he said, she said\"!).","STANDING the night → SPEECH that STANDS STRAIGHT.","At night, with no noise and no audience, the tongue and the heart are aligned — what you recite, you mean; the words \"stand straight.\" Qīl — the everyday phrase qīl wa qāl = idle chatter, gossip.","The night turns qīl from gossip into upright speech.","The q-w-m thread now includes the body and the tongue: qum (stand, 73:2), aqwam (upright speech, 73:6), istaqāmū (keep straight, Jinn 72:16), taqwīm (upright form, Tīn 95:4), qayyimah (upright religion, Bayyinah 98:5), mustaqīm (the straight path, 17× a day).","Parallel structure with ashaddu waṭʾā: two comparatives + two specifications.","Qīl wa qāl = GOSSIP — the night turns qīl into upright speech."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّ لَكَ فِي النَّهَارِ سَبْحًا طَوِيلًا", tr:"Inna laka fi'n-nahāri sabḥan ṭawīlā", v:"7", root:"Sabḥ — the \"SWIMMING / gliding / moving freely about\" family — sabaḥa = to swim; sibāḥah = SWIMMING.", meaning:"\"Indeed, for you in the day is prolonged occupation [moving about in your affairs].\"", magic:"The DAY is pictured as SWIMMING. Sabḥ = swimming — moving freely, back and forth, through many tasks — like a swimmer moving through water: busy, in motion, covering distance.", grammar:"Inna laka (fronted predicate) + subject sabḥan (delayed).", why:"Sabḥ (swimming) — pictures daytime work as movement through a medium — tiring, scattered, horizontal; the night is vertical.", note:"MASBAḤ = a SWIMMING POOL. The DAY = SWIMMING through tasks; the NIGHT = STANDING still before Him.", more:["Masbaḥ = a SWIMMING POOL; tasbīḥ = glorifying (swimming away from every flaw).","ṭawīl — LONG (ṭūl = length).","That's why the night is for prayer: the day is for swimming through your affairs (da'wah, work, people); the night is for standing still before Allah.","The s-b-ḥ root across your surahs: - Nāziʿāt 79:3 — wa's-sābiḥāti sabḥā — the angels who GLIDE/swim through the heavens - Aʿlā 87:1 — sabbiḥ isma rabbika'l-aʿlā — GLORIFY (swim away from every flaw) - Naṣr 110:3 — fa-sabbiḥ bi-ḥamdi rabbik — GLORIFY at the moment of victory - Muzzammil 73:7 — sabḥan ṭawīlā — the day's SWIMMING through tasks Same root: angels gliding, glorifying Allah, and the believer swimming through the day's work.","And here's the subtle point: sabḥ (swimming/busy-ness) vs tasbīḥ (glorification) — the same root.","Even your day's \"swimming\" can become tasbīḥ if aimed right.","The cognate-style sabḥan ṭawīlā mirrors Nāziʿāt 79:3's sabḥā.","fi'n-nahāri = \"in the day.\" Note the contrast: al-layl (v.2, 6) vs an-nahār (v.7).","S-b-ḥ: angels GLIDE (79:3), GLORIFY (87:1, 110:3), the day's SWIMMING (73:7). Your day's swimming can become tasbīḥ."]},
  {ar:"وَاذْكُرِ اسْمَ رَبِّكَ", tr:"Wa'dhkuri'sma rabbik", v:"8", root:"The \"REMEMBERING / mentioning\" family — dhikr — the juzʾ's master thread (76:29, 80:11, 81:27, 87:9, 88:21, 89:23, 114:4).", meaning:"\"And remember the name of your Lord…\"", magic:"You own this exact phrase from Aʿlā 87:15 — wa dhakara'sma rabbihi fa-ṣallā — \"he remembered the name of his Lord and prayed\" — the definition of falāḥ (success).", grammar:"Imperative (udhkur); kasrah on udhkuri before ism (two sukūns). isma = object.", why:"Dhikr of the name — the simplest, most constant form of worship, there in every moment.", note:"Aʿlā 87:15 DESCRIBED the successful man: dhakara'sma rabbih. Muzzammil 73:8 COMMANDS it: udhkuri'sma rabbik.", more:["Aʿlā described the successful man; Muzzammil COMMANDS the Prophet ﷺ to be him.","And Aʿlā 87:1 — sabbiḥi'sma rabbik — \"glorify the NAME of your Lord.\" The NAME is remembered, glorified, mentioned — the gateway to the One named.","And recall Nās 114:4 — al-khannās — the whisperer RETREATS at dhikr.","Remembering His name is the first line of defence.","Aʿlā 87:1: glorify the NAME.","Nās 114:4: the whisperer retreats at dhikr."]},
  {ar:"وَتَبَتَّلْ إِلَيْهِ تَبْتِيلًا", tr:"Wa tabattal ilayhi tabtīlā", v:"8", root:"The \"CUTTING OFF / severing completely\" family — batala = to cut off, to sever.", meaning:"\"…and devote yourself to Him with complete devotion.\"", magic:"The root is CUTTING — devotion as SEVERANCE. Tabattul = cutting every other attachment so you are wholly His.", grammar:"Tabattal (Form V imperative) + tabtīlā (Form II maṣdar) — mixed forms, deliberately combining meanings.", why:"Tabattul — devotion defined by what you let go of, not just what you add.", note:"AL-BATŪL = MARYAM's title (and FĀṬIMAH's) — \"wholly devoted, cut off from all but Him.\" Devotion = SEVERANCE.", more:["Tabattala = to devote oneself COMPLETELY, cutting oneself off from all else; al-Batūl = \"the Devoted one, cut off from all but Allah\" — the title of MARYAM (عليها السلام), and of FĀṬIMAH (رضي الله عنها)!","tabattul = devotion / (also) celibacy in some usage.","Maryam was called al-Batūl — the woman wholly devoted, cut off from worldly concerns in her miḥrāb.","Fāṭimah too was called al-Batūl.","THE GRAMMAR IS UNUSUAL — and it's a lesson.","The verb is tabattal (Form V — devote yourself, with effort).","But the cognate accusative is tabtīlā (Form II — the verbal noun of battala, to devote / sever actively).","The \"expected\" form would be tabattulā (Form V's own verbal noun).","Classical explanation: the two forms are COMBINED so the verse means BOTH — \"devote yourself (Form V) and sever yourself thoroughly (Form II).\" Effort on your part, intensity in the act.","One verse, two forms, double meaning.","Note ilayhi — devote TO HIM — it's not cutting off for its own sake (monasticism); it's cutting off in order to turn TO Him.","Tabattal (Form V) + tabtīlā (Form II) — MIXED forms on purpose: effort + intensity.","Ilayhi = cut off in order to turn TO Him."]},
  {ar:"رَّبُّ الْمَشْرِقِ وَالْمَغْرِبِ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا هُوَ", tr:"Rabbu'l-mashriqi wa'l-maghribi lā ilāha illā huwa", v:"9", root:"Mashriq — the \"RISING / shining\" family — sharaqa = to rise (sun); mashriq = the EAST (place of sunrise).", meaning:"\"Lord of the East and the West — there is no god but Him.\"", magic:"A merism: East and West = EVERYWHERE. The two ends of the sun's journey = all of space, all of time (every sunrise and sunset). And why mention it right after tabattul?", grammar:"Rabbu — nominative (a new sentence: \"[He is] the Lord…\").", why:"East and West — the widest possible frame, given to a man told to narrow his heart to one focus.", note:"AL-MAGHRIB = MOROCCO (land of SUNSET); gharīb = a STRANGER; ash-Sharq al-Awsaṭ = the MIDDLE EAST.", more:["Ishrāq = radiance; ash-Sharq al-Awsaṭ = the MIDDLE EAST.","maghrib — the \"SETTING / going away\" family — gharaba = to set, to go far; maghrib = the WEST / sunset (Ṣalāt al-Maghrib; al-Maghrib = MOROCCO — the land of the sunset!); gharīb = a STRANGER (one far from home).","ilāh — the one worshipped (Nās 114:3).","Because you're being told to cut off from everything — so you're reminded Who owns everything you're cutting off from.","You're not losing the world; you're attaching to its Lord.","Lā ilāha illā huwa — the shahādah in third person.","And ilāh connects to Nās 114:3 (ilāhi'n-nās).","Rabb (Lord) + Ilāh (God) in one verse — the two dimensions of tawḥīd (Nās's triad).","Hooks: al-Maghrib = MOROCCO (land of sunset); gharīb = a STRANGER; ash-Sharq al-Awsaṭ = the Middle East.","There's also a recognized recitation rabbi (genitive), linking to rabbika in v.8.","lā ilāha illā huwa — lā of total negation of the genus + exception.","East + West = EVERYWHERE — you cut off from the world (v.8) to attach to its Lord (v.9).","Rabb + Ilāh = Nās's triad."]},
  {ar:"فَاتَّخِذْهُ وَكِيلًا", tr:"Fa'ttakhidhhu wakīlā", v:"9", root:"The \"ENTRUSTING / relying on\" family — wakala = to entrust.", meaning:"\"…so take Him as your Trustee [to whom you entrust all affairs].\"", magic:"The fa is the logic: He is Lord of East and West, the only god — SO take Him as your wakīl. Wakīl = the one you hand your affairs to — your agent, your attorney.", grammar:"Ittakhidh + -hu + wakīlan — two objects (\"take Him [as] a trustee\"). The fa = causal.", why:"Wakīl — the precise word for handing over the outcomes while keeping the effort.", note:"ḤASBUNA'LLĀHU WA NIʿMA'L-WAKĪL — the same word. Wakīl = an AGENT/ATTORNEY; wakālat al-anbāʾ = a NEWS AGENCY.", more:["Wakīl = one to whom you ENTRUST your affairs, a trustee, an agent; tawakkul = RELIANCE on Allah; ḥasbuna'llāhu wa niʿma'l-WAKĪL = \"Allah is sufficient for us, and He is the best Trustee\" (3:173); modern: wakīl = an AGENT, an attorney, a DEPUTY (wakīl wizārah = deputy minister!); wakālah = an AGENCY (wakālat al-anbāʾ = a news agency!).","You do the standing (v.2), the reciting (v.4), the remembering (v.8) — and you hand the OUTCOMES to Him.","That's tawakkul: effort is yours; results are your Wakīl's.","And this is said right before v.10 — \"bear what they say.\" You can't control the deniers; you entrust them to your Wakīl.","Compare Ghāshiyah 88:22 (lasta ʿalayhim bi-muṣayṭir — \"you're not a controller\") and Jinn 72:21 (lā amliku lakum ḍarran wa lā rashadā).","The Prophet ﷺ controls nothing — so he entrusts everything.","And ittakhidh — Form VIII of akhadha = take FOR YOURSELF — the same verb as Insān 76:29 / 73:19 (ittakhadha ilā rabbihi sabīlā) and Jinn 72:3 (mā'ttakhadha ṣāḥibah).","He took no consort (Jinn 72:3); you take HIM as Trustee (Muzzammil 73:9).","EFFORT is yours (v.2–8); OUTCOMES are your Wakīl's (v.9).","Jinn 72:3: He took no consort.","Here: YOU take HIM as Trustee."]},
  {ar:"وَاصْبِرْ عَلَىٰ مَا يَقُولُونَ", tr:"Wa'ṣbir ʿalā mā yaqūlūn", v:"10", root:"The \"RESTRAINING / tying down / holding firm\" family — ṣabara = originally to confine, to hold something in check.", meaning:"\"And be patient over what they say…\"", magic:"Patience with WORDS specifically — ʿalā mā yaqūlūn — \"over what they SAY.\" Not physical persecution here — the mockery, the accusations (\"poet, madman, sorcerer\").", grammar:"Imperative; ṣabara ʿalā = \"be patient over / with [a hardship]\" — the preposition fixed.", why:"ṣabr — the restraint needed when the attack is verbal and the temptation is to answer back.", note:"Ṣabir = ALOES — BITTER but HEALING. Patience over what they SAY — hold your tongue while theirs runs.", more:["ṣabr = patience/steadfastness; ṣabir = ALOES (bitter, healing!); ʿAṣr 103:3 (tawāṣaw bi'ṣ-ṣabr), Balad 90:17.","Ṣabr = holding your tongue in check while theirs runs free.","And it immediately follows wakīl (v.9) — patience is easier when you've handed the outcome to your Trustee.","And Muddaththir 74:7 — wa li-rabbika fa'ṣbir — \"and for your Lord, be patient.\" Twin surahs, twin commands of ṣabr.","Ṣabir = ALOES — bitter, but it heals.","The thread of virtue as restraint: ḥijr (a wall, Fajr 89:5), ḥikmah (a bridle, Tīn 95:8), ṣabr (a tether, ʿAṣr 103:3).","mā yaqūlūna = \"what they say.\"","Twins: Muzzammil 73:10 iṣbir / Muddaththir 74:7 fa'ṣbir.","Easier once you've handed outcomes to your Wakīl (v.9)."]},
  {ar:"وَاهْجُرْهُمْ هَجْرًا جَمِيلًا", tr:"Wa'hjurhum hajran jamīlā", v:"10", root:"Hajr — the \"LEAVING / abandoning / departing from\" family — hajara = to leave, to forsake; HIJRAH = EMIGRATION.", meaning:"\"…and leave them with a beautiful leaving.\"", magic:"\"Leave them — BEAUTIFULLY.\" Classical definition of hajr jamīl: avoidance with NO harm, NO reproach, NO bitterness — you step away from their company and their argument, without insult, without revenge.", grammar:"Imperative + -hum + cognate accusative with adjective (hajran jamīlan) — the adjective on the cognate accusative specifies the MANNER.", why:"Hajr jamīl — because how you leave matters as much as that you leave.", note:"HIJRAH = EMIGRATION — same root. Hajr jamīl = leaving with NO harm, NO reproach.", more:["The Prophet's ﷺ leaving Makkah for Madīnah!; muhājir = an emigrant; hajr = avoidance, shunning; hājirah = the midday heat (when people \"leave off\" travelling!).","jamīl — BEAUTIFUL (jamāl = beauty; ṣabrun jamīl — Yaʿqūb's \"beautiful patience,\" 12:18).","You can leave someone ugly (with cursing and slammed doors) or beautiful (quietly, with dignity).","The Qurʾān commands the second.","The jamīl triad in the Qurʾān (often noted together): ṣabr jamīl — beautiful patience (complaint to no one but Allah, 12:18; 70:5); ṣafḥ jamīl — beautiful forgiveness (forgiving without reproach, 15:85); hajr jamīl — beautiful leaving (73:10). Three beautiful responses to hurt.","And the hajr root will become HIJRAH — the beautiful leaving of a whole city, years later.","Muddaththir 74:5 — wa'r-rujza fa'hjur — \"and idolatry — LEAVE it.\" 73:10: leave the PEOPLE, beautifully.","74:5: leave the IDOLATRY, absolutely.","People get gentleness; falsehood gets none.","The cognate accusative hajran jamīlā — leave them with a leaving that is beautiful.","The jamīl triad: ṣabr jamīl (patience), ṣafḥ jamīl (forgiveness), hajr jamīl (leaving).","73:10: leave PEOPLE beautifully.","74:5: leave IDOLATRY absolutely."]},
  {ar:"وَذَرْنِي وَالْمُكَذِّبِينَ", tr:"Wa dharnī wa'l-mukadhdhibīn", v:"11", root:"Dhar — the \"LEAVING / letting be\" family — wadhara — a DEFECTIVE verb: its past tense is not used (only the present yadharu and the imperative dhar survive; taraka fills in the past).", meaning:"\"And leave Me with the deniers…\" (i.e. \"leave them to Me — I'll deal with them\")", magic:"One of the most powerful threats in the Qurʾān — and it's said GENTLY to the Prophet ﷺ.", grammar:"Dhar = imperative of the defective verb wadhara (no past in use).", why:"Dharnī — a threat that requires no description of punishment — the speaker is enough.", note:"DHARNĪ = \"LEAVE THEM TO ME.\" Comfort for the Prophet ﷺ, terror for them.", more:["mukadhdhibīn — the deniers (Form II participle of kadhaba — those who call the truth a lie).","Dharnī = \"LEAVE ME [with them].\" It's what a strong protector says to someone being bullied: \"Step aside.","Leave him to me.\" To the Prophet ﷺ: comfort.","And it pairs with v.10 — \"leave them beautifully (hajr jamīl) — and leave them TO ME.\" You don't take revenge; you hand them over.","(That's the wakīl of v.9 in action.) THE CROSS-SURAH SYMMETRY: Muddaththir 74:11 — dharnī wa man khalaqtu waḥīdā — \"Leave Me with the one I created alone.\" Muzzammil 73:11 and Muddaththir 74:11 — BOTH VERSE 11, BOTH \"dharnī\" — \"Leave Me with…\" Twin surahs, twin threats, same verse number.","And the defective verb you own: Qiyāmah 75:21 — wa tadharūna'l-ākhirah (\"you forsake the Hereafter\") and Insān 76:27 (wa yadharūna warāʾahum).","The same quirky verb with no past tense.","wa'l-mukadhdhibīn — the wāw here is wāw al-maʿiyyah (\"with\"), so the noun is accusative (maf'ūl maʿah): \"leave Me together WITH the deniers.\"","Muzzammil 73:11 & Muddaththir 74:11 — BOTH VERSE 11, BOTH \"dharnī\".","Defective verb wadhara (no past) — Qiyāmah 75:21, Insān 76:27. Wāw al-maʿiyyah = \"WITH.\""]},
  {ar:"أُولِي النَّعْمَةِ", tr:"Uli'n-naʿmah", v:"11", root:"Ulī — \"possessors of / people of\" (the plural of dhū; ulū nom. / ulī acc.-gen.).", meaning:"\"…the possessors of luxury [the comfortable elite].\"", magic:"ONE VOWEL CHANGES THE MEANING — the best trap in this surah. NIʿMAH (kasrah) = a BLESSING — something given, to be thanked for.", grammar:"Ulī — written with an extra wāw (أُولِي) that is not pronounced.", why:"Naʿmah (luxury) rather than ghinā (wealth) — names the softness of their lives, which made hardship-free denial easy.", note:"NIʿMAH (kasrah) = a BLESSING. NAʿMAH (fatḥah) = LUXURY. One vowel: gratitude vs. self-indulgence.", more:["naʿmah — the \"SOFTNESS / ease / comfort\" family — naʿmah (with FATḤAH) = LUXURY, soft easy living; niʿmah (with KASRAH) = a BLESSING / favour; naʿīm = bliss (Insān 76:20); nāʿim = soft (Ghāshiyah 88:8); naʿam = yes!","(wa ammā bi-niʿmati rabbika fa-ḥaddith — Ḍuḥā 93:11.) NAʿMAH (fatḥah) = LUXURY, soft comfortable living — enjoyed, often without thanks.","(wa naʿmatin kānū fīhā fākihīn — \"and luxury they used to delight in,\" 44:27, of Pharaoh's people.) The deniers are uli'n-NAʿMAH — not \"people of blessing\" but \"people of LUXURY.\" They had the comfort without recognizing the Giver.","That's the difference between niʿmah and naʿmah: gratitude.","The wealthy chiefs of Quraysh — the same type as ʿAbasa 80:5 (istaghnā), Muddaththir 74:11–14 (al-Walīd, given wealth and sons), and Fajr 89:15 (fa-akramahu wa naʿʿamahu — \"honoured him and pampered him\" — same root!).","Luxury breeds istighnāʾ, and istighnāʾ breeds denial.","It's in apposition to al-mukadhdhibīn (accusative/genitive form ulī).","Fajr 89:15: naʿʿamahu (pampered him).","Luxury → self-sufficiency → denial."]},
  {ar:"وَمَهِّلْهُمْ قَلِيلًا", tr:"Wa mahhilhum qalīlā", v:"11", root:"The \"GENTLENESS / slowness / giving time\" family — mahala = to act slowly, gently.", meaning:"\"…and give them respite for a little while.\"", magic:"You own this from the END of Ṭāriq.", grammar:"Form II imperative (mahhil). qalīlan = adverbial (\"a little [time]\").", why:"Mahhala — makes the delay a deliberate gift of time, not neglect.", note:"MAHLAN! = \"SLOW DOWN!\"; MUHLAH = a DEADLINE. A grace period is a deadline with a smile.", more:["Mahhala (Form II) / amhala (Form IV) = to give RESPITE, to grant a delay; mahlan!","/ easy!\" (everyday Arabic!); muhlah = a DEADLINE / grace period (modern: muhlah zamaniyyah = a time limit!).","Ṭāriq 86:17 — fa-mahhili'l-kāfirīna amhilhum ruwaydā — \"so give respite to the disbelievers; give them respite a little.\" Almost identical command, two surahs.","And look at the gentle threat inside the mercy: qalīlā — \"a LITTLE while.\" The respite is real, but SHORT.","Historically: Badr came within about 15 years — and many of these \"people of luxury\" died there.","Muhlah = a DEADLINE — a grace period is a deadline with a smile.","Compare Ṭāriq 86:17's double — mahhil… amhil — Form II then Form IV.","Ṭāriq 86:17: fa-mahhili'l-kāfirīna amhilhum ruwaydā — nearly IDENTICAL."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّ لَدَيْنَا أَنكَالًا", tr:"Inna ladaynā ankālā", v:"12", root:"The \"SHACKLING / restraining\" family — nikl = a heavy SHACKLE / fetter (plural ankāl).", meaning:"\"Indeed, with Us are heavy shackles…\"", magic:"Nāziʿāt 79:25 — fa-akhadhahu'llāhu nakāla'l-ākhirati wa'l-ūlā — \"Allah seized him with the EXEMPLARY punishment of the Hereafter and the first.\" There I explained nakāl comes from nikl, a SHACKLE — a punishment that restrains others by example.", grammar:"Inna ladaynā — fronted predicate (\"with Us [are]…\"). ankālan = subject of inna (delayed), broken plural.", why:"Ankāl (heavy fetters) — the exact opposite of naʿmah's softness.", note:"Nāziʿāt 79:25: NAKĀL (exemplary punishment). Muzzammil 73:12: ANKĀL (the SHACKLES themselves).", more:["Nakāl = an EXEMPLARY punishment (one that restrains others — Nāziʿāt 79:25!); nakala ʿan = to shrink back / renege (to be held back!).","THE ROOT YOU OWN FROM PHARAOH'S PUNISHMENT.","Here is the shackle itself: ankāl.","Nāziʿāt gave you the word; Muzzammil gives you the object.","And it comes right after \"people of luxury\" (v.11) — soft living → heavy shackles.","Compare Insān 76:4 (salāsila wa aghlālā — chains and collars) and Fajr 89:26 (wa lā yūthiqu wathāqahu aḥad).","And ladaynā — \"WITH US\" — ready, present, kept close.","Same root — the word, then the object.","Soft LUXURY (v.11) → heavy SHACKLES (v.12).","Ladaynā = \"kept WITH Us,\" ready."]},
  {ar:"وَجَحِيمًا", tr:"Wa jaḥīmā", v:"12", root:"The \"FIERCELY BLAZING fire\" family — jaḥama = to burn fiercely; jaḥīm = the Blaze (a name of Hell).", meaning:"\"…and a Blazing Fire.\"", magic:"The jaḥīm thread across SIX surahs: Nāziʿāt 79:36, 39 (displayed / the refuge), Takwīr 81:12 (kindled), Infiṭār 82:14 (the wicked in it), Muṭaffifīn 83:16 (they will burn in it), Takāthur 102:6 (you WILL see it), Muzzammil 73:12 (it is with Us, ready).", grammar:"Joined to ankālan by wa; accusative (subject of inna), indefinite — indefinite = vast, beyond description.", why:"Jaḥīm — the fierce-blaze name, set against the comfortable cool of their luxury.", note:"Jaḥīm — SIX surahs: 79:36, 81:12, 82:14, 83:16, 102:6, 73:12. The four-item list: SHACKLES → BLAZE → CHOKING food → PAIN.", more:["Note the list in v.12–13: SHACKLES, BLAZE, CHOKING FOOD, PAINFUL PUNISHMENT — four items, each worse — restraint, heat, food that won't go down, and pain."]},
  {ar:"وَطَعَامًا ذَا غُصَّةٍ", tr:"Wa ṭaʿāman dhā ghuṣṣah", v:"13", root:"ṭaʿām — food (maṭʿam = a restaurant; Quraysh 106:4 aṭʿamahum). dhā — possessor of (one of the five special nouns).", meaning:"\"…and food that chokes…\"", magic:"Food that STICKS in the throat — it won't go down, won't come up. The horror isn't that it tastes bad; it's that you can't swallow it.", grammar:"Dhā — accusative of the five-noun dhū/dhā/dhī (Balad 90:15, yatīman dhā maqrabah).", why:"Ghuṣṣah — a physical sensation everyone has felt, magnified into permanence.", note:"GHUṢṢAH = a LUMP IN THE THROAT — the one you get from GRIEF. Food that won't go down.", more:["ghuṣṣah — the \"CHOKING / a lump stuck in the throat\" family — ghaṣṣa = to choke on food; ghuṣṣah = a LUMP IN THE THROAT — and in everyday Arabic, the lump in your throat from GRIEF (fī ḥalqī ghuṣṣah = \"I have a lump in my throat\")!","Classical reports name it: the zaqqūm tree, the ḍarīʿ thorn (Ghāshiyah 88:6 — the thorn a starving camel refuses), the ghislīn.","Ghāshiyah: food that doesn't NOURISH.","Muzzammil: food that won't even GO DOWN.","And the contrast with \"people of LUXURY\" (v.11): they ate the softest, finest food — now food that chokes.","And Quraysh 106:4 — aṭʿamahum min jūʿ (He FED them) — the same people, fed in dunya, choking in Hell.","The everyday hook is perfect: ghuṣṣah = the lump in your throat when you're about to cry.","Food that feels like grief.","ghuṣṣatin = genitive (iḍāfah).","Ghāshiyah 88:6: ḍarīʿ — food that doesn't NOURISH.","Here: food that won't even SWALLOW.","LUXURY (v.11) → CHOKING (v.13).","Dhā = five-noun accusative."]},
  {ar:"يَوْمَ تَرْجُفُ الْأَرْضُ وَالْجِبَالُ", tr:"Yawma tarjufu'l-arḍu wa'l-jibāl", v:"14", root:"The \"VIOLENT TREMBLING / quaking\" family — rajafa = to tremble violently; ar-rājifah = the Quaker (Nāziʿāt 79:6!).", meaning:"\"On the Day the earth and the mountains will quake…\"", magic:"You own this root from Nāziʿāt 79:6 — yawma tarjufu'r-rājifah — \"the Day the Quaker quakes.\" Same verb, same opening \"yawma tarjufu\".", grammar:"Yawma = adverb of time (accusative), linked to v.12 (\"with Us are shackles… on the Day…\").", why:"Rajf — the violent tremor that comes first, before the mountains dissolve.", note:"Yawma TARJUFU — IDENTICAL opening to Nāziʿāt 79:6. Quake-words: rajf (73:14, 79:6), zalzalah (99:1), dakk (89:21). Irjāf = alarming RUMOURS.", more:["Irjāf = spreading alarming rumours (that make hearts tremble).","And the mountains are named too — even the earth's \"pegs\" (Nabaʾ 78:7) and \"anchors\" (Nāziʿāt 79:32) tremble.","And compare Zalzalah 99:1 (zulzilati'l-arḍu zilzālahā).","Three quake-words: rajf (trembling), zalzalah (convulsive shaking), dakk (pounding flat, Fajr 89:21)."]},
  {ar:"كَثِيبًا مَّهِيلًا", tr:"Kathīban mahīlā", v:"14", root:"Kathīb — a SAND DUNE, a heap of sand (kuthbān = dunes).", meaning:"\"…and the mountains become a heap of sliding sand.\"", magic:"Mountains → a sand dune that won't hold its shape. Kathīb = a dune — still a heap. Mahīl = pouring, sliding — the dune itself won't stay put; step on it, it flows away under you.", grammar:"Kānat (past for certain future) + kathīban (khabar) + mahīlan (adjective).", why:"Mahīl — chosen for its instability: sand that can't be stood on.", note:"KATHĪB MAHĪL = a DUNE that POURS away under your foot. The firmest thing → the least firm.", more:["mahīl — the \"POURING / sliding loose\" family — hāla = to pour out (sand, earth) so that it slides and flows; mahīl = loose, flowing, sliding sand — the kind that pours through your fingers and won't hold a shape.","The firmest thing → the least firm thing.","THE MOUNTAIN-FATES THREAD — now SIX images: - Nabaʾ 78:20 — sarābā — a MIRAGE - Takwīr 81:3 — suyyirat — set MOVING - Fajr 89:21 — dakkan dakkā — POUNDED flat - Qāriʿah 101:5 — ka'l-ʿihni'l-manfūsh — CARDED WOOL - Muzzammil 73:14 — kathīban mahīlā — SLIDING SAND - (and the opposite: Ghāshiyah 88:19 — SET UP firmly, Nabaʾ 78:7 — PEGS) From pegs to sliding sand.","Every desert Arab knew how a dune collapses under a foot — that's what the mountains become.","mahīl = mafʿūl-sense on the faʿīl pattern (\"poured\").","Mountain-fates: MIRAGE (78:20), MOVING (81:3), POUNDED (89:21), CARDED WOOL (101:5), SLIDING SAND (73:14). vs.","PEGS (78:7) and SET UP (88:19)."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّا أَرْسَلْنَا إِلَيْكُمْ رَسُولًا شَاهِدًا عَلَيْكُمْ", tr:"Innā arsalnā ilaykum rasūlan shāhidan ʿalaykum", v:"15", root:"Arsalnā — the \"SENDING\" family (rasūl = messenger; risālah = a message/letter; Fīl 105:3 arsala ʿalayhim ṭayran).", meaning:"\"Indeed, We have sent to you a Messenger as a witness over you…\"", magic:"The Messenger is a WITNESS — and a witness testifies. He doesn't just deliver (balāgh, Jinn 72:23); he watches how you respond — and will testify about it.", grammar:"Innā = \"indeed We.\" shāhidan = adjective of rasūlan. ʿalaykum = \"over / against you.\"", why:"Shāhid — makes the Messenger's presence a testimony to be reckoned with later.", note:"The Messenger = a WITNESS (shāhid) — he'll TESTIFY.", more:["shāhid — the \"WITNESSING / being present\" family — shahida = to witness; shahādah = testimony; Burūj 85:3 (shāhidin wa mashhūd), ʿĀdiyāt 100:7 (la-shahīd).","And the address shifts: ilaykum — \"to YOU [people of Makkah].\" The surah, which spoke to the Prophet ﷺ (v.1–14), now turns and speaks to the deniers directly.","The Prophet's ﷺ titles across your surahs: mundhir (warner, Nāziʿāt 79:45), mudhakkir (reminder, Ghāshiyah 88:21), balāgh (deliverer, Jinn 72:23), shāhid (witness, Muzzammil 73:15). Warn, remind, deliver, testify.","Titles: mundhir (79:45), mudhakkir (88:21), balāgh (72:23), shāhid (73:15). The surah turns from addressing HIM to addressing THEM."]},
  {ar:"كَمَا أَرْسَلْنَا إِلَىٰ فِرْعَوْنَ رَسُولًا · فَعَصَىٰ فِرْعَوْنُ الرَّسُولَ", tr:"Kamā arsalnā ilā firʿawna rasūlā · fa-ʿaṣā firʿawnu'r-rasūl", v:"15–16", root:"Firʿawn — Pharaoh (a title; a diptote — firʿawna in the genitive).", meaning:"\"…just as We sent to Pharaoh a messenger. But Pharaoh disobeyed THE messenger…\"", magic:"A GRAMMAR GEM — indefinite, then definite. v.15: arsalnā ilā firʿawna rasūlan — \"A messenger\" (indefinite — first mention).", grammar:"Rasūlan (indefinite, v.15) → ar-rasūl (definite, v.16) = al-ʿahd adh-dhikrī (referring back).", why:"The same word, first indefinite then definite — makes the second mention land as \"the very one I sent.\"", note:"RASŪLAN (A messenger) → AR-RASŪL (THE messenger) = referring BACK (al-ʿahd adh-dhikrī). Like English: \"A man came in.", more:["ʿaṣā — the \"DISOBEYING\" family (and ʿaṣā = a STAFF — Mūsā's!).","v.16: fa-ʿaṣā firʿawnu ar-rasūl — \"THE messenger\" (definite — referring back).","This is al-ʿahd adh-dhikrī — the definite article pointing back to something just mentioned (\"that messenger I just told you about\").","English does it too: \"A man came in.","The man sat down.\" Recognize this and Arabic narrative reads much more smoothly.","And the Pharaoh thread — you own it fully: Nāziʿāt 79:17–25 (innahu ṭaghā… fa-kadhdhaba wa ʿaṣā), Fajr 89:10 (dhi'l-awtād), Burūj 85:18. Nāziʿāt 79:21 — fa-kadhdhaba wa ʿaṣā — SAME VERB for Pharaoh.","And Mūsā carried an ʿaṣā (a staff) — Pharaoh did ʿaṣā (disobeyed).","The logic of the comparison (kamā): \"You are being sent a messenger JUST AS Pharaoh was — and you know what happened to Pharaoh.\"","THE man sat down.\" ʿAṣā: Pharaoh DISOBEYED (79:21, 73:16); Mūsā carried an ʿAṢĀ (staff).","\"Just AS Pharaoh\" — and you know how that ended."]},
  {ar:"فَأَخَذْنَاهُ أَخْذًا وَبِيلًا", tr:"Fa-akhadhnāhu akhdhan wabīlā", v:"16", root:"Akhadha — to SEIZE, to take (Nāziʿāt 79:25 fa-akhadhahu'llāh).", meaning:"\"…so We seized him with a crushing seizure.\"", magic:"Wabīl = HEAVY like a TORRENTIAL DOWNPOUR — the image is rain so heavy it flattens everything.", grammar:"Cognate accusative + adjective (akhdhan wabīlan) — the adjective gives the manner. Past tense akhadhnā + -hu.", why:"Wabīl (downpour-heavy) — matches the weight of the punishment to the weight of the arrogance — and to the drowning.", note:"WĀBIL = a HEAVY DOWNPOUR. Wabīl = heavy as a deluge — for a man who DROWNED.", more:["wabīl — the \"HEAVY / crushing / unwholesome\" family — w-b-l: wābil = a HEAVY DOWNPOUR (torrential rain — 2:264–265!); wabīl = heavy, severe, oppressive, unwholesome (like food that sits badly, or air that's foul).","Pharaoh was seized by something that came down on him like a deluge — and fittingly, he was DROWNED.","A word rooted in heavy rain for a man destroyed by water.","The cognate accusative akhdhan wabīlā — \"seized him with a seizing that was crushing.\" Nāziʿāt 79:25 — fa-akhadhahu'llāhu nakāla'l-ākhirati wa'l-ūlā — same verb, same victim.","And Muzzammil 73:12's ankāl (shackles) is the root of Nāziʿāt's nakāl.","Two surahs telling Pharaoh's end with overlapping words.","TRAP: wabīl (w-b-l) is NOT wabāʾ (w-b-ʾ, an epidemic/plague).","They look and sound related — different roots.","Nāziʿāt 79:25: fa-akhadhahu'llāh — same verb, same Pharaoh.","Wabīl (w-b-l) ≠ wabāʾ (w-b-ʾ, EPIDEMIC)."]},
  {ar:"فَكَيْفَ تَتَّقُونَ إِن كَفَرْتُمْ يَوْمًا", tr:"Fa-kayfa tattaqūna in kafartum yawmā", v:"17", root:"Tattaqūn — the \"SHIELDING / guarding\" family — ittaqā = to protect oneself (taqwā — NOT \"fear,\" but ARMOUR; wiqāyah = protection).", meaning:"\"So how will you shield yourselves, if you disbelieve, from a Day…\"", magic:"Tattaqūn = \"how will you SHIELD yourselves?\" — the root of taqwā used in its most literal sense: putting a SHIELD between you and harm.", grammar:"Kayfa = rhetorical (\"HOW could you…?\" = you can't).", why:"Ittaqā — the clearest demonstration that taqwā means self-protection.", note:"\"HOW will you SHIELD yourselves FROM a Day?\" — taqwā in its LITERAL sense. Yawman is the OBJECT — the Day is what you shield FROM.", more:["kafartum — you disbelieve (kafara = to bury a seed / cover over).","The question: if you reject faith — what's your armour against THAT Day?","It proves the point you've learned: taqwā = armour, not trembling — here the verb literally means \"protect yourselves FROM a Day\" (yawman is its object!).","The Day is the threat; taqwā is the shield; disbelief is throwing the shield away.","You own the root from Nabaʾ 78:31 (al-muttaqīn), Shams 91:8 (taqwāhā), Layl 92:5, 17 (ittaqā, al-atqā), ʿAlaq 96:12 (bi't-taqwā).","yawman = object of tattaqūna — \"shield yourselves FROM a Day\" (not \"on a day\").","in kafartum = conditional.","Disbelief = throwing the shield away."]},
  {ar:"يَجْعَلُ الْوِلْدَانَ شِيبًا", tr:"Yajʿalu'l-wildāna shībā", v:"17", root:"Wildān — plural of walīd = young boys, children (Insān 76:19, wildānun mukhalladūn!).", meaning:"\"…a Day that will turn children grey-haired.\"", magic:"THE MOST VIVID IMAGE OF TERROR IN THE SURAH — and it inverts a verse you own. A Day so terrifying that CHILDREN'S hair turns WHITE. Not adults — children, who don't grey.", grammar:"Yajʿalu takes two objects (\"makes the children [into] grey-haired ones\"). shīban = plural of ashyab.", why:"Shīb — makes abstract terror visible on the most innocent heads.", note:"A Day that turns CHILDREN'S hair WHITE. Insān 76:19: wildān who NEVER age (Paradise). Muzzammil 73:17: wildān who age INSTANTLY.", more:["shīb — the \"GREY / white HAIR of old age\" family — shāba = to go grey; shayb = grey hair; ashyab (pl.","shīb) = grey-haired; shaybah = old age.","Fear so intense it skips a lifetime of ageing in a moment.","(The Prophet ﷺ is reported to have said: \"Hūd and its sisters have made me grey\" — surahs about the Day aged him.) NOW COMPARE INSĀN 76:19 — wa yaṭūfu ʿalayhim wildānun mukhalladūn — \"youths made ETERNAL\" (forever young) serving in Paradise.","Same word, wildān: Insān: children who NEVER age.","Muzzammil: children who age INSTANTLY.","The two destinations, shown through the same children: eternal youth in the Garden, instant greyness on the Day.","The whole clause describes yawman (a Day that makes…).","\"Hūd and its sisters made me grey.\" Shayb = GREY HAIR."]},
  {ar:"السَّمَاءُ مُنفَطِرٌ بِهِ", tr:"As-samāʾu munfaṭirun bih", v:"18", root:"The \"SPLITTING / cleaving open\" family — faṭara = to split; infaṭara = to be split apart; fiṭrah = original nature.", meaning:"\"The sky will be split apart by it [that Day].\"", magic:"You own this word from INFIṬĀR 82:1 — idha's-samāʾu'nfaṭarat — \"when the sky is split.\" Same root, same subject (the sky), same Form VII.", grammar:"Munfaṭirun is MASCULINE — but as-samāʾ is normally FEMININE! Compare Infiṭār 82:1: infaṭarat (feminine verb).", why:"Infiṭār — links this surah's terror to the splitting-sky imagery of Infiṭār.", note:"Infiṭār 82:1: infaṭarat (the sky splits). Muzzammil 73:18: munfaṭir (the sky split). Same root.", more:["Fāṭir = the Originator; ʿĪd al-Fiṭr (breaking the fast).","And recall the three \"splitting\" roots: f-j-r (fajr, dawn bursting — Fajr 89:1), f-ṭ-r (infaṭarat — Infiṭār 82:1, Muzzammil 73:18), f-l-q (falaq, daybreak — Falaq 113:1). Creation began with a splitting (Fāṭir); it ends with one.","And bihi = \"by it\" — by that Day's terror (or: on that Day).","The Day that greys children (v.17) also splits the sky (v.18).","Classical explanations: (a) munfaṭir = \"a thing of splitting\" (dhāt infiṭār), describing a quality, not agreeing in gender; (b) samāʾ can be treated as masculine in the sense of \"the canopy / the ceiling\" (saqf).","A recognized feature — not an error.","Munfaṭir is MASCULINE for a feminine samāʾ — \"a thing of splitting\" / sky as \"ceiling.\" Three splitting roots: f-j-r, f-ṭ-r, f-l-q."]},
  {ar:"كَانَ وَعْدُهُ مَفْعُولًا", tr:"Kāna waʿduhu mafʿūlā", v:"18", root:"Waʿd — the \"PROMISE\" family — waʿada = to promise; waʿd = a promise (good); waʿīd = a THREAT (a promise of harm).", meaning:"\"His promise is ever fulfilled [done].\"", magic:"The promise is described as ALREADY DONE (mafʿūl). Not \"will be fulfilled\" — mafʿūl — \"a done thing.\" His promise is as good as accomplished the moment it's made.", grammar:"Kāna with Allah's promises = permanence (\"ever has been\"). mafʿūlan = khabar of kāna.", why:"Mafʿūl — describes a future event in the language of a completed one: total certainty.", note:"MAFʿŪL = DONE — His promise is \"a done thing.\" Mafʿūl bihi = the DIRECT OBJECT (grammar!).", more:["Mawʿid = an APPOINTMENT (modern: mawʿid = a doctor's appointment!).","mafʿūl — DONE, accomplished, carried out (passive participle of faʿala; mafʿūl = the grammatical OBJECT — \"the thing acted on\"!).","And kāna — \"has always been\" — permanence.","Grammar hook: mafʿūl bihi = the DIRECT OBJECT in grammar — \"that which is acted upon.\" You've used mafʿūl in every grammar note; here it means \"carried out.\" And mawʿid = an APPOINTMENT — compare Nabaʾ 78:17 — inna yawma'l-faṣli kāna mīqātā (an appointed time).","The Day has an appointment, and the promise is already done.","Mawʿid = an APPOINTMENT; waʿīd = a THREAT.","Nabaʾ 78:17: the Day is a mīqāt (appointment)."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّ هَٰذِهِ تَذْكِرَةٌ ۖ فَمَن شَاءَ اتَّخَذَ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِ سَبِيلًا", tr:"Inna hādhihi tadhkiratun fa-man shāʾa'ttakhadha ilā rabbihi sabīlā", v:"19", root:"Tadhkirah — a REMINDER (the dhikr thread). shāʾa — to will (mashīʾah; inshāʾallāh!).", meaning:"\"Indeed, this is a reminder; so whoever wills may take a way to his Lord.\"", magic:"THIS VERSE IS WORD-FOR-WORD IDENTICAL TO INSĀN 76:29. Insān 76:29 — inna hādhihi tadhkiratun · fa-man shāʾa'ttakhadha ilā rabbihi sabīlā Muzzammil 73:19.", grammar:"Hādhihi (feminine \"this\") — referring to the surah / these verses (āyāt, feminine).", why:"Repeating Insān's exact words — so the invitation sounds identical wherever it appears.", note:"WORD-FOR-WORD = INSĀN 76:29. Fa-man shāʾa — SIX surahs: 76:29, 78:39, 80:12, 81:28, 73:19, 74:37/55. After all the terror — an OPEN DOOR.", more:["sabīl — a WAY, a road (ibn as-sabīl = a traveller, \"son of the road\"; fī sabīli'llāh).","Inna hādhihi tadhkiratun · fa-man shāʾa'ttakhadha ilā rabbihi sabīlā EXACT.","Free vocabulary — and a sign the Qurʾān repeats its key invitation in identical words.","And the \"fa-man shāʾa\" open door — now in SIX surahs: - Insān 76:29 — fa-man shāʾa'ttakhadha ilā rabbihi sabīlā - Nabaʾ 78:39 — fa-man shāʾa'ttakhadha ilā rabbihi maʾābā - ʿAbasa 80:12 — fa-man shāʾa dhakarah - Takwīr 81:28 — li-man shāʾa minkum an yastaqīm - Muzzammil 73:19 — fa-man shāʾa'ttakhadha ilā rabbihi sabīlā - Muddaththir 74:37, 55 — li-man shāʾa minkum an yataqaddama… / fa-man shāʾa dhakarah After all the terror of v.12–18 — shackles, blaze, choking, greyed children, a split sky — the surah ends its first part with an OPEN DOOR.","Fa-man shāʾa — whoever wants to, can.","ittakhadha = Form VIII (\"take for oneself\").","Ibn as-sabīl = a TRAVELLER."]},
  {ar:"أَدْنَىٰ مِن ثُلُثَيِ اللَّيْلِ وَنِصْفَهُ وَثُلُثَهُ", tr:"Adnā min thuluthayi'l-layli wa niṣfahu wa thuluthah", v:"20", root:"Adnā — the \"NEARNESS / lowness\" family — danā = to come near; adnā = NEARER / LESS / LOWER.", meaning:"\"Indeed, your Lord knows that you stand [in prayer] nearly two-thirds of the night, and half of it, and a third of it…\"", magic:"Allah RECORDS their effort precisely — before easing it. Two-thirds, half, a third — He names exactly how long they stood, night by night.", grammar:"Thuluthayi — DUAL (\"two thirds\"), the dual nūn is dropped in iḍāfah (thuluthayn → thuluthay).", why:"Exact fractions — so the believers know their effort was counted, down to the hour.", note:"TWO-THIRDS, HALF, A THIRD — He RECORDED their effort precisely. Mercy begins by being SEEN.", more:["Dunyā = \"the NEARER [life]\" (Aʿlā 87:16!). thuluth — a THIRD (thuluthayn = two thirds; thalāthah = three).","The relief begins with RECOGNITION: \"I know what you've been doing.\" Mercy starts by being seen.","Adnā = \"LESS than / NEARLY\" — the same root as dunyā (the \"nearer/lower\" life — Aʿlā 87:16). One root: nearness and lowness.","And look back at v.2–3: niṣfahu awi'nquṣ… aw zid — \"half, or less, or more.\" v.20 confirms they did exactly that: two-thirds (more), half, a third (less).","The command was obeyed in full.","niṣfahu wa thuluthahu — accusative (joined to adnā: \"you stand nearly two-thirds, and half, and a third\") — there's also an authentic recitation in the genitive (niṣfihi wa thuluthihi), joined to thuluthay (\"less than two-thirds, and [less than] half, and [less than] a third\").","Adnā (less/nearer) — same root as DUNYĀ (the nearer life, 87:16). Thuluthay = DUAL, nūn dropped.","v.3's options were all obeyed."]},
  {ar:"وَطَائِفَةٌ مِّنَ الَّذِينَ مَعَكَ", tr:"Wa ṭāʾifatun mina'lladhīna maʿak", v:"20", root:"The \"GOING AROUND / circling\" family — ṭāfa = to go around, to circle; ṭawāf = CIRCUMAMBULATION of the Kaʿbah.", meaning:"\"…and so does a group of those with you.\"", magic:"The companions stood the night too — not alone; a ṭāʾifah, a circle of people around the Prophet ﷺ.", grammar:"ṭāʾifatun — nominative, joined to the subject of taqūmu (\"you stand… and a group\"). maʿaka = \"with you.\"", why:"ṭāʾifah — a circle of companions, echoing the circling of ṭawāf.", note:"ṬAWĀF (circling the Kaʿbah) → ṬĀʾIFAH (a group that \"goes around\" together) → AṬ-ṬĀʾIF (the walled city).", more:["ṭāʾifah = a GROUP, a party (people who \"go around\" together — a circle of people); aṭ-Ṭāʾif = the CITY near Makkah (named — reportedly — for the wall that went around it!); ṭāʾifiyyah = sectarianism (modern); Ghāshiyah?","no — Insān 76:15, 19: yuṭāfu / yaṭūfu (served by those who circle around them!).","Ṭāʾifah comes from ṭawāf — a group is \"those who move around together.\" And you own the root from Insān 76:15, 19 — yuṭāfu ʿalayhim bi-āniyatin / wa yaṭūfu ʿalayhim wildānun — servants CIRCLING around the people of Paradise.","Those who circled the Prophet ﷺ in night prayer → circled by servants in Paradise.","The everyday hooks: ṭawāf (you know it from Hajj/ʿUmrah) and aṭ-Ṭāʾif (the city).","Insān 76:15, 19: servants yaṭūfu (CIRCLE) around the people of Paradise.","They circled him at night; they'll be circled in Paradise."]},
  {ar:"وَاللَّهُ يُقَدِّرُ اللَّيْلَ وَالنَّهَارَ", tr:"Wa'llāhu yuqaddiru'l-layla wa'n-nahār", v:"20", root:"The \"MEASURE / POWER / WORTH\" family — qaddara = to measure out precisely; qadr (Laylat al-Qadr).", meaning:"\"And Allah measures out the night and the day.\"", magic:"The relief's LOGIC in four words: You can't measure the night precisely — but ALLAH does (yuqaddiru). Only the One who measures the hours can know them exactly.", grammar:"Allāhu fronted (subject before verb) = emphasis/restriction. Form II present (yuqaddiru) = ongoing, precise.", why:"Qaddara — contrasts divine precision with human estimation, justifying the relief.", note:"YOU can't measure the night exactly — ALLAH does (yuqaddiru). Humans ESTIMATE; Allah MEASURES.", more:["Your most-tracked root after sawwā (75:4, 76:16, 80:19, 87:3, 89:16, 90:5, 97:1).","And that's the setup for the very next phrase: \"He knows you cannot keep count.\" Humans estimate; Allah measures.","Yuqaddiru — Form II, intensive: He measures with full precision, lengthening nights in winter, shortening them in summer, minute by minute.","And recall Qadr 97:1 — laylat al-QADR — the night of measuring.","And note the word order: wa'llāhu yuqaddiru — \"Allah\" FRONTED — \"It is ALLAH [and not you] who measures.\"","Qadr root — SEVENTH+ surah (75:4 … 97:1, 73:20). Allāhu FRONTED = \"it is ALLAH who measures.\""]},
  {ar:"عَلِمَ أَن لَّن تُحْصُوهُ", tr:"ʿalima an lan tuḥṣūh", v:"20", root:"The \"COUNTING with PEBBLES\" family — ḥaṣā = pebbles; aḥṣā = to count precisely, item by item; iḥṣāʾ = STATISTICS; ḥaṣāh = a kidney stone.", meaning:"\"He knows that you will not be able to keep exact count of it…\"", magic:"THE PEBBLE ROOT — and this time, it's about what HUMANS can't do. Nabaʾ 78:29 — wa kulla shayʾin aḥṣaynāhu kitābā — ALLAH counts every thing.", grammar:"Lan + subjunctive — tuḥṣūhu — the plural nūn dropped (subjunctive). Form IV, weak-final.", why:"Aḥṣā — the same verb used for divine counting, now denied to humans, making the contrast explicit.", note:"Iḥṣāʾ = STATISTICS (pebble-counting). Nabaʾ 78:29 & Jinn 72:28: ALLAH counts EVERYTHING.", more:["Jinn 72:28 — wa aḥṣā kulla shayʾin ʿadadā — ALLAH counts every thing by number.","Muzzammil 73:20 — ʿalima an lan tuḥṣūhu — YOU cannot count it.","Allah does iḥṣāʾ of everything (78:29, 72:28); humans can't even do iḥṣāʾ of one night (73:20). The pebble-count He keeps of your deeds — you couldn't keep of your own prayer hours.","And because He KNOWS that limit — He eases.","And lan = \"will NEVER be able to\" — a permanent human limit, acknowledged by its Creator.","Muzzammil 73:20: YOU can't even count ONE NIGHT.","He knows the limit — so He eases."]},
  {ar:"فَتَابَ عَلَيْكُمْ", tr:"Fa-tāba ʿalaykum", v:"20", root:"The \"TURNING BACK / returning\" family — tāba = to turn back; tawbah = repentance (the servant turning back to Allah).", meaning:"\"…so He has turned to you [in mercy, lightening the obligation].\"", magic:"A crucial nuance: here tāba ʿalaykum is NOT about SIN. The companions did nothing wrong — they stood the night until their feet swelled.", grammar:"Tāba ʿalā = (of Allah) \"relented toward.\" Compare tāba ilā = (of the servant) \"repented to.\" The preposition tells you who's turning.", why:"Tāba ʿalaykum — frames the easing as Allah turning toward them — personal, merciful.", note:"Tāba ʿALĀ (Allah) = turned toward you in MERCY. Tāba ILĀ (servant) = repented TO Him. The preposition shows who's turning.", more:["Tāba ʿalā (said of Allah) = He TURNED BACK toward someone in MERCY / relented / lightened; at-Tawwāb = the Ever-Relenting (Naṣr 110:3!).","Tāba ʿalā here = He TURNED TOWARD you with EASE — He LIGHTENED the burden.","Classical scholars: tawbah from Allah can mean forgiveness of sin OR the lifting of a hardship.","And you own tawwāb from Naṣr 110:3 — innahu kāna tawwābā — the faʿʿāl intensive: He TURNS BACK again and again, without limit.","Naṣr: He turns back to forgive.","Muzzammil: He turns back to EASE.","And the fa (\"SO He turned\") — because He knew you couldn't count it (ʿalima an lan tuḥṣūhu).","Here it's NOT about sin — it's LIFTING A HARDSHIP.","Naṣr 110:3: tawwāb forgives.","Muzzammil 73:20: He turns to EASE."]},
  {ar:"فَاقْرَءُوا مَا تَيَسَّرَ مِنَ الْقُرْآنِ", tr:"Fa'qraʾū mā tayassara mina'l-qurʾān", v:"20", root:"Iqraʾū — RECITE / read (iqraʾ — ʿAlaq 96:1, the first word revealed!; Qurʾān = the Recitation).", meaning:"\"So recite what is easy of the Qurʾān.\"", magic:"THE HEART OF THE RELIEF — and it completes the yusr thread you've traced for SIX surahs.", grammar:"Tayassara — Form V (became easy — reflexive). mā = \"whatever\" (relative).", why:"Tayassara — closes the hardship of v.1–19 with the exact root of Sharḥ's promise.", note:"Mā TAYASSARA = \"what is EASY\" — said TWICE (like yusrā twice in Sharḥ).", more:["tayassara — the \"EASE / smoothness\" family — Form V of yasura = to become easy, to be made easy; yusr = ease; taysīr = facilitation (modern: taysīr = making things easier, simplifying!).","The y-s-r thread: - ʿAbasa 80:20 — thumma's-sabīla yassarah (the way — He made it easy) - Inshiqāq 84:8 — ḥisāban yasīrā (an easy reckoning) - Aʿlā 87:8 — wa nuyassiruka li'l-yusrā (We ease you toward ease) - Layl 92:7, 10 — fa-sa-nuyassiruhu li'l-yusrā / li'l-ʿusrā (eased toward ease — or hardship) - Sharḥ 94:5–6 — inna maʿa'l-ʿusri yusrā (with hardship, ease — twice) - Muzzammil 73:20 — mā tayassara (what is easy) — TWICE And look at the structure of the WHOLE SURAH: v.1–19 = the ʿusr (hardship of night prayer); v.20 = the yusr (recite what is easy).","Sharḥ 94:5–6 — inna maʿa'l-ʿusri yusrā — ENACTED.","And mā tayassara appears TWICE in v.20 — just as yusrā appears twice in Sharḥ.","And the command is iqraʾū — the very first word ever revealed (ʿAlaq 96:1). The first command (read!) returns — now with the word EASE attached.","iqraʾū — plural imperative, hamzah root.","WHOLE SURAH: v.1–19 = HARDSHIP; v.20 = EASE → Sharḥ 94:5–6 ENACTED.","Y-s-r thread: 80:20, 84:8, 87:8, 92:7, 94:5, 73:20. Iqraʾū — the FIRST word revealed (96:1) returns, with EASE."]},
  {ar:"عَلِمَ أَن سَيَكُونُ مِنكُم مَّرْضَىٰ", tr:"ʿalima an sa-yakūnu minkum marḍā", v:"20", root:"The \"ILLNESS\" family — mariḍa = to fall ill; maraḍ = DISEASE; marīḍ (pl.", meaning:"\"He knows that among you there will be the sick…\"", magic:"The FIRST of three reasons for the ease — and it's ILLNESS. Allah names the sick explicitly as people whose limits He accounts for. The law is shaped around the patient.", grammar:"Sa- + yakūnu = future. marḍā — broken plural of marīḍ (faʿlā pattern), weak-final.", why:"Marḍā — the first named reason for mercy is human frailty itself.", note:"MARĪḌ = a PATIENT; mumarriḍah = a NURSE; tamrīḍ = NURSING. The law is SHAPED AROUND THE PATIENT.", more:["marḍā) = a SICK person, a PATIENT; mumarriḍ / mumarriḍah = a NURSE; tamrīḍ = NURSING; maraḍ fī qulūbihim = \"disease in their hearts\" (Muddaththir 74:31 — spiritual illness!).","This verse is a foundation for the principle that worship adjusts to capacity — lā yukallifu'llāhu nafsan illā wusʿahā (\"Allah does not burden a soul beyond its capacity,\" 2:286). Three reasons, three kinds of people (v.20): the SICK (marḍā), the TRAVELLER-TRADERS (yaḍribūna fi'l-arḍ), the FIGHTERS (yuqātilūna).","Body, livelihood, defence — every major demand on human time is acknowledged.","And note sa-yakūnu — \"there WILL be\" — future tense: Allah planned for illness before it arrived.","The root's spiritual sense returns in Muddaththir 74:31 — alladhīna fī qulūbihim maraḍ — \"those in whose hearts is DISEASE.\" Physical illness excused (73:20); spiritual illness exposed (74:31).","Three reasons: the SICK, the TRADERS, the FIGHTERS — body, livelihood, defence.","73:20: physical illness EXCUSED.","74:31: heart-disease EXPOSED.","Sa-yakūnu — He planned for illness in advance."]},
  {ar:"يَضْرِبُونَ فِي الْأَرْضِ يَبْتَغُونَ مِن فَضْلِ اللَّهِ", tr:"Yaḍribūna fi'l-arḍi yabtaghūna min faḍli'llāh", v:"20", root:"Yaḍribūn — the \"STRIKING\" family — ḍaraba = to strike; but the idiom ḍaraba fi'l-arḍ = to TRAVEL (for trade).", meaning:"\"…and others travelling through the land seeking of the bounty of Allah…\"", magic:"TRADE is called \"seeking Allah's BOUNTY.\" Earning a living isn't treated as worldly distraction — it's ibtighāʾ min faḍli'llāh — seeking HIS grace.", grammar:"ḍaraba fi'l-arḍ = an IDIOM for \"travel.\" Don't translate \"strike.\" yabtaghūna = ḥāl (\"while seeking\").", why:"Faḍl — frames earning as receiving grace, not grabbing wealth.", note:"ḌARABA FI'L-ARḌ = an IDIOM: to TRAVEL (\"strike out into the land\").", more:["Literally \"to strike out into the land\" (like a traveller's feet striking the ground); ḍarb = a type / a strike; ḍarībah = a TAX; iḍrāb = a STRIKE (workers'!).","yabtaghūn — seeking out (ibtighāʾ — Layl 92:20, ibtighāʾa wajhi rabbih).","faḍl — the \"SURPLUS / bounty / grace\" family — faḍl = bounty, extra; tafaḍḍal!","= \"PLEASE, go ahead!\" (literally \"do me the favour\"!); afḍal = better/best.","And the trade is recognized as a valid reason to ease the night prayer — you can't travel all day and stand all night.","The Qurʾān dignifies earning.","And you know who traded: Quraysh 106:2 — riḥlata'sh-shitāʾi wa'ṣ-ṣayf — the winter and summer CARAVANS.","Quraysh's travels were praised as Allah's īlāf (security); here, believers' travels are called seeking His faḍl.","And ibtighāʾ — Layl 92:20 — illa'btighāʾa wajhi rabbihi'l-aʿlā — seeking His FACE (in charity).","Here: seeking His BOUNTY (in trade).","Same verb — the face and the bounty both sought from Him.","= \"Please!\"; ḍarībah = a TAX.","wa ākharūna (\"and others\") introduces each group.","Not \"strike.\" TRADE = \"seeking Allah's BOUNTY (faḍl).\" The Qurʾān dignifies earning.","Layl 92:20: seeking His FACE.","Muzzammil 73:20: seeking His BOUNTY.","= \"PLEASE!\"; ḍarībah = a TAX.","Quraysh 106:2: the caravans."]},
  {ar:"وَآخَرُونَ يُقَاتِلُونَ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ", tr:"Wa ākharūna yuqātilūna fī sabīli'llāh", v:"20", root:"ākharūn — OTHERS (ākhar = another; NOT ākhir = last — one vowel!).", meaning:"\"…and others fighting in the way of Allah.\"", magic:"The third reason for ease: those defending the community. Form III (fāʿala) = MUTUAL action — qātala = to fight against someone who also fights — combat, not one-sided killing.", grammar:"Form III = reciprocity/mutual action (like qātala, ḥāraba, jāhada).", why:"Yuqātilūna (Form III) — names defensive, reciprocal combat as a recognized demand on time.", note:"FORM III = MUTUAL — qātala = fight someone who fights BACK (≠ qatala, to kill).", more:["yuqātilūn — Form III of qatala = to FIGHT (mutually — fighting with someone who fights back); qitāl = combat; muqātil = a fighter.","sabīl — a WAY / road (v.19 sabīlā).","(Compare qatala, Form I = to kill; ʿAbasa 80:17 qutila'l-insān.) This mention of fighting is why many scholars hold v.20 was revealed in MADĪNAH — armed defence wasn't permitted in the Makkan period.","So one verse may span a decade.","Fī sabīli'llāh — \"in the WAY of Allah\" — and v.19 just said ittakhadha ilā rabbihi sabīlā (take a way to his Lord).","The sabīl to Him (v.19) and fighting in His sabīl (v.20).","TRAP: ākharūn (OTHERS, short a after kh) ≠ ākhirah (the Hereafter / the LAST, i after kh).","ākharūna = plural of ākhar (another), a diptote-shaped pattern in some forms.","Mentions fighting → many scholars: v.20 is MADINAN.","One verse may span a decade.","ĀKHARŪN (OTHERS) ≠ ĀKHIRAH (Hereafter/LAST)."]},
  {ar:"وَأَقِيمُوا الصَّلَاةَ وَآتُوا الزَّكَاةَ", tr:"Wa aqīmu'ṣ-ṣalāta wa ātu'z-zakāh", v:"20", root:"Aqīmū — Form IV of qāma = to MAKE STAND, to establish (the q-w-m root).", meaning:"\"And establish the prayer and give the zakāh…\"", magic:"The ease does NOT remove the core. Night prayer is lightened — but the FIVE PRAYERS and ZAKĀH stand firm. The relief is in the extra; the foundation stays.", grammar:"Aqīmū — Form IV imperative of a hollow verb. ātū — Form IV imperative of a weak verb (ātā, to give).", why:"Aqīmū (make it stand) — because what's commanded is a structure kept upright, not a performance.", note:"The ease lightens the EXTRA; the five prayers and zakāh STAND FIRM. v.2 QUM (stand YOURSELF) → v.20 AQĪMŪ (make the PRAYER stand).", more:["ātū — Form IV of atā = to GIVE (yuʾtī, Layl 92:18). zakāh — purify + grow.","And you own this pair from Bayyinah 98:5 — wa yuqīmu'ṣ-ṣalāta wa yuʾtu'z-zakāh — the definition of \"the upright religion.\" Same two pillars, two surahs.","And Kawthar 108:2 — fa-ṣalli li-rabbika wa'nḥar — pray + give.","Aqīmū (MAKE IT STAND) — the q-w-m root, again.","The surah opened with qum (STAND, v.2) — and ends with aqīmū (MAKE THE PRAYER STAND, v.20).","From standing yourself up at night to standing the prayer up all day.","Bayyinah 98:5: yuqīmu'ṣ-ṣalāta wa yuʾtu'z-zakāh — same two pillars."]},
  {ar:"وَأَقْرِضُوا اللَّهَ قَرْضًا حَسَنًا", tr:"Wa aqriḍu'llāha qarḍan ḥasanā", v:"20", root:"Aqriḍū — the \"CUTTING OFF a piece\" family — qaraḍa = to cut, to snip; miqrāḍ = SCISSORS!", meaning:"\"…and lend Allah a goodly loan.\"", magic:"LEND ALLAH a loan. The One who is aṣ-Ṣamad (Ikhlāṣ 112:2) — who needs nothing — asks for a LOAN. Why \"loan\" and not \"gift\"? Because a loan is REPAID.", grammar:"Form IV imperative + Allāha (object) + cognate accusative with adjective (qarḍan ḥasanan).", why:"Qarḍ (a loan) — the most reassuring possible word for giving: it implies certain repayment.", note:"MIQRĀḌ = SCISSORS — a qarḍ (LOAN) is a piece SNIPPED from your wealth.", more:["Qarḍ = a LOAN (a piece cut off from your wealth and handed over); qurūḍ = loans (modern banking!); inqaraḍa = to become EXTINCT (cut off entirely!); qāriḍ = a RODENT (one that gnaws/cuts!).","ḥasan — good, beautiful (al-ḥusnā, Layl 92:6).","THE MOST ASTONISHING PHRASE IN THE SURAH.","The word itself is a guarantee: what you give will come back — and (2:245) multiplied many times.","Allah frames charity as borrowing from you — so you know He'll pay it back.","And the root image: qaraḍa = to CUT — miqrāḍ = SCISSORS.","A qarḍ is a piece you SNIP from your wealth and hand over.","Snip a piece off — it grows back larger.","Contrast the juzʾ's misers: Māʿūn 107:7 — yamnaʿūna'l-māʿūn (they won't even lend a POT).","Humazah 104:2 — jamaʿa mālan wa ʿaddadah (gathered and COUNTED it).","Layl 92:8 — bakhila wa'staghnā (withheld).","Here: lend to ALLAH Himself.","And ḥasanan — \"goodly\" — classical: from lawful wealth, with sincerity, without mann (reminding of the favour) — compare Muddaththir 74:6 (wa lā tamnun tastakthir) and Tīn 95:6 (ajrun ghayru mamnūn).","LEND ALLAH a loan — the One who needs NOTHING (aṣ-Ṣamad, 112:2) asks to BORROW.","\"Loan,\" not \"gift\" — because a loan is REPAID.","Māʿūn 107:7 (won't lend a POT), Humazah 104:2 (COUNTED it).","Inqaraḍa = EXTINCT; qāriḍ = a RODENT."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا تُقَدِّمُوا لِأَنفُسِكُم مِّنْ خَيْرٍ تَجِدُوهُ عِندَ اللَّهِ هُوَ خَيْرًا وَأَعْظَمَ أَجْرًا", tr:"Wa mā tuqaddimū li-anfusikum min khayrin tajidūhu ʿinda'llāhi huwa khayran wa aʿẓama ajrā", v:"20", root:"Tuqaddimū — the \"SENDING FORWARD / ahead\" family — Qiyāmah 75:13, Nabaʾ 78:40, Infiṭār 82:5, Fajr 89:24. anfus — SELVES (nafs).", meaning:"\"And whatever good you send ahead for yourselves, you will find it with Allah — better and greater in reward.\"", magic:"The \"sending ahead\" thread — and THIS time it's an encouragement, not a regret.", grammar:"Mā + jussive (tuqaddimū) + jussive (tajidūhu) = conditional (\"whatever… you will find\") — both drop the plural nūn.", why:"Qaddama — turns the Hereafter's regret-verb into today's opportunity.", note:"Fajr 89:24: \"I WISH I had sent ahead (qaddamtu) for my life\" — REGRET.", more:["tajidūhu — you will FIND it (wajada).","ajr — a WAGE / reward (ujrah = a fee; not yujīr from Jinn 72:22!).","- Qiyāmah 75:13 — man will be informed of mā qaddama wa akhkhar (what he sent ahead / left behind) - Nabaʾ 78:40 — mā qaddamat yadāh (what his hands sent ahead) - Infiṭār 82:5 — ʿalimat nafsun mā qaddamat (a soul will know what it sent ahead) - Fajr 89:24 — yā laytanī qaddamtu li-ḥayātī (\"I wish I had sent ahead for my life!\" — REGRET) - Muzzammil 73:20 — mā tuqaddimū li-anfusikum (\"whatever you send ahead for yourselves\" — INVITATION) Fajr 89:24 is the wish — too late.","Muzzammil 73:20 is the chance — now.","Same verb, same \"for my/your-SELF\"!","Fajr: li-ḥayātī (for MY life).","Muzzammil: li-anfusikum (for YOURSELVES).","Do now what the regretful man wishes he'd done.","Tajidūhu = you will FIND it — like a deposit waiting in an account.","Nothing lost (Jinn 72:13 — lā yakhāfu bakhsā, no short-changing).","And note: li-anfusikum — \"for YOURSELVES.\" The loan to Allah (previous phrase) turns out to be a deposit for YOU.","You lend Him; you're the beneficiary.","huwa khayran — huwa (separating pronoun) + khayran (ACCUSATIVE) — because tajidūhu takes TWO objects (\"find it [to be] better\"), so khayran is the second object, and huwa just adds emphasis.","Muzzammil 73:20: \"whatever you send ahead (tuqaddimū) for yourselves\" — INVITATION.","Do NOW what he wished he'd done.","Tajidūhu = you'll FIND it — a deposit waiting.","The loan to Allah is a deposit for YOU.","Huwa khayran — khayran ACCUSATIVE (2nd object of tajidū)."]},
  {ar:"وَاسْتَغْفِرُوا اللَّهَ ۖ إِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ", tr:"Wa'staghfiru'llāha inna'llāha ghafūrun raḥīm", v:"20", root:"Istaghfirū — Form X of ghafara = SEEK covering / forgiveness; mighfar = a HELMET (covers and protects!); Naṣr 110:3 wa'staghfirh.", meaning:"\"And seek forgiveness of Allah. Indeed, Allah is Ever-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\"", magic:"The surah ends — after all that worship — with ISTIGHFĀR.", grammar:"Form X imperative (istaghfirū). ghafūrun raḥīmun — two intensive adjectives (faʿūl, faʿīl), khabar of inna.", why:"Ending on istighfār + Ghafūr Raḥīm — so the believer leaves even their best effort in His covering.", note:"After ALL that worship → \"SEEK FORGIVENESS.\" Istighfār covers the GAPS in your good deeds.", more:["ghafūr — faʿūl intensive = ever-forgiving, covering again and again.","raḥīm — the \"MERCY / womb\" family — raḥim = the WOMB; raḥmah = mercy (Balad 90:17 marḥamah).","They stood the night until their feet swelled; they recited with tartīl; they're told to pray, give, lend to Allah — AND THEN: \"seek forgiveness.\" Why?","Because no worship is ever perfect.","Istighfār covers the gaps in your good deeds, not just your sins.","It's how you close every act of worship — astaghfiru'llāh ×3 after every prayer.","And you own the identical move from Naṣr 110:3 — fa-sabbiḥ bi-ḥamdi rabbika wa'staghfirh — victory ends in istighfār.","Naṣr: VICTORY ends in istighfār.","Muzzammil: WORSHIP ends in istighfār.","Every completed task closes with asking forgiveness.","And Form X again: istaghnā (\"I need nothing\" — ʿAbasa 80:5, Layl 92:8, ʿAlaq 96:7) vs istaghfir (\"I need forgiveness\").","Same form, opposite souls.","And the pair ghafūr raḥīm — Ghafūr = covers your sins (the mighfar, helmet); Raḥīm = shows active mercy (from the raḥim, womb).","The surah that began with a man WRAPPED UP in garments (muzzammil) ends with Allah's maghfirah — His covering.","Muzzammil (wrapped) → ghafūr (the One who covers).","(Astaghfiru'llāh ×3 after every prayer.) Naṣr 110:3: VICTORY ends in istighfār.","Muzzammil 73:20: WORSHIP ends in it.","MUZZAMMIL (wrapped in garments, v.1) → GHAFŪR (the One who COVERS, v.20).","The surah opens wrapped and closes covered.","Mighfar = HELMET; raḥim = WOMB."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:74, name:"Al-Muddaththir", ar:"المدثر", en:"The Cloaked One", verses:56, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"FOUR thummas = four camera cuts on a man manufacturing a lie. Thought → calculated → looked → frowned → scowled → turned → grew proud → spoke.",
  special:["It contains the Qurʾān's most detailed SLOW-MOTION portrait of a man inventing a lie — frame by frame.","V.18–24 — a powerful Makkan chief (al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah, in the reports) is asked to explain the Qurʾān away. The surah films him thinking:","> innahu fakkara wa qaddara — he thought, and calculated > fa-qutila kayfa qaddar · thumma qutila kayfa qaddar — damn how he calculated! Again — damn how he calculated! > thumma naẓar — then he looked > thumma ʿabasa wa basar — then he frowned and scowled > thumma adbara wa'stakbar — then he turned his back and grew arrogant > fa-qāla in hādhā illā siḥrun yuʾthar — and said: \"This is nothing but magic, passed down.\"","Count the thummas (\"THEN\"): four in a row. Each thumma is a camera cut — a pause, a new expression. You SEE him think, look around, screw up his face, turn away, and deliver the slander. No other passage in the Qurʾān slows down like this.","And it's the twin of Muzzammil: both open by addressing the Prophet ﷺ by his GARMENT (muzzammil / muddaththir), both say \"dharnī\" (\"Leave Me with…\") in VERSE 11, both use qum (\"rise!\") in verse 2. And Jinn 72:17 and Muddaththir 74:17 share ṣaʿad (the climbing punishment) in VERSE 17.","--"],
  big:["Seven commands (v.1–7) — rise, warn, magnify, purify, shun, give without seeking more, be patient.","The Trumpet (v.8–10) — a hard Day, not easy for the deniers.","The man given everything (v.11–17) — wealth, sons, a smoothed path — and greed for more.","The slow-motion slander (v.18–25) — four thummas.","Saqar (v.26–31) — it spares nothing; nineteen over it; the number as a test.","Three oaths (v.32–37) — moon, departing night, shining dawn.","Collateral, and the confession (v.38–48) — every soul held in pledge; four sins admitted.","The fleeing donkeys (v.49–53) — why do they bolt from the reminder?","The close (v.54–56) — it is a reminder; He is worthy of taqwā and worthy to forgive."],
  traps:["الْبَشَرِ (v.25, 29, 31, 36) — means BOTH \"humans\" AND \"skin\" (bashrah). v.29 lawwāḥatun li'l-bashar = \"scorches the SKIN\" — throwing v.25's \"just bashar\" back.","لَوَّاحَةٌ (v.29) vs اللَّوَّامَةِ (Qiyāmah 75:2) — ONE LETTER apart — the scorcher vs. the self-blaming soul.","الْيَقِينُ (v.47) — means DEATH here (the one certainty — 15:99). Not \"certainty\" in the abstract.","فَكَبِّرْ (v.3) vs اسْتَكْبَرَ (v.23) — same root: Form II (make ALLAH great) vs Form X (make YOURSELF great).","قُتِلَ (v.19–20) — an IDIOM of condemnation (\"confound him!\"), NOT \"he was killed.\"","حُمُرٌ (v.50) — ḥumur = DONKEYS; ḥumr = RED ones. One vowel.","الرُّجْزَ (v.5) — rujz (idols/filth) vs rijz (punishment) — one vowel; and a recognized recitation reads rijz.","تَسْتَكْثِرُ (v.6) — RAISED (ḍammah), not jussive — a ḥāl (\"while seeking more\"). Tamnun = unmerged doubled jussive.","لَمْ نَكُ (v.43–44) — nakun with the NŪN DROPPED (like yaku, Qiyāmah 75:37).","تِسْعَةَ عَشَرَ (v.30) — compound numbers 11–19 are INDECLINABLE.","يَزْدَادَ (v.31) — Form VIII: yaztād → yazdād (t becomes d after z).","إِنْ هَٰذَا إِلَّا (v.24–25) — in = NOT — \"this is nothing but…\"","رَهِينَةٌ (v.38) — COLLATERAL / a pledge (rahn = mortgage), not just \"responsible.\"","عَلَى الْكَافِرِينَ غَيْرُ يَسِيرٍ (v.10) — the restriction IMPLIES ease for believers (mafhūm al-mukhālafah).","الْمُدَّثِّرُ (v.1) — Form V with ASSIMILATION (mutadaththir → muddaththir). Same rule as muzzammil and ʿAbasa's yazzakkā.","Fronted objects v.3–7 — rabbaka, thiyābaka, ar-rujza, li-rabbika — FRONTED = restriction.","إِذْ أَدْبَرَ (v.33) — recognized variant idhā adbar.","مُّسْتَنفِرَةٌ (v.50) — variant mustanfarah (passive: \"made to flee\").","يَذْكُرُونَ (v.56) — variant tadhkurūna (\"you remember\").","سَقَرَ (v.26, 27, 42) — DIPTOTE (feminine proper noun)."],
  links:[{w:"الْمُدَّثِّرُ (v.1)",e:"الْمُزَّمِّلُ (Muzzammil 73:1)",t:"Twin garment-addresses — Form V assimilation"},{w:"قُمْ (v.2)",e:"قُمِ اللَّيْلَ (Muzzammil 73:2)",t:"Rise to PRAY (73:2) / rise to WARN (74:2)"},{w:"فَأَنذِرْ (v.2) · نَذِيرًا (v.36)",e:"مُنذِرُ (79:45) · أَنذَرْنَاكُمْ (78:40)",t:"The command → the content"},{w:"فَكَبِّرْ (v.3) · اسْتَكْبَرَ (v.23) · الْكُبَرِ (v.35)",e:"الْكُبْرَىٰ (79:20, 34 · 87:12)",t:"Magnify Allah / magnify yourself / the great calamity"},{w:"فَطَهِّرْ (v.4)",e:"مُّطَهَّرَةٍ (80:14 · 98:2) · طَهُورًا (76:21)",t:"Purity"},{w:"فَاهْجُرْ (v.5)",e:"هَجْرًا جَمِيلًا (Muzzammil 73:10)",t:"Leave people BEAUTIFULLY / leave idolatry ABSOLUTELY"},{w:"تَمْنُن (v.6)",e:"غَيْرُ مَمْنُونٍ (Tīn 95:6)",t:"No mann in His reward → none in your giving"},{w:"تَسْتَكْثِرُ (v.6)",e:"التَّكَاثُرُ (102:1) · الْكَوْثَرَ (108:1)",t:"Chasing more / given the most / never give to GET more"},{w:"وَلِرَبِّكَ فَاصْبِرْ (v.7)",e:"فَصَلِّ لِرَبِّكَ (108:2) · وَاصْبِرْ (73:10)",t:"Li-rabbika"},{w:"النَّاقُورِ (v.8)",e:"الصُّورِ (78:18) · الصَّاخَّةُ (80:33) · الْقَارِعَةُ (101:1)",t:"Names for the Day's sound"},{w:"عَسِيرٌ / غَيْرُ يَسِيرٍ (v.9–10)",e:"يَسِيرًا (84:8) · 94:5–6 · 73:20",t:"Hard for deniers → implied EASY for believers"},{w:"ذَرْنِي (v.11)",e:"وَذَرْنِي (Muzzammil 73:11)",t:"BOTH VERSE 11"},{w:"وَحِيدًا (v.11)",e:"شَفَاعَةُ الشَّافِعِينَ (v.48, same surah) · أَحَدٌ (112:1)",t:"Created ALONE → no one to PAIR with him"},{w:"مَّمْدُودًا (v.12)",e:"مُّمَدَّدَةٍ (Humazah 104:9)",t:"Extended wealth → extended pillars"},{w:"بَنِينَ (v.13)",e:"الْأَبْتَرُ (108:3) · وَمَا كَسَبَ (111:2) · بَنِيهِ (80:36)",t:"Sons — mocked, unavailing, fled from"},{w:"تَمْهِيدًا (v.14)",e:"مِهَادًا (Nabaʾ 78:6)",t:"The cradle"},{w:"أَزِيدَ (v.15) · يَزْدَادَ (v.31)",e:"نَزِيدَكُمْ (Nabaʾ 78:30)",t:"Craved more WEALTH / believers got more FAITH"},{w:"صَعُودًا (v.17)",e:"صَعَدًا (Jinn 72:17) · الْعَقَبَةَ (90:11)",t:"BOTH VERSE 17 — smooth (v.14) → steep (v.17)"},{w:"قَدَّرَ (v.18)",e:"يُقَدِّرُ (73:20) · قَدَّرَ فَهَدَىٰ (87:3) · الْقَدْرِ (97:1)",t:"Divine measuring / human calculating"},{w:"قُتِلَ (v.19–20)",e:"قُتِلَ الْإِنسَانُ (ʿAbasa 80:17)",t:"The imprecation idiom"},{w:"نَظَرَ (v.21)",e:"فَلْيَنظُرِ (80:24) · أَفَلَا يَنظُرُونَ (88:17)",t:"Look to FIND / look to ESCAPE"},{w:"عَبَسَ (v.22)",e:"عَبَسَ (ʿAbasa 80:1)",t:"A frown CORRECTED / a frown CONDEMNED"},{w:"بَسَرَ (v.22)",e:"بَاسِرَةٌ (Qiyāmah 75:24)",t:"His scowl now → his face scowling then"},{w:"أَدْبَرَ (v.23, 33)",e:"أَدْبَرَ يَسْعَىٰ (Nāziʿāt 79:22)",t:"Pharaoh, al-Walīd, the night"},{w:"يُؤْثَرُ (v.24)",e:"آثَرَ (79:38) · تُؤْثِرُونَ (87:16)",t:"Duʿāʾ maʾthūr"},{w:"سِحْرٌ (v.24)",e:"قُرْآنًا عَجَبًا (Jinn 72:1)",t:"Humans: \"MAGIC.\" Jinn: \"a WONDER.\""},{w:"الْبَشَرِ ×4 (v.25, 29, 31, 36)",e:"مُّسْتَبْشِرَةٌ (ʿAbasa 80:39)",t:"Bashar = HUMANS and SKIN"},{w:"سَأُصْلِيهِ (v.26)",e:"82:15 · 83:16 · 84:12 · 87:12 · 88:4 · 92:15 · 111:3",t:"Ṣaliya — EIGHT surahs"},{w:"لَا تَذَرُ (v.28)",e:"ذَرْنِي (v.11, same surah)",t:"\"Leave him to Me\" → \"it leaves nothing\""},{w:"لَوَّاحَةٌ (v.29)",e:"اللَّوَّامَةِ (Qiyāmah 75:2) · لَوْحٍ (85:22)",t:"Self-blaming soul / scorching fire — one letter"},{w:"فِتْنَةً (v.31)",e:"فَتَنُوا (85:10) · لِّنَفْتِنَهُمْ (72:17)",t:"Fire, abundance, a number"},{w:"لِيَسْتَيْقِنَ (v.31) · الْيَقِينُ (v.47)",e:"عِلْمَ / عَيْنَ الْيَقِينِ (Takāthur 102:5–7)",t:"Certainty by revelation / by death"},{w:"مَّرَضٌ (v.31)",e:"مَّرْضَىٰ (Muzzammil 73:20)",t:"Body-illness excused / heart-illness exposed"},{w:"جُنُودَ (v.31)",e:"الْجُنُودِ (Burūj 85:17)",t:"Tyrants' armies / your Lord's armies"},{w:"أَسْفَرَ (v.34)",e:"سَفَرَةٍ (80:15) · مُّسْفِرَةٌ (80:38)",t:"Scribes, faces, dawn — one root of light"},{w:"يَتَقَدَّمَ أَوْ يَتَأَخَّرَ (v.37)",e:"قَدَّمَ وَأَخَّرَ (75:13) · قَدَّمَتْ وَأَخَّرَتْ (82:5)",t:"Deeds sent forward → YOU step forward"},{w:"لِمَن شَاءَ مِنكُمْ أَن (v.37)",e:"Takwīr 81:28",t:"Identical opening"},{w:"رَهِينَةٌ (v.38)",e:"يَوْمِ الدِّينِ (82:9 · 95:7 · 107:1)",t:"Debt → collateral → settlement"},{w:"كَسَبَتْ (v.38)",e:"وَمَا كَسَبَ (111:2) · يَكْسِبُونَ (83:14)",t:"What you earned"},{w:"أَصْحَابَ الْيَمِينِ (v.39)",e:"بِيَمِينِهِ (84:7) · الْمَيْمَنَةِ (90:18)",t:"Easy reckoning / blessing / release"},{w:"يَتَسَاءَلُونَ (v.40)",e:"يَتَسَاءَلُونَ (Nabaʾ 78:1)",t:"The questioners swap places"},{w:"الْمُجْرِمِينَ (v.41)",e:"أَجْرَمُوا (Muṭaffifīn 83:29)",t:"They laughed; now they're asked about"},{w:"سَلَكَكُمْ (v.42)",e:"يَسْلُكْهُ · يَسْلُكُ (Jinn 72:17, 27)",t:"Threading"},{w:"الْمُصَلِّينَ · نُطْعِمُ الْمِسْكِينَ (v.43–44)",e:"Māʿūn 107:3–5 · Fajr 89:18",t:"Māʿūn, confessed from Saqar"},{w:"نُكَذِّبُ بِيَوْمِ الدِّينِ (v.46)",e:"Māʿūn 107:1",t:"Forward reasoning / backward confession"},{w:"لَمْ نَكُ (v.43–44)",e:"أَلَمْ يَكُ (Qiyāmah 75:37)",t:"Nūn dropped"},{w:"شَفَاعَةُ (v.48)",e:"وَالشَّفْعِ (Fajr 89:3)",t:"Shafʿ = EVEN → intercession = pairing"},{w:"تَنفَعُهُمْ (v.48)",e:"إِن نَّفَعَتِ الذِّكْرَىٰ (Aʿlā 87:9)",t:"Benefit"},{w:"فَمَا لَهُمْ (v.49)",e:"مَا لَهَا (Zalzalah 99:3)",t:"Man questions the earth / Allah questions man"},{w:"مُعْرِضِينَ (v.49)",e:"يُعْرِضْ عَن ذِكْرِ (Jinn 72:17)",t:"Same verb, same object"},{w:"مُّسْتَنفِرَةٌ (v.50)",e:"نَفَرٌ (Jinn 72:1)",t:"Jinn rush TOWARD / humans bolt AWAY"},{w:"فَرَّتْ (v.51)",e:"الْمَفَرّ (Qiyāmah 75:10) · يَفِرُّ (80:34)",t:"Flee the cure now → \"where's the escape?\" then"},{w:"صُحُفًا مُّنَشَّرَةً (v.52)",e:"الصُّحُفُ نُشِرَتْ (81:10) · صُحُفًا مُّطَهَّرَةً (98:2)",t:"Demanded scrolls / got their own records"},{w:"تَذْكِرَةٌ · فَمَن شَاءَ ذَكَرَهُ (v.54–55)",e:"ʿAbasa 80:11–12",t:"Nearly IDENTICAL"},{w:"إِلَّا أَن يَشَاءَ اللَّهُ (v.56)",e:"Takwīr 81:29 · Insān 76:30",t:"Human will inside divine will"},{w:"التَّقْوَىٰ · الْمَغْفِرَةِ (v.56)",e:"الْمُدَّثِّرُ (v.1) · غَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌ (73:20)",t:"Covered in fear → the One who covers"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"يَا أَيُّهَا الْمُدَّثِّرُ", tr:"Yā ayyuha'l-muddaththir", v:"1", root:"The \"OUTER GARMENT / covering cloak\" family — dithār = the OUTER garment (a cloak or blanket worn over the inner layer — as opposed to shiʿār, the garment touching the skin); tadaththara (Form V) = to cover oneself in a cloak; dathar = abundant wealth (a \"covering\" of property).", meaning:"\"O you who covers himself [with a cloak]!\"", magic:"The TWIN of Muzzammil 73:1. Muzzammil = wrapped (in garments); muddaththir = covered (in an outer cloak).", grammar:"Form V with ASSIMILATION: mutadaththir → muddaththir — the tāʾ melts into the dāl and doubles it.", why:"Muddaththir — the outer cloak, to be thrown off for public work.", note:"TWIN of Muzzammil 73:1 — both name him by his GARMENT.", more:["Both address the Prophet ﷺ by his STATE — with tenderness — the moment he came home overwhelmed.","The shiʿār / dithār distinction is beautiful — the Prophet ﷺ famously said of the Anṣār: \"You are the shiʿār (inner garment) and the people are the dithār (outer)\" — i.e.","The dithār is the outer layer — and he's told to throw it off and go OUT (v.2).","Same rule: Muzzammil 73:1 (muzzammil), ʿAbasa 80:3–4 (yazzakkā, yadhdhakkaru).","Shiʿār = INNER garment; dithār = OUTER cloak — \"You (Anṣār) are my shiʿār.\" Form V assimilation: mutadaththir → muddaththir."]},
  {ar:"قُمْ فَأَنذِرْ", tr:"Qum fa-andhir", v:"2", root:"Qum — STAND / rise (the q-w-m root).", meaning:"\"Rise and warn!\"", magic:"Two words — the launch of a mission. Qum — rise. Fa-andhir — the fa = \"and immediately\" — rise and straight away warn. No object is given — warn whom? Not said = everyone.", grammar:"Two imperatives; fa = immediate sequence. andhir — Form IV, object DELETED (generalizing).", why:"Andhir (warn) rather than bashshir (give good news) — the first public message is an alarm.", note:"JAHĀZ INDHĀR = an ALARM SYSTEM — the Prophet ﷺ is the alarm. Qum twins: 73:2 rise to PRAY; 74:2 rise to WARN. Warn WHOM?", more:["andhir — the \"WARNING\" family — Form IV andhara = to WARN; nadhīr = a WARNER; nadhr = a VOW (Insān 76:7 — binding yourself in advance!); indhār = a warning (modern: indhār = an ALARM / alert; jahāz indhār = an alarm system!).","The deletion makes it universal.","The qum twins: Muzzammil 73:2 — qumi'l-layla — rise to PRAY (private, at night).","Muddaththir 74:2 — qum fa-andhir — rise to WARN (public, by day).","The inner life first, then the public mission.","And andhir — the warner thread: Nāziʿāt 79:45 (innamā anta mundhir), Nabaʾ 78:40 (innā andharnākum), Layl 92:14 (fa-andhartukum nāran talaẓẓā).","And here it begins: the first command to warn.","Jahāz indhār = an ALARM SYSTEM — the Prophet ﷺ is the alarm.","Object deleted = EVERYONE.","Nadhr = a VOW (Insān 76:7)."]},
  {ar:"وَرَبَّكَ فَكَبِّرْ", tr:"Wa rabbaka fa-kabbir", v:"3", root:"The \"GREATNESS / bigness\" family — kabura = to be big; kabbara (Form II) = to MAGNIFY, to declare great → TAKBĪR = saying \"Allāhu akbar\"; akbar = greater/greatest; kibr = ARROGANCE (thinking yourself big!); kabīr = big/old.", meaning:"\"And your Lord — magnify!\"", magic:"This is the origin of TAKBĪR. Kabbir — \"declare Him GREAT\" — every Allāhu akbar you've ever said is this command.", grammar:"Rabbaka — accusative, FRONTED before its verb = restriction. fa = links the fronted object to the command.", why:"Kabbir — the first act of the mission is to put Allah in His place — as the Greatest — in the heart before the mouth.", note:"KABBIR = the origin of TAKBĪR — \"Allāhu akbar.\" Rabbaka FRONTED = magnify HIM ALONE.", more:["And look at the word order: wa RABBAKA fa-kabbir — \"your LORD\" is FRONTED — restriction: magnify HIM — and ONLY Him.","Not yourself, not the chiefs, not the idols.","And the root has a dark twin in this very surah: v.23 — thumma adbara wa'stakbar — \"then he turned away and grew ARROGANT.\" Istakbara = Form X = to CONSIDER YOURSELF great.","v.3: kabbir — make ALLAH great.","v.23: istakbara — make YOURSELF great.","Same root (k-b-r), the whole moral universe in two words.","Form II (declaring Him great) vs Form X (considering yourself great) — just like istaghnā (Form X, \"I need nothing\") vs aghnā (Form IV, \"He enriched\").","v.3 KABBIR (make ALLAH great) vs v.23 ISTAKBARA (make YOURSELF great).","Same root — Form II vs Form X."]},
  {ar:"وَثِيَابَكَ فَطَهِّرْ", tr:"Wa thiyābaka fa-ṭahhir", v:"4", root:"Thiyāb — plural of thawb = a GARMENT (thawb = the everyday Arab robe!); the root th-w-b = to RETURN (thawāb = REWARD.", meaning:"\"And your garments — purify!\"", magic:"Two classical readings — and both are taught: (a) LITERAL — clean your clothes (physical purity for prayer — a basis for the ruling that clothes must be pure in ṣalāh).", grammar:"Thiyābaka FRONTED (restriction pattern). Form II imperative (intensive: purify thoroughly).", why:"Thiyāb — a word that carries both literal clothing and moral character in Arabic usage.", note:"Ṭāhir ath-THIYĀB = \"pure of garments\" = a person of PURE CHARACTER. Both readings: clean CLOTHES and clean CONDUCT.", more:["What \"returns\" to you!; mathābah = a place people return to — the Kaʿbah, 2:125!). ṭahhir — Form II of ṭahura = to PURIFY, to cleanse thoroughly (ṭahārah = ritual purity; muṭahharah — ʿAbasa 80:14, Bayyinah 98:2; ṭahūr — Insān 76:21).","(b) METAPHORICAL — in Arabic idiom, ṭāhir ath-thiyāb (\"pure of garments\") = a person of pure CHARACTER and conduct; danis ath-thiyāb (\"dirty of garments\") = a person of bad character.","So: purify your SELF, your deeds, your intentions.","Both readings together: outside clean AND inside clean.","And the garment thread: v.1 — muddaththir — covered in a cloak.","v.4 — thiyābaka fa-ṭahhir — purify your garments.","The man wrapped in a cloak is told: rise — and make the cloak pure.","Thawb and thawāb (reward) share a root — pure garments (deeds) → reward that returns to you.","Thawb (garment) & THAWĀB (reward) — same root."]},
  {ar:"وَالرُّجْزَ فَاهْجُرْ", tr:"Wa'r-rujza fa'hjur", v:"5", root:"Rujz — the \"FILTH / abomination / what brings punishment\" family — rujz (with ḍammah) = IDOLS, filth, abomination.", meaning:"\"And idolatry [and all filth] — shun it!\"", magic:"Two cross-surah payoffs.", grammar:"Ar-rujza FRONTED (restriction).", why:"Rujz — names idolatry as filth tied to its own consequence.", note:"Muzzammil 73:10: leave PEOPLE hajran jamīlā (BEAUTIFULLY). Muddaththir 74:5: leave the FILTH — no softness.", more:["Rijz (with kasrah) = PUNISHMENT (7:134 — kashafta ʿanna'r-rijz); classical: rujz = whatever LEADS to rijz (sin that brings punishment); rajaz = a poetic metre (rhythmic trembling!).","uhjur — the \"LEAVING / abandoning\" family — hijrah = EMIGRATION; hajr = shunning.","ONE — the hajr twins: Muzzammil 73:10 — wa'hjurhum hajran jamīlā — \"leave THEM with a BEAUTIFUL leaving.\" Muddaththir 74:5 — wa'r-rujza fa'hjur — \"leave the FILTH\" — no adjective, no softness.","PEOPLE get hajr jamīl (gentle avoidance); IDOLATRY gets hajr absolute.","Be gentle with people, ruthless with falsehood.","TWO — rujz vs rijz: one vowel: rujz (idols/filth) vs rijz (punishment).","Classical: rujz is named so because it LEADS to rijz.","The sin is named after its punishment.","Note: the Prophet ﷺ never worshipped idols even before prophethood — so the command means: keep shunning, never approach (continuation, and a lesson for the community).","And hijrah — the same root — years later, he would \"shun\" a whole city full of rujz.","rujz (ḍammah) — and there's a recognized recitation rijz (kasrah) — meaning stays: shun what leads to punishment.","Gentle with people, ruthless with falsehood.","RUJZ (idols/filth) → leads to RIJZ (punishment) — one vowel.","Hijrah — same root as uhjur."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا تَمْنُن تَسْتَكْثِرُ", tr:"Wa lā tamnun tastakthir", v:"6", root:"Tamnun — the \"FAVOUR held over someone / reminding of a kindness\" family — manna = to bestow a favour.", meaning:"\"And do not give [a favour] seeking to receive more [in return].\"", magic:"THE BEST ROOT-PAYOFF IN THE OPENING — two roots you own, in three words.", grammar:"Tamnun — jussive of a DOUBLED verb, UNMERGED (tamnun, not tamunna) — both forms exist; here the separated form.", why:"Mann + istikthār — names the two corruptions of generosity: reminding and expecting.", note:"MAMNŪN! = \"much OBLIGED!\" — the receiver's side of mann. Tīn 95:6: Allah's reward has NO mann. 74:6: give with NO mann.", more:["Mann = reminding someone of what you gave them (al-mann wa'l-adhā — \"reminders of generosity and hurt,\" 2:264); minnah = a favour; mamnūn (Tīn 95:6!) — ajrun ghayru mamnūn; mamnūn in modern Arabic = \"GRATEFUL / much obliged\"!; al-Mannān = the Bestower (a Divine Name).","tastakthir — Form X of kathura (to be much) = to SEEK MORE, to regard as much — the root of TAKĀTHUR (102:1) and KAWTHAR (108:1)!","Classical readings: (a) don't give a gift expecting a BIGGER one back; (b) don't count your good deeds as MANY (don't feel you've done a lot for Allah); (c) don't remind people of your favours (mann) to extract more.","ROOT 1 — mann: Tīn 95:6 — ajrun ghayru mamnūn — a reward NEVER held over you, NEVER cut off.","Allah's reward comes with no mann — so don't attach mann to yours.","Muzzammil 73:20 — aqriḍu'llāha qarḍan ḥasanā — a goodly loan (classically: without mann).","ROOT 2 — k-th-r: Takāthur 102:1 — alhākumu't-takāthur (competing for MORE).","Kawthar 108:1 — aʿṭaynāka'l-kawthar (given the MOST).","Muddaththir 74:6 — lā tamnun tastakthir (don't give SEEKING MORE).","The rat-race (102), the gift that ends it (108), and the instruction: never give in order to GET more (74). And Form X again — istakthara = seek/consider MORE — sibling of istaghnā (I need nothing), istakbara (v.23 — I am great).","Form X: the grammar of the ego.","= \"much obliged!\" — in everyday Arabic, mamnūn means grateful — the receiver's side of mann.","tastakthiru — raised (ḍammah), NOT jussive — it's a ḥāl: \"don't give WHILE seeking more.\"","K-th-r: TAKĀTHUR (chasing more, 102:1) → KAWTHAR (given the most, 108:1) → tastakthir (never give to GET more, 74:6). Tastakthiru = a ḥāl (raised), not jussive."]},
  {ar:"وَلِرَبِّكَ فَاصْبِرْ", tr:"Wa li-rabbika fa'ṣbir", v:"7", root:"The \"RESTRAINING / holding firm\" family — ṣabara = to confine, tie down; ṣabr; ṣabir = ALOES (bitter, healing).", meaning:"\"And for your Lord, be patient!\"", magic:"The seventh and final command — and li-rabbika is the key. Li-rabbika = \"FOR your Lord\" (fronted = only for Him).", grammar:"Li-rabbika FRONTED (restriction). fa + imperative.", why:"ṣabr li-rabbika — the purpose that makes patience bearable.", note:"LI-RABBIKA = patience FOR HIM (Kawthar 108:2: pray li-rabbika). Muzzammil 73:10: patience OVER their words.", more:["Patience not for reputation, not for results, not to prove a point — for His sake.","And notice: li-rabbika is the cure from Kawthar 108:2 — fa-ṣalli li-rabbika (pray for your Lord, the answer to Māʿūn's riyāʾ).","Pray for Him (Kawthar); be patient for Him (Muddaththir).","The twin: Muzzammil 73:10 — wa'ṣbir ʿalā mā yaqūlūn (be patient over what they say).","73:10: patience OVER their words.","74:7: patience FOR your Lord.","The burden and the purpose.","The seven commands complete (v.1–7): RISE · WARN · MAGNIFY · PURIFY · SHUN · GIVE without seeking · BE PATIENT.","A whole mission statement — and patience comes LAST because every other command will need it.","74:7: patience FOR your Lord.","Seven commands: RISE · WARN · MAGNIFY · PURIFY · SHUN · GIVE without seeking · PATIENCE."]},
  {ar:"فَإِذَا نُقِرَ فِي النَّاقُورِ", tr:"Fa-idhā nuqira fi'n-nāqūr", v:"8", root:"The \"PECKING / tapping / striking a hard surface\" family — naqara = to peck (like a bird), to tap, to knock.", meaning:"\"Then when the Trumpet is sounded…\"", magic:"A new word for the Trumpet — and the root is a BEAK.", grammar:"Nuqira = passive (no doer named). nāqūr = fāʿūl pattern (an instrument).", why:"Naqr (piercing tap) — emphasizes the sharpness of the sound, not its volume.", note:"MINQĀR = a BEAK — the Trumpet is \"PECKED.\" A sharp, PIERCING blast. Naqr al-ghurāb = \"the CROW'S PECK\" — rushed sujūd (forbidden).", more:["Minqār = a BEAK!; naqr = pecking (the hadith forbidding prayer like \"the pecking of a crow\" — naqr al-ghurāb — rushing your sujūd!); nāqūr = a horn/trumpet (the instrument struck/blown with force); naqqāra = a kettle-drum.","Nāqūr = the Trumpet (the same as aṣ-ṣūr, Nabaʾ 78:18). Naqara = to PECK — a sharp, piercing strike, like a beak tapping, or a blast that pierces the ears.","The Day's sound is pictured as a PIERCING TAP — sharp, sudden, penetrating.","Names for the Day's sound across your surahs: Nabaʾ 78:18 — yunfakhu fi'ṣ-ṣūr (blown into the horn); ʿAbasa 80:33 — aṣ-ṣākhkhah (the DEAFENER); Qāriʿah 101:1 — al-qāriʿah (the STRIKER/knocker); Nāziʿāt 79:13 — zajrah wāḥidah (one SHOUT); Muddaththir 74:8 — nuqira fi'n-nāqūr (PECKED/tapped into the horn).","The prayer hook: naqr al-ghurāb — \"the crow's pecking\" — the Prophet ﷺ forbade rushing sujūd like a crow pecks.","Day-sounds: blown (78:18), DEAFENER (80:33), STRIKER (101:1), SHOUT (79:13), PECKED (74:8)."]},
  {ar:"يَوْمٌ عَسِيرٌ · عَلَى الْكَافِرِينَ غَيْرُ يَسِيرٍ", tr:"Yawmun ʿasīr · ʿala'l-kāfirīna ghayru yasīr", v:"9–10", root:"ʿasīr — the \"HARDSHIP / difficulty\" family — ʿusr = hardship; muʿsir = in financial straits.", meaning:"\"That Day will be a hard Day — for the disbelievers, not easy.\"", magic:"The ʿusr / yusr pair — and the SECOND phrase hides a mercy. v.9: yawmun ʿASĪR — a HARD Day. v.10: ʿala'l-kāfirīna ghayru YASĪR — for the disbelievers, NOT EASY. Why say it twice?", grammar:"Ghayru yasīrin — ghayr + genitive = negating an adjective (like Tīn 95:6 ghayru mamnūn).", why:"Restricting \"not easy\" to the disbelievers — so the believer hears the implied ease.", note:"\"HARD — for the disbelievers NOT EASY\" → implied: for BELIEVERS, EASY. A threat with a promise inside.", more:["yasīr — the \"EASE\" family — yusr; yasīr = easy, slight.","Classical scholars: the restriction \"FOR THE DISBELIEVERS\" implies for the BELIEVERS, it WILL be easy (mafhūm al-mukhālafah — the implied opposite).","Hard for one group — therefore easy for another.","A threat with a promise folded inside.","The y-s-r / ʿ-s-r thread — now complete across SEVEN surahs: ʿAbasa 80:20 (the way made easy), Inshiqāq 84:8 (ḥisāban yasīrā — an easy reckoning — for the right-handed!), Aʿlā 87:8 (eased toward ease), Layl 92:7, 10 (eased toward yusrā / ʿusrā), Sharḥ 94:5–6 (with hardship, ease ×2), Muzzammil 73:20 (mā tayassara ×2), Muddaththir 74:9–10 (ʿasīr / ghayru yasīr).","And Inshiqāq 84:8 is the perfect partner: fa-sawfa yuḥāsabu ḥisāban YASĪRĀ — the believer's reckoning IS easy.","Muddaththir confirms the flip side: for the deniers, NOT easy.","The repetition (positive \"hard\" + negative \"not easy\") = ta'kīd (emphasis).","Inshiqāq 84:8: the believer's reckoning is YASĪR (easy).","Y-s-r/ʿ-s-r thread — SEVEN surahs.","Ghayru yasīr — like ghayru mamnūn (Tīn 95:6)."]},
  {ar:"ذَرْنِي وَمَنْ خَلَقْتُ وَحِيدًا", tr:"Dharnī wa man khalaqtu waḥīdā", v:"11", root:"Dharnī — \"LEAVE ME [with him]\" — from the defective verb wadhara (no past tense in use).", meaning:"\"Leave Me with the one I created alone.\"", magic:"THE TWIN THREAT — same word, same verse number.", grammar:"Dhar = imperative of a DEFECTIVE verb (Qiyāmah 75:21 tadharūn). wa man — the wāw = wāw al-maʿiyyah (\"with\").", why:"Waḥīd — turns his nickname into a reminder that he arrived empty-handed.", note:"DHARNĪ — Muzzammil 73:11 & Muddaththir 74:11 — BOTH VERSE 11. Group → ONE man.", more:["waḥīd — the \"ONENESS / being ALONE\" family — waḥīd = ALONE, single, unique; waḥdah = loneliness / unity; tawḥīd = declaring Allah One; aḥad (Ikhlāṣ!).","Muzzammil 73:11 — wa dharnī wa'l-mukadhdhibīn — \"leave Me with the deniers.\" Muddaththir 74:11 — dharnī wa man khalaqtu waḥīdā — \"leave Me with the one I created alone.\" BOTH VERSE 11. BOTH \"dharnī.\" Muzzammil: the deniers as a GROUP.","Muddaththir: ONE man, singled out.","Waḥīdā — \"ALONE\" — three classical readings, all powerful: (a) \"I created him ALONE\" — he came into the world with nothing: no wealth, no sons; (b) \"leave Me ALONE with him\" — I need no help dealing with him; (c) al-Walīd was reportedly NICKNAMED \"al-Waḥīd\" — \"the UNIQUE one\" — his people's pride.","And the Qurʾān takes his boast-name and twists it: waḥīd = not \"unique\" but \"ALONE — born with nothing.\" His title of distinction becomes a reminder of his helplessness at birth.","And the w-ḥ-d root is TAWḤĪD — the One (Ikhlāṣ aḥad) speaks of the man called \"the unique one\" — and reminds him who's actually Waḥīd.","He was nicknamed \"al-WAḤĪD\" (the unique one) → the Qurʾān: \"created waḥīdā\" — ALONE, with NOTHING."]},
  {ar:"وَجَعَلْتُ لَهُ مَالًا مَّمْدُودًا", tr:"Wa jaʿaltu lahu mālan mamdūdā", v:"12", root:"Māl — WEALTH.", meaning:"\"And I gave him wealth extended [vast and continuous].\"", magic:"Mamdūd = STRETCHED OUT — classical: wealth spread far (herds, gardens from Makkah to Ṭāʾif, trade caravans) and continuous (income that never stopped — harvests, rents, trade).", grammar:"Jaʿaltu + lahu + mālan — \"I made for him wealth.\" mamdūdan = passive participle (adjective).", why:"Mamdūd — captures both breadth and continuity of wealth, with a root that will return as punishment.", note:"MADD = the tajwīd LENGTHENING — his wealth was a long madd. Not a pile — a PIPELINE.", more:["mamdūd — the \"EXTENDING / stretching out\" family — madda = to extend; mamdūd = extended, spread wide, long-lasting; madd = the LENGTHENING in tajwīd; mumaddadah (Humazah 104:9 — fī ʿamadin mumaddadah!); muddat (Inshiqāq 84:3 — the earth stretched out).","AND NOTICE: \"I gave him\" (jaʿaltu) — first person.","The wealth he boasted of was ALLAH'S gift.","Compare Fajr 89:15 — fa-akramahu wa naʿʿamahu — honoured and pampered — and he credited himself.","The m-d-d thread: Humazah 104:9 — fī ʿamadin mumaddadah — the slanderer-hoarder in EXTENDED pillars of fire.","Muddaththir 74:12 — mālan mamdūdā — wealth EXTENDED.","Extended wealth now → extended pillars later.","Same root, cause and consequence.","The tajwīd hook: madd — holding the vowel long — his wealth was a long madd.","74:12: wealth mamdūd (extended) → Humazah 104:9: pillars mumaddadah (extended).","\"I GAVE him\" — first person."]},
  {ar:"وَبَنِينَ شُهُودًا", tr:"Wa banīna shuhūdā", v:"13", root:"Banīn — SONS (plural of ibn).", meaning:"\"And sons, always present [at his side].\"", magic:"Shuhūd = PRESENT — always at home with him.", grammar:"Banīna = sound plural, accusative (joined to mālan). shuhūdan = adjective/ḥāl (plural of shāhid).", why:"Shuhūd (present) — the sons' value was their visible presence — which makes their absence on that Day sharper.", note:"Banīna SHUHŪDĀ = sons ALWAYS PRESENT — too rich to need to travel.", more:["shuhūd — the \"PRESENT / witnessing\" family — shahida = to be present, to witness; shuhūd = those PRESENT (also witnesses); Burūj 85:7 — wa hum ʿalā mā yafʿalūna bi'l-muʾminīna shuhūd (present, watching the believers burn).","Classical: his sons (reportedly ten or so) were so wealthy they NEVER had to travel for trade — always present, always beside him, his honour on display at every gathering.","In that society: sons at your side = power, protection, status.","And recall what his kind mocked the Prophet ﷺ for — Kawthar 108:3 — al-abtar — \"cut off,\" SONLESS.","Al-Walīd had sons beside him; the Prophet ﷺ lost his.","And Masad 111:2 — mā aghnā ʿanhu māluhu wa mā kasab — \"what he earned\" = his CHILDREN — availed him nothing.","Sons present in this life; no help in the next.","And ʿAbasa 80:34–36 — on that Day yafirru'l-marʾu… wa banīh — a man FLEES from his SONS.","The sons who were shuhūd (present) will be fled from.","The shuhūd irony: Burūj 85:7: the persecutors were shuhūd (present, watching cruelty).","Muddaththir 74:13: the sons were shuhūd (present, a source of pride).","His kind mocked the Prophet ﷺ as SONLESS (abtar, 108:3). Masad 111:2: \"what he earned\" (sons) availed NOTHING.","ʿAbasa 80:36: a man FLEES his sons.","Burūj 85:7: shuhūd — present at cruelty."]},
  {ar:"وَمَهَّدتُّ لَهُ تَمْهِيدًا", tr:"Wa mahhadtu lahu tamhīdā", v:"14", root:"The \"SMOOTHING / preparing a bed / paving\" family — mahada = to spread, smooth; mahd = a CRADLE.", meaning:"\"And I smoothed [everything] for him, a complete smoothing.\"", magic:"His whole life was a CRADLE — and you own the cradle. Nabaʾ 78:6 — a-lam najʿali'l-arḍa mihādā — \"Did We not make the earth a CRADLE?\" — for all humanity.", grammar:"Form II (mahhada, intensive) + cognate accusative (tamhīdan). First person again: mahhadtu — \"I smoothed.\"", why:"Tamhīd (cradle-smoothing) — his life was made as easy as a baby's bed — so his ingratitude is total.", note:"MAHD = a CRADLE; tamhīd = a PREFACE / paving. Nabaʾ 78:6: the earth is EVERYONE'S mihād (cradle). 74:14: HIS was extra-smooth.", more:["Mihād = a bed, a cradle (Nabaʾ 78:6! — a-lam najʿali'l-arḍa mihādā); tamhīd = PREPARATION / a PREFACE / paving the way (modern: tamhīd = an introduction, mumahhad = a paved road!).","Muddaththir 74:14 — wa mahhadtu lahu tamhīdā — \"I smoothed everything for him — thoroughly.\" The earth is everyone's cradle; al-Walīd got an EXTRA-soft one.","Status, leadership, ease, respect — all smoothed in advance.","And the cognate accusative tamhīdā — \"smoothed with a smoothing\" — total ease, nothing rough left.","Three gifts in a row (v.12–14): extended WEALTH, present SONS, a smoothed PATH.","Everything a man could want.","And the setup is deliberate: the smoother his road (v.14), the steeper his punishment (v.17 — ṣaʿūdā, a steep CLIMB).","Smooth path now → steep slope later.","Compare Balad 90:11 — the ʿaqabah (steep pass) he never attempted.","Hook: tamhīd = a PREFACE — his life was all preface, no climb.","SMOOTH road (v.14) → STEEP climb (v.17).","Three gifts: WEALTH, SONS, a SMOOTH PATH."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ يَطْمَعُ أَنْ أَزِيدَ", tr:"Thumma yaṭmaʿu an azīd", v:"15", root:"Yaṭmaʿu — the \"GREED / covetous hope\" family — ṭamiʿa = to covet, to crave.", meaning:"\"Then he craves that I should give more!\"", magic:"THUMMA — \"and THEN, after ALL that…\" Wealth, sons, a smoothed life — and THEN he wants MORE. Thumma here expresses ASTONISHMENT (tarākhī in rank — \"on top of everything!\").", grammar:"Thumma = rank-astonishment (\"and yet, after all that\"). an + subjunctive (azīda).", why:"ṭamaʿ — names the response to abundance that ruins everything: craving instead of thanking.", note:"ṬAMMĀʿ = a GREEDY person. Wealth + sons + smooth life → THUMMA (and THEN!) he wants MORE.", more:["ṭamaʿ = GREED (everyday Arabic: ṭammāʿ = a greedy person); maṭmaʿ = an object of desire.","azīd — \"that I INCREASE\" (zāda).","Ṭamaʿ — GREED — not gratitude for what he got, but craving for what he didn't.","The takāthur disease (Takāthur 102:1) in one man.","An azīd — \"that I should INCREASE\" — and the zāda root has been building: Nabaʾ 78:30 — fa-lan nazīdakum illā ʿadhābā (\"We will NEVER INCREASE you except in punishment\").","He craves ziyādah — and Nabaʾ already told you the only ziyādah coming for his kind.","Muzzammil 73:4 — aw zid ʿalayh (increase the night prayer — the good increase).","And kallā answers in v.16: NO!","azīd — first person (\"that I increase\").","Nabaʾ 78:30: \"We'll NEVER INCREASE you except in punishment.\" The only ziyādah coming.","Takāthur (102:1) in one man."]},
  {ar:"كَلَّا ۖ إِنَّهُ كَانَ لِآيَاتِنَا عَنِيدًا", tr:"Kallā innahu kāna li-āyātinā ʿanīdā", v:"16", root:"Kallā — NO! (stern rebuke).", meaning:"\"No! Indeed, he has been stubbornly opposed to Our signs.\"", magic:"ʿAnīd = opposing AGAINST what you KNOW is true. Not ignorance — STUBBORNNESS.", grammar:"Li-āyātinā FRONTED (spotlight: \"to Our signs — stubborn\"). ʿanīdan = intensive faʿīl, khabar of kāna.", why:"ʿanīd — names knowing rejection, the worst kind.", note:"ʿANĪD = STUBBORN — opposing what you KNOW is true. He was MOVED by the Qurʾān — and opposed it anyway.", more:["ʿanīd — the \"STUBBORN OPPOSITION\" family — ʿanada = to deviate stubbornly, to resist knowingly; ʿinād = OBSTINACY (everyday: ʿanīd = STUBBORN — \"rāsuhu yābis\"!); muʿānid = a stubborn opponent; ʿanīd also = one who turns aside from the road.","Classical reports say al-Walīd was genuinely moved when he heard the Qurʾān — he reportedly said its words had \"sweetness and elegance,\" \"its top is fruitful, its root is abundant.\" He KNEW.","Kallā answers v.15's greed directly: \"more?\" — NO!","And the reason: innahu kāna… ʿanīdā — because he's been STUBBORN.","Kāna = habitually, over time.","ʿAnīd = STUBBORN — you'd use it of a stubborn child or a mule.","The kallā thread: Qiyāmah 75:11, Nabaʾ 78:4, ʿAbasa 80:11, 23, Fajr 89:17, 21, ʿAlaq 96:6, 15, 19, Takāthur 102:3–5, Humazah 104:4 — and here.","Kallā answers his greed: \"MORE?"]},
  {ar:"سَأُرْهِقُهُ صَعُودًا", tr:"Sa-urhiquhu ṣaʿūdā", v:"17", root:"Urhiqu — the \"OVERTAKING with a burden / exhausting\" family.", meaning:"\"I will force him up a steep, exhausting climb.\"", magic:"THE VERSE-17 TWIN — and the punishment matches the life.", grammar:"Sa- + Form IV present, 1st person. ṣaʿūdan = 2nd object (urhiqu takes two: \"burden him [with] a climb\").", why:"ṣaʿūd — inverts his smoothed life into an endless upward struggle.", note:"Jinn 72:17 ṣaʿadā & Muddaththir 74:17 ṣaʿūdā — BOTH VERSE 17. v.14 SMOOTHED (tamhīd) → v.17 STEEP CLIMB (ṣaʿūd).", more:["Form IV arhaqa = to impose an exhausting burden, to force into hardship; murhaq = EXHAUSTED; rahaq — Jinn 72:6, 13; tarhaquhā — ʿAbasa 80:41. ṣaʿūd — the \"ASCENDING\" family — ṣaʿūd = a STEEP CLIMB, a hard ascent; miṣʿad = an ELEVATOR; ṣaʿad — Jinn 72:17!","Jinn 72:17 — yaslukhu ʿadhāban ṣaʿadā — \"threads him into a CLIMBING punishment.\" Muddaththir 74:17 — sa-urhiquhu ṣaʿūdā — \"I'll force him up a STEEP CLIMB.\" BOTH VERSE 17. SAME ROOT (ṣ-ʿ-d).","Two surahs, side by side in the muṣḥaf, with the same punishment in the same verse.","AND THE CONTRAST WITHIN THE SURAH: v.14 — mahhadtu lahu tamhīdā — \"I SMOOTHED everything for him\" (a flat, easy cradle).","v.17 — sa-urhiquhu ṣaʿūdā — \"I'll force him up a STEEP CLIMB.\" SMOOTH (v.14) → STEEP (v.17).","The man who never had to climb in life will climb forever.","Urhiqu — the rahaq root you own from Jinn 72:6 (fa-zādūhum rahaqā) and ʿAbasa 80:41 (tarhaquhā qatarah).","And Balad 90:11 — fa-la'qtaḥama'l-ʿaqabah — the steep pass of good deeds he refused.","He refused the ʿaqabah of generosity; he's given the ṣaʿūd of punishment.","Sa- — \"I WILL\" — near future, certain.","Never climbed in life; climbs forever.","Murhaq = EXHAUSTED; miṣʿad = an ELEVATOR.","Refused Balad's ʿaqabah → given the ṣaʿūd."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّهُ فَكَّرَ وَقَدَّرَ", tr:"Innahu fakkara wa qaddar", v:"18", root:"Fakkara — the \"THINKING / reflecting\" family — fikr = thought; tafakkur = contemplation; mufakkir = a thinker; fikrah = an idea.", meaning:"\"Indeed, he thought and calculated.\"", magic:"The camera starts rolling — and the first two words are COGNITIVE. Fakkara — he THOUGHT (Form II — intensively, hard).", grammar:"Two Form II past verbs (intensive). innahu = \"indeed he\" (emphasis).", why:"Qaddara — shows his rejection was calculated, not confused — using the very root of divine decree.", note:"Fakkara wa QADDARA — he THOUGHT and CALCULATED (which label would stick).", more:["qaddara — the \"MEASURING / calculating / weighing out\" family — qadr, qadar — your most-tracked root after sawwā.","Qaddara — he CALCULATED, weighed options — \"what can I call this that people will believe?\" Reportedly he rejected \"poetry\" (he knew poetry too well), \"soothsaying\" (not their mumbling), \"madness\" (not the ravings of the mad) — and calculated which label would stick.","THE BITTER IRONY of qaddara: this is the root of DIVINE measuring — yuqaddiru'l-layla wa'n-nahār (Muzzammil 73:20 — Allah measures night and day), qaddara fa-hadā (Aʿlā 87:3 — He measured and guided), laylat al-qadr (Qadr 97:1). Allah qaddara to create and guide; this man qaddara to deceive.","The word for divine precision, used for human calculation against the truth.","And note: fikr itself is praised in the Qurʾān (tafakkur) — the problem isn't THINKING; it's thinking in order to OPPOSE.","Qaddara — Allah measures (73:20, 87:3, 97:1); HE calculated to deceive.","Mufakkir = a THINKER — thinking isn't the sin; thinking to OPPOSE is."]},
  {ar:"فَقُتِلَ كَيْفَ قَدَّرَ · ثُمَّ قُتِلَ كَيْفَ قَدَّرَ", tr:"Fa-qutila kayfa qaddar · thumma qutila kayfa qaddar", v:"19–20", root:"Qutila — literally \"may he be KILLED\" — an IDIOM of condemnation / astonished censure (\"confound him!\", \"damn him!\").", meaning:"\"So may he be destroyed — how he calculated! Then may he be destroyed — how he calculated!\"", magic:"The verse is REPEATED with thumma — the escalation you know.", grammar:"Qutila = passive past used as a curse (duʿāʾ) — not a narrative \"he was killed.\" kayfa qaddar = exclamatory question.", why:"Qutila repeated — heaps astonished condemnation on the very act of calculating against the truth.", note:"QUTILA = an IDIOM: \"CONFOUND HIM!\" — not \"he was killed.\" ʿAbasa 80:17: qutila'l-insān.", more:["v.19: \"damn how he calculated!\" v.20: \"THEN — damn how he calculated!\" Like Nabaʾ 78:4–5 (kallā sa-yaʿlamūn · thumma kallā sa-yaʿlamūn) and Takāthur 102:3–4 (kallā sawfa taʿlamūn · thumma kallā…).","Say it, pause, say it HARDER.","Qutila = an IMPRECATION, not a literal statement — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:17 — qutila'l-insānu mā akfarah (\"confound man, how ungrateful he is!\").","And kayfa qaddar — \"HOW he calculated!\" — an exclamation of astonishment at the WAY he did it (taʿajjub).","\"Look at the calculation he came up with!\" Mocking his cleverness.","Repetition with thumma = escalation (takrār + tarākhī).","Said TWICE with thumma — like Nabaʾ 78:4–5, Takāthur 102:3–4. Kayfa qaddar = \"look HOW he calculated!\" (mockery)."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ نَظَرَ", tr:"Thumma naẓar", v:"21", root:"The \"LOOKING / regarding\" family — naẓara = to look; naẓar = sight/consideration.", meaning:"\"Then he looked [around / reconsidered].\"", magic:"Camera cut #1 — THUMMA: a pause, then he LOOKS. Two words. A full stop.", grammar:"Thumma = time-delay (a pause). naẓara — no object stated (looked — at what? left open).", why:"Naẓar — a pause-frame; the surah makes you watch him buy time.", note:"CAMERA CUT #1: thumma NAẒAR — \"then he LOOKED.\" ʿAbasa 80:24, Ghāshiyah 88:17: LOOK to FIND truth. He looked to ESCAPE it.", more:["Naẓariyyah = a THEORY (a way of \"looking\")!; naẓẓārāt = eyeglasses!; munāẓarah = a debate.","Classical readings: (a) he looked at the faces of his people, gauging what they wanted to hear; (b) he looked again at the Qurʾān, reconsidering; (c) he looked around for an argument.","The surah slows time to the length of a glance.","Compare the \"LOOK\" commands: ʿAbasa 80:24 — fal-yanẓuri'l-insānu ilā ṭaʿāmih (let man LOOK at his food) Ghāshiyah 88:17 — a-fa-lā yanẓurūna ila'l-ibil (do they not LOOK at the camels?) Ṭāriq 86:5 — fal-yanẓuri'l-insānu mimma khuliq.","Those surahs command looking to FIND the truth; al-Walīd looks to ESCAPE it.","Same verb, opposite purpose.","Hook: naẓariyyah = a THEORY — he's looking for a theory to explain the Qurʾān away.","Naẓariyyah = a THEORY; naẓẓārāt = GLASSES."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ عَبَسَ وَبَسَرَ", tr:"Thumma ʿabasa wa basar", v:"22", root:"ʿabasa — the \"FROWNING\" family — ʿabūs = a scowling face (Insān 76:10; ʿAbasa 80:1!). basara.", meaning:"\"Then he frowned and scowled.\"", magic:"Camera cut #2 — his FACE changes. And BOTH words are ones you own.", grammar:"Two past verbs joined by wa; thumma = another time-cut.", why:"ʿabasa wa basara — two near-synonyms for intensification: a frown, deepening into a dark scowl.", note:"CAMERA CUT #2: his FACE. ʿABASA — ʿAbasa 80:1: the Prophet's ﷺ frown CORRECTED. Here: a denier's frown CONDEMNED.", more:["The \"SCOWLING darkly / grimacing / acting before the time\" family — basara = to scowl, to darken the face with displeasure; busr = UNRIPE dates (sour, harsh — eaten before their time!); bāsirah (Qiyāmah 75:24!) — wa wujūhun yawmaʾidhin bāsirah (scowling faces on that Day).","ʿAbasa — the opening word of Sūrat ʿAbasa (80:1)! ʿabasa wa tawallā — the Prophet ﷺ frowned (momentarily, at a blind man) — and was CORRECTED for it.","Here a denier frowns at the TRUTH itself — and is CONDEMNED.","Same verb: a frown corrected (80:1), a frown condemned (74:22). Which shows the standard: even the Prophet's ﷺ small frown mattered.","Basara — Qiyāmah 75:24 — wa wujūhun yawmaʾidhin bāsirah — \"and faces that Day will be SCOWLING.\" His scowl now (74:22) → his face scowling on the Day (75:24). The expression he chose becomes the one he's stuck with.","And busr = unripe dates — sour, harsh.","His face soured like unripe fruit.","The progression: thought (v.18) → looked (v.21) → FROWNED and SCOWLED (v.22).","The face shows he's found the answer he wanted — and hated it.","BASARA → Qiyāmah 75:24: bāsirah — SCOWLING faces on that Day.","The face he chose = the face he keeps.","Busr = UNRIPE dates (sour)."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ أَدْبَرَ وَاسْتَكْبَرَ", tr:"Thumma adbara wa'stakbar", v:"23", root:"Adbara — the \"BACK / rear\" family — dubur = the back; adbara = to TURN ONE'S BACK, to go away.", meaning:"\"Then he turned his back and grew arrogant.\"", magic:"Camera cut #3 — his BODY turns. Two roots, two huge payoffs.", grammar:"Adbara — Form IV; istakbara — Form X (istafʿala = consider oneself X). thumma — the fourth time-cut is coming.", why:"Istakbara — names the true reason behind all the \"thinking\": pride.", note:"CAMERA CUT #3: his BODY turns. Thumma ADBARA — PHARAOH (79:22) and this man (74:23): SAME PHRASE.", more:["Tadbīr = MANAGING by foreseeing the outcome (Nāziʿāt 79:5, al-mudabbirāt); tadabbur = deep reflection on the Qurʾān.","istakbara — Form X of kabura = to CONSIDER ONESELF GREAT, to be arrogant; istikbār = arrogance; mustakbir = an arrogant one (Iblīs: abā wa'stakbara, 2:34!).","ONE — ADBARA, turning the back: Nāziʿāt 79:22 — thumma adbara yasʿā — PHARAOH \"turned his back, hurrying.\" SAME PHRASE \"thumma adbara\" — Pharaoh (79:22) and al-Walīd (74:23). Same posture, same root.","And in this surah's v.33 — wa'l-layli idh adbar — \"by the night when it TURNS ITS BACK.\" The man turns his back on the truth (v.23); the night turns its back — and DAWN comes (v.33–34).","Darkness retreats; the man retreats into darkness.","And the root's opposite meaning: tadabbur = reflecting deeply (looking at the \"back\"/outcome of a verse).","He could have done tadabbur; he did idbār.","TWO — ISTAKBARA, Form X arrogance: v.3 — wa rabbaka fa-KABBIR — \"your Lord — MAGNIFY (Him)!\" v.23 — wa'sTAKBAR — \"he considered HIMSELF great.\" SAME ROOT (k-b-r).","The Prophet ﷺ is told to make ALLAH great; the denier makes HIMSELF great.","And istakbara is IBLĪS'S word — abā wa'stakbara wa kāna mina'l-kāfirīn (2:34) — the first sin, repeated by a Makkan chief.","Form X — the grammar of the ego: istaghnā (I need nothing — 80:5, 92:8, 96:7), istakthara (seek more — 74:6), istakbara (I am great — 74:23). vs.","istaghfara (I need forgiveness — 110:3, 73:20).","v.23 the man turns his back; v.33 the NIGHT turns its back → DAWN.","v.3 KABBIR (make ALLAH great) vs v.23 ISTAKBARA (make YOURSELF great).","IBLĪS's word (2:34). Tadabbur vs idbār."]},
  {ar:"فَقَالَ إِنْ هَٰذَا إِلَّا سِحْرٌ يُؤْثَرُ", tr:"Fa-qāla in hādhā illā siḥrun yuʾthar", v:"24", root:"In… illā — \"this is NOTHING BUT…\" (in = negation, not \"if\").", meaning:"\"And he said: 'This is nothing but magic, handed down [from the ancients].'\"", magic:"Camera cut #4 — he SPEAKS. After all that thinking, calculating, looking, frowning, turning — THIS is his verdict.", grammar:"In… illā = restriction: \"NOTHING BUT\" (in an-nāfiyah).", why:"Siḥr yuʾthar — the \"theory\" he calculated: dismissing the Qurʾān as learned trickery.", note:"CAMERA CUT #4: he SPEAKS — \"nothing but MAGIC, HANDED DOWN.\" Duʿāʾ MAʾTHŪR = a TRANSMITTED supplication — same root.", more:["siḥr — the \"MAGIC / enchantment\" family — siḥr = MAGIC, sorcery; sāḥir = a magician; saḥar = the PRE-DAWN (suḥūr!) — classically linked: the dim hour when things look other than they are.","yuʾthar — the \"TRACE / transmitting / handing down\" family — athar = a trace/footprint; athara = to TRANSMIT, report, hand down; maʾthūr = TRANSMITTED (duʿāʾ maʾthūr = a narrated supplication from the Prophet ﷺ!; at-tafsīr bi'l-maʾthūr = tafsīr by transmitted reports!); āthāra = to prefer (Nāziʿāt 79:38, Aʿlā 87:16).","Siḥrun yuʾthar — \"magic, TRANSMITTED / learned from others.\" His \"theory\" (from v.21's naẓar): Muhammad ﷺ learned sorcery from earlier magicians.","The irony: the charge is maʾthūr — \"HANDED DOWN\" — and the Qurʾān IS handed down — but from Allah, through Jibrīl, not from sorcerers.","He got the transmission right and the source wrong.","The athar root hook: duʿāʾ MAʾTHŪR — a supplication TRANSMITTED from the Prophet ﷺ — the word you use for authentic adhkār.","He called the Qurʾān \"maʾthūr magic\"; Muslims call the Prophet's ﷺ words \"maʾthūr duʿāʾ.\" And āthara (to prefer) — Nāziʿāt 79:38 (wa āthara'l-ḥayāta'd-dunyā) — same root.","He PREFERRED (āthara) his status, so he called it maʾthūr (transmitted) magic.","And in hādhā illā — in = NOT (Takwīr 81:27, Jinn 72:25). Compare: the jinn heard it ONCE and called it qurʾānan ʿajabā — \"a WONDER\" (Jinn 72:1). This man heard it, was moved, thought hard — and called it siḥr.","Same Qurʾān, two verdicts.","yuʾtharu = passive present (\"is transmitted/handed down\").","He got the transmission right, the SOURCE wrong.","Jinn 72:1: heard ONCE → \"a WONDER.\" This man: heard, was moved → \"MAGIC.\" In… illā = NOTHING BUT.","Saḥar (pre-dawn) shares letters with siḥr."]},
  {ar:"إِنْ هَٰذَا إِلَّا قَوْلُ الْبَشَرِ", tr:"In hādhā illā qawlu'l-bashar", v:"25", root:"The \"SKIN / outer surface → HUMANS / good news\" family — bashrah = SKIN (the outer layer of the body!).", meaning:"\"This is nothing but the word of a human being.\"", magic:"THE BEST ROOT-PLAY IN THE SURAH — and it closes in v.29. His second accusation: qawlu'l-BASHAR — \"the word of a mere HUMAN.\" Not divine — just a man's composition.", grammar:"In… illā again (restriction). al-bashar = a collective noun (humans), definite.", why:"Bashar — because the surah will turn the same word back on him in v.29.", note:"BASHRAH = SKIN → BASHAR = HUMANS (named for bare skin). v.25: \"just the word of BASHAR\" (a human).", more:["Bashar = HUMAN BEINGS (named for their visible, bare skin — unlike furred or feathered animals!); bushrā / bishārah = GOOD NEWS (because it shows on your skin/face!); mubāsharatan = DIRECTLY (skin-to-skin contact!).","NOW WATCH THE WORD BASHAR: it appears FOUR times in this surah: v.25 (qawlu'l-bashar — \"just the word of a HUMAN\"), v.29 (lawwāḥatun li'l-bashar — \"it scorches the SKIN\" — Saqar!), v.31 (dhikrā li'l-bashar — \"a reminder for HUMANITY\"), v.36 (nadhīran li'l-bashar — \"a warning to HUMANITY\").","And bashar means BOTH \"humans\" AND \"skin\" (bashrah): v.25 — he calls it \"the word of BASHAR\" (a human).","v.29 — Saqar is lawwāḥatun li'l-BASHAR — \"SCORCHING the bashar\" (the skin / humans)!","He said \"it's just bashar\" — and the reply is a fire that scorches bashar.","His dismissive word is thrown back as his punishment.","And bushrā (good news) — ʿAbasa 80:39 — ḍāḥikatun mustabshirah (faces rejoicing at good news — it shows on the skin!).","Good news glows on the bashrah; Saqar scorches it.","v.29: Saqar SCORCHES the BASHAR (skin).","His dismissive word returns as his punishment.","Bashar ×4 in this surah (v.25, 29, 31, 36). Bushrā = GOOD NEWS (it shows on the skin) — ʿAbasa 80:39."]},
  {ar:"سَأُصْلِيهِ سَقَرَ", tr:"Sa-uṣlīhi saqar", v:"26", root:"Uṣlī — Form IV of ṣaliya = to MAKE someone burn / roast in — the ṣaliya root (not ṣalāh!) — your longest-running Hell-verb.", meaning:"\"I will make him burn in Saqar.\"", magic:"The answer to his slander — \"I will burn him.\" Sa-uṣlīhi — first person singular — matching dharnī (\"leave Me with him,\" v.11) and sa-urhiquhu (\"I'll force him up,\" v.17).", grammar:"Sa- + Form IV present, 1st person + -hi + saqara (2nd object). Saqara = DIPTOTE.", why:"Saqar (the scorcher that changes skin colour) — sets up v.29's \"it scorches the skin.\"", note:"SAQAR = the SCORCHER — from the sun scorching skin until it changes colour.", more:["saqar — a NAME of Hell — from saqara = to SCORCH, for the sun to burn something and change its colour (saqarat-hu'sh-shams = \"the sun scorched him\").","Allah speaks of him personally throughout: I created him (v.11), I gave him (v.12), I smoothed (v.14), I'll burden him (v.17), I'll burn him (v.26).","Five \"I\"s — a personal reckoning.","Saqar — a new Hell-name, from scorching sun — the fire that CHANGES THE COLOUR of the skin (connects to v.29's lawwāḥah — the same idea!).","Hell-names you now own: Jahannam, Jaḥīm (blaze — 79:36, 81:12, 82:14), Saʿīr (flare — 76:4, 84:12), Laẓā (leaping flame — 92:14 talaẓẓā), Hāwiyah (abyss — 101:9), Ḥuṭamah (crusher — 104:4), Saqar (scorcher — 74:26). The ṣaliya root — now in EIGHT surahs: Infiṭār 82:15, Muṭaffifīn 83:16, Inshiqāq 84:12, Aʿlā 87:12, Ghāshiyah 88:4, Layl 92:15, Masad 111:3, Muddaththir 74:26 (Form IV — HE makes him burn).","Saqar is a DIPTOTE (feminine proper noun) — saqara (fatḥah, no tanwīn).","FIVE \"I\"s: I created (v.11), I gave (v.12), I smoothed (v.14), I'll burden (v.17), I'll BURN (v.26).","Hell-names: Jaḥīm, Saʿīr, Laẓā, Hāwiyah, Ḥuṭamah, SAQAR."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ مَا سَقَرُ", tr:"Wa mā adrāka mā saqar", v:"27", root:"The \"knowing by perception\" family — adrāka (past) = \"what could convey to you?\"", meaning:"\"And what could make you know what Saqar is?\"", magic:"The formula you own from NINE surahs — 82:17–18, 83:8, 83:19, 86:2, 90:12, 97:2, 101:3, 101:10, 104:5 — and now 74:27. Adrāka (PAST) = an answer FOLLOWS (v.28–30 describe it); yudrīka (PRESENT, ʿAbasa 80:3) = no answer.", grammar:"Two mā's (interrogative + subject-clause).", why:"The standard Qurʾānic pause before describing something beyond imagination.", note:"TENTH appearance of wa mā adrāka. Adrāka (past) = answer follows; yudrīka (present, 80:3) = no answer.", more:["The pause before the horror."]},
  {ar:"لَا تُبْقِي وَلَا تَذَرُ", tr:"Lā tubqī wa lā tadhar", v:"28", root:"Tubqī — Form IV of baqiya (to remain) = to LEAVE REMAINING, to spare; bāqī = remaining (al-Bāqī = the Everlasting; Aʿlā 87:17 — khayrun wa abqā).", meaning:"\"It leaves nothing [remaining], and it lets nothing be.\"", magic:"Two near-synonyms — and classical scholars distinguish them precisely: lā tubqī = it doesn't leave ANYTHING UNBURNED (no flesh, no bone left over).", grammar:"Two present-tense negations (lā + muḍāriʿ) = habitual, ongoing. tadhar — from the defective verb (no past).", why:"Tubqī / tadhar — two verbs for completeness: nothing spared, and nothing left alone even after.", note:"Lā TUBQĪ = leaves NOTHING UNBURNED. Lā TADHAR = doesn't LET THEM BE (recreates and burns again).", more:["tadhar — the DEFECTIVE verb wadhara = to leave alone, let be (v.11 dharnī!).","Lā tadhar = it doesn't LET THEM BE — when it has consumed them, it doesn't leave them in that state either — they're recreated, and it burns again.","Total consumption + endless repetition.","Compare Aʿlā 87:13 — lā yamūtu fīhā wa lā yaḥyā — neither dies nor lives.","AND THE DHAR PAYOFF WITHIN THE SURAH: v.11 — DHARNĪ wa man khalaqtu waḥīdā — \"LEAVE ME with him.\" v.28 — lā TADHAR — \"it LEAVES nothing alone.\" Same defective verb.","\"Leave him to Me\" (v.11) → \"it leaves nothing alone\" (v.28).","The Prophet ﷺ left him to Allah; Saqar doesn't leave him at all.","And abqā — Aʿlā 87:17: the Hereafter is khayrun wa abqā (better and more lasting).","The Hereafter is what lasts; Saqar is what lets nothing last.","v.11 DHARNĪ (leave him to Me) → v.28 LĀ TADHAR (it leaves nothing alone).","Aʿlā 87:13: neither dies nor lives."]},
  {ar:"لَوَّاحَةٌ لِّلْبَشَرِ", tr:"Lawwāḥatun li'l-bashar", v:"29", root:"Lawwāḥah — the \"APPEARING / flashing / CHANGING COLOUR by heat\" family — lāḥa = to appear, to gleam, to flash.", meaning:"\"It scorches the skin [blackening it].\" (or: \"it looms up / is visible to humanity\")", magic:"THE PAYOFF OF v.25 — and an intensive pattern you've tracked all juzʾ.", grammar:"Faʿʿālah intensive (feminine, agreeing with Saqar). li- = \"to / for / upon\" the skin/humans.", why:"Lawwāḥah + bashar — so the man's dismissive word \"bashar\" (v.25) returns as the thing burned.", note:"V.25 \"just BASHAR\" (a human) → v.29 \"scorches the BASHAR\" (skin). His word, thrown back.", more:["Lawwaḥa = to SCORCH, to blacken/discolour by burning (as the sun darkens skin); lawḥ = a BOARD, a flat TABLET (al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ — the Preserved Tablet, Burūj 85:22!); lawḥah = a PAINTING / a signboard (modern: lawḥat mafātīḥ = a KEYBOARD!; lawḥat al-qiyādah = a dashboard!).","bashar — SKIN / humans (v.25).","v.25 — in hādhā illā qawlu'l-BASHAR — \"this is just the word of a HUMAN.\" v.29 — lawwāḥatun li'l-BASHAR — \"it SCORCHES THE SKIN.\" Bashar = skin (bashrah) AND humans.","He dismissed it as \"bashar\"; Saqar burns his bashar.","Classical scholars give both readings of v.29 — \"scorches the SKIN\" and \"visible to HUMANS\" (it looms over them from afar) — and both work.","Lawwāḥah — the faʿʿālah INTENSIVE — \"one that scorches AGAIN AND AGAIN, habitually.\" The pattern you own: lawwāmah (Qiyāmah 75:2 — the self-blaming soul), wahhāj (Nabaʾ 78:13), thajjāj (78:14), ghassāq (78:25), kidhdhāb (78:28), tawwāb (Naṣr 110:3), ḥammālah (Masad 111:4), khannās (Nās 114:4). Lawwāmah (75:2) and lawwāḥah (74:29) — one letter apart!","The soul that BLAMES itself (lawwāmah) vs.","the fire that SCORCHES (lawwāḥah).","Blame yourself now, or be scorched later.","And the lawḥ (TABLET) connection: Burūj 85:22 — fī lawḥin maḥfūẓ — the Preserved TABLET, where the Qurʾān is kept.","Same root (l-w-ḥ: a flat, visible surface).","He called the Qurʾān magic; the Qurʾān is in the Lawḥ, and Saqar is lawwāḥah.","The modern hook: lawḥat mafātīḥ = a KEYBOARD.","LAWWĀMAH (75:2, self-blaming soul) vs LAWWĀḤAH (74:29, the scorcher) — ONE LETTER apart.","Blame yourself now, or be scorched later.","Al-LAWḤ al-Maḥfūẓ (85:22) — same root; lawḥat mafātīḥ = a KEYBOARD."]},
  {ar:"عَلَيْهَا تِسْعَةَ عَشَرَ", tr:"ʿalayhā tisʿata ʿashar", v:"30", root:"Tisʿah — NINE. ʿashar — TEN (Fajr 89:2 layālin ʿashr; Takwīr 81:4 al-ʿishār — ten-month pregnant camels).", meaning:"\"Over it are nineteen [angels as guardians].\"", magic:"A NUMBER — and the number itself becomes a TEST (v.31). Nineteen keepers over Saqar. The mockers seized on it immediately: it's reported that Abū Jahl scoffed, \"Nineteen?", grammar:"Tisʿata ʿashara — compound numbers 11–19 are INDECLINABLE (mabnī), both parts fixed on fatḥah.", why:"A precise, unexplained number — designed to test whether the hearer submits or scoffs.", note:"NINETEEN guardians — and Abū Jahl MOCKED the number (\"can't ten of you handle each one?\").", more:["tisʿata ʿashar = NINETEEN.","Can't ten of you handle each one of them?\" — and another boasted he alone could take on seventeen.","Which is exactly what v.31 says the number was FOR: wa mā jaʿalnā ʿiddatahum illā fitnatan li'lladhīna kafarū — \"We made their number only a TEST for the disbelievers.\" The number was a mirror: believers accepted it; deniers mocked it — and revealed themselves.","And note the extreme brevity — no explanation, no description of the angels, just a number — leaving it to provoke a response.","(here ʿashar has sukūn for the verse-ending pause).","ʿalayhā FRONTED (\"over it [are]…\").","v.31: the number was a TEST — believers accepted, deniers revealed themselves.","Tisʿata ʿashara — numbers 11–19 are INDECLINABLE.","ʿAshr (ten) — Fajr 89:2, Takwīr 81:4."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا جَعَلْنَا أَصْحَابَ النَّارِ إِلَّا مَلَائِكَةً ۙ وَمَا جَعَلْنَا عِدَّتَهُمْ إِلَّا فِتْنَةً", tr:"Wa mā jaʿalnā aṣḥāba'n-nāri illā malāʾikah · wa mā jaʿalnā ʿiddatahum illā fitnah", v:"31", root:"Aṣḥāb an-nār — the keepers/companions of the Fire (ṣāḥib). malāʾikah — ANGELS (sing.", meaning:"\"And We have made the keepers of the Fire only angels, and We made their number only a test for those who disbelieve…\"", magic:"The longest verse in the surah opens by answering the mockery: ONE — \"only ANGELS\" — not humans you could overpower. Abū Jahl's boast (\"ten of us per guard\") assumed they were men.", grammar:"Mā… illā ×2 = restriction (\"We made them NOTHING BUT angels… their number NOTHING BUT a test\").", why:"Fitnah — reframes the controversy over \"19\" as the very purpose of the number.", note:"\"ONLY ANGELS\" — answers Abū Jahl's boast.", more:["malak; root ʾ-l-k: a messenger — alūkah = a message!).","ʿiddah — the \"COUNTING / number\" family — ʿadad = number (Jinn 72:24, 28; Humazah 104:2 ʿaddadah); ʿiddah = a NUMBER, a count (and in fiqh, ʿiddah = a woman's WAITING PERIOD — a counted number of months!).","fitnah — a TEST (from fatana = putting GOLD IN FIRE to test purity — Jinn 72:17, Burūj 85:10).","They're angels — beyond human strength.","TWO — \"their NUMBER is only a fitnah\" — fitnah = gold in fire.","The number 19 is the FIRE that tests the gold of belief.","Accept a detail you can't fully explain → the faith shows pure; mock it → the dross shows.","The fitnah thread: Burūj 85:10 (fatanu'l-muʾminīn — humans tried believers with literal FIRE), Jinn 72:17 (li-naftinahum fīh — tested by ABUNDANCE), Muddaththir 74:31 (ʿiddatahum illā fitnah — tested by a NUMBER).","Fire, abundance, a number — all fitnah.","And ʿiddah — the ʿadad root you own: Jinn 72:24 (aqallu ʿadadā — fewer in number), 72:28 (aḥṣā… ʿadadā — counted by number).","ʿIddah in fiqh = the WAITING PERIOD — a free, unforgettable hook.","aṣḥāba'n-nār = object of jaʿalnā.","The NUMBER is a FITNAH — gold in fire: accept it, faith shows pure; mock it, the dross shows.","Fitnah thread: FIRE (85:10), ABUNDANCE (72:17), a NUMBER (74:31). ʿIddah = a woman's WAITING PERIOD (fiqh) — same root as ʿadad.","Malak ← alūkah = a MESSAGE."]},
  {ar:"لِيَسْتَيْقِنَ الَّذِينَ أُوتُوا الْكِتَابَ وَيَزْدَادَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِيمَانًا", tr:"Li-yastayqina'lladhīna ūtu'l-kitāba wa yazdāda'lladhīna āmanū īmānā", v:"31", root:"Yastayqin — Form X of yaqina = to become CERTAIN, to seek/attain certainty; yaqīn = CERTAINTY (Takāthur 102:5, 7 — ʿilm al-yaqīn, ʿayn al-yaqīn!).", meaning:"\"…so that the People of the Book may become certain, and the believers may increase in faith…\"", magic:"The same \"test\" (the number 19) produces OPPOSITE results in different people.", grammar:"Yazdāda = Form VIII of zāda — the infixed tāʾ becomes dāl after zāy (yaztād → yazdād) — a regular sound change.", why:"Yazdād — establishes that faith grows — and contrasts with the greed of v.15.", note:"ONE test, THREE results: People of the Book → CERTAINTY; believers → MORE FAITH; deniers → EXPOSED.", more:["yazdād — Form VIII of zāda = to INCREASE (in oneself); the t becomes d after z (yaztād → yazdād).","īmān — FAITH (from amn, security).","For the People of the Book — li-yastayqina — classical: their own scriptures mentioned the number, so hearing it from an unlettered prophet confirmed his truth to them — CERTAINTY.","For the believers — yazdāda… īmānā — they accept it without needing explanation — FAITH INCREASES.","A key Qurʾānic principle: īmān INCREASES (and decreases) — it isn't a fixed quantity.","For the deniers — fitnah (previous phrase) — it exposes them.","Yaqīn — the Takāthur ladder: ʿilm al-yaqīn (told), ʿayn al-yaqīn (seen), ḥaqq al-yaqīn (experienced).","Here the People of the Book reach yaqīn — and in v.47 the deniers reach al-yaqīn in its other sense: DEATH.","Yazdād — the zāda root: v.15 — yaṭmaʿu an AZĪD — the greedy man wanted MORE wealth.","v.31 — yazdāda… ĪMĀNĀ — the believers get MORE faith.","Same root: he craved more money; they received more faith.","īmānan = tamyīz (\"increase in faith\").","Īmān INCREASES (a core principle).","v.15: he wanted MORE wealth (azīd).","v.31: they got MORE faith (yazdād).","Yaztād → yazdād (t → d after z).","Yaqīn — Takāthur's ladder."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا يَرْتَابَ", tr:"Wa lā yartāba", v:"31", root:"The \"DOUBT / suspicion / unease\" family — rayb = DOUBT (lā rayba fīh.", meaning:"\"…and so that the People of the Book and the believers will not doubt…\"", magic:"You know this word from the Qurʾān's very first description of itself: dhālika'l-kitābu lā RAYBA fīh — \"This is the Book.", grammar:"Form VIII (irtāba), hollow. lā + subjunctive (joined to li- of purpose).", why:"Rayb — echoes the Qurʾān's foundational claim of itself: no doubt.", note:"Lā RAYBA fīh — Baqarah 2:2, the Qurʾān's first self-description. Same root. Rayb = doubt WITH unease.", more:["\"no doubt in it,\" the Qurʾān's opening description, 2:2!); irtāba (Form VIII) = to fall into doubt, to become suspicious; murīb = disquieting; rībah = suspicion.","NO DOUBT in it\" (Baqarah 2:2). Here the verb form: lā yartāba — \"so they won't fall into doubt.\" Rayb isn't just intellectual doubt — it carries UNEASE, a disturbed heart.","The 19 settles the believers instead of disturbing them.","The pairing: li-yastayqina (to become CERTAIN) + lā yartāba (not to DOUBT) — affirmation + negation = complete certainty.","Same technique as tasbīḥ (negating flaws) + ḥamd (affirming perfections) in Naṣr 110:3.","CERTAIN (yastayqin) + NOT doubting (lā yartāb) = complete certainty."]},
  {ar:"وَلِيَقُولَ الَّذِينَ فِي قُلُوبِهِم مَّرَضٌ وَالْكَافِرُونَ مَاذَا أَرَادَ اللَّهُ بِهَٰذَا مَثَلًا", tr:"Wa li-yaqūla'lladhīna fī qulūbihim maraḍun wa'l-kāfirūna mādhā arāda'llāhu bi-hādhā mathalā", v:"31", root:"Maraḍ — DISEASE (marīḍ = a patient — Muzzammil 73:20 marḍā!).", meaning:"\"…and so that those in whose hearts is a disease, and the disbelievers, will say: 'What did Allah intend by this as an example?'\"", magic:"The fourth group's response — and it's a QUESTION dressed as mockery.", grammar:"Mādhā = \"what?\" (interrogative, emphatic). mathalan = tamyīz or ḥāl (\"as an example\").", why:"Maraḍ (disease) — diagnoses resistance to truth as a sickness, not a reasoned position.", note:"MARAḌ in the HEART = the Qurʾān's term for HYPOCRISY (2:10). Muzzammil 73:20: BODY-illness EXCUSED.", more:["qulūb — HEARTS (qalb — from qalaba = to TURN OVER — the heart is the thing that turns!).","mathal — the \"LIKENESS / example\" family — mathal = a PARABLE, an example, a saying (amthāl = proverbs!; mithl = like; tamthīl = acting / representation; timthāl = a statue).","Mādhā arāda'llāhu bi-hādhā mathalā — \"what did Allah MEAN by this example?\" — scoffing at the number 19 as if it were a pointless riddle.","Maraḍun fī qulūbihim — DISEASE IN THE HEART.","Muzzammil 73:20 — ʿalima an sa-yakūnu minkum marḍā — the physically SICK, excused from long night prayer.","Muzzammil: BODY-illness — EXCUSED with mercy.","Muddaththir: HEART-illness — EXPOSED by a test.","Same root, two kinds of patient.","This is the Qurʾān's term for the HYPOCRITES — fī qulūbihim maraḍun fa-zādahumu'llāhu maraḍā (2:10). Notable: this surah is Makkan, yet already names those with \"diseased hearts.\" And qalb — from qalaba = to TURN OVER (inqilāb = a coup / revolution!) — the heart is named for its tendency to TURN.","A diseased heart turns away.","Mathal — amthāl = PROVERBS; the Qurʾān is full of amthāl — and the diseased heart asks \"why this example?\" instead of learning from it.","li-yaqūla = lām of consequence (\"so that [the result is] they say\").","Muddaththir 74:31: HEART-illness EXPOSED.","Qalb ← qalaba = to TURN (inqilāb = a COUP) — the heart TURNS.","Amthāl = PROVERBS; timthāl = a STATUE."]},
  {ar:"كَذَٰلِكَ يُضِلُّ اللَّهُ مَن يَشَاءُ وَيَهْدِي مَن يَشَاءُ", tr:"Kadhālika yuḍillu'llāhu man yashāʾu wa yahdī man yashāʾ", v:"31", root:"Yuḍillu — Form IV of ḍalla (to go astray) = to LET GO ASTRAY / leave astray; ḍāllah = a LOST CAMEL (Ḍuḥā 93:7 ḍāllan; Fīl 105:2 taḍlīl).", meaning:"\"Thus does Allah leave astray whom He wills and guide whom He wills.\"", magic:"Kadhālika — \"THUS / IN THIS WAY.\" The same number (19) GUIDES some and leaves others ASTRAY. The test is identical; the hearts differ.", grammar:"Kadhālika = \"likewise / in this way.\" Form IV aḍalla (doubled root — yuḍillu with shaddah).", why:"One sentence showing the same event splitting hearts in two directions.", note:"KADHĀLIKA = \"THUS\" — the SAME test GUIDES some, leaves others ASTRAY.", more:["yashāʾ — WILLS (mashīʾah).","And classical scholars stress: Allah leaves astray those who CHOSE to turn away first — fa-lammā zāghū azāgha'llāhu qulūbahum (\"when they swerved, Allah made their hearts swerve,\" 61:5). The fitnah reveals; it doesn't create.","The ḍalla thread: Ḍuḥā 93:7 — wa wajadaka ḍāllan fa-hadā (found you searching → guided); Fīl 105:2 — kaydahum fī taḍlīl (their plot sent astray); Muddaththir 74:31 — yuḍillu … yahdī.","And the pair yuḍillu / yahdī — a ṭibāq in one breath.","Man yashāʾ — compare the surah's end: v.55–56 — fa-man shāʾa dhakarah · wa mā yadhkurūna illā an yashāʾa'llāh — human will inside divine will (Takwīr 81:28–29).","Parallel clauses with opposite verbs (ṭibāq).","The fitnah REVEALS; it doesn't create (61:5: \"when they swerved, He made their hearts swerve\").","Ḍalla: Ḍuḥā 93:7 (searching → guided), Fīl 105:2 (plot astray)."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا يَعْلَمُ جُنُودَ رَبِّكَ إِلَّا هُوَ", tr:"Wa mā yaʿlamu junūda rabbika illā huwa", v:"31", root:"Junūd — the \"SOLDIERS / armies\" family — jund = an ARMY; jundī = a SOLDIER (everyday Arabic!).", meaning:"\"And none knows the armies of your Lord except Him.\"", magic:"The final answer to the mockers of \"nineteen\": \"You think nineteen is all there is?", grammar:"Mā… illā huwa = restriction (\"none but He\"). junūda = object, broken plural of jund.", why:"Junūd (armies) — turns the debate about 19 into a glimpse of infinite forces.", note:"JUNDĪ = a SOLDIER. \"Nineteen? NOBODY knows His armies but Him.\" Burūj 85:17: tyrants' ARMIES — counted, destroyed.", more:["Tajnīd = conscription / recruitment; Burūj 85:17 — hal atāka ḥadīthu'l-junūd (the armies of Pharaoh and Thamūd!).","NOBODY knows the armies of your Lord but Him.\" Nineteen guard Saqar — but His soldiers are beyond counting.","Recall Jinn 72:8 — the sky muliʾat ḥarasan shadīdā — FILLED with strong guards — and Fīl 105:3 — ṭayran abābīl — birds as an army.","His junūd include angels, birds, wind, water, meteors — anything.","And Burūj 85:17 — hal atāka ḥadīthu'l-junūd · firʿawna wa thamūd — human \"armies\" (Pharaoh, Thamūd) — destroyed.","Burūj: the ARMIES of tyrants.","Muddaththir: the ARMIES of your Lord.","One is counted and destroyed; the other is uncountable.","Illā huwa — \"except HIM\" — restriction: knowledge of the unseen belongs to Him alone (Jinn 72:26 — fa-lā yuẓhiru ʿalā ghaybihi aḥadā).","74:31: your Lord's ARMIES — uncountable.","His soldiers: angels, birds (105:3), meteors (72:8)… Jinn 72:26: the unseen is His alone."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا هِيَ إِلَّا ذِكْرَىٰ لِلْبَشَرِ", tr:"Wa mā hiya illā dhikrā li'l-bashar", v:"31", root:"Dhikrā — a REMINDER (the dhikr thread). bashar — HUMANITY (and skin — v.25, 29).", meaning:"\"And it is nothing but a reminder for humanity.\"", magic:"The longest verse ends gently — on dhikrā.", grammar:"Mā… illā = restriction. dhikrā = fuʿlā verbal noun (weak-final).", why:"Dhikrā — reframes everything frightening as an act of reminding mercy.", note:"After ALL that — \"it's ONLY a REMINDER.\" Terror in the service of mercy.", more:["After the number, the test, the doubt, the disease, the armies — \"it is ONLY a REMINDER.\" Hiya = \"it\" — classical: Saqar (the description of it), or the verses, or the number.","The whole terrifying passage exists to REMIND, not merely to frighten.","And li'l-BASHAR — the third of four bashars: v.25 (the \"word of bashar\" — dismissive), v.29 (scorches the bashar — punishment), v.31 (a reminder for bashar — mercy), v.36 (a warning for bashar).","The same word: an insult, a punishment, a mercy, a warning.","The dhikr thread is the juzʾ's spine: Insān 76:29, ʿAbasa 80:11, Takwīr 81:27, Aʿlā 87:9, Ghāshiyah 88:21, Fajr 89:23, Muzzammil 73:19, Nās 114:4 (the khannās who flees dhikr).","And this surah's own end: v.49, 54–56.","Bashar ×4: INSULT (v.25), PUNISHMENT (v.29), MERCY (v.31), WARNING (v.36).","The dhikr spine of the juzʾ."]},
  {ar:"كَلَّا وَالْقَمَرِ", tr:"Kallā wa'l-qamar", v:"32", root:"Qamar — the MOON (qamarī = lunar; at-taqwīm al-qamarī = the LUNAR calendar — the Hijrī calendar!; qamar ṣināʿī = a SATELLITE — \"artificial moon\"!).", meaning:"\"No! By the moon…\"", magic:"Kallā — \"NO!\" — a stern rejection of the mockery in v.31 (\"what did Allah mean by this?\"). Then an oath.", grammar:"Kallā + oath-wāw (genitive al-qamari).", why:"The moon — light borrowed in darkness: the right witness for a \"reminder.\"", note:"Kallā — NO! to the mockers; then an oath by the MOON. Oaths move DARK → LIGHT: moon, night retreating, dawn shining.", more:["And the three oaths (moon, night, dawn) describe a TRANSITION — the moon (light in darkness) → the night retreating → dawn shining.","The movement of these oaths is from darkness toward LIGHT — exactly what the \"reminder\" (v.31) offers.","Moon-mentions you own: Qiyāmah 75:8–9 — wa khasafa'l-qamar · wa jumiʿa'sh-shamsu wa'l-qamar (the moon eclipsed on that Day); Shams 91:2 — wa'l-qamari idhā talāhā (the moon follows/recites the sun).","Here: the moon as witness.","Modern hook: qamar ṣināʿī = a SATELLITE.","Qamar ṣināʿī = a SATELLITE.","Shams 91:2: the moon RECITES the sun."]},
  {ar:"وَاللَّيْلِ إِذْ أَدْبَرَ", tr:"Wa'l-layli idh adbar", v:"33", root:"Adbara — the \"BACK / turning away\" family — to TURN ONE'S BACK, to depart (dubur = the back; tadbīr, tadabbur).", meaning:"\"…and the night when it turns its back [departs]…\"", magic:"THE SAME VERB AS v.23 — and the parallel is deliberate. v.23 — thumma ADBARA wa'stakbar — the MAN turned his back (on the truth).", grammar:"Idh + past = \"when [it did].\" Variant recitation idhā.", why:"Adbara — so the night's departure answers the man's departure.", note:"V.23: the MAN turned his back (adbara) → into darkness. v.33: the NIGHT turns its back (adbara) → DAWN.", more:["v.33 — wa'l-layli idh ADBAR — the NIGHT turns its back (and leaves).","When the NIGHT turns its back, DAWN comes (v.34).","When the MAN turns his back, he walks INTO darkness.","Darkness retreats from the world; this man retreats into it.","Same verb — two departures, opposite results.","And Pharaoh — Nāziʿāt 79:22 — thumma adbara yasʿā — the same \"turning back.\" Three adbaras: Pharaoh, al-Walīd, the night.","The night-verb thread — now SEVEN surahs: Takwīr 81:17 (ʿasʿas — creeping), Fajr 89:4 (yasr — travelling), Shams 91:4 (yaghshāhā — covering), Layl 92:1 (yaghshā — covering), Ḍuḥā 93:2 (sajā — going still), Falaq 113:3 (waqab — settling as threat), Muddaththir 74:33 (adbar — turning its back).","Note: idh (with past = \"when it turned\") — a recognized recitation also has idhā adbar (\"when it turns\") — both authentic.","Same verb, opposite results.","THREE adbaras: Pharaoh (79:22), al-Walīd (74:23), the night (74:33). Night-verbs: SEVEN surahs."]},
  {ar:"وَالصُّبْحِ إِذَا أَسْفَرَ", tr:"Wa'ṣ-ṣubḥi idhā asfar", v:"34", root:"ṣubḥ — MORNING / dawn (Ṣalāt aṣ-Ṣubḥ; Takwīr 81:18, ʿĀdiyāt 100:3). asfara — the \"UNCOVERING / shining forth\" family.", meaning:"\"…and the dawn when it shines forth.\"", magic:"THE BEST CROSS-SURAH ROOT IN THE OATHS — you own it twice in ʿAbasa. ʿAbasa 80:15 — bi-aydī safarah — the noble SCRIBES carrying revelation.", grammar:"Idhā + past = \"when it [does]\" — habitual. Form IV (asfara — to shine / enter the light).", why:"Asfara (unveiling) — the night was a covering; dawn removes it.", note:"S-F-R: safarah (SCRIBES, 80:15), musfirah (SHINING faces, 80:38), asfara (DAWN shining, 74:34). Carriers of light, the light, and faces lit by it.", more:["Safara = to uncover, to unveil (a woman's sufūr = uncovering the face); asfara = to SHINE, to become bright (dawn unveiling itself); safar = TRAVEL (it uncovers people's character!); safarah = SCRIBES (ʿAbasa 80:15!); musfirah = SHINING faces (ʿAbasa 80:38!).","ʿAbasa 80:38 — wujūhun yawmaʾidhin musfirah — faces SHINING on that Day.","Muddaththir 74:34 — wa'ṣ-ṣubḥi idhā asfar — the dawn SHINING forth.","One root (s-f-r): the scribes who carry the light, the DAWN that brings the light, and the FACES that shine with it.","And safar = TRAVEL — classically \"because travel UNCOVERS people's true character.\" Dawn uncovers the earth; travel uncovers the man.","And the oath sequence completes: moon (v.32) → night turning away (v.33) → dawn UNVEILING (v.34).","From borrowed light to full light.","The \"reminder\" (v.31) is a dawn: darkness retreats when it arrives.","Asfara vs Takwīr 81:18 — wa'ṣ-ṣubḥi idhā tanaffas (dawn when it BREATHES).","Two dawns: one breathing, one unveiling.","Safar = TRAVEL (it UNCOVERS character).","Takwīr 81:18: dawn BREATHES."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّهَا لَإِحْدَى الْكُبَرِ", tr:"Innahā la-iḥda'l-kubar", v:"35", root:"Iḥdā — \"ONE OF\" (feminine of aḥad; the w-ḥ-d root — aḥad, Ikhlāṣ 112:1!). kubar.", meaning:"\"Indeed, it [Saqar] is one of the greatest [calamities].\"", magic:"The oath's ANSWER: \"it is ONE OF THE GREAT ONES.\" Hiya = Saqar (most read) — one of the gravest of all great matters.", grammar:"Inna + emphatic lām (la-iḥdā) = double emphasis. iḥdā = feminine of aḥad (agreeing with Saqar).", why:"Kubar — places Saqar among the very greatest matters, answering the mockery of \"nineteen.\"", note:"K-B-R ×3 in this surah: v.3 KABBIR (magnify Allah), v.23 ISTAKBARA (made himself great), v.35 AL-KUBAR (the great calamities).", more:["Plural of kubrā (feminine superlative of kabīr) = the GREATEST ones, the mightiest calamities (al-kubrā — Nāziʿāt 79:20 al-āyata'l-kubrā, 79:34 aṭ-ṭāmmatu'l-kubrā, Aʿlā 87:12 an-nāra'l-kubrā).","And al-kubar — the PLURAL of kubrā — \"the great ones\" — there are many enormous things (the Day, the Trumpet, the Reckoning), and this is one of them.","The k-b-r root in THIS surah: v.3 — fa-KABBIR (magnify your Lord), v.23 — wa'sTAKBAR (he grew arrogant), v.35 — iḥda'l-KUBAR (one of the GREAT calamities).","Magnify Allah (v.3) — or make yourself great (v.23) and face one of the GREAT ones (v.35).","Three k-b-rs, one surah: the choice and its consequence.","The kubrā thread: the greatest SIGN (79:20), the greatest OVERWHELMING (79:34), the greatest FIRE (87:12), one of the greatest (74:35).","al-kubar = broken plural of kubrā.","The choice and its consequence.","Kubrā: 79:20, 79:34, 87:12, 74:35. Iḥdā = feminine of AḤAD."]},
  {ar:"نَذِيرًا لِّلْبَشَرِ", tr:"Nadhīran li'l-bashar", v:"36", root:"Nadhīr — a WARNER / a warning (v.2 andhir; nadhr = a vow). bashar — HUMANITY (the fourth bashar).", meaning:"\"…as a warning to humanity.\"", magic:"The surah's opening command returns — as a NOUN.", grammar:"Nadhīran = accusative (ḥāl or tamyīz). faʿīl pattern (here = mundhir, one who warns / a warning).", why:"Nadhīr — closes a loop from v.2's command, and gives humanity the final bashar.", note:"V.2 ANDHIR (rise and WARN — the command) → v.36 NADHĪRAN (a WARNING — the content).", more:["v.2 — qum fa-ANDHIR — \"rise and WARN.\" v.36 — NADHĪRAN li'l-bashar — \"a WARNING to humanity.\" The Prophet ﷺ was told to warn (v.2); the warning itself is described (v.36).","The command and its content.","And the FOURTH bashar: v.25 (insult: \"just bashar\"), v.29 (punishment: scorches the bashar), v.31 (mercy: a reminder for bashar), v.36 (warning: a nadhīr for bashar).","The same word used against them, and for them.","Nadhīran — grammatically debated: a ḥāl describing Saqar (\"as a warning\"), or describing the Prophet ﷺ (connected to v.2's \"rise and warn\"), or an accusative of purpose.","All classical — the meaning holds.","FOUR bashars: insult (25), punishment (29), mercy (31), warning (36). Jahāz indhār = an ALARM."]},
  {ar:"لِمَن شَاءَ مِنكُمْ أَن يَتَقَدَّمَ أَوْ يَتَأَخَّرَ", tr:"Li-man shāʾa minkum an yataqaddama aw yataʾakhkhar", v:"37", root:"Yataqaddam — Form V of qadima = to step FORWARD, advance (taqaddum = PROGRESS!; qaddama.", meaning:"\"…to whoever among you wills to advance or to lag behind.\"", magic:"THE OPEN DOOR — and two threads close in one verse.", grammar:"Two Form V subjunctives (after an) — reflexive: YOU move yourself. aw = \"or\" (genuine alternatives).", why:"Taqaddum / taʾakhkhur — frames faith as movement — every person is either advancing or falling back; no standing still.", note:"Takwīr 81:28: li-man shāʾa minkum an YASTAQĪM. Muddaththir 74:37: li-man shāʾa minkum an YATAQADDAMA aw YATAʾAKHKHAR.", more:["Qiyāmah 75:13, Infiṭār 82:5, Fajr 89:24, Muzzammil 73:20). yataʾakhkhar — Form V of akhkhara = to fall BEHIND, lag back (taʾakhkhur = DELAY / being late!; ākhir = last; ākhirah).","ONE — \"li-man shāʾa minkum\" — the open-door formula: Takwīr 81:28 — li-man shāʾa minkum an yastaqīm (whoever wills to go straight).","Muddaththir 74:37 — li-man shāʾa minkum an yataqaddama aw yataʾakhkhar (whoever wills to advance or fall back).","IDENTICAL opening — \"li-man shāʾa minkum an…\"!","Takwīr gives ONE option (go straight); Muddaththir gives TWO (forward or back).","TWO — qaddama / akhkhara — the \"sent ahead / left behind\" pair: Qiyāmah 75:13 — bimā qaddama wa akhkhar Infiṭār 82:5 — mā qaddamat wa akhkharat Muddaththir 74:37 — an yataqaddama aw yataʾakhkhar.","Qiyāmah & Infiṭār: what your DEEDS sent forward/left behind.","Muddaththir: whether YOU YOURSELF step forward or fall behind.","Toward the reminder (v.31), toward faith — or fall behind into Saqar.","Taqaddum = PROGRESS; taʾakhkhur = LATENESS.","Spiritual progress or spiritual lateness.","Qaddama/akhkhara thread: DEEDS sent forward (75:13, 82:5) → YOU step forward (74:37). TAQADDUM = PROGRESS; TAʾAKHKHUR = LATENESS."]},
  {ar:"كُلُّ نَفْسٍ بِمَا كَسَبَتْ رَهِينَةٌ", tr:"Kullu nafsin bi-mā kasabat rahīnah", v:"38", root:"Kasabat — the \"EARNING / acquiring\" family (Masad 111:2 wa mā kasab; Muṭaffifīn 83:14 mā kānū yaksibūn).", meaning:"\"Every soul, for what it has earned, is held in pledge.\"", magic:"Rahīnah = COLLATERAL / a HOSTAGE — something held until a debt is paid. Every soul is HELD — pawned — against what it earned. Your deeds are the debt; YOU are the collateral.", grammar:"Kullu nafsin = \"every soul\" (universal). bi-mā kasabat FRONTED (\"for what it earned — held\").", why:"Rahīnah (collateral/hostage) — completes the debt-language of yawm ad-dīn: you are held until you're paid out.", note:"RAHN = a MORTGAGE / COLLATERAL; RAHĪNAH = a HOSTAGE. Every soul is HELD IN PAWN against what it earned.", more:["rahīnah — the \"PLEDGE / collateral / holding as security\" family — rahana = to pledge, to give as security; rahn = a PLEDGE, COLLATERAL (rahn = a MORTGAGE — modern banking!); rahīnah = a HOSTAGE (modern: rahāʾin = hostages!); murtahan = held in pawn.","THE MOST VIVID LEGAL METAPHOR IN THE JUZʾ.","Until the account is settled, you're not released.","And this connects to the deepest legal thread in your collection: yawm ad-dīn — Settlement Day — from dayn = a DEBT (Infiṭār 82:9, Tīn 95:7, Māʿūn 107:1). The Day of settling DEBTS — and every soul is held as COLLATERAL against its own.","The financial picture is complete: debt (dayn), collateral (rahn), settlement (dīn).","And kasabat — \"what it EARNED\": Masad 111:2 — mā aghnā ʿanhu māluhu wa mā kasab (what he earned availed him nothing).","Here: what you earned is what you're HELD by.","Muṭaffifīn 83:14 — bal rāna ʿalā qulūbihim mā kānū yaksibūn (what they earned RUSTED their hearts).","And the escape is in the very next verse (v.39): illā aṣḥāba'l-yamīn — \"EXCEPT the people of the right hand\" — they're RELEASED from pledge.","Their good deeds paid the debt.","Modern hooks: rahn = a MORTGAGE; rahāʾin = HOSTAGES.","rahīnah = faʿīlah (here = marhūnah, \"pledged\") — feminine to agree with nafs.","The financial picture: DAYN (debt) → RAHN (collateral) → DĪN (settlement day).","Masad 111:2: what he EARNED availed nothing; here: what you EARNED holds you.","v.39: the right-handed are RELEASED."]},
  {ar:"إِلَّا أَصْحَابَ الْيَمِينِ", tr:"Illā aṣḥāba'l-yamīn", v:"39", root:"The \"RIGHT HAND / blessing / good fortune\" family — yamīn = the RIGHT HAND (and an OATH, sworn with the right hand!).", meaning:"\"Except the companions of the right hand.\"", magic:"v.38: every soul is HELD as collateral. v.39: EXCEPT the people of the right — their debt is paid; they're free.", grammar:"Illā = exception from kullu nafsin (v.38). aṣḥāba = accusative (exception).", why:"Yamīn — the right hand of blessing, now the hand that frees.", note:"V.38: everyone HELD IN PAWN. v.39: EXCEPT the right-handed — RELEASED.", more:["Yumn = BLESSING; al-Yaman = YEMEN (the land on the right, facing east!); maymanah (Balad 90:18!).","Classical: \"the people of the right\" are released because their good deeds redeemed them — the collateral returned to its owner.","The \"right hand\" thread: Inshiqāq 84:7 — fa-ammā man ūtiya kitābahu bi-yamīnih (given his record in his RIGHT hand → ḥisāban yasīrā, an easy reckoning).","Balad 90:18 — ulāʾika aṣḥābu'l-maymanah (the companions of the RIGHT/blessing).","Muddaththir 74:39 — aṣḥāba'l-yamīn (released from PLEDGE).","Easy reckoning (Inshiqāq), blessed company (Balad), released collateral (Muddaththir).","And the Quraysh geography (106:2): al-Yaman (right) and ash-Shām (left) — the winter and summer caravans ran between the right and the left.","Right-hand thread: EASY reckoning (84:7), BLESSED company (90:18), RELEASED from pledge (74:39). Al-YAMAN = Yemen (land of the RIGHT)."]},
  {ar:"فِي جَنَّاتٍ يَتَسَاءَلُونَ · عَنِ الْمُجْرِمِينَ", tr:"Fī jannātin yatasāʾalūn · ʿani'l-mujrimīn", v:"40–41", root:"Jannāt — GARDENS (janna = to cover).", meaning:"\"In gardens, they will ask one another — about the criminals:\"", magic:"Nabaʾ 78:1 (the first surah of Juzʾ ʿAmma) — ʿamma yatasāʾalūn — \"About what are they ASKING EACH OTHER?\" — the deniers in Makkah, buzzing in confusion about the Resurrection.", grammar:"Form VI (tafāʿala) = MUTUAL (asking EACH OTHER).", why:"Yatasāʾalūn — so the reader hears Nabaʾ's opening question, now asked from the other side.", note:"NABAʾ 78:1: DENIERS yatasāʾalūn (ask each other) about the Day. MUDDATHTHIR 74:40: BELIEVERS yatasāʾalūn about the deniers.", more:["yatasāʾalūn — Form VI of saʾala = to ASK ONE ANOTHER (Nabaʾ 78:1 — ʿamma yatasāʾalūn!).","mujrimīn — the \"CRIME / cutting off\" family — jarama = to cut off (fruit from the tree) → to commit a crime; jarīmah = a CRIME (everyday!); mujrim = a CRIMINAL; Muṭaffifīn 83:29 — inna'lladhīna ajramū.","THE FORM VI BOOKEND OF THE ENTIRE JUZʾ.","Muddaththir 74:40 — fī jannātin yatasāʾalūn — \"In gardens, they ASK EACH OTHER\" — the people of Paradise, curious about the fate of the deniers.","In dunya, the DENIERS asked each other about the Day.","In Paradise, the BELIEVERS ask each other about the deniers.","The questioners swap places.","And mujrim — from jarama = to CUT OFF (fruit) — classical: a crime is \"cutting off\" what belongs to others — or cutting yourself off from what's right.","Jarīmah = a CRIME — your free hook.","And Muṭaffifīn 83:29–34 — inna'lladhīna ajramū kānū mina'lladhīna āmanū yaḍḥakūn — the criminals LAUGHED at the believers in dunya; here the believers ASK about the criminals from Paradise.","The roles have reversed — exactly as Muṭaffifīn promised (fa'l-yawma'lladhīna āmanū mina'l-kuffāri yaḍḥakūn).","ʿani'l-mujrimīn = \"about the criminals.\" The question itself follows in v.42.","The questioners swap places.","JARĪMAH = a CRIME (jarama = to cut off).","Muṭaffifīn 83:29: criminals LAUGHED; now they're ASKED ABOUT."]},
  {ar:"مَا سَلَكَكُمْ فِي سَقَرَ", tr:"Mā salakakum fī saqar", v:"42", root:"The \"THREADING / inserting / making to go along a path\" family — salaka = to thread through, to insert, to make enter.", meaning:"\"'What led you into Saqar?'\"", magic:"The question from Paradise — and the verb is THREADING. Mā SALAKAKUM — literally \"what THREADED you into Saqar?\" Like thread forced through the narrow eye of a needle.", grammar:"Mā = interrogative (\"what?\"). salaka + -kum + fī = \"put you into.\" Saqara = DIPTOTE.", why:"Salaka — makes the descent into Saqar the result of a PATH of conduct (sulūk).", note:"Mā SALAKAKUM = \"what THREADED you in?\" Like thread through a needle's eye. SULŪK = BEHAVIOUR — the answer is four behaviours.", more:["Silk = a THREAD / WIRE (lā-silkī = WIRELESS!); maslak = a path; sulūk = BEHAVIOUR/conduct (the path you walk!); Jinn 72:17, 27 (yaslukhu… yasluku).","And the question is about BEHAVIOUR — sulūk = conduct — \"what PATH of conduct (sulūk) put you on this PATH (maslak) into Saqar?\" The next four verses answer: four behaviours.","The salaka thread: Jinn 72:17 — yaslukhu ʿadhāban ṣaʿadā (He threads him into a climbing punishment); Jinn 72:27 — yasluku min bayni yadayhi… raṣadā (guards threaded around revelation); Muddaththir 74:42 — mā salakakum fī saqar (what threaded you into Saqar?).","Threaded into punishment, threaded around for protection, threaded into Saqar.","Lā-silkī = WIRELESS — your free hook.","Salaka: Jinn 72:17 (into punishment), 72:27 (around revelation), 74:42 (into Saqar)."]},
  {ar:"لَمْ نَكُ مِنَ الْمُصَلِّينَ · وَلَمْ نَكُ نُطْعِمُ الْمِسْكِينَ", tr:"Lam naku mina'l-muṣallīn · wa lam naku nuṭʿimu'l-miskīn", v:"43–44", root:"Naku — \"we were [not]\" — nakun with the nūn DROPPED (a known contraction of kāna in the jussive).", meaning:"\"They will say: 'We were not of those who prayed, nor did we feed the poor…'\"", magic:"THE CONFESSION — and it's SŪRAT AL-MĀʿŪN, spoken from Hell. The first two sins: (1) NOT PRAYING. (2) NOT FEEDING THE POOR.", grammar:"Lam naku = lam nakun with the final nūn DROPPED — a permitted contraction of kāna in the jussive (like Qiyāmah 75:37 a-lam yaku nuṭfatan!).", why:"Prayer + feeding — the confession names exactly the two duties the juzʾ has insisted on.", note:"The CONFESSION = SŪRAT AL-MĀʿŪN spoken from Hell. NOT PRAYING + NOT FEEDING THE POOR.", more:["muṣallīn — those who PRAY (ṣalāh).","nuṭʿimu — we FED (aṭʿama; ṭaʿām).","miskīn — the POOR (one made still, sakana, by poverty).","Compare MĀʿŪN 107 — v.3 wa lā yaḥuḍḍu ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn (doesn't urge feeding the poor) + v.4–5 fa-waylun li'l-muṣallīn · alladhīna hum ʿan ṣalātihim sāhūn (woe to those who pray heedlessly).","MĀʿŪN: praying heedlessly + not urging feeding.","MUDDATHTHIR: not praying at all + not feeding.","The same two sins — Māʿūn names them in dunya; Muddaththir hears them confessed from Saqar.","And Fajr 89:18 — wa lā taḥāḍḍūna ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn — the third surah with the same failure.","And the surahs that PRAISE the opposite: Insān 76:8 — wa yuṭʿimūna'ṭ-ṭaʿāma ʿalā ḥubbihi miskīnan (the righteous feed the poor despite loving the food), Balad 90:14–16 (feeding on a day of hunger = the steep climb).","The miskīn thread runs through FIVE surahs: fed (76:8), climbed for (90:16), neglected (89:18, 107:3), confessed (74:44). And the PAIR is the two pillars: prayer (duty to Allah) + feeding (duty to people) — Bayyinah 98:5 (ṣalāh + zakāh), Kawthar 108:2 (ṣalli + inḥar), Muzzammil 73:20 (aqīmu'ṣ-ṣalāta wa ātu'z-zakāh).","The first two things confessed are the first two pillars broken.","nuṭʿimu = khabar of naku (habitual past).","Miskīn thread: FED (76:8), CLIMBED for (90:16), NEGLECTED (89:18, 107:3), CONFESSED (74:44). The two pillars: prayer (to Allah) + feeding (to people).","Lam NAKU = lam nakun, nūn dropped (like 75:37's yaku)."]},
  {ar:"وَكُنَّا نَخُوضُ مَعَ الْخَائِضِينَ", tr:"Wa kunnā nakhūḍu maʿa'l-khāʾiḍīn", v:"45", root:"The \"WADING INTO water / plunging into\" family — khāḍa = to WADE into water, to plunge in (makhāḍah = a ford, a wading-place); khawḍ = plunging into a matter — classically and in the Qurʾān, especially plunging into IDLE, FALSE, MOCKING TALK (fī khawḍin yalʿabūn — \"playing in their idle chatter,\" 52:12); modern: khāḍa maʿrakah = to WAGE a battle, khāḍa tajribah = to undergo an experience.", meaning:"\"…and we used to plunge [into falsehood and mockery] with those who plunged.\"", magic:"Sin #3 — and the image is WADING INTO WATER. Khawḍ = WADING — you step in, then deeper, then you're swimming in it — idle talk, mockery of the Qurʾān, ridiculing the believers.", grammar:"Kunnā nakhūḍu = habitual past (\"we used to plunge\"). Hollow verb (kh-w-ḍ). maʿa = \"with.\"", why:"Khawḍ (wading) — captures how mockery deepens gradually, and how it's done in a crowd.", note:"KHAWḌ = WADING INTO WATER — step in, go deeper, then you're swimming in falsehood.", more:["And maʿa'l-khāʾiḍīn — \"WITH those who plunged\" — a social sin: they did it because everyone around them did it.","They waded in because the crowd was already in the water.","And recall al-Walīd (v.18–24): he was moved by the Qurʾān — but under pressure from his tribe, he produced the slander.","That's khawḍ maʿa'l-khāʾiḍīn — plunging WITH the plungers.","Contrast the ideal: Naba 78:35 & Ghāshiyah 88:11 — lā yasmaʿūna fīhā laghwan / lā tasmaʿu fīhā lāghiyah — Paradise has NO idle talk.","The khāʾiḍūn loved idle talk; Paradise is defined by its absence.","And Muzzammil 73:7's sabḥ (SWIMMING through the day's duties) — two water-images: SWIMMING in duty vs.","Modern hook: khāḍa maʿrakah = to WAGE A BATTLE — plunging in.","MAʿA'L-KHĀʾIḌĪN = WITH the crowd — PEER PRESSURE confessed.","Like al-Walīd under his tribe's pressure.","Paradise has NO idle talk (78:35, 88:11). SWIMMING in duty (73:7) vs WADING in falsehood (74:45). Khāḍa maʿrakah = WAGE A BATTLE."]},
  {ar:"وَكُنَّا نُكَذِّبُ بِيَوْمِ الدِّينِ", tr:"Wa kunnā nukadhdhibu bi-yawmi'd-dīn", v:"46", root:"Nukadhdhibu — we DENIED / called a lie (Form II of kadhaba).", meaning:"\"…and we used to deny the Day of Reckoning…\"", magic:"Sin #4 — the ROOT of the other three. Why didn't they pray (v.43)? Why didn't they feed the poor (v.44)? Why did they plunge into mockery (v.45)?", grammar:"Kunnā nukadhdhibu = habitual past. kadhdhaba bi- (fixed preposition). yawmi'd-dīn = iḍāfah.", why:"Denial of yawm ad-dīn last — the confession digs down from symptom to cause.", note:"Sin #4 is the ROOT of #1–3: no Settlement Day → no reason to pay. MĀʿŪN 107:1–3: DENIAL first → behaviours.", more:["dīn — here: the RECKONING / settlement (from dayn = a DEBT).","Because they DENIED the Day of Reckoning (v.46).","No Settlement Day → no reason to pay your debts.","The theology is listed LAST because it's the FOUNDATION — the bottom layer under the behaviour.","MĀʿŪN 107:1–3 does it in REVERSE ORDER: a-raʾayta'lladhī yukadhdhibu bi'd-dīn · fa-dhālika'lladhī yaduʿʿu'l-yatīm · wa lā yaḥuḍḍu ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn — \"the one who DENIES the Reckoning — he's the one who shoves the orphan and doesn't urge feeding the poor.\" Māʿūn: DENIAL first → then the behaviours.","Muddaththir: the behaviours first → DENIAL last.","Māʿūn reasons forward (belief → action); Muddaththir confesses backward (action → belief).","Same truth, opposite directions.","Yukadhdhibu bi'd-dīn thread: Infiṭār 82:9 (bal tukadhdhibūna bi'd-dīn), Tīn 95:7 (fa-mā yukadhdhibuka baʿdu bi'd-dīn), Māʿūn 107:1, Muddaththir 74:46. FOUR surahs, one root sin.","Dīn = RECKONING here (dayn, debt) — NOT \"religion\" as in Kāfirūn 109:6. And v.38's rahīnah (collateral) completes it: the debt they denied, they're now held for.","MUDDATHTHIR 74:43–46: behaviours first → DENIAL last.","Forward reasoning vs backward confession.","Yukadhdhibu bi'd-dīn — FOUR surahs: 82:9, 95:7, 107:1, 74:46. Dīn = RECKONING (dayn, debt).","v.38's COLLATERAL completes it."]},
  {ar:"حَتَّىٰ أَتَانَا الْيَقِينُ", tr:"ḥattā atāna'l-yaqīn", v:"47", root:"The \"CERTAINTY\" family — yaqina = to be certain; yaqīn = CERTAINTY.", meaning:"\"…until the Certainty [death] came to us.\"", magic:"THE MOST HAUNTING PHRASE IN THE SURAH — and it closes the yaqīn thread. Ḥattā atāna'l-YAQĪN — \"until the CERTAINTY came to us\" — = until DEATH arrived.", grammar:"ḥattā + past = \"until\" (end-point). al-yaqīn = subject (fāʿil) of atā — death \"comes\" like a visitor.", why:"Al-yaqīn (death as THE certainty) — ironically names the end of their doubt as their death.", note:"AL-YAQĪN = DEATH — the ONE thing everyone is certain of (15:99). They refused certainty by LISTENING (ʿilm al-yaqīn, 102:5) → got certainty by DYING.", more:["And in the Qurʾān, al-yaqīn = DEATH (the one certainty no one doubts) — wa'ʿbud rabbaka ḥattā yaʾtiyaka'l-yaqīn (15:99) — \"worship your Lord until the CERTAINTY [death] comes to you.\"","Why call death \"the Certainty\"?","Because it's the ONE thing every human is certain of — atheist and believer, rich and poor, all KNOW they'll die.","And at death, everything they doubted becomes CERTAIN.","THE YAQĪN LADDER — Takāthur 102:5–7: ʿilm al-yaqīn (certainty by being TOLD), ʿayn al-yaqīn (certainty by SEEING), (ḥaqq al-yaqīn — by EXPERIENCING).","They refused ʿilm al-yaqīn (the reminder told them); they got al-yaqīn itself — death — and then ʿayn al-yaqīn in Saqar.","They could have been certain by LISTENING; they became certain by DYING.","And within this surah: v.31 — li-yastayqina'lladhīna ūtu'l-kitāb — the People of the Book reached CERTAINTY through the reminder.","v.47 — ḥattā atāna'l-yaqīn — the deniers reached it only through DEATH.","Same root, same surah: certainty by revelation vs certainty by death.","Ḥattā — \"UNTIL\" — they kept doing all four sins right up until the last moment.","No repentance — until it was too late (Fajr 89:23 — wa annā lahu'dh-dhikrā, \"what use is remembering now?\").","v.31: People of the Book reached yaqīn through REVELATION.","v.47: deniers reached it through DEATH.","Fajr 89:23: \"what use is remembering NOW?\""]},
  {ar:"فَمَا تَنفَعُهُمْ شَفَاعَةُ الشَّافِعِينَ", tr:"Fa-mā tanfaʿuhum shafāʿatu'sh-shāfiʿīn", v:"48", root:"Tanfaʿu — the \"BENEFIT\" family — nafaʿa = to benefit; nafʿ = benefit; manfaʿah = UTILITY/use (Aʿlā 87:9 — in nafaʿati'dh-dhikrā).", meaning:"\"So the intercession of intercessors will not benefit them.\"", magic:"THE ROOT FROM FAJR'S OATH — and the reason it fails here.", grammar:"Fa-mā = \"so NOT\" (negation + consequence). shafāʿatu = subject; ash-shāfiʿīna = genitive (iḍāfah), plural.", why:"Shafāʿah (pairing) — so the denial of help is felt as total aloneness.", note:"Fajr 89:3: ash-SHAFʿ = EVEN → SHAFĀʿAH = INTERCESSION (someone PAIRS UP with you).", more:["shafāʿah — the \"PAIRING / making even\" family — shafʿ = EVEN (Fajr 89:3 — wa'sh-shafʿi wa'l-watr!); shafāʿah = INTERCESSION — the intercessor \"PAIRS UP\" with you, so you're no longer standing alone; shāfiʿ = an intercessor.","Fajr 89:3 — wa'sh-shafʿi wa'l-watr — \"by the EVEN and the ODD.\" I explained then: shafāʿah (intercession) comes from shafʿ (even/paired) — because the intercessor JOINS you, making a pair, so you don't stand alone.","Here's the payoff: for these people, NO ONE will pair up with them.","Remember v.11 — dharnī wa man khalaqtu WAḤĪDĀ — \"the one I created ALONE.\" Created ALONE (waḥīd, v.11) → and no shafʿ (pair) to join him at the end (v.48).","He came alone and ends alone.","Because they denied the Day (v.46) — intercession on that Day is only by Allah's permission (Nabaʾ 78:38 — illā man adhina lahu'r-Raḥmān), for those He's pleased with.","Nabaʾ 78:38: no one speaks except by permission.","They rejected the very Day on which intercession happens.","Tanfaʿ — Aʿlā 87:9 — fa-dhakkir in nafaʿati'dh-dhikrā (remind, if the reminder benefits).","The reminder could have BENEFITED them in dunya; intercession won't benefit them now.","And note the plural ash-shāfiʿīn — \"ALL the intercessors\" — prophets, angels, the righteous — none of them, together, can help.","Created WAḤĪD — ALONE (v.11) → no shafʿ to pair with him (v.48).","Nabaʾ 78:38: intercession only by PERMISSION.","Aʿlā 87:9: the reminder could have BENEFITED (nafaʿa)."]},
  {ar:"فَمَا لَهُمْ عَنِ التَّذْكِرَةِ مُعْرِضِينَ", tr:"Fa-mā lahum ʿani't-tadhkirati muʿriḍīn", v:"49", root:"Mā lahum — \"WHAT IS WRONG WITH THEM?\" (idiom — Zalzalah 99:3 mā lahā!). tadhkirah — the REMINDER.", meaning:"\"Then what is wrong with them, that they turn away from the reminder?\"", magic:"The surah steps back from Hell to the PRESENT — and asks in astonishment.", grammar:"Fa-mā lahum = \"so what [is the matter] with them\" — astonished question.", why:"Iʿrāḍ — the posture of someone who could face the truth and chooses to stand sideways.", note:"FA-MĀ LAHUM = \"what's WRONG with them?\" Zalzalah 99:3: man asks \"what's wrong with the EARTH?\" Here Allah asks \"what's wrong with THEM?\" Muʿriḍ ʿan dhikr: Jinn 72:17 & Muddaththir 74:49 — same verb, same object.", more:["muʿriḍīn — the \"turning the SIDE / width\" family — aʿraḍa ʿan = to turn away (showing your side, not your face); maʿraḍ = an EXHIBITION; Jinn 72:17 — wa man yuʿriḍ ʿan dhikri rabbih.","After hearing what Saqar holds, after the confession — fa-mā lahum — \"SO WHAT'S WRONG WITH THEM?\" Why would anyone turn away from the very thing that could save them?","Mā lahā — Zalzalah 99:3 — wa qāla'l-insānu mā lahā — man asks \"what's wrong with IT [the earth]?\" Here Allah asks \"what's wrong with THEM?\" The question flips: man questions the earth; Allah questions man.","Muʿriḍīn — Jinn 72:17 — wa man yuʿriḍ ʿan dhikri rabbih — SAME verb + SAME object (the reminder).","Jinn: whoever turns away → a climbing punishment (ṣaʿadā).","Muddaththir: those turning away → the image of fleeing donkeys (next).","And both surahs have the climbing punishment in v.17.","The posture: iʿrāḍ = turning your SIDE (ʿarḍ = width) — standing sideways to the truth.","Aʿlā 87:11 used janb (side) — yatajannabuhā'l-ashqā (the wretched sidesteps it).","muʿriḍīna = ḥāl (accusative): \"[in a state of] turning away.\"","Maʿraḍ = an EXHIBITION (turning the side)."]},
  {ar:"كَأَنَّهُمْ حُمُرٌ مُّسْتَنفِرَةٌ", tr:"Ka-annahum ḥumurun mustanfirah", v:"50", root:"ḥumur — plural of ḥimār = DONKEYS (here wild asses / onagers); NOT ḥumr (plural of aḥmar, RED) — one vowel!", meaning:"\"As if they were alarmed wild donkeys…\"", magic:"The proud chiefs of Quraysh — men like al-Walīd, with wealth and sons (v.12–13) — compared to WILD DONKEYS. Why donkeys?", grammar:"Ka-anna = \"as if\" (simile). ḥumur (donkeys) ≠ ḥumr (red ones) — the ḍammah on the mīm matters.", why:"ḥumur mustanfirah — strips the deniers of dignity: their rejection isn't thought, it's panic.", note:"The proud chiefs = WILD DONKEYS BOLTING in panic. All that \"thinking\" (v.18) → a donkey running.", more:["mustanfirah — Form X of nafara = to BOLT in panic, to flee in alarm, to be stampeded — the root of nafar (Jinn 72:1!); istinfār = (modern) a STATE OF ALERT / MOBILIZATION (ḥālat istinfār = a state of emergency!); nufūr = AVERSION.","THE MOST UNFLATTERING SIMILE IN THE JUZʾ — and it's precise.","In Arab culture, the donkey was the symbol of foolishness and dull stubbornness — and the WILD ass, when startled, bolts in blind panic — no thought, no direction, just RUNNING.","That's how they flee the reminder: not with reasoning, but with mindless, scattered panic.","All of al-Walīd's \"thinking and calculating\" (v.18) — reduced to a donkey bolting.","THE NAFAR ROOT — the best cross-surah link of the section: Jinn 72:1 — istamaʿa nafarun mina'l-jinn — a BAND of jinn RUSHED TOWARD the Qurʾān and listened.","Muddaththir 74:50 — ḥumurun mustanfirah — humans BOLTING AWAY from it.","Same root (n-f-r): jinn rushing IN (72:1); humans bolting OUT (74:50). The jinn heard once and came; the Quraysh heard for years and fled.","Form X istanfara — classical: they're \"stampeded\" — as if something DROVE them to flee, or they themselves stampeding.","Modern istinfār = a STATE OF ALERT — as if the reminder triggered their alarm system.","(And there's a recognized recitation mustanfarah — \"made to flee\" — passive.) And recall Jumuʿah 62:5 — those given the Torah who don't act on it ka-mathali'l-ḥimāri yaḥmilu asfārā — \"like a DONKEY carrying books.\" The donkey: carrying what it doesn't understand, or fleeing what it should understand.","Form X participle (mustanfirah), feminine agreeing with the plural ḥumur.","N-F-R: Jinn 72:1 — a NAFAR of jinn rushed TOWARD the Qurʾān.","Muddaththir 74:50 — humans MUSTANFIRAH, bolting AWAY.","ḤĀLAT ISTINFĀR = a STATE OF ALERT.","ḤUMUR (donkeys) ≠ ḤUMR (red).","Jumuʿah 62:5: a donkey carrying books."]},
  {ar:"فَرَّتْ مِن قَسْوَرَةٍ", tr:"Farrat min qaswarah", v:"51", root:"Farrat — the \"FLEEING in panic\" family — farra = to flee; mafarr = an escape (Qiyāmah 75:10 — ayna'l-mafarr!).", meaning:"\"…fleeing from a lion [or hunters].\"", magic:"The simile completes: wild donkeys bolting — FROM A LION. And the irony is devastating: what are they running FROM? THE REMINDER — the Qurʾān. They treat the cure like a PREDATOR.", grammar:"Farrat — feminine (agreeing with ḥumur), doubled root (f-r-r).", why:"Qaswarah — the most feared predator, chosen to expose how absurdly they misread the reminder.", note:"They flee the REMINDER like donkeys from a LION — treating the CURE as a PREDATOR.", more:["ʿAbasa 80:34 — yawma yafirru'l-marʾu min akhīh.","qaswarah — classically two meanings: (a) a LION; (b) a band of HUNTERS / archers; from qasr = FORCE, compulsion, overpowering (qasran = FORCIBLY — everyday Arabic!; qasrī = compulsory).","The thing coming to SAVE them, they flee as if it were coming to EAT them.","The farra thread — FLEEING: Qiyāmah 75:10 — yaqūlu'l-insānu yawmaʾidhin ayna'l-mafarr (on that Day man will ask \"where is the ESCAPE?\").","ʿAbasa 80:34 — yawma yafirru'l-marʾu min akhīh (the Day a man FLEES from his brother).","Muddaththir 74:51 — farrat min qaswarah (they FLEE the reminder like donkeys from a lion).","They FLEE the reminder NOW (74:51) → on that Day they'll ask \"WHERE is the escape?\" (75:10) — and find none.","They ran from the cure; they'll run from their families; they'll find no exit.","Qaswarah from qasr (FORCE): the lion is the one who overpowers BY FORCE.","Qasran = FORCIBLY — your everyday hook.","But the reminder forces no one — lā ikrāha fi'd-dīn (2:256); Ghāshiyah 88:22 — lasta ʿalayhim bi-muṣayṭir (you're not a controller).","They flee as if from FORCE — from something that never forced them.","min qaswaratin = \"from a lion\" — qaswarah is faʿwalah (an intensive pattern).","FARRA: they FLEE now (74:51) → \"WHERE is the escape?\" (Qiyāmah 75:10) → FLEE from family (ʿAbasa 80:34). QASRAN = FORCIBLY — they flee \"force\" from something that never forced them (2:256, 88:22)."]},
  {ar:"بَلْ يُرِيدُ كُلُّ امْرِئٍ مِّنْهُمْ أَن يُؤْتَىٰ صُحُفًا مُّنَشَّرَةً", tr:"Bal yurīdu kullu'mriʾin minhum an yuʾtā ṣuḥufan munashsharah", v:"52", root:"Imriʾ — a PERSON / a man (al-marʾ — Nabaʾ 78:40, ʿAbasa 80:34). yuʾtā — to BE GIVEN (passive).", meaning:"\"Rather, every one of them wants to be given scrolls spread open [personally addressed to him].\"", magic:"The REAL reason they flee — EGO. Bal — \"RATHER\" — the true motive: each one wants his OWN personal scripture, delivered to HIM, unrolled for HIM.", grammar:"Bal = \"rather\" (correcting to the real reason). yuʾtā = passive subjunctive.", why:"ṣuḥuf munashsharah — exposes the ego behind the excuse, and sets up the ironic fulfilment on Judgment Day.", note:"The REAL reason: EGO — each wanted his OWN personal scripture.", more:["ṣuḥuf — SCROLLS / pages (ṣaḥīfah = a NEWSPAPER; ṣaḥāfah = JOURNALISM).","munashsharah — Form II passive of nashara = SPREAD OPEN, unrolled (nashr = PUBLISHING!; nashrah = a BULLETIN / newsletter; nushirat — Takwīr 81:10!).","Classical: they said, \"we won't believe until each of us gets a letter from heaven addressed to him by name: 'O so-and-so, follow Muhammad.'\" Not \"show us evidence\" — \"make US prophets too.\" The istikbār of v.23 — \"I am great\" — spoken by every one of them.","TWO CROSS-SURAH PAYOFFS — both exact.","ONE — Takwīr 81:10 — wa idha'ṣ-ṣuḥufu nushirat — \"when the SCROLLS are SPREAD OPEN\" (on Judgment Day).","They demanded ṣuḥuf munashsharah (spread-open scrolls) NOW → they'll get ṣuḥuf nushirat (spread-open scrolls) on that Day — but they'll be the records of THEIR OWN DEEDS.","They asked for scrolls; they'll get scrolls.","Just not the kind they wanted.","TWO — Bayyinah 98:2 — rasūlun mina'llāhi yatlū ṣuḥufan muṭahharah — \"a Messenger reciting PURIFIED scrolls.\" Ṣuḥufan MUṬAHHARAH (98:2) vs ṣuḥufan MUNASHSHARAH (74:52) — almost identical phrase!","The scrolls they WERE given (pure, recited by a Messenger) vs the scrolls they DEMANDED (unrolled, addressed to each of them personally).","They rejected the pure scrolls because they wanted personalized ones.","And kullu'mriʾin — \"EVERY single man\" — each one's ego, individually.","Compare Takāthur's takāthur — everyone competing to be the biggest.","Hooks: nashr = PUBLISHING; nashrah = a BULLETIN; ṣaḥīfah = a NEWSPAPER — they wanted their own personal NEWSPAPER from heaven.","munashsharah = Form II passive participle (intensive: fully unrolled).","They DEMANDED ṣuḥuf munashsharah (unrolled scrolls) → TAKWĪR 81:10: on that Day aṣ-ṣuḥufu nushirat — their OWN deed-records unrolled.","They got scrolls — the wrong kind.","Bayyinah 98:2 ṣuḥufan MUṬAHHARAH (given) vs 74:52 ṣuḥufan MUNASHSHARAH (demanded).","Nashr = PUBLISHING; ṣaḥīfah = a NEWSPAPER."]},
  {ar:"كَلَّا ۖ بَل لَّا يَخَافُونَ الْآخِرَةَ", tr:"Kallā bal lā yakhāfūna'l-ākhirah", v:"53", root:"Kallā — NO!", meaning:"\"No! Rather, they do not fear the Hereafter.\"", magic:"The FINAL diagnosis — beneath the ego, beneath the excuses. Kallā — \"NO!\" — their demand for personal scrolls (v.52) is rejected.", grammar:"Kallā bal = \"No! Rather…\" (rejection + correction). lā yakhāfūna = present negation (habitual).", why:"Khawf — names the absence of the most basic response, exposing the root of every excuse.", note:"TWO BALs (v.52–53) dig LAYER BY LAYER: not proof → EGO → they simply don't FEAR the Hereafter.", more:["yakhāfūn — the \"FEAR\" family — khāfa = to fear; khawf = plain FEAR (compare khashyah = awe from knowing — Nāziʿāt 79:19, Aʿlā 87:10, Bayyinah 98:8); makhāfah = fearfulness.","ākhirah — the HEREAFTER (the LAST).","Bal — \"RATHER\" — the real reason (again): they DON'T FEAR the Hereafter.","Two bals in two verses (v.52–53) — digging past each excuse to the next layer: \"it's not that they need proof — RATHER, ego (v.52).","And it's not really ego — RATHER, they simply don't FEAR the Hereafter (v.53).\" Layer by layer, down to the root.","And this is the same root as the confession's last sin: v.46 — wa kunnā nukadhdhibu bi-yawmi'd-dīn — denying the Day.","If you don't fear it, you deny it; if you deny it, you skip prayer, feeding, and wade into mockery.","v.53 is the root under v.43–46.","Khawf vs khashyah: they lack even khawf (plain fear) — let alone khashyah (the awe that comes from knowing).","Nāziʿāt 79:45 — innamā anta mundhiru man yakhshāhā — the warning only lands on those with khashyah.","These lack even the lower rung.","And al-ākhirah vs ākharūn (Muzzammil 73:20 — \"OTHERS\") — one vowel.","v.53 is the root under the confession (v.43–46).","KHAWF (plain fear) < KHASHYAH (awe from knowing) — they lack even the lower rung.","Ākhirah ≠ ākharūn (others)."]},
  {ar:"كَلَّا إِنَّهُ تَذْكِرَةٌ · فَمَن شَاءَ ذَكَرَهُ", tr:"Kallā innahu tadhkirah · fa-man shāʾa dhakarah", v:"54–55", root:"Tadhkirah — a REMINDER. dhakarahu — REMEMBERED it / took heed of it. shāʾa — WILLED.", meaning:"\"No! Indeed, it is a reminder. So whoever wills will remember it.\"", magic:"VERSE 55 IS WORD-FOR-WORD IDENTICAL TO ʿABASA 80:12. ʿAbasa 80:11–12 — kallā innahā tadhkirah · fa-man shāʾa dhakarah Muddaththir 74:54–55 — kallā innahu tadhkirah · fa-man shāʾa dhakarah NEARLY IDENTICAL — two verses!", grammar:"Kallā + innahu (masc. — the Qurʾān).", why:"Repeating ʿAbasa's exact verdict — so the two surahs about rich chiefs end identically.", note:"74:54–55 = ʿABASA 80:11–12, nearly word-for-word (innahā fem. vs innahu masc.).", more:["The only difference: innahā (feminine, ʿAbasa) vs innahu (masculine, Muddaththir) — referring to the verses/surah (fem.) or the Qurʾān (masc.).","Same kallā, same tadhkirah, same fa-man shāʾa dhakarah.","And the ʿAbasa context makes it richer: ʿAbasa: the Prophet ﷺ was corrected for prioritizing the rich chiefs — \"it's a REMINDER; whoever wants it, takes it\" (no need to chase the arrogant).","Muddaththir: the rich chief al-Walīd fled it like a donkey — \"it's a REMINDER; whoever wants it, takes it.\" Both surahs end their argument the same way: the reminder doesn't need the powerful; it's there for whoever WILLS.","Fa-man shāʾa — the open door — now in SEVEN surahs: Insān 76:29, Nabaʾ 78:39, ʿAbasa 80:12, Takwīr 81:28, Muzzammil 73:19, Muddaththir 74:37, 74:55. Even after Saqar, the confession, and the fleeing donkeys — the door is open.","And the dhikr thread — the juzʾ's spine — closes this surah too: v.31 (dhikrā li'l-bashar), v.49 (ʿani't-tadhkirah), v.54 (tadhkirah), v.55 (dhakarah), v.56 (yadhkurūn).","FIVE dhikr-words in the last 26 verses.","fa-man = conditional (\"whoever\"); dhakarahu = past in a condition = future/general meaning.","Both surahs about RICH CHIEFS end: \"it's a REMINDER — whoever WILLS takes it.\" Fa-man shāʾa — SEVEN surahs now.","FIVE dhikr-words in the last 26 verses."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا يَذْكُرُونَ إِلَّا أَن يَشَاءَ اللَّهُ", tr:"Wa mā yadhkurūna illā an yashāʾa'llāh", v:"56", root:"Yadhkurūn — they REMEMBER / take heed. yashāʾa — WILLS (mashīʾah = will; inshāʾallāh — \"if Allah wills\"!).", meaning:"\"And they will not remember unless Allah wills.\"", magic:"Human will (v.55) placed inside divine will (v.56) — the same balance as Takwīr and Insān. v.55 — fa-man SHĀʾA dhakarah — whoever WILLS (human choice — real).", grammar:"Mā… illā = restriction. an yashāʾa = subjunctive.", why:"Closing the open door of v.55 inside divine will — so the reader neither despairs nor grows proud.", note:"V.55: whoever WILLS (human). v.56: unless ALLAH wills (divine). Both true, back to back.", more:["v.56 — illā an yaSHĀʾA'llāh — unless ALLAH wills (divine will — encompassing).","Same verb (shāʾa), back to back — human and divine.","You own this exact structure: - Takwīr 81:28–29 — li-man shāʾa minkum an yastaqīm · wa mā tashāʾūna illā an yashāʾa'llāh - Insān 76:29–30 — fa-man shāʾa'ttakhadha… · wa mā tashāʾūna illā an yashāʾa'llāh - Muddaththir 74:55–56 — fa-man shāʾa dhakarah · wa mā yadhkurūna illā an yashāʾa'llāh THREE surahs, the identical two-step: your will is real; His will encompasses it.","The Qurʾān never drops either side.","Note the variation: Takwīr & Insān say tashāʾūna (you WILL); Muddaththir says yadhkurūna (they REMEMBER) — even remembering itself needs His permission.","Inshāʾallāh — you say this principle every day.","There's a recognized recitation tadhkurūna (\"YOU remember\") — both authentic.","THREE surahs: Takwīr 81:28–29, Insān 76:29–30, Muddaththir 74:55–56. Here: even REMEMBERING needs His permission.","INSHĀʾALLĀH — this principle, daily."]},
  {ar:"هُوَ أَهْلُ التَّقْوَىٰ وَأَهْلُ الْمَغْفِرَةِ", tr:"Huwa ahlu't-taqwā wa ahlu'l-maghfirah", v:"56", root:"Ahl — the \"FAMILY / people / those worthy of\" family — ahl = a FAMILY, a household (ahlī = my family.", meaning:"\"He is worthy to be feared [guarded against], and worthy to forgive.\"", magic:"THE SURAH'S LAST WORDS — and they hold the whole juzʾ in balance.", grammar:"Huwa = \"He\" (emphatic subject). ahlu + iḍāfah ×2 (parallel). at-taqwā = faʿlā verbal noun (weak-final).", why:"Ahl (worthy / family of) — makes both fear and forgiveness His natural due — and ends the surah on mercy.", note:"TAQWĀ (← wiqāyah, a SHIELD) + MAGHFIRAH (← mighfar, a HELMET) — BOTH ARMOUR. Your shield + His helmet.", more:["ʿAbasa / Inshiqāq 84:9 yanqalibu ilā ahlihi masrūrā); ahl li- = WORTHY OF, deserving (ahliyyah = QUALIFICATION / eligibility!; ahlan wa sahlan = \"WELCOME!\" — \"you're among FAMILY\"!; muʾahhal = qualified).","taqwā — SHIELDING / God-consciousness (wiqāyah = protection — NOT fear, ARMOUR).","maghfirah — FORGIVENESS — from ghafara = to COVER (mighfar = a HELMET!).","Ahl at-TAQWĀ — \"the One WORTHY of taqwā\" — the One you should shield yourself in relation to (from His displeasure).","Ahl al-MAGHFIRAH — \"the One WORTHY to FORGIVE\" — the One whose nature is to cover sins.","TAQWĀ (shield) + MAGHFIRAH (helmet): taqwā from wiqāyah (PROTECTION); maghfirah from mighfar (a HELMET).","BOTH are images of ARMOUR.","Your taqwā is the shield you raise; His maghfirah is the helmet He places over you.","The surah of Saqar ends wrapped in two layers of protection.","And the surah's arc: v.1 — al-MUDDATHTHIR — wrapped in a CLOAK (dithār, an outer covering).","v.56 — ahl al-MAGHFIRAH — the One who COVERS (ghafara).","It opens with a man covering himself in fear → it closes with Allah, whose nature is to COVER.","Exactly like Muzzammil: opens wrapped (muzzammil) → closes ghafūrun raḥīm.","The twin surahs both move from a human covering to the divine covering.","Ahl = FAMILY — ahlan wa sahlan (\"welcome!\") literally means \"you've come to FAMILY and to EASY ground.\" He is the \"ahl\" of forgiveness — forgiveness is His household, His home.","And the warning is balanced by mercy, as always: v.56 opens with taqwā (fear Him) and ends with maghfirah (He forgives) — the LAST word of the surah is FORGIVENESS.","v.1 MUDDATHTHIR (a man COVERED in a cloak) → v.56 MAGHFIRAH (Allah who COVERS).","Same as Muzzammil: wrapped → Ghafūr.","AHLAN WA SAHLAN = \"welcome — you're among FAMILY.\" Ahliyyah = ELIGIBILITY.","The LAST word: FORGIVENESS."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:75, name:"Al-Qiyāmah", ar:"القيامة", en:"The Resurrection", verses:40, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"An argument in miniature: if God can reassemble your fingertips, the Day you keep postponing is not the hard part.",
  words:[
  {ar:"أُقْسِمُ", tr:"uqsimu", v:"1–2", rk:"q-s-m", root:"√q-s-m — dividing, allotting, settling a matter by portions", meaning:"I swear / I take an oath", magic:"The surah opens on an oath rather than a claim, so the reader is inside a sworn statement before a single argument has been made.", grammar:"Form IV present verb, first person singular.", why:"The present tense makes it a standing oath rather than a one-off — it renews every time the verse is recited.", note:"Present tense = a living, renewed oath."},
  {ar:"اللَّوَّامَةِ", tr:"al-lawwāmah", v:"2", rk:"l-w-m", root:"√l-w-m — blame, reproach, holding someone to account", meaning:"the self-reproaching soul", magic:"God swears by your guilty conscience — the nagging voice inside you is offered as evidence that a reckoning exists.", grammar:"Intensive adjective on the faʿʿālah pattern, feminine, in the genitive after the oath.", why:"The faʿʿālah pattern means one who does it constantly, not once in a while.", note:"Intensive form = a soul on constant self-audit."},
  {ar:"أَيَحْسَبُ", tr:"a-yaḥsabu", v:"3, 36", rk:"ḥ-s-b", root:"√ḥ-s-b — counting, reckoning, calculating a total", meaning:"does he reckon / suppose?", magic:"The question is the argument. It makes the denier hear his own assumption spoken out loud, where it sounds much thinner.", grammar:"Interrogative hamzah + present verb; a rhetorical question expecting “no.”", why:"Ḥasiba is reckoning that turns out wrong — supposing, not knowing.", note:"A denying question: “does he really think so?”"},
  {ar:"عِظَامَهُ", tr:"ʿiẓām", v:"3", rk:"ʿ-ẓ-m", root:"√ʿ-ẓ-m — greatness, magnitude; and bones, the body's great frame", meaning:"bones", magic:"The objection is quoted at its strongest before it is answered — scattered bones, the hardest case anyone could put.", grammar:"Broken plural, accusative as the object of “gather.”", why:"Bones are what survives and what scatters — the exact part that makes resurrection sound impossible.", note:"The hardest remains — so the strongest resurrection challenge."},
  {ar:"قَادِرِينَ", tr:"qādirīn", v:"4", rk:"q-d-r", root:"√q-d-r — power joined to exact measure", meaning:"fully able", magic:"The answer arrives before the objection has finished landing — “yes,” then immediately something harder than what was asked.", grammar:"Active participle, plural, accusative as a circumstantial state (ḥāl).", why:"Qādir is settled, standing capability, not a burst of effort.", note:"Power here means strength plus precision."},
  {ar:"نُسَوِّيَ", tr:"nusawwiya", v:"4", rk:"s-w-y", root:"√s-w-y — making even, proportioning, levelling into shape", meaning:"to proportion / fashion perfectly", magic:"The reply overshoots the challenge on purpose: not merely bones reassembled, but fingertips re-formed.", grammar:"Form II present verb, subjunctive after an.", why:"Form II carries careful, repeated shaping — craftsmanship, not assembly.", note:"Form II = careful, exact crafting."},
  {ar:"بَنَانَهُ", tr:"banānahu", v:"4", rk:"b-n-n", root:"√b-n-n — the finger-ends, the tips", meaning:"his fingertips", magic:"Of everything in a body, the verse picks the one surface that is unique to you — and modern readers hear fingerprints in it.", grammar:"Collective noun with attached pronoun, accusative object.", why:"The fingertip is the smallest, most individual detail — so it settles the argument by excess.", note:"From bones to fingerprints — re-creation down to the ridges."},
  {ar:"يَفْجُرَ", tr:"yafjura", v:"5", rk:"f-j-r", root:"√f-j-r — splitting, bursting open, breaking a barrier", meaning:"to burst into sin", magic:"The verse exposes the real motive under the denial: he does not doubt the Day, he wants the road ahead of him clear.", grammar:"Present verb, subjunctive after the lām of purpose.", why:"Fajara is tearing an opening, not slipping — deliberate, not accidental.", note:"Brazen, barrier-breaking sin."},
  {ar:"أَمَامَهُ", tr:"amāmahu", v:"5", rk:"ʾ-m-m", root:"√ʾ-m-m — front, forward, that which leads", meaning:"ahead of him", magic:"One word holds both his open calendar and the Day closing in on it — the future he is counting on is the thing coming for him.", grammar:"Adverb of place with attached pronoun.", why:"It says in front of, not later — the Day is approaching, not merely scheduled.", note:"Can mean both his future and the Day ahead."},
  {ar:"أَيَّانَ", tr:"ayyāna", v:"6", root:"An interrogative reserved for weighty, dreaded times", meaning:"when?", magic:"The sneer is preserved in the grammar — the questioner is not asking for a date, he is mocking the idea of one.", grammar:"Interrogative of time, used adverbially.", why:"Ayyāna rather than matā: it is kept for momentous, feared appointments.", note:"Used for a grave, dreaded appointment."},
  {ar:"بَرِقَ", tr:"bariqa", v:"7", rk:"b-r-q", root:"√b-r-q — flashing, lightning, a dazzling glare", meaning:"dazzled / flashed with terror", magic:"The scene is told in past-tense verbs although it is still future — the Day is reported as something already done.", grammar:"Perfect verb, third person masculine singular.", why:"The lightning verb is applied to the eye itself, so the panic is seen from inside the skull.", note:"Panic strikes the eye like a blinding flash."},
  {ar:"خَسَفَ", tr:"khasafa", v:"8", rk:"kh-s-f", root:"√kh-s-f — sinking, being swallowed, losing one's light", meaning:"was eclipsed", magic:"The Qur'an reaches for the technical word an astronomer would use, so the cosmic collapse is described with precision rather than noise.", grammar:"Perfect verb, third person masculine singular.", why:"Khusūf is the moon's own eclipse-word — kusūf belongs to the sun.", note:"The moon's own precise eclipse-verb."},
  {ar:"الْمَفَرُّ", tr:"al-mafarr", v:"10", rk:"f-r-r", root:"√f-r-r — fleeing, bolting away", meaning:"the place of escape", magic:"He no longer asks whether the Day is real — he asks which way out. The argument is over; only the panic is left.", grammar:"Noun of place (ism makān) with the definite article.", why:"It names a place, not an act — he is scanning for an exit that has to exist somewhere.", note:"The desperate search for an exit."},
  {ar:"وَزَرَ", tr:"wazar", v:"11", rk:"w-z-r", root:"√w-z-r — a burden; and the mountain crag you shelter behind", meaning:"refuge / stronghold", magic:"The word for the heaviest thing you carry is also the word for the safest place to hide — and the verse denies you it.", grammar:"Indefinite noun, subject of the negation lā.", why:"It pictures the strongest shelter an Arabian imagination could reach for, then removes it.", note:"Not even a mountain-like shelter exists."},
  {ar:"الْمُسْتَقَرُّ", tr:"al-mustaqarr", v:"12", rk:"q-r-r", root:"√q-r-r — settling, coming to rest, becoming fixed", meaning:"the final resting place", magic:"Immediately after “no refuge,” the true destination is named — there is nowhere to run because there is somewhere to arrive.", grammar:"Form X noun of place, subject of the sentence.", why:"Form X carries seeking to settle — this is where the running finally stops.", note:"The only true stopping-place is with your Lord."},
  {ar:"يُنَبَّأُ", tr:"yunabbaʾu", v:"13", rk:"n-b-ʾ", root:"√n-b-ʾ — news of consequence, a weighty report", meaning:"he will be informed fully", magic:"He is told about himself. He does not testify, defend or narrate — his own life is read back to him.", grammar:"Passive Form II present verb.", why:"Nabaʾ is heavy news, and Form II makes it itemized rather than summarized.", note:"Form II suggests detailed disclosure, item by item."},
  {ar:"قَدَّمَ", tr:"qaddama", v:"13", rk:"q-d-m", root:"√q-d-m — going forward, putting something in front", meaning:"he sent ahead", magic:"Deeds are pictured as luggage shipped forward to a destination you have not reached yet.", grammar:"Form II perfect verb.", why:"Form II means he pushed it forward himself — nobody sent it for him.", note:"What a person put forward in deeds."},
  {ar:"أَخَّرَ", tr:"akhkhara", v:"13", rk:"ʾ-kh-r", root:"√ʾ-kh-r — delaying, holding back, leaving behind", meaning:"he left behind", magic:"Paired with qaddama, the two verbs close a complete ledger: what he sent forward and what he left in his wake. Nothing falls between them.", grammar:"Form II perfect verb, the exact antonym pattern of the previous word.", why:"It covers the sins of omission and the traces that outlive him, not only what he actively did.", note:"What he neglected, or left as traces after him."},
  {ar:"بَصِيرَةٌ", tr:"baṣīra", v:"14", rk:"b-ṣ-r", root:"√b-ṣ-r — seeing, perceiving, clear evidence", meaning:"an inner witness / insight", magic:"The verse does not say he has evidence against himself — it says he is evidence. The witness stand and the accused are the same person.", grammar:"Intensive feminine noun used as the predicate.", why:"Baṣīra means both insight and proof, so self-knowledge becomes testimony.", note:"The soul itself testifies against the person."},
  {ar:"مَعَاذِيرَهُ", tr:"maʿādhirahu", v:"15", rk:"ʿ-dh-r", root:"√ʿ-dh-r — excusing, clearing oneself of blame", meaning:"his excuses", magic:"The plural does the work: he does not offer one explanation, he throws every excuse he owns, and none of them lands.", grammar:"Broken plural with attached pronoun, accusative.", why:"Maʿdhirah is a formal plea, which keeps the courtroom image intact.", note:"Flimsy self-justifications cannot save him."},
  {ar:"تُحَرِّكْ", tr:"tuḥarrik", v:"16", rk:"ḥ-r-k", root:"√ḥ-r-k — movement, stirring, setting in motion", meaning:"you move / stir", magic:"In the middle of a terror scene the voice turns aside to one man, quietly, about his tongue — and then returns. The intimacy is the point.", grammar:"Form II present verb, jussive after the prohibitive lā.", why:"Form II fits small repeated motion — the lips hurrying to keep up.", note:"A command not to rush the revelation."},
  {ar:"اتَّبِعْ", tr:"ittabiʿ", v:"18", rk:"t-b-ʿ", root:"√t-b-ʿ — following, coming behind", meaning:"follow", magic:"The instruction is a whole method of learning: receive first, repeat second. Anxiety to hold on is answered with a promise to hold it for you.", grammar:"Form VIII imperative.", why:"It means follow behind, not run alongside — the order matters.", note:"Follow the Qur'an's recitation — listen first, repeat after."},
  {ar:"بَيَانَهُ", tr:"bayānahu", v:"19", rk:"b-y-n", root:"√b-y-n — becoming clear, separating out until distinct", meaning:"its clarification", magic:"God takes responsibility not only for the words but for their meaning — the text and its explanation come from the same source.", grammar:"Verbal noun with attached pronoun, accusative.", why:"Bayān is making a thing shine clear, stronger than merely explaining it.", note:"Allah guarantees its explanation."},
  {ar:"الْعَاجِلَةَ", tr:"al-ʿājila", v:"20", rk:"ʿ-j-l", root:"√ʿ-j-l — haste, hurry, wanting it now", meaning:"the fleeting world / the immediate one", magic:"The world is named by its speed rather than its pleasure — the diagnosis is in the noun before any criticism is made.", grammar:"Active participle used as a noun, accusative object.", why:"It says the attraction out loud: dunya is loved because it is near and quick, not because it is good.", note:"Dunya is loved because it feels quick and near."},
  {ar:"تَذَرُونَ", tr:"tadharūna", v:"21", rk:"w-dh-r", root:"√w-dh-r — leaving something behind, letting it go untended", meaning:"you leave / neglect", magic:"Not “you deny the Hereafter” but “you leave it” — which is exactly how most people actually lose it.", grammar:"Present verb, second person masculine plural.", why:"Wadhara is abandoning and walking on, a quieter and truer charge than denial.", note:"Used for abandoning the Hereafter."},
  {ar:"نَاضِرَةٌ", tr:"nāḍira", v:"22", rk:"n-ḍ-r", root:"√n-ḍ-r — freshness, bloom, the glow of a well-watered plant", meaning:"radiant / glowing", magic:"The reward is described as a change in the face before anything is said about gardens — bliss shows up on the skin.", grammar:"Active participle, feminine singular.", why:"Naḍāra is the freshness of green growth, so the joy reads as living, not painted on.", note:"Faces lit up with joy and bliss."},
  {ar:"نَاظِرَةٌ", tr:"nāẓira", v:"23", rk:"n-ẓ-r", root:"√n-ẓ-r — looking, gazing, directing the eye", meaning:"looking / gazing", magic:"The most famous sound-play in the surah: nāḍira and nāẓira differ by a single dot, and the glow is explained by what the eyes are turned toward.", grammar:"Active participle, feminine singular, same pattern as the previous word.", why:"Naẓara ilā is to look at something directly — and here the object is their Lord.", note:"One letter changes the meaning from glow to gaze."},
  {ar:"بَاسِرَةٌ", tr:"bāsira", v:"24", rk:"b-s-r", root:"√b-s-r — grimness, a face pulled tight and sour", meaning:"gloomy / frowning", magic:"The other group is drawn on the same participle pattern, so the contrast is exact — two faces, one word-shape, opposite weather.", grammar:"Active participle, feminine singular.", why:"Basara is the look that arrives before the blow does — dread, not yet pain.", note:"Faces darkened by dread."},
  {ar:"فَاقِرَةٌ", tr:"fāqira", v:"25", rk:"f-q-r", root:"√f-q-r — the vertebrae; breaking the spine", meaning:"a back-breaking calamity", magic:"The disaster is not described, only what it does to a body — the Qur'an names the injury and lets you supply the event.", grammar:"Active participle used as a noun, feminine.", why:"From faqār, the vertebra: it is the one word for a blow you cannot stand up after.", note:"A disaster that feels spine-snapping."},
  {ar:"التَّرَاقِيَ", tr:"al-tarāqī", v:"26", rk:"t-r-q", root:"From tarquwah — the collarbone", meaning:"the collarbones", magic:"The camera drops from cosmic collapse to one bedside detail: a soul arriving at the notch of the throat.", grammar:"Broken plural of tarquwah, accusative.", why:"Naming the exact bone makes the moment of death physical and observed, not abstract.", note:"The soul reaches the throat at death."},
  {ar:"رَاقٍ", tr:"rāqin", v:"27", rk:"r-q-y", root:"√r-q-y — rising, ascending; and reciting words of healing", meaning:"a healer / one who can cure?", magic:"The room asks for anyone who can save him while the angels ask who will take him up — one word carries both questions at once.", grammar:"Active participle, indefinite, of a defective (manqūṣ) root.", why:"The double sense is deliberate: the same letters serve the panic below and the ascent above.", note:"A deliberate double meaning: healer, and the one who ascends with the soul."},
  {ar:"الْفِرَاقُ", tr:"al-firāq", v:"28", rk:"f-r-q", root:"√f-r-q — separating, parting into two", meaning:"the parting / departure", magic:"He spent a life certain of nothing; now, too late, the verse says he knows.", grammar:"Verbal noun with the definite article, subject of the clause.", why:"Firāq is the parting of two things that were joined — soul from body, man from world, in one word.", note:"The dying person knows it is the final separation."},
  {ar:"الْتَفَّتِ", tr:"iltaffat", v:"29", rk:"l-f-f", root:"√l-f-f — wrapping, twisting, winding around", meaning:"wrapped / clung together", magic:"Distress is drawn as physical entanglement — the troubles do not queue up, they knot together.", grammar:"Form VIII perfect verb, feminine singular.", why:"Iltaffa is twisting into one another, not merely touching.", note:"One distress folds into another."},
  {ar:"السَّاقُ", tr:"al-sāq", v:"29", rk:"s-w-q", root:"√s-w-q — the shin, the leg one stands on", meaning:"the leg / shin", magic:"“Shin against shin” — the body's own posture becomes the measure of the agony, with no adjective needed.", grammar:"Noun, nominative, in the idiom sāq bi-sāq.", why:"The shin is what carries you; when it is bound to the other, movement is over.", note:"Leg upon leg — mounting agony."},
  {ar:"الْمَسَاقُ", tr:"al-masāq", v:"30", rk:"s-w-q", root:"√s-w-q — driving, herding, leading a flock onward", meaning:"the driving / being led onward", magic:"The scene's last word is driving. The man who walked wherever he pleased is now moved like livestock.", grammar:"Verbal noun / noun of place from the same root as al-sāq.", why:"Sawq is the driving of a herd — direction chosen by someone else.", note:"All are driven onward to their Lord."},
  {ar:"صَدَّقَ", tr:"ṣaddaqa", v:"31", rk:"ṣ-d-q", root:"√ṣ-d-q — truth, sincerity, holding something to be true", meaning:"affirmed as true", magic:"The indictment is built from what he did not do inside himself — no belief, no prayer — before it reaches anything he did.", grammar:"Form II perfect verb, negated.", why:"Ṣaddaqa is inner assent, not lip agreement — the exact thing that was absent.", note:"Inner assent to truth was the thing missing."},
  {ar:"تَوَلَّىٰ", tr:"tawallā", v:"32", rk:"w-l-y", root:"√w-l-y — turning; nearness, allegiance, taking a side", meaning:"turned away", magic:"The root that means drawing near is used for walking off — the same word family holds both the loyalty offered and the loyalty refused.", grammar:"Form V perfect verb.", why:"Form V makes it reflexive and deliberate: he turned himself away.", note:"A deliberate turning of the back."},
  {ar:"يَتَمَطَّىٰ", tr:"yatamaṭṭā", v:"33", rk:"m-ṭ-ṭ", root:"√m-ṭ-ṭ — stretching out, drawing oneself long", meaning:"swaggered / strutted", magic:"After the theology, the surah convicts him by his walk — the strut back to his people is the last evidence entered.", grammar:"Form V present verb, of a defective root.", why:"It pictures a body stretched tall with self-satisfaction — arrogance made visible.", note:"Arrogance shows up in how he walks."},
  {ar:"أَوْلَىٰ", tr:"awlā", v:"34–35", rk:"w-l-y", root:"√w-l-y — nearness; here, doom drawing near", meaning:"woe to you!", magic:"The phrase is fired twice, then twice again — the threat does not escalate in vocabulary, only in repetition, which is worse.", grammar:"Elative form used as a fixed threat idiom.", why:"It is the language of a street warning, not a sermon — nearer, and nearer.", note:"A threat-idiom, repeated for rising doom."},
  {ar:"سُدًى", tr:"sudan", v:"36", rk:"s-d-y", root:"√s-d-y — left loose, untended, running free like unherded cattle", meaning:"aimless / left loose", magic:"The whole case for accountability rests on one small word for “loose” — you were never released without a purpose.", grammar:"Indefinite noun in the accusative as a circumstantial state (ḥāl).", why:"Sudan is the animal nobody watches and nobody claims — precisely what a human is not.", note:"Man was not created without purpose."},
  {ar:"نُطْفَةً", tr:"nutfa", v:"37", rk:"n-ṭ-f", root:"√n-ṭ-f — dripping, trickling, a single drop", meaning:"a tiny drop", magic:"Having argued forward to the Day, the surah now argues backward to the drop — the same power, running the other direction.", grammar:"Indefinite noun, accusative.", why:"Nutfa is not a quantity but a drip — the smallest possible beginning for the largest possible claim.", note:"The resurrection proof starts from the humblest origin."},
  {ar:"مَنِيٍّ", tr:"maniyy", v:"37", rk:"m-n-y", root:"√m-n-y — measuring out, decreeing; and the fluid itself", meaning:"semen", magic:"The same letters that mean “to apportion by decree” name the fluid — origin and destiny share a root.", grammar:"Noun in the genitive after min.", why:"It quietly repeats the surah's thesis: nothing about you was left to chance.", note:"Even the origin is divinely measured."},
  {ar:"يُمْنَىٰ", tr:"yumnā", v:"37", rk:"m-n-y", root:"√m-n-y — pouring forth, emitting", meaning:"is emitted / poured forth", magic:"The verb is passive, so the man is not the actor even at his own beginning — he is what happened, not who did it.", grammar:"Passive present verb, third person, of a defective root.", why:"The passive removes him from the sentence exactly where he imagines himself in charge.", note:"Passive form — man is acted upon, not in control."},
  {ar:"عَلَقَةً", tr:"ʿalaqa", v:"38", rk:"ʿ-l-q", root:"√ʿ-l-q — clinging, hanging on, attaching", meaning:"a clinging clot", magic:"Each stage of becoming a person gets exactly one word — the speed of the telling matches the speed of the change.", grammar:"Indefinite noun, accusative.", why:"ʿAlaqa names the behaviour, not the appearance: the thing that clings to the wall.", note:"The whole embryo stage captured in one root."},
  {ar:"سَوَّىٰ", tr:"sawwā", v:"38", rk:"s-w-y", root:"√s-w-y — making even, proportioning", meaning:"proportioned perfectly", magic:"The same verb used back in verse 4 for re-forming the fingertips returns here for forming you the first time — the argument closes its own circle.", grammar:"Form II perfect verb, of a defective root.", why:"Using the identical root twice is the proof: the second making is the first making, repeated.", note:"The One who first fashioned can refashion."},
  {ar:"الزَّوْجَيْنِ", tr:"al-zawjayni", v:"39", rk:"z-w-j", root:"√z-w-j — pairing, mating, joining two into a set", meaning:"the two mates / pairs", magic:"From one drop come two kinds — the unity of the origin and the duality of the outcome sit in the same sentence.", grammar:"Dual noun, accusative.", why:"Zawj is not simply “one of two” but each half of a matched pair — neither is complete alone.", note:"Complementary duality from one origin."},
  {ar:"الذَّكَرَ", tr:"al-dhakara", v:"39", rk:"dh-k-r", root:"√dh-k-r — the male; and, in its other sense, remembrance", meaning:"the male", magic:"The pair is spelled out rather than left implied, so the claim is concrete: this specific outcome, from that specific drop.", grammar:"Noun, accusative, standing in apposition (badal) to “the two mates.”", why:"Naming both halves individually keeps the verse from becoming an abstraction.", note:"One half of the created pair."},
  {ar:"الْأُنثَىٰ", tr:"al-unthā", v:"39", rk:"ʾ-n-th", root:"√ʾ-n-th — the female", meaning:"the female", magic:"Two words, one conjunction, and the entire diversity of humankind is accounted for from a single fluid.", grammar:"Noun, accusative, the second half of the apposition.", why:"It completes the pair, so the design argument is finished rather than half-stated.", note:"The paired opposite completing humankind."},
  {ar:"يُحْيِيَ", tr:"yuḥyiya", v:"40", rk:"ḥ-y-y", root:"√ḥ-y-y — life, living, being made alive", meaning:"to give life", magic:"The surah ends where it began — with a question — but this time the reader has been handed everything needed to answer it.", grammar:"Form IV present verb, subjunctive after an.", why:"Form IV is causing life, not merely being alive — the exact act under dispute.", note:"The final question pushes the listener to say yes."},
  {ar:"الْمَوْتَىٰ", tr:"al-mawtā", v:"40", rk:"m-w-t", root:"√m-w-t — death, the dead", meaning:"the dead", magic:"The last word of the argument is the objection itself — the surah lets the hardest fact have the final position and still wins.", grammar:"Broken plural, accusative object.", why:"Placing “the dead” at the end leaves the reader holding the question, not the answer.", note:"Life versus death seals the surah's argument."}
  ]
},
{
  num:76, name:"Al-Insān", ar:"الإنسان", en:"Man", verses:31, place:"Madinan",
  blurb:"",
  big:["Where you came from — a tiny drop nobody had even heard of, plus a test built into you from day one.","The kind heroes — people who were hungry themselves, but fed a stranger, an orphan, and a prisoner anyway, and wanted nothing back.","Their reward — a garden so wild it has cups made of glass and silver at the same time (impossible on Earth), a drink that tastes like ginger, and servants who never age.","The lesson for you — this whole thing is a reminder. Now it's your move."],
  traps:["كَفُورًا (kafūran, \"ungrateful,\" v.3 & 24) vs. كَافُورًا (kāfūran, \"camphor,\" v.5) — one tiny alif apart, totally different words. Read slowly here.","أَسِيرًا (v.8, the captive) and أَسْرَهُمْ (v.28, your body's frame) — same root, but don't mix up who's bound: a prisoner in v.8, your own skeleton in v.28.","قَوَارِير (v.15–16) — \"glass\" then \"glass made of silver\" — don't rush past this, it's describing a material that can't exist on Earth, on purpose.","يَذَرُونَ — remember it has no past tense. If you ever see a \"past tense\" version, it's not really this verb.","شَاكِرًا → شُكُورًا → مَشْكُورًا — three different words, same root, three different verses (v.3, 9, 22). Learn them as a set, not separately."],
  links:[{w:"الْعَاجِلَة (v.27)",e:"الْعَاجِلَة (75:20)",t:"Identical word — \"the fleeting world\""},{w:"يَذَرُونَ (v.27)",e:"تَذَرُونَ (75:21)",t:"Same odd verb with no past tense — just \"they\" vs \"you\""},{w:"نَضْرَةً (v.11)",e:"نَاضِرَة (75:22)",t:"Same root — the glow on blessed faces"},{w:"قَدَّرُوهَا (v.16)",e:"قَادِرِين (75:4)",t:"Same root — precise measuring/power"},{w:"يُفَجِّرُونَهَا (v.6)",e:"يَفْجُرَ (75:5)",t:"Same root, opposite direction — bursting into blessing vs. bursting into sin"},{w:"أَسِيرًا / أَسْرَهُمْ (v.8, v.28)",e:"",t:"Same root within this surah — the captive's binding, echoed in your own body's binding"},{w:"مَذْكُورًا / تَذْكِرَةٌ (v.1, v.29)",e:"أَيَحْسَبُ...أَيَحْسَبُ (75:3, 36)",t:"Same trick — each surah opens and closes on the same root"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"الدَّهْر", tr:"Ad-dahr", v:"1", root:"Not just \"time\" — think Old Man Time, time as a big mysterious force, almost like fate.", meaning:"\"a period\" — before man existed, when he wasn't even a rumor.", magic:"Pre-Islamic Arabs blamed bad luck on \"Dahr,\" like it was some blind power running the universe.", grammar:"\"Hal atā…?\" (\"Has there come…?\") isn't a real question.", why:"Not the plain word for time — the loaded word, on purpose, to flip a superstition on its head.", note:"Dahr → imagine a big door swinging open across history — and stepping through it for the very first time is... you.", more:["The Prophet ﷺ later relayed that Allah said: \"Don't curse Dahr — I am Dahr.\" So this one word quietly corrects an old superstition: your existence wasn't a roll of the dice.","It's Arabic's way of saying \"Yes, obviously,\" the way \"Didn't I tell you?\" already assumes the answer."]},
  {ar:"مَذْكُورًا", tr:"Madhkūran", v:"1", root:"The \"remembering/mentioning\" family — like when your name gets called out in class.", meaning:"Man wasn't even \"a thing mentioned\" — total nobody, not on anyone's radar, not even a whisper.", magic:"None hidden here — it's just a stark, humbling word choice. Total zero, then everything.", grammar:"Passive participle — something done to the noun (\"a thing that gets mentioned\"), not something the noun does itself.", why:"Maximum humility opener — you weren't just small, you were unmentioned.", note:"Dhakara = mention → this exact root comes back at the very end of the surah (v.29, \"tadhkirah\" = reminder).", more:["The surah opens with \"not even mentioned\" and closes by \"reminding\" you."]},
  {ar:"أَمْشَاج", tr:"Amshāj", v:"2", root:"The \"mixing\" family — like stirring paint colors together.", meaning:"Describes the drop you came from as a blend — many things mixed into one.", magic:"It's grammatically plural, even though it's describing one single drop — like saying \"a drop of mixtures.\" That plural is doing work: your very first cell wasn't simple, it was already a cocktail of ingredients.", grammar:"An adjective in plural form describing a singular noun, specifically to stress internal complexity.", why:"Not \"a drop,\" but \"a drop-that-is-many-things\" — you were complicated from cell one.", note:"Amshāj sounds like \"a mash\" — picture a smoothie: fruit, ice, yogurt, all mashed into one cup. That's you, verse 2."},
  {ar:"نَبْتَلِيه", tr:"Nabtalīhi", v:"2", root:"The \"testing/trying\" family — like stress-testing a rope by pulling on it to see if it holds.", meaning:"\"to test him\" — your whole design (hearing, seeing) exists so you can be tested.", magic:"None flashy — just precise, purposeful vocabulary.", grammar:"This is the intensive \"reflexive\" verb-form (Form VIII) — testing done thoroughly, not a light poke.", why:"Frames your senses as tools for an exam, not just biology.", note:"Nabtalīhi → \"bally\" like a balance test — every day is a little balance-beam walk."},
  {ar:"شَاكِرًا / كَفُورًا", tr:"Shākiran / kafūran", v:"3", root:"The \"covering up\" family — a farmer who buries a seed in soil was called this in old Arabic.", meaning:"Two roads: thankful, or a repeat, habitual denier.", magic:"Kafūran is an intensive pattern — not \"sometimes ungrateful\" but \"ungrateful again and again,\" like a scratched record stuck on denial.", grammar:"Both are indefinite accusative nouns acting like adjectives — \"as a grateful one / as a covering-up one.\"", why:"\"kafūr\" instead of a milder word — because burying a blessing you can feel is worse than simply missing it.", note:"Kafara = to bury. Picture someone literally shoveling dirt over a gift someone gave them, pretending it's not there.", more:["A disbeliever \"covers up\" a truth they can feel is there.","That's a \"kāfir\" in the original farming sense."]},
  {ar:"أَعْتَدْنَا", tr:"Aʿtadnā", v:"4", root:"The \"getting-ready-in-advance\" family — like packing your bag the night before a trip.", meaning:"\"We have [already] prepared\" — this isn't a future maybe, it's already sitting there waiting.", magic:"The past tense for something not experienced yet makes it feel certain and already-set, not a threat that might not happen.", grammar:"Past tense used for total certainty about the future — common in Quranic style.", why:"\"prepared in advance,\" not \"will make\" — the consequences of choices are already built and waiting.", note:"Aʿtadnā sounds like \"attend-nā\" — imagine the outcome is already attending, sitting in the waiting room, before you even arrive."},
  {ar:"سَلَاسِل / أَغْلَال", tr:"Salāsil / aghlāl", v:"4", root:"The \"binding-tight\" family — anything clamped around the neck or hands.", meaning:"Literal chains and shackles, prepared for those who deny.", magic:"A matched pair (near-synonyms stacked together) piles up the picture of total restraint — not just tied, but chained and shackled.", grammar:"Two plural nouns in a row, both objects of \"prepared\" — a simple list-stack for emphasis.", why:"Two words instead of one — restraint from every angle, hands and neck both.", note:"Salāsil sounds like \"salsa\" spun wrong — no dancing here, just links of chain rattling."},
  {ar:"سَعِيرًا", tr:"Saʿīran", v:"4", root:"The \"flaring up / blazing\" family — same root idea as a market \"heating up\" with rising prices.", meaning:"A blazing fire.", magic:"None extra — a strong, vivid noun doing exactly its job.", grammar:"Indefinite accusative, third item in the \"prepared for them\" list.", why:"\"saʿīr\" (an active, flaring blaze) rather than a calmer word for fire — it's not smoldering, it's roaring.", note:"Saʿīr sounds like \"severe\" — a severe, flaring fire."},
  {ar:"الْأَبْرَار", tr:"Al-abrār", v:"5", root:"The \"solid goodness\" family — same root as \"birr\" (devotion, especially to parents), and possibly linked to \"barr\" (solid land, as opposed to sea) — someone you can stand on, dependable.", meaning:"The truly righteous — a step above just \"good,\" more like \"good all the way through.\"", magic:"None hidden — just a strong, dignified title for people of real substance.", grammar:"Plural noun, subject of the sentence.", why:"\"abrār\" instead of a plain word for \"good people\" — signals depth and consistency of goodness, not a one-off nice act.", note:"Abrār sounds like \"a-bear\" — picture a solid, steady bear, unshakeable, dependable. That's a \"birr\" kind of person."},
  {ar:"كَأْس / مِزَاجُهَا", tr:"Kaʾs / mizājuhā", v:"5", root:"The \"blending\" family — mixing a flavor into a drink.", meaning:"A cup whose blend (what's mixed into it) is special — described next.", magic:"None extra here — but this word sets up a mini-mystery (\"blended with what?\") solved by the very next word.", grammar:"\"mizājuhā\" (its mixture) is the subject of a \"kāna\" sentence — \"was, its mixture, [made of] X.\"", why:"\"mizāj\" (blend/mixture) rather than just naming the drink outright — it builds anticipation.", note:"Mizāj sounds like \"miss ah\" — imagine missing an ingredient until the next word reveals it."},
  {ar:"كَافُورًا", tr:"Kāfūran", v:"5", root:"Camphor — a cool, fragrant substance, totally different word from \"kafūr\" (ungrateful) in verse 3, even though they look almost the same written out.", meaning:"Their drink is blended with something like camphor — cool, refreshing, fragrant.", magic:"The near-identical spelling to \"kafūran\" (ungrateful, v.3) is a genuine trap for readers — flagged in the traps list at the end.", grammar:"Accusative noun, the \"what\" of the mixture.", why:"Picked for its cooling, fragrant quality — a sensory word, not just \"something nice.\"", note:"This is camphor, like the cooling menthol smell in a chest-rub.", more:["Cool drink, cool word — but watch the spelling (see the traps section!)."]},
  {ar:"يُفَجِّرُونَهَا", tr:"Yufajjirūnahā", v:"6", root:"The \"bursting open\" family — same root as fajr (dawn splitting the darkness open).", meaning:"The righteous make this spring gush out however they like — they're in control of it.", magic:"In Surah Al-Qiyāmah, this same root described someone \"bursting forth\" into sin (a bad direction).", grammar:"Intensive verb-form (Form II) — not a small trickle, a forceful, repeated gushing.", why:"Shows ownership — they don't just drink from the spring, they direct it wherever they want.", note:"You already know this root from fajr (dawn) and from Qiyāmah's \"yafjura.\" Same root, but now it's your spring, and you're in charge of where it gushes.", more:["Here, the exact same root means bursting forth a spring of blessing, under your own command — same root, opposite destiny."]},
  {ar:"يُوفُونَ / النَّذْر", tr:"Yūfūna / al-nadhr", v:"7", root:"A vow — an extra promise you make to Allah, beyond what's required.", meaning:"They keep every extra promise they made, in full.", magic:"None extra — but pairing \"fulfill completely\" with \"a voluntary vow\" raises the bar: they don't even skip the promises nobody forced them to make.", grammar:"Plural present-tense verb, ongoing/habitual action — not a one-time thing, a lifestyle.", why:"\"yūfūna\" (fulfill in full) instead of a plainer word for \"keep\" — stresses zero shortfall.", note:"Nadhr sounds like \"not yet done\" — but these people finish what they vowed, no leftovers."},
  {ar:"مُسْتَطِيرًا", tr:"Mustaṭīran", v:"7", root:"The \"flying\" family — same root as ṭāʾir (a bird) and ṭāra (to fly).", meaning:"A Day whose evil \"flies\" everywhere — spreads uncontrollably, impossible to dodge.", magic:"A flying/spreading metaphor for disaster — like picturing wildfire smoke that reaches every direction at once, not staying in one place.", grammar:"Form X active participle — literally \"that which has taken flight, spread far.\"", why:"\"flying/spreading\" rather than just \"great\" — it's the reach of the evil that's terrifying, not just its size.", note:"Mustaṭīr sounds like \"must-a-flyer\" — a disaster that must fly everywhere, no corner untouched."},
  {ar:"مِسْكِينًا، يَتِيمًا، أَسِيرًا", tr:"The Three Guests", v:"8", root:"Miskīn = someone stuck still/poor (can't move up); yatīm = a child with no father to lean on.", meaning:"Three people, in increasing vulnerability — the needy, the orphan, the prisoner — all fed despite the giver's own hunger.", magic:"Many commentators, Sunni and Shia alike, link this to a well-known story of a family (often named as ʿAlī and Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with them, and their household) who gave away their only food three nights running while staying hungry themselves.", grammar:"Three objects listed back-to-back after \"they feed\"", why:"Three specific categories, not just \"the poor\" in general.", note:"Picture three knocks at the door, three nights straight, and the family gives away dinner every single time.", more:["Asīr = someone physically bound — same root as \"asr\" (binding), which comes back later in v.28!","Historians debate some details of the chain, but it's the classic mental picture for this verse.","A simple, powerful list, no \"and\" needed between each (asyndeton) for a rapid-fire effect.","Needy, fatherless, and imprisoned each represent a different kind of powerlessness.","Three guests, three words: miskīn, yatīm, asīr."]},
  {ar:"لِوَجْهِ اللَّهِ", tr:"Li-wajhillāh", v:"9", root:"\"wajh\" = face — but idiomatically, \"for someone's face\" means \"purely for their sake,\" no other motive.", meaning:"\"we feed you only for Allah's sake\" — zero ulterior motive.", magic:"Using \"face\" for \"sake/sincerity\" is a classic Arabic idiom — imagine doing something only to see someone's approving face, nothing else.", grammar:"\"wajh\" here isn't literal — it's an idiom for pure intention (ikhlāṣ).", why:"More vivid than just saying \"sincerely\" — it pictures a face you're doing this for.", note:"Imagine doing something kind purely to see one face light up — no reward, no thanks, just that."},
  {ar:"شُكُورًا", tr:"Shukūran", v:"9", root:"Same \"gratitude\" family as \"shākiran\" (word #5) — but now as a noun.", meaning:"\"we want no reward and no thanks\" — not even a verbal thank-you.", magic:"Watch this root travel through the whole surah: shākiran (v.3) → shukūran (v.9) → mashkūran (v.22). Same seed, three different plants.", grammar:"Noun, second object of \"we do not want.\"", why:"Naming \"thanks\" specifically (not just \"reward\") shows they don't even want the small emotional payoff of being thanked.", note:"This is the second stop on the shukr-root journey — track it to word #41, where it comes back one more time."},
  {ar:"عَبُوسًا / قَمْطَرِيرًا", tr:"ʿabūsan / qamṭarīran", v:"10", root:"An extremely rare word for a disaster so severe it makes the whole face contort in distress.", meaning:"\"we fear a Day, frowning, catastrophic\" — the Day itself is personified with a scowling face.", magic:"Giving the Day a face (personification) is powerful — it's not an abstract event, it's described as if it glares at you.", grammar:"Two adjectives stacked on \"a Day\" — piling up dread with each word.", why:"\"qamṭarīr\" is one of the rarest words in the whole Qur'an — chosen precisely because nothing ordinary captures that level of severity.", note:"Qamṭarīr is a mouthful on purpose — say it slowly: qam-ṭa-REER — let the ugly, clunky sound itself feel like a terrible day.", more:["Think \"the worst possible day,\" almost too intense a word to translate cleanly."]},
  {ar:"لَقَّاهُمْ", tr:"Laqqāhum", v:"11", root:"The \"meeting/receiving\" family — bumping into someone and being handed something directly.", meaning:"Allah personally \"met them with\" — gave them directly — radiance and joy.", magic:"None extra — but the personal delivery feel (not just \"they got it,\" but \"He met them with it\") makes the reward feel intimate, not automatic.", grammar:"Intensive verb-form (Form II) — \"caused them to be met with,\" a deliberate act, not a passive happening.", why:"Frames the reward as a direct, personal gift, handed over, not just \"arriving.\"", note:"Laqqā sounds like \"la-ka\" (\"for you\") — imagine Allah handing it to them and saying \"this one's for you.\""},
  {ar:"نَضْرَةً", tr:"Naḍratan", v:"11", root:"The \"fresh glow\" family — the bloom of good health, like fruit at its ripest.", meaning:"A radiant glow given to their faces.", magic:"This is the exact same root as \"nāḍira\" in Surah Al-Qiyāmah (75:22) — \"Faces, that Day, radiant.\" Same glow, two surahs.", grammar:"Indefinite accusative noun, object of \"gave them.\"", why:"\"naḍra\" (a fresh, healthy bloom) instead of a plain word for \"happiness\" — it shows on the outside, visibly.", note:"You already met this root in Qiyāmah's glowing faces — same shine, showing up again here."},
  {ar:"حَرِيرًا", tr:"ḥarīran", v:"12", root:"Silk.", meaning:"Silk garments, given as part of their reward.", magic:"If that connection to \"ḥurr\" (free/noble) holds, then wearing silk in Paradise marks them as forever free — no more toil, no more burden.", grammar:"Accusative noun, paired with \"jannah\" (garden) as a joint reward.", why:"Silk specifically — the most luxurious, weightless fabric — a sensory reward that's easy to picture.", note:"ḥarīr sounds like \"hair-EAR\" — imagine something so smooth it feels like silk brushing your ear.", more:["Some lexicographers connect it to \"ḥurr\" (a free, noble person) — since fine silk was worn by the free and wealthy, not commoners or slaves, in old Arab society."]},
  {ar:"مُتَّكِئِينَ", tr:"Muttakiʾīn", v:"13", root:"The \"leaning back / total relaxation\" family.", meaning:"Reclining — fully at ease, nothing left to do or earn.", magic:"None extra — but it's a strong contrast to word #40 (saʿyukum, \"your effort\") — all the striving is over, now it's just leaning back.", grammar:"Intensive reflexive form (Form VIII) — an active choice to relax completely, not just sitting passively.", why:"\"reclining,\" not merely \"sitting\" — it signals total, permanent ease.", note:"Muttakiʾīn → picture the most comfortable recliner chair you've ever sat in, and you never have to get up."},
  {ar:"الْأَرَائِك", tr:"Al-arāʾik", v:"13", root:"Ornate couches or thrones, historically associated with brides and nobility — a raised, decorated seat under a canopy.", meaning:"The fancy couches they recline on.", magic:"None extra — just a luxury word doing its job.", grammar:"Plural noun, object of the preposition \"ʿalā\" (upon).", why:"Not just \"chairs\" — \"arāʾik\" specifically evokes bridal/royal-level luxury.", note:"Think \"a royal ache\" (arāʾik) — no, wait, there's no ache at all — that's the joke: total comfort, zero pain."},
  {ar:"زَمْهَرِيرًا", tr:"Zamharīran", v:"13", root:"An extremely rare word for bitter, freezing cold — found only here in the whole Qur'an.", meaning:"\"they will see no sun and no zamharīr\" — no scorching heat, no bone-chilling cold.", magic:"The perfect balance (tibāq, an opposite-pair for effect): sun = too hot, zamharīr = too cold. Paradise has neither — always exactly comfortable.", grammar:"Indefinite accusative, negated alongside \"shams\" (sun) in the same breath.", why:"A rare, unusual word for cold makes the absence of both extremes feel deliberate and total.", note:"Zamharīr sounds like \"zam-freezer\" — the one thing that's banned from Paradise's weather."},
  {ar:"دَانِيَةً", tr:"Dāniyatan", v:"14", root:"The \"coming close\" family.", meaning:"The shade hangs near them — leaning in close overhead.", magic:"None extra — a simple, cozy image: the trees literally lean down to shade you, no walking required.", grammar:"Active participle, accusative, describing \"shades\" (ẓilāl).", why:"\"near\" instead of just \"above\" — it's not just shade far up in the sky, it's close enough to touch.", note:"Dāniya sounds like \"Donia,\" a name — imagine a friend leaning in close to shield you from the sun. That's this shade."},
  {ar:"ذُلِّلَتْ", tr:"Dhullilat", v:"14", root:"The \"lowliness/submission\" family — normally a negative word (humiliation).", meaning:"The fruit is made to \"submit itself\" — it lowers down right into your hand, no climbing, no thorns, no effort.", magic:"This is the twist worth remembering: a root that usually means humiliation is flipped completely positive here — the fruit humbly \"obeys\" you, serving you effortlessly.", grammar:"Passive verb — the fruit is made to hang low, someone (Allah) is doing this for them.", why:"Using a \"lowliness\" root for fruit serving you turns a normally negative idea into total luxury — everything bends to your will there.", note:"Dhullilat sounds like \"jelly-let\" — imagine fruit so ready-to-eat it's practically jelly, lowering itself right into your hand."},
  {ar:"قُطُوفُهَا", tr:"Quṭūfuhā", v:"14", root:"The \"picking/harvesting\" family.", meaning:"Its clusters of fruit, ready to be picked.", magic:"None extra — plain, functional vocabulary.", grammar:"Plural noun, subject of \"dhullilat\" (was made low).", why:"\"clusters\" (not single fruits) — abundance built into the word itself.", note:"Quṭūf sounds like \"cut-off\" — but nothing needs cutting; it's already ripe and reachable."},
  {ar:"يُطَافُ", tr:"Yuṭāf", v:"15", root:"The \"circling around\" family — the exact same root as Ṭawāf, circling the Kaʿbah.", meaning:"Servants \"circle around\" them, passing out drinks.", magic:"Using the Ṭawāf root here is a lovely, familiar echo — instead of you circling something sacred, in Paradise, service circles around you.", grammar:"Passive verb — \"it is circled\" — the doer (servants) is left unnamed, keeping focus on the guests being served.", why:"This specific root paints a picture you already know from Hajj/Umrah — circling, but now in your honor.", note:"Same root as Ṭawāf — you already know this word from walking around the Kaʿbah. Now flip it: things circle around you."},
  {ar:"آنِيَة / أَكْوَاب", tr:"āniya / akwāb", v:"15", root:"Cups specifically without handles — round, simple, elegant.", meaning:"Silver vessels and handle-less cups circulating among them.", magic:"None extra — just precise, tactile vocabulary painting the scene.", grammar:"Two plural nouns joined by \"and,\" both objects of the preposition \"with.\"", why:"Naming the specific cup-type (no handles) makes the image concrete, not vague.", note:"Akwāb sounds like \"a cup\" — say it fast, it practically is the English word."},
  {ar:"قَوَارِير", tr:"Qawārīr", v:"15–16", root:"Glass/crystal — clear, fragile, prized.", meaning:"Glasses that are somehow made from silver (v.16) — see the twist below.", magic:"The huge one: verse 15 says \"glass,\" verse 16 clarifies \"glass — made of silver.\" That combination — clear like glass, gleaming like silver — doesn't exist on Earth.", grammar:"The second mention (v.16) restates and specifies the first (v.15) — a classic Qur'anic technique: say it, then zoom in.", why:"Picking an \"impossible\" material is the whole point — it signals this place breaks your expectations.", note:"Picture a cup that's see-through like glass but shiny like a mirror at the same time.", more:["It's a material with no earthly name, showing Paradise runs on rules our world doesn't have.","Your brain can't quite draw it — and that's exactly the message."]},
  {ar:"قَدَّرُوهَا", tr:"Qaddarūhā", v:"16", root:"The \"exact measuring\" family — same root as \"qadar\" (precise decree/amount).", meaning:"The servers pour the exact amount each person wants — not a drop more, not a drop less.", magic:"This is the same root as Qiyāmah's \"qādirīn\" (able/precisely capable, 75:4). There, it was about God's power to reshape you precisely; here, it's about drinks measured out precisely for you.", grammar:"Past-tense verb, intensive form — \"they measured it out\" with real care and precision.", why:"\"measured exactly\" instead of just \"poured\" — it's personalized service, tailored to your exact wish.", note:"Same root as qādir (able/precise) from Qiyāmah — precision that once rebuilt your fingertips now pours your drink to the last drop.", more:["Same root, same theme of exactness, aimed at two different things."]},
  {ar:"زَنْجَبِيلًا", tr:"Zanjabīlan", v:"17", root:"Ginger — a spice loanword, absorbed into Arabic long ago.", meaning:"A drink flavored with ginger.", magic:"Genuine fun fact — \"zanjabīl\" and the English word \"ginger\" are actually cousins! Both trace back through Greek and Persian to the same ancient Sanskrit word.", grammar:"Indefinite accusative — the \"what\" of the mixture, same slot-pattern as \"kāfūran\" in v.5.", why:"Ginger was a prized, valuable spice in old Arabia — a flavor everyone already recognized as special.", note:"Zanjabīl = ginger, literally the same word family. Say \"zanjabīl...", more:["Arabic and English, holding hands across 3,000 years.","ginger\" back-to-back a few times — they'll fuse in your memory."]},
  {ar:"سَلْسَبِيلًا", tr:"Salsabīlan", v:"18", root:"A name, but built from the \"smooth/easy-flowing\" idea — water that goes down easily, no effort.", meaning:"The specific name of a spring in Paradise — Salsabīl.", magic:"This exact word appears nowhere else in the Qur'an, or really anywhere in Arabic before it — some scholars think it may even be a name coined just for this verse, built to sound like water flowing smoothly.", grammar:"A proper name, so it doesn't take the definite article \"al-\".", why:"The sound of the word mimics its meaning — say it slowly and it almost trickles off the tongue.", note:"Say \"sal-sa-BEEL\" slowly — hear how smoothly it flows off your tongue? That's the water it names."},
  {ar:"وِلْدَان / مُخَلَّدُونَ", tr:"Wildān / mukhalladūn", v:"19", root:"The \"eternal/forever\" family — same root as the popular name Khālid.", meaning:"Young serving-boys who are \"made eternal\" — never aging, forever youthful.", magic:"None extra — but the pairing is striking: children who never grow up, permanently.", grammar:"Passive participle — made eternal, not naturally so; it's a granted state.", why:"\"mukhalladūn\" instead of just \"young\" — stresses it's not a phase, it's forever.", note:"Khalada = eternal.", more:["If you know anyone named Khālid, that name literally means \"the eternal one\" — same root as these forever-young servants."]},
  {ar:"لُؤْلُؤًا / مَنْثُورًا", tr:"Luʾluʾan / manthūran", v:"19", root:"The \"scattering\" family — same root as \"nithār,\" scattering sweets or rose petals at a wedding.", meaning:"The young servants look like \"scattered pearls\" — so many, so casually strewn about, it's overwhelming abundance, not neat rows.", magic:"\"scattered\" (rather than \"arranged\" or \"many\") pictures overflowing generosity — like pearls tossed everywhere, not counted out one by one.", grammar:"\"manthūran\" is a passive participle modifying \"luʾluʾan\" — pearls in a scattered state.", why:"The casualness of \"scattered\" makes the abundance feel effortless, not rationed.", note:"Picture pearls tossed like confetti at a wedding — that's manthūr, and it's how many \"pearls\" (servants) there are."},
  {ar:"نَعِيمًا", tr:"Naʿīman", v:"20", root:"The \"blessing/comfort\" family — related to \"niʿmah\" (a favor/blessing) and even \"naʿam\" (yes!) — all sharing a sense of good, agreeable abundance.", meaning:"Wherever you look there, you see pure bliss.", magic:"None extra — a warm, all-encompassing word for total comfort.", grammar:"Accusative noun, object of \"you will see.\"", why:"Broader than \"happiness\" — naʿīm covers comfort, ease, and pleasure all at once.", note:"Naʿīm shares a root with \"naʿam\" (yes!) — Paradise is basically Allah saying \"yes\" to everything good, everywhere you look."},
  {ar:"سُندُس / إِسْتَبْرَق", tr:"Sundus / istabraq", v:"21", root:"Two loanwords for two types of silk — sundus is light, thin silk; istabraq is thick, heavy brocade.", meaning:"They wear both kinds — the full spectrum of silk luxury, light and heavy.", magic:"Pairing the light fabric with the heavy one covers every kind of luxury cloth in one line — nothing missing.", grammar:"Both nouns, joined by \"and,\" describing the same garments.", why:"Naming two specific, well-known luxury fabrics (not just \"fine clothes\") makes it vivid and concrete.", note:"Sundus = light silk (think a thin scarf), istabraq = thick brocade (think a heavy royal robe). Two ends of the luxury closet."},
  {ar:"أَسَاوِرَ", tr:"Asāwir", v:"21", root:"Bracelets.", meaning:"They're adorned with silver bracelets.", magic:"None extra — straightforward luxury vocabulary.", grammar:"Plural noun, object of \"they are adorned with.\"", why:"A specific accessory (not just \"jewelry\" in general) — paints a clear picture.", note:"Asāwir sounds like \"a silver\" — a silver bracelet, exactly what it describes."},
  {ar:"طَهُورًا", tr:"ṭahūran", v:"21", root:"The \"purity\" family — but this specific form means \"actively purifying,\" not just \"clean.\"", meaning:"Their Lord gives them a drink that doesn't just taste pure — it purifies them.", magic:"Compare \"ṭāhir\" (pure in itself) with \"ṭahūr\" (that actively cleanses others) — same distinction used for water in Islamic law: some water is merely clean, some water can purify.", grammar:"Intensive adjective pattern (faʿūl) — \"purifying,\" not just \"pure.\"", why:"Picking the active form of purity means this drink does something to you, not just describes itself.", note:"ṭahūr = pure and purifying — like water that doesn't just look clean, it cleans you as you drink it.", more:["This drink does the second, stronger thing."]},
  {ar:"سَعْيُكُمْ", tr:"Saʿyukum", v:"22", root:"The \"striving/hurrying\" family — the exact same root as Saʿī, the walk between Ṣafā and Marwah in Hajj/ʿUmrah.", meaning:"\"your effort/striving\" — everything you worked for in this life.", magic:"Using the Saʿī root here connects worldly effort to a ritual you already know — every hard walk of faith, big or small, counted.", grammar:"Possessive noun (\"your effort\") — subject of \"was appreciated\" in the next word.", why:"A familiar ritual-word makes \"effort\" feel sacred, not just generic hard work.", note:"Same root as Saʿī — every step you take striving in this life is like a mini Ṣafā-Marwah walk, and it's all being counted."},
  {ar:"مَشْكُورًا", tr:"Mashkūran", v:"22", root:"Same \"gratitude\" family as words #5 and #17 — third and final stop on this root's journey through the surah.", meaning:"\"your effort has been appreciated\" — nothing you did was wasted or ignored.", magic:"Track the thread: shākiran (v.3, a person being grateful) → shukūran (v.9, thanks they don't even want) → mashkūran (v.22, their effort finally being thanked).", grammar:"Passive participle — the effort receives thanks; it doesn't have to ask for it.", why:"Closing the \"gratitude\" thread with this exact root ties the whole surah's theme together in one word.", note:"This is stop #3 on the shukr-root journey (shākiran → shukūran → mashkūran) — say all three in a row and you'll never lose the thread.", more:["The surah quietly rewards the very quality it opened by testing."]},
  {ar:"تَنْزِيلًا", tr:"Tanzīlan", v:"23", root:"The \"coming down\" family — same root as \"nāzil\" (descending) and \"manzil\" (a stopping-place/home).", meaning:"\"We sent down the Qur'an\" — piece by piece, gradually, not all at once.", magic:"None extra — but the intensive form itself carries the \"gradual, careful\" delivery built in.", grammar:"Intensive verbal noun (Form II) — stresses a process of sending down, not a single drop.", why:"\"tanzīl\" (gradual sending) rather than a word for a single instant delivery — matches how the Qur'an actually came, over 23 years.", note:"Tanzīl sounds like \"tan-reveal\" — a slow reveal, chapter by chapter, not all at once."},
  {ar:"آثِمًا", tr:"āthiman", v:"24", root:"The \"sin\" family.", meaning:"\"don't obey a sinner\" among them.", magic:"None extra — plain, direct vocabulary.", grammar:"Indefinite accusative noun, object of \"obey.\"", why:"Straightforward and unambiguous — no soft-pedaling.", note:"āthim sounds like \"a-theme\" — the theme of someone who keeps doing wrong."},
  {ar:"بُكْرَةً / أَصِيلًا", tr:"Bukratan / aṣīlan", v:"25", root:"Simply \"morning\" and \"late afternoon/evening.\"", meaning:"\"remember your Lord morning and evening\" — the two bookends of the day.", magic:"Naming both ends of the day (not \"always\") is a merism — a shorthand for \"constantly, all day,\" using just two words to mean the whole stretch.", grammar:"Two accusative time-adverbs, standing in for \"at every point in the day.\"", why:"More vivid than \"always\" — you can literally picture the sun rising and setting on someone still remembering Allah.", note:"Bukra (morning) + aṣīl (evening) = sunrise to sunset, bookends of the day."},
  {ar:"الْعَاجِلَة", tr:"Al-ʿājilah", v:"27", root:"The \"hurrying/hasty\" family.", meaning:"\"the fleeting one\" — a nickname for this quick-passing world.", magic:"This is the exact same word as Surah Al-Qiyāmah 75:20 (\"you love al-ʿājilah\").", grammar:"Active participle used as a noun, standing in for \"this fast-moving life.\"", why:"Naming dunya \"the-hurrying-one\" builds the criticism right into its label.", note:"You already know this word — it's identical to Qiyāmah's \"al-ʿājilah.\" Same trap, same warning, showing up twice so you really get it.", more:["Two surahs, same warning, same word — that repetition is itself the lesson: this world rushes past, on repeat."]},
  {ar:"يَذَرُونَ", tr:"Yadharūna", v:"27", root:"The \"neglecting/abandoning\" family.", meaning:"\"they leave/forsake\" the Hereafter behind them.", magic:"This is the same special verb as Qiyāmah's \"tadharūna\" (75:21) — just switched from \"you forsake\" to \"they forsake.\" And it's a genuinely quirky verb: it has no past tense of its own in Arabic — only present and command forms exist; \"taraka\" fills in for the past.", grammar:"Must-know: this verb is defective — you'll never see \"wadhara\" (a past-tense form) in real use.", why:"\"yadharūna\" (neglectful abandonment) is stronger than a plain word for \"leave\"", note:"Same odd verb as Qiyāmah — remember it has no past tense, like a word that only exists in \"right now\" and \"keep doing it,\" never \"did it before.\"", more:["It implies carelessly walking away from something you should have kept."]},
  {ar:"ثَقِيلًا", tr:"Thaqīlan", v:"27", root:"The \"heavy\" family.", meaning:"A \"heavy\" Day — weighty, hard to bear.", magic:"A clean opposite-pair (tibāq) with \"al-ʿājilah\" just before it: this world is light and fast, the Hereafter is heavy and permanent. Speed versus weight.", grammar:"Indefinite accusative adjective, describing \"a Day.\"", why:"\"heavy\" contrasts perfectly with \"fleeting\" — light things are easy to ignore, heavy things you feel.", note:"ʿājilah = light and fast, thaqīl = heavy and slow.", more:["Picture a feather racing past a boulder — that's dunya versus ākhira in one image."]},
  {ar:"شَدَدْنَا / أَسْرَهُمْ", tr:"Shadadnā / asrahum", v:"28", root:"The \"binding\" family — the exact same root as \"asīr\" (captive) from word #15!", meaning:"\"We firmly bound/knit together their [bodily] frame.\"", magic:"The same root that meant prisoner in verse 8 shows up here describing your own body as something \"bound/knitted\" together by its Maker.", grammar:"Past-tense verb + noun from the same family — \"We strengthened/tightened their binding.\"", why:"Using the \"binding\" root for your own skeleton makes creation itself feel like careful, deliberate craftsmanship — you were tied together on purpose.", note:"Same root as asīr (the captive from word #15) — but now it's describing your own joints, knitted together by design, not punishment.", more:["It's not a punishment this time — it's structural engineering.","Same root, totally different feeling."]},
  {ar:"بَدَّلْنَا / أَمْثَالَهُمْ", tr:"Baddalnā / amthālahum", v:"28", root:"The \"likeness/resemblance\" family — same root as \"mathal\" (an example/parable).", meaning:"\"if We wanted, We could completely swap them for others just like them\" — total creative power, on standby.", magic:"None extra — but it's a quiet flex: the same Being who built you this carefully could just as easily replace you.", grammar:"Intensive verb (Form II, \"baddalnā\") + a \"cognate accusative\" (tabdīlan) right after in the verse.", why:"\"amthālahum\" (their likenesses) rather than just \"others\"", note:"Picture your Creator holding a remote control — \"shadadnā\" clicked \"build,\" and this verse reminds you He could click \"swap\" just as easily.", more:["Repeating the verb's own root as a noun, for extra emphasis, \"swap them — a total swap.\"","Stresses these replacements would look just like them, yet be totally different people."]},
  {ar:"تَذْكِرَةٌ", tr:"Tadhkiratun", v:"29", root:"The \"remembering/mentioning\" family — the exact same root as \"madhkūran\" in verse 1.", meaning:"\"this [Qur'an/surah] is a reminder\" — plain and simple.", magic:"This is the ring that closes the circle: verse 1 said man was once \"not even mentioned\" (madhkūran); verse 29 calls the whole message a \"reminder\" (tadhkirah) — same root, opening and closing the surah like bookends.", grammar:"Verbal noun (maṣdar), the predicate of \"this is.\"", why:"Using the same root that opened the surah to close it isn't an accident — it's a signature move, tying the whole message together.", note:"Zoom back to word #2 (madhkūran) — same root, now closing the loop.", more:["This mirrors the exact same trick you saw in Qiyāmah (a-yaḥsabu opening and closing that surah).","The surah that started with \"you weren't even mentioned\" ends by \"mentioning\" you the path forward."]},
  {ar:"اتَّخَذَ", tr:"Ittakhadha", v:"29", root:"The \"taking/adopting\" family.", meaning:"\"whoever wills may take a path to their Lord\" — an active, deliberate choice.", magic:"None extra — but the reflexive form makes it clear: this path isn't handed to you passively, you have to pick it up and adopt it yourself.", grammar:"Intensive reflexive form (Form VIII) — \"took for himself,\" an active, personal decision.", why:"\"ittakhadha\" (actively adopt) instead of just \"walk\" — the emphasis is on the choice, not the journey.", note:"Ittakhadha = actively grabbing a path, not drifting onto one by accident. It's a decision, not a mood."},
  {ar:"يُدْخِلُ / أَلِيمًا", tr:"Yudkhilu / alīman", v:"31", root:"The \"pain\" family.", meaning:"\"He admits whom He wills into His mercy\" — and for the wrongdoers, a painful punishment.", magic:"The surah's final move is a clean two-sided close: mercy for one path, pain for the other — closing exactly on the same \"two roads\" idea it opened with in verse 3 (grateful or ungrateful).", grammar:"Present-tense verb (yudkhilu) for an ongoing reality; \"alīman\" is a simple, strong adjective for \"painful.\"", why:"Ending on the plainest possible words (enter / painful) after all that rich vocabulary is deliberate.", note:"The whole surah — drop, test, generosity, garden, reminder — lands on two simple doors: one you're let into, one that hurts.", more:["The final message doesn't need decoration, just clarity."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:77, name:"Al-Mursalāt", ar:"المرسلات", en:"Those Sent Forth", verses:50, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Five oaths of moving air, then one refrain struck ten times like a bell: woe, that Day, to the deniers.",
  words:[
  {ar:"الْمُرْسَلَاتِ", tr:"al-mursalāt", v:"1", rk:"r-s-l", root:"√r-s-l — sending forth smoothly, dispatching in sequence", meaning:"those sent out one after another — classically understood as the winds, and by some as angels sent on mission", magic:"The passive participle keeps the spotlight on the One who sends them, while the plural oath opening creates motion from the very first word.", grammar:"Passive participle; sound feminine plural.", why:"It suggests directed sending, not random movement.", note:"Same family as risālah — a message being sent."},
  {ar:"عُرْفًا", tr:"ʿurfan", v:"1", rk:"ʿ-r-f", root:"√ʿ-r-f — something recognized, ordered, familiar; also used for a horse's mane in successive strands", meaning:"in ordered succession, one after another", magic:"It gives the oath a sense of orderly ranks, or winds coming in recognizable waves.", grammar:"Accusative adverbial expression describing the manner of the sending.", why:"It adds order and continuity, not chaos.", note:"Picture wave after wave, like neat strands of a mane."},
  {ar:"الْعَاصِفَاتِ", tr:"al-ʿāṣifāt", v:"2", rk:"ʿ-ṣ-f", root:"√ʿ-ṣ-f — violent blasting, stormy force", meaning:"those that blow fiercely", magic:"The sound and the sense both feel forceful — the oath rises from orderly sending to powerful sweeping.", grammar:"Active participle; sound feminine plural.", why:"It is stronger than a plain word for blowing; it means a storm-like blast.", note:"Think of a wind so strong it whips everything aside."},
  {ar:"عَصْفًا", tr:"ʿaṣfan", v:"2", rk:"ʿ-ṣ-f", root:"The same √ʿ-ṣ-f field of violent scattering and storm-force", meaning:"a fierce blasting, violently", magic:"Pairing the participle with its verbal noun intensifies the action: not merely “blowing,” but “blowing with full force.”", grammar:"Verbal noun in the accusative, reinforcing the manner and intensity.", why:"It piles emphasis onto the previous oath word.", note:"The same root twice means the storm is really storming."},
  {ar:"النَّاشِرَاتِ", tr:"al-nāshirāt", v:"3", rk:"n-sh-r", root:"√n-sh-r — spreading out, unfolding, scattering wide", meaning:"those that spread and disperse", magic:"After the stormy force comes wide spreading — as if the air opens and disperses what was packed together.", grammar:"Active participle; sound feminine plural.", why:"It gives a sense of opening out over a wide range.", note:"Think of a folded cloth being opened and spread."},
  {ar:"نَشْرًا", tr:"nashran", v:"3", rk:"n-sh-r", root:"The same √n-sh-r field of spreading and unfolding", meaning:"a full spreading-out, expansively", magic:"Like ʿāṣifāt / ʿaṣfan before it, the pair strengthens the motion and makes the sequence vivid.", grammar:"Verbal noun in the accusative for emphasis.", why:"It turns the image from a simple action into an all-out dispersal.", note:"نَشَرَ is like opening and spreading pages across a table."},
  {ar:"الْفَارِقَاتِ", tr:"al-fāriqāt", v:"4", rk:"f-r-q", root:"√f-r-q — separating, distinguishing, making a clear difference", meaning:"those that separate decisively", magic:"The surah moves from motion in nature to separation and sorting — a perfect lead-in to revelation and judgment.", grammar:"Active participle; sound feminine plural.", why:"It suggests clear distinction, not vague difference.", note:"Same family as furqān — truth being separated from falsehood."},
  {ar:"فَرْقًا", tr:"farqan", v:"4", rk:"f-r-q", root:"The same √f-r-q field of cutting apart and distinguishing", meaning:"a decisive separating, distinctly", magic:"The maṣdar adds force: this separation is sharp and unmistakable.", grammar:"Verbal noun in the accusative, intensifying the act.", why:"It turns “separating” into “separating with full decisiveness.”", note:"فَرْق = a line drawn clearly between two sides."},
  {ar:"الْمُلْقِيَاتِ", tr:"al-mulqiyāt", v:"5", rk:"l-q-y", root:"√l-q-y — to cast, throw, deliver, or convey something so it reaches its target", meaning:"those that cast down or deliver the reminder", magic:"After sending, blasting, spreading and separating, the sequence lands at revelation being delivered.", grammar:"Active participle; sound feminine plural.", why:"It suggests precise delivery, not a vague arrival.", note:"Think of a messenger placing an important letter exactly where it must land."},
  {ar:"ذِكْرًا", tr:"dhikran", v:"5", rk:"dh-k-r", root:"√dh-k-r — remembering, mentioning, bringing something to mind", meaning:"a reminder or revealed message", magic:"Revelation is called dhikr because it wakes up what the heart should already remember.", grammar:"Verbal noun in the accusative.", why:"It is not just information but a reminder that stirs memory and awareness.", note:"Dhikr is what pulls a forgotten truth back into your mind."},
  {ar:"عُذْرًا أَوْ نُذْرًا", tr:"ʿudhran / nudhran", v:"6", rk:"ʿ-dh-r", root:"√ʿ-dh-r = excuse, clearing blame; √n-dh-r = warning, alerting to danger", meaning:"as excuse and as warning — revelation leaves no one able to say “I never knew”", magic:"The pair covers both sides: truth excuses the sincere and warns the stubborn.", grammar:"Two accusative verbal nouns, paired for balance.", why:"Together they show revelation completes the proof.", note:"One removes excuse; the other rings the alarm."},
  {ar:"أُقِّتَتْ", tr:"uqqitat", v:"11", rk:"w-q-t", root:"√w-q-t — time, timing, fixing an appointed moment", meaning:"the messengers were assigned to a fixed time", magic:"Everything in the surah moves toward a scheduled final appointment.", grammar:"Passive Form II perfect; feminine singular agreeing with rusul as a non-human plural.", why:"It emphasizes appointment and precision, not randomness.", note:"Same family as waqt — a time set on a calendar. Ḥafṣ reads it with hamza (uqqitat); other readings keep the wāw (wuqqitat). Same root either way."},
  {ar:"أُجِّلَتْ", tr:"ujjilat", v:"12", rk:"ʾ-j-l", root:"√ʾ-j-l — term, deadline, postponement, appointed limit", meaning:"delayed or deferred", magic:"Suspense rises — the Day was not canceled, only held to its appointed time.", grammar:"Passive Form II perfect.", why:"It signals postponement with purpose, not absence.", note:"An exam date can be postponed, but it still comes."},
  {ar:"يَوْمِ الْفَصْلِ", tr:"yawm al-faṣl", v:"13", rk:"f-ṣ-l", root:"√f-ṣ-l — separation, decisive cutting apart, making a matter clear", meaning:"the Day of Decision / Separation — truth from falsehood, believers from deniers", magic:"The surah's opening “separating” words culminate here in the ultimate separation.", grammar:"Iḍāfa phrase: “day of the separation / decision.”", why:"Faṣl means a decisive final sorting, not a vague judgment.", note:"This is the day every mixed-up case gets separated into its proper file."},
  {ar:"فُرِجَتْ", tr:"furijat", v:"9", rk:"f-r-j", root:"√f-r-j — to open a gap, to part, to relieve by making space", meaning:"the sky is split open", magic:"The same root gives faraj — relief. A gap opening is rescue in one setting and collapse in another.", grammar:"Passive perfect, feminine singular — the sky does not part itself; it is parted.", why:"Not torn or shattered: parted. The seam opens, and what was sealed is now a doorway.", note:"Think of heavy curtains being drawn apart — the gap is the point."},
  {ar:"نُسِفَتْ", tr:"nusifat", v:"10", rk:"n-s-f", root:"√n-s-f — to blow away, scatter, uproot thoroughly", meaning:"the mountains are blasted away", magic:"Mountains — symbols of stability — are turned into something removable.", grammar:"Passive perfect, feminine singular for the non-human plural subject.", why:"It shows total uprooting, not mere shaking.", note:"Even mountains get swept off the table."},
  {ar:"وَيْلٌ", tr:"waylun", v:"15", root:"A word of ruin, woe, and threatening doom", meaning:"woe, destruction, or dreadful loss", magic:"Its repetition becomes the surah's drumbeat.", grammar:"Indefinite noun used for terrifying force.", why:"It is short, heavy and memorable — perfect as a refrain.", note:"Every time the refrain hits, it lands like a warning bell."},
  {ar:"الْمُكَذِّبِينَ", tr:"al-mukadhdhibīn", v:"15", rk:"k-dh-b", root:"√k-dh-b — lying, calling something false, rejecting truth", meaning:"those who deny and call the message a lie", magic:"The surah's refrain targets not mere doubters but active deniers.", grammar:"Active participle plural, Form II sense.", why:"It points to people who brand truth as false.", note:"Not just “unconvinced” — they stamp the truth with “lie.”"},
  {ar:"نُهْلِكِ", tr:"nuhlik", v:"16", rk:"h-l-k", root:"√h-l-k — destruction, perishing, ruin", meaning:"We destroyed", magic:"The past nations are used as evidence for future judgment.", grammar:"Form IV verb from ahlaka.", why:"It carries complete ruin, not a small punishment.", note:"The Qur'an often points backward in history so you fear what lies ahead."},
  {ar:"الْأَوَّلِينَ / الْآخِرِينَ", tr:"al-awwalīn / al-ākhirīn", v:"16–17", root:"first / last · earlier / later", meaning:"the former peoples and the later peoples", magic:"The pairing sweeps across history — nobody escapes the pattern.", grammar:"Two plural adjectives used as nouns.", why:"The pair covers all generations, early and late.", note:"Old nations are not museum pieces; later ones can follow the same path."},
  {ar:"نُتْبِعُهُمُ", tr:"nutbiʿuhum", v:"17", rk:"t-b-ʿ", root:"√t-b-ʿ — following, coming after, making something follow", meaning:"We make the later ones follow them", magic:"History is shown as a chain of consequences.", grammar:"Form IV present verb with object pronoun.", why:"It means causing one group to follow another in fate.", note:"One line of deniers follows another like dominoes."},
  {ar:"نَخْلُقْكُم", tr:"nakhluqkum", v:"20", rk:"kh-l-q", root:"√kh-l-q — creating, shaping, bringing into existence with proportion", meaning:"We created you", magic:"The argument shifts from destroyed nations to your own creation.", grammar:"Present verb with direct object pronoun “you.”", why:"Creation itself becomes proof of accountability.", note:"The One who made you can surely judge you."},
  {ar:"مَاءٍ مَهِينٍ", tr:"māʾin mahīn", v:"20", rk:"m-h-n", root:"māʾ = water; √m-h-n = lowly, slight, humble", meaning:"a humble fluid — the human origin", magic:"Arrogance is crushed by reminding man of his tiny beginning.", grammar:"Noun + adjective phrase in the genitive.", why:"Mahīn humbles human pride.", note:"Proud man began as a drop."},
  {ar:"قَرَارٍ مَكِينٍ", tr:"qarārin makīn", v:"21", rk:"q-r-r", root:"√q-r-r = settling, resting; √m-k-n = firmness, stability, secure placement", meaning:"a secure lodging place — the womb", magic:"Your first home was hidden, settled and protected.", grammar:"Noun + adjective phrase in the genitive.", why:"It gives both rest and security in two compact words.", note:"Before walking the earth, you were settled in a silent, secure room."},
  {ar:"قَدَرْنَا", tr:"qadarnā", v:"23", rk:"q-d-r", root:"√q-d-r — measuring, decreeing, determining with exactness, and having power", meaning:"We measured / determined", magic:"The same root carries both exact measure and full ability.", grammar:"Perfect verb, first person plural.", why:"It shows creation is precise, not accidental.", note:"Qadar means nothing about you was thrown together."},
  {ar:"الْقَادِرُونَ", tr:"al-qādirūn", v:"23", rk:"q-d-r", root:"The able, fully capable ones — from the same √q-d-r field", meaning:"those perfectly able — here, God speaking in majesty", magic:"Measure and power meet in one root family.", grammar:"Active participle plural.", why:"Ability here means precise power, not brute force alone.", note:"The One who measures exactly is the One fully able."},
  {ar:"كِفَاتًا", tr:"kifātan", v:"25", rk:"k-f-t", root:"√k-f-t — gathering, enclosing, holding things together", meaning:"a place that gathers and contains — the earth", magic:"Earth holds both the living on top and the dead within.", grammar:"Accusative noun, complement to “We made the earth.”", why:"It captures the earth as a container and collector.", note:"The earth is a giant cradle above and a giant chamber below."},
  {ar:"أَحْيَاءً وَأَمْوَاتًا", tr:"aḥyāʾan / amwātan", v:"25–26", root:"life and death", meaning:"the living and the dead", magic:"One earth gathers both, making the scene broad and sobering.", grammar:"Two accusative plurals paired in balance.", why:"The pair shows the earth's total embrace.", note:"The same ground carries footsteps and graves."},
  {ar:"رَوَاسِيَ", tr:"rawāsiya", v:"27", rk:"r-s-w", root:"√r-s-w — firm anchoring, staying fixed, like a ship moored in place", meaning:"firmly anchored mountains", magic:"Mountains are pictured as stabilizers of the earth.", grammar:"Broken plural used as object of “placed.”", why:"It highlights steadiness, not just height.", note:"Mountains are like pegs or anchors holding the scene steady."},
  {ar:"شَامِخَاتٍ", tr:"shāmikhāt", v:"27", rk:"sh-m-kh", root:"√sh-m-kh — rising high, towering proudly", meaning:"lofty, soaring", magic:"Paired with rawāsi, the mountains are both stable and high.", grammar:"Feminine sound plural adjective.", why:"It adds vertical majesty to firmness.", note:"These are not small hills; they lift their heads high."},
  {ar:"فُرَاتًا", tr:"furātan", v:"27", rk:"f-r-t", root:"Sweet, pleasant, easy to drink", meaning:"sweet, refreshing water", magic:"After mountains comes water — stability and mercy together.", grammar:"Accusative adjective describing water.", why:"Not just drinkable water, but delightfully sweet water.", note:"Furāt is the opposite of bitter and salty — water you want to keep drinking."},
  {ar:"انطَلِقُوا", tr:"inṭaliqū", v:"29", rk:"ṭ-l-q", root:"√ṭ-l-q — setting out, being released, moving off decisively", meaning:"Go on! Move toward what you used to deny", magic:"A sharp command launches the scene into punishment.", grammar:"Imperative plural, Form VII sense.", why:"It gives force and momentum.", note:"Once judgment starts, the command sends them straight onward."},
  {ar:"شُعَبٍ", tr:"shuʿabin", v:"30", rk:"sh-ʿ-b", root:"√sh-ʿ-b — branching, splitting into divisions", meaning:"branches or columns — in the phrase “three branching columns” of smoke", magic:"The smoke is not one solid shelter but divided, deceptive layers.", grammar:"Plural noun in the genitive after dhī.", why:"It makes the image segmented and eerie.", note:"Three branches of smoke, but none of them truly protects."},
  {ar:"ظَلِيلٍ", tr:"ẓalīl", v:"31", rk:"ẓ-l-l", root:"√ẓ-l-l — shade, shelter, cool cover", meaning:"shading, protective", magic:"The verse denies it — this is a “shade” that gives no real shade.", grammar:"Adjective in the genitive after lā.", why:"It sharpens the irony of false shelter.", note:"A cloud of smoke that looks like shade but fails when you need it."},
  {ar:"شَرَرٍ", tr:"shararin", v:"32", rk:"sh-r-r", root:"√sh-r-r — sparks or fiery fragments flung off", meaning:"sparks from the Fire", magic:"The punishment is pictured through flying pieces, not just a single flame.", grammar:"Singular collective noun in the genitive.", why:"It shows danger multiplying and flying outward.", note:"Not one flame — showers of fiery fragments."},
  {ar:"كَالْقَصْرِ", tr:"kal-qaṣr", v:"32", rk:"q-ṣ-r", root:"√q-ṣ-r — a large structure, palace, or huge trunk-like mass", meaning:"like massive palaces — describing the size of the sparks", magic:"A spark is normally tiny; this simile makes it shockingly huge.", grammar:"Preposition kāf for simile + noun in the genitive.", why:"It exaggerates size in a vivid, unforgettable way.", note:"The sparks are as if buildings are being thrown."},
  {ar:"جِمَالَتٌ صُفْرٌ", tr:"jimālatun ṣufrun", v:"33", root:"jimālah = camels; ṣufr = yellowish", meaning:"like yellow camels — another simile for the sparks; some explain it as thick yellow ropes", magic:"The verse stacks one simile after another for motion, colour and size.", grammar:"Plural noun + plural adjective phrase.", why:"Camels give a familiar desert image of long, moving, swaying shapes.", note:"Giant yellow camel-like streaks of fire."},
  {ar:"لَا يَنطِقُونَ", tr:"lā yanṭiqūn", v:"35", rk:"n-ṭ-q", root:"√n-ṭ-q — speaking, articulating, making words come out", meaning:"they will not speak", magic:"The deniers who once talked back are now mute.", grammar:"Negated present plural verb.", why:"Speechlessness shows helplessness.", note:"Arguments disappear when the final truth arrives."},
  {ar:"لَا يُؤْذَنُ لَهُمْ", tr:"lā yuʾdhanu lahum", v:"36", rk:"ʾ-dh-n", root:"√ʾ-dh-n — permission, allowing, authorization", meaning:"no permission will be given to them", magic:"Not only do they not speak — they are not even allowed to speak.", grammar:"Passive present verb with indirect object.", why:"It intensifies helplessness from silence to barred speech.", note:"The courtroom is so final they do not even get the floor."},
  {ar:"كَيْدٌ", tr:"kayd", v:"39", rk:"k-y-d", root:"√k-y-d — planning, plotting, arranging a scheme", meaning:"a plan or stratagem", magic:"The challenge is sarcastic — “if you have a plan, use it now.”", grammar:"Verbal noun / noun.", why:"It exposes the emptiness of human scheming before divine judgment.", note:"All clever plots run out of road at the Last Day."},
  {ar:"الْمُتَّقِينَ", tr:"muttaqīn", v:"41", rk:"w-q-y", root:"√w-q-y — guarding, shielding, protecting yourself", meaning:"the God-conscious, those who guarded themselves through taqwā", magic:"After many refrains of warning, the surah opens a cool garden scene for the protected ones.", grammar:"Plural active participle, Form VIII sense.", why:"Taqwā is protective, like building a shield between yourself and harm.", note:"A muttaqī is someone who lives with a spiritual shield up."},
  {ar:"ظِلَالٍ وَعُيُونٍ", tr:"ẓilāl / ʿuyūn", v:"41", rk:"ẓ-l-l", root:"shade and springs", meaning:"shades and flowing springs", magic:"Earlier there was a false, useless shade; here there is real shade and real refreshment.", grammar:"Two broken plurals joined by “and.”", why:"The pair paints comfort through climate and water together.", note:"Hell had smoke without shelter; paradise has shade with water."},
  {ar:"فَوَاكِهَ", tr:"fawākih", v:"42", rk:"f-k-h", root:"Fruits, pleasant produce, delightful things enjoyed", meaning:"fruits of many kinds", magic:"The plural suggests variety and abundance.", grammar:"Broken plural in the accusative.", why:"Fruit means ease, sweetness and ready enjoyment.", note:"Not one fruit basket — a whole world of choice."},
  {ar:"هَنِيئًا", tr:"hanīʾan", v:"43", rk:"h-n-ʾ", root:"Easy to swallow, pleasant, wholesome, enjoyed without trouble", meaning:"enjoyably, in full ease", magic:"Food is not just allowed — it is enjoyed without fear or regret.", grammar:"Adverbial accusative expressing manner.", why:"It adds comfort and inner ease, not only taste.", note:"Food that goes down with zero worry and total peace."},
  {ar:"نَجْزِي", tr:"najzī", v:"44", rk:"j-z-y", root:"√j-z-y — repaying, rewarding, giving back what is due", meaning:"We reward / repay", magic:"The surah balances warning with reward.", grammar:"Present verb, first person plural.", why:"Jazāʾ is measured repayment, perfectly fitting the deed.", note:"Every act comes back wearing its true shape."},
  {ar:"الْمُحْسِنِينَ", tr:"al-muḥsinīn", v:"44", rk:"ḥ-s-n", root:"√ḥ-s-n — beauty, goodness, excellence", meaning:"those who do good beautifully and well", magic:"Not just avoiding wrong, but actively doing good with excellence.", grammar:"Active participle plural, Form IV sense from aḥsana.", why:"Iḥsān means beautiful excellence, higher than bare minimum goodness.", note:"A muḥsin is the person who does the right thing in the best way."},
  {ar:"ارْكَعُوا", tr:"irkaʿū", v:"48", rk:"r-k-ʿ", root:"√r-k-ʿ — bending, bowing low in humility", meaning:"Bow! — a command to submit in prayer", magic:"One short command exposes the stubbornness of those who still refuse.", grammar:"Imperative plural.", why:"Rukūʿ is a visible act of surrender.", note:"The back bends when the ego should bend."},
  {ar:"حَدِيثٍ", tr:"ḥadīthin", v:"50", rk:"ḥ-d-th", root:"√ḥ-d-th — speech, narration, something newly brought forth", meaning:"discourse, message, statement", magic:"The surah ends with a devastating question — if not this Qur'an, then what message will they believe?", grammar:"Singular noun in the genitive after “which.”", why:"It closes on the plainest possible word for speech, leaving no escape.", note:"If truth this clear is rejected, what talk could ever work?"}
  ]
},
{
  num:79, name:"An-Nāziʿāt", ar:"النازعات", en:"Those Who Pull Out", verses:46, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"The surah panics first, then calms down to explain. Terror in section one; a patient history lesson in section two. The rhyme carries the mood before you even understand the words.",
  special:["It has two completely different rhymes, and the switch is the point.","Verses 1–14: endings in -ah / -irah — rājifah, rādifah, wājifah, khāshiʿah, ḥāfirah, nakhirah, khāsirah, sāhirah. Sharp, quick, agitated. It sounds like a heart pounding. - Verses 15–46: endings in -ā (alif maqṣūrah) — Mūsā, ṭuwā, ṭaghā, tazakkā, takhshā, ʿaṣā, yasʿā, nādā, aʿlā, ūlā… Long, open, unhurried. It sounds like a story being told calmly."],
  big:["Five mysterious oaths (v.1–5) — beings who pull out, untie, glide, race, and manage. Most say: angels, especially those taking souls.","The Two Blasts (v.6–14) — the first shakes, the second follows right behind. Hearts pound, eyes drop.","The Mockery (v.10–14) — \"We'll be brought back after we're crumbled bones?\" Answer: one shout, and you're standing on open ground.","The Mūsā Flashback (v.15–26) — the whole story of Pharaoh, compressed into 12 short verses. You've seen this movie before.","Which is harder — you or the sky? (v.27–33) — the argument from creation.","The Overwhelming (v.34–41) — two people, two choices, two destinations.","\"When is it?\" (v.42–46) — That's not your department. You're just the warner."],
  words:[
  {ar:"النَّازِعَاتِ", tr:"An-nāziʿāt", v:"1", root:"The \"pulling out / wrenching away\" family — nazaʿa = to yank something out of where it's lodged.", meaning:"\"By those who pull out violently.\"", magic:"The verb is physical and forceful — the kind of pulling that meets resistance. Nothing is being handed over; something is being extracted.", grammar:"Active participle, feminine plural (fāʿilāt pattern).", why:"Nazaʿa over a gentle word for \"taking\" — because for some souls, leaving is a wrench.", note:"Nizāʿ = a tug-of-war. Picture something being pulled out against its will, like a deep-rooted weed that fights back.", more:["Modern Arabic: nizāʿ = a conflict (a tug-of-war), nazʿ = removal.","And notice: this is oath #1, so the surah opens on a struggle.","The described noun is deleted — Arabic often drops the obvious noun and lets the adjective stand alone."]},
  {ar:"غَرْقًا", tr:"Gharqan", v:"1", root:"The \"drowning / submerging\" family — same root as ghariqa (to drown) and ighrāq (immersion).", meaning:"Pulling out from the very depths — reaching all the way in.", magic:"A stunning istiʿāra. To describe how deep the pulling goes, the Qur'an uses drowning language — the extractor plunges in so far it's like submerging.", grammar:"Mafʿūl muṭlaq (cognate accusative) — a verbal noun reinforcing the participle. Arabic's underline key.", why:"It measures depth, not force — the soul is drawn from the deepest place in the body.", note:"Gharq = drowning. Picture someone reaching shoulder-deep into water to grab something at the very bottom.", more:["Not \"pulled hard\" but \"pulled from drowning-depth.\"","You'll see this four times in five verses here."]},
  {ar:"النَّاشِطَاتِ نَشْطًا", tr:"An-nāshiṭāt nashṭan", v:"2", root:"The \"untying a knot\" family. Old Arabic: nashaṭa'l-ʿuqdah = he untied the knot.", meaning:"\"and those who draw out gently / untie.\"", magic:"This is the surah's most beautiful contrast, and it's easy to miss. Verse 1 = violent wrenching. Verse 2 = gently untying a knot. Same job, two completely different experiences.", grammar:"Another cognate accusative (nashṭan) — matching the rhythm of v.1.", why:"The knot-image conveys ease without effort — untying, not cutting.", note:"V.1 = ripping a knot apart. v.2 = untying it. Same knot. Two ways to leave. That's the pair — hold onto it.", more:["From that came nashāṭ = energy, briskness (a freed thing moves lively).","The soul of one person is torn out; the soul of another is slipped free like a loosened knot.","Two deaths, two verbs, back to back — and the Qur'an doesn't say which is which."]},
  {ar:"السَّابِحَاتِ سَبْحًا", tr:"As-sābiḥāt sabḥan", v:"3", root:"The \"swimming / gliding effortlessly\" family — sabaḥa = to swim; sibāḥah = swimming.", meaning:"\"and those who glide [through space].\"", magic:"The motion upgrades. Verses 1–2 were pulling (effortful). Now: swimming — smooth, weightless, unresisted. Once the soul is free, the journey becomes gliding.", grammar:"Third cognate accusative in a row — the repetition is deliberate rhythm, like oars in water.", why:"Swimming implies a medium you move through easily — the perfect verb for beings crossing the heavens.", note:"You say subḥānallāh every day — same root. Picture the angels swimming through the sky the way you'd glide underwater.", more:["And the noble cousin: tasbīḥ (glorifying Allah) — from the idea of moving swiftly away from any flaw attributed to Him.","The oaths are telling a sequence."]},
  {ar:"السَّابِقَاتِ سَبْقًا", tr:"As-sābiqāt sabqan", v:"4", root:"The \"racing ahead / getting there first\" family — sabaqa = to precede; musābaqah = a race/competition; sābiq = a front-runner.", meaning:"\"and those who race ahead.\"", magic:"The escalation completes: pull → untie → glide → RACE. Speed keeps increasing across four verses.", grammar:"Fourth cognate accusative.", why:"Racing implies urgency and precedence — the command is being delivered fast.", note:"Sābiḥāt (ح) = swimming. sābiqāt (ق) = racing. One letter apart — and the second is faster. Classic mix-up: flag it.", more:["And note the near-identical sound of sābiḥāt / sābiqāt — one letter apart (ق / ح) — a jinās (near-pun) that makes the pair feel like one accelerating motion.","Notice also the particle shift: v.1–3 use وَ (and), but v.4–5 use فَ (then, immediately).","The \"fa\" signals sequence — these last two follow directly from the first three."]},
  {ar:"الْمُدَبِّرَاتِ أَمْرًا", tr:"Al-mudabbirāt amran", v:"5", root:"The \"back / rear end\" family — dubur = the back of something.", meaning:"\"then those who arrange the affair\" — carrying out the command.", magic:"The perfect closing oath. The sequence ends not in chaos but in administration — everything is being managed.", grammar:"Form II participle, feminine plural.", why:"Tadbīr means purposeful management, not random force — the universe is being run, not just happening.", note:"Tadabbur = what you do when you reflect on the Qur'an — thinking through to the end. Same root.", more:["From that: tadbīr = managing an affair by looking at how it ends up.","And the one you know: tadabbur — deep reflection on the Qur'an, i.e.","thinking through to the consequences.","And here's the internal gem: this root comes back in v.22, where Pharaoh \"adbara\" — turned his back.","The angels use their \"back-sense\" to manage; Pharaoh uses his to flee.","Same root, opposite postures.","Amran here is a direct object (not a cognate accusative) — the pattern breaks on the fifth oath, which is exactly how you know the list has landed.","The angels are the universe's project managers."]},
  {ar:"تَرْجُفُ / الرَّاجِفَةُ", tr:"Tarjufu / ar-rājifah", v:"6", root:"The \"violent trembling / quaking\" family. Related: irjāf = spreading alarming rumors — because they make hearts tremble.", meaning:"\"the Day the Quaker quakes\" — the first blast of the trumpet.", magic:"The cognate structure (tarjufu'r-rājifah — \"the shaker shakes\") is Arabic at maximum emphasis: the verb and the subject are the same root, hammering the idea.", grammar:"Rājifah is an active participle, feminine, used as a name for the event. The described noun is deleted.", why:"Naming the event \"The Quaker\" makes it an entity, not just an occurrence.", note:"Irjāf = rumors that make hearts shake. The first blast is The Shaker — and everything shakes.", more:["English can't do this gracefully; Arabic does it constantly."]},
  {ar:"الرَّادِفَةُ", tr:"Ar-rādifah", v:"7", root:"This one is a gift.", meaning:"\"the Follower follows it\" — the second blast.", magic:"The image is precise and terrifying. The second blast isn't just later — it's riding pillion on the first one, on the same animal. No gap.", grammar:"Another feminine participle used as a name; parallel construction to rājifah, which is why they rhyme.", why:"Rādifah conveys immediate succession in a way that \"the second one\" never could.", note:"Ridf = the person sitting behind you on the same bike. Blast two is riding on the back of blast one. That's how close.", more:["Ridf = the person riding behind you on the same mount (like a passenger on the back of a camel or motorbike).","Radifa = to follow immediately behind.","Compare: if a friend rides behind you on one bike, how far apart are you?","That's the spacing between the two blasts."]},
  {ar:"وَاجِفَةٌ", tr:"Wājifah", v:"8", root:"The \"rapid throbbing / palpitating\" family — wajafa = to beat fast, to pound.", meaning:"\"Hearts, that Day, will be pounding.\"", magic:"Notice the rhyme chain: rājifah → rādifah → wājifah. Three verses, three words that sound almost identical (ر-ا-ج / ر-ا-د / و-ا-ج).", grammar:"Active participle, feminine (agreeing with qulūb, hearts).", why:"Wājifah is a physical, medical word for a racing heart — not \"afraid\" (an emotion) but the body's actual response.", note:"Rājifah → rādifah → wājifah. Say them fast. The shaking travels from the earth into your chest through the rhyme.", more:["The earth shakes → the second blast follows → your heart shakes.","The sound itself transmits the tremor from the cosmos into your chest.","This is one of the finest sound-effects in the Qur'an.","Note qulūbun is indefinite — \"some hearts,\" leaving room for the other kind."]},
  {ar:"خَاشِعَةٌ", tr:"Khāshiʿah", v:"9", root:"The \"humbled, lowered, submissive\" family — and you know it: khushūʿ, the humility you're meant to have in ṣalāh.", meaning:"\"their eyes downcast.\"", magic:"A sharp contrast in the same body: the heart is pounding wildly (wājifah, v.8), but the eyes are utterly still and lowered (khāshiʿah, v.9).", grammar:"Participle agreeing with abṣār (eyes); abṣāruhā — the pronoun refers back to the hearts.", why:"Khushūʿ is the same word used for worship posture — a bitter irony: they lower their eyes now, when it no longer counts.", note:"Khushūʿ = what you're supposed to have in prayer. On that Day, everyone finally has it — too late.", more:["Chaos inside, frozen outside — exactly what terror looks like."]},
  {ar:"مَرْدُودُونَ", tr:"Mardūdūn", v:"10", root:"The \"returning / sending back\" family — radda = to send back; mardūd = something returned (in commerce: a returned item!).", meaning:"\"They say: Are we really going to be sent back?\"", magic:"The passive form does the sneering. Not \"will we return?\" but \"will we be returned?\" — like a package. Their mockery treats resurrection as absurd logistics.", grammar:"Passive participle, plural.", why:"The passive strips them of agency — which is, ironically, exactly true.", note:"Mardūd = a returned item (like a package sent back to the shop). They're mocking: \"We're getting shipped back?\"", more:["Note the doubled question-marker: a-innā la-mardūdūn — hamzah of questioning + inna + lām of emphasis = \"are we REALLY, TRULY going to be…?\" Layered sarcasm."]},
  {ar:"الْحَافِرَةِ", tr:"Al-ḥāfirah", v:"10", root:"The \"digging / hollowing out\" family — ḥafara = to dig; ḥufrah = a pit.", meaning:"Two classical readings, both good: (a) our former state — from the idiom rajaʿa ʿalā ḥāfiratih, \"he went back the way he came\" (retracing your own dug-in footprints); or (b) the grave — the dug hollow.", magic:"The footprint-image is lovely: your path has left hollows in the sand, and going back means stepping into your own old prints.", grammar:"Genitive after fī.", why:"ḥāfirah is unusual and slightly archaic — it gives their mockery a smug, literary tone.", note:"ḥafara = to dig. Picture walking backwards into your own footprints in the sand — that's \"returning to the ḥāfirah.\"", more:["Either reading, it's a rare, hard word — chosen partly because deniers were being showy with their sarcasm."]},
  {ar:"نَخِرَةً", tr:"Nakhirah", v:"11", root:"The \"hollow, crumbling, rotted\" family — nakhira = to become hollow and brittle.", meaning:"\"when we are crumbling bones?\"", magic:"The sound-origin is the horror. Nakhirah bones are so hollow the wind whistles through them. It's not \"old bones\" — it's bones that have become flutes.", grammar:"Adjective for ʿiẓāman (bones), accusative, khabar of kunnā.", why:"Maximum decay — the strongest possible version of their objection.", note:"Nakhīr = the whistle of wind through something hollow. Picture bones so rotted the wind plays them like a flute. That's the word.", more:["Related: nakhīr = the snoring/whistling sound wind makes passing through a hollow thing.","They chose the most extreme word available to make resurrection sound ridiculous."]},
  {ar:"كَرَّةٌ خَاسِرَةٌ", tr:"Karratun khāsirah", v:"12", root:"Karrah — the \"turning back / charging again\" family. War idiom: al-karr wa'l-farr = charge and retreat.", meaning:"\"They said: That, then, would be a losing return.\"", magic:"Karrah is battle vocabulary — a charge back into the fight. So they're saying: if we do get sent back, it's a losing round. And here's the devastating irony: they're right.", grammar:"Khāsirah is an adjective for karrah; both indefinite, both carrying the surah's -irah rhyme.", why:"Military language makes their mockery sound tough, which makes the collapse in v.13–14 hit harder.", note:"Al-karr wa'l-farr = charge and retreat. They sneer, \"a losing comeback.\" And they're correct.", more:["khāsirah — the \"loss\" family (khusrān = loss; khasira = to lose out).","For them, it is a losing return.","Their sarcasm accidentally states the truth.","The Qur'an lets their own words convict them."]},
  {ar:"زَجْرَةٌ", tr:"Zajrah", v:"13", root:"The \"driving away with a shout / scolding\" family — zajara = to shout at an animal to make it move; zajr = a stern rebuke.", meaning:"\"It will be but one shout.\"", magic:"This is the surah's knockout punch, and it's structural. They just spent three long verses (10–12) building an elaborate objection — sent back? crumbled bones? a losing return?", grammar:"Fa-innamā = \"it is nothing but\" — the restriction particle, cutting off all alternatives.", why:"A herding shout for the resurrection of humanity — devastatingly dismissive.", note:"Three verses of objection. One word of answer: \"a shout.\" Picture a shepherd's single call — and everyone stands up.", more:["The answer is five words: \"It's just one shout.\" Their whole argument gets dismissed in less time than it took to make.","And zajrah is the sound you make to a herd animal — the disdain is built in.","And note \"wāḥidah\" (one) — not even repeated."]},
  {ar:"السَّاهِرَةِ", tr:"As-sāhirah", v:"14", root:"The \"staying awake at night\" family — sahira = to stay up; sahar = wakefulness before dawn.", meaning:"\"and suddenly they are on the open plain\" — the flat, bare surface of the earth.", magic:"Why is a plain called \"the wakeful one\"?", grammar:"Fa-idhā al-fujāʾiyyah — \"the idhā of surprise.\" It means and all at once, there they are.", why:"Sāhirah over \"the earth\" or \"the plain\" — it names the location by its defining condition: nobody sleeps here.", note:"Sahar = staying awake.", more:["Two beautiful explanations: (a) it's the place where nobody sleeps — every single person is standing, awake, forever; (b) a barren open plain shimmers and never rests.","Either way, the earth's name on that Day is \"Insomnia.\" And look at the transition: \"fa-idhā hum\" — \"and suddenly they are…\" — with no verb at all.","No \"they were raised,\" no \"they came out.\" The shout, then they're just there.","The grammar itself skips the process.","Learn this construction; it appears whenever the Qur'an wants instant transition.","The gathering-place is called \"The Wakeful Plain.\" From the grave to standing — no in-between, just \"and suddenly, there they are.\""]},
  {ar:"طُوًى", tr:"ṭuwā", v:"16", root:"The name of the sacred valley.", meaning:"\"when his Lord called him in the sacred valley of Ṭuwā.\"", magic:"None forced — but note the ṭuwā / ṭaghā (v.17) near-rhyme: the sacred place and the tyrant named in consecutive verses, sounding almost alike. Immediate contrast.", grammar:"A proper name, and one that doesn't take tanwīn in some readings (diptote) — but here it carries the rhyme.", why:"Naming the exact place grounds the story in history — this happened somewhere real.", note:"Ṭuwā — the valley where Mūsā was called. It sits right next to ṭaghā (v.17), the tyrant. Holy ground, then the tyrant's name.", more:["The root ṭ-w-y also means \"to fold\" (ṭawā = to fold up a scroll) — so some read the name as \"folded/blessed twice over,\" others simply as a proper name."]},
  {ar:"طَغَىٰ", tr:"ṭaghā", v:"17 · 37", root:"The \"overflowing past all limits\" family — original image: water bursting its banks and flooding.", meaning:"\"Go to Pharaoh — indeed, he has transgressed all bounds.\"", magic:"You already own this root from Nabaʾ 78:22 (aṭ-ṭāghīn, \"those who transgress\"). Here it gets a face and a name: Pharaoh is the case study.", grammar:"Past tense, weak-final root (ط-غ-ي); the final alif maqṣūrah carries the section's rhyme.", why:"It defines tyranny as a boundary problem, not a power problem.", note:"V.17: Pharaoh ṭaghā. v.37: \"whoever ṭaghā.\" Same verb, twenty verses apart. The label is transferable.", more:["And it comes back in v.37 — \"as for the one who ṭaghā\" — applying Pharaoh's exact verb to any ordinary person.","The surah says: the difference between you and Pharaoh is scale, not category."]},
  {ar:"تَزَكَّىٰ", tr:"Tazakkā", v:"18", root:"The \"purifying and growing\" family — and you know it: zakāh (the purifying charity), zakiyy (pure).", meaning:"\"Say: Would you [like] to purify yourself?\"", magic:"Look at how Allah tells Mūsā to address Pharaoh — the man who claimed godhood, who enslaved a nation. Not a threat. Not a condemnation.", grammar:"Form V = reflexive, doing something to yourself.", why:"Tazakkā offers growth, not just cleansing — it frames repentance as an upgrade, not a humiliation.", note:"Zakāh = purify + grow. And remember who this gentle offer was made to: Pharaoh. If he got that tone, so does everyone.", more:["Key insight: this root means purity AND growth at once — because removing what's rotten lets a thing grow.","A gentle question: \"Would you like to be purified?\" — and Form V (tazakkā) is reflexive: purify yourself, i.e.","you'd still be the one choosing.","The most tyrannical man in history gets offered a dignified invitation.","(Elsewhere the Qur'an makes this explicit: speak to him gently.) That's the balāgha here — the mercy is in the tone.","Hal laka ilā an… = a soft, courteous idiom, roughly \"would you care to…?\""]},
  {ar:"تَخْشَىٰ", tr:"Takhshā", v:"19 · 26, 45", root:"The \"awe-filled fear\" family — khashyah is fear that comes from knowing something's greatness, not from panic.", meaning:"\"and I will guide you to your Lord, so you would be in awe.\"", magic:"The order is the lesson: guidance first, awe second.", grammar:"Weak-final verb; the ف here is fā' as-sababiyyah — \"so that you would\" — marking a result.", why:"Khashyah over khawf — because this is reverence, the fear of someone you've come to know.", note:"Khashyah = fear that comes from KNOWING. First you're shown, then you're in awe. Guidance → awe, never the reverse.", more:["(Compare khawf = plain fear.)","Not \"fear Him so you'll obey\" but \"let me show you Him — then the awe follows naturally.\" Khashyah is presented as the result of knowledge, not a substitute for it.","And this root becomes the surah's thread: v.19 (Pharaoh could have had it), v.26 (a lesson for whoever has it), v.45 (you warn whoever has it).","Three appearances, one filter."]},
  {ar:"عَصَىٰ", tr:"ʿaṣā", v:"21", root:"The \"disobeying\" family — and here's a delicious accident of Arabic: ʿaṣā (he disobeyed) is spelled identically to ʿaṣā (a staff/stick) — the very thing Mūsā was holding.", meaning:"\"But he denied and disobeyed.\"", magic:"The pair kadhdhaba (denied with his tongue) + ʿaṣā (disobeyed with his actions) covers both channels of rejection — ṭibāq-style completeness.", grammar:"Weak-final past verb.", why:"Two verbs instead of one — because rejecting is not the same as disobeying, and Pharaoh did both.", note:"Mūsā held an ʿaṣā (staff); Pharaoh ʿaṣā (disobeyed). Same letters. Easy hook, and the pairing sticks.", more:["Say it in your head and say it with your hands."]},
  {ar:"أَدْبَرَ يَسْعَىٰ", tr:"Adbara yasʿā", v:"22", root:"Adbara — the \"back / rear\" family (dubur = the back) → to turn your back and leave.", meaning:"\"Then he turned his back, hurrying.\"", magic:"Two beautiful root-echoes in one verse.", grammar:"Yasʿā is a ḥāl (state) — \"he turned his back while hurrying.\" Two simultaneous actions.", why:"\"hurrying\" adds panic — he doesn't walk away with dignity, he scurries.", note:"V.5: angels manage (dabbara). v.22: Pharaoh turns his back (adbara). Same root, opposite direction.", more:["yasʿā — the \"striving, hurrying\" family — the same root as Saʿī, the walk between Ṣafā and Marwah.","(a) adbara is the same root as al-mudabbirāt (v.5) — the angels who \"manage the affair\" by seeing the end; Pharaoh uses the same root to run from it.","Same faculty, opposite use.","(b) yasʿā is the Saʿī root — and it comes back in v.35 (mā saʿā, \"what he strove for\").","Pharaoh's saʿy is running away; on that Day, everyone's saʿy gets shown.","And yasʿā = the Saʿī root — he's striving, just the wrong way."]},
  {ar:"حَشَرَ", tr:"ḥashara", v:"23", root:"The \"gathering people together\" family — the same word used for the Gathering of the Day of Judgment (al-ḥashr).", meaning:"\"So he gathered and called out.\"", magic:"Razor-sharp irony. Pharaoh performs a mini-Judgment-Day: he gathers (ḥashara) the people and calls out (nādā) a proclamation — mimicking exactly what will be done to him.", grammar:"Notice the chain of فَ particles across v.21–24 (fa-kadhdhaba, fa-ḥashara, fa-nādā, fa-qāla).", why:"Using the Judgment-Day verb for Pharaoh's rally makes his imitation explicit.", note:"Al-Ḥashr = the Gathering of Judgment Day. Pharaoh staged his own version.", more:["He plays God, using God's own verbs.","Then v.25: God seizes him.","The counterfeit gathering is answered by the real one.","Each \"fa\" means \"and then immediately.\" The verses speed up as his arrogance accelerates.","Note how the \"fa-fa-fa\" makes the verses accelerate — arrogance picking up speed right before the crash."]},
  {ar:"نَكَالَ", tr:"Nakāl", v:"25", root:"The \"shackling / holding back\" family — a nikl is a shackle or restraint.", meaning:"\"So Allah seized him with the exemplary punishment of the Hereafter and the first [life].\"", magic:"The word's whole purpose is deterrence — it's a punishment shaped like a warning sign.", grammar:"Accusative — a mafʿūl li-ajlih-style construction: seized him as an exemplary punishment.", why:"Nakāl over a plain word for punishment — this one is meant to be seen by others.", note:"Nikl = a shackle. A nakāl punishment is one put on public display to restrain everyone else.", more:["So nakāl is a punishment that acts as a restraint on other people — an exemplary punishment, made public to stop others.","And it explains the odd order: \"the Hereafter and the first\" — ākhirah named before ūlā, reversing chronology.","Because the lasting one matters more; the drowning was just the preview.","It's on display for you, right now, in this surah.","Pharaoh's ending is a warning sign with a body attached."]},
  {ar:"عِبْرَةً", tr:"ʿibrah", v:"26", root:"The \"crossing over\" family — ʿabara = to cross (a river); ʿubūr = a crossing; ʿibārah = an expression (words that carry meaning across).", meaning:"\"Indeed, in that is a lesson for whoever is in awe.\"", magic:"This is the most useful word in the surah for a student. A ʿibrah isn't information — it's a bridge you walk across, from what happened to Pharaoh to what that means for me.", grammar:"Inna fī dhālika la-ʿibratan — a triple-emphasis frame (inna + fronted fī dhālika + emphatic lām).", why:"ʿibrah over \"a story\" or \"a warning\" — because the whole value is in the crossing.", note:"ʿabara = to cross a river. A ʿibrah is a bridge from someone else's story to your own life. Reading isn't crossing.", more:["And: taʿbīr ar-ruʾyā = dream interpretation — crossing from the image to the meaning.","The story is one bank; your life is the other.","Most people read the story and stand on the near bank.","And note the condition: a lesson only for whoever has khashyah (v.20's root again).","The bridge exists; not everyone crosses.","Arabic underlining, three times over."]},
  {ar:"سَمْكَهَا", tr:"Samkahā", v:"28", root:"The \"height / thickness\" family — samk = the vertical dimension of a structure, its roof-height.", meaning:"\"He raised its ceiling-height and proportioned it.\"", magic:"Construction vocabulary again (as in Nabaʾ 78:12's \"We built\").", grammar:"Direct object of rafaʿa; note the argument frame: a-antum ashaddu khalqan ami's-samāʾ = \"Are you harder to create, or the sky?\" A rhetorical question with only one answer.", why:"Samk is a builder's measurement — it makes the sky sound specced out.", note:"Samk = ceiling height. Picture an architect's drawing of the sky with the roof height labeled.", more:["(Not to be confused with samak, fish — different word, same letters.)","The sky is described as architecture with a measured ceiling — you'd only describe a roof height if someone designed it."]},
  {ar:"فَسَوَّاهَا", tr:"Fa-sawwāhā", v:"28", root:"The \"making even, proportioning, perfecting\" family.", meaning:"\"and proportioned it.\"", magic:"You already own this root twice from Qiyāmah: 75:4 (nusawwiya — He can re-proportion your fingertips) and 75:38 (sawwā — He proportioned you from a clot).", grammar:"Form II, weak-final root; the fa- shows sequence: raised, then perfected.", why:"Repeating the human-creation verb for the cosmos makes the comparison airtight.", note:"Sawwā built your fingertips (Qiy 75:4) and the sky (79:28). Same craftsman, same verb, different scale.", more:["Now it's used for the sky.","Same verb for your fingertip and for the heavens.","That's the entire argument of v.27 compressed into one repeated word: the precision is identical at both scales."]},
  {ar:"أَغْطَشَ", tr:"Aghṭasha", v:"29", root:"The \"darkening\" family — ghaṭash = darkness, specifically the dimming of vision or deep gloom. A rare word.", meaning:"\"and He darkened its night and brought out its morning-brightness.\"", magic:"The verb is Form IV = causative: not \"the night got dark,\" but \"He darkened it\" — an active, deliberate act. Night doesn't just happen; it's switched on.", grammar:"Form IV (أَفْعَلَ) = making something happen.", why:"The rare word makes you notice an event you've ignored ~10,000 times.", note:"He didn't let night fall — He turned it off, then turned morning on. Two switches, same verse.", more:["Paired with akhraja (He brought out) in the same verse: a perfect ṭibāq — He darkens, He brings out.","Recognize the shape: aghṭasha, akhraja, arsā, amāta — all \"He caused X.\""]},
  {ar:"ضُحَاهَا", tr:"ḍuḥāhā", v:"29 · 46", root:"The \"forenoon brightness\" family — ḍuḥā is the mid-morning, when the sun is well up and everything is lit.", meaning:"\"and brought out its morning light.\"", magic:"It comes back as the surah's final word (v.46): \"as if they had lingered only an evening or its morning.\" So ḍuḥā opens as creation's daily gift and closes as the measure of an entire lifetime.", grammar:"Object of akhraja; the -hā refers back to the sky/night.", why:"ḍuḥā names the brightest, most ordinary part of your day — used to measure both creation and mortality.", note:"You pray Ṣalāt aḍ-Ḍuḥā in that light.", more:["(You know it as Ṣalāt aḍ-Ḍuḥā, and as Sūrat aḍ-Ḍuḥā.)","Your whole life = one ḍuḥā.","This word opens the creation passage (v.29) and closes the whole surah (v.46) — where your entire life gets measured as one morning."]},
  {ar:"دَحَاهَا", tr:"Daḥāhā", v:"30", root:"The \"spreading out / expanding\" family — daḥā = to spread wide.", meaning:"\"And the earth, after that, He spread out.\"", magic:"The verb means spreading and making usable — preparing a surface for living, exactly like an ostrich flattens out a nest.", grammar:"Weak-final past verb; note \"the earth\" is fronted for emphasis before its verb.", why:"Daḥā implies preparation for habitation, not mere creation.", note:"Udḥiyy = an ostrich's spread-out nest. Picture the earth being smoothed flat like a nest being prepared — for you to live in.", more:["Related noun: udḥiyy = an ostrich's nest, the hollow it spreads out in the ground (and by extension some connect daḥya to an egg).","And note the sequence: sky first (v.27–29), then earth (v.30–32) — big to small, ceiling before floor.","(People debate what the ostrich-nest link implies about shape; the core, agreed meaning is \"spread out for use\" — that's what to hold onto.)"]},
  {ar:"مَرْعَاهَا", tr:"Marʿāhā", v:"31", root:"The \"shepherding / grazing\" family — raʿā = to graze or to tend; rāʿī = a shepherd; and riʿāyah = care, guardianship.", meaning:"\"He brought out from it its water and its pasture.\"", magic:"The root's double life is the point: the same letters give \"grazing land\" and \"taking care of someone.\" So marʿā isn't just grass — it's the place where care happens.", grammar:"Noun of place (mafʿal pattern) from a weak-final root; the alif maqṣūrah carries the rhyme.", why:"Marʿā frames the earth's produce as provision under a shepherd's care.", note:"Rāʿī = shepherd, riʿāyah = care. The pasture is literally \"the care-place.\" Water and pasture: drink and food, nothing extra.", more:["The famous hadith: \"each of you is a rāʿī (shepherd) and responsible for his flock.\"","And notice the pairing: water and pasture — drink and food, the two things a living creature needs."]},
  {ar:"أَرْسَاهَا", tr:"Arsāhā", v:"32", root:"The \"anchoring, being firmly fixed\" family — rasā = to be firm/anchored; mirsāh/marsā = a harbour or anchorage where ships are moored.", meaning:"\"and the mountains — He anchored them.\"", magic:"This root sets up the surah's finest long-range echo. Here: mountains are anchored (arsāhā, v.32).", grammar:"Form IV (causative): \"He made them firm.\" Weak-final root (ر-س-و).", why:"Arsā is nautical — anchoring — which makes the mountains sound like stabilizers on a moving vessel.", note:"Marsā = a harbour where ships anchor. Mountains = the earth's anchors (v.32).", more:["Then at v.42, the mockers ask about the Hour: \"ayyāna mursāhā\" — \"when will it be anchored / when does it dock?\" Same root.","The mountains have an anchor-point; so does the Hour — you just don't get to know it.","The surah answers a question in v.42 using a word it planted in v.32.","Then in v.42 they ask when the Hour \"drops anchor.\" Same root, ten verses apart."]},
  {ar:"مَتَاعًا لَّكُمْ وَلِأَنْعَامِكُمْ", tr:"Matāʿan lakum wa li-anʿāmikum", v:"33", root:"Matāʿ — the \"enjoyment / temporary provision\" family; matāʿ is goods you use for a while (in modern Arabic, amtiʿah = luggage!", meaning:"\"as provision for you and your livestock.\"", magic:"This entire verse appears again, word for word, in Sūrat ʿAbasa (80:32). Not a similar verse — identical.", grammar:"Maf'ūl li-ajlih (accusative of purpose) — \"[He did all that] as provision.\"", why:"Matāʿ over a permanent word for \"provision\" — it keeps the dunya frame intact.", note:"Amti'ah = luggage — stuff you carry temporarily. And this exact verse repeats in ʿAbasa 80:32. Same words, next surah.", more:["— things you carry temporarily).","anʿām — grazing livestock; related to niʿmah (a blessing).","Two consecutive surahs, the same sentence closing the same kind of passage.","Worth noticing when you get there.","And matāʿ carries a built-in expiry date: it's temporary enjoyment.","Even while listing gifts, the word whispers this doesn't last."]},
  {ar:"الطَّامَّةُ الْكُبْرَىٰ", tr:"Aṭ-ṭāmmatu'l-kubrā", v:"34", root:"The \"overwhelming / submerging completely\" family — ṭamma'l-māʾ = the water rose and buried everything.", meaning:"\"But when the Greatest Overwhelming comes.\"", magic:"Note the root-cousin to ṭaghā (v.17, 37) — both are water-overflow images. Pharaoh overflowed his limits (ṭaghā); the Day overflows everything (ṭāmmah).", grammar:"Participle used as a name, with a superlative adjective (al-kubrā, feminine of akbar).", why:"ṭāmmah = something that submerges — you don't survive it by standing taller.", note:"ṭamma = water rising over everything. Pharaoh overflowed (ṭaghā) — and he was drowned.", more:["A ṭāmmah is a calamity that covers over whatever it meets.","And there's grim symmetry: the man who flooded past his boundaries is answered by a flood that covers all boundaries.","Adding al-kubrā (the Greatest) implies there are lesser ṭāmmahs — every personal disaster is a small rehearsal.","Now the whole world meets The Great Overflowing."]},
  {ar:"يَتَذَكَّرُ / مَا سَعَىٰ", tr:"Yatadhakkaru / mā saʿā", v:"35", root:"Tadhakkara — the \"remembering\" family, Form V (reflexive: calling it back to yourself).", meaning:"\"the Day man will remember what he strove for.\"", magic:"Two hooks. (a) saʿā is the third appearance of this root in the surah — v.8 of ʿAbasa aside, here: v.22 (Pharaoh hurrying away), v.35 (everyone remembering their striving).", grammar:"Form V = doing it to yourself. Mā = \"what\" (relative), not \"not.\"", why:"Tadhakkara (remember) instead of ʿalima (come to know) — nothing gets added; the file was always yours.", note:"Not \"he'll be informed\" — \"he'll remember.\" The memory was always there.", more:["saʿā — the Saʿī root again (striving, hurrying).","(b) The verb is \"remember,\" not \"be told\" — the horror isn't new information, it's recall.","You just haven't been looking.","And saʿā is the Ṣafā–Marwah root: every bit of striving, replayed."]},
  {ar:"بُرِّزَتِ", tr:"Burrizat", v:"36", root:"The \"emerging into full view / standing out\" family — baraza = to come out prominently; bāriz = prominent; mubārazah = stepping out for single combat.", meaning:"\"and Hellfire is displayed for whoever sees.\"", magic:"The verb means made prominently visible — Hell isn't revealed (as if hidden) so much as brought out into the open, put on display.", grammar:"Form II passive (intensive + passive) — \"was made thoroughly to appear.\" The doer is unnamed, which is standard for cosmic events.", why:"Burrizat over \"appeared\" — this is an exhibition, deliberately staged.", note:"Bāriz = prominent, standing out. Hell gets wheeled out into the open — and it's visible \"for whoever sees.\"", more:["Combined with \"li-man yarā\" (for whoever sees), there's a chilling nuance: it's out in plain view — the question is who's looking."]},
  {ar:"آثَرَ", tr:"āthara", v:"38", root:"The \"trace / footprint\" family — athar = a mark left behind (archaeology = āthār!).", meaning:"\"and preferred the life of this world.\"", magic:"The root's noble meaning makes this cutting. Īthār — putting others first — is one of the highest virtues in Arabic. Here the same verb describes putting the dunya first.", grammar:"Form IV, past. Paired with ṭaghā in v.37 by wa: overstepping + misplaced preference = the full diagnosis.", why:"āthara frames the failure as a choice of priority, not ignorance. He knew. He picked.", note:"Athar = a footprint (as in āthār, archaeological remains). Īthār = putting others first. Here: he put the dunya first.", more:["From that: āthara = to prefer one thing over another, and īthār = the beautiful virtue of preferring others over yourself (selflessness).","The machinery of preference is identical; only the object changed.","He aimed it at the wrong thing."]},
  {ar:"الْمَأْوَىٰ", tr:"Al-maʾwā", v:"39 · 41", root:"The \"taking shelter / seeking refuge\" family — awā = to take shelter; maʾwā = the place you end up sheltering.", meaning:"V.39 — \"then indeed Hellfire is the refuge.\" v.41 — \"then indeed Paradise is the refuge.\"", magic:"Exactly the same structural move as maʾāban in Nabaʾ 78:22 & 39 — one word, used twice, for two opposite destinations.", grammar:"Hiya here is ḍamīr al-faṣl (the separating pronoun) — it adds restriction: \"Hell is the refuge.", why:"Maʾwā over \"destination\" — because shelter is what everyone is actually looking for.", note:"Same word, v.39 and v.41. Hell = \"the shelter.\" Paradise = \"the shelter.\" Just like maʾāb in Nabaʾ.", more:["(Related: āwā = to give someone refuge.)","But it's sharper here: the two verses are grammatically identical (fa-inna X hiya'l-maʾwā), separated by only one verse.","And the irony is in the word: maʾwā means the place you take shelter.","Hell described as a shelter.","For those who sought refuge in the dunya, the \"shelter\" they chose becomes the one they get.","That specifically.\" Small word, big emphasis.","Everyone finds shelter somewhere."]},
  {ar:"مَقَامَ رَبِّهِ", tr:"Maqāma rabbihi", v:"40", root:"The \"standing\" family — qāma = to stand; maqām = a standing-place or a station/rank.", meaning:"\"as for whoever feared the standing before his Lord.\"", magic:"Two valid readings, both beautiful: (a) the place where you'll stand before Him — i.e. the standing-place of judgment; (b) His station/majesty — awe at who He is.", grammar:"Iḍāfah (possessive): \"the standing-place of his Lord.\" Accusative as object of khāfa.", why:"Maqām makes the fear specific and located — not vague anxiety, but a scheduled standing.", note:"Maqām = a standing-spot. Picture the exact patch of ground where you'll stand in front of Him. Fear that spot.", more:["The ambiguity enriches: you fear the meeting and the One you're meeting."]},
  {ar:"نَهَى النَّفْسَ عَنِ الْهَوَىٰ", tr:"Nahā'n-nafsa ʿani'l-hawā", v:"40", root:"Nahā — the \"forbidding / bringing to an end\" family (nahy = a prohibition; nihāyah = the end).", meaning:"\"and restrained the soul from desire.\"", magic:"The root of \"desire\" is \"to FALL.\" Arabic doesn't name whims neutrally — it names them by what they do to you. Hawā isn't \"wanting\"; it's the thing you drop through.", grammar:"Nahā X ʿan Y = \"restrained X from Y\" — the preposition ʿan is fixed with this verb. Learn the pairing.", why:"Hawā over a neutral word for desire — it builds the consequence into the noun.", note:"Hāwiyah = the abyss; hawāʾ = air. Desire is named after falling. Following your hawā = stepping off an edge.", more:["hawā — the \"falling down\" family: hawā = to fall, to plunge; hāwiyah = the abyss; hawāʾ = air (empty space you fall through).","And there's a perfect internal echo: nahā (restrain / bring to an end) shares its root with v.44's muntahāhā (\"its final end\").","The one who put an END to his desires (v.40) is answered by the One who holds the END of all things (v.44)."]},
  {ar:"أَيَّانَ مُرْسَاهَا", tr:"Ayyāna mursāhā", v:"42", root:"Ayyāna — an interrogative of time reserved for a dreaded or momentous event (not everyday matā).", meaning:"\"They ask you about the Hour: when is its arrival / when does it dock?\"", magic:"Two hooks, both excellent. (a) Ayyāna is the exact same word as Qiyāmah 75:6 (ayyāna yawmu'l-qiyāmah) — same mocking question, two surahs.", grammar:"Mursā is a noun of time/place from Form IV, weak-final. Ayyāna is mabnī (indeclinable).", why:"Ayyāna smuggles in dread even when asked sarcastically — the word itself concedes the Hour's enormity.", note:"Same question word as Qiyāmah 75:6. And mursā = \"drops anchor\" — from v.32, where the mountains were anchored.", more:["mursā — the anchoring root from v.32.","(b) Mursāhā is the anchoring root planted in v.32 (the mountains were anchored).","So the question is literally \"when does it drop anchor?\" — and the answer (v.44) is: that harbour belongs to your Lord.","The surah planted this word ten verses earlier."]},
  {ar:"فِيمَ أَنتَ مِن ذِكْرَاهَا", tr:"Fīma anta min dhikrāhā", v:"43", root:"Dhikrā — the \"mentioning / reminding\" family (you own this from Insān 76:29's tadhkirah).", meaning:"\"In what [position] are you to mention it?\" — i.e. this isn't yours to announce.", magic:"Grammar hook you already own. Fīma = مَا + فِي, with the alif of mā dropped — exactly the rule from Nabaʾ 78:1 (ʿamma = عن + ما).", grammar:"Interrogative مَا loses its alif after any preposition: ʿamma, fīma, bima, lima, mimma.", why:"The phrasing is a gentle relief, not a rebuke — you don't carry this weight.", note:"Nabaʾ opened with ʿamma (عن+ما). Nāziʿāt uses fīma (في+ما). Same rule. The alif drops.", more:["Two consecutive surahs open a key verse with this same contraction.","Learn the rule once, collect it twice.","This is the single highest-yield small rule in this juzʾ."]},
  {ar:"مُنتَهَاهَا", tr:"Muntahāhā", v:"44", root:"The \"ending / final limit\" family — nihāyah = the end; muntahā = the utmost point, where something terminates.", meaning:"\"To your Lord is its final end\" — its ultimate knowledge and destination.", magic:"The internal rhyme with v.40's nahā (same root): the righteous man puts an end to his desires; Allah holds the end of the Hour.", grammar:"Noun of place from Form VIII (intahā), weak-final.", why:"Muntahā means the point past which there is nothing — the strongest possible way to close the question.", note:"Sidrat al-Muntahā = the tree at the Utmost Limit. Same word.", more:["(Sidrat al-Muntahā — the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary — same word.)","And structurally: \"ilā rabbika\" is fronted — \"To your Lord [alone] is its end\" — a restriction.","You already saw this exact fronting in Qiyāmah 75:12 (ilā rabbika yawmaʾidhin al-mustaqarr) and 75:30 (al-masāq).","Three surahs, same move: everything terminates at Him.","And notice the fronting — \"To your Lord…\" — the same construction as Qiyāmah 75:12 & 75:30."]},
  {ar:"مُنذِرُ", tr:"Mundhir", v:"45", root:"The \"warning\" family — and you already own it: the same root as andharnāhu (Nabaʾ 78:40) and an-nadhr, the vow (Insān 76:7). All share: binding something in advance.", meaning:"\"You are only a warner to whoever fears it.\"", magic:"\"innamā\" (only) is a restriction particle — it limits the Prophet's ﷺ job description. And that's the comfort: he is not responsible for the outcome, only the delivery.", grammar:"Form IV active participle; innamā = restriction (\"nothing but\").", why:"Mundhir (warner) rather than \"guide\" — because guidance isn't his to give, only warning is his to deliver.", note:"Khashyah appears 3 times: v.19 (Pharaoh could have), v.26 (the lesson is for them), v.45 (the warning reaches them).", more:["Combined with man yakhshāhā (\"whoever is in awe of it\") — the third and final appearance of the khashyah thread (v.19 → v.26 → v.45).","The surah's filter, stated three times: awe is what makes the message land.","Same filter, three checkpoints."]},
  {ar:"لَمْ يَلْبَثُوا", tr:"Lam yalbathū", v:"46", root:"The \"staying / lingering\" family — the same root as lābithīn in Nabaʾ 78:23 (\"remaining therein for ages\").", meaning:"\"as if they had not lingered except an evening or its morning.\"", magic:"The contrast is exquisite and deliberate. In Nabaʾ 78:23, this root described staying in Hell for aḥqāban — ages upon ages.", grammar:"لَمْ + jussive = past negation (\"did not\").", why:"Labitha (linger) rather than \"live\" — it makes the whole lifespan sound like waiting in a lobby.", note:"Nabaʾ 78:23: lābithīn for AGES. Nāziʿāt 79:46: lam yalbathū — barely an evening. Same root. That's the trade.", more:["Here, the same root describes their entire earthly life as less than one day.","Same verb: your whole life = an afternoon.","The two surahs use one root to set the exchange rate.","Note lam yalbathū drops the final nūn — that's the jussive at work."]},
  {ar:"عَشِيَّةً أَوْ ضُحَاهَا", tr:"ʿashiyyatan aw ḍuḥāhā", v:"46", root:"ʿashiyyah — late afternoon into evening; ḍuḥā — mid-morning (from v.29).", meaning:"\"…except an evening or its morning.\"", magic:"The most quietly devastating ending imaginable.", grammar:"Aw here means \"or\" in the sense of even less than that — a downward correction, not a real alternative.", why:"Naming the smallest natural unit of a day — and then offering an even smaller one.", note:"Your whole life: \"an evening — or just the morning of it.\" And ḍuḥā was v.29's gift of daylight.", more:["He doesn't say \"a day.\" He says \"an evening — or its morning.\" That's half a day, and then he halves it again with \"or.\" Your entire life — every plan, every worry, every year — reduced to the part of a day you barely notice passing.","And it closes the loop with v.29, where ḍuḥā was creation's daily gift.","The word that opened as a blessing closes as a measurement of how little time you had.","First word of creation's kindness; last word of your lifespan."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:81, name:"At-Takwīr", ar:"التكوير", en:"The Folding Up", verses:29, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"A twelve-hammer drumroll — then one sentence about YOU.",
  special:["Twelve verses, twelve passive verbs, one sentence.","Verses 1–13 repeat \"When… when… when…\" without ever completing the sentence. Only verse 14 gives the answer: \"Then a soul will know what it has brought.\" Twelve cosmic catastrophes grammatically prepare one quiet clause about you.","The twelve hammers, in order: kuwwirat (sun wound up), inkadarat (stars dim and fall), suyyirat (mountains moved), ʿuṭṭilat (camels abandoned), ḥushirat (beasts gathered), sujjirat (seas stoked), zuwwijat (souls paired), suʾilat (the girl questioned), nushirat (scrolls spread), kushiṭat (sky stripped), suʿʿirat (Hell kindled), uzlifat (Paradise brought near).","Nothing acts; everything is acted upon. The doer is unnamed — because you already know Who.","Then verse 14 lands: عَلِمَتْ نَفْسٌ مَّا أَحْضَرَتْ — \"A soul will know what it has brought.\" The universe is dismantled; the camera settles on one soul."],
  big:["The Great Undoing (v.1–13) — sky → earth → creatures → records → destinations.","The Point (v.14) — a soul knows what it brought.","Three Oaths (v.15–18) — hiding stars, departing night, breathing dawn.","Defense of the Messenger (v.19–25) — not madness, greed, or a devil's whisper.","The Open Door (v.26–29) — where are you going? Straightness remains open."],
  words:[
  {ar:"كُوِّرَتْ", tr:"Kuwwirat", v:"1", root:"The winding / coiling / wrapping family; kāraʾl-ʿimāmah = to wind a turban.", meaning:"The sun is wound up / folded in.", magic:"An everyday turban-motion is applied to the sun: its light is rolled away like cloth. Cosmic destruction is described with a household verb.", grammar:"Form II passive: the pattern adds intensity and leaves the doer unnamed.", why:"More physical and memorable than simply saying the sun went dark.", note:"Picture the sun rolled up like a turban and wrapped away."},
  {ar:"انكَدَرَتْ", tr:"Inkadarat", v:"2", root:"The murkiness / turbidity family; kadiraʾl-māʾ = clear water becoming cloudy.", meaning:"The stars fall scattered and become dim.", magic:"The stars lose two things together: their clarity and their place. Light is being unmade in a second way after the sun.", grammar:"Form VII (infaʿala): something happens to the subject, with no doer stated.", why:"Losing clarity is more unsettling than merely falling.", note:"Picture bright stars turning like clear water stirred into mud — dimmed and falling."},
  {ar:"سُيِّرَتْ", tr:"Suyyirat", v:"3", root:"The traveling / moving family; sayr = journey and sayyārah = car.", meaning:"The mountains are set moving.", magic:"It is identical to Nabaʾ 78:20. Nabaʾ tells what they become; Takwīr moves on immediately, making the pace faster.", grammar:"Form II passive: not drifting, but forcibly driven away.", why:"The identical wording makes this a fixed image of the Hour.", note:"Sayyārah = car: picture mountains driving away."},
  {ar:"الْعِشَارُ عُطِّلَتْ", tr:"Al-ʿishāru ʿuṭṭilat", v:"4", root:"ʿishār = ten-month pregnant she-camels; ʿuṭṭilat = left idle or out of service.", meaning:"The most valuable pregnant camels are left untended.", magic:"The Day is measured by what it does to priorities: a prized investment is still present and valuable, yet suddenly no longer matters.", grammar:"Form II passive; the scene suddenly shifts from the cosmos to one treasured possession.", why:"The first audience instantly understood this as unthinkable to abandon.", note:"ʿashr = ten: imagine your life savings about to pay out — and you simply walk away."},
  {ar:"الْوُحُوشُ حُشِرَتْ", tr:"Al-wuḥūshu ḥushirat", v:"5", root:"Wuḥūsh = wild beasts, linked with waḥshah (loneliness); ḥushirat = gathered.", meaning:"The wild beasts are gathered.", magic:"It is a deliberate contradiction: the scattered, solitary creatures become \"the loners assembled.\" Even the ungatherable is gathered.", grammar:"Simple Form I passive, creating a small rhythmic break from Form II.", why:"ḥashara is the Judgment-Day gathering verb, extending the Gathering to all creation.", note:"Wild animals never gather — yet every one of them is rounded up."},
  {ar:"الْبِحَارُ سُجِّرَتْ", tr:"Al-biḥāru sujjirat", v:"6", root:"The stoking-a-furnace family; sajjaraʾt-tannūr = to fuel and fire an oven.", meaning:"The seas are set ablaze or made to boil over.", magic:"Water — the thing that defeats fire — becomes the furnace. A basic rule of reality collapses; both classical meanings also show limits bursting.", grammar:"Form II passive: intensely stoked, not merely lit.", why:"Oven vocabulary makes the cosmic event tactile and impossible to ignore.", note:"Picture the ocean as a furnace: the thing that kills fire becomes fire."},
  {ar:"النُّفُوسُ زُوِّجَتْ", tr:"An-nufūsu zuwwijat", v:"7", root:"The pairing / mating family; zawj = one of a pair or a spouse.", meaning:"The souls are paired up.", magic:"Pairing was a blessing in Nabaʾ 78:8; here it becomes sorting — souls matched with their type, or reunited with their bodies.", grammar:"Form II passive; nufūs is the plural of nafs.", why:"Pairing implies likeness, not merely sorting into piles.", note:"Nabaʾ: pairing is a blessing. Takwīr: pairing is a verdict — you meet your own kind."},
  {ar:"الْمَوْءُودَةُ سُئِلَتْ", tr:"Al-mawʾūdatu suʾilat", v:"8 · 9", root:"Waʾd = burying an infant alive; mawʾūdah = the buried-alive baby girl.", meaning:"The murdered girl is asked: \"For what sin was she killed?\"", magic:"The victim — not her killer — is given the floor. Because she committed no sin, the question itself becomes the verdict. One child is weighed beside cosmic events.", grammar:"Mawʾūdah is a passive participle (\"the one acted upon\"); suʾilat then makes her the one addressed.", why:"Naming the crime and placing the victim as subject makes the condemnation unavoidable.", note:"The universe is ending, yet it stops for the girl nobody thought counted."},
  {ar:"الصُّحُفُ نُشِرَتْ", tr:"Aṣ-ṣuḥufu nushirat", v:"10", root:"ṣuḥuf = scrolls / pages; nushirat = spread open or unfolded.", meaning:"The records are spread open.", magic:"ʿAbasa 80:22 used the same root for you being unfolded from the grave. Here your record unfolds: you and your file open together.", grammar:"Simple Form I passive.", why:"\"spread open\" emphasizes public unrolling before anything is even read.", note:"You are unfolded; your record is unfolded — both open at once."},
  {ar:"السَّمَاءُ كُشِطَتْ", tr:"As-samāʾu kushiṭat", v:"11", root:"Kashaṭa = to flay or skin an animal, stripping its hide away.", meaning:"The sky is stripped away.", magic:"A butcher's verb treats the sky as a covering peeled off a body, exposing what lay beneath. Nabaʾ had doors in the sky; here the covering disappears.", grammar:"Form I passive.", why:"The raw physical verb conveys total removal, not merely opening.", note:"Picture the sky peeled off like an animal's hide."},
  {ar:"الْجَحِيمُ سُعِّرَتْ", tr:"Al-jaḥīmu suʿʿirat", v:"12", root:"Jaḥīm = fiercely blazing fire; suʿʿirat = kindled and stoked to a blaze.", meaning:"The Blaze is kindled fiercely.", magic:"The seas were stoked in v.6; now Hell is stoked. It is immediately paired with Paradise being brought near — both destinations prepared together.", grammar:"Form II passive, matching the surah's repeated intensive passive pattern.", why:"\"kindled\" describes deliberate preparation for those arriving.", note:"Insān 76:4 has saʿīran from the same root: Hell is being stoked."},
  {ar:"الْجَنَّةُ أُزْلِفَتْ", tr:"Al-jannatu uzlifat", v:"13", root:"The drawing-near family; zulfah = closeness, and Muzdalifah shares the root.", meaning:"Paradise is brought near.", magic:"The final hammer is kindness: after everything else is undone or taken away, one thing moves toward you. The passive comforts — you do not have to travel to it.", grammar:"Form IV passive; the twelfth verb and the only one with a positive object.", why:"Nearness is the reward for those who spent life reaching toward it.", note:"Eleven things are torn away; one thing comes closer — remember Muzdalifah."},
  {ar:"عَلِمَتْ نَفْسٌ مَّا أَحْضَرَتْ", tr:"ʿalimat nafsun mā aḥḍarat", v:"14", root:"ʿalimat = knew; nafs = soul; aḥḍarat = brought forward or made present.", meaning:"A soul will know what it brought.", magic:"This is the payoff to twelve \"when\" clauses. One unnamed soul knows rather than being told, and faces the deeds it packed and carried in.", grammar:"The jawāb (answer) to all twelve idhā clauses; past tense describes the future with certainty.", why:"Aḥḍarat turns deeds into luggage you personally brought.", note:"The universe ends, the camera finds one soul, and you open your own bag."},
  {ar:"فَلَا أُقْسِمُ", tr:"Fa-lā uqsimu", v:"15", root:"The dividing / apportioning family; an oath settles a disputed matter.", meaning:"So I swear by…", magic:"The construction is identical to Qiyāmah 75:1. Lā can rebut the objection before the oath or intensify it; the present tense makes the oath living and renewed.", grammar:"The following bāʾ is bāʾ al-qasam — the \"by\" used for oaths.", why:"The repeated Qiyāmah construction signals another contested truth being affirmed.", note:"You already own this opening from Qiyāmah 75:1."},
  {ar:"الْخُنَّسِ", tr:"Al-khunnas", v:"15", root:"The shrinking-back / withdrawing / hiding family; the same root as al-khannās in Sūrat an-Nās.", meaning:"The retreating or hiding stars.", magic:"Stars are real but invisible by day, preparing the argument that an unseen angel is not imaginary. The familiar whisperer also withdraws when Allah is remembered.", grammar:"An intensive fuʿʿal plural, deliberately rhyming with al-kunnas.", why:"The stars are named by their disappearing, not their shining.", note:"Al-khannās withdraws; these stars hide — real, even when unseen."},
  {ar:"الْجَوَارِ الْكُنَّسِ", tr:"Al-jawāriʾl-kunnas", v:"16", root:"Jawārī = running / flowing; kunnas = sweeping, or entering a gazelle's lair. Miknasah means broom.", meaning:"The running stars that sweep along or retire into hiding.", magic:"Both classical readings join motion with concealment: they sweep across the sky like a broom, or run like gazelles into their dens.", grammar:"Al-jawāri is a manqūṣ noun and drops its final yāʾ here.", why:"The gazelle-den image makes celestial motion feel alive and shy.", note:"Broom = sweep; gazelle's den = hide. They run, then disappear."},
  {ar:"عَسْعَسَ", tr:"ʿasʿasa", v:"17", root:"A reduplicated sound-root for the night's slow creeping; it can mean approaching or departing.", meaning:"The night as it comes on or moves away.", magic:"The ambiguity perfectly captures the seam between night and day. The doubled sound itself performs slow, repeated movement.", grammar:"Reduplicated roots such as waswasa and zalzala often express repeated or gradual action.", why:"One word meaning both directions fits a threshold where arrival and departure blur.", note:"Hear the doubling — waswasa, zalzala, ʿasʿasa — the night creeps either way."},
  {ar:"الصُّبْحِ إِذَا تَنَفَّسَ", tr:"Aṣ-ṣubḥi idhā tanaffas", v:"18", root:"The breath / soul family; nafas = breath, tanaffasa = breathe, and nafs = soul.", meaning:"The morning when it breathes.", magic:"Dawn becomes a living thing exhaling light into darkness. Tanaffasa here and nafs in v.14 share one root: the soul knows; the morning breathes.", grammar:"Form V, with a reflexive sense: morning breathes itself into being.", why:"Breathing means life, fitting a surah arguing for resurrection.", note:"V.14: a nafs knows. v.18: dawn breathes. Picture morning exhaling light."},
  {ar:"رَسُولٍ كَرِيمٍ", tr:"Rasūlin karīm", v:"19", root:"Rasūl = messenger / envoy; karīm belongs to the nobility and generosity family.", meaning:"It is the word carried by a noble messenger — understood here as Jibrīl.", magic:"The message's chain of custody is spotless. Calling it the messenger's qawl means carried and delivered, not composed by him.", grammar:"La-qawlu contains the lām of emphasis with inna: double emphasis.", why:"Rasūl stresses his function as carrier rather than simply identifying him as an angel.", note:"A messenger delivers another's words; he does not author them."},
  {ar:"ذِي قُوَّةٍ … مَكِينٍ", tr:"Dhī quwwatin… makīn", v:"20", root:"Quwwah = power; makīn = firmly placed and high in rank, from makān (place) and makānah (status).", meaning:"Possessing strength and firmly established in rank near the Owner of the Throne.", magic:"Place and status share one root: where he stands and how highly he ranks become one idea. Nearness to the Throne is itself honor.", grammar:"Dhū / dhā / dhī is one of the five special nouns; dhī is genitive here.", why:"Makīn makes the honor positional, firm, and unshakeable.", note:"Makān = place; makānah = status. Where you stand becomes your rank."},
  {ar:"مُطَاعٍ ثَمَّ أَمِينٍ", tr:"Muṭāʿin thamma amīn", v:"21", root:"Muṭāʿ = obeyed; thamma = there; amīn = trustworthy, from the safety / trust family.", meaning:"Obeyed there in the heavens, and trustworthy.", magic:"Authority and trust complete the argument: he can deliver the message and will not alter it. The heavenly carrier is amīn, as the earthly carrier was known as al-Amīn.", grammar:"A trap worth marking: ثَمَّ thamma = \"there,\" while ثُمَّ thumma = \"then.\"", why:"Amīn exactly describes someone trusted with property that is not his own.", note:"THAMMA = THERE, not then. Obeyed + trustworthy."},
  {ar:"صَاحِبُكُم … بِمَجْنُونٍ", tr:"ṣāḥibukum… bi-majnūn", v:"22", root:"ṣāḥib = companion; majnūn comes from \"covering,\" like jannah, jinn, and janīn.", meaning:"Your lifelong companion is not mad.", magic:"\"Your companion\" appeals to their own decades of knowing him. Majnūn means a mind covered over, while the oaths showed hidden things can still be real and ordered.", grammar:"The bāʾ in bi-majnūn is extra after negation, strengthening it: \"not at all mad.\"", why:"ṣāḥibukum makes the defense personal and relational, not merely titular.", note:"Garden, jinn, fetus, and majnūn all share the picture of being covered."},
  {ar:"بِالْأُفُقِ الْمُبِينِ", tr:"Biʾl-ufuqiʾl-mubīn", v:"23", root:"Ufuq = horizon; mubīn belongs to the clarity family, like bayān.", meaning:"He certainly saw him on the clear horizon.", magic:"The answer to \"madness\" is a clear sighting at the furthest visible point. The horizon is far but still seen: unseen does not mean imaginary.", grammar:"Laqad + past tense gives strong affirmation; mubīn is a Form IV active participle.", why:"Mubīn means unmistakable and self-evidencing, not merely bright.", note:"The horizon is the farthest visible edge — far, but not unseen."},
  {ar:"بِضَنِينٍ", tr:"Bi-ḍanīn", v:"24", root:"The miserliness / withholding family; ḍanna bi- means to hoard something precious.", meaning:"He does not withhold knowledge of the unseen.", magic:"False soothsayers rationed hidden claims for payment; he delivered the message fully and freely. The established variant ẓanīn means \"suspect\" — both readings defend him.", grammar:"Another extra bāʾ after negation, matching v.22 and v.25.", why:"ḍanīn targets the profit model of false prophecy, not just its claim.", note:"Fortune-tellers hoard and charge; he gave everything. Variant: ẓanīn = suspect."},
  {ar:"شَيْطَانٍ رَّجِيمٍ", tr:"Shayṭānin rajīm", v:"25", root:"Shayṭān carries remoteness from mercy; rajīm is from stoning / pelting, like rajm at Jamarāt.", meaning:"It is not the word of an accursed, expelled devil.", magic:"Three attacks are closed: not a defect in his mind, motive, or source. The true messenger is near and obeyed; the devil is pelted and shut out.", grammar:"The third extra bāʾ in the sequence of emphatic denials.", why:"Rajīm emphasizes exclusion and denied access, the opposite of a trusted courier.", note:"One messenger is welcomed near the Throne; the devil is stoned out."},
  {ar:"فَأَيْنَ تَذْهَبُونَ", tr:"Fa-ayna tadhhabūn", v:"26", root:"Ayna = where?; dhahaba = to go.", meaning:"So where are you going?", magic:"After the argument, the surah places a hand on the listener's shoulder. It asks rather than condemns: where else can you go?", grammar:"Ayna is an indeclinable place-question; fa- makes the question the conclusion to all that preceded.", why:"A question leaves room to turn around; a verdict would not.", note:"Four words: \"So where are you going?\" You can still change direction.", more:["Qiyāmah asks \"where is escape?\" in panic; here it is calm and early."]},
  {ar:"ذِكْرٌ لِّلْعَالَمِينَ", tr:"Dhikrun liʾl-ʿālamīn", v:"27", root:"Dhikr = remembrance / reminder; ʿālamīn = all worlds and peoples.", meaning:"It is nothing but a reminder to all the worlds.", magic:"Insān, ʿAbasa and Takwīr repeat the same reminder-root. Here it expands to everyone, answering the rank-based priorities corrected in ʿAbasa.", grammar:"إِنْ هُوَ إِلَّا is restriction: in means \"not,\" so the phrase means \"It is nothing but…\"", why:"Dhikr keeps revelation's role as reminding, not coercing.", note:"Three surahs, one declaration — and remember: in here means NOT, not \"if.\""},
  {ar:"لِمَن شَاءَ مِنكُمْ أَن يَسْتَقِيمَ", tr:"Li-man shāʾa minkum an yastaqīm", v:"28", root:"Shāʾa = to will; yastaqīm comes from standing upright — like aṣ-ṣirāṭ al-mustaqīm.", meaning:"For whoever among you wills to go straight.", magic:"Four nearby surahs repeat \"whoever wills\": the door is open, but you must walk through it. Form X suggests seeking and maintaining straightness.", grammar:"Yastaqīm is Form X; after an it is accusative / subjunctive: yastaqīma.", why:"Going straight is a posture maintained, not a single moment of belief.", note:"You ask for this root in every rakʿah: aṣ-ṣirāṭ al-mustaqīm."},
  {ar:"وَمَا تَشَاءُونَ إِلَّا أَن يَشَاءَ اللَّهُ", tr:"Wa mā tashāʾūna illā an yashāʾaʾllāh", v:"29", root:"Shāʾa = to will, repeated from v.28.", meaning:"You do not will except that Allah wills — the Lord of all worlds.", magic:"V.28 affirms your will; v.29 places it inside His. The same verb holds both truths together. Rabb al-ʿālamīn describes the greater will through nurturing care, and echoes v.27.", grammar:"An yashāʾa is an exception construction; yashāʾa is accusative after an.", why:"Ending on Rabb al-ʿālamīn closes on care, not sheer power, and echoes al-Fātiḥah.", note:"V.28: your will. v.29: His will. Same verb, both true — the sun-folding surah ends with the Nurturer."}
  ]
},
{
  num:82, name:"Al-Infiṭār", ar:"الانفطار", en:"The Cleaving", verses:19, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Takwīr takes twelve hammer-blows to reach the point. Infiṭār takes four. Same architecture, shorter runway.",
  special:["It's Takwīr's twin — and comparing them is the fastest way to lock both in.","Look at that last row: \"ʿalimat nafsun\" is IDENTICAL in both surahs. Same two words, same position, same job — the punchline after the idhā stack. Learn one, and you own half the other.","--"],
  big:["Four Undoings (v.1–4) — sky, stars, seas, graves. Notice the fourth: Takwīr never mentioned graves. Infiṭār goes underground.","The Payoff (v.5) — a soul will know what it sent ahead and what it left behind.","The Question (v.6–8) — \"What DECEIVED you about your generous Lord?\" The gentlest, most devastating question in the Qur'an.","The Recorders (v.9–12) — you are never unwatched, and the watchers are noble.","Two Groups, Two Places (v.13–16) — al-abrār in bliss, al-fujjār in blaze.","The Day Nobody Can Help Anybody (v.17–19)."],
  traps:["Infiṭār vs. Takwīr — the twin surahs. Sky: infaṭarat (split) vs. kushiṭat (flayed). Stars: intatharat (scattered) vs. inkadarat (dimmed). Seas: fujjirat (burst) vs. sujjirat (stoked). Easy to swap. Learn them as a table, not separately.","مَا أَدْرَاكَ (past) vs. مَا يُدْرِيكَ (present) — v.17 vs. ʿAbasa 80:3. Past = an explanation follows. Present = it doesn't. One letter and a vowel apart.","فُجِّرَتْ (v.3) and الْفُجَّارَ (v.14) — same root, same surah, eleven verses apart. Seas burst their limits; so did they.","الْكَرِيمِ (v.6) and كِرَامًا (v.11) — same root. The Lord is generous; the scribes are noble. Both mercies inside a warning.","The address switches from singular to plural at v.9 — gharraka, sawwāka, rakkabak (you) → tukadhdhibūn (you all). Don't miss the shift.","عَدَلَكَ (v.7) — this is the JUSTICE root (ʿadl), used for bodily symmetry. Not just \"shaped you.\"","بُعْثِرَتْ (v.4) — a quadriliteral verb: turned upside down and dumped out, not merely \"opened.\"","الدِّينِ (v.9, 15, 17, 18) — appears four times. Remember: dīn / dayn (debt) — the day the account settles."],
  links:[{w:"عَلِمَتْ نَفْسٌ (v.5)",e:"عَلِمَتْ نَفْسٌ (Takwīr 81:14)",t:"IDENTICAL — the payoff after the idhā stack"},{w:"مَّا قَدَّمَتْ وَأَخَّرَتْ (v.5)",e:"بِمَا قَدَّمَ وَأَخَّرَ (Qiy. 75:13)",t:"Near-identical — the whole ledger"},{w:"انفَطَرَتْ (v.1)",e:"انشَقَّتْ (Inshiqāq 84:1) · انكَدَرَتْ (81:2)",t:"Three surahs open with a Form VII undoing"},{w:"انتَثَرَتْ (v.2)",e:"مَنثُورًا (Ins. 76:19)",t:"Scattered pearls in joy / stars scattered in ruin"},{w:"فُجِّرَتْ (v.3)",e:"سُجِّرَتْ (Takwīr 81:6) · يُفَجِّرُونَهَا (76:6)",t:"Seas burst vs. seas stoked; springs you command"},{w:"الْقُبُورُ (v.4)",e:"فَأَقْبَرَهُ (ʿAbasa 80:21)",t:"He gave you a grave → the grave is tipped over"},{w:"سَوَّاكَ (v.7)",e:"نُسَوِّيَ / سَوَّى (75:4, 38) · سَوَّاهَا (79:28)",t:"Fourth surah with this verb"},{w:"الْكَرِيمِ (v.6) · كِرَامًا (v.11)",e:"كِرَامٍ بَرَرَةٍ (ʿAbasa 80:16) · كَرِيمٍ (81:19)",t:"Noble Lord, noble scribes — same root twice in one surah"},{w:"حَافِظِينَ (v.10)",e:"حَافِظٌ (Ṭāriq 86:4) · مَّحْفُوظٍ (Burūj 85:22)",t:"Same root — a ḥāfiẓ preserves"},{w:"الْأَبْرَارَ لَفِي نَعِيمٍ (v.13)",e:"Muṭaffifīn 83:22",t:"VERBATIM identical verse, next surah"},{w:"الْفُجَّارَ (v.14)",e:"الْفَجَرَةُ (ʿAbasa 80:42) · يَفْجُرَ (75:5)",t:"Same root as v.3's bursting seas"},{w:"جَحِيمٍ (v.14)",e:"الْجَحِيمُ (79:36, 39 · 81:12)",t:"The Blaze"},{w:"يَصْلَوْنَهَا (v.15)",e:"صَالُو (83:16) · يَصْلَىٰ (84:12)",t:"Three consecutive surahs, same verb"},{w:"بِغَائِبِينَ (v.16)",e:"الْغَيْبِ (Takwīr 81:24)",t:"They called it absent; now they can't be absent from it"},{w:"بِغَائِبِينَ (v.16)",e:"بِمَجْنُونٍ / بِضَنِينٍ (81:22, 24)",t:"Same bāʾ zāʾidah after negation"},{w:"وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ (v.17)",e:"وَمَا يُدْرِيكَ (ʿAbasa 80:3)",t:"Past vs. present — opposite jobs"},{w:"ثُمَّ مَا أَدْرَاكَ (v.18)",e:"ثُمَّ كَلَّا سَيَعْلَمُونَ (Nabaʾ 78:5)",t:"Same repeat-with-thumma escalation"},{w:"وَالْأَمْرُ (v.19)",e:"الْمُدَبِّرَاتِ أَمْرًا (Nāz. 79:5)",t:"The command — managed by angels, owned by Him"},{w:"نَفْسٌ لِّنَفْسٍ (v.19)",e:"يَفِرُّ الْمَرْءُ مِنْ أَخِيهِ (ʿAbasa 80:34)",t:"ʿAbasa showed the running; this gives the reason"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"انفَطَرَتْ", tr:"Infaṭarat", v:"1", root:"The \"splitting open / cleaving apart\" family — faṭara = to split.", meaning:"\"When the sky is cleft open.\"", magic:"The root's double life is the whole point. Faṭara means both to split and to originate. Why? Because creating something is splitting it out of nothingness.", grammar:"Form VII (اِنْفَعَلَ) — the ان- prefix means it happens to the subject with no doer named.", why:"Infaṭarat over \"broke\" — because the root carries creation inside it, making the destruction feel like a reversal, not an accident.", note:"You say this root every Ramadan: Ifṭār = breaking (splitting) your fast, ʿĪd al-Fiṭr. And fiṭrah = your original nature.", more:["And here's the treasure: the same root gives fiṭrah (your original, natural disposition — the state you were \"split into\" at creation), fāṭir (the Originator — one who splits something into being), and Ifṭār — breaking your fast (splitting the fast open)!","So when the sky is infaṭarat, it's being un-originated — the splitting that made it is now the splitting that ends it.","The verb of your beginning is the verb of your ending.","Same shape as Takwīr 81:2 (inkadarat) and Inshiqāq 84:1 (inshaqqat).","Three consecutive surahs open with a Form VII passive-sense verb.","Same root: to split = to originate."]},
  {ar:"الْكَوَاكِبُ انتَثَرَتْ", tr:"Al-kawākibu intatharat", v:"2", root:"Kawākib — plural of kawkab, a planet or bright star.", meaning:"\"and when the stars are scattered.\"", magic:"The connection to Insān is exquisite, and it's an exact reversal.", grammar:"Form VIII (the -ta- slipped inside).", why:"Intatharat pictures loss of arrangement, not loss of light — the sky's design comes apart.", note:"Nathr = PROSE (scattered words). Insān 76:19: pearls scattered in JOY. Infiṭār 82:2: stars scattered in RUIN.", more:["intatharat — the \"scattering / strewing\" family — nathara = to scatter loose things; nathr = PROSE (scattered words, as opposed to shiʿr, poetry with a thread through it!).","And you own this root from Insān 76:19: luʾluʾan manthūrā — \"scattered pearls.\"","- Insān 76:19 — the servants of Paradise are like scattered pearls (manthūrā) — beautiful, abundant, joyfully strewn.","- Infiṭār 82:2 — the stars are scattered (intatharat) — a necklace snapping, order collapsing.","Same root: scattering as GENEROSITY there, scattering as RUIN here. Picture pearls tossed at a wedding vs. a string of pearls breaking and rolling across the floor.","And compare Takwīr 81:2 (inkadarat = dimmed and fell). Takwīr's stars go MURKY; Infiṭār's stars go LOOSE. Two different failures.","Note: v.1 is Form VII, v.2 is Form VIII — small variation keeping the rhythm from flattening.","Same root — a necklace snapping."]},
  {ar:"الْبِحَارُ فُجِّرَتْ", tr:"Al-biḥāru fujjirat", v:"3", root:"The \"bursting open / cleaving forth\" family — you own this root three times over: fajr (dawn splitting the dark), Qiyāmah 75:5 (li-yafjura amāmah — bursting into sin), Insān 76:6 (yufajjirūnahā — the righteous make the spring gush), and ʿAbasa 80:42 (al-fajarah — the wicked who burst past limits).", meaning:"\"and when the seas are burst forth\" — barriers broken, waters released.", magic:"Compare with Takwīr 81:6 (sujjirat, \"stoked like an oven\").", grammar:"Form II passive (fuʿʿila shape) — same as sujjirat, kuwwirat, suyyirat. Intensity + no named doer.", why:"Fujjirat keeps the fajr/bursting family running through five surahs — a root worth owning completely.", note:"Same root as fajr (dawn splits the dark). Takwīr 81:6: seas STOKED. Infiṭār 82:3: seas BURST. Fire vs. flood, same seas.", more:["Both describe the seas, both are Form II passive, and they're describing the same event from two angles: Takwīr says the seas BURN; Infiṭār says the seas BURST.","Fire and flood — the two ways water can betray you.","And the root-echo with Insān 76:6 is the sharpest one: there, the righteous burst forth springs at will — mastery, control, blessing. Here, the seas burst forth uncontrolled — the barriers that held them are gone. Same verb: yours to command in Paradise, no one's to command on that Day."]},
  {ar:"الْقُبُورُ بُعْثِرَتْ", tr:"Al-qubūru buʿthirat", v:"4", root:"Qubūr — plural of qabr, a grave — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:21 (fa-aqbarah, \"He gave him a grave\").", meaning:"\"and when the graves are turned inside out.\"", magic:"The fourth undoing, and it's the one Takwīr never made. Takwīr's twelve stayed above ground — sky, stars, mountains, seas, beasts, records.", grammar:"A quadriliteral verb (four root letters: ب-ع-ث-ر), in the passive.", why:"Buʿthirat over \"opened\" — because opening is orderly; overturning is not.", note:"Baʿthara = to tip a container upside down and dump it out. ʿAbasa 80:21 GAVE you a grave; Infiṭār 82:4 TIPS IT OVER.", more:["buʿthirat — the \"overturning and scattering out\" family — baʿthara = to turn something upside down and dump its contents, to ransack.","(Related in sense to baʿatha, to send forth/resurrect.)","Infiṭār's fourth verse goes DOWN, and empties the earth.","And baʿthara is beautifully chosen: it's the verb for turning a container upside-down and shaking everything out — like emptying a bag onto a table. The graves aren't opened. They're INVERTED.","And connect it to ʿAbasa 80:21: there, being given a grave was listed as a blessing (aqbarah). Here that same gift gets tipped over. The resting-place you were honored with becomes the thing that empties you out.","Quadriliterals often carry a sense of jumbled, messy, repeated action — like zalzala (shake), waswasa (whisper).","The shape itself sounds chaotic.","And this is the one Takwīr didn't have — Infiṭār digs."]},
  {ar:"عَلِمَتْ نَفْسٌ مَّا قَدَّمَتْ وَأَخَّرَتْ", tr:"ʿalimat nafsun mā qaddamat wa akhkharat", v:"5", root:"Qaddama — \"to send forward\" (muqaddimah = an introduction, what comes first).", meaning:"\"A soul will know what it sent ahead and what it left behind.\"", magic:"THIS VERSE IS A DOUBLE JACKPOT — it links to TWO surahs at once.", grammar:"Nafsun is INDEFINITE — \"a soul,\" not \"every soul.\" Singular, anonymous.", why:"The forward/backward pair makes omissions count as much as actions — you're accountable for what you didn't do, too.", note:"Takwīr 81:14 + Qiyāmah 75:13 = this verse. \"Sent ahead\" (what you did) and \"left behind\" (what you didn't). Both on the bill.", more:["akhkhara — \"to put back / defer\" (ākhir = last; al-ākhirah = the Last life).","- \"ʿalimat nafsun\" is IDENTICAL to Takwīr 81:14. Same two words, same position, same function: the answer to the idhā stack.","Twelve clauses there, four here, same landing.","- \"mā qaddamat wa akhkharat\" is nearly identical to Qiyāmah 75:13 — yunabbaʾu'l-insānu yawmaʾidhin bimā qaddama wa akhkhar.","Same pair of verbs, same meaning.","So this one verse is Takwīr's opening + Qiyāmah's closing, fused. If you learned those two, you already knew this verse before you read it.","And the ṭibāq (opposite-pair) does its classic job: \"sent forward\" + \"left behind\" = the ENTIRE ledger, nothing outside it. Two words covering a whole life.","One more difference worth catching: Takwīr said mā aḥḍarat (\"what it brought with it\" — luggage). Infiṭār says mā qaddamat wa akhkharat (\"what it sent ahead and deferred\" — investments and omissions). Takwīr weighs what you carried; Infiṭār weighs what you shipped AND what you skipped.","Same move as Takwīr 81:14, Nabaʾ 78:40 (al-marʾ), and ʿAbasa 80:34 (al-marʾ).","Four surahs in a row narrow to ONE unnamed person.","Note also the past tense for a future event = certainty."]},
  {ar:"مَا غَرَّكَ", tr:"Mā gharraka", v:"6", root:"The \"deceiving by making something look better than it is\" family — gharra = to delude, beguile.", meaning:"\"O man! What deceived you concerning your generous Lord?\"", magic:"This is one of the most remarkable questions in the entire Qur'an, and everything is in the word choice.", grammar:"Mā = interrogative \"what.\" Note the address: yā ayyuha'l-insān.", why:"Gharra over \"made you disobey\" — it targets the self-deception, which is the actual mechanism.", note:"Ghurūr = false glitter, being conned.", more:["Ghurūr = delusion, false glitter; and gharīr = someone naive, easily taken in.","Related sense: ghirr = inexperienced.","Look at what it does NOT say: - Not \"How dare you?\" - Not \"What is wrong with you?\" - Not \"Why did you disobey?\"","It says: \"What DECEIVED you?\" — which quietly assumes you must have been TRICKED, because no one who saw clearly would do this. The question grants you an excuse while removing it. It treats your rebellion as a case of being conned.","And then look at the last two words: bi-Rabbika'l-KARĪM — \"concerning your GENEROUS Lord.\"","Of all the names of Allah, this verse picks al-Karīm — the Generous. Not al-Jabbār (the Compeller), not al-Muntaqim, not al-ʿAzīz. The rebuke is delivered through the name of GENEROSITY. The logic: I have been nothing but generous to you — so what could possibly have talked you out of that?","A famous scholarly observation: some said the name al-Karīm is mentioned here to teach the answer — as if man might reply, \"Your own generosity deceived me\" (i.e., You kept giving despite my sins, so I assumed there'd be no reckoning). The verse names the very attribute the deluded person exploited.","\"O man\" (humanity at large), not \"O disbelievers.\" Nobody is excluded from the question.","\"What CONNED you about your GENEROUS Lord?\" — the rebuke arrives dressed as concern, and it's delivered through the name al-Karīm."]},
  {ar:"سَوَّاكَ فَعَدَلَكَ", tr:"Sawwāka fa-ʿadalak", v:"7", root:"Sawwā — the \"proportioning / making even\" family — you own this THREE times already: Qiyāmah 75:4 (nusawwiya.", meaning:"\"Who created you, then proportioned you, then balanced you.\"", magic:"The answer to v.6's question is delivered as a résumé of favors — and ʿadalak is the stunning one.", grammar:"Form II (sawwā) = careful, deliberate crafting. ʿAdala is Form I.", why:"ʿadalak over a plain word for \"shaped\" — because justice is exactly what the deniers are rejecting, and it's built into their bones.", note:"ʿadl = JUSTICE. Your symmetrical body is described with the justice word.", more:["Re-proportion your fingertips), Qiyāmah 75:38 (sawwā — proportioned you from a clot), Nāziʿāt 79:28 (fa-sawwāhā — proportioned the sky).","ʿadala — the \"justice / balance / symmetry\" family — ʿadl = justice; iʿtidāl = equilibrium, moderation.","ʿAdala is the JUSTICE root. Applied to your body it means symmetry — two eyes level, two arms matched, limbs in proportion. Your physical body is described using the word for JUSTICE, because balance and justice are the same idea in Arabic.","Now feel the argument: \"What deceived you about your generous Lord — the One who built JUSTICE into your very limbs?\" You are walking evidence of balance, arguing against the Day of Balance.","And the three-verb chain (khalaqaka → sawwāka → ʿadalak) with فَـ between each = immediate sequence: created, then instantly proportioned, then instantly balanced. No gap, no rough draft.","Note the relative pronoun الَّذِي opening the verse — it's an extended description of Rabbika'l-Karīm, i.e.","the answer to \"what deceived you?\" is a list of what He did for you.","You are physical proof of balance — denying the Day of Balance.","And sawwā you've now met in four surahs."]},
  {ar:"فِي أَيِّ صُورَةٍ... رَكَّبَكَ", tr:"Fī ayyi ṣūratin… rakkabak", v:"8", root:"ṣūrah — a form, shape, image (taṣwīr = photography in modern Arabic; muṣawwir = the Fashioner, a name of Allah).", meaning:"\"In whatever form He willed, He assembled you.\"", magic:"Rakkaba is ENGINEERING vocabulary. It's the word for fitting components together into a working whole — assembling a machine, composing a structure.", grammar:"Form II again (rakkaba) — intensive, deliberate assembly.", why:"Rakkaba frames you as an assembled machine — and machines have makers, and makers can rebuild.", note:"Tarkīb = assembly / composition (chemistry, grammar). Four-step build: created → proportioned → balanced → ASSEMBLED.", more:["rakkaba — the \"assembling / composing / mounting\" family — rakkaba = to put parts together; tarkīb = composition, structure, assembly; and rakiba = to ride/mount (climbing onto something and joining with it).","Modern Arabic uses tarkīb for chemical composition and grammatical structure.","So the three verbs across v.7–8 form a complete build sequence: 1. khalaqaka — created you (brought into being) 2. sawwāka — proportioned you (got the measurements right) 3. ʿadalak — balanced you (made it symmetrical) 4. rakkabak — assembled you (fitted every part together)","Four verbs, a full manufacturing line — and the person who came off it is asking \"will I really be rebuilt?\"","And \"fī ayyi ṣūratin mā shāʾa\" (\"in whatever form He willed\") is the quiet answer to human vanity: your face, your height, your features — you didn't pick any of it. The variety among humans is His choice, not an achievement of yours.","Ayyi = \"whichever\"; mā here is mā zāʾidah (extra mā for emphasis), a common construction.","And you didn't choose the design."]},
  {ar:"تُكَذِّبُونَ بِالدِّينِ", tr:"Tukadhdhibūna bi'd-dīn", v:"9", root:"Kadhdhaba — the \"calling something a lie\" family — you own it from Nabaʾ 78:28 (kidhdhābā), Qiyāmah 75:32, Nāziʿāt 79:21. dīn — a double-life root: dīn = religion, and dayn = a DEBT, and dāna = to judge / to requite.", meaning:"\"No! Rather, you deny the Recompense.\"", magic:"The root's double meaning is the surah's engine. Dīn and dayn (debt) share three letters — because religion is understood as an obligation you owe and will be asked to settle.", grammar:"Notice the sudden shift from SINGULAR to PLURAL.", why:"Dīn over a word for \"judgment\" — because the debt sense makes the denial sound like defaulting, not disagreeing.", note:"Dīn (religion) and dayn (DEBT) — same three letters. Yawm ad-Dīn = the day the bill comes due.", more:["Yawm ad-Dīn = the Day of Judgment / the Day the DEBT comes due.","So yawm ad-dīn isn't just \"Judgment Day\" — it's the day the account is settled.","Now the logic snaps together: v.6 asked what deceived you? Verses 7–8 listed four favors. Verse 9 gives the real answer: you deny that there's a bill. Nothing \"deceived\" you cleverly — you just decided the debt wasn't real.","And كَلَّا (\"No!\") is the stern rebuttal-particle you own from Qiyāmah 75:11, Nabaʾ 78:4, ʿAbasa 80:11.","Verses 6–8 addressed you (singular): gharraka, sawwāka, rakkabak.","Verse 9 switches to tukadhdhibūn (you all).","The private question becomes a public verdict.","This shift (iltifāt) is deliberate.","And watch the \"you\" go from singular (v.6–8) to plural (v.9)."]},
  {ar:"لَحَافِظِينَ", tr:"La-ḥāfiẓīn", v:"10", root:"The \"guarding / preserving / memorizing\" family — ḥafiẓa = to guard, to preserve.", meaning:"\"And indeed, over you are guardians.\"", magic:"The word you already use for yourself. A ḥāfiẓ is someone who preserves the Qur'an in memory.", grammar:"Triple emphasis: wa inna ʿalaykum la-ḥāfiẓīn — inna + the lām of emphasis + the fronted ʿalaykum.", why:"ḥāfiẓīn over \"watchers\" — the preservation sense makes the record feel permanent, not merely observed.", note:"A ḥāfiẓ memorizes the Qur'an — preserves it perfectly. These angels are ḥāfiẓ of your LIFE.", more:["ḥāfiẓ = one who has memorized the Qur'an (a \"preserver\"!); maḥfūẓ = protected (as in al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ, the Preserved Tablet — Burūj 85:22); ḥifẓ = memorization.","These angels are ḥāfiẓīn of YOU — preserving your life in a record the way a ḥāfiẓ preserves a text.","And notice the tone: the root isn't menacing. Ḥifẓ means protection as much as surveillance — the same word for guarding a treasure. You're not being spied on; you're being PRESERVED. Which is worse in one way and kinder in another: nothing you did is lost.","Cross-link: the same root appears in Ṭāriq 86:4 — in kullu nafsin lammā ʿalayhā ḥāfiẓ (\"there is no soul but has over it a guardian\"). Two surahs apart, same statement.","Arabic underlining, three layers.","Same root as al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ (Burūj 85:22)."]},
  {ar:"كِرَامًا كَاتِبِينَ", tr:"Kirāman kātibīn", v:"11", root:"Kirām — the \"nobility / generosity\" family — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:16 (kirāmin bararah.", meaning:"\"Noble, recording [angels].\"", magic:"The word kirām here is doing enormous emotional work, and it's the same word used four verses earlier for Allah Himself (Rabbika'l-Karīm, v.6).", grammar:"Both accusative, describing ḥāfiẓīn in v.10.", why:"Kirām over \"strict\" or \"watchful\" — it turns surveillance into honorable witness.", note:"V.6: your Lord is al-Karīm (Generous). v.11: the scribes are kirām (noble). Same root, four verses apart.", more:["\"noble, righteous\" scribes) and Takwīr 81:19 (rasūlin karīm), and Makkah al-Mukarramah.","kātibīn — the \"writing\" family (kataba = to write; kitāb = a book; maktab = an office).","Think about why \"noble\" matters for a record-keeper. A noble scribe: - doesn't exaggerate your sins - doesn't invent anything - doesn't gossip about what he writes - records with dignity, not relish","You're being watched — but by someone honorable. Compare that to being recorded by an enemy. The surah about accountability keeps inserting mercy: a GENEROUS Lord (v.6), NOBLE scribes (v.11).","And here's the strongest link: ʿAbasa 80:16 described the angels of revelation as kirāmin bararah (noble, righteous). Infiṭār 82:11 describes the angels of record as kirāman kātibīn (noble, writing). Same adjective, two jobs — the ones who bring the message and the ones who record your response are BOTH noble.","Note the faʿīl→kirām plural — same broken-plural shape as ʿiẓām, ṭiwāl.","And ʿAbasa 80:16 used it for the revelation-angels."]},
  {ar:"الْأَبْرَارَ... نَعِيمٍ", tr:"Al-abrāra… naʿīm", v:"13", root:"Abrār — the \"solid, dependable goodness\" family — you own it from Insān 76:5 (inna'l-abrāra yashrabūn) and ʿAbasa 80:16 (bararah), and birr = devotion to parents.", meaning:"\"Indeed, the righteous will be in bliss.\"", magic:"This exact phrase — inna'l-abrāra la-fī naʿīm — appears VERBATIM in Muṭaffifīn 83:22. Next surah, same sentence. Free vocabulary, and worth noticing when you get there.", grammar:"Inna + accusative subject + la-fī (lām of emphasis on the predicate).", why:"Abrār (dependable goodness) over a word for \"believers\" — it emphasizes consistency, matching birr.", note:"Insān 76:5 (abrār) + Insān 76:20 (naʿīm) → both land in this one verse. And this exact sentence repeats in Muṭaffifīn 83:22.", more:["naʿīm — the \"blessing / comfort\" family — you own it from Insān 76:20 (raʾayta naʿīman wa mulkan kabīrā); related to niʿmah (a favor) and even naʿam (yes!).","And the pairing with v.14 is a textbook muqābala (mirror-antithesis):","Identical structure, word for word, with only the two key nouns swapped. And naʿīm / jaḥīm even rhyme — the sound makes the two fates feel like two doors in the same wall.","Same frame in both verses — that's what makes the contrast land."]},
  {ar:"الْفُجَّارَ... جَحِيمٍ", tr:"Al-fujjāra… jaḥīm", v:"14", root:"Fujjār — the \"bursting past all restraint\" family — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:42 (al-kafaratu'l-fajarah) and Qiyāmah 75:5 (li-yafjura amāmah), and fajr (dawn splitting).", meaning:"\"And indeed, the wicked will be in a blaze.\"", magic:"Note the plural form is different from ʿAbasa's. ʿAbasa 80:42 used fajarah (faʿalah pattern); here it's fujjār (fuʿʿāl pattern).", grammar:"Fuʿʿāl is an intensive plural pattern — kuffār, fujjār, kuttāb, ḥujjāj.", why:"Fujjār over a milder word — it names them by the mechanism: no brakes, no boundaries.", note:"V.3: the SEAS burst (fujjirat). v.14: the PEOPLE burst (fujjār). Same root, same surah. They lived like a sea with no shore.", more:["jaḥīm — the \"fiercely blazing\" family — you own it from Nāziʿāt 79:36, 39 and Takwīr 81:12.","Both are plurals of fājir, but fujjār is the more INTENSIVE shape — the same fuʿʿāl family as the intensive forms you learned in Nabaʾ.","And here's the beautiful full-circle: v.3 said the seas were fujjirat (burst open). v.14 says the people are fujjār (burst past limits). SAME ROOT, eleven verses apart, in the same surah. The seas broke their barriers; so did these people. The cosmos does at the end what they did their whole lives.","Recognize the doubled middle letter + long ā."]},
  {ar:"يَصْلَوْنَهَا", tr:"Yaṣlawnahā", v:"15", root:"The \"roasting / being scorched by fire\" family — ṣaliya = to be burned/roasted; and muṣallā — no, careful: that's a different root (ṣ-l-w for prayer).", meaning:"\"They will [enter to] burn in it on the Day of Recompense.\"", magic:"This root runs through the next three surahs — worth collecting now: - Infiṭār 82:15 — yaṣlawnahā (they burn in it) - Muṭaffifīn 83:16.", grammar:"Weak-final verb (ص-ل-ي).", why:"Yaṣlawna over \"enter\" — because it names the sensation, not the location.", note:"ṣaliya = to ROAST over fire. Same verb in Infiṭār 82:15, Muṭaffifīn 83:16, Inshiqāq 84:12 — three surahs running.", more:["This one is ص-ل-ي: to roast.","La-ṣālū'l-jaḥīm (they will burn in the Blaze) - Inshiqāq 84:12 — wa yaṣlā saʿīrā (he will burn in a Blaze)","Three consecutive surahs, same verb. Learn it once here.","And the specific sense — roasting, being exposed to fire — is more prolonged than \"burning.\" It's the word for meat over flame, not a quick flash.","Note the plural: yaṣlawna — the final weak letter shifts to a w before the plural ending.","Watch how weak-final verbs behave in the plural."]},
  {ar:"بِغَائِبِينَ", tr:"Bi-ghāʾibīn", v:"16", root:"The \"absence / hiddenness\" family — ghāba = to be absent; al-ghayb = the Unseen (which you own from Takwīr 81:24 — wa mā huwa ʿala'l-ghaybi bi-ḍanīn).", meaning:"\"And they will not be absent from it.\"", magic:"The double negative is the point — and it's chilling. \"They will not be absent\" is a strange way to say \"they will stay.\" Why phrase it as absence?", grammar:"Another bāʾ zāʾidah (extra bāʾ) after negation for emphasis — you own this from Takwīr 81:22, 24, 25 (bi-majnūn, bi-ḍanīn, bi-qawl).", why:"Ghāʾibīn over \"they will remain\" — it forecloses every form of relief, including a moment's break.", note:"Al-ghayb = the Unseen (Takwīr 81:24). They treated the Hereafter as ABSENT; now they can never be absent FROM it.", more:["Because ghāʾib is the word for someone who steps out — takes a break, is excused, misses a session. The verse pre-emptively closes the door on any interruption: no leave, no pause, no time off, not even briefly out of the room.","And there's a haunting echo with al-ghayb (the Unseen): they spent their lives treating the Hereafter as ghayb — absent, unreal, out of sight. Now THEY are the ones who can never be absent from it. The thing they called \"not there\" becomes the thing they can never leave.","Four instances across two surahs."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ", tr:"Wa mā adrāka", v:"17–18", root:"The \"knowing by perception\" family — darā = to become aware; Form IV adrā = to make someone know.", meaning:"\"And what can make you know what the Day of Recompense is?\" — asked twice.", magic:"MAJOR GRAMMAR GEM — and it's a direct contrast with a word you already own.", grammar:"مَا أَدْرَاكَ = past, followed by explanation. مَا يُدْرِيكَ = present, followed by nothing. Learn the pair.", why:"Asking twice without answering either time makes the Day feel beyond description — which is the answer.", note:"ʿAbasa 80:3 = yudrīka (present, you WON'T be told). Infiṭār 82:17 = adrāka (past, you WILL be told).", more:["Compare ʿAbasa 80:3: wa mā YUDRĪKA (present tense) with Infiṭār 82:17: wa mā ADRĀKA (past tense).","There's a recognized Qur'anic pattern here: - mā yudrīka (present) → introduces something the addressee will NOT be told — \"how could you know?\" (ʿAbasa 80:3: perhaps he would purify himself — unknowable to you.) - mā adrāka (past) → introduces something that IS about to be explained — \"and what made you know…?\" followed by the answer.","Two surahs apart, the same construction in two tenses, doing two opposite jobs. Catch this once and you'll read it correctly forever.","And the REPETITION here is the balāgha. Verse 17 asks it. Verse 18 asks the exact same thing again, with ثُمَّ (then) added. Same escalation technique as Nabaʾ 78:4–5 (kallā sa-yaʿlamūn / thumma kallā sa-yaʿlamūn). Ask, pause, ask again — heavier. The repetition says: you didn't grasp it the first time.","And it's asked TWICE — same escalation as Nabaʾ 78:4–5."]},
  {ar:"لَا تَمْلِكُ نَفْسٌ لِّنَفْسٍ شَيْئًا", tr:"Lā tamliku nafsun li-nafsin shayʾā", v:"19", root:"The \"ownership / control / power over\" family — malaka = to own, to have power over; malik = a king; mulk = dominion; mamlūk = owned.", meaning:"\"A day when no soul will possess [the power to do] anything for another soul.\"", magic:"Look at the exact repetition: nafsun li-nafsin — \"a soul… for a soul.\" The same word twice, both indefinite, side by side. No names, no titles, no relationships.", grammar:"Lā tamliku + li- — \"possesses [nothing] for someone.\" The لِـ marks the beneficiary.", why:"Malaka (to own/control) rather than \"help\" — because intercession, wealth, status are all forms of OWNERSHIP, and the verse cancels the whole category at once.", note:"Malik = a KING; mulk = dominion. \"A soul… for a soul… nothing.\" No names, no titles.", more:["Not \"no father for his son,\" not \"no friend for his friend\" — just soul… to soul… nothing.","The grammar strips away every label you might have leaned on.","And connect it to ʿAbasa 80:34–36, where the fleeing was listed: brother → mother → father → wife → children. ʿAbasa showed you the RUNNING; Infiṭār gives you the REASON: nobody owns anything they could give.","Then the final clause is the release: wa'l-amru yawmaʾidhin lillāh — \"and the command, that Day, belongs to Allah.\" The lām of possession (li-llāh) sits against lā tamliku — nobody OWNS anything; He owns everything. The surah that began with the sky splitting ends with ownership settled.","And note the word al-amr — you own it from Nāziʿāt 79:5 (al-mudabbirāti amrā — the angels who manage the affair). There, angels managed the command. Here, the command reverts entirely to its Owner.","Note shayʾan (accusative, indefinite) = \"anything at all\" — the smallest possible unit is denied.","And the last word of the surah: lillāh — it all belongs to Him."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:83, name:"Al-Muṭaffifīn", ar:"المطففين", en:"Those Who Give Short Measure", verses:36, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"The surah's whole logic: if you'd cheat someone by two grams, you've already told me everything about what you think of the Day of Reckoning.",
  special:["It opens with the smallest sin in the Qur'an — and treats it as a catastrophe.","Not murder. Not idolatry. Shorting someone a few grams at the market. Slightly heavy on the scale when you're buying; slightly light when you're selling. Nobody dies. Nobody notices. It's practically nothing.","And the surah opens: \"WOE to the shortchangers.\"","Then it does something extraordinary: it uses that tiny sin to build the entire architecture of the Hereafter — two registers (Sijjīn and ʿIlliyyīn), two drinks, two groups, two sets of laughter. A few grams on a scale opens onto the cosmic balance.","--"],
  big:["The Two Grams (v.1–6) — the sin, and the one question that explains it: don't these people think they'll be raised?","The Wicked's Register (v.7–17) — Sijjīn, the sealed record; the rust on the heart; and the worst punishment in the Qur'an: being VEILED from your Lord.","The Righteous' Register (v.18–28) — ʿIlliyyīn, the high places; the sealed musk-wine; the spring of Tasnīm.","The Laughter Reversed (v.29–36) — they mocked; now the believers sit on couches and watch."],
  traps:["V.2 vs. v.3 — the GRAMMAR is the sin. Taking: Form VIII + Form X (elaborate). Giving: plain Form I (lazy). If you read them as the same verb, you miss the entire point.","سِجِّينٍ (v.7) ↔ عِلِّيِّينَ (v.18) — mirrored structure, v.7–9 ↔ v.18–20, word for word. Sijjīn = prison/LOW; ʿIlliyyīn = heights/HIGH. Learn as a pair.","رَانَ (v.14) → لَّمَحْجُوبُونَ (v.15) — the rust becomes the veil. Read them as one movement, not two verses.","V.15 (veiled) comes BEFORE v.16 (fire) — deliberately. The separation is the greater punishment; the order proves it.","انقَلَبُوا (83:31) and يَنقَلِبُ (84:9) — same verb, same phrase (\"returned to his family\"), one surah apart, opposite joys. Plus 84:13 uses it a third way.","ثُوِّبَ (v.36) — thawāb is the REWARD word, used sarcastically. Don't translate it straight.","مُعْتَدٍ (v.12) — manqūṣ: the final yāʾ drops. Same as rāqin (75:27), al-jawāri (81:16).","صَالُو (v.16) — the nūn drops in iḍāfah (ṣālūna → ṣālū'l-jaḥīm). Same rule as banīh (80:36).","وَيْلٌ (v.1) ≠ وَلِيّ (ally) ≠ وَالٍ (governor) — different roots, easy to confuse when reading fast.","الْمُطَفِّفِينَ (v.1) — remember ṭaff = \"the negligible amount.\" Their excuse IS their name."],
  links:[{w:"إِنَّ الْأَبْرَارَ لَفِي نَعِيمٍ (v.22)",e:"Infiṭār 82:13",t:"VERBATIM identical verse"},{w:"انقَلَبُوا... أَهْلِهِمْ (v.31)",e:"يَنقَلِبُ إِلَىٰ أَهْلِهِ (Inshiqāq 84:9)",t:"Same verb + phrase — opposite homecomings"},{w:"لَّمَحْجُوبُونَ (v.15)",e:"نَاظِرَة (Qiy. 75:23)",t:"VEILED vs. LOOKING at their Lord"},{w:"نَضْرَةَ (v.24)",e:"نَاضِرَة (75:22) · نَضْرَةً (76:11)",t:"Third surah with the glow-root"},{w:"الْأَرَائِكِ (v.23, 35)",e:"الْأَرَائِك (Ins. 76:13)",t:"Identical word — the couches"},{w:"مِزَاجُهُ (v.27)",e:"مِزَاجُهَا (76:5, 17)",t:"Identical word — the blend"},{w:"يَسْتَوْفُونَ (v.2)",e:"يُوفُونَ بِالنَّذْرِ (76:7)",t:"Fulfilling for others vs. demanding for self"},{w:"حَافِظِينَ (v.33)",e:"حَافِظِينَ (Infiṭār 82:10) · حَافِظٌ (86:4)",t:"They took the ANGELS' job"},{w:"الْفُجَّارِ / الْأَبْرَارِ (v.7, 18)",e:"الْفُجَّارَ / الْأَبْرَارَ (82:13–14)",t:"Same pair, next-door surahs"},{w:"صَالُو الْجَحِيمِ (v.16)",e:"يَصْلَوْنَهَا (82:15) · يَصْلَىٰ (84:12)",t:"Three consecutive surahs"},{w:"يُخْسِرُونَ (v.3)",e:"كَرَّةٌ خَاسِرَةٌ (Qiy. 75:12)",t:"The loss root"},{w:"مَّبْعُوثُونَ (v.4)",e:"بُعْثِرَتْ (Infiṭār 82:4)",t:"The raising/sending forth family"},{w:"وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ (v.8, 19)",e:"(82:17–18) · (Ṭāriq 86:2)",t:"Past tense → explanation follows"},{w:"مُعْتَدٍ (v.12)",e:"رَاقٍ (75:27) · الْجَوَارِ (81:16)",t:"Third manqūṣ — final yāʾ drops"},{w:"فَلْيَتَنَافَسِ (v.26)",e:"تَنَفَّسَ (Takwīr 81:18) · نَفْسٌ (81:14, 82:5)",t:"Breath / soul / precious / compete — one root"},{w:"يَتَغَامَزُونَ (v.30)",e:"يَتَسَاءَلُونَ (Nabaʾ 78:1)",t:"Form VI — mutual action"},{w:"لِرَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ (v.6)",e:"رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ (Takwīr 81:29)",t:"Same phrase; also opens al-Fātiḥah"},{w:"الْمُقَرَّبُونَ (v.21, 28)",e:"أُزْلِفَتْ (Takwīr 81:13)",t:"Nearness as the reward"},{w:"أَسَاطِيرُ (v.13)",e:"كِتَابٌ مَّرْقُومٌ (v.9, same surah)",t:"He mocks \"old lines\" — his lines are being written"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"وَيْلٌ", tr:"Waylun", v:"1 · 10", root:"A word of doom, ruin, destruction — often translated \"woe.\" Classically also understood as a valley in Hell, but primarily it functions as an exclamation of ruin.", meaning:"\"Woe to the shortchangers!\"", magic:"Starting a surah with wayl is a slap. No introduction, no oath, no \"O mankind.\" The very first word is a curse.", grammar:"Waylun is indefinite nominative — a standalone exclamation, with li- marking who it's aimed at.", why:"The harshest available opener, applied to the mildest available sin — that mismatch is the message.", note:"The surah's FIRST WORD is a curse. And it repeats at v.10 — bookending the section.", more:["Compare the openings you know: Nabaʾ began with a question, Takwīr with \"when,\" Infiṭār with \"when.\" This one opens with the verdict already delivered.","And it repeats at v.10 (waylun yawmaʾidhin li'l-mukadhdhibīn) — framing the whole first section. Woe at the start, woe again nine verses later.","Compare Nāziʿāt's nakāl and Qiyāmah 75:34's awlā laka fa-awlā — the Qur'an has a whole family of doom-idioms. Wayl is the bluntest.","Don't confuse wayl (woe) with walī (ally) or wāl (governor) — different roots entirely."]},
  {ar:"الْمُطَفِّفِينَ", tr:"Al-muṭaffifīn", v:"1", root:"A gorgeous root — ṭaff means the tiny bit at the brim, the little that's barely there.", meaning:"\"the shortchangers\" — those who skim a little off the measure.", magic:"THE ROOT ITSELF IS THE ARGUMENT, and it's brilliant.", grammar:"Form II active participle, plural (mufaʿʿil). Recognize the shape: mu- + doubled middle letter.", why:"Naming them after \"the trivial amount\" means their defense is built into their indictment.", note:"ṭaff = the tiny bit at the brim, the negligible amount.", more:["Ṭaffafa = to give just under the full measure.","Related: ṭafīf = a trifling, negligible amount.","Ṭaff means \"the negligible amount.\" So al-muṭaffifīn literally means \"the people of the trifling bit\" — those who cheat by an amount so small it has its own word for being insignificant.","The Qur'an names them by exactly the excuse they'd use. \"It's nothing. It's a couple of grams. It doesn't matter.\" Yes — and that's your NAME now.","And the Form II (ṭaffafa) is doing work too: it's intensive/repeated — this isn't one slip, it's a habit, done again and again, each time negligibly.","Same shape as mukadhdhibīn (v.10), muqarrabūn (v.21).","They're literally called \"the people of the barely-anything.\" Their excuse is their name."]},
  {ar:"اكْتَالُوا... يَسْتَوْفُونَ", tr:"Iktālū… yastawfūn", v:"2", root:"Iktālū — the \"measuring\" family (kayl = a measure; mikyāl = a measuring vessel); Form VIII = to measure FOR ONESELF, to take one's measure.", meaning:"\"who, when they take measure from people, exact it in full.\"", magic:"The two verb-forms do all the work here, and this is a beautiful grammar lesson. - Form VIII (iktālū) = doing it FOR YOURSELF.", grammar:"Form VIII = reflexive benefit (\"for oneself\"). Form X = seeking/demanding.", why:"The forms make greed grammatical — you can feel the leaning-over-the-scale in the morphology.", note:"Form VIII = \"for MYSELF.\" Form X = \"give me ALL of it.\" Insān 76:7: fulfilling FOR OTHERS. Here: demanding FOR SELF.", more:["yastawfūn — the \"fulfilling completely\" family — you own this root from Insān 76:7 (yūfūna bi'n-nadhr — \"they fulfil the vow\"); Form X = to demand/exact the FULL amount.","Not \"they measure\" but \"they take their own measure.\" - Form X (yastawfūn) = SEEKING/DEMANDING the complete thing.","Not \"they receive fully\" but \"they insist on every last grain.\"","So the picture is precise: when RECEIVING, they're meticulous — leaning over the scale, demanding every particle. The forms themselves paint a finicky, exacting customer.","And the contrast with Insān 76:7 is quietly devastating: there, the righteous yūfūna — they fulfil what they promised, in full, to others. Here, the same root is used for demanding fullness FOR THEMSELVES. Same root: giving completely vs. taking completely.","One more subtlety: ʿala'n-nās — \"from people\" uses عَلَى (upon), which carries a hint of taking at someone's expense, weighing down on them.","You own Form X from ʿAbasa 80:5 (istaghnā) and Takwīr 81:28 (yastaqīm).","Same root, opposite direction."]},
  {ar:"كَالُوهُمْ... يُخْسِرُونَ", tr:"Kālūhum… yukhsirūn", v:"3", root:"Kālū — the same measuring root, but Form I now — plain, unemphatic.", meaning:"\"but when they measure out for them or weigh for them, they cause loss.\"", magic:"The grammar drop is the entire verse — and it's one of the finest touches in the Qur'an.", grammar:"Form IV (أَفْعَلَ) = to CAUSE X. You own it from ʿAbasa 80:21–22 (amātahu, aqbarahu, ansharahu).", why:"Yukhsirūn over \"they give less\" — it names the victim's experience, not the cheat's action.", note:"V.2 (taking) = Forms VIII & X — elaborate. v.3 (giving) = plain Form I — lazy. The GRAMMAR is the double standard.", more:["yukhsirūn — the \"loss\" family — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:12 (karratun khāsirah, \"a losing return\"); Form IV = to CAUSE someone loss.","V.2 (receiving): iktālū — Form VIII, yastawfūn — Form X. Elaborate. Emphatic. Effortful. v.3 (giving): kālū — plain Form I. Bare. Minimal. No effort at all.","The verb forms themselves show the double standard. When it's their gain: complex, intensive, demanding forms. When it's someone else's: the simplest possible verb. You can HEAR the carelessness in the morphology.","And yukhsirūn is Form IV = CAUSATIVE — they don't just \"give less,\" they actively inflict loss. The verse makes them the agents of someone else's khusrān.","Cross-link: Qiyāmah 75:12 had the deniers sneering \"tilka idhan karratun khāsirah\" (\"that would be a losing return\"). They were worried about THEIR loss; here they're busy causing others'.","Here: akhsara = to cause loss."]},
  {ar:"أَلَا يَظُنُّ... مَّبْعُوثُونَ", tr:"Alā yaẓunnu… mabʿūthūn", v:"4", root:"ẓanna — a double-edged root: usually \"to suppose/think,\" but you own its stronger sense from Qiyāmah 75:28 (wa ẓanna annahu'l-firāq — where it means CERTAINTY).", meaning:"\"Do those not think that they will be resurrected?\"", magic:"This one verse is the hinge of the entire surah, and it explains everything.", grammar:"Alā = hamzah of questioning + lā of negation = a rhetorical question expressing amazement.", why:"ẓanna (mere supposition) sets the lowest possible threshold — and they didn't even clear that.", note:"The whole surah's logic in one verse: \"Don't they even SUSPECT they'll be raised?\" The bar is set at suspicion — and they missed it.", more:["mabʿūthūn — the \"sending forth / raising\" family (baʿatha = to send, to resurrect; baʿth = the Resurrection).","The surah just described a trivial commercial sin. Now it asks ONE question — and the question is not about honesty. It's: \"Don't these people realize they'll be RAISED?\"","That's the diagnosis: cheating on the scale is not a business problem. It's a THEOLOGY problem. Nobody who genuinely expected to stand before a perfect Reckoner would shave two grams off a bag of dates. The small sin exposes the big absence.","And ẓanna here is beautifully ambiguous: \"don't they even SUSPECT?\" — the bar is set as low as possible. Not \"don't they believe,\" not \"don't they know\" — \"doesn't it even CROSS THEIR MIND?\" Even a passing suspicion would have stopped them.","And alā is a particle of attention-grabbing astonishment — \"Do they not…?!\"","Mabʿūthūn is a passive participle — they will be raised, not raise themselves."]},
  {ar:"يَقُومُ النَّاسُ لِرَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ", tr:"Yaqūmu'n-nāsu li-Rabbi'l-ʿālamīn", v:"6", root:"Yaqūmu — the \"standing\" family — you own it from Nāziʿāt 79:40 (maqāma rabbih) and Takwīr 81:28 (yastaqīm).", meaning:"\"The Day mankind will stand before the Lord of all the worlds.\"", magic:"The answer to a merchant's cheating is a courtroom image — and the scale-imagery is still running underneath.", grammar:"The lām in li-Rabb = \"before / for\" — standing at His disposal.", why:"Rabb al-ʿālamīn over any narrower title — because the offense was local; the Judge is universal.", note:"Same phrase as Takwīr 81:29 and al-Fātiḥah. He cheated on a small scale; he stands before the Lord of ALL scales.", more:["ʿālamīn — all the worlds — you own it from Takwīr 81:27, 29.","Notice the title chosen: Rabb al-ʿĀlamīn — the Lord of ALL the worlds. Not \"your Lord,\" not \"the Judge.\" The man cheating over a handful of grain is being reminded he'll stand before the Owner of every world there is. The scale he tampered with belongs to Him too.","And it's the SAME phrase that closes Takwīr (81:29) and opens al-Fātiḥah. Three occurrences you now own.","One more: yaqūmu (standing) is doing quiet work — a merchant stands behind his scale; on that Day he stands in front of it.","Note yawma is accusative (an adverb of time attached to v.5's \"a great Day\")."]},
  {ar:"سِجِّينٍ", tr:"Sijjīn", v:"7–8", root:"The \"imprisonment\" family — sijn = a prison; sajjān = a jailer; masjūn = imprisoned.", meaning:"\"Indeed, the record of the wicked is in Sijjīn.\"", magic:"The fiʿʿīl pattern is an INTENSIVE — so Sijjīn means something like \"the intensely confining place.\"", grammar:"The mā adrāka construction — you own it from Infiṭār 82:17–18 and Ṭāriq 86:2. Remember: PAST tense adrāka → an explanation follows.", why:"Sijjīn over \"a record\" — because the file and the sentence are the same object.", note:"Sijn = PRISON (Sūrat Yūsuf). Their record IS their cell. And Sijjīn (low/prison) vs.", more:["You know sijn from Sūrat Yūsuf (yā ṣāḥibayi's-sijn, \"O my two prison companions\").","The name does double duty: - It's a place (low, confined, sealed). - And it describes what the record DOES to them — the file itself is a prison. Your deeds, locked in, inescapable.","And notice the surah's structural symmetry — this is one of its most beautiful features. Watch v.7–9 against v.18–20:","Word-for-word mirrored structure, eleven verses apart. And Sijjīn (LOW, prison) vs. ʿIlliyyīn (HIGH, elevated) — the names are spatial opposites.","ʿIlliyyīn (high) — mirrored structure, v.7–9 ↔ v.18–20."]},
  {ar:"كِتَابٌ مَّرْقُومٌ", tr:"Kitābun marqūm", v:"9 · 20", root:"The \"marking / numbering / inscribing\" family — raqm = a number/digit (modern Arabic: raqm = a number, raqmī = digital!); marqūm = written, marked, numbered.", meaning:"\"A book inscribed / numbered.\"", magic:"This is the perfect word for THIS surah, and it's easy to miss why.", grammar:"Passive participle (mafʿūl) — \"that which has been inscribed.\" Someone wrote it; you didn't.", why:"Marqūm (numbered) over maktūb (written) — because the sin was numerical.", note:"Raqm = a NUMBER (raqmī = digital). They cheated the numbers; their file is numbered perfectly.", more:["The sin was tampering with NUMBERS on a scale. The punishment-record is marqūm — \"NUMBERED, precisely marked.\" Same conceptual field: quantities, digits, exactness.","They fudged the figures; their record is figure-perfect. Raqm is literally the word for a digit. You can't shave a gram off a marqūm book.","And the phrase is IDENTICAL in v.9 and v.20 — used for both Sijjīn and ʿIlliyyīn. Both records are equally precise. The system doesn't change; only what's in it does.","Same phrase for BOTH registers (v.9, v.20)."]},
  {ar:"مُعْتَدٍ أَثِيمٍ", tr:"Muʿtadin athīm", v:"12", root:"Muʿtad — the \"exceeding / transgressing\" family — iʿtadā = to cross the line, to overstep; ʿudwān = aggression.", meaning:"\"And none denies it except every transgressor, sinner.\"", magic:"The pairing is a two-part diagnosis, and it's precise: - Muʿtad = he crosses lines (a behavioral problem — boundaries) - Athīm = he is steeped in sin (a character problem.", grammar:"Muʿtadin is a manqūṣ noun — it drops its final yāʾ and takes tanwīn on the dāl.", why:"Naming both the behavior and the character closes off the excuse \"I just made a mistake.\"", note:"Iʿtadā = to CROSS THE LINE. Two-part diagnosis: crosses lines (outward) + saturated in sin (inward).", more:["Note: same letters as ʿadala (justice) in Infiṭār 82:7 — ع-د-ل vs ع-د-و — different roots, related idea of a LINE (one keeps it, one crosses it).","athīm — the \"sin\" family (ithm = sin) — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:? — no; from āthiman in Insān 76:24!","The faʿīl intensive pattern means habitual, saturated)","Crossing lines outward, rotting inward. Compare ʿAbasa 80:42's closing pair: kafarah fajarah (covering inward, bursting outward). Both surahs use a two-word diagnosis; both split into an inner and outer failure.","And kullu (\"every\") is doing heavy lifting: \"none denies it except EVERY transgressor\" — the denial is universal among that type, and exclusive to it. If you deny the Day, you're in that category, no exceptions.","You own this rule from Qiyāmah 75:27 (rāqin) and Takwīr 81:16 (al-jawāri).","And muʿtadin is manqūṣ — the yāʾ drops (like rāqin, al-jawāri)."]},
  {ar:"أَسَاطِيرُ الْأَوَّلِينَ", tr:"Asāṭīru'l-awwalīn", v:"13", root:"Asāṭīr — plural of usṭūrah, a legend, a tall tale.", meaning:"\"When Our verses are recited to him, he says: 'Legends of the ancients!'\"", magic:"The irony here is delicious and structural.", grammar:"Asāṭīr is a broken plural; al-awwalīn = \"the former peoples\" (from awwal, first).", why:"Asāṭīr is exactly the sneer a literate, worldly man would use — dismissive, cultured, and empty.", note:"Saṭr = a LINE of writing (lined paper = musaṭṭar).", more:["The root س-ط-ر means to WRITE IN LINES — saṭr = a LINE of text (modern Arabic: saṭr = a line, musaṭṭar = ruled/lined paper).","So asāṭīr = things written down → old stories, fables.","Asāṭīr comes from saṭr — \"a written LINE.\" He dismisses the revelation as \"just old lines on a page.\"","But look at what surrounds him in this very surah: his own deeds are being recorded in a kitābun marqūm (v.9) — a numbered book. He calls God's writing \"old lines\" while God's scribes are writing HIS lines.","He mocks a book. He's living in one.","And notice: this is his ONLY argument. The verse gives him one line of dialogue, and it's not a refutation — it's a dismissal. He doesn't answer the Qur'an; he labels it.","The whole phrase is his quoted speech — the Qur'an lets him convict himself.","He calls revelation \"old lines\" — while his OWN lines are being written down (v.9)."]},
  {ar:"رَانَ عَلَىٰ قُلُوبِهِم", tr:"Rāna ʿalā qulūbihim", v:"14", root:"The \"rust / film / covering stain\" family — rāna = to overlay with a dark film, the way rust builds on iron or grime settles on a mirror.", meaning:"\"No! Rather, what they used to earn has RUSTED over their hearts.\"", magic:"One of the most famous and useful images in the entire Qur'an — and there's a hadith that explains it exactly.", grammar:"Rāna is a hollow verb (weak middle و/ي).", why:"Rān over \"sealed\" or \"hardened\" — because rust is gradual, self-generated, and removable, which makes it both a warning and an invitation.", note:"Rān = RUST. The hadith: a black spot per sin, polished by repentance, or it spreads until it covers the heart.", more:["Rayn = that covering layer.","The Prophet ﷺ described (reported in Tirmidhī, Ibn Mājah, Aḥmad) that when a servant commits a sin, a BLACK SPOT is placed on his heart. If he repents, it is polished away. If he returns to the sin, it increases until it covers his heart — and that is the rān Allah mentions in this verse. The hadith names this verse directly.","Why rust is the perfect metaphor: - Rust doesn't appear all at once — it accumulates, invisibly, over time. - Rust comes from the metal's own exposure — you don't add it; it forms from what you do. - Rust makes a mirror stop reflecting — which is exactly the problem: the heart can no longer SEE. - And rust can be POLISHED OFF — which is why the hadith mentions repentance.","Now connect it to v.15 (coming next): their hearts are rusted over here, and they are VEILED from their Lord there. The veil in the Hereafter is just the rust of this life, made permanent. Same barrier, two stages.","And \"mā kānū yaksibūn\" (\"what they used to EARN\") is chosen with care: kasb is COMMERCIAL vocabulary — \"earnings, gain.\" In a surah about cheating in trade, their sins are described as their PROFITS. They earned rust.","Mā kānū yaksibūn = habitual past (\"what they used to keep earning\") — ongoing, accumulated, not a one-off.","Rust = a mirror that stops reflecting.","And their sins are called their \"earnings\" — in a surah about trade."]},
  {ar:"لَّمَحْجُوبُونَ", tr:"La-maḥjūbūn", v:"15", root:"The \"veiling / screening / barring\" family — ḥijāb = a veil, a screen, a barrier.", meaning:"\"No! Indeed, from their Lord, that Day, they will be VEILED.\"", magic:"Many scholars consider this the most severe verse about punishment in the Qur'an — and it doesn't mention fire at all.", grammar:"Triple emphasis: inna + lām + fronted ʿan rabbihim.", why:"Maḥjūbūn over \"punished\" — because the deprivation is the punishment.", note:"ḥijāb = a VEIL/SCREEN. Named BEFORE the fire — because it's worse. Qiyāmah 75:23: the righteous LOOK at their Lord.", more:["ḥajaba = to block from view, to bar entry; ḥājib = a doorkeeper (one who screens who gets in).","Read the sequence in this surah: v.16 mentions the Blaze. But v.15 comes FIRST — and it's worse. The order is deliberate: the veiling is named before the fire, because it IS the greater punishment.","Why? Imām al-Shāfiʿī is famously reported to have argued from this verse that the believers WILL see their Lord — because if being veiled is singled out as the punishment of the wicked, then being UNveiled must be the reward of the righteous. The verse proves the opposite by stating the negative.","And you already own the other half of this. Qiyāmah 75:22–23: wujūhun yawmaʾidhin nāḍirah · ilā rabbihā nāẓirah — \"faces radiant, LOOKING at their Lord.\" Qiyāmah shows you the seeing; Muṭaffifīn shows you the veil. Two surahs, one doctrine, opposite sides.","And here's the connection to v.14 that makes it perfect: their hearts were rusted (rāna) — a veil they built themselves, one sin at a time. On that Day the veil is simply... kept. They spent a lifetime building a screen; they get to keep it forever.","And the fronting is the key: \"from their Lord they are veiled\" — putting \"from their Lord\" first makes HIM the thing being withheld, the emphasis of the sentence.","Here: the wicked are VEILED.","And the veil is just v.14's rust, made permanent."]},
  {ar:"لَصَالُو الْجَحِيمِ", tr:"La-ṣālu'l-jaḥīm", v:"16", root:"The \"roasting\" family — you own this verb from Infiṭār 82:15 (yaṣlawnahā), and you'll meet it again in Inshiqāq 84:12 (yaṣlā saʿīrā).", meaning:"\"Then indeed, they will burn in the Blaze.\"", magic:"Note the ORDER once more — it's the surah's quiet argument. v.15: veiled from their Lord. v.16: then the fire. The ثُمَّ (\"then\") makes the fire the SECOND item.", grammar:"ṣālū is a plural active participle from a weak-final root, and it DROPS ITS NŪN because it's in iḍāfah (possessive construction) with al-jaḥīm.", why:"Keeping the same roasting-verb across three surahs makes it a fixed term — learn it once.", note:"Third surah with this verb (82:15, 83:16, 84:12). And notice: the VEIL comes first, the FIRE second.", more:["The separation comes first; the burning is the consequence.","Original: ṣālūna → ṣālū'l-jaḥīm.","Same nūn-dropping rule you met in ʿAbasa 80:36 (banīh)."]},
  {ar:"عِلِّيِّينَ", tr:"ʿilliyyīn", v:"18–19", root:"The \"height / elevation / loftiness\" family — ʿalā = to be high; ʿālī = high.", meaning:"\"Indeed, the record of the righteous is in ʿIlliyyīn\" — the high places.", magic:"The exact mirror of Sijjīn, and the names alone tell the story: - Sijjīn — from prison → LOW, sealed, confined - ʿIlliyyīn — from height → HIGH, open, elevated", grammar:"Note v.19 says mā ʿilliyyūn (nominative) while v.18 said fī ʿilliyyīn (genitive after fī).", why:"Naming the register by elevation answers the sin of lowering the measure.", note:"Al-ʿAliyy = the Most High; Pharaoh claimed al-aʿlā (79:24). Sijjīn (prison/low) ↔ ʿIlliyyīn (heights).", more:["Al-ʿAliyy = the Most High (a name of Allah); aʿlā = highest — you own al-aʿlā from Nāziʿāt 79:24 (Pharaoh's blasphemous \"anā rabbukumu'l-aʿlā\").","And the fiʿʿīl intensive pattern is the same in both (Sijjīn / ʿIlliyyīn) — matched names for matched opposites. They even rhyme.","Notice the direction words in this surah keep pointing: the muṭaffifīn lowered the measure; the fujjār end up in the low place; the abrār end up in the high place. The surah about tipping scales is organized by UP and DOWN.","It behaves like a sound masculine plural: -ūn / -īn.","A small but excellent case-ending lesson.","Matched names, matched rhyme, opposite directions."]},
  {ar:"الْمُقَرَّبُونَ", tr:"Al-muqarrabūn", v:"21 · 28", root:"The \"nearness\" family — qaruba = to be near; qarīb = near; qurbān = a sacrifice (something offered to draw near!); taqarrub = seeking closeness.", meaning:"\"Witnessed by those brought near.\"", magic:"This word is the perfect answer to v.15, and the pairing is the surah's spine.", grammar:"Form II passive participle (mu-faʿʿal): muqarrab, mukarram, muṭahhar.", why:"Muqarrabūn over \"the believers\" — because it names them by their proximity, which is exactly what the wicked lost.", note:"V.15: maḥjūbūn (VEILED). v.21: muqarrabūn (BROUGHT NEAR). Both passive. Both done to them. Opposite results.", more:["Distance vs. nearness. That's the real currency of this surah — not fire and gardens, but how close you get.","And muqarrabūn is a PASSIVE participle (Form II) — \"those who have been BROUGHT near.\" They didn't climb; they were drawn in. Compare maḥjūbūn — also passive: \"those who have been veiled.\" Both are done TO them. Same grammar, opposite results.","And you own this root from Hajj: Muzdalifah is from zulfah (nearness) — Takwīr 81:13 (uzlifat, Paradise brought near). Two different roots, same idea: nearness is the reward.","You own this shape from ʿAbasa 80:13–14 (mukarramah, marfūʿah, muṭahharah).","qurbān = a sacrifice, i.e."]},
  {ar:"الْأَرَائِكِ... نَضْرَةَ النَّعِيمِ", tr:"Al-arāʾiki… naḍrata'n-naʿīm", v:"23–24", root:"Arāʾik — ornate couches/thrones — you own it from Insān 76:13 (muttakiʾīna fīhā ʿala'l-arāʾik).", meaning:"\"On couches, observing… You will recognize in their faces the radiance of bliss.\"", magic:"THREE surahs now share this glow-root, and this one adds something new.", grammar:"Taʿrifu is 2nd person singular — the Qur'an suddenly addresses YOU, the reader/listener, as a witness.", why:"Naḍrah over \"happiness\" — it's physical, external, visible bloom, not an inner state.", note:"Third surah with this glow: Qiyāmah 75:22, Insān 76:11, here.", more:["naḍrah — the \"fresh glow / bloom\" family — you own it TWICE: Qiyāmah 75:22 (wujūhun yawmaʾidhin nāḍirah) and Insān 76:11 (wa laqqāhum naḍratan wa surūrā).","- Qiyāmah 75:22 — faces radiant (nāḍirah), looking at their Lord - Insān 76:11 — He gave them radiance (naḍratan) - Muṭaffifīn 83:24 — you will RECOGNIZE it in their faces (taʿrifu fī wujūhihim)","The new element is taʿrifu — \"YOU will recognize.\" The bliss is VISIBLE from outside. You don't have to ask them; you can read it on their faces across the room. Same idea as ʿAbasa 80:38–39 (musfirah, ḍāḥikah, mustabshirah — faces shining, laughing, rejoicing).","And the contrast with v.15 completes: the wicked are veiled — you can't see their Lord. The righteous are so unveiled that their joy is legible on their skin.","Note also yanẓurūn (\"observing,\" v.23) — left deliberately unspecified. Watching what? Many read it against v.34–35: they're watching the reversal (see below). Others: gazing at their Lord, or at the blessings.","A small iltifāt (turn of address) that pulls you into the scene.","New here: \"YOU will RECOGNIZE it in their faces\" — the joy is readable from outside.","And arāʾik = the same couches as Insān 76:13."]},
  {ar:"رَّحِيقٍ مَّخْتُومٍ... خِتَامُهُ مِسْكٌ", tr:"Raḥīqin makhtūm… khitāmuhu misk", v:"25–26", root:"Raḥīq — pure, unadulterated wine — the finest, undiluted vintage.", meaning:"\"They are given to drink pure sealed wine, whose seal is musk.\"", magic:"Two extraordinary details here.", grammar:"Khatam → khātam an-nabiyyīn. Recognize the root anywhere.", why:"A sealed drink in a surah about tampering — the reward is shaped like the antidote to the sin.", note:"Khatam = a SEAL → khātam an-nabiyyīn, the Seal of the Prophets.", more:["makhtūm — the \"sealing\" family — khatam = a seal/signet ring; khātam an-nabiyyīn = the SEAL of the Prophets; khitām = the seal, or the finish.","misk — musk, the most prized perfume.","First: makhtūm (SEALED). Why does a sealed bottle matter? Because a seal means: untouched, unopened, reserved. Nobody has drunk from it. Nobody has diluted it. In a surah about people who ADULTERATE what they sell — shaving grams, mixing measures — the reward is a drink that has been SEALED against tampering. The cheaters get a rusted heart; the honest get an unbroken seal.","Second: khitāmuhu misk — \"its seal is MUSK.\" On earth, the seal on a bottle is the throwaway part — wax, clay, foil. You break it and discard it. In Paradise, the part you THROW AWAY is musk — the most expensive substance they knew. The packaging is more precious than anything on earth.","And you already own the parallel from Insān 76:5, 17: drinks mixed with kāfūr (camphor) and zanjabīl (ginger). Insān names the FLAVOR; Muṭaffifīn names the SEAL. Same reward-vocabulary family.","Makhtūm is a passive participle (\"having been sealed\").","The cheaters tampered with goods; the righteous get a drink SEALED shut.","And the seal you throw away is MUSK."]},
  {ar:"فَلْيَتَنَافَسِ الْمُتَنَافِسُونَ", tr:"Fal-yatanāfasi'l-mutanāfisūn", v:"26", root:"A stunning root — n-f-s: nafas = a breath; nafs = a SOUL; nafīs = precious, valuable; munāfasah = competition.", meaning:"\"So for THIS let the competitors compete!\"", magic:"This is the surah's one moment of pure encouragement, and the root makes it soar.", grammar:"Form VI (تَفَاعَلَ) = MUTUAL action — you own it from Nabaʾ 78:1 (yatasāʾalūn, asking one another).", why:"Munāfasah over a plain word for \"strive\" — because it validates the competitive instinct while re-aiming it.", note:"Nafas (breath) + nafs (soul) + nafīs (precious) + munāfasah (competition) — ONE ROOT. They competed over 2 grams.", more:["Specifically, competing for something so precious you'd give your last breath for it.","Munāfasah is COMPETITION built from the word for BREATH and PRECIOUSNESS. It's not ordinary rivalry — it's the striving for something so nafīs (precious) that you'd spend your very nafs (soul/breath) on it.","And look at where it sits. The whole surah has been about people competing for two grams on a scale — the most worthless thing imaginable. Then, right after describing musk-sealed wine, it says: \"THIS is what's worth competing for.\" The verse redirects the exact same human drive from the trivial to the eternal.","It doesn't say \"stop competing.\" It says \"compete for the right thing.\"","And the root-link you already own: Takwīr 81:18 — wa'ṣ-ṣubḥi idhā tanaffas (\"the morning when it breathes\") — and Takwīr 81:14 / Infiṭār 82:5 — nafs (soul). Breath, soul, preciousness, competition: all one root.","Here: competing with one another.","And the lām al-amr (command lām): fal-yatanāfas = \"let them compete\" — same construction as ʿAbasa 80:24 (fal-yanẓur).","\"Compete for THIS instead.\""]},
  {ar:"مِزَاجُهُ مِن تَسْنِيمٍ", tr:"Mizājuhu min tasnīm", v:"27–28", root:"Mizāj — the \"blending\" family — you own it from Insān 76:5 (kāna mizājuhā kāfūrā) and 76:17 (kāna mizājuhā zanjabīlā).", meaning:"\"And its mixture is from Tasnīm — a spring from which those brought near drink.\"", magic:"The name Tasnīm is doing something beautiful.", grammar:"ʿaynan (v.28) is accusative — a badal or specification of Tasnīm: \"[namely] a spring.\" Note yashrabu bihā.", why:"Naming the spring by height ties the reward back to ʿIlliyyīn and away from Sijjīn.", note:"Sanām = a camel's HUMP (the highest point). Tasnīm = the high spring. Sijjīn (low) → ʿIlliyyīn (high) → Tasnīm (highest).", more:["tasnīm — the \"raising high\" family — sanima = to be elevated; sanām = a camel's HUMP (the highest point of the animal!).","From sanām — a camel's HUMP, the highest part. So Tasnīm is \"the elevated one\" — a spring that flows from above, the highest spring in Paradise.","And it completes the surah's vertical architecture: - Sijjīn — the LOW place (v.7) - ʿIlliyyīn — the HIGH place (v.18) - Tasnīm — the HIGH spring (v.27)","A surah that began with people LOWERING a scale is organized entirely by ELEVATION.","And notice the hierarchy in v.28: the abrār get Tasnīm as a MIXTURE in their drink; the muqarrabūn drink it PURE, straight from the spring. Even in Paradise there are ranks — and nearness is the ranking.","You now own the full Insān/Muṭaffifīn drink-set: kāfūr (camphor, 76:5), zanjabīl (ginger, 76:17), Salsabīl (76:18), raḥīq makhtūm (83:25), Tasnīm (83:27).","With بـ, an idiom meaning to drink from/at it.","And the muqarrabūn drink it PURE; others get it mixed."]},
  {ar:"أَجْرَمُوا... يَضْحَكُونَ", tr:"Ajramū… yaḍḥakūn", v:"29", root:"Ajrama — the \"crime / cutting off\" family — jurm = a crime; mujrim = a criminal.", meaning:"\"Indeed, those who committed crimes used to laugh at those who believed.\"", magic:"The final movement of the surah is about LAUGHTER — and it's the most humanly recognizable section in these surahs.", grammar:"Kānū… yaḍḥakūn = habitual past (\"they used to laugh\") — ongoing behavior, not one incident.", why:"Ajramū over \"disbelieved\" — it frames mockery as a CRIME, not a personality flaw.", note:"The surah opens with a tiny SIN (2 grams) and closes with a tiny CRUELTY (a smirk).", more:["(The root's older sense relates to cutting/severing — a criminal cuts himself off.) yaḍḥakūn — the \"laughing\" family — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:39 (ḍāḥikatun mustabshirah).","Notice what the mockery consists of: not violence, not persecution — laughing, winking, gossiping at home, calling them \"lost.\" It's SOCIAL cruelty: the sneer, the in-joke, the eye-roll. The surah takes schoolyard behavior seriously enough to close on it.","And it fits the opening perfectly: the surah began with a small sin taken seriously (two grams). It ends with small cruelty taken seriously (a smirk). Both are things people dismiss as \"nothing.\"","Both dismissed as \"nothing.\" Both taken seriously here."]},
  {ar:"يَتَغَامَزُونَ", tr:"Yataghāmazūn", v:"30", root:"The \"winking / signalling with the eye or eyebrow\" family — ghamaza = to wink, to nudge, to signal mockingly.", meaning:"\"And when they passed by them, they would wink at one another.\"", magic:"This is one of the most precisely observed verses in the Qur'an — it captures a gesture, not an argument.", grammar:"Form VI = mutual — third instance you own: yatasāʾalūn (Nabaʾ 78:1), yatanāfasūn (v.26 above), yataghāmazūn (here).", why:"Ghamz over \"mocked\" — because it names the exact, deniable, wordless gesture — which is what actually stings.", note:"Ghamz = the WINK, the eyebrow, the nudge. Deniable cruelty — no words, no evidence. The Qur'an recorded a raised eyebrow.", more:["Ghamz is the SIDEWAYS glance, the raised eyebrow, the elbow-nudge — mockery that leaves no evidence. You can't be accused of it. You can't be quoted. It's deniable cruelty.","And Form VI (tafāʿala) makes it MUTUAL — they wink AT EACH OTHER. It's not one person sneering; it's a group sharing a private joke at someone's expense, in front of them.","Anyone who has ever been the target of this recognizes it instantly. The Qur'an recorded a raised eyebrow.","And here's the reversal being set up: in v.23 and v.35, the righteous are yanẓurūn — LOOKING. The mockers used their eyes to wink; the believers will use theirs to watch. Eyes throughout.","Recognize the ta- + long ā shape."]},
  {ar:"انقَلَبُوا... فَكِهِينَ", tr:"Inqalabū… fakihīn", v:"31", root:"Inqalaba — the \"turning over / returning\" family — qalaba = to flip, to overturn.", meaning:"\"And when they returned to their families, they returned joking/delighted.\"", magic:"This verse is a devastating character sketch — and it has an EXACT counterpart one surah later.", grammar:"Form VII (inqalaba) — the ان- shape you own from Infiṭār 82:1 (infaṭarat), Takwīr 81:2 (inkadarat).", why:"Fakihīn (from fruit) turns mockery into a consumed pleasure — which is exactly how it functions socially.", note:"Qalb = HEART, the thing that TURNS; inqilāb = a coup.", more:["Qalb = the HEART (the thing that turns!); inqilāb = a coup/revolution (an overturning).","fakihīn — the \"delighting / joking\" family — related to fākihah = FRUIT — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:31 (wa fākihatan wa abbā).","Enjoyment as something sweet you consume.","Compare Inshiqāq 84:9: wa yanqalibu ilā ahlihi masrūrā — \"and he will RETURN to his family REJOICING\" — describing the man who gets his record in his right hand.","SAME VERB. SAME PHRASE (\"returned to their family\"). Opposite joy. One surah apart. The mocker's homecoming and the believer's homecoming, described identically.","And there's a third layer: Inshiqāq 84:13 says of the doomed man: innahu kāna fī ahlihi masrūrā — \"he used to be, among his family, rejoicing\" — the SAME joy, now listed as evidence against him. Three verses across two surahs, one homecoming, three verdicts.","And notice the detail: their mockery wasn't spontaneous — they took it HOME. It was dinner-table entertainment. Fakihīn (from fākihah, fruit) makes the cruelty into DESSERT — something enjoyed after the meal.","Fakihīn is a ḥāl (state): \"returned while joking.\"","SAME PHRASE as Inshiqāq 84:9 — \"returned to his family.\" One returns amused at others; one returns having passed.","And fakihīn ← fākihah (FRUIT): cruelty as dessert."]},
  {ar:"لَضَالُّونَ... حَافِظِينَ", tr:"La-ḍāllūn… ḥāfiẓīn", v:"32–33", root:"ḍāllūn — the \"straying / being lost\" family (ḍalāl = misguidance) — you say ghayri'l-maghḍūbi ʿalayhim wa la'ḍ-ḍāllīn in every Fātiḥah.", meaning:"V.32 — \"And when they saw them, they said: 'Indeed, these are astray!'\" v.33 — \"But they had not been sent as guardians over them.\"", magic:"V.33 is one of the most quietly cutting lines in the Qur'an, and the word ḥāfiẓīn is the blade.", grammar:"Mā ursilū — passive (\"they were not sent\").", why:"ḥāfiẓīn over \"judges\" — because it borrows the angels' exact title to expose the overreach.", note:"Infiṭār 82:10: the ANGELS are ḥāfiẓīn over you. Here: \"you were never SENT as guardians.\" They took a job that was already filled.", more:["ḥāfiẓīn — the \"guarding\" family — you own it from Infiṭār 82:10 (wa inna ʿalaykum la-ḥāfiẓīn) and Ṭāriq 86:4.","In Infiṭār 82:10, ḥāfiẓīn described the angels appointed over you — the real, authorized record-keepers. Here, the mockers are told: you were never given that job.","\"They were not SENT over them as guardians\" = \"Who appointed you? Nobody. You gave yourself a position you were never assigned.\"","The mockers were doing the ANGELS' job without the commission — assessing, judging, filing verdicts on other people's guidance. And the surah simply notes: that post was already filled, and not by you.","And notice the irony with v.32's word: they called the believers ḍāllūn (astray) — the exact word you ask Allah to protect you from seventeen times a day. They used the language of the Fātiḥah as an insult.","The passive is the point: being sent requires a SENDER, and they had none.","Compare rasūl (Takwīr 81:19) — one who IS sent.","And ḍāllīn is the word from your daily Fātiḥah."]},
  {ar:"فَالْيَوْمَ... يَضْحَكُونَ", tr:"Fal-yawma… yaḍḥakūn", v:"34–35", root:"Yaḍḥakūn — the laughing root from v.29.", meaning:"\"So today, those who believed are laughing at the disbelievers — on couches, observing.\"", magic:"The reversal is exact, verb for verb — this is muqābala at its most satisfying.", grammar:"Fa'l-yawma — \"so TODAY\" — the tense shifts to present/immediate, as if you're standing in that Day watching it happen.", why:"Re-using yaḍḥakūn rather than a different word for joy is what makes the reversal land as poetic justice, not just reward.", note:"Same verb (yaḍḥakūn) in v.29 and v.34 — the laughter changes owners. They passed by winking; the believers recline and watch.", more:["yanẓurūn — the \"looking\" family — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:24 (fal-yanẓuri'l-insān) and Qiyāmah 75:23 (ilā rabbihā nāẓirah).","The SAME VERB — yaḍḥakūn — moves from one group to the other. The laughter doesn't stop; it changes owners.","And here's the detail that makes it perfect: the mockers passed by the believers (v.30) to sneak their winks — standing, moving, furtive. The believers are ʿala'l-arāʾik — RECLINING on couches (v.35), comfortable, seated, in no hurry, openly watching. The posture is the verdict.","And yanẓurūn appears TWICE (v.23 and v.35) — framing the whole Paradise section with LOOKING. They who were looked down on now look.","A sudden shift into the scene."]},
  {ar:"هَلْ ثُوِّبَ", tr:"Hal thuwwiba", v:"36", root:"An extraordinary root choice — th-w-b: thawāb = REWARD (the standard word for the reward of good deeds!).", meaning:"\"Have the disbelievers been REWARDED for what they used to do?\"", magic:"The final word of the surah, and it's the sharpest irony in it.", grammar:"Hal here is a rhetorical question, not a real one — the answer is obviously yes, and that's the sting.", why:"Using the reward-word for punishment is the surah's last act of poetic justice.", note:"Thawāb = REWARD (the good-deeds word). Used sarcastically for the punished.", more:["Thawb = a garment; thāba = to return, to come back to a place.","Thawāb is the WORD FOR REWARD. It's what you say about good deeds — \"may Allah give you thawāb.\" And it's used here for the punishment of the disbelievers.","The tone is unmistakable: \"So — have they been well REWARDED for their efforts?\" Said about people in the Fire. It's the same register as Nabaʾ 78:30 (\"We will only INCREASE you in punishment\" — the language of a gift, delivering the opposite).","And the root's other meaning makes it richer: thāba = to RETURN / come back. So thawāb is literally \"what comes back to you.\" They laughed; it came back. They winked; it came back. They shortchanged; it came back. The final word of a surah about cheating the scales means \"what returns to you.\"","And it closes the surah's own loop: they took more than they gave (v.2–3). In the end, what came back was exactly what they sent out. The scale balanced itself.","Form II passive (thuwwiba) — intensive: \"thoroughly rewarded.\"","The vocabulary itself gets flipped, exactly as the laughter did.","And thāba = \"to RETURN\" — so thawāb = \"what comes BACK to you.\" A surah about cheating scales ends on \"what returns to you.\""]}
  ]
},
{
  num:84, name:"Al-Inshiqāq", ar:"الانشقاق", en:"The Splitting Open", verses:25, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"This surah doesn't ask you to start a journey. It informs you that you're already on one.",
  special:["It contains the single best one-line description of human life in the Qur'an.","> \"Yā ayyuha'l-insānu innaka kādiḥun ilā rabbika kadḥan fa-mulāqīh\" > \"O man! You are labouring toward your Lord with great labour — and you WILL meet Him.\" (v.6)","Every human being is already walking toward Him. Not should walk. Is walking. The believer, the denier, the person who's never thought about it — all of them are on the same road, moving at the same speed, toward the same meeting.","The only variable is what you're carrying when you arrive.","--"],
  big:["The Sky Obeys (v.1–5) — the sky splits, the earth empties — and both \"listen to their Lord, as is their duty.\" Even the cosmos submits gracefully.","The One Sentence (v.6) — you are labouring toward Him, and you will meet Him.","Two Handovers (v.7–15) — the record in the right hand vs. behind the back. Two homecomings.","Three Oaths (v.16–19) — the twilight glow, the night that gathers, the moon that assembles — and: you will surely climb stage by stage.","The Strange Silence (v.20–25) — what's wrong with them that they don't believe? And a \"good news\" that isn't."],
  traps:["يَنقَلِبُ (84:9) / انقَلَبُوا (83:31) / كَانَ... مَسْرُورًا (84:13) — Three verses, two surahs, ONE homecoming phrase, THREE verdicts. This is the most important cross-link in these five surahs.","The three sky-verbs: kushiṭat (flayed, 81:11) / infaṭarat (cleft, 82:1) / inshaqqat (split, 84:1). Learn as a set or you'll swap them.","وَسَقَ (v.17) and اتَّسَقَ (v.18) — same root, consecutive verses. Night gathers THE WORLD (outward); the moon gathers ITSELF (inward, Form VIII).","لَتَرْكَبُنَّ (v.19) — the EMPHATIC NŪN (nūn at-tawkīd): لَ + verb + نَّ = maximum certainty. Recognize this shape.","غَيْرُ مَمْنُونٍ (v.25) — BOTH \"never cut off\" AND \"never held over you.\" Translating only the first loses half the mercy.","الشَّفَقِ (v.16) — the twilight glow and tender compassion. Also the end of Maghrib time.","V.21 is a SAJDAH verse. Prostrate when you recite it. The verse says \"they don't\" — you do.","أَذِنَتْ (v.2, 5) — from udhun (EAR) / adhān. The sky LISTENS; it doesn't merely break.","تَخَلَّتْ (v.4) — Form V reflexive: the earth empties ITSELF, willingly. Not \"was emptied.\"","حِسَابًا يَسِيرًا (v.8) — NOT \"no reckoning\" — an EASY one. The file still opens."],
  links:[{w:"يَنقَلِبُ إِلَىٰ أَهْلِهِ (v.9)",e:"انقَلَبُوا إِلَىٰ أَهْلِهِمُ (Muṭ. 83:31)",t:"Same verb + phrase — opposite homecomings"},{w:"ظَنَّ أَن لَّن يَحُورَ (v.14)",e:"أَلَا يَظُنُّ... مَّبْعُوثُونَ (83:4)",t:"Muṭaffifīn asks; Inshiqāq answers"},{w:"انشَقَّتْ (v.1)",e:"شَقَقْنَا (ʿAbasa 80:26)",t:"Earth split for life; sky split for the Day"},{w:"انشَقَّتْ (v.1)",e:"انفَطَرَتْ (82:1) · كُشِطَتْ (81:11)",t:"Three surahs, three sky-verbs"},{w:"مُدَّتْ (v.3)",e:"السَّاهِرَة (Nāz. 79:14)",t:"The plain being made"},{w:"أَلْقَتْ (v.4)",e:"أَلْقَىٰ مَعَاذِيرَهُ (Qiy. 75:15)",t:"Earth casts its dead; man casts his excuses"},{w:"بُعْثِرَتْ ↔ أَلْقَتْ",e:"بُعْثِرَتْ (Infiṭār 82:4)",t:"Same event, two verbs — tipped vs. thrown out"},{w:"حِسَابًا يَسِيرًا (v.8)",e:"حِسَابًا (Nabaʾ 78:27, 36)",t:"The reckoning root"},{w:"يَسِيرًا (v.8)",e:"يَسَّرَهُ (ʿAbasa 80:20)",t:"The WAY made easy → the RECKONING made easy"},{w:"مَسْرُورًا (v.9, 13)",e:"سُرُورًا (Ins. 76:11)",t:"The joy root"},{w:"وَرَاءَ ظَهْرِهِ (v.10)",e:"أَدْبَرَ (Nāz. 79:22)",t:"Turned his back in life; record arrives at his back"},{w:"يَدْعُو ثُبُورًا (v.11)",e:"يَا لَيْتَنِي كُنتُ تُرَابًا (Nabaʾ 78:40)",t:"Wishing for non-existence"},{w:"يَصْلَىٰ سَعِيرًا (v.12)",e:"يَصْلَوْنَهَا (82:15) · صَالُو (83:16) · سَعِيرًا (76:4)",t:"Three surahs, same roasting-verb"},{w:"بَلَىٰ (v.15)",e:"بَلَىٰ قَادِرِينَ (Qiy. 75:4)",t:"The negative-flipping \"oh yes\""},{w:"بَصِيرًا (v.15)",e:"بَصِيرَة (Qiy. 75:14)",t:"He is the witness / His Lord is"},{w:"فَلَا أُقْسِمُ (v.16)",e:"(Qiy. 75:1) · (Takwīr 81:15)",t:"Third instance of the oath-construction"},{w:"اتَّسَقَ (v.18)",e:"الْمُتَّقِينَ (Nabaʾ 78:31) · اتَّخَذَ (76:29)",t:"Form VIII assimilation of initial و"},{w:"لَتَرْكَبُنَّ (v.19)",e:"رَكَّبَكَ (Infiṭār 82:8)",t:"Same root — assembled you / you mount stages"},{w:"فَمَا لَهُمْ (v.20)",e:"فَأَيْنَ تَذْهَبُونَ (Takwīr 81:26)",t:"Both pause to ask, bewildered"},{w:"يُوعُونَ (v.23)",e:"رَانَ عَلَىٰ قُلُوبِهِم (83:14)",t:"What's ON the heart / what's IN it"},{w:"فَبَشِّرْهُم بِعَذَابٍ (v.24)",e:"لَن نَّزِيدَكُمْ (78:30) · ثُوِّبَ (83:36)",t:"Three instances of reward-language irony"},{w:"بَشِّرْ (v.24)",e:"مُّسْتَبْشِرَةٌ (ʿAbasa 80:39)",t:"Bashrah = SKIN — news shows on the face"},{w:"أَلِيمٍ (v.24)",e:"عَذَابًا أَلِيمًا (Ins. 76:31)",t:"The pain root"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"انشَقَّتْ", tr:"Inshaqqat", v:"1", root:"The \"splitting / cleaving\" family — shaqqa = to split open; shiqq = a half.", meaning:"\"When the sky is split open.\"", magic:"You own this root already from ʿAbasa 80:26 — thumma shaqaqna'l-arḍa shaqqā (\"then We split the earth, a splitting\") — where the earth was cleaved so plants could come up alive.", grammar:"Form VII (اِنْفَعَلَ) — the ان- prefix, meaning it happens to the subject with no doer named.", why:"Inshaqqat over \"broke\" — because the root also gives mashaqqah (hardship), which is exactly what v.6 is about to describe.", note:"Mashaqqah = HARDSHIP (from splitting); shaqīq = a full brother.", more:["Mashaqqah = HARDSHIP (something that splits you); shaqīq = a full brother (split from the same source).","Same verb, two events: ʿAbasa splits the EARTH so life emerges. Inshiqāq splits the SKY so the Day arrives. Both are things being broken open so something can come through.","And note the family across three consecutive surahs: - Takwīr 81:11 — sky kushiṭat (flayed off) - Infiṭār 82:1 — sky infaṭarat (cleft open) - Inshiqāq 84:1 — sky inshaqqat (split apart)","Three surahs, three verbs, one sky. Learn them together or you'll swap them.","Same shape as infaṭarat (82:1), inkadarat (81:2), inqalabū (83:31). Fourth instance you own.","ʿAbasa 80:26 split the EARTH for life; here the SKY splits for the Day.","Sky verbs: kushiṭat (81) → infaṭarat (82) → inshaqqat (84)."]},
  {ar:"وَأَذِنَتْ لِرَبِّهَا", tr:"Wa adhinat li-rabbihā", v:"2, 5", root:"A beautiful double root — adhina = to LISTEN attentively and to permit/obey.", meaning:"\"and it listens to its Lord — and it is fitting for it [to do so].\"", magic:"This is the loveliest touch in the surah, and it turns a destruction scene into an act of worship.", grammar:"Li-rabbihā with لِـ — \"listened to its Lord,\" where the lām marks obedient attention, not just hearing.", why:"Adhinat over \"obeyed\" — because listening is gentler and more personal than obeying, and it makes the sky sound willing.", note:"Udhun = EAR; adhān = the CALL TO PRAYER. The sky doesn't break — it LISTENS. Repeated for sky (v.2) AND earth (v.5).", more:["Same letters give udhun = an EAR, adhān = the CALL TO PRAYER (what you listen for!), and idhn = permission.","The sky isn't destroyed. The sky OBEYS. The root means \"to give ear\" — so the sky hears the instruction and complies, the way you'd respond to the adhān.","Think about the ordering: the sky splitting is described as LISTENING. Not shattering, not failing — attending. The cataclysm is reframed as the cosmos being a good servant.","And the phrase repeats VERBATIM in v.2 and v.5 — once for the sky, once for the earth. Both listen. Both comply. Both are \"fitted\" for it. Then v.6 turns to YOU — the one creature that gets a choice.","The implicit argument: the sky listens, the earth listens — and man argues.","And ḥuqqat = \"and it was made its duty / it is only right\" — a passive from ḥaqq (truth, right, obligation)."]},
  {ar:"مُدَّتْ", tr:"Muddat", v:"3", root:"The \"stretching / extending\" family — madda = to stretch out.", meaning:"\"And when the earth is stretched out [flat].\"", magic:"You already say this word in every recitation session. Madd in tajwīd = holding a vowel long — stretching a sound.", grammar:"A DOUBLED verb (مضاعف — last two root letters identical: م-د-د), so they merge with a shaddah: muddat, not mudidat.", why:"Muddat over \"flattened\" — because stretching implies making ROOM, which is the purpose.", note:"Madd = the vowel-STRETCH in tajwīd — you use it daily. Now picture the EARTH being stretched — flattened to fit everyone.", more:["Madd = the LENGTHENING of a vowel in tajwīd (you use this word every time you recite!); imtidād = extension; māddah = material/substance.","Here the EARTH gets stretched — mountains flattened, valleys filled, curves pulled straight into an endless plain.","And the reason it's stretched is chilling in its practicality: to fit everyone. Every human who ever lived, standing at once. The earth is being resized for the gathering.","Compare Nāziʿāt 79:14 — fa-idhā hum bi's-sāhirah (\"and suddenly they are on the wakeful plain\"). Nāziʿāt names the plain; Inshiqāq shows you it being MADE. Same location, two stages.","Same doubled-verb rule as yafirru (ʿAbasa 80:34).","This is Nāziʿāt 79:14's sāhirah being built."]},
  {ar:"أَلْقَتْ... وَتَخَلَّتْ", tr:"Alqat… wa takhallat", v:"4", root:"Alqat — the \"casting / throwing\" family (alqā = to throw out) — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:15 (wa law alqā maʿādhīrah.", meaning:"\"and it casts out what is within it and empties itself.\"", magic:"Two verbs, and the second one is the remarkable one.", grammar:"Form V (تَفَعَّلَ) = reflexive — takhallat = \"it emptied itself.\" You own Form V from ʿAbasa 80:6, 10 (taṣaddā, talahhā) and Nāziʿāt 79:18 (tazakkā).", why:"Takhallat over \"was emptied\" — because the reflexive makes the earth a willing participant, matching v.5.", note:"Khālī = EMPTY; takhallā ʿan = to LET GO of. The earth doesn't get emptied — it EMPTIES ITSELF. Voluntary.", more:["\"even if he flings out his excuses\").","takhallat — the \"emptying / becoming vacant\" family — khalā = to be empty; khālī = empty; takhallā ʿan = to relinquish, to let go of.","Alqat = throws out its contents (the dead, the buried, everything held). Compare Infiṭār 82:4 — wa idha'l-qubūru buʿthirat (\"the graves are turned inside out\"). Same event, different verb: Infiṭār TIPS the graves; Inshiqāq has the earth THROW.","But takhallat is the beautiful one. Form V = REFLEXIVE: \"it empties ITSELF.\" The earth isn't emptied — the earth LETS GO.","And that fits v.5 perfectly: the very next verse says the earth \"listens to its Lord.\" So the emptying is described as VOLUNTARY — the earth is handing everything over willingly, like a servant emptying its pockets when asked.","Held next to Qiyāmah 75:15 (alqā maʿādhīrah — flinging out EXCUSES), there's a haunting pair: the earth casts out its dead; man casts out his excuses. Same verb, and one of those actions is useful.","Which is why v.5 says it \"listens.\""]},
  {ar:"كَادِحٌ... كَدْحًا... فَمُلَاقِيهِ", tr:"Kādiḥun… kadḥan… fa-mulāqīh", v:"6", root:"Kadaḥa — an extraordinary root: to labour hard, to toil, to strive to exhaustion.", meaning:"\"O man! You are labouring toward your Lord with great labour — and you will MEET Him.\"", magic:"The most important verse in the surah, and every word is chosen.", grammar:"Innaka + participle = a strong present-continuous statement of fact.", why:"Kadaḥa over any gentler verb — because it admits life is HARD, which makes the promise of the meeting land as relief, not threat.", note:"Kadḥ = a SCRAPE; kādiḥ = one who toils till worn. It's a PARTICIPLE: you don't do this, you ARE this.", more:["And the original physical sense is to SCRATCH or SCORE something, to wear at it.","A kadḥ on the skin is a scrape.","mulāqī — the \"meeting\" family (laqiya = to meet) — you own it from Insān 76:11 (fa-waqāhum… wa laqqāhum).","First: kādiḥ is not \"walking\" or \"traveling.\" The root means toiling to the point of being SCRAPED by it. Life isn't described as a stroll — it's effortful, wearing, marking you. You arrive scratched.","Second: it's a PARTICIPLE, not a verb — kādiḥun, \"you ARE a labourer.\" This isn't something you do; it's what you ARE, right now, continuously. Nobody is exempt. The atheist labours toward Him. The lazy person labours toward Him. Standing still is still moving.","Third: kadḥan is a cognate accusative (maf'ūl muṭlaq) — the verb's own noun repeated for emphasis: \"labouring a labouring.\" You own this from Nāziʿāt 79:1–4 and ʿAbasa 80:25–26. Arabic's underline key — \"and I mean REALLY labouring.\"","Fourth, and best: fa-mulāqīh — \"and you WILL MEET HIM.\" The فَـ makes it an immediate, inevitable result. Not \"you may meet Him,\" not \"you will be judged\" — you will MEET Him. The whole exhausting journey is reframed as a rendezvous.","Put it together: You are already scraping your way toward a meeting you did not schedule and cannot miss. That's human life in eleven Arabic words.","Mulāqīhi is a Form III participle (lāqā = to meet face-to-face — Form III often means mutual/directed-at-another action).","Note it DROPS ITS NŪN in iḍāfah with the pronoun: mulāqin → mulāqīhi.","Everyone is already walking toward Him — believer and denier alike.","And the ending isn't judgment."]},
  {ar:"أُوتِيَ كِتَابَهُ بِيَمِينِهِ", tr:"ūtiya kitābahu bi-yamīnih", v:"7", root:"ūtiya — the \"giving\" family (ātā = to give); passive: \"he is GIVEN.\" yamīn — the RIGHT hand.", meaning:"\"As for the one given his record in his right hand…\"", magic:"The word yamīn carries THREE meanings at once, and all three fit: - the right hand (the literal image) - an oath (a solemn, binding commitment) - blessing / good fortune (yumn)", grammar:"Fa-ammā… fa-… — the classic \"as for X… then Y\" structure.", why:"Yamīn over \"right side\" — because the oath/blessing senses turn a physical detail into an honour.", note:"Yamīn = right hand + an OATH + BLESSING (yumn). Three meanings, all fitting. And it's PASSIVE — the record is HANDED to you.", more:["And the same word means an OATH (yamīn = a solemn vow, because you raise your right hand) and relates to yumn = blessing, good fortune (hence al-Yaman, the \"blessed\" land).","So receiving your record \"in your yamīn\" is receiving it in the hand of blessing, honour, and commitment.","And the PASSIVE (ūtiya, \"he is GIVEN\") is the point: you don't take your record — it's handed to you. Same passive in v.10. Both men receive; neither chooses the manner.","You own it from Nāziʿāt 79:37–41 (fa-ammā man ṭaghā… wa ammā man khāfa).","Watch for the paired ammā — it always signals a two-group contrast."]},
  {ar:"حِسَابًا يَسِيرًا", tr:"ḥisāban yasīrā", v:"8", root:"ḥisāb — the \"counting / reckoning\" family — you own it from Nabaʾ 78:27, 36 (lā yarjūna ḥisābā / ʿaṭāʾan ḥisābā) and Qiyāmah 75:3 (a-yaḥsabu).", meaning:"\"he will be reckoned with an EASY reckoning.\"", magic:"The reward here is not \"no reckoning\" — it's an EASY one. That distinction is everything.", grammar:"ḥisāban is a cognate accusative again (\"reckoned a reckoning\") — modified by yasīrā.", why:"Yasīr over \"no reckoning\" — because being seen is part of the honour; the mercy is in the manner.", note:"Yusr = EASE. Not \"no reckoning\" — an EASY one: shown, not interrogated.", more:["yasīr — the \"ease\" family — yusr = ease (\"inna maʿa'l-ʿusri yusrā\"); you own it from ʿAbasa 80:20 (thumma's-sabīla yassarah — \"the way — He made it easy\").","Even the best believer is still accounted for. The file is still opened. The question is HOW.","And there's a well-known hadith the Prophet ﷺ related, that whoever is scrutinized in the reckoning is destroyed — with ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) asking about this very verse, and being told that this \"easy reckoning\" is the mere PRESENTATION of deeds, not the cross-examination. The hadith names this exact verse.","So ḥisāban yasīrā means: shown, not interrogated. A glance at the file, not a trial.","And the root-link to ʿAbasa 80:20 is lovely: He made the WAY easy (yassarah) in life; He makes the RECKONING easy (yasīrā) after it. Same root, both ends of the journey.","This is the cognate accusative used to describe HOW, not just to emphasize.","Third construction of this type in the surah (after kadḥan).","ʿAbasa 80:20 made the WAY easy; here the RECKONING is easy."]},
  {ar:"يَنقَلِبُ إِلَىٰ أَهْلِهِ مَسْرُورًا", tr:"Yanqalibu ilā ahlihi masrūrā", v:"9", root:"Inqalaba — the \"turning over / returning\" family — qalb = the HEART (the thing that turns); inqilāb = a coup.", meaning:"\"and he will return to his family, rejoicing.\"", magic:"This verse has an EXACT counterpart in the previous surah, and the pair is one of the finest things in this juzʾ.", grammar:"Form VII (inqalaba) — the ان- shape, fifth instance you own.", why:"Yanqalibu (turning/returning) over \"goes\" — because the root means a REVERSAL, and that's what the Day is.", note:"Qalb = HEART, the TURNER; inqilāb = a coup. Muṭaffifīn 83:31 = same phrase, mockers returning AMUSED.", more:["masrūr — the \"joy\" family — surūr = delight — you own it from Insān 76:11 (wa laqqāhum naḍratan wa surūrā).","Muṭaffifīn 83:31: wa idha'nqalabū ilā ahlihimu'nqalabū fakihīn \"And when they RETURNED to their FAMILIES, they returned AMUSED\" — the mockers, laughing at believers over dinner.","Inshiqāq 84:9: wa yanqalibu ilā ahlihi masrūrā \"And he will RETURN to his FAMILY, REJOICING\" — the man who passed.","SAME VERB. SAME PHRASE. Consecutive surahs. Opposite joys. One returns home amused at someone else's expense; one returns home having been let through.","And then Inshiqāq 84:13 completes the trilogy: innahu kāna fī ahlihi masrūrā — \"he USED TO be, among his family, rejoicing\" — said of the DOOMED man, as evidence against him.","Three verses, two surahs, one homecoming — and three completely different verdicts. This is the single most important cross-link in these five surahs.","And notice the detail: even in Paradise, the reward includes GOING HOME TO FAMILY. Not solitude, not a throne — ahlih, his people.","Masrūrā is a ḥāl (state): \"returns while rejoicing.\"","Inshiqāq 84:9 = returning REJOICING (saved).","Inshiqāq 84:13 = \"he USED TO rejoice\" (condemned).","One homecoming, three verdicts."]},
  {ar:"وَرَاءَ ظَهْرِهِ", tr:"Warāʾa ẓahrih", v:"10", root:"Warāʾ — behind; ẓahr — the BACK — and the same root gives ẓāhir = apparent, visible, outward (what shows on the surface!) and ẓuhr = NOON prayer (when the sun is most visible/prominent).", meaning:"\"But as for the one given his record behind his back…\"", magic:"The image is deliberately awkward — try to picture it physically.", grammar:"Warāʾa is a ẓarf makān (adverb of place), accusative.", why:"Naming the body position rather than the verdict makes the judgment visible before it's stated.", note:"ẓahr (back) shares a root with ẓāhir (VISIBLE) and ẓuhr (noon). The record goes to the one place he can't see.", more:["Receiving something behind your back means: - You can't see it — no chance to prepare - You must contort to take it — an undignified, twisted posture - You're turned AWAY — the body language of rejection","Compare v.7: the right hand — open, forward, honourable. Here: behind, hidden, twisted. The two postures ARE the two verdicts.","And the root-irony: ẓahr (back) shares its root with ẓāhir (apparent/visible). The record is placed at the one part of the body you can never see — from the root that means \"visible.\" Everything is now apparent, except to him.","Also connect to Nāziʿāt 79:22: thumma adbara yasʿā — Pharaoh \"turned his BACK and hurried away.\" He turned his back in life; the record arrives at his back in the end.","Note the parallel structure with v.7: bi-yamīnih (with his right hand) ↔ warāʾa ẓahrih (behind his back).","Matched phrases, opposite positions.","v.7 right hand (open) ↔ v.10 behind the back (twisted)."]},
  {ar:"يَدْعُو ثُبُورًا", tr:"Yadʿū thubūrā", v:"11", root:"Yadʿū — the \"calling\" family (duʿāʾ = a supplication, a call).", meaning:"\"he will call out for DESTRUCTION.\"", magic:"The verb choice is the horror here.", grammar:"Sawfa (v.11) = \"will\" for the DISTANT future — and note it's used in both v.8 (fa-sawfa yuḥāsabu) and v.11 (fa-sawfa yadʿū).", why:"Yadʿū over \"he will scream\" — because using the prayer-verb makes it a perverse supplication, which is far darker.", note:"Duʿāʾ = supplication. He PRAYS — for his own destruction.", more:["thubūr — the \"utter ruin / destruction / perishing\" family — thabara = to be destroyed, to perish completely.","Yadʿū is the DUʿĀʾ verb — the word for supplication, calling on Allah, asking for what you want. The verb of prayer.","And what does he pray FOR? Thubūr — his own annihilation. The prayer-verb, used to beg for non-existence.","Compare Nabaʾ 78:40 — yā laytanī kuntu turābā (\"I wish I were dust!\"). Same desperate wish for non-being, two surahs apart. Nabaʾ wishes it; Inshiqāq PRAYS for it.","And note the tragic logic: the only duʿāʾ he makes is one that cannot be answered. He spent life not calling on Him; now he calls, and asks to stop existing.","Matched openings for the two fates.","Thubūrā is accusative (the thing called for).","Compare Nabaʾ 78:40: \"I wish I were dust.\" The only duʿāʾ he makes is the one that can't be granted."]},
  {ar:"يَصْلَىٰ سَعِيرًا", tr:"Yaṣlā saʿīrā", v:"12", root:"Yaṣlā — the \"roasting\" family — THIRD surah in a row: Infiṭār 82:15 (yaṣlawnahā), Muṭaffifīn 83:16 (ṣālū'l-jaḥīm), here.", meaning:"\"and he will burn in a Blaze.\"", magic:"Both words are ones you've already collected — this verse is free vocabulary if you learned Insān and Infiṭār.", grammar:"Weak-final verb (ص-ل-ي); note the singular yaṣlā here vs.", why:"Keeping the same roasting-verb across three surahs makes it a fixed technical term — learn once, own three.", note:"Third surah with ṣaliya: 82:15, 83:16, 84:12. And saʿīr from Insān 76:4 + Takwīr 81:12. This whole verse is recycled vocabulary.", more:["saʿīr — the \"flaring blaze\" family — you own it from Insān 76:4 (aʿtadnā li'l-kāfirīna… saʿīrā) and Takwīr 81:12 (al-jaḥīmu suʿʿirat).","Note saʿīran is INDEFINITE (\"a blaze\") — leaving it unspecified and therefore more ominous.","the plural yaṣlawna in Infiṭār 82:15 — watch how the weak final letter shifts between singular and plural."]},
  {ar:"ظَنَّ أَن لَّن يَحُورَ", tr:"ẓanna an lan yaḥūr", v:"14", root:"ẓanna — you own its two senses: usually \"to suppose,\" but in Qiyāmah 75:28 (wa ẓanna annahu'l-firāq) it meant CERTAINTY, and in Muṭaffifīn 83:4 (alā yaẓunnu) it meant the faintest suspicion.", meaning:"\"Indeed, he thought he would never RETURN.\"", magic:"This is the diagnosis of the entire surah, and it links straight back to Muṭaffifīn.", grammar:"Lan + present = emphatic future negation (\"will never\") — you own it from Nabaʾ 78:30 (fa-lan nazīdakum illā ʿadhābā).", why:"Yaḥūr (a plain \"come back\") over a theological term — because his error was ordinary, not sophisticated.", note:"Muṭaffifīn 83:4 ASKED: \"don't they think they'll be raised?\" Inshiqāq 84:14 ANSWERS: \"he thought he'd never come back.\" Same verb, consecutive surahs — question and answer.", more:["yaḥūr — the \"returning\" family — ḥāra = to return, to come back to a starting point.","Compare Muṭaffifīn 83:4: alā yaẓunnu ulāʾika annahum mabʿūthūn — \"Do those not THINK they will be RAISED?\" Inshiqāq 84:14: innahu ẓanna an lan yaḥūr — \"Indeed, he THOUGHT he would never RETURN.\"","Muṭaffifīn asked the question; Inshiqāq gives the answer. Same verb (ẓanna), same subject (resurrection), consecutive surahs. \"Don't they think?\" → \"He thought he wouldn't.\"","And yaḥūr is beautifully specific: it means to RETURN to a point of origin. Not \"be resurrected\" (a technical term) but \"come back\" (a homely one). He assumed it was a one-way trip.","The whole surah's argument answers this: v.6 said you are labouring TOWARD your Lord and will MEET Him. He thought the road went nowhere. It goes to a meeting.","Yaḥūra is accusative after lan.","Hollow verb (weak middle و)."]},
  {ar:"بَلَىٰ... بَصِيرًا", tr:"Balā… baṣīrā", v:"15", root:"Balā — the emphatic \"YES / on the contrary\" used specifically to overturn a negative statement — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:4 (balā qādirīna…).", meaning:"\"Yes indeed! His Lord was ever Seeing of him.\"", magic:"Balā is a specialist word: it exists only to contradict a negative. He said \"I will NEVER return\" — balā says \"OH YES YOU WILL.\" It's a one-word demolition of the previous verse.", grammar:"Kāna + participle = a settled, ongoing attribute (\"was ever / has always been\") — not a past state that ended.", why:"Baṣīr over \"knew\" — because SEEING answers a verse about what he COULDN'T see (v.10).", note:"Balā = \"OH YES YOU WILL\" — it only exists to flip a negative. He said \"never\"; balā says otherwise.", more:["baṣīr — the \"seeing\" family — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:14 (bali'l-insānu ʿalā nafsihi baṣīrah).","And kāna bihi baṣīrā (\"was ever Seeing of him\") is the perfect answer to v.10. He received his record BEHIND HIS BACK — the place he couldn't see. But his Lord was seeing him the whole time. The blindness was only ever on one side.","Cross-link to Qiyāmah 75:14: there, man himself is baṣīrah — a clear witness against himself. Here, his Lord is baṣīr — seeing him throughout. Two witnesses, same root, and neither can be cross-examined.","Important nuance whenever you see kāna with a name/attribute of Allah.","And he couldn't see his record (v.10) — but his Lord was baṣīr all along.","Qiyāmah 75:14: HE is the witness."]},
  {ar:"الشَّفَقِ", tr:"Ash-shafaq", v:"16", root:"A gorgeous double root — shafaq = the red-orange glow at twilight (after sunset), and shafaqah = TENDER COMPASSION, tender concern (mushfiq = compassionate, anxious for someone's welfare).", meaning:"\"So I swear by the twilight glow.\"", magic:"The double meaning is one of the loveliest in the Qur'an.", grammar:"Fa-lā uqsimu — you own this construction from Qiyāmah 75:1 and Takwīr 81:15. Third instance.", why:"Shafaq over \"sunset\" — because the compassion sense makes the oath feel tender, matching a surah that ends by offering mercy.", note:"Shafaq = TWILIGHT GLOW and TENDER COMPASSION. The sky's softest light = the heart's softest quality.", more:["Shafaq is BOTH the tender red light after sunset AND tender-hearted compassion. Why the same word? Because twilight is the sky's SOFTEST light — not the harsh sun, not full dark, but a gentle, delicate, fading glow. Compassion is the same quality in a person: soft, warm, and easily lost.","And you know shafaq practically: it's the term used for the end-time of Maghrib and the start of ʿIshāʾ — the disappearance of the shafaq. You've been tracking this word your whole prayer life.","Note the oath-set (v.16–18): TWILIGHT → NIGHT → FULL MOON. All three are STAGES of the same evening — and that's the setup for v.19's \"you will surely climb STAGE by STAGE.\" The oaths demonstrate the point before it's made.","Same two readings (rebuttal-lā or emphatic-lā).","And it's the word marking the end of Maghrib time."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا وَسَقَ... اتَّسَقَ", tr:"Wa mā wasaq… ittasaq", v:"17–18", root:"The \"gathering / collecting into a whole\" family — wasaqa = to gather together, to collect and pack.", meaning:"V.17 — \"and by the night and what it GATHERS\"; v.18 — \"and by the moon when it becomes FULL / gathered together.\"", magic:"The same root, two consecutive verses, two directions — this is a masterclass in Arabic root-play.", grammar:"Form VIII assimilation — the original is iwtasaqa, and the و assimilates into the ت → ittasaqa.", why:"Using one root twice makes v.17–18 feel like one continuous thought, and it enacts the gathering it describes.", note:"Wasaqa = TO GATHER. v.17: the NIGHT gathers the world in. v.18: the MOON gathers itself full.", more:["Wasq = a load/measure (a camel-load of collected goods).","Form VIII ittasaqa = to become gathered, complete, ordered.","V.17: wasaq — the NIGHT gathers. Picture it: as darkness falls, everything comes IN. Birds to nests, people to homes, animals to shelter, travelers off the road. Night is described as a COLLECTOR — it sweeps the world's scattered things into one place.","V.18: ittasaq — the MOON gathers ITSELF. Form VIII, reflexive: the moon assembles itself night by night — crescent → half → gibbous → full: complete, gathered, whole.","So: the night gathers THE WORLD; the moon gathers ITSELF. Same root, one outward, one inward.","And THIS is the setup for v.19. The moon doesn't jump to full — it climbs there in STAGES. Which is exactly what the next verse says about you.","Same assimilation family as ittaqā → muttaqīn (Nabaʾ 78:31) and ittakhadha (Insān 76:29). Roots starting with و regularly do this in Form VIII.","Outward, then inward — and both happen in STAGES, which is v.19."]},
  {ar:"لَتَرْكَبُنَّ طَبَقًا عَن طَبَقٍ", tr:"La-tarkabunna ṭabaqan ʿan ṭabaq", v:"19", root:"Tarkabunna — the \"mounting / riding / boarding\" family — rakiba = to ride, to mount.", meaning:"\"You will surely mount stage upon stage / layer after layer.\"", magic:"The answer to the three oaths, and the most quietly profound verse in the surah.", grammar:"La-tarkabunna = the EMPHATIC NŪN (nūn at-tawkīd).", why:"ṭabaq over \"stage\" — because ṭabaqah means a FLOOR of a building — you can feel yourself ascending.", note:"ṭabaqah = a FLOOR of a building / a layer. The moon climbed to full in stages (v.18); so do you.", more:["You own the related rakkaba (to ASSEMBLE) from Infiṭār 82:8. ṭabaq — the \"layer / stage / matching level\" family — ṭabaqah = a layer, a floor of a building, a social class; ṭibāq = layered upon one another (used of the seven heavens: sabʿa samāwātin ṭibāqā).","The oaths were: twilight (a stage) → night (a stage) → full moon (the completion of stages). Then: \"you will surely climb stage by stage.\" The evening sky was the illustration; you are the subject.","What are the ṭabaqāt? Classical commentators offer several, and they layer beautifully: - Life stages — drop → clot → infant → child → youth → old age → death (exactly the sequence in ʿAbasa 80:18–22) - States of ease and hardship — one condition after another - The stages of the Hereafter — death → grave → resurrection → gathering → reckoning","All of them share the point: NOTHING IS PERMANENT. You are always moving to the next layer. Whatever stage you're in right now — it's a ṭabaq, and you're leaving it.","And tarkabunna is chosen with care: \"you will MOUNT/RIDE\" — the verb for getting onto a moving animal or vehicle. You don't walk through these stages; you're CARRIED through them.","This is v.6 restated: kādiḥun ilā rabbik (\"labouring toward your Lord\") — and here's HOW: one layer at a time.","لَ + verb + نَّ = \"you WILL MOST CERTAINLY.\" This is one of Arabic's strongest oaths-of-affirmation — reserved for absolute certainties.","Learn this shape; it always signals maximum emphasis.","Nothing you're in right now is permanent.","And la-tarkabunna uses the EMPHATIC NŪN — maximum certainty."]},
  {ar:"فَمَا لَهُمْ لَا يُؤْمِنُونَ", tr:"Fa-mā lahum lā yuʾminūn", v:"20", root:"āmana — the \"safety / trust / faith\" family — amn = security; amānah = a trust; al-Amīn = the Prophet's ﷺ title.", meaning:"\"So what is [the matter] with them that they do not believe?\"", magic:"The tone here is genuine bewilderment, and that's what makes it land.", grammar:"Mā lahum = an idiom: \"what is with them / what's the matter with them?\" — followed by a verb describing the puzzling behavior.", why:"A question rather than a condemnation — it invites the listener to answer for himself.", note:"Amn = SECURITY; al-Amīn = the Prophet's ﷺ title. To BELIEVE = to be SAFE.", more:["You own it from Takwīr 81:21 (muṭāʿin thamma amīn).","After: the sky obeys, the earth obeys, you're already walking toward Him, the moon climbs in stages, everything is ordered — the surah just stops and asks: \"What's WRONG with them?\"","It's not a threat. It's puzzlement. Compare Takwīr 81:26 — fa-ayna tadhhabūn (\"so where are you going?\"). Same register: a bewildered question rather than a verdict. Two surahs, both stopping mid-argument to ask, almost gently, what the problem is.","And note the root: āmana comes from SECURITY (amn). To believe is to find safety. So the question is really: \"what's stopping them from being safe?\"","Very common Qur'anic construction.","And compare Takwīr 81:26: \"So where are you going?\" Both surahs pause to ask, bewildered."]},
  {ar:"لَا يَسْجُدُونَ", tr:"Lā yasjudūn", v:"21", root:"The \"prostration\" family — sajada = to prostrate; sujūd = prostration; masjid = a MOSQUE (literally \"the place of prostration\").", meaning:"\"And when the Qur'ān is recited to them, they do not prostrate.\"", magic:"This is one of the verses of prostration (sajdat at-tilāwah) — meaning that when you recite it, YOU prostrate.", grammar:"Qurīʾa is passive (\"is recited\").", why:"Stating it as a simple fact (\"they don't\") rather than a rebuke makes the omission sound strange rather than sinful — which fits v.20's bewilderment.", note:"Masjid = \"the PLACE OF PROSTRATION.\" This is a SAJDAH verse: it says \"they don't prostrate\" — and you prostrate when you read it.", more:["Think about what that does. The verse says \"they do NOT prostrate\" — and the reciter's response is TO PROSTRATE. You answer the verse with your body. The criticism becomes an invitation, and you accept it physically, in the moment of reading.","No other kind of verse works quite like this. You're not told to prostrate; you're told about people who don't — and the natural response is to be the opposite of them.","And masjid means \"place of prostration\" — so the whole institution of the mosque is named after this single action.","Note lā yasjudūn — present tense negation = habitual: \"they [just] don't prostrate.\" The plainness is the point.","You answer the verse with your body."]},
  {ar:"بِمَا يُوعُونَ", tr:"Bimā yūʿūn", v:"23", root:"The \"containing / storing in a vessel\" family — wiʿāʾ = a container, a vessel, a bag.", meaning:"\"And Allah is most knowing of what they are storing up inside.\"", magic:"A stunning image, and easy to walk past.", grammar:"Form IV (awʿā), present tense. Bimā = bi + mā (\"of what\").", why:"Yūʿūn (storing in a vessel) over \"hiding\" — because a container is deliberate; you don't accidentally store something.", note:"Wiʿāʾ = a CONTAINER; waʿy = CONSCIOUSNESS (what your mind holds). He knows what's in your sealed bag.", more:["Awʿā = to store up, to keep inside a container; waʿā = to retain/comprehend (modern Arabic waʿy = CONSCIOUSNESS/awareness — what your mind \"holds\").","Wiʿāʾ is a CONTAINER. So the verse says: Allah knows what they're KEEPING IN THEIR CONTAINERS — their chests, their hearts, the private stock of intentions and denials nobody sees.","The picture: they're carrying sealed bags, and He knows the contents.","Now connect it to the surah's other container-language: - v.4: the earth casts out what is within it and empties itself - v.23: they are storing up what's inside them","The EARTH empties itself willingly. THEY keep filling themselves up. Same surah, opposite behaviors — and the earth is the one that \"listens to its Lord.\"","And there's a third link: Muṭaffifīn 83:14 — rāna ʿalā qulūbihim (\"RUST has covered their hearts\"). Muṭaffifīn shows what's ON the heart; Inshiqāq shows what's IN it.","Note aʿlamu here = \"most knowing,\" an ism tafḍīl (superlative) form.","v.4: the earth EMPTIES itself.","v.23: they keep FILLING themselves."]},
  {ar:"فَبَشِّرْهُم بِعَذَابٍ أَلِيمٍ", tr:"Fa-bashshirhum bi-ʿadhābin alīm", v:"24", root:"Bashshara — the \"good news\" family — bushrā = glad tidings; bashīr = a bearer of good news.", meaning:"\"So give them glad tidings of a painful punishment.\"", magic:"One of the sharpest ironies in the Qur'an, and the vocabulary makes it bite.", grammar:"Form II imperative (bashshir) with an attached pronoun.", why:"The irony does what a plain threat can't — it makes the punishment sound deserved and announced, like a delivery they ordered.", note:"Bashrah = SKIN → bushrā = GOOD NEWS (it shows on your face) → bashar = a human.", more:["And bashar = A HUMAN BEING, from bashrah = the SKIN — because good news SHOWS on your face.","You own this from ʿAbasa 80:39 (ḍāḥikatun mustabshirah).","alīm — the \"pain\" family — you own it from Insān 76:31 (ʿadhāban alīmā).","Bashshir is the GOOD NEWS verb. It's what you say to someone about a birth, a victory, Paradise. And it's paired here with \"a PAINFUL punishment.\"","The effect is tahakkum — mocking irony. Same technique you own from: - Nabaʾ 78:30 — fa-lan nazīdakum illā ʿadhābā (\"We will only INCREASE you in punishment\" — gift-language for the opposite) - Muṭaffifīn 83:36 — hal thuwwiba'l-kuffār (\"have the disbelievers been REWARDED?\" — the thawāb word for punishment)","Three surahs, three times the Qur'an wraps punishment in the language of reward. Collect them as a set.","And the root-detail makes it sharper: bashshara comes from bashrah, the SKIN — good news that shows on the face. In ʿAbasa 80:39, faces were mustabshirah — LIT UP with good news. Here, the \"good news\" is the kind that would drain a face white.","The bāʾ after bashshara is standard — you always give news bi- something.","\"Give them GOOD NEWS of a PAINFUL punishment.\" Third instance of this irony: Nabaʾ 78:30, Muṭaffifīn 83:36, here."]},
  {ar:"أَجْرٌ غَيْرُ مَمْنُونٍ", tr:"Ajrun ghayru mamnūn", v:"25", root:"Ajr — a wage, a reward for work.", meaning:"\"…they will have a reward UNCUT / UNBROKEN / never held over them.\"", magic:"The double meaning makes this one of the most generous endings in the Qur'an.", grammar:"Ghayr + genitive = the standard way to negate an adjective (\"non-X, un-X\"). Mamnūn is a passive participle.", why:"Mamnūn over \"endless\" — because the second meaning (no reproach) adds a DIGNITY that \"endless\" alone can't.", note:"Mann = a favour — AND the act of rubbing it in.", more:["mamnūn — a rich double root: manna = to cut off / diminish and to remind someone of a favour you did them (mann = a favour, but also the obnoxious act of bringing it up).","Related: mannān = one who reproaches with gifts.","Ghayru mamnūn means BOTH: - (a) \"NEVER CUT OFF\" — from manna = to diminish/sever. A reward that never runs out, never shrinks, never expires. - (b) \"NEVER HELD OVER YOU\" — from mann = reproaching someone with a favour. Nobody will ever say \"remember what I gave you?\"","Think about how perfect (b) is. The worst part of receiving a great gift from a person is often the reminders afterward. The verse promises: infinite generosity, and zero reproach. Given freely, and never mentioned again.","And it's the final word of the surah. A surah that opened with the sky splitting closes on \"a reward with no strings.\"","Compare the endings you've collected: - Takwīr 81:29 — closes on Rabb al-ʿālamīn (the Nurturer) - Infiṭār 82:19 — closes on lillāh (it all belongs to Him) - Inshiqāq 84:25 — closes on ghayru mamnūn (never cut, never reproached)","Notice: after all the terror, each of these surahs ends on mercy or ownership, never on the fire.","And note the illā (v.25) carving the believers OUT of the punishment announced in v.24 — a classic exception-clause rescue.","\"A reward NEVER CUT OFF and NEVER HELD OVER YOU.\" Infinite, and nobody ever mentions it again.","The last word of the surah."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:85, name:"Al-Burūj", ar:"البروج", en:"The Towering Constellations", verses:22, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Every other surah so far has shown the wicked burning. This one shows the BELIEVERS burning — and still calls them the winners.",
  special:["It tells the story of believers who lost — and calls it a victory.","The Aṣḥāb al-Ukhdūd (\"the People of the Trench\") were believers thrown into a burning ditch for refusing to abandon their faith. They were not rescued. There is no last-minute miracle in this surah. They burned, and their persecutors sat on the edge watching.","And the Qur'an calls that the great success (al-fawz al-kabīr, v.11).","This surah exists to redefine winning. For a Muslim community in Makkah being tortured and getting no rescue, it says: the people in the fire won; the people on the chairs lost.","--"],
  big:["Four Oaths (v.1–3) — the sky's constellations, the promised Day, and \"a witness and a witnessed.\"","The Trench (v.4–9) — the fire, the spectators, and the one line that explains all persecution ever.","The Two Verdicts (v.10–11) — for the burners, the Burning; for the burned, gardens.","Who Your Lord Is (v.12–16) — His grip is severe … and He is the Loving. Both, in four verses.","You've Seen This Before (v.17–20) — Pharaoh, Thamūd. Armies that lost.","The Book That Can't Burn (v.21–22) — they burned believers; the message is on a PRESERVED tablet."],
  traps:["أَصْحَابُ الْأُخْدُودِ (v.4) = the PERSECUTORS, not the victims. The curse is on the people who DUG the trench.","فَتَنُوا (v.10) — fitnah comes from PUTTING GOLD IN FIRE to test purity. Translating it as merely \"persecuted\" loses the entire reframe.","الْوَدُودُ (v.14) — \"THE LOVING,\" two verses after \"His GRIP is SEVERE,\" in a surah about believers being burned. Don't skim past it.","The sh-h-d root appears 4× in 9 verses (v.3 twice, v.7, v.9). Track it — the surah is about who was watching.","قُعُودٌ / شُهُودٌ (v.6–7) — the Qur'an describes the SEATING, not the burning. The horror is in the casualness.","قُتِلَ (v.4) — an imprecation-idiom (\"cursed be\"), NOT literal killing. Same as ʿAbasa 80:17.","فَعَّالٌ (v.16) — the intensive of the verb \"to DO\" — the same pattern as Nabaʾ's wahhāj/thajjāj.","الْمَجِيدُ appears TWICE — v.15 (Allah) and v.21 (the Qur'an). Same adjective, two subjects.","ثَمُودَ (v.18) — a DIPTOTE: fatḥah, no tanwīn. Same rule as ḥadāʾiqa (ʿAbasa 80:30), kawāʿiba (Nabaʾ 78:33).","مُّحِيطٌ (v.20) — muḥīṭ = the OCEAN in modern Arabic. Encompassing means there is no outside."],
  links:[{w:"مَّحْفُوظٍ (v.22)",e:"حَافِظِينَ (82:10 · 83:33) · حَافِظٌ (86:4)",t:"Fourth instance — the preserving root"},{w:"لَوْحٍ مَّحْفُوظٍ (v.22)",e:"صُحُفٍ مُّكَرَّمَةٍ (ʿAbasa 80:13–14)",t:"The message lives somewhere untouchable"},{w:"قُتِلَ (v.4)",e:"قُتِلَ الْإِنسَانُ (ʿAbasa 80:17)",t:"Same imprecation-idiom — \"confound them\""},{w:"هَلْ أَتَاكَ حَدِيثُ (v.17)",e:"هَلْ أَتَاكَ حَدِيثُ مُوسَىٰ (Nāz. 79:15)",t:"Identical opening — the history lesson"},{w:"فِرْعَوْنَ (v.18)",e:"Nāziʿāt 79:17–26",t:"Nāziʿāt told it at length; here it's one name"},{w:"الْفَوْزُ الْكَبِيرُ (v.11)",e:"مَفَازًا (Nabaʾ 78:31)",t:"Fawz = a win AND an escape"},{w:"مِن وَرَائِهِم (v.20)",e:"وَرَاءَ ظَهْرِهِ (Inshiqāq 84:10)",t:"The blind spot — behind you"},{w:"لَشَدِيدٌ (v.12)",e:"شِدَادًا (78:12) · شَدَدْنَا (76:28)",t:"The root that builds firm also grips firm"},{w:"فَعَّالٌ (v.16)",e:"وَهَّاجًا · ثَجَّاجًا · غَسَّاقًا · كِذَّابًا (Nabaʾ 78)",t:"Fifth instance of the faʿʿāl intensive"},{w:"يُرِيدُ (v.16)",e:"يَشَاءَ اللَّهُ (Takwīr 81:29)",t:"Absolute will"},{w:"ذُو الْعَرْشِ (v.15)",e:"عِندَ ذِي الْعَرْشِ (Takwīr 81:20)",t:"Same phrase; the five special nouns"},{w:"ذَاتِ (v.1, 5)",e:"ذِي قُوَّةٍ (Takwīr 81:20)",t:"Dhū / dhī / dhāt — three times here"},{w:"الْحَرِيقِ (v.10)",e:"سَعِيرًا (76:4 · 84:12) · سُعِّرَتْ (81:12)",t:"The burning family"},{w:"مَشْهُودٍ / شُهُودٌ / شَهِيدٌ (v.3, 7, 9)",e:"",t:"Four times in nine verses — who was watching"},{w:"الْمَوْعُودِ (v.2)",e:"مِيقَاتًا (Nabaʾ 78:17)",t:"The Day is scheduled / promised"},{w:"وَمَا نَقَمُوا... إِلَّا (v.8)",e:"إِنْ هُوَ إِلَّا (81:27) · وَمَا يُكَذِّبُ... إِلَّا (83:12)",t:"The restriction structure"},{w:"الْوَدُودُ (v.14)",e:"رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ (81:29) · غَيْرُ مَمْنُونٍ (84:25)",t:"Tenderness inserted at the hardest moment"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"الْبُرُوجِ", tr:"Al-burūj", v:"1", root:"The \"tower / fortress / prominent structure\" family — burj = a TOWER (modern Arabic: Burj Khalīfa, burj = a tower/high-rise!); tabarruj = to display oneself prominently.", meaning:"\"By the sky containing the great constellations.\"", magic:"The choice of \"TOWERS\" for the opening oath is not decoration — it's the surah's first argument.", grammar:"Dhāt = \"possessor of / the one having\" (feminine of dhū). You own dhū/dhī from Takwīr 81:20 (dhī quwwatin).", why:"Burūj (towers) over \"stars\" — because the surah is about who really holds the high ground.", note:"Burj = a TOWER (Burj Khalīfa!). The surah opens with TOWERS and is about a TRENCH. High and low, from the first word.", more:["Applied to the sky: the great constellations / the mansions the sun and moon pass through — the sky's towers.","A burj is a FORTRESS TOWER — a place of strength, height, and protection. And the surah is about to describe believers being burned in a DITCH — the lowest possible place.","Towers above; a trench below. The first word sets the vertical frame, and the whole surah works inside it.","And the constellations are the sky's most ORDERED feature — fixed, mapped, returning on schedule for millennia. Swearing by cosmic order, right before describing an event that looks like chaos.","Compare the sky-oaths you own: Ṭāriq 86:1 (wa's-samāʾi wa'ṭ-ṭāriq), Takwīr 81:15–16 (al-khunnas, al-jawāri'l-kunnas). Three consecutive surahs swear by the night sky.","Note it appears THREE times in this surah: dhāti'l-burūj (v.1), dhāti'l-waqūd (v.5), dhu'l-ʿArsh (v.15)."]},
  {ar:"الْيَوْمِ الْمَوْعُودِ", tr:"Al-yawmi'l-mawʿūd", v:"2", root:"The \"promising\" family — waʿada = to promise; waʿd = a promise; mīʿād = an appointment/appointed time; mawʿid = a rendezvous.", meaning:"\"And by the PROMISED Day.\"", magic:"Notice it's not called \"the Day of Judgment\" or \"the Day of Resurrection\" here — it's called the day that was PROMISED.", grammar:"Passive participle (mawʿūd) — \"that which has been promised.\" The promiser is unnamed because obvious.", why:"Mawʿūd over any other title — because the people who need this surah need a PROMISE, not a description.", note:"Waʿd = a PROMISE; mīʿād = an appointment. The Day is named after the fact that someone GAVE THEIR WORD.", more:["To a community being tortured with no rescue coming, the operative word is promise. The Day is named by the fact that someone gave their word.","Compare Nabaʾ 78:17 — inna yawma'l-faṣli kāna mīqātā (\"an appointed time\"). Same root-family idea: this is SCHEDULED. Nabaʾ says it has an appointment; Burūj says it was promised."]},
  {ar:"شَاهِدٍ وَمَشْهُودٍ", tr:"Shāhidin wa mashhūd", v:"3", root:"The \"witnessing / being present\" family — shahida = to witness, to be present.", meaning:"\"And by a WITNESS and that which is WITNESSED.\"", magic:"This is the surah's thesis statement, hidden in an oath — and once you see it, the whole surah reorganizes around it.", grammar:"Active participle (shāhid, the doer) vs.", why:"Leaving it unspecified lets the root do its work across the surah rather than pinning it to one reference.", note:"Shahādah = testimony (and your SHAHĀDAH); shahīd = a MARTYR and a witness. The root appears 4× in 9 verses. They watched (v.7).", more:["Shahādah = testimony (and the SHAHĀDAH you declare!); shahīd = a martyr (one who bears witness with his life) and a witness; mashhad = a scene.","The active/passive pair — shāhid (one who witnesses) and mashhūd (that which is witnessed) — is deliberately LEFT UNSPECIFIED. Commentators offer many readings (Friday and the Day of ʿArafah; the Prophet ﷺ and his ummah; the witness and the scene).","But watch what the surah does with the root immediately afterward: - v.7 — wa hum ʿalā mā yafʿalūna bi'l-muʾminīna SHUHŪD (\"and they were WITNESSES to what they were doing to the believers\") - v.9 — wa'llāhu ʿalā kulli shayʾin SHAHĪD (\"and Allah is WITNESS over everything\")","THE ROOT APPEARS FOUR TIMES IN NINE VERSES. The persecutors sat and WATCHED (v.7) — they made themselves witnesses. And v.9 answers: there was Someone ELSE watching.","The whole surah is about who was watching whom. And shahīd also means MARTYR — so the believers in the trench are, by the root itself, witnesses too. Three sets of eyes on that ditch: the persecutors, the martyrs, and Allah.","passive participle (mashhūd, the receiver) — the cleanest example of this pair in the Qur'an.","Learn the shapes side by side: fāʿil / mafʿūl.","The whole surah is about WHO WAS WATCHING."]},
  {ar:"قُتِلَ أَصْحَابُ الْأُخْدُودِ", tr:"Qutila aṣḥābu'l-ukhdūd", v:"4", root:"Qutila — the imprecation-idiom you own from ʿAbasa 80:17 (qutila'l-insānu mā akfarah — \"Confound man!\").", meaning:"\"Cursed were the people of the TRENCH.\"", magic:"Two things worth catching.", grammar:"Qutila is grammatically passive past, used idiomatically as a curse. Don't translate literally.", why:"Ukhdūd (a dug furrow) over \"fire\" or \"pit\" — because it emphasizes that this was PREPARED, dug in advance, premeditated.", note:"Khadd = the CHEEK; ukhdūd = a FURROW in the earth — a scar in the ground.", more:["Not literal killing but \"may they perish / cursed be.\" ukhdūd — the \"furrow / groove / long cut in the ground\" family — khadd = the CHEEK (the furrowed side of the face!); ukhdūd = a long trench dug in the earth.","First: ukhdūd shares its root with khadd, the CHEEK. A trench is a furrow in the earth's face — the same shape as the lines on a face. The word makes the ditch feel like a scar on the ground.","Second, and more important: notice WHO is cursed. \"Aṣḥāb al-ukhdūd\" — the COMPANIONS/PEOPLE of the trench — refers to the PERSECUTORS, the ones who dug it and lit it. Not the victims.","And the same imprecation-word (qutila) that ʿAbasa 80:17 aimed at ungrateful humanity is aimed here at torturers. Second instance of this idiom.","The historical background (as related in reports about the boy and the king, and connected by many commentators to Najrān): believers were given a choice — renounce your faith or burn. They chose the fire.","Aṣḥāb = \"companions/people of\" — from ṣāḥib, which you own from ʿAbasa 80:36 and Takwīr 81:22.","And \"the people of the trench\" = the PERSECUTORS, not the victims.","Same curse-word as ʿAbasa 80:17."]},
  {ar:"ذَاتِ الْوَقُودِ", tr:"Dhāti'l-waqūd", v:"5", root:"The \"kindling / fuel\" family — waqada = to burn/kindle; waqūd = FUEL (modern Arabic: waqūd = petrol/fuel!); mawqid = a stove/hearth.", meaning:"\"the fire full of FUEL.\"", magic:"The word waqūd is chosen to show PREMEDITATION, and it's chilling.", grammar:"Dhāt + genitive = \"the one possessing X.\" Second of three instances in this surah.", why:"Waqūd over \"burning\" — because fuel implies a supplier.", note:"Waqūd = FUEL (petrol, in modern Arabic). Someone GATHERED it. Dug (v.4) + fueled (v.5) = premeditated.", more:["A fire \"possessing fuel\" means someone GATHERED the fuel. Somebody chopped the wood. Somebody carried it. Somebody stacked it and kept it burning. This wasn't a fire that happened; it was a fire that was maintained.","Combined with ukhdūd (a DUG trench) in v.4, you get: they dug it, they fueled it, they kept it going. Two words establishing full intent.","And dhāt appears again (v.1 dhāti'l-burūj, v.5 dhāti'l-waqūd) — the same construction for the sky's TOWERS and the trench's FUEL. Same grammar, opposite worlds."]},
  {ar:"قُعُودٌ... شُهُودٌ", tr:"Quʿūd… shuhūd", v:"6–7", root:"Quʿūd — the \"sitting\" family — qaʿada = to sit; qāʿidah = a base/foundation (something that sits); maqʿad = a seat.", meaning:"V.6 — \"when they were SITTING by it\"; v.7 — \"and they were WITNESSES to what they were doing to the believers.\"", magic:"These two verses are, in my view, the most disturbing pair in this juzʾ — and the horror is entirely in the vocabulary.", grammar:"Quʿūd and shuhūd are both broken plurals on the same fuʿūl pattern — and they RHYME, which makes the pairing unforgettable.", why:"Describing the chairs instead of the fire is far more disturbing than describing the fire — it indicts the casualness.", note:"The Qur'an doesn't describe the burning. It describes the SEATING. quʿūd = SITTING; shuhūd = WATCHING. They pulled up chairs.", more:["shuhūd — the WITNESSING root from v.3 — plural of shāhid.","The Qur'an does NOT describe the burning. No screams, no flames, no bodies. It describes the SEATING ARRANGEMENT.","Quʿūd — they were SITTING. Sitting means: comfortable. Settled. Not in a hurry. Watching an event. They pulled up chairs.","Shuhūd — they were WITNESSES. They didn't just kill; they made themselves an AUDIENCE. The word turns the torture into a SPECTACLE with attendance.","And this is the surah's genius: by using shuhūd, the persecutors are placed in the SAME ROOT as the oath in v.3 and as Allah's own attribute in v.9 (shahīd). They appointed themselves witnesses — and v.9 names the real One.","One more layer: the believers in the trench are, by the root, shuhadāʾ — MARTYRS/witnesses. So in one scene you have: the seated witnesses (persecutors), the burning witnesses (martyrs), and the Witness over all (v.9).","And note the horrific detail: \"what they were doing to the believers\" — yafʿalūn, present-tense, making it ONGOING while they sat. They watched it happen in real time.","The rhyme yokes \"sitting\" to \"witnessing.\"","And shuhūd puts them in the same root as v.3's oath and v.9's shahīd."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا نَقَمُوا مِنْهُمْ إِلَّا أَن يُؤْمِنُوا", tr:"Wa mā naqamū minhum illā an yuʾminū", v:"8", root:"The \"resenting / holding a grievance / taking vengeance for\" family.", meaning:"\"And they RESENTED them for nothing except that they believed in Allah, the Almighty, the Praiseworthy.\"", magic:"This is one of the most quietly devastating verses in the Qur'an, and the structure is what does it.", grammar:"Mā … illā = the restriction structure (\"nothing… except\").", why:"Naqamū (resented/held against) over \"punished\" — because it frames the persecution as a GRIEVANCE, which forces the question: a grievance about what?", note:"Intiqām = VENGEANCE; naqama = to hold a grievance.", more:["Naqama = to find fault with, to resent, to avenge oneself for something; intiqām = vengeance; al-Muntaqim = the Avenger.","Naqama means \"to hold something AGAINST someone\" — to have a grievance. So the verse asks: what was their grievance? What was the charge?","And the answer, with mā… illā (nothing… except): \"that they believed in Allah.\" That's it. That's the entire indictment.","Look at what this does: it strips away every possible justification. Not \"they rebelled.\" Not \"they threatened us.\" Not \"they broke the law.\" The only crime was faith.","And then the two names chosen are perfect: al-ʿAzīz (the Almighty/Invincible) and al-Ḥamīd (the Praiseworthy). - al-ʿAzīz — the ALMIGHTY. Said about the God of people burning in a ditch. The verse insists on His invincibility at the exact moment it looks least true. - al-Ḥamīd — the PRAISEWORTHY. He deserves praise even here. Even from inside the trench.","This verse is the template for understanding all persecution: the \"reason\" is never the reason. The grievance is the faith itself.","You own it from Takwīr 81:27 (in huwa illā dhikr) and Muṭaffifīn 83:12 (wa mā yukadhdhibu bihi illā…).","It EXCLUDES everything else.","\"They held NOTHING against them EXCEPT that they believed.\" The only charge was faith.","And He's called al-ʿAzīz (Almighty) — while His believers burn."]},
  {ar:"فَتَنُوا... عَذَابُ الْحَرِيقِ", tr:"Fatanū… ʿadhābu'l-ḥarīq", v:"10", root:"Fatana — an extraordinary root: the original meaning is to put GOLD IN FIRE to test its purity.", meaning:"\"Indeed, those who PERSECUTED / put to trial the believing men and women, then did not repent — for them is the punishment of Hell, and for them is the punishment of the BURNING.\"", magic:"The root f-t-n is the single most important word in this surah, and the metallurgy is the whole point.", grammar:"Note al-muʾminīna wa'l-muʾmināt — both masculine AND feminine explicitly named.", why:"Fatanū over \"tortured\" — because the root turns the persecution into a purity test they passed.", note:"Fitnah comes from PUTTING GOLD IN FIRE to test it. They thought they were destroying; the word says they were ASSAYING.", more:["From that: fitnah = a trial, a test, a temptation, persecution, civil strife — all senses of being put through fire.","ḥarīq — the \"burning\" family — ḥaraqa = to burn; ḥarīq = a conflagration, a fire.","Fatana = putting GOLD IN FIRE to see if it's real. Impure metal is destroyed by the assay; pure gold comes out unchanged.","So the very word used for what the persecutors DID to the believers means \"TESTING FOR PURITY BY FIRE.\" They thought they were destroying. The vocabulary says they were ASSAYING. And the gold passed.","This single root reframes the entire event. The trench wasn't a defeat — it was a furnace, and what came out was proven.","And then the justice is exact (wifāq-style, like Nabaʾ 78:26): they used FIRE — they get ʿadhāb al-ḥarīq, the punishment of BURNING. Note it's listed SEPARATELY from \"the punishment of Hell\" — two punishments named, and the second one names their own method.","One more mercy hidden here: thumma lam yatūbū — \"THEN they did not repent.\" The door was open even for them. Even for people who burned believers alive, the verse pauses to note: they could have repented. That clause did not have to be there.","The women are not folded into the masculine plural; they are stated.","And note: \"then they did not REPENT\" — the door was open even for them."]},
  {ar:"الْفَوْزُ الْكَبِيرُ", tr:"Al-fawzu'l-kabīr", v:"11", root:"The \"triumph / attainment / escape to safety\" family — fāza = to win, to attain; fawz = victory.", meaning:"\"That is THE GREAT SUCCESS.\"", magic:"This is the verse the whole surah exists for.", grammar:"Dhālika (\"THAT\") is a far demonstrative — used for something grand and elevated, not merely distant.", why:"Al-kabīr (\"the GREAT\") added — because the world's verdict on that trench was \"total defeat,\" and the correction has to be emphatic.", note:"They burned. They weren't rescued.", more:["You own mafāzan from Nabaʾ 78:31 (inna li'l-muttaqīna mafāzā — a place of triumph/escape).","Read the sequence: believers are thrown into a fire (v.4–7) → their only crime was faith (v.8) → they are not rescued → and then: \"THAT is the great success.\"","The Qur'an looks at people who were burned alive and calls it WINNING.","This is a total redefinition of victory — and it was revealed to a community in Makkah being tortured, watching no rescue arrive. The message: your outcome in this world is not the scoreboard.","And fawz carries BOTH senses (as in Nabaʾ 78:31): a great WIN and a safe ESCAPE. They didn't escape the fire; they escaped everything the fire was threatening them with.","Compare the \"success\" language you own: - Nabaʾ 78:31 — mafāzan (a place of triumph) - Burūj 85:11 — al-fawzu'l-kabīr (the GREAT success)","\"THAT — [that magnificent thing] — is the great success.\"","And the Qur'an says: \"THAT is the GREAT SUCCESS.\" fawz = a WIN and an ESCAPE (Nabaʾ 78:31). The scoreboard is not in this world."]},
  {ar:"بَطْشَ رَبِّكَ لَشَدِيدٌ", tr:"Baṭsha rabbika la-shadīd", v:"12", root:"The \"seizing violently / gripping with force\" family — baṭasha = to grab hold of forcefully, to strike, to seize with the hand.", meaning:"\"Indeed, the GRIP of your Lord is SEVERE.\"", magic:"The word is physical, and deliberately so.", grammar:"Inna + accusative + la- — the double-emphasis frame you own from Infiṭār 82:10 and Muṭaffifīn 83:7. Note the iḍāfah: baṭsha rabbika = \"the grip OF your Lord.\"", why:"Baṭsh over a word for \"punishment\" — because a grip is personal and close, not distant.", note:"Baṭsh = a violent GRIP of the hand. They used their hands on the believers.", more:["Baṭsh = a violent, powerful grip.","Baṭsh is HAND vocabulary — a seizing, a grabbing, a strike. Not abstract power (qudrah), not authority (sulṭān) — a GRIP.","And think about who's being addressed. The persecutors used their hands: they dug the trench, gathered the fuel, threw people in. The answer is described as a HAND too. Their grip vs. His.","And shadīd is a root you own — Nabaʾ 78:12 (sabʿan shidādā — seven firm heavens) and Insān 76:28 (shadadnā asrahum — We tightened their frame). The same root that BUILDS firmly also GRIPS firmly.","And shadīd = the same root that built the firm heavens (78:12) and knit your frame (76:28)."]},
  {ar:"يُبْدِئُ وَيُعِيدُ", tr:"Yubdiʾu wa yuʿīd", v:"13", root:"Yubdiʾu — the \"beginning / originating\" family — badaʾa = to begin; bidāyah = a beginning; mubtadiʾ = a beginner.", meaning:"\"Indeed, it is He who ORIGINATES and REPEATS.\"", magic:"A perfect ṭibāq (opposite-pair), and it's the resurrection argument in three words.", grammar:"Innahu HUWA yubdiʾu — the extra pronoun huwa is ḍamīr al-faṣl (the separating pronoun), adding restriction: \"it is HE [and no other] who originates.\" You own this from Nāziʿāt 79:39 (fa-inna'l-jaḥīma hiya'l-maʾwā).", why:"The pair rather than one verb — because the argument needs both halves.", note:"ʿĪd = \"the RETURNING one\" — a festival that comes back. He ORIGINATES and REPEATS.", more:["yuʿīd — the \"returning / repeating\" family — ʿāda = to return; iʿādah = repetition; ʿĪd = a festival that RETURNS each year!; muʿīd = one who repeats.","He STARTS things and He BRINGS THEM BACK. That's the entire case for resurrection: the One who did it once can do it again.","And placed HERE, after the trench, it means something specific: those believers were \"ended.\" He originates and repeats. They're coming back.","And the ʿĪd hook is perfect: ʿĪd is literally \"the RETURNING one\" — the festival that comes back every year. You celebrate this root twice a year.","Said right after the trench: those believers are coming back."]},
  {ar:"الْغَفُورُ الْوَدُودُ", tr:"Al-Ghafūru'l-Wadūd", v:"14", root:"Ghafūr — the \"covering / concealing in order to forgive\" family — ghafara = to cover over.", meaning:"\"And He is the Forgiving, the LOVING.\"", magic:"This is the most surprising word in the surah, and its placement is everything.", grammar:"Faʿūl is an INTENSIVE adjective pattern — you own it from Nabaʾ 78:13 (wahhāj is faʿʿāl.", why:"Al-Wadūd over ar-Raḥīm — because wudd is warmer and more personal, and that's exactly what a persecuted believer needs to hear.", note:"Mighfar = a HELMET (forgiveness = protective covering). wudd = TENDER, WARM affection.", more:["Mighfar = a HELMET (what covers the head in battle!); maghfirah = forgiveness.","wadūd — the \"love / affection\" family — wudd = loving affection, tender attachment; mawaddah = love; Wadd was even an old idol-name, and widād = affection.","Two verses ago: \"the GRIP of your Lord is SEVERE\" (v.12). Now: \"He is the Forgiving, the LOVING.\"","In a surah about believers being burned alive, Allah names Himself al-Wadūd — THE LOVING.","And wudd is a specific kind of love: TENDER, AFFECTIONATE ATTACHMENT. Not abstract benevolence — the warm, personal fondness you have for someone you cherish. The faʿūl pattern makes it INTENSE and CONSTANT (same intensive shape as ghafūr, ṣabūr, shakūr).","Think about why this is here. A community watching its members tortured could easily conclude: \"He doesn't care.\" The surah answers directly: al-Wadūd. Not \"He is powerful\" (already said in v.12), not \"He is just\" — \"He LOVES you.\"","And al-Ghafūr first is deliberate too: from ghafara, to COVER — like mighfar, a helmet. Forgiveness as PROTECTIVE COVERING.","Compare the endings you've collected: Takwīr closed on Rabb al-ʿālamīn (the Nurturer); Inshiqāq closed on ghayru mamnūn (a reward with no reproach); here, mid-surah, al-Wadūd. These surahs keep inserting tenderness at the hardest moments.","Ghafūr/wadūd are faʿūl) and Insān 76:21 (ṭahūrā — actively purifying).","Both patterns mean \"does it a LOT.\"","Two verses after \"His GRIP is SEVERE,\" He calls Himself THE LOVING.","Said to people whose friends were burned alive."]},
  {ar:"ذُو الْعَرْشِ الْمَجِيدُ", tr:"Dhu'l-ʿArshi'l-Majīd", v:"15", root:"ʿarsh — the Throne.", meaning:"\"Owner of the Throne, the Glorious.\"", magic:"The third and final dhū/dhāt in the surah (v.1 the sky's towers, v.5 the fire's fuel, v.15 the Throne). Three \"possessors\": a sky, a fire, and Him.", grammar:"Dhū (nominative) vs. dhī (genitive) vs.", why:"Majīd over \"great\" — because the root carries glory AND generosity together.", note:"Majd = GLORY. Used TWICE: for Allah (v.15) and for the QUR'AN (v.21). And dhi'l-ʿArsh is the same phrase as Takwīr 81:20.", more:["majīd — the \"glory / nobility / abundance\" family — majd = glory, honour, magnificence; the name Majīd/Mājid.","The root also carries a sense of abundance and generosity.","And note al-Majīd appears TWICE in this surah: v.15 for the Owner of the Throne, and v.21 for the QUR'AN itself (bal huwa qurʾānun majīd). The same adjective for Allah and for His speech.","Placement matters: the persecutors had a throne too (a king ordered this). The surah quietly names the real Throne-owner.","dhā (accusative) — one of the FIVE SPECIAL NOUNS (الأسماء الخمسة) that show case with a long vowel.","You own this from Takwīr 81:20 (dhī quwwatin ʿinda dhi'l-ʿArsh).","Note Takwīr also said dhi'l-ʿArsh — same phrase!"]},
  {ar:"فَعَّالٌ لِّمَا يُرِيدُ", tr:"Faʿʿālun li-mā yurīd", v:"16", root:"Faʿʿāl — the INTENSIVE pattern you own from Nabaʾ 78:13 (sirājan wahhājā), 78:14 (mā'an thajjājā), 78:25 (ghassāq), 78:28 (kidhdhābā) — from faʿala, to do.", meaning:"\"Doer of whatever He wills.\"", magic:"You already own this word-SHAPE — now watch it applied to Allah Himself.", grammar:"The faʿʿāl intensive — FIFTH instance you own. Learn the shape once, collect it everywhere.", why:"The intensive of the verb \"to do\" is the most economical way to say absolute agency.", note:"Faʿʿāl = the INTENSIVE pattern (wahhāj, thajjāj, ghassāq, kidhdhāb — all from Nabaʾ). Built on \"to DO\" = THE Doer.", more:["yurīd — the \"willing/wanting\" family (irādah = will).","The faʿʿāl pattern means \"does it INTENSELY, CONSTANTLY, ABUNDANTLY.\" You met it four times in Nabaʾ describing a blazing lamp, pouring rain, foul discharge, and relentless lying. Here it's built on the root faʿala — \"to DO\" itself.","So faʿʿāl literally means \"THE ONE WHO DOES, INTENSELY.\" The intensive form of the verb \"to do\" — the ultimate Doer.","And li-mā yurīd (\"of whatever He wills\") removes every limit. Placed after the trench, it means: what happened was not outside His will, and what comes next is not outside it either.","Compare Takwīr 81:29 — wa mā tashāʾūna illā an yashāʾa'llāh (\"you do not will except that Allah wills\"). Same doctrine, two surahs.","\"Doer of whatever He wills\" — after the trench, this is the answer."]},
  {ar:"حَدِيثُ الْجُنُودِ", tr:"ḥadīthu'l-junūd", v:"17", root:"ḥadīth — \"a narration / account / something newly told\"", meaning:"\"Has the story of the ARMIES reached you?\"", magic:"The word junūd (ARMIES) is the point, and it's a devastating choice.", grammar:"Hal atāka… = a rhetorical question introducing a known lesson.", why:"Junūd (armies) over \"peoples\" — because it directly answers the fear of being outnumbered.", note:"Jundī = a SOLDIER; junūd = ARMIES. The believers had no army.", more:["From ḥadatha = to happen, to be new (ḥadīth = new, and also a report — the same word as the Prophetic ḥadīth!).","junūd — plural of jund, an ARMY/troops — related to jundī = a soldier.","Pharaoh and Thamūd are introduced not as \"peoples\" or \"nations\" but as ARMIES — military forces, organized power, troops.","Why? Because the believers in the trench had NO army. The surah says: you're worried about being outmatched? Let me tell you about some ARMIES. And then names two that no longer exist.","And hal atāka (\"has it reached you?\") is a construction you own from Nāziʿāt 79:15 (hal atāka ḥadīthu Mūsā — \"has the story of Mūsā reached you?\"). Same opening, same word ḥadīth, same purpose: a history lesson mid-surah.","Nāziʿāt told the Pharaoh story at LENGTH (12 verses). Burūj compresses it to a NAME (v.18). Because you already know it — Nāziʿāt told you.","Note Thamūda in v.18 — it's a DIPTOTE (mamnūʿ min aṣ-ṣarf), so it takes fatḥah without tanwīn.","\"Let me tell you about some armies.\" And hal atāka ḥadīth… is the same opening as Nāziʿāt 79:15."]},
  {ar:"مِن وَرَائِهِم مُّحِيطٌ", tr:"Min warāʾihim muḥīṭ", v:"20", root:"Warāʾ — behind — you own it from Inshiqāq 84:10 (warāʾa ẓahrih — the record behind his back).", meaning:"\"But Allah encompasses them from behind.\"", magic:"A stunning image, and the two words work together perfectly.", grammar:"Active participle (muḥīṭ) = a continuous, present state — \"is [always] encompassing.\"", why:"Muḥīṭ over \"knows\" or \"sees\" — because encompassment means there is no OUTSIDE.", note:"Muḥīṭ = the OCEAN (al-Muḥīṭ al-Hindī) — surrounding on all sides. \"From BEHIND them\" — the one direction they never check.", more:["muḥīṭ — the \"encompassing / surrounding\" family — aḥāṭa = to encircle completely; muḥīṭ = the OCEAN in modern Arabic (al-Muḥīṭ al-Hindī = the Indian Ocean — \"the encompassing one\")!","Muḥīṭ means SURROUNDING ON ALL SIDES — like an OCEAN around an island. There's no edge you can reach and step past.","And min warāʾihim — \"from BEHIND them\" — is the killer detail. Behind is the direction you can't watch. They're facing forward, denying (v.19), confident — and He has them from the one angle they never check.","Compare Inshiqāq 84:10: the condemned man's record is handed to him warāʾa ẓahrih — BEHIND HIS BACK, where he can't see. Same blind spot, two surahs.","And it answers v.7 exactly: they sat WATCHING (shuhūd) the believers burn — thinking they had the best view. v.20: someone was behind them the whole time. The watchers were being watched from a direction they couldn't turn toward.","They sat WATCHING (v.7); Someone was behind them all along."]},
  {ar:"لَوْحٍ مَّحْفُوظٍ", tr:"Lawḥin maḥfūẓ", v:"22", root:"Lawḥ — a TABLET / flat board — the surface you write on; also lawḥah = a painting/panel in modern Arabic.", meaning:"\"In a PRESERVED TABLET.\"", magic:"The final verse of the surah, and it is the perfect answer to the trench.", grammar:"Both indefinite (lawḥin maḥfūẓin) — and note the passive participle maḥfūẓ (\"that which HAS BEEN preserved\").", why:"Ending on a tablet rather than a threat — because the surah's real answer to fire is PERMANENCE.", note:"Lawḥ = a TABLET; maḥfūẓ = PRESERVED (same root as ḥāfiẓ, one who memorizes the Qur'an). They burned people.", more:["maḥfūẓ — the \"guarding / preserving\" family — you own it richly: Infiṭār 82:10 (la-ḥāfiẓīn), Muṭaffifīn 83:33 (ḥāfiẓīn), Ṭāriq 86:4 (ḥāfiẓ), and ḥāfiẓ = one who has memorized the Qur'an.","Line up the surah's images: - They dug a trench (v.4) — a thing in the ground - They gathered fuel (v.5) — a thing that burns - They burned believers (v.7) — flesh, destructible - And the message is on al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ — a PRESERVED TABLET.","You can burn a person. You cannot burn this. The surah ends by naming the one thing their fire could never reach.","And maḥfūẓ is the passive of the root you've now seen FOUR times across these surahs — the guarding angels, the guardians they weren't appointed as, the guardian over every soul. Here the root reaches its highest object: the Book itself.","Compare ʿAbasa 80:13–14: fī ṣuḥufin mukarramah · marfūʿatin muṭahharah — \"honoured, exalted, purified scrolls.\" Same idea: the message lives somewhere untouchable. ʿAbasa said it to a Prophet ﷺ tempted by worldly endorsement; Burūj says it to a community facing fire. Same reassurance, two crises.","And the last two verses pair beautifully: v.21 — \"it is a GLORIOUS Qur'an\" (majīd, the same word used for Allah in v.15). v.22 — \"in a PRESERVED tablet.\" Glorious and safe. Neither diminished nor destructible.","Someone is doing the preserving.","The message is on a tablet fire can't touch.","Compare ʿAbasa 80:13–14 — same reassurance, different crisis."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:86, name:"Aṭ-Ṭāriq", ar:"الطارق", en:"The Night-Comer", verses:17, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Rain comes back. Seeds split the ground. So will you.",
  special:["Seventeen verses, and it runs the argument for resurrection in a perfect circle — using ONE root twice.","V.8: innahu ʿalā RAJʿIHI la-qādir — \"He is able to RETURN him.\" - v.11: wa's-samāʾi dhāti'r-RAJʿ — \"By the sky possessing RETURN\" (the rain that comes back every year).","Same root, three verses apart. The sky RETURNS its rain. The earth SPLITS for the seed. Therefore: you will be RETURNED, and the earth will SPLIT for you.","The oath in v.11–12 isn't decoration — it's the EVIDENCE for v.8. Every year, in front of your eyes, the whole cycle rehearses your resurrection.","--"],
  big:["The Night-Knocker (v.1–4) — a piercing star, and: every soul has a guard.","Look At Your Origin (v.5–8) — \"Let man look at what he was made from\" — a gushing drop. He who made it can return it.","The Day Secrets Are Tested (v.9–10) — no power, no helper.","Rain and Seed (v.11–14) — the evidence. And: this is a decisive word, not a joke.","Two Plots (v.15–17) — they scheme; I scheme. Give them a little time."],
  traps:["رَجْعِهِ (v.8) and الرَّجْعِ (v.11) — same root, three verses apart. The claim and its evidence share a word. Miss this and you miss the surah.","إِنْ كُلُّ نَفْسٍ لَّمَّا (v.4) — in = \"NOT\" (not \"if\"); lammā = \"EXCEPT.\" \"There is NO soul EXCEPT that it has a guardian.\" The single easiest misreading in the surah.","مِمَّ (v.5) — من + ما, alif dropped. Third instance: ʿamma (78:1), fīma (79:43), mimma (86:5).","رُوَيْدًا (v.17) — a DIMINUTIVE (\"a teeny little while\"). Translating it as just \"a while\" loses the tone entirely.","تُبْلَى (v.9) — the secrets are TESTED, not merely \"revealed.\" Same test-family as nabtalīhi (76:2) and fatanū (85:10).","الطَّارِقِ (v.1) — ṭaraqa = TO KNOCK (miṭraqah = a hammer, ṭarīq = a beaten road). Not just \"night star.\"","الثَّاقِبُ (v.3) — mithqab = a DRILL. The star PIERCES; it doesn't merely shine.","فَصْلٌ (v.13) — a maṣdar used as an adjective = more emphatic than a normal adjective. And fāṣilah is the name for a Quranic verse-ending.","أَكِيدُ (v.16) — first person SINGULAR (\"I\"), not the usual \"We.\" The shift is deliberate.","مَهِّلِ / أَمْهِلْ (v.17) — two different forms (II and IV) of the same command, repeated for emphasis. Not a typo."],
  links:[{w:"فَلْيَنظُرِ الْإِنسَانُ (v.5)",e:"فَلْيَنظُرِ الْإِنسَانُ (ʿAbasa 80:24)",t:"IDENTICAL four words — outward vs. inward"},{w:"مِمَّ (v.5)",e:"عَمَّ (Nabaʾ 78:1) · فِيمَ (Nāz. 79:43)",t:"Third instance — مَا drops its alif"},{w:"رَجْعِهِ (v.8) ↔ الرَّجْعِ (v.11)",e:"",t:"Same root, internal — claim + evidence"},{w:"لَقَادِرٌ (v.8)",e:"قَادِرِينَ (Qiy. 75:4)",t:"Same word, same argument, same objection"},{w:"حَافِظٌ (v.4)",e:"حَافِظِينَ (82:10 · 83:33) · مَّحْفُوظٍ (85:22)",t:"Fourth instance of the preserving root"},{w:"تُبْلَى (v.9)",e:"نَبْتَلِيهِ (Ins. 76:2) · فَتَنُوا (85:10)",t:"The test BEGINS (76:2), the test is GRADED (86:9)"},{w:"الصَّدْعِ (v.12)",e:"شَقَقْنَا (ʿAbasa 80:26) · انشَقَّتْ (84:1)",t:"Earth splits for seeds; sky splits for the Day"},{w:"فَصْلٌ (v.13)",e:"يَوْمَ الْفَصْلِ (Nabaʾ 78:17)",t:"The DAY separates people; the QUR'AN separates truth"},{w:"مَّاءٍ دَافِقٍ (v.6)",e:"يُمْنَىٰ (75:37) · أَمْشَاجٍ (76:2) · نُّطْفَةٍ (80:19)",t:"Four surahs on your origin"},{w:"الصُّلْبِ (v.7)",e:"فَاقِرَة (Qiy. 75:25)",t:"Two spine-words: where life begins / where strength breaks"},{w:"نَاصِرٍ (v.10)",e:"لَا وَزَرَ (Qiy. 75:11) · لَا تَمْلِكُ نَفْسٌ (82:19)",t:"Three surahs, one closed door"},{w:"بِالْهَزْلِ (v.14)",e:"أَسَاطِيرُ الْأَوَّلِينَ (Muṭ. 83:13)",t:"The dismissal-move, answered"},{w:"بِالْهَزْلِ (v.14)",e:"بِمَجْنُونٍ (81:22) · بِغَائِبِينَ (82:16)",t:"Sixth bāʾ zāʾidah after negation"},{w:"كَيْدًا (v.15, 16)",e:"غَرْقًا/نَشْطًا (79:1–2) · صَبًّا/شَقًّا (80:25–26) · كَدْحًا (84:6)",t:"Cognate accusative — 7th & 8th instances"},{w:"ذَاتِ (v.11, 12)",e:"ذَاتِ الْبُرُوجِ / الْوَقُودِ (85:1, 5)",t:"Five instances across two surahs"},{w:"وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ (v.2)",e:"(82:17–18) · (83:8, 19)",t:"Past tense → explanation follows"},{w:"النَّجْمُ (v.3)",e:"انكَدَرَتْ (81:2) · انتَثَرَتْ (82:2) · الْخُنَّسِ (81:15)",t:"Four surahs, four star-verbs"},{w:"إِنْ كُلُّ نَفْسٍ (v.4)",e:"إِنْ هُوَ إِلَّا (Takwīr 81:27)",t:"In = NOT, not \"if\""}],
  words:[
  {ar:"الطَّارِقِ", tr:"Aṭ-ṭāriq", v:"1–2", root:"A gorgeous, tactile root — ṭaraqa = TO KNOCK, to strike, to beat. Ṭarq = a knock/blow. Miṭraqah = a HAMMER!", meaning:"\"By the sky and the NIGHT-COMER — and what will make you know what the night-comer is?\"", magic:"This word is a whole atmosphere, and every layer of the root matters.", grammar:"Wa mā adrāka mā… — you own this construction from Infiṭār 82:17–18 and Muṭaffifīn 83:8, 19. Remember the rule: PAST tense adrāka → an explanation FOLLOWS.", why:"ṭāriq over \"star\" — because the KNOCK is the point, not the astronomy.", note:"ṭaraqa = TO KNOCK; miṭraqah = a HAMMER; ṭarīq = a ROAD (a beaten path).", more:["Ṭarīq = a ROAD (the path beaten by feet!).","And classically, ṭāriq = anyone who arrives AT NIGHT — because in the old world, a night visitor had to KNOCK to be let in.","A ṭāriq is a NIGHT VISITOR — someone who arrives when you're asleep and KNOCKS. Think about how that feels: a knock at your door in the dead of night. Unexpected. Startling. It could be anything.","The word carries that entire emotional payload before it's even defined. And then the Qur'an doesn't define it immediately — v.2 asks \"and what will make you know what the ṭāriq is?\" It makes you WAIT one more verse. The suspense IS the technique.","And here's the connection to the whole surah: v.4 says every soul has a GUARD. A night-knocker is exactly what a guard exists for. The oath sets up the theme: something comes in the night, and someone is watching.","You already own ṭāriq's cousins: ṭarīq = a ROAD — literally \"the beaten path.\" And miṭraqah = a HAMMER.","Contrast with mā yudrīka (present, ʿAbasa 80:3) → no explanation.","A ṭāriq = someone who arrives at night and KNOCKS on your door.","And the surah answers: there's a guard on duty (v.4)."]},
  {ar:"النَّجْمُ الثَّاقِبُ", tr:"An-najmu'th-thāqib", v:"3", root:"Najm — a star (also najm = a plant without a stem, and nujūm = instalments — things that appear in stages).", meaning:"\"The PIERCING star.\"", magic:"The answer to the suspense — and thāqib is a startlingly physical word.", grammar:"Active participle (thāqib) describing an-najm. Both definite — \"THE piercing star,\" a specific, known thing.", why:"Thāqib over \"bright\" — because piercing implies the darkness RESISTS and loses.", note:"Mithqab = a DRILL; thuqb = a HOLE. The star doesn't glow — it DRILLS through the dark. v.1 KNOCKS, v.3 PIERCES.", more:["thāqib — the \"piercing / boring through\" family — thaqaba = to bore a hole, to pierce; thuqb = a hole; mithqab = a DRILL.","Thaqaba means TO DRILL A HOLE THROUGH something. So the star doesn't \"shine\" — it BORES THROUGH the darkness, punching a hole in it.","Picture it: not a glow spread over the sky, but a single point of light PUNCTURING the black, the way a drill goes through a board.","And connect the two words: v.1: a KNOCKER (something that strikes). v.3: a PIERCER (something that bores through). Both are words of FORCE and PENETRATION. This star doesn't visit; it breaks through.","Compare the star-vocabulary you own: Takwīr 81:2 (an-nujūmu inkadarat — stars go murky and fall), Infiṭār 82:2 (al-kawākibu intatharat — stars scattered), Takwīr 81:15–16 (al-khunnas… al-kunnas — stars that hide and sweep). Four surahs, four different star-verbs."]},
  {ar:"لَّمَّا عَلَيْهَا حَافِظٌ", tr:"Lammā ʿalayhā ḥāfiẓ", v:"4", root:"The \"guarding / preserving / memorizing\" family — ḥāfiẓ = one who has memorized the Qur'an; maḥfūẓ = preserved; ḥifẓ = memorization.", meaning:"\"There is NO soul but that it has over it a GUARDIAN.\"", magic:"The payoff of the oath, and it's a double negative for maximum coverage.", grammar:"In here = in an-nāfiyah — it means \"NOT,\" not \"if.\" You own this from Takwīr 81:27 (in huwa illā dhikr = \"it is NOTHING BUT a reminder\").", why:"ḥāfiẓ over \"watcher\" — because the preservation sense makes the record PERMANENT, and offers protection at the same time.", note:"A ḥāfiẓ preserves the Qur'an perfectly — these preserve YOU. in = NOT (not \"if\"); lammā = EXCEPT.", more:["You now own this root FOUR times: Infiṭār 82:10, Muṭaffifīn 83:33, Burūj 85:22, and here.","\"There is no soul EXCEPT that…\" The construction covers EVERY single person, with zero exceptions. Not \"most people have,\" not \"believers have\" — there is no one who doesn't.","And the tone is deliberately DOUBLE-EDGED: - A guard means you're WATCHED (nothing goes unrecorded) - A guard means you're PROTECTED (ḥifẓ = preservation, safekeeping)","After a ṭāriq — a night-knocker — which reading do you want? Something arrives in the dark, and someone is on duty. The surah lets both senses stand.","Compare Infiṭār 82:10–11: there the guardians were named as kirāman kātibīn — NOBLE, RECORDING. Infiṭār tells you WHO they are; Ṭāriq tells you that NOBODY is without one.","And lammā here = illā (\"except\").","So: in kullu nafsin lammā ʿalayhā ḥāfiẓ = \"there is NOT a soul EXCEPT that over it is a guardian.\" Misreading in as \"if\" destroys the sentence.","\"There is NO soul WITHOUT a guardian.\" Fourth surah with this root."]},
  {ar:"فَلْيَنظُرِ الْإِنسَانُ مِمَّ خُلِقَ", tr:"Fal-yanẓuri'l-insānu mimma khuliq", v:"5", root:"Yanẓur — the \"looking\" family — naẓara + ilā = to look AT. mimma — مِنْ + مَا = \"from what?\"", meaning:"\"So let man look at what he was created from.\"", magic:"TWO major hooks in one verse, and you own both already.", grammar:"Lām al-amr (the command lām) on a third-person verb: fal-yanẓur = \"let him look\"", why:"Yanẓur (physical looking) over \"reflect\" — because the evidence is meant to be OBSERVED, not theorized.", note:"ʿAbasa 80:24: \"let man look at his FOOD\" (outward). Ṭāriq 86:5: \"let man look at his ORIGIN\" (inward).", more:["First: fal-yanẓuri'l-insān is the EXACT SAME opening as ʿAbasa 80:24 — fal-yanẓuri'l-insānu ilā ṭaʿāmih (\"So let man look at his FOOD\"). Identical four words, two surahs apart. - ʿAbasa: look OUTWARD — at your lunch, the rain, the split earth, the crops. - Ṭāriq: look INWARD — at where you came from. Same command, opposite directions. Two halves of one argument.","Second: mimma = مِنْ + مَا, with the alif of mā DROPPED. THIS IS THE THIRD TIME you've met this rule: - Nabaʾ 78:1 — ʿamma (عن + ما) — \"about what?\" - Nāziʿāt 79:43 — fīma (في + ما) — \"in what?\" - Ṭāriq 86:5 — mimma (من + ما) — \"from what?\"","Three surahs, three prepositions, one rule. You now own ʿamma, fīma, mimma, bima, lima completely.","You own it from ʿAbasa 80:24 and Muṭaffifīn 83:26 (fal-yatanāfas).","Note the verb goes JUSSIVE.","Same four words, opposite directions.","And mimma = من+ما — THIRD instance of the alif-dropping rule (ʿamma, fīma, mimma)."]},
  {ar:"مَّاءٍ دَافِقٍ", tr:"Māʾin dāfiq", v:"6", root:"The \"gushing / pouring forth with force\" family — dafaqa = to pour out forcefully, to gush; dafq = a gushing outflow.", meaning:"\"He was created from a GUSHING fluid.\"", magic:"The word emphasizes FORCE and lack of control, and that's the argument.", grammar:"Active participle (dāfiq) describing māʾ; both indefinite.", why:"Dāfiq over \"a drop\" — because gushing conveys FORCE without control, which is the humbling part.", note:"Tadaffuq = a SURGE/FLOW (cash flow, traffic flow). You began as something that GUSHED — propelled, not chosen.", more:["Tadaffuq = a surge/flow (modern Arabic uses it for cash flow, traffic flow).","Dāfiq = gushing, surging, propelled. Not a drop that sits — fluid in forceful motion, going where it's driven.","And that's the humbling point: your origin was something that GUSHED — it wasn't placed, it wasn't chosen, it was propelled. You had no agency in your own beginning whatsoever.","Compare the origin-vocabulary you own: - Qiyāmah 75:37 — nuṭfatan min maniyyin yumnā (a drop emitted — passive) - Insān 76:2 — nuṭfatin amshāj (a MIXED drop) - ʿAbasa 80:19 — min nuṭfatin khalaqahu fa-qaddarah (a drop, precisely measured) - Ṭāriq 86:6 — māʾin dāfiq (a GUSHING fluid)","Four surahs, four angles on the same origin: emitted, mixed, measured, gushing. The Qur'an keeps returning to this because it's the argument that can't be dodged — you were THERE, in a sense, and you contributed nothing.","Note khuliqa is PASSIVE (\"he WAS created\") — matching the passivity of the origin itself.","Fourth surah on your origin: yumnā (75), amshāj (76), qaddarah (80), dāfiq (86)."]},
  {ar:"الصُّلْبِ وَالتَّرَائِبِ", tr:"Aṣ-ṣulbi wa't-tarāʾib", v:"7", root:"ṣulb — the \"hardness / firmness\" family — ṣalaba = to be hard.", meaning:"\"Emerging from between the BACKBONE and the RIBS.\"", magic:"The anatomical precision is the point, and the two words are a deliberate pair.", grammar:"Min bayni… = \"from between\" — the preposition bayn (between) is the same root as bayān (clarity, Qiyāmah 75:19) and mubīn (Takwīr 81:23). Clarity = distinguishing between things.", why:"Naming two bones rather than an organ — because bones are what everyone can feel on themselves.", note:"ṣulb = the SPINE (and STEEL in modern Arabic); tarāʾib = the RIBS.", more:["ṣulb = the BACKBONE / spine (the body's hard part), and ṣulb also = hard/solid in modern Arabic (ṣulb = steel!).","tarāʾib — the RIBS / the chest-bones, plural of tarībah — the front of the torso.","Ṣulb = the BACK (behind). Tarāʾib = the CHEST/RIBS (front). So \"from between the backbone and the ribs\" means from the very CORE of the body — the deepest, most protected, most central place.","It's a merism: naming the back and the front to mean EVERYTHING BETWEEN THEM — the same technique as qaddama wa akhkhara (Qiyāmah 75:13 / Infiṭār 82:5), where \"forward and back\" means the whole ledger.","And connect ṣulb to Qiyāmah 75:25 — tazunnu an yufʿala bihā FĀQIRAH (\"expecting a BACK-BREAKING calamity\" — from faqār, the spine). Two surahs, two spine-words, opposite scenes: one is where life begins, one is where strength breaks.","And ṣulb = STEEL in modern Arabic — the hardest thing, naming the hardest part of you.","Back + front = \"from your very CORE.\" Compare Qiyāmah 75:25's fāqirah — the other spine-word."]},
  {ar:"عَلَىٰ رَجْعِهِ لَقَادِرٌ", tr:"ʿalā rajʿihi la-qādir", v:"8", root:"Rajʿ — the \"returning / bringing back\" family — rajaʿa = to return; rujūʿ = a return; rājiʿūn.", meaning:"\"Indeed, He is ABLE to RETURN him.\"", magic:"The hinge of the surah, and both words are ones you already own.", grammar:"Inna + la- = the double-emphasis frame — fourth instance you own (82:10, 83:7, 85:12, here).", why:"Rajʿ (a plain \"return\") over a technical resurrection term.", note:"Innā ilayhi RĀJIʿŪN — you say this root at every funeral.", more:["As in innā lillāhi wa innā ilayhi rājiʿūn, which you say when someone dies!","qādir — the \"power AND precise measuring\" family — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:4 (balā qādirīna ʿalā an nusawwiya banānah) and Insān 76:16 (qaddarūhā) and ʿAbasa 80:19 (fa-qaddarah).","First, rajʿ: this word comes back in v.11 (wa's-samāʾi dhāti'r-rajʿ). The claim is made in v.8; the EVIDENCE for it arrives in v.11, using the SAME ROOT. \"He can RETURN you\" … \"by the sky that RETURNS [its rain].\" The proof is built into the vocabulary.","Second, qādir: the root means BOTH \"powerful\" AND \"precisely measuring.\" You met it in Qiyāmah 75:4, where the same word answered the same objection (\"does man think We won't gather his bones?\" → \"Yes — qādirīn — able even to re-proportion his fingertips\").","So Ṭāriq 86:8 and Qiyāmah 75:4 are the SAME ARGUMENT with the SAME WORD. Qiyāmah: He can rebuild your fingertips. Ṭāriq: He can return you. And both use qādir — power that MEASURES.","And note rajʿ is what you SAY at a funeral: innā ilayhi rājiʿūn — \"to Him we are RETURNING.\" You already affirm this verse every time someone dies.","Note ʿalā rajʿihi is FRONTED before la-qādir — spotlighting \"returning him\" as the thing He's able to do.","Because it makes the claim ordinary, and then v.11 shows it happening annually.","And qādir = the SAME word answering the SAME objection in Qiyāmah 75:4. This claim (v.8) gets its evidence in v.11 — same root."]},
  {ar:"تُبْلَى السَّرَائِرُ", tr:"Tublā's-sarāʾir", v:"9", root:"Tublā — the \"testing / trying / examining\" family — balā = to test; balāʾ = a trial; you own it from Insān 76:2 (nabtalīhi — \"We TEST him\").", meaning:"\"The Day when the SECRETS will be TESTED.\"", magic:"A phrase worth memorizing, and the verb choice is remarkable.", grammar:"Passive present (tublā), weak-final root (ب-ل-و). The doer is unnamed, as always in these Day-scenes.", why:"Tublā over \"exposed\" — because testing implies a STANDARD, and exposure alone doesn't.", note:"Sirr = a SECRET; sarīrah = your INNER REALITY. They aren't merely REVEALED — they're TESTED (assayed).", more:["sarāʾir — plural of sarīrah: the \"SECRET\" family — sirr = a secret; asrār = secrets; sarīrah = what you keep INSIDE — your inner reality, your private self.","It does NOT say the secrets will be \"revealed\" or \"exposed.\" It says they will be TESTED (tublā) — the metallurgy/assay word again, the same family as ibtilāʾ.","Compare Burūj 85:10's fatanū (putting gold in fire to test purity). Consecutive surahs, the same idea: what's inside you gets ASSAYED, not just displayed.","And sarāʾir is beautifully specific: sarīrah is not just a secret you're hiding — it's your INNER REALITY, the version of you that only you know. Your actual intentions. What you're really like when nobody's looking.","So the verse says: on that Day, the thing being examined is not your public record but your PRIVATE SELF — and it isn't merely shown, it's put to the test.","Connect to Inshiqāq 84:23: wa'llāhu aʿlamu bimā yūʿūn — \"Allah knows best what they store up inside\" (wiʿāʾ = a container). Inshiqāq says He KNOWS what's in the container; Ṭāriq says the contents get TESTED.","And Insān 76:2 said the whole point of giving you hearing and sight was nabtalīhi — TO TEST YOU. The test that began at your creation (76:2) gets graded here (86:9).","Insān 76:2: the test BEGINS (nabtalīhi).","Ṭāriq 86:9: the test is GRADED (tublā).","Same root, first surah to last."]},
  {ar:"مِن قُوَّةٍ وَلَا نَاصِرٍ", tr:"Min quwwatin wa lā nāṣir", v:"10", root:"Quwwah — strength/power — you own it from Takwīr 81:20 (dhī quwwatin, describing the noble messenger).", meaning:"\"Then he will have no POWER and no HELPER.\"", magic:"A perfect two-part denial, covering every possible resource.", grammar:"Mā lahu min quwwatin — the min here is min zāʾidah (extra min after negation for emphasis) = \"not ANY power at all.\" Same emphatic-particle logic as the bāʾ zāʾidah you own from Takwīr 81:22, 24, 25 and Infiṭār 82:16.", why:"Naming both inner and outer resources — because denying one alone would leave a door open.", note:"Anṣār = THE HELPERS of Madīnah. No power (inside) + no helper (outside) = nothing left.", more:["nāṣir — the \"helping / granting victory\" family — naṣara = to help/support; naṣr = victory/help (Sūrat an-Naṣr!); anṣār = the ANṢĀR of Madīnah — \"the Helpers.\"","Quwwah = strength FROM WITHIN yourself. Nāṣir = help FROM OUTSIDE yourself. Between them, there is nothing left. You can't save yourself, and no one can save you.","And nāṣir is the ANṢĀR root — the honoured title of the people of Madīnah who took in the Muhājirūn. The word for the greatest historical example of people helping people — negated on that Day.","Compare Infiṭār 82:19: yawma lā tamliku nafsun li-nafsin shayʾā (\"a Day when no soul will possess anything for another soul\"). Same doctrine, two surahs. Infiṭār denies OWNERSHIP; Ṭāriq denies POWER and HELP.","And Qiyāmah 75:11: kallā lā wazar (\"No! There is no refuge\"). Three surahs closing the same door.","Compare Qiyāmah 75:11 (lā wazar) and Infiṭār 82:19 (lā tamliku nafsun li-nafs).","Three surahs, one closed door."]},
  {ar:"ذَاتِ الرَّجْعِ", tr:"Dhāti'r-rajʿ", v:"11", root:"The RETURNING root from v.8 — rajaʿa = to return; rujūʿ = a return.", meaning:"\"By the sky possessing RETURN\" — i.e. the sky that sends rain back, season after season.", magic:"THIS IS THE SURAH'S MASTERSTROKE, and if you catch nothing else, catch this.", grammar:"Dhāt + genitive = \"possessor of.\" Feminine because samāʾ is feminine.", why:"Rajʿ rather than a word for \"rain\" — because using the SAME ROOT as v.8 makes the evidence unmissable.", note:"V.8: \"He can RETURN you.\" v.11: \"the sky POSSESSING RETURN.\" SAME ROOT — the claim and its proof.", more:["V.8 claimed: innahu ʿalā RAJʿIHI la-qādir — \"He is able to RETURN him.\" v.11 swears by: as-samāʾi dhāti'r-RAJʿ — \"the sky possessing RETURN.\"","SAME ROOT. THREE VERSES APART. The claim and its proof share a word.","The argument, spelled out: \"You doubt I can bring you back? Look up. Every single year, the sky BRINGS BACK the rain. The same water that evaporated, gone, invisible — RETURNS. It has done this your entire life, in front of you.","And dhāt — \"POSSESSING return\" — makes it an attribute of the sky, not an event. Returning is what the sky IS. You own dhāt from Burūj 85:1, 5, 15 (dhāti'l-burūj, dhāti'l-waqūd, dhu'l-ʿArsh) — consecutive surahs, same construction.","And it links to your funeral phrase: innā ilayhi rājiʿūn. The sky returns its rain; we return to Him. Same root.","The rain comes back every year, in front of your eyes.","And innā ilayhi rājiʿūn — same root."]},
  {ar:"ذَاتِ الصَّدْعِ", tr:"Dhāti'ṣ-ṣadʿ", v:"12", root:"The \"splitting / cracking open\" family — ṣadaʿa = to split, to crack; ṣadʿ = a crack/fissure.", meaning:"\"And by the earth possessing SPLITTING\" — the earth that cracks open for shoots to emerge.", magic:"The second half of the evidence, and the pair is perfect.", grammar:"Third dhāt in two consecutive surahs (85:1, 85:5, 85:15, 86:11, 86:12). Five instances — the construction is fully yours now.", why:"ṣadʿ over a growth-word — because it emphasizes the FORCE required: hard ground must be broken from below, which is exactly what resurrection is.", note:"ṣudāʿ = a HEADACHE (a \"splitting\" head). v.11 sky RETURNS (rain down) + v.12 earth SPLITS (life up) = the annual resurrection.", more:["ṣudāʿ = a HEADACHE (a \"splitting\" head!); and ṣadaʿa bi'l-ḥaqq = to declare the truth openly (to \"split\" the silence).","V.11: the SKY returns (rain comes down). v.12: the EARTH splits (life comes up). Together: the complete annual resurrection you watch every single year.","And you OWN this root already: - ʿAbasa 80:26 — thumma shaqaqna'l-arḍa shaqqā (\"then We SPLIT the earth, a splitting\") — same idea, different root (ش-ق-ق). - Inshiqāq 84:1 — idha's-samāʾu inshaqqat (the sky splits).","So across three surahs: the earth splits for SEEDS (80:26, 86:12), and the sky splits for the DAY (84:1). The same action, small scale and cosmic scale.","And ṣudāʿ = a HEADACHE is the hook you'll never lose — a \"splitting\" head.","Compare ʿAbasa 80:26 — the earth split for seeds."]},
  {ar:"لَقَوْلٌ فَصْلٌ", tr:"La-qawlun faṣl", v:"13", root:"The \"cutting apart / separating / deciding\" family — faṣala = to separate.", meaning:"\"Indeed, it is a DECISIVE word.\"", magic:"The word means \"a word that CUTS,\" and that's the claim.", grammar:"Inna + la- — fifth instance of the double-emphasis frame.", why:"Faṣl over \"true\" — because it claims a FUNCTION (it decides), not just a property.", note:"Faṣl = a school TERM, a CHAPTER; fāṣilah = a COMMA — and the name for a QURANIC VERSE-ENDING.", more:["Faṣl = a school TERM or a CHAPTER (a cut-off section); fāṣilah = a COMMA / a separator — and the technical term for a Quranic VERSE-ENDING (the very thing you've been studying!).","You own it from Nabaʾ 78:17 (inna yawma'l-faṣli kāna mīqātā).","Faṣl = separating, cutting cleanly between things. So qawlun faṣl = speech that DECIDES — that draws the line between truth and falsehood and leaves no blur.","And the cross-link is beautiful: Nabaʾ 78:17 called the Day itself yawm al-faṣl — \"the Day of SEPARATION.\" So: the DAY separates people (78:17), and the QUR'AN separates truth (86:13). Same root, same function, two objects. The Book does now what the Day will do then.","And the fāṣilah hook is one you'll appreciate: the technical Arabic name for a Quranic verse-ending is fāṣilah — \"a separator.\" The very feature you set out to master is named from this root.","Note faṣl is a MAṢDAR (verbal noun) used as an ADJECTIVE — which in Arabic is more emphatic than a normal adjective: not \"a decisive word\" but \"a word that IS decisiveness.\"","Nabaʾ 78:17: the DAY separates people.","Ṭāriq 86:13: the QUR'AN separates truth."]},
  {ar:"بِالْهَزْلِ", tr:"Bi'l-hazl", v:"14", root:"The \"joking / triviality / lack of seriousness\" family — hazala = to jest, to joke; hazl = jesting.", meaning:"\"And it is NOT a jest.\"", magic:"A perfect ṭibāq (antithesis) with v.13, and the pairing is razor-sharp:", grammar:"Mā huwa bi-… = the standard emphatic negation frame with the extra bāʾ. Recognize it instantly now.", why:"Hazl over \"false\" — because the danger isn't that people will call it a LIE; it's that they'll treat it LIGHTLY.", note:"Al-jidd wa'l-hazl = the serious and the jocular. v.13 DECISIVE ↔ v.14 NOT A JOKE.", more:["And it is the direct opposite of jidd (seriousness).","The classic Arabic pair: al-jidd wa'l-hazl = the serious and the jocular.","V.13: qawlun FAṢL — a word that CUTS/DECIDES. v.14: wa mā huwa bi'l-HAZL — and it is NOT a JOKE.","Decisive vs. trivial. The two verses define the Qur'an by stating what it is and what it isn't.","And hazl is a specific accusation being pre-empted. A \"joke\" is something you can enjoy and then set down. The verse forecloses the most common way people neutralize a serious message: treating it as entertainment.","Compare Muṭaffifīn 83:13 — qāla asāṭīru'l-awwalīn (\"he says: legends of the ancients!\"). Same dismissal-move: reduce it to a story, and you don't have to obey it. Ṭāriq answers it head-on.","And note the bāʾ zāʾidah again (bi'l-hazl after negation) — you own this from Takwīr 81:22, 24, 25 and Infiṭār 82:16. Sixth instance.","And compare Muṭaffifīn 83:13 — \"just old legends.\" Same dismissal, answered here."]},
  {ar:"يَكِيدُونَ كَيْدًا... وَأَكِيدُ كَيْدًا", tr:"Yakīdūna kaydan… wa akīdu kaydā", v:"15–16", root:"The \"plotting / scheming / contriving\" family — kāda = to plot, to devise a stratagem; kayd = a scheme, a plan devised against someone.", meaning:"V.15 — \"Indeed, they are plotting a plot.\" v.16 — \"And I am planning a plan.\"", magic:"One of the most quietly terrifying pairs of verses in the Qur'an — and the balance is exact.", grammar:"Kāda is a HOLLOW verb (weak middle ي): kāda → yakīdu → akīdu.", why:"Using THE SAME WORD for both plots — rather than a nobler word for His.", note:"Same verb, same noun, both verses. THEY plot… I plot. The Qur'an doesn't argue — it just puts the two side by side.", more:["Note: kayd isn't always negative in Arabic — it can mean skilful planning; in Sūrat Yūsuf, Allah's arranging is called kidnā li-Yūsuf.","Look at the perfect symmetry:","Identical verb. Identical cognate accusative. And the numbers are the whole point: THEY (many) vs. I (One).","The Qur'an doesn't say \"their plot will fail\" or \"I will punish them.\" It simply places the two plots side by side, in matching grammar, and lets you do the arithmetic. The confidence is in the SYMMETRY.","And note the shift to the FIRST PERSON SINGULAR — akīdu, \"I.\" Most of these surahs use \"We\" (naḥnu) — the plural of majesty. Here it's \"I.\" Intensely personal, and directly matched against their \"they.\"","The cognate accusative (kaydan) appears TWICE — you own this construction from Nāziʿāt 79:1–4 (gharqan, nashṭan, sabḥan, sabqan), ʿAbasa 80:25–26 (ṣabban, shaqqan), Inshiqāq 84:6 (kadḥan). Seventh and eighth instances.","Same hollow pattern as yaḥūr (84:14), dhāqa/yadhūqu (78:24), ẓanna… Watch how the weak middle letter behaves.","Is what makes the verse land: the matching makes the mismatch obvious."]},
  {ar:"فَمَهِّلِ... أَمْهِلْهُمْ رُوَيْدًا", tr:"Fa-mahhili… amhilhum ruwaydā", v:"17", root:"Mahhil / amhil — the \"granting respite / giving time / going gently\" family — mahala = to give time.", meaning:"\"So give the disbelievers respite — grant them respite, [just] a LITTLE.\"", magic:"The final verse, and it's a masterpiece of restraint.", grammar:"Ruwaydan is a DIMINUTIVE (اسم تصغير) — Arabic shrinks words to signal smallness, gentleness, or endearment (kutayyib = a booklet, bunayy = \"my dear little son\").", why:"Ending on a diminutive — the smallest possible word — after the biggest possible claim, is the ultimate expression of composure.", note:"Muhlah = a GRACE PERIOD / deadline extension.", more:["Muhlah = a grace period, an extension (modern Arabic: muhlah = a deadline extension!); tamahhal = take your time, slow down.","ruwaydan — a DIMINUTIVE — from rūd (gentleness/slowness): \"just a LITTLE while,\" \"gently, softly.\"","After \"I am planning a plan\" (v.16), you expect a threat. Instead: \"give them time.\"","And the command is given TWICE, with two different verb forms: mahhil (Form II) and amhil (Form IV) — both meaning \"grant respite,\" repeated for emphasis. The repetition is a calming gesture: let it go, let it go.","Then the last word: ruwaydan — a DIMINUTIVE. Arabic diminutives (taṣghīr) shrink a word to make it small, or gentle, or affectionate. So the surah's final word means \"a teeny little while.\"","Read the whole effect: \"They're plotting. I'm planning. Give them time — just a tiny bit.\" The confidence is TOTAL, and the tone is almost casual. You only speak this calmly when the outcome isn't in doubt.","And it's the perfect close for the surah that began with a ṭāriq — a night-knocker. Something is coming. It knocks. Wait a little.","Compare the endings you've collected: Takwīr 81:29 (Rabb al-ʿālamīn), Infiṭār 82:19 (lillāh), Inshiqāq 84:25 (ghayru mamnūn), Burūj 85:22 (lawḥin maḥfūẓ), Ṭāriq 86:17 (ruwaydan). Not one of them ends on a threat.","A rare and precious example in the Qur'an.","Note also the two imperative forms: mahhil (Form II) and amhil (Form IV) — both causative-ish, both \"give respite.\"","After \"I am planning a plan,\" the answer is: \"give them time — just a LITTLE.\" ruwaydan is a DIMINUTIVE — \"a teeny while.\" Total confidence, delivered in the smallest word available."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:87, name:"Al-Aʿlā", ar:"الأعلى", en:"The Most High", verses:19, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"This is the surah the Prophet ﷺ is reported to have loved reciting in Witr and in ʿĪd and Jumuʿah prayers. You have almost certainly heard it hundreds of times. The rhyme is already in your ear — now attach the meanings to it.",
  special:["Every single verse ends in the same long \"-ā\" sound — all nineteen of them.","Al-Aʿlā, sawwā, hadā, al-marʿā, aḥwā, tansā, yakhfā, al-yusrā, adh-dhikrā, yakhshā, al-ashqā, al-kubrā, yaḥyā, tazakkā, fa-ṣallā, ad-dunyā, abqā, al-ūlā, wa Mūsā.","Nineteen verses, one vowel. It's the most sustained single rhyme in this whole section of the Qurʾān. Say it aloud and it feels like a long exhale that never breaks — which is exactly right for a surah whose command is sabbiḥ (glorify).","And here's the gift for you specifically: almost every one of those endings is a word ending in alif maqṣūrah (ى) — the exact category of verse-ending noun/verb you said trips you up. This surah is nineteen of them in a row.","--"],
  big:["Glorify the Highest (v.1–5) — He created, proportioned, measured, guided, and grows the pasture… then turns it to dark debris.","A promise to the Prophet ﷺ (v.6–8) — We will make you recite; you will not forget. We will ease you toward ease.","So remind (v.9–13) — the one who is in awe will take heed; the wretched avoids it.","Who succeeds (v.14–15) — the one who purifies, remembers the Name, and prays.","The real problem (v.16–19) — but you prefer this world. And this was in the earliest scriptures too."],
  traps:["يَصْلَى (v.12, \"is roasted\") vs. صَلَّى (v.15, \"prayed\") — THREE VERSES APART, same three letters ص-ل-ي, different roots. Ṣaliya = burn; ṣallā = pray. The single sharpest trap in this surah.","يَذَّكَّرُ (v.10) — this is Form V with the ت assimilated (يَتَذَكَّر → يَذَّكَّر). Don't misread it as Form II. Same rule as ʿAbasa 80:4.","سَوَّى (v.2) — if you learn one root in this whole juzʾ, make it s-w-y. It appears in seven surahs.","أَفْلَحَ (v.14) — from fallāḥ, a FARMER (splitting soil). Not a generic \"win\" word — it carries cultivation and effort.","غُثَاءً أَحْوَى (v.5) — not just \"dry grass\": ghuthāʾ = the worthless scum a flood dumps on a bank. The worthlessness is the point.","الْيُسْرَى / الْكُبْرَى / الْأُولَى — all feminine superlatives (fuʿlā pattern). Recognize the shape; it recurs constantly in these surahs.","يَتَجَنَّبُهَا (v.11) — from janb, a SIDE. It means physically sidestepping, not intellectually rejecting.","نُقْرِئُكَ (v.6) — Form IV = \"make you recite,\" not \"you recite.\" The causative is the whole comfort of the verse.","الدِّينِ-style note: دُنْيَا comes from adnā (nearer/lower) — it's named for proximity, not quality.","Every verse ends in alif maqṣūrah (ى) — nineteen in a row. Use the rhyme as your recall hook: if a word doesn't end in \"-ā,\" it isn't a verse-ending here."],
  links:[{w:"خَلَقَ فَسَوَّى (v.2)",e:"خَلَقَ فَسَوَّى (Qiy. 75:38)",t:"Verbatim identical two words"},{w:"سَوَّى (v.2)",e:"75:4 · 75:38 · 79:28 · 82:7 · 91:7 · 91:14",t:"SEVEN appearances — the master root"},{w:"قَدَّرَ (v.3)",e:"قَادِرِين (75:4) · قَدَّرُوهَا (76:16) · فَقَدَّرَهُ (80:19)",t:"Same root — precise measuring"},{w:"الْمَرْعَى (v.4)",e:"مَرْعَاهَا (Nāz. 79:31)",t:"Identical word — the \"care-place\""},{w:"سَنُقْرِئُكَ فَلَا تَنسَى (v.6)",e:"لَا تُحَرِّكْ بِهِ لِسَانَكَ (Qiy. 75:16–19)",t:"Same promise — preservation is His"},{w:"نُيَسِّرُكَ لِلْيُسْرَى (v.8)",e:"يَسَّرَهُ (80:20) · يَسِيرًا (84:8)",t:"Three surahs — the way, the reckoning, and YOU"},{w:"فَذَكِّرْ / الذِّكْرَى (v.9)",e:"76:29 · 80:11 · 81:27 · 88:21",t:"Five surahs — always reminding"},{w:"يَذَّكَّرُ (v.10)",e:"يَذَّكَّرُ (ʿAbasa 80:4)",t:"Same Form V assimilation"},{w:"يَخْشَى (v.10)",e:"79:19 · 79:26 · 79:45",t:"Fourth appearance — awe is the filter"},{w:"الْأَشْقَى (v.11)",e:"أَشْقَاهَا (Shams 91:12)",t:"Same superlative — Shams gives it a face"},{w:"يَصْلَى (v.12)",e:"82:15 · 83:16 · 84:12 · 88:4",t:"Five surahs — ṣaliya, roasted"},{w:"قَدْ أَفْلَحَ مَن تَزَكَّى (v.14)",e:"قَدْ أَفْلَحَ مَن زَكَّاهَا (Shams 91:9)",t:"Near-identical verse"},{w:"تَزَكَّى (v.14)",e:"79:18 (Pharaoh) · 80:3, 7 (blind man)",t:"Four surahs, full moral range"},{w:"فَصَلَّى (v.15)",e:"وَلَا صَلَّى (Qiy. 75:31)",t:"Did both vs. did neither"},{w:"تُؤْثِرُونَ (v.16)",e:"وَآثَرَ الْحَيَاةَ الدُّنْيَا (Nāz. 79:38)",t:"Same verb + object — \"he\" → \"you\""},{w:"الْحَيَاةَ الدُّنْيَا (v.16)",e:"الْعَاجِلَة (75:20 · 76:27)",t:"Dunya's nicknames: the near one, the quick one"},{w:"الصُّحُفِ (v.18)",e:"صُحُفٍ (80:13) · الصُّحُفُ (81:10)",t:"Three archives, one word"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"سَبِّحِ", tr:"Sabbiḥ", v:"1", root:"The \"swimming / gliding swiftly\" family — sabaḥa = to swim.", meaning:"\"Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High.\"", magic:"Understanding the root changes what subḥānallāh means to you. It's not simply \"praise\" (ḥamd) — tasbīḥ is DECLARING HIM FAR ABOVE any defect.", grammar:"Form II imperative (sabbiḥ). Form II often = doing something intensively or causing it.", why:"Sabbiḥ over iḥmad (praise) — because the surah is about His height above everything, not about thanking Him for a favor.", note:"Sabaḥa = to SWIM (Nāziʿāt 79:3, as-sābiḥāt). Tasbīḥ = swimming AWAY from every flaw — declaring Him far above defect.", more:["You met this exact root in Nāziʿāt 79:3 (as-sābiḥāt — those who glide).","From that motion comes tasbīḥ: declaring Allah free of every flaw — the idea of moving swiftly away from any imperfection attributed to Him.","It's a word of distance and elevation, not just appreciation.","Which is why it's paired with al-Aʿlā (the Most High). Sabbiḥ = push away all flaws; al-Aʿlā = He is highest. The verb and the name are doing the same work.","And note it's the name that's glorified: sabbiḥ isma rabbik — \"glorify the NAME of your Lord.\" Some read this as: even His name is beyond flaw, so how much more His essence.","Note the kasrah on the ḥāʾ when joined to the next word (sabbiḥi'sma) — that's just to avoid two sukūns colliding.","You say it dozens of times a day."]},
  {ar:"فَسَوَّى", tr:"Fa-sawwā", v:"2", root:"The \"making even / proportioning / perfecting\" family.", meaning:"\"who created and proportioned.\"", magic:"THIS IS THE FIFTH APPEARANCE of sawwā in your collection — it is officially the most important cross-surah root you own.", grammar:"Form II past, weak-final. The فَـ shows immediate sequence: created → then straight away proportioned.", why:"Sawwā frames creation as precise craftsmanship, not just bringing-into-being.", note:"Khalaqa fa-sawwā here = the exact same two words as Qiyāmah 75:38. And this root appears in SEVEN surahs.", more:["Track it: - Qiyāmah 75:4 — He can re-proportion your fingertips - Qiyāmah 75:38 — He proportioned you from a clot - Nāziʿāt 79:28 — He proportioned the sky - Infiṭār 82:7 — He proportioned you - Aʿlā 87:2 — He created and proportioned (everything) - (and twice more in Shams 91:7 and 91:14 — coming up)","Seven appearances across seven surahs. If you learn one root perfectly, make it s-w-y.","Note the compression here: Qiyāmah 75:38 said \"khalaqa fa-sawwā\" — the exact same two words as here. Aʿlā 87:2 is a verbatim echo of Qiyāmah 75:38.","Same \"fa\" chain you saw in Infiṭār 82:7.","Fingertips, clot, sky, you, everything, the soul, a flattened town."]},
  {ar:"قَدَّرَ فَهَدَى", tr:"Qaddara fa-hadā", v:"3", root:"Qaddara — the \"precise measuring / decreeing\" family (qadar = measure/decree).", meaning:"\"and who measured out and then guided.\"", magic:"The pairing is a complete theology in three words.", grammar:"Both weak-final past verbs; الَّذِي (who) links them as descriptions of rabbik.", why:"Qaddara over \"created\" — because measuring implies a spec sheet, which implies a purpose.", note:"Qadar = the exact measurement. Hudā = the operating instructions. He specced you, then told you how to run.", more:["You own it from Qiyāmah 75:4 (qādirīn), Insān 76:16 (qaddarūhā), ʿAbasa 80:19 (fa-qaddarah).","hadā — the \"guiding\" family (hudā = guidance; hādī = a guide).","Qaddara = He gave every created thing its exact specification — its size, capacity, limits, purpose. Hadā = He then showed it how to operate within that specification. Measuring without guidance would be cruel (you'd have a design with no instructions); guidance without measuring would be useless (instructions for nothing in particular).","And it covers everything, not just humans. The bee is measured and guided. The seed is measured and guided. You are too.","Note the four fa-chains in v.2–5: khalaqa fa-sawwā, qaddara fa-hadā, akhraja… fa-jaʿalahu. Three matched pairs, each with an instant consequence. The rhythm is deliberate."]},
  {ar:"الْمَرْعَى", tr:"Al-marʿā", v:"4", root:"The \"shepherding / grazing / tending\" family — raʿā = to graze or to tend; rāʿī = a SHEPHERD; riʿāyah = care, guardianship.", meaning:"\"and who brings out the pasture.\"", magic:"You own this word exactly — it's identical to Nāziʿāt 79:31 (akhraja minhā māʾahā wa marʿāhā). Same word, same verb (akhraja), two surahs. Free vocabulary.", grammar:"Noun of place (mafʿal) from a weak-final root; the alif maqṣūrah carries the surah's rhyme.", why:"Marʿā over a plain word for plants — it names them by their function for living creatures.", note:"Rāʿī = shepherd; riʿāyah = CARE. The pasture is the \"care-place.\" Identical to Nāziʿāt 79:31.", more:["The famous hadith: \"each of you is a rāʿī and responsible for his flock.\"","And the root's double life matters: marʿā isn't just \"grass\" — because the root also means to take care of, the pasture is literally \"the care-place.\" Grazing land is named after guardianship.","Notice the ascent in v.1–4: al-Aʿlā (the Most High) → created → proportioned → measured → guided → grass. From the highest name to a field of green. The Qurʾān keeps doing this: cosmic scale, then something you can touch."]},
  {ar:"غُثَاءً أَحْوَى", tr:"Ghuthāʾan aḥwā", v:"5", root:"Ghuthāʾ — the scum, froth, and debris a flood carries — the dried, tangled rubbish left on a riverbank after the water drops.", meaning:"\"then makes it dark, withered debris.\"", magic:"The most sobering two words in the surah, and they arrive without warning.", grammar:"Ghuthāʾan is the second object of jaʿalahu (\"made it [into] debris\").", why:"Ghuthāʾ carries worthlessness and displacement — not just death, but being swept aside as rubbish.", note:"Ghuthāʾ = the dried scum a flood leaves on the bank. v.4: fresh green pasture. v.5: black worthless debris.", more:["aḥwā — the \"dark colour\" family — blackish-brown, the colour of dried, rotted vegetation.","(Interestingly aḥwā can also describe the deep green-black of very lush growth, which some read as describing the pasture before it dries.)","Look at the sequence: v.4 = He brings out the pasture (fresh, green, alive). v.5 = He turns it into dried black scum. One verse apart. No transition, no explanation. Growth, then rot.","And ghuthāʾ is precisely the right word — it's not \"dead grass,\" it's flood-debris: the stuff that gets swept along, tangled, and dumped worthless on a bank. Something that was alive, now just matter in the way.","Why put this in a surah about glorifying the Most High? Because it's the argument: the same hand that grows also withers. And v.16–17 will say the quiet part out loud: \"you prefer this life — but the Hereafter is better and more lasting.\" Al-marʿā is dunya. Ghuthāʾ aḥwā is what dunya becomes.","Aḥwā is an adjective on the afʿal colour-pattern (like aḥmar red, aswad black).","That's dunya, in two verses."]},
  {ar:"سَنُقْرِئُكَ فَلَا تَنسَى", tr:"Sa-nuqriʾuka fa-lā tansā", v:"6", root:"Nuqriʾu — Form IV of qaraʾa (to recite/read) = to make someone recite.", meaning:"\"We will make you recite, so you will not forget.\"", magic:"This is the same reassurance you already met in Qiyāmah 75:16–19 — where the Prophet ﷺ was told not to move his tongue rushing to catch the revelation, because \"upon Us is its collection and its recital.\"", grammar:"سَ + present tense = near future (\"We will soon…\"). Note tansā is weak-final, carrying the rhyme.", why:"Nuqriʾuka (Form IV) places all the effort on the divine side — which is exactly the comfort intended.", note:"Qurʾān = \"The Recitation.\" Same root. Qiyāmah 75:16–19 said \"don't rush.\" Aʿlā 87:6 says \"you won't forget.\" Same promise, twice.", more:["Same root as Qurʾān (the Recitation) and qirāʾah (recitation).","tansā — the \"forgetting\" family (nisyān = forgetfulness).","Qiyāmah said: don't rush, We'll gather it for you. Aʿlā says: We'll make you recite, and you won't forget. Two surahs, one promise: the preservation is Ours, not yours.","And nuqriʾuka is Form IV — CAUSATIVE. Not \"you will recite\" but \"We will MAKE you recite.\" The ability itself is being given, not just the content.","The فَـ = \"so / with the result that.\""]},
  {ar:"الْجَهْرَ وَمَا يَخْفَى", tr:"Al-jahra wa mā yakhfā", v:"7", root:"Jahr — the \"loud, open, out-in-the-open\" family — you know it from prayer: ṣalāh jahriyyah = the audible prayers (Fajr, Maghrib, ʿIshāʾ), where the imam recites aloud.", meaning:"\"Indeed, He knows what is open and what is hidden.\"", magic:"Two free hooks from things you already do. - Jahr — you distinguish jahrī from sirrī prayers every single day. Same word.", grammar:"Al-jahra is a noun; mā yakhfā is a relative clause (\"what is hidden\").", why:"Naming the audible first, then the hidden, moves from easy to hard — He knows both equally.", note:"Ṣalāh jahriyyah = the out-loud prayers. Ikhfāʾ = the hiding rule in tajwīd. Both from these two words.", more:["yakhfā — the \"hiding\" family (khafiyy = hidden; ikhfāʾ = concealment — also a tajwīd rule!).","- Ikhfāʾ — if you've studied tajwīd, you know ikhfāʾ is the rule where the nūn is hidden.","And it's a perfect ṭibāq (opposite-pair) covering everything: what's broadcast and what's buried. Placed right after the promise about memory (v.6), it quietly says: We know what you retain and what slips — nothing is unaccounted for.","Note the mismatch — Arabic happily pairs a noun with a clause."]},
  {ar:"نُيَسِّرُكَ لِلْيُسْرَى", tr:"Nuyassiruka li'l-yusrā", v:"8", root:"The \"ease / smoothness\" family — yusr = ease (the opposite of ʿusr, hardship).", meaning:"\"And We will ease you toward the easiest way.\"", magic:"The phrasing is unusual and beautiful — notice it's the PERSON being eased, not the path.", grammar:"Form II (yassara = to make easy).", why:"Nuyassiruka frames divine help as changing the person, not just the circumstances.", note:"Not \"the path is eased\" — \"YOU are eased.\" Yusr vs ʿusr, from \"inna maʿa'l-ʿusri yusrā.\" Three surahs: 80:20, 84:8, 87:8.", more:["You know the verse: inna maʿa'l-ʿusri yusrā (\"with hardship comes ease\").","It doesn't say \"We will make the way easy for you.\" It says \"We will ease YOU toward ease.\" The change happens in you. You get made into someone for whom this is easy.","And the root repeats within the verse (nu-yassir-uka li'l-yusrā) — verb and object from the same root, which is Arabic's way of hammering an idea.","Cross-surah gold — you own this root three times over: - ʿAbasa 80:20 — thumma's-sabīla yassarah (\"then the way — He made it easy\") - Inshiqāq 84:8 — ḥisāban yasīrā (\"an easy reckoning\") - Aʿlā 87:8 — nuyassiruka li'l-yusrā","Three surahs: the way is eased, the reckoning is easy, and YOU are eased.","Note al-yusrā is a superlative feminine (fuʿlā pattern, like al-kubrā, al-ūlā) — \"the easiest [way/state].\""]},
  {ar:"فَذَكِّرْ... الذِّكْرَى", tr:"Fa-dhakkir… adh-dhikrā", v:"9", root:"The \"remembering / mentioning\" family — you own it from Insān 76:1 (madhkūran), Insān 76:29 (tadhkirah), ʿAbasa 80:11 (tadhkirah), Takwīr 81:27 (dhikr).", meaning:"\"So remind, if the reminder benefits.\"", magic:"The surah's job description for the Prophet ﷺ — and it's the same one every time.", grammar:"Form II imperative (dhakkir = cause to remember). Note the root repeats in verb and object (dhakkir… dhikrā).", why:"Dhakkir (remind) rather than ʿallim (teach) — because the truth is already in them; it needs waking, not installing.", note:"FIVE surahs, same root: 76:29, 80:11, 81:27, 87:9, 88:21. The job is always reminding.", more:["Track this across five surahs now: - Insān 76:29 — \"this is a tadhkirah\" - ʿAbasa 80:11 — \"indeed it is a tadhkirah\" - Takwīr 81:27 — \"it is only a dhikr for all the worlds\" - Ghāshiyah 88:21 — \"so remind — you are only a reminder\" (coming next) - Aʿlā 87:9 — \"so remind, if the reminder benefits\"","Five surahs, one root, one message: reminding, never forcing.","And \"in nafaʿati'dh-dhikrā\" (\"if the reminder benefits\") is subtle — it isn't permission to give up on people. Most read it as: remind regardless, since reminding is what benefits — or as guidance on wisdom in timing, not on abandoning anyone. The very next verse (v.10) shows the reminder does land — on whoever has awe."]},
  {ar:"سَيَذَّكَّرُ مَن يَخْشَى", tr:"Sa-yadhdhakkaru man yakhshā", v:"10", root:"Yadhdhakkaru — same reminding root, Form V.", meaning:"\"The one who is in awe will take heed.\"", magic:"Two things you already own, side by side.", grammar:"Form V assimilation: when Form V's ت meets ذ، ز، ص، ض، ط، ظ، د، ث, it commonly merges and doubles.", why:"Khashyah over khawf — reverence from knowledge, which is why it reliably produces heed.", note:"يَتَذَكَّر → يَذَّكَّر (the ت melts). And khashyah is now in FOUR verses: Nāziʿāt 79:19, 26, 45 + here. Awe is the filter.", more:["yakhshā — the \"awe-filled fear\" family — fear that comes from knowing something's greatness (compare khawf, plain fear).","- Yadhdhakkaru is the same assimilated Form V you learned in ʿAbasa 80:4 — originally يَتَذَكَّر, with the ت melting into the ذ and doubling it.","Recognize it or you'll misparse it.","- Yakhshā completes the khashyah thread from Nāziʿāt — v.19 (Pharaoh could have had it), v.26 (the lesson is for whoever has it), v.45 (you warn whoever has it).","Now a fourth: whoever has it will take heed.","And note the certainty: sa-yadhdhakkaru — \"he WILL take heed.\" Not \"might.\" Awe is presented as a reliable filter: put the reminder in front of someone who has it, and it lands.","(يتذكر→يذّكر، يتزكى→يزّكى.)"]},
  {ar:"وَيَتَجَنَّبُهَا الْأَشْقَى", tr:"Wa yatajannabuhā'l-ashqā", v:"11", root:"Yatajannabu — the \"side\" family — janb = a SIDE/flank; tajannaba = to step to the side of something, to give it a wide berth.", meaning:"\"But the most wretched will avoid it.\"", magic:"Tajannaba is a beautifully physical word. It literally means to turn your side to something and go around it — like crossing the street to avoid someone.", grammar:"Form V (tafaʿʿala) here = doing something for/to yourself — putting yourself to the side of it.", why:"Tajannaba makes avoidance bodily and deliberate, not intellectual.", note:"Janb = a SIDE. Tajannaba = to turn your side and walk around it — like crossing the road to dodge someone.", more:["ashqā — the \"wretchedness / misery\" family (shaqiyy = wretched; shaqāwah = misery — the opposite of saʿādah, happiness).","Not \"rejects it\" (an argument) but \"SIDESTEPS it\" (a body movement).","He doesn't debate the reminder; he swerves.","And the contrast with v.10 is exact:","Same reminder, two body-languages.","Cross-surah: al-ashqā returns in Shams 91:12 — idhi'nbaʿatha ashqāhā (\"when the most wretched of them was sent forth\") — describing the man who hamstrung the she-camel of Thamūd. Same superlative, and Shams gives it a face.","Ashqā is a superlative (afʿal) carrying the rhyme.","And al-ashqā gets a face in Shams 91:12."]},
  {ar:"لَا يَمُوتُ فِيهَا وَلَا يَحْيَى", tr:"Lā yamūtu fīhā wa lā yaḥyā", v:"13", root:"Yamūtu — the \"death\" family (mawt). yaḥyā — the \"life\" family (ḥayāh; al-Ḥayy = the Ever-Living).", meaning:"\"Then he will neither die therein nor live.\"", magic:"One of the most chilling lines in the Qurʾān, and it works by removing an option you didn't know you were counting on.", grammar:"Two negated present-tense verbs; yaḥyā is weak-final (ح-ي-ي), carrying the rhyme.", why:"Naming both poles and denying both is stronger than any description of pain.", note:"\"Neither dies nor lives.\" Both exits sealed. Compare Qiyāmah 75:40, where giving life was the mercy.", more:["A perfect ṭibāq (opposite-pair) — but BOTH sides are negated. Normally life and death are the only two possibilities; here both are denied at once, leaving a state with no name. You can't even use death as an escape.","And notice the contrast with Qiyāmah 75:40: \"Is not that [Creator] able to give life to the dead?\" — where yuḥyī was the great mercy. Here, not-living is the punishment. Same root, opposite function.","Compare also Nabaʾ 78:30 — \"We will never increase you except in punishment\" — same technique: closing the last exit."]},
  {ar:"قَدْ أَفْلَحَ", tr:"Qad aflaḥa", v:"14", root:"A farming root! falaḥa = to split/plough the earth; fallāḥ = a FARMER (still the everyday word in Arabic).", meaning:"\"He has certainly succeeded who purifies himself.\"", magic:"The etymology is a whole lesson. Arabic's word for success comes from splitting hard ground open to plant something.", grammar:"قَدْ + past tense = certainty/completion (\"has certainly succeeded\").", why:"Falāḥ over a generic word for winning — it builds effort and cultivation into the concept.", note:"Fallāḥ = A FARMER (from splitting soil). Ḥayya ʿala'l-falāḥ — you hear it in every adhān. Success = the farmer's kind.", more:["From ploughing comes falāḥ = success — because the farmer who breaks open the soil is the one who gets the harvest.","Success isn't luck or arrival — it's the farmer's kind: break the surface, put something in, wait.","And you say this root every day: ḥayya ʿala'l-falāḥ — \"come to success\" — in every adhān. The call to prayer is literally calling you to \"the harvest.\"","Cross-surah: qad aflaḥa man tazakkā here ↔ Shams 91:9 — qad aflaḥa man zakkāhā (\"he has succeeded who purifies it\"). Nearly identical verses, four surahs apart. Free vocabulary again.","Compare لَقَدْ in Takwīr 81:23 — same emphasis, with an added lām.","And Shams 91:9 says it almost identically."]},
  {ar:"تَزَكَّى", tr:"Tazakkā", v:"14", root:"The \"purify + grow\" family — zakāh!", meaning:"\"who purifies himself.\"", magic:"The fourth surah in a row for this root — and it has a remarkable range.", grammar:"Form V, weak-final, carrying the rhyme.", why:"Tazakkā over \"was good\" — it makes success an active, self-directed cultivation.", note:"Zakāh = purify AND grow. Four surahs: offered to Pharaoh (79:18), sought by the blind man (80:3), defines success (87:14, 91:9).", more:["Key insight: this root means cleansing AND increase at once — removing what's rotten is what lets a thing grow.","- Nāziʿāt 79:18 — the gentle offer made to Pharaoh: \"would you like to purify yourself?\" - ʿAbasa 80:3, 7 — the blind man who came seeking to purify himself - Aʿlā 87:14 — \"he has succeeded who purifies himself\" - Shams 91:9 — \"he has succeeded who purifies it [the soul]\" (coming up)","From the worst tyrant to the sincerest seeker to the definition of success — one root.","And note the FORM V reflexive: tazakkā = purify YOURSELF. Nobody does it for you. Compare Shams 91:9 (zakkāhā, Form II — \"purified it\") — slightly different grammar, same demand.","Beautiful placement: falāḥ (v.14) is a farming word and zakāh is a growth word. Two agricultural roots in one verse: plough the ground, and the thing grows."]},
  {ar:"وَذَكَرَ اسْمَ رَبِّهِ فَصَلَّى", tr:"Wa dhakara'sma rabbihi fa-ṣallā", v:"15", root:"Dhakara — the remembering/mentioning root again. ṣallā — the \"prayer\" family (ṣalāh).", meaning:"\"and mentions the name of his Lord and prays.\"", magic:"Look at the three-step recipe for success in v.14–15: purify yourself → remember His name → pray. Inner, verbal, physical. Nothing missing.", grammar:"TRAP: صَلَّى (ṣallā, prayed) vs. يَصْلَى (yaṣlā, is roasted) in v.12.", why:"Ending on ṣallā grounds all the abstraction (purify, remember) in one concrete action you can do right now.", note:"Purify → remember the Name → pray. v.1 commanded \"glorify the NAME\"; v.15 shows the man who did.", more:["Note: different root from ṣaliya (to be roasted) in v.12 — the surah uses both, three verses apart!","And notice the inclusio (ring) with verse 1. The surah opened: \"Glorify the NAME of your Lord.\" It now closes its instruction: \"and he mentioned the NAME of his Lord and prayed.\" Verse 1 gives the command; verse 15 shows the man who obeyed it.","The exact same pair appears in Qiyāmah 75:31 — fa-lā ṣaddaqa wa lā ṣallā (\"he neither affirmed the truth nor prayed\"). Qiyāmah shows the man who did neither; Aʿlā shows the man who did both.","Same three letters ص-ل-ي, but different roots and meanings.","And watch: yaṣlā (v.12, roasted) vs ṣallā (v.15, prayed) — three verses apart."]},
  {ar:"بَلْ تُؤْثِرُونَ الْحَيَاةَ الدُّنْيَا", tr:"Bal tuʾthirūna'l-ḥayāta'd-dunyā", v:"16", root:"Tuʾthirūna — the \"trace / footprint\" family — athar = a mark left behind (āthār = archaeological remains).", meaning:"\"But you prefer the life of this world.\"", magic:"You own this exact move from Nāziʿāt 79:38 — wa āthara'l-ḥayāta'd-dunyā (\"and preferred the life of this world\"). Same verb, same object, two surahs.", grammar:"Form IV present, plural.", why:"Tuʾthirūna frames it as a deliberate ranking, not ignorance. You knew. You chose.", note:"Athar = a footprint; īthār = putting OTHERS first.", more:["From it, āthara = to prefer, and īthār = the virtue of preferring OTHERS over yourself (selflessness).","dunyā — from danā = to be near/low — \"the nearer life.\"","Only the person changes: Nāziʿāt said \"he\"; Aʿlā says \"YOU.\"","And the root's noble meaning makes it cut: īthār — putting others first — is one of the highest virtues in Arabic. Here the same machinery of preference is aimed at the dunya. You have the capacity for īthār. You pointed it at the wrong thing.","Bonus: dunyā means \"the NEARER one\" (from adnā, nearer/lower) — it's named for being close, not for being good. Compare Qiyāmah 75:20 and Insān 76:27, which call it al-ʿājilah — \"the hurrying one.\" Three different nicknames, all critical: the near one, the quick one, the debris (v.5).","Note the sudden shift to \"you\" — after fifteen verses of \"he/they,\" the surah turns and points.","Nāziʿāt 79:38: \"HE preferred.\" Aʿlā 87:16: \"YOU prefer.\" And dunyā = \"the NEARER one.\""]},
  {ar:"خَيْرٌ وَأَبْقَى", tr:"Khayrun wa abqā", v:"17", root:"Abqā — the \"remaining / lasting\" family — baqiya = to remain; bāqī = remaining; al-Bāqī = the Everlasting.", meaning:"\"while the Hereafter is better and more lasting.\"", magic:"The two-word answer to v.16, and each word does separate work.", grammar:"Both are comparatives (afʿal pattern); abqā is weak-final, carrying the rhyme.", why:"Naming duration explicitly is the killer argument, because nobody disputes that dunya ends.", note:"Bāqī = remaining (al-Bāqī, bāqiyāt ṣāliḥāt). \"Better AND longer-lasting\" — closes both excuses at once.", more:["Bāqiyāt ṣāliḥāt = \"the enduring good deeds.\"","Khayr (better) answers quality. Abqā (more lasting) answers duration. Together they close both possible defences of choosing dunya: \"but it's good\" — no, that's better; \"but it's here now\" — yes, and it ends.","And abqā is a comparative built from the root of bāqī — set that against v.5's ghuthāʾan aḥwā (dark withered debris). The surah has already SHOWN you what dunya becomes; now it names the alternative \"more-lasting.\" v.5 is the evidence; v.17 is the verdict.","Compare Nāziʿāt 79:46 — your whole life was \"an evening or its morning.\" Same argument, different image.","v.5 already showed you the debris."]},
  {ar:"الصُّحُفِ الْأُولَى", tr:"Aṣ-ṣuḥufi'l-ūlā", v:"18", root:"ṣuḥuf — scrolls/pages (ṣaḥīfah; modern ṣaḥāfah = journalism, ṣaḥīfah = a newspaper).", meaning:"\"Indeed, this is in the earliest scrolls — the scrolls of Ibrāhīm and Mūsā.\"", magic:"The most surprising ending in the surah, and it's an argument about age.", grammar:"Note v.19 (ṣuḥufi Ibrāhīma wa Mūsā) is a badal — an appositive explaining v.18.", why:"Al-ūlā (the earliest) is chosen precisely to answer the charge of novelty.", note:"ṣaḥīfah = a newspaper page.", more:["You own it from ʿAbasa 80:13 and Takwīr 81:10. al-ūlā — \"the first / the earliest\" (feminine superlative of awwal).","Quraysh's standard objection was that this was something new — an innovation, a break from the ancestors. The surah's closing move: \"this isn't new. It was in the oldest scriptures there are.\"","And notice WHICH message is being called ancient. Not a ritual, not a law — the message of v.14–17: purify yourself, remember His name, pray, and don't prefer the fleeting over the lasting. That's what Ibrāhīm and Mūsā were given too.","Cross-surah: ṣuḥuf has now appeared three ways: ʿAbasa 80:13 (honored scrolls of revelation), Takwīr 81:10 (the scrolls of your deeds, spread open), Aʿlā 87:18 (the earliest scrolls of previous prophets). Same word, three archives.","Arabic often states something, then immediately specifies it.","(Same technique as Takwīr 81:15–16.)","Three archives, one word: revelation's scrolls (80:13), YOUR scrolls (81:10), the ancient scrolls (87:18). And the surah ends by saying: \"this message is the OLDEST thing there is.\""]}
  ]
},
{
  num:88, name:"Al-Ghāshiyah", ar:"الغاشية", en:"The Overwhelming", verses:26, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"This surah does not condemn laziness. It condemns misdirected effort. Everyone is tired at the end. The question is what you were tired FOR.",
  special:["It's built as two perfectly mirrored panels — and the mirroring is the argument.","Both groups WORKED. That's the sting. Panel A's people are ʿāmilah (working) and nāṣibah (worn out) — they weren't lazy. They toiled. Panel B's people are rāḍiyah li-saʿyihā — pleased with their striving.","The difference isn't effort. It's direction. One group exhausted itself and got thorns; the other strove and got a garden.","--"],
  big:["The Enveloper (v.1) — has the report of the Overwhelming reached you?","Panel A — the exhausted (v.2–7) — worked hard, got a boiling spring and thorns.","Panel B — the blissful (v.8–16) — pleased with their striving; the furniture of Paradise, listed piece by piece.","Look at four things (v.17–20) — the camel, the sky, the mountains, the earth. The most grounded evidence in the Qurʾān.","Your job (v.21–22) — remind. You are not a controller over them.","The return (v.23–26) — to Us is their return; upon Us is their reckoning."],
  traps:["آنِيَةٍ (v.5) vs. آنِيَة (Insān 76:15) — IDENTICAL SPELLING, completely different words. Āniyah here = a spring at BOILING POINT (root: reaching peak). Āniyah there = VESSELS of silver (root: a container). The single best trap in this surah.","عَامِلَةٌ نَّاصِبَةٌ (v.3) — these people WORKED HARD. The surah condemns misdirected effort, not laziness. Compare v.9: \"pleased with its striving.\"","نَّاصِبَةٌ (v.3) and نُصِبَتْ (v.19) — same root, sixteen verses apart: human toil that exhausts vs. divine setting-up that stands forever.","تَصْلَى (v.4) — from ṣaliya (to be roasted). NOT the root of ṣalāh (prayer). Now in five consecutive surahs.","مُصَيْطِرٍ (v.22) — written with ص in the muṣḥaf, with a known variant in س. Don't be thrown by the spelling. Meaning: a controller/enforcer.","بِمُصَيْطِرٍ (v.22) — the بـ is EXTRA (emphasis after negation), not \"with.\" Same as Takwīr 81:22, 24, 25 and Infiṭār 82:16.","V.13–16 are all PASSIVE participles (marfūʿah, mawḍūʿah, maṣfūfah, mabthūthah) — the room was prepared before you arrived. Learn the mafʿūlah shape once.","V.17–20 are all PASSIVE verbs (khuliqat, rufiʿat, nuṣibat, suṭiḥat) — the doer is never named. That's the argument.","ضَرِيعٍ (v.6) — not generic \"thorns\": the specific plant a starving camel refuses. And its root gives taḍarruʿ — humble supplication.","زَرَابِيُّ (v.16) — a diptote: takes ḍammah without tanwīn. Same class as ḥadāʾiq (Nabaʾ 78:32)."],
  links:[{w:"الْغَاشِيَةِ (v.1)",e:"يَغْشَاهَا (Shams 91:4)",t:"Same root — night covers the sun / the Day covers you"},{w:"الْغَاشِيَةِ (v.1)",e:"النَّبَإ (78:2) · الطَّامَّةُ (79:34) · الصَّاخَّةُ (80:33)",t:"Four names for the Day"},{w:"خَاشِعَةٌ (v.2)",e:"خَاشِعَةٌ (Nāz. 79:9)",t:"Identical word"},{w:"تَصْلَى (v.4)",e:"82:15 · 83:16 · 84:12 · 87:12",t:"FIVE consecutive surahs — ṣaliya"},{w:"حَامِيَةً (v.4)",e:"حَمِيمًا (Nabaʾ 78:25)",t:"Same root — intense heat (and ḥamīm = a close friend!)"},{w:"آنِيَةٍ (v.5)",e:"آنِيَة (Ins. 76:15)",t:"IDENTICAL SPELLING, opposite meanings — boiling vs. vessels"},{w:"يُغْنِي (v.7)",e:"اسْتَغْنَىٰ (80:5) · يُغْنِيهِ (80:37)",t:"\"I need nothing\" → \"it supplies nothing\""},{w:"نَّاعِمَةٌ (v.8)",e:"نَعِيمًا (76:20) · نَعِيمٍ (82:13)",t:"Same root — bliss and softness"},{w:"سَعْيِهَا (v.9)",e:"76:22 · 79:22 · 79:35 · 80:8",t:"The Ṣafā–Marwah root, five verses"},{w:"لَاغِيَةً (v.11)",e:"لَغْوًا (Nabaʾ 78:35)",t:"Same root — no small talk in Paradise"},{w:"مَّرْفُوعَةٌ (v.13)",e:"مَّرْفُوعَةٍ (ʿAbasa 80:14)",t:"Identical word"},{w:"سُرُرٌ (v.13)",e:"الْأَرَائِكِ (76:13 · 83:23, 35)",t:"Three surahs of Paradise couches"},{w:"أَكْوَابٌ (v.14)",e:"أَكْوَابٌ (Ins. 76:15)",t:"Identical word — handle-less cups"},{w:"مَصْفُوفَةٌ (v.15)",e:"صَفًّا (78:38) · صَفًّا صَفًّا (89:22)",t:"Angels in ranks / cushions in rows"},{w:"مَبْثُوثَةٌ (v.16)",e:"مَّنثُورًا (Ins. 76:19)",t:"Same feeling — generous scattering"},{w:"نُصِبَتْ (v.19)",e:"نَّاصِبَةٌ (v.3, same surah)",t:"Same root — human toil vs. divine setting-up"},{w:"الْجِبَالِ (v.19)",e:"أَوْتَادًا (78:7) · أَرْسَاهَا (79:32)",t:"Pegs, anchors, uprights"},{w:"أَفَلَا يَنظُرُونَ (v.17)",e:"فَلْيَنظُرِ الْإِنسَانُ (80:24 · 86:5)",t:"Look at your food / look at the camel"},{w:"فَذَكِّرْ / مُذَكِّرٌ (v.21)",e:"76:29 · 80:11 · 81:27 · 87:9",t:"Five surahs — the job is reminding"},{w:"إِنَّمَا أَنتَ (v.21)",e:"إِنَّمَا أَنتَ مُنذِرُ (Nāz. 79:45)",t:"Same restriction structure"},{w:"تَوَلَّىٰ (v.23)",e:"تَوَلَّى (75:32) · عَبَسَ وَتَوَلَّىٰ (80:1)",t:"Three surahs"},{w:"إِيَابَهُمْ (v.25)",e:"مَآبًا (Nabaʾ 78:22, 39)",t:"Same root — two maʾābs → one iyāb"},{w:"حِسَابَهُم (v.26)",e:"75:3 · 78:27, 36 · 84:8",t:"Five surahs — the reckoning"},{w:"إِنَّ إِلَيْنَا (v.25)",e:"إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ (75:12, 30 · 79:44)",t:"Everything terminates at Him"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"الْغَاشِيَةِ", tr:"Al-ghāshiyah", v:"1", root:"The \"covering over / enveloping / overwhelming\" family — ghashiya = to cover something completely, to come over it.", meaning:"\"Has there come to you the report of the Overwhelming / the Enveloper?\"", magic:"Another name for the Day, and each surah picks a different sense.", grammar:"Active participle used as a name, feminine (the described noun — as-sāʿah, the Hour — is deleted, standard Qurʾānic practice).", why:"Ghāshiyah implies being covered from above with no gap — you don't dodge something that envelops.", note:"Ghashiya = to COVER completely. Shams 91:4 uses it for night covering the sun.", more:["Related: ghishāwah = a covering over the eyes; ghāshiyah also meant the cover thrown over a saddle.","Track them: - Nabaʾ 78:2 — an-Nabaʾ, the News (what you hear about) - Nāziʿāt 79:34 — aṭ-Ṭāmmah, the Overwhelming Flood (what submerges) - ʿAbasa 80:33 — aṣ-Ṣākhkhah, the Deafener (what destroys hearing) - Ghāshiyah 88:1 — al-Ghāshiyah, the Enveloper (what covers you over)","Four surahs, four names, four ways of experiencing the same Day.","And ghashiya has a stunning internal echo coming up: in Shams 91:4 — wa'l-layli idhā yaghshāhā (\"by the night when it covers it\"). Same root: night covering the sun, and the Day covering you. What night does gently every evening, this Day does permanently.","Four names for the Day: the News, the Flood, the Deafener, the Enveloper."]},
  {ar:"خَاشِعَةٌ", tr:"Khāshiʿah", v:"2", root:"The \"humbled, lowered, submissive\" family — khushūʿ, the humility you're meant to have in ṣalāh.", meaning:"\"Faces, that Day, will be humbled / downcast.\"", magic:"You own this word exactly — it's identical to Nāziʿāt 79:9 (abṣāruhā khāshiʿah, \"their eyes downcast\"). Same word, two surahs. Free.", grammar:"Active participle, feminine (agreeing with wujūh).", why:"Khāshiʿah over \"sad\" — because it names a submission posture, not just an emotion.", note:"Khushūʿ = what you're supposed to have in prayer. Identical to Nāziʿāt 79:9. On that Day everyone has it — too late to count.", more:["And the bitter irony is worth sitting with: khushūʿ is what you're asked to bring to prayer voluntarily. On that Day, everyone has it — involuntarily, and too late. The posture you could have chosen becomes the posture you're given.","Note it describes wujūh (faces) — the surah is going to spend eleven verses on faces, because a face is where an inner state becomes visible.","Note wujūhun is INDEFINITE — \"some faces\" — which is what makes room for the other kind in v.8."]},
  {ar:"عَامِلَةٌ نَّاصِبَةٌ", tr:"ʿāmilatun nāṣibah", v:"3", root:"ʿāmilah — the \"working / doing\" family (ʿamal = work, deed; ʿāmil = a worker).", meaning:"\"working, exhausted.\"", magic:"THE most important two words in the surah, and most readers skip past them.", grammar:"Two participles in a row, both feminine, both describing wujūh.", why:"Nāṣibah over \"tired\" — naṣab is the specific word for the weariness that comes FROM labour.", note:"They WORKED. They're EXHAUSTED. And they got nothing. The surah condemns misdirected effort, not laziness.", more:["nāṣibah — the \"toil to the point of exhaustion\" family — naṣab = weariness, hardship; also naṣaba = to set something upright (hence manṣib = a post/position, and nuṣibat in v.19 — \"the mountains, how they were set up\"!).","These people are not condemned for being lazy. They are described as ʿāmilah (working) and nāṣibah (worn out from it). They put in the hours. They're tired. And it earned them nothing.","Two classical readings, both devastating: - (a) They toiled in dunya — striving hard at the wrong things, or doing \"good\" deeds with no faith behind them. Effort without direction. - (b) They toil on that Day — dragging chains, climbing, laboring endlessly. Effort that never ends and never pays.","Either way the lesson is identical: being busy is not the same as being on the right road.","And the contrast in Panel B is exact: v.9 says they are rāḍiyah li-saʿyihā — \"pleased with its striving.\" Both groups strove. One is exhausted; one is pleased.","Note there's no \"and\" between them (asyndeton) — which makes them land as one compound state: working-exhausted.","Compare v.9: \"pleased with its striving.\" (And note naṣaba also = to set upright — it reappears in v.19!)"]},
  {ar:"تَصْلَى نَارًا حَامِيَةً", tr:"Taṣlā nāran ḥāmiyah", v:"4", root:"Taṣlā — the \"being roasted / scorched\" family (ṣaliya) — not the root of ṣalāh.", meaning:"\"Roasting in a scorching Fire.\"", magic:"The ṣaliya root has now appeared in FIVE consecutive surahs — this is one of the best runs available to you: - Infiṭār 82:15 — yaṣlawnahā (they burn in it) - Muṭaffifīn 83:16.", grammar:"Taṣlā is weak-final, feminine (the subject is wujūh); nāran is its object; ḥāmiyah an adjective.", why:"ḥāmiyah (actively scorching) rather than just \"hot\" — the fire is doing something.", note:"Ṣaliya = roasted IN it — FIVE consecutive surahs: 82:15, 83:16, 84:12, 87:12, 88:4. Not the ṣalāh root.", more:["ḥāmiyah — the \"intense heat\" family — the same root as ḥamīm (scalding water) in Nabaʾ 78:25; also ḥummā = a fever.","La-ṣālū'l-jaḥīm (surely burning in the Blaze) - Inshiqāq 84:12 — wa yaṣlā saʿīrā (and he will burn in a Blaze) - Aʿlā 87:12 — alladhī yaṣlā'n-nāra'l-kubrā (who burns in the greatest Fire) - Ghāshiyah 88:4 — taṣlā nāran ḥāmiyah","Five surahs, one verb. Learn it once, collect five.","And ḥāmiyah ties to Nabaʾ 78:25's ḥamīm — remember the strange double life of that root: it also gives ḥamīm = a close, intimate friend. Warmth at both extremes.","Note the rhyme: ḥāmiyah, āniyah, nāʿimah, rāḍiyah, ʿāliyah, lāghiyah, jāriyah — the whole surah rides the \"-iyah\" ending.","And ḥāmiyah shares a root with ḥamīm (Nabaʾ 78:25)."]},
  {ar:"عَيْنٍ آنِيَةٍ", tr:"ʿaynin āniyah", v:"5", root:"The \"reaching its full time / reaching peak intensity\" family — anā = for something to reach its moment.", meaning:"\"Given to drink from a spring at boiling point.\"", magic:"A GENUINE TRAP — and one of the best in the whole juzʾ.", grammar:"Tusqā is passive (\"they are given to drink\") — no choice in the matter.", why:"āniyah over \"hot\" — it means at the absolute peak, not merely warm.", note:"آنِيَة (76:15) = SILVER VESSELS in Paradise. آنِيَة (88:5) = BOILING SPRING in Hell. Same spelling, opposite worlds.", more:["ānin = boiling at maximum — water that has reached the absolute peak of heat.","Related: al-ān = now, this very moment.","آنِيَة here (āniyah, \"boiling\") is NOT the same word as آنِيَة in Insān 76:15 (āniyah, \"vessels\")! Identical spelling. Completely different roots and meanings. - Insān 76:15 — bi-āniyatin min fiḍḍah = \"with VESSELS of silver\" (root: inā', a container) - Ghāshiyah 88:5 — ʿaynin āniyah = \"a spring at BOILING POINT\" (root: anā, to reach peak)","Same letters, one is a silver cup in Paradise, one is scalding water in Hell. Flag this one hard.","And the irony of the word: ānin comes from \"reaching its time\" — the water has reached its moment of maximum heat, exactly when they reach theirs.","Āniyah is an active participle, feminine.","Al-ān = now — same root: \"reached its moment.\""]},
  {ar:"ضَرِيعٍ", tr:"ḍarīʿ", v:"6", root:"ḍarīʿ is a specific desert plant — a dry, thorny bramble (often identified with shibriq when dried) that camels won't eat, and which provides no nourishment at all.", meaning:"\"They will have no food except from a bitter thorn-bush.\"", magic:"The plant is chosen because of what it fails to do.", grammar:"Genitive after min; note the exception structure: laysa lahum ṭaʿāmun illā min ḍarīʿ.", why:"A real, recognizable, useless plant — far more visceral than an abstract description.", note:"Ḍarīʿ = the thorn even a starving CAMEL refuses. And its root gives taḍarruʿ — humble begging.", more:["The root ḍ-r-ʿ also carries lowliness, abasement (taḍarruʿ = humble supplication — the word for begging Allah humbly!).","Every desert Arab knew ḍarīʿ: even a starving camel refuses it. So the \"food\" of Hell is the one thing that the least fussy animal on earth will not eat.","And the root connection is haunting: ḍ-r-ʿ also gives taḍarruʿ — humbling yourself before Allah in supplication. The root of humble begging names the food of those who refused to humble themselves. They wouldn't do taḍarruʿ; now they eat ḍarīʿ.","\"no food except.\" Same cruel technique as Nabaʾ 78:25 (illā ḥamīman wa ghassāqā): the \"except\" raises hope for one syllable, then destroys it.","They wouldn't beg humbly; now they eat the thorn."]},
  {ar:"لَّا يُسْمِنُ وَلَا يُغْنِي مِن جُوعٍ", tr:"Lā yusminu wa lā yughnī min jūʿ", v:"7", root:"Yusminu — the \"fattening\" family — samīn = fat; samn = clarified butter (ghee)! Form IV = to make fat/nourish.", meaning:"\"It neither nourishes nor avails against hunger.\"", magic:"The verse defines food by the two things food does — and denies both.", grammar:"يُغْنِي مِنْ = \"to avail/suffice against\" — note the preposition. Both verbs negated by lā.", why:"Naming both functions of food is what makes the denial total.", note:"Samīn = fat; samn = ghee. Food does two things: builds you up, and stops the hunger. This does NEITHER.", more:["yughnī — the \"sufficiency / richness\" family — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:5 (istaghnā) and ʿAbasa 80:37 (yughnīh).","jūʿ — hunger (jāʾiʿ = hungry).","Food does two jobs: (a) it builds you up over time (yusmin), and (b) it stops the pain right now (yughnī min jūʿ). This \"food\" does neither. So it isn't food at all — it's the experience of eating with none of the point.","And the ghinā root connection is superb. In ʿAbasa 80:5, the rich chief istaghnā — \"considered himself in need of nothing.\" Here, the thorn lā yughnī — \"does not suffice.\" The man who felt he needed nothing ends up with food that supplies nothing. Same root, exact inversion.","And yughnī ← ʿAbasa 80:5's istaghnā: \"I need nothing\" → \"it supplies nothing.\""]},
  {ar:"نَّاعِمَةٌ", tr:"Nāʿimah", v:"8", root:"The \"blessing / softness / comfort\" family — niʿmah = a favour; naʿīm = bliss.", meaning:"\"[Other] faces, that Day, will be blissful / in comfort.\"", magic:"The perfect mirror of v.2. Khāshiʿah (humbled) vs. nāʿimah (blissful) — identical grammar (wujūhun yawmaʾidhin + participle), opposite content.", grammar:"Active participle, feminine; note wujūhun is again indefinite.", why:"Nāʿimah carries both inner bliss and visible physical ease — a face you could pick out across a room.", note:"Naʿam = YES; nāʿim = SOFT; niʿmah = a blessing. v.2 face = creased with humiliation. v.8 face = SMOOTH with ease.", more:["Nāʿim also means SOFT/smooth to the touch; and naʿam = \"YES.\" All from one root of agreeable abundance.","Read v.2 and v.8 back to back and you have the whole surah.","And the root's \"softness\" sense is doing quiet work: nāʿimah means their faces are literally SMOOTH — no lines of strain, no tension. Set that against v.3's nāṣibah (worn out) and v.40-style exhaustion: one face is creased with toil, the other is smooth.","Cross-surah: naʿīm you own from Insān 76:20 and Infiṭār 82:13 (inna'l-abrāra la-fī naʿīm).","And naʿīm is in Insān 76:20 and Infiṭār 82:13."]},
  {ar:"لِّسَعْيِهَا رَاضِيَةٌ", tr:"Li-saʿyihā rāḍiyah", v:"9", root:"Saʿy — the \"striving / hurrying\" family — the Saʿī between Ṣafā and Marwah.", meaning:"\"Pleased with its striving.\"", magic:"Look at the pair with v.3 one more time:", grammar:"Note li-saʿyihā is FRONTED before rāḍiyah — \"for its striving — pleased.\" Arabic moves a word forward to spotlight it.", why:"Rāḍiyah (pleased) rather than \"rewarded\" — because the reward described is a feeling about your own past.", note:"V.3: worked → EXHAUSTED. v.9: strove → PLEASED. Same effort, different direction.", more:["rāḍiyah — the \"contentment / pleasure\" family (riḍā = satisfaction; marḍiyy = pleasing).","THE VERSE THAT UNLOCKS THE SURAH.","Both worked. Both strove. One ended exhausted, one ended satisfied. The verse says the reward is being pleased with your own striving — looking back at what you spent your life on and being glad.","And saʿy is now in FOUR surahs: - Insān 76:22 — kāna saʿyukum mashkūrā (\"your striving has been appreciated\") - Nāziʿāt 79:22 — Pharaoh turned his back yasʿā (hurrying away) - Nāziʿāt 79:35 — man remembers mā saʿā (what he strove for) - ʿAbasa 80:8 — the blind man came yasʿā (running toward) - Ghāshiyah 88:9 — pleased with its striving","The Ṣafā–Marwah root, tracking every direction a person can run.","The emphasis is on what they strove for, not on the pleasure.","And saʿy = the Ṣafā–Marwah root, now in five verses across four surahs."]},
  {ar:"لَاغِيَةً", tr:"Lāghiyah", v:"11", root:"The \"empty, void, pointless speech\" family — laghw = idle talk; laghā = to be void / to annul.", meaning:"\"They will hear no idle talk therein.\"", magic:"You own this exactly — same root as Nabaʾ 78:35 (lā yasmaʿūna fīhā laghwan wa lā kidhdhāban). Two surahs, same feature of Paradise: no nonsense.", grammar:"Active participle, feminine (the noun kalimah, \"a word,\" is deleted); accusative object of tasmaʿu.", why:"Lāghiyah names a very specific irritation — words that waste your time — which most visions of paradise wouldn't think to remove.", note:"Nabaʾ 78:35 = laghwan. Ghāshiyah 88:11 = lāghiyah. Same root, same reward: no small talk, ever.", more:["And think about how strange this is as a reward. Not \"no pain,\" not \"no fear\" — \"no pointless conversation.\" The Qurʾān lists the absence of small talk as a luxury. Which tells you something about how much of your life is spent on words that mean nothing.","Note also lāghiyah is a participle (\"a thing that is idle\") rather than laghw (the abstract noun in Nabaʾ). Slightly more vivid: not even one idle utterance."]},
  {ar:"سُرُرٌ مَّرْفُوعَةٌ", tr:"Sururun marfūʿah", v:"13", root:"Surur — plural of sarīr, a raised couch/throne.", meaning:"\"Therein are elevated couches.\"", magic:"V.13–16 are a furniture catalogue — and that's deliberate.", grammar:"Passive participle pattern mafʿūlah — recognize the ma- prefix + -ū- shape across all four verses.", why:"\"raised\" couches means a vantage point — which is why Muṭaffifīn 83:23, 35 has them looking from the couches.", note:"V.13–16: raised, placed, lined up, spread out — ALL PASSIVE. The room was prepared before you arrived.", more:["Related to surūr = JOY (the couch of gladness) — the two words share letters and the connection is often noted.","marfūʿah — the \"raising\" family — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:14 (ṣuḥufin… marfūʿah); also marfūʿ = the NOMINATIVE case in grammar (the \"raised\" case!).","Four items, four verses, each with a passive participle describing its state: - v.13 — couches, marfūʿah (raised) - v.14 — cups, mawḍūʿah (set out / placed ready) - v.15 — cushions, maṣfūfah (lined up in rows) - v.16 — carpets, mabthūthah (spread out everywhere)","Every single one is a PASSIVE participle: raised, placed, arranged, spread. Someone has already set the room up for you. Nothing needs doing. That's the whole feeling — walking into a room prepared in advance.","Compare Insān 76:13 — muttakiʾīna fīhā ʿala'l-arāʾik (reclining on ornate couches) and Muṭaffifīn 83:23, 35 (ʿala'l-arāʾiki yanẓurūn). Three surahs of Paradise furniture.","A single pattern, four words.","And marfūʿ is the grammar term for nominative — the \"raised\" case."]},
  {ar:"أَكْوَابٌ مَّوْضُوعَةٌ", tr:"Akwābun mawḍūʿah", v:"14", root:"Akwāb — plural of kūb, cups without handles — round, simple, elegant.", meaning:"\"and cups placed ready.\"", magic:"Identical word to Insān 76:15 (wa akwābin kānat qawārīrā). Same handle-less cups, two surahs. Free vocabulary.", grammar:"Akwābun is indefinite nominative (subject of an implied \"there are\"); mawḍūʿah is its adjective.", why:"Mawḍūʿah over \"full\" — the point is readiness, not quantity.", note:"Akwāb sounds like \"a cup\" — say it fast. Identical to Insān 76:15. Mawḍūʿ = a topic — literally \"placed before you.\"", more:["mawḍūʿah — the \"placing / putting down\" family (waḍaʿa = to place; mawḍūʿ = a topic/subject — literally \"something placed before you\"!).","And mawḍūʿah is quietly perfect: the cups are already poured and set out — you don't ask, you don't wait, you don't pour. Compare Insān 76:16's qaddarūhā taqdīrā (\"measured to exact measure\") — Insān shows the serving; Ghāshiyah shows the setup."]},
  {ar:"نَمَارِقُ مَصْفُوفَةٌ", tr:"Namāriqu maṣfūfah", v:"15", root:"Namāriq — plural of numruqah, small cushions / pillows you lean against. maṣfūfah — the \"row / rank\" family — ṣaff!", meaning:"\"and cushions lined up in rows.\"", magic:"The ṣaff root turns up in the most unexpected place — Paradise's soft furnishings.", grammar:"Maṣfūfah is a passive participle — the cushions were lined up by someone.", why:"Maṣfūfah conveys arrangement and hospitality — a room set for guests, not a room with things in it.", note:"Ṣaff = the prayer row you stand in every day. Nabaʾ 78:38 = angels in ranks. Fajr 89:22 = angels rank upon rank.", more:["The same word as the rows you stand in for prayer (sawwū ṣufūfakum), and Nabaʾ 78:38 (aṣ-ṣaffan), and Fajr 89:22 (ṣaffan ṣaffā).","Track it: Nabaʾ 78:38 — the angels and the Spirit standing in ṣaff (awe). Fajr 89:22 — the angels coming ṣaffan ṣaffā (rank upon rank, terror). Ghāshiyah 88:15 — CUSHIONS in ṣaff (comfort).","Same word: angels in ranks at judgment, and pillows in rows in Paradise. The universe is orderly in both directions.","And you stand in ṣaff five times a day — you already know this word with your body.","Third of the four passives in v.13–16.","Ghāshiyah 88:15 = CUSHIONS in rows.","Same word, terror and comfort."]},
  {ar:"زَرَابِيُّ مَبْثُوثَةٌ", tr:"Zarābiyyu mabthūthah", v:"16", root:"Zarābiyy — rich, luxurious carpets — plural of zarbiyyah, a fine ornamented rug.", meaning:"\"and carpets spread all around.\"", magic:"The final item, and the verb makes it feel abundant rather than tidy.", grammar:"Note zarābiyyu takes a ḍammah without tanwīn — it's a diptote (mamnūʿ min aṣ-ṣarf), like ḥadāʾiq in Nabaʾ 78:32. Plural patterns of this shape usually are.", why:"Mabthūthah over \"laid\" — generosity is in the scattering.", note:"Couches, cups, cushions, carpets — the room is COMPLETE.", more:["mabthūthah — the \"scattering / spreading widely\" family (batha = to spread out, disperse; inbithāth = dispersal).","Mabthūthah = spread everywhere, scattered generously — not laid out in neat squares, but strewn liberally, more than you could use. Compare manthūran in Insān 76:19 (scattered pearls) — same feeling of casual excess.","And notice the room is now complete: raised couches (to sit), cups (to drink), cushions (to lean on), carpets (underfoot). Every surface of the experience is covered — vertical, horizontal, in hand, underfoot.","Mabthūthah = strewn everywhere, like manthūran (Insān 76:19). More than you could use."]},
  {ar:"الْإِبِلِ كَيْفَ خُلِقَتْ", tr:"Al-ibili kayfa khuliqat", v:"17", root:"Ibil — camels (a collective noun — the whole category, not one animal).", meaning:"\"Do they not look at the camels — how they were created?\"", magic:"One of the most famous verses in the Qurʾān for its choice of subject — and the sequence is the genius.", grammar:"أَفَلَا يَنظُرُونَ = a (question) + fa + lā + verb = \"Do they not then look…?\" — a rebuking rhetorical question.", why:"The camel is maximally ordinary and maximally essential — the perfect blind spot.", note:"Camel → sky → mountains → earth. Beside you, then up, then across, then down.", more:["Look at the order of the four things: - v.17 — the CAMEL (right beside you) - v.18 — the SKY (highest thing you can see) - v.19 — the MOUNTAINS (the horizon) - v.20 — the EARTH (under your feet)","It starts with the camel, then goes UP to the sky, then DOWN to the mountains, then all the way down to the ground. A complete 360° sweep — and it begins with the animal you're standing next to.","Why the camel first? Because it was the single most important creature in that world — transport, milk, meat, wool, wealth, survival. You saw one every day and never once thought about it. The Qurʾān's method throughout these surahs: the evidence is not exotic. It's the thing you stopped noticing.","Compare ʿAbasa 80:24 — fal-yanẓuri'l-insānu ilā ṭaʿāmih (\"let man look at his FOOD\"). ʿAbasa says look at your lunch; Ghāshiyah says look at your camel. Same technique, two surahs.","And kayfa (\"how\") makes it about the MANNER of creation, not the fact of it.","A full 360° — starting with the animal you never look at.","(ʿAbasa 80:24 said: look at your FOOD.)"]},
  {ar:"رُفِعَتْ / نُصِبَتْ / سُطِحَتْ", tr:"Rufiʿat / nuṣibat / suṭiḥat", v:"18–20", root:"Rufiʿat — raised (the rafʿ root, as in marfūʿah v.13!).", meaning:"\"and at the sky — how it was raised? and at the mountains — how they were set up? and at the earth — how it was spread flat?\"", magic:"Three passive verbs in a row, and the pattern is the message.", grammar:"All Form I passives (fuʿilat shape: rufiʿat, nuṣibat, suṭiḥat, khuliqat).", why:"Suṭiḥat (\"spread flat\") describes the earth as it functions for you — a usable surface — which is the whole point of the passage.", note:"ALL FOUR VERBS ARE PASSIVE — created, raised, set up, spread. The doer is never named because you already know.", more:["nuṣibat — set upright, firmly planted — the same root as nāṣibah in v.3!","(naṣaba = to set up; manṣib = a post/office).","suṭiḥat — spread out flat (saṭḥ = a flat roof/surface — in modern Arabic saṭḥ al-maktab = a desktop!).","All three are PASSIVE: raised, set up, spread. Nobody asks \"how did the sky rise?\" — the grammar has already answered: it was RAISED. By someone. Four verses, four passives (khuliqat, rufiʿat, nuṣibat, suṭiḥat) — the doer never named, because you already know.","And the naṣaba echo is stunning. In v.3, the wretched faces were nāṣibah — worn out from toil. In v.19, the mountains were nuṣibat — SET UP FIRMLY. Same root: human effort that collapses in exhaustion, and divine setting-up that stands forever. One root, two kinds of labour.","Cross-surah: mountains as firmly fixed you own from Nabaʾ 78:7 (awtādan, tent pegs) and Nāziʿāt 79:32 (arsāhā, anchored them). Three surahs, three images: pegs, anchors, uprights.","Recognize the ـُ ـِ vowel pattern = passive.","And nuṣibat (v.19) = same root as nāṣibah (v.3): human toil that exhausts vs.","divine setting-up that lasts.","Mountains: pegs (78:7), anchors (79:32), uprights (88:19)."]},
  {ar:"مُذَكِّرٌ", tr:"Mudhakkir", v:"21", root:"The \"reminding\" family — Form II active participle = one who reminds.", meaning:"\"So remind — you are only a reminder-giver.\"", magic:"The FIFTH surah to state this job description, and now it's a noun, not just a verb.", grammar:"إِنَّمَا = restriction (\"nothing but\").", why:"The participle makes it an identity, not just a task: you ARE a reminder-giver.", note:"Innamā = \"ONLY.\" Five surahs: your job is reminding. Delivery is yours; results are not.", more:["- Insān 76:29 — \"this is a tadhkirah\" - ʿAbasa 80:11 — \"it is a tadhkirah\" - Takwīr 81:27 — \"it is only a dhikr\" - Aʿlā 87:9 — \"so remind\" - Ghāshiyah 88:21 — \"so remind — you are only a mudhakkir\"","And innamā (\"only\") is a restriction particle — it LIMITS the job. This is a comfort, not a demotion: you are responsible for delivery, not for results.","Compare Nāziʿāt 79:45 — innamā anta mundhiru man yakhshāhā (\"you are only a warner\"). Same innamā + same limitation, two surahs.","Note the imperative dhakkir followed immediately by the participle mudhakkir — root repeated for emphasis."]},
  {ar:"بِمُصَيْطِرٍ", tr:"Bi-muṣayṭir", v:"22", root:"The \"controlling / dominating / having authority over\" family — sayṭara = to have complete control.", meaning:"\"You are not over them a controller.\"", magic:"The sharpest statement in the Qurʾān about the limits of daʿwah.", grammar:"SPELLING TRAP: this word is written with ص (ṣād) — مُصَيْطِر.", why:"Musayṭir names exactly the temptation — the wish to make people believe.", note:"Sayṭarah = CONTROL (everyday modern Arabic). \"You remind (v.21).", more:["Modern Arabic uses sayṭarah constantly for \"control/domination.\" (Originally connected to a sāṭir, one who writes/registers, hence one who keeps the ledger and enforces.)","Musayṭir is a strong word — a dominator, an enforcer, one who compels. The verse doesn't say \"be gentle with them\"; it says the role of forcing doesn't exist for you at all. You remind (v.21). You do not control (v.22).","And notice the balance with ʿAbasa 80:1–10: there, the Prophet ﷺ was corrected for over-prioritizing someone who seemed strategically valuable. Here he's told he has no power over outcomes anyway. Both verses remove the burden of managing results.","But pronounced by most readers as muṣayṭir, and there's a well-known variant with س (musayṭir).","The ص spelling in the muṣḥaf is a known orthographic feature; don't be thrown by it.","Note also the bāʾ zāʾidah (extra bāʾ) after the negation lasta — emphasis, same as Takwīr 81:22, 24, 25 and Infiṭār 82:16.","You do not control (v.22).\" Note the ص spelling — a known orthographic feature."]},
  {ar:"تَوَلَّىٰ وَكَفَرَ", tr:"Tawallā wa kafar", v:"23", root:"Tawallā — Form V of the \"turning\" root = to turn your back deliberately.", meaning:"\"Except for whoever turns away and disbelieves.\"", magic:"The pair covers both directions of rejection. Tawallā = turning away (a movement outward). Kafara = covering over (a movement inward). You leave, and you bury.", grammar:"إِلَّا مَن = \"except whoever\" — this exception connects back to v.22 (\"you are not a controller over them.", why:"Two verbs instead of one, because leaving and denying are separate acts.", note:"Kafara = to BURY a seed. Tawallā is in three surahs: 75:32 (the denier), 80:1 (a frown — corrected), 88:23 (rejection).", more:["You own it from Qiyāmah 75:32 and ʿAbasa 80:1. kafara — the \"covering up\" family (burying a seed) — you own it from Insān 76:3, ʿAbasa 80:17, 42, Infiṭār 82:14.","And tawallā is now in three surahs — with a remarkable range: - Qiyāmah 75:32 — the arrogant denier turned away - ʿAbasa 80:1 — the Prophet ﷺ turned away (a momentary frown — corrected) - Ghāshiyah 88:23 — whoever turns away and disbelieves","Same verb for a shoulder-turn and for total rejection. Which tells you how seriously the direction you face is taken.","Except the one who turns away and disbelieves, whom Allah will punish\").","The exception clarifies who faces consequence, and from Whom."]},
  {ar:"إِيَابَهُمْ", tr:"Iyābahum", v:"25", root:"The \"returning\" family — āba = to return; the same root as maʾāb in Nabaʾ 78:22 and 78:39! And you say it every journey: āyibūna tāʾibūna ʿābidūna li-rabbinā ḥāmidūn — \"returning, repenting, worshipping, praising our Lord\" — the travel duʿāʾ.", meaning:"\"Indeed, to Us is their return.\"", magic:"This closes a thread that started three surahs ago.", grammar:"Verbal noun (maṣdar) from a hollow verb (weak middle letter) — note the madd formed by the weak letter: iyāb.", why:"Iyāb (return) rather than \"arrival\" — you're going back, not forward. You came from Him.", note:"āyibūna — the TRAVEL DUʿĀʾ you say every journey. Same root. Nabaʾ 78:22 & 39: two maʾābs. Ghāshiyah 88:25: only ONE.", more:["In Nabaʾ 78:22, Hell was a maʾāb (a place of return) for the transgressors. In Nabaʾ 78:39, your Lord was a maʾāb. Two destinations, one word. Here in Ghāshiyah 88:25, the ambiguity is gone: ilaynā iyābahum — \"to US is their return.\" No alternative offered. Everyone, one address.","And the word order is emphatic: inna ilaynā iyābahum — \"to Us\" is FRONTED. Same move as ilā rabbika'l-mustaqarr (Qiyāmah 75:12), ilā rabbika'l-masāq (75:30), ilā rabbika muntahāhā (Nāziʿāt 79:44). Five surahs, one closing structure: everything terminates at Him.","And \"to US\" is fronted — five surahs use this move."]},
  {ar:"حِسَابَهُم", tr:"ḥisābahum", v:"26", root:"The \"counting / calculating\" family — ḥisāb = arithmetic, an account, a bill.", meaning:"\"Then upon Us is their reckoning.\"", magic:"The surah's final verse, and it's structurally beautiful.", grammar:"Note thumma here isn't just \"then\" in time — it signals an additional, weightier point.", why:"Ending on ḥisāb closes the surah on precise accounting — which is the answer to the two panels of faces it opened with.", note:"V.25 \"TO Us their return\" + v.26 \"UPON Us their reckoning.\" Perfectly matched final pair.", more:["You own it from Qiyāmah 75:3 (a-yaḥsabu), Nabaʾ 78:27, 36, Inshiqāq 84:8 (ḥisāban yasīrā).","Two clauses, perfectly matched: - v.25 — inna ilaynā iyābahum — \"to Us is their return\" - v.26 — thumma inna ʿalaynā ḥisābahum — \"upon Us is their reckoning\"","Both fronted, both inna-emphasized, both ending in -hum. Return, then reckoning. The two halves of the same arrival.","And note the reassurance hidden in \"ʿalaynā\" (upon Us): the reckoning is Our responsibility, not yours. Read it against v.21–22 (remind, don't control): the surah ends by explicitly taking the job you can't do off your hands.","The ḥ-s-b root is now in five surahs — and remember its sharpest irony from Qiyāmah 75:3: the calculating creature (a-yaḥsabu, \"does he reckon?\") who never calculated the day of calculation.","And ʿalaynā (upon Us) = obligation/undertaking — the same construction as ʿalaynā jamʿahu wa qurʾānah in Qiyāmah 75:17.","And ʿalaynā = it's OUR job — the same construction as Qiyāmah 75:17. The ḥisāb root is now in five surahs."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:89, name:"Al-Fajr", ar:"الفجر", en:"The Dawn", verses:30, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"The surah begins with an oath by the DAWN (al-fajr) and ends with a soul being welcomed home. It's a journey from first light to final arrival.",
  special:["It ends with the only verse in the Qurʾān where Allah invites a soul in by name — and it's the surah that spent 26 verses describing destruction to get there.","> \"O tranquil soul! Return to your Lord, well-pleased and well-pleasing. So enter among My servants. And enter My Garden.\" (v.27–30)","Notice what makes it extraordinary: four verses, and three of them are direct address in the imperative — irjiʿī (return), fa'dkhulī (enter), wa'dkhulī (and enter). Nowhere else does the Qurʾān end a surah by speaking to an individual soul like this.","And notice the possessives: ʿibādī (MY servants), jannatī (MY Garden). Not \"the Garden\" — \"My Garden.\" The most personal two words in the surah, placed at the very last.","--"],
  big:["Five oaths (v.1–5) — dawn, ten nights, the even and the odd, the departing night. Is there in that an oath for a person of sense?","Three civilizations (v.6–14) — ʿĀd, Thamūd, Pharaoh. All powerful. All gone. Your Lord is ever watchful.","The test misread (v.15–16) — He honoured me! / He humiliated me! Both wrong.","The real charge (v.17–20) — you don't honour the orphan, don't urge feeding the poor, devour inheritance, love wealth.","When the earth is pounded (v.21–26) — and man remembers — but what good is remembering then?","The invitation (v.27–30)."],
  traps:["أَكْرَمَنِ (v.15) and تُكْرِمُونَ (v.17) — SAME ROOT, two verses apart. \"My Lord HONOURED me!\" → \"You do NOT HONOUR the orphan.\" The single sharpest link in the surah. Don't read them separately.","ابْتَلَاهُ appears TWICE (v.15, 16) — wealth AND poverty are both called tests. He calls one honour and one humiliation. Wrong both times.","الْمِرْصَادِ (v.14) — the same word Nabaʾ 78:21 used for HELL. Here it's your Lord. Same ambush-image, far more sobering.","يَسْرِ (v.4) and بِالْوَادِ (v.9) — both have DROPPED their final yāʾ for the rhyme. A known orthographic feature — the words aren't broken.","لَيَالٍ (v.2) — manqūṣ: yāʾ drops, tanwīn on the lām. Same as rāqin (75:27) and al-jawāri (81:16).","إِرَمَ (v.7) and ثَمُودَ (v.9) — DIPTOTES: fatḥah instead of kasrah, no tanwīn. Standard for foreign proper names.","قَدَرَ عَلَيْهِ (v.16) — with ʿalā it means \"restricted/straitened.\" Without it, the same verb means \"measured/decreed.\" The preposition changes the meaning.","أَنَّىٰ (v.23) — means \"HOW / from where?\" — a rhetorical impossibility. Not anna (that), not innā (indeed we).","حِجْرٍ (v.5) — means INTELLECT here, from the root meaning a WALL/restraint (like the Ḥijr of the Kaʿbah). Not \"stone\" in this context.","رَاضِيَةً / مَّرْضِيَّةً (v.28) — active vs. passive participle of ONE root. She is pleased / she is pleased-with. Don't collapse them into one meaning."],
  links:[{w:"الْفَجْرِ (v.1)",e:"75:5 · 76:6 · 80:42 · 82:3 · 82:14",t:"SIX surahs — sin, a spring, the wicked, seas, dawn"},{w:"لَيَالٍ (v.2)",e:"رَاقٍ (75:27) · الْجَوَارِ (81:16)",t:"Manqūṣ: the yāʾ drops — third appearance"},{w:"عَشْرٍ (v.2)",e:"الْعِشَارُ (Takwīr 81:4)",t:"Same root — ten"},{w:"ذِي (v.5, 10)",e:"ذِي قُوَّةٍ (Takwīr 81:20)",t:"The five special nouns — case by long vowel"},{w:"الْأَوْتَادِ (v.10)",e:"أَوْتَادًا (Nabaʾ 78:7)",t:"Identical word — Allah's pegs vs. Pharaoh's"},{w:"طَغَوْا (v.11)",e:"78:22 · 79:17 · 79:37 · 91:11",t:"FIVE surahs — bursting all banks"},{w:"فَصَبَّ (v.13)",e:"صَبَبْنَا (ʿAbasa 80:25)",t:"Same verb — poured water vs. poured a whip"},{w:"الْمِرْصَادِ (v.14)",e:"مِرْصَادًا (Nabaʾ 78:21)",t:"Identical word — Hell watches / your Lord watches"},{w:"ابْتَلَاهُ (v.15, 16)",e:"نَبْتَلِيه (Insān 76:2)",t:"Same root — you were made to be tested"},{w:"قَدَرَ (v.16)",e:"75:4 · 76:16 · 80:19 · 87:3",t:"FIFTH surah — measuring, not humiliating"},{w:"نَعَّمَهُ (v.15)",e:"نَعِيمًا (76:20) · نَّاعِمَةٌ (88:8)",t:"Same root — blessing"},{w:"تُكْرِمُونَ (v.17)",e:"أَكْرَمَنِ (v.15, same surah)",t:"SAME ROOT, two verses apart — the sting"},{w:"الْيَتِيمَ / الْمِسْكِينِ (v.17–18)",e:"يَتِيمًا · مِسْكِينًا (Insān 76:8)",t:"The Three Guests"},{w:"تَحَاضُّونَ (v.18)",e:"يَتَسَاءَلُونَ (Nabaʾ 78:1)",t:"Same Form VI (mutual)"},{w:"أَكْلًا / حُبًّا (v.19–20)",e:"صَبًّا شَقًّا (80:25–26) · كَيْدًا (86:15)",t:"Cognate accusative — Arabic's underline"},{w:"صَفًّا صَفًّا (v.22)",e:"صَفًّا (78:38) · مَصْفُوفَةٌ (88:15)",t:"Three surahs — angels & cushions"},{w:"يَتَذَكَّرُ (v.23)",e:"يَتَذَكَّرُ (Nāziʿāt 79:35)",t:"Identical word — Fajr adds \"too late\""},{w:"الذِّكْرَىٰ (v.23)",e:"76:29 · 80:11 · 81:27 · 87:9 · 88:21",t:"The Prophet's ﷺ job for five surahs"},{w:"يَا لَيْتَنِي (v.24)",e:"يَا لَيْتَنِي كُنتُ تُرَابًا (Nabaʾ 78:40)",t:"Identical construction"},{w:"قَدَّمْتُ (v.24)",e:"75:13 · 78:40 · 82:5",t:"FOURTH — and the only first-person one"},{w:"وَثَاقَهُ (v.26)",e:"سَلَاسِلَ وَأَغْلَالًا (Insān 76:4)",t:"Chains named / binding beyond compare"},{w:"رَاضِيَةً (v.28)",e:"رَاضِيَةٌ (Ghāshiyah 88:9)",t:"Same word — pleased with striving / with Him"},{w:"جَنَّتِي (v.30)",e:"بِمَجْنُونٍ (Takwīr 81:22)",t:"Same root — janna = TO COVER"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"الْفَجْرِ", tr:"Al-fajr", v:"1", root:"The \"splitting / cleaving open\" family — fajara = to burst open.", meaning:"\"By the dawn.\"", magic:"You now own this root across SIX surahs — it's one of the best-tracked words in your collection: - Qiyāmah 75:5 — li-yafjura amāmah — man wants to burst into sin - Insān 76:6.", grammar:"The wāw here is wāw al-qasam (the \"by\" of oaths) — which is why the noun is genitive (al-fajri).", why:"Dawn is the moment of transition — and this surah is entirely about people who didn't notice the transition coming.", note:"Fajr = dawn, because light SPLITS the dark.", more:["Fajr = DAWN, because the light SPLITS the darkness open.","Also: infijār = an explosion.","Yufajjirūnahā — the righteous make a spring gush at will - ʿAbasa 80:42 — al-fajarah — the wicked (those who burst past limits) - Infiṭār 82:3 — fujjirat — the seas burst their bounds - Infiṭār 82:14 — al-fujjār — the wicked (second plural) - Fajr 89:1 — al-fajr — the DAWN","One root: sinning, a heavenly spring, two words for the wicked, the seas bursting, and daybreak. The unifying idea is always: something breaking through a barrier.","And the placement is deliberate. A surah that will describe three civilizations shattered opens with the gentlest possible \"splitting\": light breaking through night. Same verb, and one version is beautiful.","Five oaths in a row, all genitive.","SIX surahs, one root: 75:5, 76:6, 80:42, 82:3, 82:14, 89:1. Sin, a spring, the wicked, the seas, and daybreak."]},
  {ar:"لَيَالٍ عَشْرٍ", tr:"Layālin ʿashr", v:"2", root:"Layālī — plural of laylah, a night.", meaning:"\"and by ten nights.\"", magic:"The most widely-held view is the first ten nights of Dhu'l-Ḥijjah — the days of Hajj, which the Prophet ﷺ described as the days in which righteous deeds are most beloved to Allah.", grammar:"Layālin is a manqūṣ noun — weak-final, so it DROPS its yāʾ and takes tanwīn on the lām: layālin, not layālī.", why:"Naming a span of nights rather than a place or event makes the oath about time you're given to use.", note:"ʿashr = TEN (as in Takwīr 81:4's ten-month camels). Most likely the ten of Dhu'l-Ḥijjah.", more:["ʿashr — ten — you own this root from Takwīr 81:4 (al-ʿishār, the ten-month pregnant camels).","Others have suggested the last ten of Ramaḍān or the first ten of Muḥarram.","What's beautiful either way: the oath is by a period of intense worship. Set that against the surah's later charge (v.17–20): you don't honour the orphan, you don't feed the poor. The surah swears by the season of devotion, then indicts a life empty of it.","And note layālin is INDEFINITE — \"ten nights,\" not \"the ten nights.\" The indefiniteness adds weight and mystery, letting the listener supply which ten.","Same rule as rāqin (Qiyāmah 75:27) and al-jawāri (Takwīr 81:16). Third time you've met this rule — it's high-yield.","layālin is manqūṣ: the yāʾ drops — same as rāqin and al-jawāri."]},
  {ar:"الشَّفْعِ وَالْوَتْرِ", tr:"Ash-shafʿi wa'l-watr", v:"3", root:"Shafʿ — the \"pairing / doubling / adding to\" family — shafaʿa = to make something even by adding a second.", meaning:"\"and by the even and the odd.\"", magic:"Two hooks, both from things you already do. - Witr — you pray it. It's called that because it's an ODD number of rakʿahs. Same word, right here in the oath.", grammar:"Both genitive after the oath-wāw; note they're definite (al-) in contrast to v.2's indefinite layālin.", why:"Shafʿ/watr covers all of number itself — the most complete pair available.", note:"Witr = the ODD prayer — you pray it.", more:["And shafāʿah = INTERCESSION (because the intercessor joins himself to the one he pleads for — making a pair!).","watr — the odd, the single, the unpaired — and you know it: Ṣalāt al-Witr, the odd-numbered prayer.","- Shafāʿah (intercession) comes from shafʿ (even/paired) — because an intercessor pairs up with you, so you're no longer standing alone.","Beautiful etymology worth keeping.","And the oath itself is a merism: \"the even and the odd\" = EVERY number that exists. Swearing by even and odd is swearing by all of creation, since everything is one or the other. Two words, total coverage.","Scholars have offered many specific readings (Allah is watr and creation is shafʿ; the 9th and 10th of Dhu'l-Ḥijjah; the prayers) — but the sweep of \"everything countable\" is what makes it land.","Shafāʿah (intercession) ← shafʿ (EVEN) — the intercessor pairs up with you so you're not alone.","\"Even and odd\" = literally everything."]},
  {ar:"إِذَا يَسْرِ", tr:"Idhā yasr", v:"4", root:"The \"travelling by night\" family — sarā = to journey through the night; al-Isrāʾ — the Night Journey of the Prophet ﷺ!", meaning:"\"and by the night when it departs / journeys on.\"", magic:"The night is described as a TRAVELLER. Not \"when it ends\" — when it moves along, like a caravan passing through. It personifies the night as something with somewhere to be.", grammar:"يَسْرِ has DROPPED its final yāʾ (from yasrī).", why:"Sarā makes the night an active passer-by, not just a state that ends.", note:"Al-Isrāʾ — the NIGHT JOURNEY. Same root. The night is a TRAVELLER passing through. يَسْرِ drops its yāʾ for the rhyme.", more:["And the oath-set is now a complete arc: v.1 the DAWN (light arriving) → v.4 the NIGHT departing. The surah's oaths bracket the whole cycle of a day — which is exactly what it will do with civilizations (v.6–14): they arrive, they pass.","Compare Takwīr 81:17 — wa'l-layli idhā ʿasʿas (\"by the night when it creeps\"). Two surahs, two verbs for night's movement: creeping and journeying.","This happens in the muṣḥaf's spelling to preserve the verse-ending rhyme (-r), matching al-fajr, ʿashr, al-watr, ḥijr.","A known orthographic feature — don't think the verb is broken.","v.1 dawn arrives, v.4 night departs — the full cycle."]},
  {ar:"لِّذِي حِجْرٍ", tr:"Li-dhī ḥijr", v:"5", root:"The \"restraining / walling off / forbidding\" family — ḥajara = to restrain, to block; ḥijr = a stone enclosure (the Ḥijr of the Kaʿbah!", meaning:"\"Is there in that an oath for one possessing sense / intellect?\"", magic:"The most beautiful definition of intelligence in the Qurʾān — and it's not what you'd expect.", grammar:"ذِي = \"possessor of\" — one of the five special nouns (al-asmāʾ al-khamsah) that show case with a long vowel: dhū (nom.), dhā (acc.), dhī (gen.).", why:"ḥijr over ʿaql — because the surah is specifically about people who had no restraints.", note:"Ḥijr = A WALL (the Ḥijr of the Kaʿbah!). Intelligence = HAVING FENCES. And ʿaql originally = the rope tying a camel's leg.", more:["— the semicircular wall); maḥjūr = forbidden; ḥajr = legal interdiction.","The word for \"intellect\" here is ḥijr — literally \"a WALL, a restraint.\" So a person of sense is defined as someone with fences — someone whose mind restrains him from doing what he shouldn't.","Arabic does this repeatedly: ʿaql (the common word for intellect) originally meant the rope that ties a camel's leg so it can't wander off. Ḥijr = a wall. Both name intelligence by its ability to HOLD YOU BACK, not by how much it knows.","And this is the surah's thesis, stated in verse 5. Every group that follows — ʿĀd, Thamūd, Pharaoh — is described in v.11 as those who ṭaghaw (OVERFLOWED all bounds). The intelligent man has walls; the destroyed nations had none. Ḥijr (v.5) vs ṭaghaw (v.11) is the whole surah.","You already own the contrast: ṭaghā = water bursting its banks (Nabaʾ 78:22, Nāziʿāt 79:17, 37).","Same word you met in Takwīr 81:20 (dhī quwwah).","v.5 ḥijr (walls) vs v.11 ṭaghaw (overflowing) — that's the surah."]},
  {ar:"إِرَمَ ذَاتِ الْعِمَادِ", tr:"Irama dhāti'l-ʿimād", v:"7", root:"Iram — a proper name associated with the people of ʿĀd.", meaning:"\"Iram, possessor of the lofty pillars.\"", magic:"The description is doing double duty, and both readings are classical: - (a) Architectural — a people famed for towering columns, monumental building.", grammar:"Irama is a DIPTOTE (mamnūʿ min aṣ-ṣarf) — a foreign proper name, so it takes fatḥah instead of kasrah in the genitive and no tanwīn.", why:"ʿimād (pillars) is the universal symbol of civilization that intends to last.", note:"ʿamūd = a PILLAR; iʿtamada = to LEAN ON. The Qurʾān CREDITS them: \"the like of whom was never created.\" Then asks where they went.", more:["ʿimād — the \"pillar / support column\" family — ʿamūd = a pillar; ʿimād = the tall tent-pole, and by extension lofty columns; iʿtamada = to rely on (to lean on a pillar).","- (b) Physical — \"pillars\" describing the people themselves — tall, mighty in stature.","Either way, the point is the same: they were IMPRESSIVE. The verse doesn't mock them — it credits them. v.8 goes further: \"the like of whom had never been created in the lands.\" The Qurʾān concedes they were unmatched.","Which makes the argument stronger, not weaker: \"the greatest civilization that ever existed — and where is it now?\"","Same class as Thamūda in v.9.","Watch for this with non-Arabic names.","Diptote: Irama, not Iramin."]},
  {ar:"جَابُوا الصَّخْرَ بِالْوَادِ", tr:"Jābū'ṣ-ṣakhra bi'l-wād", v:"9", root:"Jābū — the \"cutting through / traversing\" family — jāba = to carve out, to hollow, to cut through.", meaning:"\"And Thamūd, who carved out the rocks in the valley.\"", magic:"Thamūd's signature achievement was carving homes directly into cliff faces — you can still see such rock-cut façades in the region historically associated with them (al-Ḥijr / Madāʾin Ṣāliḥ).", grammar:"Bi'l-wād has dropped its final yāʾ (from al-wādī) — for the rhyme, exactly like yasr in v.4.", why:"Jāba (to carve through solid rock) conveys the maximum human effort at permanence.", note:"ʿĀd built UP (pillars). Thamūd carved IN (rock). Pharaoh anchored DOWN (stakes). Three strategies for permanence. Three ruins.", more:["Also \"to travel across\" (jawwāb = a great traveller).","ṣakhr — solid rock (ṣakhrah = a boulder).","And notice the pattern the surah is building: - ʿĀd (v.7) — built UP with pillars - Thamūd (v.9) — carved INTO solid rock - Pharaoh (v.10) — dhi'l-awtād (\"of the stakes/pegs\") — anchored DOWN","Up, in, down. Three ways of making yourself permanent. All three gone.","Second time in this surah.","al-wād drops its yāʾ — like yasr."]},
  {ar:"ذِي الْأَوْتَادِ", tr:"Dhi'l-awtād", v:"10", root:"Watad = A TENT PEG — the stake hammered into the ground so your tent doesn't blow away.", meaning:"\"and Pharaoh, possessor of the stakes.\"", magic:"You own this word EXACTLY — it's identical to Nabaʾ 78:7 (wa'l-jibāla awtādā — \"and the mountains as pegs\"). Same word, two surahs — and the contrast is extraordinary.", grammar:"Dhī again — \"possessor of\" — the same five-noun construction as dhī ḥijr (v.5) and dhī quwwah (Takwīr 81:20). Third appearance; learn it.", why:"Awtād ties Pharaoh to the illusion of being anchored — the very word used for genuine anchoring elsewhere.", note:"Watad = A TENT PEG. Nabaʾ 78:7: Allah's mountains hold the EARTH. Fajr 89:10: Pharaoh's pegs held NOTHING.", more:["Nabaʾ 78:7: ALLAH made the mountains as awtād — pegs that genuinely hold the earth steady. Fajr 89:10: PHARAOH is \"possessor of awtād\" — his own version of permanence. One set of pegs holds a planet; the other held nothing.","The classical readings of Pharaoh's awtād: (a) his armies and pyramids — the stakes that anchored his power; (b) the stakes he used to torture and pin down his victims. Both are attested, and both are grim: his \"stability\" rested on cruelty.","Same word, two kinds of permanence."]},
  {ar:"طَغَوْا / الْفَسَادَ", tr:"ṭaghaw / al-fasād", v:"11–12", root:"ṭaghaw — the \"overflowing all bounds\" family — original image: water bursting its banks and flooding.", meaning:"\"who transgressed all bounds in the lands, and multiplied corruption therein.\"", magic:"Verse 11 is the ONE-LINE DIAGNOSIS of all three civilizations — and it answers verse 5.", grammar:"ṭaghaw is a weak-final past verb, plural (note the wāw al-jamāʿah).", why:"Fasād (rot/spoiling) rather than \"evil\" — it frames corruption as something that SPREADS, like mould.", note:"Ṭaghā = water bursting its banks. v.5 asked for WALLS; v.11 shows people with NONE.", more:["fasād — the \"corruption / rot / spoiling\" family (fasada = to go bad, to rot — used for food spoiling!).","V.5 asked: is there an oath here for someone with ḥijr (WALLS)? v.11 answers: these three had none — they ṭaghaw, OVERFLOWED. The intelligent man has fences; these nations burst their banks.","You own ṭaghā across four surahs now: - Nabaʾ 78:22 — aṭ-ṭāghīn (those who transgress — Hell awaits them) - Nāziʿāt 79:17 — innahu ṭaghā (Pharaoh — the case study) - Nāziʿāt 79:37 — fa-ammā man ṭaghā (whoever transgresses — transferable!) - Fajr 89:11 — alladhīna ṭaghaw (these three civilizations) - Shams 91:11 — bi-ṭaghwāhā (Thamūd, by its transgression) (coming up)","And fasād is the RESULT of ṭaghā: overflow the banks (ṭaghā) → the water spreads and rots everything (fasād). The two words are cause and effect, in sequence.","Fa-aktharū (\"so they multiplied\") — Form IV = to make much of something.","FIVE surahs: 78:22, 79:17, 79:37, 89:11, 91:11. And fasada = FOOD GOING BAD — corruption spreads like rot."]},
  {ar:"فَصَبَّ... سَوْطَ عَذَابٍ", tr:"Fa-ṣabba… sawṭa ʿadhāb", v:"13", root:"ṣabba — the \"pouring\" family — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:25 (annā ṣababnā'l-māʾa ṣabbā — \"We poured down water\").", meaning:"\"So your Lord poured upon them a whip of punishment.\"", magic:"One of the most striking mixed images in the Qurʾān — and the cross-surah echo makes it land.", grammar:"Sawṭa ʿadhābin is an iḍāfah (\"a whip of punishment\") — the accusative object of ṣabba.", why:"Sawṭ (a whip) is a disciplinary instrument, not a weapon of war — implying correction, not just destruction.", note:"ʿAbasa 80:25: ṣabba poured WATER (life). Fajr 89:13: ṣabba poured a WHIP. Same verb, opposite gifts.", more:["In ʿAbasa 80:25, the same verb ṣabba poured WATER — which made grain, olives, gardens, and pasture grow. Here, ṣabba pours a WHIP. Same verb: rain that gives life, and a lash that ends it.","And \"pouring a whip\" is deliberately odd — you don't pour a whip. The mixture conveys punishment that came down like rainfall: from above, continuous, inescapable, everywhere at once.","Some scholars also note that a sawṭ is what you'd use on an animal that won't stay in line — which fits perfectly with ṭaghaw (v.11): creatures that wouldn't stay within bounds.","And you don't \"pour\" a whip — the image means it came down like rain."]},
  {ar:"لَبِالْمِرْصَادِ", tr:"La-bi'l-mirṣād", v:"14", root:"The \"watching / lying in wait\" family — raṣada = to observe, to lie in ambush.", meaning:"\"Indeed, your Lord is ever on the watch / in the place of observation.\"", magic:"THE BEST CROSS-SURAH PAYOFF IN THIS ENTIRE GROUP — and it's a direct callback.", grammar:"Noun of place on the mifʿāl pattern — \"the place from which watching is done.\" Same pattern as miḥrāb, miftāḥ.", why:"Mirṣād makes divine awareness positional and patient — not just \"He knows,\" but \"He is stationed.\"", note:"Marṣad = AN OBSERVATORY. Nabaʾ 78:21: HELL is the ambush. Fajr 89:14: YOUR LORD is at the lookout.", more:["Marṣad = AN OBSERVATORY in modern Arabic (marṣad falakī = an astronomical observatory).","Nabaʾ 78:21: inna jahannama kānat mirṣādā — \"Hell is an ambush/lookout.\" Fajr 89:14: inna rabbaka la-bi'l-mirṣād — \"your Lord is in the place of watching.\"","SAME WORD. In Nabaʾ, Hell is the one lying in wait. Here, it's your Lord. Two surahs, one image of patient observation — and this one is far more sobering, because it isn't a place. It's Him.","And read it in context: the verse comes immediately after three civilizations are destroyed. The message: this wasn't three isolated accidents. Someone was watching the road the whole time. A mirṣād is where you sit to see who passes.","Note the DOUBLE emphasis: inna + the lām of emphasis (la-bi'l-mirṣād). The Qurʾān is insisting.","Same word, and this one is worse.","Placed right after three empires fall — \"someone was watching the whole time.\""]},
  {ar:"ابْتَلَاهُ", tr:"Ibtalāhu", v:"15, 16", root:"The \"testing / trying\" family — balā = to test; balāʾ = a trial (which can mean either a hardship OR a blessing — both are tests!).", meaning:"\"when his Lord tests him\" — said twice, once for each scenario.", magic:"THE HINGE OF THE SURAH, and the repetition is everything.", grammar:"Form VIII (ibtalā), weak-final, with attached pronoun.", why:"Ibtalā (test) rather than \"gave\" or \"withheld\" — it names both situations by their true category.", note:"SAME WORD opens both verses: ibtalāhu — HE TESTED HIM. Rich = a test. Poor = a test.", more:["Look at the structure of v.15–16: - v.15 — idhā mā ibtalāhu rabbuhu fa-akramahu wa naʿʿamahu → he says \"my Lord has honoured me!\" - v.16 — idhā mā ibtalāhu fa-qadara ʿalayhi rizqah → he says \"my Lord has humiliated me!\"","THE SAME WORD OPENS BOTH: ibtalāhu — HE TESTED HIM. Wealth is a test. Poverty is a test. Man calls the first \"honour\" and the second \"humiliation\" — and he is WRONG BOTH TIMES.","The error isn't misreading poverty. It's misreading BOTH. He thinks blessings are a verdict and hardship is a verdict. They're both just the exam paper.","You own this root from Insān 76:2 — khalaqna'l-insāna min nuṭfatin amshājin nabtalīhi — \"We created man from a mixed drop, to test him.\" Insān said your senses exist FOR testing; Fajr shows you failing to notice you're being tested.","Note idhā mā — the mā is zāʾidah (extra), adding emphasis: \"whenever.\"","He calls one \"honour\" and one \"humiliation\" — WRONG BOTH TIMES.","And Insān 76:2 said your senses exist to test you."]},
  {ar:"فَقَدَرَ عَلَيْهِ رِزْقَهُ", tr:"Fa-qadara ʿalayhi rizqah", v:"16", root:"Qadara — the \"measuring / apportioning\" family — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:4 (qādirīn), Insān 76:16 (qaddarūhā), ʿAbasa 80:19 (fa-qaddarah), Aʿlā 87:3 (qaddara fa-hadā).", meaning:"\"and restricts his provision.\"", magic:"The word choice quietly refutes the man's own complaint.", grammar:"قَدَرَ عَلَى = \"to straiten/restrict for someone\" — note the preposition changes the meaning.", why:"Qadara frames restricted provision as a deliberate portion, not a punishment.", note:"He says \"HUMILIATED.\" The verse says qadara — MEASURED. A measurement is not an insult.", more:["Here qadara ʿalā = to restrict/straiten someone's provision.","rizq — provision, sustenance (ar-Razzāq = the Provider).","He says \"my Lord has HUMILIATED me\" (ahānan). But the verse describes what actually happened using qadara — the root of MEASURING. Allah didn't humiliate him; He measured out his provision.","And you know this root's other face: in Aʿlā 87:3, qaddara fa-hadā — \"He measured and then guided.\" Measuring is an act of precision and care, not contempt. The man reads a measurement as an insult.","Note also the FIFTH appearance of this root across your surahs — it's a top-tier tracked word alongside sawwā and ṣaliya.","Same verb without ʿalā means \"to measure/decree.\"","And Aʿlā 87:3: \"He measured and then guided.\" Fifth surah for this root."]},
  {ar:"لَّا تُكْرِمُونَ الْيَتِيمَ", tr:"Lā tukrimūna'l-yatīm", v:"17", root:"Tukrimūna — the \"nobility / generosity / honouring\" family.", meaning:"\"No! Rather, you do not honour the orphan.\"", magic:"THE MOST DEVASTATING WORD-LINK IN THE SURAH — and it's only two verses away.", grammar:"Form IV (akrama = to honour).", why:"Tukrimūna (honour) over tuṭʿimūna (feed) — because it mirrors the man's own word in v.15.", note:"V.15: \"My Lord HONOURED me (akraman)!\" v.17: \"You do NOT HONOUR (tukrimūna) the orphan.\" SAME ROOT, TWO VERSES APART.", more:["You own it from ʿAbasa 80:13 (mukarramah), ʿAbasa 80:16 (kirām), Takwīr 81:19 (karīm), Infiṭār 82:6 (al-Karīm) and 82:11 (kirāman).","yatīm — an orphan (a child with no father).","V.15: the man says \"rabbī akraman\" — \"my Lord has HONOURED me!\" v.17: \"lā tukrimūna'l-yatīm\" — \"you do NOT HONOUR the orphan.\"","SAME ROOT. TWO VERSES APART. \"You are delighted that God HONOURED you — and you refuse to HONOUR the orphan.\" The reply isn't theological. It's: you received the very thing you won't pass on.","And notice: it doesn't say \"you don't FEED the orphan\" — it says you don't HONOUR him. The failure isn't only material. It's about dignity. An orphan can be fed and still be humiliated.","You own yatīm from Insān 76:8 — the Three Guests: miskīnan wa yatīman wa asīrā. Insān showed people who fed them while hungry themselves. Fajr shows people who wouldn't.","Note the shift to PLURAL \"you\" — v.15–16 described \"man\" in the third person; now the surah turns and addresses you directly.","You won't pass on what you were given.","And note: honour, not just feed."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا تَحَاضُّونَ", tr:"Wa lā taḥāḍḍūn", v:"18", root:"The \"urging / inciting / spurring on\" family — ḥaḍḍa = to strongly encourage someone toward something.", meaning:"\"and you do not urge one another to feed the poor.\"", magic:"The bar is set shockingly low — and that's the point.", grammar:"Form VI (تَفَاعَلَ) = mutual action between people. Spot the ta- + long ā. (tasāʾala, taʿāwana, taḥāḍḍa.)", why:"The mutual form is what makes it damning — it indicts the whole social atmosphere, not just individuals.", note:"Not \"you don't feed them\" — \"you don't even ENCOURAGE EACH OTHER to.\" The free part. And they failed at that.", more:["It doesn't say \"you don't feed the poor.\" It says you don't even encourage each other to feed them. Form VI (taḥāḍḍūn) is the MUTUAL form — \"urging one another.\"","So the charge is: you won't even do the free part. Feeding costs money. Encouraging someone else to feed costs NOTHING. And they failed at that.","Form VI you own from Nabaʾ 78:1 — ʿamma yatasāʾalūn (\"what are they asking each other about?\"). Same mutual form: a society talking to itself. In Nabaʾ they talk endlessly about the Resurrection; in Fajr they won't say one word about the hungry.","And miskīn you own from Insān 76:8 — again, the Three Guests.","Form VI = mutual (same as yatasāʾalūn, Nabaʾ 78:1)."]},
  {ar:"التُّرَاثَ أَكْلًا لَّمًّا", tr:"At-turātha aklan lammā", v:"19", root:"Turāth — inheritance / heritage — from waritha (to inherit); modern Arabic turāth = cultural heritage.", meaning:"\"and you devour inheritance with a devouring altogether / greedily sweeping it all up.\"", magic:"The image is physical and greedy. Lamm means sweeping everything together into one pile — like using both arms to rake the whole table toward yourself.", grammar:"Cognate accusative + adjective: aklan lammā = \"a devouring that sweeps everything.\" The adjective on the cognate accusative intensifies further.", why:"Lamm conveys indiscriminate totality — not stealing a portion, but taking the lot.", note:"Lamm = raking EVERYTHING toward yourself with both arms. And whose inheritance is it? THE ORPHAN'S (v.17).", more:["lamm — the \"gathering up completely / sweeping together\" family — lamma = to gather everything up, leaving nothing behind.","And the connection to v.17 is direct: whose inheritance do you think is being devoured? The ORPHAN'S. v.17 said you don't honour him; v.19 says you eat his share. The verses are describing one continuous crime.","The maf'ūl muṭlaq (cognate accusative) — ta'kulūna… aklan — is Arabic's underline key, which you own from Nāziʿāt 79:1–4, ʿAbasa 80:25–26 (ṣabban, shaqqan), and Ṭāriq 86:15–16 (kaydan). Here it appears twice in two verses (v.19 aklan, v.20 ḥubban).","v.17, 19 describe one crime."]},
  {ar:"حُبًّا جَمًّا", tr:"ḥubban jammā", v:"20", root:"Jamm — the \"abundance / heaping up\" family — jamma = to become plentiful; related to the sense of a well filling up to overflowing.", meaning:"\"and you love wealth with an overwhelming love.\"", magic:"The fourth and final charge, and it's the ROOT CAUSE of the other three.", grammar:"Second cognate accusative in two verses (tuḥibbūna… ḥubban jammā).", why:"Jamm names the quantity of the love, which is precisely the problem.", note:"Four charges: orphan → poor → inheritance → LOVE OF WEALTH. The last one CAUSES the first three.", more:["Read v.17–20 as a chain: don't honour the orphan → don't urge feeding the poor → devour inheritance → LOVE WEALTH EXCESSIVELY. The last item explains the first three. The Qurʾān lists the symptoms, then names the disease.","And jamm (overflowing abundance) is the same idea as ṭaghā (v.11, water bursting its banks) — excessive love that overflows its proper limits. The nations overflowed with power; this man overflows with love of money. Same failure of ḥijr (v.5, walls).","Note: the charge is not loving wealth — it's loving it jammā, to overflowing.","The pair aklan lammā / ḥubban jammā even RHYME — deliberate.","And jamm (overflowing) = the same failure as ṭaghā (v.11): no walls."]},
  {ar:"دُكَّتِ الْأَرْضُ دَكًّا دَكًّا", tr:"Dukkati'l-arḍu dakkan dakkā", v:"21", root:"The \"pounding flat / crushing level\" family — dakka = to pound something until it's completely flattened.", meaning:"\"No! When the earth is pounded, pounding upon pounding.\"", magic:"The repetition is the earthquake.", grammar:"Passive verb + REPEATED cognate accusative = maximum intensity in Arabic.", why:"Dakka means flattened to a level surface — the earth loses all its features, all at once.", note:"Dakkah = a flat bench. \"Pounded, pounded\" — say it out loud: dak-kan dak-kā. The word IS the hammer.", more:["Dakkah = a flat bench/platform — something levelled.","Dakkan dakkā — the cognate accusative REPEATED. Not just \"pounded thoroughly\" but \"pounded, pounded\" — blow after blow. You can HEAR it: dak-kan dak-kā. The sound of the word IS the hammering.","And it's mirrored immediately in v.22: ṣaffan ṣaffā (\"rank upon rank\"). Two consecutive verses, each with a doubled cognate accusative. The earth pounds flat; the angels come in ranks. Both doubled, both relentless.","Compare Nabaʾ 78:20 — wa suyyirati'l-jibālu fa-kānat sarābā (mountains → mirage) and Takwīr 81:3. Three surahs, three ways the landscape ends.","This doubling is rare — note where it appears.","And v.22 mirrors it: ṣaffan ṣaffā."]},
  {ar:"وَالْمَلَكُ صَفًّا صَفًّا", tr:"Wa'l-malaku ṣaffan ṣaffā", v:"22", root:"The \"row / rank\" family — ṣaff — the row you stand in for prayer (sawwū ṣufūfakum — \"straighten your rows\").", meaning:"\"and your Lord comes, and the angels, rank upon rank.\"", magic:"The ṣaff root is now in THREE surahs, and the range is remarkable: - Nabaʾ 78:38 — yawma yaqūmu'r-rūḥu wa'l-malāʾikatu ṣaffā (the Spirit and angels in rank, silent) - Ghāshiyah 88:15 — namāriqu maṣfūfah (CUSHIONS in rows — comfort!) - Fajr 89:22 — wa'l-malaku ṣaffan ṣaffā (rank upon rank)", grammar:"Doubled ḥāl (circumstantial accusative) — \"in a state of rank upon rank.\" Note al-malak is SINGULAR in form but collective in meaning (\"the angels\").", why:"The ritual word makes an unimaginable scene immediately picturable.", note:"You stand in ṣaff FIVE TIMES A DAY. Nabaʾ 78:38 (angels, awe), Ghāshiyah 88:15 (cushions, comfort), Fajr 89:22 (rank upon rank).", more:["Angels in terrifying ranks, and pillows in tidy rows. Same word. The universe is orderly in both directions.","And ṣaffan ṣaffā is doubled, exactly like dakkan dakkā the verse before — row behind row behind row, no end visible.","The hook that costs you nothing: you stand in ṣaff five times a day. You are rehearsing v.22 constantly.","Same word, terror and comfort."]},
  {ar:"وَأَنَّىٰ لَهُ الذِّكْرَىٰ", tr:"Wa annā lahu'dh-dhikrā", v:"23", root:"Annā — \"how / from where?\" — an interrogative expressing impossibility, not curiosity.", meaning:"\"That Day man will remember — but how will the remembrance help him?\"", magic:"The saddest half-verse in the surah, and it hinges on WHEN.", grammar:"أَنَّى = \"how / whence?\" — not to be confused with anna (that) or innā (indeed we).", why:"Annā (how?) rather than a statement — the question form makes the futility land harder than \"it will not benefit him.\"", note:"He REMEMBERS — and \"what good is remembering now?\" Nāziʿāt 79:35 says he remembers; Fajr 89:23 adds the \"too late.\" The reminder was offered for FIVE SURAHS.", more:["dhikrā — the \"remembering\" family — you own it from Insān 76:29, ʿAbasa 80:4, 11, Takwīr 81:27, Aʿlā 87:9.","The full verse: yawmaʾidhin yatadhakkaru'l-insānu wa annā lahu'dh-dhikrā. \"He remembers\" — and then immediately: \"and what use is remembering now?\"","The tragedy isn't that he forgot. It's that he remembers TOO LATE. The faculty worked perfectly — at the wrong moment.","And you own yatadhakkaru from Nāziʿāt 79:35 — yawma yatadhakkaru'l-insānu mā saʿā (\"the Day man remembers what he strove for\"). Same word, same Day, two surahs. Nāziʿāt says he remembers; Fajr adds: and it's no longer any use.","And dhikrā has been the Prophet's ﷺ JOB for five surahs (76:29, 80:11, 81:27, 87:9, 88:21). The reminder was offered his whole life. He takes it up on the last day.","It's a rhetorical question meaning \"there's no way.\"","أَنَّى = \"how?\", not anna (that)."]},
  {ar:"يَا لَيْتَنِي قَدَّمْتُ لِحَيَاتِي", tr:"Yā laytanī qaddamtu li-ḥayātī", v:"24", root:"Layta — a particle expressing a wish for something IMPOSSIBLE.", meaning:"\"He will say: 'Oh, would that I had sent ahead for my life!'\"", magic:"Two hooks, and both are payoffs of threads you've been tracking.", grammar:"يَا لَيْتَ = \"Oh, if only…\" — layta is one of inna's sisters, so its subject is accusative (here the attached -nī).", why:"Qaddamtu (sent ahead) frames deeds as luggage he could have forwarded — and didn't.", note:"Yā laytanī — IDENTICAL to Nabaʾ 78:40 (\"I wish I were dust\").", more:["qaddamtu — the \"sending forward\" family — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:13, Nabaʾ 78:40, Infiṭār 82:5.","First: yā laytanī is EXACTLY the construction in Nabaʾ 78:40 — yā laytanī kuntu turābā (\"Oh, would that I were dust!\"). Same wish-particle, same hopeless regret, two surahs.","Second: qaddamtu is the fourth appearance of the \"sending ahead\" root: - Qiyāmah 75:13 — bimā qaddama wa akhkhar - Nabaʾ 78:40 — mā qaddamat yadāh - Infiṭār 82:5 — mā qaddamat wa akhkharat - Fajr 89:24 — law qaddamtu li-ḥayātī","THE KEY DIFFERENCE: in the first three, the verb is in the THIRD person — a description of what happened. Here it's FIRST PERSON, and it's a WISH: \"I wish I HAD sent ahead.\" He finally uses the word about himself — as a regret.","And \"li-ḥayātī\" — \"for MY LIFE.\" He calls the Hereafter \"my life,\" implying dunya wasn't really living. He gets the terminology right at exactly the moment it stops mattering.","And qaddama is now in FOUR surahs — but this is the only time it's FIRST PERSON, as a regret.","He calls the Hereafter \"MY LIFE.\""]},
  {ar:"وَلَا يُوثِقُ وَثَاقَهُ أَحَدٌ", tr:"Wa lā yūthiqu wathāqahu aḥad", v:"26", root:"The \"binding / tying firmly\" family — wathiqa = to be firm/reliable; wathīqah = a DOCUMENT (something binding!).", meaning:"\"and none binds as He binds.\"", magic:"V.25–26 are a matched pair, both stating divine uniqueness through comparison: - v.25 — fa-yawmaʾidhin lā yuʿadhdhibu ʿadhābahu aḥad — \"none punishes as He punishes\" - v.26.", grammar:"Cognate accusative structure: yūthiqu wathāqahu — \"binds His binding.\" The possessive -hu is what makes it a comparison: no one binds the way He does.", why:"Wathāq over \"chains\" — it's abstract, which makes it worse.", note:"Mīthāq = a COVENANT; wathīqah = a DOCUMENT. Same root as being BOUND.", more:["Mīthāq = a covenant (a binding pact); awthaqa = to bind tightly.","Wa lā yūthiqu wathāqahu aḥad — \"none binds as He binds\"","Both use a cognate structure (verb + its own verbal noun + aḥad). The rhythm is identical, and the two together cover pain and restraint.","And the root's other meanings make it richer: mīthāq = a covenant; wathīqah = a document. The same root that means a binding agreement means being bound. Those who broke every covenant end up in the tightest binding.","Compare Insān 76:4 — aʿtadnā li'l-kāfirīna salāsila wa aghlālā (chains and shackles). Insān names the instruments; Fajr names the incomparability.","You can picture a chain; you can't picture \"His binding.\"","v.25 punishing + v.26 binding — matched pair, both \"none like Him.\" Compare Insān 76:4's chains and shackles."]},
  {ar:"النَّفْسُ الْمُطْمَئِنَّةُ", tr:"An-nafsu'l-muṭmaʾinnah", v:"27", root:"The \"settling into stillness / being at rest and secure\" family.", meaning:"\"O tranquil soul!\"", magic:"THE HOOK OF THE ENTIRE SURAH, and it's already in your prayer.", grammar:"يَا أَيَّتُهَا = the FEMININE vocative (\"O you [f.]!\") — compare yā ayyuhā (masculine).", why:"Muṭmaʾinnah names the one state the whole surah's chaos cannot touch.", note:"Ṭumaʾnīnah = THE STILLNESS REQUIRED IN YOUR PRAYER. Same word.", more:["Iṭmaʾanna = to become tranquil, to settle down and stop trembling; ṭumaʾnīnah — the required STILLNESS in prayer!","(the pause in rukūʿ and sujūd before moving on).","Ṭumaʾnīnah is the technical term for the stillness you must have in each posture of ṣalāh — the moment where you stop and settle before moving. The Prophet ﷺ taught a man who prayed hastily to go back and pray again, precisely because he lacked it.","So the soul being called here is described with the exact quality your prayer trains. A nafs muṭmaʾinnah is a soul that has stopped trembling — settled, secure, no longer thrashing after the dunya.","And look at the CONTRAST with everything before it. The whole surah has been motion and violence: overflowing (ṭaghaw), pouring (ṣabba), pounding (dakkan dakkā), sweeping up greedily (lamman), ranks advancing (ṣaffan ṣaffā). Then, in v.27, everything goes STILL. The surah's most violent vocabulary is answered by its calmest word.","Also note nafs here is DEFINITE (an-nafs) — unlike Takwīr 81:14 and Infiṭār 82:5, where it was indefinite (\"a soul\"). There, an anonymous soul discovers its record. Here, a specific soul is addressed by name.","Nafs is feminine in Arabic, so the feminine form is used.","The participle is Form XII-style (iṭmaʾanna), a quadriliteral-augmented form.","The surah is all violent motion — then goes completely STILL at v.27.","And nafs is DEFINITE here (vs.","indefinite in 81:14 and 82:5)."]},
  {ar:"رَاضِيَةً مَّرْضِيَّةً", tr:"Rāḍiyatan marḍiyyah", v:"28", root:"The \"contentment / being pleased\" family — riḍā = satisfaction, pleasure.", meaning:"\"Return to your Lord, well-pleased [and] well-pleasing.\"", magic:"One of the most elegant word-pairs in the Qurʾān — same root, active and passive.", grammar:"The active/passive participle pair — fāʿil vs mafʿūl — learn this contrast; it's everywhere.", why:"Using one root twice is far more economical and beautiful than two different words for the two directions.", note:"Rāḍiyah = SHE is pleased. Marḍiyyah = she is pleased-WITH. Active and passive of ONE root — mutual satisfaction in two words.", more:["Rāḍiyah = ACTIVE participle — she is pleased (with Him). Marḍiyyah = PASSIVE participle — she is pleased-WITH (by Him).","One root, both directions. She is happy with Him, and He is happy with her. Arabic can express mutual satisfaction in two words that differ only in vowel pattern.","And you own rāḍiyah from Ghāshiyah 88:9 — li-saʿyihā rāḍiyah (\"pleased with its striving\"). Ghāshiyah: pleased with what she DID. Fajr: pleased with HIM, and He with her.","Note irjiʿī (return) — the imperative of rajaʿa. The same idea as maʾāb (Nabaʾ 78:22, 39) and iyāb (Ghāshiyah 88:25): you're going BACK, not arriving somewhere new.","Both are ḥāl (accusative states): \"return in a state of being pleased and pleasing.\"","And Ghāshiyah 88:9 had rāḍiyah too."]},
  {ar:"فَادْخُلِي فِي عِبَادِي وَادْخُلِي جَنَّتِي", tr:"Fa'dkhulī fī ʿibādī wa'dkhulī jannatī", v:"29–30", root:"Udkhulī — enter (feminine imperative). ʿibādī — My servants (from ʿabd).", meaning:"\"So enter among My servants. And enter My Garden.\"", magic:"The final two verses, and the order is the miracle.", grammar:"Feminine singular imperatives (irjiʿī, udkhulī, udkhulī) — note the -ī ending, addressing an-nafs (feminine).", why:"Ending on jannatī — \"MY Garden\" — puts the possessive pronoun of Allah as the final sound of the surah.", note:"\"Enter among MY SERVANTS\" comes FIRST — the COMPANY before the PLACE.", more:["jannatī — My Garden — from janna = to COVER (the same root as jinn, janīn (fetus), and majnūn — all covered things!","— you own this from Takwīr 81:22).","Notice: \"enter among MY SERVANTS\" comes BEFORE \"enter MY GARDEN.\" The company is named first, then the place. Belonging precedes location.","And both are possessive: ʿibādī (MY servants), jannatī (MY Garden). Not \"the servants,\" not \"the Garden.\" The two most personal words in the surah are its last two.","Set this against everything the surah has shown you: ʿĀd built pillars, Thamūd carved rock, Pharaoh drove stakes — three civilizations trying to secure a permanent place. And the soul that gets one is simply invited in. You cannot build your way there. You are called.","And the root of jannah is \"to cover\" — a garden so densely planted it covers you. The surah that opened with the DAWN (light breaking through) closes with a covering — but this time it's shade, not darkness.","Three imperatives in four verses — the surah ends on pure invitation.","Both possessive: ʿibādī, jannatī — \"MY.\" Three empires built for permanence; this soul is simply INVITED.","And jannah = from \"to COVER\" (like jinn, janīn, majnūn)."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:90, name:"Al-Balad", ar:"البلد", en:"The City", verses:20, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"The mountain you have to climb is made of people. A slave's neck, a hungry man's stomach, an orphan's need. That's the ʿaqabah.",
  special:["It asks a question, refuses to answer it, and then answers it with four rhyming words.","V.11: \"But he has not attempted the steep pass.\" v.12: \"And what can make you know what the steep pass is?\" v.13–16: the answer — and it isn't a mountain at all:","> Fakku raqabah — freeing a neck (raqabah) > Miskīnan dhā matrabah — a poor man in the dust (matrabah) > Yatīman dhā maqrabah — an orphan of near kin (maqrabah) > and later, aṣḥābu'l-maymanah — the companions of the right (maymanah)","Listen to the rhyme: ʿaqabah… raqabah… masghabah… maqrabah… matrabah… maymanah… mashʾamah. Seven words, one drumbeat. The \"steep pass\" is defined by words that sound like it.","--"],
  big:["An oath by Makkah (v.1–3) — and you, [O Prophet], are free of restriction in this city.","Man was made in toil (v.4) — kabad — struggle is the design, not a malfunction.","Two arrogant assumptions (v.5–7) — does he think none has power over him? Does he think no one saw?","Three gifts, then the choice (v.8–10) — two eyes, a tongue, two lips… and the two paths.","The steep pass (v.11–16) — freeing a neck, feeding on a day of hunger.","Two companies (v.17–20) — the right hand and the ill-omened."],
  traps:["فَكُّ (v.13) and مُّؤْصَدَةٌ (v.20) — the surah's ethical spine. Fakk = UNTIE. Muʾṣadah = BOLTED SHUT. Untie a neck, or find the door sealed.","مَتْرَبَةٍ (v.16) — from turāb, DUST — not a random word for poverty. Same root as Nabaʾ 78:33 (atrāb) and 78:40 (turābā).","أَهْلَكْتُ (v.6) — he says \"I DESTROYED,\" not \"I spent.\" His own verb convicts him.","حِلٌّ (v.2) — from the root of ḥalāl (\"untied\") and iḥlāl (leaving iḥrām). Multiple valid classical readings — don't flatten it to one.","النَّجْدَيْنِ (v.10) — means \"the two HIGH GROUNDS,\" not just \"two ways.\" Both paths are VISIBLE — that's the point.","اقْتَحَمَ (v.11) — not \"climbed\" — PLUNGED IN HEADLONG (modern iqtiḥām = storming a building).","ذِي (v.14) vs. ذَا (v.15) — same word, genitive vs. accusative, one verse apart. The five special nouns show case with a LONG vowel.","الْمَيْمَنَةِ / الْمَشْأَمَةِ (v.18–19) — each carries BOTH a direction and a fortune. al-Yaman and ash-Shām come from these roots.","تَوَاصَوْا (v.17) — Form VI = MUTUAL, like yatasāʾalūn (78:1) and taḥāḍḍūn (89:18). Not \"he advised.\"","كَبَدٍ (v.4) — from kabid, THE LIVER. Man was created IN toil, not merely with it."],
  links:[{w:"لَا أُقْسِمُ (v.1)",e:"75:1 · 81:15 · 84:16",t:"FOUR surahs, same oath construction"},{w:"أَيَحْسَبُ (v.5, 7)",e:"أَيَحْسَبُ (Qiy. 75:3, 36)",t:"Identical word — twice in each surah"},{w:"يَقْدِرَ (v.5)",e:"75:4 · 76:16 · 80:19 · 87:3 · 89:16",t:"SIXTH surah — the qadar root"},{w:"لُّبَدًا (v.6)",e:"حُبًّا جَمًّا (Fajr 89:20)",t:"Consecutive surahs on excessive māl"},{w:"أَن لَّمْ يَرَهُ أَحَدٌ (v.7)",e:"الْمِرْصَادِ (89:14) · حَافِظٌ (86:4)",t:"Three surahs answer the same delusion"},{w:"عَيْنَيْنِ / شَفَتَيْنِ / النَّجْدَيْنِ (v.8–10)",e:"الزَّوْجَيْن (Qiy. 75:39)",t:"Dual: -ayn (acc./gen.) vs -ān (nom.)"},{w:"ذِي / ذَا (v.14, 15)",e:"ذِي قُوَّةٍ (81:20) · ذِي حِجْرٍ (89:5)",t:"The five special nouns — case by long vowel"},{w:"رَقَبَةٍ (v.13)",e:"أَغْلَالًا (Insān 76:4)",t:"Neck-collars in Hell / untie a neck now"},{w:"مَسْغَبَةٍ (v.14)",e:"يُطْعِمُونَ الطَّعَامَ عَلَىٰ حُبِّهِ (Ins. 76:8)",t:"Feeding when it COSTS you"},{w:"يَتِيمًا / مِسْكِينًا (v.15–16)",e:"Insān 76:8 · Fajr 89:17–18",t:"Three surahs, same two categories"},{w:"مَقْرَبَةٍ (v.15)",e:"التُّرَاثَ (Fajr 89:19)",t:"Fajr: devour the orphan's inheritance. Balad: the reverse."},{w:"مَتْرَبَةٍ (v.16)",e:"تُرَابًا (78:40) · أَتْرَابًا (78:33)",t:"Same root — Paradise's companions / \"I wish I were dust\" / the man in the dust"},{w:"تَوَاصَوْا (v.17)",e:"تَحَاضُّونَ (Fajr 89:18)",t:"Both Form VI (mutual) — the charge and its answer"},{w:"الْمَيْمَنَةِ (v.18)",e:"بِيَمِينِهِ (Inshiqāq 84:7)",t:"The right hand — Balad names the group, Inshiqāq shows the moment"},{w:"الْمَشْأَمَةِ (v.19)",e:"وَرَاءَ ظَهْرِهِ (Inshiqāq 84:10)",t:"Right hand vs. behind the back"},{w:"أَصْحَابُ (v.18–19)",e:"صَاحِبُكُم (81:22) · صَاحِبَتِهِ (80:36)",t:"Same root — companion"},{w:"الْعَقَبَةَ (v.11)",e:"عُقْبَاهَا (Shams 91:15)",t:"Same root — the pass / its consequence"},{w:"مَا أَدْرَاكَ (v.12)",e:"82:17–18 · 83:8, 19 · 86:2",t:"The \"what could convey it to you\" formula"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"لَا أُقْسِمُ بِهَٰذَا الْبَلَدِ", tr:"Lā uqsimu bi-hādha'l-balad", v:"1", root:"Uqsimu — the \"dividing / apportioning\" family (qismah = division) — an oath settles a disputed matter.", meaning:"\"I swear by this city.\"", magic:"You own this construction from THREE surahs already: - Qiyāmah 75:1 — lā uqsimu bi-yawmi'l-qiyāmah - Takwīr 81:15 — fa-lā uqsimu bi'l-khunnas - Inshiqāq 84:16.", grammar:"The بـ is bāʾ al-qasam (the \"by\" of oaths), which is why al-balad is genitive.", why:"Balad over \"Makkah\" — it's the ordinary word for a town, which makes v.2's twist land harder.", note:"FOUR surahs open this way: 75:1, 81:15, 84:16, 90:1. And hādhā — \"THIS city,\" the ground he was standing on.", more:["balad — a town, a city, a country.","Fa-lā uqsimu bi'sh-shafaq - Balad 90:1 — lā uqsimu bi-hādha'l-balad","Four surahs, same opening move. The لا either (a) rebuts an objection before the oath lands, or (b) intensifies it. Both readings are classical.","And note hādhā — \"THIS city.\" The demonstrative makes it immediate and physical: the ground under the Prophet's ﷺ feet as he spoke. Not \"a city\" — this one, right here.","Present tense uqsimu (not aqsamtu) = a living, renewed oath."]},
  {ar:"وَأَنتَ حِلٌّ بِهَٰذَا الْبَلَدِ", tr:"Wa anta ḥillun bi-hādha'l-balad", v:"2", root:"The \"untying / making lawful / releasing\" family — ḥalla = to untie a knot, to settle in a place.", meaning:"\"and you are free of restriction in this city\" — several classical readings, explored below.", magic:"One of the most layered verses in this juzʾ, and the root's meanings all fit: - (a) A prophecy of the Conquest.", grammar:"ḥillun is a verbal noun used adjectivally — indefinite nominative, khabar of anta.", why:"ḥill is chosen precisely because Makkah is ḥaram. The whole verse lives on that contrast.", note:"Ḥalāl = \"UNTIED.\" Iḥrām (bound) / iḥlāl (released) — you do both in Hajj.", more:["ḥalāl = lawful (literally \"untied,\" released from restriction) — the opposite of ḥarām (bound/forbidden).","And iḥlāl = leaving iḥrām — the moment restrictions are lifted in Hajj!","The Prophet ﷺ would one day enter Makkah with fighting made lawful for him for a period, though he entered with famous mercy.","Makkah is ḥaram (sacred, restricted) for everyone — and this verse foretells the exception.","- (b) \"You are a dweller in this city\" — from ḥalla = to settle/reside.","- (c) A note of pathos — \"they treat you as ḥalāl (fair game) in this sacred city\" — the sanctuary protects animals and plants, but not the Prophet ﷺ.","The root-irony is the point either way: Makkah is the ḥaram — the place where everything is BOUND. And in that bound place, this word means UNBOUND.","The Hajj hook is free: iḥrām = binding yourself; iḥlāl = releasing. You do both every pilgrimage. Same root.","Note the verse repeats bi-hādha'l-balad verbatim from v.1 — an emphatic echo.","Makkah is the place where everything is BOUND — and this word means UNBOUND."]},
  {ar:"وَوَالِدٍ وَمَا وَلَدَ", tr:"Wa wālidin wa mā walad", v:"3", root:"The \"begetting / giving birth\" family — wālid = a father/parent; walad = a child; wilādah = birth.", meaning:"\"and by a begetter and what he begot.\"", magic:"The third oath, and it's deliberately unspecified.", grammar:"Both genitive after the oath-wāw. Mā walad is a relative clause — \"that which he begot.\"", why:"The indefiniteness lets the oath work at both levels — the specific founders and every family that ever existed.", note:"\"A begetter and what he begot\" — no names given. Possibly Ibrāhīm & Ismāʿīl; possibly every parent and child.", more:["Note both are INDEFINITE: \"a begetter\" and \"what he begot\" — no names. Many commentators read it as Ibrāhīm and Ismāʿīl, given the Makkan setting (they built the Kaʿbah). Others read it universally: every parent and every child.","And the universal reading connects straight to v.4: swear by every parent and child — then say \"We created man in kabad (toil).\" Because the very first thing any parent and child experience together is labour and pain. The oath is the evidence for the claim.","Note the shift: wālid is masculine (\"a begetter\") and mā walad uses mā (\"WHAT he begot\"), not man (\"who\"). Some read this as emphasizing the nature of the offspring rather than identity.","Either way it sets up v.4: parents and children know about TOIL."]},
  {ar:"فِي كَبَدٍ", tr:"Fī kabad", v:"4", root:"A superb root — kabid = THE LIVER, and by extension the middle/core of something.", meaning:"\"We have certainly created man in toil / hardship.\"", magic:"THE THESIS OF THE SURAH, and the word choice is anatomical.", grammar:"لَقَدْ + past = strong affirmation (\"We have certainly\"). Fī here means immersed in, not merely \"with.\"", why:"Kabad over a generic word for difficulty — it names hardship by the organ that feels it.", note:"Kabid = THE LIVER — hardship felt in your gut. Not \"man MEETS hardship\" — man was created IN it.", more:["From that: kabad = hardship, strain, toil — the kind that hits you in the gut.","(Arabic locates deep feeling in the liver the way English locates it in the heart.)","It doesn't say man encounters hardship. It says he was created fī kabad — IN it, as his native environment. Struggle isn't an interruption of your life. It's the medium of it.","And kabid = the LIVER — the deep interior organ. So kabad means hardship felt in your core, not on the surface.","Why does this matter so much? Because it pre-empts the complaint of Fajr 89:16 — \"my Lord has humiliated me!\" If you know you were built for toil, you stop reading hardship as a verdict. Fajr showed the man who misread the test; Balad explains why he misread it.","And it sets up v.11: you were made for climbing — so climb the ʿaqabah.","Struggle is the design, not the malfunction.","This answers Fajr 89:16's complaint."]},
  {ar:"أَيَحْسَبُ أَن لَّن يَقْدِرَ عَلَيْهِ أَحَدٌ", tr:"A-yaḥsabu an lan yaqdira ʿalayhi aḥad", v:"5", root:"Yaḥsabu — the \"counting / reckoning / calculating\" family (ḥisāb = accounting) — by extension, to suppose (the mind \"computing\" a conclusion).", meaning:"\"Does he think that no one has power over him?\"", magic:"You own this opening EXACTLY — it's the same word that opens and closes Qiyāmah.", grammar:"أَ = hamzat al-istifhām fused to the verb.", why:"Yaḥsabu (to conclude by reasoning) rather than ẓanna (a loose guess) — it targets his confident verdict, which the root itself indicts.", note:"A-yaḥsabu opens AND closes Qiyāmah (75:3, 75:36), and appears TWICE here (90:5, 90:7). The calculating man miscalculates the day of calculation.", more:["yaqdira — the \"power + precise measuring\" family — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:4, Insān 76:16, ʿAbasa 80:19, Aʿlā 87:3, Fajr 89:16.","- Qiyāmah 75:3 — a-yaḥsabu'l-insānu allan najmaʿa ʿiẓāmah - Qiyāmah 75:36 — a-yaḥsabu'l-insānu an yutraka sudā - Balad 90:5 — a-yaḥsabu an lan yaqdira ʿalayhi aḥad - Balad 90:7 — a-yaḥsabu an lam yarahu aḥad","Qiyāmah asks it twice (opening and closing). Balad asks it twice (v.5 and v.7). Four instances of the same rebuking question across two surahs.","And the irony is built into the root: the calculating creature (yaḥsabu) makes a fatal miscalculation about the Day of calculation (ḥisāb).","Note the qadara root again — FIFTH+ appearance. Here it's the \"power\" sense: no one has power over him.","Lan + present = emphatic future negation (\"will never\"), which makes his assumption absolute — and therefore absolutely wrong."]},
  {ar:"أَهْلَكْتُ مَالًا لُّبَدًا", tr:"Ahlaktu mālan lubadā", v:"6", root:"Ahlaktu — Form IV of halaka (to perish) = to destroy / squander; halāk = destruction.", meaning:"\"He says, 'I have squandered vast wealth.'\"", magic:"The verb he chooses convicts him without any comment needed.", grammar:"Form IV past, 1st person. Lubadā is an adjective for mālan, accusative, carrying the rhyme.", why:"Ahlaktu over anfaqtu (spent) — because he's not spending, he's destroying, and the Qurʾān makes him say so.", note:"He boasts: \"I DESTROYED vast wealth.\" He picked the verb ahlaka — to annihilate. Proud of destruction.", more:["lubad — the \"piling up / matting together\" family — labada = to stick together in a mass; lubdah = a matted clump (as of felted wool).","He's boasting. But the word he uses for \"spent\" is ahlaktu — literally \"I DESTROYED / I caused to perish.\" He thinks he's saying \"look how much I spent\"; the Qurʾān lets his own verb say \"look how much I annihilated.\"","He is proud of destruction. And note what's missing: no mention of what it was spent ON. The boast is about the amount, not the purpose.","And lubad means \"heaped, matted, piled in a solid mass\" — wealth so abundant it clumps together. Compare Fajr 89:20 — tuḥibbūna'l-māla ḥubban jammā (\"you love wealth with an overflowing love\"). Two consecutive surahs, both on excessive attachment to māl.","And lubad = wealth so much it CLUMPS.","(Compare Fajr 89:20's ḥubban jammā.)"]},
  {ar:"أَن لَّمْ يَرَهُ أَحَدٌ", tr:"An lam yarahu aḥad", v:"7", root:"Raʾā — to see (ruʾyah = seeing; raʾy = an opinion, a \"view\").", meaning:"\"Does he think that no one saw him?\"", magic:"The second assumption, and it's more damning than the first.", grammar:"لَمْ + jussive = past negation (\"did not see\"). Note the contrast with v.5's lan (future).", why:"The plain verb \"see\" is stronger than any theological term — it's the simplest possible refutation.", note:"V.5 = \"no one can STOP me\" (lan, future). v.7 = \"no one SAW me\" (lam, past). The two pillars of wrongdoing.", more:["V.5 was about POWER: \"no one can control me.\" v.7 is about OBSERVATION: \"no one saw me.\" Together they're the two pillars of every wrongdoing: I can't be stopped and I can't be caught.","And v.7 is the more revealing. A man who boasts about squandering (v.6) implies he wants to be SEEN spending — but assumes he wasn't seen doing whatever he actually did. He wants witnesses for his generosity and none for his life.","Compare Fajr 89:14 — inna rabbaka la-bi'l-mirṣād (\"your Lord is at the lookout\") — and Ṭāriq 86:4 — in kullu nafsin lammā ʿalayhā ḥāfiẓ (\"over every soul there is a guardian\"). Three consecutive surahs answering the same delusion.","v.5 denies future power; v.7 denies past observation.","Answered by Fajr 89:14 (mirṣād) and Ṭāriq 86:4 (ḥāfiẓ)."]},
  {ar:"عَيْنَيْنِ... لِسَانًا وَشَفَتَيْنِ", tr:"ʿaynayn… lisānan wa shafatayn", v:"8–9", root:"ʿaynayn — two eyes (dual of ʿayn; ʿayn also means a spring and the essence of a thing).", meaning:"\"Have We not made for him two eyes, and a tongue and two lips?\"", magic:"The answer to v.7, delivered as a body inventory — and the logic is precise.", grammar:"DUAL nouns in the accusative: ʿaynayni, shafatayni — note the ending -ayn (accusative/genitive) vs -ān (nominative).", why:"Naming sensory equipment answers a claim about being unobserved — the reply is physical, not philosophical.", note:"He said \"no one SAW me\" → \"didn't We give you EYES?\" TWO eyes, TWO lips, ONE tongue — the tongue kept behind two gates.", more:["lisān — a tongue (also a language!","— al-lughah al-ʿarabiyyah / lisān ʿarabī).","shafatayn — two lips (dual of shafah).","He said \"no one SAW me\" (v.7). The reply: \"didn't We give you EYES?\" The One who manufactured sight is being told that seeing is impossible. The refutation is in your own face.","And notice the DUALS: two eyes, two lips — but ONE tongue. Classical scholars noted this: two eyes to see with, two lips to close over the tongue, and only one tongue — kept behind two gates.","The three gifts also map onto the surah's demands: eyes (to SEE the orphan and the poor), tongue and lips (to speak up for them, and to urge one another — the very thing Fajr 89:18 said they failed to do).","You met this in Qiyāmah 75:39 (az-zawjayn).","And a-lam najʿal = a rhetorical question expecting \"yes.\"","Dual: -ayn (acc./gen.) vs -ān (nom.)."]},
  {ar:"النَّجْدَيْنِ", tr:"An-najdayn", v:"10", root:"The \"high, elevated, open ground\" family — najd = raised land, an elevated plateau (hence Najd, the highland region of Arabia!).", meaning:"\"And We guided him to the two heights / the two paths.\"", magic:"The word for \"path\" here is a word for HIGH GROUND, and that's the whole design.", grammar:"DUAL, accusative (an-najdayni) — third dual in three verses (ʿaynayn, shafatayn, najdayn).", why:"Najd over sabīl (path) — because najd means you can SEE it.", note:"Najd = HIGH, VISIBLE GROUND (like the region Najd). Both paths are on high ground — neither is hidden.", more:["Also najdah = help/rescue (from the sense of standing out prominently to aid someone).","Most translations say \"the two ways,\" which loses the image. Najd means an ELEVATED, VISIBLE path — high ground you can see clearly from a distance. So both roads — good and evil — are described as obvious. Neither is hidden.","That answers a possible excuse before it's raised: \"I didn't know which way to go.\" Both routes are on high ground. You could see them both.","And it sets up v.11 with a mountaineering metaphor already running: you were shown two heights (najdayn), and you didn't attempt the steep pass (ʿaqabah). The vocabulary of terrain runs straight through.","The surah is drilling the dual form.","\"I didn't know\" isn't available.","Third dual in a row: eyes, lips, paths."]},
  {ar:"فَلَا اقْتَحَمَ الْعَقَبَةَ", tr:"Fa-la'qtaḥama'l-ʿaqabah", v:"11", root:"Iqtaḥama — a magnificent word: the \"plunging in headlong / storming through with difficulty\" family.", meaning:"\"But he has not attempted the steep ascent.\"", magic:"The two words together are the surah's centre of gravity.", grammar:"The lā here is a NEGATION of the past (\"he has not attempted\"), a known construction often read as implying \"he ought to have.\" Some readers take it as a wish/incitement: \"would that he had!\" Both are attested.", why:"Iqtaḥama frames righteousness as something you charge at, not something you drift into.", note:"Iqtiḥām = STORMING a building in modern Arabic. Not \"climbed\" — PLUNGED IN HEADLONG.", more:["Iqtiḥām = to throw yourself into something hard and frightening, to charge in.","(Modern Arabic uses iqtiḥām for storming a building.) ʿaqabah — a steep mountain pass, a difficult uphill road — from ʿ-q-b, the \"following / consequence / heel\" family (ʿāqibah = the outcome; ʿuqbā = the final end — which closes Shams 91:15!).","Iqtaḥama is not \"climbed\" — it's \"PLUNGED IN HEADLONG.\" It's the word for charging into something you'd normally avoid. Which tells you the ʿaqabah is not gentle. It requires a running start and a decision.","And ʿaqabah's root is the \"consequence\" root — so the steep pass is literally named after what comes after. It's the climb whose whole meaning is its outcome.","Now connect it to v.4: man was created fī kabad (IN TOIL). So you're already climbing — you're built for it. The charge in v.11 isn't \"climbing is too hard for you.\" It's \"you're toiling anyway, and you didn't spend the toil on THIS.\"","And you'll meet the root again as the last word of Shams 91:15 (ʿuqbāhā, \"its consequence\"). Two consecutive surahs, same root, opening and closing.","And ʿaqabah ← the root of ʿāqibah (the OUTCOME).","You were made fī kabad (v.4) — you're climbing anyway."]},
  {ar:"فَكُّ رَقَبَةٍ", tr:"Fakku raqabah", v:"13", root:"Fakk — the \"undoing / unlocking / releasing\" family — fakka = to untie, to unfasten, to break something open; infikāk = separation.", meaning:"\"[It is] the freeing of a neck.\"", magic:"The first answer to \"what is the steep pass?\" — and it's stunningly concrete.", grammar:"Iḍāfah (\"the freeing of a neck\"); fakku is a verbal noun (maṣdar).", why:"Raqabah (a neck) rather than ʿabd (a slave) — because it names the point of bondage, not the person's status.", note:"Raqabah = A NECK = a slave (the collared part).", more:["raqabah — A NECK — and by a beautiful Qurʾānic convention, \"a neck\" means \"a slave\" (the part standing for the whole, because the neck bore the collar).","Also raqīb = a watcher (one who watches over — from craning the neck!), and murāqabah = surveillance.","The question in v.12 was cosmic-sounding: \"what can make you know what the steep ascent is?\" You expect something vast. The answer is: untie a neck.","And the kināya (metonymy) is powerful: the human being is called simply \"a NECK\" — because that's all a slave was reduced to: the part you put a collar on. The word itself carries the indignity, which is why freeing it matters.","Compare Insān 76:4 — aʿtadnā li'l-kāfirīna salāsila wa aghlālā (chains and shackles), where ghull is specifically the neck-collar. Insān: necks bound in Hell. Balad: unbind a neck now. What you do to necks here is what happens to yours there.","And listen: ʿaqabah → raqabah. The answer RHYMES with the question. The mountain and the neck sound the same.","Note there's a well-known variant reading as a verb (fakka raqabatan) — both are established.","The cosmic-sounding question gets answered: \"UNTIE A NECK.\" ʿaqabah → raqabah — the answer RHYMES with the question.","And Insān 76:4 had aghlāl — neck-collars in Hell."]},
  {ar:"يَوْمٍ ذِي مَسْغَبَةٍ", tr:"Yawmin dhī masghabah", v:"14", root:"The \"severe hunger\" family — saghiba = to be famished; masghabah = a state or occasion of hunger.", meaning:"\"or feeding on a day of severe hunger.\"", magic:"The timing is the entire moral point, and it's easy to miss.", grammar:"ذِي = \"possessor of\" — the five special nouns again (genitive: dhī).", why:"Masghabah names a day of hunger, not a hungry person — which is what makes the giving costly.", note:"Not \"feed the hungry\" — feed ON A DAY WHEN EVERYONE IS STARVING, including you. That's why it's a mountain.", more:["A noun of place/state built on the mafʿalah pattern.","It doesn't say \"feeding the hungry.\" It says feeding on a day of hunger — a day when EVERYONE is starving, including YOU. A famine day. A day when giving food away means going without.","That's why it belongs in the ʿaqabah. Feeding someone from surplus is easy. Feeding them when you're hungry is a mountain.","And you own the exact scene from Insān 76:8 — wa yuṭʿimūna'ṭ-ṭaʿāma ʿalā ḥubbihi miskīnan wa yatīman wa asīrā (\"they feed, despite loving it, the poor, the orphan, and the captive\"). Insān showed people doing it. Balad names it as the climb. Same three-category structure too.","Fourth appearance across your surahs (Takwīr 81:20, Fajr 89:5, 89:10, here).","The mafʿalah pattern (masghabah, maqrabah, matrabah) recurs three times in three verses.","And Insān 76:8 showed exactly this: feeding despite loving the food."]},
  {ar:"يَتِيمًا ذَا مَقْرَبَةٍ", tr:"Yatīman dhā maqrabah", v:"15", root:"Yatīm — an orphan — you own it from Insān 76:8 and Fajr 89:17. maqrabah — the \"nearness / kinship\" family.", meaning:"\"an orphan of near relationship.\"", magic:"The detail \"of near kin\" is doing something specific and uncomfortable.", grammar:"Dhā — accusative of the five special nouns (compare dhī genitive in v.14).", why:"Maqrabah (kinship) rather than a general word — because proximity makes it harder, not easier.", note:"A RELATED orphan — harder, because he may have a CLAIM on you.", more:["Qarīb = near and a relative; qarābah = kinship; al-muqarrabūn = those brought near (Muṭaffifīn 83:21, 28).","Why specify a RELATED orphan? Two classical readings, both excellent: - (a) The reward is greater — you've combined charity with maintaining family ties (ṣilat ar-raḥim). - (b) It's HARDER. A relative's orphan may be entitled to inheritance you're holding. Helping a stranger costs money; helping a related orphan may cost you an argument, a claim, and your pride.","And reading (b) connects directly to Fajr 89:19 — wa ta'kulūna't-turātha aklan lammā (\"you devour inheritance, sweeping it all up\"). Fajr accused them of eating the orphan's inheritance. Balad, the very next surah, names the opposite as the steep climb. Consecutive surahs, one continuous argument.","Same word, different case, one verse apart — a free grammar lesson.","Fajr 89:19 accused them of devouring the orphan's inheritance; Balad 90:15 names the reverse as the climb.","Note dhā (acc., v.15) vs dhī (gen., v.14)."]},
  {ar:"مِسْكِينًا ذَا مَتْرَبَةٍ", tr:"Miskīnan dhā matrabah", v:"16", root:"Miskīn — the \"stillness / being stuck\" family (sakana = to be still) — someone so poor they can't move up.", meaning:"\"or a poor person in the dust.\"", magic:"THE BEST CROSS-SURAH CONNECTION IN THIS SURAH — and it's a genuine payoff.", grammar:"Matrabah — same mafʿalah pattern as masghabah and maqrabah.", why:"Matrabah over \"very poor\" — it puts him physically on the ground, which is what makes lifting him a climb.", note:"Matrabah = \"IN THE DUST\" — from turāb.", more:["— turāb = dust/earth — you own it from Nabaʾ 78:40 (yā laytanī kuntu turābā) and Nabaʾ 78:33 (atrābā).","Matrabah literally means \"a state of DUST\" — someone so destitute they're clinging to the bare earth, with nothing between them and the ground. No mat, no bedding, no possessions — just dust.","Now recall the t-r-b root's journey: - Nabaʾ 78:33 — atrāb — \"well-matched companions\" in Paradise (from children who played in the same dust) - Nabaʾ 78:40 — turābā — the denier's wish: \"I wish I were DUST\" - Balad 90:16 — matrabah — the poor man lying in the dust","One root: the company of Paradise, the wish to be nothing, and the person you're being told to lift out of the dirt.","And the moral logic is beautiful: the denier in Nabaʾ wishes he were dust to escape. The poor man in Balad is in the dust, right now, and you can do something about it.","Three in three verses, all rhyming with ʿaqabah and raqabah.","The rhyme IS the structure.","Nabaʾ 78:33 (atrāb, Paradise's companions), Nabaʾ 78:40 (turābā, \"I wish I were dust\"), Balad 90:16 (matrabah, the man in the dust).","He's already in the dust — go lift him."]},
  {ar:"تَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْمَرْحَمَةِ", tr:"Tawāṣaw bi'ṣ-ṣabri wa tawāṣaw bi'l-marḥamah", v:"17", root:"Tawāṣaw — the \"enjoining / advising / bequeathing\" family — waṣiyyah = a WILL/bequest; awṣā = to enjoin.", meaning:"\"Then he was of those who believed and enjoined one another to patience and enjoined one another to compassion.\"", magic:"The ʿaqabah turns out to require a COMMUNITY, not just an individual — and tawāṣaw is how you know.", grammar:"Form VI (تَفَاعَلَ) = mutual — you've now met it three times: yatasāʾalūn (78:1), taḥāḍḍūn (89:18), tawāṣaw (90:17). Spot the ta- + long ā.", why:"Marḥamah (the noun of the raḥim root) rather than a generic word for kindness — it names mercy at its most instinctive.", note:"Raḥim = THE WOMB. Compassion = womb-mercy. And tawāṣaw is FORM VI (mutual) — the exact answer to Fajr 89:18's taḥāḍḍūn.", more:["Form VI = MUTUAL: advising ONE ANOTHER.","marḥamah — the \"mercy / womb\" family — raḥmah = mercy; raḥim = THE WOMB; ar-Raḥmān/ar-Raḥīm.","Form VI (tawāṣaw) is MUTUAL — not \"he advised\" but \"they advised each other.\" The climb can't be done alone; you need people urging you.","And this is the exact answer to Fajr 89:18 — wa lā taḥāḍḍūna ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn (\"you do not urge one another to feed the poor\"). Both are Form VI mutual verbs. Fajr's charge: you don't urge each other. Balad's solution: they urge each other. Consecutive surahs, problem and answer.","And marḥamah shares a root with raḥim, THE WOMB — so \"compassion\" in Arabic is built from the organ that carries a child. The mercy being asked for is womb-mercy: the instinctive, protective kind. Which is exactly right for a passage about orphans.","Note also thumma (\"THEN he was of those who believed\") — many scholars note that thumma here isn't chronological but indicates rank: this is the higher, weightier step. The deeds of v.13–16 without īmān don't complete the climb.","Note tawāṣaw is weak-final, so the plural ending contracts.","The climb needs a community."]},
  {ar:"أَصْحَابُ الْمَيْمَنَةِ", tr:"Aṣḥābu'l-maymanah", v:"18", root:"The \"right hand / blessing / good fortune\" family — yamīn = the RIGHT HAND (and an oath, sworn with the right hand!).", meaning:"\"Those are the companions of the right hand.\"", magic:"The word carries BOTH meanings simultaneously and you shouldn't choose: - \"the right side\" — where the record is handed to you (Inshiqāq 84:7 — man ūtiya kitābahu bi-yamīnih!) - \"blessedness / good fortune\" — from yumn", grammar:"Aṣḥāb = plural of ṣāḥib (companion) — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:36 (ṣāḥibatih, his wife) and Takwīr 81:22 (ṣāḥibukum, \"your companion\").", why:"Maymanah over a plain word for the saved — it embeds blessing into the very name of the group.", note:"Yamīn = RIGHT HAND; yumn = BLESSING; al-Yaman = the country. All one root.", more:["Yumn = blessing, good fortune; and al-Yaman — the country — from the same root (the land \"on the right\" as you face east, and the \"blessed\" land).","So aṣḥāb al-maymanah = simultaneously \"the people of the right hand\" AND \"the people of blessing.\" In Arabic these are the same idea.","And you own the receiving-scene from Inshiqāq 84:7–9 — the man given his record in his right hand, reckoned easily, returning to his family masrūrā (delighted). Balad names the group; Inshiqāq shows the moment.","\"People of the right\" = \"people of blessing\" — same word.","See the moment in Inshiqāq 84:7."]},
  {ar:"أَصْحَابُ الْمَشْأَمَةِ", tr:"Aṣḥābu'l-mashʾamah", v:"19", root:"The \"left hand / ill omen / misfortune\" family — shuʾm = bad luck, ill omen; ash-shimāl = the left.", meaning:"\"they are the companions of the left / of ill fortune.\"", magic:"A perfect mirror of v.18, built the same way:", grammar:"Note the hamzah in mashʾamah (م-ش-أ-م) — don't confuse the root with sh-m-l (shimāl, left), though they're related in meaning.", why:"Mashʾamah embeds misfortune into the group's name, exactly as maymanah embeds blessing.", note:"Al-Yaman (right/blessed) vs. ash-Shām (left) — two countries named from these two roots. Direction and fortune, one word each.", more:["And ash-Shām (Greater Syria) — from the same root (the land \"on the left\" as you face east).","Both words carry a direction AND a fortune, and both gave their names to a country. Arabic's geography and its moral vocabulary run on the same roots.","And note the grammatical difference: v.18 says ulāʾika hum (\"THOSE are they\") for the right; v.19 says hum (\"THEY are\") for the left. Both use the separating pronoun for emphasis — the two groups are being sharply distinguished.","You'll also see the pairing in Inshiqāq 84:10 — man ūtiya kitābahu warāʾa ẓahrih (\"given his record behind his back\"). Right hand vs. behind the back.","And Inshiqāq 84:7 vs 84:10: right hand vs."]},
  {ar:"نَارٌ مُّؤْصَدَةٌ", tr:"Nārun muʾṣadah", v:"20", root:"The \"closing / shutting tight / sealing\" family — awṣada = to shut a door firmly and bolt it; wiṣād = the bolt or covering of a door.", meaning:"\"Over them will be a Fire closed in upon them.\"", magic:"The final word of the surah, and it closes a door the surah opened.", grammar:"Passive participle (Form IV, awṣada → mūṣad/muʾṣad).", why:"Muʾṣadah focuses on the sealing, which is worse than the burning.", note:"Awṣada = TO BOLT A DOOR SHUT. The horror is the LID, not the fire. v.13: fakk — UNTIE a neck. v.20: muʾṣadah — the door BOLTED.", more:["Muʾṣadah = shut, bolted, sealed over. The horror isn't the fire — it's the LID. A fire in the open has an escape direction. A fire with the door bolted has none.","And here's the connection that makes it land: the surah opened with v.2 — wa anta ḥillun bi-hādha'l-balad — \"you are UNBOUND, RELEASED (ḥill) in this city.\" It ends with a fire that is BOLTED SHUT (muʾṣadah). Released at the start; sealed at the end. The surah's first and last images are a knot untied and a door locked.","And one more: v.13 asked you to perform fakk — an UNTYING (freeing a neck). v.20 shows what happens to those who wouldn't: a door muʾṣadah — SHUT. Untie a neck, or find the door bolted. The surah's whole ethic in two words.","Note there are two established recitations of the spelling here (muʾṣadah / mūṣadah) — both correct."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:91, name:"Ash-Shams", ar:"الشمس", en:"The Sun", verses:15, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Seven verses apart, the same word. He evened you into a person; He evened them into rubble. If you remember ONE thing from this surah, make it this.",
  special:["One word appears twice — and it means \"He shaped a soul\" the first time and \"He flattened a town\" the second.","V.7: wa nafsin wa mā sawwāhā — \"By the soul and the One who PROPORTIONED IT.\" v.14: fa-damdama ʿalayhim rabbuhum bi-dhanbihim fa-sawwāhā — \"so their Lord crushed them for their sin and LEVELLED IT.\"","Identical word. Sawwāhā. In v.7 it means to perfect, to balance, to bring into beautiful proportion. In v.14 it means to flatten to the ground, to level, to make even with the dust.","Same verb builds a soul and razes a civilization. Because \"making even\" (sawwā) can mean perfectly balanced or perfectly flat — and which one you get depends on what you did with the soul in v.7.","--"],
  big:["Eleven oaths (v.1–7) — the longest oath-run in the Qurʾān: sun, its brightness, moon, day, night, sky, earth, and the SOUL.","The design of the soul (v.8) — He inspired it with its corruption and its guarding.","The verdict (v.9–10) — succeeded who purified it; failed who buried it.","The case study (v.11–15) — Thamūd, the she-camel, and one man who acted for everyone."],
  traps:["سَوَّاهَا appears TWICE (v.7 and v.14) — \"He PERFECTED a soul\" and \"He FLATTENED a town.\" Identical word, opposite outcomes. The single most important thing in this surah.","طَحَاهَا (v.6, ṬĀʾ) vs. دَحَاهَا (Nāziʿāt 79:30, DĀL) — near-identical sound, same meaning (\"spread out\"), different letters. A genuine confusion risk.","زَكَّاهَا (v.9) vs. دَسَّاهَا (v.10) — both are things you do to a SEED: clear it to grow vs. bury it out of sight. Don't read them as generic good/bad.","دَسَّاهَا (v.10) — means BURY/CONCEAL, not \"destroy.\" The soul is still down there, buried alive.","أَلْهَمَ (v.8) — from lahima, TO GULP DOWN WHOLE. Inspiration, not education — it was pre-installed, not taught.","تَقْوَاهَا (v.8) — NOT \"fear.\" From wiqāyah = PROTECTION/ARMOUR. A muttaqī carries a shield; he doesn't cower.","انبَعَثَ أَشْقَاهَا (v.12) — SINGULAR (one man) — but v.14's punishment is PLURAL (ʿalayhim). One acted; all were destroyed, because all sent him.","بِذَنبِهِمْ (v.14) — SINGULAR sin, PLURAL people. One dhanb, all of them.","وَمَا سَوَّاهَا (v.7) — note mā, not man. Classical readings treat it as maṣdariyyah or as emphasizing the attribute of power. Don't assume it's an error.","عُقْبَاهَا (v.15) — from ʿaqib, A HEEL. Same root as al-ʿaqabah (Balad 90:11), the steep climb. Two consecutive surahs, one root."],
  links:[{w:"سَوَّاهَا (v.7 and v.14)",e:"75:4 · 75:38 · 79:28 · 82:7 · 87:2",t:"EIGHT appearances, SEVEN surahs — a soul perfected, a town levelled"},{w:"ضُحَاهَا (v.1)",e:"ضُحَاهَا (Nāz. 79:29, 79:46)",t:"Identical word — creation's gift / your whole lifespan"},{w:"يَغْشَاهَا (v.4)",e:"الْغَاشِيَةِ (Ghāsh. 88:1)",t:"Same root — night covers the sun / the Day covers you"},{w:"طَحَاهَا (v.6)",e:"دَحَاهَا (Nāz. 79:30)",t:"Near-identical sound & meaning — ṬĀʾ vs DĀL"},{w:"طَحَاهَا (v.6)",e:"مِهَادًا (78:6) · سُطِحَتْ (88:20)",t:"Four verbs for spreading the earth"},{w:"نَفْسٍ (v.7)",e:"نَفْسٌ (81:14) · نَفْسٌ (82:5)",t:"Indefinite — \"a soul,\" yours"},{w:"نَفْسٍ (v.7)",e:"تَنَفَّسَ (Takwīr 81:18)",t:"Nafs (soul) ← nafas (breath)"},{w:"فُجُورَهَا (v.8)",e:"75:5 · 76:6 · 80:42 · 82:3 · 82:14 · 89:1",t:"SEVEN appearances — bursting past limits"},{w:"تَقْوَاهَا (v.8)",e:"الْمُتَّقِينَ (Nabaʾ 78:31)",t:"Same root — wiqāyah = ARMOUR, not fear"},{w:"فَأَلْهَمَهَا (v.8)",e:"النَّجْدَيْنِ (Balad 90:10)",t:"Roads visible OUTSIDE, map installed INSIDE"},{w:"قَدْ أَفْلَحَ مَن زَكَّاهَا (v.9)",e:"قَدْ أَفْلَحَ مَن تَزَكَّىٰ (Aʿlā 87:14)",t:"Near-identical verse (Form II vs Form V)"},{w:"زَكَّاهَا (v.9)",e:"79:18 (Pharaoh) · 80:3, 7 (blind man) · 87:14",t:"FIVE surahs"},{w:"دَسَّاهَا (v.10)",e:"كَفُورًا (Insān 76:3) · رَانَ (Muṭaff. 83:14)",t:"Burying a seed / rust over the heart"},{w:"بِطَغْوَاهَا (v.11)",e:"78:22 · 79:17 · 79:37 · 89:11",t:"SIX appearances — bursting the banks"},{w:"انبَعَثَ (v.12)",e:"يَوْمَ الْبَعْثِ",t:"The RESURRECTION root, used for standing up to sin"},{w:"أَشْقَاهَا (v.12)",e:"الْأَشْقَى (Aʿlā 87:11)",t:"Same superlative — Shams gives him a face"},{w:"دَمْدَمَ (v.14)",e:"عَسْعَسَ (Takwīr 81:17)",t:"Reduplicated roots — the sound IS the meaning"},{w:"فَـ فَـ فَـ (v.14)",e:"فَكَذَّبَ فَحَشَرَ فَنَادَىٰ (Nāz. 79:21–23)",t:"Accelerating fa-chain"},{w:"عُقْبَاهَا (v.15)",e:"الْعَقَبَةَ (Balad 90:11)",t:"Same root — the CLIMB and its OUTCOME"},{w:"ثَمُودُ (v.11)",e:"ثَمُودَ (Fajr 89:9) · (Burūj 85:18)",t:"Three surahs — the same case study"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"وَضُحَاهَا", tr:"Wa ḍuḥāhā", v:"1", root:"The \"forenoon brightness\" family — ḍuḥā = mid-morning, when the sun is well up and everything is fully lit.", meaning:"\"By the sun and its brightness.\"", magic:"You own this word from Nāziʿāt — it appears there twice, and its journey is worth holding: - Nāziʿāt 79:29 — wa akhraja ḍuḥāhā — creation's daily gift of light - Nāziʿāt 79:46.", grammar:"The wāw is wāw al-qasam (the \"by\" of oaths), so the noun is genitive.", why:"ḍuḥā names the brightest, most ordinary part of your day — evidence you walk through constantly.", note:"You pray Ṣalāt aḍ-Ḍuḥā in this light. Nāziʿāt 79:29 (creation's gift) → 79:46 (your whole life) → 91:1 (an oath).", more:["You know it as Ṣalāt aḍ-Ḍuḥā and Sūrat aḍ-Ḍuḥā.","Illā ʿashiyyatan aw ḍuḥāhā — the measure of your ENTIRE LIFE (\"an evening or its morning\") - Shams 91:1 — the sun's brightness, sworn by","Same word: a daily gift, the length of a lifetime, and now an oath.","And note the pairing: the SUN and ITS brightness. Not two separate things — a thing and what it gives off. That pattern runs through all seven oath-pairs: an object and its function.","Note the -hā suffix returns at the end of EVERY verse in this surah — all fifteen.","And EVERY verse in this surah ends in -hā."]},
  {ar:"تَلَاهَا", tr:"Talāhā", v:"2", root:"The \"following after\" family — talā = to follow, to come after.", meaning:"\"and by the moon when it follows it.\"", magic:"The double meaning is a gift you use every day.", grammar:"Weak-final past verb with attached pronoun -hā (referring to the sun).", why:"Talā over a plain word for \"rises\" — it makes the moon a follower, which is the whole relationship.", note:"Talā = TO FOLLOW and TO RECITE. Tilāwah = \"following along.\" The moon RECITES the sun — transmitting light it didn't make.", more:["And from that: talā = TO RECITE (reciting is following the text word by word!), tilāwah = recitation.","Talā means both \"to follow\" AND \"to recite.\" Why? Because when you recite, you're following the words in sequence. So tilāwah (the word for Qurʾān recitation) literally means \"following along.\"","And here the moon follows the sun — rising as it sets, taking over the shift, borrowing its light (recall Nabaʾ 78:13: the sun is a sirāj — a burning lamp — while the moon merely reflects).","A beautiful thought worth keeping: the moon recites the sun. It has no light of its own; it faithfully transmits what it received. Which is exactly what tilāwah is.","Note idhā + past tense = \"when it [does]\" — a habitual, recurring action."]},
  {ar:"جَلَّاهَا", tr:"Jallāhā", v:"3", root:"The \"making clear / unveiling / polishing to a shine\" family — jalā = to reveal, to make manifest, to burnish.", meaning:"\"and by the day when it displays it.\"", magic:"The verb means \"to POLISH into visibility.\"", grammar:"Form II (jallā) = intensive/causative — \"made it fully clear.\" Weak-final, with -hā.", why:"Jallā over \"showed\" — it carries the sense of polishing, of active display, not passive presence.", note:"Tajallī = a MANIFESTATION; jalāʾ = POLISH. The day BUFFS the sun into view. v.3 unveils, v.4 covers — perfect opposites.", more:["Tajallī = a manifestation; jaliyy = crystal clear; and jalāʾ = polish/burnishing (what you do to metal to make it gleam).","The day doesn't create the sun — it jallāhā: buffs it into full view, like polishing a mirror until it gleams. The image is of something being revealed by removal of what covered it, not by being made.","And notice the pair with v.4: the day unveils (jallāhā); the night covers (yaghshāhā). A perfect ṭibāq — reveal and conceal, one verse apart.","Keep this word: tajallī — it's the word used for divine manifestation (as when the mountain crumbled at Allah's tajallī). Same root."]},
  {ar:"يَغْشَاهَا", tr:"Yaghshāhā", v:"4", root:"The \"covering over / enveloping\" family — ghashiya = to cover completely; ghishāwah = a covering over the eyes.", meaning:"\"and by the night when it covers it.\"", magic:"You own this root exactly — it names the previous surah's Day.", grammar:"Present tense, weak-final, with -hā. Note the shift from past to present mid-oath — deliberate, not a slip.", why:"Ghashiya implies covering with no gaps, which is what night does and what the Day will do.", note:"Al-Ghāshiyah (88:1) — THE ENVELOPER, a name for the Day. Same verb here for night covering the sun. Every sunset is a rehearsal.", more:["Ghāshiyah 88:1 — hal atāka ḥadīthu'l-ghāshiyah — \"the report of THE ENVELOPER.\" The Day of Judgment is named with this verb.","And here it describes the night covering the sun — something that happens every single evening. So the connection is: what night does to the sun gently and daily, the Ghāshiyah does to everything, permanently. You watch a rehearsal of that Day every sunset.","Note also the tense shift: v.2–3 use PAST verbs (talāhā, jallāhā); v.4 uses PRESENT (yaghshāhā). Classical readers note this gives the covering a sense of ongoing, continuous action — the night is always in the process of covering somewhere."]},
  {ar:"طَحَاهَا", tr:"ṭaḥāhā", v:"6", root:"The \"spreading out / rolling flat\" family — ṭaḥā = to spread wide, to extend outward, also to push/drive something away and out.", meaning:"\"and by the earth and the One who spread it out.\"", magic:"A rare word — it appears only here in the entire Qurʾān.", grammar:"Wa mā ṭaḥāhā — note mā, not man.", why:"ṭaḥā conveys spreading with force and reach — the earth pushed out wide.", note:"Daḥāhā (Nāziʿāt 79:30, DĀL) vs ṭaḥāhā (Shams 91:6, ṬĀʾ). Nearly identical sound and meaning — a real trap.", more:["And you own its twin from Nāziʿāt 79:30 — wa'l-arḍa baʿda dhālika daḥāhā (\"and the earth, after that, He spread it out\"). Two verbs, nearly identical in sound (daḥā / ṭaḥā) and identical in meaning — one surah apart in your study, twelve in the muṣḥaf. This is a genuine confusion risk: daḥāhā (79:30, with dāl) vs. ṭaḥāhā (91:6, with ṭāʾ).","Both mean \"spread out for use\" — the earth prepared as a livable surface, echoing Nabaʾ 78:6 (mihādan, a cradle) and Ghāshiyah 88:20 (suṭiḥat, spread flat).","Four surahs, four verbs for the same act: mihād (cradled), daḥā (spread), suṭiḥat (flattened), ṭaḥā (spread wide).","Classical scholars discuss this: mā here is widely read as maṣdariyyah (\"and the spreading of it\") or as referring to Allah with emphasis on the attribute of power rather than the person.","Four verbs for spreading the earth: mihād (78:6), daḥā (79:30), suṭiḥat (88:20), ṭaḥā (91:6)."]},
  {ar:"وَنَفْسٍ وَمَا سَوَّاهَا", tr:"Wa nafsin wa mā sawwāhā", v:"7", root:"Nafs — the SOUL — from the root of nafas, BREATH (you met tanaffasa in Takwīr 81:18 — \"the morning when it breathes\").", meaning:"\"By the soul and the One who proportioned it.\"", magic:"THE SEVENTH AND FINAL OATH — and it's the turn that makes the surah.", grammar:"Note nafsin is INDEFINITE (\"a soul\") — the same indefiniteness as Takwīr 81:14 and Infiṭār 82:5. Any soul.", why:"Ending the oath-list on the soul shifts the whole surah from astronomy to psychology in one word.", note:"Six cosmic oaths… then YOUR SOUL, as the climax. Sawwā — SEVEN surahs, and TWICE in this one (v.7 and v.14).", more:["sawwā — the \"making even / proportioning / perfecting\" family.","Look at what the first six oaths were: sun, moon, day, night, sky, earth. Six cosmic objects. Then the seventh: YOUR SOUL. The Qurʾān places your inner self on the same list as the sun and the sky — and saves it for last, as the climax.","And sawwā — you now own it across SEVEN surahs. This is your most-tracked root: - Qiyāmah 75:4 — re-proportioning your fingertips - Qiyāmah 75:38 — proportioning you from a clot - Nāziʿāt 79:28 — proportioning the sky - Infiṭār 82:7 — proportioning you - Aʿlā 87:2 — khalaqa fa-sawwā - Shams 91:7 — proportioning the soul - Shams 91:14 — levelling a town to the ground (seven verses later!)","A perfectly balanced soul (v.7) and a perfectly flattened city (v.14). One verb.","A balanced soul, and a levelled town."]},
  {ar:"فَأَلْهَمَهَا", tr:"Fa-alhamahā", v:"8", root:"The \"swallowing whole / taking in at once\" family — lahima = to gulp down, to absorb in one go.", meaning:"\"And He inspired it [with] its corruption and its guarding.\"", magic:"The root explains exactly what kind of knowledge this is — and it's not education.", grammar:"Form IV (alhama = to inspire). Note it takes TWO objects: -hā (it, the soul) and then fujūrahā wa taqwāhā.", why:"Alhama over ʿallama (taught) — because taught knowledge can be forgotten or never received; ilhām is built in.", note:"Lahima = TO GULP DOWN WHOLE. Ilhām = knowledge SWALLOWED, not studied. Pre-installed. Balad 90:10: the roads are visible OUTSIDE.", more:["From that, ilhām = INSPIRATION — knowledge placed directly in the heart, not learned step by step but swallowed whole.","Ilhām comes from GULPING SOMETHING DOWN IN ONE SWALLOW. So this moral knowledge wasn't taught, argued, or reasoned into you — it was placed inside you whole, pre-installed.","Which means: you don't need to be told that cruelty is wrong. You already swallowed that. The surah is saying every soul comes with the software already loaded.","And this connects to Balad 90:10 — wa hadaynāhu an-najdayn (\"We guided him to the two heights\"). Balad said both paths are on visible high ground. Shams says the knowledge of both is inside you. Consecutive surahs: the roads are visible outside, and the map is installed inside. No excuse remains.","Shams 91:8: the map is INSIDE."]},
  {ar:"فُجُورَهَا وَتَقْوَاهَا", tr:"Fujūrahā wa taqwāhā", v:"8", root:"Fujūr — the \"bursting through / splitting past all restraint\" family — the same root as fajr, DAWN (light splitting the dark).", meaning:"\"its corruption and its guarding.\"", magic:"The two words are perfect physical opposites, and that's the design.", grammar:"Both are verbal nouns with the -hā possessive; accusative as second objects of alhama.", why:"Naming them as the soul's own (fujūra-hā, taqwā-hā) means both potentials belong to you — neither is external.", note:"Fujūr = BURSTING OUT (like fajr, dawn splitting). Taqwā = BUILDING A SHIELD (wiqāyah = protection). Break barriers, or build them.", more:["taqwā — the \"shielding / guarding\" family — waqā = to protect; wiqāyah = PROTECTION (modern Arabic: preventive medicine).","Fujūr = BURSTING OUT past your limits. Taqwā = PUTTING A SHIELD between yourself and harm. One breaks through barriers; the other builds them. The soul was given the blueprint for both.","And CRITICALLY: taqwā is NOT \"fear.\" The root means making a shield. A muttaqī isn't cowering — he's carrying armour. You own this from Nabaʾ 78:31 (inna li'l-muttaqīna mafāzā).","You own fujūr across SIX surahs now: Qiyāmah 75:5 (yafjura), Insān 76:6 (yufajjirūnahā), ʿAbasa 80:42 (al-fajarah), Infiṭār 82:3 (fujjirat), Infiṭār 82:14 (al-fujjār), Fajr 89:1 (al-fajr), and here.","And note the connection to Balad 90:5 — the surah just before described a man with no ḥijr (walls). Here we learn: he had the blueprint for walls all along.","Both blueprints are inside you.","Taqwā is NOT \"fear\" — it's ARMOUR."]},
  {ar:"قَدْ أَفْلَحَ مَن زَكَّاهَا", tr:"Qad aflaḥa man zakkāhā", v:"9", root:"Aflaḥa — a FARMING root: falaḥa = to split/plough the earth; fallāḥ = A FARMER; from ploughing comes falāḥ = success.", meaning:"\"He has certainly succeeded who purifies it.\"", magic:"You own this verse almost verbatim from the surah two back.", grammar:"قَدْ + past = certainty (\"has certainly succeeded\"). Man here = \"whoever\" (relative), not \"who?\".", why:"Falāḥ over a generic word for winning — it builds effort and cultivation into the concept of success.", note:"Fallāḥ = A FARMER. Ḥayya ʿala'l-falāḥ — every adhān. Aʿlā 87:14 and Shams 91:9 are near-identical (Form V vs Form II).", more:["The harvest belongs to whoever broke the soil open.","zakkā — the \"purify + grow\" family — zakāh.","Aʿlā 87:14 — qad aflaḥa man tazakkā. Shams 91:9 — qad aflaḥa man zakkāhā.","Nearly identical. The only difference: Aʿlā uses Form V (tazakkā = \"purified himself\"), Shams uses Form II (zakkā-hā = \"purified it\"). Aʿlā emphasizes the reflexive; Shams names the object — the soul from v.7.","And the FARMING logic runs through both words: falāḥ = breaking soil open; zakāh = purifying so a thing can GROW. Two agricultural roots, one verse. Success is cultivation.","You say falāḥ in every adhān — ḥayya ʿala'l-falāḥ, \"come to success.\" You are being called to the harvest.","And zakkā is now across FIVE surahs: Nāziʿāt 79:18 (offered to Pharaoh), ʿAbasa 80:3 & 80:7 (the blind man), Aʿlā 87:14, Shams 91:9.","Both words are farming words: plough it, and it grows."]},
  {ar:"وَقَدْ خَابَ مَن دَسَّاهَا", tr:"Wa qad khāba man dassāhā", v:"10", root:"Khāba — the \"failure / disappointment / coming away empty\" family — khaybah = utter disappointment, the state of the one whose hopes came to nothing.", meaning:"\"And he has certainly failed who buries it.\"", magic:"THE MOST PRECISE WORD-PAIR IN THE SURAH, and the contrast is agricultural.", grammar:"Form II (dassā), weak-final. Note the perfect parallel with v.9: qad + past + man + Form II verb + -hā.", why:"Dassā (bury/conceal) rather than \"corrupted\" — because the soul isn't destroyed, only hidden.", note:"Dassa = TO BURY IN THE GROUND out of shame. v.9 PURIFIES the seed so it grows; v.10 BURIES it.", more:["dassā — a magnificent word: from dassa = to bury something in the ground, to hide it away out of shame; the same root as dasīsah = a covert plot, and indassa = to sneak in.","V.9: zakkāhā — PURIFIED it, so it GROWS. v.10: dassāhā — BURIED it in the dirt.","Both are things you do to a seed. One clears it to grow; the other shoves it under the soil to hide it.","And here's the connection that makes it unforgettable: the root k-f-r (disbelief) originally meant A FARMER BURYING A SEED — you own this from Insān 76:3. Now dassā means burying too. Two different roots, same image: covering something over so it can't be seen.","And Qurʾānic usage of dassa elsewhere carries the sense of hiding something in the dust out of shame — which is exactly what you do to a conscience you don't want to hear. You don't kill it. You bury it alive.","Compare Muṭaffifīn 83:14 — bal rāna ʿalā qulūbihim (\"RUST has covered their hearts\"). Two surahs, two images of the soul being covered over.","Identical grammar, opposite verbs.","And kafara also means burying a seed (Insān 76:3). You don't kill the conscience — you bury it alive."]},
  {ar:"بِطَغْوَاهَا", tr:"Bi-ṭaghwāhā", v:"11", root:"The \"overflowing all bounds\" family — original image: water bursting its banks and flooding.", meaning:"\"Thamūd denied by reason of its transgression.\"", magic:"The SIXTH appearance of this root, and by now it should be automatic: - Nabaʾ 78:22 — aṭ-ṭāghīn (Hell awaits those who transgress) - Nāziʿāt 79:17 — innahu ṭaghā (Pharaoh.", grammar:"بـ here = bāʾ as-sababiyyah (\"because of / by reason of\").", why:"ṭaghwā (the noun) over the verb — it makes overflowing their identity, not their behaviour.", note:"Ṭaghā = water BURSTING ITS BANKS.", more:["The case study) - Nāziʿāt 79:37 — fa-ammā man ṭaghā (whoever transgresses — transferable) - Fajr 89:11 — alladhīna ṭaghaw (ʿĀd, Thamūd, Pharaoh) - Shams 91:11 — bi-ṭaghwāhā (Thamūd)","And now connect it to THIS surah's own vocabulary: v.8 said the soul was given fujūr (bursting out) and taqwā (shielding). v.11 shows a whole nation that chose fujūr. Ṭaghā IS fujūr at national scale.","Note the grammatical form: ṭaghwā — a verbal noun, not a verb. It's not \"because they transgressed\" but \"by reason of ITS TRANSGRESSION\" — transgression as a settled characteristic, a permanent state of the nation, not a series of acts.","Thamūdu is often a diptote (foreign proper name) — watch its case endings; they vary by position and reading.","SIX surahs: 78:22, 79:17, 79:37, 89:11, 91:11. And v.8 already named it: they picked fujūr over taqwā."]},
  {ar:"انبَعَثَ أَشْقَاهَا", tr:"Inbaʿatha ashqāhā", v:"12", root:"Inbaʿatha — the \"being sent forth / raised up / dispatched\" family — the same root as baʿth = THE RESURRECTION!", meaning:"\"When the most wretched of them was sent forth.\"", magic:"Two hooks, both excellent.", grammar:"Form VII (اِنْفَعَلَ) — the ان- prefix; intransitive/passive in sense.", why:"Inbaʿatha implies being dispatched by others, which is precisely why the punishment is collective.", note:"Baʿth = THE RESURRECTION. The raising-verb used for one man standing up to do evil.", more:["(yawm al-baʿth) and baʿatha = to send (a messenger).","ashqā — the \"wretchedness / misery\" family — shaqiyy = wretched; shaqāwah = misery (opposite of saʿādah, happiness).","First: inbaʿatha is the RESURRECTION root. The verb for the dead being raised is used here for one man standing up to commit a crime. He \"resurrected himself\" into action — and it destroyed his nation.","Second: ashqā — you own it from Aʿlā 87:11 — wa yatajannabuhā'l-ashqā (\"the most wretched will sidestep it\"). Aʿlā described al-ashqā abstractly; Shams gives him a face and a crime. Same superlative, four surahs apart.","And note: it's SINGULAR. \"The most wretched of them\" — ONE man. But v.14 says \"their Lord crushed THEM\" — plural. One man acted; a nation was destroyed. Because the rest sent him — the passive inbaʿatha implies he was dispatched, with the community's consent. Silence made them partners.","You met it in Takwīr 81:2 (inkadarat) and Infiṭār 82:1 (infaṭarat).","ONE man acted (ashqāhā, singular); the WHOLE nation was destroyed (ʿalayhim, plural).","And al-ashqā appeared in Aʿlā 87:11."]},
  {ar:"نَاقَةَ اللَّهِ وَسُقْيَاهَا", tr:"Nāqata'llāhi wa suqyāhā", v:"13", root:"Nāqah — a she-camel.", meaning:"\"The Messenger of Allah said to them: [Beware] the she-camel of Allah and her drink!\"", magic:"The whole crime is about a DRINKING SCHEDULE — and that's the point.", grammar:"Nāqata'llāh is accusative — a taḥdhīr construction (a warning): \"[Beware] the she-camel!\" Arabic can issue a warning by putting the noun in the accusative with the verb implied.", why:"Suqyā (her drinking turn) makes the transgression absurdly small and absolutely clear.", note:"A nation destroyed over A DRINKING SCHEDULE. The boundary was tiny and clearly stated — that was the test.", more:["suqyā — the \"giving to drink\" family — saqā = to give water; sqiyā/suqyā = a turn at the water, a drinking-share; and istisqāʾ = the PRAYER FOR RAIN!","The she-camel of Ṣāliḥ (peace be upon him) was given a designated day at the well; the people had theirs. That's it. That's the test. Not a war, not an idol — a rota for water.","And notice the ownership: nāqata 'LLĀH — \"the she-camel of ALLAH.\" The animal is attributed directly to Him. Which is why harming her isn't cruelty to an animal — it's a direct challenge.","The lesson worth keeping: they were destroyed over a small, clearly-stated boundary. Which is exactly what ṭaghā (v.11) means — refusing to stay within the banks. The size of the boundary was never the issue.","Istisqāʾ — the rain-prayer — is a free hook for this root.","Istisqāʾ = the prayer for rain (same root).","And she's called the she-camel OF ALLAH."]},
  {ar:"فَعَقَرُوهَا فَدَمْدَمَ", tr:"Fa-ʿaqarūhā fa-damdama", v:"14", root:"ʿaqarū — the \"wounding / hamstringing\" family — ʿaqara = to hamstring an animal (cut the leg tendon so it collapses).", meaning:"\"But they hamstrung her, so their Lord crushed them for their sin.\"", magic:"The sound of damdama is the punishment.", grammar:"Quadriliteral (four-letter) verb, past tense.", why:"Damdama over a plain word for destroy — it's onomatopoeic; the pounding is audible.", note:"Say it: dam-da-ma. The word IS the pounding.", more:["Also ʿāqir = barren (cut off from offspring).","damdama — a reduplicated root: damdama ʿalā = to crush utterly, to level with overwhelming force, piling destruction on top of something until nothing remains.","Dam-da-ma — a doubled, hammering syllable, like the sound of something being pounded flat. You've met this reduplication pattern three times now: - waswasa (to whisper repeatedly — Sūrat an-Nās) - zalzala (to shake violently) - ʿasʿasa (the night creeping — Takwīr 81:17) - damdama (to crush utterly — here)","Arabic uses doubled roots for repeated, relentless action. The word sounds like what it does.","And note the sequence of fa's: fa-kadhdhabūhu fa-ʿaqarūhā fa-damdama. Denied → hamstrung → crushed. Three \"fa\"s = three immediate consequences, no gaps. Same accelerating technique as Nāziʿāt 79:21–24 (Pharaoh's chain of fa's).","And bi-dhanbihim (\"for their sin\") — SINGULAR dhanb, PLURAL -him. One sin, all of them. Because one man's act became everyone's.","Damdama ʿalā takes the preposition ʿalā — \"crushed down upon them.\"","Same doubling as waswasa, zalzala, ʿasʿasa (Takwīr 81:17). And ʿaqara = to HAMSTRING (cut the leg tendon).","One dhanb (singular sin), all of -him (plural)."]},
  {ar:"فَسَوَّاهَا", tr:"Fa-sawwāhā", v:"14", root:"The \"making even / levelling\" family — the SAME ROOT as v.7.", meaning:"\"and levelled it [the town / them]\" — flattened to the ground, made even with the earth.", magic:"V.7: wa nafsin wa mā sawwāhā — \"By the soul and the One who PROPORTIONED IT.\" v.14: fa-damdama ʿalayhim rabbuhum bi-dhanbihim fa-sawwāhā — \"so He crushed them and LEVELLED IT.\"", grammar:"Form II, weak-final, with -hā. The -hā is debated: the town? the nation? the punishment?", why:"Using the creation-verb for the destruction is the most economical possible way to say the One who made you can unmake you.", note:"SAWWĀHĀ IN v.7 = HE PERFECTED A SOUL. SAWWĀHĀ IN v.14 = HE FLATTENED A TOWN. Same word, seven verses apart.", more:["THE SINGLE BEST FIND IN THIS SURAH, AND POSSIBLY IN YOUR WHOLE COLLECTION.","IDENTICAL WORD. SEVEN VERSES APART.","How can one verb mean both? Because sawwā means \"to make even\" — and \"even\" can mean: - PERFECTLY BALANCED (a soul with all faculties in proportion) — v.7 - PERFECTLY FLAT (a town levelled with the ground) — v.14","The same act of \"evening out\" builds and destroys.","And the moral logic is exact: He sawwā-ed your soul (v.7). What you do with it decides whether the second sawwā — the flattening one — ever finds you. The surah gives you the verb twice and lets you choose which meaning applies.","This is now the SEVENTH surah for s-w-y, and its EIGHTH total appearance in your study. Fingertips (75:4), a clot (75:38), the sky (79:28), you (82:7), everything (87:2), the soul (91:7), a ruined town (91:14).","— all readings are classical, and all mean \"levelled flat.\"","\"Making even\" = balanced, or flat.","Eighth appearance of this root across seven surahs."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا يَخَافُ عُقْبَاهَا", tr:"Wa lā yakhāfu ʿuqbāhā", v:"15", root:"Yakhāfu — the \"fear\" family (khawf).", meaning:"\"And He does not fear its consequence.\"", magic:"The final verse, and it's a statement about God that answers a question you'd never think to ask.", grammar:"There is a well-known discussion of the subject here.", why:"ʿuqbā (consequence) rather than \"punishment\" — it's the neutral word for what comes next, which makes the absence of fear absolute.", note:"ʿAqib = A HEEL. A consequence is what follows on your heels. Balad 90:11 = al-ʿaqabah (THE CLIMB).", more:["ʿuqbā — the \"following / heel / consequence\" family — ʿaqib = a HEEL; ʿāqibah = the outcome; and ʿaqabah = a steep pass — which you just met as the centre of Balad 90:11!","Why mention that He doesn't fear consequences? Because every human ruler who destroys a city must. There'll be retaliation, reputation damage, a reckoning, someone to answer to. The verse says: not Him. There is no one above Him to whom an account is owed.","And the root-connection is beautiful: ʿaqib = a HEEL — the consequence is what \"follows on the heels\" of an action. Arabic names outcomes after the part of the foot that comes behind.","And here's the payoff with the surah before it: Balad 90:11 built its whole message on al-ʿaqabah — the steep pass, from this same root (the climb whose meaning is its outcome). Shams 91:15 closes with ʿuqbāhā — the outcome itself. Consecutive surahs, opening and closing on one root: the climb, and what follows it.","And note the very last sound: -hā. All fifteen verses end in it. The rhyme holds to the final syllable.","Most read it as Allah (\"He does not fear the consequence\"); some read it as referring to the one who hamstrung the camel (he did not fear the outcome).","ʿUqbā is weak-final, carrying the rhyme.","Shams 91:15 = ʿuqbāhā (THE OUTCOME).","Consecutive surahs, one root.","And every verse of this surah ends in -hā."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:92, name:"Al-Layl", ar:"الليل", en:"The Night", verses:21, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Both men are given a smooth road. The roads go opposite ways.",
  special:["Two people are described with mirrored three-item lists — and only ONE word changes the ending.","Look at the last row. The result sentence is WORD-FOR-WORD IDENTICAL — fa-sanuyassiruhu li'l-… — until the very last word. Yusrā (ease) vs ʿusrā (hardship) — one letter's difference in the root, opposite destinies.","And here's the shock: the withholder is ALSO \"eased\" — nuyassiruhu. He's made smooth toward hardship. Sin gets easier too. That's the terrifying part.","--"],
  big:["Three oaths (v.1–3) — night, day, and the making of male and female.","Your efforts are scattered (v.4) — inna saʿyakum la-shattā.","Two men, two roads (v.5–11) — the mirrored panels above.","What's Ours (v.12–13) — upon Us is the guidance; to Us belong the last and the first.","The blazing fire (v.14–16) — only the most wretched enters it.","The most God-conscious (v.17–21) — who gives for no repayment, seeking only His Face."],
  traps:["V.7 and v.10 are IDENTICAL except the last word — nuyassiruhu li'l-YUSRĀ / nuyassiruhu li'l-ʿUSRĀ. Both men are \"EASED.\" Sin gets EASIER too. Don't miss this.","سَيُجَنَّبُهَا (v.17) vs. يَتَجَنَّبُهَا (Aʿlā 87:11) — same root, but Form II PASSIVE vs Form V ACTIVE. The wretched sidesteps the reminder; the God-conscious is sidestepped away from the Fire.","اسْتَغْنَىٰ (v.8) — Form X = \"to CONSIDER yourself rich,\" not \"became rich.\" Three surahs: 80:5, 92:8, 96:7.","يُغْنِي (v.11) and اسْتَغْنَىٰ (v.8) — same root, three verses apart. \"I need nothing\" → \"nothing will avail him.\"","يَصْلَاهَا (v.15) — from ṣaliya (roasted). NOT the ṣalāh root, despite the resemblance. Six surahs now.","تَرَدَّىٰ (v.11) — means TUMBLING HEADLONG into a pit, not simply \"dies.\"","الْحُسْنَىٰ / الْيُسْرَىٰ / الْعُسْرَىٰ / الْأُولَىٰ — all fuʿlā feminine superlatives. Four in one surah. Learn the shape once.","وَمَا خَلَقَ (v.3) — mā, not man. Same construction as Shams 91:5–7. Classical readings: maṣdariyyah, or emphasizing the attribute of power.","يَرْضَىٰ (v.21) — NO OBJECT given. \"Pleased with what?\" is left unanswered on purpose — the reward is as big as you can imagine.","صَدَّقَ (v.6) — Form II = \"affirmed as TRUE.\" Form I ṣadaqa = \"was truthful.\" And ṣadaqah (charity) is the same root — giving proves the belief."],
  links:[{w:"يَغْشَىٰ (v.1)",e:"الْغَاشِيَةِ (88:1) · يَغْشَاهَا (91:4)",t:"THREE surahs — and Layl deletes the object = everything"},{w:"تَجَلَّىٰ (v.2)",e:"جَلَّاهَا (Shams 91:3)",t:"Form V (itself) vs Form II (the sun)"},{w:"الذَّكَرَ وَالْأُنثَىٰ (v.3)",e:"الذَّكَرَ وَالْأُنثَىٰ (Qiy. 75:39)",t:"Identical pair"},{w:"سَعْيَكُمْ (v.4)",e:"سَعْيُكُم (Insān 76:22)",t:"Identical word — the Saʿī root, six surahs"},{w:"شَتَّىٰ (v.4)",e:"عَامِلَةٌ نَّاصِبَةٌ / رَاضِيَةٌ (88:3, 9)",t:"Same insight — everyone strives, different directions"},{w:"اتَّقَىٰ / الْأَتْقَى (v.5, 17)",e:"الْمُتَّقِينَ (78:31) · تَقْوَاهَا (91:8)",t:"Wiqāyah = ARMOUR, not fear"},{w:"صَدَّقَ (v.6)",e:"فَلَا صَدَّقَ وَلَا صَلَّىٰ (Qiy. 75:31)",t:"Same Form II — affirming the truth"},{w:"نُيَسِّرُهُ لِلْيُسْرَىٰ (v.7)",e:"نُيَسِّرُكَ لِلْيُسْرَىٰ (Aʿlā 87:8)",t:"Same verse, one pronoun changed"},{w:"الْيُسْرَىٰ / الْعُسْرَىٰ (v.7, 10)",e:"80:20 · 84:8 · 94:5–6",t:"FIVE surahs for y-s-r"},{w:"اسْتَغْنَىٰ (v.8)",e:"اسْتَغْنَىٰ (80:5) · اسْتَغْنَىٰ (96:7)",t:"THREE surahs, identical word"},{w:"يُغْنِي (v.11)",e:"يُغْنِيهِ (80:37) · لَا يُغْنِي (88:7)",t:"\"I need nothing\" → \"nothing suffices him\""},{w:"تَرَدَّىٰ (v.11)",e:"الْهَوَىٰ (Nāziʿāt 79:40)",t:"Two falling-roots: desire makes you fall, then you fall"},{w:"عَلَيْنَا (v.12)",e:"75:17 · 75:19 · 88:26",t:"FOUR undertakings He took upon Himself"},{w:"الْآخِرَةَ وَالْأُولَىٰ (v.13)",e:"الْآخِرَةِ وَالْأُولَىٰ (Nāziʿāt 79:25)",t:"Identical pair, same reversed order"},{w:"تَلَظَّىٰ (v.14)",e:"سُعِّرَتْ (Takwīr 81:12)",t:"Self-blazing vs. being stoked"},{w:"يَصْلَاهَا (v.15)",e:"82:15 · 83:16 · 84:12 · 87:12 · 88:4",t:"SIX surahs — ṣaliya"},{w:"الْأَشْقَى (v.15)",e:"الْأَشْقَى (87:11) · أَشْقَاهَا (91:12)",t:"THREE surahs"},{w:"كَذَّبَ وَتَوَلَّىٰ (v.16)",e:"Qiy. 75:32 · ʿAlaq 96:13",t:"THREE surahs, identical phrase"},{w:"سَيُجَنَّبُهَا (v.17)",e:"يَتَجَنَّبُهَا (Aʿlā 87:11)",t:"SAME ROOT, opposite forms — the best reciprocity in the juzʾ"},{w:"يَتَزَكَّىٰ (v.18)",e:"79:18 · 80:3, 7 · 87:14 · 91:9",t:"SIX surahs — and Layl ties it to māl"},{w:"وَجْهِ رَبِّهِ (v.20)",e:"لِوَجْهِ اللَّهِ (Insān 76:9)",t:"Same idiom, same portrait of pure intention"},{w:"الْأَعْلَىٰ (v.20)",e:"الْأَعْلَى (Aʿlā 87:1)",t:"Identical title"},{w:"وَلَسَوْفَ يَرْضَىٰ (v.21)",e:"وَلَسَوْفَ... فَتَرْضَىٰ (Ḍuḥā 93:5)",t:"Consecutive surahs, same closing construction"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"إِذَا يَغْشَىٰ", tr:"Idhā yaghshā", v:"1", root:"The \"covering over / enveloping\" family — ghashiya = to cover completely.", meaning:"\"By the night when it covers.\"", magic:"THIRD consecutive appearance of this root, and it's a perfect run: - Ghāshiyah 88:1 — al-Ghāshiyah — THE ENVELOPER, a name for the Day - Shams 91:4 — wa'l-layli idhā yaghshāhā.", grammar:"The wāw is wāw al-qasam. Note idhā + PRESENT tense here (yaghshā), vs.", why:"Ghashiya implies covering with no gaps — the same verb that names the Day of Judgment.", note:"THREE surahs: 88:1 (al-Ghāshiyah), 91:4 (yaghshāhā, covers the sun), 92:1 (yaghshā, covers — NO OBJECT = everything).", more:["Night covering the sun - Layl 92:1 — wa'l-layli idhā yaghshā — night covering…?","Notice the difference: Shams says yaghshā-HĀ (covers IT — the sun). Layl says just yaghshā — with NO OBJECT. Covers what? It doesn't say.","That deletion is the balāgha. By leaving the object out, the covering becomes universal: night covers everything. Shams was specific; Layl is total.","idhā + past elsewhere — the present gives ongoing, recurring action."]},
  {ar:"إِذَا تَجَلَّىٰ", tr:"Idhā tajallā", v:"2", root:"The \"unveiling / manifesting / polishing to a shine\" family — tajallī = a MANIFESTATION; jaliyy = crystal clear; jalāʾ = burnishing.", meaning:"\"and by the day when it becomes manifest.\"", magic:"You own this exact root from the surah before.", grammar:"Form V (تَفَعَّلَ) = reflexive — doing it to itself.", why:"Tajallā over \"came\" — the day doesn't arrive, it unveils itself.", note:"Shams 91:3 = jallāhā (Form II, polishes THE SUN). Layl 92:2 = tajallā (Form V, polishes ITSELF). And tajallī = a MANIFESTATION.", more:["Shams 91:3 — wa'n-nahāri idhā jallāhā (\"the day when it DISPLAYS it\" — Form II, transitive). Layl 92:2 — wa'n-nahāri idhā tajallā (\"the day when it REVEALS ITSELF\" — Form V, reflexive).","Same root, consecutive surahs, one shift: Shams's day polishes the sun; Layl's day polishes itself.","And notice the pattern repeating from v.1: Shams was transitive and specific; Layl is reflexive and total. Both verses do the same move.","Compare Form II jallā (Shams 91:3) = doing it to something else.","One root, two forms, two surahs — a free grammar lesson."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا خَلَقَ الذَّكَرَ وَالْأُنثَىٰ", tr:"Wa mā khalaqa'dh-dhakara wa'l-unthā", v:"3", root:"Dhakar — the male (also the root of dhikr, remembrance!). unthā — the female (root связан with softness/pliancy).", meaning:"\"and by the One who created the male and the female.\"", magic:"You own this exact pair from Qiyāmah 75:39 — fa-jaʿala minhu'z-zawjayni adh-dhakara wa'l-unthā. Same two words, same order.", grammar:"Both accusative (objects of khalaqa).", why:"Naming the human pair after the cosmic pair brings the oath from the sky down into your body.", note:"Same pair as Qiyāmah 75:39. Three oaths, three built-in DIVISIONS: night/day, male/female — then your own striving (v.4).", more:["And the placement is the argument. v.1 night, v.2 day — two opposites. v.3 male and female — two more opposites. Then v.4: \"your efforts are SCATTERED\" — a third pair-of-opposites, this time in YOU.","The three oaths are all about built-in duality. Night/day, male/female — and then human striving, which also splits two ways. Division is designed into the universe. That's the setup for v.5–10.","Note wa mā khalaqa — mā, not man — the same construction as Shams 91:5–7 (wa mā banāhā, wa mā ṭaḥāhā, wa mā sawwāhā). Classical readings: maṣdariyyah (\"and the creating of…\") or referring to Allah with emphasis on the power, not the person.","al-unthā is weak-final, carrying the surah's -ā rhyme — which every verse here shares."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّ سَعْيَكُمْ لَشَتَّىٰ", tr:"Inna saʿyakum la-shattā", v:"4", root:"Saʿy — the \"striving / hurrying\" family — the Saʿī between Ṣafā and Marwah.", meaning:"\"Indeed, your efforts are diverse / scattered in different directions.\"", magic:"THE THESIS VERSE, and it's about direction, not amount.", grammar:"Inna + emphatic lām = double emphasis (\"most certainly diverse\").", why:"Shattā (scattered in direction) rather than mukhtalif (different) — it's a dispersal word: arrows flying every which way.", note:"Shattā = SCATTERED IN ALL DIRECTIONS. Not \"some work harder\" — everyone runs, pointed different ways.", more:["shattā — the \"scattering / diverging\" family — shatta = to be dispersed; shattā = diverse, going in all directions; tashattut = fragmentation.","It doesn't say your efforts are unequal in size. It says they are shattā — going different WAYS. Everyone is running. The runners are pointed at different things.","This is the same insight as Ghāshiyah 88:3 and 88:9 — there, one group was ʿāmilatun nāṣibah (working, EXHAUSTED) and the other was li-saʿyihā rāḍiyah (pleased with its STRIVING). Both worked. Different directions.","And saʿyakum is IDENTICAL to Insān 76:22 — inna hādhā kāna lakum jazāʾan wa kāna saʿyukum mashkūrā (\"your striving has been appreciated\"). Same word, same possessive.","The Ṣafā–Marwah root is now across SIX surahs: Insān 76:22, Nāziʿāt 79:22 & 79:35, ʿAbasa 80:8, Ghāshiyah 88:9, Layl 92:4.","Shattā is a plural adjective (of shatīt), indeclinable here, carrying the rhyme.","Same insight as Ghāshiyah 88:3 vs 88:9. And saʿyakum is IDENTICAL to Insān 76:22."]},
  {ar:"أَعْطَىٰ وَاتَّقَىٰ", tr:"Aʿṭā wa'ttaqā", v:"5", root:"Aʿṭā — the \"giving\" family (ʿaṭāʾ = a gift — you own it from Nabaʾ 78:36). ittaqā — the \"shielding / guarding\" family.", meaning:"\"So as for he who gives and guards himself…\"", magic:"The order matters: GIVING comes first.", grammar:"Form VIII (ittaqā ← waqā, with the wāw assimilated into a doubled tāʾ).", why:"Aʿṭā with no object makes generosity an identity, not a transaction.", note:"Wiqāyah = ARMOUR, not fear. And GIVING is listed FIRST — it's the test of whether the taqwā is real.", more:["Wiqāyah = PROTECTION (modern Arabic: preventive medicine).","You'd expect piety (taqwā) to be listed before generosity. The surah puts giving first — because giving is the test of whether the taqwā is real.","And ittaqā is NOT \"feared.\" The root means making a shield. A muttaqī carries armour; he doesn't cower. You own this from Nabaʾ 78:31 (al-muttaqīn) and Shams 91:8 (taqwāhā).","Note both verbs are absolute — no object given. \"He gave\" — gave what? To whom? Not said. The deletion generalizes it: he is a giver, as a character trait.","Same assimilation as muttaqīn (Nabaʾ 78:31).","Both verbs have NO OBJECT — he's just a giver."]},
  {ar:"وَصَدَّقَ بِالْحُسْنَىٰ", tr:"Wa ṣaddaqa bi'l-ḥusnā", v:"6", root:"ṣaddaqa — Form II of the \"truth\" root = to declare something TRUE, to affirm — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:31 (fa-lā ṣaddaqa wa lā ṣallā).", meaning:"\"and affirms the Best [reward].\"", magic:"The third item, and it's the belief underneath the first two.", grammar:"ṣaddaqa + بـ = \"to affirm the truth OF\" — note the preposition, same as kadhdhaba bi- (to deny).", why:"ḥusnā (the Best) rather than a specific noun — it keeps the reward as big as the reader's imagination.", note:"Al-asmāʾ al-ḥUSNĀ — the Most Beautiful Names. Same superlative.", more:["Note: Form II ṣaddaqa = affirm; Form I ṣadaqa = be truthful; and ṣadaqah = charity — all one root.","ḥusnā — the feminine superlative of ḥasan (good) = THE BEST / the most beautiful — as in al-asmāʾ al-ḥusnā, the Most Beautiful Names!","Al-ḥusnā is left deliberately unspecified. Classical readings: Paradise, the reward, the promise, lā ilāha illā Allāh. All fit — the point is he believes something better is coming, which is what makes giving possible.","And the root-play is gorgeous: ṣaddaqa (to affirm as true) shares its root with ṣadaqah (CHARITY) — because giving charity is proving your belief true. v.5 \"he gave\" and v.6 \"he affirmed\" are the same root logic in two words.","You say al-asmāʾ al-ḥusnā constantly — free hook for the superlative.","Ḥusnā is the fuʿlā feminine superlative pattern — same shape as al-yusrā, al-ʿusrā, al-kubrā, al-ūlā.","And ṣaddaqa (affirm) shares a root with ṣadaqah (CHARITY) — giving PROVES the belief.","Compare Qiyāmah 75:31: \"he neither affirmed nor prayed.\""]},
  {ar:"فَسَنُيَسِّرُهُ لِلْيُسْرَىٰ", tr:"Fa-sa-nuyassiruhu li'l-yusrā", v:"7", root:"The \"ease / smoothness\" family — yusr = ease (opposite of ʿusr) — inna maʿa'l-ʿusri yusrā.", meaning:"\"We will ease him toward ease.\"", magic:"You own this verse almost verbatim from Aʿlā 87:8.", grammar:"سَ + present = near future. Form II (yassara = to make easy). al-yusrā = fuʿlā superlative.", why:"Nuyassiruhu frames divine help as changing the person, not the circumstances.", note:"Aʿlā 87:8 = \"We ease YOU.\" Layl 92:7 = \"We ease HIM.\" Same verse, one pronoun. And it's the PERSON eased, not the road.", more:["Aʿlā 87:8 — wa nuyassiruka li'l-yusrā (\"We will ease YOU toward ease\") — addressed to the Prophet ﷺ. Layl 92:7 — fa-sa-nuyassiruhu li'l-yusrā (\"We will ease HIM toward ease\") — the general principle.","Same construction, one pronoun changed. Free vocabulary.","And the profound point: it's the PERSON being eased, not the path. \"We will ease HIM toward ease\" — you get made into someone for whom good is easy. Righteousness becomes your default setting.","The y-s-r root is now across FIVE surahs: ʿAbasa 80:20 (yassarah, the way made easy), Inshiqāq 84:8 (ḥisāban yasīrā, an easy reckoning), Aʿlā 87:8, Layl 92:7 & 92:10, Sharḥ 94:5–6."]},
  {ar:"بَخِلَ وَاسْتَغْنَىٰ", tr:"Bakhila wa'staghnā", v:"8", root:"Bakhila — the \"miserliness\" family — bukhl = stinginess; bakhīl = a miser.", meaning:"\"But as for he who withholds and thinks himself self-sufficient…\"", magic:"THE EXACT MIRROR of v.5 — and istaghnā is a word you already own deeply.", grammar:"Form X (اسْتَفْعَلَ) very often = \"to CONSIDER something to be X.\" Istaghnā = considered himself rich.", why:"Istaghnā moves the fault from circumstance (being rich) to attitude (feeling complete).", note:"Form X = \"to CONSIDER yourself X.\" Istaghnā = \"I NEED NOTHING.\" THREE surahs: ʿAbasa 80:5, Layl 92:8, ʿAlaq 96:7. The opposite of taqwā (a shield) is thinking you don't need one.", more:["istaghnā — Form X of the \"richness\" root = to CONSIDER yourself free of need.","V.5: aʿṭā (GAVE) + ittaqā (SHIELDED himself). v.8: bakhila (WITHHELD) + istaghnā (thought himself NEEDING NOTHING).","The opposite of taqwā isn't sin — it's istighnāʾ. The opposite of carrying a shield is thinking you don't need one.","Istaghnā is now in THREE surahs: - ʿAbasa 80:5 — ammā mani staghnā — the rich chief the Prophet ﷺ attended to - Layl 92:8 — the withholder - ʿAlaq 96:7 — an raʾāhu staghnā — man transgresses because he sees himself as needing nothing","Three surahs, one diagnosis: the root of every refusal is \"I don't need anything.\"","And remember ʿAbasa's payoff: istaghnā (v.5) → yughnīh (v.37). He wanted to need nothing; he got a concern that left room for nothing.","Istakbara = considered himself great."]},
  {ar:"فَسَنُيَسِّرُهُ لِلْعُسْرَىٰ", tr:"Fa-sa-nuyassiruhu li'l-ʿusrā", v:"10", root:"The \"hardship / difficulty\" family — ʿusr = hardship (opposite of yusr); ʿasīr = difficult; muʿsir = someone in financial straits.", meaning:"\"We will ease him toward hardship.\"", magic:"THE MOST CHILLING VERSE IN THE SURAH — and it works by repeating v.7 exactly.", grammar:"Note the deliberate muqābala (mirror-antithesis) — identical syntax across six verses (5–10) with only the key words swapped.", why:"Using the same verb for both destinies is the most economical way to say \"you get made into what you choose.\"", note:"V.7 and v.10 are IDENTICAL except the last word. He is EASED toward HARDSHIP. Sin gets EASIER, not harder.", more:["V.7: fa-sa-nuyassiruhu li'l-YUSRĀ v.10: fa-sa-nuyassiruhu li'l-ʿUSRĀ","IDENTICAL, until the final word. Even the verb is the same: nuyassiruhu — \"We will EASE him.\"","Read that again: he is EASED toward HARDSHIP. The road to ruin is made SMOOTH for him.","That's the terrifying insight: sin doesn't get harder. It gets EASIER. Every refusal makes the next one smoother. The withholder isn't fighting an uphill battle — he's coasting.","And yusrā / ʿusrā are the same fuʿlā superlative shape — which is why they rhyme, which is why the substitution lands like a slap.","You'll meet the pair again two surahs later in Sharḥ 94:5–6 — inna maʿa'l-ʿusri yusrā. There they're together as comfort; here they're apart as verdict.","And Sharḥ 94:5–6 puts ʿusr and yusr back together."]},
  {ar:"إِذَا تَرَدَّىٰ", tr:"Idhā taraddā", v:"11", root:"The \"falling / tumbling down\" family — radā = to fall to destruction, especially to fall headlong into a pit or off a height; mutaraddiyah = an animal that died by falling (a category in the rules of slaughter!).", meaning:"\"And his wealth will not avail him when he falls.\"", magic:"The verb is a falling word, and that's not accidental.", grammar:"Form V (taraddā), weak-final, carrying the rhyme.", why:"Taraddā (falling headlong) makes death a plunge, not a passage.", note:"Taraddā = TO TUMBLE HEADLONG into a pit. And yughnī here = the SAME ROOT as istaghnā three verses earlier.", more:["It doesn't say \"when he dies.\" It says taraddā — when he tumbles down, falls into the pit. Classical readings: falling into the grave, falling into the Fire, or perishing.","And connect it to Nāziʿāt 79:40 — wa nahā'n-nafsa ʿani'l-hawā — where hawā (DESIRE) comes from the root meaning TO FALL. Two surahs, two falling-words: desire makes you fall, and then you fall.","And yughnī here — the same root as istaghnā in v.8, three verses earlier! He thought himself in no need (istaghnā) — and his wealth won't suffice (mā yughnī) when he falls. The word convicts him.","Same root-payoff as ʿAbasa 80:5 → 80:37, and Ghāshiyah 88:7 (lā yughnī min jūʿ).","Mā yughnī ʿan = \"does not avail against/for\" — note the preposition.","\"I need nothing\" → \"nothing will suffice him.\""]},
  {ar:"إِنَّ عَلَيْنَا لَلْهُدَىٰ", tr:"Inna ʿalaynā la'l-hudā", v:"12", root:"The \"guiding\" family — hudā = guidance; hādī = a guide; hadiyyah = a gift (something guided to you!).", meaning:"\"Indeed, upon Us is the guidance.\"", magic:"The word ʿalaynā (\"UPON Us\") is doing enormous work.", grammar:"Inna + emphatic lām = double emphasis.", why:"Hudā (the guidance) with the definite article — not \"some guidance,\" THE guidance.", note:"ʿAlaynā = \"it is UPON Us\" — an obligation He took on.", more:["ʿAlā + pronoun = an obligation undertaken. So: guidance is something He has taken upon Himself. Not \"We may guide\" — it is upon Us.","You own this construction from THREE places: - Qiyāmah 75:17 — inna ʿalaynā jamʿahu wa qurʾānah (\"upon Us is its collection and recital\") - Qiyāmah 75:19 — thumma inna ʿalaynā bayānah (\"then upon Us is its clarification\") - Ghāshiyah 88:26 — thumma inna ʿalaynā ḥisābahum (\"upon Us is their reckoning\") - Layl 92:12 — inna ʿalaynā la'l-hudā","Four undertakings: gathering, clarifying, reckoning, and guiding. Same structure every time.","And placed right after v.10's terrible verdict, it's a mercy: the road to ʿusrā is smooth, yes — but guidance was always provided. Nobody walks it uninformed.","Note ʿalaynā is FRONTED before al-hudā — Arabic moves a word forward to spotlight it.","FOUR undertakings: Qiyāmah 75:17 (gather), 75:19 (clarify), Ghāshiyah 88:26 (reckon), Layl 92:12 (guide)."]},
  {ar:"لَلْآخِرَةَ وَالْأُولَىٰ", tr:"La'l-ākhirata wa'l-ūlā", v:"13", root:"ākhirah — the LAST / the Hereafter (from ākhir, last). ūlā — the FIRST / this world (from awwal, first).", meaning:"\"And indeed, to Us belong the last and the first.\"", magic:"The pairing is a merism: \"last and first\" = EVERYTHING.", grammar:"Both accusative as ism inna; lanā is the fronted khabar.", why:"Naming the Hereafter first quietly re-ranks your priorities inside the grammar.", note:"\"The LAST and the FIRST\" — reversed on purpose.", more:["And note the ORDER: ākhirah (the LAST) is named BEFORE ūlā (the FIRST). Reversed chronology, on purpose. The lasting one is named first because it matters more.","You own this exact reversal from Nāziʿāt 79:25 — fa-akhadhahu'llāhu nakāla'l-ākhirati wa'l-ūlā (\"the exemplary punishment of the Hereafter and the first\"). Same two words, same reversed order, two surahs.","And compare Aʿlā 87:17 — wa'l-ākhiratu khayrun wa abqā (\"the Hereafter is better and more lasting\"). Aʿlā ranks them; Layl says He owns both.","al-ūlā is a fuʿlā feminine superlative — same shape as yusrā, ʿusrā, ḥusnā in this very surah.","Identical to Nāziʿāt 79:25. And ūlā is the same fuʿlā shape as yusrā, ʿusrā, ḥusnā."]},
  {ar:"نَارًا تَلَظَّىٰ", tr:"Nāran talaẓẓā", v:"14", root:"The \"blazing / flaming up fiercely\" family — laẓā = a flame that leaps; Laẓā is itself a NAME for the Fire elsewhere in the Qurʾān.", meaning:"\"So I have warned you of a Fire that blazes.\"", magic:"Form V (talaẓẓā) = the fire doing it to itself — flaming up, feeding its own blaze. It's self-sustaining, not being stoked.", grammar:"Form V (تَفَعَّلَ) = reflexive — third one in this surah (tajallā v.2, taraddā v.11, talaẓẓā v.14).", why:"Talaẓẓā (self-blazing) makes the Fire alive and self-driven, not a tool being operated.", note:"Laẓā = a LEAPING FLAME (also a name for the Fire). Form V = it blazes ITSELF.", more:["Compare Takwīr 81:12 — wa idha'l-jaḥīmu suʿʿirat (\"when the Blaze is kindled\" — PASSIVE, someone stokes it). Here it's REFLEXIVE: it blazes itself. Two surahs, two grammars of fire.","And note anzartukum — \"I have warned you\" — first-person singular, which is striking. You own the root from Nabaʾ 78:40 (andharnāhu), Nāziʿāt 79:45 (mundhir), and Insān 76:7 (an-nadhr, the vow). All share: binding something in advance.","Note the original tatalaẓẓā has one tāʾ dropped — common in Form V present tense.","Compare Takwīr 81:12: suʿʿirat = it IS STOKED (passive)."]},
  {ar:"لَا يَصْلَاهَا إِلَّا الْأَشْقَى", tr:"Lā yaṣlāhā illa'l-ashqā", v:"15", root:"Yaṣlā — the \"being roasted\" family (ṣaliya) — not the ṣalāh root.", meaning:"\"None will enter it to burn except the most wretched.\"", magic:"The ṣaliya root is now across SIX surahs — one of your best-tracked verbs: - Infiṭār 82:15 · Muṭaffifīn 83:16 · Inshiqāq 84:12 · Aʿlā 87:12 · Ghāshiyah 88:4 · Layl 92:15", grammar:"Exception structure: lā… illā = \"none… except\" — a restriction.", why:"Ashqā (superlative) makes it the extreme case, which is a mercy in the phrasing.", note:"Ṣaliya = roasted — SIX surahs now. Aʿlā 87:11: the wretched SIDESTEPS (yatajannabu) the reminder.", more:["ashqā — the \"wretchedness\" family (shaqiyy = wretched; shaqāwah = misery, opposite of saʿādah).","And al-ashqā is now across THREE: - Aʿlā 87:11 — wa yatajannabuhā al-ashqā — the most wretched SIDESTEPS the reminder - Shams 91:12 — idhi'nbaʿatha ashqāhā — the most wretched of Thamūd (given a face) - Layl 92:15 — lā yaṣlāhā illa al-ashqā","And here's the exquisite payoff with Aʿlā: In Aʿlā 87:11, al-ashqā sidesteps (yatajannabu) the reminder. In Layl 92:17, al-atqā will be sidestepped-AWAY-from the Fire (sa-yujannabuhā) — THE SAME ROOT!","He sidestepped the reminder; the reminder-keeper gets sidestepped away from the Fire. Perfect reciprocity, in one root, across two surahs.","Layl 92:17: the God-conscious IS SIDESTEPPED AWAY (yujannabu) from the Fire."]},
  {ar:"الَّذِي كَذَّبَ وَتَوَلَّىٰ", tr:"Alladhī kadhdhaba wa tawallā", v:"16", root:"Kadhdhaba — to call something a lie. tawallā — Form V of the \"turning\" root = to turn your back deliberately.", meaning:"\"who denied and turned away.\"", magic:"This exact two-verb phrase is a Qurʾānic signature, and you own it from THREE places: - Qiyāmah 75:32 — wa lākin kadhdhaba wa tawallā - Layl 92:16 — alladhī kadhdhaba wa tawallā - ʿAlaq 96:13 — in kadhdhaba wa tawallā", grammar:"Form V, weak-final.", why:"Two verbs because saying no and walking away are separate acts.", note:"Kadhdhaba wa tawallā — THREE surahs, identical phrase: 75:32, 92:16, 96:13. And tawallā is in FIVE surahs — including ʿAbasa 80:1, where it described a FROWN.", more:["Three surahs, identical pairing. Denial with the tongue + turning with the body. Both channels of rejection.","And tawallā is now across FIVE surahs — with a remarkable range: Qiyāmah 75:32 (the arrogant denier), ʿAbasa 80:1 (the Prophet's ﷺ momentary frown — corrected!), Ghāshiyah 88:23, Layl 92:16, ʿAlaq 96:13.","The same verb for a shoulder-turn and for total rejection. That tells you how seriously direction is taken.","Alladhī is a relative pronoun describing al-ashqā in v.15 — note the parallel with v.18's alladhī describing al-atqā.","Two men, two relative clauses."]},
  {ar:"وَسَيُجَنَّبُهَا الْأَتْقَى", tr:"Wa sa-yujannabuha'l-atqā", v:"17", root:"Yujannabu — the \"side\" family — janb = a SIDE/flank; jannaba = to keep something away to the side.", meaning:"\"But the most God-conscious will be kept far away from it.\"", magic:"Aʿlā 87:11 — wa yatajannabuhā al-ashqā — \"the most wretched SIDESTEPS it\" (Form V, he does it himself, actively avoiding the reminder).", grammar:"Form II PASSIVE (jannaba → yujannabu). Compare Aʿlā's Form V ACTIVE (yatajannabu).", why:"The passive is the mercy — he doesn't have to save himself.", note:"Aʿlā 87:11: al-ashqā sidesteps the reminder (ACTIVE). Layl 92:17: al-atqā is sidestepped away from the Fire (PASSIVE).", more:["atqā — superlative of taqiyy — the most God-conscious, from wiqāyah (protection/armour).","THE BEST CROSS-SURAH RECIPROCITY IN YOUR WHOLE COLLECTION.","Layl 92:17 — wa sa-yujannabuhā al-atqā — \"the most God-conscious WILL BE KEPT AWAY from it\" (Form II PASSIVE, it's done FOR him).","SAME ROOT. Opposite forms. Opposite people. Opposite objects.","He turned his side to the reminder → so he faces the Fire. He faced the reminder → so the Fire is turned aside from him.","And note the grammar carries the theology: the wretched man does his own avoiding (Form V, active). The God-conscious man doesn't avoid the Fire himself — he IS avoided-away from it (passive). Someone does it FOR him.","Also: al-ashqā (v.15) vs al-atqā (v.17) — both superlatives, rhyming, one letter apart in shape. The two extremes of humanity in two matching words.","Same root, and the form is the whole point.","SAME ROOT (janb = a side).","Perfect reciprocity, and the grammar carries it."]},
  {ar:"يُؤْتِي مَالَهُ يَتَزَكَّىٰ", tr:"Yuʾtī mālahu yatazakkā", v:"18", root:"Yuʾtī — Form IV of \"to come\" = to give / hand over. yatazakkā — the \"purify + grow\" family — zakāh.", meaning:"\"who gives his wealth, purifying himself.\"", magic:"The zakāh root is now across SIX surahs — and its range is remarkable: - Nāziʿāt 79:18 — offered to PHARAOH: \"would you like to purify yourself?\" - ʿAbasa 80:3, 7.", grammar:"Yatazakkā is a ḥāl (circumstantial) — \"while purifying himself.\" Form V = reflexive.", why:"Yatazakkā names giving as self-purification, not loss.", note:"Zakāh = purify AND grow.", more:["The blind man who came seeking it - Aʿlā 87:14 — qad aflaḥa man tazakkā - Shams 91:9 — qad aflaḥa man zakkāhā - Layl 92:18 — yuʾtī mālahu yatazakkā","From the worst tyrant, to the humblest seeker, to the definition of success, to the man giving his money away.","And notice what Layl adds: it CONNECTS zakāh to māl explicitly. The other surahs said \"purify yourself.\" This one shows you how: give your wealth away. The root that means purify AND grow is attached to the thing people think shrinks when you give it.","That's the point of the root: giving zakāh doesn't reduce your wealth — it PURIFIES it, which is what lets it GROW.","Note: unlike ʿAbasa 80:3 (yazzakkā, assimilated), this one keeps the full yatazakkā.","SIX surahs: 79:18 (Pharaoh!), 80:3 & 80:7 (blind man), 87:14, 91:9, 92:18. And Layl is the one that ties it to MĀL: giving doesn't shrink wealth — it purifies it so it GROWS."]},
  {ar:"مِن نِّعْمَةٍ تُجْزَىٰ", tr:"Min niʿmatin tujzā", v:"19", root:"Niʿmah — the \"blessing / favour\" family (naʿam = YES; nāʿim = soft).", meaning:"\"And no one has any favour with him that is to be repaid.\"", magic:"The verse that defines pure intention — by describing what's ABSENT.", grammar:"The بـ… مِن construction — mā li-aḥadin ʿindahu min niʿmatin.", why:"Tujzā (to be repaid) rather than \"thanked\" — it's the language of debt settlement, which is exactly what's being ruled out.", note:"He's not paying anyone back. No debt, no credit, no transaction. Which raises the question v.20 answers: then WHY give?", more:["You own it from Insān 76:20, Infiṭār 82:13, Ghāshiyah 88:8. tujzā — the \"recompense / repayment\" family (jazāʾ = reward/requital).","It's saying: he isn't paying anyone back. No one did him a favour that he's settling. There's no transaction underneath the generosity.","Why does that matter? Because most giving is quietly reciprocal — returning a kindness, building an obligation, buying goodwill. This man's giving has nothing owed on either end.","And that sets up v.20 perfectly: if he's not repaying anyone, and not building credit — then WHY is he giving? v.20 answers.","The min is zāʾidah (extra) after negation, adding emphasis: \"not any favour whatsoever.\"","(And the min is EXTRA — \"not ANY favour whatsoever.\")"]},
  {ar:"إِلَّا ابْتِغَاءَ وَجْهِ رَبِّهِ الْأَعْلَىٰ", tr:"Illa'btighāʾa wajhi rabbihi'l-aʿlā", v:"20", root:"Ibtighāʾ — the \"seeking / desiring\" family (baghā = to seek; ibtaghā = to seek out deliberately).", meaning:"\"Except seeking the Face of his Lord, the Most High.\"", magic:"The answer to v.19's implied question, and you own the idiom exactly.", grammar:"Ibtighāʾa is a verbal noun in the accusative as maf'ūl li-ajlih (purpose): \"[he gives] for the purpose of seeking.\" Form VIII (ibtaghā).", why:"Wajh over \"sake\" — it pictures a face you're doing this FOR.", note:"Insān 76:9 (li-wajhi'llāh) and Layl 92:20 (ibtighāʾa wajhi rabbih) are the SAME portrait. \"For the FACE\" = purely for their sake.", more:["wajh — a FACE — but idiomatically, \"for someone's face\" = purely for their sake.","aʿlā — the MOST HIGH — you own it from Aʿlā 87:1 (sabbiḥ isma rabbika'l-aʿlā).","Insān 76:9 — innamā nuṭʿimukum li-wajhi'llāhi lā nurīdu minkum jazāʾan wa lā shukūrā — \"we feed you only for the Face of Allah; we want no reward and no thanks.\"","Look at how closely Layl 92:19–20 tracks Insān 76:9:","And al-Aʿlā is the title from Aʿlā 87:1."]},
  {ar:"وَلَسَوْفَ يَرْضَىٰ", tr:"Wa la-sawfa yarḍā", v:"21", root:"The \"contentment / pleasure\" family — riḍā = satisfaction; you own it from Ghāshiyah 88:9 (li-saʿyihā rāḍiyah) and Fajr 89:28 (rāḍiyatan marḍiyyah).", meaning:"\"And he is going to be pleased.\"", magic:"The final verse — and it's deliberately, gloriously vague.", grammar:"La-sawfa = the emphatic lām + the future particle sawfa — the strongest future affirmation in Arabic.", why:"Leaving yarḍā without an object is the most generous ending available.", note:"\"He WILL be pleased\" — pleased with WHAT? Not said. Deliberately.", more:["Pleased with WHAT? The verse doesn't say. No object, no specification. Just: he will be pleased.","That deletion is the mercy. By not naming the reward, it's left as large as you can imagine — and larger.","And the emphasis is stacked high: wa + la (emphatic lām) + sawfa (definite future) + yarḍā — \"and he most certainly, definitely, WILL be pleased.\" Three emphasis markers on a four-word verse.","AND THE PAYOFF: the very next surah, Aḍ-Ḍuḥā 93:5, ends the same way — wa la-sawfa yuʿṭīka rabbuka fa-tarḍā (\"and your Lord is going to give you, and you will be pleased\"). SAME construction (wa la-sawfa), SAME verb (tarḍā/yarḍā), CONSECUTIVE SURAHS.","Layl ends with the giver being pleased. Ḍuḥā ends with the Prophet ﷺ being pleased. Two surahs, one closing promise.","And Aḍ-Ḍuḥā 93:5 ends with the SAME construction: wa la-sawfa… fa-tarḍā.","Consecutive surahs, one promise."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:93, name:"Aḍ-Ḍuḥā", ar:"الضحى", en:"The Morning Brightness", verses:11, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"What was done FOR you becomes what you must do FOR others. Three for three. That's the whole surah.",
  special:["Three gifts are listed — then three commands, and they map onto each other in exact order.","Three finds, three fixes, three duties. You were an orphan — so don't crush one. You were lost and asked — so don't rebuff an asker. You were poor and were enriched — so talk about the favour.","Every command is generated by a corresponding gift. The ethics aren't arbitrary; they're your own biography, handed back to you as instructions.","--"],
  big:["Two oaths (v.1–2) — the morning brightness and the night when it settles.","The denial (v.3) — He has not forsaken you, nor does He detest you.","The promise (v.4–5) — the last is better than the first… and you will be pleased.","Three finds (v.6–8) — orphan, lost, needy.","Three commands (v.9–11)."],
  traps:["وَمَا قَلَىٰ (v.3) — the \"you\" pronoun is DELETED. \"Has not forsaken YOU… nor does He detest —\" The word \"detest\" is never allowed to touch him. Don't read a -ka that isn't there.","ضَالًّا (v.7) — does NOT mean \"misguided\" in the sense of false belief. Root image = ḍāllah, a LOST CAMEL — something valuable, searching for the way.","فَأَغْنَىٰ (v.8) — Form IV (He MADE you rich), not Form X istaghnā (you CONSIDERED yourself rich). Same root, opposite morality. The form is everything.","سَجَىٰ (v.2) — the ONLY night-verb in this juzʾ that means STILLNESS. All others (ʿasʿas, yasr, yaghshā) are motion. The stillness is the whole point.","وَدَّعَكَ (v.3) — from wadāʿ, a CEREMONIAL FAREWELL (Ḥajjat al-Wadāʿ). Not just \"left\" — said goodbye, deliberately.","السَّائِلَ (v.10) — means BOTH a beggar AND a questioner. Don't narrow it to one.","تَقْهَرْ (v.9) — from al-Qahhār, a NAME OF ALLAH. Means \"overpower by might,\" not merely \"be unkind.\"","فَحَدِّثْ (v.11) — the command is \"NARRATE IT,\" not \"be grateful.\" Same root as ḥadīth.","خَيْرٌ (v.4) — an IRREGULAR comparative (no afʿal form). Same for sharr. Worth memorizing as an exception.","The v.6–8 / v.9–11 mapping — read them as PAIRS, not two separate lists. Orphan→sheltered/don't crush; lost→guided/don't rebuff; needy→enriched/speak of it."],
  links:[{w:"الضُّحَىٰ (v.1)",e:"ضُحَاهَا (79:29, 79:46, 91:1)",t:"FOURTH appearance — daily gift, a whole lifetime, an oath"},{w:"سَجَىٰ (v.2)",e:"عَسْعَسَ (81:17) · يَسْرِ (89:4) · يَغْشَىٰ (91:4, 92:1)",t:"FIVE night-verbs — this is the ONLY still one"},{w:"وَدَّعَكَ (v.3)",e:"حَجَّة الْوَدَاع",t:"The FAREWELL root — \"He didn't say goodbye\""},{w:"الْآخِرَةُ... الْأُولَىٰ (v.4)",e:"79:25 · 87:17 · 92:13",t:"THREE surahs — and Ḍuḥā adds LAKA (\"for YOU\")"},{w:"وَلَسَوْفَ... تَرْضَىٰ (v.5)",e:"وَلَسَوْفَ يَرْضَىٰ (Layl 92:21)",t:"Back-to-back surahs, identical construction"},{w:"يُعْطِيكَ (v.5)",e:"أَعْطَىٰ (Layl 92:5) · عَطَاءً (Nabaʾ 78:36)",t:"Layl: the man GIVES. Ḍuḥā: the Lord GIVES."},{w:"يَتِيمًا (v.6, 9)",e:"76:8 · 89:17 · 90:15",t:"FOUR surahs — the orphan"},{w:"فَآوَىٰ (v.6)",e:"الْمَأْوَىٰ (Nāziʿāt 79:39, 41)",t:"Same root — Hell's shelter, Paradise's shelter, an orphan's shelter"},{w:"ضَالًّا (v.7)",e:"",t:"Ḍāllah = a LOST CAMEL — something valuable that wandered"},{w:"فَهَدَىٰ (v.7)",e:"قَدَّرَ فَهَدَىٰ (87:3) · هَدَيْنَاهُ (90:10)",t:"THREE surahs — the guidance root"},{w:"فَأَغْنَىٰ (v.8)",e:"اسْتَغْنَىٰ (80:5, 92:8) · يُغْنِيهِ (80:37) · يُغْنِي (88:7)",t:"The root's REDEMPTION — Form IV (He enriches) vs Form X (I need nothing)"},{w:"السَّائِلَ (v.10)",e:"يَتَسَاءَلُونَ (Nabaʾ 78:1)",t:"Same root — asking"},{w:"بِنِعْمَةِ (v.11)",e:"نَعِيمًا (76:20) · نَّاعِمَةٌ (88:8) · نِّعْمَةٍ (92:19)",t:"FOUR surahs — the blessing root"},{w:"فَحَدِّثْ (v.11)",e:"لَا نُرِيدُ... شُكُورًا (Insān 76:9)",t:"Don't seek thanks / do give testimony"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"وَالضُّحَىٰ", tr:"Wa'ḍ-ḍuḥā", v:"1", root:"The \"forenoon brightness\" family — ḍuḥā = mid-morning, when the sun is well up and everything is fully, warmly lit.", meaning:"\"By the morning brightness.\"", magic:"FOURTH appearance of this word in your study — and now it's a surah title: - Nāziʿāt 79:29 — wa akhraja ḍuḥāhā — creation's daily gift of light - Nāziʿāt 79:46.", grammar:"The wāw is wāw al-qasam (the \"by\" of oaths), so the noun is genitive.", why:"ḍuḥā is light at its most reassuring — exactly what a distressed person needs sworn to them.", note:"FOURTH appearance: 79:29 (daily gift), 79:46 (your whole life!), 91:1, 93:1. He swears by the FULLEST light to a man who felt left in the dark.", more:["You know it as Ṣalāt aḍ-Ḍuḥā.","Illā ʿashiyyatan aw ḍuḥāhā — your ENTIRE LIFE, measured as one morning - Shams 91:1 — wa'sh-shamsi wa ḍuḥāhā — the sun's brightness - Ḍuḥā 93:1 — the surah's name","And the choice is perfectly targeted at the situation. The Prophet ﷺ was being told his Lord had abandoned him — so the surah swears by the moment the light is fullest. Not dawn (which is a beginning), not noon — ḍuḥā, the settled, warm, unmistakable brightness.","The oath IS the argument: look at the light. Does that look like abandonment?","Note: every verse in this surah ends in -ā — the same sustained rhyme as Aʿlā (87) and Layl (92)."]},
  {ar:"إِذَا سَجَىٰ", tr:"Idhā sajā", v:"2", root:"The \"becoming still, calm, settled\" family — sajā = to grow quiet and motionless.", meaning:"\"and by the night when it settles into stillness.\"", magic:"The word choice here is a masterpiece of emotional precision, and it's easy to miss.", grammar:"Weak-final past verb (س-ج-و), carrying the rhyme. Idhā + past = a habitual, recurring state.", why:"Sajā over any of night's other verbs — because the complaint was about silence, and this word makes silence peaceful.", note:"Sajā = a CALM, WINDLESS SEA — total stillness. Five surahs describe night; this is the ONLY one where it just goes QUIET.", more:["Used of a calm, windless sea and of an eye that becomes still and languid.","Related: sājin = tranquil.","Every other surah you've studied describes night as something ACTIVE: - Takwīr 81:17 — idhā ʿasʿas — the night CREEPING - Fajr 89:4 — idhā yasr — the night TRAVELLING/departing - Shams 91:4 — idhā yaghshāhā — the night COVERING - Layl 92:1 — idhā yaghshā — the night COVERING - Ḍuḥā 93:2 — idhā sajā — the night GOING STILL","This is the ONLY one where the night just... stops. No creeping, no covering, no journeying. Stillness.","And that's the entire point. The Prophet ﷺ was distressed by a silence — revelation had paused. So the oath is by a stillness that is not abandonment. The night going quiet doesn't mean the morning left. Silence is not absence.","Two oaths: LIGHT (v.1) and QUIET (v.2). Both are things the Prophet ﷺ needed reassuring about.","He was upset by a silence — so the oath is by a silence that isn't abandonment."]},
  {ar:"مَا وَدَّعَكَ... وَمَا قَلَىٰ", tr:"Mā waddaʿaka… wa mā qalā", v:"3", root:"Waddaʿa — the \"leaving / bidding farewell\" family — tawdīʿ = A FAREWELL (Ḥajjat al-Wadāʿ — the Farewell Pilgrimage!).", meaning:"\"Your Lord has not taken leave of you, nor does He detest [you].\"", magic:"Two extraordinary things happen in this short verse.", grammar:"مَا + past tense = past negation.", why:"Waddaʿa (a ceremonial farewell) rather than taraka (left) — because a farewell is intentional, and that's what he feared.", note:"Ḥajjat al-Wadāʿ = the FAREWELL Pilgrimage. Same root.", more:["Form II = to take leave of someone.","qalā — the \"intense dislike / detesting\" family — qilā = severe hatred, the kind of loathing that makes you cast someone off.","FIRST — waddaʿa is a FAREWELL word. You know it from Ḥajjat al-Wadāʿ, the Farewell Pilgrimage, and ṭawāf al-wadāʿ, the Farewell Circuit. A wadāʿ is a deliberate, ceremonial goodbye — you don't waddaʿ someone by accident. So the denial is precise: He didn't say goodbye to you. The silence wasn't a departure.","SECOND — and this is exquisite — look at what's MISSING. The verse says mā waddaʿa-KA (\"has not forsaken YOU\") — but then just wa mā qalā — \"nor does He detest\" — WITH NO \"YOU\" ATTACHED.","The pronoun is deliberately deleted. Classical scholars note this as a mercy of the highest order: the word \"detest\" is not allowed to sit next to \"you\" in the same breath. Even grammatically, He won't place that word against His Prophet ﷺ.","(The rhyme also required it — and that's the beauty: the sound and the mercy arrive together.)","Note the dropped object pronoun on qalā — a known and much-discussed omission.","\"He didn't say goodbye to you.\" And \"detest\" has NO \"you\" attached — the pronoun is dropped so the word never touches him."]},
  {ar:"وَلَلْآخِرَةُ خَيْرٌ لَّكَ مِنَ الْأُولَىٰ", tr:"Wa la'l-ākhiratu khayrun laka mina'l-ūlā", v:"4", root:"ākhirah — the last / the Hereafter. ūlā — the first / this world.", meaning:"\"And the last is better for you than the first.\"", magic:"You own this pairing from TWO surahs already: - Nāziʿāt 79:25 — nakāla'l-ākhirati wa'l-ūlā - Layl 92:13 — wa inna lanā la'l-ākhirata wa'l-ūlā - Aʿlā 87:17 — wa'l-ākhiratu khayrun wa abqā (\"better and more lasting\")", grammar:"Wa + emphatic lām on al-ākhiratu.", why:"Adding laka (\"for you\") turns a doctrine into a personal promise.", note:"Aʿlā 87:17 said \"better and more lasting.\" Ḍuḥā 93:4 adds LAKA — \"better FOR YOU.\" And a second reading: every later part of your life beats the earlier part.", more:["And Aʿlā 87:17 is nearly the same sentence — but with a crucial addition here: khayrun LAKA — \"better FOR YOU.\"","Aʿlā stated a general truth: the Hereafter is better. Ḍuḥā makes it personal: better for YOU, specifically.","And there's a second, gentler reading widely held: al-ākhirah can mean \"what comes later\" and al-ūlā \"what came before\" — i.e. every later stage of your life will be better than the earlier one. To a man in the middle of a hard season, that's the exact comfort needed: it gets better from here.","khayrun is a comparative used without afʿal form (like khayr and sharr — irregular comparatives, worth knowing)."]},
  {ar:"وَلَسَوْفَ يُعْطِيكَ رَبُّكَ فَتَرْضَىٰ", tr:"Wa la-sawfa yuʿṭīka rabbuka fa-tarḍā", v:"5", root:"Yuʿṭī — the \"giving\" family (ʿaṭāʾ = a gift — Nabaʾ 78:36; aʿṭā — Layl 92:5). tarḍā — the \"contentment\" family (riḍā).", meaning:"\"And your Lord is going to give you, and you will be pleased.\"", magic:"Layl 92:21 (the final verse of the previous surah) — wa la-sawfa yarḍā (\"and he will surely be pleased\").", grammar:"La-sawfa = emphatic lām + future particle = the strongest future affirmation in Arabic.", why:"Leaving tarḍā unspecified is the most generous possible promise.", note:"Layl 92:21 ends wa la-sawfa YARḌĀ. Ḍuḥā 93:5 says wa la-sawfa… fa-TARḌĀ. Back-to-back surahs, same construction.", more:["THE BEST CONSECUTIVE-SURAH LINK IN THE WHOLE JUZʾ.","Ḍuḥā 93:5 — wa la-sawfa yuʿṭīka rabbuka fa-tarḍā (\"and your Lord will surely give you, and you will be pleased\").","IDENTICAL construction (wa la-sawfa), IDENTICAL verb (r-ḍ-ā), BACK-TO-BACK SURAHS.","Layl ended by promising the generous giver he'd be pleased. Ḍuḥā opens the same promise to the Prophet ﷺ. Read them in sequence and it's one continuous sentence across a surah boundary.","And yuʿṭī connects to Layl 92:5 — fa-ammā man aʿṭā (\"as for he who GAVE\"). Layl: the man who GIVES. Ḍuḥā: the Lord who GIVES to him. Same root, consecutive surahs, both directions.","And note tarḍā has no object — pleased with what? Not said. Same deliberate deletion as Layl 92:21. As big as you can imagine.","The fa- on fa-tarḍā = \"with the result that.\"","And aʿṭā (Layl 92:5, the man gives) → yuʿṭīka (Ḍuḥā 93:5, the Lord gives)."]},
  {ar:"أَلَمْ يَجِدْكَ يَتِيمًا فَآوَىٰ", tr:"A-lam yajidka yatīman fa-āwā", v:"6", root:"Yajid — to find.", meaning:"\"Did He not find you an orphan and give you shelter?\"", magic:"The āwā connection is the gem here, and it's a genuine payoff.", grammar:"أَلَمْ + jussive = a rhetorical question expecting agreement.", why:"āwā (Form IV, to give shelter) — the causative makes God the active shelterer, not a passive provider.", note:"Maʾwā = SHELTER — Nāziʿāt 79:39 & 41 used it for HELL and PARADISE. Here the same root shelters one orphan.", more:["yatīm — an orphan — you own it from Insān 76:8, Fajr 89:17, Balad 90:15. āwā — the \"taking shelter / giving refuge\" family — maʾwā = a SHELTER/abode (Nāziʿāt 79:39, 41!); awā = to take shelter; āwā (Form IV) = to GIVE shelter.","Nāziʿāt 79:39 — fa-inna'l-jaḥīma hiya'l-maʾwā (\"Hellfire is the shelter\"). Nāziʿāt 79:41 — fa-inna'l-jannata hiya'l-maʾwā (\"Paradise is the shelter\"). Ḍuḥā 93:6 — fa-āwā (\"He gave [you] shelter\").","Nāziʿāt named the two eternal shelters. Ḍuḥā shows Him personally sheltering one orphan boy. Same root: the cosmic destination, and the roof over a child's head.","And notice the structure of v.6–8: three rhetorical questions, all beginning with \"did He not FIND you…\" The verb is wajada (to FIND) — which implies He came looking.","And a-lam is a question expecting \"yes.\" He's not informing the Prophet ﷺ of anything new — He's asking him to remember his own life. The comfort is: your entire biography is the evidence.","Note yajid has dropped its final vowel (jussive after lam).","Fa- = \"and so / and then,\" showing the response was immediate.","And \"did He not FIND you\" — He came looking."]},
  {ar:"وَوَجَدَكَ ضَالًّا فَهَدَىٰ", tr:"Wa wajadaka ḍāllan fa-hadā", v:"7", root:"ḍāll — the \"straying / being lost / not knowing the way\" family — ḍalla = to lose one's way.", meaning:"\"And He found you lost/unaware, and guided you.\"", magic:"An important clarification, because this word is often misread.", grammar:"ḍāllan is an active participle (accusative, as ḥāl — the state he was found in).", why:"ḍāll (a searching wanderer) rather than a word for error — it describes someone looking, which is what he was.", note:"Ḍāllah = A LOST CAMEL — something VALUABLE that wandered off. You don't search for worthless things.", more:["ḍāllah = A LOST CAMEL (an animal that has wandered off and needs finding!).","hadā — the \"guiding\" family (hudā).","Ḍāll here does NOT mean \"misguided\" in the sense of being on a false religion. The root's core image is a lost camel (ḍāllah) — something wandering, looking for the way, not yet arrived. Classical scholars explain it as not yet knowing the details of the revelation and Sharīʿah before prophethood — searching without a map.","And the beautiful implication: a ḍāllah is something valuable that got separated — you don't search for worthless things. He was sought because he mattered.","The hudā root you own from Aʿlā 87:3 (qaddara fa-hadā — \"measured, then guided\") and Balad 90:10 (wa hadaynāhu'n-najdayn — \"guided him to the two heights\").","Note the pattern across all three verses (v.6–8): a LACK, then fa- + the CURE. Orphan → sheltered. Lost → guided. Needy → enriched. Three problems, three immediate solutions.","Note the doubled lām (ḍ-l-l) — a doubled root.","Not \"misguided\" — searching without a map yet."]},
  {ar:"وَوَجَدَكَ عَائِلًا فَأَغْنَىٰ", tr:"Wa wajadaka ʿāʾilan fa-aghnā", v:"8", root:"ʿāʾil — the \"having dependents / being in need\" family — ʿāla = to have a family to support, and hence to be in need.", meaning:"\"And He found you in need, and enriched you.\"", magic:"Two hooks, and one of them completes a thread you've been tracking for surahs.", grammar:"Form IV (aghnā = to make rich) — compare Form X istaghnā = to consider yourself rich.", why:"Aghnā with God as the subject corrects everything istaghnā got wrong.", note:"ʿĀʾilah = A FAMILY — poverty named after having mouths to feed.", more:["ʿāʾilah = A FAMILY in modern Arabic!","aghnā — Form IV of the \"richness\" root = to make rich / suffice.","FIRST: ʿāʾil shares its root with ʿāʾilah, a FAMILY. Because having dependents is what makes you need. Poverty is named after having people to feed.","SECOND — and this is the big one — aghnā is the root of istaghnā, and this is its REDEMPTION.","Track this root across FOUR surahs: - ʿAbasa 80:5 — mani staghnā — the rich chief who thought he needed nothing (bad) - ʿAbasa 80:37 — shaʾnun yughnīh — a concern that leaves room for nothing (his punishment) - Ghāshiyah 88:7 — lā yughnī min jūʿ — food that doesn't suffice (bad) - Layl 92:8 — bakhila wa'staghnā — the withholder who needed nothing (bad) - Ḍuḥā 93:8 — fa-aghnā — HE enriched you (good!)","Every previous appearance was negative — a delusion of self-sufficiency. Here, for the first time, the root is used correctly: God is the one who makes rich; you are the one who is made rich. The problem was never ghinā — it was claiming it for yourself.","Same root, and the form is the entire moral difference.","And aghnā REDEEMS the root: ʿAbasa 80:5 & Layl 92:8 = istaghnā (\"I need nothing\" — Form X, a delusion).","Ḍuḥā 93:8 = aghnā (\"HE enriched you\" — Form IV, the truth).","The form is the whole difference."]},
  {ar:"فَلَا تَقْهَرْ", tr:"Fa-lā taqhar", v:"9", root:"The \"overpowering / subduing by force\" family — qahara = to dominate, to crush, to overwhelm someone weaker.", meaning:"\"So as for the orphan — do not oppress [him].\"", magic:"The verb is chosen with terrifying precision.", grammar:"لا الناهية (lā of prohibition) + jussive — note taqhar has dropped its final vowel.", why:"Qahara names the specific abuse a powerless person is vulnerable to.", note:"Al-Qahhār — \"The Subduer,\" a NAME OF ALLAH. Same root. \"Don't use your power on the powerless\" — that's His role, not yours.", more:["Al-Qahhār — \"The Subduer,\" a Name of Allah; qahr = coercion.","Qahara means to overpower someone because you can — using your strength on someone who has none. It's not \"don't be unkind\" — it's don't use your power against the powerless.","And an orphan is defined by having no protector. He's the easiest person in society to qahar. That's exactly why the word is used.","And here's the weight of it: al-Qahhār is one of Allah's own Names. Overpowering-by-might is His attribute — and the Prophet ﷺ is told not to exercise it on an orphan. The verse quietly says: that's not your role.","Compare Fajr 89:17 — bal lā tukrimūna'l-yatīm (\"you do not HONOUR the orphan\"). Fajr: don't fail to honour him. Ḍuḥā: don't crush him. Two surahs, floor and ceiling.","The fa-ammā… fa- structure is a \"as for X… then Y\" frame, used three times in v.9–11.","Compare Fajr 89:17: don't fail to HONOUR him."]},
  {ar:"فَلَا تَنْهَرْ", tr:"Fa-lā tanhar", v:"10", root:"Tanhar — the \"scolding harshly / rebuffing / driving away with a shout\" family — nahara = to snap at someone, to repel them roughly.", meaning:"\"And as for the one who asks — do not repel [him].\"", magic:"The ambiguity of sāʾil is a feature, and both readings are classical.", grammar:"Lā of prohibition + jussive.", why:"Nahara targets the manner, not the outcome — you're being told how to say no.", note:"Sāʾil = a BEGGAR and a QUESTIONER (same root as yatasāʾalūn, Nabaʾ 78:1). Both covered.", more:["sāʾil — one who ASKS (from saʾala — the same root as yatasāʾalūn, Nabaʾ 78:1!) — means both \"a beggar\" AND \"a questioner.\"","Sāʾil = someone asking for MONEY (a beggar) OR someone asking for KNOWLEDGE (a questioner). The Qurʾān doesn't disambiguate — so the command covers both. Don't snap at the beggar; don't snap at the student.","And it maps back onto v.7: the Prophet ﷺ was ḍāll — searching, not knowing — and he was guided, not rebuffed. So: do the same for the next person who's searching.","Note the nahara image: it's the verb for shooing someone away with a raised voice. Not refusing — refusing rudely. You may not have money to give. You must still not snap.","Note the deliberate rhyme with v.9: taqhar / tanhar — two prohibitions, matched sounds.","And nahara = shooing someone away with a raised voice — you may refuse, but never snap.","Maps to v.7: he was searching, and was guided, not rebuffed."]},
  {ar:"فَحَدِّثْ", tr:"Fa-ḥaddith", v:"11", root:"The \"newness / relating news\" family — ḥadatha = to happen/be new; ḥadīth = (a) speech/narration and (b) NEW.", meaning:"\"And as for the favour of your Lord — speak of it.\"", magic:"The third command, and it's the one nobody expects.", grammar:"Form II imperative.", why:"ḥaddith (narrate) rather than ushkur (be grateful) — because gratitude is internal, and this command is about making it audible.", note:"Ḥadīth = a NARRATION and \"NEW.\" Same root.", more:["And of course al-Ḥadīth, the Prophet's ﷺ narrations!","Form II ḥaddatha = to narrate, to tell about.","You'd expect: \"be grateful,\" or \"don't boast.\" Instead: TALK ABOUT IT.","The verb is ḥaddith — the very root of ḥadīth, a narration. So: narrate the favour. Make it into speech others hear.","And why is that a command, not vanity? Because of the two verses before it. v.9: don't crush the orphan. v.10: don't rebuff the asker. v.11: TELL people what was done for you — so that the next orphan and the next asker know that rescue is possible.","Your testimony is someone else's evidence.","And note the root also means NEW (ḥadīth = new) — a favour spoken about stays fresh; a favour kept silent goes stale.","Compare Insān 76:9 — lā nurīdu minkum jazāʾan wa lā shukūrā (they want no thanks). Insān: don't seek thanks from people. Ḍuḥā: do speak of what Allah gave. Not a contradiction — one is about taking credit, the other about giving evidence.","Note bi-niʿmati rabbika is FRONTED before the verb — \"as for the favour of your Lord — speak!\" The fronting spotlights whose favour it is: not yours.","The command isn't \"be grateful\" — it's \"NARRATE IT.\" Because v.9 and v.10 are about orphans and askers — and your story is their evidence that rescue happens."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:94, name:"Ash-Sharḥ", ar:"الشرح", en:"The Relief", verses:8, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"THE hardship (one, definite). A ease… a ease (two, indefinite). And they arrive together, not in sequence.",
  special:["One sentence is repeated twice in a row — and the repetition is a mathematical argument.","V.5: fa-inna maʿa'l-ʿusri yusrā — \"So indeed, with the hardship is ease.\" v.6: inna maʿa'l-ʿusri yusrā — \"Indeed, with the hardship is ease.\"","Classical scholars noticed something in the Arabic that English cannot show:","Al-ʿusr (hardship) is DEFINITE — \"THE hardship\" — so both verses refer to the same one. - yusran (ease) is INDEFINITE — \"a ease\" — so each mention is a different one.","The result: ONE hardship, TWO eases. A famous saying attributed to the early generations captures it: \"one hardship will never overcome two eases.\"","And note the preposition: maʿa = \"WITH,\" not \"after.\" The ease isn't waiting on the far side of the hardship — it's travelling alongside it.","--"],
  big:["Three gifts (v.1–4) — the chest expanded, the burden removed, the mention raised.","The law (v.5–6) — with hardship comes ease. Twice.","Two commands (v.7–8) — when you finish, exert yourself; and to your Lord, turn with longing."],
  traps:["الْعُسْرِ is DEFINITE; يُسْرًا is INDEFINITE (v.5–6) — this is the entire argument. ONE hardship, TWO eases. If you don't see the al- vs. the tanwīn, you miss the verse.","مَعَ (v.5–6) means \"WITH,\" not \"AFTER.\" The ease accompanies the hardship — it isn't waiting on the other side.","فَانصَبْ (v.7) — same root as nāṣibah (Ghāshiyah 88:3, EXHAUSTED faces) and nuṣibat (88:19, mountains SET UP). Two kinds of toil — the command is to be the second kind.","فَارْغَب (v.8) — raghiba FĪ = to DESIRE. raghiba ʿAN = to SHUN. The preposition reverses the meaning entirely.","وَإِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ is FRONTED (v.8) — fronting = RESTRICTION. \"To your Lord alone,\" not merely \"also to your Lord.\"","وِزْرَكَ (v.2) — from the root of wazīr (a minister — \"the load-bearer\") and wazar (Qiyāmah 75:11, a mountain shelter). Not just \"sin\" — a physical crushing load.","أَنقَضَ (v.3) — means \"made it CREAK/CRACK,\" the audible groan of something under unbearable weight. Not merely \"was heavy.\"","فَرَغْتَ (v.7) — from farāgh, EMPTINESS / free time. \"When you become EMPTY,\" not just \"when you finish.\"","نَشْرَحْ / نَجْعَل-type jussives — after lam, the verb DROPS its final vowel. Same rule as Ḍuḥā 93:6 (yajidka).","The three gifts (v.1–4) are in CAUSAL order — opened → unloaded → raised. You can only be lifted once the weight is off."],
  links:[{w:"أَلَمْ نَشْرَحْ (v.1)",e:"أَلَمْ يَجِدْكَ (Ḍuḥā 93:6–8)",t:"Consecutive surahs — comfort by rhetorical question"},{w:"نَشْرَحْ (v.1)",e:"",t:"Sharḥ = A COMMENTARY (\"opening a text out\")"},{w:"وَضَعْنَا (v.2)",e:"مَّوْضُوعَةٌ (Ghāshiyah 88:14)",t:"Same root — placing / setting down"},{w:"وِزْرَكَ (v.2)",e:"وَزَر (Qiyāmah 75:11)",t:"Same root — a crushing load / a mountain shelter. And wazīr = a MINISTER"},{w:"ظَهْرَكَ (v.3)",e:"وَرَاءَ ظَهْرِهِ (Inshiqāq 84:10)",t:"Same word — the back"},{w:"رَفَعْنَا (v.4)",e:"مَّرْفُوعَةٍ (80:14) · مَّرْفُوعَةٌ (88:13) · رُفِعَتْ (88:18)",t:"FOUR surahs — and marfūʿ = the nominative case"},{w:"ذِكْرَكَ (v.4)",e:"76:29 · 80:11 · 81:27 · 87:9 · 88:21 · 89:23",t:"SEVEN surahs — the dhikr thread"},{w:"الْعُسْرِ / يُسْرًا (v.5–6)",e:"80:20 · 84:8 · 87:8 · 92:7 · 92:10",t:"SIX surahs for y-s-r"},{w:"فَانصَبْ (v.7)",e:"نَّاصِبَةٌ (88:3) · نُصِبَتْ (88:19)",t:"Same root — exhausted faces / mountains set up / you choose"},{w:"وَإِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ (v.8)",e:"75:12 · 75:30 · 79:44 · 88:25",t:"FIVE surahs — the fronting that means \"to Him ALONE\""}],
  words:[
  {ar:"أَلَمْ نَشْرَحْ لَكَ صَدْرَكَ", tr:"A-lam nashraḥ laka ṣadrak", v:"1", root:"Sharaḥa — the \"opening out / expanding / laying open\" family — originally to slice meat open and spread it flat.", meaning:"\"Did We not expand for you your chest?\"", magic:"The root gives you a free everyday word and a perfect image.", grammar:"أَلَمْ + jussive = rhetorical question expecting agreement. Note nashraḥ has dropped its final vowel.", why:"Sharaḥa over \"calmed\" — it's a word of making space, not of soothing.", note:"Sharḥ = A COMMENTARY — \"opening a text out\" so you can see inside. Same verb: your chest was OPENED OUT.", more:["From that: to expand, and sharḥ = AN EXPLANATION/commentary (you \"open up\" a text so people can see inside it!).","ṣadr — the CHEST — and by extension the front of anything (ṣadr al-majlis = the head of the gathering; ṣadara = to issue forth).","Sharḥ is what a scholar writes to explain a difficult book — a commentary \"opens the text out\" so you can see what's inside. Same verb here: your chest was opened out.","And why the CHEST specifically? In Arabic the ṣadr is where you carry anxiety, grief, and constriction — the opposite state is ḍayq aṣ-ṣadr (tightness of chest), the standard idiom for distress. So sharḥ aṣ-ṣadr = the removal of that tightness: capacity, calm, room to breathe.","Notice the sequence in v.1–4: three gifts, and they're all about weight and space: the chest EXPANDED (room made), the burden REMOVED (weight lifted), the mention RAISED (elevation given). Open, unload, lift.","And a-lam is a question expecting \"yes\" — the same technique as Ḍuḥā 93:6–8 (a-lam yajidka…). Two consecutive surahs comforting by asking him to remember.","And laka (\"for you\") is fronted before ṣadrak — spotlighting that it was done FOR HIM.","And ḍayq aṣ-ṣadr (tight chest) = the standard Arabic idiom for distress."]},
  {ar:"وَوَضَعْنَا عَنكَ وِزْرَكَ", tr:"Wa waḍaʿnā ʿanka wizrak", v:"2", root:"Waḍaʿa — the \"putting down / setting aside\" family — you own it from Ghāshiyah 88:14 (akwābun mawḍūʿah, cups set out).", meaning:"\"And We removed from you your burden.\"", magic:"The wazīr connection is one of the best free hooks in Arabic.", grammar:"Waḍaʿa ʿan = \"to remove from\" — note the preposition.", why:"Wizr (a crushing physical load) rather than an abstract word for sin or worry — it makes the relief bodily.", note:"WAZĪR = A MINISTER — \"the one who CARRIES THE LOAD.\" Same root as this burden. And Qiyāmah 75:11 used wazar = a MOUNTAIN SHELTER.", more:["Mawḍūʿ = a TOPIC (something \"placed before you\").","wizr — the \"heavy burden / load\" family — wizr = a crushing load and, by extension, a sin; wazīr = a MINISTER (one who carries the ruler's load!) — and you own the root from Qiyāmah 75:11 (lā wazar, \"no refuge/mountain-shelter\").","A wazīr (minister/vizier) is literally \"the one who bears the weight\" — the person who carries the burden of governing so the ruler doesn't have to. Same root as the wizr being lifted here.","And you already met the root in Qiyāmah 75:11 — kallā lā wazar (\"No! There is no refuge\"). There, wazar meant a mountain stronghold — a place that bears you. Two surahs, one root: a load that crushes, and a shelter that carries.","Note the verb: waḍaʿnā ʿanka — \"We PUT [it] DOWN, OFF you.\" Not \"We forgave\" or \"We lightened\" — it was physically taken off your back. And the ʿan (\"off/away from\") makes the removal directional.","ʿanka is fronted before wizrak — the same emphatic fronting as v.1's laka.","One root: the load, and the thing that bears it."]},
  {ar:"الَّذِي أَنقَضَ ظَهْرَكَ", tr:"Alladhī anqaḍa ẓahrak", v:"3", root:"Anqaḍa — an extraordinary word: from naqaḍa = to undo, unravel, break apart.", meaning:"\"which had weighed down / made your back creak.\"", magic:"This is the most physically vivid word in the surah, and it's about SOUND.", grammar:"Form IV (anqaḍa = to cause to creak). Alladhī is a relative pronoun describing wizrak in v.2.", why:"Anqaḍa (made it creak) over \"was heavy\" — because a sound is more real than an adjective.", note:"Anqaḍa = TO MAKE SOMETHING CREAK/CRACK under weight. The sound a beam makes before it breaks. You could HEAR the burden.", more:["Anqaḍa specifically = to make something CREAK/CRACK under weight — the sound a loaded structure makes just before it breaks.","ẓahr — the BACK (also ẓahara = to appear/become visible — what's on your back is what shows).","Anqaḍa describes the AUDIBLE CRACK of something under unbearable load — like a wooden beam groaning, or a saddle creaking on an overloaded camel. The burden wasn't just heavy: you could HEAR it.","And naqaḍa's core meaning — to unravel / undo a rope's twist — adds another layer: the load was coming apart at the seams.","Compare Qiyāmah 75:29 — wa'ltaffati's-sāqu bi's-sāq (legs twisting together in agony) — and Ghāshiyah 88:3 — ʿāmilatun nāṣibah (working, worn out). The Qurʾān keeps describing distress through the body's mechanics, not through emotion words.","And note the tenderness of the sequence: v.2 says the burden was removed. v.3 pauses to acknowledge how heavy it actually was. He doesn't just fix it — He names how bad it was.","And v.2 removes it; v.3 acknowledges how heavy it was."]},
  {ar:"وَرَفَعْنَا لَكَ ذِكْرَكَ", tr:"Wa rafaʿnā laka dhikrak", v:"4", root:"Rafaʿa — the \"raising / elevating\" family — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:14 (ṣuḥufin marfūʿah), Ghāshiyah 88:13 (sururun marfūʿah), Ghāshiyah 88:18 (as-samāʾi kayfa rufiʿat); and marfūʿ = the NOMINATIVE case in grammar (the \"raised\" case!).", meaning:"\"And We raised high for you your mention.\"", magic:"The third gift, and it completes the pattern perfectly.", grammar:"Laka fronted again — third time in four verses (laka v.1, ʿanka v.2, laka v.4).", why:"Rafaʿnā (We raised) with the divine subject — the elevation wasn't earned by reputation-building; it was granted.", note:"Three gifts in causal order: chest OPENED → burden REMOVED → mention RAISED. You can only be lifted once the weight is off.", more:["dhikr — the \"mention / remembrance\" family — you own it across SIX surahs now (76:29, 80:11, 81:27, 87:9, 88:21, 89:23).","V.1: chest OPENED (space made inside). v.2–3: burden REMOVED (weight taken off). v.4: mention RAISED (elevation given).","Notice the physics: you can only be raised once the weight is off. The three gifts are in causal order — empty, unload, lift.","And the dhikr root is the one that has defined the Prophet's ﷺ mission for six surahs — he was told to remind (dhakkir) again and again. Here the same root turns around: the reminder's own mention is raised. He spent his life mentioning; his mention was elevated.","And there's a reality every Muslim lives: his name is joined to Allah's in the shahādah, in the adhān, in every tashahhud. You enact verse 4 several times a day.","The surah keeps putting \"for you / from you\" ahead of the object.","Everything is personalized.","And you enact v.4 in every adhān and tashahhud."]},
  {ar:"فَإِنَّ مَعَ الْعُسْرِ يُسْرًا", tr:"Fa-inna maʿa'l-ʿusri yusrā", v:"5–6", root:"ʿusr — the \"hardship / difficulty\" family (ʿasīr = difficult; muʿsir = in financial straits).", meaning:"\"So indeed, with the hardship is ease. Indeed, with the hardship is ease.\"", magic:"THE MOST QUOTED VERSE IN THIS JUZʾ — and there are THREE things in the Arabic that translation hides.", grammar:"Maʿa = \"with/alongside\" (an adverb of accompaniment). The definite al- vs.", why:"Maʿa over baʿda — because a person in the middle of hardship needs to know the ease is already here, not scheduled.", note:"THE hardship (definite = ONE). A ease… a ease (indefinite = TWO).", more:["yusr — the \"ease / smoothness\" family (yassara = to make easy; and al-yusrā, yasīr — you own these from ʿAbasa 80:20, Inshiqāq 84:8, Aʿlā 87:8, Layl 92:7 & 92:10).","ONE — THE DEFINITE/INDEFINITE ASYMMETRY. al-ʿusr = THE hardship (definite, so the same one both times). yusran = A ease (indefinite, so a different one each time). Result: ONE hardship, TWO eases. The classical maxim: \"one hardship will never defeat two eases.\"","TWO — THE PREPOSITION. It says maʿa = \"WITH,\" NOT baʿda (\"after\"). The ease is not on the far side of the hardship — it is accompanying it, right now, inside it.","THREE — THE REPETITION ITSELF. Saying the identical sentence twice is takrār — and the second one drops the fa-, making it stand alone as a bare, absolute law. v.5 is a consequence (\"SO indeed…\"); v.6 is a principle (\"Indeed…\").","And the placement is everything: it comes right after three verses describing a burden so heavy it made his back CREAK. This isn't abstract encouragement — it's said to someone who was just described as breaking.","The y-s-r root is now across SIX surahs: ʿAbasa 80:20, Inshiqāq 84:8, Aʿlā 87:8, Layl 92:7, Layl 92:10, Sharḥ 94:5–6.","the tanwīn on yusran is the whole argument — learn to see definiteness in Arabic and this verse opens up.","\"One hardship will never defeat two eases.\" And maʿa = \"WITH,\" not \"after.\" The ease travels alongside."]},
  {ar:"فَإِذَا فَرَغْتَ فَانصَبْ", tr:"Fa-idhā faraghta fa'nṣab", v:"7", root:"Faragha — the \"emptying / becoming free / finishing\" family.", meaning:"\"So when you have finished, then exert yourself / stand up [to the next task].\"", magic:"THE BEST ROOT-PAYOFF IN THIS SURAH — and it connects straight back to Ghāshiyah.", grammar:"Imperative (inṣab, with the connecting hamzah).", why:"Inṣab (a toil word) rather than a word for worship — because the point is effort, whatever it's directed at.", note:"Naṣab: Ghāshiyah 88:3 = faces WORN OUT (wasted effort). Ghāshiyah 88:19 = mountains SET UP (lasting effort).", more:["Farāgh = EMPTINESS / free time (modern Arabic: waqt al-farāgh = leisure time!); fārigh = empty.","inṣab — the \"setting upright / toiling to exhaustion\" family — THE SAME ROOT as nāṣibah (Ghāshiyah 88:3, \"exhausted\") and nuṣibat (Ghāshiyah 88:19, \"the mountains were SET UP\")!","The root n-ṣ-b has two faces you already own: - Ghāshiyah 88:3 — ʿāmilatun nāṣibah — faces WORN OUT from toil (effort that exhausts and earns nothing) - Ghāshiyah 88:19 — wa ila'l-jibāli kayfa nuṣibat — the mountains SET UP FIRMLY (effort that stands forever) - Sharḥ 94:7 — fa'nṣab — YOU exert yourself / stand up","The command is: be the mountain kind of naṣab, not the exhausted-faces kind. Same root, and you choose which one you are.","And faragha is beautiful: farāgh = EMPTY TIME. So the verse literally says: \"when you become EMPTY — FILL UP.\" The moment a task is done, the vacancy is to be occupied.","Classical readings of what to move on to: from worldly affairs to worship; from one act of worship to another; from daʿwah to prayer. All share one idea: rest is not the reward for finishing. The next thing is.","Note the placement — right after \"with hardship comes ease.\" The surah refuses to let \"ease\" mean \"stop.\"","Idhā + past tense = \"when you have [finished]\" — a definite future condition.","Sharḥ 94:7 = YOU — choose which.","\"When you become EMPTY — FILL UP.\""]},
  {ar:"وَإِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ فَارْغَب", tr:"Wa ilā rabbika fa'rghab", v:"8", root:"The \"desiring / yearning / turning toward with longing\" family — raghiba fī = to want something eagerly.", meaning:"\"And to your Lord turn [all] longing.\"", magic:"The final verse, and the grammar carries the whole meaning.", grammar:"Imperative.", why:"Raghiba (yearning) rather than a word for worship or obedience.", note:"\"TO YOUR LORD\" is FRONTED = to Him ALONE.", more:["But raghiba ʿAN = to turn AWAY from in distaste!","The preposition flips the meaning completely.","Also raghbah = a strong desire.","Look at the word order: wa ilā rabbika fa'rghab — \"and TO YOUR LORD — [then] direct your longing.\" The phrase \"to your Lord\" is FRONTED before the verb.","In Arabic, fronting = RESTRICTION. So it means: to your Lord ALONE direct your longing. Not \"also want Allah\" — \"want Him, and specifically Him.\"","You've seen this exact fronting move across FIVE surahs now: - Qiyāmah 75:12 — ilā rabbika yawmaʾidhin al-mustaqarr - Qiyāmah 75:30 — ilā rabbika yawmaʾidhin al-masāq - Nāziʿāt 79:44 — ilā rabbika muntahāhā - Ghāshiyah 88:25 — inna ilaynā iyābahum - Sharḥ 94:8 — wa ilā rabbika fa'rghab","Four of them are about where you END UP. This one is about where you want to end up. The others describe the destination; this one commands the desire.","And the preposition trap is important: raghiba FĪ = to desire something; raghiba ʿAN = to shun it. Here it's ilā — direct your longing TOWARD.","Beautiful pairing with v.7: v.7 = WORK (inṣab — exert your body). v.8 = WANT (irghab — direct your heart). Effort and longing, action and intention, in two final commands.","Note the fa- on fa'rghab even though ilā rabbika comes first — this is a known construction where the fronted phrase is emphasized and the fa keeps the sentence connected.","Because the last command is about what you want, which is upstream of everything you do.","FIVE surahs use this move: 75:12, 75:30, 79:44, 88:25, 94:8. raghiba FĪ = desire it; raghiba ʿAN = shun it."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:95, name:"At-Tīn", ar:"التين", en:"The Fig", verses:8, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Highest form, then lowest of the low, one verse apart. The distance a human can travel is the surah's entire subject.",
  special:["Two verses hold the highest and lowest points in the Qurʾān's description of a human being — back to back.","V.4: laqad khalaqna'l-insāna fī AḤSANI TAQWĪM — \"the BEST possible form.\" v.5: thumma radadnāhu ASFALA SĀFILĪN — \"the LOWEST of the low.\"","Both are superlatives. Aḥsan = the most beautiful. Asfal = the very lowest. And sāfilīn is itself a plural of \"low ones\" — so it's \"the lowest of those who are already low.\" A superlative stacked on a plural.","One verse apart, and the only thing connecting them is thumma (\"then\"). No explanation. No transition. Just: made at the top → returned to the bottom.","And then v.6 carves out the exception — the only escape hatch in the surah.","--"],
  big:["Four oaths (v.1–3) — the fig, the olive, Mount Sinai, and this secure city.","The height (v.4) — created in the best possible form.","The fall (v.5) — returned to the lowest of the low.","The exception (v.6) — except those who believed and did righteous deeds.","Two closing questions (v.7–8) — so what makes you deny the Reckoning? Is Allah not the most just of judges?"],
  traps:["أَسْفَلَ سَافِلِينَ (v.5) — a SUPERLATIVE stacked on a PLURAL: \"the lowest of [those already] low.\" Not just \"very low.\" And note v.8 uses the identical construction for Allah's Name.","مَمْنُونٍ (v.6) — DOUBLE meaning: (a) cut off and (b) a favour held over your head. The reward is endless AND never thrown back at you.","الدِّينِ (v.7) — means \"the RECKONING / settlement of accounts,\" NOT \"religion.\" Root = dayn, A DEBT.","تَقْوِيمٍ (v.4) — from qāma, TO STAND. Same root as al-mustaqīm (the straight path). Not merely \"beautiful form\" — properly upright.","ثُمَّ (v.5) — the ONLY word connecting the highest and lowest verses. No explanation is given. That silence is deliberate.","بِأَحْكَمِ (v.8) — the bāʾ is EXTRA (emphasis after laysa), not \"with.\" Same as Takwīr 81:22, 24, 25 and Infiṭār 82:16.","بَعْدُ (v.7) — ḍammah, no tanwīn = \"after [that]\" with the object deleted but understood. A known construction.","غَيْرُ (v.6) — takes the following noun in the GENITIVE (ghayru mamnūnin). A very common negating construction.","الْأَمِينِ (v.3) — same root as ĪMĀN (faith). Security and faith are one word-family in Arabic.","The four oaths (v.1–3) — may be a GEOGRAPHICAL MAP of prophethood (ʿĪsā's land → Sinai/Mūsā → Makkah/Muḥammad ﷺ), not just a list of good things."],
  links:[{w:"الزَّيْتُونِ (v.1)",e:"زَيْتُونًا (ʿAbasa 80:29)",t:"Identical word — and zayt = OIL"},{w:"طُورِ سِينِينَ (v.2)",e:"بِالْوَادِ الْمُقَدَّسِ طُوًى (Nāziʿāt 79:16)",t:"Same event — the valley / the mountain"},{w:"هَٰذَا الْبَلَدِ (v.3)",e:"هَٰذَا الْبَلَدِ (Balad 90:1–2)",t:"Same phrase — and Balad called him ḥill (unbound) in it"},{w:"الْأَمِينِ (v.3)",e:"ثَمَّ أَمِينٍ (Takwīr 81:21)",t:"Same root — amn, amānah, ĪMĀN, al-Amīn"},{w:"أَحْسَنِ (v.4)",e:"الْحُسْنَىٰ (Layl 92:6, 9)",t:"Same root — beauty/goodness"},{w:"تَقْوِيمٍ (v.4)",e:"يَسْتَقِيمَ (Takwīr 81:28) · الْمُسْتَقِيم",t:"Same root — made UPRIGHT, asked to stay upright"},{w:"تَقْوِيمٍ (v.4)",e:"سَوَّاكَ (82:7) · فَسَوَّى (87:2) · سَوَّاهَا (91:7)",t:"Different root, same claim — built to spec"},{w:"رَدَدْنَاهُ (v.5)",e:"لَمَرْدُودُونَ (Nāziʿāt 79:10)",t:"Same root — being sent back"},{w:"أَسْفَلَ سَافِلِينَ (v.5)",e:"أَحْكَمِ الْحَاكِمِينَ (v.8, same surah)",t:"Identical construction — superlative on a plural"},{w:"أَجْرٌ غَيْرُ مَمْنُونٍ (v.6)",e:"Inshiqāq 84:25",t:"VERBATIM identical phrase"},{w:"بِالدِّينِ (v.7)",e:"الدِّينِ (Infiṭār 82:9, 15, 17, 18)",t:"Same word — dayn = A DEBT, Settlement Day"},{w:"فَمَا يُكَذِّبُكَ (v.7)",e:"مَا غَرَّكَ (Infiṭār 82:6)",t:"Two unanswerable questions aimed at the reader"},{w:"بِأَحْكَمِ (v.8)",e:"بِمَجْنُونٍ (81:22) · بِغَائِبِينَ (82:16)",t:"The EXTRA bāʾ after negation"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"وَالتِّينِ وَالزَّيْتُونِ", tr:"Wa't-tīni wa'z-zaytūn", v:"1", root:"Tīn — the FIG. zaytūn — the OLIVE (zayt = oil!", meaning:"\"By the fig and the olive.\"", magic:"The most-discussed oath in this juzʾ, and both main readings are worth holding.", grammar:"The wāw is wāw al-qasam (the \"by\" of oaths), so both nouns are genitive.", why:"Starting with two humble fruits and ending at the Kaʿbah moves from the ordinary to the sacred, which is this juzʾ's signature method.", note:"Zayt = OIL (zayt zaytūn = olive oil).", more:["— the olive is literally \"the oil-thing\") — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:29 (wa zaytūnan wa nakhlā).","READING ONE — the literal fruits. Two of the most nourishing, useful plants known to that world. The fig is eaten whole, seed and skin — nothing wasted. The olive gives food, oil, fuel, light, and medicine. Two plants that give everything they have.","READING TWO — two LANDS. Many classical scholars read tīn and zaytūn as pointing to the regions famous for them — the land of Damascus/Jerusalem — which makes all four oaths a map of revelation: - v.1 — fig & olive → the land of ʿĪsā (peace be upon him) - v.2 — Mount Sinai → Mūsā (peace be upon him) - v.3 — this secure city → Muḥammad ﷺ","Read that way, the oaths trace prophethood across three places, ending at Makkah. A geographical chain, sworn in order.","And zayt = OIL is a free hook — you know it from zayt zaytūn (olive oil) in any Arabic kitchen.","Note the surah's rhyme: -īn / -ūn / -īm — a nasal ending sustained across all eight verses.","And the four oaths may be a MAP: fig & olive (ʿĪsā's land) → Sinai (Mūsā) → this city (Muḥammad ﷺ).","Prophethood, in geographical order."]},
  {ar:"وَطُورِ سِينِينَ", tr:"Wa ṭūri sīnīn", v:"2", root:"ṭūr — a MOUNTAIN (a Syriac-origin word absorbed into Arabic, used specifically for Mount Sinai in the Qurʾān).", meaning:"\"and by Mount Sinai.\"", magic:"The mountain where Mūsā (peace be upon him) received revelation and spoke with his Lord.", grammar:"Iḍāfah (\"the mountain of Sinai\") — ṭūri is genitive after the oath-wāw, and Sīnīn is genitive by iḍāfah.", why:"Naming the specific mountain of revelation ties this surah's argument to the chain of prophets.", note:"Ṭūr = MOUNTAIN (specifically Sinai in the Qurʾān). Nāziʿāt 79:16 named the VALLEY (Ṭuwā); Tīn 95:2 names the MOUNTAIN. Same event.", more:["Sīnīn — Sinai (also appears as Sīnāʾ).","You own the Mūsā-at-the-mountain scene from Nāziʿāt 79:16 — idh nādāhu rabbuhu bi'l-wādi'l-muqaddasi Ṭuwā (\"when his Lord called him in the sacred valley of Ṭuwā\"). Same event, two surahs — Nāziʿāt names the valley, Tīn names the mountain.","And note the escalation of the oath-objects: v.1 = two plants (small, everyday). v.2 = a mountain (huge, singular, historic). v.3 = a city (inhabited, sacred). Plant → mountain → city: from what grows, to what stands, to where people live.","Note Sīnīn keeps its -īn ending to carry the rhyme.","Oaths escalate: plants → mountain → city."]},
  {ar:"وَهَٰذَا الْبَلَدِ الْأَمِينِ", tr:"Wa hādha'l-baladi'l-amīn", v:"3", root:"Balad — a city/town — you own it from Balad 90:1–2 (lā uqsimu bi-hādha'l-balad).", meaning:"\"and by this secure city.\"", magic:"You own this phrase almost exactly, and the comparison is illuminating.", grammar:"Al-amīn is an adjective agreeing with al-balad in case, definiteness, gender, and number.", why:"Amīn over \"sacred\" — because security is what the sanctuary actually provides, and it's the same root as faith.", note:"Amn (security), amānah (a trust), ĪMĀN (FAITH), al-Amīn (his title), āmīn — ALL ONE ROOT.", more:["amīn — the \"safety / security / trust\" family — amn = security; amānah = a trust; īmān = FAITH; and al-Amīn — the Prophet's ﷺ own title before prophethood — you own it from Takwīr 81:21 (muṭāʿin thamma amīn).","Balad 90:1 — lā uqsimu bi-hādha'l-balad (\"I swear by THIS city\"). Tīn 95:3 — wa hādha'l-baladi'l-amīn (\"and by THIS SECURE city\").","Same demonstrative (hādhā), same noun (al-balad) — Tīn adds al-amīn (the secure).","And the contrast is remarkable. Balad 90:2 immediately said wa anta ḥillun bi-hādha'l-balad — \"you are UNBOUND / treated as fair game in this city.\" Tīn calls the same city al-amīn — the SECURE one.","The city is secure by divine designation — and yet its own Prophet ﷺ wasn't safe in it. Two surahs, one city, two truths held together.","And the amīn hook is superb: amn (security), amānah (a trust), īmān (FAITH), al-Amīn (his title), and āmīn (the word you say after al-Fātiḥah) — all from one root of security and trustworthiness.","The four-way agreement rule for Arabic adjectives.","Both genitive after the oath.","Faith and safety are the same word-family.","And Balad 90:1 swore by \"THIS city\" too."]},
  {ar:"فِي أَحْسَنِ تَقْوِيمٍ", tr:"Fī aḥsani taqwīm", v:"4", root:"Aḥsan — superlative of ḥasan (good/beautiful) = the BEST/most beautiful — you own the family from al-ḥusnā (Layl 92:6) and al-asmāʾ al-ḥusnā.", meaning:"\"We have certainly created man in the best form / stature.\"", magic:"The root is about standing upright, and that's not decoration.", grammar:"لَقَدْ + past = strong affirmation.", why:"Taqwīm (uprightness/proper standing) over a word for beauty — because the fall in v.5 is precisely a collapse from upright.", note:"Taqwīm ← qāma (to STAND). Same root as aṣ-ṣirāṭ al-MUSTAQĪM — you say it 17× a day.", more:["taqwīm — Form II verbal noun from qāma (to stand) = the act of making something STAND UPRIGHT, straightening it, giving it proper form and proportion — the same root as mustaqīm (straight — as in aṣ-ṣirāṭ al-mustaqīm!) and taqwīm = a CALENDAR in modern Arabic (something set in proper order!).","Taqwīm = making something stand straight and properly proportioned. So aḥsan taqwīm isn't just \"beautiful\" — it's optimally structured, uprightly built, correctly ordered.","And you say this root seventeen times a day: ihdina'ṣ-ṣirāṭa'l-MUSTAQĪM — \"guide us to the STRAIGHT path.\" Same root. You were made upright (v.4); you ask to be kept on the upright road.","You also own it from Takwīr 81:28 — li-man shāʾa minkum an yastaqīm (\"for whoever wills to go straight\"). Made upright → asked to stay upright.","And this connects to the sawwā thread: Aʿlā 87:2 (khalaqa fa-sawwā), Infiṭār 82:7 (fa-sawwāka fa-ʿadalak), Shams 91:7. Different root, same claim: you were built to spec.","Aḥsani is a superlative in iḍāfah with taqwīm — \"the best OF forming.\" Note fī = \"in\" (immersed in that state), same as Balad 90:4's fī kabad.","You were made UPRIGHT; you ask to stay UPRIGHT.","And taqwīm = a CALENDAR in modern Arabic (properly ordered)."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ رَدَدْنَاهُ أَسْفَلَ سَافِلِينَ", tr:"Thumma radadnāhu asfala sāfilīn", v:"5", root:"Radadnā — the \"returning / sending back\" family — radda = to send back; you own it from Nāziʿāt 79:10 (a-innā la-mardūdūna fi'l-ḥāfirah).", meaning:"\"Then We returned him to the lowest of the low.\"", magic:"The harshest sentence in the surah, and the grammar makes it worse than any translation shows.", grammar:"Asfala is a superlative in iḍāfah, accusative.", why:"Radadnā (returned) rather than \"cast down\" — it implies a place he is sent back to, which is more damning.", note:"Asfala sāfilīn = \"the LOWEST of the [already] LOW.\" A superlative stacked on a plural.", more:["asfal — superlative of sāfil (low) = the LOWEST; safala = to be low, to sink; sufl = the bottom part.","ONE — it's a SUPERLATIVE ON A PLURAL. Asfala sāfilīn = \"the lowest of [those who are already] low ones.\" Not \"very low\" — the bottom of a group that is itself at the bottom.","TWO — the verb is radadnā, \"we RETURNED him.\" Not \"we lowered him\" — RETURNED, as if the low place is where he came from, or where he belongs once he abandons the upright form.","THREE — the connector is just thumma (\"then\"). No explanation. No \"because.\" Highest form, then, lowest of the low. The silence between the verses is deliberate — you supply the reason from v.6.","And note the perfect muqābala (mirror-antithesis):","Two superlatives, opposite directions, one verse apart.","Classical readings of asfala sāfilīn: the lowest depths of Hell; or decrepit old age (a reversal of strength); or moral degradation. The exception in v.6 supports the moral/eschatological reading most strongly.","Note sāfilīn is a sound masculine plural in the genitive (-īn), carrying the rhyme.","And radadnā is a doubled verb (ر-د-د) — the doubled letters separate in the past tense with -nā.","And the only word between v.4 and v.5 is thumma — \"then.\" No explanation given.","Best form → lowest of the low."]},
  {ar:"أَجْرٌ غَيْرُ مَمْنُونٍ", tr:"Ajrun ghayru mamnūn", v:"6", root:"Ajr — a wage/reward (ajīr = a hired worker — wage language, not gift language).", meaning:"\"Except those who believed and did righteous deeds — for them is a reward uninterrupted / without reproach.\"", magic:"The double meaning of mamnūn gives you two promises for the price of one, and both are classical.", grammar:"Ghayru = \"other than / without\" — it takes the following noun in the genitive (ghayru mamnūnin).", why:"Mamnūn over \"endless\" — because the double sense covers both duration and dignity.", note:"Mamnūn = (a) CUT OFF and (b) a FAVOUR HELD OVER YOUR HEAD. So: never-ending AND never thrown back in your face.", more:["mamnūn — the \"favour / cutting off\" family — a magnificent double root: minnah = a favour held over someone's head, a reminder of obligation (mann = to remind someone of what you did for them); AND manna = to CUT OFF / diminish.","Both senses live in mamnūn.","READING ONE — \"never cut off.\" From manna = to sever/diminish. The reward never stops, never shrinks, never runs out.","READING TWO — \"without a favour held over you.\" From minnah. This is the deeper one: you will never be reminded of the debt. Nobody will say \"look what I gave you.\" The reward comes with no strings, no obligation, no reminder attached.","Think about how rare that is. Almost every human gift comes with an invisible invoice. This one doesn't.","AND — you own this ENTIRE PHRASE verbatim from Inshiqāq 84:25: Inshiqāq 84:25 — illa'lladhīna āmanū wa ʿamilu'ṣ-ṣāliḥāti lahum ajrun ghayru mamnūn. Tīn 95:6 — illa'lladhīna āmanū wa ʿamilu'ṣ-ṣāliḥāti fa-lahum ajrun ghayru mamnūn. ALMOST WORD-FOR-WORD IDENTICAL (only the fa- differs). Free vocabulary.","And note: this is the ONLY escape hatch in the surah. The fall in v.5 was universal until illā (\"except\") arrived.","A very common construction: ghayr + genitive = negation of an adjective.","And this phrase is IDENTICAL to Inshiqāq 84:25. Minnah = reminding someone what you did for them."]},
  {ar:"فَمَا يُكَذِّبُكَ بَعْدُ بِالدِّينِ", tr:"Fa-mā yukadhdhibuka baʿdu bi'd-dīn", v:"7", root:"Yukadhdhibu — Form II of the \"lying\" root = to call something a lie / to deny.", meaning:"\"So what makes you deny the Reckoning after [this]?\"", magic:"The question is the argument, and dīn is the key.", grammar:"Baʿdu with a ḍammah and no tanwīn = \"after [that]\" with the object DELETED but understood.", why:"Dīn (debt-settlement) over a word for religion — it makes the denial financial and concrete, not theological.", note:"Dayn = A DEBT. Yawm ad-Dīn = \"SETTLEMENT DAY.\" Not \"religion.\" And baʿdu = \"after all that\" — with the object deleted.", more:["You own it across many surahs (Nabaʾ 78:28, Qiyāmah 75:32, Layl 92:16). dīn — NOT \"religion\" here — the root d-y-n means DEBT and its SETTLEMENT: dayn = a DEBT; dāna = to judge/requite.","You own this from Infiṭār 82:9, 15, 17, 18.","Yawm ad-Dīn = \"SETTLEMENT DAY\" — the day the accounts close. Not \"the Day of Religion.\" The root is the same as dayn, a DEBT.","And the logic of baʿdu (\"after [this]\") is devastating: \"after what?\" After everything just said: after being made in the best form, after seeing how far you can fall, after the exception being offered. The evidence is your own design and your own capacity for ruin.","You own this exact question-shape from Infiṭār 82:6 — mā gharraka bi-rabbika'l-karīm (\"what deceived you about your Lord, the Generous?\"). Two surahs, two unanswerable questions aimed straight at the reader.","A known construction (mabnī ʿala'ḍ-ḍamm).","And kadhdhaba + بـ is the fixed pairing.","Compare Infiṭār 82:6: \"what DECEIVED you?\""]},
  {ar:"أَلَيْسَ اللَّهُ بِأَحْكَمِ الْحَاكِمِينَ", tr:"A-laysa'llāhu bi-aḥkami'l-ḥākimīn", v:"8", root:"The \"judging / wisdom / restraining\" family — a superb triple root: ḥakama = to judge; ḥākim = a JUDGE/ruler.", meaning:"\"Is Allah not the most just / wisest of judges?\"", magic:"The final verse, and the root is a whole philosophy.", grammar:"أَلَيْسَ = \"is [he] not?\" — a rhetorical question expecting \"yes, He is.\" The bi- on bi-aḥkam is a bāʾ zāʾidah (EXTRA bāʾ) after the negative laysa — for emphasis.", why:"Ending on a question, not a statement, makes the reader supply the answer — which is far harder to argue with.", note:"Ḥakamah = THE BIT/BRIDLE in a horse's mouth. So ḥikmah (WISDOM) = the power to RESTRAIN and put things in their proper place.", more:["ḥikmah = WISDOM; and originally ḥakamah = THE BIT/BRIDLE in a horse's mouth — the thing that restrains it!","So judgment, wisdom, and restraint are one idea in Arabic.","The bridle-image is the key: ḥikmah (wisdom) comes from the thing that HOLDS A HORSE BACK. So wisdom in Arabic isn't cleverness — it's the capacity to restrain, to hold something in its proper place. And a ḥākim (judge) is one who puts things back in their proper place.","Now connect it to the whole surah: v.4 said you were made in aḥsan taqwīm — properly standing, correctly ordered. v.5 said you were returned to asfala sāfilīn — out of place. v.8 asks: is the One who puts things in their proper place not the best at it?","The surah began with things in their right place (a fig, an olive, a mountain, a sanctuary) and ends by naming Him the best at putting things right.","And note the superlative-on-plural again: aḥkami'l-ḥākimīn = \"the most judging of the judges\" — exactly the same shape as asfala sāfilīn in v.5! The surah's lowest point and its highest name use the identical grammatical construction.","You own the ḥ-k-m family from al-Ḥakīm, one of the Names.","You own this from Takwīr 81:22, 24, 25 and Infiṭār 82:16.","And aḥkami'l-ḥākimīn has the SAME shape as asfala sāfilīn (v.5) — superlative on a plural, the surah's lowest depth and highest Name."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:96, name:"Al-ʿAlaq", ar:"العلق", en:"The Clinging Clot", verses:19, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"First word ever revealed: READ. First object mentioned: THE PEN. Last command in the surah: PROSTRATE AND DRAW NEAR. From reading to bowing.",
  special:["Its first five verses are the first Qurʾān ever revealed — and they are about READING and WRITING.","The very first command given to humanity through the final Prophet ﷺ was not \"pray,\" not \"believe,\" not \"fight.\" It was IQRAʾ — READ / RECITE.","And look at what the opening five verses contain: - iqraʾ (read) — twice - ʿallama (taught) — twice - al-qalam (THE PEN) - mā lam yaʿlam (what he did not know)","Five verses, and knowledge vocabulary appears five times. The religion opens with a reading lesson.","And then the surah splits in two. v.1–5 are the first revelation. v.6–19 came later — and they're about a man who tried to stop someone praying. The surah joins the beginning of knowledge to the first attempt to suppress it.","--"],
  big:["The first revelation (v.1–5) — read, from a clot, by the pen, what you didn't know.","Man overreaches (v.6–8) — he transgresses because he sees himself as needing nothing.","The man who forbids (v.9–14) — have you seen the one who forbids a servant when he prays?","The threat (v.15–18) — the forelock, and the guards of Hell.","The command (v.19) — prostrate, and draw near. (A verse of prostration.)"],
  traps:["لَنَسْفَعًا (v.15) — that final alif is the nūn at-tawkīd al-khafīfah (the LIGHT NŪN OF EMPHASIS), written as an alif but pronounced as a nūn-sound. Not a normal tanwīn.","اسْتَغْنَىٰ (v.7) — Form X = \"to CONSIDER yourself rich,\" a PERCEPTION, not a fact. And ʿAlaq names it as the CAUSE of all transgression (ʿAbasa 80:5 and Layl 92:8 gave you the cases).","يَعْلَمْ (v.5) and يَعْلَم (v.14) — same root, nine verses apart. \"Taught him what he did NOT know\" → \"does he NOT know that Allah sees?\"","النَّاصِيَةِ (v.15–16) — the forelock was the SEAT OF ARAB HONOUR, not just hair. And in v.16 the forelock itself is called \"lying, sinning\" — a metaphorical transfer.","يَنْهَىٰ (v.9) — the SAME verb praised in Nāziʿāt 79:40 (nahā'n-nafsa ʿani'l-hawā). The verb is neutral; the OBJECT decides everything.","أَرَأَيْتَ (v.9, 11, 13) — an IDIOM meaning \"tell me / consider,\" not a literal question about seeing. Three times, none answered.","نَادِيَهُ (v.17) — means his ASSEMBLY / social circle. Modern Arabic nādī = A CLUB.","صَلَّىٰ (v.10) — the ṣalāh root. Remember the Aʿlā trap: ṣallā (prayed, 87:15) vs yaṣlā (roasted, 87:12), three verses apart.","V.19 is a SAJDAH verse — you prostrate when you recite it. The surah about a man stopping prayer generates a prostration every time it's read.","The surah has TWO parts — v.1–5 (the first revelation) and v.6–19 (revealed later). Read them as joined by theme, not by a single occasion."],
  links:[{w:"اقْرَأْ (v.1, 3)",e:"سَنُقْرِئُكَ (Aʿlā 87:6)",t:"\"Read\" / \"We will MAKE you recite\" — command and enablement"},{w:"عَلَقٍ (v.2)",e:"عَلَقَةً (Qiyāmah 75:38)",t:"Same word — and ʿalāqah = a RELATIONSHIP"},{w:"الْأَكْرَمُ (v.3)",e:"الْكَرِيمِ (82:6) · كِرَامٍ (80:16) · أَكْرَمَنِ (89:15)",t:"SIX surahs — and this is the SUPERLATIVE"},{w:"عَلَّمَ / يَعْلَمْ (v.4–5, 14)",e:"",t:"V.5 \"what he did NOT know\" → v.14 \"does he not KNOW?\""},{w:"لَيَطْغَىٰ (v.6)",e:"78:22 · 79:17 · 79:37 · 89:11 · 91:11",t:"SEVENTH appearance — and it lands on MAN himself"},{w:"اسْتَغْنَىٰ (v.7)",e:"اسْتَغْنَىٰ (80:5) · اسْتَغْنَىٰ (92:8)",t:"THREE surahs — and ʿAlaq names it as THE CAUSE"},{w:"الرُّجْعَىٰ (v.8)",e:"رَجْعِهِ (86:8) · الرَّجْعِ (86:11)",t:"Same root — the return"},{w:"إِنَّ إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ (v.8)",e:"75:12 · 75:30 · 79:44 · 88:25 · 94:8",t:"SIX surahs — the fronting that means \"to Him ALONE\""},{w:"يَنْهَىٰ (v.9)",e:"نَهَى النَّفْسَ (Nāziʿāt 79:40)",t:"Restrain YOURSELF (noble) / restrain ANOTHER from prayer (monstrous)"},{w:"صَلَّىٰ (v.10)",e:"وَلَا صَلَّىٰ (75:31) · فَصَلَّىٰ (87:15)",t:"THREE surahs — didn't pray / did pray / prevented from praying"},{w:"الْهُدَىٰ (v.11)",e:"لَلْهُدَىٰ (Layl 92:12)",t:"Same word — the guidance"},{w:"بِالتَّقْوَىٰ (v.12)",e:"تَقْوَاهَا (91:8) · الْمُتَّقِينَ (78:31)",t:"Wiqāyah = ARMOUR, not fear"},{w:"كَذَّبَ وَتَوَلَّىٰ (v.13)",e:"Qiyāmah 75:32 · Layl 92:16",t:"THREE surahs, identical phrase"},{w:"يَرَىٰ (v.14)",e:"لَمْ يَرَهُ أَحَدٌ (90:7) · الْمِرْصَادِ (89:14) · حَافِظٌ (86:4)",t:"FOUR surahs — and this is the ANSWER"},{w:"فَلْيَدْعُ (v.17)",e:"فَلْيَنظُرِ (ʿAbasa 80:24)",t:"Same lām of command on a third-person verb"},{w:"نَادِيَهُ (v.17)",e:"نَادَاهُ (Nāziʿāt 79:16)",t:"Same root — calling out"},{w:"وَاقْتَرِب (v.19)",e:"الْمُقَرَّبُونَ (83:21, 28) · مَقْرَبَةٍ (90:15)",t:"Same root — drawing near"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"اقْرَأْ", tr:"Iqraʾ", v:"1, 3", root:"The \"reading / reciting / gathering together\" family — qaraʾa = to read aloud, to recite.", meaning:"\"Read / Recite in the name of your Lord who created.\"", magic:"The first word of revelation, and the choice is the entire message.", grammar:"Imperative with a hamzah as the final root letter (ق-ر-أ) — note the sukūn on the hamzah: iqraʾ.", why:"Iqraʾ over \"listen\" or \"proclaim\" — because reading implies a text, and a text implies preservation.", note:"QURʾĀN = \"The Recitation\" — SAME ROOT as the first word ever revealed.", more:["And the root also carries \"to gather/collect\" (a recitation gathers letters into words).","QURʾĀN = \"The Recitation\" — the same root.","A man who could not read was commanded to read. Which is precisely why the next words matter: bi'smi rabbika — \"in the name of your Lord.\" The command is impossible by yourself. It becomes possible through Him.","And notice it's repeated in v.3 — iqraʾ twice. First: \"read in the name of your Lord who created.\" Second: \"read, and your Lord is the Most Generous.\" The first grounds reading in creation; the second in generosity.","And you own the root's promise from Aʿlā 87:6 — sa-nuqriʾuka fa-lā tansā (\"We will MAKE YOU RECITE, so you will not forget\"). Form IV — the causative. ʿAlaq says \"read\"; Aʿlā says \"We will make you able to.\" The command and its enablement, in two surahs.","And bi'smi = bi + ism (name) — the same construction as bismillāh.","A man who couldn't read was told to READ — \"in the NAME of your Lord.\" And Aʿlā 87:6: \"We will MAKE you recite.\""]},
  {ar:"مِنْ عَلَقٍ", tr:"Min ʿalaq", v:"2", root:"The \"clinging / hanging / adhering\" family — ʿaliqa = to cling, to stick to.", meaning:"\"created man from a clinging clot.\"", magic:"You own this word exactly from Qiyāmah 75:38 — thumma kāna ʿalaqatan fa-khalaqa fa-sawwā (\"then he was a clinging clot, and He created and proportioned\").", grammar:"ʿalaq is a collective noun (the ʿalaqah is its singular). Genitive after min.", why:"ʿalaq names the stage by its defining physical behaviour — clinging — which is startlingly accurate for an implanted embryo.", note:"ʿAlāqah = A RELATIONSHIP (a bond that CLINGS). Same root. v.1 = READ (the highest act).", more:["ʿalāqah = a RELATIONSHIP (a bond that clings!); ʿalaq = a clinging clot of congealed blood, and also a leech (which clings).","Same word (singular ʿalaqah there, collective ʿalaq here).","And the placement is the whole argument of v.1–2. Verse 1: READ — the highest human activity. Verse 2: you came from a clinging clot — the humblest possible origin.","Reading and a blood-clot, in consecutive verses. The surah's opening move is: the being capable of literacy started as something stuck to a wall.","And ʿalāqah = a RELATIONSHIP is a free hook — you use it constantly in Arabic. Both are about clinging.","v.2 = a CLINGING CLOT (the humblest origin).","And Qiyāmah 75:38 used the same word."]},
  {ar:"الْأَكْرَمُ", tr:"Al-akram", v:"3", root:"The \"nobility / generosity / honour\" family — karam = generosity.", meaning:"\"Read, and your Lord is the Most Generous.\"", magic:"It's a SUPERLATIVE — and that's rare and significant.", grammar:"Afʿal superlative pattern (like aḥsan, asfal, akbar).", why:"The superlative signals that what comes next (teaching) is generosity at its peak.", note:"Al-AKRAM = \"the MOST Generous\" — a SUPERLATIVE, used here only. And the context is TEACHING.", more:["You own it from ʿAbasa 80:13 (mukarramah), ʿAbasa 80:16 (kirām), Takwīr 81:19 (karīm), Infiṭār 82:6 (al-Karīm), Infiṭār 82:11 (kirāman), Fajr 89:15 & 17 (akramahu, tukrimūna).","Not al-Karīm (the Generous) but al-Akram — THE MOST GENEROUS, the superlative form. This is the only place in the Qurʾān this exact form is used as a description of Allah.","And why here? Because of what follows: He teaches by the pen, and teaches man what he did not know. The context of the superlative is KNOWLEDGE. The surah is saying: the highest expression of His generosity is that He teaches you.","Compare Infiṭār 82:6 — mā gharraka bi-rabbika'l-Karīm (\"what deceived you about your Lord, the Generous?\"). There, generosity is what you took advantage of. Here, generosity is what teaches you. Same root, two surahs, two responses.","And notice: v.1's iqraʾ is grounded in creation (alladhī khalaq); v.3's iqraʾ is grounded in generosity (al-Akram). He made you, and He's generous enough to teach you.","Note it's definite (al-akram) without a following iḍāfah — a known usage of the superlative.","The peak of His generosity is that He teaches you.","Compare Infiṭār 82:6's al-Karīm — generosity you took advantage of."]},
  {ar:"عَلَّمَ بِالْقَلَمِ", tr:"ʿallama bi'l-qalam", v:"4", root:"ʿallama — Form II of the \"knowing\" root = to TEACH (ʿilm = knowledge; ʿālim = a scholar; ʿālam = a world — that by which He is known!).", meaning:"\"who taught by the pen.\"", magic:"The first physical object ever mentioned in revelation is A PEN.", grammar:"Form II (ʿallama = to cause to know = to teach). The bi- on bi'l-qalam = by means of (instrumental bāʾ).", why:"Qalam is the humblest possible tool for the grandest possible claim — that knowledge can outlive the knower.", note:"The FIRST OBJECT in the FIRST REVELATION is A PEN. And qalam ← qalama = TO TRIM (as in taqlīm al-aẓāfir, nail-trimming).", more:["qalam — A PEN — originally a reed cut and trimmed for writing (qalama = to trim/pare — still used for trimming nails: taqlīm al-aẓāfir!).","Think about that. Not a sword, not a throne, not a mountain. A pen. The tool of preservation and transmission.","And qalam comes from qalama = TO TRIM. A pen is literally \"the trimmed thing\" — a reed cut to a point so it can hold ink. You use the same verb for trimming your nails (taqlīm). Free hook.","And notice the two teachings in v.4–5: - v.4 — taught BY THE PEN (mediated knowledge — what humans pass to humans, across time) - v.5 — taught man WHAT HE DID NOT KNOW (direct knowledge — what only He can give)","Two channels of learning, named in the first revelation. The pen carries what's known; He supplies what isn't.","And this answers v.1's problem: an unlettered man is told to READ — and immediately told that the One commanding him is the One who invented writing itself.","A pen is \"the trimmed reed.\" v.4 = knowledge via the PEN (human to human).","v.5 = knowledge only HE can give."]},
  {ar:"مَا لَمْ يَعْلَمْ", tr:"Mā lam yaʿlam", v:"5", root:"The \"knowing\" family — ʿalima = to know; ʿilm = knowledge.", meaning:"\"taught man what he did not know.\"", magic:"The last verse of the first revelation — and it's the one that should humble everyone reading it.", grammar:"لَمْ + jussive = past negation (\"did not know\"). Note yaʿlam has dropped its final vowel (jussive).", why:"Stating it as \"what he did NOT know\" rather than \"knowledge\" makes the prior ignorance the emphasis.", note:"\"Taught man WHAT HE DID NOT KNOW.\" Everything you know was given.", more:["Every single thing you know, you were taught. You did not generate it. The verse frames all human knowledge as received.","And notice the deliberate contrast with v.6–7, which follow immediately: - v.5 — man was TAUGHT what he did not know (he began ignorant) - v.6–7 — man TRANSGRESSES because he sees himself as needing nothing","The one who had to be taught everything concludes he needs nothing. That's the surah's indictment, and it's set up by v.5.","Also worth noting: the root ʿ-l-m appears THREE times in v.4–5 (ʿallama, ʿallama, yaʿlam) — hammering the theme of the first revelation.","And v.6–7 immediately say: this creature then decides he needs nothing.","The taught one claims self-sufficiency."]},
  {ar:"لَيَطْغَىٰ", tr:"La-yaṭghā", v:"6", root:"The \"overflowing all bounds\" family — original image: water bursting its banks and flooding.", meaning:"\"No! Indeed, man does transgress all bounds.\"", magic:"The SEVENTH appearance of this root in your study — one of your most-tracked words: - Nabaʾ 78:22 — aṭ-ṭāghīn (Hell is the refuge for those who transgress) - Nāziʿāt 79:17.", grammar:"كَلَّا = a particle of rebuke — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:11, Nabaʾ 78:4, ʿAbasa 80:11. Weak-final verb carrying the -ā rhyme.", why:"ṭaghā frames the failure as a boundary problem, not a knowledge problem — which is the point, since v.5 just said he was taught.", note:"Ṭaghā = WATER BURSTING ITS BANKS.", more:["Innahu ṭaghā (Pharaoh) - Nāziʿāt 79:37 — fa-ammā man ṭaghā (whoever — transferable) - Fajr 89:11 — alladhīna ṭaghaw (ʿĀd, Thamūd, Pharaoh) - Shams 91:11 — bi-ṭaghwāhā (Thamūd) - ʿAlaq 96:6 — inna'l-insāna la-yaṭghā (MAN, as such)","And notice the progression across those six. It started with a category (the transgressors), got a face (Pharaoh), became transferable (whoever), covered civilizations (ʿĀd, Thamūd), and now lands on al-insān — MAN HIMSELF.","The diagnosis has widened to include everyone.","And the emphasis is heavy: kallā (No!) + inna + emphatic lām — \"No! Indeed, man MOST CERTAINLY overflows.\"","SEVEN appearances: 78:22, 79:17, 79:37, 89:11, 91:11, 96:6. And the progression ends here at al-insān — MAN HIMSELF."]},
  {ar:"أَن رَّآهُ اسْتَغْنَىٰ", tr:"An raʾāhu'staghnā", v:"7", root:"Raʾā — to see — here in the sense of \"to regard/consider.\" istaghnā — Form X of the \"richness\" root = to CONSIDER yourself free of need.", meaning:"\"because he sees himself as self-sufficient.\"", magic:"This verse gives the reason for v.6 — and it's a word you own deeply.", grammar:"Form X (اسْتَفْعَلَ) = \"to CONSIDER yourself X\" — the highest-yield form-pattern in these surahs.", why:"Istaghnā moves the fault from circumstance to self-assessment — which is why anyone can fall into it.", note:"THIS IS THE DIAGNOSIS: man transgresses because he sees himself as needing nothing.", more:["Istaghnā is now in THREE surahs, and this one names it as the ROOT CAUSE: - ʿAbasa 80:5 — ammā mani staghnā — the rich chief the Prophet ﷺ turned toward - Layl 92:8 — bakhila wa'staghnā — the withholder - ʿAlaq 96:7 — an raʾāhu'staghnā — WHY man transgresses at all","ʿAbasa and Layl gave you cases. ʿAlaq gives you the diagnosis. Every transgression traces back to one sentence: \"I don't need anything.\"","And raʾāhu (\"he SEES himself\") is crucial — it's a perception, not a fact. He isn't self-sufficient; he sees himself that way. Form X (istaghnā) already means \"to consider yourself rich\" — so the verse doubles the illusion: he sees himself as one who considers himself independent.","And remember ʿAbasa's payoff: istaghnā (80:5) → yughnīh (80:37) — he wanted to need nothing, and got a concern that left room for nothing.","And Ḍuḥā 93:8's redemption: aghnā — HE enriched you (Form IV). The problem was never wealth — it was claiming the sufficiency for yourself.","The an here is causal (\"because\").","THREE surahs: ʿAbasa 80:5 (a case), Layl 92:8 (a case), ʿAlaq 96:7 (the CAUSE).","And Form X = \"to CONSIDER yourself X\" — it's a perception, not a fact."]},
  {ar:"الرُّجْعَىٰ", tr:"Ar-rujʿā", v:"8", root:"The \"returning\" family — rajaʿa = to return; you own it from Ṭāriq 86:8 (ʿalā rajʿihi la-qādir) and Ṭāriq 86:11 (wa's-samāʾi dhāti'r-rajʿ).", meaning:"\"Indeed, to your Lord is the return.\"", magic:"The answer to istaghnāʾ, delivered in four words.", grammar:"Rujʿā is a verbal noun on the fuʿlā pattern, carrying the rhyme.", why:"Rujʿā (the return) rather than \"judgment\" — because \"returning\" refutes \"self-sufficiency\" more directly than any threat.", note:"\"I need nothing\" (v.7) → \"you're going BACK\" (v.8). The perfect refutation.", more:["He thinks he needs nothing (v.7). The reply: you're going back. Independence is impossible for something that has to return to its source.","And notice the FRONTING: inna ilā rabbika'r-rujʿā — \"to your Lord\" comes before the subject. In Arabic, fronting = RESTRICTION: to Him ALONE.","This is now the SIXTH surah using this exact move: - Qiyāmah 75:12 — ilā rabbika yawmaʾidhin al-mustaqarr - Qiyāmah 75:30 — ilā rabbika yawmaʾidhin al-masāq - Nāziʿāt 79:44 — ilā rabbika muntahāhā - Ghāshiyah 88:25 — inna ilaynā iyābahum - Sharḥ 94:8 — wa ilā rabbika fa'rghab - ʿAlaq 96:8 — inna ilā rabbika'r-rujʿā","Six surahs, one structure: everything terminates at Him.","And the rujʿā form is a fuʿlā noun — same shape as al-yusrā, al-ʿusrā, al-ḥusnā, al-ūlā.","And \"to your Lord\" is FRONTED = to Him ALONE — SIX surahs use this: 75:12, 75:30, 79:44, 88:25, 94:8, 96:8."]},
  {ar:"أَرَأَيْتَ الَّذِي يَنْهَىٰ", tr:"A-raʾayta'lladhī yanhā", v:"9", root:"A-raʾayta — \"have you seen…?\" — an idiom meaning \"consider / tell me about…\", inviting the listener to look at something outrageous.", meaning:"\"Have you seen the one who forbids…?\"", magic:"The repetition of a-raʾayta three times (v.9, 11, 13) is the surah's rhetorical hammer.", grammar:"أَرَأَيْتَ is an idiom (\"tell me / consider\"), not a literal question about eyesight.", why:"Yanhā (to forbid) rather than a word for persecution — it's the smallness of the act that's damning: he's just telling someone to stop.", note:"Nihāyah = THE END. Nāziʿāt 79:40: nahā = restraining YOURSELF from desire (noble).", more:["yanhā — the \"forbidding / bringing to an end\" family — nahy = a prohibition; nihāyah = the END; you own it from Nāziʿāt 79:40 (wa nahā'n-nafsa ʿani'l-hawā) and 79:44 (muntahāhā).","V.9 — \"Have you seen the one who forbids a servant when he prays?\" v.11 — \"Have you seen if he [the servant] were upon guidance?\" v.13 — \"Have you seen if he [the forbidder] denied and turned away?\"","Three questions, none answered. The Qurʾān just keeps asking you to look. The absurdity is left to speak for itself: someone is standing between a worshipper and his Lord.","And the nahā root has a beautiful contrast you own: Nāziʿāt 79:40 — wa nahā'n-nafsa ʿani'l-hawā (\"restrained the soul from desire\") — a PRAISED act of forbidding. Here, the same verb is used for forbidding someone from prayer.","Same verb: restrain yourself from desire (noble) / restrain another from worship (monstrous). The verb is neutral; the object is everything.","Weak-final yanhā carries the rhyme.","ʿAlaq 96:9: yanhā = restraining ANOTHER from prayer (monstrous).","Same verb, and the object decides everything.","Three a-raʾayta's (v.9, 11, 13), none answered."]},
  {ar:"عَبْدًا إِذَا صَلَّىٰ", tr:"ʿabdan idhā ṣallā", v:"10", root:"ʿabd — a servant/slave (ʿibādah = worship — the same root: worship is servanthood).", meaning:"\"a servant when he prays.\"", magic:"The word ʿabd is doing quiet, devastating work.", grammar:"ʿabdan is indefinite accusative — the object of yanhā. The indefiniteness generalizes: any servant.", why:"ʿabd names him by his relationship to Allah, which makes interference an offence against Allah, not just against him.", note:"ʿAbd = a SERVANT; ʿibādah = worship (SERVANTHOOD). Someone is stopping a servant from serving his Master.", more:["ṣallā — the \"prayer\" family — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:31 (fa-lā ṣaddaqa wa lā ṣallā) and Aʿlā 87:15 (wa dhakara'sma rabbihi fa-ṣallā).","The one being forbidden is called ʿabdan — A SERVANT — indefinite, unnamed. Just: a servant.","And that's the horror of it: someone is preventing a servant from serving his Master. The word ʿabd already establishes the relationship being interfered with. It's not \"a man\" or \"a believer\" — it's a servant, and you're standing in his way.","And ṣallā completes a thread across three surahs: - Qiyāmah 75:31 — the denier \"neither affirmed nor prayed\" (didn't pray) - Aʿlā 87:15 — the successful one \"remembered his Lord's name and prayed\" (did pray) - ʿAlaq 96:10 — a servant \"when he prays\" (is stopped from praying)","Three surahs: not praying, praying, and being prevented from praying.","And remember the Aʿlā trap: ṣallā (prayed, v.15) vs yaṣlā (roasted, v.12) — three verses apart!","The word already names the crime.","Three surahs of ṣallā: 75:31 (didn't), 87:15 (did), 96:10 (prevented)."]},
  {ar:"كَذَّبَ وَتَوَلَّىٰ", tr:"Kadhdhaba wa tawallā", v:"13", root:"Kadhdhaba — to call something a lie. tawallā — Form V of the \"turning\" root = to turn your back deliberately.", meaning:"\"Have you seen if he denied and turned away?\"", magic:"The THIRD surah to use this exact two-verb phrase — a Qurʾānic signature: - Qiyāmah 75:32 — wa lākin kadhdhaba wa tawallā - Layl 92:16 — alladhī kadhdhaba wa tawallā - ʿAlaq 96:13 — in kadhdhaba wa tawallā", grammar:"Form V, weak-final.", why:"Two verbs because saying no and walking away are separate acts.", note:"Kadhdhaba wa tawallā — THREE surahs, identical phrase: 75:32, 92:16, 96:13. And tawallā is in SIX surahs — including ʿAbasa 80:1, where it described a FROWN.", more:["Identical pairing, three surahs. Denial with the tongue + turning with the body — both channels of rejection.","And tawallā is now across SIX surahs, with a range that should stay with you: Qiyāmah 75:32 (the arrogant denier), ʿAbasa 80:1 (the Prophet's ﷺ momentary frown — CORRECTED!), Ghāshiyah 88:23, Layl 92:16, ʿAlaq 96:13.","The same verb for a shoulder-turn and for total rejection. That's how seriously direction is taken.","Note the conditional in (\"if\") in v.11 and v.13 — the surah lays out two possibilities and lets you weigh them."]},
  {ar:"أَلَمْ يَعْلَم بِأَنَّ اللَّهَ يَرَىٰ", tr:"A-lam yaʿlam bi-anna'llāha yarā", v:"14", root:"Yaʿlam — to know — the same root as ʿallama in v.4–5! yarā — to see.", meaning:"\"Does he not know that Allah sees?\"", magic:"The verse that closes the loop back to the first revelation — and it's brilliant.", grammar:"أَلَمْ + jussive = rhetorical question. Note yaʿlam drops its final vowel.", why:"The plainest possible verb (yarā, \"sees\") — no theology needed, just a fact.", note:"V.5: taught him what he did not know (mā lam yaʿlam). v.14: does he not know (a-lam yaʿlam) that Allah SEES?", more:["V.5 said: Allah taught man what he did not know (mā lam yaʿlam). v.14 asks: does he not KNOW (a-lam yaʿlam) that Allah sees?","SAME ROOT, SAME NEGATION-STRUCTURE, nine verses apart. He was taught what he didn't know — and he still doesn't know the most basic thing: that he's being watched.","And this answers Balad 90:7 — a-yaḥsabu an lam yarahu aḥad (\"does he think no one saw him?\"). Balad asked the question; ʿAlaq answers it: Allah sees.","You own this thread across FOUR surahs: - Ṭāriq 86:4 — in kullu nafsin lammā ʿalayhā ḥāfiẓ (over every soul, a guardian) - Fajr 89:14 — inna rabbaka la-bi'l-mirṣād (your Lord is at the lookout) - Balad 90:7 — a-yaḥsabu an lam yarahu aḥad (does he think no one saw?) - ʿAlaq 96:14 — a-lam yaʿlam bi-anna'llāha yarā (Allah SEES)","The bi- on bi-anna is standard after ʿalima.","Same root, nine verses apart.","And Balad 90:7 asked \"does he think no one saw?\" — this is the answer."]},
  {ar:"لَنَسْفَعًا بِالنَّاصِيَةِ", tr:"La-nasfaʿan bi'n-nāṣiyah", v:"15", root:"Nasfaʿu — the \"seizing violently and dragging\" family — safaʿa = to grab roughly and pull, also carries a sense of scorching/blackening (the mark left by fire).", meaning:"\"We will surely drag him by the forelock.\"", magic:"The forelock is chosen with total precision, and it's cultural.", grammar:"La-nasfaʿan ends with a nūn at-tawkīd al-khafīfah — the light nūn of emphasis, written in the muṣḥaf as an alif (ـًا) but pronounced as a nūn-sound.", why:"Nāṣiyah over \"head\" — because it's the honour-symbol, which is exactly what's being taken.", note:"The NĀṢIYAH (forelock) = THE SEAT OF ARAB HONOUR AND PRIDE.", more:["nāṣiyah — THE FORELOCK — the hair at the very front of the head.","In Arab culture, the nāṣiyah (forelock) was the seat of honour and pride. A noble man's forelock was his dignity; to seize someone's forelock was the ultimate gesture of total domination — you had taken his honour in your hand.","So the punishment is precisely fitted to the crime: the man who istaghnā — thought himself above needing anyone (v.7) — is grabbed by the very symbol of his self-importance.","And v.16 doubles it: nāṣiyatin kādhibatin khāṭiʾah — \"a LYING, SINNING forelock.\" The forelock itself is called lying and sinning — a majāz (metaphorical transfer): the part is described with the character of the whole person. His head is the lie.","A rare and important orthographic feature — don't misread it as a normal tanwīn.","The man who thought he needed nothing (v.7) is dragged by the symbol of his pride.","And la-nasfaʿan has the light nūn of emphasis, written as an alif."]},
  {ar:"فَلْيَدْعُ نَادِيَهُ", tr:"Fal-yadʿu nādiyah", v:"17", root:"Yadʿu — the \"calling\" family (duʿāʾ = a call/supplication).", meaning:"\"Then let him call his assembly / his people.\"", magic:"The challenge is dripping with irony, and the modern word makes it stick.", grammar:"لام الأمر (the lām of command) on a third-person verb: fal-yadʿu = \"let him call.\" You own this from ʿAbasa 80:24 (fal-yanẓur).", why:"Nādī (his social circle) rather than \"his helpers\" — it names exactly what he was relying on.", note:"NĀDĪ = A CLUB in modern Arabic (a sports club!).", more:["nādī — an ASSEMBLY / a gathering-place of one's people — from nādā = to call out (you own nādāhu from Nāziʿāt 79:16!); modern Arabic nādī = A CLUB (a sports club, a social club!).","Nādī in modern Arabic is a CLUB — Nādī ar-Riyāḍī, a sports club. Same word. It's your circle, your backers, the people who'd stand behind you.","So the verse says: \"Go ahead — call your crew.\" It's a taunt. He derived his sense of istighnāʾ (self-sufficiency, v.7) partly from his social backing — so he's invited to summon exactly that.","And v.18 answers instantly: sa-nadʿu'z-zabāniyah — \"WE will call the Zabāniyah.\" The same verb (daʿā) on both sides. You call yours; We'll call Ours.","The rhetorical shape is taʿjīz — challenging someone to do something to expose that they can't.","Note yadʿu drops its final wāw (jussive).","\"Go on — call your CLUB.\" And v.18: \"WE will call the Zabāniyah.\" Same verb, both sides."]},
  {ar:"الزَّبَانِيَةَ", tr:"Az-zabāniyah", v:"18", root:"The guards/angels of Hell — the root carries the sense of pushing, thrusting, shoving forcefully (zabana = to push away violently — used of a camel kicking out).", meaning:"\"We will call the Zabāniyah.\"", magic:"The contrast with v.17 is the entire point, and it's brutal.", grammar:"سَ + present = near future (\"We will call\"). Accusative as the object of nadʿu.", why:"An unusual, heavy-sounding name for the opposing party makes the mismatch feel total.", note:"Zabana = TO SHOVE VIOLENTLY (like a camel kicking out). v.17: \"call your CLUB\" (a dare).", more:["V.17 — fal-yadʿu nādiyah — \"let him call his club\" (human, social, negotiable) v.18 — sa-nadʿu'z-zabāniyah — \"We will call the Zabāniyah\" (not human, not social, not negotiable)","And note the tense shift: his call is a command (let him try); Ours is a promise (sa-nadʿu, \"We WILL\"). One is hypothetical, one is scheduled.","The root's meaning — violent pushing/thrusting — tells you what they do. They are the ones who shove.","And the sound is deliberate: az-zabāniyah is a heavy, unfamiliar, slightly frightening word — it lands harder precisely because you don't hear it often.","v.18: \"WE will call the Zabāniyah\" (a promise)."]},
  {ar:"وَاسْجُدْ وَاقْتَرِب", tr:"Wa'sjud wa'qtarib", v:"19", root:"Usjud — PROSTRATE (sujūd = prostration; masjid = A MOSQUE — literally \"the place of prostration\"!).", meaning:"\"No! Do not obey him. But prostrate and draw near.\"", magic:"The final verse of the surah — and it is a sajdah verse, meaning you actually prostrate when you recite it.", grammar:"Two imperatives (usjud Form I, iqtarib Form VIII).", why:"Ending on two physical commands — after all the argument — turns the surah into an action rather than a conclusion.", note:"MASJID = \"the place of PROSTRATION.\" This is a SAJDAH verse — you prostrate when you read it.", more:["iqtarib — the \"drawing near\" family — qarīb = near; qurb = nearness; al-muqarrabūn = those brought NEAR (Muṭaffifīn 83:21, 28!); and maqrabah — Balad 90:15 (an orphan of near kin).","Think about what that means. The surah describes a man trying to stop someone from praying (v.9–10). And it ends by commanding you to prostrate — and you physically do it.","The reader's own body becomes the refutation. He tried to stop one prostration; the verse generates one every time it's recited, forever.","And look at the pairing: usjud (go DOWN) + iqtarib (come NEAR). The lowest physical position produces the closest spiritual position. Down IS near.","Compare the surah's arc: v.1 = IQRAʾ (read — an act of the mind). v.19 = USJUD (prostrate — an act of the body). Knowledge to submission. The first revelation begins with reading and ends with your forehead on the ground.","And masjid = \"the place of prostration\" is your free hook — you use the word daily.","The kallā + lā tuṭiʿhu (\"do not obey him\") is a prohibition with the jussive.","He tried to stop one prostration; this verse creates one every time it's recited.","And DOWN (usjud) is how you get NEAR (iqtarib)."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:97, name:"Al-Qadr", ar:"القدر", en:"The Night of Decree", verses:5, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"A five-verse surah that says one name three times — the name meaning \"the night of MEASURING.\" And the thing measured, in v.3, is a thousand months.",
  special:["Five verses — and the phrase Laylat al-Qadr appears THREE times in them.","V.1: innā anzalnāhu fī laylati'l-qadr v.2: wa mā adrāka mā laylatu'l-qadr v.3: laylatu'l-qadr khayrun min alfi shahr","Three mentions in three consecutive verses — in a surah of only five. That density is unusual even for the Qurʾān. The repetition does what repetition always does in Arabic: it fixes the thing in place — appropriate for a night whose name means the night of DECREE and MEASURE.","And notice the opening pronoun: anzalnā-HU — \"We sent IT down.\" \"It\" is never named. The Qurʾān is so obviously the subject that it doesn't need saying.","--"],
  big:["The sending down (v.1) — We sent it down on the Night of Decree.","The unanswerable question (v.2) — and what could convey to you what it is?","The measure (v.3) — better than a thousand months.","The descent (v.4) — angels and the Spirit come down, by their Lord's permission.","The single word (v.5) — Peace — until dawn breaks."],
  traps:["أَنزَلْنَا (v.1, Form IV) vs. نَزَّلْنَا (Insān 76:23, Form II) — same root, different forms, different modes. Form IV = ALL AT ONCE. Form II = IN STAGES. Classical scholars tie this to the two descents of the Qurʾān.","الْقَدْرِ (v.1) — carries THREE meanings at once: MEASURE/decree, POWER, and WORTH. Don't pick one — all three are classical and all three fit.","تَنَزَّلُ (v.4) — originally tatanazzalu, with one tāʾ DROPPED. Same contraction as talaẓẓā (Layl 92:14).","سَلَامٌ هِيَ (v.5) — the predicate is FRONTED. Not \"it is peace\" but \"PEACE is what it is\" — a restriction.","أَنزَلْنَاهُ (v.1) — the \"it\" is NEVER named. A deliberate omission — the Qurʾān is too obvious to specify.","مَا أَدْرَاكَ (v.2) — PAST tense = an answer follows. Compare mā yudrīka (ʿAbasa 80:3, present) = no answer follows. The tense changes the function.","بِإِذْنِ (v.4) — from the root of udhun (EAR) and ADHĀN. Permission is what you \"give ear to.\"","مِّن كُلِّ أَمْرٍ (v.4) — the min is debated (as bi-, or partitive). Both classical; the comprehensiveness is the constant.","خَيْرٌ (v.3) — IRREGULAR comparative (no afʿal form). Same for sharr. A memorized exception.","مَطْلَعِ (v.5) — a noun of TIME/PLACE (\"the point of rising\"), not a verb. From ṭalaʿa — the same root as ṭulūʿ ash-shams and iṭṭalaʿa (to become informed)."],
  links:[{w:"أَنزَلْنَاهُ (v.1)",e:"نَزَّلْنَا... تَنزِيلًا (Insān 76:23) · أَنزَلْنَا (Nabaʾ 78:14)",t:"Form IV (all at once) vs Form II (in stages) — two descents"},{w:"الْقَدْرِ (v.1–3)",e:"75:4 · 76:16 · 80:19 · 87:3 · 89:16 · 90:5",t:"SEVEN+ appearances — measure, power, worth"},{w:"وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ (v.2)",e:"82:17–18 · 83:8, 19 · 86:2 · 90:12",t:"SIX surahs — and adrāka vs yudrīka (ʿAbasa 80:3)"},{w:"خَيْرٌ (v.3)",e:"خَيْرٌ وَأَبْقَىٰ (87:17) · خَيْرٌ لَّكَ (93:4)",t:"THREE comparisons — irregular comparative"},{w:"تَنَزَّلُ (v.4)",e:"تَلَظَّىٰ (Layl 92:14)",t:"Form V with a DROPPED tāʾ"},{w:"الرُّوحُ (v.4)",e:"الرُّوحُ (Nabaʾ 78:38)",t:"Same being — silent in ranks / descending freely"},{w:"بِإِذْنِ (v.4)",e:"إِلَّا مَنْ أَذِنَ لَهُ (Nabaʾ 78:38)",t:"Same root — permission to SPEAK / to DESCEND"},{w:"أَمْرٍ (v.4)",e:"الْمُدَبِّرَاتِ أَمْرًا (79:5) · وَالْأَمْرُ (82:19)",t:"The angels who ARRANGE the amr descend WITH it"},{w:"سَلَامٌ (v.5)",e:"",t:"Fronted predicate — like 75:12, 79:44, 88:25, 94:8, 96:8"},{w:"الْفَجْرِ (v.5)",e:"75:5 · 76:6 · 80:42 · 82:3 · 82:14 · 89:1 · 91:8",t:"EIGHT appearances, SEVEN surahs"},{w:"لَيْلَةِ (v.1)",e:"الليل (81:17 · 89:4 · 91:4 · 92:1 · 93:2)",t:"Night across six surahs — creeping, travelling, covering, still"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"أَنزَلْنَاهُ", tr:"Anzalnāhu", v:"1", root:"The \"coming down / descending\" family — nazala = to descend.", meaning:"\"Indeed, We sent it down on the Night of Decree.\"", magic:"You own this root from two surahs, and the form difference matters.", grammar:"Form IV (anzala), past, with -nā (We) and -hu (it).", why:"Anzala (a single complete descent) rather than nazzala — it marks this night as the moment of wholesale revelation.", note:"Manzil = A HOUSE (where you come DOWN and settle).", more:["Manzil = a HOUSE/stopping-place (where you come down and settle!); nāzil = descending; Form IV anzala = to SEND DOWN.","- Insān 76:23 — innā naḥnu nazzalnā ʿalayka'l-qurʾāna tanzīlā — Form II (nazzala) = sending down gradually, in stages.","- Nabaʾ 78:14 — wa anzalnā mina'l-muʿṣirāti māʾan thajjājā — Form IV, used for rain.","- Qadr 97:1 — anzalnāhu — Form IV = a single, complete sending-down.","Classical scholars connect exactly this distinction to the two descents of the Qurʾān: the whole revelation descended at once (anzala, Form IV) on this night, and then came to the Prophet ﷺ piece by piece over 23 years (nazzala, Form II). Two forms of one root, two modes of revelation.","And manzil = a HOUSE is your free hook — the place where you come down and stay.","Note also the parallel with Nabaʾ 78:14: rain sent down (anzalnā) → grain, gardens, life. Here: revelation sent down (anzalnāhu) → guidance, life of a different kind. Same verb, two rains.","The -hu has no antecedent in the surah — a deliberate omission (iḍmār): the Qurʾān is too obvious to name.","Form IV anzala = ALL AT ONCE (Qadr 97:1). Form II nazzala = IN STAGES (Insān 76:23). Two forms, two descents.","And the \"it\" is never named."]},
  {ar:"الْقَدْرِ", tr:"Al-qadr", v:"1, 2, 3", root:"A spectacular double root — q-d-r means BOTH: (a) to MEASURE OUT precisely, to decree, to apportion (qadar = destiny/measure; taqdīr = precise estimation) — AND (b) to have POWER/capacity (qudrah = power; qādir = able).", meaning:"\"the Night of Decree / Measure / Worth\" — all three readings are classical.", magic:"THE SEVENTH+ APPEARANCE of this root in your study — and here it becomes a surah title.", grammar:"Iḍāfah (\"the night of decree\").", why:"No other Arabic word could carry decree, power, and worth simultaneously.", note:"Qadr = (a) MEASURE/decree, (b) POWER, (c) WORTH — ALL THREE.", more:["And a third sense: qadr = WORTH/rank (qadruhu ʿaẓīm = his worth is great).","Track it across your surahs: - Qiyāmah 75:4 — bālā qādirīna ʿalā an nusawwiya banānah (POWER + precision to remake fingertips) - Insān 76:16 — qawārīra min fiḍḍatin qaddarūhā taqdīrā (drinks measured EXACTLY) - ʿAbasa 80:19 — min nuṭfatin khalaqahu fa-qaddarah (proportioned him) - Aʿlā 87:3 — wa'lladhī qaddara fa-hadā (measured, then guided) - Fajr 89:16 — fa-qadara ʿalayhi rizqah (restricted his provision) - Balad 90:5 — an lan yaqdira ʿalayhi aḥad (no one has POWER over him) - Qadr 97:1 — laylati'l-QADR.","And ALL THREE meanings fit this night perfectly: - The night of MEASURING — decrees for the year are apportioned. - The night of POWER — revelation descends, angels fill the earth. - The night of WORTH — better than a thousand months.","The Qurʾān names the night with a word that says all three at once. That's not ambiguity — it's compression.","Note laylat becomes laylati in genitive as the second term of the oath-less construction.","SEVEN+ appearances across your surahs: 75:4, 76:16, 80:19, 87:3, 89:16, 90:5, 97:1. And all three meanings fit this night."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ", tr:"Wa mā adrāka", v:"2", root:"The \"knowing by perception\" family — darā = to become aware; Form IV adrā = to make someone know.", meaning:"\"And what can make you know what the Night of Decree is?\"", magic:"You own this formula across SIX surahs now — and the adrāka / yudrīka distinction is high-yield:", grammar:"Note the TWO mā's — the first is interrogative (\"what?\"), the second introduces the subject-clause.", why:"The question form conveys immensity more effectively than any adjective could.", note:"Yudrīka (present, ʿAbasa 80:3) = \"you couldn't know\" — NO answer follows.", more:["Wa mā YUDRĪKA (PRESENT tense) — introduces something you could not know and won't be told. ʿAbasa 80:3. wa mā ADRĀKA (PAST tense) — introduces something so immense that an answer follows, but no description could be adequate. - Infiṭār 82:17–18 (twice!) · Muṭaffifīn 83:8, 19 · Ṭāriq 86:2 · Balad 90:12 · Qadr 97:2","And here, unusually, the answer DOES come (v.3–5). But notice what the answer is: a comparison (better than a thousand months), a description (angels descend), and one word (peace). Never a direct definition.","The question is doing something specific: it pauses you. v.1 stated a fact. v.2 stops and says do you actually grasp what that means? Then v.3–5 try.","Same structure in all six surahs.","adrāka (past) = \"what could convey it to you?\" — an answer follows.","SIX surahs: 82:17–18, 83:8, 83:19, 86:2, 90:12, 97:2."]},
  {ar:"خَيْرٌ مِّنْ أَلْفِ شَهْرٍ", tr:"Khayrun min alfi shahr", v:"3", root:"Khayr — \"better\" — an IRREGULAR comparative (no afʿal form).", meaning:"\"The Night of Decree is better than a thousand months.\"", magic:"The number is doing precise work, and so is the word for \"month.\"", grammar:"Khayr and sharr are IRREGULAR comparatives — they don't take the afʿal pattern. Memorize them as exceptions.", why:"A thousand months ≈ a lifetime — the comparison is calibrated to the human span.", note:"A thousand months ≈ 83 years ≈ a whole lifetime.", more:["You own it from Aʿlā 87:17 (wa'l-ākhiratu khayrun wa abqā) and Ḍuḥā 93:4. alf — a thousand.","shahr — a MONTH — from shahara = to make well-known/visible (a month is named after the sighting of the new moon — ishhār = making public!).","A thousand months is roughly 83 years — more than a typical human lifetime. So the comparison is: this ONE night outweighs a whole life of nights.","And notice it doesn't say \"equal to\" — it says khayrun min — BETTER THAN. Not equivalent. Superior.","And shahr (month) coming from shahara (to make visible/known) is a lovely hook: a month is literally \"the sighting\" — named after the crescent you look for. Same root as mashhūr (famous) and ishhār (publicity).","The khayr thread across three surahs: Aʿlā 87:17 (the Hereafter is better and more lasting), Ḍuḥā 93:4 (the last is better for you), Qadr 97:3 (this night is better than a thousand months). Three comparisons, all about what actually outweighs what.","The min here is min at-tafḍīl (the \"than\" of comparison).","And it says \"BETTER than,\" not \"equal to.\" Shahr (month) ← shahara = TO MAKE VISIBLE — a month is \"the sighting.\" And khayr is an IRREGULAR comparative."]},
  {ar:"تَنَزَّلُ", tr:"Tanazzalu", v:"4", root:"The \"descending\" family again — but here in Form V (tanazzala).", meaning:"\"The angels and the Spirit descend therein.\"", magic:"The FORM is the whole picture, and it contrasts beautifully with v.1.", grammar:"Form V present, with a dropped tāʾ. Recognize this: tanazzalu ← tatanazzalu. Very common.", why:"Tanazzalu (continuous descending) over tanzilu — it makes the night full of traffic, not marked by one arrival.", note:"V.1 anzalnā (Form IV) = ONE decisive descent. v.4 tanazzalu (Form V) = CONTINUOUS, wave after wave.", more:["V.1 — anzalnā (Form IV) = ONE decisive sending-down, done by Allah. v.4 — tanazzalu (Form V) = CONTINUOUS, repeated, wave-after-wave descending, done by the angels.","One thing came down once (the revelation); many beings come down repeatedly (the angels). Two verbs, two motions, three verses apart.","And Form V here carries a sense of gradual, successive arrival — not a single event but a stream. Picture it: the descent doesn't happen at a moment; it happens throughout the night.","Note: the original form is tatanazzalu — one tāʾ has been DROPPED, a standard contraction in Form V/VI present tense. You met the same dropping in Layl 92:14 (talaẓẓā from tatalaẓẓā).","And tanazzalu ← tatanazzalu with a DROPPED tāʾ (like talaẓẓā, Layl 92:14)."]},
  {ar:"وَالرُّوحُ", tr:"Wa'r-rūḥ", v:"4", root:"The \"spirit / breath / relief\" family — rūḥ = spirit; related to rīḥ (wind) and rāḥah (REST/relief) — all sharing an idea of moving air and ease.", meaning:"\"the angels and the Spirit\" — most commentators identify this as Jibrīl, mentioned separately for honour.", magic:"You own this exact usage from Nabaʾ 78:38 — yawma yaqūmu'r-Rūḥu wa'l-malāʾikatu ṣaffā (\"the Day the Spirit and the angels stand in rank\").", grammar:"Joined to al-malāʾikah by wa — a construction called ʿaṭf al-khāṣṣ ʿala'l-ʿāmm (joining the specific to the general) for emphasis and honour.", why:"Ar-Rūḥ (a majestic title) rather than the name Jibrīl — it maximizes the weight of the descent.", note:"Rūḥ, rīḥ (wind), rāḥah (REST) — one root. Nabaʾ 78:38: the Rūḥ stands SILENT on Judgment Day.", more:["And the contrast between the two scenes is extraordinary: - Nabaʾ 78:38 — the Rūḥ stands SILENT, in ranks, awaiting permission to speak. A scene of awe and stillness. - Qadr 97:4 — the Rūḥ DESCENDS, with permission, bringing decrees. A scene of motion and mercy.","Same being, two appearances: on Judgment Day he cannot move or speak; on this night he comes down freely.","And in BOTH, he's named separately from \"the angels\" — even though he is one. The separate mention is the honour.","And note rāḥah = REST — the same root as the Rūḥ. Fitting for a night that ends on the word salām.","Qadr 97:4: the Rūḥ DESCENDS on this night.","Same being, opposite postures."]},
  {ar:"بِإِذْنِ رَبِّهِم", tr:"Bi-idhni rabbihim", v:"4", root:"The \"permission / listening\" family — adhina = BOTH \"to permit\" AND \"to listen/give ear\" — udhun = AN EAR.", meaning:"\"by the permission of their Lord.\"", magic:"The root's double life is the hook — and it's one you hear five times a day.", grammar:"The bi- is instrumental/circumstantial (\"by means of / with\").", why:"Idhn keeps the hierarchy visible even in a scene of overwhelming honour.", note:"Udhun = AN EAR. ADHĀN = the call to prayer. Idhn = PERMISSION. All one root — permission is what you give ear to.", more:["Adhān = THE CALL TO PRAYER (the announcement you give ear to!); idhn = permission.","Udhun = an EAR. Adhān = the CALL TO PRAYER. Idhn = PERMISSION. All one root. Because permission is something you give ear to — you listen for the go-ahead.","And the theological weight of bi-idhn here: even the angels — even the Rūḥ — descend only by permission. Nothing moves on its own authority.","Compare Nabaʾ 78:38 — lā yatakallamūna illā man adhina lahu'r-Raḥmān (\"none speak except whom the Most Merciful permits\"). SAME ROOT, same restriction, and again involving the Rūḥ! In Nabaʾ, permission is needed to speak; here, permission is needed to descend.","Idhni is genitive after bi-, and rabbihim genitive by iḍāfah.","And Nabaʾ 78:38 used the same root: none SPEAK except by permission.","Here: none DESCEND except by permission."]},
  {ar:"مِّن كُلِّ أَمْرٍ", tr:"Min kulli amr", v:"4", root:"The \"command / matter / affair\" family — amara = to command; amr = a command AND a matter/affair; amīr = a commander; imārah = leadership.", meaning:"\"with every matter / from every command.\"", magic:"The phrase is deliberately comprehensive.", grammar:"The min here is debated — read as bi- (\"with every matter\") or as partitive/causal (\"concerning every matter\").", why:"Amr (command AND matter) covers both the instruction and the thing instructed about.", note:"Amīr = A COMMANDER. Same root. Nāziʿāt 79:5: the angels who ARRANGE the amr. Qadr 97:4: they descend WITH every amr.", more:["Kulli amr = \"EVERY matter\" — no category excluded. The decrees descending cover all affairs, not a selection.","And this is where the qadr (measure/decree) of v.1 becomes concrete: the night is named \"the Night of Measuring,\" and here you see the measuring being delivered. The title and the action match.","You own amr from Infiṭār 82:19 — wa'l-amru yawmaʾidhin lillāh (\"the command, that Day, belongs to Allah\"). Infiṭār: the command is His on Judgment Day. Qadr: His commands descend on this night. Same word, both directions.","And from Nāziʿāt 79:5 — fa'l-mudabbirāti amrā (\"those who arrange the affair\"). The angels who manage the amr (Nāziʿāt) are the ones who descend with every amr (Qadr). Same beings, same word, two surahs.","Both are classical; the sense of comprehensiveness is constant.","And Infiṭār 82:19: the amr belongs to Allah."]},
  {ar:"سَلَامٌ هِيَ", tr:"Salāmun hiya", v:"5", root:"The \"wholeness / soundness / safety\" family — salima = to be safe, whole, free from defect.", meaning:"\"Peace it is — until the emergence of dawn.\"", magic:"The most striking word-order in the surah, and it's easy to miss in translation.", grammar:"Khabar muqaddam — a fronted predicate.", why:"One word, fronted, is the most economical possible summary of everything v.1–4 described.", note:"Salāmun hiya = \"PEACE — it is.\" The predicate is FRONTED = \"nothing BUT peace.\" And salima = TO BE WHOLE/INTACT.", more:["Islām = submission that brings wholeness; muslim = one who submits; salām = peace; and as-Salām — a NAME OF ALLAH (The Source of Peace!).","The Arabic is salāmun hiya — \"PEACE — it is.\" The predicate comes FIRST, before the subject.","In Arabic, fronting = EMPHASIS and RESTRICTION. So it isn't \"it is peaceful\" — it's \"PEACE is what it is,\" or \"nothing but peace.\" The night is defined by the word, not merely described by it.","And think about how much the surah packs into one word: after angels, the Spirit, decrees for every matter, a night worth a lifetime — the summary is one syllable: salām.","And the root means WHOLENESS, not just absence of conflict. Salima = to be intact, sound, undamaged. So salām is a night with nothing broken in it.","You say this root constantly: as-salāmu ʿalaykum, islām, muslim, and as-Salām is one of the Divine Names.","Normal order would be hiya salāmun; the reversal is deliberate emphasis.","You own this fronting technique from Qiyāmah 75:12, 75:30, Nāziʿāt 79:44, Ghāshiyah 88:25, Sharḥ 94:8, ʿAlaq 96:8.","Not just \"no conflict\" but \"nothing broken.\" Same root as islām, muslim, as-Salām."]},
  {ar:"حَتَّىٰ مَطْلَعِ الْفَجْرِ", tr:"ḥattā maṭlaʿi'l-fajr", v:"5", root:"Maṭlaʿ — the \"rising / emerging / coming into view\" family — ṭalaʿa = to rise (of the sun), to appear.", meaning:"\"until the emergence of dawn.\"", magic:"The final word closes a root-thread that runs through SEVEN of your surahs.", grammar:"ḥattā here is a preposition of limit (\"until\"), so maṭlaʿi is genitive. Noun of time on the mafʿal pattern.", why:"Maṭlaʿ al-fajr gives an exact, observable, natural boundary — one anyone can verify by looking east.", note:"Fajr = dawn, because light SPLITS the dark — EIGHT appearances across SEVEN surahs: 75:5, 76:6, 80:42, 82:3, 82:14, 89:1, 91:8, 97:5. And ṭulūʿ ash-shams = SUNRISE; iṭṭalaʿa = to become informed (knowledge rises into view).", more:["ṭulūʿ ash-shams = SUNRISE; and iṭṭalaʿa = to look into / become informed (to have something rise into your view!).","fajr — DAWN — from fajara = TO SPLIT OPEN (light cleaving the dark).","The f-j-r root, tracked: - Qiyāmah 75:5 — li-yafjura amāmah (bursting into sin) - Insān 76:6 — yufajjirūnahā (a spring made to gush) - ʿAbasa 80:42 — al-fajarah (the wicked) - Infiṭār 82:3 — fujjirat (seas burst their bounds) - Infiṭār 82:14 — al-fujjār (the wicked) - Fajr 89:1 — wa'l-fajr (the DAWN, as an oath) - Shams 91:8 — fujūrahā (the soul's capacity to burst limits) - Qadr 97:5 — maṭlaʿi'l-FAJR (dawn ENDS the night of peace)","EIGHT appearances across seven surahs. One root: sinning, a heavenly spring, the wicked (twice), the seas, the soul's dark potential, and daybreak (twice).","And note how the surah ENDS: not at midnight, not at a fixed hour — at the moment light splits the dark. The night of peace is bounded by the first crack of dawn.","And maṭlaʿ is a noun of time/place — \"the point of rising.\" A precise boundary, not a vague ending.","Ṭalaʿa → iṭṭalaʿa (to become informed) is a lovely bonus: knowledge rises into view, like the sun.","The night of peace is bounded by the first crack of light."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:98, name:"Al-Bayyinah", ar:"البينة", en:"The Clear Proof", verses:8, place:"Madinan",
  blurb:"The surah is named \"The Clear Evidence\" — and its own most famous verse is the clear evidence: a definition so tight nothing can be added to it.",
  special:["It contains the Qurʾān's shortest complete definition of religion — and it's five items in one verse.","V.5: wa mā umirū illā li-yaʿbudu'llāha mukhliṣīna lahu'd-dīna ḥunafāʾa wa yuqīmu'ṣ-ṣalāta wa yuʾtu'z-zakāta — wa dhālika dīnu'l-qayyimah","> \"They were commanded nothing except to worship Allah, making the religion purely His, inclining to truth, and to establish the prayer and give the zakāh — and that is the upright religion.\"","Note the restriction: mā umirū illā — \"they were commanded NOTHING EXCEPT.\" Not \"among other things\" — this is the whole list.","Five items: worship Him · purely · inclining to truth · establish prayer · give zakāh. Three about the heart, two about the limbs.","--"],
  big:["The state before (v.1) — they were not going to break off until the Clear Evidence came.","What the Evidence is (v.2–3) — a Messenger reciting purified scrolls, containing upright writings.","The split (v.4) — they only divided AFTER the Evidence came.","The definition (v.5) — the five items.","Two verdicts (v.6–7) — the worst of creation and the best of creation.","The reward (v.8) — He is pleased with them, and they with Him."],
  traps:["الدِّينَ (v.5) — means RELIGION/DEVOTION here, NOT \"the Reckoning\" as in Infiṭār 82:9 and Tīn 95:7. One root (obligation), two senses. Context decides.","مُنفَكِّينَ (v.1) — from fakka, TO UNTIE — the same root as fakku raqabah (Balad 90:13, freeing a slave). Two bondages, one verb.","يُقِيمُوا الصَّلَاةَ (v.5) — NOT \"they pray\" but \"they MAKE IT STAND.\" And q-w-m appears three times in three verses (qayyimah, yuqīmū, al-qayyimah).","حُنَفَاءَ (v.5) — a DIPTOTE: fatḥah, no tanwīn. And it's Ibrāhīm's title.","الْبَيِّنَةُ (v.1 vs v.4) — v.1: they were stuck UNTIL it came. v.4: they SPLIT only AFTER it came. Read them together or you miss the irony.","رَّضِيَ عَنْ (v.8) — takes ʿan, not bi-. Learn the preposition with the verb.","يُقِيمُوا / يُؤْتُوا (v.5) — subjunctive after the lām of purpose: they DROP their final nūn.","خَيْرُ / شَرُّ (v.6–7) — IRREGULAR comparatives/superlatives. No afʿal form.","الْبَرِيَّةِ (v.6–7) — means ALL CREATION, not just people. Root of al-Bāriʾ, a Divine Name.","عَدْنٍ (v.8) — not a place-name: it means PERMANENT RESIDENCE. Same root as maʿdin, A MINE."],
  links:[{w:"مُنفَكِّينَ (v.1)",e:"فَكُّ رَقَبَةٍ (Balad 90:13)",t:"Same root — untying a slave's neck / being untied from disbelief"},{w:"الْبَيِّنَةُ (v.1, 4)",e:"بَيَانَهُ (Qiy. 75:19) · الْمُبِينِ (81:23)",t:"THREE surahs — and bayn = BETWEEN"},{w:"يَتْلُو (v.2)",e:"تَلَاهَا (Shams 91:2)",t:"Same root — the moon FOLLOWS / the Messenger RECITES"},{w:"صُحُفًا مُّطَهَّرَةً (v.2)",e:"صُحُفٍ... مُّطَهَّرَةٍ (ʿAbasa 80:13–14)",t:"Nearly verbatim"},{w:"صُحُفًا (v.2)",e:"80:13 · 81:10 · 87:18",t:"FOUR archives, one word"},{w:"قَيِّمَةٌ (v.3, 5)",e:"تَقْوِيمٍ (95:4) · يَسْتَقِيمَ (81:28)",t:"And al-QAYYŪM — Āyat al-Kursī"},{w:"تَفَرَّقَ (v.4)",e:"مُخْتَلِفُونَ (Nabaʾ 78:3)",t:"Two surahs on the same failure — and al-Furqān is this root"},{w:"الدِّينَ (v.5)",e:"يَوْمِ الدِّينِ (82:9 · 95:7)",t:"SAME WORD, different sense — devotion vs. the Reckoning"},{w:"يُقِيمُوا (v.5)",e:"قَيِّمَةٌ (v.3, same surah) · الْمُسْتَقِيم",t:"THREE q-w-m words in three verses"},{w:"الزَّكَاةَ (v.5)",e:"79:18 · 80:3, 7 · 87:14 · 91:9 · 92:18",t:"SEVEN surahs — here as an INSTITUTION"},{w:"خَيْرُ / شَرُّ (v.6–7)",e:"خَيْرٌ (87:17 · 93:4 · 97:3)",t:"IRREGULAR comparatives"},{w:"خَالِدِينَ (v.6, 8)",e:"مُّخَلَّدُونَ (Insān 76:19)",t:"Same root — and the name KHĀLID"},{w:"رَّضِيَ... وَرَضُوا (v.8)",e:"رَاضِيَةً مَّرْضِيَّةً (Fajr 89:28)",t:"Same mutual riḍā — two participles vs. two verbs"},{w:"رَّضِيَ (v.8)",e:"88:9 · 89:28 · 92:21 · 93:5 · 101:7",t:"SIX surahs"},{w:"خَشِيَ (v.8)",e:"79:19, 26, 45 · 87:10",t:"FIVE verses, THREE surahs — always the filter"},{w:"أُولَٰئِكَ هُمْ (v.6–7)",e:"هِيَ الْمَأْوَىٰ (79:39, 41)",t:"The separating pronoun = restriction"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"مُنفَكِّينَ", tr:"Munfakkīn", v:"1", root:"The \"undoing / unfastening / separating apart\" family — fakka = to untie, to release, to break something apart at its joint; fakk = also the JAW (the joint that opens!); infikāk = coming loose, detachment.", meaning:"\"were not going to break off / be released [from their state] until the Clear Evidence came to them.\"", magic:"You own this root from Balad 90:13 — and the connection is remarkable.", grammar:"Form VII (infakka) active participle, plural accusative as khabar of lam yakun.", why:"Munfakkīn frames disbelief as a knot, which makes revelation a release rather than an imposition.", note:"Fakka = TO UNTIE. Balad 90:13: fakku raqabah — untying a SLAVE'S NECK. Bayyinah 98:1: munfakkīn — not being untied from disbelief.", more:["Balad 90:13 — fakku raqabah — \"the FREEING of a neck\" (the first answer to \"what is the steep pass?\"). Bayyinah 98:1 — munfakkīn — \"[not] being released.\"","Same root: untying a slave's neck, and being untied from a state of disbelief. Both are releases from bondage — one physical, one spiritual.","And the word here is a Form VII passive-sense participle — they weren't going to come loose by themselves. Something external had to arrive: the Bayyinah.","The verse describes a stuck condition — a knot that wasn't going to untie on its own. The Messenger is the hand that unties it.","Note: this verse has several classical interpretations (about what exactly they weren't separating from, and whether it describes them before or after). The root image — being stuck until released — holds across all of them.","You own Form VII from Takwīr 81:2 (inkadarat), Infiṭār 82:1 (infaṭarat), Shams 91:12 (inbaʿatha).","And fakk also = THE JAW (the joint that opens)."]},
  {ar:"الْبَيِّنَةُ", tr:"Al-bayyinah", v:"1, 4", root:"The \"clarity / separating and making distinct\" family — bāna = to become clear.", meaning:"\"the Clear Evidence\" — the surah's name.", magic:"The root's core idea explains what \"evidence\" means in Arabic.", grammar:"Bayyinah is an intensive adjective (fayʿilah) used as a noun.", why:"Bayyinah over \"proof\" — because it names the function (separating) rather than the form.", note:"Bayn = BETWEEN. A bayyinah puts distance between truth and falsehood.", more:["Bayn = BETWEEN (clarity comes from separating one thing from another!); bayyana = to clarify; bayān = eloquent exposition (you own it from Qiyāmah 75:19); mubīn = self-evidently clear (Takwīr 81:23).","Bayn = BETWEEN. So bayyinah = something that puts distance BETWEEN truth and falsehood — it separates them so you can tell which is which. Clarity, in Arabic, is distinguishing, not brightness.","You own the root across three surahs: - Qiyāmah 75:19 — thumma inna ʿalaynā bayānah (\"upon Us is its clarification\") - Takwīr 81:23 — bi'l-ufuqi'l-mubīn (\"the clear horizon\") - Bayyinah 98:1, 4 — al-bayyinah (\"the Clear Evidence\")","And here is the surah's sharpest irony: v.1 says they were stuck until the Clear Evidence came. v.4 says they divided only AFTER the Clear Evidence came. The thing that was supposed to unite them by clarifying is what split them. Not because it was unclear — because clarity forces a choice.","Feminine, which is why tāʾtiyahum (v.1) and jāʾathum (v.4) are feminine verbs.","And the irony: v.1 they were stuck UNTIL it came; v.4 they SPLIT only AFTER it came."]},
  {ar:"يَتْلُو", tr:"Yatlū", v:"2", root:"The \"following after\" family — talā = to follow; and from that: talā = TO RECITE (reciting is following the words in order!); tilāwah = recitation.", meaning:"\"a Messenger from Allah, reciting purified scrolls.\"", magic:"You own this exact root from Shams 91:2 — wa'l-qamari idhā talāhā (\"the moon when it FOLLOWS it\").", grammar:"Weak-final verb (ت-ل-و) — note the final wāw appears as the long ū.", why:"Yatlū (follow/recite) rather than yaqraʾ — it stresses faithful transmission, not performance.", note:"Talā = TO FOLLOW and TO RECITE. Shams 91:2: the moon FOLLOWS the sun.", more:["Same root: the moon following the sun, and the Messenger reciting revelation. And the connection is beautiful: the moon has no light of its own; it faithfully transmits what it received. That's exactly what tilāwah is — following along, transmitting without altering.","And note the word choice against iqraʾ (ʿAlaq 96:1): iqraʾ = the command to read; yatlū = the ongoing action of reciting. ʿAlaq gave the order; Bayyinah shows it being carried out.","The present tense (yatlū) makes it continuous — this is what he does, not something he did once.","Rasūlun is indefinite, in apposition to al-bayyinah — the Evidence IS a Messenger.","The moon RECITES the sun — transmitting light it didn't make.","And ʿAlaq 96:1 gave the command iqraʾ; here it's being done."]},
  {ar:"صُحُفًا مُّطَهَّرَةً", tr:"ṣuḥufan muṭahharah", v:"2", root:"ṣuḥuf — scrolls/pages (ṣaḥīfah; modern ṣaḥāfah = JOURNALISM, ṣaḥīfah = a newspaper).", meaning:"\"purified scrolls.\"", magic:"This is nearly a verbatim echo of ʿAbasa 80:13–14.", grammar:"Passive participle, Form II (muṭahharah) — recognize the mu-...-a- shape. Both accusative as objects of yatlū.", why:"Muṭahharah (made pure) rather than \"true\" — because purity is about what's been kept out, which is the transmission claim.", note:"Ṣaḥāfah = JOURNALISM; ṣaḥīfah = a NEWSPAPER.", more:["muṭahharah — the \"purity\" family — you own it from Insān 76:21 (sharāban ṭahūrā, a purifying drink) and ʿAbasa 80:14.","ʿAbasa 80:13–14 — fī ṣuḥufin mukarramatin · marfūʿatin muṭahharah (\"in scrolls honoured, exalted, purified\"). Bayyinah 98:2 — yatlū ṣuḥufan muṭahharah (\"reciting purified scrolls\").","Same noun, same adjective, two surahs. Free vocabulary.","And muṭahharah is a passive participle — they were made pure. Someone purified them. Not \"clean by nature\" but actively protected from contamination.","The ṣuḥuf root now appears across FOUR surahs: - ʿAbasa 80:13 — the scrolls of revelation (honoured, in angels' hands) - Takwīr 81:10 — the scrolls of your deeds (spread open) - Aʿlā 87:18–19 — the earliest scrolls (of Ibrāhīm and Mūsā) - Bayyinah 98:2 — the purified scrolls being recited","And remember the everyday hook: ṣaḥāfah = JOURNALISM, ṣaḥīfah = a newspaper.","Nearly identical to ʿAbasa 80:13–14. FOUR archives, one word: revelation's scrolls (80:13), YOUR scrolls (81:10), the ancient scrolls (87:18), these purified scrolls (98:2)."]},
  {ar:"كُتُبٌ قَيِّمَةٌ", tr:"Kutubun qayyimah", v:"3", root:"Kutub — writings/books (kataba = to write; you own kātibīn from Infiṭār 82:11 — the writing scribes).", meaning:"\"within them are upright writings.\"", magic:"The q-w-m root is one of your best-tracked, and qayyimah is its sharpest form here.", grammar:"Qayyimah is an intensive adjective (fayʿil pattern), feminine agreeing with kutub.", why:"Qayyimah (upright/sustaining) rather than ṣaḥīḥah (correct) — it names what the books do to you, not just what they are.", note:"Al-QAYYŪM — the Divine Name in Āyat al-Kursī — same root. And aṣ-ṣirāṭ al-MUSTAQĪM — 17× a day.", more:["qayyimah — the \"standing / uprightness\" family — qāma = to stand; qayyim = upright, correct, well-established, self-sustaining; and al-Qayyūm — a NAME OF ALLAH (The Self-Subsisting, who stands and sustains all).","Track it: - Takwīr 81:28 — an yastaqīm (\"to go straight\") - Tīn 95:4 — fī aḥsani taqwīm (\"the best standing/form\") - Bayyinah 98:3 — kutubun qayyimah (\"upright writings\") - Bayyinah 98:5 — dīnu'l-qayyimah (\"the upright religion\") - And aṣ-ṣirāṭ al-MUSTAQĪM — which you say 17× a day.","The root means standing upright AND making others stand. So kutub qayyimah aren't just \"correct books\" — they're books that hold things up, that keep a person and a society standing straight.","And al-Qayyūm (a Divine Name, in Āyat al-Kursī) is the same root: the One who stands by Himself and sustains everything else. Free hook from a verse you likely know by heart.","Note the structure of v.2–3: a Messenger → reciting scrolls → containing writings. Three layers: the man, the medium, the content.","Note kutubun is a broken plural treated as feminine singular — a standard Arabic agreement rule for non-human plurals.","Also taqwīm (Tīn 95:4) and yastaqīm (Takwīr 81:28). These books don't just state truth — they HOLD YOU UP."]},
  {ar:"تَفَرَّقَ", tr:"Tafarraqa", v:"4", root:"The \"separating / splitting into groups\" family — faraqa = to separate; firqah = a sect/faction.", meaning:"\"And those given the Scripture did not become divided until AFTER the Clear Evidence came to them.\"", magic:"The verse's timing is the whole indictment.", grammar:"Form V (tafaʿʿala) = reflexive — doing it to themselves.", why:"Tafarraqa (they split themselves) rather than a passive — it places the fault entirely with them.", note:"Al-FURQĀN — a name of the Qurʾān = \"THE SEPARATOR.\" Same root.", more:["Al-Furqān = a name of the Qurʾān (\"The Criterion\" — that which SEPARATES truth from falsehood!); and yawm al-faṣl, a different root with the same job (Nabaʾ 78:17).","It doesn't say they were divided and the Evidence failed to unite them. It says the division began after it arrived. The clarity itself was the trigger.","And the root-irony is sharp: the Qurʾān is called al-Furqān — the Separator — because it separates truth from falsehood. Here, the same root describes people separating from each other. The book that separates truth from error caused people who wouldn't accept it to separate from those who would.","Form V (tafarraqa) = they split themselves — reflexive, self-inflicted. Nobody divided them; they came apart.","Compare Nabaʾ 78:3 — alladhī hum fīhi mukhtalifūn (\"about which they DIFFER\"). Nabaʾ: differing about the News. Bayyinah: splitting after the Evidence. Two surahs on the same human failure.","Illā min baʿdi mā = \"not except after\" — an emphatic time-restriction.","The book that separates TRUTH from FALSEHOOD caused PEOPLE to separate from each other.","And they split only AFTER clarity arrived."]},
  {ar:"مُخْلِصِينَ لَهُ الدِّينَ", tr:"Mukhliṣīna lahu'd-dīn", v:"5", root:"Mukhliṣ — the \"purifying / making something unmixed\" family — khalaṣa = to become pure, to be free of admixture.", meaning:"\"worshipping Allah, making the religion purely His.\"", magic:"The root is a purity-of-substance word, and that's what makes it the right word for sincerity.", grammar:"Form IV active participle, plural accusative as ḥāl (\"while making it pure\").", why:"Mukhliṣ (unmixed) rather than a word for honesty — because the test is what else is in there, not whether you mean it.", note:"Khāliṣ = PURE GOLD / UNMIXED MILK. Ikhlāṣ = removing every OTHER ingredient — and Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ is named for it.", more:["Khāliṣ = pure, unadulterated (used of pure gold, pure milk!); ikhlāṣ = SINCERITY — and Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ!; khalāṣ = deliverance.","dīn — here: devotion/religion (a different sense from Infiṭār 82:9!).","Khāliṣ is what you say of pure gold or unmixed milk — something with nothing else in it. So ikhlāṣ isn't \"trying hard\" — it's removing every other ingredient from your intention.","And Sūrat al-Ikhlāṣ (112) is named for this — the surah of pure, unmixed monotheism. Free hook.","MAJOR TRAP: dīn here means RELIGION/DEVOTION — not \"the Reckoning.\" - Infiṭār 82:9, 15, 17, 18 — yawm ad-dīn = SETTLEMENT DAY (from dayn, a debt) - Tīn 95:7 — bi'd-dīn = the Reckoning - Bayyinah 98:5 — lahu'd-dīn = RELIGION / devotion","Same word, and the root unifies them: d-y-n = obligation. A dayn is a debt you owe; a dīn is a devotion you owe. Both are things you're obligated to. Context decides which.","And note lahu is FRONTED: mukhliṣīna LAHU'd-dīn — \"making the religion TO HIM purely\" = to Him ALONE.","The fronting of lahu = restriction.","And dīn HERE = RELIGION, not \"the Reckoning\" (Infiṭār 82:9). One root: dayn = a debt you owe; dīn = a devotion you owe."]},
  {ar:"حُنَفَاءَ", tr:"ḥunafāʾ", v:"5", root:"The \"inclining / turning away from crookedness toward straightness\" family — ḥanafa = to incline.", meaning:"\"inclining to truth / as pure monotheists.\"", magic:"The word's direction is the point.", grammar:"ḥunafāʾa is a DIPTOTE (mamnūʿ min aṣ-ṣarf) — it takes fatḥah without tanwīn in the accusative.", why:"ḥanīf carries Ibrāhīm's authority in a single word.", note:"Ḥanīf = IBRĀHĪM'S TITLE — one who TURNS AWAY from all false worship. And Aʿlā 87:19 named the scrolls of IBRĀHĪM.", more:["ḥanīf = one who turns away from all false worship toward pure monotheism — the title given to Ibrāhīm (peace be upon him) repeatedly in the Qurʾān.","Ḥanīf means someone who has turned — away from something, toward something else. It's a word of motion and preference, not just a label.","And it carries enormous weight because it's Ibrāhīm's title. So v.5 is saying: what you were commanded is exactly what Ibrāhīm did. Which answers the whole context — the People of the Scripture are being told this isn't a new religion but the original one.","Compare Aʿlā 87:18–19 — inna hādhā la-fi'ṣ-ṣuḥufi'l-ūlā · ṣuḥufi IBRĀHĪMA wa Mūsā (\"this is in the earliest scrolls — the scrolls of IBRĀHĪM and Mūsā\"). Aʿlā names Ibrāhīm's scrolls; Bayyinah names Ibrāhīm's posture. Both say: this message is the oldest thing there is.","And note the pairing in v.5: mukhliṣīn (purity of content — nothing else mixed in) + ḥunafāʾ (purity of direction — turned away from all else). Inward and outward.","Plural patterns ending in -āʾ usually are.","You own diptotes from ḥadāʾiq (78:32), zarābiyy (88:16), Irama (89:7).","Both say: this is the ORIGINAL religion.","And mukhliṣīn (pure content) + ḥunafāʾ (pure direction) = inward and outward."]},
  {ar:"يُقِيمُوا الصَّلَاةَ وَيُؤْتُوا الزَّكَاةَ", tr:"Yuqīmu'ṣ-ṣalāta wa yuʾtu'z-zakāh", v:"5", root:"Yuqīmū — Form IV of qāma (to stand) = to MAKE something stand, to establish it properly — the same root as qayyimah two verses earlier!", meaning:"\"and to establish the prayer and give the zakāh.\"", magic:"The verb for prayer is the most under-appreciated word here.", grammar:"Both are subjunctive after the lām of purpose (li-yaʿbudū… wa yuqīmū… wa yuʾtū) — note they DROP their final nūn.", why:"Yuqīmū (make it stand) rather than yuṣallū — because a prayer can be performed and still not be standing.", note:"NOT \"they pray\" — \"they MAKE IT STAND.\" And q-w-m appears THREE times in three verses: qayyimah (v.3), yuqīmū (v.5), al-qayyimah (v.5).", more:["yuʾtū — Form IV of \"to come\" = to give/hand over (you own it from Layl 92:18, yuʾtī mālahu).","zakāh — the \"purify + grow\" family.","It does NOT say yuṣallū (\"they pray\"). It says yuqīmū — \"they MAKE IT STAND.\" Prayer isn't something you perform; it's something you erect, like a structure — built properly, upright, and kept standing.","And here's the payoff: v.3 said the writings are QAYYIMAH (upright). v.5 says you must YUQĪMŪ the prayer (make it stand). SAME ROOT, two verses apart. Upright books produce an upright prayer. And the verse ends dīnu'l-QAYYIMAH — THREE appearances of q-w-m in three verses.","And zakāh is your most-tracked reward-root, now across SEVEN surahs: - Nāziʿāt 79:18 (offered to Pharaoh) · ʿAbasa 80:3, 7 (the blind man) · Aʿlā 87:14 · Shams 91:9 · Layl 92:18 · Bayyinah 98:5","And Bayyinah is where it becomes an institution. The other surahs used it as a verb (purify yourself); here it's a noun with the definite article — az-Zakāh, the obligation.","Note the pairing: ṣalāh (your relationship with Allah) + zakāh (your relationship with people). Two limbs of the same religion.","A standard subjunctive marker for these plural forms.","Upright books → upright prayer → upright religion.","And zakāh is now in SEVEN surahs — here as an INSTITUTION, not just a verb."]},
  {ar:"شَرُّ الْبَرِيَّةِ / خَيْرُ الْبَرِيَّةِ", tr:"Sharru'l-bariyyah / khayru'l-bariyyah", v:"6, 7", root:"Sharr / khayr — worst / best — BOTH are IRREGULAR comparatives/superlatives (no afʿal form).", meaning:"\"those are the worst of creation\" (v.6) / \"those are the best of creation\" (v.7).", magic:"A perfect muqābala (mirror-antithesis) — and the word for \"creation\" is the key.", grammar:"Khayr and sharr are IRREGULAR — they serve as comparative AND superlative with no afʿal form.", why:"Bariyyah (all created things) rather than nās (people) — it makes the ranking universal.", note:"Al-BĀRIʾ — a NAME OF ALLAH (The Originator). Same root as bariyyah. \"Worst/best of ALL CREATION,\" not just of people.", more:["bariyyah — the \"creating from nothing\" family — baraʾa = to create, to originate; al-Bāriʾ — a NAME OF ALLAH (The Originator!); and barāʾah = innocence/acquittal (being clear of something).","The two verses are grammatically identical apart from the contents:","Same frame, opposite superlative.","And bariyyah (from baraʾa, to create from nothing) is doing something specific: it doesn't say \"the worst of people\" — it says \"the worst of everything that was created.\" Including animals, including everything.","And al-Bāriʾ is a Divine Name — so bariyyah quietly reminds you who made them even as it ranks them.","Note ulāʾika hum in both — the ḍamīr al-faṣl (separating pronoun) = restriction: \"those, they specifically, are…\" You own this from Nāziʿāt 79:39, 41 (hiya'l-maʾwā).","You met khayr in Aʿlā 87:17, Ḍuḥā 93:4, Qadr 97:3.","And khayr/sharr are IRREGULAR — no afʿal form."]},
  {ar:"جَنَّاتُ عَدْنٍ... خَالِدِينَ فِيهَا أَبَدًا", tr:"Jannātu ʿadnin… khālidīna fīhā abadā", v:"8", root:"ʿadn — the \"permanent residence / staying put\" family — ʿadana = to remain, to dwell permanently.", meaning:"\"Gardens of perpetual residence… abiding therein forever.\"", magic:"THREE eternity-words stacked in one verse — and ʿadn is the best of them.", grammar:"Khālidīna is a plural active participle in the accusative as ḥāl (\"in a state of abiding\").", why:"ʿadn names the garden by the permanence of residence, which is the actual reward.", note:"Maʿdin = A MINE — where a mineral resides PERMANENTLY in the rock. That's ʿadn.", more:["Maʿdin = A MINE (the place where a mineral permanently resides in the earth!","— and modern maʿdinī = mineral).","khālidīn — the \"eternity\" family — the same root as the name KHĀLID (you own it from Insān 76:19, wildānun mukhalladūn).","abadā — forever (abadī = eternal).","Jannātu ʿadn = \"Gardens of PERMANENT RESIDENCE.\" Not \"Gardens of Eden\" as a place-name — the word describes the nature of the stay.","And maʿdin = A MINE is the perfect hook: a mine is where gold or iron has settled permanently in the rock. It doesn't move. It's from there. That's ʿadn.","Then the verse stacks two more: khālidīna (abiding) + abadā (forever). Three words for permanence in one clause — because the surah has just said the disbelievers are khālidīna fīhā in Hell (v.6). The same eternity, two addresses.","And Khālid is a name you've likely heard all your life — it literally means \"the eternal one.\"","Abadā is an accusative adverb of time.","And KHĀLID = \"the eternal one\" (Insān 76:19's mukhalladūn).","THREE permanence-words in one verse: ʿadn, khālidīn, abadā."]},
  {ar:"رَّضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ وَرَضُوا عَنْهُ", tr:"Raḍiya'llāhu ʿanhum wa raḍū ʿanh", v:"8", root:"The \"contentment / pleasure\" family — riḍā = satisfaction.", meaning:"\"Allah is pleased with them, and they are pleased with Him.\"", magic:"The same mutual-satisfaction idea as Fajr 89:28 — but expressed with two full verbs instead of two participles.", grammar:"Raḍiya ʿan = \"to be pleased with\" — note the preposition ʿan, not bi-. Learn the verb with its preposition.", why:"Stating both directions explicitly rules out a one-sided reward.", note:"Fajr 89:28 compressed it: rāḍiyatan marḍiyyah (two participles). Bayyinah 98:8 spells it out: two full verbs.", more:["You own it from Ghāshiyah 88:9 (li-saʿyihā rāḍiyah), Fajr 89:28 (rāḍiyatan marḍiyyah), Layl 92:21 (la-sawfa yarḍā), Ḍuḥā 93:5 (fa-tarḍā), Qāriʿah 101:7 (ʿīshatin rāḍiyah).","Fajr 89:28 — rāḍiyatan marḍiyyah — active participle + passive participle (she is pleased / she is pleased-with). Bayyinah 98:8 — raḍiya'llāhu ʿanhum wa raḍū ʿanh — two complete verbs, both directions spelled out.","Fajr compressed it into two words; Bayyinah expands it into a full sentence. Same mutual riḍā, two grammatical strategies.","And notice the ORDER: HIS pleasure is stated FIRST. Raḍiya'llāhu ʿanhum comes before wa raḍū ʿanh. His satisfaction precedes and causes theirs.","And riḍā is now across SIX surahs — making it one of the most consistent reward-words in the juzʾ. Track it: Ghāshiyah 88:9 (pleased with their striving), Fajr 89:28 (mutual), Layl 92:21 (he will be pleased), Ḍuḥā 93:5 (you will be pleased), Bayyinah 98:8 (mutual, spelled out), Qāriʿah 101:7 (a pleasing life).","And HIS pleasure is stated FIRST.","The verb takes ʿan, not bi-."]},
  {ar:"لِمَنْ خَشِيَ رَبَّهُ", tr:"Li-man khashiya rabbah", v:"8", root:"The \"awe-filled fear\" family — khashyah = fear that comes from KNOWING something's greatness (compare khawf, plain fear).", meaning:"\"That is for whoever fears his Lord in awe.\"", magic:"The surah's final words — and they name the one qualifying condition.", grammar:"Man here = \"whoever\" (relative), not \"who?\". Past tense khashiya after man = a general, timeless condition.", why:"Khashyah over khawf — because this is reverence from knowledge, which is the only kind that produces the five items of v.5.", note:"Khashyah = fear that comes from KNOWING (not khawf, plain fear).", more:["After everything — the Evidence, the definition, the two verdicts, the eternal gardens — the whole thing is filed under a single filter: khashyah.","And khashyah is now across FOUR surahs, functioning as the same filter every time: - Nāziʿāt 79:19 — wa ahdiyaka ilā rabbika fa-takhshā (Pharaoh could have had it) - Nāziʿāt 79:26 — la-ʿibratan li-man yakhshā (the lesson is for whoever has it) - Nāziʿāt 79:45 — innamā anta mundhiru man yakhshāhā (you warn whoever has it) - Aʿlā 87:10 — sa-yadhdhakkaru man yakhshā (whoever has it WILL take heed) - Bayyinah 98:8 — li-man khashiya rabbah (the reward is for whoever has it)","FIVE verses across THREE surahs, and the word always does the same job: it separates who the message lands on.","And remember the crucial nuance from Nāziʿāt 79:19: guidance comes FIRST, awe SECOND. Khashyah is the result of knowledge, not a substitute for it.","FIVE verses, THREE surahs: 79:19, 79:26, 79:45, 87:10, 98:8. Always the same filter.","And Nāziʿāt 79:19: guidance FIRST, awe SECOND."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:99, name:"Az-Zalzalah", ar:"الزلزلة", en:"The Earthquake", verses:8, place:"Madinan",
  blurb:"The earth is going to talk. And the word for what it does is the root of ḥadīth — a narration.",
  special:["The earth becomes a witness — and the verb used for its testimony is the same one you were commanded to use about Allah's favour.","Ḍuḥā 93:11 — wa ammā bi-niʿmati rabbika fa-ḥaddith — \"as for your Lord's favour — NARRATE IT.\" Zalzalah 99:4 — yawmaʾidhin tuḥaddithu akhbārahā — \"that Day it will NARRATE its news.\"","SAME ROOT, SAME FORM II VERB. You are told to narrate what was done for you. The earth will narrate what was done on it.","And think about what that means: the ground you're standing on right now is taking notes. Every place you've ever prayed, every place you've ever sinned — the location itself is a witness with a voice.","--"],
  big:["The shaking (v.1–3) — the earth quakes, throws out its weights, and man asks \"what's wrong with it?\"","The testimony (v.4–5) — it narrates its news, because your Lord inspired it.","The procession (v.6) — people issue forth SCATTERED, to be shown their deeds.","The atom's weight (v.7–8) — the most famous couplet in the juzʾ."],
  traps:["أَثْقَالَهَا (v.2) and مِثْقَالَ (v.7) — SAME ROOT, six verses apart. The earth's heaviest loads and the lightest conceivable weight. Both measured.","تُحَدِّثُ (v.4) — the same Form II verb as Ḍuḥā 93:11's fa-ḥaddith. You narrate the favour; the earth narrates the record.","أَخْبَارَهَا (v.4) — ordinary khabar (a report), NOT nabaʾ (momentous news, Nabaʾ 78:2). The plainness is the point.","أَوْحَىٰ (v.5) — this is the PROPHETIC REVELATION word (waḥy), used here for the earth. Don't downgrade it to \"told.\"","أَشْتَاتًا (v.6) — same root as shattā in Layl 92:4. Efforts scattered in life → people scattered at the exit.","يَرَهُ (v.7–8) — jussive: the final alif of yarā is DROPPED, and the pronoun becomes -h. Read carefully.","V.7 and v.8 are word-for-word identical except khayran/sharran — read them as a matched pair, like Layl 92:7/92:10.","زُلْزِلَتِ (v.1) — PASSIVE (\"it is shaken\"), not active. No doer named — standard for cosmic events.","لِّيُرَوْا (v.6) — PASSIVE subjunctive: \"to BE SHOWN,\" not \"to see.\" They don't choose what to review.","أَوْحَىٰ لَهَا (v.5) — note the preposition li-, not ilā. A recognized shade: command/permission given TO it."],
  links:[{w:"زُلْزِلَتْ زِلْزَالَهَا (v.1)",e:"عَسْعَسَ (81:17) · دَمْدَمَ (91:14)",t:"Reduplicated roots — the sound IS the meaning"},{w:"زِلْزَالَهَا (v.1)",e:"دَكًّا دَكًّا (Fajr 89:21)",t:"Two surahs, two doubled destructions of the earth"},{w:"أَثْقَالَهَا (v.2)",e:"ثَقِيلًا (Insān 76:27)",t:"Same root — a heavy Day / a heavy load"},{w:"أَثْقَالَهَا (v.2)",e:"مِثْقَالَ (v.7, same surah)",t:"SAME ROOT — greatest weights and smallest weight"},{w:"وَأَخْرَجَتِ (v.2)",e:"أَخْرَجَ... مَرْعَاهَا (79:31) · الْمَرْعَى (87:4)",t:"Same verb — the earth brings out pasture / brings out its dead"},{w:"الْإِنسَانُ (v.3)",e:"75:3 · 80:17 · 82:6 · 96:6",t:"The unnamed human"},{w:"تُحَدِّثُ (v.4)",e:"فَحَدِّثْ (Ḍuḥā 93:11)",t:"SAME ROOT, SAME FORM — you narrate / the earth narrates"},{w:"أَخْبَارَهَا (v.4)",e:"النَّبَإ (Nabaʾ 78:2)",t:"Ordinary khabar vs. weighty nabaʾ"},{w:"أَخْبَارَهَا (v.4)",e:"لَّخَبِيرٌ (ʿĀdiyāt 100:11)",t:"Same root, consecutive surahs"},{w:"أَوْحَىٰ (v.5)",e:"",t:"The PROPHETIC revelation word, used for the EARTH"},{w:"يَصْدُرُ (v.6)",e:"صَدْرَكَ (Sharḥ 94:1)",t:"Same root — and maṣdar = A SOURCE"},{w:"أَشْتَاتًا (v.6)",e:"لَشَتَّىٰ (Layl 92:4)",t:"Same root — efforts scattered → people scattered"},{w:"أَعْمَالَهُمْ (v.6)",e:"عَامِلَةٌ نَّاصِبَةٌ (Ghāshiyah 88:3)",t:"Same root — work/deeds"},{w:"يَرَهُ (v.7–8)",e:"لَمْ يَرَهُ أَحَدٌ (90:7) · يَرَىٰ (96:14)",t:"FOUR surahs — \"no one saw me\" → \"Allah sees\" → \"he sees it\""},{w:"خَيْرًا / شَرًّا (v.7–8)",e:"خَيْرُ / شَرُّ الْبَرِيَّةِ (98:6–7)",t:"Consecutive surahs, same pair"},{w:"V.7 vs v.8 identical",e:"Layl 92:7 vs 92:10",t:"Two verses differing by ONE word"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"زُلْزِلَتِ... زِلْزَالَهَا", tr:"Zulzilati… zilzālahā", v:"1", root:"A REDUPLICATED root (z-l-z-l) — the \"violent, repeated shaking\" family.", meaning:"\"When the earth is shaken with its [own] quaking.\"", magic:"The doubled root is the earthquake, and you own this pattern from three surahs.", grammar:"Passive verb (zulzilat) + cognate accusative with a possessive pronoun.", why:"The reduplication makes the verse onomatopoeic — the sound performs the meaning.", note:"Say it: zul-zi-lat zil-zā-la-hā. The word IS the earthquake.", more:["Zalzala = to shake convulsively, again and again; zilzāl = AN EARTHQUAKE (still the everyday Arabic word).","Arabic uses reduplicated roots for actions that are repeated, quivering, or relentless: - waswasa — to whisper repeatedly (Sūrat an-Nās) - ʿasʿasa — the night creeping (Takwīr 81:17) - damdama — to crush utterly, blow on blow (Shams 91:14) - zalzala — to shake convulsively (here)","Say it out loud: zul-zi-lat… zil-zā-la-hā. The word IS the tremor. You can hear the ground moving in the syllables.","And zilzālahā is a cognate accusative (maf'ūl muṭlaq) — the verb repeated as a noun for emphasis. You own this from Nāziʿāt 79:1–4, ʿAbasa 80:25–26, Fajr 89:19–22, Ṭāriq 86:15–16.","But here it has a possessive: zilzāla-HĀ — \"ITS quaking.\" Not just \"a shaking\" — the specific, final, appointed quake that belongs to it. The earth has one earthquake with its name on it.","Compare Fajr 89:21 — idhā dukkati'l-arḍu dakkan dakkā (\"when the earth is pounded, pounding upon pounding\"). Two surahs, two doubled-emphasis destructions of the earth.","The passive means no doer is named — standard for cosmic events.","Same doubling as waswasa, ʿasʿasa (81:17), damdama (91:14). And zilzāla-HĀ = \"ITS quake\" — the one with its name on it."]},
  {ar:"أَثْقَالَهَا", tr:"Athqālahā", v:"2", root:"The \"heaviness / weight\" family — thaqula = to be heavy; thiql = a burden/weight.", meaning:"\"and the earth throws out its burdens.\"", magic:"The word is deliberately ambiguous, and both meanings land.", grammar:"Broken plural of thiql; accusative object of akhrajat.", why:"Athqāl frames the dead as a load the earth has been carrying, which makes the release feel like relief.", note:"Mīthqāl = a unit of WEIGHT (coming in v.7!).", more:["Mīthqāl = a unit of weight (coming in v.7!); and ath-Thaqalān = \"the two weighty ones\" (jinn and mankind).","Classical readings of athqāl: (a) the dead buried in it — the bodies it has been carrying; (b) its treasures — the gold, minerals, and hidden things in its depths.","Either way, the image is: the earth has been CARRYING something heavy for a very long time, and now it puts it down.","And that's the emotional logic of the surah: the earth is treated as a burdened creature. v.2 it unloads. v.4 it speaks. It's been holding weight and staying silent — and both end on the same day.","You own this root from Insān 76:27 — wa yadharūna warāʾahum yawman thaqīlā (\"they leave behind them a HEAVY Day\"). Insān: a heavy DAY. Zalzalah: the earth's heavy LOAD. Same root.","And it comes back in v.7 as mithqāl — a weight so small it's the opposite of athqāl. The surah opens with the earth's GREATEST weights and closes with the SMALLEST weight imaginable. Same root, both ends.","Note akhrajat (Form IV) = \"brought OUT\" — the same verb used in Nāziʿāt 79:31 (akhraja minhā māʾahā) and Aʿlā 87:4 (akhraja'l-marʿā) for the earth producing life.","Same verb: the earth brings out pasture, and brings out its dead.","The surah opens with the earth's HEAVIEST loads (athqāl) and closes with the LIGHTEST weight (mithqāl dharrah).","And Insān 76:27 had a \"heavy Day.\""]},
  {ar:"مَا لَهَا", tr:"Mā lahā", v:"3", root:"An idiom: mā la- + pronoun = \"what's wrong with it? / what's the matter with it?\"", meaning:"\"And man says, 'What is wrong with it?'\"", magic:"Three words, and they're doing something psychologically exact.", grammar:"Mā here is interrogative; lahā = li- + hā. A very common Arabic idiom for \"what's up with…?\"", why:"The idiom's casualness is the point — it sounds almost like confusion rather than terror, which makes it worse.", note:"The ONLY human speech in the surah is a question about the EARTH, not about himself.", more:["This is the only human speech in the entire surah — and it's a question about the earth, not about himself.","Think about that. The graves are opening, the ground is convulsing, and man's reaction is: \"what's wrong with it?\" He's still treating himself as the observer and the earth as the problem.","And the surah answers by letting the earth speak instead of him (v.4). He asks a question about the earth; the earth gives testimony about him. The roles invert in a single verse.","Note also the word for man is al-insān — the same generic term used in Qiyāmah 75:3, 75:13, Nabaʾ 78:40 (al-marʾ), ʿAbasa 80:17, Infiṭār 82:6, ʿAlaq 96:6. Always the same: the camera narrows to one unnamed human.","\"What's wrong with it?\" And the earth answers by testifying about him.","The roles invert in one verse."]},
  {ar:"تُحَدِّثُ أَخْبَارَهَا", tr:"Tuḥaddithu akhbārahā", v:"4", root:"Tuḥaddithu — Form II of the \"newness / relating\" root — ḥadatha = to happen, to be new.", meaning:"\"That Day it will narrate its news.\"", magic:"THE BEST CROSS-SURAH LINK IN THIS SURAH — and it's a direct command answered by the earth.", grammar:"Form II present tense.", why:"Tuḥaddithu (narrate) rather than tashhadu (testify) — narration is telling the story, which is more complete than answering charges.", note:"Ḍuḥā 93:11: YOU narrate the favour (fa-ḥaddith). Zalzalah 99:4: the EARTH narrates the record (tuḥaddithu). Same root, same form.", more:["ḥadīth = (a) a NARRATION and (b) NEW; Form II ḥaddatha = TO NARRATE, to tell about.","akhbār — plural of khabar = news, a report (modern akhbār = THE NEWS on TV!); and khabīr = one deeply informed (coming in ʿĀdiyāt 100:11!).","Ḍuḥā 93:11 — wa ammā bi-niʿmati rabbika FA-ḤADDITH — \"as for your Lord's favour — NARRATE IT.\" Zalzalah 99:4 — yawmaʾidhin TUḤADDITHU akhbārahā — \"that Day it will NARRATE its news.\"","SAME ROOT, SAME FORM II. You were commanded to narrate the favour. The earth will narrate the record. Two narrations, one verb.","And the khabar / nabaʾ distinction is worth knowing: - nabaʾ (Nabaʾ 78:2) = MOMENTOUS, weighty news — the kind that changes everything - khabar (here) = an ordinary report — the everyday word for news","And that's devastating. The earth doesn't deliver a grand announcement — it just gives its report, plainly, like a witness reciting facts. No drama. Just: here's what happened here.","And ḥadīth also means NEW — so the earth's testimony arrives fresh, as if it all happened yesterday.","Note akhbārahā is a broken plural of khabar — plural, because there are many reports, not one.","And akhbār = THE NEWS (ordinary khabar, not weighty nabaʾ — Nabaʾ 78:2). A plain report, which is worse."]},
  {ar:"أَوْحَىٰ لَهَا", tr:"Awḥā lahā", v:"5", root:"The \"swift, subtle communication\" family — waḥā = to signal quickly and secretly; waḥy = REVELATION.", meaning:"\"because your Lord inspired / commanded it.\"", magic:"The astonishing thing here is which word is used.", grammar:"Form IV (awḥā), weak-final. Bi-anna = \"because,\" giving the cause of the narration.", why:"Awḥā (the revelation word) rather than amara (commanded) — it dignifies the earth's testimony as divinely authorized speech.", note:"WAḤY = REVELATION — the word used for PROPHETS. And here it's used for the EARTH. The ground receives instructions.", more:["The word used for what comes to prophets!; the root carries speed and hiddenness (a message passed so fast no one else catches it).","Waḥy is the technical term for REVELATION to prophets. And here it's used for Allah communicating with the EARTH.","The Qurʾān uses the prophetic-revelation word for the ground. Which tells you the earth's testimony isn't guesswork or memory — it's commanded, directed, and authorized from above.","And the root's core sense — swift, subtle signalling — explains why you can't see it happening. The earth is receiving instructions right now, imperceptibly.","Note the preposition: awḥā LAHĀ (with li-), not awḥā ilayhā. Classical grammarians note this shade: li- can carry a sense of command/permission given TO something, slightly different from ilā (to someone as a recipient). Both are used in the Qurʾān.","And the bi-anna (\"because\") makes v.5 the reason for v.4: the earth speaks because it was told to. Not because it's angry, not because it's alive in a pagan sense — because it was given the order.","The root means swift, subtle signalling — which is why you can't see it."]},
  {ar:"يَصْدُرُ النَّاسُ أَشْتَاتًا", tr:"Yaṣduru'n-nāsu ashtātā", v:"6", root:"Yaṣduru — the \"issuing forth / proceeding out\" family — ṣadara = to come out, to proceed from.", meaning:"\"That Day people will issue forth, scattered in groups, to be shown their deeds.\"", magic:"Two words, two payoffs — and one of them closes a thread from Layl.", grammar:"Ashtātā is an accusative ḥāl (\"in a state of being scattered\").", why:"Ashtāt (scattered) rather than a word for ordered ranks — because the surah is about individual accounting, not collective.", note:"Layl 92:4: your EFFORTS are shattā (scattered). Zalzalah 99:6: YOU come out ashtātā (scattered).", more:["ṣadr = the CHEST / the FRONT of something (you own it from Sharḥ 94:1, nashraḥ laka ṣadrak); and maṣdar = A SOURCE (also the grammar term for a verbal noun — \"the thing everything issues from\"!).","ashtāt — the \"scattering / dispersal\" family — the plural of shatt.","FIRST — ashtātā. You own this root from Layl 92:4 — inna saʿyakum la-SHATTĀ (\"indeed, your efforts are SCATTERED in different directions\").","Layl 92:4 said your strivings go in scattered directions. Zalzalah 99:6 says on that Day you come out scattered. You scatter in life; you scatter at the exit. Same root, and the second is the consequence of the first.","SECOND — yaṣduru. The root of ṣadr (chest) and maṣdar (a SOURCE). The image is of people issuing from a source, like water from a spring — coming out in streams, not marching in formation.","And maṣdar is your free grammar hook: you already know it as the term for a verbal noun — \"the source from which verbs derive.\"","And note li-yurau aʿmālahum — \"to BE SHOWN their deeds\" — PASSIVE. They don't look; they are shown. No choosing what to review.","li-yurau is a passive subjunctive after the lām of purpose — note the dropped nūn.","Same root — the second follows from the first.","And maṣdar = A SOURCE (the grammar term!).","People issue forth like water from a spring.","And they are SHOWN their deeds — passive."]},
  {ar:"مِثْقَالَ ذَرَّةٍ", tr:"Mithqāla dharrah", v:"7, 8", root:"Mithqāl — a UNIT OF WEIGHT — the same root as athqāl in v.2! (thaqula = to be heavy).", meaning:"\"the weight of an atom / a mote.\"", magic:"The surah's masterstroke — and it's an internal root-echo across six verses.", grammar:"Mithqāla is accusative (object of yaʿmal); dharrah genitive by iḍāfah.", why:"Dharrah is the smallest unit the audience could picture, which makes the claim maximally comprehensive.", note:"Dharrah = AN ATOM in modern Arabic (al-qunbulah adh-dharriyyah). Classically: the dust in a sunbeam.", more:["dharrah — the tiniest possible particle — classically described as a speck of dust visible in a shaft of sunlight, or the smallest ant; modern Arabic: dharrah = AN ATOM, and dharrī = ATOMIC (al-qunbulah adh-dharriyyah = the atomic bomb!).","V.2 — the earth throws out its ATHQĀL — its GREATEST weights (the dead, the treasures, everything it has carried). v.7 — MITHQĀLA dharrah — the SMALLEST conceivable weight.","SAME ROOT, opposite ends of the scale. The surah opens by unloading mountains of weight and closes by weighing a speck of dust. And the message is: both are measured.","And dharrah = AN ATOM in modern Arabic — a free, unforgettable hook. Classical scholars described it as the dust you see floating in a sunbeam, which is the same idea: the smallest thing your eye can register.","The genius of the choice: a dharrah is not \"small\" — it's the threshold of visibility. Below that, you literally cannot see it. So the verse says: even what you couldn't see yourself doing is on the scale.","The mifʿāl pattern here names an instrument/unit of measure.","And v.2's ATHQĀL (greatest weights) vs v.7's MITHQĀL (smallest weight) — SAME ROOT, both ends of the scale."]},
  {ar:"خَيْرًا يَرَهُ / شَرًّا يَرَهُ", tr:"Khayran yarah / sharran yarah", v:"7, 8", root:"Khayr / sharr — good / evil — both IRREGULAR (serving as noun and comparative).", meaning:"\"So whoever does an atom's weight of good will SEE it. And whoever does an atom's weight of evil will SEE it.\"", magic:"The most perfectly balanced couplet in the juzʾ — and the verb choice is everything.", grammar:"Man = \"whoever\" (conditional), so yaʿmal and yarah are both jussive — note yarah has lost its final alif (from yarā).", why:"Yarah (SEE) rather than yujzā (be repaid) — because seeing your own deed is a more total accountability than being told about it.", note:"V.7 and v.8 are IDENTICAL except khayran / sharran.", more:["yarah — \"he will SEE it\" — from raʾā (to see).","IDENTICAL word for word — except khayran / sharran. The same technique as Layl 92:7/92:10 (yusrā / ʿusrā) — two verses that differ by one word.","And the verb: YARAH — \"he will SEE it.\" Not \"he will be told,\" not \"he will be judged,\" not \"he will be repaid.\" SEE it. With his own eyes.","And that closes a thread running through FOUR surahs: - Balad 90:7 — a-yaḥsabu an lam yarahu aḥad (\"does he think NO ONE SAW him?\") - ʿAlaq 96:14 — a-lam yaʿlam bi-anna'llāha YARĀ (\"does he not know that Allah SEES?\") - Zalzalah 99:6 — li-yurau aʿmālahum (\"to BE SHOWN their deeds\") - Zalzalah 99:7–8 — YARAH (\"he will SEE it\")","He thought no one saw (Balad). Allah sees (ʿAlaq). He is shown (99:6). And he sees it himself (99:7–8). Four surahs, one closing argument.","And note yarah is jussive with a short vowel — read carefully; the pronoun -hu becomes -h.","A conditional sentence: man + jussive + jussive.","Same technique as Layl 92:7/92:10. And the verb is YARAH — \"he will SEE it\" — not be told, not be judged.","FOUR surahs: Balad 90:7 (\"no one saw me\"), ʿAlaq 96:14 (\"Allah SEES\"), Zalzalah 99:6 (shown), 99:7–8 (he sees it)."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:100, name:"Al-ʿĀdiyāt", ar:"العاديات", en:"The Chargers", verses:11, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Five verses of a horse's total devotion, then one verse about human ingratitude. The animal shames the man.",
  special:["Five oaths in five verses — and every single one is a WAR HORSE in motion, described by a different sense.","V.1 — wa'l-ʿādiyāti ḍabḥā — SOUND: you hear them panting. v.2 — fa'l-mūriyāti qadḥā — SIGHT: you see sparks strike from their hooves. v.3 — fa'l-mughīrāti ṣubḥā — TIME: they raid at dawn. v.4 — fa-atharna bihi naqʿā — DUST: they kick up a cloud. v.5 — fa-wasaṭna bihi jamʿā — POSITION: they plunge into the middle of the enemy.","Five verses, and every one ends in a cognate accusative — ḍabḥan, qadḥan, ṣubḥan, naqʿan, jamʿan — Arabic's underline key, five times in a row.","And then v.6 lands: inna'l-insāna li-rabbihi la-KANŪD — \"indeed, man is ungrateful to his Lord.\"","The horse gives everything — breath, hooves, life — for a master it can't even understand. The human, who can understand, gives nothing. The oath IS the argument.","--"],
  big:["Five oaths (v.1–5) — a charging war horse, sense by sense.","Three charges (v.6–8) — ungrateful · a witness against himself · intensely loving wealth.","The reversal (v.9–11) — the graves emptied, the chests extracted, and their Lord fully informed."],
  traps:["فَأَثَرْنَ (v.4) — root th-w-r (to STIR UP — as in thawrah, a REVOLUTION). NOT ʾ-th-r (athar, a trace / āthara, to prefer — Nāziʿāt 79:38, Aʿlā 87:16). Two different roots that look alike.","الْخَيْرِ (v.8) — means WEALTH here, not \"good.\" A recognized Qurʾānic euphemism — the verse uses his own word for money.","صُبْحًا (v.3) — an ADVERB OF TIME (\"at dawn\"), NOT a cognate accusative like the other four, even though it matches the rhyme. A subtle break in the pattern.","كَنُودٌ (v.6) — from BARREN GROUND that takes rain and gives nothing back. Faʿūl intensive — a disposition, not a lapse.","حُصِّلَ (v.10) — means THRESHED — grain separated from chaff, not merely \"revealed.\" The real is separated from the performed.","لَّخَبِيرٌ (v.11) — khabīr ≠ ʿalīm. Khabīr = knowing the INSIDE (as in khibrah, expertise).","V.1–3 are PARTICIPLES; v.4–5 switch to FINITE VERBS — the grammar accelerates deliberately.","The fa's in v.2–5 — make the five oaths a SEQUENCE, not a list. Run → spark → raid → dust → plunge.","بُعْثِرَ (v.9) — a QUADRILITERAL root, like zalzala (99:1) and damdama (91:14). Four-letter roots = messy, tumbling action.","مَا فِي الْقُبُورِ (v.9) — \"WHAT is in the graves,\" not \"who.\" The wider word is deliberate."],
  links:[{w:"Five oaths + cognate accusatives (v.1–5)",e:"Nāziʿāt 79:1–5",t:"IDENTICAL opening architecture — five participles, no subject named"},{w:"صُبْحًا (v.3)",e:"وَالصُّبْحِ إِذَا تَنَفَّسَ (Takwīr 81:18)",t:"Two surahs swear by dawn — breathing / attacking"},{w:"فَأَثَرْنَ (v.4)",e:"آثَرَ (79:38) · تُؤْثِرُونَ (87:16)",t:"DIFFERENT ROOTS — th-w-r vs ʾ-th-r. A real trap"},{w:"جَمْعًا (v.5)",e:"نَجْمَعَ عِظَامَهُ (Qiyāmah 75:3)",t:"Same root — gathering"},{w:"لَكَنُودٌ (v.6)",e:"كَفُورًا (Insān 76:3)",t:"Same faʿūl intensive — Insān offered two roads; this says which one man takes"},{w:"لَشَهِيدٌ (v.7)",e:"بَصِيرَةٌ (Qiyāmah 75:14)",t:"Two surahs: you're your OWN witness"},{w:"لَشَهِيدٌ (v.7)",e:"شَاهِدٍ وَمَشْهُودٍ (Burūj 85:3)",t:"Same root — witnessing"},{w:"لَشَدِيدٌ (v.8)",e:"شِدَادًا (78:12) · شَدَدْنَا (76:28) · لَشَدِيدٌ (85:12)",t:"FOUR firmnesses: the sky, your bones, His grip, your grip on money"},{w:"حُبِّ الْخَيْرِ (v.8)",e:"تُحِبُّونَ الْمَالَ حُبًّا جَمًّا (Fajr 89:20)",t:"Fajr: how MUCH. ʿĀdiyāt: how HARD."},{w:"بُعْثِرَ... الْقُبُورِ (v.9)",e:"بُعْثِرَتْ (Infiṭār 82:4) · أَقْبَرَهُ (80:21)",t:"Same rare quadriliteral — one layer deeper"},{w:"الصُّدُورِ (v.10)",e:"صَدْرَكَ (Sharḥ 94:1)",t:"Expanded in mercy / extracted in judgment"},{w:"لَّخَبِيرٌ (v.11)",e:"أَخْبَارَهَا (Zalzalah 99:4)",t:"Same root, BACK-TO-BACK surahs"},{w:"إِنَّ... لَـ (v.6, 7, 8, 11)",e:"Throughout the juzʾ",t:"FOUR double-emphasis constructions in one short surah"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"الْعَادِيَاتِ ضَبْحًا", tr:"Al-ʿādiyāti ḍabḥā", v:"1", root:"ʿādiyāt — the \"running fast / exceeding\" family — ʿadā = to run, to gallop.", meaning:"\"By the chargers that pant.\"", magic:"The surah opens with a SOUND, and it's a very specific one.", grammar:"Active participle, feminine plural (fāʿilāt pattern) — the described noun (al-khayl, horses) is DELETED, as in Nāziʿāt.", why:"ḍabḥ is chosen because it can only describe extreme exertion — the horse is already giving everything in verse one.", note:"Ḍabḥ = the RASPING PANT of a horse running beyond comfort. You hear it.", more:["And ʿadwān = transgression (running past your limits!); ʿadū = an ENEMY (one who runs against you); modern ʿadw = running/jogging.","ḍabḥ — the SOUND a horse makes when galloping hard — a heavy, rasping pant through the nostrils.","Ḍabḥ is not \"breathing.\" It's the particular rasping, forced panting of an animal running beyond comfort. The word exists for almost nothing else.","Which means the first thing the surah does is put you close enough to hear the horse's lungs. Not \"horses that run\" — horses running so hard you can hear them.","And the ʿ-d-w root's other meanings are rich: ʿadū = an ENEMY (the one who runs at you) and ʿudwān = aggression (running past the boundary). Running, hostility, and transgression share three letters.","Compare the oath-structure to Nāziʿāt 79:1–5 — five feminine plural participles, five cognate accusatives, no subject named. The two surahs use the identical opening architecture. Nāziʿāt's five were angels; ʿĀdiyāt's five are horses.","Ḍabḥan is a maf'ūl muṭlaq (cognate accusative).","And ʿadū = AN ENEMY; ʿudwān = aggression — all from \"running.\" Same five-oath architecture as Nāziʿāt 79:1–5."]},
  {ar:"الْمُورِيَاتِ قَدْحًا", tr:"Al-mūriyāti qadḥā", v:"2", root:"Mūriyāt — Form IV of warā = to strike fire, to produce a spark; wariyah = the tinder or flint used to start fire.", meaning:"\"and those that strike sparks [with their hooves].\"", magic:"From sound (v.1) to SIGHT (v.2) — and the image is night-time.", grammar:"Form IV participle, feminine plural. Second cognate accusative in two verses.", why:"Qadḥ (flint-striking) rather than a word for fire — it names the friction, which is what effort looks like.", note:"Hooves striking rock throw SPARKS — which you can only see at NIGHT. So v.2 = night, v.3 = dawn. They ran all night.", more:["qadḥ — the ACT of striking flint to produce a spark; qadaḥa = to strike sparks.","Iron-shod hooves striking rock at full gallop throw sparks. You only see that in darkness.","Which sets up v.3 perfectly: v.2 is night (you can see the sparks), v.3 is dawn (ṣubḥan). The horses have been running all night and arrive at daybreak. The chronology is embedded in the imagery.","And qadḥ — the flint-strike — is the sound-and-light of effort against stone. The horse is striking fire out of the ground with its feet.","Note the particle shift: v.1 begins with wa (the oath-wāw); v.2–5 all begin with fa. The fa means \"and then immediately\" — so the five oaths are a sequence, not a list. Run → spark → raid → dust → plunge. Same technique as Nāziʿāt 79:4–5.","And the fa's in v.2–5 make it a SEQUENCE, not a list."]},
  {ar:"الْمُغِيرَاتِ صُبْحًا", tr:"Al-mughīrāti ṣubḥā", v:"3", root:"Mughīrāt — Form IV of ghāra = to raid, to make a sudden attack; ghārah = A RAID.", meaning:"\"and those that raid at dawn.\"", magic:"The timing is historically exact, and you pray at that hour.", grammar:"Form IV participle, feminine plural.", why:"ṣubḥ (the everyday word for morning) rather than a poetic term — it grounds an epic scene in a familiar hour.", note:"Ṣalāt aṣ-ṢUBḤ — you pray at this exact hour. Dawn raids were standard Arab warfare.", more:["And the related ghayr = other/different (a raid comes from outside, from the other).","ṣubḥ — early morning / daybreak — Ṣalāt aṣ-Ṣubḥ!","And aṣbaḥa = to become/to enter the morning.","Dawn raids were standard Arab warfare — you travel at night and strike when there's just enough light to see, before the camp is awake.","And ṣubḥ is the word you use every day for Fajr prayer time. Free hook.","Note what the surah has done in three verses: it has built a complete military operation — the exhausting night ride (v.1), the sparks in the dark (v.2), the dawn strike (v.3) — without narrating anything. Just three oaths.","And compare: Takwīr 81:18 — wa'ṣ-ṣubḥi idhā tanaffas (\"and by the morning when it BREATHES\"). Two surahs swear by the dawn: one where it breathes gently, one where it's the hour of attack.","Ṣubḥan here is an accusative adverb of time (\"at dawn\") — NOT a cognate accusative like the others, even though it matches the rhyme.","A subtle variation in an otherwise uniform pattern.","Three verses build a whole battle without narrating anything.","And ṣubḥan is an adverb of time, not a cognate accusative — a subtle break in the pattern."]},
  {ar:"فَأَثَرْنَ بِهِ نَقْعًا", tr:"Fa-atharna bihi naqʿā", v:"4", root:"Atharna — Form IV of thāra = to stir up, to raise, to kick up; DIFFERENT ROOT from athar (a trace).", meaning:"\"and stir up thereby a cloud of dust.\"", magic:"The root is one you know from the news, and the image is what a whole army looks like from a distance.", grammar:"NOTE THE SHIFT: v.1–3 were participles (al-ʿādiyāt, al-mūriyāt, al-mughīrāt).", why:"Naqʿ is the visible consequence of everything in v.1–3 — the proof from far away.", note:"Thawrah = A REVOLUTION (a \"stirring up\"). Same root as atharna.", more:["This is th-w-r, the root of thawrah = A REVOLUTION!","naqʿ — DUST raised in the air — a cloud of it.","Thāra → thawrah = A REVOLUTION — literally \"an uprising, a stirring-up.\" Modern Arabic uses it constantly. Free hook.","CAREFUL — this is NOT the athar (trace/footprint) root you own from Nāziʿāt 79:38 (wa āthara'l-ḥayāta'd-dunyā) and Aʿlā 87:16 (tuʾthirūna). Those are ʾ-th-r; this is th-w-r. They look similar and mean completely different things. A real trap.","And naqʿ (a raised dust-cloud) is what you see before you see the army. From a distance, a cavalry charge is a cloud on the horizon.","Note the bihi (\"thereby / with it\") in v.4 and v.5 — the \"it\" is unstated: the place, the time, or the raid itself. Classical readings differ; the ambiguity keeps the scene fast-moving.","V.4–5 switch to finite verbs (atharna, wasaṭna) with the feminine plural nūn.","The grammar accelerates — from describing them to watching them act.","NOT the same root as āthara (to prefer, Nāziʿāt 79:38) — that's ʾ-th-r, this is th-w-r.","And v.4–5 switch from PARTICIPLES to VERBS — the scene speeds up."]},
  {ar:"فَوَسَطْنَ بِهِ جَمْعًا", tr:"Fa-wasaṭna bihi jamʿā", v:"5", root:"Wasaṭna — the \"middle\" family — wasaṭ = the middle/centre; ummatan wasaṭā = a middle/balanced community.", meaning:"\"and penetrate thereby into the midst of a gathering [of the enemy].\"", magic:"The fifth and final oath, and it's the climax of the charge.", grammar:"Finite verb with feminine plural nūn (wasaṭna), matching atharna. Fifth cognate accusative (jamʿan).", why:"Wasaṭ (the centre) rather than a word for \"attacked\" — because how deep it went is the measure of the devotion.", note:"Jumuʿah = the day of GATHERING; masjid jāmiʿ = where people GATHER. Same root.", more:["And wasaṭa = to penetrate into the middle of something.","jamʿ — a gathering, a massed group — the same root as yawm al-jamʿ, and jāmiʿ (a congregational mosque!), and jumuʿah.","Wasaṭna = they reached the very CENTRE — not the edge, not the flank. The deepest, most dangerous point of the enemy formation.","And that completes the horse's devotion: it ran all night (v.1), struck sparks (v.2), raided at dawn (v.3), raised the dust (v.4), and plunged into the middle of the enemy (v.5).","The horse doesn't understand the cause. It doesn't get the spoils. It won't be thanked. And it goes to the centre anyway. Then v.6 says: man is ungrateful to his Lord.","And jamʿ is your everyday word: jumuʿah (the day of gathering), masjid jāmiʿ (the mosque where people gather), jamīʿ (all/altogether). Free hook.","Compare Qiyāmah 75:3 — allan najmaʿa ʿiẓāmah (\"that We will not gather his bones\"). Same root: gathering enemies, gathering bones.","And wasaṭna = they reached the CENTRE — the most dangerous point.","The horse understands nothing, gets nothing, and goes to the middle anyway."]},
  {ar:"لَكَنُودٌ", tr:"La-kanūd", v:"6", root:"The \"withholding good / being stubbornly ungrateful\" family — kanada = to deny a favour, to be miserly with thanks.", meaning:"\"Indeed, man is ungrateful to his Lord.\"", magic:"The word is an INTENSIVE, and the barren-ground image is the key.", grammar:"Inna + emphatic lām = double emphasis. Faʿūl intensive pattern. Fronting of li-rabbihi = emphasis.", why:"Kanūd over kāfir — it names unproductiveness, not hostility, which is a more accurate and more damning charge.", note:"Kanūd = BARREN GROUND that takes rain and gives NOTHING BACK.", more:["Classical lexicographers connect kanūd to barren ground that grows nothing — land that takes the rain and gives back nothing.","Kanūd is on the faʿūl intensive pattern — the same shape as kafūr (Insān 76:3), ghafūr, shakūr. It means habitually, characteristically ungrateful — not a lapse but a disposition.","And the barren-land connection makes it unforgettable: a kanūd is ground that receives rain and yields nothing. It's not hostile — it's just unproductive.","NOW PUT IT AGAINST THE HORSE. v.1–5: an animal that gives everything — its breath, its hooves, its life — for a master whose purposes it cannot comprehend. v.6: a human who receives everything and returns nothing, to a Lord he can comprehend.","The five oaths weren't scene-setting. They were the prosecution's exhibit.","And you own the sibling word from Insān 76:3 — immā shākiran wa immā kafūrā (\"either grateful or utterly ungrateful\"). Insān offered two roads; ʿĀdiyāt says which one man takes.","Note li-rabbihi is FRONTED: inna'l-insāna LI-RABBIHI la-kanūd — \"TO HIS LORD — ungrateful.\" The one he's ungrateful to is spotlighted first.","The horse gives everything for a master it can't understand; man receives everything and returns nothing to a Lord he CAN understand.","That's why the five oaths are there.","And Insān 76:3 offered the two roads: shākir or kafūr."]},
  {ar:"لَشَهِيدٌ", tr:"La-shahīd", v:"7", root:"The \"witnessing / being present\" family — shahida = to witness, to be present.", meaning:"\"And indeed, he is a witness to that.\"", magic:"\"a witness to that\" — to WHAT? And WHO is the witness? Both are debated, and both readings are devastating.", grammar:"Inna + emphatic lām again — three verses in a row (v.6, 7, 8) all use inna + lām. Triple hammer.", why:"Shahīd (one present at the scene) rather than ʿālim (one who knows) — presence is harder to deny than knowledge.", note:"You say the SHAHĀDAH — the TESTIMONY — daily. Same root. Qiyāmah 75:14: man is a BAṢĪRAH against himself.", more:["Shahādah = TESTIMONY (and the shahādah you declare!); shahīd = a witness AND a martyr; mashhad = a scene; and shahr = a month (the sighted, witnessed crescent!).","READING ONE (most common): man is a witness against himself. He KNOWS he's ungrateful. It isn't ignorance — he can testify to his own ingratitude if asked.","READING TWO: Allah is witness to man's ingratitude.","The first reading connects to a thread you own: - Qiyāmah 75:14 — bali'l-insānu ʿalā nafsihi BAṢĪRAH (\"man is a clear witness against himself\") - ʿĀdiyāt 100:7 — wa innahu ʿalā dhālika la-SHAHĪD (\"he is a witness to that\")","Two surahs, two words (baṣīrah = insight-witness; shahīd = present-witness), one claim: you don't need a prosecutor. You already know.","And you own shahīd from Burūj 85:3, 9 (wa shāhidin wa mashhūd, and wa'llāhu ʿalā kulli shayʾin shahīd).","The free hook: you say the shahādah — the testimony — every day. Same root.","ʿĀdiyāt 100:7: he is a SHAHĪD to it.","Two surahs: you're your own witness.","And v.6, 7, 8 ALL use inna + emphatic lām."]},
  {ar:"لِحُبِّ الْخَيْرِ لَشَدِيدٌ", tr:"Li-ḥubbi'l-khayri la-shadīd", v:"8", root:"ḥubb — love (you own it from Fajr 89:20, wa tuḥibbūna'l-māla ḥubban jammā).", meaning:"\"And indeed, he is intense in his love of wealth.\"", magic:"Two things: the euphemism, and the choice of shadīd.", grammar:"Li-ḥubbi'l-khayr is fronted before la-shadīd — spotlighting the object of the intensity.", why:"Shadīd (firm/intense) rather than kathīr (much) — it describes the grip, not the quantity.", note:"Al-khayr HERE = WEALTH, not \"good.\" The verse uses his own word for money.", more:["khayr — here it means WEALTH/good things (a recognized Qurʾānic usage — \"the good\" as a euphemism for money!).","shadīd — the \"firm / intense / severe\" family — you own it from Nabaʾ 78:12 (sabʿan shidādā), Insān 76:28 (shadadnā asrahum), Burūj 85:12 (inna baṭsha rabbika la-shadīd).","Al-khayr here means WEALTH, not \"good.\" A recognized Qurʾānic usage — money is called \"the good\" because that's what people call it.","And that's the quiet sting: the surah uses his own word for it. He calls money \"the good\" — so the verse does too, and lets the irony sit.","And shadīd is the stunning part. You own this root from three places, and look where: - Nabaʾ 78:12 — seven shidād heavens (FIRMLY built) - Insān 76:28 — we shadadnā their frame (your skeleton, tightly knitted) - Burūj 85:12 — your Lord's grip is shadīd (severe) - ʿĀdiyāt 100:8 — he is shadīd in loving wealth","The same word describes: the firmness of the sky, the firmness of your bones, the severity of divine grip — and the intensity of your grip on money. Four firmnesses. Three are His. One is yours, and it's aimed at cash.","Compare Fajr 89:20 — tuḥibbūna'l-māla ḥubban jammā (\"you love wealth with an overflowing love\"). Fajr says how much; ʿĀdiyāt says how hard.","Third inna + lām in three verses.","And shadīd: the SKY is firm (78:12), your BONES are firm (76:28), His GRIP is firm (85:12) — and your grip on MONEY is firm (100:8). Four firmnesses; only one is yours."]},
  {ar:"بُعْثِرَ مَا فِي الْقُبُورِ", tr:"Buʿthira mā fi'l-qubūr", v:"9", root:"Buʿthira — a QUADRILITERAL root (ب-ع-ث-ر) meaning to overturn, to turn inside out, to scatter the contents.", meaning:"\"when what is in the graves is overturned.\"", magic:"You own this verse ALMOST VERBATIM from Infiṭār 82:4.", grammar:"Quadriliteral passive.", why:"Buʿthira conveys total emptying and disorder — the opposite of a dignified resting place.", note:"Infiṭār 82:4: the GRAVES are overturned. ʿĀdiyāt 100:9: WHAT IS IN the graves is overturned. Same rare verb, one layer deeper.", more:["Like tipping a container upside down and shaking it.","Related to baʿatha = to raise up / resurrect.","qubūr — graves (you own aqbarah from ʿAbasa 80:21 — \"He GAVE him a grave\").","Infiṭār 82:4 — wa idha'l-qubūru BUʿTHIRAT (\"when the graves are overturned\"). ʿĀdiyāt 100:9 — idhā BUʿTHIRA mā fi'l-QUBŪR (\"when what is IN the graves is overturned\").","Same rare quadriliteral verb, same noun, two surahs. But note the difference: Infiṭār overturns the graves; ʿĀdiyāt overturns what is in them. ʿĀdiyāt goes one layer deeper.","And buʿthira is precisely the verb: not \"opened\" but tipped over and emptied, like a bag shaken out. Nothing left behind, nothing neat.","And the ʿAbasa payoff still applies: ʿAbasa 80:21 listed being given a grave among Allah's blessings. Here that gift is turned upside down.","Four-letter roots in Arabic often describe messy, tumbling, repeated actions — compare zalzala (99:1), damdama (91:14), waswasa.","Mā fi'l-qubūr (\"what is in the graves\") is the subject-substitute — note it says \"what,\" not \"who,\" which widens it.","And ʿAbasa 80:21 listed being GIVEN a grave as a BLESSING.","Picture a bag tipped over and shaken empty."]},
  {ar:"وَحُصِّلَ مَا فِي الصُّدُورِ", tr:"Wa ḥuṣṣila mā fi'ṣ-ṣudūr", v:"10", root:"ḥuṣṣila — the \"extracting the essence / obtaining the final result\" family — ḥaṣala = to result, to be obtained.", meaning:"\"and what is in the breasts is extracted / brought out.\"", magic:"The grain-and-chaff image is the whole verse, and the pairing with v.9 is perfect.", grammar:"Form II passive (ḥuṣṣila) — intensive + no doer named.", why:"ḥaṣṣala (thresh out the essence) rather than ubdiya (revealed) — because the issue is separating the real from the performed.", note:"Ḥaṣṣala = TO THRESH — separate the GRAIN from the CHAFF. And taḥṣīl = ATTAINMENT in modern Arabic.", more:["Taḥṣīl = ACQUISITION / attainment (modern Arabic: taḥṣīl ʿilmī = educational attainment; taḥṣīl = collecting/extracting); the root originally refers to separating the grain from the chaff — extracting what's valuable from what's mixed in.","ṣudūr — CHESTS (you own ṣadr from Sharḥ 94:1, a-lam nashraḥ laka ṣadrak).","V.9 — the GRAVES are overturned → what's in the GROUND comes out (bodies). v.10 — the CHESTS are extracted → what's in the HEART comes out (intentions).","Two containers, two excavations. The earth gives up its dead; the chest gives up its secrets. And neither is voluntary.","And ḥaṣṣala is far more precise than \"revealed.\" The root means separating out the essence — threshing grain, so the valuable kernel is separated from the husk.","So it isn't that your chest is opened — it's that your chest is THRESHED, and what was actually in there is separated out from everything you pretended.","And taḥṣīl = ATTAINMENT/acquisition in modern Arabic — your free hook. What you actually acquired gets extracted.","And the Sharḥ connection is lovely: Sharḥ 94:1 — your ṣadr was EXPANDED as a gift. ʿĀdiyāt 100:10 — every ṣadr is EXTRACTED. Same word, one opened in mercy, one opened in judgment.","Note the deliberate parallel with buʿthira: two passives, two mā fi'l-… phrases.","v.9: the GROUND gives up bodies.","v.10: the CHEST gives up intentions.","And Sharḥ 94:1 EXPANDED the ṣadr as a gift; here every ṣadr is EXTRACTED."]},
  {ar:"لَّخَبِيرٌ", tr:"La-khabīr", v:"11", root:"The \"deep, inside knowledge\" family — khabara = to know something by testing and experiencing it from within.", meaning:"\"Indeed, their Lord, that Day, is fully Acquainted with them.\"", magic:"The final word, and the root is precisely chosen.", grammar:"Inna + emphatic lām — the FOURTH in this surah (v.6, 7, 8, 11). Faʿīl intensive adjective pattern.", why:"Khabīr (inside-knowledge) rather than ʿalīm — because the verse before it opened people's insides.", note:"Khibrah = EXPERTISE — knowledge from being INSIDE something. Khabīr ≠ ʿalīm: He knows the inside, not just the facts.", more:["Khabar = news/a report (you own akhbārahā from Zalzalah 99:4 — the surah right before!); khibrah = EXPERTISE / practical experience; and al-Khabīr — a NAME OF ALLAH.","Khabīr is NOT the same as ʿalīm. ʿAlīm = knowing (all knowledge). Khabīr = knowing the INSIDE of a thing — its hidden reality, learned by penetration.","And khibrah in modern Arabic = EXPERTISE, the knowledge you only get from being inside a field. Free hook.","So why khabīr here? Because v.10 just said the chests are threshed open. The verse pairs the extraction of the inside with the One who knows insides. Perfect fit.","And the consecutive-surah link is beautiful: Zalzalah 99:4 — tuḥaddithu AKHBĀRAHĀ (the earth narrates its NEWS) ʿĀdiyāt 100:11 — inna rabbahum bihim yawmaʾidhin la-KHABĪR (their Lord is fully ACQUAINTED)","SAME ROOT, back-to-back surahs. The earth gives its report (khabar) — and the One receiving it already has full inside-knowledge (khabīr). The testimony confirms what He already knew.","And note bihim is FRONTED: inna rabbahum BIHIM yawmaʾidhin la-khabīr — \"about THEM — fully acquainted.\"","And Zalzalah 99:4 (akhbārahā, the earth's NEWS) → ʿĀdiyāt 100:11 (khabīr, fully ACQUAINTED).","Same root, back-to-back surahs."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:101, name:"Al-Qāriʿah", ar:"القارعة", en:"The Striking Calamity", verses:11, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"A surah that repeats its own name three times before explaining it — because the name is a SOUND, and sounds repeat.",
  special:["It asks \"what is it?\" THREE TIMES in the first three verses — and one of those verses is just two words.","V.1: al-Qāriʿah — one word. A noun standing alone. v.2: mā l-Qāriʿah — \"What is the Striker?\" v.3: wa mā adrāka mā l-Qāriʿah — \"And what could convey to you what the Striker is?\"","Three verses, the same word three times, and no answer yet. The surah names it, questions it, then says the question can't be answered — and only then describes it.","And then, at the very end, it does it AGAIN: v.10 — wa mā adrāka mā hiyah? (\"And what could convey to you what IT is?\") before the final answer in v.11.","Two unanswerable questions bracket the surah. One at the start about the Day; one at the end about the destination.","--"],
  big:["The name, three times (v.1–3) — the Striker. What is the Striker? What could tell you?","Two similes (v.4–5) — people like scattered moths, mountains like carded wool.","The scales (v.6–9) — heavy scales → a pleasing life; light scales → his mother is the Abyss.","The second question, and the answer (v.10–11) — a scorching Fire."],
  traps:["هَاوِيَةٌ (v.9) — SAME ROOT as al-hawā (DESIRE) in Nāziʿāt 79:40. Both mean \"to FALL.\" Desire is named after falling; the Abyss is where the fall ends.","رَّاضِيَةٍ (v.7) — it's an ACTIVE participle: \"a life that is PLEASED,\" not \"pleasing to him.\" A majāz ʿaqlī — the life itself is content.","أُمُّهُ (v.9) — means \"his MOTHER\", read as where you run for refuge or the source you return to. Not a copying error — the dissonance is deliberate.","مَوَازِينُهُ (v.6, 8) — PLURAL (\"scaleS\"). The accounting is itemized, not lumped. And note it takes feminine singular agreement (thaqulat, khaffat) — the broken-plural rule.","مَا هِيَهْ (v.10) — the final hāʾ is a hāʾ as-sakt (a PAUSE-hāʾ), not a pronoun. It exists purely for the voice to land.","الْمَبْثُوثِ (v.4) — IDENTICAL to Ghāshiyah 88:16's mabthūthah — luxurious carpets there, terrified people here. Same word, opposite scenes.","فَرَاشِ (v.4) — MOTHS, chosen for BEHAVIOUR (swarming toward flame), not appearance. And firāsh = A BED (same root, \"spread out\").","الْمَنفُوشِ (v.5) — CARDED wool — a solid mass pulled apart into fibres, not merely \"crushed.\"","الْقَارِعَةُ (v.1) — v.1 is a ONE-WORD verse whose predicate arrives later. A suspense construction.","V.11 is deliberately PLAIN — after asking \"what could convey it to you?\", the answer is two simple words. The restraint is the rhetoric."],
  links:[{w:"الْقَارِعَةُ (v.1–3)",e:"النَّبَإ (78:2) · الطَّامَّةُ (79:34) · الصَّاخَّةُ (80:33) · الْغَاشِيَةِ (88:1)",t:"FIVE names for the Day, five sensations"},{w:"وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ (v.3, 10)",e:"82:17–18 · 83:8, 19 · 86:2 · 90:12 · 97:2",t:"SEVEN surahs — and Qāriʿah is the only one that brackets with it"},{w:"الْمَبْثُوثِ (v.4)",e:"زَرَابِيُّ مَبْثُوثَةٌ (Ghāshiyah 88:16)",t:"IDENTICAL word — carpets strewn / people scattered"},{w:"كَالْعِهْنِ الْمَنفُوشِ (v.5)",e:"سَرَابًا (78:20) · سُيِّرَتْ (81:3) · دَكًّا دَكًّا (89:21)",t:"FIVE mountain-fates across five surahs"},{w:"ثَقُلَتْ (v.6)",e:"أَثْقَالَهَا (99:2) · مِثْقَالَ (99:7) · ثَقِيلًا (76:27)",t:"Zalzalah MEASURES; Qāriʿah WEIGHS — consecutive surahs"},{w:"عِيشَةٍ (v.7)",e:"مَعَاشًا (Nabaʾ 78:11)",t:"Same root — dunya's working-life / the life that pleases"},{w:"رَّاضِيَةٍ (v.7)",e:"88:9 · 89:28 · 92:21 · 93:5 · 98:8",t:"SEVEN surahs — the most consistent reward-word"},{w:"هَاوِيَةٌ (v.9)",e:"الْهَوَىٰ (Nāziʿāt 79:40)",t:"SAME ROOT — desire is named after falling; the Abyss is where it lands"},{w:"أُمُّهُ (v.9)",e:"",t:"The warmest word in the language, for the coldest place"},{w:"نَارٌ حَامِيَةٌ (v.11)",e:"نَارًا حَامِيَةً (Ghāshiyah 88:4)",t:"IDENTICAL phrase"},{w:"حَامِيَةٌ (v.11)",e:"حَمِيمًا (Nabaʾ 78:25)",t:"Same root — and ḥamīm = a close friend too"},{w:"فَأَمَّا... وَأَمَّا (v.6–9)",e:"79:37–41 · 84:7–12 · 92:5–10 · 93:9–11",t:"The two-panel frame, five surahs"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"الْقَارِعَةُ", tr:"Al-Qāriʿah", v:"1, 2, 3", root:"The \"striking with a loud bang / knocking violently\" family — qaraʿa = to strike something so it makes a noise.", meaning:"\"The Striker / The Crashing Blow.\"", magic:"Another NAME for the Day — and each surah picks a different sense.", grammar:"Active participle used as a NAME, feminine (the noun as-sāʿah is deleted).", why:"Qāriʿah over a word for destruction — because it names the impact on you, not the event itself.", note:"Qarʿ al-bāb = A KNOCK ON THE DOOR. Same root.", more:["Qarʿ al-bāb = KNOCKING ON A DOOR (still everyday Arabic!); miqraʿah = a mallet; and the root carries the idea of a blow that shocks and startles.","You now own FIVE: - Nabaʾ 78:2 — an-Nabaʾ — THE NEWS (what you hear about) - Nāziʿāt 79:34 — aṭ-Ṭāmmah — THE OVERWHELMING FLOOD (what submerges you) - ʿAbasa 80:33 — aṣ-Ṣākhkhah — THE DEAFENER (what destroys hearing) - Ghāshiyah 88:1 — al-Ghāshiyah — THE ENVELOPER (what covers you) - Qāriʿah 101:1 — al-Qāriʿah — THE STRIKER (what hits and startles)","Five surahs, five names, five sensations. And al-Qāriʿah is the most sudden of them: a qarʿ is a single shocking blow, not a process.","And the everyday hook is perfect: qarʿ al-bāb = a KNOCK ON THE DOOR. You hear one every week. That's the root — something striking a surface and making a noise you can't ignore.","And now the repetition makes sense: a qarʿ is a SOUND. Sounds repeat, echo, knock again. Saying al-Qāriʿah three times in three verses is the word knocking.","v.1 is a single-word verse — a mubtadaʾ whose predicate arrives later (a known construction for creating suspense).","FIVE names for the Day: the News (78:2), the Flood (79:34), the Deafener (80:33), the Enveloper (88:1), the STRIKER (101:1). And it's repeated 3× because a qarʿ is a SOUND — and sounds knock again."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ", tr:"Wa mā adrāka", v:"3, 10", root:"The \"knowing by perception\" family — darā = to become aware; Form IV adrā = to make someone know.", meaning:"\"And what could convey to you what the Striker is?\" (v.3) and \"...what it is?\" (v.10)", magic:"You own this formula across SEVEN surahs now — and Qāriʿah is the only one that uses it TWICE as a frame.", grammar:"Note the TWO mā's (first interrogative, second introducing the subject-clause).", why:"The doubled framing makes the whole surah an admission of inadequacy — which is more effective than any description.", note:"SEVEN surahs use this formula. adrāka (past) = an answer follows. yudrīka (present, ʿAbasa 80:3) = no answer follows.", more:["- Infiṭār 82:17–18 (twice, consecutive) · Muṭaffifīn 83:8, 19 · Ṭāriq 86:2 · Balad 90:12 · Qadr 97:2 · Qāriʿah 101:3, 10","And the adrāka / yudrīka distinction stays crucial: - mā YUDRĪKA (present) — ʿAbasa 80:3 — \"you couldn't know\" — NO answer follows. - mā ADRĀKA (past) — \"what could convey it to you?\" — an answer DOES follow.","What makes Qāriʿah unique: it uses adrāka at the START (v.3, about the Day) and at the END (v.10, about the destination). Two brackets.","And notice what's between them: the entire content of the surah sits inside two admissions that no description is adequate.","V.10 has an unusual feature: mā hiyah — with a hāʾ as-sakt (a \"pause hāʾ\") added to hiya. It exists purely to let the voice land at the end of the verse. A rare, beautiful recitation feature.","And hāʾ as-sakt in v.10 — a silent/pausal hāʾ, not a pronoun.","Qāriʿah uses it TWICE — at the start (the Day) and at the end (the destination).","And mā hiyah has a hāʾ as-sakt — a pause-hāʾ, not a pronoun."]},
  {ar:"كَالْفَرَاشِ الْمَبْثُوثِ", tr:"Ka'l-farāshi'l-mabthūth", v:"4", root:"Farāsh — MOTHS / small fluttering insects that swarm toward a flame — from farasha = to spread out (firāsh = A BED / bedding, something spread!).", meaning:"\"the Day people will be like scattered moths.\"", magic:"The simile is chosen for behaviour, not just appearance — and that's what makes it devastating.", grammar:"The ka- is kāf at-tashbīh (the \"like\" of simile).", why:"Farāsh names a creature defined by moving urgently toward its own destruction — the exact human pattern the juzʾ has described.", note:"Farāsh = MOTHS — which swarm TOWARD THE FLAME. Agitated, numerous, directionless, heading for what destroys them.", more:["mabthūth — the \"scattering / spreading widely\" family — batha = to strew, disperse.","Why farāsh (moths) specifically? Because of what moths DO: they swarm toward the fire, in chaotic, aimless, colliding motion — each one moving, none of them going anywhere on purpose.","So the simile says: on that Day people will move like moths around a flame — agitated, numerous, directionless, and heading toward the very thing that destroys them.","And mabthūth — you own it EXACTLY from Ghāshiyah 88:16 — wa zarābiyyu mabthūthah (\"and carpets spread all around\").","SAME WORD, two surahs, opposite scenes: carpets generously strewn in Paradise (88:16), and people chaotically scattered on Judgment Day (101:4). The same word for luxurious abundance and for terrified dispersal.","And the free hook: firāsh = A BED — something spread out. Same root as the moth.","Mabthūth is a passive participle — they are scattered, not scattering themselves.","And mabthūth is IDENTICAL to Ghāshiyah 88:16 — carpets generously strewn in Paradise / people chaotically scattered here.","And firāsh = A BED (spread out)."]},
  {ar:"كَالْعِهْنِ الْمَنفُوشِ", tr:"Ka'l-ʿihni'l-manfūsh", v:"5", root:"ʿihn — WOOL — specifically DYED, coloured wool.", meaning:"\"and the mountains will be like carded wool.\"", magic:"The most precise destruction-image in the juzʾ — and every detail is doing work.", grammar:"Second kāf at-tashbīh. Manfūsh is a passive participle — the mountains are carded; someone does it to them.", why:"Manfūsh (carded) rather than \"crushed\" — because carding is a craft word.", note:"Manfūsh = CARDED WOOL — a solid mass pulled apart into loose floating fibres.", more:["manfūsh — the \"teasing apart / carding / fluffing\" family — nafasha = to card wool, to pull a compact mass apart into loose, floating fibres.","THREE things this simile achieves at once: - WEIGHT: a mountain is the heaviest thing you know; carded wool is the lightest. Maximum reversal. - COHESION: carding means pulling a solid mass apart into separate fibres. The mountain doesn't just get light — it comes apart at the fibre level. - COLOUR: ʿihn means DYED wool — which some classical commentators connect to the multi-coloured appearance of mountains (rock strata are genuinely banded). So the image may be visually exact too.","And compare the mountain-fates you now own: - Nabaʾ 78:20 — fa-kānat sarābā (a MIRAGE — visible but not there) - Takwīr 81:3 — suyyirat (set MOVING) - Fajr 89:21 — dukkat… dakkan dakkā (POUNDED flat) - Ghāshiyah 88:19 — nuṣibat (SET UP firmly — before the Day) - Qāriʿah 101:5 — manfūsh (CARDED into floating fluff)","Five surahs, five ways mountains end. This is the gentlest-sounding and the most total.","It sounds almost gentle — which makes the totality more unsettling.","Heaviest thing → lightest thing, AND it comes apart at the fibre level.","ʿIhn = DYED wool (mountains really are banded!).","FIVE mountain-fates: mirage (78:20), moving (81:3), pounded (89:21), carded (101:5)."]},
  {ar:"ثَقُلَتْ / خَفَّتْ مَوَازِينُهُ", tr:"Thaqulat / khaffat mawāzīnuh", v:"6, 8", root:"Thaqulat — the \"heaviness\" family — you own it from Insān 76:27 (yawman thaqīlā) and Zalzalah 99:2 (athqālahā) and 99:7 (mithqāla dharrah).", meaning:"\"As for one whose scales are heavy… and as for one whose scales are light…\"", magic:"A perfect muqābala, and the connection to the previous surah is exact.", grammar:"Fa-ammā X fa-Y = \"as for X, then Y\" — a standard two-panel construction.", why:"Mīzān (a physical balance) rather than an abstract word for judgment — it makes accountability mechanical and visible.", note:"Mīzān = a WEIGHING SCALE (the everyday word). Zalzalah 99:7: an atom's weight is SEEN. Qāriʿah 101:6: the atoms are WEIGHED.", more:["khaffat — the \"lightness\" family — khaffa = to be light; takhfīf = making something lighter/easier.","mawāzīn — plural of mīzān = A SCALE/BALANCE — from wazana = to weigh (wazn = weight; mīzān = the instrument).","The two verses are structurally identical:","AND HERE'S THE PAYOFF: Zalzalah 99:7 said an atom's weight (mithqāla dharrah) of good will be SEEN. Qāriʿah 101:6 says what happens when those atoms are WEIGHED. Same root (th-q-l), consecutive surahs — Zalzalah measures, Qāriʿah weighs.","And mawāzīn is PLURAL — \"scaleS,\" not \"scale.\" Classical readings: many scales, or many weighed things. Either way, the accounting is itemized, not lumped.","The free hook: mīzān is the everyday Arabic word for a weighing scale — the kitchen scale, the bathroom scale. And mīzān also means balance/justice generally.","Note the fa-ammā… wa ammā… structure — the same two-panel frame you own from Nāziʿāt 79:37–41, Inshiqāq 84:7–12, Layl 92:5–10, Ḍuḥā 93:9–11.","Mawāzīnuhu is the subject of the verb, which is feminine singular (thaqulat) — broken plurals take feminine singular agreement.","Same root, consecutive surahs.","And mawāzīn is PLURAL — the accounting is ITEMIZED."]},
  {ar:"فِي عِيشَةٍ رَّاضِيَةٍ", tr:"Fī ʿīshatin rāḍiyah", v:"7", root:"ʿīshah — a MODE OF LIVING / a way of life — from ʿāsha = to live (you own it from Nabaʾ 78:11, wa jaʿalna'n-nahāra maʿāshā, \"the day as a time of living\"; and the name ʿĀʾishah!).", meaning:"\"he will be in a pleasing life.\"", magic:"The adjective is doing something grammatically strange and beautiful.", grammar:"Rāḍiyah is ACTIVE, not passive — a majāz ʿaqlī (attributing to the life what belongs to the liver).", why:"ʿīshah (a mode of living) rather than jannah — because the reward described is how you live, not just where.", note:"Rāḍiyah is ACTIVE — \"a life that is PLEASED.\" Not \"pleasing to him\" — the LIFE ITSELF is content.", more:["rāḍiyah — the \"contentment\" family (riḍā).","Rāḍiyah is an ACTIVE participle — \"a life that is PLEASED.\" Literally: the life itself is content.","You'd expect marḍiyyah (a life that is pleasing TO him — passive). Instead the Qurʾān says the life is pleased. Classical grammarians call this a majāz ʿaqlī — attributing to the thing what belongs to its owner. The effect: the contentment is so total that even the life is happy.","And riḍā is now across SEVEN surahs — your most consistent reward-word: - Ghāshiyah 88:9 (li-saʿyihā rāḍiyah) · Fajr 89:28 (rāḍiyatan marḍiyyah) · Layl 92:21 (la-sawfa yarḍā) · Ḍuḥā 93:5 (fa-tarḍā) · Bayyinah 98:8 (raḍiya… wa raḍū) · Qāriʿah 101:7","And ʿīshah (from ʿāsha, to live) ties back to Nabaʾ 78:11's maʿāsh — \"the day as a time of LIVING.\" Two surahs, one root: dunya's working-life, and the life that finally pleases.","The free hook: the name ʿĀʾishah means \"one who lives.\"","And ʿĀʾishah = \"one who lives\"; maʿāsh (Nabaʾ 78:11) = \"the time of living.\" And riḍā is now in SEVEN surahs."]},
  {ar:"فَأُمُّهُ هَاوِيَةٌ", tr:"Fa-ummuhu hāwiyah", v:"9", root:"Umm — A MOTHER — and also \"the source/origin\" of something (umm al-kitāb = the mother of the Book; umm al-qurā = the mother of cities).", meaning:"\"his mother is the Abyss.\" (or: his refuge/shelter is the Abyss)", magic:"THE MOST HAUNTING PHRASE IN THE SURAH — and the root-payoff is the best in this batch.", grammar:"A nominal sentence: ummuhu (subject) + hāwiyah (predicate).", why:"Umm — the single warmest word in the language — used for the coldest destination. Maximum dissonance.", note:"Al-HAWĀ (DESIRE, Nāziʿāt 79:40) and HĀWIYAH (THE ABYSS) are THE SAME ROOT — \"to FALL.\" Desire is named after falling; the Abyss is where the falling ends.", more:["hāwiyah — the \"falling / plunging down\" family — hawā = to fall headlong; hāwiyah = AN ABYSS, a bottomless pit; and the SAME ROOT as al-hawā = DESIRE (Nāziʿāt 79:40!) and hawāʾ = AIR (the empty space you fall through!).","Why \"his MOTHER\"? Two classical readings, both extraordinary: - (a) Umm = where you take refuge. A child runs to his mother when terrified. So: the only shelter he has left is a bottomless pit. - (b) Umm = the source/origin you return to. Like umm al-kitāb, the root of a thing. So: the Abyss is where he came from and goes back to.","Either way it is unbearable: the word for the safest place a human knows is attached to the word for a bottomless fall.","AND THE ROOT-PAYOFF: Hāwiyah and al-hawā (DESIRE) are THE SAME ROOT. - Nāziʿāt 79:40 — wa nahā'n-nafsa ʿani'l-HAWĀ (\"restrained the soul from DESIRE\") - Qāriʿah 101:9 — fa-ummuhu HĀWIYAH (\"his mother is the ABYSS\")","DESIRE is named after FALLING. And where does following it land you? In the thing named after falling. Two surahs, one root, and the second is the destination of the first.","And hawāʾ = AIR — empty space, nothing to catch you. Free hook.","Hāwiyah is an active participle used as a name for the Fire.","And umm = MOTHER = where you run for refuge.","His refuge is a bottomless pit.","(And hawāʾ = AIR — nothing to catch you.)"]},
  {ar:"نَارٌ حَامِيَةٌ", tr:"Nārun ḥāmiyah", v:"11", root:"The \"intense heat\" family — ḥamiya = to become scorching hot; ḥammām = A BATHHOUSE (the hot room!); ḥummā = A FEVER.", meaning:"\"A scorching Fire.\"", magic:"You own this exact phrase from Ghāshiyah 88:4 — and the surah's ending is a masterclass in restraint.", grammar:"A nominal sentence with a deleted subject: \"[It is] a scorching Fire.\" Ḥāmiyah is an active participle.", why:"Two plain words after two unanswerable questions — the restraint is the rhetoric.", note:"Ḥammām = A BATHHOUSE (the HOT room); ḥummā = A FEVER. Same root.", more:["And ḥamīm = BOTH scalding water AND a close intimate friend (Nabaʾ 78:25!).","Ghāshiyah 88:4 — taṣlā nāran ḥāmiyah (\"roasting in a scorching Fire\"). Qāriʿah 101:11 — nārun ḥāmiyah (\"a scorching Fire\").","Identical phrase, two surahs. Free vocabulary.","But look at HOW the surah ends. After al-Qāriʿah (the Striker), scattered moths, carded mountains, the scales, and ummuhu hāwiyah (his mother is the Abyss) — the final answer is just two plain words.","No elaboration. No adjectives piled up. Just: \"a hot fire.\"","That plainness is deliberate. v.10 asked \"what could convey to you what it is?\" — and the answer is deliberately under-whelming in vocabulary, because the point is that nothing could convey it. The simplest possible words, after the biggest possible question.","And the root's double life stays haunting: ḥamīm is both boiling water and a dear friend. Warmth at both extremes.","You own this root from Nabaʾ 78:25 (ḥamīman) and Ghāshiyah 88:4 (ḥāmiyah).","The fire is actively scorching, not passively hot.","IDENTICAL phrase to Ghāshiyah 88:4. And the surah ends with TWO PLAIN WORDS after asking \"what could convey it to you?\" The plainness IS the point.","(And ḥamīm = boiling water AND a dear friend — Nabaʾ 78:25.)"]}
  ]
},
{
  num:102, name:"At-Takāthur", ar:"التكاثر", en:"Rivalry in Increase", verses:8, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Told → Seen → Touched. The surah begs you to be convinced at \"told.\"",
  special:["It climbs a ladder of certainty — and you're standing on the bottom rung.","V.5: law taʿlamūna ʿilma'l-yaqīn — \"if only you knew with the KNOWLEDGE of certainty\" v.7: la-tarawunnahā ʿayna'l-yaqīn — \"you will see it with the EYE of certainty\"","Two different kinds of certainty, named one after the other: - ʿilm al-yaqīn = certainty by INFORMATION — you've been told, and you believe it. - ʿayn al-yaqīn = certainty by SIGHT — you've seen it with your own eyes.","Scholars name a third rung found elsewhere in the Qurʾān: ḥaqq al-yaqīn — certainty by EXPERIENCE, being inside it.","Classic illustration: being told there's a fire (ʿilm) → seeing the fire (ʿayn) → being burned by it (ḥaqq). The surah's point: you could have climbed to the top rung by knowing. Instead you'll get there by seeing.","--"],
  big:["The distraction (v.1–2) — competing for more distracted you — until you visited the graves.","The double warning (v.3–4) — No! You will know. Then no! You will know.","The ladder (v.5–7) — knowledge of certainty → eye of certainty.","The question (v.8) — then you will be asked about the blessings."],
  traps:["لَوْ تَعْلَمُونَ (v.5) — the ANSWER of law is DELETED. \"If only you knew —\" and the sentence stops. Don't look for the missing clause; the silence is the rhetoric.","النَّعِيمِ (v.8) — here it's what you're QUESTIONED about, not a reward. The only time in your study it's flipped.","عِلْمَ الْيَقِينِ (v.5) vs عَيْنَ الْيَقِينِ (v.7) — two DIFFERENT certainties: by being TOLD vs by SEEING. Don't merge them.","لَتَرَوُنَّ / لَتُسْأَلُنَّ (v.6–8) — HEAVY nūn of emphasis (ـنَّ). Compare the LIGHT nūn in la-nasfaʿan (ʿAlaq 96:15).","التَّكَاثُرُ (v.1) — Form VI = competing with EACH OTHER, not merely \"having much.\" The disease is comparison.","زُرْتُمُ (v.2) — \"VISITED,\" not \"entered.\" A visitor leaves — the resurrection is implied.","سَوْفَ (v.3–4) vs سَـ (Nabaʾ 78:4–5) — both future markers; sawfa often a touch more distant.","الْمَقَابِرَ (v.2) — a DIPTOTE (mafāʿil): fatḥah, no tanwīn.","أَلْهَاكُمُ (v.1) — YOU are the object — the rivalry distracted you. And what you were distracted FROM is deleted.","الْيَقِينِ — elsewhere (15:99) al-yaqīn = DEATH, the one thing no one doubts."],
  links:[{w:"أَلْهَاكُمُ (v.1)",e:"تَلَهَّىٰ (ʿAbasa 80:10)",t:"Same root — da'wah misplaced / just wanting more"},{w:"التَّكَاثُرُ (v.1)",e:"يَتَسَاءَلُونَ (78:1) · تَحَاضُّونَ (89:18) · تَوَاصَوْا (90:17, 103:3)",t:"Form VI — what society does TOGETHER"},{w:"زُرْتُمُ (v.2)",e:"",t:"A visitor doesn't stay — resurrection in one verb"},{w:"الْمَقَابِرَ (v.2)",e:"أَقْبَرَهُ (80:21) · الْقُبُورُ (82:4) · الْقُبُورِ (100:9)",t:"FOUR surahs — given, overturned, emptied, visited"},{w:"كَلَّا سَوْفَ تَعْلَمُونَ ×2 (v.3–4)",e:"كَلَّا سَيَعْلَمُونَ ×2 (Nabaʾ 78:4–5)",t:"Near-verbatim — THEY → YOU"},{w:"لَتَرَوُنَّ (v.6–7)",e:"لَنَسْفَعًا (ʿAlaq 96:15)",t:"Heavy nūn vs. light nūn of emphasis"},{w:"لَتَرَوُنَّ (v.6)",e:"يَرَهُ (99:7–8) · يَرَىٰ (96:14) · لَمْ يَرَهُ (90:7)",t:"The seeing thread — see your deeds, then their destination"},{w:"الْجَحِيمَ (v.6)",e:"79:36 · 81:12 · 82:14 · 83:16",t:"The Blaze"},{w:"لَتُسْأَلُنَّ (v.8)",e:"سُئِلَتْ (Takwīr 81:8)",t:"The victim was asked / now YOU are"},{w:"النَّعِيمِ (v.8)",e:"76:20 · 82:13 · 83:22, 24 · 88:8",t:"Always a REWARD before — here, an EXAM TOPIC"},{w:"عَيْنَ (v.7)",e:"عَيْنٌ جَارِيَةٌ (Ghāshiyah 88:12)",t:"Eye / spring / the thing itself"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"أَلْهَاكُمُ", tr:"Alhākum", v:"1", root:"The \"distraction / amusement\" family — lahw = diversion, entertainment, the stuff that pulls your attention off what matters; malhā = a place of amusement (modern: a nightclub/amusement hall).", meaning:"\"[Competing for] more has distracted you.\"", magic:"You own this root from ʿAbasa 80:10 — fa-anta ʿanhu talahhā (\"you were distracted from him\").", grammar:"Form IV past (alhā), weak-final, with -kum (you, plural). Past tense = it has already happened to you.", why:"Lahw (frivolous diversion) rather than \"occupied\" — it judges the pursuit as trivial.", note:"Malhā = an AMUSEMENT HALL. ʿAbasa 80:10: even da'wah to VIPs was lahw when misplaced. Here: just wanting MORE.", more:["And the comparison is sobering: in ʿAbasa, even da'wah to powerful chiefs was filed as lahw when it cost a sincere seeker. Here the lahw is something far less noble — just wanting MORE.","And notice the grammar: alhā-KUM — the distraction is the subject, and you are the object. You didn't pursue the distraction — it took you. Form IV = it caused you to be distracted.","And the verb has no object for what you were distracted from. Deleted on purpose — from everything that mattered.","And YOU are the object — it took you."]},
  {ar:"التَّكَاثُرُ", tr:"At-takāthur", v:"1", root:"The \"muchness / abundance\" family — kathura = to be many; kathīr = much/many.", meaning:"\"the mutual rivalry for more.\"", magic:"The FORM is the whole diagnosis.", grammar:"Form VI verbal noun (tafāʿul) — spot the ta- + long ā. Definite (at-) = \"THE well-known rivalry.\"", why:"Takāthur (Form VI) over kathrah — it names the social engine of greed: rivalry.", note:"NOT \"having much\" — \"out-competing EACH OTHER for more.\" The disease is COMPARISON.", more:["Al-Kawthar = abundance (Sūrah 108!). Form VI (takāthara) = competing with EACH OTHER in having more.","It's not kathrah (having much). It's takāthur — Form VI, the MUTUAL form: \"competing with ONE ANOTHER for more.\"","So the problem isn't wealth. It's comparison. You don't want more — you want more than him.","And note: WHAT you compete in isn't named. Money? Children? Followers? Status? Square footage? The deletion makes it universal — whatever your scoreboard is.","You own Form VI from FOUR places now: - Nabaʾ 78:1 — yatasāʾalūn (asking each other) - Fajr 89:18 — taḥāḍḍūn (urging each other — they didn't) - Balad 90:17 / ʿAṣr 103:3 — tawāṣaw (enjoining each other — the good version) - Takāthur 102:1 — takāthur (out-competing each other)","Form VI shows what a society does together. Fajr's people wouldn't urge each other to feed the poor; Takāthur's people will compete with each other for more.","Form VI = mutual: yatasāʾalūn (78:1), taḥāḍḍūn (89:18), tawāṣaw (90:17), takāthur (102:1). And al-Kawthar = same root."]},
  {ar:"حَتَّىٰ زُرْتُمُ الْمَقَابِرَ", tr:"ḥattā zurtumu'l-maqābir", v:"2", root:"Zurtum — the \"visiting\" family — zāra = to visit; ziyārah = A VISIT (you use it for visiting Madīnah, visiting family); zāʾir = a visitor.", meaning:"\"until you visited the graveyards.\"", magic:"The most quietly profound verb in the surah — and a bedouin is said to have caught it instantly.", grammar:"ḥattā = \"until\" — a limit. Maqābir is a diptote (mafāʿil plural) — fatḥah, no tanwīn.", why:"Zurtum (visited) over dakhaltum (entered) — because a visit implies departure — the resurrection hidden inside one verb.", note:"Ziyārah = A VISIT. And A VISITOR DOESN'T STAY. The resurrection is hidden in one verb.", more:["maqābir — plural of maqbarah = a GRAVEYARD — from qabr (ʿAbasa 80:21 aqbarah, Infiṭār 82:4, ʿĀdiyāt 100:9).","It doesn't say \"until you were buried\" or \"until you died.\" It says zurtum — \"until you VISITED.\"","And what's the defining feature of a visit? YOU LEAVE. A zāʾir doesn't stay. There's a well-known report of an Arab who heard this verse and said: \"By Allah — a visitor does not remain. They will be raised.\" The grave is a stop, not a destination.","Two classical readings of the whole verse: (a) you competed until death took you to the graves; (b) the tribes literally went to the graveyards to count their dead as part of the boasting — \"we have more graves than you!\" Either way, the competition doesn't stop until the cemetery.","The qabr root across four surahs: ʿAbasa 80:21 (He gave you a grave — a blessing), Infiṭār 82:4 (graves overturned), ʿĀdiyāt 100:9 (what's in them tipped out), Takāthur 102:2 (you only visit them).","Zurtum — hollow verb (z-w-r), the long vowel shortens before the suffix.","The qabr thread: GIVEN a grave (80:21) → graves OVERTURNED (82:4) → contents TIPPED OUT (100:9) → you only VISIT (102:2)."]},
  {ar:"كَلَّا سَوْفَ تَعْلَمُونَ ثُمَّ كَلَّا", tr:"Kallā sawfa taʿlamūn, thumma kallā…", v:"3–4", root:"Kallā — the stop-sign particle: \"No! Absolutely not!\" sawfa — the future particle (\"will\"). taʿlamūn — you will know.", meaning:"\"No! You will come to know. Then — no! You will come to know.\"", magic:"You own this almost word-for-word from Nabaʾ 78:4–5.", grammar:"سَوْفَ + present = future (\"will\").", why:"The repeated, unfinished threat forces you to supply what you'll know.", note:"Nabaʾ 78:4–5 = \"THEY will know\" (sa-). Takāthur 102:3–4 = \"YOU will know\" (sawfa). Same double warning — now pointed at you.", more:["Nabaʾ 78:4–5 — kallā sa-yaʿlamūn · thumma kallā sa-yaʿlamūn (\"No! THEY will know. Then no! THEY will know.\") Takāthur 102:3–4 — kallā sawfa taʿlamūn · thumma kallā sawfa taʿlamūn (\"No! YOU will know. Then no! YOU will know.\")","Two shifts: - THEY → YOU. Nabaʾ talked about the deniers. Takāthur turns and talks to you. - sa- → sawfa. Sa- = near future; sawfa = a touch further future. Some scholars read it as: you'll know at death (v.3), and again at resurrection (v.4). Two moments of knowing.","And the thumma escalation is the same: say it, pause, say it harder. Like a parent: \"You'll see.\" … \"Oh, you will SEE.\"","And note — the threat is deliberately unfinished: know what? Not said. An open threat is scarier than a stated one.","Compare sa- (Nabaʾ 78:4) — both future markers, sawfa often slightly more distant.","Possibly: you'll know at DEATH, then again at RESURRECTION."]},
  {ar:"لَوْ تَعْلَمُونَ عِلْمَ الْيَقِينِ", tr:"Law taʿlamūna ʿilma'l-yaqīn", v:"5", root:"Law — \"if only / if\" — for a condition that didn't happen. ʿilm — knowledge.", meaning:"\"No! If only you knew with the knowledge of certainty…\"", magic:"Two masterstrokes in five words.", grammar:"Law = hypothetical condition (contrary to fact). Its answer is DELETED here.", why:"Deleting the answer is far more powerful than any stated consequence.", note:"\"If only you knew with certainty — [ ].\" The ANSWER IS DELETED. Your mind fills it, and always with something bigger.", more:["yaqīn — the \"certainty / settled conviction\" family — yaqina = to be certain, for doubt to settle out like still water; al-yaqīn is also a Qurʾānic name for DEATH (15:99 — \"worship your Lord until al-yaqīn comes to you\" — the one certain thing).","ONE — THE DELETED ANSWER. Law introduces a condition: \"If you knew…\" — and then the sentence just… stops. \"If you knew with certainty — [then ___].\" The jawāb (answer) of law is deliberately DELETED.","Why? Because no filler would be big enough. If you truly knew — you'd never have competed. You'd never have laughed. You'd never have slept. The reader's mind supplies the answer, and it's always bigger than anything written. Ḥadhf jawāb ash-sharṭ — a classic Qurʾānic technique.","TWO — ʿilm al-yaqīn, the first rung. Knowledge-certainty: certainty by INFORMATION. You were told. That should have been enough.","And al-yaqīn = DEATH elsewhere — because death is the one thing nobody doubts. Free, haunting hook.","ʿilma'l-yaqīn is a cognate-style accusative (maf'ūl muṭlaq) — \"know a knowing of certainty.\"","Rung 1: ʿilm al-yaqīn = TOLD.","And al-yaqīn = DEATH in 15:99 — the one certainty."]},
  {ar:"لَتَرَوُنَّ الْجَحِيمَ", tr:"La-tarawunna'l-jaḥīm", v:"6", root:"Tarawunna — \"you WILL see\" (from raʾā) with heavy emphasis.", meaning:"\"You will surely see the Blaze.\"", magic:"The grammar carries the force — and it's a construction you've seen once before in its light form.", grammar:"Lām al-qasam + nūn at-tawkīd ath-thaqīlah = maximum emphasis.", why:"The heavy nūn converts a prediction into something sworn.", note:"ـنَّ = THE HEAVY NŪN OF EMPHASIS — \"you WILL, by Allah.\" ʿAlaq 96:15's la-nasfaʿan = the LIGHT nūn. Learn them as a pair.", more:["jaḥīm — the \"fiercely blazing fire\" family — you own it from Nāziʿāt 79:36, 39, Takwīr 81:12, Infiṭār 82:14, Muṭaffifīn 83:16.","La-tarawunna = lām of oath + verb + HEAVY NŪN OF EMPHASIS (nūn at-tawkīd ath-thaqīlah, ـنَّ). The strongest \"you WILL\" Arabic can produce. Like an oath: \"By Allah, you WILL see it.\"","Compare ʿAlaq 96:15 — la-nasfaʿan — the LIGHT nūn of emphasis (written as an alif). Here: the HEAVY nūn (doubled). Two surahs, both emphatic nūns — learn them as a pair.","And the shift is crucial: v.5 was taʿlamūn (KNOW). v.6 is tarawunna (SEE). You refused to know it — so you'll see it.","The \"seeing\" thread you own: Balad 90:7 (\"does he think no one SAW him?\"), ʿAlaq 96:14 (\"Allah SEES\"), Zalzalah 99:7–8 (\"he will SEE it\" — his deeds), Takāthur 102:6 (\"you will SEE the Blaze\"). First you see your deeds, then their destination.","The original tarawūna loses its nūn of plural and gains the doubled emphatic nūn; the wāw takes a ḍammah to avoid two sukūns.","A tricky form — recognize it, don't parse it from scratch.","You refused to know; you'll see."]},
  {ar:"عَيْنَ الْيَقِينِ", tr:"ʿayna'l-yaqīn", v:"7", root:"ʿayn = an EYE — and also a SPRING (ʿayn jāriyah, Ghāshiyah 88:12), and the ESSENCE/very self of a thing (ʿayn ash-shayʾ = the thing itself).", meaning:"\"Then you will surely see it with the eye of certainty.\"", magic:"The second rung of the ladder — and the word ʿayn has three meanings that all fit.", grammar:"Same heavy-nūn structure as v.6 (la-tarawunna-hā), now with the pronoun -hā attached.", why:"ʿayn (the eye / the thing itself) — the shortest word for \"no more intermediaries.\"", note:"TOLD (ʿilm, v.5) → SEEN (ʿayn, v.7) → BEING IN IT (ḥaqq, elsewhere).", more:["Rung 1 (v.5) — ʿilm al-yaqīn — certainty by HEARING about it. Rung 2 (v.7) — ʿayn al-yaqīn — certainty by SEEING it. (Rung 3, elsewhere — ḥaqq al-yaqīn — certainty by BEING IN it.)","And ʿayn means: - an EYE — you see it with your own eyes - the ESSENCE — you see the thing itself, not a description - a SPRING — (and the irony: the Blaze is the opposite of a spring)","The doctor's version (useful for you): ʿilm al-yaqīn = reading the lab report. ʿayn al-yaqīn = seeing the scan yourself. ḥaqq al-yaqīn = being the patient. Same fact, three degrees of certainty.","And note the thumma (then) — v.6 \"you'll see the Blaze,\" v.7 \"THEN you'll see it with the eye of certainty.\" First a sighting; then a sighting that leaves zero doubt.","ʿAyna'l-yaqīn is accusative — a maf'ūl muṭlaq-like phrase (\"a seeing of certainty\").","Doctor's version: the lab REPORT → the SCAN → being the PATIENT.","ʿAyn = eye, spring, and the THING ITSELF."]},
  {ar:"ثُمَّ لَتُسْأَلُنَّ يَوْمَئِذٍ عَنِ النَّعِيمِ", tr:"Thumma la-tusʾalunna yawmaʾidhin ʿani'n-naʿīm", v:"8", root:"Tusʾalunna — the \"asking\" family (suʾāl = a question) — PASSIVE: \"you WILL BE ASKED.\" naʿīm.", meaning:"\"Then you will surely be asked, that Day, about the blessings.\"", magic:"The twist ending — and it's a word you've only ever seen as a REWARD.", grammar:"THIRD heavy nūn in three verses (la-tarawunna, la-tarawunnahā, la-tusʾalunna). Triple oath-emphasis.", why:"Naʿīm (the reward-word) — turning the language of Paradise into the language of the exam is the surah's sting.", note:"Naʿīm was PARADISE in four surahs (76:20, 82:13, 83:22, 88:8). Here it's the EXAM QUESTION.", more:["The \"blessing / softness / comfort\" family — niʿmah = favour; naʿam = YES; nāʿim = soft — you own it from Insān 76:20, Infiṭār 82:13, Muṭaffifīn 83:22, 24, Ghāshiyah 88:8.","Every previous appearance of naʿīm in your study was PARADISE: - Insān 76:20 — raʾayta naʿīman (you see bliss) - Infiṭār 82:13 — la-fī naʿīm (the righteous are in bliss) - Muṭaffifīn 83:22, 24 — naḍrata'n-naʿīm (the glow of bliss on their faces) - Takāthur 102:8 — you will be ASKED ABOUT the naʿīm","Here, for the first time, naʿīm isn't a reward — it's an EXAMINATION TOPIC. The blessings you competed for (takāthur, v.1) become the subject of the questioning.","And it's naʿīm in DUNYA — every blessing. A well-known hadith: the Prophet ﷺ, after a simple meal of dates, meat, and cool water, said this is among the naʿīm you will be asked about. Not just mansions — a cold drink of water.","And the full circle: v.1 — you were distracted by getting MORE. v.8 — you'll be asked about everything you GOT. The surah opens with acquisition and closes with the audit.","And tusʾalunna is PASSIVE — you don't get to choose the questions. Compare Takwīr 81:8 — wa idha'l-mawʾūdatu suʾilat (the buried girl was asked). There the victim was asked; here you are.","Passive + ʿan = \"asked ABOUT.\"","Even a COLD DRINK OF WATER counts (the hadith).","v.8: you're AUDITED on all of it.","And THREE heavy nūns in three verses."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:103, name:"Al-ʿAṣr", ar:"العصر", en:"The Declining Day", verses:3, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Time is passing → everyone is losing → except four things. Two you do alone, two you do together.",
  special:["Three verses. Fourteen words. And the whole human condition, with the only exit, in one sentence.","The structure is mathematical: - v.1 — an oath: TIME. - v.2 — the verdict on EVERYONE: in loss. - v.3 — the exception, with FOUR conditions: 1. āmanū — they believed 2. ʿamilu'ṣ-ṣāliḥāt — they did righteous deeds 3. tawāṣaw bi'l-ḥaqq — they urged each other to truth 4. tawāṣaw bi'ṣ-ṣabr — they urged each other to patience","Two are personal (believe, act). Two are social (urge each other). You can't escape loss alone — half the exit is helping others out.","A saying commonly attributed to Imām ash-Shāfiʿī: \"If people reflected on this surah alone, it would suffice them.\"","--"],
  big:["The oath (v.1) — by Time.","The verdict (v.2) — man is immersed in loss.","The exit (v.3) — four conditions."],
  traps:["الْعَصْرِ (v.1) — root = SQUEEZING (ʿaṣīr = juice). Same root as al-muʿṣirāt (Nabaʾ 78:14). Time is pressed; what does your life yield?","خُسْرٍ (v.2) — a TRADE word: losing CAPITAL. And loss is the DEFAULT — doing nothing is losing.","الصَّبْرِ (v.3) — originally means RESTRAINT / tying down, not \"waiting.\" Ṣabir = ALOES (bitter, healing).","تَوَاصَوْا (v.3) — Form VI = MUTUAL: you both give and receive. Same verb as Balad 90:17.","الْحَقِّ (v.3) — means TRUTH AND what is OWED (ḥuqūq = rights). Don't narrow it to one.","الْإِنسَانَ (v.2) — the al- of GENUS = all mankind, which is why v.3 can carve an exception.","خُسْرٍ is INDEFINITE — \"a loss\" = an enormous, unspecified loss.","The verb is REPEATED (tawāṣaw… wa tawāṣaw) — each object gets its own full verb, its own weight.","Belief before deeds — always this order in āmanū wa ʿamilu'ṣ-ṣāliḥāt.","Four conditions, not one — two personal (believe, act), two SOCIAL (urge truth, urge patience). Half the exit is collective."],
  links:[{w:"وَالْعَصْرِ (v.1)",e:"الْمُعْصِرَاتِ (Nabaʾ 78:14)",t:"SAME ROOT — clouds squeezed into rain / time squeezed out of you"},{w:"وَالْعَصْرِ (v.1)",e:"الدَّهْر (Insān 76:1)",t:"Two words for time — the vast force / the squeezed hour"},{w:"خُسْرٍ (v.2)",e:"خَاسِرَةٌ (Nāz. 79:12) · يُخْسِرُونَ (Muṭaff. 83:3)",t:"THREE surahs — the trader's loss"},{w:"لَفِي (v.2)",e:"فِي كَبَدٍ (90:4) · فِي أَحْسَنِ تَقْوِيمٍ (95:4)",t:"IN toil, IN the best form, IN loss"},{w:"إِلَّا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ (v.3)",e:"Tīn 95:6 · Inshiqāq 84:25 · Bayyinah 98:7",t:"Same exit — Tīn even has the same \"except\""},{w:"وَتَوَاصَوْا ×2 (v.3)",e:"وَتَوَاصَوْا ×2 (Balad 90:17)",t:"IDENTICAL verb, twice in each — together: truth + patience + mercy"},{w:"تَوَاصَوْا (v.3)",e:"لَا تَحَاضُّونَ (Fajr 89:18)",t:"The cure and the failure — both Form VI"},{w:"بِالْحَقِّ (v.3)",e:"الْيَوْمُ الْحَقُّ (78:39) · وَحُقَّتْ (84:2, 5)",t:"Truth and what is owed"},{w:"بِالصَّبْرِ (v.3)",e:"بِالصَّبْرِ (Balad 90:17)",t:"The ONE word both \"urge\" verses share"},{w:"بِالصَّبْرِ (v.3)",e:"حِجْرٍ (89:5) · أَحْكَمِ (95:8)",t:"Virtue as RESTRAINT — wall, bridle, tether"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"وَالْعَصْرِ", tr:"Wa'l-ʿaṣr", v:"1", root:"The \"SQUEEZING / PRESSING\" family — ʿaṣara = to squeeze, to press out; ʿaṣīr = JUICE (what you squeeze out of fruit!).", meaning:"\"By Time.\" (Classical readings: time itself, the age/era, or the afternoon — the time of ʿAṣr prayer.)", magic:"One of the most beautiful root-connections in the whole juzʾ — and you already own the other half.", grammar:"The wāw is wāw al-qasam (the \"by\" of oaths) — al-ʿaṣri is genitive.", why:"ʿaṣr over zamān or dahr — because only ʿaṣr carries the idea of pressure and yield.", note:"ʿAṢĪR = JUICE. TIME IS SQUEEZED. What does your life yield when pressed? Nabaʾ 78:14: muʿṣirāt = clouds SQUEEZED into rain.", more:["Miʿṣarah = a PRESS (olive press); and ʿaṣr = time / an era / the afternoon.","Why would \"time\" and \"juice\" share a root? Because time is squeezed. Every day presses something out of your life — and what's left is either juice or pulp.","And the afternoon (ʿaṣr) is the squeezed end of the day — the light is compressed, the hours remaining are few. The day is being wrung out.","NOW THE PAYOFF: you own this root from Nabaʾ 78:14 — wa anzalnā mina'l-MUʿṢIRĀTI māʾan thajjājā (\"We sent down from the RAIN-CLOUDS abundant water\") — clouds named \"the squeezed ones,\" wrung out like a wet towel.","Nabaʾ: clouds are squeezed → rain → life. ʿAṣr: time is squeezed → and what comes out of YOU? Same root. The question is what your life yields when pressed.","The everyday hooks are free: ʿaṣīr burtuqāl = orange JUICE; Ṣalāt al-ʿAṣr — you pray in the squeezed hour.","(Compare dahr in Insān 76:1 — time as a vast force.)","And you pray ʿAṣr in the squeezed end of the day."]},
  {ar:"لَفِي خُسْرٍ", tr:"La-fī khusr", v:"2", root:"The \"LOSS — specifically in TRADE\" family — khasira = to lose money in a deal, to lose capital.", meaning:"\"Indeed, man is in loss.\"", magic:"The verse is a BUSINESS STATEMENT — and that's what makes it devastating.", grammar:"Inna + emphatic lām on fī.", why:"Khusr (trade loss) rather than ḍalāl (being astray) — it frames life as a transaction already in progress.", note:"Rabḥ wa KHASĀRAH = PROFIT AND LOSS. Your CAPITAL is TIME (v.1), and it's draining.", more:["Khasārah = a financial LOSS (modern Arabic: rabḥ wa khasārah = profit and loss!); khāsir = a loser.","Khusr is a TRADER'S word. It means losing your capital — not just failing to profit, but your principal shrinking.","And what is the capital? v.1 just told you: TIME. Every hour is capital being spent. If it buys nothing lasting, the account is running down, automatically.","Which means loss is the DEFAULT. You don't have to do anything wrong to lose — you just have to do nothing right. Time is melting like ice; doing nothing is losing.","Three grammatical details make it heavier: - inna + la- — double emphasis (\"most certainly\"). - fī — \"IN\" loss — immersed in it, surrounded by it. Compare Balad 90:4 (fī kabad — created IN toil) and Tīn 95:4 (fī aḥsani taqwīm — IN the best form). Three surahs: you're IN toil, IN the best form, IN loss. - khusrin is INDEFINITE — \"a loss\" = an enormous, unspecified loss.","You own this root from TWO surahs: Nāziʿāt 79:12 — tilka idhan karratun khāsirah (\"that would be a losing return\" — the mockers, accidentally right) and Muṭaffifīn 83:3 — yukhsirūn (the cheaters who cause loss by short-measuring). The fraudsters made others lose; ʿAṣr says everyone is losing.","Al-insān = all of mankind (definite article of genus — al-jins), which is why v.3 can make an exception from it.","LOSS IS THE DEFAULT — you don't have to do wrong; you just have to do nothing right.","Fī = IMMERSED (like fī kabad 90:4, fī aḥsani taqwīm 95:4). And Nāziʿāt 79:12, Muṭaffifīn 83:3."]},
  {ar:"الصَّالِحَاتِ", tr:"Aṣ-ṣāliḥāt", v:"3", root:"The \"being sound / fit / repairing\" family — ṣalaḥa = to be good, sound, fit for purpose; iṣlāḥ = REFORM / repair.", meaning:"\"except those who believed and did righteous deeds.\"", magic:"The root means fit for purpose, and that reframes \"righteous.\"", grammar:"Aṣ-ṣāliḥāt — feminine plural active participle used as a noun (the word aʿmāl, deeds, is understood).", why:"ṣāliḥ (fit for purpose / repairing) rather than khayr (good) — it's about function, not just virtue.", note:"Iṣlāḥ = REPAIR; ṣulḥ = a PEACE TREATY. Ṣāliḥ = FIT FOR PURPOSE. Righteous deeds = the investments that actually return.", more:["Maṣlaḥah = benefit/public interest; ṣulḥ = reconciliation, a peace treaty.","Ṣāliḥ isn't \"nice.\" It's sound, functioning, doing what it was built to do — like a ṣāliḥ tool or a road ṣāliḥ for travel. And iṣlāḥ = REPAIR. So ʿamal ṣāliḥ = action that WORKS, that fixes, that's fit for the purpose you were made for.","And this connects to v.2's trade metaphor: if time is draining capital, ṣāliḥāt are the investments that actually return something.","And the phrase āmanū wa ʿamilu'ṣ-ṣāliḥāt is a Qurʾānic signature you own from three places: Inshiqāq 84:25, Tīn 95:6 (illa'lladhīna āmanū wa ʿamilu'ṣ-ṣāliḥāt), Bayyinah 98:7. Tīn 95:6 even has the SAME \"except\" structure — everyone falls to the lowest of the low except these. ʿAṣr: everyone is in loss except these. Two surahs, same exit.","Note: belief comes FIRST, deeds SECOND. Always that order.","Illā = exception — from al-insān (all mankind) in v.2.","Tīn 95:6: everyone falls EXCEPT these.","ʿAṣr 103:3: everyone loses EXCEPT these."]},
  {ar:"وَتَوَاصَوْا", tr:"Wa tawāṣaw", v:"3", root:"The \"enjoining / advising / bequeathing\" family — waṣiyyah = A WILL / last testament (instructions you leave behind!).", meaning:"\"and urged one another to truth, and urged one another to patience.\"", magic:"You own this EXACT verb from Balad 90:17 — and the comparison is the best cross-surah gem in this batch.", grammar:"Form VI past, weak-final — the original tawāṣayū contracts to tawāṣaw.", why:"Tawāṣā (mutual bequeathing) rather than amara (commanding) — it's peer-to-peer, not top-down.", note:"Waṣiyyah = A WILL — urge each other like a dying man advises his children. Balad 90:17: PATIENCE + MERCY.", more:["Awṣā = to enjoin; Form VI (tawāṣā) = to enjoin ONE ANOTHER.","Balad 90:17 — wa tawāṣaw bi'ṣ-ṣabri wa tawāṣaw bi'l-marḥamah — urged each other to PATIENCE and to COMPASSION. ʿAṣr 103:3 — wa tawāṣaw bi'l-ḥaqqi wa tawāṣaw bi'ṣ-ṣabr — urged each other to TRUTH and to PATIENCE.","Same verb, used TWICE in each verse, in BOTH surahs. And ṣabr appears in both — it's the constant. Balad pairs it with mercy; ʿAṣr pairs it with truth.","Put them together and you get the complete social ethic: TRUTH + PATIENCE + MERCY. Truth without patience becomes harsh; truth without mercy becomes cruel. The two surahs supply each other's missing piece.","Waṣiyyah = A WILL — the instructions a dying person leaves. So tawāṣī has a gravity to it: you urge each other the way a dying man advises his children — seriously, lovingly, with nothing to gain.","And Form VI = MUTUAL — you both give and receive the reminder. Nobody is only the preacher; everybody is also preached to.","Compare Fajr 89:18 — wa lā taḥāḍḍūna (\"you do NOT urge one another\") — the failure. ʿAṣr and Balad show the cure.","Note the verb is REPEATED rather than saying \"urged one another to truth and patience\" — each gets its own full verb, its own weight.","ʿAṣr 103:3: TRUTH + PATIENCE.","Together: TRUTH + PATIENCE + MERCY.","And Form VI = you both GIVE and RECEIVE the reminder.","Fajr 89:18 = the failure (lā taḥāḍḍūn)."]},
  {ar:"بِالْحَقِّ", tr:"Bi'l-ḥaqq", v:"3", root:"The \"firmly established / binding / real\" family — ḥaqqa = to be true, to be established, to be due.", meaning:"\"[urged one another] to the truth.\"", magic:"The root holds TRUTH and RIGHTS together — and that's exactly why it's the first thing to urge.", grammar:"Bi- after tawāṣaw = \"to / with\" (what is enjoined). Definite = \"THE truth.\"", why:"ḥaqq (truth + right) rather than ṣidq (honesty) — because it covers both belief and obligation.", note:"Ḥuqūq al-insān = HUMAN RIGHTS. Ḥaqq = TRUTH and what is OWED. Doctrine and justice in one word.", more:["ḥaqq = TRUTH AND A RIGHT (\"ḥuqūq\" = RIGHTS — human rights = ḥuqūq al-insān!); al-Ḥaqq — a NAME OF ALLAH; taḥqīq = investigation/verification.","Ḥaqq means both what is TRUE and what is OWED. Ḥuqūq = rights — what you're owed and what you owe.","So urging each other bi'l-ḥaqq covers: (a) the TRUTH of belief, and (b) giving everyone their DUE. Doctrine and justice, one word.","You own the root from Nabaʾ 78:39 — dhālika'l-yawmu'l-ḥaqq (\"that is the TRUE Day\" — the established, certain Day), and Inshiqāq 84:2, 5 — wa ḥuqqat (the sky \"was made fit / rightly obliged\").","And the order matters: TRUTH first, then PATIENCE. Because the moment you stand for the truth, you will need patience.","TRUTH first, then PATIENCE — because standing for truth REQUIRES patience.","And al-Ḥaqq is a Divine Name."]},
  {ar:"بِالصَّبْرِ", tr:"Bi'ṣ-ṣabr", v:"3", root:"The \"RESTRAINING / BINDING / HOLDING FIRM\" family — ṣabara = originally: to CONFINE, to hold something in check, to tie it down; ṣabr = patience/steadfastness; ṣabir = ALOES (a famously bitter plant!", meaning:"\"[urged one another] to patience/steadfastness.\"", magic:"The final word of the surah — and the root is a word of RESTRAINT, which closes a loop you've been tracking.", grammar:"Bi- again after the repeated tawāṣaw. Definite = \"THE patience.\"", why:"ṣabr (restraint) — because truth (the previous word) provokes resistance, and only restraint survives it.", note:"Ṣabir = ALOES — a BITTER plant that HEALS. Ṣabr originally = TO TIE DOWN / CONFINE.", more:["— patience is bitter); al-Ṣabūr — a NAME OF ALLAH.","Ṣabr doesn't mean \"waiting calmly.\" It originally means to HOLD YOURSELF IN, to confine, to tie down. Restraining the tongue from complaint, the limbs from panic, the heart from despair.","And ṣabir = ALOES — the bitter plant. Classical Arabic links them: patience tastes bitter, but it heals. Free, unforgettable hook.","NOW THE THREAD: restraint as wisdom runs through your surahs: - Fajr 89:5 — li-dhī ḥijr — intellect = a WALL (restraint) - Fajr 89:11 — ṭaghaw — the nations OVERFLOWED (no restraint) - Tīn 95:8 — aḥkami'l-ḥākimīn — ḥikmah from the BRIDLE (restraint) - ʿAṣr 103:3 — ṣabr — holding yourself in (restraint)","Arabic keeps defining virtue as the ability to HOLD BACK. Intelligence is a wall; wisdom is a bridle; patience is a tether.","And ṣabr is in BOTH Balad 90:17 and ʿAṣr 103:3 — the only word the two \"urge one another\" verses share. It's the constant.","And note: the surah ENDS on ṣabr. The last instruction for escaping loss is: hold on.","Virtue as RESTRAINT: ḥijr = a WALL (89:5), ḥikmah = a BRIDLE (95:8), ṣabr = a TETHER (103:3). And the surah ENDS on \"hold on.\""]}
  ]
},
{
  num:104, name:"Al-Humazah", ar:"الهمزة", en:"The Slanderer", verses:9, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"HUMAZAH → ḤUṬAMAH. Same pattern. He was the crusher; now he meets the Crusher.",
  special:["The sinner and his punishment share the same word-shape. He was a HUMAZAH — he goes to the ḤUṬAMAH.","V.1: wayl li-kulli humazatin lumazah — \"woe to every backbiter, fault-finder\" v.4: la-yunbadhanna fi'l-ḥuṭamah — \"he will surely be thrown into the Crusher\"","All three words are on the SAME rare pattern: fuʿalah (فُعَلَة) — humazah, lumazah, ḥuṭamah. An intensive pattern meaning \"one who does X habitually, compulsively.\"","So the logic is built into the morphology: - Humazah = one who habitually JABS at people. - Ḥuṭamah = that which habitually SMASHES things.","He broke people into pieces with his tongue. He's thrown into the thing that breaks people into pieces. Same shape, same habit, reversed.","--"],
  big:["The charge (v.1–3) — backbiting, fault-finding, hoarding, counting — and believing money makes him immortal.","The answer (v.4–5) — No! He'll be flung into the Crusher. And what could tell you what that is?","The description (v.6–9) — Allah's kindled Fire, rising over hearts, sealed over them, in extended columns."],
  traps:["هُمَزَةٍ / لُّمَزَةٍ / الْحُطَمَةِ — all THREE on the fuʿalah pattern (habitual doer). The punishment is shaped like the sin. Read them as one set.","The tāʾ marbūṭah in humazah/lumazah — marks INTENSITY, not the feminine. Same as ʿallāmah (a very learned man).","يَحْسَبُ (v.3) — NO question-hamzah — a statement of his delusion. Compare a-yaḥsabu (75:3, 90:5) = a rebuking question.","عَدَّدَهُ (v.2) — Form II = counting REPEATEDLY, not once. The obsession is in the form.","لَيُنبَذَنَّ (v.4) — \"FLUNG AWAY like rubbish,\" not merely \"thrown.\" And HEAVY nūn of emphasis.","الْأَفْئِدَةِ (v.7) — fuʾād (heart) comes from faʾada = TO ROAST. The burning is already in the word.","تَطَّلِعُ (v.7) — Form VIII with ASSIMILATION (doubled ṭ). And it means both \"rises over\" and \"looks into.\"","نَارُ اللَّهِ (v.6) — the Fire attributed to Allah BY NAME — a highly distinctive construction.","مُّؤْصَدَةٌ (v.8) — IDENTICAL to Balad 90:20; two established recitations (muʾṣadah / mūṣadah).","وَيْلٌ (v.1) — an indefinite noun starting a sentence — allowed because it's a curse/supplication."],
  links:[{w:"وَيْلٌ (v.1)",e:"وَيْلٌ (Muṭaffifīn 83:1, 10)",t:"Woe to those who cheat with SCALES / with TONGUES"},{w:"لُّمَزَةٍ (v.1)",e:"يَتَغَامَزُونَ (Muṭaffifīn 83:30)",t:"Muṭaffifīn showed the WINKING; Humazah names the character"},{w:"هُمَزَةٍ... الْحُطَمَةِ (v.1, 4)",e:"",t:"Same fuʿalah pattern — punishment shaped like the sin"},{w:"جَمَعَ مَالًا (v.2)",e:"89:20 · 90:6 · 92:11",t:"FOUR surahs on māl — love, boast, trust, count"},{w:"يَحْسَبُ (v.3)",e:"أَيَحْسَبُ (75:3, 36 · 90:5, 7)",t:"No question-hamzah here — a statement, not a rebuke"},{w:"أَخْلَدَهُ (v.3)",e:"خَالِدِينَ (Bayyinah 98:6, 8) · مُّخَلَّدُونَ (76:19)",t:"He wanted eternity — he gets khālidīna fīhā"},{w:"لَيُنبَذَنَّ (v.4)",e:"لَتَرَوُنَّ · لَتُسْأَلُنَّ (Takāthur 102:6–8)",t:"Heavy nūn — consecutive surahs"},{w:"وَمَا أَدْرَاكَ (v.5)",e:"82:17–18 · 83:8, 19 · 86:2 · 90:12 · 97:2 · 101:3, 10",t:"EIGHT surahs"},{w:"الْحُطَمَةِ (v.4)",e:"هَاوِيَةٌ (101:9) · غُثَاءً (87:5)",t:"New Hell-name — and dunya ends as debris"},{w:"نَارُ اللَّهِ (v.6)",e:"نَاقَةَ اللَّهِ (Shams 91:13)",t:"Attributed to Allah BY NAME"},{w:"الْمُوقَدَةُ (v.6)",e:"ذَاتِ الْوَقُودِ (Burūj 85:5)",t:"Humans kindled a fire / Allah's Fire is kindled"},{w:"تَطَّلِعُ (v.7)",e:"مَطْلَعِ الْفَجْرِ (Qadr 97:5)",t:"LIGHT rises / FIRE rises"},{w:"الْأَفْئِدَةِ (v.7)",e:"الصُّدُورِ (ʿĀdiyāt 100:10)",t:"The chest extracted / the heart reached"},{w:"مُّؤْصَدَةٌ (v.8)",e:"مُّؤْصَدَةٌ (Balad 90:20)",t:"IDENTICAL WORD"},{w:"عَمَدٍ (v.9)",e:"الْعِمَادِ (Fajr 89:7)",t:"Iram's pillars of permanence → the Fire's pillars"},{w:"مُّمَدَّدَةٍ (v.9)",e:"مُدَّتْ (Inshiqāq 84:3)",t:"Same root — and madd in tajwīd"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"وَيْلٌ", tr:"Wayl", v:"1", root:"A word of doom / woe / ruin — a cry of destruction. (Some classical reports also name Wayl as a valley in Hell.)", meaning:"\"Woe to every…\"", magic:"You own this word from Muṭaffifīn 83:1 — waylun li'l-muṭaffifīn (\"woe to the short-measurers\") and 83:10.", grammar:"Waylun is an indefinite noun used as a subject — normally Arabic doesn't start a sentence with an indefinite noun, but wayl is allowed because it's a supplication/curse (duʿāʾ).", why:"Wayl (a cry of ruin) rather than a description of punishment — it's a verdict shouted, not explained.", note:"Muṭaffifīn 83:1: wayl to those who cheat with SCALES. Humazah 104:1: wayl to those who cheat with TONGUES. Money vs.", more:["And the pairing is perfect: Muṭaffifīn opens with wayl for those who cheat with their SCALES. Humazah opens with wayl for those who cheat with their TONGUES. Two surahs that begin with \"woe,\" aimed at two kinds of taking what isn't yours: people's money, and people's dignity.","And li-kulli — \"to EVERY\" — no exceptions, no rank, no excuse."]},
  {ar:"هُمَزَةٍ لُّمَزَةٍ", tr:"Humazatin lumazah", v:"1", root:"Humazah — the \"JABBING / poking / pressing\" family — hamaza = to poke, to prod, to press hard; and hamzah — THE LETTER (ء)!", meaning:"\"to every slanderer, fault-finder\" — the classical distinction: hamz behind someone's back / by words; lamz to their face / by gesture (the two are also described the other way round by some — either way, both directions covered).", magic:"Two words, rhymed, covering EVERY way of tearing someone down.", grammar:"Both on the fuʿalah intensive pattern — masculine in meaning despite the tāʾ marbūṭah (the tāʾ here is for intensity, like ʿallāmah, a very learned man).", why:"The rhymed pair makes the condemnation feel complete and inescapable, covering both channels.", note:"HAMZAH (ء) = named after a JAB in the throat. A humazah JABS people. Hamz = WORDS; lamz = LOOKS (the smirk, the eyebrow).", more:["— named because it's a \"jab\" in the throat, a sudden stop.","Also hamazāt ash-shayāṭīn = the \"jabs/goadings\" of devils (23:97). lumazah — the \"finding fault / mocking by gesture\" family — lamaza = to criticize, to point out faults, often with the eyes, eyebrows, or face (a wink, a smirk, a raised eyebrow).","Hamz = JABBING — the verbal stab, often behind their back. Lamz = FAULT-FINDING — the look, the gesture, the face you make when they speak.","Together: words AND looks, behind AND in front. Every channel of contempt.","And the letter hook is unforgettable: hamzah (ء) is named after a JAB — the glottal stop, the little catch in your throat. Every time you write a hamzah, you're writing \"a poke.\"","And the PATTERN is the key to the surah: fuʿalah (فُعَلَة) = one who does it HABITUALLY, compulsively, as a trait. Not someone who once said something cruel — someone for whom it's a character. The same pattern returns as al-ḥuṭamah in v.4.","Compare Muṭaffifīn 83:30 — wa idhā marrū bihim yataghāmazūn (\"they winked at each other\" as the believers passed). Muṭaffifīn showed the lamz in action — winking. Humazah names the character.","Don't read it as feminine.","Pattern fuʿalah = a HABIT, a character — and it returns in v.4 as ḥuṭamah.","The tāʾ = INTENSITY, not feminine.","Muṭaffifīn 83:30 showed them WINKING."]},
  {ar:"جَمَعَ مَالًا وَعَدَّدَهُ", tr:"Jamaʿa mālan wa ʿaddadah", v:"2", root:"Jamaʿa — to GATHER (jumuʿah, jāmiʿ — you own it from Qiyāmah 75:3, ʿĀdiyāt 100:5). ʿaddada.", meaning:"\"who gathered wealth and counted it [again and again].\"", magic:"Form II is the whole portrait.", grammar:"Form II (ʿaddada) = intensive/repetitive.", why:"ʿaddada (counting repeatedly) rather than iddakhara (saved) — it captures the obsessive relationship, not just the act of saving.", note:"ʿAddād = A METER (taxi meter, electricity meter). Form II ʿaddada = COUNTING OVER AND OVER. He GATHERS and COUNTS — never SPENDS.", more:["The \"counting / numbering\" family — ʿadad = a NUMBER; ʿaddād = a METER (electricity meter, taxi meter!); Form II ʿaddada = to count OVER AND OVER.","It's not ʿadda (counted once). It's ʿaddada — Form II, INTENSIVE: he counted it repeatedly. Pulling it out, recounting, putting it back, recounting. The compulsive check of someone whose security lives in a number.","And notice: he GATHERS and COUNTS — he never SPENDS. Two verbs of accumulation, zero verbs of use.","And here's why v.1 and v.2 belong together: the man who tears people down (v.1) is the man who builds up his pile (v.2). Contempt for people + attachment to money — same disease, two symptoms. When your worth is a number, everyone else's worth becomes something to jab at.","The māl thread across four surahs: - Fajr 89:20 — tuḥibbūna'l-māla ḥubban jammā (loving it OVERFLOWINGLY) - Balad 90:6 — ahlaktu mālan lubadā (boasting of piles DESTROYED) - Layl 92:11 — mā yughnī ʿanhu māluhu (his wealth won't avail him) - Humazah 104:2 — jamaʿa mālan wa ʿaddadah (counting it over and over)","Four surahs, four postures toward money: loving it, boasting of it, trusting it, counting it.","Mālan is indefinite — \"wealth\" in general — or \"a great deal of wealth.\"","And the jabber (v.1) is the counter (v.2): when your worth is a number, everyone else becomes a target.","Four surahs on māl: loving it (89:20), boasting (90:6), trusting (92:11), counting (104:2)."]},
  {ar:"يَحْسَبُ أَنَّ مَالَهُ أَخْلَدَهُ", tr:"Yaḥsabu anna mālahu akhladah", v:"3", root:"Yaḥsabu — the \"counting / reckoning / supposing\" family — ḥisāb = accounting.", meaning:"\"He thinks his wealth has made him immortal.\"", magic:"TWO roots you own, and BOTH are ironic.", grammar:"Yaḥsabu WITHOUT hamzah = a STATEMENT, not a question. Form IV akhlada = causative (made eternal).", why:"Akhlada (made eternal) rather than \"protected\" — because the delusion is specifically about escaping death.", note:"Ḥisāb = ACCOUNTING — the man who only does arithmetic gets the ONE sum that matters wrong.", more:["You own it from Qiyāmah 75:3, 36 and Balad 90:5, 7. akhlada — Form IV of khalada = to MAKE someone eternal — the root of KHĀLID, khālidīn — you own it from Insān 76:19 (mukhalladūn) and Bayyinah 98:6, 8 (khālidīna fīhā).","IRONY ONE — yaḥsabu: the root of ḥisāb — ACCOUNTING. v.2 just said he counts (ʿaddada) his money; v.3 says he reckons (yaḥsabu) it makes him eternal. A man who does nothing but arithmetic gets the one calculation that matters wrong. Exactly like Qiyāmah 75:3 — the calculating creature miscalculating the Day of Calculation.","Note: in Qiyāmah 75:3 and Balad 90:5, 7 it's a-yaḥsabu (WITH the question-hamzah — \"DOES he think…?\"). Here it's yaḥsabu — NO hamzah — \"he THINKS…\" Not a rebuking question; a flat statement of his delusion. Spot the difference.","IRONY TWO — akhlada: the root of ETERNITY. He thinks money gives him khulūd (permanence). And he's right that there's khulūd coming — Bayyinah 98:6 says the disbelievers will be khālidīna fīhā — ETERNAL in the Fire. He gets his eternity. Just not the kind he expected.","And the past tense akhlada-hu is telling — \"has MADE him eternal\" — as if it's already done, already secured. He's living as though death has been paid off.","Past tense = he treats it as already accomplished.","NO question-hamzah here (unlike 75:3, 90:5) — it's a FLAT STATEMENT of his delusion.","Khulūd = ETERNITY — he wanted it; Bayyinah 98:6 says he'll get it: khālidīna fīhā.","And Khālid = \"the eternal one.\""]},
  {ar:"لَيُنبَذَنَّ", tr:"La-yunbadhanna", v:"4", root:"The \"THROWING AWAY as worthless / discarding\" family — nabadha = to fling something away carelessly, like rubbish.", meaning:"\"No! He will surely be flung into the Crusher.\"", magic:"The verb is chosen for its CONTEMPT — and it answers v.2 precisely.", grammar:"Lām al-qasam + passive + nūn at-tawkīd ath-thaqīlah — maximum emphasis. Same family as la-tarawunna (102:6).", why:"Nabadha (discard as worthless) rather than alqā (throw) — it carries contempt, which mirrors his contempt for people in v.1.", note:"Manbūdh = AN OUTCAST. Nabadha = to FLING AWAY LIKE RUBBISH.", more:["Nabīdh = dates/raisins STEEPED in water (\"thrown\" into water!); manbūdh = an outcast, someone discarded by society.","Nabadha isn't \"to throw.\" It's to throw AWAY — the motion you use for garbage, something you don't look at as you discard it. A manbūdh is a social outcast.","NOW SET IT AGAINST v.2: he spent his life GATHERING and COUNTING — carefully, attentively, item by item. He will be FLUNG AWAY — carelessly, without being counted. The man who treated money as precious is treated as rubbish.","And the grammar maxes out the emphasis: la- (oath) + PASSIVE + HEAVY NŪN (ـنَّ) = \"By Allah, he WILL be flung.\" You own the heavy nūn from Takāthur 102:6–8 (la-tarawunna, la-tusʾalunna) — consecutive surahs, same emphatic oath-form.","And the PASSIVE matters: he doesn't walk; he's thrown. No agency left.","And kallā (No!) opens the verse — the direct rebuttal of v.3's \"he thinks his wealth has made him immortal.\" No. He'll be thrown away.","He GATHERED and COUNTED carefully (v.2) → he is FLUNG without being counted (v.4).","The man who treated money as precious is treated as trash.","HEAVY NŪN again — like Takāthur 102:6."]},
  {ar:"الْحُطَمَةِ", tr:"Al-ḥuṭamah", v:"4, 5", root:"The \"SMASHING into fragments / crushing\" family — ḥaṭama = to break something brittle into pieces.", meaning:"\"the Crusher\" — a name for Hell. (And v.5: \"and what could convey to you what the Crusher is?\")", magic:"THE KEY WORD OF THE SURAH — and it's built on the same pattern as the sinner.", grammar:"Fuʿalah intensive pattern used as a proper name for the Fire. The tāʾ marbūṭah = intensity, as in humazah.", why:"ḥuṭamah is chosen for its pattern — the rhyme with humazah IS the argument.", note:"ḤUṬĀM = WRECKAGE / RUBBLE (the wreckage of a plane). HUMAZAH, LUMAZAH, ḤUṬAMAH — ALL fuʿalah.", more:["ḥuṭām = WRECKAGE / DEBRIS / RUBBLE (modern Arabic: the wreckage of a plane, the rubble of a building!); and ḥaṭm = shattering.","Ḥuṭamah = that which HABITUALLY SMASHES — on the fuʿalah intensive pattern.","AND fuʿalah IS EXACTLY THE PATTERN OF v.1: - HUMAZAH — one who habitually JABS - LUMAZAH — one who habitually FINDS FAULT - ḤUṬAMAH — that which habitually SMASHES","Three fuʿalah words in one surah. He broke people's dignity into pieces with his tongue — and the place he's thrown into is named for breaking things into pieces. The punishment is morphologically shaped like the sin.","And ḥuṭām = WRECKAGE — so the Crusher reduces things to rubble. Everything he gathered and counted (v.2) — ḥuṭām. Compare Aʿlā 87:5 — fa-jaʿalahu ghuthāʾan aḥwā (the pasture turned to dark debris) — dunya ends as debris either way.","And it's a NEW NAME for Hell — added to Jaḥīm (blaze), Saʿīr (flare), Jahannam, Hāwiyah (abyss, Qāriʿah 101:9). Each name a different sensation; this one is being broken.","And v.5's wa mā adrāka mā'l-ḥuṭamah — the formula you own from EIGHT surahs now (82:17–18, 83:8, 19, 86:2, 90:12, 97:2, 101:3, 101:10, and here).","He smashed people with his tongue → the SMASHER is his destination.","Punishment shaped like the sin.","New Hell-name: Jaḥīm (blaze), Saʿīr (flare), Hāwiyah (abyss), Ḥuṭamah (crusher)."]},
  {ar:"نَارُ اللَّهِ الْمُوقَدَةُ", tr:"Nāru'llāhi'l-mūqadah", v:"6", root:"Nār — fire.", meaning:"\"[It is] the Fire of Allah, kindled.\"", magic:"One phrase here is unique in the Qurʾān.", grammar:"A nominal sentence with a deleted subject — \"[It is] the Fire of Allah.\" Nāru'llāh is iḍāfah (possession/attribution).", why:"Attributing it to Allah by name removes every possibility of negotiation.", note:"NĀRU'LLĀH — \"the Fire OF ALLAH.\" Attributed by name. Compare Shams 91:13: nāqata'llāh (the she-camel OF ALLAH).", more:["mūqadah — the \"kindling / igniting\" family — waqada = to burn, to blaze; waqūd = FUEL (modern: waqūd = petrol, maḥaṭṭat waqūd = a GAS STATION!); Form IV passive ūqida = to BE kindled.","Nāru'LLĀH — \"the Fire OF ALLAH.\" The Fire is attributed DIRECTLY to Allah by name. This construction is highly distinctive.","Compare Shams 91:13 — nāqata'LLĀH (\"the she-camel OF ALLAH\"). Something attributed to Him directly carries His weight. The she-camel couldn't be harmed without challenging Him; this Fire can't be escaped without escaping Him.","And why HERE? Because the man thought his money made him eternal (v.3) — independent, untouchable. The answer: this isn't an ordinary fire you can buy your way out of. It belongs to Allah.","Mūqadah is PASSIVE — \"KINDLED\" — someone lit it, and keeps it lit. You own the root from Burūj 85:5 — an-nāri dhāti'l-waqūd (\"the fire full of fuel\" — the People of the Ditch). Burūj: humans kindled a fire for believers. Humazah: Allah's Fire, kindled for the jabber.","Free hook: maḥaṭṭat waqūd = A GAS STATION.","Al-mūqadah = Form IV passive participle.","Waqūd = FUEL; maḥaṭṭat waqūd = a GAS STATION.","Burūj 85:5: humans kindled a fire; here Allah's Fire is kindled."]},
  {ar:"الَّتِي تَطَّلِعُ عَلَى الْأَفْئِدَةِ", tr:"Allatī taṭṭaliʿu ʿala'l-afʾidah", v:"7", root:"Taṭṭaliʿu — Form VIII of ṭalaʿa (to rise) = iṭṭalaʿa = to RISE OVER, to look into, to become informed of.", meaning:"\"which rises over / reaches into the hearts.\"", magic:"Two roots, two extraordinary hooks.", grammar:"Form VIII with assimilation: taṭalaʿu → taṭṭaliʿu — the infixed tāʾ merges with the root's ṭāʾ and doubles.", why:"Fuʾād (the searing-heart) rather than qalb — because the root already carries the burning.", note:"Fuʾād (HEART) ← faʾada = TO ROAST. The word for the feeling-heart already contains searing.", more:["You own the root from Qadr 97:5 (maṭlaʿi'l-fajr, \"the rising of dawn\").","afʾidah — plural of fuʾād = THE HEART — specifically the heart as the seat of feeling and inner intention; and the root faʾada = to ROAST, to sear (mufaʾʾad = roasted meat!).","ONE — iṭṭalaʿa = TO RISE OVER and TO BECOME INFORMED. The Fire climbs up to the heart — and it also \"looks into\" it, as if reading what's there. A fire that knows what it's burning.","And you own ṭalaʿa from Qadr 97:5 — maṭlaʿi'l-fajr, the rising of dawn. Same root: in Qadr, LIGHT rises to end the Night of Peace. In Humazah, FIRE rises to reach the heart. Two risings.","TWO — fuʾād = THE HEART, from a root meaning TO ROAST. The Arabic word for the feeling-heart already contains searing. A heart that burns with rage, envy, contempt — ends up literally burned.","And why the HEART specifically? Because that's where the sin lived. The jabbing (v.1) came from contempt in the heart; the counting (v.2) came from love of money in the heart; the delusion of immortality (v.3) was a belief in the heart. The Fire goes straight to the source.","Compare ʿĀdiyāt 100:10 — wa ḥuṣṣila mā fi'ṣ-ṣudūr (\"what is in the chests is extracted\"). ʿĀdiyāt extracts the heart's contents; Humazah's Fire reaches the heart itself.","Afʾidah — broken plural of fuʾād.","Iṭṭalaʿa = to RISE OVER and to BECOME INFORMED — a fire that KNOWS what it burns.","Qadr 97:5: LIGHT rises (maṭlaʿ).","Humazah 104:7: FIRE rises (taṭṭaliʿ)."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّهَا عَلَيْهِم مُّؤْصَدَةٌ", tr:"Innahā ʿalayhim muʾṣadah", v:"8", root:"The \"closing / bolting shut / sealing\" family — awṣada = to shut a door firmly and bolt it; wiṣād = the bolt or covering of a door.", meaning:"\"Indeed, it will be closed down upon them.\"", magic:"You own this word EXACTLY — it's the final word of Balad 90:20.", grammar:"Inna + -hā (it, the Fire). Muʾṣadah = Form IV passive participle.", why:"Muʾṣadah focuses on the sealing, which is worse than the burning.", note:"IDENTICAL to Balad 90:20. Awṣada = TO BOLT A DOOR. Balad: UNTIE a neck (v.13) or be SEALED (v.20).", more:["Balad 90:20 — ʿalayhim nārun muʾṣadah (\"over them is a Fire bolted shut\"). Humazah 104:8 — innahā ʿalayhim muʾṣadah (\"it will be bolted shut over them\").","IDENTICAL WORD, same preposition (ʿalayhim), two surahs.","And in Balad, the contrast was: v.13 fakk (UNTIE a neck) → v.20 muʾṣadah (door BOLTED). Untie, or be sealed in. In Humazah, the contrast is: v.3 akhlada (he thought money made him ETERNAL, free) → v.8 muʾṣadah (SEALED in). He thought he was untouchable; he's enclosed.","And the horror is the LID, not the fire. A fire in the open has an escape direction. A fire with the door bolted has none.","Two recognized recitations: muʾṣadah / mūṣadah.","Humazah: he thought he was ETERNAL (v.3) → he's SEALED (v.8)."]},
  {ar:"فِي عَمَدٍ مُّمَدَّدَةٍ", tr:"Fī ʿamadin mumaddadah", v:"9", root:"ʿamad — plural of ʿamūd = COLUMNS / PILLARS — you own it from Fajr 89:7 (Irama dhāti'l-ʿimād, \"Iram of the lofty PILLARS\"); iʿtamada = to LEAN ON / rely on.", meaning:"\"in extended columns.\"", magic:"The final image, and it closes on a word you own from the most powerful civilization in the juzʾ.", grammar:"ʿamad — a collective plural of ʿamūd.", why:"ʿamad (pillars) — because pillars are humanity's symbol of permanence, and the surah ends by turning that symbol into a cage.", note:"Fajr 89:7: Iram's ʿIMĀD — pillars built to LAST FOREVER. Humazah 104:9: ʿAMAD — pillars holding the Fire SHUT. Same root.", more:["mumaddadah — the \"extending / stretching out\" family — madda = to stretch, extend; madd = the LENGTHENING in tajwīd!","Form II passive = stretched out long.","Fajr 89:7 — Irama dhāti'l-ʿIMĀD — Iram, the civilization of TOWERING PILLARS, \"the like of which was never created in the lands\" (89:8). Pillars as the symbol of permanence and power — built to last forever. Humazah 104:9 — fī ʿAMADIN mumaddadah — COLUMNS, stretched long — as the structure of the Fire.","SAME ROOT. The pillars people built to make themselves eternal (Fajr) — and the pillars that hold the eternal Fire shut (Humazah). And the man in v.3 thought his wealth made him eternal — he gets eternal pillars.","Classical readings of ʿamadin mumaddadah: (a) columns of fire stretched long; (b) the bars/pillars that hold the bolted doors (v.8) shut; (c) they are bound to long pillars. All share: long, enclosing, permanent.","And madd is your free tajwīd hook — the LENGTHENING of a vowel (madd ṭabīʿī, madd lāzim). Same root: mumaddadah = stretched long, like a madd held for six counts. The duration is built into the word.","And you own madda from Inshiqāq 84:3 — wa idha'l-arḍu muddat (\"when the earth is stretched out\").","Mumaddadah = Form II passive participle — intensive: stretched very long.","Fī = \"in\" (enclosed within).","He thought money made him ETERNAL (v.3) — he gets eternal pillars.","Madd = the LENGTHENING in tajwīd — mumaddadah = stretched long like a 6-count madd."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:105, name:"Al-Fīl", ar:"الفيل", en:"The Elephant", verses:5, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"ELEPHANT → birds → pebbles → CHEWED STRAW. And the general's name isn't worth mentioning.",
  special:["The biggest animal on earth is defeated by the smallest creatures in the sky — and ends up as chewed straw.","Look at the scale of the reversal: - v.1 — al-fīl — THE ELEPHANT, the largest land animal, the ancient world's tank. - v.3 — ṭayran — BIRDS, small, light, ordinary. - v.4 — ḥijārah — little STONES, pebbles. - v.5 — ʿaṣfin maʾkūl — CHEWED STRAW, the most worthless leftover of a harvest.","Heaviest → lightest → smallest → most worthless. Five verses, and the army goes from an elephant to cattle-fodder.","And notice: the army's commander (Abrahah) is NEVER NAMED. They're called aṣḥāb al-fīl — \"the people of the ELEPHANT.\" Named after their animal, not their king. Contempt by omission.","And this happened in the year the Prophet ﷺ was born — ʿĀm al-Fīl, the Year of the Elephant. The first audience's grandparents watched it.","--"],
  big:["The question (v.1) — have you not seen what your Lord did to the People of the Elephant?","The plot undone (v.2) — did He not make their scheme go astray?","The birds (v.3–4) — flocks upon flocks, pelting with stones of baked clay.","The ending (v.5) — like straw, chewed."],
  traps:["أَبَابِيلَ (v.3) — has NO commonly used SINGULAR. It only exists as waves of flocks. And it's a DIPTOTE (no tanwīn).","عَصْفٍ مَّأْكُولٍ (v.5) — not just straw — straw ALREADY EATEN/chewed. The leftover of a leftover. And elephants eat straw.","حِجَارَةٍ (v.4) — same root as ḥijr (WALL / intellect, Fajr 89:5). Don't treat \"stones\" as an isolated word.","أَلَمْ تَرَ (v.1) — \"SEE,\" though the Prophet ﷺ was born that year. Means \"know with eyewitness certainty.\" And tara drops its alif (jussive).","تَضْلِيلٍ (v.2) — Form II of ḍalla — the plan was SENT ASTRAY like a lost camel (ḍāllah, Ḍuḥā 93:7).","Abrahah is NEVER NAMED — the army is called after its ELEPHANT. Contempt by omission.","تَرْمِيهِم (v.4) — FEMININE singular verb for the birds — non-human plurals take feminine singular agreement.","سِجِّيلٍ (v.4) — commonly explained as Arabized Persian sang (stone) + gil (clay).","تَرْمِيهِم is PRESENT tense — continuous pelting, wave after wave.","فَجَعَلَهُمْ (v.5) — jaʿala takes TWO objects (\"made THEM into straw\"). Same structure as Aʿlā 87:5."],
  links:[{w:"أَلَمْ تَرَ كَيْفَ فَعَلَ رَبُّكَ (v.1)",e:"Fajr 89:6",t:"IDENTICAL six words — ʿĀd → the Elephant"},{w:"أَلَمْ تَرَ (v.1)",e:"عِلْمَ / عَيْنَ الْيَقِينِ (Takāthur 102:5–7)",t:"Knowledge so certain it counts as SIGHT"},{w:"أَصْحَابِ الْفِيلِ (v.1)",e:"أَصْحَابُ الْأُخْدُودِ (Burūj 85:4)",t:"Both named after their WEAPON — ditch / elephant"},{w:"أَصْحَابِ (v.1)",e:"الْمَيْمَنَةِ / الْمَشْأَمَةِ (Balad 90:18–19)",t:"The aṣḥāb al-X pattern"},{w:"كَيْدَهُمْ (v.2)",e:"يَكِيدُونَ كَيْدًا وَأَكِيدُ كَيْدًا (Ṭāriq 86:15–16)",t:"Ṭāriq states the principle; Fīl shows it"},{w:"تَضْلِيلٍ (v.2)",e:"ضَالًّا (Ḍuḥā 93:7)",t:"Same root — a LOST CAMEL"},{w:"فِي تَضْلِيلٍ (v.2)",e:"فِي خُسْرٍ (103:2) · فِي كَبَدٍ (90:4)",t:"IMMERSED in failure / loss / toil"},{w:"طَيْرًا (v.3)",e:"مُسْتَطِيرًا (Insān 76:7)",t:"Same root — and ṭāʾirah = AIRPLANE"},{w:"أَبَابِيلَ (v.3)",e:"حَدَائِقَ (78:32) · زَرَابِيُّ (88:16) · الْمَقَابِرَ (102:2)",t:"Diptotes — and abābīl has no singular"},{w:"تَرْمِيهِم (v.4)",e:"رَّجِيمٍ (Takwīr 81:25)",t:"Two Hajj-stoning associations — ramy / rajm"},{w:"حِجَارَةٍ (v.4)",e:"حِجْرٍ (Fajr 89:5)",t:"Same root — stones / WALL / intellect / Ḥijr Ismāʿīl"},{w:"فَجَعَلَهُمْ كَعَصْفٍ (v.5)",e:"فَجَعَلَهُ غُثَاءً (Aʿlā 87:5)",t:"Same structure — grass → debris / army → straw"},{w:"عَصْفٍ (v.5)",e:"الْعِهْنِ الْمَنفُوشِ (101:5) · الْحُطَمَةِ (104:4)",t:"The worthless-debris thread"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"أَلَمْ تَرَ كَيْفَ فَعَلَ رَبُّكَ", tr:"A-lam tara kayfa faʿala rabbuka", v:"1", root:"Tara — \"see\" (from raʾā) — here meaning \"know with the certainty of sight.\" faʿala — to do/act (fiʿl = an act; and in grammar, fiʿl = A VERB!).", meaning:"\"Have you not seen how your Lord dealt with the People of the Elephant?\"", magic:"You own this opening WORD-FOR-WORD from Fajr 89:6.", grammar:"أَلَمْ + jussive = a rhetorical question expecting \"yes.\" tara has lost its final alif (jussive of yarā).", why:"Tara (see) instead of taʿlam (know) — it elevates common knowledge to eyewitness certainty.", note:"IDENTICAL to Fajr 89:6 — a-lam tara kayfa faʿala rabbuka — only the target changes: ʿĀd → the Elephant.", more:["Fajr 89:6 — a-lam tara kayfa faʿala rabbuka bi-ʿĀd (\"have you not seen how your Lord dealt with ʿĀd?\") Fīl 105:1 — a-lam tara kayfa faʿala rabbuka bi-aṣḥābi'l-fīl","IDENTICAL six words — only the target changes. Fajr asked about an ancient civilization; Fīl asks about something sixty years old.","And \"SEEN\" is striking: the Prophet ﷺ was born that very year — he didn't literally watch it. So tara here = \"know so certainly it's as if you saw it.\" The event was so famous, so widely witnessed, that knowing it = seeing it.","Connect it to Takāthur 102:5–7 — ʿilm al-yaqīn (certainty by knowledge) vs ʿayn al-yaqīn (certainty by sight). Here the two merge: the knowledge is so certain the Qurʾān calls it seeing.","And kayfa (\"HOW\") — not \"THAT\" He dealt with them, but HOW. The method is the miracle.","You own this from Fajr 89:6, Ḍuḥā 93:6, Sharḥ 94:1.","\"SEEN\" — though he was born that year.","Knowledge so certain it counts as sight (Takāthur 102:5–7). And fiʿl = A VERB in grammar."]},
  {ar:"بِأَصْحَابِ الْفِيلِ", tr:"Bi-aṣḥābi'l-fīl", v:"1", root:"Aṣḥāb — plural of ṣāḥib = COMPANION / owner / people of.", meaning:"\"the People of the Elephant.\"", magic:"The naming is an insult, and it's precise.", grammar:"Iḍāfah (\"the companions of the elephant\"); genitive after bi-.", why:"Naming them by the elephant erases the general and makes their strength the punchline.", note:"ABRAHAH IS NEVER NAMED. They're called \"the ELEPHANT people\" — defined by their animal.", more:["You own it from Burūj 85:4 (aṣḥābu'l-ukhdūd), Balad 90:18–19 (aṣḥābu'l-maymanah / al-mashʾamah), Takwīr 81:22 (ṣāḥibukum).","The army belonged to Abrahah, the Abyssinian viceroy of Yemen, who marched to destroy the Kaʿbah. The Qurʾān never names him.","Instead: aṣḥāb al-FĪL — \"the ELEPHANT people.\" Defined by their ANIMAL. The one thing they thought made them unstoppable becomes their whole identity — and the thing that's about to become a joke.","The aṣḥāb al-X pattern across your surahs: - Burūj 85:4 — aṣḥābu'l-ukhdūd (the People of the DITCH — persecutors) - Balad 90:18 — aṣḥābu'l-maymanah (the people of the RIGHT) - Balad 90:19 — aṣḥābu'l-mashʾamah (the people of the LEFT) - Fīl 105:1 — aṣḥābu'l-fīl (the people of the ELEPHANT)","Burūj and Fīl are a matched pair: both named after the instrument of their aggression — a ditch of fire, an elephant. Both against believers / the House. Both undone.","Al-fīl is singular — though they had several, one is named — or the collective \"elephant-kind.\"","Burūj 85:4: aṣḥāb al-ukhdūd (the DITCH people).","Fīl 105:1: aṣḥāb al-fīl (the ELEPHANT people).","Named after their weapons.","And ʿĀm al-Fīl = the Prophet's ﷺ birth year."]},
  {ar:"كَيْدَهُمْ فِي تَضْلِيلٍ", tr:"Kaydahum fī taḍlīl", v:"2", root:"Kayd — the \"scheming / plotting / covert stratagem\" family — kāda = to plot; makīdah = a trap/snare.", meaning:"\"Did He not make their plot go astray?\"", magic:"Two roots you own, and the image is beautiful.", grammar:"أَلَمْ يَجْعَلْ = rhetorical question (expecting \"yes\").", why:"Taḍlīl (sent astray) rather than ibṭāl (nullified) — it pictures the plan losing its way, which is exactly what happened.", note:"Ḍāllah = A LOST CAMEL (Ḍuḥā 93:7). Their master plan became a lost camel — wandering, never arriving.", more:["taḍlīl — Form II verbal noun of ḍalla (to go astray / be lost) = to CAUSE to go astray, to send something off course, to make it come to nothing — the root of ḍāllah = A LOST CAMEL (Ḍuḥā 93:7!).","ONE — kayd (the PLOT). You own it from Ṭāriq 86:15–16 — innahum yakīdūna kaydā · wa akīdu kaydā (\"they plot a plot, and I plot a plot\"). Ṭāriq stated the principle: their kayd vs. His kayd. Fīl shows the principle in action: their kayd (march an army to destroy the Kaʿbah) vs. His (birds).","TWO — taḍlīl (sent astray). The root of ḍāllah — A LOST CAMEL. You own it from Ḍuḥā 93:7 — wa wajadaka ḍāllan fa-hadā (\"He found you searching/lost and guided you\").","So their master plan became a lost camel — wandering off, never arriving, going nowhere. They had a destination (the Kaʿbah); their plan simply lost its way.","And note fī taḍlīl — \"IN\" a state of being lost — immersed in failure, like fī khusr (ʿAṣr 103:2) and fī kabad (Balad 90:4).","Historical note: the lead elephant (named Maḥmūd in the reports) famously refused to advance toward Makkah, kneeling down, though it would walk any other direction. The plan literally went astray at the gate.","Taḍlīl = Form II verbal noun (tafʿīl) — intensive/causative.","Ṭāriq 86:15–16 stated it: their kayd vs His kayd.","And the lead elephant refused to walk toward Makkah."]},
  {ar:"طَيْرًا أَبَابِيلَ", tr:"ṭayran abābīl", v:"3", root:"ṭayr — BIRDS — from ṭāra = to fly; ṭāʾirah = an AIRPLANE (modern!); maṭār = an AIRPORT.", meaning:"\"And He sent against them birds in flocks.\"", magic:"Two hooks — one modern, one grammatical.", grammar:"Abābīl is a DIPTOTE (mamnūʿ min aṣ-ṣarf) — no tanwīn, fatḥah in the accusative.", why:"Abābīl (endless successive flocks, no singular) — conveys overwhelming numbers made of tiny units.", note:"Ṭāʾirah = AN AIRPLANE; maṭār = AN AIRPORT. Same root as ṭayr. ABĀBĪL HAS NO SINGULAR — it only exists as WAVES of flocks.", more:["And you own the root from Insān 76:7 (sharruhu mustaṭīrā — an evil that flies/spreads everywhere).","abābīl — FLOCKS, group after group, in successive waves — a word with no commonly used singular.","ONE — the ṭ-y-r root is everywhere in modern Arabic: ṭāʾirah = an AIRPLANE; maṭār = an AIRPORT; ṭayyār = a PILOT. Free hooks. And Insān 76:7's mustaṭīr — \"an evil that flies out in every direction.\"","TWO — abābīl is famous for having NO SINGULAR. You can't say \"one ababīl.\" The word only exists as a multitude — flocks arriving in waves, one after another.","And that's the point: the defense of the Kaʿbah wasn't one dramatic act — it was wave after wave of small things. The word can't even be singular, because the event wasn't.","Note ṭayran is INDEFINITE — \"birds\" — unspecified kind. Not eagles, not hawks — just birds. The ordinariness is the insult to the elephant.","And arsala (\"He SENT\") — the same verb as rasūl (messenger)! The birds were sent, like messengers.","You own diptotes from ḥadāʾiq (78:32), zarābiyy (88:16), ḥunafāʾ (98:5), maqābir (102:2).","The rescue wasn't one act; it was wave after wave of small things.","And arsala (sent) = same root as rasūl."]},
  {ar:"تَرْمِيهِم بِحِجَارَةٍ مِّن سِجِّيلٍ", tr:"Tarmīhim bi-ḥijāratin min sijjīl", v:"4", root:"Tarmī — the \"THROWING / pelting / shooting\" family — ramā = to throw; ramy al-jamarāt = THE STONING OF THE PILLARS IN HAJJ!; rāmī = an archer.", meaning:"\"striking them with stones of baked clay.\"", magic:"THREE hooks, and two of them are ones you physically perform.", grammar:"Tarmī — weak-final present (ر-م-ي), feminine singular because its subject ṭayr is a non-human plural (treated as feminine singular).", why:"Ramā (pelting) rather than qatala (killed) — it describes the method, which is the humiliation.", note:"RAMY AL-JAMARĀT — the STONING in Hajj. Same root, same act. Pilgrims still do ramy near the House the birds defended.", more:["ḥijārah — STONES — from ḥ-j-r — the root of ḥijr = a WALL / restraint / INTELLECT (Fajr 89:5!) and the Ḥijr of the Kaʿbah.","sijjīl — baked clay, stone-hard clay — commonly explained as an Arabized Persian compound: sang (stone) + gil (clay) = \"stone-clay.\"","ONE — ramā = the STONING IN HAJJ. In Hajj, pilgrims perform ramy al-jamarāt — throwing small pebbles at the pillars. Same root, same act: small stones thrown at a target. The birds did ramy on the army that came to destroy the House; pilgrims do ramy every year, near that same House.","TWO — ḥijārah (stones) shares a root with ḥijr (WALL / INTELLECT). Fajr 89:5 — hal fī dhālika qasamun li-dhī ḥijr (\"is there an oath here for one with sense?\" — literally one with a WALL). And Ḥijr Ismāʿīl — the semicircular wall of the Kaʿbah. Stones build walls; walls protect. The Kaʿbah's own Ḥijr, and the stones that defended it, share three letters.","THREE — sijjīl = \"stone-clay\" — clay baked so hard it behaves like stone. Small, but lethal.","And the verb tarmī is PRESENT tense — \"they KEEP pelting\" — ongoing, continuous, wave after wave (matching abābīl).","Bi-ḥijāratin — instrumental bi- (\"with stones\").","sijjīl carries the -īl rhyme.","Ḥijārah (stones) ← ḥ-j-r — same as ḥijr (WALL/INTELLECT, Fajr 89:5) and Ḥijr Ismāʿīl.","Sijjīl = sang + gil = STONE-CLAY (Persian origin)."]},
  {ar:"فَجَعَلَهُمْ كَعَصْفٍ مَّأْكُولٍ", tr:"Fa-jaʿalahum ka-ʿaṣfin maʾkūl", v:"5", root:"ʿaṣf — the dry leaves, husks, and stalks of grain crops — what's LEFT after the grain is harvested; straw, chaff.", meaning:"\"And He made them like straw that has been eaten / chewed.\"", magic:"The surah's final image — and the most perfectly calculated humiliation in the juzʾ.", grammar:"Ka- = kāf at-tashbīh (simile).", why:"ʿaṣfin maʾkūl — the most worthless organic matter imaginable, deliberately chosen to match the most powerful animal imaginable.", note:"ʿĀṣifah = A STORM — ʿaṣf = chaff the wind blows away. AND THE ELEPHANT EATS STRAW.", more:["From ʿaṣafa = the wind blowing violently: ʿāṣifah = A STORM / gale (ʿāṣifah thaljiyyah = a snowstorm!) — because chaff is what the wind blows away.","maʾkūl — EATEN — passive participle of akala (to eat).","Look at what ʿaṣfin maʾkūl actually describes: not just straw — straw that has already been EATEN. Chewed by cattle and spat out, or eaten through by insects. The leftover OF a leftover.","NOW TRACE THE WHOLE SURAH'S SCALE: v.1 — the ELEPHANT (largest, heaviest, mightiest) v.5 — CHEWED STRAW (lightest, emptiest, most worthless)","And the elephant is a HERBIVORE. It eats straw. The army of the elephant ends up as the kind of thing an elephant chews. The predator becomes the fodder.","And the ʿāṣifah (storm) connection: ʿaṣf is named after the wind that blows it away. So they became something the wind scatters.","The \"worthless debris\" thread across your surahs: - Aʿlā 87:5 — fa-jaʿalahu ghuthāʾan aḥwā (pasture → dark flood-debris) - Qāriʿah 101:5 — ka'l-ʿihni'l-manfūsh (mountains → carded wool) - Humazah 104:4 — al-ḥuṭamah (the Crusher — ḥuṭām = wreckage) - Fīl 105:5 — ʿaṣfin maʾkūl (an army → chewed straw)","And fa-jaʿalahu in Aʿlā 87:5 vs fa-jaʿalahum here — SAME verb + same structure: \"and He MADE it/them into [debris].\" Aʿlā did it to grass; Fīl does it to an army.","Maʾkūl = passive participle — \"having been eaten.\" Note fa-jaʿala = \"and He made [X into Y]\" — jaʿala takes two objects.","The army of the elephant became what an elephant chews.","The predator became the fodder.","Debris thread: grass → debris (87:5), mountains → wool (101:5), wreckage (104:4), army → chewed straw (105:5)."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:106, name:"Quraysh", ar:"قريش", en:"Quraysh", verses:4, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"WINTER → Yemen (yamīn, RIGHT). SUMMER → Shām (shimāl, LEFT). Two caravans, two hands, one House in the middle.",
  special:["Its two caravan routes go to the two countries whose names you learned in Surah Al-Balad.","V.2: riḥlata'sh-shitāʾi wa'ṣ-ṣayf — \"the journey of WINTER and of SUMMER.\"","Quraysh ran two great trade caravans a year: - WINTER → YEMEN (south, warm) - SUMMER → ASH-SHĀM (north, Greater Syria, cooler)","NOW RECALL BALAD 90:18–19: - aṣḥābu'l-maymanah — \"people of the RIGHT\" — from y-m-n, the root of AL-YAMAN - aṣḥābu'l-mashʾamah — \"people of the LEFT\" — from sh-ʾ-m, the root of ASH-SHĀM","The two destinations of Quraysh's caravans are the two countries named after the RIGHT hand and the LEFT hand. Face east from Makkah: Yemen is on your right, Shām on your left. Their entire economy ran between the two words Balad used for the saved and the damned.","--"],
  big:["The security (v.1–2) — for the familiar safety of Quraysh — their winter and summer journeys.","The command (v.3) — so let them worship the Lord of THIS House.","The two gifts (v.4) — who FED them against hunger and SECURED them against fear."],
  traps:["الشِّتَاءِ وَالصَّيْفِ (v.2) — Winter → YEMEN, Summer → SHĀM. And those two countries are named from the roots of maymanah (right) and mashʾamah (left) in Balad 90:18–19. Don't miss the geography.","آمَنَهُم (v.4) — same root as ĪMĀN (faith) and amīn (Tīn 95:3). The gift of security points at the response of faith.","V.4's TWO gifts (food + security) — match exactly the TWO requests in Ibrāhīm's duʿāʾ (2:126). Read v.4 as the answer to that prayer.","لِإِيلَافِ (v.1) — the li- has a DEBATED attachment — to Sūrat al-Fīl, to v.3, or as a lām of wonder. All classical.","إِيلَافِ — means the safety of the FAMILIAR, not military protection. The blessing became so routine it became invisible. Same root as muʾallif (author).","رَبَّ هَٰذَا الْبَيْتِ (v.3) — \"the LORD of this House,\" not the House itself. They honoured the building and filled it with idols.","إِيلَافِهِمْ (v.2) — a badal (appositive): v.2 restates and specifies v.1.","رِحْلَةَ (v.2) — SINGULAR though there were two journeys — read as a generic/system singular.","فَلْيَعْبُدُوا (v.3) — the fa- is CAUSAL (\"SO\") and the lām is a command — jussive, final nūn dropped.","قُرَيْشٍ (v.1) — a DIMINUTIVE (fuʿayl), from qarasha (to gather/trade) or qirsh (shark)."],
  links:[{w:"لِإِيلَافِ (v.1)",e:"Sūrat al-Fīl (105)",t:"The li- may link straight back — the House protected → its people prospered"},{w:"الشِّتَاءِ وَالصَّيْفِ (v.2)",e:"الْمَيْمَنَةِ / الْمَشْأَمَةِ (Balad 90:18–19)",t:"Winter → YEMEN (right), Summer → SHĀM (left) — the two hands"},{w:"رِحْلَةَ (v.2)",e:"",t:"Riḥlah jawwiyyah = a FLIGHT"},{w:"فَلْيَعْبُدُوا (v.3)",e:"فَلْيَنظُرِ (80:24, 86:5) · فَلْيَدْعُ (96:17)",t:"The lām of command"},{w:"هَٰذَا الْبَيْتِ (v.3)",e:"هَٰذَا الْبَلَدِ (90:1) · هَٰذَا الْبَلَدِ الْأَمِينِ (95:3)",t:"The ZOOM: this city → this secure city → this House"},{w:"أَطْعَمَهُم (v.4)",e:"يُطْعِمُونَ (76:8) · طَعَامِ (89:18) · إِطْعَامٌ (90:14)",t:"They were FED — yet wouldn't feed others"},{w:"مِّن جُوعٍ (v.4)",e:"لَا يُغْنِي مِن جُوعٍ (Ghāshiyah 88:7)",t:"Same phrase — Hell's food fails / Allah's provision succeeds"},{w:"آمَنَهُم (v.4)",e:"الْبَلَدِ الْأَمِينِ (Tīn 95:3) · أَمِينٍ (81:21)",t:"Tīn: the city is secure. Quraysh: WHO made it secure"},{w:"أَطْعَمَهُم + آمَنَهُم (v.4)",e:"Ibrāhīm's duʿāʾ (2:126)",t:"Two requests, two gifts — answered"},{w:"خَوْفٍ (v.4)",e:"خَشِيَ / يَخْشَىٰ (79:19 · 87:10 · 98:8)",t:"Plain fear (khawf) vs awe-fear (khashyah)"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"لِإِيلَافِ", tr:"Li-īlāf", v:"1, 2", root:"The \"FAMILIARITY / becoming accustomed / bonding\" family.", meaning:"\"For the [accustomed] security of Quraysh — their security in the journey of winter and summer.\"", magic:"The word is repeated in two consecutive verses, and its root makes it far richer than \"protection.\"", grammar:"The opening li- (\"FOR\") has a debated attachment — (a) to Sūrat al-Fīl (\"We did that FOR Quraysh's security\"), (b) to v.3 (\"FOR their security, LET THEM worship\"), (c) a lām of wonder (\"Marvel at Quraysh's security!\").", why:"īlāf (the familiar) rather than amn (security) — because the point is a blessing so regular it became invisible.", note:"MUʾALLIF = AN AUTHOR; ulfah = INTIMACY. Īlāf = the safety of THE FAMILIAR. A blessing so routine they STOPPED SEEING IT.", more:["Alifa = to become familiar with, to grow used to, to feel at home with; ulfah = INTIMACY, close companionship; taʾlīf = COMPOSING / authoring a book (putting things together so they belong!); muʾallif = an AUTHOR; and allafa bayna qulūbihim = \"He joined their hearts together.\" Īlāf (Form IV verbal noun) = making something familiar and secure; also, historically, the trade covenants/safe-passage pacts Quraysh made with surrounding powers.","Īlāf isn't \"safety\" in the military sense. It's the safety of THE FAMILIAR — routine, habit, the comfort of something that always works. Quraysh's caravans crossed bandit-filled desert, and nobody touched them — because they were the guardians of the Kaʿbah. The protection had become so routine they stopped noticing it.","And THAT is the surah's critique. The danger of īlāf is that familiarity breeds forgetfulness. You stop seeing the blessing because you've never been without it.","And the historical sense — īlāf = the PACTS of safe passage Quraysh negotiated (with Byzantium, Persia, Abyssinia, Yemen) — they thought their diplomacy kept them safe. The surah says: no — the House did.","Free hooks you use: muʾallif = an AUTHOR, taʾlīf = composing a book, ulfah = intimacy.","Why REPEATED (v.1 and v.2)? v.2 is a badal — it restates and specifies v.1: \"for their īlāf — [that is,] their īlāf of the winter and summer journey.\" Name it, then zoom in. Same technique as Takwīr 81:15–16, Aʿlā 87:18–19.","They credited their PACTS; the surah credits the HOUSE.","And the li- may link straight back to Sūrat al-Fīl."]},
  {ar:"قُرَيْشٍ", tr:"Quraysh", v:"1", root:"The tribe's name — a DIMINUTIVE form (fuʿayl) — with two classical etymologies: (a) from qarasha = to GATHER / to earn by trade (they gathered together and lived by commerce!); (b) from qirsh = a SHARK — the dominant creature of the sea, which eats and is not eaten.", meaning:"\"Quraysh\" — the Prophet's ﷺ own tribe, custodians of the Kaʿbah.", magic:"One of the very few tribes NAMED in the Qurʾān — and both etymologies fit the surah.", grammar:"Fuʿayl = the diminutive pattern (taṣghīr). Genitive by iḍāfah after īlāf.", why:"Naming the tribe directly makes the surah a personal letter, not a general sermon.", note:"Quraysh = a DIMINUTIVE — from qarasha (to GATHER/TRADE) or qirsh (a SHARK). Traders, and untouchable.", more:["Etymology (a) — qarasha = to gather/earn by trade: perfect for a surah about trade caravans. The name itself means \"traders.\"","Etymology (b) — qirsh = SHARK: the creature no one dares attack. Exactly like the caravans: untouched in the desert.","And the DIMINUTIVE (Quraysh, not Qarsh) adds affection or smallness — \"the little gatherers\" or \"the little shark.\" Small tribe, untouchable — but only because of the House.","Note: the modern coin qirsh (piastre) shares these letters — a fun echo for a trading tribe.","And notice the surah's intimacy: the Prophet ﷺ's OWN tribe is addressed by name — the people who knew the Kaʿbah best and owed it most.","One of the very few tribes NAMED in the Qurʾān — the Prophet's ﷺ own."]},
  {ar:"رِحْلَةَ الشِّتَاءِ وَالصَّيْفِ", tr:"Riḥlata'sh-shitāʾi wa'ṣ-ṣayf", v:"2", root:"Riḥlah — a JOURNEY / trip — from raḥala = to set out, to saddle up and depart; raḥl = a camel saddle.", meaning:"\"the journey of winter and summer.\"", magic:"The BEST cross-surah payoff in this batch — the geography connects to Balad.", grammar:"Riḥlata is accusative (object of the verbal noun īlāf).", why:"Naming both seasons makes the security year-round and therefore invisible.", note:"WINTER → YEMEN (yamīn, RIGHT). SUMMER → SHĀM (shimāl, LEFT).", more:["Riḥlah = a TRIP / voyage (modern: riḥlah jawwiyyah = a FLIGHT!).","The winter caravan went SOUTH to YEMEN (warm); the summer caravan went NORTH to ASH-SHĀM (cooler). Seasonal logistics, perfectly planned.","And Balad 90:18–19 gave you the roots: - maymanah (right/blessed) ← y-m-n ← AL-YAMAN - mashʾamah (left/ill-omened) ← sh-ʾ-m ← ASH-SHĀM","Facing the sunrise from Makkah: Yemen is on your RIGHT, Shām on your LEFT. The countries are literally named \"Right-land\" and \"Left-land.\"","So Quraysh's entire livelihood shuttled between the right and the left — the same two words Balad used for the companions of Paradise and the companions of Fire. And the question the surah leaves open: which hand will Quraysh end up on?","And the pairing shitāʾ / ṣayf is a ṭibāq (opposite-pair) forming a merism — \"winter and summer\" = ALL YEAR ROUND. The protection never took a season off.","Free hook: riḥlah = a TRIP — you'd use it for any journey.","Riḥlah is SINGULAR though there were two journeys — read as \"the journey-system of winter and summer,\" or a generic singular.","Balad 90:18–19: maymanah (Yemen's root) = the SAVED; mashʾamah (Shām's root) = the DAMNED.","Their trade ran between the two hands.","Riḥlah jawwiyyah = A FLIGHT.","Winter + summer = ALL YEAR."]},
  {ar:"فَلْيَعْبُدُوا رَبَّ هَٰذَا الْبَيْتِ", tr:"Fal-yaʿbudū rabba hādha'l-bayt", v:"3", root:"Yaʿbudū — the \"servitude / worship\" family — ʿabd = a SERVANT; ʿibādah = worship (servanthood).", meaning:"\"So let them worship the Lord of THIS House.\"", magic:"The demonstrative \"THIS\" is the key — and you've now seen it three times.", grammar:"لام الأمر on a third-person verb — \"LET them worship.\" Jussive: yaʿbudū drops its final nūn.", why:"\"the Lord of THIS House\" rather than \"Allah\" — it ties the obligation to the exact source of their prosperity.", note:"ZOOM: \"THIS city\" (90:1) → \"THIS SECURE city\" (95:3) → \"THIS HOUSE\" (106:3). Three surahs, closing in on the Kaʿbah.", more:["bayt — a HOUSE (al-Bayt al-Ḥarām, Bayt Allāh — the Kaʿbah; and bayt = a line of POETRY!).","The hādhā (\"THIS\") thread: - Balad 90:1 — lā uqsimu bi-hādha'l-balad (\"I swear by THIS city\") - Tīn 95:3 — wa hādha'l-baladi'l-amīn (\"by THIS secure city\") - Quraysh 106:3 — rabba hādha'l-bayt (\"the Lord of THIS House\")","Three surahs, and the camera ZOOMS IN: this CITY → this SECURE city → this HOUSE. From the town to the building at its centre.","And \"THIS\" makes it physical and immediate — the building they could see from their doorsteps, the one they guarded, profited from, and filled with idols. Not \"a house\" — the one right there.","And the fa- is CAUSAL: \"SO let them worship.\" Because of v.1–2. The argument is a straight line: the House protected you → your trade prospered → SO worship the House's Lord.","And note the precise object: \"the LORD of this House\" — not the House itself. They honoured the building and worshipped idols inside it. The surah redirects: the House is not the point; its Owner is.","The lām of command (fal-yaʿbudū) — you own it from ʿAbasa 80:24 (fal-yanẓur), Ṭāriq 86:5 (fal-yanẓur), ʿAlaq 96:17 (fal-yadʿu).","The fa- is causal (\"so / therefore\").","\"The LORD of this House\" — not the House itself.","The fa- = \"SO\" — because of everything before.","And bayt also = a line of POETRY."]},
  {ar:"أَطْعَمَهُم مِّن جُوعٍ", tr:"Aṭʿamahum min jūʿ", v:"4", root:"Aṭʿama — Form IV of ṭaʿima (to taste/eat) = to FEED someone; ṭaʿām = FOOD; maṭʿam = A RESTAURANT (a place of eating!).", meaning:"\"who fed them against hunger.\"", magic:"The first gift — and it answers hunger threads from FOUR surahs.", grammar:"Form IV (aṭʿama) = causative (to cause to eat = to feed).", why:"Aṭʿama (He fed) with Allah as subject — it credits the food to Him, not to the caravans.", note:"Maṭʿam = A RESTAURANT; majāʿah = FAMINE. Ghāshiyah 88:7: Hell's food does NOT protect min jūʿ.", more:["jūʿ — HUNGER (jāʾiʿ = hungry; majāʿah = FAMINE).","Makkah sits in a barren valley — no farms, no rivers, nothing grows. Yet its people ate well, because the caravans (v.2) brought the world's food to them. Food in a place that produces none.","The hunger/feeding thread you own: - Insān 76:8 — wa yuṭʿimūna'ṭ-ṭaʿāma ʿalā ḥubbihi (the righteous FEED others despite loving the food) - Ghāshiyah 88:7 — lā yusminu wa lā yughnī min jūʿ (Hell's \"food\" does NOT avail against HUNGER) - Fajr 89:18 — wa lā taḥāḍḍūna ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn (you don't urge FEEDING the poor) - Balad 90:14 — aw iṭʿāmun fī yawmin dhī masghabah (FEEDING on a day of HUNGER — the steep climb) - Quraysh 106:4 — aṭʿamahum min jūʿ (HE fed THEM against hunger)","And here's the sting: Allah FED Quraysh against hunger. Fajr 89:18 said they wouldn't even urge each other to feed the poor. Balad 90:14 said feeding the hungry is the steep climb they didn't attempt. They received the very thing they refused to pass on.","And compare Ghāshiyah 88:7 exactly: lā yughnī MIN JŪʿ (Hell's food doesn't protect against hunger) vs. aṭʿamahum MIN JŪʿ (Allah did protect them against hunger). Same phrase min jūʿ, opposite outcome.","Free hook: maṭʿam = A RESTAURANT.","Min here = \"from / against\" — rescuing them FROM hunger.","Jūʿin is indefinite — \"from (any) hunger.\"","Quraysh 106:4: Allah DID feed them min jūʿ.","And Fajr 89:18 / Balad 90:14: they wouldn't feed the hungry — yet THEY were fed."]},
  {ar:"وَآمَنَهُم مِّنْ خَوْفٍ", tr:"Wa āmanahum min khawf", v:"4", root:"āmana — Form IV of amina (to be safe) = to MAKE SAFE, to grant security.", meaning:"\"and made them safe from fear.\"", magic:"The final gift — and it completes the most important thread in the last three surahs.", grammar:"Form IV (āmana) = causative (to cause to be safe).", why:"āmana (made secure) — because amn and īmān share a root, the gift points straight at the response.", note:"Tīn 95:3: the city is AMĪN (secure). Quraysh 106:4: ĀMANAHUM — HE made it secure.", more:["The root of amn (security), amānah (a trust), ĪMĀN (faith), al-Amīn (the Prophet's ﷺ title), and āmīn.","khawf — FEAR (plain fear — compare khashyah, awe-filled fear).","TĪN 95:3 — wa hādha'l-baladi'l-AMĪN — \"by this SECURE city.\" QURAYSH 106:4 — wa ĀMANAHUM min khawf — \"and He MADE THEM SECURE from fear.\"","Tīn called the city amīn (secure). Quraysh tells you WHO made it secure. Same root, and the second answers the first.","AND THIS IS IBRĀHĪM'S DUʿĀʾ, ANSWERED. When Ibrāhīm (peace be upon him) left his family in this barren valley, he prayed (2:126): \"My Lord, make this a SECURE land (balad āmin) and provide its people with FRUITS.\" TWO requests: SECURITY and FOOD. And Quraysh 106:4 lists exactly TWO gifts: aṭʿamahum (He FED them) + āmanahum (He made them SECURE). Ibrāhīm asked for two things; the surah confirms both were granted — to his descendants, who then forgot Whom to thank.","And the root's link to faith is the final, quiet point: amn (security) and īmān (faith) are the SAME ROOT. The One who gave them amn is asking for īmān in return. Safety and faith are one word-family; the gift and its proper response share three letters.","And āmīn — you say it after al-Fātiḥah: \"make it secure / so be it.\" Free hook.","Min khawfin — indefinite: \"from (any) fear.\" Perfectly parallel with min jūʿin: two Form IV verbs, two min's, two indefinite threats.","IBRĀHĪM'S DUʿĀʾ (2:126) asked for TWO things: SECURITY + FOOD.","Quraysh 106:4 lists TWO gifts: FED + SECURED.","Amn (security) and ĪMĀN (faith) = SAME ROOT.","The gift of safety asks for the response of faith."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:107, name:"Al-Māʿūn", ar:"الماعون", en:"Small Kindnesses", verses:7, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"ʿAN, not FĪ. Forgetting IN prayer = sujūd as-sahw. Forgetting the prayer itself = wayl.",
  special:["It says \"woe to those who PRAY\" — and one preposition is the difference between you and them.","V.4: fa-waylun li'l-muṣallīn — \"So woe to those who pray —\" v.5: alladhīna hum ʿAN ṣalātihim sāhūn — \"who are heedless OF their prayer.\"","The word is ʿAN (of/away from), not FĪ (in). A famous early observation (reported from Anas and others): \"Praise be to Allah it says ʿan ṣalātihim and not fī ṣalātihim.\" - sāhūn FĪ ṣalātihim = absent-minded IN their prayer — a lapse every human has (which is why sujūd as-sahw exists!). - sāhūn ʿAN ṣalātihim = heedless OF their prayer — neglecting it, delaying it, not caring about it at all.","One preposition separates the normal human from the condemned.","And the surah's structure is a mirror: - v.1–3 — failure toward PEOPLE (shoves the orphan, doesn't urge feeding the poor) - v.4–7 — failure toward ALLAH (heedless prayer, showing off) — ending back on people (withholding small kindnesses)","--"],
  big:["The question (v.1) — have you seen the one who denies the Reckoning?","The answer is behaviour (v.2–3) — he shoves the orphan, doesn't urge feeding the poor.","The shock (v.4–6) — woe to those who pray — heedlessly, for show.","The smallest failure (v.7) — they withhold even the small things."],
  traps:["عَن صَلَاتِهِمْ (v.5) — ʿAN, not FĪ. Fī = forgetting IN prayer (human, fixed by sujūd as-sahw); ʿan = neglecting prayer itself (condemned). One preposition is the whole verse.","فَوَيْلٌ لِّلْمُصَلِّينَ (v.4) — NEVER quote it alone. The meaning is completed in v.5–6.","الدِّينِ (v.1) — means the RECKONING here (dayn = debt). In Kāfirūn 109:6, two surahs later, dīn = RELIGION. Watch the switch.","يَحُضُّ (v.3) — Form I (HE doesn't urge) vs Fajr 89:18's Form VI (you don't urge EACH OTHER). Same verse, sharpened to the individual.","يَدُعُّ (v.2) — means SHOVE VIOLENTLY, not \"neglect.\" Same verb as being shoved into Hell (52:13).","يُرَاءُونَ (v.6) — Form III of raʾā = arranging to BE SEEN (riyāʾ). Hamzah-carrying root.","الْمَاعُونَ (v.7) — SMALL household items (pot, bucket, salt, axe), not \"charity\" in general. (Some also read it as zakāh.)","يَمْنَعُونَ (v.7) — same root as mamnūʿ min aṣ-ṣarf (DIPTOTE). A free grammar anchor.","فَذَٰلِكَ (v.2) — the FAR demonstrative (\"THAT one\") — distancing, contemptuous.","سَاهُونَ (v.5) — a PARTICIPLE = a settled state, not a single lapse."],
  links:[{w:"أَرَأَيْتَ (v.1)",e:"أَرَأَيْتَ ×3 (ʿAlaq 96:9, 11, 13)",t:"ʿAlaq's man STOPS prayer; Māʿūn's NEGLECTS his own"},{w:"يُكَذِّبُ بِالدِّينِ (v.1)",e:"82:9 · 95:7",t:"THREE surahs — dīn = RECKONING"},{w:"يَدُعُّ الْيَتِيمَ (v.2)",e:"76:8 · 89:17 · 90:15 · 93:9",t:"The orphan ladder — feed → honour → climb → don't crush → shove"},{w:"يَدُعُّ (v.2)",e:"يُدَعُّونَ (52:13)",t:"Same verb for being SHOVED into Hell"},{w:"لَا يَحُضُّ عَلَىٰ طَعَامِ الْمِسْكِينِ (v.3)",e:"لَا تَحَاضُّونَ عَلَىٰ طَعَامِ الْمِسْكِينِ (Fajr 89:18)",t:"Near-verbatim — Form VI (society) → Form I (individual)"},{w:"فَوَيْلٌ (v.4)",e:"83:1 · 83:10 · 104:1",t:"Woe to cheaters, deniers, slanderers, careless prayers"},{w:"سَاهُونَ (v.5)",e:"تَلَهَّىٰ (80:10) · أَلْهَاكُمُ (102:1)",t:"Three heedlessness words"},{w:"يُرَاءُونَ (v.6)",e:"لَمْ يَرَهُ (90:7) · يَرَىٰ (96:14) · يَرَهُ (99:7) · لَتَرَوُنَّ (102:6)",t:"The seeing thread — wants to be UNSEEN in sin / SEEN in worship"},{w:"يُرَاءُونَ (v.6)",e:"فَصَلِّ لِرَبِّكَ (Kawthar 108:2)",t:"The cure in the very next surah"},{w:"الْمَاعُونَ (v.7)",e:"مِثْقَالَ ذَرَّةٍ (Zalzalah 99:7)",t:"An atom's weight — in daily life"},{w:"يَمْنَعُونَ (v.7)",e:"Mamnūʿ min aṣ-ṣarf",t:"The grammar term for DIPTOTE = \"withheld\""}],
  words:[
  {ar:"أَرَأَيْتَ الَّذِي يُكَذِّبُ بِالدِّينِ", tr:"A-raʾayta'lladhī yukadhdhibu bi'd-dīn", v:"1", root:"A-raʾayta — \"have you seen…?\" = an idiom: \"consider / tell me about.\" dīn — here: the RECKONING (from dayn, a DEBT — Settlement Day).", meaning:"\"Have you seen the one who denies the Reckoning?\"", magic:"The surah defines a theological crime by a social behaviour — and that's revolutionary.", grammar:"أَرَأَيْتَ = idiom (\"tell me\"). kadhdhaba bi- = \"deny something\" (fixed preposition).", why:"Defining the denier through v.2–3's actions refuses to let belief stay abstract.", note:"\"Who denies the Reckoning?\" → \"the one who SHOVES THE ORPHAN.\" Behaviour IS creed.", more:["You'd expect the description of a Reckoning-denier to be about beliefs: he doubts resurrection, mocks scripture. Instead v.2: \"That is the one who SHOVES THE ORPHAN.\"","The Qurʾān identifies denial of the Afterlife by how you treat orphans. Because if you really believed in Settlement Day, you couldn't push a child away. Your behaviour IS your creed.","You own a-raʾayta from ʿAlaq 96:9, 11, 13 — three unanswered \"have you seen?\"s about the man who forbids prayer. Next surah-group, same idiom: ʿAlaq's man stops others' prayer; Māʿūn's man neglects his own.","And yukadhdhibu bi'd-dīn you own EXACTLY: - Infiṭār 82:9 — bal tukadhdhibūna bi'd-dīn (you deny the Reckoning) - Tīn 95:7 — fa-mā yukadhdhibuka baʿdu bi'd-dīn (what makes you deny the Reckoning?) - Māʿūn 107:1 — alladhī yukadhdhibu bi'd-dīn","Dīn here = the RECKONING. Not \"religion\" (as in Bayyinah 98:5 and Kāfirūn 109:6). Watch the sense switch two surahs from now.","Yukadhdhibu bi'd-dīn: Infiṭār 82:9, Tīn 95:7, Māʿūn 107:1. Dīn = RECKONING here (dayn = debt).","And a-raʾayta from ʿAlaq 96:9."]},
  {ar:"يَدُعُّ الْيَتِيمَ", tr:"Yaduʿʿu'l-yatīm", v:"2", root:"The \"SHOVING violently / pushing roughly away\" family — daʿʿa = to push someone forcefully, to repel with harsh shoves — used elsewhere for people being shoved into Hell (52:13: yawma yudaʿʿūna ilā nāri jahannama daʿʿā).", meaning:"\"That is the one who shoves the orphan away.\"", magic:"The verb is PHYSICAL and VIOLENT — and it's the very verb used for how sinners are shoved into Hell.", grammar:"Doubled root (د-ع-ع) — yaduʿʿu — the doubled letter merges with a shaddah.", why:"Daʿʿa (violent shoving) — to name the cruelty physically, so it can't be softened.", note:"Daʿʿa = TO SHOVE VIOLENTLY — the SAME VERB used for sinners SHOVED into Hell (52:13). He shoves; he'll be shoved.", more:["Daʿʿa isn't \"neglects\" or \"ignores.\" It's to SHOVE, to drive away roughly with your hands. The orphan comes asking — and gets pushed.","And elsewhere (52:13), the SAME VERB describes sinners being shoved into Jahannam. The man who shoves the orphan away will himself be shoved. Measure for measure.","The orphan thread across FIVE surahs — every possible attitude: - Insān 76:8 — yuṭʿimūna — the righteous FEED him despite their own hunger - Fajr 89:17 — lā tukrimūna — you don't HONOUR him - Balad 90:15 — yatīman dhā maqrabah — feeding him is THE STEEP CLIMB - Ḍuḥā 93:9 — fa-lā taqhar — do not CRUSH him - Māʿūn 107:2 — yaduʿʿu — he SHOVES him","Feed → honour → climb for → don't crush → (he) shoves. Māʿūn is the lowest rung.","Note fa-dhālika (\"SO THAT is the one\") — dhālika is the FAR demonstrative (\"that one, over there\") — distancing, contemptuous.","Present tense = HABITUAL: this is what he does.","Orphan ladder: FEED (76:8) → HONOUR (89:17) → CLIMB (90:15) → don't CRUSH (93:9) → he SHOVES (107:2)."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا يَحُضُّ عَلَىٰ طَعَامِ الْمِسْكِينِ", tr:"Wa lā yaḥuḍḍu ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn", v:"3", root:"The \"URGING / spurring on\" family — ḥaḍḍa = to urge strongly, to incite someone toward something.", meaning:"\"and does not urge the feeding of the poor.\"", magic:"You own this verse almost WORD-FOR-WORD from Fajr 89:18 — but one change in FORM tells you something.", grammar:"Form I (ḥaḍḍa) vs Fajr's Form VI (taḥāḍḍa). Doubled root — note yaḥuḍḍu.", why:"Moving from Form VI to Form I personalizes the charge.", note:"Fajr 89:18 = taḥāḍḍūna (Form VI — \"you don't urge EACH OTHER\"). Māʿūn 107:3 = yaḥuḍḍu (Form I — \"HE doesn't urge\").", more:["Fajr 89:18 — wa lā taḥāḍḍūna ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn — Form VI: \"you don't urge ONE ANOTHER\" (a society's failure) Māʿūn 107:3 — wa lā yaḥuḍḍu ʿalā ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn — Form I: \"HE doesn't urge\" (an individual's failure)","Same root, same object, same preposition. Fajr indicted the COMMUNITY; Māʿūn indicts the PERSON. No hiding in the crowd.","And remember Fajr's insight: the bar is shockingly low. It doesn't say \"he doesn't FEED the poor\" — \"he doesn't even URGE it.\" Urging costs NOTHING. He fails at the free part.","And note the wording: ṭaʿāmi'l-miskīn — \"the FOOD OF the poor.\" Not \"food FOR the poor\" — food that BELONGS to them. Their right, held in your hands. (Compare Bayyinah/ʿAṣr's ḥaqq — what is owed.)","Ṭaʿām al-miskīn = food that BELONGS to the poor."]},
  {ar:"فَوَيْلٌ لِّلْمُصَلِّينَ", tr:"Fa-waylun li'l-muṣallīn", v:"4", root:"Wayl — a cry of doom / woe — you own it from Muṭaffifīn 83:1, 10 and Humazah 104:1. muṣallīn — those who PRAY — Form II participle of ṣallā.", meaning:"\"So woe to those who pray —\"", magic:"One of the most shocking phrases in the Qurʾān — and it's deliberately left hanging for a moment.", grammar:"Waylun — indefinite noun starting a sentence, allowed because it's a curse/supplication (as in Humazah 104:1).", why:"Muṣallīn (people who pray) — the most unexpected target, chosen precisely to shock.", note:"\"WOE… to those who PRAY\" — a deliberate shock. Never quote it without v.5.", more:["\"WOE… to those who PRAY.\" Read alone, it's almost impossible. Woe has been for cheaters (83:1), deniers (83:10), slanderers (104:1). Now: people who PRAY?","The suspense is the teaching. v.5–6 resolve it: woe to those who pray — heedlessly, and for show. But for one breath, you hear \"woe to the praying\" and it shocks you into checking your own prayer.","Note: a well-known warning about this verse — never quote v.4 alone, just as you'd never quote \"do not approach prayer\" (4:43) without its continuation. The meaning lives in v.5.","The wayl thread now covers FOUR kinds of people: - Muṭaffifīn 83:1 — the CHEATERS (short-measuring) - Muṭaffifīn 83:10 — the DENIERS - Humazah 104:1 — the SLANDERERS - Māʿūn 107:4 — the CARELESS PRAYERS","And the fa- (\"SO woe\") connects it to v.1–3 — the same man who shoves the orphan is the man whose prayer is empty. The two halves are one portrait.","Wayl targets: CHEATERS (83:1), DENIERS (83:10), SLANDERERS (104:1), CARELESS PRAYERS (107:4). And the fa- joins them to the orphan-shover."]},
  {ar:"عَن صَلَاتِهِمْ سَاهُونَ", tr:"ʿan ṣalātihim sāhūn", v:"5", root:"The \"heedlessness / forgetful neglect / inattention\" family — sahā = to be heedless, absent-minded, to forget.", meaning:"\"who are heedless OF their prayer.\"", magic:"It says ʿAN ṣalātihim, NOT FĪ ṣalātihim. - sāhūn FĪ ṣalātihim = absent-minded WITHIN prayer — losing count, forgetting a rakʿah, your mind wandering. Every human does this.", grammar:"Sahā ʿan = heedless OF/AWAY FROM; sahā fī = absent-minded IN. The preposition is the meaning.", why:"Sahw (heedlessness) with ʿan — precise enough to exclude the ordinary human lapse.", note:"ʿAN, NOT FĪ. Fī = forgetting IN prayer (everyone does it). ʿAn = neglecting the prayer ITSELF.", more:["Sujūd as-SAHW = the PROSTRATION OF FORGETFULNESS (the two extra prostrations you make when you forget something in prayer!).","THE MOST IMPORTANT PREPOSITION IN THE JUZʾ.","The Prophet ﷺ himself did it — which is WHY sujūd as-sahw was legislated.","- sāhūn ʿAN ṣalātihim = heedless OF prayer itself — delaying it out of its time, skipping it, treating it as unimportant.","A famous early remark (reported from Anas, and others): \"Praise be to Allah who said 'ʿan ṣalātihim' and not 'fī ṣalātihim.'\" Because if it had said fī, every one of us would be condemned.","And the free hook is perfect: SUJŪD AS-SAHW. The same root names the forgiven kind of forgetting (with a built-in fix) and the condemned kind (without one). One gets two prostrations; the other gets wayl.","Compare ʿAbasa 80:10 — fa-anta ʿanhu talahhā — distracted FROM the blind man (ʿan). And Takāthur 102:1 — alhākum — the rivalry DISTRACTED you. Three heedlessness words: lahw twice, sahw here.","Note: sāhūn is a PARTICIPLE — a settled STATE, not a one-off lapse.","Sāhūn — active participle, plural (weak-final: sāhūna).","SUJŪD AS-SAHW — same root: the forgiven forgetting gets 2 prostrations; the condemned kind gets wayl.","\"Praise Allah it says ʿan and not fī.\""]},
  {ar:"يُرَاءُونَ", tr:"Yurāʾūn", v:"6", root:"The \"SEEING\" family — raʾā = to see; Form III rāʾā = to make sure others SEE you, to act FOR an audience → riyāʾ = SHOWING OFF / ostentation — called \"the minor shirk\" in hadith.", meaning:"\"those who make a show [of their deeds].\"", magic:"The SEEING thread reaches its darkest point — and it's a grammatical inversion.", grammar:"Form III present (yurāʾūna). Note the hamzah (ء) — the root is ر-أ-ي, and Form III produces yurāʾūn.", why:"Riyāʾ (performing to be seen) — names the corruption of an act that looks perfect from outside.", note:"RIYĀʾ = arranging to BE SEEN — \"the minor shirk.\" INVERSION: Balad 90:7's man wants to be UNSEEN in SIN; Māʿūn 107:6's man wants to be SEEN in WORSHIP.", more:["Riyāʾ literally means \"arranging to BE SEEN.\" The worship is real in form, but its audience is people, not Allah.","Track the seeing thread through your surahs: - Balad 90:7 — a-yaḥsabu an lam yarahu aḥad (\"does he think NO ONE SEES him?\" — he wants to be UNSEEN in sin) - ʿAlaq 96:14 — bi-anna'llāha yarā (\"ALLAH SEES\") - Zalzalah 99:7–8 — yarah (\"he will SEE\" his deeds) - Takāthur 102:6–7 — la-tarawunna (\"you WILL SEE\" the Blaze) - Māʿūn 107:6 — yurāʾūn (he wants PEOPLE to see him in worship)","THE INVERSION: Balad's man wanted to be UNSEEN while sinning. Māʿūn's man wants to be SEEN while worshipping. Both have forgotten the one Viewer who matters (ʿAlaq 96:14). Hide the sin from people, display the prayer to people — the same blindness, two directions.","And Form III often carries a sense of directing an action at others: rāʾā = \"to show [yourself] TO people.\"","The antidote is in the very next surah: Kawthar 108:2 — fa-ṣalli LI-RABBIKA — \"pray FOR YOUR LORD\" — to Him, not to the audience.","No object stated — they show off, full stop.","Both forget ʿAlaq 96:14: \"Allah SEES.\" And the cure is next surah: fa-ṣalli LI-RABBIKA (108:2)."]},
  {ar:"وَيَمْنَعُونَ الْمَاعُونَ", tr:"Wa yamnaʿūna'l-māʿūn", v:"7", root:"Yamnaʿūn — the \"preventing / withholding\" family — manaʿa = to withhold, to prevent.", meaning:"\"and they withhold [even] small kindnesses.\"", magic:"The surah is NAMED after the SMALLEST thing in it — and that's the whole point.", grammar:"Yamnaʿūna — present = habitual.", why:"Naming the surah after māʿūn makes the smallest kindness the measure of the whole person.", note:"MĀʿŪN = a borrowed POT, BUCKET, SALT, AXE. The surah is NAMED after the smallest kindness.", more:["Mamnūʿ = FORBIDDEN (\"mamnūʿ at-tadkhīn\" = no smoking!); and mamnūʿ min aṣ-ṣarf = A DIPTOTE (literally \"PREVENTED from full declension\" — the grammar term you've been learning all juzʾ!).","māʿūn — small, everyday household items lent between neighbours — a pot, a bucket, salt, an axe, a needle, water, fire to light a lamp — from maʿn = something small, easy, ordinary.","Al-Māʿūn = the little things — the cup of sugar, the borrowed ladder, the pinch of salt. Things that cost nearly nothing to lend. Classical scholars list: a pot, a bucket, an axe, a sieve, water, salt, fire. (Some also read it as zakāh.)","And the surah's descent is now complete: He denies the Reckoning (v.1) → shoves the orphan (v.2) → doesn't urge feeding the poor (v.3) → neglects his prayer (v.5) → shows off (v.6) → won't even lend a pot (v.7).","The last item is the SMALLEST — and that's what makes it damning. A man who won't lend salt to a neighbour has a heart that's closed at every level. The tiny refusal reveals everything.","And notice the parallel with Zalzalah 99:7–8 — mithqāla dharrah — an ATOM'S weight is counted. Māʿūn shows you what atom-sized deeds look like in daily life: lending a bucket.","And the grammar hook is a gift: MAMNŪʿ min aṣ-ṣarf = a DIPTOTE — a noun \"WITHHELD\" from full case-endings. You've learned ḥadāʾiq, zarābiyy, abābīl, maqābir as diptotes — every one of them is mamnūʿ, \"withheld,\" the same word as v.7.","The rhyme: dīn, yatīm, miskīn, muṣallīn, sāhūn, yurāʾūn, māʿūn — closing on -ūn.","Al-māʿūn — definite: \"THE [known] small necessities.\" Note: manaʿa takes the thing withheld as a direct object.","MAMNŪʿ min aṣ-ṣarf = A DIPTOTE — \"WITHHELD\" from full declension.","Descent: deny → shove → don't urge → neglect prayer → show off → won't lend a pot.","Zalzalah 99:7's atom, in daily life."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:108, name:"Al-Kawthar", ar:"الكوثر", en:"Abundance", verses:3, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"Māʿūn: won't lend a POT. Kawthar: was given an OCEAN. Three verses, ten words, the whole answer.",
  special:["It is the SHORTEST surah in the Qurʾān — and it is the exact mirror-image of the surah before it.","Classical scholars (notably ar-Rāzī) observed that Kawthar answers Māʿūn point by point:","Every disease in Māʿūn has its medicine in Kawthar. Read them together; they're one argument across two surahs.","--"],
  big:["The gift (v.1) — We have given you al-Kawthar.","The response (v.2) — so pray to your Lord, and sacrifice.","The reversal (v.3) — your hater — HE is the cut-off one."],
  traps:["Kawthar is the MIRROR of Māʿūn — read them together: withhold/give, heedless/pray, for show/for your Lord, deny/cut off.","الْكَوْثَرَ (v.1) — same root as at-takāthur (102:1). Fawʿal = the most extreme \"abundance.\" Primary hadith meaning: a river in Paradise.","أَعْطَيْنَاكَ (v.1) — PAST tense — the fulfilment of Ḍuḥā 93:5's FUTURE yuʿṭīka.","هُوَ (v.3) — the SEPARATING PRONOUN = \"HE, and not you.\" Not a filler word.","الْأَبْتَرُ (v.3) — originally a TAILLESS animal — their insult, returned to them.","وَانْحَرْ (v.2) — SACRIFICE (at the naḥr, the throat) — Yawm an-Naḥr = ʿĪd al-Aḍḥā. (A minority reads it as hand placement in prayer.)","فَصَلِّ (v.2) — imperative of a weak-final verb: the final yāʾ is DROPPED.","لِرَبِّكَ (v.2) — the lām specifies the AUDIENCE — the precise cure for riyāʾ.","شَانِئَكَ (v.3) — hamzah on the middle letter (ش-ن-أ). Bitter hatred, not mere dislike.","The shortest surah — three verses, ten words — and it ends on the word they used to mock him."],
  links:[{w:"The whole surah",e:"Māʿūn 107",t:"Point-by-point mirror — every disease, its cure"},{w:"أَعْطَيْنَاكَ (v.1)",e:"يُعْطِيكَ (Ḍuḥā 93:5)",t:"\"He WILL give you\" → \"We HAVE given you\""},{w:"أَعْطَيْنَاكَ (v.1)",e:"عَطَاءً (78:36) · أَعْطَىٰ (92:5)",t:"The ʿaṭāʾ thread"},{w:"الْكَوْثَرَ (v.1)",e:"التَّكَاثُرُ (Takāthur 102:1)",t:"SAME ROOT — chasing more / given the most"},{w:"فَصَلِّ (v.2)",e:"75:31 · 87:15 · 96:10 · 107:4",t:"The ṣalāh thread RESOLVES"},{w:"لِرَبِّكَ (v.2)",e:"يُرَاءُونَ (Māʿūn 107:6)",t:"For your LORD vs. for SHOW"},{w:"فَصَلِّ (v.2)",e:"فَلْيَعْبُدُوا (Quraysh 106:3)",t:"Causal fa- — you were given → so worship"},{w:"وَانْحَرْ (v.2)",e:"يَمْنَعُونَ الْمَاعُونَ (Māʿūn 107:7)",t:"Won't lend a pot → sacrifice a camel"},{w:"فَصَلِّ... وَانْحَرْ (v.2)",e:"الصَّلَاةَ... الزَّكَاةَ (Bayyinah 98:5)",t:"The two limbs — to Allah, to people"},{w:"شَانِئَكَ (v.3)",e:"أَصْحَابِ الْفِيلِ (105:1)",t:"Enemies UNNAMED — only the trait"},{w:"هُوَ الْأَبْتَرُ (v.3)",e:"هِيَ الْمَأْوَىٰ (79:39) · أُولَٰئِكَ هُمْ (98:6)",t:"The separating pronoun = restriction"},{w:"الْأَبْتَرُ (v.3)",e:"وَرَفَعْنَا لَكَ ذِكْرَكَ (Sharḥ 94:4)",t:"\"Cut off\"? His mention was RAISED"},{w:"الْأَبْتَرُ (v.3)",e:"الْأَعْمَىٰ (ʿAbasa 80:2)",t:"Afʿal pattern for physical traits"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"إِنَّا أَعْطَيْنَاكَ", tr:"Innā aʿṭaynāka", v:"1", root:"The \"GIVING\" family — aʿṭā = to give; ʿaṭāʾ = a GIFT (Nabaʾ 78:36); ʿaṭiyyah = a present.", meaning:"\"Indeed, We have given you…\"", magic:"The tense is a promise kept — and you own the promise from four surahs ago.", grammar:"Form IV past (aʿṭā) with -nā (We) + -ka (you). aʿṭā takes two objects: you (-ka) and the gift (al-kawthar).", why:"The past tense turns a promise into a completed fact — at the exact moment of mockery.", note:"Ḍuḥā 93:5: \"He WILL give you\" (yuʿṭīka). Kawthar 108:1: \"We HAVE given you\" (aʿṭaynāka). Promise → fulfilled.", more:["Ḍuḥā 93:5 — wa la-sawfa yuʿṭīka rabbuka fa-tarḍā — \"your Lord WILL GIVE you, and you will be pleased.\" — FUTURE tense. Kawthar 108:1 — innā aʿṭaynāka'l-kawthar — \"We HAVE GIVEN you al-Kawthar.\" — PAST tense.","SAME VERB, same recipient. Ḍuḥā promised; Kawthar says it's done. The \"will give\" became \"have given.\"","And the ʿaṭāʾ thread across four surahs: - Nabaʾ 78:36 — jazāʾan min rabbika ʿaṭāʾan ḥisābā (a GIFT, well-calculated) - Layl 92:5 — fa-ammā man aʿṭā (the man who GIVES) - Ḍuḥā 93:5 — yuʿṭīka (He WILL give you) - Kawthar 108:1 — aʿṭaynāka (We HAVE given you)","And the emphasis: INNĀ — \"Indeed, WE\" — the plural of majesty + inna. The Giver names Himself first, before the gift.","Note the -ka (\"you\", singular) — addressed directly to the Prophet ﷺ, at the moment his enemies called him cut off.","ʿAṭāʾ thread: a gift (78:36), a giver (92:5), will give (93:5), have given (108:1)."]},
  {ar:"الْكَوْثَرَ", tr:"Al-kawthar", v:"1", root:"The \"MUCHNESS / abundance\" family — kathura = to be many; kathīr = much.", meaning:"\"al-Kawthar\" — abundant good; and in authentic hadith, specifically a river in Paradise given to the Prophet ﷺ, with the Ḥawḍ (his pool) on the Day of Judgment fed from it.", magic:"Takāthur 102:1 — alhākumu't-TAKĀTHUR — \"competing with each other for MORE distracted you.\" Kawthar 108:1 — aʿṭaynāka'l-KAWTHAR — \"We gave you the MOST.\"", grammar:"Fawʿal intensive pattern. Definite (al-) — \"THE Abundance\" — a specific, known thing.", why:"Kawthar — the most extreme possible form of \"abundance,\" chosen to answer a charge of lack.", note:"TAKĀTHUR (102:1) = CHASING more. KAWTHAR (108:1) = GIVEN the most. Same root — the rat-race and the gift that ends it.", more:["Kawthar is on the fawʿal intensive pattern = OVERWHELMING, limitless abundance — the most that \"much\" can be.","THE BEST CROSS-SURAH ROOT-PAYOFF IN THIS BATCH.","SAME ROOT (k-th-r). Takāthur: people exhausting themselves chasing more, until the grave. Kawthar: the most there is, simply given — no chasing. One surah is the rat-race; the other is the gift that ends it.","And the fawʿal pattern makes it superlative: kathīr = much; akthar = more; kawthar = abundance beyond measure. The word itself is the biggest form of \"much\" Arabic has.","Classical scholars include under al-Kawthar: the river in Paradise (the primary meaning in hadith), the Ḥawḍ, prophethood, the Qurʾān, his followers, his name raised high (Sharḥ 94:4 wa rafaʿnā laka dhikrak!). All forms of \"abundant good.\"","And it answers the mockery perfectly: they said he had NO descendants — abtar, cut off, too little. The reply: he was given KAWTHAR — too MUCH to count.","Fawʿal = the BIGGEST form of \"much.\" The river in Paradise + the Ḥawḍ.","They said \"too little\" (abtar) → He gave \"too much\" (kawthar)."]},
  {ar:"فَصَلِّ لِرَبِّكَ", tr:"Fa-ṣalli li-rabbika", v:"2", root:"ṣalli — imperative of ṣallā = PRAY. li-rabbika = FOR your Lord (the lām of purpose/dedication).", meaning:"\"So pray to [for] your Lord.\"", magic:"The cure for Māʿūn's disease, in three words.", grammar:"Imperative of a weak-final Form II verb — ṣalli has dropped its final yāʾ (from ṣallī).", why:"Li-rabbika — one preposition specifying the audience — the exact repair for yurāʾūn.", note:"Māʿūn 107:6: yurāʾūn — praying FOR PEOPLE. Kawthar 108:2: li-rabbika — praying FOR YOUR LORD. The lām specifies the audience.", more:["Māʿūn 107:5–6 — they were ʿan ṣalātihim sāhūn (heedless OF prayer) and yurāʾūn (praying FOR SHOW). Kawthar 108:2 — fa-ṣalli (so PRAY) li-rabbika (FOR YOUR LORD).","Li-rabbika is the antidote to riyāʾ. Māʿūn's people prayed for the audience; Kawthar says pray for your Lord — and only Him. The lām specifies the audience.","And the fa- = \"SO\" — CAUSAL: because We gave you al-Kawthar, SO pray. Gratitude, not obligation, drives the prayer. Compare Quraysh 106:3 — fal-yaʿbudū rabba hādha'l-bayt — \"SO let them worship\" — same causal fa, same logic: you were given → so worship.","The ṣalāh thread across your surahs: - Qiyāmah 75:31 — wa lā ṣallā (he did NOT pray) - Aʿlā 87:15 — fa-ṣallā (the successful one prayed) - ʿAlaq 96:10 — ʿabdan idhā ṣallā (a servant STOPPED from praying) - Māʿūn 107:4 — li'l-muṣallīn (those who pray EMPTILY) - Kawthar 108:2 — fa-ṣalli li-rabbika (PRAY — for your Lord)","Didn't pray → prayed → prevented → prayed emptily → pray sincerely. The thread resolves.","Li- = the lām of dedication.","Ṣalāh thread: didn't (75:31) → did (87:15) → prevented (96:10) → empty (107:4) → SINCERE (108:2). And the fa- = \"SO\" — because you were GIVEN."]},
  {ar:"وَانْحَرْ", tr:"Wa'nḥar", v:"2", root:"The \"THROAT / upper chest\" family — naḥr = the upper part of the chest where it meets the neck.", meaning:"\"and sacrifice.\"", magic:"One word, and it's the answer to Māʿūn's final line.", grammar:"Imperative, Form I (inḥar, with connecting hamzah). No object stated — \"sacrifice\" generally.", why:"Naḥr — the most costly offering in that world, answering Māʿūn's most trivial refusal.", note:"YAWM AN-NAḤR = ʿĪD AL-AḌḤĀ, the Day of Sacrifice. Naḥr = the THROAT where the camel is sacrificed.", more:["Naḥara = to SACRIFICE a camel (by stabbing at the base of the throat — the naḥr!); Yawm an-Naḥr = THE DAY OF SACRIFICE — 10th of Dhu'l-Ḥijjah, the day of ʿĪd al-Aḍḥā!","Māʿūn 107:7 — wa yamnaʿūna'l-māʿūn — \"they WITHHOLD even small things.\" Kawthar 108:2 — wa'nḥar — \"and SACRIFICE\" — give something of real value, slaughtered and distributed to the poor.","Māʿūn's man won't lend a pot; Kawthar commands giving a CAMEL. From the smallest withheld to the largest given.","And the pairing ṣalli + anḥar is the complete religion in two words: ṣalāh = your duty to Allah (body, worship); naḥr = your duty to people (wealth, feeding). Compare Bayyinah 98:5 — yuqīmu'ṣ-ṣalāta wa yuʾtu'z-zakāh — prayer + giving, the same two limbs.","The everyday hook: Yawm an-NAḤR = the Day of Sacrifice — the ʿĪd day when the sacrifice is made, directly after ʿĪd prayer. The order in the verse is the order of the day: PRAY, then SACRIFICE. Many scholars connect the verse to exactly this sequence.","(A minority reading interprets naḥr as placing the hands on the chest in prayer — wa'nḥar = \"and place [your hands] at the naḥr.\" The sacrifice reading is the widely held one.)","Māʿūn's man won't lend a POT → Kawthar commands giving a CAMEL.","PRAY (to Allah) + SACRIFICE (for people) = the two limbs (like Bayyinah 98:5's ṣalāh + zakāh).","And the ʿĪd order: pray, THEN sacrifice."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّ شَانِئَكَ", tr:"Inna shāniʾaka", v:"3", root:"The \"intense, bitter HATRED\" family — shaniʾa = to hate fiercely, with enmity.", meaning:"\"Indeed, your hater…\"", magic:"The word is RARE, and its rarity marks the intensity.", grammar:"Active participle (fāʿil) with possessive -ka. Note the hamzah on the middle root letter (ش-ن-أ).", why:"Shāniʾ (bitter hater) rather than ʿaduww (enemy) — it names the emotion behind the mockery.", note:"Shanaʾān = bitter hatred (5:8: \"don't let hatred of a people…\").", more:["Shanaʾān = deep animosity (5:2, 5:8: lā yajrimannakum shanaʾānu qawmin — \"do not let hatred of a people…\").","Shāniʾ isn't ordinary dislike. It's active, bitter, lasting enmity — the kind that makes you mock someone's grief. And that's exactly what happened: they mocked the Prophet ﷺ when his infant sons died.","And notice: the hater is NOT NAMED. Classical reports name several individuals (al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil is commonly mentioned). The Qurʾān names none of them — only the trait. Compare Fīl 105 — Abrahah never named. And contrast Masad 111 — Abū Lahab IS named (in three surahs' time).","And the -ka (\"YOUR hater\") keeps it personal — the whole surah speaks directly to him: aʿṭaynā-ka, rabbi-ka, shāniʾa-ka. Three -ka's in three verses.","The hater is NOT NAMED — only the trait (like Abrahah, Fīl 105). But Abū Lahab WILL be named (Masad 111). Three -ka's in three verses — a personal letter."]},
  {ar:"هُوَ الْأَبْتَرُ", tr:"Huwa'l-abtar", v:"3", root:"The \"CUTTING OFF / severing\" family — batara = to cut off (especially a tail).", meaning:"\"— HE is the one cut off.\"", magic:"The insult is returned to sender — and the grammar does it with one pronoun.", grammar:"Huwa = ḍamīr al-faṣl — restriction.", why:"Returning their exact word (abtar) is more devastating than any new insult.", note:"ABTAR = a TAILLESS animal — they called him \"cut off\" when his sons died. HUWA = \"HE — not you\" (restriction). Returned to sender.", more:["Abtar = an animal whose TAIL has been cut off — and by extension, a man cut off from posterity, from good, from remembrance; and in hadith, any important matter begun without Allah's name is abtar — \"cut off from blessing.\"","They called the Prophet ﷺ al-abtar — \"the tailless one,\" a man with no surviving son, whose line and name would end with him.","The reply: inna shāniʾaka HUWA'l-abtar — \"your hater — HE is the cut-off one.\"","And the huwa is crucial: it's the ḍamīr al-faṣl (separating pronoun), creating RESTRICTION: \"HE — and not you — is the cut-off one.\" You own this construction from Nāziʿāt 79:39, 41 (hiya'l-maʾwā) and Bayyinah 98:6–7 (ulāʾika hum).","And history confirmed it word for word: the Prophet's ﷺ name is spoken in every adhān, every shahādah, every tashahhud, by billions, fourteen centuries later (Sharḥ 94:4 — wa rafaʿnā laka dhikrak, \"We RAISED your mention\"). His mockers? Most people couldn't name one. The \"tailless\" man has the longest tail in history; the mockers were cut off.","And now read the whole surah as a single contrast: KAWTHAR (v.1, the MOST) vs ABTAR (v.3, CUT OFF). Abundance vs. severance — first word and last word of the shortest surah.","The rhyme: kawthar, wa'nḥar, abtar — all -ar. The insult rhymes with the gift.","Abtar — the afʿal pattern used for physical defects/characteristics (like aʿmā, blind — ʿAbasa 80:2).","And history agrees: his name is in every adhān (Sharḥ 94:4); theirs are forgotten.","First word KAWTHAR (the most) ↔ last word ABTAR (cut off)."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:109, name:"Al-Kāfirūn", ar:"الكافرون", en:"The Disbelievers", verses:6, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"It's not repetition — it's a CONTRACT with every loophole closed: now, later, before, and by nature.",
  special:["It seems to repeat itself — but every \"repetition\" changes the GRAMMAR, and the grammar changes the meaning.","Look closely at v.2 and v.4: - v.2: lā aʿbudu mā taʿbudūn — \"I do not worship [VERB, present] what you worship [present].\" - v.4: wa lā anā ʿābidun mā ʿabadtum — \"Nor am I a worshipper [NOUN, participle] of what you worshipped [past].\"","Two changes, both deliberate: - aʿbudu (VERB) → ʿābid (NOUN) — from an ACTION to an IDENTITY. \"I don't do it\" → \"I'm not the kind of person who does it.\" - taʿbudūn (present) → ʿabadtum (PAST) — \"what you worship now\" → \"what you have worshipped.\"","Put together, the surah denies compromise in every tense and every mode: present action, future action, past history, and permanent character. Nothing left to negotiate.","Context: Quraysh proposed a deal — \"You worship our gods for a year, we'll worship your God for a year.\" This surah is the reply. Every clause closes a loophole.","--"],
  big:["The address (v.1) — Say: O disbelievers.","The first refusal (v.2–3) — my worship and yours, in the present.","The second refusal (v.4–5) — my identity and your past — in different grammar.","The separation (v.6) — To you your religion, and to me mine."],
  traps:["It's NOT repetition — v.2 (VERB, present) vs v.4 (NOUN, past). Action → identity; present → past. Only v.3 and v.5 are truly identical.","دِينِ (v.6) — means RELIGION here, but meant RECKONING in Māʿūn 107:1, two surahs earlier. Same word, switched sense.","لَكُمْ دِينُكُمْ (v.6) — a declaration of SEPARATION/disavowal, NOT \"all religions are equally valid.\" Read it as the end of a four-fold refusal.","دِينِ (v.6) — the possessive yāʾ is DROPPED (dīnī → dīn) for the rhyme. Meaning \"MY religion\" intact.","قُلْ (v.1) — recited, not skipped — evidence of verbatim transmission.","مَا (v.2–5) — \"what\" — possibly maṣdariyyah (\"your MANNER of worship\"). Explains how mā can refer to Allah.","لَا أَعْبُدُ (v.2) — the muḍāriʿ negates present AND future.","أَنَا / أَنتُمْ — stated explicitly for EMPHASIS — two columns, kept apart.","عَبَدتُّمْ (v.4) — PAST tense — your whole inherited tradition, not just today's idols.","قُلْ — hollow verb imperative: the long ū drops (qūl → qul)."],
  links:[{w:"قُلْ (v.1)",e:"Ikhlāṣ 112:1 · Falaq 113:1 · Nās 114:1",t:"The FOUR QULS — and qul proves verbatim transmission"},{w:"الْكَافِرُونَ (v.1)",e:"76:3 · 80:17, 42 · 88:23 · 98:1, 6",t:"Kafara = to BURY a seed — first DIRECT address"},{w:"الْكَافِرُونَ (v.1)",e:"دَسَّاهَا (Shams 91:10)",t:"Two burying-roots — covering truth / burying the soul"},{w:"أَعْبُدُ / عَابِدُونَ (v.2–5)",e:"عَبْدًا (96:10) · عِبَادِي (89:29)",t:"Servanthood — the root appears 8 times here"},{w:"V.3 = v.5",e:"Layl 92:7 vs 92:10 · Zalzalah 99:7 vs 99:8",t:"Near-identical verses — here, the identical ones are THEIRS"},{w:"V.2 vs v.4",e:"",t:"Verb → noun, present → past — every loophole closed"},{w:"دِينُكُمْ (v.6)",e:"بِالدِّينِ (Māʿūn 107:1)",t:"Same word, switched sense — Reckoning → religion"},{w:"دِينُكُمْ (v.6)",e:"دِينِ اللَّهِ (Naṣr 110:2)",t:"\"Your religion\" vs. crowds entering \"Allah's religion\" — next surah"},{w:"لَكُمْ دِينُكُمْ (v.6)",e:"75:12 · 79:44 · 88:25 · 94:8 · 96:8",t:"Fronting = restriction"},{w:"دِينِ (v.6)",e:"يَسْرِ (Fajr 89:4) · بِالْوَادِ (89:9)",t:"Dropped yāʾ for the rhyme"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"قُلْ", tr:"Qul", v:"1", root:"The imperative of qāla = SAY.", meaning:"\"Say:\"", magic:"One tiny word that proves the Qurʾān was transmitted verbatim.", grammar:"Hollow verb (ق-و-ل) — the imperative qul drops the long ū (from qūl) because of the sukūn. Very common.", why:"Qul marks the whole surah as a quoted message — not the Prophet's ﷺ own opinion but a delivered statement.", note:"QUL is RECITED — proof of verbatim transmission. Like a messenger reading \"Tell them this:\" aloud.", more:["If the Prophet ﷺ were composing this himself, he'd just say \"O disbelievers…\" Instead, the word qul (\"SAY\") — the instruction given TO him — is recited as part of the text. He passed on the command exactly as he received it, including the word \"say.\"","It's like a messenger who reads a letter aloud and includes the line \"Tell them this:\" — you know he isn't editing.","And this begins the run of the FOUR QULS — the \"qul surahs\": - Kāfirūn 109:1 — qul yā ayyuha'l-kāfirūn - Ikhlāṣ 112:1 — qul huwa'llāhu aḥad - Falaq 113:1 — qul aʿūdhu bi-rabbi'l-falaq - Nās 114:1 — qul aʿūdhu bi-rabbi'n-nās","Four surahs, four commands to SAY — disavowal, monotheism, and two prayers of protection. Kāfirūn and Ikhlāṣ are the famous pair (both recited in Fajr sunnah).","The FOUR QULS: Kāfirūn 109, Ikhlāṣ 112, Falaq 113, Nās 114."]},
  {ar:"يَا أَيُّهَا الْكَافِرُونَ", tr:"Yā ayyuha'l-kāfirūn", v:"1", root:"The \"COVERING / burying\" family — kafara = originally a farmer BURYING a seed under soil.", meaning:"\"O disbelievers!\"", magic:"The most direct address in the juzʾ.", grammar:"Yā ayyuhā + definite noun = vocative. Al-kāfirūn — sound masculine plural, nominative (-ūn).", why:"The direct vocative — the clarity is the point; this is a statement made to their faces.", note:"Kafara = TO BURY A SEED. Kaffārah = EXPIATION (covering a sin). Covering truth vs. covering sin — same root.", more:["Kāfir = one who covers up a truth he can feel is there; kaffārah = EXPIATION (an act that \"covers over\" a sin!).","Every other surah described disbelievers in the third person — al-kafarah (ʿAbasa 80:42), alladhīna kafarū (Burūj 85:19, Bayyinah 98:1, 6). Here, for the first time, they are ADDRESSED: \"O you who disbelieve!\" Face to face.","And remember the root: kafara = to BURY a seed. A kāfir isn't someone who doesn't know — it's someone who covers over something he can feel is there. Shams 91:10 has a parallel root — dassāhā, \"buried it\" (the soul).","And kaffārah = EXPIATION — the same root used for the act that covers over a sin. Covering can be good (kaffārah covers your sin) or bad (kufr covers the truth). Free hook.","You own the k-f-r family across six surahs: Insān 76:3 (kafūrā), ʿAbasa 80:17 (mā akfarah), 80:42 (al-kafarah), Ghāshiyah 88:23 (kafar), Bayyinah 98:1, 6, Kāfirūn 109:1.","The FIRST time in the juzʾ they're ADDRESSED directly, not described."]},
  {ar:"لَا أَعْبُدُ مَا تَعْبُدُونَ", tr:"Lā aʿbudu mā taʿbudūn", v:"2", root:"The \"SERVITUDE / worship\" family — ʿabd = a SERVANT/slave; ʿibādah = worship (= servanthood); maʿbūd = the one worshipped.", meaning:"\"I do not worship what you worship.\"", magic:"The root appears EIGHT times in five verses — the densest repetition of any root in the juzʾ.", grammar:"Lā + present = negation of present AND future. mā — relative (\"what\") or maṣdariyyah (\"your worshipping\").", why:"ʿibādah (servanthood) — makes the refusal about ownership, not preference.", note:"The ʿ-b-d root appears EIGHT times in five verses. The whole surah is ONE question: who do you SERVE?", more:["Count them: aʿbudu, taʿbudūna, ʿābidūna, aʿbudu, ʿābidun, ʿabadtum, ʿābidūna, aʿbudu — EIGHT. The whole surah is about ONE question: who is the maʿbūd?","And the root is SERVANTHOOD. ʿIbādah isn't just ritual — it's being someone's ʿabd (servant). So \"I don't worship what you worship\" = \"I don't SERVE what you serve.\" A question of who owns you.","And note: aʿbudu is PRESENT tense — \"I do not worship\" — in Arabic the present (muḍāriʿ) covers now AND the future. So v.2 = \"not now, not later.\"","And mā (\"WHAT\") for the object of worship — used for their idols (non-rational things) naturally. But it's also used in v.3 for Allah (mā aʿbudu). Scholars explain: (a) mā here refers to the attributes/nature of the worshipped, or (b) it's mā maṣdariyyah — \"your manner of worship\" vs \"mine.\" Both classical — and (b) makes v.6's dīn (way of worship) flow perfectly.","Connect to ʿAlaq 96:10 — ʿabdan idhā ṣallā (\"a SERVANT when he prays\") and Fajr 89:29 — fa'dkhulī fī ʿibādī (\"enter among MY SERVANTS\").","ʿIbādah = SERVANTHOOD (ʿabd = servant).","And lā aʿbudu (present) = not NOW and not LATER.","Mā may mean \"your MANNER of worship.\""]},
  {ar:"وَلَا أَنتُمْ عَابِدُونَ مَا أَعْبُدُ", tr:"Wa lā antum ʿābidūna mā aʿbud", v:"3", root:"The same servitude root — but now as ʿābid — an ACTIVE PARTICIPLE (a noun): \"a worshipper.\"", meaning:"\"Nor are you worshippers of what I worship.\"", magic:"V.3 and v.5 are WORD-FOR-WORD IDENTICAL — and that's the only true repetition in the surah.", grammar:"ʿābidūna — sound masculine plural participle, in the nominative as khabar of lā (functioning like laysa).", why:"A participle (identity) for them — because the point is what they ARE, not what they did once.", note:"V.3 and v.5 are IDENTICAL (their side). v.2 and v.4 DIFFER (his side).", more:["V.3: wa lā antum ʿābidūna mā aʿbud. v.5: wa lā antum ʿābidūna mā aʿbud.","Identical. Meanwhile v.2 and v.4 — the Prophet's ﷺ own statements — are DIFFERENT from each other. Classical observation: his side of the statement changes grammatical form (verb → noun, present → past); their side stays fixed. His refusal is examined from every angle; their state is described as unchanging.","And notice ʿābidūna is a NOUN (participle) — \"you are not WORSHIPPERS of…\" — a statement about their settled character, not a single act. Some scholars read it as a prophecy about those specific individuals: the leaders who proposed the deal would never become believers — and those named in the reports didn't.","The pronoun dance: anā / antum (I / you) alternate throughout — the surah is built like two columns, kept apart.","Note antum (you, plural) stated explicitly — emphasis.","His refusal is examined from every angle; their state is fixed.","ʿĀbidūn = a NOUN — their settled character.","And anā / antum alternate like two separate columns."]},
  {ar:"وَلَا أَنَا عَابِدٌ مَّا عَبَدتُّمْ", tr:"Wa lā anā ʿābidun mā ʿabadtum", v:"4", root:"The same servitude root — but now with TWO grammatical changes from v.2.", meaning:"\"Nor will I ever be a worshipper of what you have worshipped.\"", magic:"THE KEY VERSE — the one that proves it isn't repetition.", grammar:"Participle (ism fāʿil) = permanence/identity; verb (fiʿl) = action/event.", why:"Switching to the participle and the past is the only way Arabic could say \"never, in any form, from any angle.\"", note:"V.2: lā AʿBUDU (VERB — \"I don't DO it\"). v.4: lā anā ʿĀBIDUN (NOUN — \"I'm not someone who WOULD\").", more:["Compare v.2 and v.4 side by side:","CHANGE ONE — VERB → NOUN. Aʿbudu = \"I worship\" (an act). ʿĀbid = \"a worshipper\" (a person). v.2 says \"I don't DO it.\" v.4 says \"I am not someone who EVER WOULD.\" From behaviour to essence.","CHANGE TWO — PRESENT → PAST. Taʿbudūn = what you worship now. ʿAbadtum = what you have worshipped (your whole tradition, your ancestors' gods). Not just today's idols — the whole inherited system.","And note the explicit anā (\"I\") — stated even though the participle doesn't need it. \"Nor am I — me — a worshipper…\" Personal, emphatic.","Result: the surah denies the compromise in every dimension: present (v.2), future (v.2, muḍāriʿ), past (v.4, ʿabadtum), and character (v.4, ʿābid). Every loophole in the proposed deal — \"just one year\" — closed.","Compare the Fajr 89:15–16 pattern and Layl 92:7/10 — verses that look the same but differ by one element. Here the differences are grammatical, not lexical. You need to read Arabic grammar to see it — which is why translations make the surah look repetitive.","ʿAbadtum — past tense, 2nd person plural.","v.2: taʿBUDŪN (PRESENT — your gods now).","v.4: ʿABADTUM (PAST — your whole tradition).","Present, future, past, and character — EVERY loophole closed.","Translations hide this; the grammar shows it."]},
  {ar:"لَكُمْ دِينُكُمْ وَلِيَ دِينِ", tr:"Lakum dīnukum wa liya dīn", v:"6", root:"Dīn — the \"obligation / what is owed / a way of life\" family — dayn = a DEBT.", meaning:"\"To you your religion, and to me mine.\"", magic:"Three things — a sense-switch, a word-order, and a missing letter.", grammar:"Khabar muqaddam (fronted predicate) ×2. Dropped possessive yāʾ on dīn. Liya = li- + -ya (to me).", why:"Dīn (the way you're bound to) — because what's being separated isn't opinions but whole systems of obligation.", note:"DĪN SENSE-SWITCH: Māʿūn 107:1 = RECKONING. Kāfirūn 109:6 = RELIGION. Naṣr 110:2 = RELIGION. One root: OBLIGATION (dayn = debt).", more:["Dīn = religion, devotion, a way you're bound to; yawm ad-dīn = the Day of Reckoning (Settlement Day).","ONE — THE SENSE OF DĪN HAS SWITCHED from two surahs ago. - Māʿūn 107:1 — yukadhdhibu bi'd-dīn = the RECKONING - Kāfirūn 109:6 — lakum dīnukum wa liya dīn = RELIGION / way of worship - Naṣr 110:2 — fī dīni'llāh = RELIGION (next surah!)","Same word, and the root unifies them: d-y-n = OBLIGATION. A dayn is what you owe a creditor; a dīn is the devotion you owe your Lord; the Yawm ad-Dīn is when everything owed is settled. Context decides which.","TWO — THE WORD ORDER is RESTRICTION. LAKUM dīnukum — \"to YOU [is] your religion\" — the khabar (lakum) is FRONTED. Fronting = restriction: \"yours is YOURS — it stays with you, not with me.\" And wa LIYA dīn — \"and MINE is mine.\" Two sealed compartments.","You own this fronting from SEVEN surahs (Qiyāmah 75:12, 75:30, Nāziʿāt 79:44, Ghāshiyah 88:25, Sharḥ 94:8, ʿAlaq 96:8, Qadr 97:5).","This is NOT \"all religions are equal.\" It's a declaration of separation, coming at the end of a surah that has just refused, four times, to blend. Classical reading: \"you'll bear the consequence of yours, I of mine\" — disavowal (barāʾah), not endorsement.","THREE — THE MISSING YĀʾ. dīn at the end should be dīnī (\"MY religion\") — the possessive yāʾ is DROPPED in the muṣḥaf's spelling, to keep the rhyme (-ūn / -īn). You own this exact feature from Fajr 89:4 (yasr) and 89:9 (bi'l-wād). A known orthographic feature — the meaning \"my\" is fully intact.","And note the elegant chiasmus: LAKUM dīnu-KUM / wa LIYA dīn-[Ī] — you-yours / me-mine. The pronouns wrap around the word like bookends.","LAKUM is FRONTED = \"yours stays YOURS.\" Disavowal, NOT \"all religions are equal.\" Dīn[ī] — the yāʾ is DROPPED for the rhyme (like yasr, Fajr 89:4)."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:110, name:"An-Naṣr", ar:"النصر", en:"Divine Support", verses:3, place:"Madinan",
  blurb:"The work is DONE → so the worker prepares to go home. Victory isn't the end of the story; it's the signal that the story is ending.",
  special:["It's a victory announcement — and its only instruction is to ask for FORGIVENESS.","V.1–2: the help of Allah comes, Makkah opens, people enter the religion in crowds — the greatest triumph imaginable. v.3: fa-sabbiḥ bi-ḥamdi rabbika wa'staghfirh — \"So glorify with the praise of your Lord, and SEEK HIS FORGIVENESS.\"","No celebration. No victory parade. No \"you did it.\" The response to total success is: glorify Him, praise Him, ask forgiveness.","And the companions read something deeper in it. When ʿUmar (رضي الله عنه) asked the senior companions about this surah, most said it commands praise at victory. Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما), still young, said: \"It is the announcement of the Messenger's ﷺ approaching death\" — his mission complete. ʿUmar said: \"I know of it only what you know.\" It is reported to be among the last complete surahs revealed.","And ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that after it, the Prophet ﷺ would often say in his rukūʿ and sujūd: \"Subḥānaka'llāhumma rabbanā wa bi-ḥamdik, Allāhumma'ghfir lī\" — \"carrying out [the command of] the Qurʾān.\"","--"],
  big:["The arrival (v.1) — when Allah's help comes, and the Opening.","The sight (v.2) — people entering Allah's religion in crowds.","The response (v.3) — glorify, praise, seek forgiveness — He is ever Relenting."],
  traps:["وَاسْتَغْفِرْهُ (v.3) — the command at VICTORY is to seek FORGIVENESS. Not a sign of wrongdoing — humility, completion, and (per Ibn ʿAbbās) the signal of the Prophet's ﷺ approaching end.","اسْتَغْفِرْ vs اسْتَغْنَىٰ — SAME Form X, opposite meanings. \"Seek forgiveness\" (I NEED) vs. \"consider oneself self-sufficient\" (I need NOTHING).","أَفْوَاجًا (v.2) — IDENTICAL to Nabaʾ 78:18 — crowds to Judgment there, crowds into faith here.","دِينِ اللَّهِ (v.2) — dīn = RELIGION — and set directly against Kāfirūn 109:6's \"your religion.\"","الْفَتْحُ (v.1) — \"the OPENING\" = the Conquest of Makkah. Arabic says a city is OPENED, not conquered.","تَوَّابًا (v.3) — faʿʿāl intensive = relenting AGAIN AND AGAIN. And kāna = He has ALWAYS been so.","نَصْرُ اللَّهِ (v.1) — \"the help OF ALLAH\" — the victory is attributed to Him, not to the Muslims.","إِذَا جَاءَ (v.1) — idhā + past = a CERTAIN future (\"when [not if] it comes\").","سَبِّحْ بِحَمْدِ (v.3) — tasbīḥ = negation of every flaw; ḥamd = affirmation of every perfection. Two different jobs.","يَدْخُلُونَ (v.2) — a ḥāl: \"WHILE entering\" — describing what he sees them doing."],
  links:[{w:"نَصْرُ اللَّهِ (v.1)",e:"وَلَا نَاصِرٍ (Ṭāriq 86:10)",t:"The denier has NO helper / the believers receive Allah's help"},{w:"الْفَتْحُ (v.1)",e:"Al-Fātiḥah · miftāḥ",t:"The Qurʾān OPENS with \"the Opener\" and announces \"the Opening\""},{w:"وَرَأَيْتَ (v.2)",e:"90:7 · 96:14 · 99:7 · 102:6 · 107:6",t:"The seeing thread ends on JOY"},{w:"دِينِ اللَّهِ (v.2)",e:"لَكُمْ دِينُكُمْ (Kāfirūn 109:6)",t:"\"Your religion\" → \"ALLAH'S religion\" — back-to-back"},{w:"دِينِ (v.2)",e:"الدِّينِ (Māʿūn 107:1)",t:"The dīn switch — Reckoning → your religion → Allah's religion"},{w:"يَدْخُلُونَ (v.2)",e:"فَادْخُلِي... وَادْخُلِي (Fajr 89:29–30)",t:"Enter the religion → enter the Garden"},{w:"أَفْوَاجًا (v.2)",e:"أَفْوَاجًا (Nabaʾ 78:18)",t:"IDENTICAL — crowds to Judgment / crowds into faith"},{w:"فَسَبِّحْ (v.3)",e:"سَبِّحِ (Aʿlā 87:1) · السَّابِحَاتِ (79:3)",t:"Glorify at the START (Aʿlā) and the END (Naṣr)"},{w:"بِحَمْدِ (v.3)",e:"",t:"Ḥamd → MUḤAMMAD, \"the praised one\""},{w:"وَاسْتَغْفِرْهُ (v.3)",e:"اسْتَغْنَىٰ (80:5 · 92:8 · 96:7)",t:"FORM X: \"I need NOTHING\" vs. \"I need FORGIVENESS\""},{w:"تَوَّابًا (v.3)",e:"75:2 · 78:13, 14, 25, 28 · 85:16",t:"The faʿʿāl thread ends on relentless MERCY"},{w:"تَوَّابًا (v.3)",e:"الرُّجْعَىٰ (96:8) · إِيَابَهُمْ (88:25)",t:"Everything returns to Him — He returns to whoever returns"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"نَصْرُ اللَّهِ", tr:"Naṣru'llāh", v:"1", root:"The \"HELP / aid that brings victory\" family — naṣara = to help someone against an opponent; nāṣir = a helper/supporter.", meaning:"\"When the help of Allah comes…\"", magic:"The possessive is the theology — and the root closes a thread from Ṭāriq.", grammar:"Idhā + past (jāʾa) = a certain future (\"when it comes\" — guaranteed). Naṣru'llāh = iḍāfah (attribution).", why:"Naṣr (help that wins) rather than fawz (success) — because naṣr always implies someone else doing the helping.", note:"AL-ANṢĀR = THE HELPERS of Madīnah. Same root. Ṭāriq 86:10: the denier has NO nāṣir. Naṣr 110:1: naṣr ALLĀH arrives.", more:["Al-Anṣār = THE HELPERS of Madīnah (who gave the Prophet ﷺ refuge!); intiṣār = victory/triumph.","It's naṣru'LLĀH — \"the help OF ALLAH.\" Not \"your victory,\" not \"the Muslims' victory.\" The triumph is attributed to Him in the first two words. Which is exactly why v.3 commands humility, not pride: you didn't win it.","And you own the root from Ṭāriq 86:10 — fa-mā lahu min quwwatin wa lā NĀṢIR — \"he will have no strength and NO HELPER\" — describing the denier on the Day of Judgment. Ṭāriq: the denier has NO nāṣir. Naṣr: the believers receive naṣr Allāh. Same root, opposite fates.","And al-Anṣār is your free hook: the people of Madīnah who HELPED — the human naṣr that preceded the divine one. They gave refuge in hardship; Allah gave victory in the end.","Compare Muṭaffifīn/Burūj-style scenes of persecution — Burūj 85 told of believers burned in a ditch with no visible help. Naṣr tells of help arriving. The arc of the juzʾ bends here.","\"The help OF ALLAH\" — not yours — which is why v.3 commands humility."]},
  {ar:"وَالْفَتْحُ", tr:"Wa'l-fatḥ", v:"1", root:"The \"OPENING\" family — fataḥa = to open; miftāḥ = A KEY; al-Fātiḥah = THE OPENER (the first surah!).", meaning:"\"…and the Opening [the Conquest of Makkah].\"", magic:"In Arabic, a city isn't \"conquered\" — it's \"OPENED.\"", grammar:"Al-fatḥ joined to naṣr by wa — both subjects of jāʾa.", why:"Fatḥ (opening) rather than ghalabah (overpowering) — it describes the conquest as an unlocking, which matches its mercy.", note:"MIFTĀḤ = A KEY. AL-FĀTIḤAH = THE OPENER. Same root.", more:["Fatḥ = an opening AND a conquest (a city \"opened\"); al-Fattāḥ = a NAME OF ALLAH (The Opener, The Judge).","Fatḥ Makkah = \"the OPENING of Makkah\" — the standard term for its conquest (8 AH). And the word choice is perfect for what happened: the city was entered almost without bloodshed; the Prophet ﷺ declared a general amnesty — \"go, you are free.\" Less a conquest than an unlocking.","And the root gives you two hooks you use daily: - MIFTĀḤ = A KEY — the thing that opens. - AL-FĀTIḤAH = THE OPENER — the surah that opens the Qurʾān and every rakʿah.","So the Qurʾān OPENS with al-Fātiḥah and, near its end, announces al-Fatḥ. The book that begins with \"the Opening\" ends its revelation with \"the Opening.\"","And note the DEFINITE article: AL-fatḥ — \"THE Opening\" — a specific, known, long-awaited one.","Classical note: fatḥ also means a JUDGMENT/decision (al-Fattāḥ = the Judge who \"opens\" a case) — the Opening of Makkah was a verdict between the two sides.","The help comes FIRST, the opening SECOND — cause, then effect.","In Arabic a city isn't \"conquered\" — it's \"OPENED.\" And Makkah was opened with an amnesty.","The Qurʾān OPENS with al-Fātiḥah and announces al-Fatḥ near the end."]},
  {ar:"يَدْخُلُونَ فِي دِينِ اللَّهِ", tr:"Yadkhulūna fī dīni'llāh", v:"2", root:"Yadkhulūna — ENTERING (dakhala = to enter; madkhal = an entrance). dīn — here: RELIGION — from the obligation root.", meaning:"\"and you see the people entering into the religion of Allah…\"", magic:"The BEST consecutive-surah link in this batch — the previous surah set it up.", grammar:"Raʾayta + an-nāsa (object) + yadkhulūna (a ḥāl, describing them: \"while entering\"). Fī dīni'llāh = iḍāfah.", why:"Dīnu'llāh — attributing the religion to Allah, right after Kāfirūn's \"your religion / my religion.\"", note:"Kāfirūn 109:6: \"YOUR religion\" (separate). Naṣr 110:2: \"ALLAH'S religion\" — they ENTER it. Back-to-back surahs.", more:["Kāfirūn 109:6 — lakum dīnukum wa liya dīn — \"to you YOUR religion, and to me mine\" — total separation, the door closed. Naṣr 110:2 — yadkhulūna fī dīni'llāh afwājā — \"entering into ALLAH'S religion in crowds.\"","Back to back. Kāfirūn: \"YOUR religion\" (dīnukum) — theirs, separate. Naṣr: \"ALLAH'S religion\" (dīnu'llāh) — and they are walking INTO it. The door Kāfirūn closed to compromise, Naṣr shows opening to sincerity.","And the dīn sense-switch is now complete across three surahs: - Māʿūn 107:1 — bi'd-dīn = the RECKONING - Kāfirūn 109:6 — dīnukum = YOUR religion - Naṣr 110:2 — dīni'llāh = ALLAH'S religion","And note the verb: yadkhulūna fī — \"entering INTO.\" Religion described as a space you walk into — a house with a door. Compare Fajr 89:29–30 — fa'dkhulī fī ʿibādī · wa'dkhulī jannatī — \"ENTER among My servants, ENTER My Garden.\" Enter the religion in dunya → enter the Garden in the Hereafter. Same verb.","Wa raʾayta (\"and you SEE\") — the Prophet ﷺ is promised he'll witness it with his own eyes. The seeing thread (90:7, 96:14, 99:7, 102:6) ends on joy.","Dīn: Reckoning (107:1) → your religion (109:6) → Allah's religion (110:2). And yadkhulūna → Fajr 89:29's udkhulī — enter the religion, then the Garden."]},
  {ar:"أَفْوَاجًا", tr:"Afwājā", v:"2", root:"Fawj = a TROOP, a throng, a wave of people moving together — afwāj = throngs upon throngs (modern: fawj = a regiment/battalion!).", meaning:"\"…in crowds / in multitudes.\"", magic:"You own this word EXACTLY from Nabaʾ 78:18 — and the contrast is one of the most beautiful in the juzʾ.", grammar:"Afwājan — accusative ḥāl (\"in a state of crowds\"). Broken plural of fawj.", why:"Afwāj (crowds of crowds) — after years of individuals converting one by one in secret, now whole tribes at once.", note:"IDENTICAL to Nabaʾ 78:18 — afwājā. Nabaʾ: crowds SUMMONED to Judgment. Naṣr: crowds ENTERING faith. Same image — terror vs. faith.", more:["Nabaʾ 78:18 — yawma yunfakhu fi'ṣ-ṣūri fa-taʾtūna AFWĀJĀ — \"the Day the Trumpet is blown, and you come forth in CROWDS\" — to JUDGMENT. Naṣr 110:2 — yadkhulūna fī dīni'llāhi AFWĀJĀ — \"entering Allah's religion in CROWDS.\"","IDENTICAL WORD, same position (verse-ending), same image of streaming multitudes. Nabaʾ: crowds summoned to Judgment. Naṣr: crowds choosing to enter faith. The same picture of humanity in waves — once in terror, once in faith. And the first group that enters in crowds now won't need to fear the crowds of that Day.","And historically exact: after the Opening of Makkah, delegations came from tribes across Arabia — the year 9 AH was called ʿĀm al-Wufūd, \"the Year of Delegations.\" Whole tribes at once, afwājan.","Free hook: fawj = a REGIMENT in modern Arabic.","ʿĀm al-Wufūd = the Year of Delegations (9 AH)."]},
  {ar:"فَسَبِّحْ بِحَمْدِ رَبِّكَ", tr:"Fa-sabbiḥ bi-ḥamdi rabbika", v:"3", root:"Sabbiḥ — the \"swimming / gliding\" family → tasbīḥ = declaring Allah FAR ABOVE every flaw (moving swiftly away from any imperfection).", meaning:"\"Then glorify with the praise of your Lord…\"", magic:"Two words you say every day — joined in a formula you also say every day.", grammar:"Form II imperative (sabbiḥ). Bi-ḥamdi — the bi- = \"with / accompanied by\" praise.", why:"Tasbīḥ + ḥamd — the formula that most directly cancels self-congratulation.", note:"SUBḤĀNA'LLĀHI WA BI-ḤAMDIH — the formula you say daily, commanded here. Tasbīḥ = NO flaw; ḥamd = ALL perfection.", more:["ḥamd — PRAISE / grateful acknowledgement (al-ḥamdu lillāh; Muḥammad, Aḥmad — \"the praised one\"!).","Sabbiḥ bi-ḥamdi rabbik = the root of subḥāna'llāhi wa bi-ḥamdih — \"Glory be to Allah, and with His praise.\" You say it in rukūʿ (subḥāna rabbiya'l-ʿaẓīm wa bi-ḥamdih), in morning and evening adhkār.","And the two words do different jobs: - TASBĪḤ = NEGATION — declaring Him free of every flaw (\"He is not lacking, not weak, not in need\") - ḤAMD = AFFIRMATION — declaring every perfection His (\"all good, all praise belongs to Him\")","Together: remove every defect, affirm every perfection. The complete description in two words.","And at the moment of VICTORY, why tasbīḥ? Because the danger of victory is thinking you did it. Tasbīḥ clears that thought: He alone is perfect; your role was always lacking.","You own sabbiḥ EXACTLY from Aʿlā 87:1 — sabbiḥi'sma rabbika'l-aʿlā (\"GLORIFY the name of your Lord, the Most High\"). Same imperative. Aʿlā commanded it at the start of the journey; Naṣr commands it at the end.","And the s-b-ḥ root's origin: swimming — you own it from Nāziʿāt 79:3 (as-sābiḥāt, those who glide).","Ḥamd → MUḤAMMAD — \"the much-praised one.\" The command to praise is given to the man whose name means \"praised.\"","The fa- = \"SO / then\" — the response to v.1–2.","Aʿlā 87:1: sabbiḥ at the START of the journey.","Naṣr 110:3: sabbiḥ at the END.","Ḥamd → MUḤAMMAD = \"the praised one.\""]},
  {ar:"وَاسْتَغْفِرْهُ", tr:"Wa'staghfirh", v:"3", root:"The \"COVERING / shielding\" family — ghafara = to cover over, to shield.", meaning:"\"…and seek His forgiveness.\"", magic:"The most surprising command in the surah — and Form X tells the whole story.", grammar:"Form X imperative (istaghfir) + -hu (Him).", why:"Istighfār at the moment of victory — the clearest possible signal that success belongs to Allah.", note:"MIGHFAR = A HELMET. Maghfirah = being COVERED so the consequence can't strike.", more:["Mighfar = A HELMET (the armour that covers and protects the head!); maghfirah = FORGIVENESS (sin covered over, and you shielded from its consequence); al-Ghafūr = a NAME OF ALLAH.","Form X istaghfara = to SEEK covering / seek forgiveness.","The Prophet ﷺ has just been given total victory. The command isn't \"rejoice\" — it's \"SEEK FORGIVENESS.\" The most honoured human, at his moment of greatest success.","Why? Classical answers: (a) humility at victory, so no trace of pride remains; (b) completion of a lifetime's mission — every great task ends in istighfār, as every prayer ends in istighfār (astaghfiru'llāh ×3 after ṣalāh!); (c) preparation to meet his Lord (Ibn ʿAbbās's reading). Note (b): istighfār closes the ṣalāh, closes the Hajj (2:199), closes the night (51:18) — and here, closes a mission.","And the mighfar (HELMET) image is unforgettable: maghfirah isn't just \"erasing\" sin — it's covering you so its consequences can't strike. Like a helmet. And notice: he'd just come out of years of battle, wearing an actual mighfar. Now: seek the maghfirah that protects in a different way.","THE FORM X CONTRAST — the best grammar payoff in your collection: - ISTAGHNĀ (Form X) — ʿAbasa 80:5, Layl 92:8, ʿAlaq 96:7 — \"to consider oneself NEEDING NOTHING\" — the root of all transgression. - ISTAGHFIR (Form X) — Naṣr 110:3 — \"SEEK covering\" — admitting you NEED.","SAME FORM. Opposite souls. The mustaghnī says \"I need nothing.\" The mustaghfir says \"I need forgiveness.\" The surah of victory ends on the posture of need — the exact cure for istighnāʾ.","Form X = SEEKING (istaghfara = seek forgiveness; istaʿāna = seek help) or CONSIDERING (istaghnā = consider oneself rich).","FORM X: ISTAGHNĀ (80:5, 92:8, 96:7) = \"I NEED NOTHING.\" ISTAGHFIR (110:3) = \"I NEED FORGIVENESS.\" Same form, opposite souls.","And istighfār closes the ṣalāh (astaghfiru'llāh ×3) — and here, a whole mission."]},
  {ar:"إِنَّهُ كَانَ تَوَّابًا", tr:"Innahu kāna tawwābā", v:"3", root:"The \"RETURNING / turning back\" family — tāba = to turn back, to return; tawbah = REPENTANCE (\"turning back\" to Allah).", meaning:"\"Indeed, He is ever Accepting of repentance.\"", magic:"The final word of the surah — and it's on the intensive pattern you've tracked through the whole juzʾ.", grammar:"Kāna with Allah's attributes = permanence (\"has always been\").", why:"Ending on Tawwāb — so that the command to seek forgiveness is immediately followed by the certainty it's granted.", note:"TAWWĀB = faʿʿāl — RELENTING AGAIN AND AGAIN, without limit.", more:["When said of ALLAH, tāba ʿalā = He turns back toward the servant in mercy; at-Tawwāb = a NAME OF ALLAH — The Ever-Relenting.","Tawwāb is on the faʿʿāl INTENSIVE pattern — \"one who does it A LOT, constantly, by nature.\" You own this shape from: - Qiyāmah 75:2 — al-lawwāmah (the soul that blames relentlessly) - Nabaʾ 78:13 — wahhājā (blazing fiercely) - Nabaʾ 78:14 — thajjājā (pouring in torrents) - Nabaʾ 78:25 — ghassāqā (foul, freezing) - Nabaʾ 78:28 — kidhdhābā (lying relentlessly) - Burūj 85:16 — faʿʿālun li-mā yurīd (doer of whatever He wills) - Naṣr 110:3 — TAWWĀBĀ (RELENTING, again and again)","Nabaʾ used the pattern for blazing suns, pouring rain, freezing pus, and relentless lies. The juzʾ's last use of it is relentless mercy.","And tāba = to TURN BACK — so tawbah is the servant turning back to Allah, and at-Tawwāb is Allah turning back to the servant. Every time you return, He returns — and the faʿʿāl pattern says He does it without limit.","And note KĀNA (\"He WAS / has always been\") — not \"He will be\" — He has ALWAYS been Tawwāb. The mercy isn't new; it's His permanent nature.","And the return-root thread: ʿAlaq 96:8 — inna ilā rabbika'r-rujʿā (\"to your Lord is the RETURN\") — and Ghāshiyah 88:25 — iyābahum (their RETURN). Different roots, same idea: everything returns to Him — and He returns to whoever returns.","Tawwāban — faʿʿāl intensive, accusative as khabar of kāna.","Nabaʾ used faʿʿāl for blazing suns and relentless LIES; the juzʾ's last faʿʿāl is relentless MERCY.","Tawbah = YOU turn back; at-Tawwāb = HE turns back to you.","KĀNA = He has ALWAYS been this."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:111, name:"Al-Masad", ar:"المسد", en:"The Palm Fibre", verses:5, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"ABŪ LAHAB → DHĀTA LAHAB. Father of Flame → a fire of flame. The one enemy the Qurʾān names — and it names him by his destination.",
  special:["His nickname was \"Father of FLAME\" — and the surah tells him his destination is a fire \"of FLAME.\"","V.1: tabbat yadā abī LAHAB — \"perish the hands of Abū LAHAB (Father of Flame)\" v.3: sa-yaṣlā nāran dhāta LAHAB — \"he will burn in a fire of FLAME\"","SAME WORD — lahab. His nickname (Abū Lahab, given for his ruddy, glowing face) becomes the description of his fire. His name was his prophecy.","And notice two more firsts: - He is the ONLY enemy of the Prophet ﷺ NAMED in the entire Qurʾān. Abrahah? unnamed (Fīl 105). The hater in Kawthar 108? unnamed. Abū Lahab — the Prophet's ﷺ own UNCLE — named. - It was revealed roughly a decade before his death. All he had to do to \"disprove\" it was say the shahādah — even falsely. He never did. A living prophecy he could have broken any day, and didn't.","--"],
  big:["The curse returned (v.1) — may the hands of Abū Lahab perish — and he has perished.","Nothing helps (v.2) — his wealth and what he earned avail him nothing.","His fire (v.3) — a fire of flame.","His wife (v.4–5) — carrier of firewood, a rope of palm-fibre on her neck."],
  traps:["لَهَبٍ appears TWICE (v.1, v.3) — his NICKNAME and his FIRE. Abū Lahab → dhāta lahab. Read them together.","حَمَّالَةَ (v.4) — ACCUSATIVE in Ḥafṣ = naṣb ʿala'dh-dhamm (accusative of CENSURE). Not a mistake — the grammar points a finger. (Other recitations: nominative.)","حَمَّالَةَ الْحَطَبِ — an Arabic IDIOM for a TALEBEARER (carrying kindling between people), plus the literal thorns, plus her fate. Don't flatten it to one.","تَبَّتْ... وَتَبَّ (v.1) — first a CURSE (duʿāʾ), then a STATEMENT (khabar). Same verb, two functions.","وَمَا كَسَبَ (v.2) — widely read as \"his CHILDREN\" (hadith: children are of a man's earning). Not just a repeat of \"wealth.\"","جِيدِهَا (v.5) — specifically the neck AS A PLACE FOR JEWELLERY, not just \"neck.\" Necklace → rope.","أَبِي لَهَبٍ — a KUNYAH (nickname), not his name (ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā). And abī is one of the FIVE NOUNS (genitive by long vowel).","يَدَا (v.1) — DUAL nominative with the nūn DROPPED (in iḍāfah).","سَيَصْلَىٰ (v.3) — ṣaliya (roasted), NOT ṣalāh. And sa- = a testable prophecy, ~10 years before his death.","مَا أَغْنَىٰ (v.2) — mā = NEGATION (\"did not\") — or rhetorical \"what did it avail?\" — not the relative \"what.\""],
  links:[{w:"تَبَّتْ (v.1)",e:"الْأَبْتَرُ (Kawthar 108:3)",t:"Two insults RETURNED — abtar and tabban"},{w:"تَبَّتْ... وَتَبَّ (v.1)",e:"",t:"A CURSE, then a FACT — same verb, two functions"},{w:"أَبِي لَهَبٍ (v.1)",e:"أَصْحَابِ الْفِيلِ (105:1) · شَانِئَكَ (108:3)",t:"The ONLY enemy NAMED — the others are not"},{w:"يَدَا (v.1)",e:"قَدَّمَتْ يَدَاهُ (Nabaʾ 78:40)",t:"The two hands = the record of deeds"},{w:"أَبِي (v.1)",e:"ذِي (81:20 · 89:5 · 90:14)",t:"The five special nouns"},{w:"مَا أَغْنَىٰ عَنْهُ مَالُهُ (v.2)",e:"مَا يُغْنِي عَنْهُ مَالُهُ (Layl 92:11)",t:"Near-verbatim — principle (present) → named case (past)"},{w:"أَغْنَىٰ (v.2)",e:"80:5, 37 · 88:7 · 92:8 · 93:8 · 96:7",t:"The ghinā thread ends on a NAME"},{w:"وَمَا كَسَبَ (v.2)",e:"مَا كَانُوا يَكْسِبُونَ (Muṭaff. 83:14)",t:"Earnings that RUST / earnings that FAIL"},{w:"وَمَا كَسَبَ (v.2)",e:"الْأَبْتَرُ (108:3)",t:"He had SONS, mocked the sonless — sons availed nothing"},{w:"سَيَصْلَىٰ (v.3)",e:"82:15 · 83:16 · 84:12 · 87:12 · 88:4 · 92:15",t:"Ṣaliya — EIGHT surahs"},{w:"ذَاتَ لَهَبٍ (v.3)",e:"ذَاتِ الْوَقُودِ (Burūj 85:5)",t:"Fire full of FUEL / fire full of FLAME"},{w:"لَهَبٍ (v.3)",e:"أَبِي لَهَبٍ (v.1, same surah)",t:"Father of Flame → fire of flame"},{w:"حَمَّالَةَ الْحَطَبِ (v.4)",e:"هُمَزَةٍ لُّمَزَةٍ (Humazah 104:1)",t:"The slanderer type → given a face"},{w:"حَمَّالَةَ (v.4)",e:"لَوَّامَة (75:2) · تَوَّابًا (110:3)",t:"Faʿʿāl(ah) intensive"},{w:"جِيدِهَا (v.5)",e:"أَغْلَالًا (76:4) · رَقَبَةٍ (90:13)",t:"The NECK thread — collared, freed, roped"},{w:"امْرَأَتُهُ (v.4)",e:"الْمَرْءُ (78:40 · 80:34)",t:"Same root — a person"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"تَبَّتْ... وَتَبَّ", tr:"Tabbat… wa tabb", v:"1", root:"The \"PERISHING / ruin / loss that ends in destruction\" family — tabba = to perish, to be lost, to come to ruin.", meaning:"\"Perish the hands of Abū Lahab — and he has perished.\"", magic:"His own curse, sent back word for word — and the verb appears TWICE in two different functions.", grammar:"Tabbat — past tense, feminine (agreeing with yadā, hands — feminine in Arabic).", why:"Tabba — because it was HIS word. Returning an insult verbatim is the strongest possible reply.", note:"At Ṣafā he shouted \"TABBAN laka!\" → the surah's first word: TABBAT. His own curse, returned.", more:["Tabāb = utter loss and destruction (40:37: wa mā kaydu Firʿawna illā fī tabāb — \"Pharaoh's plot was only in ruin\"!).","At Ṣafā, Abū Lahab shouted at the Prophet ﷺ: \"TABBAN laka!\" (\"May you PERISH!\"). The surah's very first word: TABBAT. The same root, returned to sender. Compare Kawthar 108:3: they called him abtar (cut off) → \"your hater is the abtar.\" Two consecutive-ish surahs, two insults thrown back.","And tabba appears TWICE — with two different jobs: - TABBAT yadā = a CURSE (duʿāʾ) — \"may his hands perish\" - wa TABBA = a STATEMENT OF FACT (khabar) — \"and he HAS perished\"","First a wish, then a verdict. The curse isn't hoping — it's announcing what's already decided. Past tense for a future certainty — the device you've seen all juzʾ.","Compare Fīl 105:2's taḍlīl and Ṭāriq's kayd — and note 40:37, Pharaoh's kayd \"in tabāb.\" The tyrant's plot and the uncle's hands — both tabāb.","wa tabba — past, masculine (him).","The past tense as duʿāʾ is standard Arabic for curses and blessings (raḥimahu'llāh = \"may Allah have mercy on him\").","Tabbat = a CURSE (\"may…\"); wa tabba = a FACT (\"and he HAS…\").","Like Kawthar 108:3: abtar returned to sender."]},
  {ar:"يَدَا أَبِي لَهَبٍ", tr:"Yadā abī lahab", v:"1", root:"Yadā — the TWO HANDS (dual of yad).", meaning:"\"the two hands of Abū Lahab\"", magic:"Three things are happening in three words.", grammar:"Yadā — dual NOMINATIVE, the nūn of the dual (yadāni) is DROPPED in iḍāfah.", why:"Abū Lahab — because the nickname is the verdict.", note:"ABŪ LAHAB (v.1) → DHĀTA LAHAB (v.3). Father of Flame → a fire of flame. ILTIHĀB = INFLAMMATION (clinical!) — same root.", more:["Abū Lahab — \"Father of FLAME\" — a kunyah (nickname), given for his glowing, ruddy, handsome face; his actual name was ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā (\"servant of al-ʿUzzā,\" an idol).","Lahab = a FLAME, the leaping tongue of a fire (iltahaba = to blaze up; modern iltihāb = INFLAMMATION — a word you use clinically!).","ONE — \"HANDS\" = his deeds, his efforts. The hands are what DO things. You own this from Nabaʾ 78:40 — mā qaddamat yadāh (\"what his two HANDS sent forth\"). Same dual, same meaning: the hands as the record of deeds. Classical reports also mention he raised his hand to throw a stone at the Prophet ﷺ at Ṣafā.","TWO — the KUNYAH, not the NAME. His real name was ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā — \"servant of an idol.\" The Qurʾān will not call him \"servant of al-ʿUzzā\" — it uses his nickname instead. Classical note: the Qurʾān avoids attaching ʿabd (servant) to an idol.","THREE — LAHAB (FLAME) returns in v.3. Abū Lahab (v.1) → nāran dhāta lahab (v.3). The nickname given for his beautiful glowing face becomes the word for his fire. The thing he was praised for becomes what he's punished in.","The clinical hook: iltihāb = INFLAMMATION — literally a \"flaring up.\" Iltihāb al-zāʾidah = appendicitis. Same root as lahab.","And the naming itself is unique: the only enemy of the Prophet ﷺ named in the Qurʾān. Compare Fīl 105:1 (Abrahah unnamed), Kawthar 108:3 (the hater unnamed).","Abī — genitive of Abū — one of the FIVE SPECIAL NOUNS (al-asmāʾ al-khamsah): abū/abā/abī — case shown by the long vowel (same family as dhū/dhā/dhī, Takwīr 81:20, Fajr 89:5, Balad 90:14–15).","Real name ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā (\"servant of an idol\") — the Qurʾān won't say it.","The ONLY enemy NAMED in the Qurʾān.","Abī = one of the FIVE NOUNS (like dhī)."]},
  {ar:"مَا أَغْنَىٰ عَنْهُ مَالُهُ", tr:"Mā aghnā ʿanhu māluh", v:"2", root:"The \"richness / sufficiency\" family — ghaniya = to be rich; aghnā ʿan = to AVAIL / suffice FOR someone.", meaning:"\"His wealth did not avail him.\"", magic:"You own this phrase almost VERBATIM from Layl 92:11 — and the ghinā root is your most-developed thread.", grammar:"Form IV past (aghnā), weak-final. aghnā ʿan = \"avail/suffice for\" — note the fixed preposition.", why:"Aghnā — to close the juzʾ's great thread about the illusion of self-sufficiency on one real, named person.", note:"Layl 92:11: mā YUGHNĪ ʿanhu māluh (PRESENT — \"WILL not avail\"). Masad 111:2: mā AGHNĀ ʿanhu māluh (PAST — \"DID not avail\").", more:["Layl 92:11 — wa mā yughnī ʿanhu māluhu idhā taraddā (\"his wealth will not avail him when he falls\") Masad 111:2 — mā aghnā ʿanhu māluhu (\"his wealth did not avail him\")","Same verb, same preposition, same subject. One change: Layl = PRESENT (yughnī, \"will not avail\"), Masad = PAST (aghnā, \"did not avail\"). Layl stated the principle; Masad shows it already happened to a named man.","And the whole ghinā thread — now SIX surahs: - ʿAbasa 80:5 — mani'staghnā (the chief who considered himself needing nothing) - ʿAbasa 80:37 — shaʾnun yughnīh (a concern that leaves room for nothing) - Ghāshiyah 88:7 — lā yughnī min jūʿ (Hell's food doesn't suffice) - Layl 92:8, 11 — istaghnā… mā yughnī ʿanhu māluh (self-sufficient → wealth won't avail) - Ḍuḥā 93:8 — fa-aghnā (HE enriched you — the redemption) - ʿAlaq 96:7 — an raʾāhu'staghnā (the CAUSE of transgression) - Masad 111:2 — mā aghnā ʿanhu māluh (didn't avail a NAMED man)","The thread ends on a specific name. Abū Lahab was wealthy and powerful in Quraysh; none of it helped.","Note: mā here is NEGATION (\"did NOT\"), not \"what.\" (Some read it as interrogative: \"what did his wealth avail him?\" — same effect.)","The ghinā thread across SIX surahs ends on a NAME."]},
  {ar:"وَمَا كَسَبَ", tr:"Wa mā kasab", v:"2", root:"The \"EARNING / acquiring\" family — kasaba = to earn, to gain, to acquire (by effort); kasb = earnings, income; maksab = a gain/profit.", meaning:"\"…nor what he earned.\"", magic:"Classical scholars read \"what he EARNED\" as something beyond money — and the reading is moving.", grammar:"Mā — relative (\"what\") — or a second negation (\"nor did he earn anything [useful]\"). Kasaba — past tense.", why:"Mā kasab — broad enough to include his sons, which completes the reply to the abtar mockery.", note:"\"His wealth AND what he earned\" — \"earned\" = his CHILDREN (hadith: \"your children are of your earning\").", more:["V.2 already said \"his WEALTH\" (māluhu). So what's left for \"what he EARNED\" (mā kasab)? Many classical commentators: his CHILDREN.","Because of a well-known hadith: \"The best of what a man eats is from his own earning, and his children are of his earning.\" Children counted as a man's kasb.","And here's the irony: Kawthar 108:3 mocked the Prophet ﷺ as abtar — sonless, cut off — by people like Abū Lahab, who HAD sons. Masad: his sons — \"what he earned\" — won't help him. The man with sons mocked the man without; the sons availed nothing.","(Other readings: his status, his deeds, his power.)","You own kasaba from Muṭaffifīn 83:14 — bal rāna ʿalā qulūbihim mā kānū yaksibūn (\"what they used to EARN has rusted over their hearts\"). Muṭaffifīn: what they earned RUSTED their hearts. Masad: what he earned didn't SAVE him. Earning that corrodes, earning that fails.","He mocked the Prophet ﷺ as SONLESS (abtar, 108:3) — his own sons availed him nothing.","Muṭaffifīn 83:14: what they EARNED rusted their hearts."]},
  {ar:"سَيَصْلَىٰ نَارًا ذَاتَ لَهَبٍ", tr:"Sa-yaṣlā nāran dhāta lahab", v:"3", root:"Yaṣlā — the \"being ROASTED / burned in\" family (ṣaliya) — not the ṣalāh root.", meaning:"\"He will burn in a Fire of [blazing] flame.\"", magic:"THE PAYOFF — and two threads close at once.", grammar:"سَـ + present = near future. dhāta — accusative of dhāt (feminine \"possessor of\"), describing nāran.", why:"Lahab — because his name was already the word for flame.", note:"FATHER OF FLAME (v.1) → FIRE OF FLAME (v.3). His nickname became his fire. Ṣaliya — EIGHT surahs now. Not the ṣalāh root.", more:["dhāt — \"possessor of / full of\" (feminine of dhū).","LAHAB — his name. v.1: Abū LAHAB (Father of Flame). v.3: nāran dhāta LAHAB (a fire full of flame). The nickname given in admiration of his glowing face becomes the substance of his fire. \"Father of Flame\" — literally, the flame's father; now its fuel.","And ṣaliya — your longest-running Hell-verb — now in EIGHT surahs: - Infiṭār 82:15 · Muṭaffifīn 83:16 · Inshiqāq 84:12 · Aʿlā 87:12 · Ghāshiyah 88:4 · Layl 92:15 · Masad 111:3","Watch: Aʿlā 87:12 has yaṣlā (roasted) and 87:15 ṣallā (prayed) — three verses apart. Same letters, different roots.","And note sa- (near future): revealed roughly ten years BEFORE he died. A man told \"you WILL burn\" — who had a decade to falsify the prophecy by simply embracing Islam, even outwardly. He never did. The sa- is a living, testable prediction.","The dhāt construction you own: Burūj 85:1 (as-samāʾi dhāti'l-burūj), 85:5 (an-nāri dhāti'l-waqūd — \"the fire FULL OF fuel\"), Fajr 89:7 (Iram dhāti'l-ʿimād), Ṭāriq 86:11–12. Burūj 85:5 — the persecutors' nār dhāt al-waqūd (fire full of FUEL); Masad 111:3 — the persecutor's nār dhāt lahab (fire full of FLAME).","Sa- = \"WILL\" — said ~10 years before his death; he could have falsified it any day.","Burūj 85:5: fire full of FUEL.","Masad 111:3: fire full of FLAME."]},
  {ar:"وَامْرَأَتُهُ حَمَّالَةَ الْحَطَبِ", tr:"Wa'mraʾatuhu ḥammālata'l-ḥaṭab", v:"4", root:"Imraʾah — a woman/wife (same root as al-marʾ, \"the person,\" Nabaʾ 78:40, ʿAbasa 80:34). ḥammālah.", meaning:"\"And his wife — the carrier of firewood.\"", magic:"A phrase with two meanings, both classical, both devastating — and an unusual grammatical case.", grammar:"ḥammālata = accusative of censure (naṣb ʿala'dh-dhamm) in Ḥafṣ.", why:"ḥammālat al-ḥaṭab — one phrase covering her literal cruelty, her gossip, and her fate.", note:"Ḥammālat al-ḥaṭab = a TALEBEARER (Arabic idiom: carrying KINDLING between people). Also: she strewed THORNS on his path.", more:["The \"CARRYING\" family — ḥamala = to carry; ḥammāl = a PORTER (one who carries loads for a living!); ḥammālah = a habitual/professional carrier (the faʿʿālah intensive).","ḥaṭab — FIREWOOD, kindling, dry sticks for burning.","His wife was Umm Jamīl (Arwā bint Ḥarb), sister of Abū Sufyān — a woman of high status in Quraysh.","READING ONE — LITERAL: she gathered thorny branches and scattered them on the Prophet's ﷺ path at night, to hurt his feet. A \"carrier of firewood\" — the thorny kind.","READING TWO — IDIOMATIC: in Arabic, ḥammālat al-ḥaṭab (\"the firewood-carrier\") was an established expression for a TALEBEARER — one who spreads gossip and slander to kindle hostility between people. She carries the \"kindling\" that sets people against each other. Rumours as firewood.","And reading two connects to Humazah 104:1 — wayl li-kulli humazatin lumazah — woe to every SLANDERER, FAULT-FINDER. Humazah named the type; Masad gives it a face — a talebearer who lit fires between people.","And READING THREE (eschatological, often combined): in Hell, she will carry the firewood for her husband's fire. The couple who fuelled hostility in life fuel each other's flame. v.3: his fire of flame. v.4: she carries the wood.","The faʿʿālah intensive (ḥammālah) — a habitual carrier — the same shape you've tracked: lawwāmah (75:2), tawwāb (110:3), wahhāj (78:13). It's a character, not a single act.","THE GRAMMAR GEM: ḥammālata is ACCUSATIVE — in the standard Ḥafṣ reading, where you'd expect nominative. Classical grammarians call this naṣb ʿala'dh-dhamm — accusative of CENSURE: \"and his wife — I mean/I condemn the carrier of firewood.\" The grammar itself points a finger. (Other recitations read ḥammālatu, nominative — both established.)","Free hook: ḥammāl = a PORTER — someone who carries loads for pay.","Imraʾatuhu — nominative, joined to the subject of sa-yaṣlā (she burns too).","Also: she carries wood for her husband's fire.","Humazah 104:1 named the type; Masad gives it a FACE.","Ḥammālata = ACCUSATIVE OF CENSURE — the grammar points a finger."]},
  {ar:"فِي جِيدِهَا حَبْلٌ مِّن مَّسَدٍ", tr:"Fī jīdihā ḥablun min masad", v:"5", root:"Jīd — THE NECK — specifically a long, graceful neck, the place where JEWELLERY is worn (different from raqabah or ʿunuq).", meaning:"\"Around her neck is a rope of twisted palm-fibre.\"", magic:"The final image of the surah is a NECKLACE replaced by a ROPE — and it's historically specific.", grammar:"A nominal sentence with fronted predicate: fī jīdihā (predicate, fronted) + ḥablun (subject, indefinite).", why:"Jīd (the ornamented neck) rather than ʿunuq — to set the jewellery against the rope.", note:"JĪD = the neck WHERE JEWELLERY HANGS. She vowed her precious NECKLACE against the Prophet ﷺ → a rope of the COARSEST palm-fibre.", more:["ḥabl — A ROPE (ḥabl Allāh = the rope of Allah, 3:103; ḥabl al-warīd = the jugular vein, 50:16 — \"closer than the jugular\").","masad — coarse rope of twisted PALM-FIBRE — the roughest, cheapest rope; from masada = to twist tightly.","Classical reports: Umm Jamīl owned a valuable necklace, and swore she would spend it in opposing the Prophet ﷺ. And jīd is specifically the neck AS A PLACE FOR ORNAMENT — the word you'd use for a beautiful neck with jewellery on it.","So the verse says: where the precious necklace hung — a rope of the COARSEST palm-fibre. The finest ornament → the roughest rope. And it fits the firewood-carrier image (v.4): a rope like the ones used to bind bundles of firewood on a labourer's back. The noblewoman ends with a porter's rope.","The NECK thread across your surahs: - Insān 76:4 — aghlāl (neck-collars in Hell) - Balad 90:13 — fakku raqabah (freeing a NECK — the steep climb) - Masad 111:5 — fī jīdihā ḥablun (a rope on a NECK)","Free the neck of another (Balad), or find a rope on your own (Masad).","And the surah is NAMED after this last, humblest word — al-Masad. Like Māʿūn (107), named after the smallest kindness; Masad named after the coarsest rope.","And ḥabl has a beautiful opposite: ḥabl Allāh — \"hold fast to the ROPE of Allah\" (3:103). One rope saves; this one binds.","Fronting = emphasis: \"ON HER NECK — a rope.\" Min masad = \"made of palm-fibre\" (min of material).","Ḥabl Allāh (3:103) = the rope that SAVES; ḥabl min masad = the rope that BINDS.","Neck thread: collars (76:4), FREE a neck (90:13), a ROPE on hers (111:5). The surah is named after its humblest word."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:112, name:"Al-Ikhlāṣ", ar:"الإخلاص", en:"Sincerity", verses:4, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"AḤAD … AḤAD. First: \"He is the One.\" Last: \"there is no one like Him.\" Same word, both ends.",
  special:["It opens and closes on the same word — aḥad — and that one word means two opposite things.","V.1: qul huwa'llāhu AḤAD — \"Say: He is Allah, the ONE.\" v.4: wa lam yakun lahu kufuwan AḤAD — \"And there is not to Him any equal — ANYONE.\"","Aḥad has two lives in Arabic: - In an AFFIRMATIVE sentence — aḥad = THE absolutely unique One — and it is used this way ONLY for Allah. - In a NEGATIVE sentence — aḥad = \"anyone at all\" — a general word for any person.","So the surah begins \"He is AḤAD\" and ends \"there is no AḤAD like Him.\" The first aḥad is Him; the last aḥad is everyone else — and none of them compares. One word as the two bookends of tawḥīd.","The name of the surah is the word you met in Bayyinah 98:5 — mukhliṣīna lahu'd-dīn — ikhlāṣ = PURITY, unmixed, like pure gold. This is the surah of unmixed belief about Allah. The Prophet ﷺ said it is equal to a third of the Qurʾān.","--"],
  big:["Who He is (v.1) — He is Allah, the One.","What He is (v.2) — Allah, the Self-Sufficient Master to whom all turn.","What He is not (v.3) — He neither begets nor is begotten.","The seal (v.4) — nothing is comparable to Him."],
  traps:["أَحَدٌ vs وَاحِد — wāḥid = one (a countable number); aḥad = UNIQUE (outside every count). In affirmation, aḥad is used ONLY for Allah.","أَحَدٌ appears TWICE with DIFFERENT senses — v.1 (affirmative) = THE One; v.4 (negative) = ANYONE. Same word, two meanings.","الصَّمَدُ (v.2) — BOTH \"needs nothing\" AND \"all turn to Him.\" Don't translate it with only half. And it occurs ONLY here in the Qurʾān.","هُوَ (v.1) — possibly ḍamīr ash-shaʾn (\"the matter is…\"), not only \"He.\"","لَمْ يَلِدْ before لَمْ يُولَدْ — the order refutes the MORE COMMON error first (claims of divine offspring).","V.4 word order — lahu and kufuwan FRONTED, aḥad DELAYED to the end. Deliberate, so the surah closes on the word it opened with.","يَلِدْ — the initial wāw of walada DROPS in the present/jussive (yalid); it reappears in the passive yūlad.","الصَّمَدُ is DEFINITE, أَحَدٌ INDEFINITE — definite = restriction (\"THE only Ṣamad\"); indefinite = magnificence beyond grasp.","كُفُوًا — recognized recitation variants (kufuʾan, kufʾan) — same meaning.","\"A third of the Qurʾān\" — in reward/meaning (the Qurʾān's themes: law, stories, and tawḥīd), not a replacement for reciting the rest."],
  links:[{w:"Surah name: al-Ikhlāṣ",e:"مُخْلِصِينَ (Bayyinah 98:5)",t:"Khāliṣ = PURE GOLD — unmixed belief"},{w:"قُلْ (v.1)",e:"109:1 · 113:1 · 114:1",t:"The four Quls"},{w:"Kāfirūn + Ikhlāṣ",e:"",t:"What I DON'T worship + Whom I DO = lā ilāha + illa'llāh"},{w:"أَحَدٌ... أَحَدٌ (v.1, 4)",e:"",t:"Same word, both bookends — HIM / anyone else"},{w:"الصَّمَدُ (v.2)",e:"اسْتَغْنَىٰ (80:5 · 92:8 · 96:7)",t:"Istighnāʾ = claiming aṣ-Ṣamad's attribute — the root of transgression"},{w:"الصَّمَدُ (v.2)",e:"فَأَغْنَىٰ (93:8) · وَاسْتَغْفِرْهُ (110:3)",t:"The cure — HE enriches; YOU admit need"},{w:"الصَّمَدُ (v.2)",e:"أَطْعَمَهُم مِّن جُوعٍ (Quraysh 106:4)",t:"Creatures are hollow and hunger; He fills every hunger"},{w:"لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ (v.3)",e:"وَوَالِدٍ وَمَا وَلَدَ (Balad 90:3)",t:"Balad swore by begetting; Ikhlāṣ places Him outside it"},{w:"يَلِدْ (v.3)",e:"وِلْدَانٌ (Insān 76:19)",t:"Same root — begetting"},{w:"لَمْ يَكُن (v.4)",e:"أَلَمْ يَكُ (Qiyāmah 75:37)",t:"Yakun keeps the nūn here; dropped there"},{w:"أَحَدٌ (v.4, delayed)",e:"Fronting throughout",t:"Word order that makes the last word echo the first"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"قُلْ هُوَ", tr:"Qul huwa", v:"1", root:"Qul — SAY (the second of the four qul surahs). huwa — HE.", meaning:"\"Say: He is…\"", magic:"The answer begins with a PRONOUN — before the Name.", grammar:"Huwa as ḍamīr ash-shaʾn (pronoun of the matter) or as a direct referent. Qul — hollow-verb imperative.", why:"Starting with huwa treats Him as already known — the opposite of how you'd introduce a stranger.", note:"The answer starts with HUWA — \"HE\" — before the Name. \"The matter is this…\" (ḍamīr ash-shaʾn).", more:["They asked: \"Who is your Lord?\" The answer doesn't start with \"Allah.\" It starts with HUWA — \"HE.\"","Two classical readings, both beautiful: - (a) ḍamīr ash-shaʾn — the \"pronoun of the matter\": \"The matter is this: Allah is One.\" A pronoun that announces something weighty is coming. - (b) \"He\" — the One you're asking about — as if He needs no introduction; the questioner already, deep down, knows Who is meant.","And qul again proves verbatim transmission — you own this from Kāfirūn 109:1. The four Quls: Kāfirūn 109 (disavowal), Ikhlāṣ 112 (who He is), Falaq 113 & Nās 114 (seeking refuge in Him).","And the pairing with Kāfirūn is famous: the Prophet ﷺ recited them together in the two sunnah rakʿahs before Fajr. Kāfirūn says what I do NOT worship; Ikhlāṣ says Whom I DO. Negation, then affirmation — exactly like lā ilāha illa'llāh.","Kāfirūn = what I DON'T worship; Ikhlāṣ = Whom I DO — like lā ilāha + illa'llāh.","Recited together before Fajr."]},
  {ar:"أَحَدٌ", tr:"Aḥad", v:"1, 4", root:"The \"ONENESS\" family — w-ḥ-d (the hamzah in aḥad replaced an original wāw) — wāḥid = one (a number).", meaning:"\"the One [and Only].\"", magic:"Why aḥad and not wāḥid? — the most important word distinction in the surah.", grammar:"Allāhu aḥad — Allāh is the khabar of huwa (or subject), and aḥad a second khabar.", why:"Aḥad over wāḥid — because wāḥid would allow a second.", note:"WĀḤID = one (can begin a COUNT). AḤAD = UNIQUE (outside every count). In AFFIRMATION, aḥad is used ONLY for Allah.", more:["Tawḥīd = making/declaring ONE; waḥdah = unity / solitude.","WĀḤID = \"one\" as a NUMBER — it can START a count: one, two, three… One apple; there could be another. AḤAD = ABSOLUTELY UNIQUE — one that has no second of any kind, cannot be counted with anything, cannot be divided. Not \"the first of a series\" — outside every series.","And in an AFFIRMATIVE statement, aḥad is used for NO ONE but Allah. You can say \"wāḥid man came\" — you cannot say \"aḥad man came\" as a positive statement. The word is reserved.","And then v.4 uses the SAME word in a NEGATIVE sentence — wa lam yakun lahu kufuwan aḥad — \"not ANYONE is equal to Him.\" In negation, aḥad = \"anyone.\"","So the surah is framed: v.1 aḥad = HIM; v.4 aḥad = ALL OTHERS. He is aḥad; no aḥad is like Him.","The historical hook: Bilāl ibn Rabāḥ (رضي الله عنه), pinned under a boulder in the Makkan sun, repeating only: \"AḤAD… AḤAD…\" One word was his entire creed.","Note aḥadun is INDEFINITE here — while v.2's aṣ-ṣamad is DEFINITE. Classical note: the indefinite aḥad expresses magnificence beyond full comprehension; the definite aṣ-ṣamad expresses a known, established reality (everyone turns to Him).","Aḥad in affirmation = only Allah; in negation = \"anyone.\"","In NEGATION, it means \"anyone.\" v.1: He is AḤAD.","Bilāl under the rock: \"AḤAD, AḤAD.\""]},
  {ar:"اللَّهُ الصَّمَدُ", tr:"Allāhu'ṣ-ṣamad", v:"2", root:"The \"SOLID / turned-to / relied upon\" family — ṣamad has two classical core meanings, both ancient: (a) the MASTER to whom people GO in their needs (ṣamada ilayhi = to direct oneself toward someone for help); (b) SOLID, with NO HOLLOW inside — something that doesn't eat, doesn't drink, has no inner need to be filled.", meaning:"\"Allah, the Self-Sufficient, the One to whom all turn in need.\"", magic:"THE GREATEST PAYOFF IN THE ENTIRE JUZʾ — it answers istaghnā.", grammar:"Allāhu'ṣ-ṣamad — a nominal sentence; the definite predicate = restriction (\"Allah — and only Allah — is the Ṣamad\").", why:"ṣamad — because it names in one word both His total independence and everyone's total dependence on Him.", note:"AṢ-ṢAMAD = (a) needs NOTHING (solid, no hollow) + (b) EVERYTHING needs Him.", more:["Aṣ-Ṣamad holds two facts at once: - HE needs NOTHING — solid, no hollow, no inner lack. - EVERYTHING needs HIM — every creature turns to Him for its needs.","NOW RECALL THE JUZʾ'S DEEPEST DISEASE — istaghnāʾ: - ʿAbasa 80:5 — ammā mani'staghnā (the chief who considered himself needing nothing) - Layl 92:8 — bakhila wa'staghnā (the withholder) - ʿAlaq 96:7 — an raʾāhu'staghnā (the ROOT CAUSE of all transgression)","Self-sufficiency — needing nothing — is aṣ-Ṣamad's attribute. The mustaghnī was claiming a quality that belongs only to Allah. That's why it's the root of transgression: it's a quiet claim to be ṣamad yourself.","And the cure was always the same: Ḍuḥā 93:8 — wa wajadaka ʿāʾilan fa-aghnā (\"He found you in need and HE enriched you\"), Naṣr 110:3 — wa'staghfirh (\"seek His forgiveness\" — admit your need). The servant needs; only aṣ-Ṣamad doesn't.","And the \"no hollow\" meaning has a vivid echo: Quraysh 106:4 — aṭʿamahum min jūʿ — He FED them from hunger. Creatures are hollow — they get hungry, they must be filled. He is ṣamad — never hungry, and the One who fills every hunger.","Note Allāh is REPEATED (v.1 huwa'llāhu aḥad, v.2 Allāhu'ṣ-ṣamad) — rather than \"He is the Ṣamad.\" The Name stated twice for emphasis and majesty.","And aṣ-ṣamad is DEFINITE — \"THE Ṣamad\" = the ONLY one (restriction by the article). Not \"a\" master — the one, exclusively.","This is the ONLY occurrence of aṣ-Ṣamad in the entire Qurʾān.","Istaghnā (80:5, 92:8, 96:7) = claiming to need nothing = claiming aṣ-Ṣamad's attribute.","That's why it's the root of transgression.","The cure: aghnā (93:8, HE enriches) and istaghfir (110:3, admit your need).","The ONLY occurrence of aṣ-Ṣamad in the Qurʾān."]},
  {ar:"لَمْ يَلِدْ وَلَمْ يُولَدْ", tr:"Lam yalid wa lam yūlad", v:"3", root:"The \"BEGETTING / giving birth\" family — walada = to beget, give birth; wālid = a parent; walad = a child.", meaning:"\"He neither begets, nor is He begotten.\"", magic:"Two verbs — active then passive — and the root closes a thread from Balad.", grammar:"Lam + jussive ×2. yalid — from walada, the initial wāw drops in the present (yalidu), jussive yalid.", why:"Two verbs, active then passive — the most complete possible denial of lineage.", note:"Lam YALID = no CHILD (downstream). Lam YŪLAD = no PARENT (upstream). Both directions closed.", more:["Mawlid = a birth/birthday; wildān = boys (Insān 76:19!).","Lam YALID (active) = \"He does NOT BEGET\" — no children, no offspring. Lam YŪLAD (passive) = \"He was NOT BEGOTTEN\" — no parent, no origin.","Downstream and upstream, both closed. Nothing came from Him (as a child), nothing came before Him (as a parent). The ṣamad of v.2 has no lineage in either direction.","And the ORDER is deliberate: lam yalid comes FIRST — because the more widespread false claim was that Allah HAS offspring (angels as \"daughters,\" or a divine son). Refute the common error first, then the rarer one.","NOW THE BALAD CONNECTION: Balad 90:3 — wa wālidin wa mā walad — \"by a BEGETTER and what he BEGOT.\" Balad SWORE BY the pattern of begetting — every parent and child — as the defining pattern of creation, the root of human kabad (toil, 90:4). Ikhlāṣ declares Allah OUTSIDE that pattern entirely. Every created thing is either a wālid or a mawlūd (usually both); He is neither.","And it answers the original question (\"what is His lineage?\"): He has none. Lineage is a creaturely thing.","Note lam + jussive = PAST negation (\"did not\"), but with Allah's attributes it means never, at any time.","yūlad — passive, the wāw returns as the long ū.","Balad 90:3 swore by \"a BEGETTER and what he BEGOT\" — creation's pattern.","Mawlid = a birthday — same root.","Common error refuted FIRST."]},
  {ar:"وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُ كُفُوًا أَحَدٌ", tr:"Wa lam yakun lahu kufuwan aḥad", v:"4", root:"The \"EQUIVALENCE / being a match\" family — kafuʾa = to be equal, equivalent; kufʾ = an equal, a peer, a match.", meaning:"\"And there is none comparable to Him.\"", magic:"The final verse seals every remaining door — and the word order does the work.", grammar:"Lam yakun (past negation of kāna) — jussive yakun keeps its nūn here (unlike yaku in Qiyāmah 75:37's a-lam yaku).", why:"Kufʾ (a match/peer) — the broadest word for comparability, closing the final possibility.", note:"Kafāʾah = a MARRIAGE MATCH / COMPETENCE. No kufʾ = He has no match, no peer.", more:["Kafāʾah = COMPATIBILITY in marriage (and in modern Arabic, kafāʾah = COMPETENCE / qualification!); mukāfaʾah = a reward (an equivalent return).","V.1 said He is ONE. v.2 said He needs nothing. v.3 said He has no lineage. One door remained: could there be something that, without being His child or parent, is simply EQUAL to Him? v.4 closes it: no kufʾ — no equal, no match, no peer, no rival, no comparison.","And the WORD ORDER is unusual: normal Arabic would be lam yakun aḥadun kufuwan lahu. Instead: lam yakun LAHU KUFUWAN aḥad — \"TO HIM\" and \"COMPARABLE\" are both FRONTED, and aḥad is pushed to the very end. The fronting puts \"to Him — an equal\" up front (the very idea being denied), and ends the surah on aḥad — so the last word rhymes with, and echoes, the first.","And the everyday hooks: kafāʾah in marriage = being a suitable MATCH — so lā kufuwa lahu = He has no match. Kafāʾah in modern Arabic = COMPETENCE.","And the full four-verse structure: - v.1 — He is ONE (no second) - v.2 — He NEEDS nothing; all need Him (no dependence) - v.3 — No child, no parent (no lineage) - v.4 — No equal (no comparison)","Every way the human mind imagines a god — with partners, needs, family, or rivals — removed.","Kufuwan has recognized recitation variants (kufuʾan, kufʾan) — same meaning.","Kufuwan = khabar of yakun, fronted; aḥadun = its subject, delayed to the end.","Word order: \"TO HIM — COMPARABLE — anyone\" — aḥad pushed to the END so the surah's last word echoes its first.","Four verses: no SECOND, no NEED, no LINEAGE, no EQUAL."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:113, name:"Al-Falaq", ar:"الفلق", en:"The Daybreak", verses:5, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"FALAQ = 1 Name vs 4 evils (to your BODY). NĀS = 3 Names vs 1 evil (to your FAITH). The bigger danger gets more Names.",
  special:["ONE Name of Allah against FOUR evils — while the very next surah does the reverse.","Al-Falaq (113): seek refuge in ONE attribute — Rabb al-Falaq (Lord of the Daybreak) — from FOUR evils: everything created, the settling dark, the knot-blowers, the envier.","An-Nās (114): seek refuge in THREE attributes — Rabb, Malik, Ilāh of mankind — from ONE evil: the whisperer.","Classical scholars (notably Ibn al-Qayyim) explain the imbalance: Falaq's evils threaten your BODY and WORLDLY life (darkness, magic, envy) — Nās's evil threatens your FAITH (whispering into the heart). The harm to your religion is greater — so you call on MORE of His Names against it. Four evils need one Name; one whisper needs three.","These two are al-Muʿawwidhatān — \"the two [surahs] of seeking refuge.\" The Prophet ﷺ would recite them (with al-Ikhlāṣ) into his cupped hands each night before sleep and wipe them over his body; and he advised reciting them after every prayer.","--"],
  big:["The refuge (v.1) — in the Lord of the Daybreak.","The general (v.2) — from the evil of what He created.","Three specifics (v.3–5) — the settling dark · those who blow on knots · the envier when he envies."],
  traps:["مِن شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ (v.2) — \"the evil OF what He created,\" NOT \"the evil He created.\" Evil is attached to creation, never to His Name — a deliberate courtesy.","الْعُقَدِ (v.4) — KNOTS — same root as ʿaqd (contract) and ʿaqīdah (creed). Tying for harm vs. tying in faith.","Falaq vs Nās — 1 Name / 4 evils (bodily) vs 3 Names / 1 evil (faith). Read them as a matched pair.","حَاسِدٍ إِذَا حَسَدَ (v.5) — \"WHEN he envies\" — the harm is tied to activation. And ḥasad ≠ ghibṭah.","النَّفَّاثَاتِ (v.4) — feminine plural widely read as \"SOULS that blow\" — any practitioner, not a statement about women.","غَاسِقٍ (v.3) — same root as ghassāq (Nabaʾ 78:25). And waqab = settling like water in a hollow.","الْفَلَقِ (v.1) — the THIRD splitting root (with fajr and faṭara). And chosen because every evil named is a form of DARKNESS.","أَعُوذُ (v.1) — present tense = an ongoing refuge — and the image is CLINGING, not filing a request.","Definite vs indefinite — ghāsiq (indef.), an-naffāthāt (def.), ḥāsid (indef.). Known practitioners vs. dangers from anywhere.","إِذَا appears twice (v.3, v.5) but NOT in v.4 — sorcery is always active; darkness and envy become harmful when activated."],
  links:[{w:"قُلْ أَعُوذُ (v.1)",e:"قُلْ أَعُوذُ (Nās 114:1)",t:"The Muʿawwidhatān"},{w:"أَعُوذُ (v.1)",e:"رَّجِيمٍ (Takwīr 81:25)",t:"Aʿūdhu billāhi mina'sh-shayṭāni'r-rajīm"},{w:"أَعُوذُ (v.1)",e:"فَأُمُّهُ هَاوِيَةٌ (Qāriʿah 101:9)",t:"Refuge in the Abyss / refuge in the Lord of Daybreak"},{w:"الْفَلَقِ (v.1)",e:"الْفَجْرِ (89:1, 97:5) · انفَطَرَتْ (82:1)",t:"THREE splitting-roots: f-j-r, f-ṭ-r, f-l-q"},{w:"شَرِّ (v.2–5)",e:"شَرُّ الْبَرِيَّةِ (98:6) · شَرًّا (99:8)",t:"Sharr — and evil kept off His Name"},{w:"غَاسِقٍ (v.3)",e:"غَسَّاقًا (Nabaʾ 78:25)",t:"Same root — Hell's dark drink / night's darkness"},{w:"إِذَا وَقَبَ (v.3)",e:"إِذَا سَجَىٰ (Ḍuḥā 93:2)",t:"Night still in comfort / night settling as threat"},{w:"الْعُقَدِ (v.4)",e:"فَكُّ (90:13) · مُنفَكِّينَ (98:1) · النَّاشِطَاتِ (79:2)",t:"Knots tied for harm vs. the juzʾ's untying"},{w:"الْعُقَدِ (v.4)",e:"ʿaqd · ʿaqīdah",t:"Knot / contract / creed — all \"tying\""},{w:"النَّفَّاثَاتِ (v.4)",e:"تَوَّابًا (110:3) · حَمَّالَةَ (111:4) · الْخَنَّاسِ (114:4)",t:"Faʿʿāl intensive — habitual doers"},{w:"حَاسِدٍ (v.5)",e:"التَّكَاثُرُ (102:1) · هُمَزَةٍ (104:1)",t:"Envy — the soil of rivalry and slander"},{w:"1 Name vs 4 evils",e:"Nās 114: 3 Names vs 1 evil",t:"Bodily harm vs. harm to faith"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"أَعُوذُ", tr:"Aʿūdhu", v:"1", root:"The \"seeking refuge / clinging for protection\" family — ʿādha = to take refuge with, to cling to someone for safety; maʿādh = a refuge (maʿādha'llāh!", meaning:"\"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of the daybreak.\"", magic:"You say this word before every recitation — and the image is a child running to its mother.", grammar:"Hollow verb (ع-و-ذ), present 1st person. aʿūdhu bi- = \"I seek refuge IN/WITH\" — the bi- is fixed.", why:"ʿādha (clinging for refuge) — more intimate and urgent than istaʿāna (seeking help).", note:"MAʿĀDHA'LLĀH! = \"God forbid!\" — literally \"REFUGE in Allah!\" And aʿūdhu billāh before every recitation.", more:["= \"Allah forbid!\" — literally \"refuge with Allah!\"); taʿawwudh = saying aʿūdhu billāh.","Classical lexicographers connect it to a young animal pressing itself against its mother.","Aʿūdhu isn't just \"I ask for protection.\" It's I CLING — like a frightened child pressing against a parent, body to body. Not a request filed from a distance — a run toward safety.","And you already say it constantly: - aʿūdhu billāhi mina'sh-shayṭāni'r-rajīm — before every recitation (and rajīm you own from Takwīr 81:25!) - maʿādha'llāh! — \"God forbid!\" — literally \"refuge in Allah!\"","And the PRESENT tense aʿūdhu — \"I AM seeking refuge,\" now, ongoing. Not \"I sought\" — a refuge you live in continuously.","Compare Qāriʿah 101:9 — fa-ummuhu hāwiyah — \"his MOTHER [refuge] is the Abyss.\" There, the refuge-image was horrifying: the only place left to run was a pit. Here, the refuge is the Lord of the Daybreak.","ʿĀdha = a young animal PRESSING against its mother.","Compare Qāriʿah 101:9: his \"mother\" was the ABYSS — here it's the Lord of the Daybreak."]},
  {ar:"بِرَبِّ الْفَلَقِ", tr:"Bi-rabbi'l-falaq", v:"1", root:"The \"SPLITTING / cleaving apart\" family — falaqa = to split, to cleave (falaqa'l-ḥabba = He split the seed).", meaning:"\"the Lord of the Daybreak.\"", magic:"THE THIRD \"SPLITTING\" ROOT in your study — and the logic of the refuge is perfect.", grammar:"Bi-rabbi — bi- after aʿūdhu; iḍāfah (\"Lord of the daybreak\").", why:"Falaq (splitting dawn) — chosen precisely because every evil named next is a form of darkness.", note:"THREE \"SPLITTING\" ROOTS: f-j-r (FAJR, 89:1), f-ṭ-r (INFAṬARAT/FĀṬIR, 82:1), f-l-q (FALAQ, 113:1). Why \"Lord of the DAYBREAK\"?", more:["Al-falaq = DAYBREAK — the dawn SPLITTING the night; modern Arabic: falaq also = a split, a cleft.","You now own THREE different Arabic roots that all mean \"to split\" — and all used of dawn or creation: - F-J-R — fajr (Fajr 89:1, Qadr 97:5) — dawn BURSTING through - F-Ṭ-R — infaṭarat (Infiṭār 82:1) — the sky SPLITTING; Fāṭir = the Originator who split creation into being - F-L-Q — al-falaq (Falaq 113:1) — daybreak CLEAVING the night","And elsewhere the Qurʾān says: inna'llāha fāliqu'l-ḥabbi wa'n-nawā — \"Allah is the SPLITTER of the seed and the date-stone\" (6:95), and fāliqu'l-iṣbāḥ — \"SPLITTER of the dawn\" (6:96). The same verb for a seed cracking open in soil and the sun cracking open the sky.","NOW THE LOGIC OF THE REFUGE: why seek protection in \"the Lord of the Daybreak\" specifically? Because the evils that follow are DARK: the settling night (v.3), hidden sorcery (v.4), concealed envy (v.5). And who do you go to against darkness? The One who SPLITS darkness every single morning. Every dawn is evidence that He can tear open whatever night has closed over you.","And Rabb = the NURTURER, the Sustainer — not \"the Mighty\" — refuge in His care.","Because every evil that follows is DARK — and He splits darkness every morning.","Fāliq al-ḥabb = He splits the SEED (6:95)."]},
  {ar:"مِن شَرِّ مَا خَلَقَ", tr:"Min sharri mā khalaq", v:"2", root:"Sharr — EVIL / harm (also an irregular comparative \"worse\" — you own it from Bayyinah 98:6, sharru'l-bariyyah, and Zalzalah 99:8, sharran yarah).", meaning:"\"from the evil of what He created.\"", magic:"One grammatical choice here is a lesson in adab (courtesy) toward Allah.", grammar:"Min sharri = \"from the evil of\" — iḍāfah. mā khalaq — relative (\"what He created\").", why:"Mā khalaq (what He created) — broad enough to cover everything, while keeping evil off His Name.", note:"\"The evil OF what He created\" — NOT \"the evil He created.\" Evil is attached to CREATION, never to His Name.", more:["It doesn't say \"from the evil He created.\" It says min sharri MĀ KHALAQ — \"from the evil OF what He created.\" The evil is attributed to creation, not to the act of creating. He created things; some created things cause harm. Evil is never attached to His name directly.","This is a consistent Qurʾānic courtesy: Ibrāhīm says \"when I am ill, He heals me\" (26:80) — illness attributed to himself, healing to Allah. Same adab here.","And the STRUCTURE: general, then specific. v.2 = ALL harm from ALL creation (the umbrella). v.3–5 = three particular evils singled out (because they're hidden and especially dangerous). Like a prescription: a broad-spectrum cover, then three targeted ones.","Note sharr repeats FOUR times — min sharri… wa min sharri… wa min sharri… wa min sharri — the four evils, each introduced by the same phrase, like four doors being bolted one by one.","Like Ibrāhīm (26:80): \"when I am ill, HE heals me.\" General (v.2) → three specifics (v.3–5) — broad-spectrum cover, then targeted.","Sharr ×4 = four doors bolted."]},
  {ar:"غَاسِقٍ إِذَا وَقَبَ", tr:"Ghāsiqin idhā waqab", v:"3", root:"Ghāsiq — the \"DARKNESS deepening / intense gloom\" family — ghasaqa = for the night to become fully dark.", meaning:"\"and from the evil of darkness when it settles.\"", magic:"The root gives you a stunning link to Nabaʾ — and the night here is the opposite of Ḍuḥā's.", grammar:"Idhā + past = \"when it [does]\" — the evil is tied to a CONDITION: when it settles. Ghāsiq — active participle.", why:"Waqaba (settling like water in a hollow) — pictures darkness as something that fills a space and stays.", note:"Waqb = a HOLLOW where water POOLS. Darkness that has SETTLED around you. Nabaʾ 78:25: GHASSĀQ (Hell's dark foul drink).", more:["Ghasaq al-layl = the darkness of night (ṣalāh… ilā ghasaqi'l-layl, 17:78 — prayer times \"until the darkness of night\").","waqaba — the \"ENTERING / settling in / penetrating\" family — waqb = a HOLLOW in rock where water collects; waqaba = to enter and settle, to come down into.","Ghāsiq = darkness that has deepened fully. Waqaba = SETTLED IN, like water pooling in a hollow. So: the dark when it has fully sunk in and pooled around you. Why fear it? Because darkness is when harm moves unseen — predators, thieves, and the hidden evils that follow in v.4–5.","And you own this root from Nabaʾ 78:25 — illā ḥamīman wa GHASSĀQĀ — the dark, freezing, foul drink of Hell. Same root (gh-s-q): Nabaʾ: the ghassāq of Hell; Falaq: the ghāsiq of night — and you seek refuge from both.","(A narration from ʿĀʾishah reports the Prophet ﷺ pointing to the MOON and saying \"seek refuge from this, for it is the ghāsiq when it waqab\" — read as the moon when it eclipses/darkens. Both readings: darkness descending.)","And the night-verb thread across your surahs now has a dark pole: - Takwīr 81:17 — ʿasʿas (creeping) - Fajr 89:4 — yasr (travelling on) - Shams 91:4 / Layl 92:1 — yaghshā (covering) - Ḍuḥā 93:2 — sajā (going peacefully STILL — no threat at all) - Falaq 113:3 — waqab (SETTLING IN as a threat)","Ḍuḥā's still night is a comfort; Falaq's settled night is a danger. Same darkness, two experiences — and the difference is whether you're under His protection.","Note ghāsiqin is INDEFINITE — \"a darkness\" — any darkness, any night, any eclipse.","Falaq 113:3: GHĀSIQ (night's darkness).","Ḍuḥā 93:2: night goes STILL (comfort).","Falaq 113:3: night SETTLES IN (danger)."]},
  {ar:"النَّفَّاثَاتِ فِي الْعُقَدِ", tr:"An-naffāthāti fi'l-ʿuqad", v:"4", root:"Naffāthāt — the \"BLOWING with a light spray\" family — nafatha = to blow with a little spittle (between nafkh, pure blowing, and tafl, spitting); nafth is exactly what the Prophet ﷺ did into his hands with these very surahs!", meaning:"\"and from the evil of those who blow on knots\" — sorcerers, tying knots and blowing on them with spells.", magic:"The root of \"knot\" is the root of \"creed\" — and the juzʾ has taught you about untying.", grammar:"An-naffāthāt — definite (\"THE knot-blowers,\" a known category) vs v.3's indefinite ghāsiq.", why:"ʿuqad (knots) — names the actual mechanism of sorcery, and shares a root with the believer's counter-weapon (ʿaqīdah).", note:"ʿUqdah = A KNOT; ʿAQD = A CONTRACT; ʿAQĪDAH = CREED — ALL \"tying.\" Sorcerers tie knots to harm; the believer's ʿaqīdah is a knot tied to Allah.", more:["naffāthāt = faʿʿālāt intensive, feminine plural = those who HABITUALLY blow.","ʿuqad — plural of ʿuqdah = A KNOT — the root of ʿaqd = A CONTRACT and ʿaqīdah = CREED/BELIEF (what's firmly TIED in the heart!).","Sorcery in that world involved tying KNOTS in a cord and BLOWING spells into each one — binding the victim symbolically. Reports connect the revelation of the Muʿawwidhatān to an act of sorcery against the Prophet ﷺ involving a knotted cord; as each verse was recited, a knot loosened.","And the ʿ-q-d root is enormous: - ʿuqdah = A KNOT (here) - ʿaqd = A CONTRACT (a binding tie between parties — ʿaqd az-zawāj, a marriage contract!) - ʿaqīdah = CREED (belief firmly TIED in the heart)","So the sorcerer TIES knots to harm; the believer's protection is an ʿaqīdah — a different kind of knot, tied to Allah. Knots against knots.","And the UNTYING thread you own: - Balad 90:13 — fakku raqabah (UNTIE a slave's neck — the steep climb) - Bayyinah 98:1 — munfakkīn (not UNTIED from disbelief until the Evidence came) - Nāziʿāt 79:2 — an-nāshiṭāt (the soul UNTIED like a knot — gently drawn out) - Falaq 113:4 — fi'l-ʿuqad (knots TIED for harm)","The juzʾ praises untying — necks, hearts, souls — and seeks refuge from those who tie knots to hurt.","The FEMININE plural (naffāthāt) — classical readings: (a) an-nufūs — \"SOULS\" (feminine in Arabic) that blow — i.e. any practitioner, male or female; (b) female practitioners specifically, as reported in the incident. Reading (a) is widely held — it's about the act, not the gender.","And the faʿʿāl intensive — those who do it HABITUALLY — same shape as tawwāb (110:3), ḥammālah (111:4), khannās (114:4).","The irony that heals: nafth — the light blowing — is also exactly what the Prophet ﷺ did with these surahs into his palms. The same action: the sorcerer's nafth binds; the believer's nafth with the Qurʾān unbinds.","Fi'l-ʿuqad — \"ON/INTO the knots.\"","UNTYING thread: fakk (90:13), munfakkīn (98:1), nāshiṭāt (79:2) — the juzʾ praises untying.","Nafth = light blowing — what the sorcerer does, and what the Prophet ﷺ did with these surahs into his palms."]},
  {ar:"حَاسِدٍ إِذَا حَسَدَ", tr:"ḥāsidin idhā ḥasad", v:"5", root:"The \"ENVY\" family — ḥasada = to envy — to wish that someone LOSE a blessing they have (distinct from ghibṭah, wishing to have the same blessing yourself, which is permitted); ḥasad = envy.", meaning:"\"and from the evil of an envier when he envies.\"", magic:"The final evil — and the idhā condition is a mercy hidden in grammar.", grammar:"Idhā + past = conditional \"when.\" Ḥāsidin — active participle, indefinite.", why:"ḥasad with idhā ḥasad — names envy precisely and limits the harm to its activation.", note:"Ḥasad = wishing someone LOSES their blessing. Ghibṭah = wishing to HAVE it too (permitted).", more:["It doesn't say \"from the evil of an envier.\" It says ḥāsidin IDHĀ ḤASAD — \"an envier WHEN HE ENVIES.\" The harm is tied to the moment he ACTS on it — when the envy moves from feeling to deed (or to the evil eye). A passing feeling suppressed harms no one but its owner.","And notice the pairing of conditions: ghāsiqin IDHĀ waqab (v.3) and ḥāsidin IDHĀ ḥasad (v.5). Both evils are dangerous when ACTIVATED: darkness when it settles, envy when it acts. The middle one (v.4) has no idhā — sorcery is always active by definition.","And envy is the sin that sits at the root of the juzʾ's earlier stories: the rivalry of takāthur (102:1) — competing for more — is envy's soil. The humazah (104:1) jabs at others because their worth threatens his. Iblīs's first sin was envy of Ādam. Falaq ends on the evil that begins so many others.","Note ḥāsidin is INDEFINITE — \"an envier\" — any envier, anywhere. The surah's pattern: v.3 indefinite (ghāsiq), v.4 definite (an-naffāthāt), v.5 indefinite (ḥāsid). Definite for the known practitioners; indefinite for the darkness and envy that can come from anywhere.","The rhyme: falaq, khalaq, waqab, ʿuqad, ḥasad — a sharp -aq / -ad / -ab — clipped, guarding sounds.","\"An envier WHEN HE ENVIES\" — harm only when ACTIVATED.","Paired with v.3: darkness WHEN it settles, envy WHEN it acts.","Envy is the soil of takāthur (102:1) and humazah (104:1) — and Iblīs's first sin."]}
  ]
},
{
  num:114, name:"An-Nās", ar:"الناس", en:"Mankind", verses:6, place:"Makkan",
  blurb:"FĀTIḤAH: Rabb · Mālik · worship. NĀS: Rabb · Malik · Ilāh. The Qurʾān's first page and last page say the same three things. Last word: PEOPLE.",
  special:["The Qurʾān ENDS with the same three ideas it BEGINS with.","Al-Fātiḥah (the first surah): - Rabbi'l-ʿālamīn — LORD of the worlds - Māliki yawmi'd-dīn — KING/Master of the Day of Judgment - Iyyāka naʿbudu — You ALONE we WORSHIP","An-Nās (the last surah): - Rabbi'n-nās — LORD of mankind - Maliki'n-nās — KING of mankind - Ilāhi'n-nās — GOD (the one WORSHIPPED) of mankind","LORD · KING · GOD — at the first page and the last. The book closes on exactly the three truths it opened with. Everything in between unfolds those three.","And the very last word of the Qurʾān is an-nās — \"PEOPLE.\" The book ends on the ones it was sent to.","--"],
  big:["Three Names (v.1–3) — the Lord, the King, the God of mankind.","One enemy (v.4) — the whisperer who withdraws.","His method (v.5) — he whispers into people's chests.","His forms (v.6) — from jinn and from people."],
  traps:["رَبِّ · مَلِكِ · إِلَٰهِ (v.1–3) — NOT three synonyms. Nurturer (rubūbiyyah), Sovereign, and the One Worshipped (ulūhiyyah) — and they mirror Al-Fātiḥah exactly.","الْخَنَّاسِ (v.4) — same root as al-khunnas (Takwīr 81:15). \"The one who RETREATS\" — and the name tells you the defence: dhikr.","صُدُورِ (v.5) — \"CHESTS,\" not \"hearts\" — the ṣadr is the courtyard; he whispers at the GATE.","مِنَ الْجِنَّةِ وَالنَّاسِ (v.6) — whisperers come from BOTH jinn AND people (min bayāniyyah). Not only an unseen threat.","النَّاسِ is REPEATED, not replaced by a pronoun — five times in five verses after v.1 — each Name gets full weight.","مَلِكِ (v.2) — KING (malik) — compare Fātiḥah 1:4, which has two authentic readings: Māliki (Owner) and Maliki (King).","الْوَسْوَاسِ (v.4) — a REDUPLICATED root — originally the faint jingle of jewellery. The noun names the whisperer himself.","الْجِنَّةِ (v.6) — \"the JINN\" (collective), not al-jannah (the Garden) — same root (janna, to cover), different word. Watch the vowels.","يُوَسْوِسُ (v.5) — PRESENT = continuous, matched by v.1's present aʿūdhu.","The LAST WORD of the Qurʾān is an-nās — \"PEOPLE\" — the ones the whole book was sent to."],
  links:[{w:"رَبِّ · مَلِكِ · إِلَٰهِ (v.1–3)",e:"Al-Fātiḥah 1:2, 1:4, 1:5",t:"The Qurʾān's FIRST and LAST surahs: Lord · King · Worship"},{w:"قُلْ أَعُوذُ (v.1)",e:"قُلْ أَعُوذُ (Falaq 113:1)",t:"The Muʿawwidhatān — 1 Name/4 evils vs 3 Names/1 evil"},{w:"النَّاسِ (v.1–6)",e:"نِسْيَان · فَلَا تَنسَىٰ (87:6)",t:"Nās ← nisyān — the forgetting creature"},{w:"مَلِكِ (v.2)",e:"لَا تَمْلِكُ نَفْسٌ (82:19) · مُلْكُ (85:9) · يَمْلِكُونَ (78:37)",t:"No one owns anything — there is one KING"},{w:"إِلَٰهِ (v.3)",e:"لَا أَعْبُدُ مَا تَعْبُدُونَ (Kāfirūn 109:2)",t:"Refusing false gods / refuge in the true one"},{w:"الْوَسْوَاسِ (v.4)",e:"عَسْعَسَ (81:17) · دَمْدَمَ (91:14) · زُلْزِلَتْ (99:1)",t:"The reduplicated-root family"},{w:"الْخَنَّاسِ (v.4)",e:"بِالْخُنَّسِ (Takwīr 81:15)",t:"SAME ROOT — stars retreat at sunrise; the whisperer at dhikr"},{w:"الْخَنَّاسِ (v.4)",e:"76:29 · 80:11 · 81:27 · 87:9, 15 · 88:21 · 89:23",t:"The whole juzʾ's dhikr thread lands here"},{w:"الْخَنَّاسِ (v.4)",e:"تَوَّابًا (Naṣr 110:3)",t:"Two \"returners\" (faʿʿāl) — mercy vs. whispering"},{w:"صُدُورِ (v.5)",e:"صَدْرَكَ (94:1) · الصُّدُورِ (100:10) · يَصْدُرُ (99:6)",t:"The chest: EXPANDED → WHISPERED into → EXTRACTED"},{w:"الْجِنَّةِ (v.6)",e:"بِمَجْنُونٍ (81:22) · جَنَّتِي (89:30)",t:"Janna = to COVER — hidden ones, the shaded Garden, a covered mind"},{w:"النَّاسِ (last word)",e:"رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ (Fātiḥah 1:2)",t:"From \"the WORLDS\" to \"PEOPLE\" — first verse to last word"}],
  words:[
  {ar:"بِرَبِّ النَّاسِ", tr:"Bi-rabbi'n-nās", v:"1", root:"Rabb — the \"NURTURING / raising / sustaining\" family — rabba = to raise a child, to nurture something step by step to completion; tarbiyah = UPBRINGING / education; rabīb = a stepchild (one raised in your home).", meaning:"\"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind.\"", magic:"The first of three Names — and the first is the most tender.", grammar:"Bi-rabbi — bi- after aʿūdhu (fixed). Iḍāfah — \"Lord of mankind.\"", why:"Rabb first — because a frightened creature runs first to the one who raised it.", note:"TARBIYAH = UPBRINGING — Rabb = the one who RAISED you. RABB (as a child) → MALIK (as you grow) → ILĀH (as you mature).", more:["nās — PEOPLE / mankind — classically linked to uns (familiarity, intimacy) and, by a famous play, to nisyān (FORGETTING): \"man was called insān because of his nisyān.\"","Rabb isn't \"Lord\" in the sense of a ruler. It's the one who RAISES you — feeds you, grows you, brings you step by step to maturity. Tarbiyah = upbringing. The first Name you run to is the One who raised you.","And the three Names follow the order of a human life: - RABB — as a CHILD, you first know the one who raises and feeds you - MALIK — as you GROW, you learn there is an authority over you - ILĀH — as you MATURE, you understand Who deserves your worship","Nurture → authority → worship. Classical scholars note this ascending order.","And an-nās — the classical play on nisyān (forgetting) is perfect for this surah: humans FORGET — and the whisperer (v.4) works precisely through forgetting. The one who withdraws (khannās) returns whenever you forget Allah. The cure for a forgetful creature is dhikr — which the whole juzʾ has commanded.","And you own the forgetting thread: Aʿlā 87:6 — sa-nuqriʾuka fa-lā tansā (\"you will NOT forget\") and Fajr 89:23 — yatadhakkaru'l-insān (man REMEMBERS — too late).","Nās ← nisyān (FORGETTING) — \"man is called insān for his forgetting\" — and the whisperer works through forgetting."]},
  {ar:"مَلِكِ النَّاسِ", tr:"Maliki'n-nās", v:"2", root:"The \"OWNERSHIP / sovereignty / kingship\" family — malik = a KING (one with authority); mālik = an OWNER.", meaning:"\"the King of mankind.\"", magic:"The second Name — and it closes a thread from Infiṭār and Fātiḥah.", grammar:"Maliki — genitive, a badal/ṣifah of rabbi (v.1). v.1–3 all hang off the bi- of aʿūdhu.", why:"Malik (king) — the authority who can expel an intruder from his realm.", note:"Fātiḥah 1:4: MĀLIKI/MALIKI yawmi'd-dīn (King of the Day). Nās 114:2: MALIKI'n-nās (King of PEOPLE).", more:["Mulk = dominion; mamlakah = a KINGDOM; malak = an ANGEL (different root, similar shape).","Al-Fātiḥah 1:4 — Māliki yawmi'd-dīn — with TWO established recitations: MĀLIKI (OWNER/Master) and MALIKI (KING). Both are authentic. An-Nās 114:2 — MALIKI'n-nās — KING.","Fātiḥah says He is King/Owner of the Day of Judgment; Nās says He is King of mankind — now, every day. Sovereign over the end AND over the present.","And the m-l-k thread across your surahs: - Nabaʾ 78:37 — lā yamlikūna minhu khiṭābā (they possess no right to address Him) - Infiṭār 82:19 — yawma lā tamliku nafsun li-nafsin shayʾā (no soul OWNS anything for another) - Burūj 85:9 — alladhī lahu mulku's-samāwāti wa'l-arḍ (His is the DOMINION) - Nās 114:2 — Maliki'n-nās (the KING of people)","No one owns anything for anyone on that Day (Infiṭār) — because there is only one Malik.","And why \"KING\" when facing the whisperer? Because a whisperer is an INTRUDER in the King's territory. Your chest belongs to His dominion; you call the Sovereign to expel the trespasser.","Note the repetition: an-nās is NOT replaced by a pronoun. It doesn't say \"Lord of mankind, their King, their God.\" It REPEATS an-nās each time — five times in six verses. Each Name gets its full, separate weight.","Sovereign over the END and the NOW.","The whisperer is a TRESPASSER in the King's realm.","An-nās repeated 5× — no pronoun shortcut."]},
  {ar:"إِلَٰهِ النَّاسِ", tr:"Ilāhi'n-nās", v:"3", root:"The \"the one WORSHIPPED / adored / turned to\" family — ilāh = a god, the one worshipped.", meaning:"\"the God of mankind.\"", magic:"The third Name — and it's the word at the centre of the shahādah.", grammar:"Ilāhi — genitive, third in the chain after bi-. Three Names in apposition, each in full iḍāfah with an-nās.", why:"Ilāh last — because worship is the culmination, and the very thing the enemy targets.", note:"LĀ ILĀHA ILLA'LLĀH — the ilāh of the shahādah. Fātiḥah's iyyāka naʿbudu (You alone we WORSHIP) = Nās's Ilāh.", more:["Allāh (by most accounts: al-ilāh, \"THE God\"); lā ilāha illa'llāh = \"no ilāh except Allah\" — the shahādah itself.","Classical lexicographers connect the root to being devoted to, and to finding refuge and tranquility in someone.","Ilāh = the one WORSHIPPED. Every time you say lā ilāha illa'llāh, you use this word. \"There is no ilāh — no one worthy of worship — except Allah.\"","And it maps onto Fātiḥah's third element: iyyāka naʿbudu — \"You ALONE we WORSHIP\" = ilāh (the one worshipped). The Qurʾān's first and last surahs end their triad on worship.","And the three Names together are the classical three dimensions of tawḥīd: - Rabb — tawḥīd ar-rubūbiyyah — He alone creates and sustains - Malik — His sole sovereignty and ownership (also within rubūbiyyah, and His Names and Attributes) - Ilāh — tawḥīd al-ulūhiyyah — He alone deserves worship","And here's why it ends on ilāh: the whisperer's whole project is to redirect your worship — to make you serve your desire (hawā, Nāziʿāt 79:40), your wealth (takāthur, 102:1), your image (riyāʾ, 107:6). So the final Name you seek refuge in is the One who alone deserves to be worshipped — the exact thing the whisperer is trying to steal.","And compare Kāfirūn 109:2 — lā aʿbudu mā taʿbudūn — \"I do not worship what you worship.\" Kāfirūn refused false ilāhs; Nās takes refuge in the true one.","The three dimensions of tawḥīd: RABB (creating/sustaining) · MALIK (sovereignty) · ILĀH (worship).","The whisperer wants to steal your WORSHIP — so the last Name is the One who deserves it."]},
  {ar:"الْوَسْوَاسِ", tr:"Al-waswās", v:"4", root:"A REDUPLICATED root (w-s-w-s) — originally the soft, repeated JINGLING of jewellery (waswās al-ḥulī).", meaning:"\"from the evil of the whisperer…\"", magic:"The reduplicated root is the sound of the enemy — and you've learned the whole family.", grammar:"Quadriliteral reduplicated root (faʿlāl pattern noun). Genitive after min sharri.", why:"Waswās — because its sound and its origin (faint jingling) capture an enemy who works quietly and repeatedly, not loudly.", note:"WASWĀS = the faint JINGLE of jewellery — quiet, repeated, impossible to fully silence.", more:["A faint, continuous, almost-inaudible sound — from that: waswasa = to WHISPER repeatedly, to insinuate; waswasah = an intrusive whisper / obsessive doubt; and modern Arabic uses waswās for OBSESSIVE thoughts.","The original image: the faint tinkle of a bracelet or anklet — soft, repeated, barely noticed, impossible to fully silence. That's the whisper: not a shout you'd resist, but a quiet, recurring jingle in the background of your mind.","And the reduplicated-root family you now own — all REPEATED, relentless actions: - ʿasʿasa — the night creeping (Takwīr 81:17) - damdama — crushing, blow on blow (Shams 91:14) - zalzala — shaking convulsively (Zalzalah 99:1) - waswasa — whispering, again and again (Nās 114:4)","Say it: was-wās. The sound IS the whisper — soft sibilants, repeated. The whole surah rides that s-sound: an-nās, al-waswās, al-khannās — you're hissing as you recite.","And the noun al-waswās is used for the WHISPERER himself — a verbal noun used as a name: he is so defined by whispering that he IS the whisper.","The clinical hook: waswās = obsessive, intrusive thoughts in modern Arabic (and religious OCD is called waswasah). The word you'd know from patients.","Reduplicated family: ʿasʿasa (81:17), damdama (91:14), zalzala (99:1), waswasa (114:4). Say was-wās — you're whispering.","Waswās = OBSESSIVE THOUGHTS in modern Arabic."]},
  {ar:"الْخَنَّاسِ", tr:"Al-khannās", v:"4", root:"The \"SHRINKING BACK / withdrawing / retreating and hiding\" family.", meaning:"\"…the one who withdraws [and returns].\"", magic:"THE CROSS-SURAH PAYOFF YOU'VE BEEN WAITING FOR — Takwīr told you this word nearly thirty surahs ago.", grammar:"Faʿʿāl intensive. A second description (ṣifah) of al-waswās.", why:"Khannās — because naming him \"the one who retreats\" hands you the weapon: remembrance.", note:"Takwīr 81:15: AL-KHUNNAS = stars that RETREAT by day. Nās 114:4: AL-KHANNĀS = the whisperer who RETREATS. Same root.", more:["Khanasa = to withdraw, to slink back, to retreat out of sight; khannās = faʿʿāl intensive = one who HABITUALLY retreats — pulls back, then returns, again and again.","Takwīr 81:15 — fa-lā uqsimu bi'l-KHUNNAS — \"I swear by the RETREATING ones\" — the stars and planets that disappear by day and reappear by night. Visible, then hidden, then visible again. Nās 114:4 — al-KHANNĀS — the whisperer who retreats — and returns.","SAME ROOT, same behaviour: appearing, vanishing, reappearing. The stars retreat when the SUN rises; the whisperer retreats when DHIKR rises.","And that's the method of defence, built into his name. A well-known narration (Ibn ʿAbbās and others): \"Satan is crouched upon the heart of the son of Ādam. When he forgets and is heedless, he whispers. When he remembers Allah, he withdraws (khanasa).\" His name tells you how to defeat him: remember Allah, and he SHRINKS BACK. Forget, and he RETURNS.","And so the WHOLE JUZʾ'S DHIKR THREAD lands here: - Insān 76:29 / ʿAbasa 80:11 — innahā tadhkirah (it is a REMINDER) - Takwīr 81:27 — in huwa illā dhikr (only a REMINDER) - Aʿlā 87:9 — fa-dhakkir (so REMIND) - Aʿlā 87:15 — wa dhakara'sma rabbihi fa-ṣallā (REMEMBERED his Lord's name and prayed) - Ghāshiyah 88:21 — innamā anta mudhakkir (you are only a REMINDER) - Fajr 89:23 — wa annā lahu'dh-dhikrā (what use is REMEMBERING now?) - Nās 114:4 — al-khannās — the one DEFEATED by remembrance","The juzʾ commanded dhikr for thirty-seven surahs; its last surah names the enemy that dhikr defeats.","And khannās is a faʿʿāl intensive — the last one in the Qurʾān: retreating and returning, HABITUALLY. Like tawwāb (110:3) — Allah who returns in mercy without limit — and khannās who returns in whispering without limit. The contest is between two returners: who do you let in?","Stars retreat when the SUN rises; the whisperer retreats when DHIKR rises.","\"When he remembers Allah, he withdraws.\" The juzʾ commanded dhikr for 37 surahs — its last names the enemy dhikr defeats."]},
  {ar:"يُوَسْوِسُ فِي صُدُورِ النَّاسِ", tr:"Yuwaswisu fī ṣudūri'n-nās", v:"5", root:"Yuwaswisu — the verb of waswās (v.4) — he WHISPERS (present = continuously).", meaning:"\"who whispers into the chests of mankind.\"", magic:"Why \"CHESTS\" and not \"HEARTS\"? — and the ṣadr thread closes here.", grammar:"Quadriliteral present (yuwaswisu). Fī = \"in/into.\" Alladhī — relative pronoun describing al-waswās.", why:"ṣudūr (chests — the courtyard) rather than qulūb — because the whisper reaches the doorway; entry is your choice.", note:"\"Into the CHESTS,\" not the HEARTS — the ṣadr is the COURTYARD / entrance hall around the heart.", more:["ṣudūr — plural of ṣadr = THE CHEST — the front, the courtyard around the heart.","It says fī ṣudūri'n-nās — \"in the CHESTS,\" not fī qulūbi'n-nās (\"in the hearts\"). Classical scholars explain: the ṣadr is the courtyard around the heart — its entrance hall. The whisperer works at the GATE — he presents his suggestions at the entrance. Whether they get into the HEART depends on whether you let them in.","So the verse is quietly hopeful: he only reaches the doorway. The heart's lock is yours.","And the ṣadr thread across your surahs: - Sharḥ 94:1 — a-lam nashraḥ laka ṣadrak (\"did We not EXPAND your chest?\" — a gift) - ʿĀdiyāt 100:10 — wa ḥuṣṣila mā fi'ṣ-ṣudūr (what is in the chests is EXTRACTED — judgment) - Zalzalah 99:6 — yaṣduru'n-nās (people ISSUE FORTH — same root, maṣdar, source) - Nās 114:5 — yuwaswisu fī ṣudūri'n-nās (the whisperer works at the chest — the battleground)","The chest that can be EXPANDED by Allah (Sharḥ), WHISPERED into by the enemy (Nās), and EXTRACTED on the Day (ʿĀdiyāt). What you allow into it now is what's pulled out of it then.","And the PRESENT tense yuwaswisu — he whispers continuously, now. Matched by v.1's present aʿūdhu — I seek refuge continuously, now. An ongoing threat, an ongoing refuge.","And an-nās AGAIN — the fourth time. The whisperer targets people — the same people whose Lord, King, and God you just named. He's working in territory that belongs to Another.","He whispers at the GATE; you hold the lock.","Ṣadr: EXPANDED by Allah (Sharḥ 94:1) → WHISPERED into (Nās 114:5) → EXTRACTED on the Day (ʿĀdiyāt 100:10). What you let in now is what's pulled out then."]},
  {ar:"مِنَ الْجِنَّةِ وَالنَّاسِ", tr:"Mina'l-jinnati wa'n-nās", v:"6", root:"Jinnah — the JINN (collective) — from janna = TO COVER / conceal — the same root as jannah (a garden.", meaning:"\"from among the jinn and mankind.\"", magic:"The final verse of the Qurʾān — and it carries two lessons, then one word.", grammar:"Min here is bayāniyyah (explanatory) — \"[whisperers] consisting of jinn and people.\" Al-jinnah — collective noun with tāʾ marbūṭah.", why:"Ending on an-nās — because the Qurʾān is addressed to people, and its final protection is for them.", note:"Whisperers come from JINN and PEOPLE — the friend, the feed, the voice that normalizes sin.", more:["Covered in foliage), janīn (a fetus — covered in the womb), majnūn (one whose mind is covered), junnah (a shield).","LESSON ONE — the whisperer has TWO forms. Classical reading: whisperers come from the jinn (unseen) AND from people (seen). The friend who talks you into the sin, the voice that makes the forbidden seem normal, the feed that normalizes what you knew was wrong — human waswās. Seek refuge from both.","LESSON TWO — the j-n-n (\"covering\") root. You own it from Takwīr 81:22 — wa mā ṣāḥibukum bi-majnūn — \"your companion is not MAD\" (mind not covered). And from Fajr 89:30 — wa'dkhulī jannatī — \"enter My GARDEN.\" Same root: jinn = the hidden ones; jannah = the covered (shaded) garden; majnūn = a covered mind. The hidden whisperer would keep you from the shaded Garden — by covering your mind.","And then the FINAL WORD: AN-NĀS.","The sixth time in six verses — an-nās ends every single verse of this surah. And it is the LAST WORD of the entire Qurʾān.","The book begins: al-ḥamdu lillāhi rabbi'l-ʿālamīn — \"Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds.\" It ends: mina'l-jinnati wa'n-NĀS — \"…from the jinn and PEOPLE.\" From the worlds to people. The widest possible frame at the start; the ones it was actually sent to, at the close.","And remember the classical play on nās ← nisyān (forgetting). The Qurʾān's last word may whisper its last lesson: you are the forgetting creature — so you were sent a Reminder (dhikr, tadhkirah), and taught the one practice that makes the whisperer retreat.","The rhyme: every verse ends in -ās — an-nās, an-nās, an-nās, al-khannās, an-nās, an-nās — the softest, most sustained ending in the Qurʾān. The book closes in a whisper — and the whisper is about Allah, not against Him.","Janna = TO COVER: jinn (hidden), jannah (shaded GARDEN, 89:30), majnūn (covered mind, 81:22), janīn (fetus).","THE LAST WORD OF THE QURʾĀN: AN-NĀS — PEOPLE.","First verse: \"Lord of the WORLDS.\" Last word: \"PEOPLE.\" The book closes in a whisper — about Allah."]}
  ]
}
];
